mistaken notion that the palate is the organ of taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hard task! to hit the <qex>palate</qex> of such guests.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>Mental relish; intellectual
taste.</def> <rj><au>T. Baker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A projection in the throat of such
flowers as the snapdragon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To perceive by the taste.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatial</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*la"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palatium</ets> palace. See <er>Palace</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to a palace; suitable for a palace; resembling a palace;
royal; magnificent; <as>as, <ex>palatial</ex> structures</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Palatial</xex> style.<rdquo/ <rj><au>A.
Drummond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatial</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*la"tial</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<it>From</it>
<er>Palate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Palatal; palatine.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatial</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*la"tial</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A palatal letter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Jones.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Palatal; palatine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lat"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Phon.)</fld> <def>A
palatal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palatinate</ent><br/
<hw>pa*lat"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>palatinat</ets>. See <er>Palatine</er>.]</ety> <def>The province or
seigniory of a palatine; the dignity of a palatine.</def>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lat"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>Either of
two regions in Germany, formerly divisions of the Holy Roman Empire; the
Lower Palatinate or Rhine Palatinate is now within the
Rhineland-Palatinate; the Upper Palatinate is now within Bavaria. It is
usually referred to as <altname>the Palatinate</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <b>Palatinate The</b>
<pr>(p<acr/*l<acr/t"<icr/*n<asl/t)</pr>. <ety>[F. Palatinat, G. Pfalz,
ML. Palatinatus, the province of count palatine, from palatinatus,
palatine.]</ety> A former German State. Its territories were originally
in the region of the Rhine, and from the 14th century to 1620 embraced
two separate regions, the Rhine (or Lower) Palatinate (distinctively the
Palatinate), and the Upper Palatinate (see below). The palsgraves on the
Rhine, whose original seat was at Aix-la-Chapelle, were important
princes of the empire as early as the 11th century. Early in the 13th
century the Palatinate passed to the Bavarian dynasty of Wittelsbach,
which soon after branched off into the Bavarian and palatine lines. The
Palatinate was enlarged early in the 14th Century with a part of Bavaria
(the Upper Palatinate). The Golden Bull of 1356 designated the
Palatinate as one of the seven electorates. In the 16th century
Heidelberg, the capital of the electors palatine, became a great center
of Calvinism. The elector <person>Frederick V.</person>, having accepted
the Bohemian crown in 1619, and having been overthrown in 1620, was
stripped of his dominions. The electoral dignity was transferred to
Bavaria in 1623, and the Upper Palatinate was annexed to it. By the
treaty of 1648 the Rhine Palatinate was restored to its former rulers,
and an eighth electorate created for it, the Upper Palatinate being
confirmed to Bavaria. The Rhine palatinate was terribly ravaged by the
French in 1674 and 1689. The Palatinate and the Bavarian lands were
united in 1777. In 1801 the Rhine Palatinate was divided: all west of
the Rhine was ceded to France; Baden received Heidelberg, Mannheim,
etc.; and the rest fell to Hesse-Darmstadt, Nassau, etc. By the treaties
of 1814-15 the French portion west of the Rhine was restored to Germany:
Prussia and Hesse-Darmstadt received portions, but the greater portion
fell to Bavaria. This part is the present Rhine Palatinate, or Lower
Palatinate (G. Rheinpfalz or Unterpfalz): it is bounded by the Rhine on
the east, and borders on Hesse, Prussia, and Alsace-Lorraine. It forms a
"Regierungs-bezirk" of Bavaria, with Spires as Capital. It is traversed
by the Hardt Mountains, and produces grain, wine, coal, etc. Area, 2,289
square miles. Population (1890), 728,339. The Upper Palatinate (9.
Oberpfalz) forms a "Regierungs-bezirk" of Bavaria under the title Upper
Palatinate and Ratisbon (Regensburg). It borders on Bohemia. Capital,
Ratisbon. It has extensive forests and flourishing industries. Area,
3,729 square miles. Population (1890), 537,954.</note><br/
[<source>Century Dict., 1906</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lat"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make a
palatinate of.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palatine</ent><br/
<hw>pal"a*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>palatin</ets>, L. <ets>palatinus</ets>, fr. <ets>palatium</ets>.
See <er>Palace</er>, and cf. <er>Paladin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to a palace, or to a high officer of a
palace;</def> <specif>hence,</specif> <def>possessing royal
privileges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Palatinate.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a Palatine{1}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Count palatine</b></col>, <col><b>County
palatine</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Count</er>, and
<er>County</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Palatine hill</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>The palatine</b></col></mcol>, <cd>one of the seven
hills of Rome, once occupied by the palace of the C<ae/sars. See also
<er>Palatine Hill</er> in the vocabulary, and
<er>Palace</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatine</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"a*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
invested with royal privileges and rights within his domains; a count
palatine. See <cref>Count palatine</cref>, under 4th
<er>Count</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The Palatine hill in Rome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palatine</ent><br/
<hw>pal"a*tine</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From Palate.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the palate;
palatal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Palatine bones</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a pair
of bones (often united in the adult) in the root of the mouth, back of
and between the maxillaries.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatine</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"a*tine</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A palatine
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatine Hill</ent><br/
<hw>Palatine Hill</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"<adot/*t<imac/n)</pr>, <pos>prop.
n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Mons Palatinus</ets>, It. <ets>Monte
Palatino</ets>.]</ety> <def>One of the "seven hills" of Rome, situated
southeast of the Capitoline and north-northeast of the Aventine. It
borders on the Roman Forum; is the traditional seat of the city founded
by Romulus; was the seat of private and later of imperial residences;
and contains many antiquities.</def><br/
[<source>Century Dict., 1906</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palative</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pleasing to the
taste; palatable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Palative</xex>
delights.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatize</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"a*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To modify, as
the tones of the voice, by means of the palate; to palatalize; <as>as,
to <ex>palatize</ex> a letter or sound</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pal`a*ti*za"tion</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>J. Peile.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palato-</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"a*to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From <er>Palate</er>.]</ety>
<def>A combining form used in anatomy to indicate <sig>relation to the
palate</sig>, or <sig>connection with the palate</sig>; as in
<ex>palatolingual</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatonares</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal`a*to*na"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Palato-</er>, and <er>Nares</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The posterior nares. See <er>Nares</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palatopterygoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`a*top*ter"y*goid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Palato-</ets> + <ets>pterygoid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the palatine and pterygoid region
of the skull; <as>as, the <ex>palatopterygoid</ex> cartilage, or rod,
from which the palatine and pterygoid bones are
developed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palaver</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*la"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>palabra</ets>, or Pg. <ets>palavra</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>parabola</ets> a comparison, a parable, LL., a word. See
<er>Parable</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Talk; conversation; esp., idle or beguiling talk;
talk intended to deceive; flattery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In Africa, a parley with the natives; a talk; hence,
a public conference and deliberation; a debate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This epoch of parliaments and eloquent <qex>palavers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palaver</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*la"ver</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Palavered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Palavering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make palaver with,
or to; to used palaver; to talk idly or deceitfully; to employ flattery;
to cajole; <as>as, to <ex>palaver</ex> artfully</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Palavering</qex> the little language for her benefit.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Bront<eacute/</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palaverer</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*la"ver*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who palavers;
a flatterer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pale</ent><br/
<hw>Pale</hw> <pr>(p<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Paler</adjf>
<pr>(p<amac/l"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Palest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[F. <ets>p<acir/le</ets>, fr.
<ets>p<acir/lir</ets> to turn pale, L. <ets>pallere</ets> to be or look
pale. Cf. <er>Appall</er>, <er>Fallow</er>, <er>pall</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>, <er>Pallid</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Wanting in color; not ruddy; dusky white; pallid;
wan; <as>as, a <ex>pale</ex> face; a <ex>pale</ex> red; a <ex>pale</ex>
blue.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Pale</xex> as a forpined ghost.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Speechless he stood and <qex>pale</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are not of complexion red or <qex>pale</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Randolph.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not bright or brilliant; of a faint luster or hue;
dim; <as>as, the <ex>pale</ex> light of the moon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The night, methinks, is but the daylight sick;<br/
It looks a little <qex>paler</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pale</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>pale</xex>-colored,
<xex>pale</xex>-eyed, <xex>pale</xex>-faced, <xex>pale</xex>-looking,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pale</ent><br/
<hw>Pale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Paleness; pallor.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pale</ent><br/
<hw>Pale</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paled</conjf> <pr>(p<amac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To turn pale; to lose color or
luster.</def> <rj><au>Whittier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Apt to <qex>pale</qex> at a trodden worm.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pale</ent><br/
<hw>Pale</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make pale; to diminish the
brightness of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The glowworm shows the matin to be near,<br/
And 'gins to <qex>pale</qex> his uneffectual fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pale</ent><br/
<hw>Pale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pal</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>palus</ets>: cf. D. <ets>paal</ets>. See <er>Pole</er> a stake, and
1st <er>Pallet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pointed stake or slat,
either driven into the ground, or fastened to a rail at the top and
bottom, for fencing or inclosing; a picket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Deer creep through when a <qex>pale</qex> tumbles down.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which incloses or fences in; a boundary; a
limit; a fence; a palisade.</def> <ldquo/Within one <xex>pale</xex> or
hedge.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Robynson (More's Utopia).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A space or field having bounds or limits; a limited
region or place; an inclosure; -- often used figuratively.</def>
<ldquo/To walk the studious cloister's <xex>pale</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au> <ldquo/Out of the <xex>pale</xex> of
civilization.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A region within specified
bounds, whether or not enclosed or demarcated.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A stripe or band, as on a garment.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>One of the greater ordinaries,
being a broad perpendicular stripe in an escutcheon, equally distant
from the two edges, and occupying one third of it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A cheese scoop.</def>
<rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A shore for bracing a
timber before it is fastened.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>English pale</b></col>, <col><b>Irish
pale</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Hist.)</fld>, <cd>the limits or territory in
Eastern Ireland within which alone the English conquerors of Ireland
held dominion for a long period after their invasion of the country by
<person>Henry II</person> in 1172. See note, below.</cd> --
<col><b>beyond the pale</b></col> <cd>outside the limits of what is
allowed or proper; also, outside the limits within which one is
protected.</cd> <rj><au>Spencer.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note> <b>The English Pale</b>. That part of
<country>Ireland</country> in which English law was acknowledged, and
within which the dominion of the English was restricted, for some
centuries after the conquests of <person>Henry II</person>.
<persfn>John</persfn> distributed the part of <country>Ireland</country>
then subject to England into 12 counties palatine, and this region
became subsequently known as the <ex>Pale</ex>, but the limits varied at
different times.</note><br/
[<source>Century Dict., 1906</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pale</ent><br/
<hw>Pale</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To inclose with pales, or as with
pales; to encircle; to encompass; to fence off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Your isle, which stands] ribbed and <qex>paled</qex> in<br/
With rocks unscalable and roaring waters.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"le*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pale<ae/</plw> <pr>(-<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.,
chaff.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The interior chaff or
husk of grasses.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the chaffy scales or
bractlets growing on the receptacle of many compound flowers, as the
Coreopsis, the sunflower, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A pendulous process of the skin
on the throat of a bird, as in the turkey; a dewlap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palea</ets> chaff.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Chaffy; resembling
or consisting of pale<ae/, or chaff; furnished with chaff; <as>as, a
<ex>paleaceous</ex> receptacle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palearctic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*arc"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>arctic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging to a
region of the earth's surface which includes all Europe to the Azores,
Iceland, and all temperate Asia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paled</ent><br/
<hw>Paled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 5th
<er>Pale</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Striped.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/[Buskins] . . . <xex>paled</xex> part per
part.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclosed with a paling.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>paled</xex> green.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleechinoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`le*<eum/ch`i*noi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Paleo-</er>, and <er>Echinoidea</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extinct order of sea urchins found in the
Paleozoic rocks. They had more than twenty vertical rows of plates.
Called also <altname>Palaeechini</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Palaeechinoidea</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paleencephalon</ent><br/
<hw>paleencephalon</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The more primitive parts of
the brain phylogenetically; it includes most structures other than the
cerebral cortex.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paleoencephalon, palaeoencephalon.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1032 --></p>

<p><ent>Paleface</ent><br/
<hw>Pale"face`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/l"f<amac/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
white person; -- an appellation supposed to have been applied to the
whites by the American Indians.</def> <rj><au>J. F.
Cooper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleichthyes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`le*ich"thy*es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Paleo-</er>, and <er>Ichthyology</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A comprehensive division of fishes which includes the elasmobranchs
and ganoids.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Pal<ae/ichthyes</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palely</ent><br/
<hw>Pale"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Pale</ets>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>In a pale manner; dimly;
wanly; not freshly or ruddily.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palempore</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`em*pore"</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l`<ecr/m*p<omac/r")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A superior kind of dimity made in India, -- used for bed
coverings.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>palampore</asp>,
<asp>palampoor</asp>, etc.]</altsp> <rj><au>De Colange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleness</ent><br/
<hw>Pale"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
condition of being pale; want of freshness or ruddiness; a sickly
whiteness; lack of color or luster; wanness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The blood the virgin's cheek forsook;<br/
A livid <qex>paleness</qex> spreads o'er all her look.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palenque</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*len"que</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>A collective name for the Indians of <country>Nicaragua</country>
and <country>Honduras</country>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"le*o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>palaio`s</grk>,
adj.]</ety> <def>A combining form meaning <sig>old</sig>,
<sig>ancient</sig>; <as>as, <ex>pale</ex>arctic, <ex>pale</ex>ontology,
<ex>paleo</ex>there, <ex>paleo</ex>graphy</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>pal<ae/o-</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleobotanist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*bot"a*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed
in paleobotany.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleobotany</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*bot"a*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>botany</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of
paleontology which treats of fossil plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleocarida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`le*o*car"ida</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>palaio`s</grk> ancient + <grk>kari`s</grk>,
<grk>-i`dos</grk>, a kind of crustacean.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Merostomata</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<ets>Pal<ae/ocarida</ets>.]</altsp> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleocrinoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`le*o*cri*noi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Paleo-</er>, and <er>Crinoidea</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of <ord>Crinoidea</ord> found chiefly
in the Paleozoic rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleocrystic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*crys"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>kry`stallos</grk> ice.]</ety>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or derived from, a former glacial
formation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleogaean</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*g<ae/"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>gai^a</grk> the earth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Eastern
hemisphere.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pal<ae/og<ae/an</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paleogeography</ent><br/
<hw>paleogeography</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The study of the geography of
ancient times or ancient epochs.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> palaeogeography.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paleogeology</ent><br/
<hw>paleogeology</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The study of geologic features
once at the surface of the earth but now buried beneath rocks.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> palaeogeology.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"le*o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An ancient
manuscript.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleographer</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*og"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in paleography; a paleographist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleographical</ent><br/
<ent>Paleographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa`le*o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pa`le*o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pal<eacute/ographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to paleography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleographist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*og"ra*phist</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One versed in paleography; a paleographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleography</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*og"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pal<eacute/ographie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An ancient
manner of writing; ancient writings, collectively; <as>as, Punic
<ex>paleography</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The study of ancient inscriptions and modes of
writing; the art or science of deciphering ancient writings, and
determining their origin, period, etc., from external characters;
diplomatics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleola</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*le"o*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Paleol<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>palea</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A diminutive or
secondary palea; a lodicule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleolith</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"le*o*lith</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"l<esl/*<osl/*l<icr/th)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>-lith</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A relic of the Paleolithic era.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleolithic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*lith"ic</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*l<icr/th"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to an era marked by early stone
implements. The <xex>Paleolithic</xex> era (as proposed by Lubbock)
includes the earlier half of the <ldquo/Stone Age;<rdquo/ the remains
belonging to it are for the most part of extinct animals, with relics of
human beings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*ol"ogist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
paleology; a student of antiquity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleology</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The study or
knowledge of antiquities, esp. of prehistoric antiquities; a discourse
or treatise on antiquities; arch<ae/ology .</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleontographical</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*on`to*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the description of fossil remains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleontography</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*on*tog"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocr/n*t<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'o`nta</grk> existing things +
<ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The description of fossil
remains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleontological</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*on`to*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocr/n`t<osl/*l<ocr/j"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to paleontology.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pa`le*on`to*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleontologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*on*tol"o*gist</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocr/n*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pal<eacute/ontologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One versed
in paleontology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleontology</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*on*tol"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocr/n*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'o`nta</grk> existing things +
<ets>-logy</ets>. Cf. <er>Ontology</er>.]</ety> <def>The science which
treats of the ancient life of the earth, or of fossils which are the
remains of such life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleophytologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*phy*tol"o*gist</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*f<imac/*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A paleobotanist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleophytology</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*phy*tol"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*f<imac/*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>phytology</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Paleobotany.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleornithology</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*or`ni*thol"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ocir/r`n<icr/*th<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>ornithology</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The branch of paleontology which treats of fossil birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleosaurus</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*sau"rus</hw> <pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*s<add/"r<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>palaio`s</grk> ancient +
<grk>say^ros</grk> a lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of
fossil saurians found in the Permian formation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleotechnic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*tech"nic</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*t<ecr/k"n<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>technic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging to,
or connected with, ancient art.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>paleotechnic</xex>
men of central France.<rdquo/ <rj><au>D. Wilson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleothere</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"le*o*there</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"l<esl/*<osl/*th<emac/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pal<eacute/oth<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Any species of
<gen>Paleotherium</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleotherian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*the"ri*an</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*th<emac/"r<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>pal<eacute/oth<eacute/rien</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
<gen>Paleotherium</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleotherium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`le*o*the"ri*um</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*th<emac/"r<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>palaio`s</grk> ancient + <grk>qhri`on</grk>
beast.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct genus of herbivorous
Tertiary mammals, once supposed to have resembled the tapir in form, but
now known to have had a more slender form, with a long neck like that of
a llama.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Pal<ae/otherium</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleotheroid</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*the"roid</hw> <pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*th<emac/"roid)</pr>,
<ety>[<ets>Paleothere</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Resembling Paleotherium.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>An animal resembling, or allied to, the
paleothere.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleotype</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"le*o*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pal<ae/otype</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"le*ous</hw> <pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>palea</ets> chaff.]</ety> <def>Chaffy; like chaff;
paleaceous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleozoic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*zo"ic</hw> <pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*z<omac/"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>zwh`</grk> life, fr.
<grk>zh^n</grk> to live.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to, or designating, the older division of geological time during which
life is known to have existed, including the Silurian, Devonian, and
Carboniferous ages, and also to the life or rocks of those ages. See
<xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleozoic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*zo"ic</hw> <pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*z<omac/"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The Paleozoic time or
strata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paleozoology</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`le*o*zo*<oum/l"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/`l<esl/*<osl/*z<osl/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Paleo-</ets> + <ets>Zoology</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The science of extinct animals, a branch of paleontology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palesy</ent><br/
<ent>Palesie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pale"sie</hw> <pr>(p<add/l"z<esl/)</pr>, <hw>Pale"sy</hw>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Palsy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palestinean</ent><br/
<ent>Palestinian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pal`es*tin"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pal`es*tin"e*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Palestine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palestra</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*les"tra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Palestr<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Palestras</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>palaestra</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to wrestle.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pal<ae/stra</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Antiq.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
wrestling school; hence, a gymnasium, or place for athletic exercise in
general.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A wrestling; the exercise of
wrestling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palestrical</ent><br/
<ent>Palestric</ent><br/
<ent>Palestrian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*les"tri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*les"tric</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*les"tric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>palaestricus</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to the palestra, or to wrestling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palet</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Palea</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Palea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paletot</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"e*tot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paletot</ets>, OF. <ets>palletoc</ets>, prob. fr. L.
<ets>palla</ets> (see <er>Palla</er>) + F. <ets>toque</ets> cap, and so
lit., a frock with a cap or hood; cf. Sp. <ets>paletoque</ets>.]</ety>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An overcoat.</def> <au>Dickens.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A lady's outer garment, -- of varying fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palette</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ette</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pallet</er>
a thin board.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>A thin, oval or square board, or
tablet, with a thumb hole at one end for holding it, on which a painter
lays and mixes his pigments. Hence, any other object, usually one with a
flat surface, used for the same purpose.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pallet</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>The complete set of colors
used by an artist or other person in creating an image, in any medium.
The meaning of this term has been extended in modern times to include
the set of colors used in a particular computer application, or the
complete set of of colors available in computer displays or printing
techniques.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>The complete range of
resources and techniques used in any art, such as music.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld> <def>One of the plates covering
the points of junction at the bend of the shoulders and elbows.</def>
<rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A breastplate for a breast
drill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Palette knife</b></col>, <cd>a knife with a very flexible
steel blade and no cutting edge, rounded at the end, used by painters to
mix colors on the grinding slab or palette.</cd> -- <col><b>To set the
palette</b></col> <fld>(Paint.)</fld>, <cd>to lay upon it the required
pigments in a certain order, according to the intended use of them in a
picture.</cd> <rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palewise</ent><br/
<hw>Pale"wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>In the manner of a pale or pales; by perpendicular lines or
divisions; <as>as, to divide an escutcheon
<ex>palewise</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palfrey</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"frey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>palefrai</ets>, OF. <ets>palefrei</ets>, F. <ets>palefroi</ets>,
LL. <ets>palafredus</ets>, <ets>parafredus</ets>, from L.
<ets>paraveredus</ets> a horse for extraordinary occasions, an extra
post horse; Gr. <grk>para`</grk> along, beside + L. <ets>veredus</ets> a
post horse.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A saddle horse for the road, or for state occasions,
as distinguished from a war horse.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small saddle horse for ladies.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Call the host and bid him bring<br/
Charger and <qex>palfrey</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palfreyed</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"freyed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Mounted on a
palfrey.</def> <rj><au>Tickell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palgrave</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"grave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Palsgrave</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pali</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Palus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pali</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ceylonese, fr. Skr.
<ets>p<amac/li</ets> row, line, series, applied to the series of
Buddhist sacred texts.]</ety> <def>A dialect descended from Sanskrit,
and like that, a dead language, except when used as the sacred language
of the Buddhist religion in Farther India, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palification</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palus</ets> a stake + <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make: cf. F.
<ets>palification</ets>. See <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <def>The act or
practice of driving piles or posts into the ground to make it
firm.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Resembling a palus; <as>as, the <ex>paliform</ex> lobes of the
septa in corals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palilogy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lil"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palilogia</ets>, Gr. <grk>palillogi`a</grk>; <grk>pa`lin</grk>
again + <grk>le`gein</grk> to speak.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>The
repetition of a word, or part of a sentence, for the sake of greater
emphasis; <as>as, <ldquo/The <ex>living</ex>, the <ex>living</ex>, he
shall praise thee</as>.<rdquo/</def> <rj><au>Is. xxxviii.
19.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palimony</ent><br/
<hw>pal"i*mo`ny</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"<icr/*m<omac/`n<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>pal</ets> + ali<ets>mony</ets>. (ca.
1975)]</ety> <def>a form of alimony paid to a former partner in a
romantic relationship after a period of living together, even though the
two persons involved were not married to each other. The absence of a
formal marriage distinguishes it from <contr>alimony</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palimpsest</ent><br/
<hw>pal"imp*sest</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"<icr/mp*s<ecr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>palimpsestus</ets>, Gr. <grk>pali`mpshstos</grk> scratched
or scraped again, <grk>pali`mpshston</grk> a palimpsest;
<grk>pa`lin</grk> again + <grk>psh^n</grk> to rub, rub away: cf. F.
<ets>palimpseste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A parchment which has been written
upon twice, the first writing having been erased to make place for the
second. The erasures of ancient writings were usually carried on in
monasteries, to allow the production of ecclesiastical texts, such as
copies of church services and lives of the saints. The difficulty of
recovering the original text varied with the process used to prepare the
parchment for a fresh writing; the original texts on parchments which
had been washed with lime-water and dried were easily recovered by a
chemical process, but those erased by scraping the parchment and
bleaching are difficult to interpret. Most of the manuscripts underlying
the palimpsests that have been revived are fragmentary, but some are of
great historical value. One Syriac version of the Four Gospels was
discovered in 1895 in St. Catherine's Monastery at Mount Sinai by
<person>Mrs. Agnes Smith Lewis</person>. See also the notes below.</def>
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <b>Palimpsest</b> is the name given to ancient
parchments which have been used more than once for writing purposes. The
conquest of Egypt by the Saracens in the 7th century cut off from Europe
the papyrus which was used to write on, and parchment could be had only
in limited quantities. So through the dark ages, old manuscripts were
used, after removing the first writing upon them. Sometimes the writing
was washed off with a sponge, and the parchment smoothed with pumice
stone; at other times the letters were scraped away with a sharp blade.
Nearly all ancient manuscripts, however, were written with an ink which
could not be entirely removed, and traces of a former writing could be
seen beneath the new copy. In modern times there have been various
efforts to restore these ancient writings by some chemical treatment. In
this way have been found copies of the <booki>Republic</booki> of
<persfn>Cicero</persfn>, the <booki>Institutes</booki> of
<persfn>Gaius</persfn>, a part of the <booki>Epistle to the
Romans</booki>, and other parts of the Old and New Testaments. The
<booki>Republic</booki> of <persfn>Cicero</persfn> was covered by a
commentary on the Psalms, written by <person>St.
Augustine</person>.</note> <au>Student's Cyclopedia, 1897.</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In an auction on November 6, 1998, a 12th-century
palimpsest of one of Archimedes' works was sold for 2 million dollars.
The 174-page book, the oldest known copy of Archimedes' work, had been
owned by a French family since the 1920s, and was sold by Christie's
auction house in New York to an unidentified private American collector.
The palimpsest volume includes notes and calculations for two of the
Greek mathematician's most famous theories, <it>On Floating Bodies</it>
and <it>Method of Mechanical Theorems</it>. A Christie's spokesperson
said the buyer, who was not identified, indicated that the work would be
made available to scholars. Also bidding was the Greek government, which
claimed the work was stolen from a library in the former Constantinople,
now Istanbul, and belonged to Greece. According to the Athens News
Agency, the Patriarchate of Jerusalem took Christie's to court claiming
that the manuscript was part of its library, which had been transferred
to Istanbul and later to Athens for safekeeping. The court, however,
ruled that Christie's had the right to auction the manuscript for a
French family, which claimed to own it for the last 75 years since one
of the family's ancestors bought it from Orthodox monks in Istanbul.
According to the court's ruling, French law applied in the case, under
which a person who holds any object for more than 30 years becomes its
rightful owner.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palindrome</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"in*drome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pali`ndromos</grk> running back again; <grk>pa`lin</grk> again +
<grk>dramei^n</grk> to run: cf. F. <ets>palindrome</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
word, verse, or sentence, that is the same when read backward or
forward; <as>as, <examp>madam</examp>; <examp>Hannah</examp>; or
<examp>Lewd did I live, & evil I did dwel</examp>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palindromical</ent><br/
<ent>Palindromic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pal`in*drom"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pal`in*drom"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining to, or like, a
palindrome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palindromist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lin"dro*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A writer of
palindromes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paling</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pales, in
general; a fence formed with pales or pickets; a limit; an
inclosure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They moved within the <qex>paling</qex> of order and decorum.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of placing pales or stripes on cloth; also,
the stripes themselves.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paling board</b></col>, <cd>one of the slabs sawed from
the sides of a log to fit it to be sawed into boards.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palingenesia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal`in*ge*ne"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>See <er>Palingenesis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palingenesy</ent><br/
<ent>Palingenesis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pal`in*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pal`in*gen"e*sy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <grk>pa`lin</grk>
again + <?/ birth: cf. F. <ets>paling<eacute/n<eacute/sie</ets>. See
<er>Genesis</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A new birth; a re-creation; a regeneration; a
continued existence in different manner or form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>The passing over of the soul
of one person or animal into the body of another person or animal, at
the time of the death of the first; the transmigration of souls. Called
also <altname>metempsychosis</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>That form of development of an
individual organism in which in which ancestral characteristics
occurring during its evolution are conserved by heredity and reproduced,
sometimes transiently, in the course of individual development; original
simple descent; -- distinguished from <contr>cenogenesis</contr>
(<contr>kenogenesis</contr> or <contr>coenogenesis</contr>), in which
the mode of individual development has been modified so that the
evolutionary process had become obscured. Sometimes, in Zoology, the
term is applied to the abrupt metamorphosis of insects, crustaceans,
etc. See also the note under <er>recapitulation</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palingenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`in*ge*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to palingenesis: <as>as, a <ex>palingenetic</ex>
process</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pal`in*ge*net"ic*al*ly</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palinode</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"i*node</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palinodia</ets>, from Gr. <?/; <grk>pa`lin</grk> again + <?/ a
song. See <er>Ode</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An ode recanting, or
retracting, a former one; also, a repetition of an ode.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A retraction; esp., a formal retraction.</def>
<rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palinodial</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*no"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a palinode, or retraction.</def> <rj><au>J. Q.
Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palinody</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"i*no*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Palinode</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palinurus</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`inu"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from L.
<etsep>Palinurus</etsep>, the pilot of Aeneas.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>An instrument for obtaining directly, without calculation, the true
bearing of the sun, and thence the variation of the compass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palisade</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*sade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>palissade</ets>, cf. Sp. <ets>palizada</ets>, It.
<ets>palizzata</ets>, <ets>palizzo</ets>, LL. <ets>palissata</ets>; all
fr. L. <ets>palus</ets> a stake, pale. See <er>Pale</er> a stake.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A strong, long stake, one end of
which is set firmly in the ground, and the other is sharpened; also, a
fence formed of such stakes set in the ground as a means of
defense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any fence made of pales or sharp stakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A line of bold cliffs, esp. one showing basaltic
columns; -- usually in <pluf>pl.</pluf>, and orig. used as the name of
the cliffs on the west bank of the lower Hudson.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Palisade cells</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>vertically elongated parenchyma cells, such as are seen beneath the
epidermis of the upper surface of many leaves.</cd> -- <col><b>Palisade
worm</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a nematoid worm (<spn>Strongylus
armatus</spn>), parasitic in the blood vessels of the horse, in which it
produces aneurisms, often fatal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palisade</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*sade"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Palisaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Palisading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>palissader</ets>.]</ety> <def>To surround, inclose, or fortify,
with palisades.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palisading</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*sad"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Fort.)</fld>
<def>A row of palisades set in the ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palisado</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*sa*"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Palisadoes</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A palisade{1}.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palisado</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`i*sa"do</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To palisade.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palish</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat pale or
wan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palissander</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`is*san"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>palissandre</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Violet wood.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Rosewood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palissy</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"is*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Designating, or of
the nature of, a kind of pottery made by <person>Bernard
<etsep>Palissy</etsep></person>, in France, in the 16th
centry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Palissy ware</b></col>, <cd>glazed pottery like that made
by <person>Bernard Palissy</person>; especially, that having figures of
fishes, reptiles, etc., in high relief. See <er>Palissy</er>,
below.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallissy</ent><br/
<hw>Pallissy</hw> <pr>(p<aum/*l<esl/*s<esl/")</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<bio>Bernard Pallissy, the great French potter, was born in
<city>Agen</city>, in 1509, and wandered as a glass and portrait painter
until he married and settled in <city>Saintes</city> in 1538. While
working here as a surveyor his attention was attracted by an enameled
cup, and he determined to discover the process and after 16 years of
continuous labor and experiment in which he used all his resources and
burned the tables and floors for fuel, he succeeded, and though
imprisoned in 1562 as a Huguenot he was released by royal edict and
appointed "inventor of figulines" to the king. He removed to Paris in
1564, and through the aid of <person>Catherine de Medici</person> was
saved from the massacre of St. Bartholomew. From 1575 to 1584 he gave a
course of lectures on physics and natural history, demonstrating the
origin of springs, the formation of fossil shell, and the best method of
purifying water. In 1585, however, he was again arrested as a Huguenot
and imprisoned in the Bastille, where he died in 1589. See <person>H.
Morley's</person> <booki>Palissy the Potter</booki>.</bio> <au>Student's
Cyclopedia, 1897.</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palkee</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"kee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>p<amac/lk<imac/</ets>; of the same origin as E.
<ets>palanquin</ets>.]</ety> <def>A palanquin.</def>
<rj><au>Malcom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall</hw> <pr>(p<add/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pawl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pal</ets>, AS.
<ets>p<ae/l</ets>, from L. <ets>pallium</ets> cover, cloak, mantle,
pall; cf. L. <ets>palla</ets> robe, mantle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
outer garment; a cloak mantle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His lion's skin changed to a <qex>pall</qex> of gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of rich stuff used for garments in the Middle
Ages.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif (Esther viii.
15).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pallium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>About this time Pope Gregory sent two archbishop's
<qex>palls</qex> into England, -- the one for London, the other for
York.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A figure resembling the Roman
Catholic pallium, or pall, and having the form of the letter
Y.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A large cloth, esp., a heavy black cloth, thrown
over a coffin at a funeral; sometimes, also, over a tomb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Warriors carry the warrior's <qex>pall</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A piece of cardboard, covered
with linen and embroidered on one side; -- used to put over the
chalice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cloak.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Palled</conjf> <pr>(p<add/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Palling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Either shortened fr.
<ets>appall</ets>, or fr. F. <ets>p<acir/lir</ets> to grow pale. Cf.
<er>Appall</er>, <er>Pale</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To become
vapid, tasteless, dull, or insipid; to lose strength, life, spirit, or
taste; <as>as, the liquor <ex>palls</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beauty soon grows familiar to the lover,<br/
Fades in the eye, and <qex>palls</qex> upon the sense.</q>
<rj><qau>Addisin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make vapid or
insipid; to make lifeless or spiritless; to dull; to weaken.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Reason and reflection . . . <qex>pall</qex> all his
enjoyments.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To satiate; to cloy; <as>as, to <ex>pall</ex> the
appetite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Nausea.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shaftesbury.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Pall</er> a cloak.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An oblong
rectangular piece of cloth, worn by Roman ladies, and fastened with
brooches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladian</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"di*an</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l*l<amac/"d<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>prop. a.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
designating, a variety of the revived classic style of architecture,
founded on the works of <person>Andrea <etsep>Palladio</etsep></person>,
an Italian architect of the 16th century; <as>as, a <ex>Palladian</ex>
window</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladian</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"di*an</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l*l<amac/"d<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>prop. a.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>Pallas</ets>, Athena.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Greek goddess
<persfn>Athena</persfn>, also called <persfn>Pallas</persfn>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to wisdom or knowledge; -- Athena being
the goddess of wisdom.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladian</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"di*an</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l*l<amac/"d<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A follower of the
architectural style of <person>Andrea
<etsep>Palladio</etsep></person>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladic</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"dic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l*l<amac/"d<icr/k <it>or</it>
p<acr/l*l<acr/d"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or derived from, palladium; -- used specifically to
designate those compounds in which the element has a higher valence as
contrasted with <contr>palladious</contr> compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladious</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"di*ous</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l*l<amac/"d<icr/*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or containing,
palladium; -- used specifically to designate those compounds in which
palladium has a lower valence as compared with <contr>palladic</contr>
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladium</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"di*um</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l*l<amac/"d<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>Palla`dion</grk>, fr.
<grk>Palla`s</grk>, <grk>Palla`dos</grk>, Pallas.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>Any statue of the goddess
Pallas; esp., the famous statue on the preservation of which depended
the safety of ancient Troy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>That which affords effectual
protection or security; a safeguard; <as>as, the trial by jury is the
<ex>palladium</ex> of our civil rights</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palladium</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*la"di*um</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A rare metallic element of the light platinum group, found native,
and also alloyed with platinum and gold. It is a silver-white metal
resembling platinum, and like it permanent and untarnished in the air,
but is more easily fusible, with a melting point of 1555<deg/ C. It can
also be prepared as a finely divided black powder. It is unique in its
power of absorbing hydrogen, which it does to the extent of nearly a
thousand volumes, forming the alloy <chform>Pd2H</chform>. It is used
for graduated circles and verniers, for plating certain silver goods,
and somewhat in dentistry. It was so named in 1804 by
<persfn>Wollaston</persfn> from the asteroid <etsep>Pallas</etsep>,
which was discovered in 1802. Symbol Pd. Atomic number, 46. Atomic
weight, 106.42. Density 12.0.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1033 --></p>

<p><ent>palladiumize</ent><br/
<hw>pal*la"di*um*ize</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/l"l<amac/"d<icr/*<ucr/m*<imac/z)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Palladiumized</conjf>
<pr>(p<acr/l"l<amac/"d<icr/*<ucr/m*<imac/zd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Palladiumizing</conjf>
<pr>(p<acr/l"l<amac/"d<icr/*<ucr/m*<imac/`z<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<def>To cover or coat with palladium.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pallah</ent><br/
<hw>pal"lah</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"l<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large South African antelope (<spn>Aepyceros
melampus</spn>). The male has long lyrate and annulated horns. The
general color is bay, with a black crescent on the croup. Called also
<altname>roodebok</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallas</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"las</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"l<ait/s)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>Palla`s</grk>, <grk>Palla`dos</grk>.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Myth.)</fld> <def><person>Pallas Athena</person>, the Grecian
goddess of wisdom, called also <altname>Athena</altname>,
<altname>Pallas Athene</altname> or <altname>Athene</altname>, and
identified, at a later period, with the Roman
<persfn>Minerva</persfn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pallbearer</ent><br/
<hw>pall"bear*er</hw> <pr>(p<add/l"b<acir/r*<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One of those who attend the coffin at a funeral; -- so called from
the pall being formerly carried by them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pallet</ent><br/
<hw>pal"let</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"l<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>paillet</ets>, F. <ets>paillet</ets> a heap of straw, fr.
<ets>paille</ets> straw, fr. L. <ets>palea</ets> chaff; cf. Gr. <?/ fine
meal, dust, Skr. <ets>pala</ets> straw, <ets>pal<amac/va</ets> chaff.
Cf. <er>Paillasse</er>.]</ety> <def>A small and mean bed; a bed of
straw.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallet</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"let</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of <ets>pale</ets>. See
<er>Pale</er> a stake.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A perpendicular
band upon an escutcheon, one half the breadth of the pale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallet</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"let</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>palette</ets>: af. It.
<ets>paletta</ets>; prop. and orig., a fire shovel, dim. of L.
<ets>pala</ets> a shovel, spade. See <er>Peel</er> a shovel.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Palette</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pottery)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A wooden implement
used by potters, crucible makers, etc., for forming, beating, and
rounding their works. It is oval, round, and of other forms.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A potter's wheel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gilding)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An instrument used
to take up gold leaf from the pillow, and to apply it.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A tool for gilding the backs of books over the
bands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Brickmaking)</fld> <def>A board on which a newly
molded brick is conveyed to the hack.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A click or pawl for
driving a ratchet wheel.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the series of
disks or pistons in the chain pump.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Horology)</fld> <def>One of the pieces or levers
connected with the pendulum of a clock, or the balance of a watch, which
receive the immediate impulse of the scape-wheel, or balance
wheel.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>In the organ, a valve between the
wind chest and the mouth of a pipe or row of pipes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a pair of shelly plates
that protect the siphon tubes of certain bivalves, as the Teredo. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Teredo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A cup containing three ounces, -- formerly used by
surgeons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A low movable platform used for temporary storage
of objects so that they can be conveniently moved; it is commonly made
of wooden boards, about 4 inches high, and typically has openings in the
side into which the blades of a fork-lift truck may be inserted so as to
lift and move the pallet and the objects on it.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallial</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pallium</ets> a mantle. See <er>Pall</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a mantle, especially to the
mantle of mollusks; produced by the mantle; <as>as, the <ex>pallial</ex>
line, or impression, which marks the attachment of the mantle on the
inner surface of a bivalve shell</as>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Bivalve</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pallial chamber</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
cavity inclosed by the mantle.</cd> -- <col><b>Pallial sinus</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an inward bending of the pallial line, near the
posterior end of certain bivalve shells, to receive the siphon. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Bivalve</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliament</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*a*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>palliare</ets> to clothe, fr. L. <ets>pallium</ets> a mantle. See
<er>Pall</er> the garment.]</ety> <def>A dress; a robe.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliard</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"liard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paillard</ets>, orig., one addicted to the couch, fr.
<ets>paille</ets> straw. See <er>Pallet</er> a small bed.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A born beggar; a vagabond.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A lecher; a lewd person.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliasse</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*liasse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Paillasse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palliatus</ets>, fr. <ets>pallium</ets> a cloak. See <er>Pall</er>
the garment.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered with a mantle; cloaked;
hidden; disguised.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Eased; mitigated; alleviated.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Fell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Palliated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Palliating</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cover with a mantle or cloak; to cover up; to hide.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Being <qex>palliated</qex> with a pilgrim's coat.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
T. Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover with excuses; to conceal the enormity of,
by excuses and apologies; to extenuate; <as>as, to <ex>palliate</ex>
faults</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They never hide or <qex>palliate</qex> their vices.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To reduce in violence; to lessen or abate; to
mitigate; to ease without curing; <as>as, to <ex>palliate</ex> a
disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>palliate</qex> dullness, and give time a shove.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To cover; cloak; hide; extenuate; conceal.</syn>
<usage> -- To <er>Palliate</er>, <er>Extenuate</er>, <er>Cloak</er>.
These words, as here compared, are used in a figurative sense in
reference to our treatment of wrong action. We <xex>cloak</xex> in order
to conceal completely. We <xex>extenuate</xex> a crime when we endeavor
to show that it is <xex>less</xex> than has been supposed; we
<xex>palliate</xex> a crime when we endeavor to <xex>cover</xex> or
<xex>conceal</xex> its enormity, at least in part. This naturally leads
us to soften some of its features, and thus <xex>palliate</xex>
approaches <xex>extenuate</xex> till they have become nearly or quite
identical. <ldquo/To <xex>palliate</xex> is not now used, though it once
was, in the sense of wholly cloaking or covering over, as it might be,
our sins, but in that of <xex>extenuating</xex>; to <xex>palliate</xex>
our faults is not to hide them altogether, but to seek to diminish their
guilt in part.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Trench.</au></rj></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliation</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`li*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>palliation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of palliating, or
state of being palliated; extenuation; excuse; <as>as, the
<ex>palliation</ex> of faults, offenses, vices</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Mitigation; alleviation, as of a disease.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which cloaks or covers; disguise; also, the
state of being covered or disguised.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliative</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>palliatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Serving to palliate; serving to
extenuate, mitigate, or alleviate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliative</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
palliates; a palliative agent.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"li*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Palliative;
extenuating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallid</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"lid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pallidus</ets>, fr. <ets>pallere</ets> to be or look pale. See
<er>pale</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Deficient in color; pale; wan;
<as>as, a <ex>pallid</ex> countenance; <ex>pallid</ex> blue.</as></def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallidity</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*lid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pallidness;
paleness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallidly</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"lid*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pallid
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallidness</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"lid*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
pallid; paleness; pallor; wanness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliobranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal`li*o*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Brachiopoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palliobranchiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`li*o*bran"chi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pallium</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the pallium, or mantle, acting as a gill, as in
brachiopods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Pallia</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, E. <plw>Palliums</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See <er>Pall</er> the garment.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Costume)</fld> <def>A large, square, woolen cloak
which enveloped the whole person, worn by the Greeks and by certain
Romans. It is the Roman name of a Greek garment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A band of white wool, worn on
the shoulders, with four purple crosses worked on it; a pall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The wool is obtained from two lambs brought to the
basilica of St. Agnes, Rome, and blessed. It is worn by the pope, and
sent to patriarchs, primates, and archbishops, as a sign that they share
in the plenitude of the episcopal office. Before it is sent, the pallium
is laid on the tomb of St. Peter, where it remains all night.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The mantle of a
bivalve. See <er>Mantle</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The mantle of a
bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pall-mall</ent><br/
<hw>Pall`-mall"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>palemail</ets>, It. <ets>pallamagio</ets>; <ets>palla</ets> a ball
(of German origin, akin to E. <ets>ball</ets>) + <ets>magio</ets>
hammer, fr. L. <ets>malleus</ets>. See 1st <er>Ball</er>, and
<er>Mall</er> a beetle.]</ety> <def>A game formerly common in England,
in which a wooden ball was driven with a mallet through an elevated hoop
or ring of iron. The name was also given to the mallet used, to the
place where the game was played, and to the street, in London, still
called <etsep>Pall Mall</etsep>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pail-mail</asp> and <asp>pell-mell</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Sir K.
Digby.</au> <au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pallone</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*lo"ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., a large ball,
fr. <ets>palla</ets> ball. See <er>Balloon</er>.]</ety> <def>An Italian
game, played with a large leather ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pallor</ent><br/
<hw>pal"lor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>pallere</ets> to be or look pale. See <er>Pale</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Paleness; want of color; pallidity; <as>as,
<ex>pallor</ex> of the complexion</as>.</def> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pally</ent><br/
<hw>pal"ly</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Having the relationship of friends
or pals; -- used colloquially.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> chummy, matey, palsy-walsy.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palm</ent><br/
<hw>palm</hw> <pr>(p<aum/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>paume</ets>, F. <ets>paume</ets>, L. <ets>palma</ets>, Gr.
<grk>pala`mh</grk>, akin to Skr. <ets>p<amac/<nsdot/i</ets> hand, and E.
<ets>fumble</ets>. See <er>Fumble</er>, <er>Feel</er>, and cf. 2d
<er>Palm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The inner and
somewhat concave part of the hand between the bases of the fingers and
the wrist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Clench'd her fingers till they bit the <qex>palm</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A lineal measure equal either to the breadth of the
hand or to its length from the wrist to the ends of the fingers; a hand;
-- used in measuring a horse's height.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In Greece, the palm was reckoned at three inches. The
Romans adopted two measures of this name, the lesser palm of 2.91
inches, and the greater palm of 8.73 inches. At the present day, this
measure varies in the most arbitrary manner, being different in each
country, and occasionally varying in the same.</note> <rj><au>Internat.
Cyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Sailmaking)</fld> <def>A metallic disk, attached to
a strap, and worn on the palm of the hand, -- used to push the needle
through the canvas, in sewing sails, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The broad flattened part of an
antler, as of a full-grown fallow deer; -- so called as resembling the
palm of the hand with its protruding fingers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The flat inner face of an anchor
fluke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>to grease the palm of</b></col>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <cd>To
bribe or tip.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</cs></p>

<p><ent>Palm</ent><br/
<hw>Palm</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>palm</ets>, L.
<ets>palma</ets>; -- so named fr. the leaf resembling a hand. See 1st
<er>Palm</er>, and cf. <er>Pam</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any endogenous tree of the order
<ord>Palm<ae/</ord> or <ord>Palmace<ae/</ord>; a palm tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Palms are perennial woody plants, often of majestic
size. The trunk is usually erect and rarely branched, and has a
roughened exterior composed of the persistent bases of the leaf stalks.
The leaves are borne in a terminal crown, and are supported on stout,
sheathing, often prickly, petioles. They are usually of great size, and
are either pinnately or palmately many-cleft. There are about one
thousand species known, nearly all of them growing in tropical or
semitropical regions. The wood, petioles, leaves, sap, and fruit of many
species are invaluable in the arts and in domestic economy. Among the
best known are the date palm, the cocoa palm, the fan palm, the oil
palm, the wax palm, the palmyra, and the various kinds called cabbage
palm and palmetto.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A branch or leaf of the palm, anciently borne or
worn as a symbol of victory or rejoicing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A great multitude . . . stood before the throne, and before the
Lamb, clothed with white robes, and <qex>palme</qex> in their hands.</q>
<rj><qau>Rev. vii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any symbol or token of
superiority, success, or triumph; also, victory; triumph;
supremacy.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>palm</xex> of martyrdom.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So get the start of the majestic world<br/
And bear the <qex>palm</qex> alone.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Molucca palm</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a labiate
herb from Asia (<spn>Molucella l<ae/vis</spn>), having a curious
cup-shaped calyx.</cd> -- <col><b>Palm cabbage</b></col>, <cd>the
terminal bud of a cabbage palm, used as food.</cd> -- <col><b>Palm
cat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common paradoxure.</cd> --
<col><b>Palm crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the purse crab.</cd>
-- <col><b>Palm oil</b></col>, <cd>a vegetable oil, obtained from the
fruit of several species of palms, as the African oil palm
(<spn>El<ae/is Guineensis</spn>), and used in the manufacture of soap
and candles. See <er>El<ae/is</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Palm swift</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small swift (<spn>Cypselus Batassiensis</spn>)
which frequents the palmyra and cocoanut palms in India. Its peculiar
nest is attached to the leaf of the palmyra palm.</cd> -- <col><b>Palm
toddy</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Palm wine</cref>.</cd> --
<col><b>Palm weevil</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
mumerous species of very large weevils of the genus
<gen>Rhynchophorus</gen>. The larv<ae/ bore into palm trees, and are
called <altname>palm borers</altname>, and <altname>grugru
worms</altname>. They are considered excellent food.</cd> --
<col><b>Palm wine</b></col>, <cd>the sap of several species of palms,
especially, in India, of the wild date palm (<spn>Ph<oe/nix
sylvestrix</spn>), the palmyra, and the <spn>Caryota urens</spn>. When
fermented it yields by distillation <cnvto>arrack</cnvto>, and by
evaporation <cnvto>jaggery</cnvto>. Called also <altname>palm
toddy</altname>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Palm worm</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Palmworm</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The larva of a palm weevil</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
centipede.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palm</ent><br/
<hw>Palm</hw> <pr>(p<aum/m)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Palmed</conjf> <pr>(p<aum/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Palming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
handle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To manipulate with, or conceal in, the palm of the
hand; to juggle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>palmed</qex> the trick that lost the game.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To take (something small)
stealthily, especially by concealing it in the palm of the hand; <as>as,
he <ex>palmed</ex> one of the coins and walked out with
it</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To impose by fraud, as by sleight of hand; to put by
unfair means; -- usually with <ptcl>on</ptcl> or <ptcl>upon</ptcl>;
<as>as, to <ex>palm</ex> a stolen coin on an unsuspecting dealer</as>.
See also <er>palm off</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>For you may <qex>palm</qex> upon us new for old.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmaceae</ent><br/
<hw>Palmaceae</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A natural family of chiefly
tropical trees and shrubs and vines usually having a tall columnar trunk
bearing a crown of very large leaves; coextensive with the order
<ord>Palmales</ord>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Palmae, family <fam>Palmae</fam>, family
<fam>Palmaceae</fam>, Arecaceae, family <fam>Arecaceae</fam>, palm
family.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*ma"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to palms; of the nature of, or resembling,
palms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palma Christi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"ma Chris"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., palm of
Christ.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant (<spn>Ricinus
communis</spn>) with ornamental peltate and palmately cleft foliage,
growing as a woody perennial in the tropics, and cultivated as an
herbaceous annual in temperate regions; -- called also
<altname>castor-oil plant</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Sometimes corrupted
into <asp>palmcrist</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmacite</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ma*cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>A fossil palm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmae</ent><br/
<hw>Palmae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of chiefly
tropical trees and shrubs; same as <er>Palmaceae</er>; coextensive with
the order <ord>Palmales</ord>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Palmae</fam>, <fam>Palmaceae</fam>,
<fam>Arecaceae</fam>, family <fam>Arecaceae</fam>, palm
family.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmales</ent><br/
<hw>Palmales</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of chiefly
tropical trees and shrubs coextensive with the family <fam>Palmae</fam>;
-- the palms.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order <ord>Palmales</ord>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmar</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palmaris</ets>, fr. <ets>palma</ets> the palm of the hand: cf. F.
<ets>palmaire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or corresponding with, the palm of the
hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the under
side of the wings of birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal*ma"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Palmaria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Palmar</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the bifurcations
of the brachial plates of a crinoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmary</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ma*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Palmar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmary</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ma*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>palmarius</ets>,
<ets>palmaris</ets>, belonging to palms, deserving the palm or prize,
fr. <ets>palma</ets> a palm.]</ety> <def>Worthy of the palm; palmy;
pre<eum/minent; superior; principal; chief; <as>as, <ex>palmary</ex>
work</as>.</def> <rj><au>Br. Horne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
salt of palmic acid; a ricinoleate.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmated</ent><br/
<ent>Palmate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pal"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pal"ma*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>palmatus</ets> marked with the palm
of a hand, from <ets>palma</ets> the palm of the hand.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the shape of the hand; resembling a hand with
the fingers spread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Spreading from the apex of a
petiole, as the divisions of a leaf, or leaflets, so as to resemble the
hand with outspread fingers.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having the anterior
toes united by a web, as in most swimming birds; webbed. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> <it>(i)</it> under <er>Aves</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Having the distal portion broad, flat, and more or less divided
into lobes; -- said of certain corals, antlers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmately</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a palmate
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmatifid</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*mat"i*fid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palmatus</ets> palmate + root of <ets>findere</ets> to
split.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Palmate, with the divisions
separated but little more than halfway to the common center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmatilobed</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*mat"i*lobed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palmatus</ets> palmate + E. <ets>lobed</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Palmate, with the divisions separated less than
halfway to the common center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmatisected</ent><br/
<ent>Palmatisect</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pal*mat"i*sect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pal*mat`i*sect"ed</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>palmatus</ets> palmate
+ <ets>secare</ets> to cut.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided, as a
palmate leaf, down to the midrib, so that the parenchyma is
interrupted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmcrist</ent><br/
<hw>Palm"crist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The palma Christi.
(<au>Jonah iv. 6, margin, and Douay version, note.</au>)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmed</ent><br/
<hw>Palmed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having or bearing a
palm or palms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Palmed deer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a stag of
full growth, bearing palms. See 1st <er>Palm</er>, 4.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmer</ent><br/
<hw>Palm"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Palm</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>One who palms or cheats, as at cards or
dice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmer</ent><br/
<hw>Palm"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Palm</er> the
tree.]</ety> <def>A wandering religious votary; especially, one who bore
a branch of palm as a token that he had visited the Holy Land and its
sacred places.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pilgrims and <qex>palmers</qex> plighted them together.</q>
<rj><qau>P. Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pilgrim had some home or dwelling place, the <qex>palmer</qex>
had none. The pilgrim traveled to some certain, designed place or
places, but the <qex>palmer</qex> to all.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Staveley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmerworm</ent><br/
<hw>Palm"er*worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any hairy caterpillar which appears in great numbers,
devouring herbage, and wandering about like a palmer. The name is
applied also to other voracious insects.</def> <au>Joel. i. 4.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>In America, the larva of any one of several moths,
which destroys the foliage of fruit and forest trees, esp. the larva of
<spn>Ypsolophus pometellus</spn>, which sometimes appears in vast
numbers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmette</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*mette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>palme</ets> a palm.]</ety> <def>A floral ornament, common in Greek
and other ancient architecture; -- often called <altname>the honeysuckle
ornament</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmetto</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*met"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>palm</ets> the tree: cf. Sp. <ets>palmito</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to palms of several genera and
species growing in the West Indies and the Southern United States. In
the United States, the name is applied especially to the
<spn>Cham<ae/rops Palmetto</spn>, <it>or</it> <spn>Sabal Palmetto</spn>,
the cabbage tree of Florida and the Carolinas. See <cref>Cabbage
tree</cref>, under <er>Cabbage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1034 --></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Royal palmetto</b></col>, <cd>the West Indian <spn>Sabal
umbraculifera</spn>, the trunk of which, when hollowed, is used for
water pipes, etc. The leaves are used for thatching, and for making
hats, ropes, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw palmetto</b></col>, <cd><spn>Sabal
serrulata</spn>, a native of Georgia, South Carolina, and Florida. The
nearly impassable jungle which it forms is called <partof>palmetto
scrub</partof>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmetto flag</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*met"to flag</hw>. <def>Any of several flags adopted by South
Carolina after its secession. That adopted in November, 1860, had a
green cabbage palmetto in the center of a white field; the final one,
January, 1861, had a white palmetto in the center of a blue field and a
white crescent in the upper left-hand corner.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmetto State</ent><br/
<hw>Palmetto State</hw>. <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>South Carolina; -- a
nickname alluding to the State Arms, which contain a representation of a
palmetto tree.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmic</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>palmique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to,
or derived from, the castor-oil plant (<spn>Ricinus communis</spn>, or
<spn>Palma Christi</spn>) and other species of the family
<fam>Euphorbiaceae</fam>; -- formerly used to designate an acid now
called <altname>ricinoleic</altname> acid (d-12-hydroxyoleic acid,
<chform>C18H34O3</chform>).</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmidactyles</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal`mi*dac"ty*les</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Palm</er>, and <er>Dactyl</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
group of wading birds having the toes webbed, as the avocet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*mif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palmifer</ets>; <ets>palma</ets> a palm + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear:
cf. F. <ets>palmif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <def>Bearing palms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmigrade</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mi*grade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palma</ets> palm of the hand + <ets>gradi</ets> to walk.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Putting the whole foot upon the ground in
walking, as some mammals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmin</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"min</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>palma</ets>
Christi: cf. F. <ets>palmine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A white waxy or fatty substance obtained from castor
oil.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Ricinolein.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmiped</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mi*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palmipes</ets>, <ets>-edis</ets>, broad-footed; <ets>palma</ets>
the palm of the hand + <ets>pes</ets> a foot; cf. F.
<ets>palmip<egrave/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Web-footed,
as a water fowl.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A swimming bird; a
bird having webbed feet.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmipedes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal*mip"e*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Natatores</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmister</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mis*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Palm</er> of the hand.]</ety> <def>One who practices
palmistry.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmistry</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`mis*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Palmister</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art or practice of
divining or telling fortunes, or of judging of character, by the lines
and marks in the palm of the hand; chiromancy.</def>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au> <au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A dexterous use or trick of the hand.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mi*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of palmitic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmite</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Palm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A South African plant
(<spn>Prionium Palmita</spn>) of the Rush family, having long serrated
leaves. The stems have been used for making brushes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmitic</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*mit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or obtained from, palmitin or palm oil;
<as>as, <ex>palmitic</ex> acid (<chform>C16H32O2</chform>), a white
crystalline substance belonging to the fatty acid series</as>. It is
readily soluble in hot alcohol, and melts to a liquid oil at 62<deg/
C.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmitin</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mi*tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called because
abundant in <ets>palm</ets> oil.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>
<def>A solid crystallizable fat, found abundantly in animals and in
vegetables. It occurs mixed with stearin and olein in the fat of animal
tissues, with olein and butyrin in butter, with olein in olive oil, etc.
Chemically, it is a glyceride of palmitic acid, three molecules of
palmitic acid being united to one molecule of glyceryl, and hence it is
technically called <altname>tripalmitin</altname>, or <altname>glyceryl
tripalmitate</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmitolic</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`mi*tol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Palmit</ets>ic + <ets>-ol</ets>eic + <ets>ic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, an artificial
acid of the oleic acid series, isomeric with linoleic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmitone</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"mi*tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The ketone of palmitic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palm off</ent><br/
<hw>palm` off"</hw> <pr>(p<aum/m)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To dispose of fraudulently or deceptively; <as>as, to <ex>palm
off</ex> a counterfeit item as genuine</as>.</cd>
<specif>Hence:</specif> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To induce acceptance of
(something) by misrepresenting it as something better.</cd><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palm Sunday</ent><br/
<hw>Palm" Sun`day</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The Sunday
next before <er>Easter</er>; -- so called in commemoration of the
triumphal entry of Jesus Christ into Jerusalem, when the multitude
strewed palm branches in the way. The event is commemorated in Christian
churches by distribution of blessed palm leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmy</ent><br/
<hw>Palm"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Bearing
palms; abounding in palms; derived from palms; <as>as, a <ex>palmy</ex>
shore</as>.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His golden sands and <qex>palmy</qex> wine.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Worthy of the palm; flourishing;
prosperous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the most high and <qex>palmy</qex> state of Rome.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palmyra</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*my"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
species of palm (<spn>Borassus flabelliformis</spn>) having a straight,
black, upright trunk, with palmate leaves. It is found native along the
entire northern shores of the Indian Ocean, from the mouth of the Tigris
to New Guinea. More than eight hundred uses to which it is put are
enumerated by native writers. Its wood is largely used for building
purposes; its fruit and roots serve for food, its sap for making toddy,
and its leaves for thatching huts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. See
<er>Pale</er> a stake.]</ety> <def>A pole or timber of any kind; -- in
the names of trees.</def> <mark>[Sp. Amer.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palo blanco</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"lo blan"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Sp. <ets>blanco</ets>
white.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A western American hackberry
(<spn>Celtis reticulata</spn>), having light-colored bark.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A Mexican mimosaceous tree (<spn>Lysiloma
candida</spn>), the bark of which is used in tanning.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palolo worm</ent><br/
<ent>Palolo</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*lo"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Palolo worm</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[From native name.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A polystome worm (<spn>Palolo viridis</spn>)
that burrows in the coral reefs of certain of the Pacific Islands. A
little before the last quarter of the moon in October and November, they
swarm in vast numbers at the surface of the sea for breeding, and are
gathered and highly esteemed as food by the natives. An allied species
inhabits the tropical Atlantic and swarms in June or July.</def>
<note>In the 1890 Webster, called <altname>palola</altname> and
<spn>Palola viridis</spn>. A misspelling?</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palometa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`lo*me"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A type of pompano (<spn>Palometa simillima</spn>) that is smaller
than the Florida pompano; it is common in West Indies. Called also the
<altname>California pompano</altname>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> California pompano, <spn>Palometa
simillima</spn>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palomino</ent><br/
<hw>pal`o*mi"no</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l`<ucr/*m<emac/"n<osl/)</pr>
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>palomino</ets>, resembling a dove.]</ety>
<def>A horse of light tan or golden color with cream or white mane and
tail, and often having white markings on the legs and face.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palp</ent><br/
<hw>Palp</hw> <pr>(p<acr/lp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>palpe</ets>. See <er>Palpable</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Palpus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palp</ent><br/
<hw>Palp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>palpare</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>palper</ets>.]</ety> <def>To have a distinct touch or feeling of;
to feel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To bring a <qex>palp<egrave/d darkness o'er the earth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Heywood.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpability</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`pa*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being palpable, or perceptible by the touch.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpable</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pa*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>palpable</ets>, L. <ets>palpabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>palpare</ets> to
feel, stroke; cf. <ets>palpus</ets> the soft palm of the hand.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being touched and felt; perceptible by the
touch; <as>as, a <ex>palpable</ex> form</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Darkness must overshadow all his bounds,<br/
<qex>Palpable</qex> darkness.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Easily perceptible; plain; distinct; obvious;
readily perceived and detected; gross; <as>as, <ex>palpable</ex>
imposture; <ex>palpable</ex> absurdity; <ex>palpable</ex>
errors.</as></def> <ldquo/Three persons <xex>palpable</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>P. Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Lies] gross as a mountain, open, <qex>palpable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A hit, A very <qex>palpable</qex> hit.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.
(Hamlet)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pal"pa*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pal"pa*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palpate</ent><br/
<hw>palpate</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To examine for medical purposes
by touching, as of body parts; <as>as, the nurse <ex>palpated</ex> the
patient's stomach</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> feel.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpation</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*pa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palpatio</ets>, fr. <ets>palpare</ets>. See
<er>Palpable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act of touching or
feeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Examination of a patient by
touch.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpator</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal*pa"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
stroker.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a family of clavicorn
beetles, including those which have very long maxillary palpi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpebra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"pe*bra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Palpebr<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The eyelid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpebral</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pe*bral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palpebralis</ets>, fr. <ets>palpebra</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>palp<eacute/bral</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the
eyelids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpebrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pe*brate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having eyelids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palped</ent><br/
<hw>Palped</hw> <pr>(p<acr/lpt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a palpus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"pi</hw> <pr>(p<acr/l"p<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<def><pos>pl.</pos> of <er>Palpus</er>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> See
<er>Palpus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpicorn</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pi*corn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Palpus</er>, and <er>Cornu</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One
of a group of aquatic beetles (<fam>Palpicornia</fam>) having short
club-shaped antenn<ae/, and long maxillary palpi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpifer</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pi*fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Palpus</ets>
+ L. <ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Palpiger</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pi*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Palpus</ets>
+ <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>palpiforme</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the form of a palpus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpiger</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pi*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Palpigerous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>That portion of the
labium which bears the palpi in insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Pal*pig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Palpus</ets> + <ets>-gerous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Bearing a palpus.</def> <rj><au>Kirby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpitant</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pi*tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palpitans</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Palpitating; throbbing;
trembling.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"pi*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Palpitated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Palpitating</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>palpitare</ets>, <ets>palpitatum</ets>, v. intens. fr.
<ets>pappare</ets>. See <er>Palpable</er>.]</ety> <def>To beat rapidly
and more strongly than usual; to throb; to bound with emotion or
exertion; to pulsate violently; to flutter; -- said specifically of the
heart when its action is abnormal, as from excitement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>palpitating</ent><br/
<hw>palpitating</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Beating irregularly; -- of the
heart.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> palpitant.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpitation</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`pi*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palpitatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>palpitation</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
rapid pulsation; a throbbing; esp., an abnormal, rapid beating of the
heart as when excited by violent exertion, strong emotion, or by
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpless</ent><br/
<hw>Palp"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Without a palpus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpocil</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"po*cil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Palpus</er>, and <er>Cilium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
minute soft filamentary process springing from the surface of certain
hydroids and sponges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palpus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Palpi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Palp</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A feeler; especially, one of the jointed sense
organs attached to the mouth organs of insects, arachnids, crustaceans,
and annelids; <as>as, the mandibular <ex>palpi</ex>, maxillary
<ex>palpi</ex>, and labial <ex>palpi</ex></as>. The palpi of male
spiders serve as sexual organs. Called also <altname>palp</altname>. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Arthrogastra</er> and
<er>Orthoptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsgrave</ent><br/
<hw>Pals"grave`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>paltsgraaf</ets>; <ets>palts</ets> palace (l. <ets>palatium</ets>)
+ <ets>graaf</ets> count; cf. G. <ets>pfalzgraf</ets>. See
<er>Palace</er>, and <er>Landgrave</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Ger. Hist.)</fld>
<def>A count or earl who presided in the domestic court, and had the
superintendence, of a royal household in Germany.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsgravine</ent><br/
<hw>Pals"gra*vine`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>paltsgravin</ets>: cf. G. <ets>pfalzgrafin</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
consort or widow of a palsgrave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsical</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"si*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Palsy</er>.]</ety> <def>Affected with palsy; palsied;
paralytic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsied</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"sied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with palsy;
paralyzed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palstave</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"stave`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dan.
<ets>paalstav</ets>.]</ety> <def>A peculiar bronze adz, used in
prehistoric Europe about the middle of the bronze age.</def>
<rj><au>Dawkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palster</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>palsterstaf</ets>.]</ety> <def>A pilgrim's staff.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsy</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Palsies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>palesie</ets>,
<ets>parlesy</ets>, OF. <ets>paralesie</ets>, F. <ets>paralysie</ets>,
L. <ets>paralysis</ets>. See <er>Paralysis</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Paralysis, complete or partial. See
<er>Paralysis</er>.</def> <ldquo/One sick of the
<xex>palsy</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mark ii. 3.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bell's palsy</b></col>, <cd>paralysis of the facial
nerve, producing distortion of one side of the face; -- so called from
<person>Sir Charles <etsep>Bell</etsep></person>, an English surgeon who
described it.</cd> -- <col><b>Scrivener's palsy</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Writer's cramp</cref>, under <er>Writer</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Shaking palsy</b></col>, <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd><it>paralysis
agitans</it>, a disease usually occurring in old people, characterized
by muscular tremors and a peculiar shaking and tottering gait; now
called <altname>parkinsonism</altname>, or <altname>Parkinson's
disease</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsy</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"sy</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Palsied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Palsying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To affect with palsy, or as with
palsy; to deprive of action or energy; to paralyze.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palsywort</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"sy*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The cowslip (<spn>Primula veris</spn>); -- so called from its
supposed remedial powers.</def> <rj><au>Dr. Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palter</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Paltered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Paltering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Paltry</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To haggle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To act in insincere or deceitful manner; to play
false; to equivocate; to shift; to dodge; to trifle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Romans, that have spoke the word,<br/
And will not <qex>palter</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who never sold the truth to serve the hour,<br/
Nor <qex>paltered</qex> with eternal God for power.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To babble; to chatter.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palter</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To trifle with; to waste; to
squander in paltry ways or on worthless things.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Palter</xex> out your time in the penal
statutes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palterer</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
palters.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palterly</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ter*ly</hw>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <def>Paltry; shabby; shabbily;
paltrily.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/In
<xex>palterly</xex> clothes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pepys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paltock</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"tock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Paletot</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of doublet; a jacket.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paltrily</ent><br/
<hw>pal"tri*ly</hw> <pr>(p<add/l"tr<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a paltry manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paltriness</ent><br/
<hw>pal"tri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of being
paltry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paltry</ent><br/
<hw>pal"try</hw> <pr>(p<add/l"tr<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Paltrier</adjf>
<pr>(p<add/l"tr<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Paltriest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E. <ets>paltry</ets>
refuse, rubbish, LG. <ets>palterig</ets> ragged, <ets>palte</ets>,
<ets>palter</ets>, a rag, a tatter, Dan. <ets>pialt</ets>, Sw.
<ets>palta</ets>, pl. <ets>paltor</ets>.]</ety> <def>Mean; vile;
worthless; despicable; contemptible; pitiful; trifling; <as>as, a
<ex>paltry</ex> excuse; <ex>paltry</ex> gold.</as></def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>paltry</qex> prize is hardly worth the cost.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Contemptible</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lu"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palus</ets>, <ets>-udis</ets>, a marsh.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to marshes or fens; marshy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paludal fever</b></col>, <cd>malarial fever; -- so called
because generated in marshy districts.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludament</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lu"da*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Paludamentum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludamentum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*lu`da*men*tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paladumenta</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu>
<fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A military cloak worn by a general and his
principal officers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludicolae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal`u*dic"o*l<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>palus</ets>, <ets>-udis</ets>, a marsh +
<ets>colere</ets> to inhabit.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division
of birds, including the cranes, rails, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludicole</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lu"di*cole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>paludicole</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Marsh-inhabiting;
belonging to the Paludicol<ae/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal`u*di"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
L. <plw>Paludin<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Paludinas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>palus</ets>,
<ets>-udis</ets>, a marsh, pool.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of numerous species of freshwater pectinibranchiate mollusks, belonging
to <gen>Paludina</gen>, <gen>Melantho</gen>, and allied genera. They
have an operculated shell which is usually green, often with brown
bands. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Pond snail</cref>, under
<er>Pond</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pal`u*di"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inhabiting ponds
or swamps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludine</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"u*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>palus</ets>, <ets>-udis</ets>, a marsh.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to a marsh.</def> <rj><au>Buckland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludinous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lu"di*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Paludinal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Like or pertaining to the genus <gen>Paludina</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a marsh or fen.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lu"dism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The morbid phenomena produced by dwelling among marshes; malarial
disease or disposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paludose</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"u*dose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paludosus</ets> marshy.]</ety> <def>Growing or living in marshy
places; marshy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palule</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Palulus</er> <it>or</it> <er>Palus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palulus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pal"u*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Paluli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>palus</ets> a stake.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Palus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pali</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a stake.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of several upright slender calcareous
processes which surround the central part of the calicle of certain
corals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palustral</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lus"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paluster</ets>, <ets>-ustris</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
a bog or marsh; boggy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Palustrine</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*lus"trine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining
to, or living in, a marsh or swamp; marshy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paly</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pale</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Pale; wanting color; dim.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au> <au>Whittier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paly</ent><br/
<hw>Pal"y</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pal<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Pale</er> a stake.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Divided into four
or more equal parts by perpendicular lines, and of two different
tinctures disposed alternately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pam</ent><br/
<hw>pam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Palm</er>
victory; cf. <ets>trump</ets>, fr. <ets>triumph</ets>, and perh. fr. F.
<ets>pamphile</ets> from Pamphile, a man's name.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The knave of clubs.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A card game in which the jack of clubs is
trump.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pam</ent><br/
<hw>Pam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A form of the female
given name <altname>Pamela</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pament</ent><br/
<hw>pa"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pavement.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pampa</ent><br/
<hw>pam"pa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A plain. See
<er>pampas</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pampano</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pam"pa*no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pompano</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pampas</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"pas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Sp., fr. Peruv.
<ets>pampa</ets> a field, plain.]</ety> <def>Vast grass-covered plains
in the central and southern part of the Argentine Republic in South
America. The term is sometimes used in a wider sense for the plains east
of the Andes extending from Bolivia to Southern Patagonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pampas cat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a South
American wild cat (<spn>Felis pajeros</spn>). It has oblique transverse
bands of yellow or brown. It is about three and a half feet long. Called
also <altname>straw cat</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pampas deer</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small, reddish-brown, South American deer
(<spn>Cervus campestris</spn> <it>syn.</it> <spn>Blastocerus
campestris</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Pampas grass</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a very tall ornamental grass (<spn>Gynerium
argenteum</spn>) with a silvery-white silky panicle. It is a native of
the pampas of South America.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamper</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pampered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pampering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>pampen</ets>, <ets>slampampen</ets>, to live luxuriously,
<ets>pampe</ets> thick pap, and E. <ets>pap</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To feed to the full; to feed luxuriously; to glut;
<as>as, to <ex>pamper</ex> the body or the appetite</as>.</def> <ldquo/A
body . . . <xex>pampered</xex> for corruption.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dr. T.
Dwight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To gratify inordinately; to indulge to excess;
<as>as, to <ex>pamper</ex> pride; to <ex>pamper</ex> the
imagination</as>.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pampered</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"pered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Fed luxuriously;
indulged to the full; hence, luxuriant; <as>as, <ex>pampered</ex>
children</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pampered</xex> boughs.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Pampered</xex> insolence.<rdquo/
<au>Pope.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Pam"pered*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamperer</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"per*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, pampers.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamperize</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"per*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
pamper.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sydney Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pampero</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pam*pe"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., fr.
<ets>pampa</ets> a plain.]</ety> <def>A violent wind from the west or
southwest, which sweeps over the pampas of South America and the
adjacent seas, often doing great damage.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Parish.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamperos</ent><br/
<hw>Pam*pe"ros</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Pampero</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <ety>[Sp. American.]</ety>
<fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A tribe of Indians inhabiting the pampas of
South America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamphlet</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"phlet</hw> <pr>(p<acr/m"fl<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pamflet</ets>, <ets>pamfilet</ets>, <ets>paunflet</ets>, possibly
fr. OF. <ets>palme</ets> the palm of the hand, F. <ets>paume</ets> (see
<er>Palm</er>) + OF. <ets>fueillet</ets> a leaf, dim. of
<ets>fueil</ets>, m., F. <ets>feuille</ets>, f., fr. L.
<ets>folium</ets>, pl. <ets>folia</ets>, thus meaning, a leaf to be held
in the hand; or perh. through old French, fr. L. <ets>Pamphila</ets>, a
female historian of the first century who wrote many epitomes; prob.,
however, fr. OF. <ets>Pamflette</ets>, the Old French name given to
<ets>Pamphilus</ets>, a poem in Latin verse of the 12th century,
pamphlets being named from the popularity of this poem.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A writing; a book.</def> <rj><au>Testament of
love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sir Thomas More in his <qex>pamphlet</qex> of Richard the
Third.</q> <rj><qau>Ascham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small book consisting of a few sheets of printed
paper, stitched together, often with a paper cover, but not bound; a
short essay or written discussion, usually on a subject of current
interest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1035 --></p>

<p><ent>Pamphlet</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"phlet</hw> <pr>(p<acr/m"fl<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
write a pamphlet or pamphlets.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamphleteer</ent><br/
<hw>Pam`phlet*eer"</hw> <pr>(p<acr/m`fl<ecr/t*<emac/r")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A writer of pamphlets; a scribbler.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden. Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamphleteer</ent><br/
<hw>Pam`phlet*eer"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To write or publish
pamphlets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By <qex>pamphleteering</qex> we shall not win.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Kingsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pampiniform</ent><br/
<hw>Pam*pin"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pampinus</ets> a tendril + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>In the form of tendrils; -- applied especially
to the spermatic and ovarian veins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pampre</ent><br/
<hw>Pam"pre</hw> <pr>(p<acr/m"p<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pampre</ets> a vine branch, L. <ets>pampinus</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Sculp.)</fld> <def>An ornament, composed of vine leaves and
bunches of grapes, used for decorating spiral columns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pamprodactylous</ent><br/
<hw>Pam`pro*dac"tyl*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + Gr. <grk>pro`</grk> forward +
<grk>da`ktylos</grk> finger.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having all
the toes turned forward, as the colies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panto-</ent><br/
<ent>Panta-</ent><br/
<ent>Pan-</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan"ta-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pan"to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>. <ety>[Gr. <?/, m., <?/, neut.,
gen. <?/, all.]</ety> <def>Combining forms signifying <sig>all</sig>,
<sig>every</sig>; <as>as, <ex>pano</ex>rama, <ex>pan</ex>theism,
<ex>panta</ex>graph, <ex>panto</ex>graph</as>. <ex>Pan-</ex> becomes
<ex>pam-</ex> before <xex>b</xex> or <xex>p</xex>, as
<ex>pam</ex>prodactylous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. See 2d <er>Pane</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A part; a portion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>The distance comprised between
the angle of the epaule and the flanked angle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Perh. a different word.]</ety> <def>A leaf of gold
or silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pan</ets> skirt,
lappet, L. <ets>pannus</ets> a cloth, rag, W. <ets>panu</ets> to fur, to
full.]</ety> <def>To join or fit together; to unite.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>p<amac/n</ets>, Skr. <ets>parna</ets> leaf.]</ety> <def>The betel
leaf; also, the masticatory made of the betel leaf, etc. See
<er>Betel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Myth.)</fld> <def>The god of shepherds, guardian
of bees, and patron of fishing and hunting. He is usually represented as
having the head and trunk of a man, with the legs, horns, and tail of a
goat, and as playing on the shepherd's pipe (also called the <it>pipes
of Pan</it>), which he is said to have invented.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>panne</ets>, AS.
<ets>panne</ets>; cf. D. <ets>pan</ets>, G. <ets>pfanne</ets>, OHG.
<ets>pfanna</ets>, Icel., Sw., LL., & Ir. <ets>panna</ets>, of uncertain
origin; cf. L. <ets>patina</ets>, E. <ets>paten</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A shallow, open dish or vessel, usually of metal,
employed for many domestic uses, as for setting milk for cream, for
frying or baking food, etc.; also employed for various uses in
manufacturing.</def> <ldquo/A bowl or a <xex>pan</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Manuf.)</fld> <def>A closed vessel for boiling or
evaporating. See <cref>Vacuum pan</cref>, under
<er>Vacuum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The part of a flintlock which holds the
priming.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The skull, considered as a vessel containing the
brain; the upper part of the head; the brainpan; the cranium.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>A recess, or bed, for the leaf of
a hinge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The hard stratum of earth that lies below the soil.
See <cref>Hard pan</cref>, under <er>Hard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A natural basin, containing salt or fresh water, or
mud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Flash in the pan</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Flash</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To savor of the pan</b></col>, <cd>to
suggest the process of cooking or burning; in a theological sense, to be
heretical.</cd> <rj><au>Ridley. Southey.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Panned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Panning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>To separate, as gold, from dirt or sand, by washing in a kind of
pan.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We . . . witnessed the process of cleaning up and
<qex>panning</qex> out, which is the last process of separating the pure
gold from the fine dirt and black sand.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. W. T.
Sherman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To criticise (a drama or literary work)
harshly.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>To
yield gold in, or as in, the process of panning; -- usually with
<ptcl>out</ptcl>; <as>as, the gravel <ex>panned</ex> out
richly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To turn out (profitably or unprofitably); to result;
to develop; <as>as, the investigation, or the speculation,
<ex>panned</ex> out poorly</as>.</def> <mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan</ent><br/
<hw>Pan</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <fld>(Cinematography)</fld> <def>To
scan (a movie camera), usu. in a horizontal direction, to obtain a
panoramic effect; also, to move the camera so as to keep the subject in
view.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panabase</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"a*base</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> +
<ets>base</ets>. So called in allusion to the number of metals contained
in it.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Tetrahedrite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panacea</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`a*ce"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>pana`keia</grk> fr. <grk>panakh`s</grk> all-healing;
<grk>pa^s</grk> <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <grk>'akei^sqai</grk> to
heal.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A remedy for all diseases; a universal medicine; a
cure-all; catholicon; hence, a relief or solace for
affliction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The herb allheal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panacean</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`a*ce"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
properties of a panacea.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Panacean</xex> dews.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Whitehead.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panache</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*nache"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>penna</ets> a feather. See <er>Pen</er> a feather.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A plume or bunch of feathers, esp. such a bunch worn on
the helmet; any military plume, or ornamental group of
feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>panache</qex> of variegated plumes.</q>
<rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pleasingly flamboyant style or manner; flair{4};
verve.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panade</ent><br/
<ent>Panada</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*na"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*nade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>panada</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>panis</ets> bread: cf. F. <ets>panade</ets>. See
<er>Pantry</er>.]</ety> <def>Bread boiled in water to the consistence of
pulp, and sweetened or flavored.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>panado</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panade</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*nade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dagger.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panama hat</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`a*ma" hat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A fine plaited hat, made in
Central America of the young leaves of a plant (<spn>Carludovica
palmata</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panamanian</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`a*ma"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pert. to
Panama.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or citizen of
Panama.</def></def2><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan-American</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`-A*mer"i*can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pan-</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to both North and South
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan-American Congress</ent><br/
<hw>Pan-American Congress</hw>. <def>Any of several meetings of
delegates from various American states;</def> <specif>esp.:</specif>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One held in 1889-90 in the United States, at which all
the independent states except Santo Domingo were represented and of
which the practical result was the establishment of the Bureau of
American Republics for the promotion of trade relations.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One held in Mexico in 1901-1902.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>One held at Rio de Janeiro in 1906.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan-Americanism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan-A*mer"i*can*ism</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The principle or
advocacy of a political alliance or union of all the states of
America.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panamica</ent><br/
<hw>panamica</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A low stingless nettle (<spn>Pilea
involucrata</spn>) of Central and South America having velvety
brownish-green toothed leaves and clusters of small green
flowers.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> friendship plant, panamiga, <spn>Pilea
involucrata</spn>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panamiga</ent><br/
<hw>panamiga</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>panamica</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> friendship plant, panamica, Pilea
involucrata.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pan-Anglican</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`-An"gli*can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>Anglican</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>Belonging to, or representing, the whole Church of England; used
less strictly, to include the Protestant Episcopal Church of the United
States; <as>as, the <ex>Pan-Anglican</ex> Conference at Lambeth, in
1888</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panary</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>panis</ets>
bread.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to bread or to
breadmaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panary</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A storehouse for bread.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panetella</ent><br/
<ent>panatella</ent><br/
<ent>panatela</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>panatela</hw>, <hw>panatella</hw>, <hw>panetella</hw></mhw>
<pr>(p<acr/n`<icr/*t<ecr/l"l<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A long
slender cigar.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> panetela.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panathenaea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan*ath`e*nae"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/; <grk>pa^s</grk> <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <?/ Athena.]</ety>
<def>The most ancient and important festival of Athens, celebrated in
honor of <persfn>Athena</persfn>, the tutelary goddess of the
city.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panax</ent><br/
<hw>Panax</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A genus of perennial herbs of eastern
North America and Asia having aromatic tuberous roots:
ginseng.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Panax</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancake</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"cake`</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"k<amac/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
thin cake of batter fried in a pan or on a griddle; a griddlecake; a
flapjack.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pancake</xex> for Shrove Tuesday.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancarte</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"carte`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>pancharta</ets>. See <er>Pan-</er>, and <er>Carte</er>.]</ety>
<def>A royal charter confirming to a subject all his possessions.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pance</ent><br/
<hw>Pance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
pansy.</def> <altsp>[Also <asp>paunce</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panch</ent><br/
<hw>Panch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Paunch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panchway</ent><br/
<hw>Panch"way</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>pan<cced/oi</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A Bengalese
four-oared boat for passengers.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>panshway</asp> and <asp>paunchwas</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Malcom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratian</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*cra"tian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pancratic;
athletic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratiast</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*cra"ti*ast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who engaged
in the contests of the pancratium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratiastic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*cra`ti*as"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the pancratium.</def> <rj><au>G. West.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*crat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pagkrath`s</grk> all-powerful.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>Having
all or many degrees of power; having a great range of power; -- said of
an eyepiece made adjustable so as to give a varying magnifying
power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratical</ent><br/
<ent>Pancratic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan*crat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan*crat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pancratium</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the pancratium;
athletic.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"cra*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An athlete; a
gymnast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancratium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan*cra"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>pagkra`tion</grk> a complete contest, fr. <grk>pagkrath`s</grk>
all-powerful; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <grk>kra`tos</grk>
strength.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An athletic contest
involving both boxing and wrestling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of Old World amaryllideous
bulbous plants, having a funnel-shaped perianth with six narrow
spreading lobes. The American species are now placed in the related
genus <gen>Hymenocallis</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancreas</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"cre*as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/;
<grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <grk>kre`as</grk> flesh, meat:
cf. F. <ets>pancr<eacute/as</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
sweetbread, a gland connected with the intestine of nearly all
vertebrates. It is usually elongated and light-colored, and its
secretion, called the pancreatic juice, is discharged, often together
with the bile, into the upper part of the intestines, and is a powerful
aid in digestion. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Digestive
apparatus</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pancreatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`cre*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pancr<eacute/atique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to the pancreas; <as>as, the <ex>pancreatic</ex> secretion,
digestion, ferments</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pancreatic juice</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>a
colorless alkaline fluid secreted intermittently by the pancreatic
gland. It is one of the most important of the digestive fluids,
containing at least three distinct enzymes, trypsin, steapsin (lipase)
and an amylase, by which it acts upon all three classes of food stuffs.
See <er>Pancreas</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pancreatin</ent><br/
<hw>pan"cre*a*tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>pancreas</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>One of the
digestive enzymes of the pancreatic juice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ By some the term <xex>pancreatin</xex> is restricted to
the amylolytic ferment of the pancreatic juice (amylase), by others it
is applied to trypsin, and by still others to <xex>steapsin</xex> (the
lipase of pancreatic juice).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A preparation of pancreatic juice, usually obtained
from the ox or hog, containing the three main digestive enzymes trypsin,
amylase, and lipase, and used in medicine as an aid to
digestion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pancy</ent><br/
<hw>pan"cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>pansy</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panda</ent><br/
<hw>pan"da</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"d<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small Asiatic mammal (<spn>Ailurus
fulgens</spn>) having fine soft fur, which inhabits the mountains of
Northern India. It was once thought to be related to the bears, but is
now believed to be more closely related to raccoons. It has
reddish-brown fur on the back and sides, and black fur on the legs and
underside. Called also the <altname>lesser panda</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The <altname>giant panda</altname> (<spn>Ailuropoda
melanoleuca</spn>), a bearlike black-and white mammal now found wild
only in the central forests of China, which lives mainly on on bamboo.
It is an endangered species, and is a popular attraction in the few zoos
which have bveen able to obtain specimens.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandanaceae</ent><br/
<hw>Pandanaceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of woody
plants including the pandanus tree (the screw pine) and
freycinetia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Pandanaceae</fam>, screw-pine
family.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandanales</ent><br/
<hw>Pandanales</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>An order of plants
including the families <fam>Typhaceae</fam>; <fam>Sparganiaceae</fam>;
and <fam>Pandanaceae</fam>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order Pandanales.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandanus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan*da"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Malay <ets>pandan</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
endogenous plants, native to tropical lands from Africa to Polynesia.
See <cref>Screw pine</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fiber from leaves of the pandanus tree; used for
woven articles (such as mats).</def> <wns>[wns16=1]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any of various Old World tropical palmlike trees
having huge prop roots and pineapplelike leaves and edible conelike
fruits; also called the <altname>screw pine</altname>.</def>
<wns>[wns16=2]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandar</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pander</er>.</def> <ldquo/Seized by the <xex>pandar</xex> of
Appius.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandarism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dar*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Panderism</er>.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandarize</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
pander.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandarous</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dar*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Panderous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandean</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*de"an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 4th <er>Pan</er>.]</ety>
<def>Of or relating to the god Pan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandean pipes</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*de"an pipes</hw>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>, <def>A primitive wind
instrument, consisting of a series of short hollow reeds or pipes,
graduated in length by the musical scale, and fastened together side by
side; a syrinx; a mouth organ; -- said to have been invented by the god
Pan. Called also <altname>pipes of Pan</altname>, <altname>Pan's
pipes</altname> and <altname>Panpipes</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandect</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pandecta</ets>, <ets>pandectes</ets>, Gr. <?/ all-receiving,
all-containing; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <?/ to receive:
cf. F. <ets>pandectes</ets>, pl.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A treatise
which comprehends the whole of any science.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Thou] a <qex>pandect</qex> mak'st, and universal book.</q>
<rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The digest, or abridgment, in fifty
books, of the decisions, writings, and opinions of the old Roman
jurists, made in the sixth century by direction of the emperor
Justinian, and forming the leading compilation of the Roman civil
law.</def> <rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandemic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*dem"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pandemus</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all
+ <?/ the people: cf. F. <ets>pand<eacute/mique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Affecting a whole people or a number of countries; everywhere
epidemic.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A pandemic
disease.</def></def2> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandemonium</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`de*mo"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <grk>dai`mwn</grk> a
demon.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The great hall or council chamber of
demons or evil spirits.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An utterly lawless, riotous place or
assemblage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A condition of unrestrained disorder and chaotic,
riotous uproar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pander</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Pandarus</ets>, a leader in the Trojan army, who is represented by
Chaucer and Shakespeare as having procured for <persfn>Troilus</persfn>
the possession of <persfn>Cressida</persfn>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A male bawd; a pimp; a procurer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou art the <qex>pander</qex> to her dishonor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, one who ministers to the evil designs and
passions of another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those wicked <qex>panders</qex> to avarice and ambition.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pander</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pandered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pandering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To play the pander
for.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>to pander to</b></col> <pos>v. t.</pos> <cd>To appeal to
(base emotions or less noble desires), so as to achieve one's purpose;
to exploit (base emotions, such as lust, prejudice, or
hate).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pander</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act the part of a
pander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panderage</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
pandering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panderism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The employment,
arts, or practices of a pander.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panderly</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the quality of a
pander.</def> <ldquo/O, you <xex>panderly</xex> rascals.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandermite</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*der"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Panderma</ets>, a port on the Black Sea from which it is
exported.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous borate of lime, near
priceite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panderous</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"der*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or relating to
a pander; characterizing a pander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandiculated</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*dic"u*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pandiculation</er>.]</ety> <def>Extended; spread out;
stretched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandiculation</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*dic`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pandiculari</ets> to stretch one's self, fr. <ets>pandere</ets> to
spread out.]</ety> <def>A stretching and stiffening of the trunk and
extremities, as when fatigued and drowsy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandit</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pundit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandoor</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"door</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pandour</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandora</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*do"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>Pandw`ra</grk>; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all +
<grk>dw^ron</grk> a gift.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld>
<def>A beautiful woman (<xex>all-gifted</xex>), whom
<persfn>Jupiter</persfn> caused <persfn>Vulcan</persfn> to make out of
clay in order to punish the human race, because
<persfn>Prometheus</persfn> had stolen the fire from heaven.
<persfn>Jupiter</persfn> gave <persfn>Pandora</persfn> a box containing
all human ills, which, when the box was opened, escaped and spread over
the earth. Hope alone remained in the box. Another version makes the box
contain all the blessings of the gods, which were lost to men when
Pandora opened it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of marine bivalves, in
which one valve is flat, the other convex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pandore</ent><br/
<hw>pan"dore</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"d<omac/r <it>or</it>
p<acr/n*d<omac/r")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Bandore</er>.]</ety> <def>An ancient musical instrument, of the lute
kind; a bandore.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pandora</asp>,
<asp>pandoura</asp>. <asp>pandure</asp>, and
<asp>pandoran</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandour</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"dour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of a
class of Hungarian mountaineers serving in the Austrian army that served
as local militia in Croatia; -- so called from
<city><etsep>Pandur</etsep></city>, a principal town in the region from
which they originally came. They were noted for their
ruthlessness.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pandoor</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her whiskered <qex>pandours</qex> and her fierce hussars.</q>
<rj><qau>Campbell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A brutal soldier.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pandoura</ent><br/
<hw>pan*dour"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>pandore</er> .</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pandowdy</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*dow"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A deep pie or
pudding made of baked apples, or of sliced bread and apples baked
together, with no bottom crust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panduriform</ent><br/
<ent>Pandurate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan"du*rate</hw>, <hw>Pan*du"ri*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pandura</ets> a pandore +
<ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>panduriforme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Obovate, with a concavity in each side, like the body of a violin;
fiddle-shaped; <as>as, a <ex>panduriform</ex> leaf; <ex>panduriform</ex>
color markings of an animal.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Shaped like a fiddle; -- of a
leaf.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> fiddle-shaped.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panduriform</ent><br/
<hw>panduriform</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>pandurate</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pandurate, fiddle-shaped.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pane</ent><br/
<hw>Pane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>panne</ets>.]</ety> <def>The narrow edge of a hammer head. See
<er>Peen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pane</ent><br/
<hw>Pane</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pan</ets> part, portion of a
thing, F. <ets>pan</ets> a skirt, lappet, part or piece of a wall, side,
fr. L. <ets>pannus</ets> a cloth, fillet, rag; akin to E.
<ets>vane</ets>. See <er>Vane</er>, and cf. <er>Panel</er>,
<er>Pawn</er> pledge.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A division; a distinct
piece, limited part, or compartment of any surface; a patch; hence, a
square of a checkered or plaided pattern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the openings in a slashed garment, showing
the bright colored silk, or the like, within; hence, the piece of
colored or other stuff so shown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A compartment of a
surface, or a flat space; hence, one side or face of a building; <as>as,
an octagonal tower is said to have eight <ex>panes</ex></as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Especially, in modern use, the glass in one
compartment of a window sash; a windowpane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In irrigating, a subdivision of an irrigated surface
between a feeder and an outlet drain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the flat surfaces, or facets, of
any object having several sides.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the
eight facets surrounding the table of a brilliant cut diamond.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paned</ent><br/
<hw>Paned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having
panes; provided with panes; also, having openings; <as>as, a
<ex>paned</ex> window; <ex>paned</ex> window sash.</as></def>
<ldquo/<xex>Paned</xex> hose.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Having flat sides or surfaces;
<as>as, a six-<ex>paned</ex> nut</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyric</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`e*gyr"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panegyricus</ets>, Gr. <grk>panhgyriko`s</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>pan<eacute/gyrique</ets>. See <er>Panegyric</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>An oration or eulogy in praise of some person
or achievement; a formal or elaborate encomium; a laudatory discourse;
laudation. See Synonym of <er>Eulogy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyrical</ent><br/
<ent>Panegyric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan`e*gyr"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan`e*gyr"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>panegyricus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>panhgyriko`s</grk>, from <grk>panh`gyris</grk> an assembly of the
people, a high festival; <grk>pa^</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk> all +
<grk>'a`gyris</grk>, <grk>'agora`</grk>, an assembly.]</ety>
<def>Containing praise or eulogy; encomiastic; laudatory.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Panegyric</xex> strains.<rdquo/ <au>Pope.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pan`e*gyr"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some of his odes are <qex>panegyrical</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyris</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*neg"y*ris</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/. See <er>Panegyric</er>.]</ety> <def>A festival; a public
assembly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>S. Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyrist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"e*gyr`ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panegyrista</ets>, Gr. <grk>panhgyristh`s</grk>, one who attends a
<grk>panh`gyris</grk>: cf. <grk>panhgyri`zein</grk> to celebrate or
attend a public festival, to make a set speech, esp. a panegyric, in a
public assembly. See <er>Panegyric</er>.]</ety> <def>One who delivers a
panegyric; a eulogist; one who extols or praises, either by writing or
speaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If these <qex>panegyrists</qex> are in earnest.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyrize</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"e*gy*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Panegyrized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Panegyrizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gr.
<?/. See <er>Panegyrist</er>.]</ety> <def>To praise highly; to extol in
a public speech; to write or deliver a panegyric upon; to
eulogize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyrize</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"e*gy*rize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To indulge in
panegyrics.</def> <rj><au>Mitford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panegyry</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"e*gyr`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A panegyric.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panel</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Orig., a little piece;
OF. <ets>panel</ets>, <ets>pannel</ets>, F. <ets>panneau</ets>, dim. of
<ets>pan</ets> skirt, lappet, part or piece of a wall, side. See 2d
<er>Pane</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A sunken
compartment with raised margins, molded or otherwise, as in ceilings,
wainscotings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1036 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A piece of parchment
or a schedule, containing the names of persons summoned as jurors by the
sheriff;</def> <specif>hence, more generally,</specif> <def>the whole
group of persons summoned on a particular day, from whom a jury is to be
selected; also, the jury selected from that group.</def>
<au>Blackstone.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>A prisoner
arraigned for trial at the bar of a criminal court.</def>
<au>Burrill.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any group of persons
selected to judge a contest, conduct a discussion, serve as advisers, or
participate in any group activity in which they will provide information
or make judgments.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Formerly, a piece of cloth serving as a saddle;
hence, a soft pad beneath a saddletree to prevent chafing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>A board having its edges
inserted in the groove of a surrounding frame; <as>as, the
<ex>panel</ex> of a door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>One of the faces of a hewn
stone.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Painting)</fld> <def>A slab or plank of wood upon
which, instead of canvas, a picture is painted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A heap of dressed
ore.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the districts divided by pillars of
extra size, into which a mine is laid off in one system of extracting
coal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Dressmaking)</fld> <def>A plain strip or band, as
of velvet or plush, placed at intervals lengthwise on the skirt of a
dress, for ornament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A portion of a framed structure between adjacent
posts or struts, as in a bridge truss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(A<eum/ronautics)</fld> <def>A segment of an
a<eum/roplane wing. In a biplane the outer panel extends from the wing
tip to the next row of posts, and is trussed by oblique stay
wires.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Panel game</b></col>, <cd>a method of stealing money in a
panel house.</cd> -- <col><b>Panel house</b></col>, <cd>a house of
prostitution in which the rooms have secret entrances to facilitate
theft from customers by accomplices of the inmates.</cd> --
<col><b>Panel saw</b></col>, <cd>handsaw with fine teeth, -- used for
cutting out panels, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Panel thief</b></col>, <cd>one
who robs in a panel house.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panel</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Paneled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Panelled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Paneling</conjf> or
<conjf>Panelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To form in or with panels;
<as>as, to <ex>panel</ex> a wainscot</as>.</def><-- to put panels on
(e.g. a wall) --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paneled back</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>the
paneled work covering the window back. See <er>Window
back</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panelation</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`el*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
impaneling a jury.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>panellation</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paneled</ent><br/
<hw>paneled</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Decorated with panels or
wainscoting; -- used of walls; <as>as, a <ex>paneled</ex> family
room</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> wainscoted.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paneless</ent><br/
<hw>Pane"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without
panes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To patch his <qex>paneless</qex> window.</q>
<rj><qau>Shenstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paneling</ent><br/
<hw>pan"el*ing</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"<ecr/l*<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of forming in panels or decorating
with panels.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>panelling</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The panels which decorate the walls of a
room.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panelwork</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"el*work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Wainscoting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panetella</ent><br/
<hw>panetella</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>panatela</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> panatela, panatella, panetela.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paneulogism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*eu"lo*gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pan-</er>, <er>Eulogy</er>.]</ety> <def>Eulogy of everything;
indiscriminate praise.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her book has a trace of the cant of <qex>paneulogism</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>National Rev.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panfish</ent><br/
<hw>panfish</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any of numerous small
food fishes; especially those not available on the market.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any of numerous small food fishes taken with hook
and line.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pan-fry</ent><br/
<hw>pan-fry</hw> <pos>v.</pos> <def>To fry in a pan.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panful</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ful</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Panfuls</plw>
<pr>(p<acr/n"f<usdot/lz)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See 5th <er>Pan</er>.]</ety>
<def>Enough to fill a pan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pang</ent><br/
<hw>pang</hw> <pr>(p<acr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. for older
<ets>prange</ets>. Cf. <er>Prong</er>.]</ety> <def>A paroxysm of extreme
pain or anguish; a sudden and transitory agony; a throe; <as>as, the
<ex>pangs</ex> of death</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Agony; anguish; distress. See
<er>Agony</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pang</ent><br/
<hw>Pang</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To torture; to cause to have great
pain or suffering; to torment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pangenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets>
+ <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>An hypothesis
advanced by <persfn>Darwin</persfn> in explanation of
heredity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The theory rests on the assumption, that the whole
organization, in the sense of every separate atom or unit, reproduces
itself, the cells throwing off minute granules called
<xex>gemmules</xex>, which circulate freely throughout the system and
multiply by subdivision. These gemmules collect in the reproductive
organs and products, or in buds, so that the egg or bud contains
gemmules from all parts of the parent or parents, which in development
give rise to cells in the offspring similar to those from which they
were given off in the parent. The hypothesis also assumes that these
gemmules need not in all cases develop into cells, but may lie dormant,
and be transmitted from generation to generation without producing a
noticeable effect until a case of atavism occurs. This is an ingenious
hypothesis, but now known to be wrong. Although now, a hundred years
later, we know that all transmitted genetic information (other than that
in plasmids) is contained in the genome of a single cell, scientists are
still only beginning to understand the development process.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pangenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ge*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to pangenesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pangful</ent><br/
<hw>Pang"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of pangs.</def>
<rj><au>Richardson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pangless</ent><br/
<hw>Pang"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a pang; painless.</def>
<rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pangolin</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"go*lin</hw> <pr>(p<acr/<nsm/"g<osl/*l<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Malay <ets>pang<umac/lang</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of <gen>Manis</gen>,
<gen>Pholidotus</gen>, and related genera, found in Africa and Asia.
They are covered with imbricated scales, and feed upon ants. Called also
<altname>scaly ant-eater</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pangothic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*goth"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> +
<ets>Gothic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to, or including, all the
Gothic races.</def> <ldquo/Ancestral <xex>Pangothic</xex> stock.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhandle</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"han`dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The handle of a
pan;</def> <specif>hence,</specif> <mark>fig.</mark>, <def>any arm or
projection suggestive of the handle of a pan; <as>as, the
<ex>panhandle</ex> of West Virginia, Texas, or Idaho</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhandle State</ent><br/
<hw>Panhandle State</hw>. <def>West Virginia; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panhandle</ent><br/
<hw>pan"han`dle</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"h<acr/n`d'l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<def>To accost people in a public place and ask for money; to beg.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>pan"hand`ler</wf>
<pr>(p<acr/n"h<acr/nd`l<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>pan"hand`ling</wf> <pr>(p<acr/n"h<acr/nd`l<icr/ng)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhandle</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"han`dle</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"h<acr/n`d'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>to obtain by panhandling.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To accost in a public place and ask for money
from.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhellenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`hel*len"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Panhellenium</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to all Greece, or to
Panhellenism; including all Greece, or all the Greeks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhellenism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*hel"len*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scheme to
unite all the Greeks in one political body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhellenist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*hel"len*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An advocate of
Panhellenism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panhellenium</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`hel*le"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <grk>Panellh`nion</grk>; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all +
<grk>"E`llhnes</grk> the Greeks.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An
assembly or association of Greeks from all the states of
Greece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panicum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant of the genus
<gen>Panicum</gen>; panic grass; also, the edible grain of some species
of panic grass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Panic grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any grass of
the genus <gen>Panicum</gen>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paniko`s</grk> of or
pertaining to <grk>Pa`n</grk> Pan, to whom the causing of sudden fright
was ascribed: cf. F. <ets>panique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Extreme or sudden
and causeless; unreasonable; -- said of fear or fright; <as>as,
<ex>panic</ex> fear, terror, alarm</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>panic</xex>
fright.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>to~ paniko`n</grk> (with
or without <grk>dei^ma</grk> fear): cf. F. <ets>panique</ets>. See
<er>Panic</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden,
overpowering fright; esp., a sudden and groundless fright; terror
inspired by a trifling cause or a misapprehension of danger; <as>as, the
troops were seized with a <ex>panic</ex>; they fled in a
<ex>panic</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>By extension: A sudden widespread fright or
apprehension concerning financial affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panical</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Panic</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panicky</ent><br/
<hw>pan"ick*y</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Same as <er>panic-stricken</er>;
<as>as, the travellers became <ex>panicky</ex> as the snow
deepened</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> panic-stricken, panic-struck, petrified, terrified,
frightened.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panicle</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panicula</ets> a tuft on plants, dim. of <ets>panus</ets> the
thread wound upon the bobbin in a shuttle; cf. Gr. <?/, <?/; prob. akin
to E. <ets>pane</ets>: cf. F. <ets>panicule</ets>. See 2d
<er>Pane</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A pyramidal form of
inflorescence, in which the cluster is loosely branched below and
gradually simpler toward the end.</def><-- Illustr. of a panicle --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panicled</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"i*cled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Furnished with panicles; arranged in, or like, panicles;
paniculate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panic-struck</ent><br/
<ent>Panic-stricken</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan"ic-strick`en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan"ic-struck`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Struck with a panic, or sudden
fear; thrown into a state of intense fear; <as>as, trying to keep back
the <ex>panic-stricken</ex> crowd</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> panicky, petrified, terrified, frightened.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paniculated</ent><br/
<ent>Paniculate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*nic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*nic"u*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Panicle</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Panicled</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panicum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan"i*cum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., panic
grass.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of grasses, including
several hundred species, some of which are valuable; panic
grass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panidiomorphic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*id`i*o*mor"phic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>idiomorphic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Having a completely idiomorphic structure; --
said of certain rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panier</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Pannier</er>,
3.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panification</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panis</ets> bread + <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make: cf. F.
<ets>panification</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of making
bread.</def> <rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panim</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"nim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Painim</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panislamism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*is"lam*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>Islamism</ets>.]</ety> <def>A desire or
plan for the union of all Muslim nations for the conquest of the
world.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panivorous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*niv"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panis</ets> bread + <ets>vorare</ets> to devour.]</ety> <def>Eating
bread; subsisting on bread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannade</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*nade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The curvet of a
horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannage</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"nage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pasnage</ets>, LL. <ets>pasnadium</ets>, <ets>pastinaticum</ets>,
fr. <ets>pastionare</ets> to feed on mast, as swine, fr. L.
<ets>pastio</ets> a pasturing, grazing. See <er>Pastor</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The food of swine in the
woods, as beechnuts, acorns, etc.; -- called also
<altname>pawns</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A tax paid for the
privilege of feeding swine in the woods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannary</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Panary</er>.</def> <rj><au>Loudon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panne</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Panne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
fabric resembling velvet, but having the nap flat and less
close.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannel</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Panel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of rustic saddle.</def>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>The stomach of a hawk.</def>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A carriage for conveying a mortar
and its bed, on a march.</def> <rj><au>Farrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannier</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"nier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>panier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>panarium</ets> a bread basket, fr.
<ets>panis</ets> bread. Cf. <er>Pantry</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
bread basket; also, a wicker basket (used commonly in pairs) for
carrying fruit or other things on a horse or an ass</def>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A shield of basket work
formerly used by archers as a shelter from the enemy's
missiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A table waiter at the Inns of Court,
London.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A framework of steel or whalebone, worn by women to
expand their dresses; a kind of bustle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panniered</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"niered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bearing
panniers.</def> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannikel</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ni*kel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pan</er> a
dish.]</ety> <def>The brainpan, or skull; hence, the crest.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannikin</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ni*kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>pan</ets> a dish.]</ety> <def>A small pan or cup.</def>
<rj><au>Marryat.</au> <au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panning</ent><br/
<hw>pan"ning</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process in
which heavier ores are concentrated by agitating a sample of crushed ore
under water in a shallow pan, thus washing away the lighter particles
from the sample; <as>as, <ex>panning</ex> for gold</as>. See pan{1},
<pos>v. t.</pos> and pan{1}, <pos>v. i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannose</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pannus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Similar in texture or
appearance to felt or woolen cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pannus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., cloth. See 2d
<er>Pane</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A very vascular superficial
opacity of the cornea, usually caused by granulation of the
eyelids.</def> <rj><au>Foster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panoistic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`o*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ an egg.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Producing ova only; --
said of the ovaries of certain insects which do not produce
vitelligenous cells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panomphean</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`om*phe"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>panomphaeus</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Uttering ominous or
prophetic voices; divining.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We want no half gods, <qex>panomphean</qex> Joves.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panoplied</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"o*plied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dressed in
panoply.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panoply</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"o*ply</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"<osl/*pl<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>panopli`a</grk>; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all +
<grk>"o`plon</grk> tool, implement, in pl., armor, arms.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Defensive armor in general; a full suit of defensive
armor.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We had need to take the Christian <qex>panoply</qex>, to put on
the whole armor of God.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any full set of elaborate
attire, complete with accessories or accompanying paraphernalia; any
elaborate special or ceremonial attire and equipment.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any impressive complete
array; <as>as, the full <ex>panoply</ex> of a presidential
funeral</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <mark>[Fig.:]</mark> <def>Any
complete array of devices used in an endeavor; <as>as, to deploy the
full <ex>panoply</ex> of writer's techniques</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panoptical</ent><br/
<ent>panoptic</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>panoptic</hw> <hw>panoptical</hw></mhw> <pos>adj.</pos>
<def>Including everying visible in one view; <as>as, a <ex>panoptic</ex>
aerial photograph of the missile base; a <ex>panoptic</ex> stain used in
microscopy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panopticon</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*nop"ti*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pan-</er>, and <er>Optic</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A prison so contructed that the inspector can see
each of the prisoners at all times, without being seen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A room for the exhibition of novelties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panorama</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`o*ra"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <?/ that which is seen, a view,
fr. <?/ to see. See <er>Pan-</er>, and <er>Wary</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A complete view in every direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A comprehensive survey of a
particular topic; also, a broad view of the development of a series of
events.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A picture presenting a view of objects in every
direction, as from a central point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A picture representing scenes too extended to be
beheld at once, and so exhibited a part at a time, by being unrolled,
and made to pass continuously before the spectator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panoramical</ent><br/
<ent>Panoramic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan`o*ram"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan`o*ram"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of, pertaining to,
or like, a panorama; exhibiting a very broad view; <as>as, a
<ex>panoramic</ex> view</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Panoramic camera</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Camera</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Presented with a broad
perspective; <as>as, a <ex>panoramic</ex> view of European
history</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panorpian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*nor"pi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like, or pertaining to, the genus <gen>Panorpa</gen>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Panorpid</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panorpid</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*nor"pid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any neuropterous insect of the genus <gen>Panorpa</gen>, and allied
genera. The larv<ae/ feed on plant lice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pan out</ent><br/
<hw>pan` out"</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n`out")</pr>, <sn>1.</sn> <pos>v. i.</pos>
<def>To succeed; <as>as, the project didn't <ex>pan
out</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To turn out (profitably or unprofitably); to result;
to develop; <as>as, the investigation, or the speculation, <ex>panned
out</ex> poorly</as>.</def> <mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panpharmacon</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*phar"ma*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pan-</er>, and <er>Pharmacon</er>.]</ety> <def>A medicine for all
diseases; a panacea.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panpresbyterian</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`pres`by*te"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>Presbyterian</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging
to, or representative of, those who hold Presbyterian views in all parts
of the world; <as>as, a <ex>Panpresbyterian</ex> council</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panpsychism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*psy"chism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pan-</er>; <er>Psychic</er>.]</ety> <def>The theory that all nature
is psychical or has a psychical aspect; the theory that every particle
of matter has a psychical character or aspect.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pan*psy"chic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Pan*psy"chist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pan`psy*chis"tic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms></p>

<p><q>Fechner affords a conspicuous instance of the idealistic tendency
to mysterize nature in his <qex>panpsychicism</qex>, or that form of
noumenal idealism which holds that the universe is a vast communion of
spirits, souls of men, of animals, of plants, of earth and other
planets, of the sun, all embraced as different members in the soul of
the world.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc. Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pansclavonian</ent><br/
<ent>Pansclavist</ent><br/
<ent>Pansclavism</ent><br/
<ent>Pansclavic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan`sclav"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan`sclav"ism</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan`sclav"ist</hw>, <hw>Pan`scla*vo"ni*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>. <def>See <er>Panslavic</er>, <er>Panslavism</er>,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panshon</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"shon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An earthen vessel
wider at the top than at the bottom, -- used for holding milk and for
various other purposes.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pansied</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"sied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pansy</er>.]</ety> <def>Covered or adorned with pansies.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>pansied</xex> grounds.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panslavic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`slav"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> +
<ets>Slavic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to all the Slavic
races.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panslavism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`slav"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scheme or
desire to unite all the Slavic races into one confederacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panslavist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`slav"ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who favors
Panslavism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panslavonian</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`sla*vo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Panslavic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pansophical</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*soph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pansophy</er>.]</ety> <def>All-wise; claiming universal knowledge;
<as>as, <ex>pansophical</ex> pretenders</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>John Worthington.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pansophy</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"so*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> +
Gr. <?/ wisdom, <?/ wise: cf. F. <ets>pansophie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Universal wisdom; esp., a system of universal knowledge proposed by
<persfn>Comenius</persfn> (1592 -- 1671), a Moravian educator.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Hartlib.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panspermism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`sperm"ism</hw> <def>Same as <er>panspermia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panspermist</ent><br/
<ent>Panspermatist</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan*sper"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan"sper`mist</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A believer
in panspermy; one who rejects the theory of spontaneous generation; a
biogenist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panspermic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`sper"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to panspermy; <as>as, the <ex>panspermic</ex>
hypothesis</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panspermy</ent><br/
<ent>Panspermia</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pan`sper"mi*a</hw>, <hw>Pan"sper`my</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a seed.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The doctrine of the widespread
distribution of germs, from which under favorable circumstances
bacteria, vibrios, etc., may develop.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
doctrine that all organisms must come from living parents; biogenesis;
-- the opposite of <ant>spontaneous generation</ant>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>The theory that life on earth originated from spores or germs that
evolved elsewhere in the uiniverse; -- in contradistinction to the
theory that life evolved on earth from inanimate matter. This theory,
originally suggested by <person>S. Arrhenius</person> in 1907, is
sometimes advanced by those who feel that the time required for
evolution of life is too long for life to have evolved on Earth from
inanimate matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panstereorama</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*ste`re*o*ra"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <?/ solid + <?/ a
view.]</ety> <def>A model of a town or country, in relief, executed in
wood, cork, pasteboard, or the like.</def> <rj><au>Brande &
C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pansy</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pansies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>Pens<eacute/e</ets>
thought, pansy, fr. <ets>penser</ets> to think, L. <ets>pensare</ets> to
weigh, ponder. See <er>Pensive</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant of the genus <gen>Viola</gen> (<spn>Viola tricolor</spn>) and its
blossom, originally purple and yellow. Cultivated varieties have very
large flowers of a great diversity of colors. Called also
<altname>heart's-ease</altname>, <altname>love-in-idleness</altname>,
and many other quaint names.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pant</ent><br/
<hw>Pant</hw> <pr>(p<adot/nt)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Panted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Panting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>panteler</ets> to
gasp for breath, OF. <ets>panteisier</ets> to be breathless, F.
<ets>pantois</ets> out of breath; perh. akin to E. <ets>phantom</ets>,
the verb prob. orig. meaning, to have the nightmare.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To breathe quickly or in a labored manner, as after exertion or
from eagerness or excitement; to respire with heaving of the breast; to
gasp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pluto <qex>plants</qex> for breath from out his cell.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To long eagerly; to desire
earnestly; -- often used with <ptcl>for</ptcl> or
<ptcl>after</ptcl>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the hart <qex>panteth</qex> after the water brooks.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xlii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who <qex>pants</qex> for glory finds but short repose.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To beat with unnatural violence or rapidity; to
palpitate, or throb; -- said of the heart.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To sigh; to flutter; to languish.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whispering breeze<br/
<qex>Pants</qex> on the leaves, and dies upon the trees.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pant</ent><br/
<hw>Pant</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To breathe forth
quickly or in a labored manner; to gasp out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a cavern where my spirit<br/
Was <qex>panted</qex> forth in anguish.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To long for; to be eager after.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then shall our hearts <qex>pant</qex> thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pant</ent><br/
<hw>Pant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A quick breathing; a
catching of the breath; a gasp.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A violent palpitation of the heart.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pant</ent><br/
<hw>pant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A single leg of a pair of pants. See
<er>pants</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pant</ent><br/
<hw>pant</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to pants.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panta-</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ta-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Pan-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantable</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ta*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pantofle</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantacosm</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ta*cosm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Panta-</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>ko`smos</grk> universe.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Cosmolabe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantagraph</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ta*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pantograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantagruelism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tag"ru*el*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Pantagruel</ets>, one of the characters of Rabelais.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The theory or practice of the medical profession; --
used in burlesque or ridicule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An assumption of buffoonery to cover some serious
purpose.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Donaldson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantalet</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ta*let"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>pantaloon</ets>.]</ety> <def>One of the legs of the loose drawers
worn by children and women; a pant leg; particularly, the lower part of
such a garment, coming below the knee, often made in a separate piece;
-- chiefly in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantaloon</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ta*loon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pantalon</ets>, fr. It. <ets>pantalone</ets>, a masked character in
the Italian comedy, who wore breeches and stockings that were all of one
piece, from <ets>Pantaleone</ets>, the patron saint of Venice, which, as
a baptismal name, is very frequent among the Venetians, and is applied
to them by the other Italians as a nickname, fr. Gr.
<grk>Pantale`wn</grk>, lit., all lion, a Greek personal name.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A ridiculous character, or an old dotard, in the
Italian comedy; also, a buffoon in pantomimes.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sixth age shifts<br/
Into the lean and slippered <qex>pantaloon</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A bifurcated garment for a man,
covering the body from the waist downwards, and consisting of breeches
and stockings in one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>In recent times, a loose-fitting
variety of <er>Trousers</er>, often of less than ankle length.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantaloonery</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ta*loon"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The character or performances of a pantaloon; buffoonery.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Lamb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Materials for pantaloons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantamorph</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ta*morph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
assumes, or exists in, all forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantamorphic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ta*mor"phic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panta-</ets> + Gr. <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety> <def>Taking
all forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantascope</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ta*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panta-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Photog.)</fld>
<def>A pantascopic camera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantascopic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ta*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Viewing all;
taking a view of the whole. See under <er>Camera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1037 --></p>

<p><ent>Pantastomata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan`ta*stom"a*ta</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/n`t<adot/*st<ocr/m"<adot/*t<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>panto`s</grk>, all +
<grk>sto`ma</grk>, <grk>sto`matos</grk>, mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the divisions of Flagellata, including
the monads and allied forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantechnicon</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tech"ni*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pan-</er>, and <er>Technic</er>.]</ety> <def>A depository or place
where all sorts of manufactured articles are collected for
sale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantelegraph</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tel"e*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>telegraph</ets>.]</ety> <def>See under
<er>Telegraph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panter</ent><br/
<hw>Pant"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pants.</def>
<rj><au>Congreve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panter</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>panetier</ets>. See <er>Pantry</er>.]</ety> <def>A keeper of the
pantry; a pantler.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tyndale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panter</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Painter</er> a
rope.]</ety> <def>A net; a noose.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panteutonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`teu*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>Teutonic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to all the Teutonic races.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantheism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"the*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> +
<ets>theism</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrine that the universe, taken or
conceived of as a whole, is God; the doctrine that there is no God but
the combined force and natural laws which are manifested in the existing
universe; cosmotheism. The doctrine denies that God is a rational
personality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantheist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"the*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds to
pantheism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantheistical</ent><br/
<ent>Pantheistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan`the*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan`the*is"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to pantheism;
founded in, or leading to, pantheism.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pan`the*is"tic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantheologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`the*ol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed
in pantheology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantheology</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`the*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> + <ets>theology</ets>.]</ety> <def>A system of
theology embracing all religions; a complete system of
theology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantheon</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*the"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pantheon</ets>, <ets>pantheum</ets>, Gr. <grk>pa`nqeion</grk> (sc.
<grk>'iero`n</grk>), fr. <grk>pa`nqeios</grk> of all gods;
<grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <grk>qe`os</grk> a god: cf. F.
<ets>panth<eacute/on</ets>. See <er>Pan-</er>, and
<er>Theism</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A temple dedicated to all the
gods; especially, the building so called at Rome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The collective gods of a people, or a work treating
of them; <as>as, a divinity of the Greek
<ex>pantheon</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panther</ent><br/
<hw>pan"ther</hw> <pr>(p<acr/n"th<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pantere</ets>, F. <ets>panth<egrave/re</ets>, L.
<ets>panthera</ets>, Gr. <grk>pa`nqhr</grk>, prob. fr. Skr.
<ets>pundr<imac/ka</ets> a tiger.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large dark-colored variety of
the leopard, by some Zoologists considered a distinct species. It is
marked with large ringlike spots, the centers of which are darker than
the color of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>In America, the name is applied
to the puma, or cougar, and sometimes to the jaguar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Illustr. of Panther (Felis leopardus, or pardus) --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Panther cat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
ocelot.</cd> -- <col><b>Panther cowry</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a spotted East Indian cowry (<spn>Cypr<ae/a pantherina</spn>); -- so
called from its color.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panthera</ent><br/
<hw>Panthera</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The genus of large felines
including the lions; leopards; snow leopards; jaguars; tigers; cheetahs;
and saber-toothed tigers.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Panthera</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pantheress</ent><br/
<hw>pan"ther*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A female
panther.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantherine</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ther*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a panther,
esp. in color; <as>as, the <ex>pantherine</ex> snake (<spn>Ptyas
mucosus</spn>) of Brazil</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pantie</ent><br/
<hw>pantie</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pair of short underpants for women
or children (usually used in the plural).</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> panty, scanty, step-in.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantile</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"tile`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[5th <ets>pan +
tile</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A roofing tile, of peculiar
form, having a transverse section resembling an elongated S laid on its
side (<?/).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panting</ent><br/
<hw>panting</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Breathing laboriously or
convulsively.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> gasping, out-of-breath(predicate), pursy,
short-winded, winded.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panting</ent><br/
<hw>panting</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process of breathing
heavily, usually after exertion.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> heaving.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any fabric used to make trousers.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> trousering.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pantingly</ent><br/
<hw>pant"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With palpitation
or rapid breathing.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantisocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ti*soc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panto-</ets> + Gr. <?/ equal + <?/ to rule.]</ety> <def>A
Utopian community, in which all should rule equally, such as was devised
by Coleridge, Lovell, and Southey, in their younger days.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantisocrat</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tis"o*crat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
pantisocratist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantisocratic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ti*so*crat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a pantisocracy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantisocratist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`ti*soc"ra*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
favors or supports the theory of a pantisocracy.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantler</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"tler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>panetier</ets>. See <er>Panter</er>, <er>Pantry</er>.]</ety>
<def>The servant or officer, in a great family, who has charge of the
bread and the pantry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panto-</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Pan-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantochronometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`to*chro*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panto-</ets> + <ets>chronometer</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
instrument combining a compass, sundial, and universal time dial.</def>
<rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantofle</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*to"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pantoufle</ets>.]</ety> <def>A slipper for the foot.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pantable</asp> and
<asp>pantoble</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"to*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panto-</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pantographe</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for copying plans,
maps, and other drawings, on the same, or on a reduced or an enlarged,
scale.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pantagraph</asp>, and incorrectly
<asp>pentagraph</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An electrical conducting device consisting of a
collapsible frame resembling a pantograph{1}, connected to the top of an
electrically-powered vehicle such as a trolley, and used to conduct
electrical current between the vehicle and an overhead electric wire,
which supplies the power to the vehicle. The variable height of the
pantograph ensures that it can move to follow variations in the height
of the overhead wires, and thus make constant contact with the
wires.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skew pantograph</b></col>, <cd>a kind of pantograph for
drawing a copy which is inclined with respect to the original figure; --
also called <altname>plagiograph</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantographical</ent><br/
<ent>Pantographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan`to*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pan`to*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pantographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
a pantograph; relating to pantography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantography</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pantographie</ets>.]</ety> <def>A general description; entire view
of an object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantological</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`to*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to pantology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
pantology; a writer of pantology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantology</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panto-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A systematic
view of all branches of human knowledge; a work of universal
information.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panto-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pantom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for measuring
angles for determining elevations, distances, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*tom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Universal
measurement.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Pan`to*met"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantomime</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"to*mime</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pantomimus</ets>, Gr. <?/, lit., all-imitating; <grk>pa^s</grk>,
<grk>panto`s</grk>, all + <?/ to imitate: cf. It. <ets>pantomimo</ets>.
See <er>Mimic</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A universal mimic; an actor
who assumes many parts; also, any actor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who acts his part by gesticulation or dumb show
only, without speaking; a pantomimist; a mime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] saw a <qex>pantomime</qex> perform so well that he could
follow the performance from the action alone.</q>
<rj><qau>Tylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A dramatic representation by actors who use only
dumb show; a depiction of an event, narrative, or situation using only
gestures and bodily movements, without speaking; hence, dumb show,
generally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A dramatic and spectacular entertainment of which
dumb acting as well as burlesque dialogue, music, and dancing by Clown,
Harlequin, etc., are features.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantomime</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"to*mime</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Representing only in mute
actions; pantomimic; <as>as, a <ex>pantomime</ex> dance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantomimical</ent><br/
<ent>Pantomimic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pan`to*mim"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pan`to*mim"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pantomimique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the pantomime;
representing by dumb show.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pantomimic</xex>
gesture.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Warburton.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pan`to*mim"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantomimist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"to*mi`mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An actor in
pantomime; also, a composer of pantomimes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panton</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>patin</ets>.
See <er>Patten</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A horseshoe to
correct a narrow, hoofbound heel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantophagist</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*toph"a*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pantophagous</er>.]</ety> <def>A person or an animal that has the
habit of eating all kinds of food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantophagous</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*toph"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pantofa`gos</grk>; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>panto`s</grk>, all +
<grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat.]</ety> <def>Eating all kinds of
food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantophagy</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*toph"a*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pantofagi`a</grk>.]</ety> <def>The habit or power of eating all
kinds of food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pan*top"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Panto-</er>, & <er>-poda</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Pycnogonida</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantoscopic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan`to*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Panto-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets> + <ets>-ic</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Literally, seeing everything; -- a term applied to eyeglasses or
spectacles divided into two segments, the upper being designed for
distant vision, the lower for vision of near objects.</def><-- = bifocal
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pantry</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pantries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>pantrie</ets>, F.
<ets>paneterie</ets>, fr. <ets>panetier</ets> pantler, LL.
<ets>panetarius</ets> baker, <ets>panetus</ets> small loaf of bread, L.
<ets>panis</ets> bread. Cf. <er>Company</er>, <er>Pannier</er>,
<er>Pantler</er>.]</ety> <def>An apartment or closet in which bread and
other provisions are kept.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pants</ent><br/
<hw>pants</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A
garment extending from the waist to the knee or ankle, covering each leg
separately.</def> <syn><b>Syn. --</b> trousers.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Underpants.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> drawers.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>Underpants worn by
women; panties.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bloomers, drawers, panties.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pantywaist</ent><br/
<hw>pantywaist</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A timid man or boy considered
childish or unassertive.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> sissy, pansy, milksop, Milquetoast.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panurgic</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*ur"gic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr.
<grk>panoyrgiko`s</grk> knavish.]</ety> <def>Skilled in all kinds of
work.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>panurgic</xex> Diderot.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J.
Morley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panurgy</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"ur*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>panoyrgi`a</grk>, fr. <grk>panoy^rgos</grk>, properly, ready to do
anything; hence, knavish, roguish; <grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all
+ <grk>'e`rgon</grk> work.]</ety> <def>Skill in all kinds of work or
business; craft.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panyard</ent><br/
<hw>Pan"yard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pannier</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Pepys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panym</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"nym</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Panim</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panzer</ent><br/
<hw>panzer</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A German tank of the kind used in
World War II.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>panzer</ent><br/
<hw>panzer</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <ety>[German, <ets>Panzer</ets>
armor.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Equipped with armored vehicles; armored;
<as>as, a <ex>panzer</ex> division</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of, pertaining to, or involving armored military
vehicles; <as>as, a <ex>panzer</ex> attack</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Panzoism</ent><br/
<hw>Pan*zo"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pan-</ets> +
Gr. <grk>zw^,on</grk> an animal.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A term
used to denote all of the elements or factors which constitute vitality
or vital energy.</def> <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paolo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"o*lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. Cf.
<er>Paul</er>.]</ety> <def>An old Italian silver coin, worth about ten
cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pap</ent><br/
<hw>Pap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OSw. <ets>papp</ets>.
Cf. <er>Pap</er> soft food.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A nipple; a mammilla; a
teat.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>paps</qex> which thou hast sucked.</q> <rj><qau>Luke xi.
27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rounded, nipplelike hill or peak; anything
resembling a nipple in shape; a mamelon.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pap</ent><br/
<hw>Pap</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>pap</ets>, G.
<ets>pappe</ets>, both perh. fr. L. <ets>papa</ets>, <ets>pappa</ets>,
the word with which infants call for food: cf. It.
<ets>pappa</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A soft food for infants, made
of bread boiled or softened in milk or water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any speech, writing, or idea
lacking substance, or of trivial content; oversimplified, trite, or
worthless ideas.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pablum, drivel, twaddle.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Nourishment or support from official patronage;
<as>as, <ex>treasury</ex> pap</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. &
Contemptuous]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The pulp of fruit.</def>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pap</ent><br/
<hw>Pap</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To feed with pap.</def>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papa</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pa"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>papa</ets>, L.
<ets>papa</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/, <?/, a child's word meaning father. Cf.
<er>Pope</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A child's word for father.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A parish priest in the Greek Church.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papabote</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`pa*bo"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably of
Creole origin.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The upland plover.</def>
<mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pa*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>papatia</ets>, fr. L. <ets>papa</ets> a father, bishop. See
<er>Pope</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The office and dignity of the
pope, or pontiff, of Rome; papal jurisdiction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The popes, collectively; the succession of
popes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The Roman Catholic religion; -- commonly used by the
opponents of the Roman Catholics in disparagement or in an opprobrious
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papagay</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"a*gay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Popinjay</er>, 1 <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papain</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pa"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Papaw</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A proteolytic
ferment, like trypsin, present in the juice of the green fruit of the
papaw (<spn>Carica Papaya</spn>) of tropical America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>papa</ets> bishop. See <er>Papacy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the pope of Rome; proceeding
from the pope; ordered or pronounced by the pope; <as>as, <ex>papal</ex>
jurisdiction; a <ex>papal</ex> edict; the <ex>papal</ex>
benediction.</as></def> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Roman Catholic Church.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Papal</xex> Christians.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp.
Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Papal cross</b></col>. <cd>See <xex>Illust.</xex> 3 of
<er>Cross</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Papal crown</b></col>, <cd>the
tiara.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papalist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pal*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A papist.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papality</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>papalitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>papaut<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
papacy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ld. Berners.</au>
<au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papalize</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make
papal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papalize</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pal*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To conform to popery.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papally</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a papal manner;
popishly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papalty</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pal*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The papacy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaphobia</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`pa*pho"bi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>papa</ets> bishop + Gr. <?/ to fear.]</ety> <def>Intense fear or
dread of the pope, or of the Roman Catholic Church.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paparazzo</ent><br/
<hw>paparazzo</hw> <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>paparazzi</plw></plu>.
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[from Signore <etsep>Paparazzo</etsep>, a
photographer in <person>Fellini's</person> film <film>La Dolce
Vita</film>.]</ety><sn>1.</sn> <def>A free-lance photographer that
specializes in following and photographing celebrities such as movie
stars, especially to obtain candid photographs in private situations;
<as>as, her dogged pursuit by the <ex>paparazzi</ex> was believed to be
a major factor in Princess Diana's death</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paparchy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"par*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>papa</ets>
bishop + <ets>-archy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Government by a pope; papal
rule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaver</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*pa"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
poppy.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants, including the
poppy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaveraceae</ent><br/
<hw>Papaveraceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of herbs
or shrubs having milky and often colored juices and capsular
fruits.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Papaveraceae</fam>, poppy
family.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaveraceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pav`er*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or resembling, a natural family of plants
(<fam>Papaveraceae</fam>) of which the poppy, the celandine, and the
bloodroot are well-known examples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaverine</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pav"er*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An alkaloid found in opium. It has a weaker therapeutic action than
morphine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaverous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pav"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the poppy; of the nature of the poppy.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papaw</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*paw"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. from the native
name in the West Indies; cf. Sp. <ets>papayo</ets> papaw,
<ets>papaya</ets> the fruit of the papaw.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pawpaw</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>papaya</er>, senses 1 and 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tree of the genus
<gen>Asimina</gen> (<spn>Asimina triloba</spn>), growing in the western
and southern parts of the United States, and producing a sweet edible
fruit; also, the fruit itself.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>papaya</ent><br/
<hw>pa*pa"ya</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. from the
native name in the West Indies; cf. Sp. <ets>papayo</ets> papaw,
<ets>papaya</ets> the fruit of the papaw.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tree (<spn>Carica Papaya</spn>) of tropical
America, belonging to the order <ord>Passiflore<ae/</ord>; called also
<altname>papaw</altname> and <altname>pawpaw</altname>. It has a soft,
spongy stem, eighteen or twenty feet high, crowned with a tuft of large,
long-stalked, palmately lobed leaves. The milky juice of the plant is
said to have the property of making meat tender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The fruit of the papaya tree; it is a dull
orange-colored, melon-shaped fruit, which is eaten both raw and cooked
or pickled. The fruit contains <prod>papain</prod>, a
protease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papboat</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"boat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind
of sauce boat or dish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large spiral East Indian marine
shell (<spn>Turbinella rapha</spn>); -- so called because used by native
priests to hold the oil for anointing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pape</ent><br/
<hw>Pape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pape</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>papa</ets>. See <er>Pope</er>.]</ety> <def>A spiritual
father; specifically, the pope.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papejay</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pe*jay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A popinjay.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paper</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"per</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>papier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>papyrus</ets> papyrus, from which the
Egyptians made a kind of paper, Gr. <grk>pa`pyros</grk>. Cf.
<er>Papyrus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A substance in the form of
thin sheets or leaves intended to be written or printed on, or to be
used in wrapping. It is made of rags, straw, bark, wood, or other
fibrous material, which is first reduced to pulp, then molded, pressed,
and dried.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sheet, leaf, or piece of such substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A printed or written instrument; a document, essay,
or the like; a writing; <as>as, a <ex>paper</ex> read before a
scientific society</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They brought a <qex>paper</qex> to me to be signed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A printed sheet appearing periodically; a newspaper;
a journal; <as>as, a daily <ex>paper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Negotiable evidences of indebtedness; notes; bills
of exchange, and the like; <as>as, the bank holds a large amount of his
<ex>paper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Decorated hangings or coverings for walls, made of
paper. See <cref>Paper hangings</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A paper containing (usually) a definite quantity;
<as>as, a <ex>paper</ex> of pins, tacks, opium, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A medicinal preparation spread upon paper, intended
for external application; <as>as, cantharides
<ex>paper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Documents establishing a person's
identity, or status, or attesting to some right, such as the right to
drive a vehicle; <as>as, the border guard asked for his
<ex>papers</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Paper is manufactured in sheets, the trade names of
which, together with the regular sizes in inches, are shown in the
following table. But paper makers vary the size somewhat.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- insert table of paper trade names and sizes --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note> In the manufacture of books, etc., a sheet, of whatever size
originally, is termed, when folded once, a <xex>folio</xex>; folded
twice, a <xex>quarto</xex>, or <xex>4to</xex>; three times, an
<xex>octavo</xex>, or <xex>8vo</xex>; four times, a
<xex>sextodecimo</xex>, or 16<xex>mo</xex>; five times, a
32<xex>mo</xex>; three times, with an offcut folded twice and set in, a
<xex>duodecimo</xex>, or 12<xex>mo</xex>; four times, with an offcut
folded three times and set in, a 24<xex>mo</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1038 --></p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Paper</xex> is often used adjectively or in
combination, having commonly an obvious signification; as,
<ex>paper</ex> cutter or <ex>paper</ex>-cutter; <ex>paper</ex> knife,
<ex>paper</ex>-knife, or <ex>paper</ex>knife; <ex>paper</ex> maker,
<ex>paper</ex>-maker, or <ex>paper</ex>maker; <ex>paper</ex> mill or
<ex>paper</ex>-mill; <ex>paper</ex> weight, <ex>paper</ex>-weight, or
<ex>paper</ex>weight, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Business paper</b></col>, <cd>checks, notes, drafts,
etc., given in payment of actual indebtedness; -- opposed to
<xex>accommodation paper</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Fly paper</b></col>,
<cd>paper covered with a sticky preparation, -- used for catching
flies.</cd> -- <col><b>Laid paper</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Laid</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper birch</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the canoe birch tree (<spn>Betula papyracea</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Paper blockade</b></col>, <cd>an ineffective blockade, as by a
weak naval force.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper boat</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a boat made of water-proof paper.</cd> --
<col><b>Paper car wheel</b></col> <fld>(Railroad)</fld>, <cd>a car wheel
having a steel tire, and a center formed of compressed paper held
between two plate-iron disks.</cd> <au>Forney.</au> -- <col><b>Paper
credit</b></col>, <cd>credit founded upon evidences of debt, such as
promissory notes, duebills, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper hanger</b></col>,
<cd>one who covers walls with paper hangings.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper
hangings</b></col>, <cd>paper printed with colored figures, or otherwise
made ornamental, prepared to be pasted against the walls of apartments,
etc.; wall paper.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper house</b></col>, <cd>an audience
composed of people who have come in on free passes.</cd>
<mark>[Cant]</mark> -- <col><b>Paper money</b></col>, <cd>notes or
bills, usually issued by government or by a banking corporation,
promising payment of money, and circulated as the representative of
coin.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper mulberry</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See under Mulberry.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper muslin</b></col>,
<cd>glazed muslin, used for linings, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper
nautilus</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Argonauta</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Paper reed</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the papyrus.</cd>
-- <col><b>Paper sailor</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
Argonauta.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper stainer</b></col>, <cd>one who colors
or stamps wall paper.</cd> <au>De Colange.</au> -- <col><b>Paper
wasp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any wasp which makes a nest of
paperlike material, as the yellow jacket.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper
weight</b></col>, <cd>any object used as a weight to prevent loose
papers from being displaced by wind, or otherwise.</cd> -- <col><b>on
paper</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>in writing; <as>as, I would like to
see that <ex>on paper</ex></as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>in theory, though
not necessarily in paractice.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>in the design state;
planned, but not yet put into practice.</cd> -- <col><b>Parchment
paper</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Papyrine</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Tissue
paper</b></col>, <cd>thin, gauzelike paper, such as is used to protect
engravings in books.</cd> -- <col><b>Wall paper</b></col>. <cd>Same as
<cref>Paper hangings</cref>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Waste
paper</b></col>, <cd>paper thrown aside as worthless or useless, except
for uses of little account.</cd> -- <col><b>Wove paper</b></col>, <cd>a
writing paper with a uniform surface, not ribbed or watermarked.</cd> --
<col><b>paper tiger</b></col>, <cd>a person or group that appears to be
powerful and dangerous but is in fact weak and
ineffectual.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paper</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to paper; made of paper; resembling
paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Existing only on paper; unsubstantial; having very
overrated power; <as>as, a <ex>paper</ex> box; a <ex>paper</ex> army; a
<ex>paper</ex> tiger.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paper</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>papered</conjf> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>papering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover or
line with paper, especially with wallpaper; to furnish with paper
hangings; to wallpaper; <as>as, to <ex>paper</ex> a room or a
house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fold or inclose in paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put on paper; to make a memorandum of.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperback</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*back`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*b<acr/k`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A book with paper covers; -- contrasted with
<contr>hardback</contr> or <contr>hardcover</contr>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> softback, soft-cover.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperback</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*back</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>having a flexible binding; --
of books. Contrasted to <contr>hardcover</contr> or
<contr>hardbacked</contr> or <contr>hardbound</contr>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paperbacked, soft-bound, softcover,
softback.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperbacked</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*backed</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*b<acr/kt`)</pr>,
<pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>same as <er>paperback</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paperback.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paper boy</ent><br/
<ent>paperboy</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pa"per*boy`</hw>, <hw>pa"per boy`</hw></mhw>
<pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*boi`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boy who sells or
delivers newspapers.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperclip</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*clip`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*kl<icr/p`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A folder wire or plastic fastener for holding sheets
of paper together.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> gem clip.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperhanger</ent><br/
<ent>paperer</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pa"per*er</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*<etil/r)</pr>,
<hw>pa"per*hang`er</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*h<acr/<nsm/`<etil/r)</pr>,</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One whose occupation is decorating walls with wallpaper.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperhanging</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*hang`ing</hw>
<pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*h<acr/<nsm/`<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
application of wallpaper to walls for decorative purposes.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> papering.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>papering</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*ing</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Same as <er>paperhanging</er>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperknife</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*knife`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*n<imac/f`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A dull knife used to cut open the envelopes in which
letters are mailed or to slit uncut pages of books.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> letter opener.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paper over</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per o"ver</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*<omac/"v<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To ignore or conceal (a disagreement or dispute)
so as to continue friendly or productive relations; <as>as, to <ex>paper
over</ex> differences</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>papers</ent><br/
<hw>pa"pers</hw> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Documents providing information,
esp. of an official nature about a person, vehicle, business, etc. See
<er>paper</er>{9}, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> document, written document.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperweight</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*weight`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*w<amac/t`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See under <er>Paper</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paperwork</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*work`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*w<ucir/rk`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Work that involves handling or writing documents such
as forms, letters, reports, sales records, etc.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>papery</ent><br/
<hw>pa"per*y</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"p<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Like paper; having the thinness or consistency of paper.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papescent</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pes"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pap</er>
soft food.]</ety> <def>Containing or producing pap; like pap.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papess</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>papesse</ets>.]</ety> <def>A female pope; <it>i. e.</it>, the
fictitious <person>pope Joan</person>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papeterie</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`pe*terie"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., paper
manufacture, fr. <ets>papier</ets> paper.]</ety> <def>A case or box
containing paper and materials for writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paphian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"phi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Paphius</ets>, Gr. <?/, from <?/ the city Paphos.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to <city>Paphos</city>, an ancient city of Cyprus, having a
celebrated temple of <persfn>Venus</persfn>; hence, pertaining to
<persfn>Venus</persfn>, or her rites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paphian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"phi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
<city>Paphos</city>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papier-mache</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`pier"-ma`ch<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>papier m<acir/ch<eacute/</ets>, lit., chewed or mashed
paper.]</ety> <def>A hard and strong substance made of a pulp from
paper, mixed with size or glue, etc. It is formed into various articles,
usually by means of molds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papilio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*pil"i*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>peop. n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
butterfly.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
butterflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Formerly it included numerous species which are now
placed in other genera. By many writers it is now restricted to the
swallow-tailed butterflies, like <spn>Papilio polyxenes</spn>, syn.
<spn>Papilio asterias</spn>, and related species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papilionaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pil`io*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Resembling the butterfly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having a winged
corolla somewhat resembling a butterfly, as in the blossoms of the bean
and pea.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Belonging to that suborder of
leguminous plants (<subord>Papilionace<ae/</subord>) which includes the
bean, pea, vetch, clover, and locust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papiliones</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*pil`i*o"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Papilio</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The division of
<ord>Lepidoptera</ord> which includes the butterflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papilionides</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*pil`i*on"i*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The typical
butterflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papilla</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pil"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Papill<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a nipple,
pimple.]</ety> <def>Any minute nipplelike projection; <as>as, the
<ex>papill<ae/</ex> of the tongue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillar</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>papillaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Papillose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillary</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>papillaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to, or resembling, a
papilla or papill<ae/; bearing, or covered with, papill<ae/;
papillose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillate</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To cover
with papill<ae/; to take the form of a papilla, or of
papill<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillate</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Papillose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papilliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pil"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Papilla</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Shaped like a
papilla; mammilliform.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papilloma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pap`il*lo"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Papillomata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Papilla</er>, and <er>-Oma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
tumor formed by hypertrophy of the papill<ae/ of the skin or mucous
membrane, as a corn or a wart.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Pap`il*lo"ma*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or consisting of, papillomata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillose</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>papilleux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Covered with, or bearing, papill<ae/;
resembling papill<ae/; papillate; papillar; papillary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillote</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*lote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>papillon</ets> a butterfly.]</ety> <def>A small piece of paper on
which women roll up their hair to make it curl; a curl paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillous</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"il*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Papillary;
papillose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papillulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pil"lu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a minute papilla in the center of a larger elevation or
depression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papion</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pi*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. from native
name: cf. Sp. <ets>papion</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A West
African baboon (<spn>Cynocephalus sphinx</spn>), allied to the chacma.
Its color is generally chestnut, varying in tint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>papisme</ets>. See <er>Pape</er>, <er>Pope</er>.]</ety>
<def>Popery; -- an offensive term.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>papiste</ets>. See <er>Pape</er>, <er>Pope</er>.]</ety> <def>A
Roman Catholic; one who adheres to the Church of Rome and the authority
of the pope; -- an offensive designation applied to Roman Catholics by
their opponents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papistical</ent><br/
<ent>Papistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*pis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*pis"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>papistique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Church of
Rome and its doctrines and ceremonies; pertaining to popery; popish; --
used disparagingly.</def> <ldquo/The old <xex>papistic</xex>
worship.<rdquo/ <au>T. Warton.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pa*pis"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papistry</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pist*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrine and
ceremonies of the Church of Rome; popery.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Whitgift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papized</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"pized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pape</er>.]</ety> <def>Conformed to popery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Papized</xex> writers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papoose</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*poose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A babe or young
child of Indian parentage in North America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pappiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"pi*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Resembling the pappus of composite plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pappoose</ent><br/
<hw>Pap*poose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Papoose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>papoose root</ent><br/
<ent>papooseroot</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>papooseroot</hw>, <hw>papoose root</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tall herb (<spn>Caulophyllum
thalictroides</spn>) of eastern North America and Asia having blue
berrylike fruit and a thick knotty rootstock formerly used medicinally;
the <altname>Cohosh</altname>. See also <er>Cohosh</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> blue cohosh, blueberry root, papoose root,
squawroot, squaw root, Caulophyllum thalictrioides, <spn>Caulophyllum
thalictroides</spn>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pappose</ent><br/
<hw>Pap*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Furnished with a pappus; downy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pappous</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"pous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Pappose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pappus</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., an old man or
grandfather; hence, a substance resembling gray hairs, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The hairy or feathery appendage of the achenes of
thistles, dandelions, and most other plants of the order
<ord>Composit<ae/</ord>; also, the scales, awns, or bristles which
represent the calyx in other plants of the same order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pappy</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pap</er> soft
food.]</ety> <def>Like pap; soft; succulent; tender.</def>
<rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paprica</ent><br/
<ent>Paprika</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa"pri*ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> Also
<hw>Pa"pri*ca</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[Hung. <ets>paprika</ets> Turkish
pepper; prob. through G.]</ety> <def>The dried ripened fruit of
<spn>Capsicum annuum</spn> or various other species of pepper; also, the
mildly pungent condiment prepared from it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papua</ent><br/
<hw>Papua</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A Pacific island north of
Australia; governed by Australia and Indonesia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> New Guinea.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papuan</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"u*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Papua.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papuars</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"u*ars</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Papuan</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>The native black race of Papua or New Guinea, and the adjacent
islands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pap"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Papul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A pimple; a small, usually
conical, elevation of the cuticle, produced by congestion, accumulated
secretion, or hypertrophy of tissue; a papule.</def>
<rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the numerous small hollow
processes of the integument between the plates of starfishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papular</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered
with papules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Consisting of papules;
characterized by the presence of papules; <as>as, a <ex>papular</ex>
eruption</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papule</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Papules</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Same as
<er>Papula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papulose</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"u*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Having papul<ae/; papillose; <as>as, a <ex>papulose</ex>
leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>papuleux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Covered with, or characterized by,
papul<ae/; papulose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papyraceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pap`y*ra"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>papyraceus</ets> made of <ets>papyrus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Made of
papyrus; of the consistency of paper; papery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papyrean</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pyr"e*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to papyrus, or to paper; papyraceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papyrine</ent><br/
<hw>Pap"y*rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>papyrin</ets> made of paper. See <er>Paper</er>.]</ety>
<def>Imitation parchment, made by soaking unsized paper in dilute
sulphuric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papyrograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*pyr"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Papyrus</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>An apparatus
for multiplying writings, drawings, etc., in which a paper stencil,
formed by writing or drawing with corrosive ink, is used. The word is
also used of other means of multiplying copies of writings, drawings,
etc. See <er>Copygraph</er>, <er>Hectograph</er>,
<er>Manifold</er>.</def> <mark>[Obsolescent]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papyrography</ent><br/
<hw>Pap`y*rog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The process
of multiplying copies of writings, etc., by means of the
papyrograph.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pap`y*ro*graph"ic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Papyrus</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*py"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Papyri</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>pa`pyros</grk>. See <er>Paper</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tall rushlike plant (<spn>Cyperus
Papyrus</spn>) of the Sedge family, formerly growing in Egypt, and now
found in Abyssinia, Syria, Sicily, etc. The stem is triangular and about
an inch thick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The material upon which the ancient Egyptians wrote.
It was formed by cutting the stem of the plant into thin longitudinal
slices, which were gummed together and pressed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A manuscript written on papyrus; esp.,
<pluf>pl.</pluf>, written scrolls made of papyrus; <as>as, the
<ex>papyri</ex> of Egypt or Herculaneum</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paque</ent><br/
<hw>P<acir/que</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<acir/que</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Pasch</er> and
<er>Easter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Par</ent><br/
<hw>Par</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Parr</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Par</ent><br/
<hw>Par</hw>, <pos>prep.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>per</ets>. See
<er>Per</er>.]</ety> <def>By; with; -- used frequently in Early English
in phrases taken from the French, being sometimes written as a part of
the word which it governs; <as>as, <ex>par</ex> amour, or
<ex>par</ex>amour; <ex>par</ex> cas, or <ex>par</ex>case; <ex>par</ex>
fay, or <ex>par</ex>fay.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Par</ent><br/
<hw>Par</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>par</ets>, adj.,
equal. See <er>Peer</er> an equal.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Equal value; equality of nominal and actual value;
the value expressed on the face or in the words of a certificate of
value, as a bond or other commercial paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Equality of condition or circumstances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An amount which is taken as an average or
mean.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>The number of strokes required for
a hole or a round played without mistake, two strokes being allowed on
each hole for putting. <xex>Par</xex> represents perfect play, whereas
<contr>bogey</contr> makes allowance on some holes for human frailty.
Thus if <xex>par</xex> for a course is 75, <xex>bogey</xex> is usually
put down, arbitrarily, as 81 or 82. If <ex>par</ex> for one hole is 5, a
<contr>bogey</contr> is 6, and a score of 7 strokes would be a
<contr>double bogey</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At par</b></col>, <cd>at the original price; neither at a
discount nor at a premium; -- used especially of financial instruments,
such as bonds.</cd> -- <col><b>Above par</b></col>, <cd>at a
premium.</cd> -- <col><b>Below par</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>at a
discount.</cd> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>less than the expected or usual quality;
-- of the quality of objects and of the performance of people; <as>as,
he performed <ex>below par</ex> in the game</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>On a
par</b></col>, <cd>on a level; in the same condition, circumstances,
position, rank, etc.; <as>as, their pretensions are <ex>on a par</ex>;
his ability is <ex>on a par</ex> with his ambition</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Par of exchange</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Exchange</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Par value</b></col>, <cd>nominal value; face value; -- used
especially of financial instruments, such as bonds.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para-</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>para`</grk> beside; prob.
akin to E. <ets>for-</ets> in <ets>forgive</ets>. Cf.
<er>For-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A prefix signifying
<sig>alongside of</sig>, <sig>beside</sig>, <sig>beyond</sig>,
<sig>against</sig>, <sig>amiss</sig>; as <xex>para</xex>ble, literally,
a placing beside; <xex>para</xex>dox, that which is contrary to opinion;
<xex>para</xex>chronism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A prefix denoting: <sd>(a)</sd>
<xex>Likeness</xex>, <xex>similarity</xex>, or <xex>connection</xex>, or
that the substance <xex>resembles</xex>, but is <xex>distinct
from</xex>, that to the name of which it is prefixed; as
<xex>par</xex>aldehyde, <xex>para</xex>conine, etc.; also, an
<xex>isomeric modification</xex>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<specif>Specifically:</specif> <fld>(Organ. Chem.)</fld> <cd>That two
groups or radicals substituted in the benzene nucleus are
<sig>opposite</sig>, or in the respective positions 1 and 4; 2 and 5; or
3 and 6, as <xex>para</xex>xylene; <xex>par</xex>oxybenzoic acid.</cd>
Cf. <er>Ortho-</er>, and <er>Meta-</er>. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ra"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk., fr. Per.
<ets>p<amac/rah</ets> a piece.]</ety> <def>A piece of Turkish money,
usually copper, the fortieth part of a piaster, or about one ninth of a
cent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*r<aacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
southern arm of the Amazon in Brazil; also, a seaport on this
arm.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Short for <er>Par<aacute/ rubber</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Par-anesthesia</ent><br/
<ent>Para-anaesthesia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Par`a-an`aes*the"si*a</hw>, <hw>Par`-an`es*the"si*a</hw>
}</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.; <ets>para-</ets> +
<ets>an<ae/sthesia</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An<ae/sthesia of
both sides of the lower half of the body.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabanic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*ban"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to pass
over.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, a
nitrogenous acid which is obtained by the oxidation of uric acid, as a
white crystalline substance (<chform>C3N2H2O3</chform>); -- also called
<xex>oxalyl urea</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parablast</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*blast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/ to
grow beside. See <er>Para-</er>, and <er>-blast</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A portion of the mesoblast (of peripheral
origin) of the developing embryo, the cells of which are especially
concerned in forming the first blood and blood vessels.</def> <rj><au>C.
S. Minot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parablastic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*blas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the parablast; <as>as, the <ex>parablastic</ex>
cells</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parable</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>parare</ets> to provide.]</ety>
<def>Procurable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parable</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>parabole</ets>, L.
<ets>parabola</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ a placing beside or together, a
comparing, comparison, a parable, fr. <?/ to throw beside, compare;
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ to throw; cf. Skr. <ets>gal</ets> to drop.
Cf. <er>Emblem</er>, <er>Gland</er>, <er>Palaver</er>,
<er>Parabola</er>, <er>Parley</er>, <er>Parabole</er>,
<er>Symbol</er>.]</ety> <def>A comparison; a similitude; specifically, a
short fictitious narrative of something which might really occur in life
or nature, by means of which a moral is drawn; <as>as, the
<ex>parables</ex> of Christ</as>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Declare unto us the <qex>parable</qex> of the tares.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xiii. 36.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Allegory</er>, and Note under
<er>Apologue</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parable</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To represent by parable.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which by the ancient sages was thus parabled.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabola</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rab"o*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Parabolas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/; -- so
called because its axis is parallel to the side of the cone. See
<er>Parable</er>, and cf. <er>Parabole</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A kind of curve; one of the conic sections formed by
the intersection of the surface of a cone with a plane parallel to one
of its sides. It is a curve, any point of which is equally distant from
a fixed point, called the <xex>focus</xex>, and a fixed straight line,
called the <xex>directrix</xex>. See <er>Focus</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>One of a group of curves defined by the equation <mathex>y =
ax<exp>n</exp></mathex> where <it>n</it> is a positive whole number or a
positive fraction. For the <stype>cubical parabola</stype> n = 3; for
the <stype>semicubical parabola</stype> n = <frac32/. See under
<er>Cubical</er>, and <er>Semicubical</er>. The parabolas have infinite
branches, but no rectilineal asymptotes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabole</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rab"o*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/. See <er>Parable</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>Similitude;
comparison.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabolical</ent><br/
<ent>Parabolic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*bol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*bol"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paraboliko`s</grk>
figurative: cf. F. <ets>parabolique</ets>. See
<er>Parable</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a parable; expressed by a parable
or figure; allegorical; <as>as, <ex>parabolical</ex>
instruction</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[From <er>Parabola</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having the form or nature of a parabola; pertaining
to, or resembling, a parabola; <as>as, a <ex>parabolic</ex>
curve</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having a form like that generated by
the revolution of a parabola, or by a line that moves on a parabola as a
directing curve; <as>as, a <ex>parabolic</ex> conoid; a
<ex>parabolic</ex> reflector; a <ex>parabolic</ex>
antenna</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parabolic conoid</b></col>, <cd>a paraboloid; a conoid
whose directing curve is a parabola. See <er>Conoid</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Parabolic mirror</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>a mirror
having a paraboloidal surface which gives for parallel rays (as those
from very distant objects) images free from aberration. It is used in
reflecting telescopes.</cd> -- <col><b>Parabolic spindle</b></col>,
<cd>the solid generated by revolving the portion of a parabola cut off
by a line drawn at right angles to the axis of the curve, about that
line as an axis.</cd> -- <col><b>Parabolic spiral</b></col>, <cd>a
spiral curve conceived to be formed by the periphery of a semiparabola
when its axis is wrapped about a circle; also, any other spiral curve
having an analogy to the parabola.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1039 --></p>

<p><ent>Parabolically</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*bol"ic*al*ly</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*b<ocr/l"<icr/*k<ait/l*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>By way of parable; in a parabolic manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the form of a parabola.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraboliform</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*bol"i*form</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*b<ocr/l"<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Parabola</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a
parabola in form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabolism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rab"o*lism</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<acr/b"<osl/*l<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Parabola</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Alg.)</fld>
<def>The division of the terms of an equation by a known quantity that
is involved in the first term.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabolist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rab"o*list</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
narrator of parables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraboloid</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rab"o*loid</hw> <pr>(-loid)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Parabola</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>parabolo<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The solid
generated by the rotation of a parabola about its axis; any surface of
the second order whose sections by planes parallel to a given line are
parabolas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>paraboloid</xex> has sometimes been
applied also to the parabolas of the higher orders.</note>
<rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraboloidal</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*bo*loid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or resembling, a paraboloid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parabronchium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*bron"chi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parabronchia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Para-</er>, <er>Bronchia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>One of the branches of an ectobronchium or entobronchium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracelsian</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cel"si*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. a.</pos> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or in conformity with, the practice of
<etsep>Paracelsus</etsep>, a Swiss physician and alchemist of the 15th
century.</def> <rj><au>Ferrand.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracelsian</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cel"si*an</hw>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A follower of
<persfn>Paracelsus</persfn> or his practice or teachings.</def>
<rj><au>Hakewill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracelsist</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cel"sist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A
Paracelsian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracelsus</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cel"sus</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<ecr/l"s<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n.</pos> <def><person>Philippus Aureolus Paracelsus</person>
(originally <person>Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim</person>, also
called <person>Theophrastus Paracelsus</person> and <person>Theophrastus
von Hohenheim</person>). Born at <city>Maria-Einsiedeln</city>, in the
Canton of Schwyz, <country>Switzerland</country>, Dec. 17 (or 10 Nov.),
1493: died at <city>Salzburg</city>, Sept. 23 (or 24), 1541. A
celebrated German-Swiss physician, reformer of therapeutics,
iatrochemist, and alchemist. He attended school in a small lead-mining
district where his father, <person>William Bombast von
Hohenheim</person>, was a physician and teacher of alchemy. The family
originally came from <city>W<uum/rtemberg</city>, where the noble family
of Bombastus was in possession of the ancestral castle of Hohenheim near
<city>Stuttgart</city> until 1409. He entered the University of
<city>Basel</city> at the age of sixteen, where he adopted the name
<qex>Paracelsus</qex>, after <persfn>Celsius</persfn>, a noted Roman
physician. But he left without a degree, first going to
<city>Wurtzburg</city> to study under <person>Joannes
Trithemius</person>, Abbot of Sponheim (1462-1516), a famous astrologer
and alchemist, who initiated him into the mysteries of alchemy. He then
spent many years in travel and intercourse with distinguished scholars,
studied and practiced medicine and surgery, and at one point attended
the Diet of Worms. He was appointed to the office of city physician of
Basel, which also made him a lecturer on medicine at <city>Basel</city>
about 1526, where, through the publisher <person>Johan
Frobenius</person> he made friends with the scholar
<persfn>Erasmus</persfn>; and there he fulminated against the medical
pseudo-science of his day, and against the blind adherence to ancient
medical authorities such as <persfn>Hippocrates</persfn>,
<persfn>Galen</persfn>, and <persfn>Avicenna</persfn>, which was still
the prevalent philosophy of medicine in the sixteenth century. But soon,
in 1528, he was driven from the city by the medical corporations, whose
methods he had severely criticized. He found refuge with friends, and
traveled and practiced medicine, but could not find a publisher willing
to print his books. He preached frequently the need for experimentation
in medicine. He is important in the history of medicine chiefly on
account of the impetus which he gave to the development of
pharmaceutical chemistry. He was also the author of a visionary and
theosophic system of philosophy. The first collective edition of his
works appeared at <city>Basel</city> in 1589-91. Among the many legends
concerning him is that concerning his long sword, which he obtained
while serving as barber-surgeon during the Neapolitan wars. It was
rumored that in the hilt of the sword he kept a familiar or small demon;
some thought he carried the elixer of life in the sword. He is buried in
the cemetary of the <org>Hospital of St. Sebastian</org> in
<city>Salzburg</city>. For more detailed information about Paracelsus,
there is a special project, the <a
href="https://www.paracelsus.uzh.ch/">Zurich Paracelsus Project</a>
available on the Web.</def> <au>Century Dict., 1906</au>; <au>Bernard
Jaffe (Crucibles: The Story of Chemistry, Revised Edition,
1948).</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The apothecaries, too, were enraged against this iconoclast
[Paracelsus]. For had he not, as official town physician, demanded the
right to inspect their stocks and rule over their prescriptions which he
denounced as "foul broths"? These apothecaries had grown fat on the
barbarous prescriptions of the local doctors. "The physician's duty is
to heal the sick, not to enrich the apothecaries," he had warned them,
and refused to send his patients to them to have the prescriptions
compounded. He made his own medicines instead, and gave them free to his
patients.<br/
. . .<br/
Then they hatched a plot and before long <city>Basel</city> had lost
<qex>Paracelsus</qex>, ostensibly because of the meanness of a wealthy
citizen. <qex>Paracelsus</qex> had sued <person>Canon
Lichtenfels</person> for failure to pay him one hundred guldens promised
for a cure. The patient had offered only six guldens, and the fiery
<qex>Paracelsus</qex>, when the court deliberately handed in a verdict
against him, rebuked it in such terms that his life was in imminent
danger. In the dead of night, he was persuaded by his friends to leave
secretly the city where he had hurled defiance at the pseudo-medicos of
the world.</q> <rj><qau>Bernard Jaffe (Crucibles: The Story of
Chemistry, Revised Edition, 1948)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q> Although the theories of <qex>Paracelsus</qex> as contrasted with
the Galeno-Arabic system indicate no advance, inasmuch as they ignore
entirely the study of anatomy, still his reputation as a reformer of
therapeutics is justified in that he broke new paths in the science. He
may be taken as the founder of modern materia medica, and pioneer of
scientific chemistry, since before his time medical science received no
assistance from alchemy. To <qex>Paracelsus</qex> is due the use of
mercury for syphilis as well as a number of other metallic remedies,
probably a result of his studies in Schwaz, and partly his acquaintance
with the quicksilver works in Idria.</q> <rj><qau>Catholic Encyclopedia,
1911</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracentesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*cen*te"sis</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<ecr/n*t<emac/"s<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>parake`nthsis</grk>, fr. <grk>parakentei^n</grk>
to pierce at the side, to tap.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
perforation of a cavity of the body with a trocar, aspirator, or other
suitable instrument, for the evacuation of effused fluid, pus, or gas;
tapping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paracentrical</ent><br/
<ent>paracentric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>par`a*cen"tric</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<ecr/n"tr<icr/k)</pr>, <hw>par`a*cen"tric*al</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<ecr/n"tr<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> + <ets>centric</ets>, <ets>-ical</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>paracentrique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Deviating from circularity;
changing the distance from a center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paracentric curve</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>a
curve having the property that, when its plane is placed vertically, a
body descending along it, by the force of gravity, will approach to, or
recede from, a fixed point or center, by equal distances in equal times;
-- called also a <altname>paracentric</altname>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Paracentric motion</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Paracentric velocity</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the motion or
velocity of a revolving body, such as a planet, by which it approaches
to, or recedes from, the center, without reference to its motion in
space, or to its motion as reckoned in any other
direction.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parachordal</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*chor"dal</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*k<ocir/r"d<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> + <ets>chordal</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated on either side of the notochord; --
applied especially to the cartilaginous rudiments of the skull on each
side of the anterior part of the notochord.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A parachordal cartilage.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parachronism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rach"ro*nism</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<acr/k"r<osl/*n<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> + Gr. <?/ time: cf. F.
<ets>parachronisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>An error in chronology, by which
the date of an event is set later than the time of its occurrence.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parachrose</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*chrose</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*kr<omac/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>para`chrwsis</grk> false coloring; <grk>para`</grk>
beside, beyond + <grk>chrw`s</grk> color.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Changing color by exposure</def> <rj><au>Mohs.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parachute</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*chute</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*sh<oomac/t <it>or</it>
p<acr/r"<adot/*sh<udd/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>parer</ets> to ward off, guard + <ets>chute</ets> a fall. See
<er>Parry</er>, and <er>Chute</er>, <er>Chance</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A device made of a piece of cloth, usually silk,
attached to multiple chords fastened to a harness; when attached to a
person or object falling through the air, it opens from a folded
configuration into an umbrella-shaped form, thus slowing the rate of
descent so that a safe descent and landing may be made through the air
from an airplane, balloon, or other high point. It is commonly used for
descending to the ground from a flying airplane, as for military
operations (as of airborne troops) or in an emergency, or for sport. In
the case of use as a sport, the descent from an airplane by parachute is
called <er>sky diving</er>. Some older versions of parachute were more
rigid, and were shaped somewhat in the form of an umbrella.</def></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A web or fold of skin which
extends between the legs of certain mammals, as the flying squirrels,
colugo, and phalangister.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parachute</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*chute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>TO descend to
th ground from an airplane or other high place using a parachute;
<as>as, when the plane stalled, he parachuted safely to the
ground.</as></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>golden parachute</b></col> <cd>a generous set of
financial benefits, including severance pay, provided by contract to a
high-level corporate employee in the event s/he is dismissed or his/her
job is lost in a corporate takeover or merger; also, the contract
providing for such benefits.</cd> -- <col><b>drogue parachute</b></col>
<cd>a small parachute that is first released and opened in order to more
reliably deploy a larger parachute. Also called
<altname>drogue</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parachutist</ent><br/
<ent>parachuter</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>par"a*chut`er</hw>, <hw>par`a*chut"ist</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who descends from a high altitude to the ground
by means of a parachute, especially one who does so for sport or in a
military operation.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parachuting</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*chut*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of descending from a high altitude to the ground by means of a
parachute.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraclete</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*clete</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paracletus</ets>, Gr. <grk>para`klhtos</grk>, from
<grk>parakalei^n</grk> to call to one, to exhort, encourage;
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>kalei^n</grk> to call.]</ety> <def>An
advocate; one called to aid or support; hence, the Consoler, Comforter,
or Intercessor; -- a term applied to the Holy Spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From which intercession especially I conceive he hath the name of
the <qex>Paraclete</qex> given him by Christ.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Pearson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraclose</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*close</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Parclose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracmastic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ac*mas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Acme</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Gradually decreasing; past the acme, or crisis, as a
distemper.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraconic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*con"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>par</ets>a- + <ets>acon</ets>itic.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an organic acid obtained as a
deliquescent white crystalline substance, and isomeric with itaconic,
citraconic, and mesaconic acids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraconine</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*co"nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>conine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
base resembling and isomeric with conine, and obtained as a colorless
liquid from butyric aldehyde and ammonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracorolla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*co*rol"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>corolla</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
secondary or inner corolla; a corona, as of the Narcissus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para cress</ent><br/
<hw>Par<aacute/ cress</hw>. <def>An annual asteraceous herb
(<spn>Spilances oleracea</spn>) grown in tropical countries as a pungent
salad, and also used medicinally.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracrostic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cros"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>acrostic</ets>.]</ety> <def>A poetical
composition, in which the first verse contains, in order, the first
letters of all the verses of the poem.</def> <rj><au>Brande &
C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracyanogen</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cy*an"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>cyanogen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
polymeric modification of cyanogen, obtained as a brown or black
amorphous residue by heating mercuric cyanide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paracymene</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*cy"mene</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> +
<ets>cymene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Cymene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradactylum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*dac"ty*lum</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Paradactyla</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Para-</er>,
and <er>Dactyl</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The side of a toe or
finger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parade</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. Sp.
<ets>parada</ets> a halt or stopping, an assembling for exercise, a
place where troops are assembled to exercise, fr. <ets>parar</ets> to
stop, to prepare. See <er>Pare</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The ground where a military display is held, or where troops are
drilled. Also called <altname>parade ground</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>An assembly and orderly
arrangement or display of troops, in full equipments, for inspection or
evolutions before some superior officer; a review of troops. Parades are
general, regimental, or private (troop, battery, or company), according
to the force assembled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any imposing procession; the
movement of any group of people marshaled in military order, especially
a festive public procession, which may include a marching band, persons
in varied costume, vehicles with elaborate displays, and other forms of
entertainment, held in commemoration or celebration of an event or in
honor of a person or persons; <as>as, a <ex>parade</ex> of firemen; a
Thanksgiving Day <ex>parade</ex>; a Memorial Day <ex>parade</ex>; a
ticker-tape <ex>parade</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>In state returned the grand <qex>parade</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A pompous show; a formal or
ostentatious display or exhibition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be rich, but of your wealth make no <qex>parade</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Posture of defense; guard.</def> <mark>[A
Gallicism.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When they are not in <qex>parade</qex>, and upon their guard.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A public walk; a promenade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Dress parade</b></col>, <col><b>Undress
parade</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Dress</er>, and
<er>Undress</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Parade rest</b></col>, <cd>a position
of rest for soldiers, in which, however, they are required to be silent
and motionless.</cd> <au>Wilhelm.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Ostentation; display; show.</syn> <usage> --
<er>Parade</er>, <er>Ostentation</er>. <xex>Parade</xex> is a pompous
exhibition of things for the purpose of display; <xex>ostentation</xex>
now generally indicates a <xex>parade</xex> of virtues or other
qualities for which one expects to be honored. <ldquo/It was not in the
mere <xex>parade</xex> of royalty that the Mexican potentates exhibited
their power.<rdquo/ <au>Robertson.</au> <ldquo/We are dazzled with the
splendor of titles, the <xex>ostentation</xex> of learning, and the
noise of victories.<rdquo/ <au>Spectator.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parade</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Paraded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Parading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parader</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To exhibit in a showy or
ostentatious manner; to show off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Parading</qex> all her sensibility.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To assemble and form; to marshal; to cause to
maneuver or march ceremoniously; <as>as, to <ex>parade</ex>
troops</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parade</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rade"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make an
exhibition or spectacle of one's self, as by walking in a public
place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To assemble in military order for evolutions and
inspection; to form or march, as in review or in a public celebratory
parade{3}.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parader</ent><br/
<hw>parader</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who walks with regular or
stately step.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> marcher.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paradiddle</ent><br/
<hw>paradiddle</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The sound of a drum (especially a
snare drum) beaten rapidly and continuously.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> roll, drum roll.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradigm</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*digm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paradigme</ets>, L. <ets>paradigma</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
show by the side of, to set up as an example; <grk>para`</grk> beside +
<?/ to show. See <er>Para-</er>, and <er>Diction</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>An example; a model; a pattern.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>paradigms</xex> and patterns of all
things.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>An example of a conjugation or
declension, showing a word in all its different forms of
inflection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>An illustration, as by a parable
or fable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Science)</fld> <def>A theory providing a unifying
explanation for a set of phenomena in some field, which serves to
suggest methods to test the theory and develop a fuller understanding of
the topic, and which is considered useful until it is be replaced by a
newer theory providing more accurate explanations or explanations for a
wider range of phenomena.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradigmatical</ent><br/
<ent>Paradigmatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*dig*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Par`a*dig*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>paradeigmatiko`s</grk>.]</ety> <def>Exemplary.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`a*dig*mat"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradigmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dig*mat"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>A
writer of memoirs of religious persons, as examples of Christian
excellence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradigmatize</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dig"ma*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Paradigmatized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Paradigmatizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paradeigmati`zein</grk>. See
<er>Paradigm</er>.]</ety> <def>To set forth as a model or example.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisaical</ent><br/
<ent>Paradisaic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*di*sa"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*di*sa"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to, or
resembling, paradise; paradisiacal; <as>as, <ex>paradisaical</ex>
innocence; an age of <ex>paradisaical</ex> happiness</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Paradisaical</xex> pleasures.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paradisiacal, paradisiac, paradisaic, paradisal,
elysian, paradisial.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Causing happinesss.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisal</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*di`sal</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<imac/`s<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Paradisiacal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradise</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*dise</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<imac/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. & F. <ets>paradis</ets>, L. <ets>paradisus</ets>, fr. Gr.
<grk>para`deisos</grk> park, paradise, fr. Zend
<ets>pairida<emac/za</ets> an inclosure; <ets>pairi</ets> around (akin
to Gr. <grk>peri`</grk>) + <ets>diz</ets> to throw up, pile up; cf. Skr.
<ets>dih</ets> to smear, and E. <ets>dough</ets>. Cf.
<er>Parvis</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The garden of Eden, in which Adam and Eve were
placed after their creation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The abode of sanctified souls after death.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To-day shalt thou be with me in <qex>paradise</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke xxiii. 43.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It sounds to him like her mother's voice,<br/
Singing in <qex>Paradise</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place of bliss; a region of supreme felicity or
delight; hence, a state of happiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The earth<br/
Shall be all <qex>paradise</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wrapt in the very <qex>paradise</qex> of some creative vision.</q>
<rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An open space within a monastery
or adjoining a church, as the space within a cloister, the open court
before a basilica, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A churchyard or cemetery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Oxf. Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fool's paradise</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Fool</er>,
and <er>Limbo</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Grains of paradise</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Melequeta pepper</cref>, under
<er>Pepper</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Paradise bird</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <er>Bird of paradise</er>. Among the most
beautiful species are the superb (<spn>Lophorina superba</spn>); the
magnificent (<spn>Diphyllodes magnifica</spn>); and the six-shafted
paradise bird (<spn>Parotia sefilata</spn>). The long-billed paradise
birds (<fam>Epimachin<ae/</fam>) also include some highly ornamental
species, as the twelve-wired paradise bird (<spn>Seleucides alba</spn>),
which is black, yellow, and white, with six long breast feathers on each
side, ending in long, slender filaments. See <er>Bird of paradise</er>
in the Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Paradise fish</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a beautiful fresh-water Asiatic fish
(<spn>Macropodus viridiauratus</spn>) having very large fins. It is
often kept alive as an ornamental fish.</cd> -- <col><b>Paradise
flycatcher</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any flycatcher of the genus
<gen>Terpsiphone</gen>, having the middle tail feathers extremely
elongated. The adult male of <spn>Terpsiphone paradisi</spn> is white,
with the head glossy dark green, and crested.</cd> -- <col><b>Paradise
grackle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a very beautiful bird of New
Guinea, of the genus <gen>Astrapia</gen>, having dark velvety plumage
with brilliant metallic tints.</cd> -- <col><b>Paradise nut</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the sapucaia nut. See <er>Sapucaia nut</er>.</cd>
<mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Paradise whidah bird</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Whidah</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradise</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*dise</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<imac/s)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To affect or exalt with visions of felicity; to entrance; to
bewitch.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisean</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dis"e*an</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*d<icr/s"<esl/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Paradisiacal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradised</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*dised</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<imac/st)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Placed in paradise; enjoying delights as of paradise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisiacal</ent><br/
<ent>Paradisiac</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*dis"i*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*di*si"a*cal</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paradisiacus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to paradise;
suitable to, or like, paradise; paradisaic.</def> <au>C. Kingsley.</au>
<au>T. Burnet.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>paradisiacal</xex> scene.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The valley . . . is of quite <qex>paradisiac</qex> beauty.</q>
<rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisian</ent><br/
<ent>Paradisial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*dis"i*al</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*d<imac/s"<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*dis"i*an</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*d<imac/s"<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Paradisiacal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dis"ic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*d<imac/s"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Paradisiacal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Broome.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradisical</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dis"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Paradisiacal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parados</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*dos</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paradoses</plw>
<pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/s*<ecr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>parer</ets> to defend + <ets>dos</ets> back, L.
<ets>dorsum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>An intercepting mound,
erected in any part of a fortification to protect the defenders from a
rear or ricochet fire; a traverse.</def> <rj><au>Farrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paradox</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*dox</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>paradoxes</plw>
<pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks*<ecr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>paradoxe</ets>, L. <ets>paradoxum</ets>, fr. Gr.
<grk>para`doxon</grk>; <grk>para`</grk> beside, beyond, contrary to +
<grk>dokei^n</grk> to think, suppose, imagine. See <er>Para-</er>, and
<er>Dogma</er>.]</ety> <def>A tenet or proposition contrary to received
opinion; an assertion or sentiment seemingly contradictory, or opposed
to common sense; that which in appearance or terms is absurd, but yet
may be true in fact.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gloss there is to color that <qex>paradox</qex>, and make it
appear in show not to be altogether unreasonable.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This was sometime a <qex>paradox</qex>, but now the time gives it
proof.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Hydrostatic paradox</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Hydrostatic</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paradoxal</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*dox`al</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks`<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Paradoxical.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradoxist</ent><br/
<ent>Paradoxer</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Par"a*dox`er</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks`<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Par"a*dox`ist</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks`<icr/st)</pr></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who proposes a paradox.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradoxical</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dox"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*d<ocr/ks"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a paradox.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclined to paradoxes, or to tenets or notions
contrary to received opinions.</def> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Par`a*dox"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Par`a*dox"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradoxides</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*dox"i*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of large trilobites
characteristic of the primordial formations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paradoxism</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*dox`ism</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks`<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>paradox</ets> + <ets>-ism</ets>. <fu>ca.
1980</fu>]</ety> <def>An avant-garde movement in literature, art, and
philosophy, based on excessive used of antitheses, antinomies,
contradictions, oxymorons, and paradoxes.</def> <au>Charles Le
(https://web.archive.org/web/20091018202429/http://www.geocities.com/charlestle/paradoxism.html)</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note> Paradoxism originated in Romania in the 1980s as a way of
protesting against a closed society and a totalitarian regime. It is
based on an excessive use of antimonies, antitheses, contradictions,
oxymorons, and paradoxes. It was set up and led by the writer
<person>Florentin Smarandache</person> in the 1980's, who said:
<ldquo/The goal is the enlargement of the artistic sphere through
non-artistic elements<rdquo/.<br/
One example of such a paradox is the two-line poem:<br/
Even if he didn't<br/
He did<br/
<br/
A free eBook on "Paradoxism and Postmodernism" can be downloaded from
https://zenodo.org/record/8865#.YIWBb_kvBhE</note>.<br/
[<source>Charles Le</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradoxology</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dox*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Paradox</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The use of
paradoxes.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradoxure</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*dox"ure</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*d<ocr/ks"<usl/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>para`doxos</grk> incredible, paradoxical +
<grk>o'yra`</grk> tail. So called because its tail is unlike that of the
other animals to which it was supposed to be related.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of <gen>Paradoxurus</gen>, a genus
of Asiatic viverrine mammals allied to the civet, as the musang, and the
luwack or palm cat (<spn>Paradoxurus hermaphroditus</spn>). See
<er>Musang</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paradoxy</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*dox`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
paradoxical statement; a paradox.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being paradoxical.</def>
<rj><au>Coleridge</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraffine</ent><br/
<ent>Paraffin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"af*fin</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<acr/f*f<icr/n)</pr>,
<hw>Par"af*fine</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<acr/f*f<icr/n <it>or</it>
p<acr/r"<acr/f*f<emac/n)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paraffine</ets>, fr. L. <ets>parum</ets> too little +
<ets>affinis</ets> akin. So named in allusion to its chemical
inactivity.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white waxy substance,
resembling spermaceti, tasteless and odorless, and obtained from coal
tar, wood tar, petroleum, etc., by distillation. It is used in candles,
as a sealing agent (such as in canning of preserves), as a waterproofing
agent, as an illuminant and as a lubricant. It is very inert, not being
acted upon by most of the strong chemical reagents. It was formerly
regarded as a definite compound, but is now known to be a complex
mixture of several higher hydrocarbons of the methane or marsh-gas
series; hence, by extension, any substance, whether solid, liquid, or
gaseous, of the same chemical series; thus gasoline, coal gas and
kerosene consist largely of <xex>paraffins</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the present chemical usage this word is spelled
<xex>paraffin</xex>, but in commerce it is commonly spelled
<xex>paraffine</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Native paraffin</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Ozocerite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Paraffin series</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Methane series</cref>, under <er>Methane</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paraformaldehyde</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*form*al"de*hyde</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>formaldehyde</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A polymer of formaldehyde obtained as a white crystalline substance
by concentration of a formaldehyde solution. Formula
<chform>(CH20)n</chform>. It dissolves in hot water with the release of
formaldehyde, and is used as a disinfectant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parage</ent><br/
<hw>Par"age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>par</ets>, adj.,
equal. Cf. <er>Peerage</er>, <er>Peer</er> an equal.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Old Eng. Law)</fld> <def>Equality of condition, blood, or dignity;
also, equality in the partition of an inheritance.</def>
<rj><au>Spelman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Feudal Law)</fld> <def>Equality of condition
between persons holding unequal portions of a fee.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1040 --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Kindred; family; birth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ld. Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We claim to be of high <qex>parage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*gen"e*sis</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*j<ecr/n"<esl/*s<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> + <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The science which treats of minerals with special
reference to their origin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The formation of
minerals in contact, so as to affect one another's development.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The order in which minerals occurring together in
rocks and veins have developed.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragenic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*j<ecr/n"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> the root of <grk>ge`nos</grk>
birth.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Originating in the character of
the germ, or at the first commencement of an individual; -- said of
peculiarities of structure, character, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraglobulin</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*glob"u*lin</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*gl<ocr/b"<usl/*l<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>para-</ets> + <ets>globulin</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A protein in blood serum, belonging to
the group of globulins. See <er>Fibrinoplastin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraglossa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*glos"sa</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*gl<ocr/s"s<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paragloss<ae/</plw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*gl<ocr/s"s<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>glw^ssa</grk> tongue.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a pair of small appendages of the lingua
or labium of certain insects. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Hymenoptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragnath</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ag*nath</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<acr/g*n<acr/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Paragnathus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragnathous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rag"na*thous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having both mandibles of equal length, the tips meeting, as in
certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragnathus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rag"na*thus</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<acr/g"n<adot/*th<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paragnathi</plw>
<pr>(p<adot/*r<acr/g"n<adot/*th<imac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Gnathic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the two lobes which form the lower lip, or
metastome, of Crustacea.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the small,
horny, toothlike jaws of certain annelids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragoge</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*go"ge</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*g<omac/"j<esl/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>paragwgh`</grk>, from
<grk>para`gein</grk> to lead beside, protract; <grk>para`</grk> beside +
<grk>'a`gein</grk> to lead.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>The addition of a letter or syllable to the end of a word, as
<xex>withouten</xex> for <xex>without</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Coaptation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragogical</ent><br/
<ent>Paragogic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*gog"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*gog"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>paragogique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to, or constituting,
a paragoge; added to the end of, or serving to lengthen, a
word.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paragogic letters</b></col>, <cd>in the Semitic
languages, letters which are added to the ordinary forms of words, to
express additional emphasis, or some change in the sense.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragon</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*gon</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*g<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OF. <ets>paragon</ets>, F. <ets>parangon</ets>; cf. It.
<ets>paragone</ets>, Sp. <ets>paragon</ets>, <ets>parangon</ets>; prob.
fr. Gr. <?/ to rub against; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ whetstone; cf.
LGr. <?/ a polishing stone.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A companion; a
match; an equal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Philoclea, who indeed had no <qex>paragon</qex> but her
sister.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Emulation; rivalry; competition.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full many feats adventurous<br/
Performed, in <qex>paragon</qex> of proudest men.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A model or pattern;</def>
<specif>especially</specif> <def>a pattern of excellence or perfection;
<as>as, a <ex>paragon</ex> of beauty or eloquence</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Udall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man, . . . the <qex>paragon</qex> of animals !</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The riches of sweet Mary's son,<br/
Boy-rabbi, Israel's <qex>paragon</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A size of type between great
primer and double pica. See the Note under <er>Type</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragon</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*gon</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>paragonner</ets>, F. <ets>parangonner</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To compare; to parallel; to put in rivalry or
emulation with.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To compare with; to equal; to rival.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In arms anon to <qex>paragon</qex> the morn,<br/
The morn new rising.</q> <rj><qau>Glover.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To serve as a model for; to surpass.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hath achieved a maid<br/
That <qex>paragons</qex> description and wild fame.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragon</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*gon</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be equal; to hold
comparison.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Few or none could . . . <qex>paragon</qex> with her.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragonite</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rag"o*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/,
p. pr. of <?/ to mislead.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of mica
related to muscovite, but containing soda instead of potash. It is
characteristic of the <partof>paragonite schist</partof> of the
Alps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragram</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ that which
one writes beside. See <er>Paragraph</er>.]</ety> <def>A pun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Puns, which he calls <qex>paragrams</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragrammatist</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*gram"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
punster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragrandine</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`ra*gran"di*ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.,
from <ets>parare</ets> to parry + <ets>grandine</ets> hail.]</ety>
<def>An instrument to avert the occurrence of hailstorms. See
<er>Paragr<ecir/le</er>.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragraph</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paragraphe</ets>, LL. <ets>paragraphus</ets>, fr. Gr.
<grk>para`grafos</grk> (sc. <grk>grammh`</grk>) a line or stroke drawn
in the margin, fr. <grk>paragra`fein</grk> to write beside;
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>gra`fein</grk> to write. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Graphic</er>, and cf. <er>Paraph</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Originally, a marginal mark or note, set in the margin
to call attention to something in the text, <it>e. g.</it>, a change of
subject; now, the character <para/, commonly used in the text as a
reference mark to a footnote, or to indicate the place of a division
into sections.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This character is merely a modification of a capital P
(the initial of the word <xex>paragraph</xex>), the letter being
reversed, and the black part made white and the white part black for the
sake of distinctiveness.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A distinct part of a discourse or writing; any
section or subdivision of a writing or chapter which relates to a
particular point, whether consisting of one or many sentences. The
division is sometimes noted by the mark <para/, but usually, by
beginning the first sentence of the paragraph on a new line and at more
than the usual distance from the margin, also called indenting the line.
See <er>indentation</er>{4}.</def></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A brief composition complete in one typographical
section or paragraph; an item, remark, or quotation comprised in a few
lines forming one paragraph; <as>as, a column of news
<ex>paragraphs</ex>; an editorial <ex>paragraph</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragraph</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*graph</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paragraphed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paragraphing</conjf>.]</vmorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To divide into paragraphs; to mark with the
character <para/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To express in the compass of a paragraph; <as>as, to
<ex>paragraph</ex> an article</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To mention in a paragraph or paragraphs</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragrapher</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*graph`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A writer of
paragraphs; a paragraphist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragraphical</ent><br/
<ent>Paragraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*graph"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or consisting
of, a paragraph or paragraphs.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`a*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragraphist</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*graph`ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
paragrapher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragraphistical</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*gra*phis"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
relating to a paragraphist.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para grass</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pa*ra" grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par<aacute/
grass</hw></mhw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tall rather
coarse grass (<spn>Panicum molle</spn> formerly <spn>Panicum
barbinode</spn>) grown in the tropics for pasturage, and introduced into
the southern United States from Brazilas a valuable pasture grass.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Piassaba fiber.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paragrele</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`ra`gr<ecir/le"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>parer</ets> to guard + <ets>gr<ecir/le</ets> hail.]</ety> <def>A
lightning conductor erected, as in a vineyard, for drawing off the
electricity in the atmosphere in order to prevent hailstorms.</def>
<mark>[France]</mark> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraguayan</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*guay"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Paraguay.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Paraguay.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraguay tea</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`ra*guay" tea"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Mate</er>, the leaf
of the Brazilian holly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parail</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Apparel</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/In the
<xex>parail</xex> of a pilgrim.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parakeet</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*keet`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Parrakeet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parakite</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*kite`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Para-</ets>
+ <ets>kite</ets>.]</ety> <def>A train or series of kites on one string
and flying tandem, used for attaining great heights and for sending up
instruments for meteorological observations or a man for military
reconnaissance; also, a kite of such a train.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralactic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>lactic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>
<def>Designating an acid called <xex>paralactic acid</xex>. See
<cref>Lactic acid</cref>, under <er>Lactic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralbumin</ent><br/
<hw>Par`al*bu"min</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>albumin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A proteidlike body found in the fluid from ovarian
cysts and elsewhere. It is generally associated with a substance related
to, if not identical with, glycogen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraldehyde</ent><br/
<hw>Par*al"de*hyde</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>aldehyde</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
trimer of acetaldehyde (<chform>C6H12O3</chform>), prepared by
polymerization of acetaldehyde with hydrochloric and sulfuric acids. It
has sedative and hypnotic properties.</def> <au>MI11</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraleipsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*leip"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to leave on one side, to omit; <grk>para`</grk> beside
+ <?/ to leave.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A pretended or apparent
omission; a figure by which a speaker artfully pretends to pass by what
he really mentions; as, for example, if an orator should say, <ldquo/I
do not speak of my adversary's scandalous venality and rapacity, his
brutal conduct, his treachery and malice.<rdquo/</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>paralepsis</asp>, <asp>paralepsy</asp>,
<asp>paralipsis</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralepsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*lep"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Paraleipsis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralgesia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`al*ge"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.;
<ets>para-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'a`lghsis</grk> sense of pain.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Disordered sensibility to pain, including absence
of sensibility to pain, excessive sensibility to pain, and abnormal
painful results of stimuli.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Par`al*ge"sic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ra"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pa`ralos</grk> near the sea; <grk>para`</grk> beside +
<grk>"a`ls</grk> the sea.]</ety> <def>A dweller by the sea.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralipomenon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*li*pom"e*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>paraleipome`nwn</grk> of things omitted, pass. p.
pr. (neuter genitive plural) fr. <?/ to omit.]</ety> <def>A title given
in the Douay Bible to the Books of Chronicles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the Septuagint these books are called
<grk>Paraleipome`nwn prw^ton</grk> and <grk>dey`teron</grk>, which is
understood, after Jerome's explanation, as meaning that they are
supplementary to the Books of Kings</note> <rj><au>W.
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralipsis</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*lip"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Paraleipsis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallactical</ent><br/
<ent>Parallactic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`al*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`al*lac"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parallactique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
parallax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallax</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
alternation, the mutual inclination of two lines forming an angle, fr.
<?/ to change a little, go aside, deviate; <grk>para`</grk> beside,
beyond + <?/ to change: cf. F. <ets>parallaxe</ets>. Cf.
<er>Parallel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The apparent displacement, or
difference of position, of an object, as seen from two different
stations, or points of view.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The apparent difference in
position of a body (as the sun, or a star) as seen from some point on
the earth's surface, and as seen from some other conventional point, as
the earth's center or the sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The annual parallax. See
<cref>annual parallax</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Annual parallax</b></col>, <cd>the greatest value of the
heliocentric parallax, or the greatest annual apparent change of place
of a body as seen from the earth and sun; it is equivalent to the
parallax of an astronomical object which would be observed by taking
observations of the object at two different points one astronomical unit
(the distance of the Earth from the sun) apart, if the line joining the
two observing points is perpendicular to the direction to the observed
object; <as>as, the <ex>annual parallax</ex> of a fixed star</as>. The
distance of an astronomical object from the Earth is inversely
proportional to the <ex>annual parallax</ex>. A star which has an annual
parallax of one second of an arc is considered to be one parsec (3.26
light years) distant from the earth; a star with an annual parallax of
one-hundredth second of an arc is 326 light years distant. See
<er>parsec</er> in the vocabulary, and <cref>stellar parallax</cref>,
below.</cd> -- <col><b>Binocular parallax</b></col>, <cd>the apparent
difference in position of an object as seen separately by one eye, and
then by the other, the head remaining unmoved.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Diurnal parallax</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Geocentric
parallax</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the parallax of a body with reference to
the earth's center. This is the kind of parallax that is generally
understood when the term is used without qualification.</cd> --
<col><b>Heliocentric parallax</b></col>, <cd>the parallax of a body with
reference to the sun, or the angle subtended at the body by lines drawn
from it to the earth and sun; <as>as, the <ex>heliocentric parallax</ex>
of a planet</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Horizontal parallax</b></col>, <cd>the
geocentric parallx of a heavenly body when in the horizon, or the angle
subtended at the body by the earth's radius.</cd> -- <col><b>Optical
parallax</b></col>, <cd>the apparent displacement in position undergone
by an object when viewed by either eye singly.</cd> <au>Brande & C.</au>
-- <col><b>Parallax of the cross wires</b></col><cd> (of an optical
instrument), their apparent displacement when the eye changes its
position, caused by their not being exactly in the focus of the object
glass.</cd> -- <col><b>Stellar parallax</b></col>, <cd>the annual
parallax of a fixed star.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parallax second</ent><br/
<hw>par"al*lax sec"ond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
<cref>annual parallax</cref> of one second of an arc; -- applied to
celestial objects outside the solar system. It is used to measure the
distance of an astronomical object from the Earth. A star which has an
annual parallax of one second of an arc is considered to be one parsec
(3.26 light years) distant from the earth. See <er>parsec</er> in the
vocabulary.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parall<egrave/le</ets>, L. <ets>parallelus</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/;
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ of one another, fr. <?/ other, akin to L.
<ets>alius</ets>. See <er>Alien</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Extended in the same direction, and in all parts
equally distant; <as>as, <ex>parallel</ex> lines; <ex>parallel</ex>
planes.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Revolutions . . . <qex>parallel</qex> to the equinoctial.</q>
<rj><qau>Hakluyt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Curved lines or curved planes are said to be parallel
when they are in all parts equally distant.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the same direction or tendency; running side
by side; being in accordance (with); tending to the same result; -- used
with <xex>to</xex> and <xex>with</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When honor runs <qex>parallel</qex> with the laws of God and our
country, it can not be too much cherished.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Continuing a resemblance through many particulars;
applicable in all essential parts; like; similar; <as>as, a
<ex>parallel</ex> case; a <ex>parallel</ex> passage.</as></def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parallel bar</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Steam
Eng.)</fld> <cd>A rod in a parallel motion which is parallel with the
working beam</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>One of a pair of bars raised about
five feet above the floor or ground, and parallel to each other, -- used
for gymnastic exercises.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel circles of a
sphere</b></col>, <cd>those circles of the sphere whose planes are
parallel to each other.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Parallel
columns</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Parallels</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Printing)</fld>, <cd>two or more passages of reading matter
printed side by side, for the purpose of emphasizing the similarity or
discrepancy between them.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel forces</b></col>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>forces which act in directions parallel to each
other.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel motion</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>A jointed system of links, rods, or bars, by
which the motion of a reciprocating piece, as a piston rod, may be
guided, either approximately or exactly in a straight line</cd>.
<au>Rankine.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <cd>The ascending or
descending of two or more parts at fixed intervals, as thirds or
sixths.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Parallel rod</b></col> <fld>(Locomotive
Eng.)</fld>, <cd>a metal rod that connects the crank pins of two or more
driving wheels; -- called also <altname>couping rod</altname>, in
distinction from the <xex>connecting rod</xex>. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
of <er>Locomotive</er>, in App.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel
ruler</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an instrument for drawing parallel lines, so
constructed as to have the successive positions of the ruling edge
parallel to each other; also, one consisting of two movable parts, the
opposite edges of which are always parallel.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel
sailing</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>sailing on a parallel of
latitude.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel sphere</b></col> <fld>(Astron. &
Geog.)</fld>, <cd>that position of the sphere in which the circles of
daily motion are parallel to the horizon, as to an observer at either
pole.</cd> -- <col><b>Parallel vise</b></col>, <cd>a vise having jaws so
guided as to remain parallel in all positions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A line
which, throughout its whole extent, is equidistant from another line; a
parallel line, a parallel plane, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who made the spider <qex>parallels</qex> design,<br/
Sure as De Moivre, without rule or line ?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Direction conformable to that of another
line,</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lines that from their <qex>parallel</qex> decline.</q>
<rj><qau>Garth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Conformity continued through many particulars or in
all essential points; resemblance; similarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Twixt earthly females and the moon<br/
All <qex>parallels</qex> exactly run.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A comparison made; elaborate tracing of similarity;
<as>as, Johnson's <ex>parallel</ex> between Dryden and
Pope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Anything equal to, or resembling, another in all
essential particulars; a counterpart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>None but thyself can be thy <qex>parallel</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Geog.)</fld> <def>One of the imaginary circles on
the surface of the earth, parallel to the equator, marking the latitude;
also, the corresponding line on a globe or map; <as>as, the counry was
divided into North and South at the 38th
<ex>parallel</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>One of a series of long trenches
constructed before a besieged fortress, by the besieging force, as a
cover for troops supporting the attacking batteries. They are roughly
parallel to the line of outer defenses of the fortress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A character consisting of two
parallel vertical lines (thus, \'d8) used in the text to direct
attention to a similarly marked note in the margin or at the foot of a
page.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>That arrangement of an electrical
system in which all positive poles, electrodes, terminals, etc., are
joined to one conductor, and all negative poles, etc., to another
conductor; -- called also <altname>multiple</altname>. Opposed to
<contr>series</contr>.</def></p>

<p><note><hand/ Parts of a system so arranged are said to be <col><b>in
parallel</b></col> or <col><b>in multiple</b></col>.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Limiting parallels</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Limit</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Parallel of
altitude</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>one of the small circles of
the sphere, parallel to the horizon; an almucantar.</cd> --
<col><b>Parallel of declination</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>one
of the small circles of the sphere, parallel to the equator.</cd> --
<col><b>Parallel of latitude</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Geog.)</fld>
<cd>See def. 6. above.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>One of
the small circles of the sphere, parallel to the ecliptic.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paralleled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paralleling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
place or set so as to be parallel; to place so as to be parallel to, or
to conform in direction with, something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The needle . . . doth <qex>parallel</qex> and place itself upon
the true meridian.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To make to conform to something else in
character, motive, aim, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His life is <qex>paralleled</qex><br/
Even with the stroke and line of his great justice.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To equal; to match; to correspond to.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To produce or adduce as a parallel.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My young remembrance can not <qex>parallel</qex><br/
A fellow to it.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be parallel; to
correspond; to be like.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelable</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel`a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being paralleled, or equaled.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parallelepiped</ent><br/
<hw>parallelepiped</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geometry)</fld> <def>A prism
whose bases are parallelograms.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> parallelopiped, parallelepipedon,
parallelopipedon.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parallelepipedon</ent><br/
<hw>parallelepipedon</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geometry)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>parallelepiped</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> parallelepiped, parallelopiped,
parallelopipedon.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelism</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to place side by side, or parallel: cf. F.
<ets>parall<eacute/lisme</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being parallel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resemblance; correspondence; similarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A close <qex>parallelism</qex> of thought and incident.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Warton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Similarity of construction or meaning of clauses
placed side by side, especially clauses expressing the same sentiment
with slight modifications, as is common in Hebrew poetry; <it>e.
g.</it>: --<br/
<br/
<q>At her feet he bowed, he fell:<br/
Where he bowed, there he fell down dead.</q> <rj><qau>Judg. v.
27.</qau></rj></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelistic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`al*lel*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the
nature of a parallelism; involving parallelism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The antithetic or <qex>parallelistic</qex> form of Hebrew poetry
is entirely lost.</q> <rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelize</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render
parallel.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelless</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Matchless.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelly</ent><br/
<hw>Par"al*lel*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a parallel manner; with
parallelism.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelogram</ent><br/
<hw>Par`al*lel"o*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<?/ parallel + <?/ to write: cf. F. <ets>parall<eacute/logramme</ets>.
See <er>Parallel</er>, and <er>-gram</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>A right-lined quadrilateral figure, whose opposite sides are
parallel, and consequently equal; -- sometimes restricted in popular
usage to a rectangle, or quadrilateral figure which is longer than it is
broad, and with right angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Parallelogram of velocities</b></col>,
<col><b>parallelogram of forces</b></col>, <col><b>parallelogram of
accelerations</b></col>, <col><b>parallelogram of momenta</b></col>,
etc.</mcol> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a parallelogram the diagonal of
which represents the resultant of two velocities, forces, accelerations,
momenta, etc., both in quantity and direction, when the velocities,
forces, accelerations, momenta, etc., are represented in quantity and
direction by the two adjacent sides of the parallelogram.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelogrammatic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`al*lel`o*gram*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a parallelogram; parallelogrammic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1041 --></p>

<p><ent>Parallelogrammical</ent><br/
<ent>Parallelogrammic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`al*lel`o*gram"mic</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<ait/l*l<ecr/l`<osl/*gr<acr/m"m<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Par`al*lel`o*gram"mic*al</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<ait/l*l<ecr/l`<osl/*gr<acr/m"m<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the properties of a parallelogram.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelopiped</ent><br/
<hw>Par`al*lel`o*pi"ped</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<ait/l*l<ecr/l`<osl/*p<imac/"p<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>para`llhlos</grk> parallel + <grk>'epi`pedon</grk> a
plane surface, <grk>'epi`pedos</grk> on the ground, or level with it,
level, flat; <grk>'epi`</grk> on + <grk>pe`don</grk> the ground: cf. F.
<ets>parall<eacute/lopip<egrave/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>A solid, the faces of which are six parallelograms, the opposite
pairs being parallel, and equal to each other; a prism whose base is a
parallelogram.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> parallelepiped, parallelepipedon,
parallelopipedon.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallelopipedon</ent><br/
<hw>Par`al*lel`o*pip"e*don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>A parallelopiped.</def>
<rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parallel-park</ent><br/
<hw>parallel-park</hw> <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To park (a vehicle)
parallel to the curb; -- contrasted with <contr>angle-park</contr>;
<as>as, to get a driver's license, one needs to be able to
parallel-park</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parallel-parking</ent><br/
<hw>parallel-parking</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process of
parking parallel to the curb; -- contrasted with
<contr>angle-parking</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel standards</ent><br/
<hw>Parallel standards</hw>. <fld>(Numismatics)</fld> <def>Two or more
metals coined without any attempt by the government to regulate their
values.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel sulcus</ent><br/
<hw>Parallel sulcus</hw>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A sulcus parallel to,
but some distance below, the horizontal limb of the fissure of
Sylvius.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel transformer</ent><br/
<hw>Parallel transformer</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A transformer
connected <cref>in parallel</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parallel vise</ent><br/
<hw>Parallel vise</hw>. <def>A vise with jaws so guided as to remain
parallel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralogical</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing
paralogism; illogical.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Paralogical</xex> doubt.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralogism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ral"o*gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paralogismo`s</grk>, fr. <grk>paralogi`zesqai</grk> to reason
falsely; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>logi`zesqai</grk> to reason,
<grk>lo`gos</grk> discourse, reason: cf. F.
<ets>paralogisme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A reasoning which
is false in point of form, that is, which is contrary to logical rules
or formul<ae/; a formal fallacy, or pseudo-syllogism, in which the
conclusion does not follow from the premises.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralogize</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ral"o*gize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Paralogized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Paralogizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paralogi`zesqai</grk>.]</ety> <def>To reason falsely; to draw
conclusions not warranted by the premises.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralogy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ral"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paralogi`a</grk>; <grk>para`</grk> beside, beyond +
<grk>lo`gos</grk> reason.]</ety> <def>False reasoning;
paralogism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralyse</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*lyse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Paralyze</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralysis</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ral"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>para`lysis</grk>, fr. <grk>paraly`ein</grk> to loosen, dissolve, or
disable at the side; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>ly`ein</grk> to
loosen. See <er>Para-</er>, and <er>Loose</er>, and cf.
<er>Palsy</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Abolition of function,
whether complete or partial; esp., the loss of the power of voluntary
motion, with or without that of sensation, in any part of the body;
palsy. See <er>Hemiplegia</er>, and <er>Paraplegia</er>. Also used
figuratively; <as>as, <ex>paralysis</ex> of the will</as>.</def>
<ldquo/Utter <xex>paralysis</xex> of memory.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G.
Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mischievous practices arising out of the <qex>paralysis</qex> of
the powers of ownership.</q> <rj><qau>Duke of Argyll
(1887).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralytic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*lyt"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paralyticus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>paralytique</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to paralysis; resembling
paralysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Affected with paralysis, or palsy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The cold, shaking, <qex>paralytic</qex> hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Inclined or tending to paralysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paralytic secretion</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>,
<cd>the fluid, generally thin and watery, secreted from a gland after
section or paralysis of its nerves, as the paralytic
saliva.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralytic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*lyt"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person affected with
paralysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralytical</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*lyt"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Paralytic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralyzation</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*ly*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of paralyzing, or the state of being paralyzed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paralyze</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*lyze</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Paralyzed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Paralyzing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>paralyser</ets>. See <er>Paralysis</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To affect or strike with paralysis or
palsy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>To unnerve; to destroy or impair
the energy of; to render ineffective; <as>as, the occurrence
<ex>paralyzed</ex> the community; despondency <ex>paralyzed</ex> his
efforts.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Param</ent><br/
<hw>Par"am</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
white crystalline nitrogenous substance (<chform>C2H4N4</chform>); --
called also <altname>dicyandiamide</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paramagnet</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*mag"net</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A substance exhibiting
<er>paramagnetism</er>, i.e. a substance whose magnetization is
proportional to the strength of the magnetic field applied to
it.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramagnetic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mag*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>magnetic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>Having or exhibiting paramagnetism; -- opposed to
<ant>diamagnetic</ant> and contrasted with
<contr>ferromagnetic</contr>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
paramagnetic substance.</def> <au>Faraday.</au></def2> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`a*mag*net"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramagnetism</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mag"net*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A property of materials which are not
magnetized in the absence of an external magnetic field, but in which
the magnetic moments of their constituents align with and enhance an
applied magnetic field; the induced magnetic field of the substance is
in direct proportion to the strength of the applied magnetic field; --
opposed to <ant>diamagnetic</ant> and contrasted with
<contr>ferromagnetic</contr>.</def> <rj><au>Faraday.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramaleic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*ma*le"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>maleic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid obtained from malic acid,
and now called <altname>fumaric</altname> <xex>acid</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramalic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*ma"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>malic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an organic acid metameric with malic
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramastoid</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mas"toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>mastoid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated beside, or near, the mastoid portion of the temporal bone;
paroccipital; -- applied especially to a process of the skull in some
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramatta</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mat"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from
<ets>Paramatta</ets>, in Australia.]</ety> <def>A light fabric of cotton
and worsted, resembling bombazine or merino.</def> <rj><au>Beck
(Draper's Dict.)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parament</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>paramento</ets>, from <ets>parar</ets> to prepare, L.
<ets>parare</ets>.]</ety> <def>Ornamental hangings, furniture, etc., as
of a state apartment; rich and elegant robes worn by men of rank; --
chiefly in the plural.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lords in <qex>paraments</qex> on their coursers.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Chamber of paraments</b></col>, <cd>presence chamber of a
monarch.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramento</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`ra*men"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>Ornament; decoration.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramere</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*mere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>-mere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One
of the symmetrical halves of any one of the radii, or spheromeres, of a
radiate animal, as a starfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parameter</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ram"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>param<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A constant number
which is part of a theory, function, or calculation, whose value is not
determined by the form of the theory or equation itself, and may in some
cases be arbitrary assigned.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A term applied to some characteristic magnitude
whose value, invariable as long as one and the same function, curve,
surface, etc., is considered, serves to distinguish that function,
curve, surface, etc., from others of the same kind or family.</def>
<au>Brande & C.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Conic Sections)</fld> <def>(in
the ellipse and hyperbola), a third proportional to any diameter and its
conjugate, or in the parabola, to any abscissa and the corresponding
ordinate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>parameter</xex> of the principal axis of a
conic section is called the <xex>latus rectum</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Science)</fld> <def>Any constant number which is
required to calculate values of observed phenomena according to a
theory, but the value of which must be determined by experiment, and
cannot be calculated from the fundamental assumptions of the theory. In
general, a theory which has a large number of <ex>parameters</ex>,
though it may accurately predict experimental results, is considered as
having less explanatory power and as being less esthetically pleasing
than a theory with fewer <ex>parameters</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>The ratio of the three
crystallographic axes which determines the position of any plane; also,
the fundamental axial ratio for a given species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The limits, guidelines, or assumptions from within
which an activity is carried out; <as>as, new arrivals need to learn the
<ex>parameters</ex> of the research in our department</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A variable used in a
calculation within a computer program which must be assigned a value
before the calculation can be performed; <as>as, let's plug in the
<ex>parameters</ex> and see what the result is</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A characteristic or element, especially one used as
a criterion for evaluation or judgment; <as>as, a useful
<ex>parameter</ex> for determining efficiency</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paramedic</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>par`a*med"ic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*m<ecr/d"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A person who is trained to give
first aid and other emergency medical assistance in the absence of a
physician, and to assist a physician in the administration of health
care. Paramedics are often associated with police, firefighting units,
or rescue squads.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paramedical</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>par`a*med"i*cal</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*m<ecr/d"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to those providing
medical care who are not physicians but serve in the capacity of
assisting physicians; nurses, physiotherapists, dieticians, Physical
Therapy instructors, and respiratory therapy technicians are examples of
<ex>paramedical</ex> personnel.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the type of medical care
provided by paramedics or other <ex>paramedical</ex> personnel; <as>as,
<ex>paramedical</ex> treatment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parametritis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*me*tri"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Metritis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the cellular tissue in the vicinity of the
uterus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramiographer</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mi*og"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
proverb + <ets>-graph</ets> + <ets>-er</ets>.]</ety> <def>A collector or
writer of proverbs.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramitome</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mi"tome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>mitome</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The
fluid portion of the protoplasm of a cell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"ra*mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Paramos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>p<ae/ramo</ets>.]</ety> <def>A high, bleak plateau or district,
with stunted trees, and cold, damp atmosphere, as in the Andes, in South
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramorph</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*morph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + Gr. <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of pseudomorph, in which there has been a
change of physical characters, by a change in crystal structure without
alteration of chemical composition, as the change of aragonite to
calcite; called also <altname>allomorph</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A taxonomic variant observed
within a species, for which a more specific term has not been
assigned.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>par`a*mor"phic</wf>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>par`a*mor"phous</wf>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramorphism</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mor"phism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>The change of one mineral species to another, so as to involve a
change in physical characters without alteration of chemical
composition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Relating to paramorphism; exhibiting paramorphism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramount</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*mount</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>par
amont</ets> above; <ets>par</ets> through, by (L. <ets>per</ets>) +
<ets>amont</ets> above. See <er>Amount</er>.]</ety> <def>Having the
highest rank or jurisdiction; superior to all others; chief; supreme;
pre<eum/minent; <as>as, a <ex>paramount</ex> duty</as>.</def> <ldquo/A
traitor <xex>paramount</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Lady paramount</b></col> <fld>(Archery)</fld>, <cd>the
lady making the best score.</cd> -- <col><b>Lord paramount</b></col>,
<cd>the king.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> Superior; principal; preeminent; chief.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramount</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*mount</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The highest or chief.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramountly</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*mount`ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a paramount
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramour</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*mour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>par
amour</ets>, lit., by or with love. See 2d <er>Par</er>, and
<er>Amour</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A lover, of either sex; a wooer
or a mistress (formerly in a good sense, now only in a bad one); one who
takes the place, without possessing the rights, of a husband or wife; --
used of a man or a woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The seducer appeared with dauntless front, accompanied by his
<qex>paramour</qex></q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Love; gallantry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/For <xex>paramour</xex> and jollity.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramours</ent><br/
<ent>Paramour</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"a*mour`</hw>, <hw>Par"a*mours`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>By or with love, esp. the love of the sexes; --
sometimes written as two words.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For <qex>par amour</qex>, I loved her first ere thou.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paramylum</ent><br/
<hw>Par*am"y*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ starch.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
substance resembling starch, found in the green frothy scum formed on
the surface of stagnant water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranaphthalene</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*naph"tha*lene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>naphthalene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Anthracene; -- called also
<altname>paranaphthaline</altname>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parang</ent><br/
<hw>parang</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stout strait-edged knife used in
<country>Malayasia</country> and <country>Indonesia</country>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paranoia</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*noi"a</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*noi"<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>para`noia</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A chronic form of insanity characterized by very
gradual impairment of the intellect, systematized delusion, and usually
by delusions of persecution or mandatory delusions producing homicidal
tendency. In its mild form paranoia may consist in the well-marked
crotchetiness exhibited in persons commonly called <ldquo/cranks.<rdquo/
Paranoiacs usually show evidences of bodily and nervous degeneration,
and many have hallucinations, esp. of sight and hearing.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unwarranted suspicion of the motives of others; --
used colloquially in a non-technical sense.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paranoid</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*noid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Affected with paranoia; <as>as, a
<ex>paranoid</ex> schizophrenic</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paranoiac.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suffering from delusions of being persecuted by the
conspiratorial actions of others; having an unwarranted suspicion of the
motives of others; -- used loosely in a negative sense, not as a medical
term; <as>as, just because you're <ex>paranoid</ex> doesn't mean they
are not out to get you</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranoiac</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*noi"ac</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*noi"<acr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to paranoia;
affected with, or characteristic of, paranoia; paranoid.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranoiac</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*noi"ac</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person affected with
paranoia.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paranormal</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*nor"mal</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*n<ocir/r"m<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>adj.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to parapsychology; pertaining to
forces or mental processes, such as extrasensory perception or
psychokinesis, outside the possibilities defined by natural or
scientific laws; <as>as, <ex>paranormal</ex> phenomena</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> psychic, psychical, parapsycholological.</syn>
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranthracene</ent><br/
<hw>Par*an"thra*cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>par</ets>a- + <ets>anthracene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An inert isomeric modification of anthracene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranucleus</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*nu"cle*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>nucleus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Some as <er>Nucleolus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para nut</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pa*ra" nut`</hw>, <hw>Par<aacute/ nut</hw></mhw>
<pr>(p<adot/*r<aum/" n<ucr/t`)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The Brazil
nut.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranymph</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*nymph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paranymphus</ets>, Gr. <?/; <grk>para`</grk> beside, near + <?/ a
bride: cf. F. <ets>paranymphe</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A friend of the bridegroom who went with
him in his chariot to fetch home the bride.</def> <au>Milton.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The bridesmaid who conducted the bride to the
bridegroom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>An ally; a supporter or
abettor.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paranymphal</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*nym"phal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bridal;
nuptial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At some <qex>paranymphal</qex> feast.</q>
<rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapectin</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*pec"tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>pectin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
gelatinous modification of pectin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapegm</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*pegm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parapegma</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to fix beside; <grk>para`</grk>
beside + <?/ to fix: cf. F. <ets>parapegme</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
engraved tablet, usually of brass, set up in a public place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Parapegms</xex> were used for the publication of
laws, proclamations, etc., and the recording of astronomical phenomena
or calendar events.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapeptone</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*pep"tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>peptone</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Phisiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A proteinaceous body formed in small quantity by the
peptic digestion of proteids. It can be converted into peptone by
pancreatic juice, but not by gastric juice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapet</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. It.
<ets>parapetto</ets>, fr. <ets>parare</ets> to ward off, guard (L.
<ets>parare</ets> to prepare, provide) + <ets>petto</ets> the breast, L.
<ets>pectus</ets>. See <er>Parry</er>, and <er>Pectoral</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A low wall, especially one
serving to protect the edge of a platform, roof, bridge, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A wall, rampart, or elevation of
earth, for covering soldiers from an enemy's fire; a breastwork. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Casemate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapetalous</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>petal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Growing by the side of a petal, as a stamen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapeted</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*pet`ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a parapet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraph</ent><br/
<hw>Par"aph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paraphe</ets>, <ets>parafe</ets>, contr. fr.
<ets>paragraphe</ets>.]</ety> <def>A flourish made with the pen at the
end of a signature. In the Middle Ages, this formed a sort of rude
safeguard against forgery.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraph</ent><br/
<hw>Par"aph</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paraphed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paraphing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>parapher</ets>,
<ets>parafer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To add a paraph to; to sign, esp. with
the initials.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapherna</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*pher"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Law)</fld> <def>The property of a woman
which, on her marriage, was not made a part of her dower, but remained
her own.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphernal</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*pher"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>paraphernal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to paraphernalia;
<as>as, <ex>paraphernal</ex> property</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphernalia</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*pher*na"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>paraphernalia bona</ets>, fr. L. <ets>parapherna</ets>, pl.,
parapherna, Gr. <?/; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ a bride's dowry, fr.
<grk>fe`rein</grk> to bring. See 1st <er>Bear</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Something reserved to a wife, over
and above her dower, being chiefly apparel and ornaments suited to her
degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Personal belongings; ornaments; finery; sundry
objects carried about for personal convenience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The appendages, apparatus, or equipment used in a
particular activity; <as>as, surveyors unloading their
<ex>paraphernalia</ex> from a van</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphimosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*phi*mo"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/; <?/ beyond + <?/ to muzzle.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
condition in which the prepuce, after being retracted behind the glans
penis, is constricted there, and can not be brought forward into place
again.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphosphoric</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*phos*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>phosphoric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pyrophosphoric.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphragma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*phrag"ma</hw> <pr>(-fr<acr/g"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paraphragmata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/, <?/, an
inclosure.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the outer divisions of
an endosternite of Crustacea.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`a*phrag"mal</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphrase</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*phrase</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*fr<amac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>paraphrasis</ets>, Gr. <grk>para`frasis</grk>, from
<grk>parafra`zein</grk> to say the same thing in other words;
<grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>fra`zein</grk> to speak: cf. F.
<ets>paraphrase</ets>. See <er>Para-</er>, and <er>Phrase</er>.]</ety>
<def>A restatement of a text, passage, or work, expressing the meaning
of the original in another form, generally for the sake of its clearer
and fuller exposition; a setting forth the signification of a text in
other and ampler terms; a free translation or rendering; -- opposed to
<contr>metaphrase</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>paraphrase</qex>, or translation with latitude, the
author's words are not so strictly followed as his sense.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Excellent <qex>paraphrases</qex> of the Psalms of David.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Disraeli.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His sermons a living <qex>paraphrase</qex> upon his practice.</q>
<rj><qau>Sowth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Targums are also called the Chaldaic or Aramaic
<qex>Paraphrases</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shipley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphrase</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*phrase</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paraphrased</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paraphrasing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To express,
interpret, or translate with latitude; to give the meaning of a passage
in other language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are put to construe and <qex>paraphrase</qex> our own
words.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Stillingfleet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphrase</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*phrase</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a
paraphrase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphraser</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*phra`ser</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
paraphrases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphrasian</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*phra"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
paraphraser.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphrast</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*phrast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paraphrastes</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>paraphraste</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A paraphraser.</def> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphrastic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*phras"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Par`a*phras"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>paraphrastique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Paraphrasing; of the nature of paraphrase; explaining, or
translating in words more clear and ample than those of the author; not
literal; free.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Par`a*phras"tic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraphysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*raph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paraphyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ growth.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A minute jointed filament growing among the archegonia and
antheridia of mosses, or with the spore cases, etc., of other flowerless
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraplegy</ent><br/
<ent>Paraplegia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Par`a*ple"gi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par"a*ple`gy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>paraplegia</ets>, fr.
Gr. <?/ hemiplegia, fr. <?/ to strike at the side; <grk>para`</grk>
beside + <?/ to strike: cf. F. <ets>parapl<eacute/gie</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Palsy of the lower half of the body on both
sides, caused usually by disease of the spinal cord.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`a*pleg"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapleura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*pleu"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parapleur<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Para-</er>, and 2d <er>Pleura</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A chitinous piece between the metasternum and the pleuron of
certain insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parapodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/, dim. of <?/ foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the lateral appendages of an annelid; --
called also <altname>foot tubercle</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They may serve for locomotion, respiration, and
sensation, and often contain spines or set<ae/. When well developed, a
dorsal part, or <part>notopodium</part>, and a ventral part, or
<part>neuropodium</part>, are distinguished.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapophysis</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*poph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Parapophyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Apophysis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The ventral transverse, or capitular, process of a vertebra. See
<er>Vertebra</er>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Par*ap`o*phys"ic*al</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parapsychologist</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*psy*chol"o*gist</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who studies or is
versed in parapsychology.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parapsychology</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*psy*chol"o*gy</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The field of study
concerned with psychological phenomena not explainable by the laws of
physics, especially extrasensory perception, telepathy, psychokinesis,
precognition, and clairvoyance.</def> <note>The field is controversial,
most scientists holding the opinion that the supposed phenomena observed
result from poor experimental design, or are mere statistical artifacts
or in some cases deliberate fraud.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parapsychological</ent><br/
<hw>parapsychological</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
parapsychology; pertaining to forces or mental processes outside the
possibilities defined by natural or scientific laws; <as>as,
<ex>parapsychological</ex> research</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> psychic, psychical, parapsycholological,
paranormal.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parapterum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rap"te*rum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paraptera</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Pteron</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
special plate situated on the sides of the mesothorax and metathorax of
certain insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraquito</ent><br/
<ent>Paraquet</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*quet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`a*qui"to</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Paroquet</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Parrakeet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Para rubber</ent><br/
<hw>Par<aacute/ rubber</hw>. <def>The caoutchouc obtained from the South
American euphorbiaceous tree <spn>Hevea brasiliensis</spn>, hence called
the Par<aacute/ rubber tree, from the Brazilian river and seaport named
Par<aacute/; also, the similar product of other species of
<gen>Hevea</gen>. It is usually exported in flat round cakes, and is a
chief variety of commercial India rubber.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasang</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*sang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parasanga</ets>, Gr. <?/, from Old Persian; cf. Per.
<ets>farsang</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Persian measure of length, which,
according to <persfn>Herodotus</persfn> and <persfn>Xenophon</persfn>,
was thirty stadia, or somewhat more than three and a half miles. The
measure varied in different times and places, and, as now used, is
estimated at from three and a half to four English miles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parascenium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*sce"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parascenia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ stage.]</ety> <fld>(Greek &
Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>One of two apartments adjoining the stage,
probably used as robing rooms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasceve</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*sce"ve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from Gr.
<?/, lit., preparation.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Among the Jews, the
evening before the Sabbath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mark xv.
42 (Douay ver.)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A preparation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraschematic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*sche*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
change from the true form.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a change from
the right form, as in the formation of a word from another by a change
of termination, gender, etc.</def> <rj><au>Max M<uum/ller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraselene</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*se*le"ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paraselen<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>selh`nh</grk> the
moon: cf. F. <ets>paras<eacute/l<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <def>A mock moon; an image of the moon which
sometimes appears at the point of intersection of two lunar halos. Cf.
<er>Parhelion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parashah</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par"a*shah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl.
<plw>-shoth</plw> <pr>(#)</pr> or <plw>-shioth</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>.
<ety>[Heb. <ets>p<amac/r<amac/sh<amac/h</ets>.]</ety> <def>A lesson from
the Torah, or Law, from which at least one section is read in the Jewish
synagogue on every Sabbath and festival.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parashoth</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par"a*shoth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Parashah</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*si"ta</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<imac/"t<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>An artificial group formerly made for parasitic insects, as lice,
ticks, mites, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A division of copepod
Crustacea, having a sucking mouth, as the lerneans. They are mostly
parasites on fishes. Called also
<altname><ord>Siphonostomata</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1042 --></p>

<p><ent>Parasital</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*si`tal</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*s<imac/`t<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
parasites; parasitic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasite</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*site</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*s<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>parasitus</ets>, Gr. <grk>para`sitos</grk>, lit.,
eating beside, or at the table of, another; <grk>para`</grk> beside +
<grk>sitei^n</grk> to feed, from <grk>sitos</grk> wheat, grain,
food.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who frequents the tables of the rich, or who
lives at another's expense, and earns his welcome by flattery; a
hanger-on; a toady; a sycophant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou, with trembling fear,<br/
Or like a fawning <qex>parasite</qex>, obey'st.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Parasites</qex> were called such smell-feasts as would seek
to be free guests at rich men's tables.</q>
<rj><qau>Udall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A plant obtaining
nourishment immediately from other plants to which it attaches itself,
and whose juices it absorbs; -- sometimes, but erroneously, called
<xex>epiphyte</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A plant living on or within
an animal, and supported at its expense, as many species of fungi of the
genus <gen>Torrubia</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An animal which
lives during the whole or part of its existence on or in the body of
some other animal, feeding upon its food, blood, or tissues, as lice,
tapeworms, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An animal which steals the food
of another, as the parasitic jager.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An animal
which habitually uses the nest of another, as the cowbird and the
European cuckoo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasitical</ent><br/
<ent>Parasitic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`a*sit"ic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<icr/t"<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Par`a*sit"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<icr/t"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>parasiticus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>parasitique</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a parasite; having the habits of a
parasite; fawning for food or favors; sycophantic.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Parasitic</xex> preachers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> leechlike, bloodsucking.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
parasites; living on, or deriving nourishment from, some other living
animal or plant. See <er>Parasite</er>, 2 & 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Parasitic gull</b></col>, <col><b>Parasitic
jager</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Jager</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Par`a*sit"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Par`a*sit"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parasiticidal</ent><br/
<hw>par`a*sit`i*cid"al</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Capable of destroying
parasites, especially capable of expelling or destroying parasitic
worms.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> anthelmintic, anthelminthic, helminthic.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasiticide</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*sit"i*cide</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<adot/*s<icr/t"<icr/*s<imac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Parasite</ets> + L. <ets>caedere</ets> to kill.]</ety>
<def>Anything used to destroy parasites.</def>
<rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasitism</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*si`tism</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*s<imac/`t<icr/z'm; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>parasitisme</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or behavior of a parasite; the act of a
parasite.</def> <ldquo/Court <xex>parasitism</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>The state of being
parasitic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parasol</ent><br/
<hw>par"a*sol`</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<adot/*s<ocr/l`; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. Sp. or Pg. <ets>parasol</ets>, or It.
<ets>parasole</ets>; It. <ets>parare</ets> to ward off, Sp. & Pg.
<ets>parar</ets> (L. <ets>parare</ets> to prepare) + It. <ets>sole</ets>
sun, Sp. & Pg. <ets>sol</ets> (L. <ets>sol</ets>). See <er>Parry</er>,
<er>Solar</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of small umbrella used by women as a
protection from the sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasol</ent><br/
<hw>Par"a*sol`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shade as with a
parasol.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasolette</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*sol*ette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small
parasol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasphenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*sphe"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>sphenoid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Near the sphenoid bone; -- applied especially to a bone situated
immediately beneath the sphenoid in the base of the skull in many
animals.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The parasphenoid
bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parastichy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ras"ti*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + Gr. <grk>sti`chos</grk> a row.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A secondary spiral in phyllotaxy, as one of the
evident spirals in a pine cone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasynaxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*syn*ax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <?/, from <?/ to assemble illegally or secretly.]</ety> <fld>(Civil
Law)</fld> <def>An unlawful meeting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parasynthetic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*syn*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.
See <er>Para-</er>, and <er>Synthetic</er>.]</ety> <def>Formed from a
compound word.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Parasynthetic</xex> derivatives.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dr. Murray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paratactic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*tac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>Of pertaining to, or characterized by, parataxis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parataxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`a*tax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a placing beside, fr. <?/ to place beside.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>The mere ranging of propositions one after another, without
indicating their connection or interdependence; -- opposed to
<contr>syntax</contr>.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parathesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rath"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paratheses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/ a putting beside, from <?/ to put beside.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The placing of two or more nouns
in the same case; apposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A parenthetical notice, usually
of matter to be afterward expanded.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>The matter contained within
brackets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A commendatory prayer.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parathetic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to parathesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paratonnerre</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`ra`ton`nerre"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>parer</ets> to parry + <ets>tonnerre</ets> thunderbolt.]</ety>
<def>A conductor of lightning; a lightning rod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraunter</ent><br/
<hw>Par*aun"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Par</ets>
+ <ets>aunter</ets>.]</ety> <def>Peradventure. See
<er>Paraventure</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parauque</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rauque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A bird (<spn>Nyctidromus albicollis</spn>) ranging from Texas to
South America. It is allied to the night hawk and goatsucker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paravail</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*vail"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>par
aval</ets> below; <ets>par</ets> through (L. <ets>per</ets>) +
<ets>aval</ets> down; <ets>a-</ets> (L. <ets>ad</ets>) + <ets>val</ets>
(L. <ets>vallis</ets>) a valley. Cf. <er>Paramount</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>At the bottom; lowest.</def>
<rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In feudal law, the tenant <xex>paravail</xex> is the
lowest tenant of the fee, or he who is immediate tenant to one who holds
over of another.</note> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paravant</ent><br/
<ent>Paravant</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"a*vant`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par"a*vant`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>par avant</ets>.
See <er>Par</er>, and 1st <er>Avaunt</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>In front; publicly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Beforehand; first.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraventure</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*ven"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Par</ets> + <ets>aventure</ets>.]</ety> <def>Peradventure;
perchance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraxanthin</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*xan"thin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>Para-</ets> + <ets>xanthin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A crystalline substance closely related to xanthin,
present in small quantity in urine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraxial</ent><br/
<hw>Par*ax"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>axial</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>On
either side of the axis of the skeleton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paraxylene</ent><br/
<hw>Par`a*xy"lene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A hydrocarbon (<chform>C6H4(CH3)2</chform>) of the aromatic series
obtained as a colorless liquid by the distillation of camphor with zinc
chloride. It is one of the three metamers of xylene. Cf.
<er>Metamer</er>, and <er>Xylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parboil</ent><br/
<hw>Par"boil`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Parboiled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Parboiling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>parboilen</ets>, OF. <ets>parbouillir</ets> to cook well;
<ets>par</ets> through (see <er>Par</er>) + <ets>bouillir</ets> to boil,
L. <ets>bullire</ets>. The sense has been influenced by E.
<ets>part</ets>. See 1st <er>Boil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To boil
or cook thoroughly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To boil in part; to cook partially by
boiling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <mark>(Fig.)</mark> <def>To do
(something) only part way, or incompletely. Also used
intransitively.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parbreak</ent><br/
<hw>Par"break`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Par</ets> + <ets>break</ets>.]</ety> <def>To throw out; to
vomit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parbreak</ent><br/
<hw>Par"break`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Vomit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parbuckle</ent><br/
<hw>Par"buc`kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
kind of purchase for hoisting or lowering a cylindrical burden, as a
cask. The middle of a long rope is made fast aloft, and both parts are
looped around the object, which rests in the loops, and rolls in them as
the ends are hauled up or payed out.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A double
sling made of a single rope, for slinging a cask, gun, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parbuckle</ent><br/
<hw>Par"buc`kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Parbuckled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Parbuckling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To hoist or
lower by means of a parbuckle.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcae</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>The Fates. See <er>Fate</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcase</ent><br/
<hw>Par*case"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Par</ets> +
<ets>case</ets>.]</ety> <def>Perchance; by chance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parcelle</ets> a small part, fr. (assumed) LL.
<ets>particella</ets>, dim. of L. <ets>pars</ets>. See <er>Part</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Particle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
portion of anything taken separately; a fragment of a whole; a
part.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>parcel</xex> of her
woe.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two <qex>parcels</qex> of the white of an egg.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>parcels</qex> of the nation adopted different forms of
self-government.</q> <rj><qau>J. A. Symonds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A part; a portion; a piece; <as>as,
a certain piece of land is part and <ex>parcel</ex> of another
piece</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An indiscriminate or indefinite number, measure, or
quantity; a collection; a group.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This youthful <qex>parcel</qex><br/
Of noble bachelors stand at my disposing.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A number or quantity of things put up together; a
bundle; a package; a packet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'Tis like a <qex>parcel</qex> sent you by the stage.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bill of parcels</b></col>. <cd>See under 6th
<er>Bill</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Parcel office</b></col>, <cd>an office
where parcels are received for keeping or forwarding and delivery.</cd>
-- <col><b>Parcel post</b></col>, <cd>that department of the post office
concerned with the collection and transmission of parcels; also, the
transmission through the parcel post deparment; <as>as, to send a
package by <ex>parcel post</ex></as>. See <er>parcel post</er> in the
vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Part and parcel</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Part</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Parceled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Parcelled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Parceling</conjf> or
<conjf>Parcelling</conjf>.]</vmorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To divide and distribute by parts or portions; --
often with <ptcl>out</ptcl> or <ptcl>into</ptcl>.</def> <ldquo/Their
woes are <xex>parceled</xex>, mine are general.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These ghostly kings would <qex>parcel</qex> out my power.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The broad woodland <qex>parceled</qex> into farms.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To add a parcel or item to; to itemize.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That mine own servant should<br/
<qex>Parcel</qex> the sum of my disgraces by<br/
Addition of his envy.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make up into a parcel; <as>as, to <ex>parcel</ex>
a customer's purchases; the machine <ex>parcels</ex> yarn, wool,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To parcel a rope</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
wind strips of tarred canvas tightly arround it.</cd> <au>Totten.</au>
-- <col><b>To parcel a seam</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to cover
it with a strip of tarred canvas.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcel</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cel</hw>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <def>Part or half; in part;
partially.</def> <au>Shak.</au> <mark>[Sometimes hyphened with the word
following.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The worthy dame was <qex>parcel</qex>-blind.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One that . . . was <qex>parcel</qex>-bearded [partially
bearded].</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parcel poet</b></col>, <cd>a half poet; a poor poet.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parceling</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cel*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>parcelling</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of dividing and distributing in portions or
parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Long, narrow slips of canvas
daubed with tar and wound about a rope like a bandage, before it is
served; used, also, in mousing on the stayes, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcel-mele</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cel-mele`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parcel</er>, and <er>Meal</er> a part.]</ety> <def>By parcels or
parts.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcel post</ent><br/
<hw>Par"cel post</hw>. <def>That branch of the post office having to do
with the collection, transmission, and delivery of parcels{4}. The
British Inland Parcel Post was established in 1883. The rates in 1913,
dating from 1897, were 3d. for parcels not exceeding one pound and 1d.
for each additional pound up to the limit of 10 pounds. A general
<ex>parcel post</ex> was established in the United States by Act of
August 24, 1912, which took effect Jan. 1, 1913. At that time, parcels
could not exceed 11 pounds in weight nor 72 inches in length and girth
combined. Provision is made from insuring parcels, and also for sending
parcels C.O.D. The rates of postage vary with the distance. See
<er>Zone</er>, below.</def><-- ##?? update! --><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcenary</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ce*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parcener</er>, <er>partner</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The
holding or occupation of an inheritable estate which descends from the
ancestor to two or more persons; coheirship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It differs in many respects from <xex>joint
tenancy</xex>, which is created by deed or devise. In the United States
there is no essential distinction between parcenary and tenancy in
common.</note> <rj><au>Wharton. Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcener</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ce*ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of.
<ets>par<cced/onnier</ets>, <ets>parsonnier</ets>, fr.
<ets>parzon</ets>, <ets>par<cced/un</ets>, <ets>parcion</ets>, part,
portion, fr. L. <ets>partitio</ets> a division. See <er>Partition</er>,
and cf. <er>Partner</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A coheir, or one
of two or more persons to whom an estate of inheritance descends
jointly, and by whom it is held as one estate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parch</ent><br/
<hw>Parch</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Parched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Parching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>perchen</ets>
to pierce, hence used of a piercing heat or cold, OF.
<ets>perchier</ets>, another form of <ets>percier</ets>, F.
<ets>percer</ets>. See <er>Pierce</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To burn
the surface of; to scorch; to roast over the fire, as dry grain; <as>as,
to <ex>parch</ex> the skin; to <ex>parch</ex> corn.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye shall eat neither bread, nor <qex>parched</qex> corn.</q>
<rj><qau>Lev. xxiii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dry to extremity; to shrivel with heat; <as>as,
the mouth is <ex>parched</ex> from fever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ground below is <qex>parched</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parch</ent><br/
<hw>Parch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become scorched or
superficially burnt; to be very dry.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Parch</xex> in
Afric sun.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parchedness</ent><br/
<hw>Parch"ed*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
parched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcheesi</ent><br/
<hw>Par*chee"si</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*ch<emac/"z<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>See <er>Pachisi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parchesi</ent><br/
<hw>par*che"si</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*ch<emac/"z<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>See <er>Pachisi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parching</ent><br/
<hw>parch"ing</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rch"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Scorching; burning; drying.</def> <ldquo/Summer's
<xex>parching</xex> heat.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Parch"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parchisi</ent><br/
<hw>Par*chi"si</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*ch<emac/"z<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>same as <er>Pachisi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parchment</ent><br/
<hw>Parch"ment</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rch"m<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>parchemin</ets>, <ets>perchemin</ets>, F.
<ets>parchemin</ets>, LL. <ets>pergamenum</ets>, L.
<ets>pergamena</ets>, <ets>pergamina</ets>, fr. L. <ets>Pergamenus</ets>
of or belonging to <etsep>Pergamus</etsep> an ancient city of Mysia in
Asia Minor, where parchment was first used.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
skin of a lamb, sheep, goat, young calf, or other animal, prepared for
writing on. See <er>Vellum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But here's a <qex>parchment</qex> with the seal of C<ae/sar.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The envelope of the coffee grains, inside the
pulp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parchment paper</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Papyrine</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parchmentize</ent><br/
<hw>Parch"ment*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>-ized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>-izing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<def>To convert to a parchmentlike substance, especially by sulphuric
acid.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parcity</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ci*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parcitas</ets>, fr. <ets>parcus</ets> sparing.]</ety>
<def>Sparingless.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parclose</ent><br/
<hw>Par"close</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. See
<er>Perclose</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Arch.)</fld> <def>A screen
separating a chapel from the body of the church.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>paraclose</asp> and <asp>perclose</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Hook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pard</ent><br/
<hw>Pard</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pardus</ets>, Gr. <grk>pa`rdos</grk>; cf. Skr.
<ets>p<rsdot/d<amac/ku</ets> tiger, panther.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A leopard; a panther.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And more pinch-spotted make them<br/
Than <qex>pard</qex> or cat o'mountain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardale</ent><br/
<hw>Par"dale</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"d<asl/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pardalis</ets>, Gr. <grk>pa`rdalis</grk>. Cf. <er>Pard</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A leopard.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardie</ent><br/
<ent>Parde</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par*de"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par*die"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>adv. <or/ interj.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pardi</ets>, for
<ets>par Dieu</ets> by God.]</ety> <def>Certainly; surely; truly;
verily; -- originally an oath.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pardee</asp>, <asp>pardieux</asp>, <asp>perdie</asp>, etc.]</altsp>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was, <qex>parde</qex>, an old fellow of yours.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardine</ent><br/
<hw>Par"dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Spotted like a pard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pardine lynx</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a species
of lynx (<spn>Felis pardina</spn>) inhabiting Southern Europe. Its color
is rufous, spotted with black.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardo</ent><br/
<hw>Par"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pg. <ets>pardao</ets>,
fr. Skr. <ets>prat<amac/pa</ets> splendor, majesty.]</ety> <def>A money
of account once used in in Goa, India, equivalent to about 2s. 6d.
sterling. or 60 cts. (in 1913).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardon</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"d'n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>pardonner</ets> to pardon. See <er>Pardon</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pardoning; forgiveness, as
of an offender, or of an offense; release from penalty; remission of
punishment; absolution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pardon</qex>, my lord, for me and for my tidings.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But infinite in <qex>pardon</qex> was my judge.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><usage>Used in expressing courteous denial or contradiction; <as>as,
I beg your <ex>pardon</ex></as>; or in indicating that one has not
understood another; <as>as, I beg <ex>pardon</ex>; or <ex>pardon</ex>
me?</as>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An official warrant of remission of
penalty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sign me a present <qex>pardon</qex> for my brother.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The state of being forgiven.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A release, by a sovereign, or
officer having jurisdiction, from the penalties of an offense, being
distinguished from <contr>amnesty</contr>, which is a general
obliteration and canceling of a particular line of past
offenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Forgiveness; remission. See
<er>Forgiveness</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardon</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pardoned</conjf> <pr>(p<aum/r"d'nd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pardoning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Either fr.
<ets>pardon</ets>, n., or from F. <ets>pardonner</ets>, LL.
<ets>perdonare</ets>; L. <ets>per</ets> through, thoroughly, perfectly +
<ets>donare</ets> to give, to present. See <er>Par-</er>, and
<er>Donation</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To absolve from the
consequences of a fault or the punishment of crime; to free from
penalty; -- applied to the offender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In this thing the Lord <qex>pardon</qex> thy servant.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Kings v. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I pray you, <qex>pardon</qex> me; pray heartily, <qex>pardon</qex>
me.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To remit the penalty of; to suffer to pass without
punishment; to forgive; -- applied to offenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I pray thee, <qex>pardon</qex> my sin.</q> <rj><qau>1 Sam. xv.
25.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Apollo, <qex>pardon</qex><br/
My great profaneness 'gainst thine oracle!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To refrain from exacting as a penalty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>pardon</qex> thee thy life before thou ask it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To give leave (of departure) to.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even now about it! I will <qex>pardon</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pardon me</b></col>, <cd>forgive me; excuse me; -- a
phrase used also to express courteous denial or contradiction, or to
request forgiveness for a mild transgression, such as bumping a person
while passing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To forgive; absolve; excuse; overlook; remit;
acquit. See <er>Excuse</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardonable</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pardonnable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Admitting of pardon; not requiring
the excution of penalty; venial; excusable; -- applied to the offense or
to the offender; <as>as, a <ex>pardonable</ex> fault, or
culprit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardonableness</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don*a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being pardonable; <as>as, the <ex>pardonableness</ex> of sin</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardonably</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don*a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a manner admitting of
pardon; excusably.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardoner</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pardons.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A seller of indulgences.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pardoning</ent><br/
<hw>Par"don*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to pardon; having or
exercising the right to pardon; willing to pardon; merciful; <as>as, the
<ex>pardoning</ex> power; a <ex>pardoning</ex> God.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pare</ent><br/
<hw>Pare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>parer</ets> to pare, as a
horse's hoofs, to dress or curry, as, leather, to clear, as anchors or
cables, to parry, ward off, fr. L. <ets>parare</ets> to prepare. Cf.
<er>Empire</er>, <er>Parade</er>, <er>Pardon</er>, <er>Parry</er>,
<er>Prepare</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut off, or shave off, the
superficial substance or extremities of; <as>as, to <ex>pare</ex> an
apple; to <ex>pare</ex> a horse's hoof.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To remove; to separate; to cut or shave, as the
skin, rind, or outside part, from anything; -- followed by
<ptcl>off</ptcl> or <ptcl>away</ptcl>; <as>as, to <ex>pare</ex> off the
rind of fruit; to <ex>pare</ex> away redundancies</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: To diminish the bulk of; to reduce; to
lessen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king began to <qex>pare</qex> a little the privilege of
clergy.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paregmenon</ent><br/
<hw>pa*reg"me*non`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>Juxtaposing words having a common derivation, as in the phrase
"sense and sensibility".</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paregoric</ent><br/
<hw>Par`e*gor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paregoricus</ets>, Gr. <?/, from <?/ addressing, encouraging,
soothing; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ an assembly: cf. F.
<ets>par<eacute/gorique</ets>. See <er>Allegory</er>.]</ety>
<def>Mitigating; assuaging or soothing pain; <as>as, <ex>paregoric</ex>
elixir</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paregoric</ent><br/
<hw>Par`e*gor"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
that mitigates pain; an anodyne; specifically, camphorated tincture of
opium; -- called also <altname>paregoric elexir</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parelcon</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rel"con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to draw
aside, to be redundant; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ to draw.]</ety>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The addition of a syllable or particle to the
end of a pronoun, verb, or adverb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parelectronomic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`e*lec`tro*nom"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or relating to parelectronomy; <as>as, the
<ex>parelectronomic</ex> part of a muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parelectronomy</ent><br/
<hw>Par*e`lec*tron"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>electro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ law.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A condition of the muscles induced by
exposure to severe cold, in which the electrical action of the muscle is
reversed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parelle</ent><br/
<ent>Parella</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pa*rel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pa`relle</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parelle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A name
for two kinds of dock (<spn>Rumex Patientia</spn> and <spn>Rumex
Hydrolapathum</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A kind of lichen
(<spn>Lecanora parella</spn>) once used in dyeing and in the preparation
of litmus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parembole</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rem"bo*le</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ an insertion beside. See <er>Para-</er>, and
<er>Embolus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A kind of
parenthesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1043 --></p>

<p><ent>parement</ent><br/
<hw>pare"ment</hw> <pr>(p<acir/r"m<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parament</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paremptosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>par`emp*to"sis</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r`<ecr/mp*t<omac/"s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>pare`mptwsis</grk> a coming in
beside; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>'empiptein</grk> to fall
in.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Parembole</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parenchyma</ent><br/
<hw>pa*ren"chy*ma</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<ecr/<nsm/"k<icr/*m<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>pare`gchyma</grk>, fr.
<grk>paregchei^n</grk> to pour in beside; <grk>para`</grk> beside +
<grk>'en</grk> in + <grk>chei^n</grk> to pour: cf. F.
<ets>parenchyme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The soft cellular
substance of the tissues of plants and animals, like the pulp of leaves,
the soft tissue of glands, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parenchymal</ent><br/
<hw>pa*ren"chy*mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining
to, or consisting of, parenchyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenchymous</ent><br/
<ent>Parenchymatous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`en*chym"a*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pa*ren"chy*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>parenchymateux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
connected with, the parenchyma of a tissue or an organ; <as>as,
<ex>parenchymatous</ex> degeneration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ren"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paraenesis</ets>, Gr. <grk>parai`nesis</grk>, fr.
<grk>parainei^n</grk> to advise.]</ety> <def>Exhortation.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenetical</ent><br/
<ent>Parenetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`e*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par`e*net"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>parainetiko`s</grk>:
cf. F. <ets>par<eacute/n<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Hortatory;
encouraging; persuasive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>F.
Potter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parent</ent><br/
<hw>par"ent</hw> <pr>(p<acir/r"<eit/nt <it>or</it> p<amac/r"<eit/nt;
277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>parens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>;
akin to <ets>parere</ets> to bring forth; cf. Gr. <grk>porei^n</grk> to
give, beget: cf. F. <ets>parent</ets>. Cf. <er>Part</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who begets, or brings forth, offspring; a father or
a mother.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Children, obey your <qex>parents</qex> in the Lord.</q>
<rj><qau>Eph. vi. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which produces; cause; source; author;
begetter; <as>as, idleness is the <ex>parent</ex> of
vice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Regular industry is the <qex>parent</qex> of sobriety.</q>
<rj><qau>Channing.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parent cell</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Mother cell</cref>, under <er>Mother</er>, also
<er>Cytula</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Parent nucleus</b></col>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>a nucleus which, in cell division, divides, and
gives rise to two or more daughter nuclei. See <er>Karyokinesis</er>,
and <cref>Cell division</cref>, under <er>Division</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parentage</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ent*age</hw> <pr>(p<acir/r"<eit/nt*<asl/j; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>parentage</ets> relationship.]</ety>
<def>Descent from parents or ancestors; parents or ancestors considered
with respect to their rank or character; extraction; birth; <as>as, a
man of noble <ex>parentage</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/Wilt thou deny thy
<xex>parentage</xex>?<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though men esteem thee low of <qex>parentage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parental</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ren"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parentalis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a
parent or to parents; <as>as, <ex>parental</ex> authority;
<ex>parental</ex> obligations; <ex>parental</ex>
affection.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Becoming to, or characteristic of, parents; tender;
affectionate; devoted; <as>as, <ex>parental</ex> care</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The careful course and <qex>parental</qex> provision of
nature.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parentally</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ren"tal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a parental
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parentation</ent><br/
<hw>Par`en*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parentatio</ets>, fr. <ets>parentare</ets> to offer a solemn
sacrifice in honor of deceased parents. See <er>Parent</er>.]</ety>
<def>Something done or said in honor of the dead; obsequies.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Abp. Potter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parentele</ent><br/
<hw>Par"en`tele`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parent<egrave/le</ets>, L. <ets>parentela</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Kinship; parentage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenthesis</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ren"the*sis</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<ecr/n"th<esl/*s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parentheses</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pare`nqesis</grk>, fr.
<grk>parentiqe`nai</grk> to put in beside, insert; <grk>para`</grk>
beside + <grk>'en</grk> in + <grk>tiqe`nai</grk> to put, place. See
<er>Para-</er>, <er>En-</er>, 2, and <er>Thesis</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A word, phrase, or sentence, by way of comment or
explanation, inserted in, or attached to, a sentence which would be
grammatically complete without it. It is usually inclosed within curved
lines (see def. 2 below), or dashes.</def> <ldquo/Seldom mentioned
without a derogatory <xex>parenthesis</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Don't suffer every occasional thought to carry you away into a
long <qex>parenthesis</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>One of the curved lines () which
inclose a parenthetic word or phrase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Parenthesis</xex>, in technical grammar, is that
part of a sentence which is inclosed within the recognized sign; but
many phrases and sentences which are punctuated by commas are logically
parenthetical. In def. 1, the phrase <ldquo/by way of comment or
explanation<rdquo/ is inserted for explanation, and the sentence would
be grammatically complete without it. The present tendency is to avoid
using the distinctive marks, except when confusion would arise from a
less conspicuous separation.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenthesize</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ren"the*size</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<ecr/n"th<esl/*s<imac/z)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make a parenthesis of; to include within
parenthetical marks.</def> <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parenthetical</ent><br/
<ent>parenthetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>par`en*thet"ic</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<ecr/n*th<ecr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <hw>par`en*thet"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<ecr/n*th<ecr/t"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. Gr. <grk>pare`nqetos</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the
nature of a parenthesis; pertaining to, or expressed in, or as if in, a
parenthesis; <as>as, a <ex>parenthetical</ex> clause; a
<ex>parenthetic</ex> remark; a <ex>parenthetical</ex> style.</as></def>
<wns>[wns=1]</wns><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>parenthetical</qex> observation of Moses himself.</q>
<rj><qau>Hales.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Using or containing parentheses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Added within parentheses to amplify or explain;
<as>as, <ex>parenthetical</ex> remarks</as>.</def>
<wns>[wns=2]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenthetically</ent><br/
<hw>Par`en*thet"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a parenthetical
manner; by way of parenthesis; by parentheses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenthood</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ent*hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of a
parent; the office or character of a parent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parenticide</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ren"ti*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parenticida</ets> a parricide; <ets>parens</ets> parent +
<ets>caedere</ets> to kill.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who kills one's own parent.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who kills one's own parent; a parricide.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parentless</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ent*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deprived of
parents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parepididymis</ent><br/
<hw>Par*ep`i*did"y*mis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Epididymis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A small body containing convoluted tubules, situated near the
epididymis in man and some other animals, and supposed to be a remnant
of the anterior part of the Wolffian body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parer</ent><br/
<hw>Par"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pare</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, pares; an
instrument for paring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parergon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rer"gon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Parergy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parergy</ent><br/
<hw>Par"er*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parergon</ets>, Gr. <?/; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ work.]</ety>
<def>Something unimportant, incidental, or superfluous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paresis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>pa`resis</grk>, fr. <grk>parie`nai</grk> to let go;
<grk>para`</grk> from + <grk>"ie`nai</grk> to send.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Incomplete paralysis, affecting motion but not
sensation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parethmoid</ent><br/
<hw>Par*eth"moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>ethmoid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Near or beside the ethmoid bone or cartilage; -- applied especially
to a pair of bones in the nasal region of some fishes, and to the
ethmoturbinals in some higher animals.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A parethmoid bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paretic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ret"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
paresis; affected with paresis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parfay</ent><br/
<hw>Par*fay"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Par</ets>
+ <ets>fay</ets>.]</ety> <def>By my faith; verily.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parfit</ent><br/
<hw>Par"fit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Perfect.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parfitly</ent><br/
<hw>Par"fit*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Perfectly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parfleche</ent><br/
<hw>Par*fleche"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. through
Canadian F. fr. Amer. Indian.]</ety> <def>A kind of rawhide consisting
of hide, esp. of the buffalo, which has been soaked in crude wood-ash
lye to remove the hairs, and then dried.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parfocal</ent><br/
<hw>Par*fo"cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pari-</ets> +
<ets>focal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>With the lower focal
points all in the same plane; -- said of sets of eyepieces so mounted
that they may be interchanged without varying the focus of the
instrument (as a microscope or telescope) with which they are
used.</def> <ldquo/the convenience of a microscope with
<xex>parfocal</xex> objective lenses<rdquo/<br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parfourn</ent><br/
<ent>Parforn</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par*forn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par*fourn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To perform.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pargasite</ent><br/
<hw>Par"gas*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
<etsep>Pargas</etsep>, in Finland.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A dark
green aluminous variety of amphibole, or hornblende.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pargeboard</ent><br/
<hw>Parge"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Bargeboard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parget</ent><br/
<hw>Par"get</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pargeted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pargeting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pargeten</ets>, also
<ets>spargeten</ets>, <ets>sparchen</ets>; of uncertain origin.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To coat with parget; to plaster, as walls, or the
interior of flues; <as>as, to <ex>parget</ex> the outside of their
houses</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pargeted</qex> ceiling with pendants.</q> <rj><qau>R. L.
Stevenson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To paint; to cover over.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parget</ent><br/
<hw>Par"get</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay on
plaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To paint, as the face.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parget</ent><br/
<hw>Par"get</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Gypsum or plaster
stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plaster, as for lining the interior of flues, or for
stuccowork.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Paint, especially for the face.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pargeter</ent><br/
<hw>Par"get*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A plasterer.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pargeting</ent><br/
<hw>Par"get*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pargetting</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plasterwork;</def>
<specif>esp.:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A kind of decorative
plasterwork in raised ornamental figures, formerly used for the internal
and external decoration of houses.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(In modern
architecture)</fld>, <def>the plastering of the inside of flues,
intended to give a smooth surface and help the draught.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pargetory</ent><br/
<hw>Par"get*o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something made
of, or covered with, parget, or plaster.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parhelic</ent><br/
<hw>Par*he"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
parhelia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parhelion</ent><br/
<hw>Par*hel"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Parhelia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>parelion</ets>,
Gr. <?/, <?/; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <?/ the sun.]</ety> <def>A mock
sun appearing in the form of a bright light, sometimes near the sun, and
tinged with colors like the rainbow, and sometimes opposite to the sun.
The latter is usually called an <altname>anthelion</altname>. Often
several mock suns appear at the same time. Cf.
<er>Paraselene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parhelium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*he"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parhelion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pari-</ent><br/
<hw>Par"i-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>par</ets>, <ets>paris</ets>,
equal.]</ety> <def>A combining form signifying <sig>equal</sig>; <as>as,
<ex>pari</ex>digitate, <ex>pari</ex>pinnate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pariah</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"ri*ah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Tamil
<ets>paraiyan</ets>, pl. <ets>paraiyar</ets>, one of the low caste, fr.
<ets>parai</ets> a large drum, because they beat the drums at certain
festivals.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One of an aboriginal people of Southern India,
regarded by the four castes of the Hindus as of very low grade. They are
usually the serfs of the Sudra agriculturalists. See
<er>Caste</er>.</def> <rj><au>Balfour (Cyc. of India).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An outcast; one despised by society.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pariah dog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a mongrel
race of half-wild dogs which act as scavengers in Oriental cities.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pariah kite</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a species of
kite (<spn>Milvus govinda</spn>) which acts as a scavenger in
India.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parial</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <cref>Pair
royal</cref>, under <er>Pair</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Parius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to <etsep>Paros</etsep>,
an island in the Aegean Sea noted for its excellent statuary marble;
<as>as, <ex>Parian</ex> marble</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parian chronicle</b></col>, <cd>a most ancient chronicle
of the city of <city>Athens</city>, engraved on marble in the Isle of
Paros, now among the Arundelian marbles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or inhabitant
of Paros.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A ceramic ware, resembling unglazed porcelain
biscuit, of which are made statuettes, ornaments, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paridigitata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`i*dig`i*ta"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pari-</er>, and <er>Digitate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Artiodactyla</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paridigitate</ent><br/
<hw>Par`i*dig"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Having an even number of digits on the hands or the feet.</def>
<rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paries</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"ri*es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Parietes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See
<er>Parietes</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The triangular middle
part of each segment of the shell of a barnacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"e*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parietalis</ets>, fr. <ets>paries</ets>, <ets>-ietis</ets>, a wall:
cf. F. <ets>pari<eacute/tal</ets>. Cf. <er>Parietary</er>,
<er>Pellitory</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a wall; hence, pertaining to
buildings or the care of them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resident within the walls or buildings of a
college.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At Harvard College, the officers resident within the college walls
constitute a permanent standing committee, called the
<qex>Parietal</qex> Committee.</q> <rj><qau>B. H. Hall
(1856).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to
the parietes.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of, pertaining to, or in the
region of, the parietal bones, which form the upper and middle part of
the cranium, between the frontals and occipitals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Attached to the main wall of the
ovary, and not to the axis; -- said of a placenta.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"e*tal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>One of the parietal bones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the special scales, or
plates, covering the back of the head in certain reptiles and
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietary</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"e*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parietal</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietary</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"e*ta*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>parietaria</ets>,
fr. <ets>parietarius</ets> parietal. Cf. <er>Pellitory</er>,
<er>Parietal</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any one of several
species of <gen>Parietaria</gen>. See 1st <er>Pellitory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ri"e*tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paries</ets> a wall.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The walls of a cavity or an
organ; <as>as, the abdominal <ex>parietes</ex>; the <ex>parietes</ex> of
the cranium.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The sides of an ovary or of a
capsule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`ri*et"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid found in the lichen
<spn>Parmelia parietina</spn>, and called also
<altname>chrysophanic</altname> <it>acid</it>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parietine</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"e*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parietinus</ets> parietal: cf. <ets>parietinae</ets> ruined
walls.]</ety> <def>A piece of a fallen wall; a ruin.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parieto-</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"e*to-</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A combining
form used to indicate <xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>relation
to</xex>, <xex>the parietal bones or the parietal segment of the
skull</xex>; <as>as, the <ex>parieto</ex>-mastoid suture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parigenin</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rig"e*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pari</ets>llin + <ets>-gen</ets> + <ets>-in</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A curdy white substance, obtained by the
decomposition of parillin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parillin</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ril"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Shortened fr.
<ets>sarsaparillin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A glucoside
resembling saponin, found in the root of sarsaparilla, smilax, etc., and
extracted as a bitter white crystalline substance; -- called also
<altname>smilacin</altname>, <altname>sarsaparilla saponin</altname>,
and <altname>sarsaparillin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parimutuel</ent><br/
<hw>par`i*mu"tu*el</hw>
<pr>(p<acr/r`<icr/*m<umac/"ch<oomac/*<ucr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
form of betting where winners share the total amount wagered, in
proporation to their bets, and less a portion for the management; --
used commonly in betting at horse racing track. In <ex>parimutuel</ex>
betting, the payoff for a bet does not have fixed odds, but depends on
the amount bet on each outcome.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paring</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pare</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of cutting off the
surface or extremites of anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is pared off.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pare off the surface of the earth, and with the <qex>parings</qex>
raise your hills.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parings</ent><br/
<hw>parings</hw> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Plural of
<er>paring</er>{2}.]</ety> <def>Parts that are pared or cut
off.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paring.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paripinnate</ent><br/
<hw>Par`i*pin"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pari-</ets> + <ets>pinnate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Pinnate with an equal number of leaflets on each side; having no
odd leaflet at the end.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paris</ent><br/
<hw>Par"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<etsep>Paris</etsep>, the son of Priam.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant common in Europe (<spn>Paris quadrifolia</spn>); herb Paris;
truelove. It has been used as a narcotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It much resembles the American genus
<gen>Trillium</gen>, but has usually four leaves and a tetramerous
flower.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paris</ent><br/
<hw>Par"is</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The chief city of France.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paris green</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Green</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Paris white</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>purified chalk used as a pigment; whiting; Spanish
white.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parish</ent><br/
<hw>par"ish</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>parishe</ets>, <ets>paresche</ets>, <ets>parosche</ets>, OF.
<ets>paroisse</ets>, <ets>parosse</ets>, <ets>paroiche</ets>, F.
<ets>paroisse</ets>, L. <ets>parochia</ets>, corrupted fr.
<ets>paroecia</ets>, Gr. <grk>paroiki`a</grk>, fr. <grk>pa`roikos</grk>
dwelling beside or near; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>o'i^kos</grk> a
house, dwelling; akin to L. <ets>vicus</ets> village. See
<er>Vicinity</er>, and cf. <er>Parochial</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. & Eng. Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>That
circuit of ground committed to the charge of one parson or vicar, or
other minister having cure of souls therein.</def> <au>Cowell.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The same district, constituting a civil jurisdiction,
with its own officers and regulations, as respects the poor, taxes,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Populous and extensive parishes are now divided, under
various parliamentary acts, into smaller ecclesiastical districts for
spiritual purposes.</note> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An ecclesiastical society, usually not bounded by
territorial limits, but composed of those persons who choose to unite
under the charge of a particular priest, clergyman, or minister; also,
loosely, the territory in which the members of a congregation
live.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In Louisiana, a civil division corresponding to a
<simto>county</simto> in other States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parish</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a parish;
parochial; <as>as, a <ex>parish</ex> church; <ex>parish</ex> records; a
<ex>parish</ex> priest</as>; maintained by the parish; <as>as,
<ex>parish</ex> poor</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parish clerk</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The clerk or
recording officer of a parish</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A layman who leads
in the responses and otherwise assists in the service of the Church of
England.</cd> -- <col><b>Parish court</b></col>, <cd>in Louisiana, a
court in each parish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parishen</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ish*en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
parishioner.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parishional</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rish"ion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a parish; parochial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parishioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rish"ion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paroissien</ets>, LL. <ets>parochianus</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who
belongs to, or is connected with, a parish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parisian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parisen</ets>.]</ety> <def>A native or inhabitant of
<city>Paris</city>, the capital of France.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parisian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ri"sian</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Paris.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parisienne</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`ri`si`enne"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A female native or resident of <city>Paris</city>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parisology</ent><br/
<hw>Par`i*sol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ almost
equal, evenly balanced + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The use of
equivocal or ambiguous words.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parison</ent><br/
<hw>par"i*son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Glassworking)</fld>
<def>An intermediate stage or shape of a glass object which is produced
in more than one stage.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parisyllabical</ent><br/
<ent>Parisyllabic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`i*syl*lab"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Par`i*syl*lab"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pari-</ets> + <ets>syllabic</ets>, <ets>-ical</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>parisyllabique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the same number of
syllables in all its inflections.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paritor</ent><br/
<hw>Par"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. fr.
<ets>apparitor</ets>: cf. L. <ets>paritor</ets> a servant,
attendant.]</ety> <def>An apparitor.</def> <ldquo/Summoned by an host of
<xex>paritors</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paritory</ent><br/
<hw>Par"i*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pellitory.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parity</ent><br/
<hw>Par"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paritas</ets>, fr. <ets>par</ets>, <ets>paris</ets>, equal: cf. F.
<ets>parit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Pair</er>, <er>Peer</er> an
equal.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or condition of being equal
or equivalent; a like state or degree; equality; equivalence; close
correspondence; analogy; <as>as, <ex>parity</ex> of
reasoning</as>.</def> <ldquo/No <xex>parity</xex> of principle.<rdquo/
<rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Equality of length and <qex>parity</qex> of numeration.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <fld>(Finance)</fld>
<def>Equivalence in value to the currency of another country.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A property assigned to
elementary particles, conceptualized as a form of symmetry, representing
the fact that no fundamental distinctions can be observed between
right-handed and left-handed systems of particles in their interactions,
and supported by the typical observation that the total parity of a
system is unchanged as particles are created or annihilated; however,
certain interactions involving the weak force have been shown to violate
the principle of conservation of parity.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A property of the wave function
of a system, which takes the value of +1 or -1, indicating whether the
value of the wave function changes sign if each of the variables of the
system is replaced by its negative.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The condition of having borne a
child or children, alive or dead.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The property of being even or
odd; <as>as, 3 has odd <ex>parity</ex>, but 6 has even
<ex>parity</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>The
property of having an even or odd number of bits set to the value of 1
(as opposed to 0); -- applied to bytes or larger groups of bits in a
data structure. It is used mostly in the process of <er>parity
checking</er>. The <ex>parity</ex> of a data structure can be changed by
changing the value of the <er>parity bit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parity bit</ent><br/
<hw>par"i*ty bit`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>The bit within a data structure which is
assigned a value of 1 or 0 so as to make the parity{7} of the data
structure odd or even. Data structures may or may not have <ex>parity
bits</ex>, dpending on whether the system does or does not perform
<er>parity checking</er>. The most commonly used parity bit is the eigth
(higher-order) bit of a byte, which is used when data transmission uses
only the 7 lower-order bits of each byte as significant data; some
memory systems use a ninth bit as a parity bit for each eight bits (one
byte) of significant data in memory.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parity check</ent><br/
<hw>par"i*ty check`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>The act or process of testing whether a byte
or other data structure has an even or odd number of bits set to the
value of 1; -- it is used primarily to detect errors in data, especially
in memory banks or in data transmitted over a communications line. The
parity can be changed by assigning one bit in each data structure as the
<er>parity bit</er>, so that the total number of bits set to the value
of 1 is odd (odd parity) or even (even parity). If parity is used for
error checking the writing and reading systems must first agree on which
type of parity (odd or even) to use; if the reading system detects a
deviation from the agreed parity, it signals an error, to be handled by
the error-handling processes of the system.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parity checking</ent><br/
<hw>par"i*ty check`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>The process of performing a <er>parity
check</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parjanya</ent><br/
<hw>Parjanya</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Hinduism)</fld>
<def>The Hindu god of rain; sometimes identified with
<altname>Indra</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Park</ent><br/
<hw>Park</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rk)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>pearroc</ets>, or perh. rather fr. F. <ets>parc</ets>; both being
of the same origin; cf. LL. <ets>parcus</ets>, <ets>parricus</ets>, Ir.
& Gael. <ets>pairc</ets>, W. <ets>park</ets>, <ets>parwg</ets>. Cf.
<er>Paddock</er> an inclosure, <er>Parrock</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A piece of ground inclosed, and stored with
beasts of the chase, which a man may have by prescription, or the king's
grant.</def> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tract of ground kept in its natural state, about
or adjacent to a residence, as for the preservation of game, for
walking, riding, or the like.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While in the <qex>park</qex> I sing, the listening deer<br/
Attend my passion, and forget to fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A piece of ground, in or near a city or town,
inclosed and kept for ornament and recreation; <as>as, Hyde
<ex>Park</ex> in London; Central <ex>Park</ex> in New
York.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A space occupied by the animals,
wagons, pontoons, and materials of all kinds, as ammunition, ordnance
stores, hospital stores, provisions, etc., when brought together; also,
the objects themselves; <as>as, a <ex>park</ex> of wagons; a
<ex>park</ex> of artillery.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A partially inclosed basin in which oysters are
grown.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>parc</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Any place where vehicles are assembled according to
a definite arrangement; also, the vehicles.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A position of the gear lever in a vehicle with
automatic transmission, used when the vehicle is stopped, in which the
transmission is in neutral and a brake is engaged.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Park of artillery</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Artillery</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Park phaeton</b></col>, <cd>a small,
low carriage, for use in parks.</cd> -- <col><b>industrial
park</b></col> <cd>a region located typically in a suburban or rural
area, zoned by law for specific types of business use (as, retail
business, light industry, and sometimes heavy industry), often having
some parklike characteristics, and having businesses, parking lots, and
sometimes recreation areas and restaurants. The sponsoring agency may
also provide supporting facilities, such as water towers, office
buildings, or for large industrial parks, an airport.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Park</ent><br/
<hw>Park</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Parked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Parking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To inclose in a park,
or as in a park.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How are we <qex>parked</qex>, and bounded in a pale.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To bring together in a park, or
compact body; <as>as, to <ex>park</ex> artillery, wagons, automobiles,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In oyster culture, to inclose in a park.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bring (a vehicle) to a stop and leave it
standing; -- typically a parked vehicle is off of the public road, the
motor is not running, and the driver has left the vehicle.</def> <note>a
vehicle stopped but still running with the driver in it is said to be
<contr>standing</contr>.</note> <stype>parallel-park</stype>
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To place (an object) in a temporary location;
<as>as, to <ex>park</ex> oneself on the couch; to <ex>park</ex> one's
money in a mutual fund</as>.</def> <mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Park</ent><br/
<hw>Park</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To promenade or drive
in a park; also, of horses, to display style or gait on a park
drive.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To come to a stop [in a vehicle] off of the public
road and leave the vehicle standing; -- typically the motor of a parked
vehicle is not left running; <as>as, he <ex>parked</ex> in a no-parking
zone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parkee</ent><br/
<ent>Parka</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par"kee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ. <ets>parka</ets>, <ets>parki</ets>,
dim. of <ets>para</ets> a pair, fr. G. <ets>paar</ets>, L.
<ets>par</ets>. Cf. <er>Pair</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An outer
garment made of the skins of birds or mammals, worn by Eskimos,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A coat with a fur lining and a hood, used for
protection in very cold weather.</def><br/
[<source>PJC.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any of a variety of jackets
with attached hoods, usually of very warm or waterproof construction so
as to provide protection from cold or rainy weather.</def><br/
[<source>PJC.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parked</ent><br/
<hw>parked</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of <er>park</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, 2; -- of vehicles; <as>as, there were four
<ex>parked</ex> cars across the street</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parker</ent><br/
<hw>Park"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The keeper of a
park.</def> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parkeria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*ke"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So
named from <person>W. K. <etsep>Parker</etsep></person>, a British
zoologist.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of large arenaceous
fossil Foraminifera found in the Cretaceous rocks. The species are
globular, or nearly so, and are of all sizes up to that of a tennis
ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parkeriaceae</ent><br/
<hw>Parkeriaceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of ferns
coextensive with the genus <gen>Ceratopteris</gen>; sometimes it is
included in the family <fam>Polypodiaceae</fam>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Parkeriaceae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1044 --></p>

<p><ent>Parkesine</ent><br/
<hw>Parkes"ine</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rks"<icr/n <it>or</it>
p<aum/rks"<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from Mr.
<etsep>Parkes</etsep>, the inventor.]</ety> <def>A compound, originally
made from gun cotton and castor oil, but later from different materials,
and used as a substitute for vulcanized India rubber and for ivory; --
called also <altname>xylotile</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parkia</ent><br/
<hw>Parkia</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of tropical Old World
trees, including the nitta trees.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parkia</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parking</ent><br/
<hw>parking</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of maneuvering a vehicle
into a location where it can be left temporarily.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A space or spaces in which a vehicle can be parked;
<as>as, there is plenty of <ex>parking</ex> behind the
store</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parkinsonia</ent><br/
<hw>Parkinsonia</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A small genus of spiny
shrubs or small trees.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parkinsonia</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parkinsonism</ent><br/
<hw>parkinsonism</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A degenerative nervous disorder
occurring mostly in persons older than 60 years, characterized in
advanced stages by rhythmic muscular tremors, especially in the hands,
and by rigidity of movement, a drooping posture, slow speech, and a
masklike facial expression; the term designates a set of symptoms, and
the underlying disease causing these symptoms is most commonly
<er>Parkinson's disease</er>. Parkinsonism results from a deficiency in
the neurotransmitter dopamine caused by degeneration of the cells
producing that agent. It can be mitigated by chemotherapy with agents
such as levodopa (<chname>3-hydroxy-L-tyrosine</chname>).</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paralysis agitans, Parkinson's disease, Parkinson's
syndrome, Parkinson's, shaking palsy.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><q>A Mayo Clinic study finds <qex>parkinsonism</qex>, a group of
ailments that includes Parkinson's disease, is three times more common
than previously thought and men are more likely to develop it than
women.<br/
The study, published Tuesday in the journal Neurology, found the
lifetime risk of developing parkinsonism is 7.5 percent, three times
higher than previously thought.</q> <rj><qau>UPI (Jan. 2,
1999)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parkinson's disease</ent><br/
<ent>Parkinson's</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Parkinson's</hw>, <hw>Parkinson's disease</hw></mhw> <pos>prop.
n.</pos> <def>A disease causing <er>parkinsonism</er>, a degenerative
nervous disorder; sometimes used loosely as a synonym for parkinsonism.
More precisely, <ex>parkinson's disease</ex> is the term for the
underlying physiological disorder and parkinsonism is the term for the
set of symptoms associated with the disease. See
<er>parkinsonism</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paralysis agitans, Parkinsonism, Parkinson's disease
syndrome, shaking palsy.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Parkinson's disease</qex> is the most common form of
parkinsonism, a group of disorders that are the result of the loss of
dopamine-producing brain cells.</q> <rj><qau>Mayo Clinic (Report,
1999)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>According to the National Parkinson's Foundation (NPF), 1 million
Americans -- including former heavyweight boxing champion
<person>Muhammad Ali</person>, U.S. Attorney General <person>Janet
Reno</person>, and television and film star <person>Michael J.
Fox</person> -- are affected by <qex>Parkinson's disease</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mayo Clinic (Report, 1999)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parkleaves</ent><br/
<hw>park"leaves`</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rk"l<emac/vz`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A European species of Saint John's-wort; the
tutsan. See <er>Tutsan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parkway</ent><br/
<hw>park"way`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wide scenic road planted with
trees.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> drive.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parky</ent><br/
<hw>park"y</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Cold; -- of weather.</def>
<mark>[British informal]</mark><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parlance</ent><br/
<hw>Par"lance</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"l<ait/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.,
fr. F. <ets>parler</ets> to speak. See <er>Parley</er>.]</ety>
<def>Conversation; discourse; talk; diction; phrase; <as>as, in legal
<ex>parlance</ex>; in common <ex>parlance</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A hate of gossip <qex>parlance</qex> and of sway.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parlante</ent><br/
<ent>Parlando</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Par*lan"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Par*lan"te</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Speaking; in a speaking or declamatory manner; to
be sung or played in the style of a recitative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parlay</ent><br/
<hw>par"lay</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wager the winnings
plus the initial stake of one bet on a subsequent wager, or to engage in
a series of such increased wagers.</def> <note>This is the
characteristic act in a common betting system used by gamblers to try to
achieve a large win from a small initial stake. Statistically, it is
similar to betting a small stake on a game with a large payoff and a
proportionately small chance of winning.</note>
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exploit success in one endeavor so as to achieve
greater success in subsequent endeavors.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig., to transform a gain into something of greater
value.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parlay</ent><br/
<hw>par"lay</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of betting the winnings from
a prior bet, plus the original stake, on a subsequent bet; the second or
later such bet in a series; also, a series of such bets, with the stakes
for later bets coming from the winnings of earlier bets.</def>
<note>This is the characteristic act in a common betting system used by
gamblers to try to achieve a large win from a small initial stake.
Statistically, it is similar to betting a small stake on a game with a
large payoff, but with a proportionately small chance of
winning.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parle</ent><br/
<hw>Parle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parler</ets>. See <er>Parley</er>.]</ety> <def>To talk; to
converse; to parley.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Finding himself too weak, began to <qex>parle</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parle</ent><br/
<hw>Parle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Conversation; talk; parley.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They ended <qex>parle</qex>, and both addressed for fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parley</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Parleys</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>parler</ets>
speech, talk, fr. <ets>parler</ets> to speak, LL. <ets>parabolare</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>parabola</ets> a comparison, parable, in LL., a word. See
<er>Parable</er>, and cf. <er>Parliament</er>, <er>Parlor</er>.]</ety>
<def>Mutual discourse or conversation; discussion; hence, an oral
conference with an enemy, as with regard to a truce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We yield on <qex>parley</qex>, but are stormed in vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To beat a parley</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>to beat
a drum, or sound a trumpet, as a signal for holding a conference with
the enemy.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parley</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ley</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Parleyed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Parleying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To speak with another; to
confer on some point of mutual concern; to discuss orally; hence,
specifically, to confer orally with an enemy; to treat with him by
words, as on an exchange of prisoners, an armistice, or terms of
peace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are at hand,<br/
To <qex>parley</qex> or to fight; therefore prepare.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parliament</ent><br/
<hw>Par"lia*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>parlement</ets>, F. <ets>parlement</ets>, fr. <ets>parler</ets> to
speak; cf. LL. <ets>parlamentum</ets>, <ets>parliamentum</ets>. See
<er>Parley</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A parleying; a discussion; a
conference.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But first they held their <qex>parliament</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Rom.
of R.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A formal conference on public affairs; a general
council;</def> <specif>esp.,</specif> <def>an assembly of
representatives of a nation or people having authority to make
laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They made request that it might be lawful for them to summon a
<qex>parliament</qex> of Gauls.</q> <rj><qau>Golding.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The assembly of the three estates of the United
Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, viz., the lords spiritual, lords
temporal, and the representatives of the commons, sitting in the House
of Lords and the House of Commons, constituting the legislature, when
summoned by the royal authority to consult on the affairs of the nation,
and to enact and repeal laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Thought the sovereign is a constituting branch of
Parliament, the word is generally used to denote the three estates named
above.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In France, before the Revolution of 1789, one of the
several principal judicial courts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parliament heel</b></col>, <cd>the inclination of a ship
when made to careen by shifting her cargo or ballast.</cd> --
<col><b>Parliament hinge</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a hinge with
so great a projection from the wall or frame as to allow a door or
shutter to swing back flat against the wall.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Long
Parliament</b></col>, <col><b>Rump Parliament</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See
under <er>Long</er>, and <er>Rump</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parliamental</ent><br/
<hw>Par`lia*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Parliamentary.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parliamentarian</ent><br/
<hw>Par`lia*men*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Parliament.</def> <rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parliamentarian</ent><br/
<hw>Par`lia*men*ta"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eng.
Hist.)</fld> <def>One who adhered to the Parliament, in opposition to
King Charles I.</def> <rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One versed in the rules and usages of Parliament or
similar deliberative assemblies; <as>as, an accomplished
<ex>parliamentarian</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parliamentarily</ent><br/
<hw>Par`lia*men"ta*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
parliamentary manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parliamentary</ent><br/
<hw>Par`lia*men"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parlementaire</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to Parliament; <as>as,
<ex>parliamentary</ex> authority</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Enacted or done by Parliament; <as>as, a
<ex>parliamentary</ex> act</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>According to the rules and usages of Parliament or
of deliberative bodies; <as>as, a <ex>parliamentary</ex> motion;
<ex>parliamentary</ex> order; <ex>parliamentary</ex>
procedure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parliamentary agent</b></col>, <cd>a person, usually a
solicitor, professionally employed by private parties to explain and
recommend claims, bills, etc., under consideration of Parliament.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Parliamentary train</b></col>, <cd>one of
the trains which, by act of Parliament, railway companies are required
to run for the conveyance of third-class passengers at a reduced
rate.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parlor</ent><br/
<hw>Par"lor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>parlour</ets>, <ets>parlur</ets>, F. <ets>parloir</ets>, LL.
<ets>parlatorium</ets>. See <er>Parley</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>parlour</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A room for business or
social conversation, for the reception of guests, etc.</def>
<specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The apartment in a
monastery or nunnery where the inmates are permitted to meet and
converse with each other, or with visitors and friends from
without.</def> <au>Piers Plowman.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In large
private houses, a sitting room for the family and for familiar guests,
-- a room for less formal uses than the drawing-room. Esp., in modern
times, the dining room of a house having few apartments, as a London
house, where the dining parlor is usually on the ground floor.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>Commonly, in the United States, a drawing-room, or the
room where visitors are received and entertained; a room in a private
house where people can sit and talk and relax, not usually the same as
the dining room.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ldquo/In England people who have a drawing-room no
longer call it a <xex>parlor</xex>, as they called it of old and till
recently.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Fitzed. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A room in an inn or club where visitors can be
received.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parlor car</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Palace car</cref>,
under <er>Car</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parlor match</ent><br/
<hw>Par"lor match`</hw>. <def>A friction match that contains little or
no sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parlour</ent><br/
<hw>parlour</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Same as
<er>parlor</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> living room, sitting room, front room,
parlor.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A room in an inn or club where visitors can be
received.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> parlor.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parlous</ent><br/
<hw>Par"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>perlous</ets>, a contr. fr. <ets>perilous</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Attended with peril; dangerous; <as>as, a
<ex>parlous</ex> cough</as>. </def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>parlous</xex> snuffing.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Venturesome; bold; mischievous; keen.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>parlous</xex> boy.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>parlous</xex> wit.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au>
-- <wordforms><wf>Par"lous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
-- <wf>Par"lous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parmelia</ent><br/
<hw>Parmelia</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The type genus of the
<fam>Parmeliaceae</fam>; a large genus of chiefly alpine foliaceous
lichens.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parmelia</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parmesan</ent><br/
<hw>Par`me*san"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parmesan</ets>, It. <ets>parmigiano</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to <city>Parma</city> in
<country>Italy</country>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Parmesan cheese.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parmesan cheese</b></col>, <cd>a kind of cheese of a rich
flavor, though from skimmed milk, made in Parma, Italy.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnassia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*nas"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of herbs growing in wet places, and
having white flowers; grass of Parnassus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnassian</ent><br/
<hw>Par*nas"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Parnassius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
Parnassus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnassian</ent><br/
<hw>Par*nas"sian</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parnassus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of butterflies belonging to the genus <gen>Parnassius</gen>.
They inhabit the mountains, both in the Old World and in
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnassian</ent><br/
<hw>Par*nas"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parnassien</ets>.]</ety> <def>One of a school of French poets of
the Second Empire (1852-70) who emphasized metrical form and made little
use of emotion as poetic material; -- so called from the name
(<etsep>Parnasse contemporain</etsep>) of the volume in which their
first poems were collected in 1866.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnassien</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`nas`si`en"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Parnassian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnassus</ent><br/
<hw>Par*nas"sus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Geog. & Gr. Myth.)</fld> <def>A mountain in
Greece, sacred to Apollo and the Muses, and famous for a temple of
Apollo and for the Castalian spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Grass of Parnassus</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Grass</er>, and <er>Parnassia</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To climb
Parnassus</b></col>, <cd>to write poetry.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnellism</ent><br/
<hw>Par"nell*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng.
Politics)</fld> <def>The policy or principles of the
Parnellites.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parnellite</ent><br/
<hw>Par"nell*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng.
Politics)</fld> <def>One of the adherents of <person>Charles Stewart
Parnell</person> (1846-91) in his advocacy of home rule for
Ireland.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroccipital</ent><br/
<hw>Par`oc*cip"i*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>occipital</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated near or beside the occipital condyle or the occipital
bone; paramastoid; -- applied especially to a process of the skull in
some animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochial</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>parochialis</ets>, from L. <ets>parochia</ets>. See
<er>Parish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a parish;
restricted to a parish; <as>as, <ex>parochial</ex> duties;
<ex>parochial</ex> schools</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Parochial</xex>
pastors.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Atterbury.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Limited; narrow; having or
characterized by narrow interests centered on oneself or one's local
community; narrow-minded; provincial; <as>as, <ex>parochial</ex>
views</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>parochial</xex> mind.<rdquo/ <au>W.
Black.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochialism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro"chi*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being parochial in form or nature; a system of management
peculiar to parishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochiality</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro`chi*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being parochial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir J.
Marriot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochialize</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro"chi*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render
parochial; to form into parishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochially</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro"chi*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a parochial manner; by
the parish, or by parishes.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Stillingfleet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro"chi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parochial</er>, <er>Parishioner</er>.]</ety> <def>Parochial.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Parochian</xex> churches.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parochian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ro"chi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>parochianus</ets>.]</ety> <def>A parishioner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ld. Burleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parodical</ent><br/
<ent>Parodic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*rod"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*rod"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>parodique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the character of
parody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Very paraphrastic, and sometimes <qex>parodical</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Warton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parodist</ent><br/
<hw>par"o*dist</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<osl/*d<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>parodiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who writes a parody;
one who parodies.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parody</ent><br/
<hw>par"o*dy</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"<osl/*d<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parodies</plw>
<pr>(p<acr/r"<osl/*d<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>parodia</ets>, Gr.
<grk>parw,di`a</grk>; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>'w,dh`</grk> a
song: cf. F. <ets>parodie</ets>. See <er>Para-</er>, and
<er>Ode</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A writing in which the language or sentiment of an
author is mimicked; especially, a kind of literary pleasantry, in which
what is written on one subject is altered, and applied to another by way
of burlesque; travesty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lively <qex>parody</qex> which he wrote . . . on Dryden's
<ldquo/Hind and Panther<rdquo/ was received with great applause.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A popular maxim, adage, or proverb.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parody</ent><br/
<hw>par"o*dy</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>parodied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>parodying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parodier</ets>.]</ety> <def>To write a parody upon; to
burlesque.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have translated, or rather <qex>parodied</qex>, a poem of
Horace.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroket</ent><br/
<hw>Par"o*ket`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Paroquet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parol</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rol"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Parole</er>,
the same word.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A word; an oral utterance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Oral declaration; word of mouth;
also, a writing not under seal.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parol</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rol"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Given or done by word of mouth;
oral; also, given by a writing not under seal; <as>as, <ex>parol</ex>
evidence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parol arrest</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an arrest in
pursuance of a verbal order from a magistrate.</cd> -- <col><b>Parol
contract</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>any contract not of record or
under seal, whether oral or written; a simple contract.</cd>
<rj><au>Chitty. Story.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parole</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*role"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parole</ets>. See <er>Parley</er>, and cf. <er>Parol</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A word; an oral utterance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Word of promise; word of honor; plighted
faith;</def> <specif>especially</specif> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>,
<def>promise, upon one's faith and honor, to fulfill stated conditions,
as not to bear arms against one's captors, to return to custody, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This man had forfeited his military <qex>parole</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A watchword given only to officers
of guards; -- distinguished from <contr>countersign</contr>, which is
given to all guards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Oral declaration. See 1st
<er>Parol</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The release of a prisoner from confinement prior to
the end of the original sentence, conditioned on good behavior and often
with other specific conditions, such as not to associate with known
criminals. Such early release is common where the sentence provides a
minimum and maximum term; <as>as, he was released on <ex>parole</ex>
after three years of his five-year sentence; he is out on
<ex>parole</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A document authorizing a parole{5}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parole</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*role"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See 2d <er>Parol</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parole</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*role"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paroled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paroling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Mil. and Penology)</fld>
<def>To set at liberty on parole; <as>as, to <ex>parole</ex>
prisoners</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parole board</ent><br/
<hw>pa*role" board`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A group of individuals with
authority to determine whether a prisoner will be granted parole{5} from
a particular prison.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paromology</ent><br/
<hw>Par`o*mol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. fr. <?/,
fr. <?/ to grant; <?/ by, near + <?/ to speak together, agree. See
<er>Homologous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A concession to an
adversary in order to strengthen one's own argument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronomasia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`o*no*ma"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to form a word by a slight change; <grk>para`</grk>
beside + <?/ to name, fr. <grk>'o`noma</grk> a name.]</ety>
<fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A play upon words; a figure by which the same
word is used in different senses, or words similar in sound are set in
opposition to each other, so as to give antithetical force to the
sentence; punning.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronomastical</ent><br/
<ent>Paronomastic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par`o*no*mas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Par`o*no*mas"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to paronomasia; consisting in a play upon words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronomasy</ent><br/
<hw>Par`o*nom"a*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>paronomasie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Paronomasia.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronychia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`o*nych"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <grk>parwnychi`a</grk>; <grk>para`</grk> beside + <grk>'o`nyx</grk>,
<grk>'o`nychos</grk>, a nail{1}.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
whitlow, or felon{3}.</def> <rj><au>Quincy.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronym</ent><br/
<hw>Par"o*nym</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A paronymous
word.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>paronyme</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronymous</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ron"y*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>parw`nymos</grk>; <grk>para`</grk> beside, near +
<grk>'o`noma</grk> a name.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the same
derivation; allied radically; conjugate; -- said of certain words, as
<examp>man</examp>, <examp>mankind</examp>, <examp>manhood</examp>,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a similar sound, but different orthography
and different meaning; -- said of certain words, as <examp>all</examp>
and <examp>awl</examp>; <examp>hair</examp> and <examp>hare</examp>,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paronymy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ron"y*my</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
paronymous; also, the use of paronymous words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroophoron</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`o*<oum/ph"o*ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/ (see <er>Para-</er>) + <?/ an egg + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to
bear.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A small mass of tubules near the
ovary in some animals, and corresponding with the parepididymis of the
male.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroquet</ent><br/
<hw>Par"o*quet`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>perroquet</ets>, or Sp. <ets>periquito</ets>; both prob. orig.
meaning, little Peter. See <er>Parrot</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Parrakeet</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>paroket</asp>, <asp>parroquet</asp>, and
<asp>perroquet</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Paroquet auk</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Paroquet
auklet</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small auk
(<spn>Cyclorrhynchus psittaculus</spn>) inhabiting the coast and islands
of Alaska. The upper parts are dark slate, under parts white, bill
orange red. Called also <altname>perroquet auk</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parorchis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ror"chis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Orchis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
part of the epididymis, or the corresponding part of the excretory duct
of the testicle, which is derived from the Wolffian body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parosteal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ros"te*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to parostosis; <as>as, <ex>parosteal</ex>
ossification</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parostosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`os*to"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Ostosis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>Ossification which takes place in purely fibrous tracts; the
formation of bone outside of the periosteum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parostotic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`os*tot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
parostosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parotic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parotid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>On the side of the
auditory capsule; near the external ear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parotic region</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
space around the ears.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parotid</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rot"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parotis</ets>, <ets>-idis</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/; <grk>para`</grk>
beside, near + <?/, <?/, the ear: cf. F. <ets>parotide</ets>. ]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated near the ear; -- applied
especially to the salivary gland near the ear. </def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or in the region of, the parotid
gland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parotid gland</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>one of
the salivary glands situated just in front of or below the ear. It is
the largest of the salivary glands in man, and its duct opens into the
interior of the mouth opposite the second molar of the upper
jaw.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parotid</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*rot"id</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The parotid
gland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parotitis</ent><br/
<hw>Par`o*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Parotid</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the parotid glands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Epidemic parotitis</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Infectious parotitis</b></col></mcol>, <cd>mumps.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parotoid</ent><br/
<hw>Par"o*toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Parot</ets>id
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Resembling the parotid
gland; -- applied especially to cutaneous glandular elevations above the
ear in many toads and frogs.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
parotoid gland.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parousia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*rou"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/. See <er>Parusia</er>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The nativity of our
Lord.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The last day.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parovarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par`o*va"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Para-</er>, and <er>Ovarium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
group of tubules, a remnant of the Wolffian body, often found near the
ovary or oviduct; the epoophoron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroxysm</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ox*ysm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>paroxysme</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to sharpen, irritate;
<grk>para`</grk> beside, beyond + <?/ to sharpen, from <?/ sharp.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The fit, attack, or exacerbation, of
a disease that occurs at intervals, or has decided remissions or
intermissions.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any sudden and violent emotion; spasmodic passion or
action; a convulsion; a fit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The returning <qex>paroxysms</qex> of diffidence and despair.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroxysmal</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ox*ys"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the nature of
a paroxysm; characterized or accompanied by paroxysms; <as>as, a
<ex>paroxysmal</ex> pain; <ex>paroxysmal</ex> temper.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`ox*ys"mal*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paroxytone</ent><br/
<hw>Par*ox"y*tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<pos>a.</pos> See <er>Para-</er>, and <er>Oxytone</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr.
Gram.)</fld> <def>A word having an acute accent on the penultimate
syllable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parquet</ent><br/
<hw>Par*quet"</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*k<amac/" or p<aum/r*k<ecr/t")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See <er>Parquetry</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A body of seats on the floor of a music hall or
theater nearest the orchestra; but commonly applied to the whole lower
floor of a theater, from the orchestra to the dress circle; the
pit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Parquetry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In various European public bourses, the railed-in
space within which the <ldquo/agents de change,<rdquo/ or privileged
brokers, conduct business; also, the business conducted by them; --
distinguished from the <contr>coulisse</contr>, or outside
market.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In most European countries, the branch of the
administrative government which is charged with the prevention,
investigation, and punishment of crime, representing the public and not
the individual injured.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parquetage</ent><br/
<hw>Par"quet*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parquetry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parquet circle</ent><br/
<hw>Parquet circle</hw>. <def>That part of the lower floor of a theater
with seats at the rear of the parquet and beneath the galleries; --
called also, esp. in U. S., <altname>orchestra circle</altname> or
<altname>parterre</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parqueted</ent><br/
<hw>Par"quet*ed</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*k<amac/d")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Formed in parquetry; inlaid with wood in small and differently
colored figures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One room <qex>parqueted</qex> with yew, which I liked well.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parquetry</ent><br/
<hw>Par"quet*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parqueterie</ets>, fr. <ets>parquet</ets> inlaid flooring, fr.
<ets>parquet</ets>, dim. of <ets>parc</ets> an inclosure. See
<er>Park</er>.]</ety> <def>A species of joinery or cabinet-work
consisting of an inlay of geometric or other patterns, generally of
different colors, -- used especially for floors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parquette</ent><br/
<hw>Par*quette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parquet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parr</ent><br/
<hw>Parr</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gael. & Ir.
<ets>bradan</ets> a salmon.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A young salmon in the stage when it has dark transverse bands; --
called also <altname>samlet</altname>, <altname>skegger</altname>, and
<altname>fingerling</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A young
leveret.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1045 --></p>

<p><ent>Parakeet</ent><br/
<ent>Parrakeet</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"ra*keet`</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<adot/*k<emac/t`)</pr>,
<hw>Par"a*keet`</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Paroquet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of small parrots having a graduated tail, which is frequently
very long; -- called also <altname>paroquet</altname> and
<altname>paraquet</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Many of the Asiatic and Australian species belong to the
genus <gen>Paleornis</gen>; others belong to <gen>Polytelis</gen>,
<gen>Platycercus</gen>, <gen>Psephotus</gen>, <gen>Euphema</gen>, and
allied genera. The American parrakeets mostly belong to the genus
<gen>Conurus</gen>, as the Carolina parrakeet (<spn>Conurus
Carolinensis</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrel</ent><br/
<ent>Parral</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par"rel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>appareil</ets>. See
<er>Apparel</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The rope or collar by which a yard or spar is held to the mast in
such a way that it may be hoisted or lowered at pleasure.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A chimney-piece.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parraqua</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*ra"qua</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A curassow of the genus <gen>Ortalida</gen>, allied to the
guan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrhesia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*rhe"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/; <grk>para`</grk> beside, beyond + <?/ a speaking.]</ety>
<fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>Boldness or freedom of speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parricidal</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ri*ci`dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parricidalis</ets>, <ets>parricidialis</ets>. See
<er>Parricide</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to parricide; guilty of
parricide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parricide</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ri*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>parricida</ets>; <ets>pater</ets> father + <ets>caedere</ets> to
kill. See <er>Father</er>, <er>Homicide</er>, and cf.
<er>Patricide</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Properly, one who murders one's own father; in a
wider sense, one who murders one's father or mother or any
ancestor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[L. <ets>parricidium</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or
crime of murdering one's own father or any ancestor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parricidious</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ri*cid"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Parricidal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrock</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rock</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>pearruc</ets>, <ets>pearroc</ets>. See <er>Park</er>.]</ety> <def>A
croft, or small field; a paddock.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrot</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rot</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob.
fr. F. <ets>Pierrot</ets>, dim. of <ets>Pierre</ets> Peter. F.
<ets>pierrot</ets> is also the name of the sparrow. Cf.
<er>Paroquet</er>, <er>Petrel</er>, <er>Petrify</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>In a general sense, any bird of the order
<ord>Psittaci</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of
<gen>Psittacus</gen>, <gen>Chrysotis</gen>, <gen>Pionus</gen>, and other
genera of the family <fam>Psittacid<ae/</fam>, as distinguished from the
parrakeets, macaws, and lories. They have a short rounded or even tail,
and often a naked space on the cheeks. The gray parrot, or jako
(<spn>Psittacus erithacus</spn>) of Africa (see <er>Jako</er>), and the
species of Amazon, or green, parrots (<gen>Chrysotis</gen>) of America,
are examples. Many species, as cage birds, readily learn to imitate
sounds, and to repeat words and phrases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Carolina parrot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
Carolina parrakeet. See <er>Parrakeet</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Night
parrot</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Owl parrot</b></col></mcol>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Kakapo</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Parrot
coal</b></col>, <cd>cannel coal; -- so called from the crackling and
chattering sound it makes in burning.</cd> <mark>[Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
-- <col><b>Parrot green</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Scheele's green</cref>, under <er>Green</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd>
-- <col><b>Parrot weed</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a suffrutescent
plant (<spn>Bocconia frutescens</spn>) of the Poppy family, native of
the warmer parts of America. It has very large, sinuate, pinnatifid
leaves, and small, panicled, apetalous flowers.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Parrot wrasse</b></col>, <col><b>Parrot
fish</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any fish of the genus
<gen>Scarus</gen>. One species (<spn>Scarus Cretensis</spn>), found in
the Mediterranean, is esteemed by epicures, and was highly prized by the
ancient Greeks and Romans.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrot</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To repeat by rote, without
understanding, as a parrot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrot</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rot</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To chatter like a
parrot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parroter</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rot*er</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/t*<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who simply repeats what he has heard.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>J. S. Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parrotfish</ent><br/
<hw>par"rot*fish`</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/t*f<icr/sh`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Any of several gaudy tropical fishes of the family
<fam>Scaridae</fam> having parrotlike beaks formed by fusion of
teeth.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pollyfish.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrotia</ent><br/
<hw>Parrotia</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus containing one
species, the irontree.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parrotia</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrotiopsis</ent><br/
<hw>Parrotiopsis</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of trees
containing only one species, a deciduous tree of Himalaya.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parrotiopsis</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parrotlike</ent><br/
<hw>par"rot*like`</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/t*l<imac/k`)</pr>,
<pos>adj.</pos> <def>Mechanically imitated or repeated without thought
or understanding; <as>as, a mere <ex>parrotlike</ex> word-calling
process; a voice quality sounding <ex>parrotlike</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrotry</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rot*ry</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/t*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Servile imitation or repetition.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<au>Coleridge.</au> <ldquo/The supine <xex>parrotry</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Fitzed. Hall.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parrot's-bill</ent><br/
<hw>Par"rot's-bill`</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ucr/ts*b<icr/l`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from the resemblance of its curved
superior petal to a parrot's bill.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
glory pea. See under <er>Glory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parry</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ry</hw> <pr>(p<acr/r"r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Parried</conjf>
<pr>(p<acr/r"r<ecr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Parrying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>par<eacute/</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>parer</ets>. See <er>Pare</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To ward off; to stop, or to turn aside; <as>as, to
<ex>parry</ex> a thrust, a blow, or anything that means or threatens
harm</as>.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vice <qex>parries</qex> wide<br/
The undreaded volley with a sword of straw.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To avoid; to shift or put off; to evade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The French government has <qex>parried</qex> the payment of our
claims.</q> <rj><qau>E. Everett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parry</ent><br/
<hw>par"ry</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To ward off, evade, or turn aside
something, as a blow, argument, etc.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parry</ent><br/
<hw>par"ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parries</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A warding off of a thrust or blow, as in
sword and bayonet exercises or in boxing; hence, figuratively, a
defensive movement in debate or other intellectual encounter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parse</ent><br/
<hw>parse</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rs)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>parsed</conjf> <pr>(p<aum/rst)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>parsing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>pars</ets>
a part; <ets>pars orationis</ets> a part of speech. See <er>Part</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>To resolve into its
elements, as a sentence, pointing out the several parts of speech, and
their relation to each other by government or agreement; to analyze and
describe grammatically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let him construe the letter into English, and <qex>parse</qex> it
over perfectly.</q> <rj><qau>Ascham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parsec</ent><br/
<hw>par"sec</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"s<ecr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>par</ets>allax <ets>sec</ets>ond. See <er>parallax second</er> and
<cref>annual parallax</cref>, under <er>parallax</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A unit of length used in describing
astronomical distances, equal to 3.26 light years, or 3.086 x
10<sup>13</sup> km. Abbreviated <abbr>pc</abbr>. The derived units of
kiloparsec (<abbr>kpc</abbr>, 1000 parsecs) and megaparsec
(<abbr>mpc</abbr>, one million parsecs) are often found in discussion of
astronomical distances.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsee</ent><br/
<hw>Par"see</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"s<emac/ <it>or</it>
p<aum/r*s<emac/")</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. & Per.
<ets>p<amac/rs<imac/</ets> a Persian, a follower of Zoroaster, a fire
worshiper. Cf. <er>Persian</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the adherents of the Zoroastrian or ancient
Persian religion, descended from Persian refugees settled in India, and
now found in western India; a fire worshiper; a Gheber.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Parsi.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The Iranian dialect of much of the religious
literature of the Parsees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parseeism</ent><br/
<hw>Par"see*ism</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"s<emac/*<icr/z'm <it>or</it>
p<aum/r*s<emac/"<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The religion
and customs of the Parsees.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Parsiism.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parser</ent><br/
<hw>pars"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
parses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsi</ent><br/
<hw>Parsi</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"s<emac/ <it>or</it> p<aum/r*s<emac/")</pr>
<pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Parsee</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Parsee.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsiism</ent><br/
<hw>Parsiism</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"s<emac/*<icr/z'm <it>or</it>
p<aum/r*s<emac/"<icr/z'm)</pr> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Same
as <er>Parseeism</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Parseeism.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsimonious</ent><br/
<hw>Par`si*mo"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>parcimonieux</ets>. See <er>Parsimony</er>.]</ety> <def>Exhibiting
parsimony; sparing in expenditure of money; frugal to excess; penurious;
niggardly; stingy.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Par`si*mo"ni*ous*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Par`si*mo"ni*ous*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A prodigal king is nearer a tyrant than a
<qex>parsimonious</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Extraordinary funds for one campaign may spare us the expense of
many years; whereas a long, <qex>parsimonious</qex> war will drain us of
more men and money.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Covetous; niggardly; miserly; penurious; close;
saving; mean; stingy; frugal. See <er>Avaricious</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parsimoniousness</ent><br/
<hw>parsimoniousness</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of
being parsimonious; extreme care in spending money; reluctance to spend
money unnecessarily.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> parsimony, thrift, penny-pinching.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An extreme stinginess.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> meanness, minginess, niggardliness, niggardness,
parsimony, tightfistedness.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsimony</ent><br/
<hw>Par"si*mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parsimonia</ets>, <ets>parcimonia</ets>; cf. <ets>parcere</ets> to
spare, <ets>parsus</ets> sparing: cf. F. <ets>parcimonie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Closeness or sparingness in the expenditure of money; -- generally
in a bad sense; excessive frugality; niggardliness.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Awful <qex>parsimony</qex> presided generally at the table.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Economy; frugality; illiberality; covetousness;
closeness; stinginess. See <er>Economy</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsley</ent><br/
<hw>Pars"ley</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rs"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>persely</ets>, <ets>persil</ets>, F. <ets>persil</ets>, L.
<ets>petroselinum</ets> rock parsley, Gr. <grk>petrose`linon</grk>;
<grk>pe`tros</grk> stone + <grk>se`linon</grk> parsley. Cf.
<er>Celery</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An aromatic umbelliferous
herb (<spn>Carum Petroselinum</spn>), having finely divided leaves which
are used in cookery and as a garnish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As she went to the garden for <qex>parsley</qex>, to stuff a
rabbit.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fool's parsley</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Fool</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Hedge parsley</b></col>,
<col><b>Milk parsley</b></col>, <col><b>Stone parsley</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>names given to various weeds of similar appearance to the
parsley.</cd> -- <col><b>Parsley fern</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
small fern with leaves resembling parsley (<spn>Cryptogramme
crispa</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Parsley piert</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small herb (<spn>Alchemilla arvensis</spn>)
formerly used as a remedy for calculus.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsnip</ent><br/
<hw>Pars"nip</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rs"n<icr/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>parsnepe</ets>, from a French form, fr. L. <ets>pastinaca</ets>;
cf. <ets>pastinare</ets> to dig up, <ets>pastinum</ets> a kind of
dibble; cf. OF. <ets>pastenade</ets>, <ets>pastenaque</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The aromatic and edible spindle-shaped root of
the cultivated form of the <spn>Pastinaca sativa</spn>, a biennial
umbelliferous plant which is very poisonous in its wild state; also, the
plant itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cow parsnip</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Cow parsnip</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Meadow parsnip</b></col>, <cd>the European cow parsnip.</cd>
-- <col><b>Poison parsnip</b></col>, <cd>the wild stock of the
parsnip.</cd> -- <col><b>Water parsnip</b></col>, <cd>any plant of the
umbelliferous genus <gen>Sium</gen>, the species of which are
poisonous.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parson</ent><br/
<hw>Par"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>persone</ets> person, parson, OF. <ets>persone</ets>, F.
<ets>personne</ets> person, LL. <ets>persona</ets> (sc.
<ets>ecclesiae</ets>), fr. L. <ets>persona</ets> a person. See
<er>Person</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Eccl. Law)</fld> <def>A person who represents
a parish in its ecclesiastical and corporate capacities; hence, the
rector or incumbent of a parochial church, who has full possession of
all the rights thereof, with the cure of souls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any clergyman having ecclesiastical preferment; one
who is in orders, or is licensed to preach; a preacher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hears the <qex>parson</qex> pray and preach.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parson bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a New
Zealand bird (<spn>Prosthemadera Nov<ae/seelandi<ae/</spn>) remarkable
for its powers of mimicry and its ability to articulate words. Its color
is glossy black, with a curious tuft of long, curly, white feathers on
each side of the throat. It is often kept as a cage bird.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsonage</ent><br/
<hw>Par"son*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eng.
Eccl. Law)</fld> <def>A certain portion of lands, tithes, and offerings,
for the maintenance of the parson of a parish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The glebe and house, or the house only, owned by a
parish or ecclesiastical society, and appropriated to the maintenance or
use of the incumbent or settled pastor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Money paid for the support of a parson.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What have I been paying stipend and teind, <qex>parsonage</qex>
and vicarage, for?</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsoned</ent><br/
<hw>Par"soned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with a
parson.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsonical</ent><br/
<ent>Parsonic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par*son"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par*son"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a parson;
clerical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vainglory glowed in his <qex>parsonic</qex> heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Colman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Par*son"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parsonish</ent><br/
<hw>Par"son*ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Appropriate to, or
like, a parson; -- used in disparagement.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Part</ent><br/
<hw>Part</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>part</ets>, L. <ets>pars</ets>, gen. <ets>partis</ets>; cf.
<ets>parere</ets> to bring forth, produce. Cf. <er>Parent</er>,
<er>Depart</er>, <er>Parcel</er>, <er>Partner</er>, <er>Party</er>,
<er>Portion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the portions, equal or
unequal, into which anything is divided, or regarded as divided;
something less than a <contr>whole</contr>; a number, quantity, mass, or
the like, regarded as going to make up, with others, a larger number,
quantity, mass, etc., whether actually separate or not; a piece; a
fragment; a fraction; a division; a member; a constituent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And kept back <qex>part</qex> of the price, . . . and brought a
certain <qex>part</qex> and laid it at the apostles'feet.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts v. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our ideas of extension and number -- do they not contain a secret
relation of the <qex>parts</qex> ?</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a <qex>part</qex> of all that I have met.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence, specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>An equal constituent portion; one of several or many like
quantities, numbers, etc., into which anything is divided, or of which
it is composed; proportional division or ingredient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An homer is the tenth <qex>part</qex> of an ephah.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xvi. 36.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thought which, quartered, hath but one <qex>part</qex>
wisdom,<br/
And ever three <qex>parts</qex> coward.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A constituent portion of a living or spiritual
whole; a member; an organ; an essential element.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the <qex>parts</qex> were formed . . . into one harmonious
body.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pulse, the glow of every <qex>part</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>A constituent of character or capacity; quality;
faculty; talent; -- usually in the plural with a collective sense.</def>
<ldquo/Men of considerable <xex>parts</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Burke.</au>
<ldquo/Great quickness of <xex>parts</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which maintained so politic a state of evil, that they will not
admit any good <qex>part</qex> to intermingle with them.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>Quarter; region; district; -- usually in the
plural.</def> <ldquo/The uttermost <xex>part</xex> of the heaven.<rdquo/
<au>Neh. i. 9.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>parts</qex> resound with tumults, plaints, and fears.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Such portion of any quantity, as
when taken a certain number of times, will exactly make that quantity;
<as>as, 3 is a <ex>part</ex> of 12;</as> -- the opposite of
<contr>multiple</contr>. Also, a line or other element of a geometrical
figure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which belongs to one, or which is assumed by
one, or which falls to one, in a division or apportionment; share;
portion; lot; interest; concern; duty; office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have no <qex>part</qex> in David.</q> <rj><qau>2 Sam. xx.
1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Accuse not Nature! she hath done her <qex>part</qex>;<br/
Do thou but thine.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let me bear<br/
My <qex>part</qex> of danger with an equal share.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence, specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>One of the opposing parties or sides in a conflict or a
controversy; a faction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For he that is not against us is on our <qex>part</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mark ix. 40.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make whole kingdoms take her brother's <qex>part</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A particular character in a drama or a play; an
assumed personification; also, the language, actions, and influence of a
character or an actor in a play; or, figuratively, in real life; <as>as,
to play the <ex>part</ex> of Macbeth</as>. See <cref>To act a
part</cref>, under <er>Act</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>part</qex><br/
Was aptly fitted and naturally performed.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was a brute <qex>part</qex> of him to kill so capital a
calf.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Honor and shame from no condition rise;<br/
Act well your <qex>part</qex>, there all the honor lies.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>One of the different melodies of
a concerted composition, which heard in union compose its harmony; also,
the music for each voice or instrument; <as>as, the treble, tenor, or
bass <ex>part</ex>; the violin <ex>part</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>For my part</b></col>, <cd>so far as concerns me; for my
share.</cd> -- <col><b>For the most part</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Most</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>In good part</b></col>,
<cd>as well done; favorably; acceptably; in a friendly manner; <as>as,
to take an act <ex>in good part</ex></as>.</cd> <au>Hooker.</au> --
<col><b>In ill part</b></col>, <cd>unfavorably; with displeasure.</cd>
-- <col><b>In part</b></col>, <cd>in some degree; partly.</cd> --
<col><b>Part and parcel</b></col>, <cd>an essential or constituent
portion; -- a reduplicative phrase. Cf. <cref>might and main</cref>,
<cref>kith and kin</cref>, etc.</cd> <ldquo/She was . . . <xex>part and
parcel</xex> of the race and place.<rdquo/ <au>Howitt.</au> --
<col><b>Part of speech</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>a sort or class
of words of a particular character; <as>thus, the noun is a <ex>part of
speech</ex> denoting the name of a thing; the verb is a <ex>part of
speech</ex> which asserts something of the subject of a
sentence</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Part owner</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>one of several owners or tenants in common. See <cref>Joint
tenant</cref>, under <er>Joint</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Part
singing</b></col>, <cd>singing in which two or more of the harmonic
parts are taken.</cd> -- <col><b>Part song</b></col>, <cd>a song in two
or more (commonly four) distinct vocal parts.</cd> <ldquo/A <xex>part
song</xex> differs from a madrigal in its exclusion of contrapuntual
devices; from a glee, in its being sung by many voices, instead of by
one only, to each part.<rdquo/ <au>Stainer & Barrett.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Portion; section; division; fraction; fragment;
piece; share; constituent. See <er>Portion</er>, and
<er>Section</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Part</ent><br/
<hw>Part</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Parted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Parting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>partir</ets>, L.
<ets>partire</ets>, <ets>partiri</ets>, p. p. <ets>partitus</ets>, fr.
<ets>pars</ets>, gen. <ets>partis</ets>, a part. See <er>Part</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To divide; to separate into distinct parts; to break
into two or more parts or pieces; to sever.</def> <ldquo/Thou shalt
<xex>part</xex> it in pieces.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lev. ii. 6.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There, [celestial love] <qex>parted</qex> into rainbow hues.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To divide into shares; to divide and distribute; to
allot; to apportion; to share.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>part</qex> his throne, and share his heaven with thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>parted</qex> my raiment among them.</q> <rj><qau>John
xix. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To separate or disunite; to cause to go apart; to
remove from contact or contiguity; to sunder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord do so to me, and more also, if aught but death
<qex>part</qex> thee and me.</q> <rj><qau>Ruth i. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While he blessed them, he was <qex>parted</qex> from them, and
carried up into heaven.</q> <rj><qau>Luke xxiv. 51.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The narrow seas that <qex>part</qex><br/
The French and English.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To hold apart; to stand
between; to intervene betwixt, as combatants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stumbling night did <qex>part</qex> our weary powers.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To separate by a process of extraction, elimination,
or secretion; <as>as, to <ex>part</ex> gold from silver</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The liver minds his own affair, . . .<br/
And <qex>parts</qex> and strains the vital juices.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To leave; to quit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Since presently your souls must <qex>part</qex> your bodies.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To separate (a collection of objects) into smaller
collections; <as>as, to <ex>part</ex> one's hair in the
middle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To part a cable</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
break it.</cd> -- <col><b>To part company</b></col>, <cd>to separate, as
travelers or companions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Part</ent><br/
<hw>Part</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be broken or divided
into parts or pieces; to break; to become separated; to go asunder;
<as>as, rope <ex>parts</ex>; his hair <ex>parts</ex> in the
middle.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To go away; to depart; to take leave; to quit each
other; hence, to die; -- often with <xex>from</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He wrung Bassanio's hand, and so they <qex>parted</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He owned that he had <qex>parted</qex> from the duke only a few
hours before.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His precious bag, which he would by no means <qex>part</qex>
from.</q> <rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To perform an act of parting; to relinquish a
connection of any kind; -- followed by <ptcl>with</ptcl> or
<ptcl>from</ptcl>; <as>as, to <ex>part</ex> with one's
money</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Celia, for thy sake, I <qex>part</qex><br/
With all that grew so near my heart.</q> <rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Powerful hands . . . will not <qex>part</qex><br/
Easily from possession won with arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was strange to him that a father should feel no tenderness at
<qex>parting</qex> with an only son.</q> <rj><qau>A.
Trollope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To have a part or share; to partake.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/They shall <xex>part</xex> alike.<rdquo/
<rj><au>1 Sam. xxx. 24.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Part</ent><br/
<hw>Part</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Partly; in a measure.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partable</ent><br/
<hw>Part"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Partible</er>.</def> <rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partage</ent><br/
<hw>Part"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Part</er>, <pos>v.</pos> & <pos>n.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Division; the act of dividing or sharing.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Part; portion; share.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partake</ent><br/
<hw>Par*take"</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<amac/k")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Partook</conjf>
<pr>(p<aum/r*t<oocr/k")</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Partaken</conjf>
<pr>(p<aum/r*t<amac/k"'n)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Partaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[<ets>Part</ets> +
<ets>take</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To take a part, portion, lot, or share, in common
with others; to have a share or part; to participate; to share; <as>as,
to <ex>partake</ex> of a feast with others</as>.</def> <ldquo/Brutes
<xex>partake</xex> in this faculty.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When I against myself with thee <qex>partake</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have something of the properties, character, or
office; -- usually followed by <ptcl>of</ptcl>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The attorney of the Duchy of Lancaster <qex>partakes</qex> partly
of a judge, and partly of an attorney-general.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1046 --></p>

<p><ent>partake</ent><br/
<hw>par*take"</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<amac/k")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To partake of; to have a part or share in; to
share.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let every one <qex>partake</qex> the general joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To admit to a share; to cause to participate; to
give a part to.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To distribute; to communicate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partaker</ent><br/
<hw>par*tak"er</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<amac/k"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who partakes; a sharer; a participator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Partakers</qex> of their spiritual things.</q> <rj><qau>Rom.
xv. 27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wish me <qex>partaker</qex> in my happiness.</q>
<rj><qau>Shark.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An accomplice; an associate; a partner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Partakers</qex> wish them in the blood of the prophets.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xxiii. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partan</ent><br/
<hw>par"tan</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"t<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Ir.
& Gael. <ets>partan</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An edible
British crab.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parted</ent><br/
<hw>part"ed</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt"<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Separated; divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Endowed with parts or abilities.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Cleft so that the divisions reach
nearly, but not quite, to the midrib, or the base of the blade; -- said
of a leaf, and used chiefly in composition; <as>as,
three-<ex>parted</ex>, five-<ex>parted</ex>, etc.</as></def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parter</ent><br/
<hw>Part"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or which,
parts or separates.</def> <rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parterre</ent><br/
<hw>Par*terre"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>par</ets> on, by (L. <ets>per</ets>) + <ets>terre</ets> earth,
ground, L. <ets>terra</ets>. See <er>Terrace</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>An ornamental and diversified arrangement of
beds or plots, in which flowers are cultivated, with intervening spaces
of gravel or turf for walking on.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The pit of a theater; the parquet.</def>
<mark>[France]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partheniad</ent><br/
<hw>Par*the"ni*ad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parthenic</er>.]</ety> <def>A poem in honor of a virgin.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenic</ent><br/
<hw>Par*then"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>parqeniko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>parqe`nos</grk> a maid, virgin.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Spartan Partheni<ae/, or sons of unmarried
women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenocissus</ent><br/
<hw>Parthenocissus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of woody vines
having disklike tips on the tendrils.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parthenocissus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parthenogenesis</ent><br/
<hw>par`the*no*gen"e*sis</hw>
<pr>(p<aum/r`th<esl/*n<osl/*j<ecr/n"<esl/*s<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>parqe`nos</grk> a virgin + E. <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The production of new individuals
from virgin females by means of ova which have the power of developing
without the intervention of the male element; the production, without
fertilization, of cells capable of germination. It is one of the
phenomena of alternate generation. Cf. <er>Heterogamy</er>, and
<er>Metagenesis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The production of seed without
fertilization, believed to occur through the nonsexual formation of an
embryo extraneous to the embryonic vesicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenogenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Par`the*no*ge*net"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or produced by, parthenogenesis; <as>as,
<ex>parthenogenetic</ex> forms</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Par`the*no*ge*net"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenogenitive</ent><br/
<hw>Par`the*no*gen"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Parthenogenetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenogeny</ent><br/
<hw>Par`the*nog"e*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Parthenogenesis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenon</ent><br/
<hw>Par"the*non</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"th<esl/*n<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>prop.
n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>Parqenw`n</grk>, fr.<grk>parqe`nos</grk>
a virgin, i. e., Athena, the Greek goddess called also Pallas.]</ety>
<def>A celebrated marble temple of <persfn>Athena</persfn>, on the
Acropolis at <city>Athens</city>. It was of the pure Doric order, and
has had an important influence on art.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthenope</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*then"o*pe</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*th<ecr/n"<osl/*p<emac/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the name of a Siren, fr. Gr.
<grk>Parqeno`ph</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Myth.)</fld>
<def>One of the Sirens, who threw herself into the sea, in despair at
not being able to beguile <persfn>Ulysses</persfn> by her
songs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the asteroids between Mars and Jupiter,
discovered by <person>M. de Gasparis</person> in 1850.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parthian</ent><br/
<hw>Par"thi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
ancient Parthia, in Asia.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native of
Parthia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parthian arrow</b></col>, <cd>an arrow discharged at an
enemy when retreating from him, as was the custom of the ancient
Parthians; hence, a parting shot.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partial</ent><br/
<hw>par"tial</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"sh<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. LL. <ets>partials</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pars</ets>, gen.
<ets>partis</ets>, a part; cf. (for sense 1) F. <ets>partiel</ets>. See
<er>Part</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of, pertaining to,
or affecting, a part only; not general or universal; not total or
entire; <as>as, a <ex>partial</ex> eclipse of the moon</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Partial</xex> dissolutions of the earth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>T.
Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclined to favor one party in a cause, or one side
of a question, more then the other; biased; not indifferent; <as>as, a
judge should not be <ex>partial</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye have been <qex>partial</qex> in the law.</q> <rj><qau>Mal. ii.
9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having a predilection for; inclined to favor
unreasonably; foolishly fond.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>partial</xex>
parent.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not <qex>partial</qex> to an ostentatious display.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to a subordinate
portion; <as>as, a compound umbel is made up of a several
<ex>partial</ex> umbels; a leaflet is often supported by a
<ex>partial</ex> petiole.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Partial differentials</b></col>, <col><b>Partial
differential coefficients</b></col>, <col><b>Partial
differentiation</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. (of a function of two or more
variables), the differentials, differential coefficients,
differentiation etc., of the function, upon the hypothesis that some of
the variables are for the time constant.</cd> -- <col><b>Partial
fractions</b></col> <fld>(Alg.)</fld>, <cd>fractions whose sum equals a
given fraction.</cd> -- <col><b>Partial tones</b></col>
<fld>(Music)</fld>, <cd>the simple tones which in combination form an
ordinary tone; the overtones, or harmonics, which, blending with a
fundamental tone, cause its special quality of sound, or
<xex>timbre</xex>, or tone color. See, also,
<er>Tone</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partialism</ent><br/
<hw>Par"tial*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Partiality;</def>
<specif>specifically</specif> <fld>(Theol.)</fld>, <def>the doctrine of
the Partialists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partialist</ent><br/
<hw>Par"tial*ist</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is
partial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>One who holds that the atonement
was made only for a part of mankind, that is, for the elect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partiality</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ti*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>partialit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being partial; inclination to favor one party, or one side of a
question, more than the other; undue bias of mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A predilection or inclination to one thing rather
than to others; special taste or liking; <as>as, a <ex>partiality</ex>
for poetry or painting</as>.</def> <rj><au>Roget.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partialize</ent><br/
<hw>Par"tial*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To make
or be partial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partially</ent><br/
<hw>Par"tial*ly</hw> <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In part; not
totally; <as>as, <ex>partially</ex> true; the sun <ex>partially</ex>
eclipsed.</as></def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a partial manner; with undue bias of mind; with
unjust favor or dislike; <as>as, to judge <ex>partially</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partibility</ent><br/
<hw>Part`i*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Partible</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being partible;
divisibility; separability; <as>as, the <ex>partibility</ex> of an
inherttance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partible</ent><br/
<hw>Part"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>partibilis</ets>, fr. <ets>partire</ets> to part, divide, fr. L.
<ets>pars</ets>: cf. F. <ets>partible</ets>. See <er>Part</er>.]</ety>
<def>Admitting of being parted; divisible; separable; susceptible of
severance or partition; <as>as, an estate of inheritance may be
<ex>partible</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/Make the molds
<xex>partible</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participable</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pa*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being participated or shared.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participant</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>participans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>participare</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>participant</ets>. See <er>Participate</er>.]</ety> <def>Sharing;
participating; having a share or part.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participant</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A participator; a
partaker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Participants</qex> in their . . . mysterious rites.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participantly</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a participant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participate</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>participatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>participare</ets> to participate;
<ets>pars</ets>, <ets>partis</ets>, part + <ets>capere</ets> to take.
See <er>Part</er>, and <er>Capacious</er>.]</ety> <def>Acting in common;
participating.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participate</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Participated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Participating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To have a share in common
with others; to take a part; to play a role; to partake; -- followed by
<ptcl>in</ptcl>, formerly by <ptcl>of</ptcl>; <as>as, to
<ex>participate</ex> in a debate; to <ex>participate</ex> in a
discussion</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So would he <qex>participate</qex> of their wants.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mine may come when men<br/
With angels may <qex>participate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participate</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To partake
of; to share in; to receive a part of.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fit to <qex>participate</qex> all rational delight.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To impart, or give, or share of.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>participating</ent><br/
<hw>participating</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Taking part in an activity;
<as>as, the <ex>participating</ex> organizations</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> active.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participation</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic`i*pa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>participation</ets>, L. <ets>participatio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or state of participating, or sharing in common with
others; <as>as, a <ex>participation</ex> in joy or
sorrows</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These deities are so by <qex>participation</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Stillingfleet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What an honor, that God should admit us into such a blessed
<qex>participation</qex> of himself!</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Distribution; division into shares.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Community; fellowship; association.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participative</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pa*tive</hw>
<pr>(p<aum/r*t<icr/s"<icr/*p<asl/*t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>participatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
participating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participator</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"i*pa`tor</hw>
<pr>(p<aum/r*t<icr/s"<icr/*p<amac/`t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>One who participates, or shares with another; a
partaker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>participatory</ent><br/
<hw>par*tic"i*pa*tor`y</hw>
<pr>(p<aum/r*t<icr/s"<icr/*p<ucr/*t<ocir/r`<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adj.</pos>
<def>Affording the opportunity for individual participation; <as>as,
<ex>participatory</ex> democracy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participial</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ti*cip"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>participialis</ets>: cf. E. <ets>participal</ets>. See
<er>Participle</er>.]</ety> <def>Having, or partaking of, the nature and
use of a participle; formed from a participle; <as>as, a
<ex>participial</ex> noun</as>.</def> <rj><au>Lowth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participial</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ti*cip"i*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A participial
word.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participialize</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ti*cip"i*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Participialized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Participializing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To form into, or put in
the form of, a participle.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participially</ent><br/
<hw>Par`ti*cip"i*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the sense or manner
of a participle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Participle</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*ci*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>participe</ets>, L. <ets>participium</ets>, fr.
<ets>particeps</ets> sharing, participant; <ets>pars</ets>, gen.
<ets>partis</ets>, a part + <ets>capere</ets> to take. See
<er>Participate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A part
of speech partaking of the nature of both verb and adjective; a form of
a verb, or verbal adjective, modifying a noun, but taking the adjuncts
of the verb from which it is derived. In the sentences: a letter is
<examp>written</examp>; <examp>being</examp> asleep he did not hear;
<examp>exhausted</examp> by toil he will sleep soundly, --
<examp>written</examp>, <examp>being</examp>, and
<examp>exhausted</examp>are <ex>participles</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By a <qex>participle</qex>, [I understand] a verb in an adjectival
aspect.</q> <rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Present participles</xex>, called also
<xex>imperfect</xex>, or <xex>incomplete</xex>, <xex>participles</xex>,
end in <xex>-ing</xex>. <xex>Past participles</xex>, called also
<xex>perfect</xex>, or <xex>complete</xex>, <xex>participles</xex>, for
the most part end in <xex>-ed</xex>, <xex>-d</xex>, <xex>-t</xex>,
<xex>-en</xex>, or <xex>-n</xex>. A participle when used merely as an
attribute of a noun, without reference to time, is called an
<xex>adjective</xex>, or a <xex>participial adjective</xex>; as, a
<xex>written</xex> constitution; a <xex>rolling</xex> stone; the
<xex>exhausted</xex> army. The verbal noun in <xex>-ing</xex> has the
form of the present participle. See <cref>Verbal noun</cref>, under
<er>Verbal</er>, <pos>a.</pos></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything that partakes of the nature of different
things.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>participles</qex> or confines between plants and living
creatures.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particle</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>particula</ets>, dim. of <ets>pars</ets>, gen <ets>partis</ets>, a
part: cf. F. <ets>particule</ets>. See <er>Part</er>, and cf.
<er>Parcel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A minute part or portion of
matter; a morsel; a little bit; an atom; a jot; <as>as, a
<ex>particle</ex> of sand, of wood, of dust</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The small size of atoms which unite<br/
To make the smallest <qex>particle</qex> of light.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackmore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any very small portion or part; the smallest
portion; <as>as, he has not a <ex>particle</ex> of patriotism or
virtue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The houses had not given their commissioners authority in the
least <qex>particle</qex> to recede.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A crumb or
little piece of consecrated host.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The smaller
hosts distributed in the communion of the laity.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Fitzpatrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A subordinate word that is never
inflected (a preposition, conjunction, interjection); or a word that can
not be used except in compositions; <as>as, <ex>ward</ex> in
back<ex>ward</ex>, <ex>ly</ex> in love<ex>ly</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An <er>elementary
particle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particle accelerator</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*cle ac*cel"er*a*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A large and expensive scientific instrument
used by physicists to accelerate elementary particles (such as protons
or electrons) to speeds near that of light, for the purpose of
investigating the fundamental properties of matter; sometimes also
called an <altname>atom smasher</altname>, since the particles thus
accelerated are often directed at targets of atoms which are fragmented
by the impact into their more fundamental component particles.</def>
<note>The particles generated by impact of a beam in an accelerator on
its target are detected by various types of detecting apparatus, and
procedures are required to sort and identify the many particles created.
The fundamental particles generated by impacts in a particle accelerator
are often those not actually present inside atoms; and in certain types
of particle accelerator, such as the <stype>colliding beam
accelerator</stype>, the impact which generates energetic particles is
with other fundamental particles, and not with atoms.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particle physics</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*cle phys`ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That
branch of physics which investigates the nature of matter, and in
particular the properties and behavior of the elementary particles
(fundamental particles), of which matter is composed. Included in this
field is the more specialized branch of <er>high-energy
physics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parti-color</ent><br/
<hw>parti-color</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make motley or
parti-colored.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> motley.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parti-colored</ent><br/
<hw>par"ti-col`ored</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having sections or patches
colored differently and usually brightly. Same as
<er>Party-colored</er>.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>parti-coloured</asp>.]</altsp><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> motley, multicolor, culticolour, multicolored,
multicoloured, painted, piebald, pied, varicolored,
varicoloured.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particular</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>particuler</ets>, F. <ets>particulier</ets>, L.
<ets>particularis</ets>. See <er>Particle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Relating to a part or portion of anything; concerning a part
separated from the whole or from others of the class; separate; sole;
single; individual; specific; <as>as, the <ex>particular</ex> stars of a
constellation</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Make] each <qex>particular</qex> hair to stand an end,<br/
Like quills upon the fretful porpentine.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Seken in every halk and every herne<br/
<qex>Particular</qex> sciences for to lerne.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a single person, class, or
thing; belonging to one only; not general; not common; hence, personal;
peculiar; singular.</def> <ldquo/Thine own <xex>particular</xex>
wrongs.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wheresoever one plant draweth such a <qex>particular</qex> juice
out of the earth.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Separate or distinct by reason of superiority;
distinguished; important; noteworthy; unusual; special; <as>as, he
brought no <ex>particular</ex> news; she was the <ex>particular</ex>
belle of the party.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Concerned with, or attentive to, details; minute;
circumstantial; precise; <as>as, a full and <ex>particular</ex> account
of an accident</as>; hence, nice; fastidious; <as>as, a man
<ex>particular</ex> in his dress</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Containing a part
only; limited; <as>as, a <ex>particular</ex> estate, or one precedent to
an estate in remainder</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Holding a
particular estate; <as>as, a <ex>particular</ex> tenant</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>Forming a part of a genus;
relatively limited in extension; affirmed or denied of a part of a
subject; <as>as, a <ex>particular</ex> proposition; -- opposed to
<contr>universal</contr>: e. g. (<ex>particular</ex> affirmative) Some
men are wise; (<ex>particular</ex> negative) Some men are not
wise.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Particular average</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Average</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Particular Baptist</b></col>, <cd>one
of a branch of the Baptist denomination the members of which hold the
doctrine of a particular or individual election and reprobation.</cd> --
<col><b>Particular lien</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a lien, or a
right to retain a thing, for some charge or claim growing out of, or
connected with, that particular thing.</cd> -- <col><b>Particular
redemption</b></col>, <cd>the doctrine that the purpose, act, and
provisions of redemption are restricted to a limited number of the human
race. See <er>Calvinism</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Minute; individual; respective; appropriate;
peculiar; especial; exact; specific; precise; critical; circumstantial.
See <er>Minute</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particular</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
separate or distinct member of a class, or part of a whole; an
individual fact, point, circumstance, detail, or item, which may be
considered separately; <as>as, the <ex>particulars</ex> of a
story</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Particulars</qex> which it is not lawful for me to
reveal.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is the greatest interest of <qex>particulars</qex> to advance
the good of the community.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Special or personal peculiarity, trait, or
character; individuality; interest, etc.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For his <qex>particular</qex> I'll receive him gladly.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the <qex>particulars</qex> of each person be considered.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Temporal blessings, whether such as concern the public . . . or
such as concern our <qex>particular</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Whole Duty of
Man.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One of the details or items of
grounds of claim; -- usually in the <pluf>pl.</pluf>; also, a bill of
particulars; a minute account; <as>as, a <ex>particular</ex> of
premises</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The reader has a <qex>particular</qex> of the books wherein this
law was written.</q> <rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bill of particulars</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Bill</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>In particular</b></col>, <cd>specially;
specifically; peculiarly; particularly; especially.</cd> <ldquo/This,
<xex>in particular</xex>, happens to the lungs.<rdquo/
<au>Blackmore.</au> -- <col><b>To go into particulars</b></col>, <cd>to
relate or describe in detail or minutely.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>particularise</ent><br/
<hw>particularise</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>particularize</er>.</def> <mark>[Chiefly Brit.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>particularized</ent><br/
<ent>particularised</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>particularised</hw> <hw>particularized</hw></mhw>
<pos>adj.</pos> <def>Stated or described in detail.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> circumstantial, detailed.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularism</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>particularisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A minute description;
a detailed statement.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine of particular
election.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(German Politics)</fld> <def>Devotion to the
interests of one's own kingdom or province rather than to those of the
empire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularist</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>particulariste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who holds to
particularism.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Par*tic`u*lar*is"tic</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularity</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic`u*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Particularities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>particularit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The state or quality of being particular; distinctiveness;
circumstantiality; minuteness in detail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is particular</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Peculiar quality; individual characteristic; peculiarity.</def>
<ldquo/An old heathen altar with this <xex>particularity</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Addison.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Special circumstance; minute detail;
particular.</def> <ldquo/Even descending to
<xex>particularities</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Sir P. Sidney.</au> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>Something of special or private concern or interest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the general trumpet blow his blast,<br/
<qex>Particularities</qex> and petty sounds<br/
To cease!</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularization</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic`u*lar*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
act of particularizing.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularize</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Particularized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Particularizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>particulariser</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To give as a particular, or as the particulars; to mention
particularly; to give the particulars of; to enumerate or specify in
detail.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>particularise</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He not only boasts of his parentage as an Israelite, but
<qex>particularizes</qex> his descent from Benjamin.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularize</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To mention or attend
to particulars; to give minute details; to be circumstantial; <as>as, to
<ex>particularize</ex> in a narrative</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularly</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
particular manner; expressly; with a specific reference or interest; in
particular; distinctly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In an especial manner; especially; in a high degree;
<as>as, a <ex>particularly</ex> fortunate man; a <ex>particularly</ex>
bad failure.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The exact propriety of <person>Virgil I</person>
<qex>particularly</qex> regarded as a great part of his character.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particularment</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*lar*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
particular; a detail.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particulate</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*late</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<icr/k"<usl/*l<amac/t)</pr>,
<pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Particle</er>.]</ety> <def>To
particularize.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Particulate</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tic"u*late</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<icr/k"<usl/*l<asl/t)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the form of a particle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Referring to, or produced by, particles, such as
dust, minute germs, etc.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The smallpox is a <qex>particulate</qex> disease.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Composed of particles; finely divided.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>particulate</ent><br/
<hw>par*tic"u*late</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<icr/k"<usl/*l<asl/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Matter composed of particles; -- often used in the
<pluf>pl.</pluf>; <as>as, the quantity of <ex>particulates</ex> in auto
exhaust is strictly regulated</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parting</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ting</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Part</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Serving to part;
dividing; separating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Given when departing; <as>as, a <ex>parting</ex>
shot; a <ex>parting</ex> salute.</as></def> <ldquo/Give him that
<xex>parting</xex> kiss.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Departing.</def> <ldquo/Speed the <xex>parting</xex>
guest.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Admitting of being parted; partible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Parting fellow</b></col>, <cd>a partner.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>Parting
pulley</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Pulley</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Parting
sand</b></col> <fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>dry, nonadhesive sand,
sprinkled upon the partings of a mold to facilitate the separation.</cd>
-- <col><b>Parting strip</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>in a sash
window, one of the thin strips of wood let into the pulley stile to keep
the sashes apart; also, the thin piece inserted in the window box to
separate the weights.</cd> -- <col><b>Parting tool</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a thin tool, used in turning or planing, for
cutting a piece in two.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1047 --></p>

<p><ent>Parting</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ting</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of parting or dividing; the state of being parted;
division; separation.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>parting</xex> of the
way.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ezek. xxi. 21.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A separation; a leave-taking.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And there were sudden <qex>partings</qex>, such as press<br/
The life from out young hearts.</q> <rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A surface or line of separation where a division
occurs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>The surface of the sand of one
section of a mold where it meets that of another section.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The separation and determination
of alloys; esp., the separation, as by acids, of gold from silver in the
assay button.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A joint or fissure, as in a coal
seam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The breaking, as of a cable, by
violence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Lamellar separation in a
crystallized mineral, due to some other cause than cleavage, as to the
presence of twinning lamell<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partisan</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*san</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"t<icr/*z<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F., fr. It. <ets>partigiano</ets>. See <er>Party</er>, and cf.
<er>Partisan</er> a truncheon.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>partizan</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An adherent to a party or
faction; esp., one who is strongly and passionately devoted to a party
or an interest.</def> <ldquo/The violence of a
<xex>partisan</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Both sides had their <qex>partisans</qex> in the colony.</q>
<rj><qau>Jefferson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The commander of a
body of detached light troops engaged in making forays and harassing an
enemy.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any member of such a corps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partisan</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*san</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>partizan</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Adherent to a party or
faction; especially, having the character of blind, passionate, or
unreasonable adherence to a party; <as>as, blinded by <ex>partisan</ex>
zeal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>Serving as a partisan in a
detached command; <as>as, a <ex>partisan</ex> officer or
corps</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Partisan ranger</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a member
of a partisan corps.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partisan</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*san</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pertuisane</ets>, prob.
fr. It. <ets>partigiana</ets>, influenced in French by OF.
<ets>pertuisier</ets> to pierce. It was prob. so named as the weapon of
some <ets>partisans</ets>, or party men. Cf. <er>Partisan</er> one of a
corps of light troops.]</ety> <def>A kind of halberd or pike; also, a
truncheon; a staff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And make him with our pikes and <qex>partisans</qex> a grave.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partisanship</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*san*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being a
partisan, or adherent to a party; feelings or conduct characteristic of
a partisan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Par*ti"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A suite; a set of variations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partite</ent><br/
<hw>Par"tite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>partitus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>partire</ets> to part, divide, from
<ets>pars</ets>. See <er>Part</er>, and cf. <er>Party</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided nearly
to the base; <as>as, a <ex>partite</ex> leaf is a simple separated down
nearly to the base</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of parts; usually used in compounds
specifying the number of parts; <as>as, a tri<ex>partite</ex>
treaty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partition</ent><br/
<hw>Par*ti"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>partition</ets>, L. <ets>partitio</ets>. See <er>Part</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of parting or dividing;
the state of being parted; separation; division; distribution; <as>as,
the <ex>partition</ex> of a kingdom</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And good from bad find no <qex>partition</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which divides or separates; that by which
different things, or distinct parts of the same thing, are separated;
separating boundary; dividing line or space; specifically, an interior
wall dividing one part or apartment of a house, a compartment of a room,
an inclosure, or the like, from another; <as>as, a brick
<ex>partition</ex>; lath and plaster <ex>partitions</ex>; cubicles with
four-foot high <ex>partitions</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>No sight could pass<br/
Betwixt the nice <qex>partitions</qex> of the grass.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A part divided off by walls; an apartment; a
compartment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Lodged in a small
<xex>partition</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law.)</fld> <def>The severance of common or
undivided interests, particularly in real estate. It may be effected by
consent of parties, or by compulsion of law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A score.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Partition of numbers</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>,
<cd>the resolution of integers into parts subject to given
conditions.</cd> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partition</ent><br/
<hw>Par*ti"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Partitioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Partitioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
divide into parts or shares; to divide and distribute; <as>as, to
<ex>partition</ex> an estate among various heirs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To divide into distinct parts by lines, walls, etc.;
<as>as, to <ex>partition</ex> a house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Uniform without, though severally <qex>partitioned</qex>
within.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partitioned</ent><br/
<hw>partitioned</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Divided into
partitions.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> partitioned off.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partitioning</ent><br/
<hw>partitioning</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>An analysis into mutually
exclusive categories.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> breakdown.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Separation by the creation of a boundary that
divides or keeps apart.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> division, partition, segmentation,
subdivision.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partitionist</ent><br/
<hw>partitionist</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>An advocate of partitioning a
country.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partitionment</ent><br/
<hw>Par*ti"tion*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
partitioning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partitive</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>partitif</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Denoting a part;
<as>as, a <ex>partitive</ex> genitive</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partitive</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A word or
phrase expressing partition, or denoting a part; <as>as, the phrase
<ldquo/of the team<rdquo/ in <ldquo/half of the
team<rdquo/</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partitively</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ti*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a partitive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partlet</ent><br/
<hw>Part"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>part</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A covering for the neck, and
sometimes for the shoulders and breast; originally worn by both sexes,
but later by women alone; a ruff.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hen; -- so called from the ruffing of her neck
feathers.</def> <ldquo/Dame <xex>Partlett</xex>, the hen.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partly</ent><br/
<hw>Part"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In part; in some measure of
degree; not wholly.</def> <ldquo/I <xex>partly</xex> believe it.<rdquo/
<rj><au>1 Cor. xi. 18.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partner</ent><br/
<hw>Part"ner</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt"n<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>parcener</ets>, influenced by <ets>part</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who has a part in anything with an other; a partaker; an
associate; a sharer.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Partner</xex> of his
fortune.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <specif>Hence:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A husband or a wife.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Either one of a couple
who dance together.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One who shares as a member
of a partnership in the management, or in the gains and losses, of a
business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My other self, the <qex>partner</qex> of my life.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An associate in any business or
occupation; a member of a partnership. See
<er>Partnership</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A framework of
heavy timber surrounding an opening in a deck, to strengthen it for the
support of a mast, pump, capstan, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Dormant partner</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Silent partner</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Dormant</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Associate; colleague; coadjutor; confederate;
partaker; participator; companion; comrade; mate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partner</ent><br/
<hw>Part"ner</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To associate, to join.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partnership</ent><br/
<hw>Part"ner*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or
condition of being a partner; <as>as, to be in <ex>partnership</ex> with
another; to have <ex>partnership</ex> in the fortunes of a family or a
state.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A division or sharing among partners; joint
possession or interest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rome, that ne'er knew three lordly heads before,<br/
First fell by fatal <qex>partnership</qex> of power.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He does possession keep,<br/
And is too wise to hazard <qex>partnership</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An alliance or association of persons for the
prosecution of an undertaking or a business on joint account; a company;
a firm; a house; <as>as, to form a <ex>partnership</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A contract between two or more
competent persons for joining together their money, goods, labor, and
skill, or any or all of them, under an understanding that there shall be
a communion of profit between them, and for the purpose of carrying on a
legal trade, business, or adventure.</def> <rj><au>Kent.</au>
<au>Story.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Community of profit is absolutely essential to, though
not necessarily the test of, a partnership.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>See <er>Fellowship</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Limited partnership</b></col>, <cd>a form of partnership
in which the firm consists of one or more general partners, jointly and
severally responsible as ordinary partners, and one or more special
partners, who are not liable for the debts of the partnership beyond the
amount of cash they contribute as capital.</cd> -- <col><b>Partnership
in commendam</b></col>, <cd>the title given to the limited partnership
(F. <xex>soci<eacute/t<eacute/ en commandit<eacute/</xex>) of the French
law, introduced into the code of Louisiana.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> --
<col><b>Silent partnership</b></col>, <cd>the relation of partnership
sustained by a person who furnishes capital only.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partook</ent><br/
<hw>Par*took"</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r*t<oocr/k")</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Partake</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partridge</ent><br/
<hw>Par"tridge</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"tr<icr/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>partriche</ets>, <ets>pertriche</ets>, OF. <ets>pertris</ets>,
<ets>perdriz</ets>, F. <ets>perdrix</ets>, L. <ets>perdix</ets>,
<ets>-icis</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>pe`rdix</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Any one of numerous species of small gallinaceous birds
of the genus <gen>Perdix</gen> and several related genera of the family
<fam>Perdicid<ae/</fam>, of the Old World. The partridge is noted as a
game bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full many a fat <qex>partrich</qex> had he in mew.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common European, or gray, partridge (<spn>Perdix
cinerea</spn>) and the red-legged partridge (<spn>Caccabis rubra</spn>)
of Southern Europe and Asia are well-known species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any one of several species of quail-like birds
belonging to <gen>Colinus</gen>, and allied genera.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Among them are the bobwhite (<spn>Colinus
Virginianus</spn>) of the Eastern States; the plumed, or mountain,
partridge (<spn>Oreortyx pictus</spn>) of California; the Massena
partridge (<spn>Cyrtonyx Montezum<ae/</spn>); and the California
partridge (<spn>Callipepla Californica</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The ruffed grouse (<spn>Bonasa
umbellus</spn>).</def> <mark>[New Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bamboo partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
spurred partridge of the genus <gen>Bambusicola</gen>. Several species
are found in China and the East Indies.</cd> -- <col><b>Night
partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the woodcock.</cd>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Painted partridge</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a francolin of South Africa (<spn>Francolinus
pictus</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Partridge berry</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The scarlet berry of a trailing
american plant (<spn>Mitchella repens</spn>) of the order
<ord>Rubiace<ae/</ord>, having roundish evergreen leaves, and white
fragrant flowers sometimes tinged with purple, growing in pairs with the
ovaries united, and producing the berries which remain over winter;
also, the plant itself</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The fruit of the creeping
wintergreen (<spn>Gaultheria procumbens</spn>); also, the plant
itself.</cd> -- <col><b>Partridge dove</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <cref>Mountain witch</cref>, under <er>Mountain</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Partridge pea</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
yellow-flowered leguminous herb (<spn>Cassia Cham<ae/crista</spn>),
common in sandy fields in the Eastern United States.</cd> --
<col><b>Partridge shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large marine
univalve shell (<spn>Dolium perdix</spn>), having colors variegated like
those of the partridge.</cd> -- <col><b>Partridge wood</b></col>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A variegated wood, much esteemed for cabinetwork. It is
obtained from tropical America, and one source of it is said to be the
leguminous tree <spn>Andira inermis</spn>. Called also <altname>pheasant
wood</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A name sometimes given to the
dark-colored and striated wood of some kind of palm, which is used for
walking sticks and umbrella handles.</cd> -- <col><b>Sea
partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Asiatic sand partridge
(<spn>Ammoperdix Bonhami</spn>); -- so called from its note.</cd> --
<col><b>Snow partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large spurred
partridge (<spn>Lerwa nivicola</spn>) which inhabits the high mountains
of Asia; called also <altname>jermoonal</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spruce partridge</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Spruce</er>.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Wood partridge</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Hill
partridge</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any small Asiatic
partridge of the genus <gen>Arboricola</gen>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>parts</ent><br/
<hw>parts</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rts)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>The local
environment; <as>as, he hasn't been seen around these <ex>parts</ex> in
years</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partsong</ent><br/
<hw>part"song`</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rt"s<ocr/ng`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
song with two or more voice parts.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>part-time</ent><br/
<hw>part-time</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Occupying less than
the entire time appropriate to an activity; <as>as, a <ex>part-time</ex>
job</as>. Opposed to <ant>full-time</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Employed for less than the full time usually
expended at a task or occupation; <as>as, <ex>part-time</ex> employees;
a <ex>part-time</ex> teacher</as>. Opposed to <ant>full-time</ant>.
Commonly, thirty-five hours per week or more is considered full-time,
and less than 30 is <ex>part-time</ex> work.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>part-time</ent><br/
<hw>part-time</hw> <pos>adv.</pos> <def>For less than the usual full
time appropriate to an activity; on a part-time basis; <as>as, to sell
real estate <ex>part-time</ex></as>. Opposed to
<ant>full-time</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>part-time</ent><br/
<hw>part-time</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A working schedule occupying less
than full time, i.e. less than 35 hours per week.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> part time.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>part-timer</ent><br/
<hw>part-timer</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Somone who works part-time; a
part-time employee. Contrasted with <ant>full-timer</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parture</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Departure.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturiate</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tu"ri*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Parturient</er>.]</ety> <def>To bring forth young.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturiency</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tu"ri*en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Parturition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturient</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tu"ri*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parturiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>parturire</ets> to desire to bring
forth, fr. <ets>parere</ets>, <ets>partum</ets>, to bring forth. See
<er>Parent</er>.]</ety> <def>Bringing forth, or about to bring forth,
young; fruitful.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturifacient</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tu`ri*fa"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parturire</ets> to desire to bring forth + <ets>facere</ets> to
make.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine tending to cause
parturition, or to give relief in childbearing.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturious</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tu"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Parturient.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturition</ent><br/
<hw>Par`tu*ri"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parturitio</ets>, fr. <ets>parturire</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>parturition</ets>. See <er>Parturient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of bringing forth, or being delivered of, young; the act of
giving birth; delivery; childbirth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is brought forth; a birth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parturitive</ent><br/
<hw>Par*tu"ri*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
parturition; obstetric.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Party</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ty</hw> <pr>(p<aum/r"t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Parties</plw> <pr>(p<aum/r"t<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[F. <ets>parti</ets> and <ets>partie</ets>, fr. F.
<ets>partir</ets> to part, divide, L. <ets>partire</ets>,
<ets>partiri</ets>. See <er>Part</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A part or portion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The most
<xex>party</xex> of the time.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A number of persons united in opinion or action, as
distinguished from, or opposed to, the rest of a community or
association; esp., one of the parts into which a people is divided on
questions of public policy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Win the noble Brutus to our <qex>party</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The peace both <qex>parties</qex> want is like to last.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A part of a larger body of company; a
detachment;</def> <specif>especially</specif> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <def>a
small body of troops dispatched on special service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A number of persons invited to a social
entertainment; a select company; <as>as, a dinner <ex>party</ex></as>;
also, the entertainment itself; <as>as, to give a
<ex>party</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>One concerned or interested in an affair; one who
takes part with others; a participator; <as>as, he was a <ex>party</ex>
to the plot; a <ex>party</ex> to the contract.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The plaintiff or the defendant in a lawsuit, whether
an individual, a firm, or corporation; a litigant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The cause of both <qex>parties</qex> shall come before the
judges.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Hence, any certain person who is regarded as being
opposed or antagonistic to another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the jury found that the <qex>party</qex> slain was of English
race, it had been adjudged felony.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Cause; side; interest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have you nothing said<br/
Upon this <qex>Party</qex> 'gainst the Duke of Albany?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A person; <as>as, he is a queer
<ex>party</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Now accounted a vulgarism.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><ldquo/For several generations, our ancestors largely employed
<xex>party</xex> for <xex>person</xex>; but this use of the word, when
it appeared to be reviving, happened to strike, more particularly, the
fancy of the vulgar; and the consequence has been, that the polite have
chosen to leave it in their undisputed possession.<rdquo/ <au>Fitzed.
Hall.</au></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Party jury</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a jury
composed of different parties, as one which is half natives and half
foreigners.</cd> -- <col><b>Party man</b></col>, <cd>a partisan.</cd>
<au>Swift.</au> -- <col><b>Party spirit</b></col>, <cd>a factious and
unreasonable temper, not uncommonly shown by party men.</cd>
<au>Whately.</au> -- <col><b>Party verdict</b></col>, <cd>a joint
verdict.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Party wall</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <cd>A wall built upon the dividing line
between two adjoining properties, usually having half its thickness on
each property</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>A wall that
separates adjoining houses, as in a block or row.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Party</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ty</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>parti</ets> divided, fr.
<ets>partir</ets> to divide. See <er>Part</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Partite</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Parted or
divided, as in the direction or form of one of the ordinaries; <as>as,
an escutcheon <ex>party</ex> per pale</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Partial; favoring one party; partisan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will be true judge, and not <qex>party</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Charter party</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Charter</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Party</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ty</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Partly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Party-coated</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ty-coat`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a motley
coat, or coat of divers colors.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parti-colored</ent><br/
<ent>Party-colored</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"ty-col`ored</hw>, <hw>Par"ti-col`ored</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Colored with different tints;
variegated; <as>as, a <ex>party-colored</ex> flower</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Parti-colored</xex> lambs.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>partygoer</ent><br/
<hw>par"ty*go`er</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Someone who is attending a
party{4}; <as>as, the hall was crowded with an overflow of
<ex>partygoers</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Partyism</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ty*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Devotion to
party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parula</ent><br/
<hw>Parula</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The type genus of the
<fam>Parulidae:</fam> wood warblers.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parula</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parulidae</ent><br/
<hw>Parulidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of New
World warblers.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Parulidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parumbilical</ent><br/
<hw>Par`um*bil"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>para-</ets> + <ets>umbilical</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Near the umbilicus; -- applied especially to one or more small
veins which, in man, connect the portal vein with the epigastric veins
in the front wall of the abdomen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parure</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`rure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., OF.
<ets>pareure</ets>, LL. <ets>paraura</ets>, fr. L. <ets>parare</ets> to
prepare. See <er>Pare</er>.]</ety> <def>An ornament or decoration for
the person; esp., a decoration consisting of a set of ornaments to be
used together; <as>as, a <ex>parure</ex> of rubies or of
embroideries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parus</ent><br/
<hw>Parus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The type genus of the family
<fam>Paridae</fam>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Parus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parusia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ru"si*a</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*r<udd/"zh<icr/*<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>paroysi`a</grk> presence, fr.
<grk>parei^nai</grk> to be present; <grk>para`</grk> beside +
<grk>e'i^nai</grk> to be.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure of
speech by which the present tense is used instead of the past or the
future, as in the animated narration of past, or in the prediction of
future, events.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvanimity</ent><br/
<hw>Par`va*nim"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>parvus</ets> little + <ets>animus</ets> mind.]</ety> <def>The state
or quality of having a little or ignoble mind; pettiness; meanness; --
opposed to <ant>magnanimity</ant>.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvati</ent><br/
<hw>Parvati</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <fld>(Hinduism)</fld> <def>The wife
of <persfn>Siva</persfn> and a benevolent aspect of
<persfn>Devi</persfn>; the goddess of plenty.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Anapurna, Annapurna.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvenu</ent><br/
<hw>Par"ve*nu`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., prop. p. p. of
<ets>parvenir</ets> to attain to, to succeed, to rise to high station,
L. <ets>pervenire</ets> to come to; <ets>per</ets> through +
<ets>venire</ets> to come. See <er>Par</er>, prep., and
<er>Come</er>.]</ety> <def>An upstart; a man newly risen into
notice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvise</ent><br/
<ent>Parvis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"vis</hw>, <hw>Par"vise</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>parvis</ets>, fr. LL. <ets>paravisus</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>paradisus</ets>. See <er>Paradise</er>.]</ety> <def>A court
of entrance to, or an inclosed space before, a church; hence, a church
porch; -- sometimes formerly used as place of meeting, as for
lawyers.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvity</ent><br/
<ent>Parvitude</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Par"vi*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Par"vi*ty</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>parvitas</ets>, fr.
<ets>parvus</ets> little: cf. OF. <ets>parvit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Littleness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Glanvill.
Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvolin</ent><br/
<hw>Par"vo*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A nonoxygenous ptomaine, formed in the putrefaction of
albuminous matters, especially of horseflesh and mackerel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Parvoline</ent><br/
<hw>Par"vo*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A liquid base, <chform>C9H13N</chform>, of the pyridine group,
found in coal tar; also, any one of the series of isomeric substances of
which it is the type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pas</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pace</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pace; a step, as in a
dance.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Right of going foremost; precedence.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasan</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"san</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
gemsbok.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pascha</ent><br/
<ent>Pasch</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pasch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pas"cha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>pascha</ets>, L. <ets>pascha</ets>,
Gr. <?/, fr. Heb. <ets>pesach</ets>, fr. <ets>p<amac/sach</ets> to pass
over: cf. OF. <ets>pasque</ets>, F. <ets>p<acir/que</ets>. Cf.
<er>Paschal</er>, <er>Paas</er>, <er>Paque</er>.]</ety> <def>The
passover; the feast of Easter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pasch egg</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Easter egg</cref>,
under <er>Easter</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pasch flower</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Pasque flower</cref>, under <er>Pasque</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paschal</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"chal</hw> <pr>(p<acr/s"k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paschalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pascal</ets>. See
<er>Pasch</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the passover, or to
Easter; <as>as, a <ex>paschal</ex> lamb; <ex>paschal</ex>
eggs.</as></def> <rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paschal candle</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>, <cd>a
large wax candle, blessed and placed on the altar on Holy Saturday, or
the day before Easter.</cd> -- <col><b>Paschal flower</b></col>. See
<cref>Pasque flower</cref>, under <er>Pasque</er>.</cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1048 --></p>

<p><ent>Paseng</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*seng"</hw> <pr>(p<aum/*s<ecr/ng")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The wild or bezoar goat. See
<er>Goat</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pash</ent><br/
<hw>Pash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Prob. of imitative
origin, or possibly akin to <ets>box</ets> to fight with the
fists.]</ety> <def>To strike; to crush; to smash; to dash in
pieces.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>P. Plowman.</au> <ldquo/I'll
<xex>pash</xex> him o'er the face.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pash</ent><br/
<hw>Pash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot., the pate. Cf. <er>Pash</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The head; the poll.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A rough <xex>pash</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A crushing blow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A heavy fall of rain or snow.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasha</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*sha"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk.
<ets>p<amac/sh<amac/</ets>, <ets>b<amac/sh<amac/</ets>; cf. Per.
<ets>b<amac/sh<amac/</ets>, <ets>b<amac/dsh<amac/h</ets>; perh. a
corruption of Per. <ets>p<amac/dish<amac/h</ets>. Cf. <er>Bashaw</er>,
<er>Padishah</er>, <er>Shah</er>.]</ety> <def>An honorary title given to
officers of high rank in Turkey, as to governers of provinces, military
commanders, etc. The earlier form was <altname>bashaw</altname>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pacha</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ There are three classes of pashas, whose rank is
distinguished by the number of the horsetails borne on their standards,
being one, two, or three, a <xex>pasha</xex> of three tails being the
highest.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pashalic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*sha"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pachalic</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Turk.]</ety> <def>The jurisdiction
of a pasha.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pashaw</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*shaw"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pasha</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasigraphical</ent><br/
<ent>Pasigraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pas`i*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pas`i*graph"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
pasigraphy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasigraphy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*sig"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ for all
(dat. pl. of <?/ all) + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A system of
universal writing, or a manner of writing that may be understood and
used by all nations.</def> <rj><au>Good.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasilaly</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"i*la`ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ for all
(dat. pl. of <?/ all) + <?/ talking.]</ety> <def>A form of speech
adapted to be used by all mankind; universal language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pask</ent><br/
<hw>Pask</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pasque</er>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Pasch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paspy</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>passe-pied</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of minuet, in triple time, of
French origin, popular in the reign of Queen Elizabeth and for some time
after; -- called also <altname>passing measure</altname>, and
<altname>passymeasure</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Percy
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasque</ent><br/
<hw>Pasque</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pasque</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Pasch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pasque flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name of
several plants of the genus <gen>Anemone</gen>, section
<gen>Pulsatilla</gen>. They are perennial herbs with rather large
purplish blossoms, which appear in early spring, or about Easter, whence
the common name. Called also <altname>campana</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquil</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"quil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pasquillo</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Pasquin</er>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquil</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"quil</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <mark>[R.]</mark> <def>See
<er>Pasquin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquilant</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"quil*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lampooner; a
pasquiler.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquiler</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"quil*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lampooner.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquin</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"quin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pasquino</ets> a mutilated statue at Rome, set up against the wall
of the place of the Orsini; -- so called from a witty cobbler or tailor,
near whose shop the statue was dug up. On this statue it was customary
to paste satiric papers.]</ety> <def>A lampooner; also, a lampoon. See
<er>Pasquinade</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Grecian wits, who satire first began,<br/
Were pleasant <qex>pasquins</qex> on the life of man.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquin</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"quin</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lampoon; to satiraze.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To see himself <qex>pasquined</qex> and affronted.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquinade</ent><br/
<hw>Pas`quin*ade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pasquinade</ets>, It. <ets>pasquinata</ets>.]</ety> <def>A lampoon
or satirical writing.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasquinade</ent><br/
<hw>Pas`quin*ade"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lampoon, to
satirize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pass</ent><br/
<hw>Pass</hw> <pr>(p<adot/s, p<acr/s)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Passed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Passing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>passer</ets>, LL. <ets>passare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>passus</ets>
step, or from <ets>pandere</ets>, <ets>passum</ets>, to spread out, lay
open. See <er>Pace</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go; to move; to
proceed; to be moved or transferred from one point to another; to make a
transit; -- usually with a following adverb or adverbal phrase defining
the kind or manner of motion; <as>as, to <ex>pass</ex> on, by, out, in,
etc.; to <ex>pass</ex> swiftly, directly, smoothly, etc.; to
<ex>pass</ex> to the rear, under the yoke, over the bridge, across the
field, beyond the border, etc.</as></def> <ldquo/But now <xex>pass</xex>
over [i. e., <xex>pass</xex> on].<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On high behests his angels to and fro<br/
<qex>Passed</qex> frequent.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sweet sounds rose slowly through their mouths,<br/
And from their bodies <qex>passed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move or be transferred from one state or
condition to another; to change possession, condition, or circumstances;
to undergo transition; <as>as, the business has <ex>passed</ex> into
other hands</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Others, dissatisfied with what they have, . . . <qex>pass</qex>
from just to unjust.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To move beyond the range of the senses or of
knowledge; to pass away; hence, to disappear; to vanish; to depart;
specifically, to depart from life; to die.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Disturb him not, let him <qex>pass</qex> paceably.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beauty is a charm, but soon the charm will <qex>pass</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>passing</qex> of the sweetest soul<br/
That ever looked with human eyes.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To move or to come into being or under notice; to
come and go in consciousness; hence, to take place; to occur; to happen;
to come; to occur progressively or in succession; to be present
transitorily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So death <qex>passed</qex> upon all men.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. v.
12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our own consciousness of what <qex>passes</qex> within our own
mind.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To go by or glide by, as time; to elapse; to be
spent; <as>as, their vacation <ex>passed</ex> pleasantly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now the time is far <qex>passed</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Mark vi.
35</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To go from one person to another; hence, to be given
and taken freely; <as>as, clipped coin will not <ex>pass</ex></as>; to
obtain general acceptance; to be held or regarded; to circulate; to be
current; -- followed by <xex>for</xex> before a word denoting value or
estimation.</def> <ldquo/Let him <xex>pass</xex> for a man.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>False eloquence <qex>passeth</qex> only where true is not
understood.</q> <rj><qau>Felton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This will not <qex>pass</qex> for a fault in him.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To advance through all the steps or stages necessary
to validity or effectiveness; to be carried through a body that has
power to sanction or reject; to receive legislative sanction; to be
enacted; <as>as, the resolution <ex>passed</ex>; the bill
<ex>passed</ex> both houses of Congress.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To go through any inspection or test successfully;
to be approved or accepted; <as>as, he attempted the examination, but
did not expect to <ex>pass</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To be suffered to go on; to be tolerated; hence, to
continue; to live along.</def> <ldquo/The play may
<xex>pass</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To go unheeded or neglected; to proceed without
hindrance or opposition; <as>as, we let this act
<ex>pass</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To go beyond bounds; to surpass; to be in
excess.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/This <xex>passes</xex>, Master
Ford.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>To take heed; to care.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As for these silken-coated slaves, I <qex>pass</qex> not.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>To go through the intestines.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To be conveyed or transferred by
will, deed, or other instrument of conveyance; <as>as, an estate
<ex>passes</ex> by a certain clause in a deed</as>.</def> <rj><au>Mozley
& W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>To make a lunge or pass; to
thrust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>To decline to play in
one's turn; in euchre, to decline to make the trump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She would not play, yet must not <qex>pass</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To bring to pass</b></col>, <col><b>To come to
pass</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Bring</er>, and
<er>Come</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To pass away</b></col>, <cd>to disappear;
to die; to vanish.</cd> <ldquo/The heavens shall <xex>pass
away</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>2 Pet. iii. 10.</au> <ldquo/I thought to
<xex>pass away</xex> before, but yet alive I am.<rdquo/
<au>Tennyson.</au> -- <col><b>To pass by</b></col>, <cd>to go near and
beyond a certain person or place; as, he <xex>passed by</xex> as we
stood there.</cd> -- <col><b>To pass into</b></col>, <cd>to change by a
gradual transmission; to blend or unite with.</cd> -- <col><b>To pass
on</b></col>, <cd>to proceed.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To pass on</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>To pass upon</b></col></mcol>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
happen to; to come upon; to affect</cd>. <ldquo/So death <xex>passed
upon</xex> all men.<rdquo/ <au>Rom. v. 12.</au> <ldquo/Provided no
indirect act <xex>pass upon</xex> our prayers to define them.<rdquo/
<au>Jer. Taylor.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To determine concerning; to give
judgment or sentence upon.</cd> <ldquo/We may not <xex>pass upon</xex>
his life.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To pass off</b></col>, <cd>to
go away; to cease; to disappear; <as>as, an agitation <ex>passes
off</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To pass over</b></col>, <cd>to go from
one side or end to the other; to cross, as a river, road, or
bridge.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pass</ent><br/
<hw>Pass</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> In simple,
transitive senses; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To go by, beyond, over,
through, or the like; to proceed from one side to the other of; <as>as,
to <ex>pass</ex> a house, a stream, a boundary, etc.</as></def>
<sd>(b)</sd> Hence: <def>To go from one limit to the other of; to spend;
to live through; to have experience of; to undergo; to suffer.</def>
<ldquo/To <xex>pass</xex> commodiously this life.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She loved me for the dangers I had <qex>passed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To go by without noticing; to omit attention to; to
take no note of; to disregard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Please you that I may <qex>pass</qex> This doing.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>pass</qex> their warlike pomp, their proud array.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>To transcend; to surpass; to excel; to
exceed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And strive to <qex>pass</qex> . . . <br/
Their native music by her skillful art.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whose tender power<br/
<qex>Passes</qex> the strength of storms in their most desolate
hour.</q> <rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>To go successfully through, as an examination,
trail, test, etc.; to obtain the formal sanction of, as a legislative
body; <as>as, he <ex>passed</ex> his examination; the bill
<ex>passed</ex> the senate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> In causative senses: as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To cause to
move or go; to send; to transfer from one person, place, or condition to
another; to transmit; to deliver; to hand; to make over; <as>as, the
waiter <ex>passed</ex> bisquit and cheese; the torch was <ex>passed</ex>
from hand to hand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had only time to <qex>pass</qex> my eye over the medals.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Waller <qex>passed</qex> over five thousand horse and foot by
Newbridge.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To cause to pass the lips; to utter; to pronounce;
hence, to promise; to pledge; <as>as, to <ex>pass</ex>
sentence</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Father, thy word is <qex>passed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To cause to advance by stages of progress; to carry
on with success through an ordeal, examination, or action; specifically,
to give legal or official sanction to; to ratify; to enact; to approve
as valid and just; <as>as, he <ex>passed</ex> the bill through the
committee; the senate <ex>passed</ex> the law</as>.</def> <sd>(e)</sd>
<def>To put in circulation; to give currency to; <as>as, to
<ex>pass</ex> counterfeit money</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pass</xex> the
happy news.<rdquo/ <au>Tennyson.</au> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>To cause to
obtain entrance, admission, or conveyance; <as>as, to <ex>pass</ex> a
person into a theater, or over a railroad</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To emit from the bowels; to evacuate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To take a turn with (a line,
gasket, etc.), as around a sail in furling, and make secure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>To make, as a thrust, punto,
etc.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passed midshipman</b></col>. <cd>See under
Midshipman.</cd> -- <col><b>To pass a dividend</b></col>, <cd>to omit
the declaration and payment of a dividend at the time when due.</cd> --
<col><b>To pass away</b></col>, <cd>to spend; to waste.</cd> <ldquo/Lest
she <xex>pass away</xex> the flower of her age.<rdquo/ <au>Ecclus. xlii.
9.</au><-- (b) to die --> -- <col><b>To pass by</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To disregard; to neglect</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To excuse; to spare;
to overlook.</cd> -- <col><b>To pass off</b></col>, <cd>to impose
fraudulently; to palm off.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>Passed</xex> himself
<xex>off</xex> as a bishop.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>To pass (something) on (some one)</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>To pass (something) upon (some one)</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to put
upon as a trick or cheat; to palm off.</cd> <ldquo/She <xex>passed</xex>
the child <xex>on</xex> her husband for a boy.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au>
-- <col><b>To pass over</b></col>, <cd>to overlook; not to note or
resent; <as>as, to <ex>pass over</ex> an affront</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pass</ent><br/
<hw>Pass</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pas</ets> (for sense 1),
and <ets>passe</ets>, fr. <ets>passer</ets> to pass. See <er>Pass</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An opening, road, or track,
available for passing; especially, one through or over some dangerous or
otherwise impracticable barrier; a passageway; a defile; a ford; <as>as,
a mountain <ex>pass</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Try not the <qex>pass</qex>!<rdquo/ the old man said.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>A thrust or push; an attempt to
stab or strike an adversary.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A movement of the hand over or along anything; the
manipulation of a mesmerist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Rolling Metals)</fld> <def>A single passage of a
bar, rail, sheet, etc., between the rolls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>State of things; condition; predicament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have his daughters brought him to this <qex>pass</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Matters have been brought to this <qex>pass</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Permission or license to pass, or to go and come; a
psssport; a ticket permitting free transit or admission; <as>as, a
railroad or theater <ex>pass</ex>; a military
<ex>pass</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A ship sailing under the flag and <qex>pass</qex> of an enemy.</q>
<rj><qau>Kent.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Fig.: a thrust; a sally of wit.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Estimation; character.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Common speech gives him a worthy <qex>pass</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Passus</er>.]</ety> <def>A part; a
division.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Sports)</fld> <def>In football, hockey, and other
team sports, a transfer of the ball, puck, etc., to another player of
one's own team, usually at some distance. In American football, the
<ex>pass</ex> is through the air by an act of throwing the
ball.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pass boat</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a punt, or
similar boat.</cd> -- <col><b>Pass book</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
book in which a trader enters articles bought on credit, and then passes
or sends it to the purchaser</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>See <er>Bank
book</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pass box</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a
wooden or metallic box, used to carry cartridges from the service
magazine to the piece.</cd> -- <col><b>Pass check</b></col>, <cd>a
ticket of admission to a place of entertainment, or of readmission for
one who goes away in expectation of returning.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passable</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>passable</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being passed,
traveled, navigated, traversed, penetrated, or the like; <as>as, the
roads are not <ex>passable</ex>; the stream is <ex>passable</ex>in
boats.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His body's a <qex>passable</qex> carcass if it be not hurt; it is
a throughfare for steel.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being freely circulated or disseminated;
acceptable; generally receivable; current.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With men as with false money -- one piece is more or less
<qex>passable</qex> than another.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Could they have made this slander <qex>passable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Such as may be accepted or allowed to pass without
serious objection; adequate; acceptable; tolerable; admissable;
moderate; mediocre.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>My version will appear a <qex>passable</qex> beauty when the
original muse is absent.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passableness</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
passable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passably</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Tolerably;
moderately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passacaglio</ent><br/
<ent>Passacaglia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pas`sa*ca*glia</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
\'d8<hw>Pas`sa*ca*glio</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Sp. <ets>pasacalle</ets> a certain tune on the guitar, prop., a
tune played in passing through the streets.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>An old Italian or Spanish dance tune, in slow three-four measure,
with divisions on a ground bass, resembling a chaconne.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passado</ent><br/
<ent>Passade</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pas*sade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pas*sa"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>passade</ets>; cf. Sp.
<ets>pasada</ets>. See <er>Pass</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>A pass or thrust.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>A turn or course of a horse
backward or forward on the same spot of ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passage</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>passage</ets>. See <er>Pass</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of passing; transit from one place to another;
movement from point to point; a going by, over, across, or through;
<as>as, the <ex>passage</ex> of a man or a carriage; the
<ex>passage</ex> of a ship or a bird; the <ex>passage</ex> of light; the
<ex>passage</ex> of fluids through the pores or channels of the
body.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What! are my doors opposed against my <qex>passage</qex>!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Transit by means of conveyance; journey, as by
water, carriage, car, or the like; travel; right, liberty, or means, of
passing; conveyance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ship in which he had taken <qex>passage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Price paid for the liberty to pass; fare; <as>as, to
pay one's <ex>passage</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Removal from life; decease; departure; death.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Endure thy mortal <xex>passage</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he is fit and season'd for his <qex>passage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Way; road; path; channel or course through or by
which one passes; way of exit or entrance; way of access or transit.
Hence, a common avenue to various apartments in a building; a hall; a
corridor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And with his pointed dart<br/
Explores the nearest <qex>passage</qex> to his heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Persian army had advanced into the . . . <qex>passages</qex>
of Cilicia.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A continuous course, process, or progress; a
connected or continuous series; <as>as, the <ex>passage</ex> of
time</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The conduct and <qex>passage</qex> of affairs.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>passage</qex> and whole carriage of this action.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A separate part of a course, process, or series; an
occurrence; an incident; an act or deed.</def> <ldquo/In thy
<xex>passages</xex> of life.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The . . . almost incredible <qex>passage</qex> of their
unbelief.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A particular portion constituting a part of
something continuous; esp., a portion of a book, speech, or musical
composition; a paragraph; a clause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How commentators each dark <qex>passage</qex> shun.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Reception; currency.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir K. Digby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A pass or en encounter; <as>as, a <ex>passage</ex>
at arms</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>passages</qex> of love<br/
Betwixt us twain henceforward evermore.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A movement or an evacuation of the
bowels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>In parliamentary proceedings: <sd>(a)</sd> The
course of a proposition (bill, resolution, etc.) through the several
stages of consideration and action; <as>as, during its <ex>passage</ex>
through Congress the bill was amended in both Houses</as>. <sd>(b)</sd>
The advancement of a bill or other proposition from one stage to another
by an affirmative vote; esp., the final affirmative action of the body
upon a proposition; hence, adoption; enactment; <as>as, the
<ex>passage</ex> of the bill to its third reading was
delayed</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>passage</xex> of the Stamp
Act.<rdquo/ <rj><au>D. Hosack.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The final question was then put upon its <qex>passage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cushing.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In passage</b></col>, <cd>in passing; cursorily.</cd>
<ldquo/These . . . have been studied but <xex>in passage</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Bacon.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Middle passage</b></col>,
<col><b>Northeast passage</b></col>, <col><b>Northwest
passage</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Middle</er>,
<er>Northeast</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Of passage</b></col>,
<cd>passing from one place, region, or climate, to another; migratory;
-- said especially of birds.</cd> <ldquo/Birds <xex>of
passage</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Longfellow.</au> -- <col><b>Passage
hawk</b></col>, <cd>a hawk taken on its passage or migration.</cd> --
<col><b>Passage money</b></col>, <cd>money paid for conveyance of a
passenger, -- usually for carrying passengers by water.</cd> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1049 --></p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Vestibule; hall; corridor. See
<er>Vestibule</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passager</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sa*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Passenger</er>.]</ety> <def>A passenger; a bird or boat of
passage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ld. Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passageway</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sage*way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A way for
passage; a hall. See <er>Passage</er>, 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passant</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., p. pr. of
<ets>passer</ets>. See <er>Pass</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Passing from one to another; in circulation;
current.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many opinions are <qex>passant</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cursory, careless.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On a <qex>passant</qex> rewiew of what I wrote to the bishop.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Pett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Surpassing; excelling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Walking; -- said of any animal on
an escutcheon, which is represented as walking with the dexter paw
raised.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passee</ent><br/
<ent>Passe</ent><br/
<ent>passe</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>pas*se"</hw>, \'d8<hw>Pas`s<eacute/"</hw>, <pos>masc.</pos>
\'d8<hw>Pas`s<eacute/"e</hw>, <pos>fem.</pos> }</mhw>
<pr>(p<acr/*s<asl/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>Past; gone
by; hence, past one's prime; worn; faded; <as>as, a
<ex>pass<eacute/e</ex> belle</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ld.
Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>old-fashioned</er>, <pos>a.</pos>,
2.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> antique, demode, old-fashioned, old-hat(predicate),
outmoded, out-of-date, out of fashion(predicate), out of
style(predicate), passe.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Past; -- used appositively; <as>as, time
<ex>passe</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passegarde</ent><br/
<hw>Passe"garde`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld> <def>A ridge or projecting edge on a shoulder
piece to turn the blow of a lance or other weapon from the joint of the
armor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passement</ent><br/
<hw>Passe"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Lace, gimp, braid etc., sewed on a garment.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passementerie</ent><br/
<hw>Passe*men"terie</hw> <pr>(E. p<adot/s*m<ecr/n"tr<icr/; F.
p<aum/`s'm<aum/N`t'r<esl/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Trimmings, esp. of braids, cords, gimps, beads, or
tinsel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>passenger</ent><br/
<hw>pas"sen*ger</hw> <pr>(p<acr/s"s<ecr/n*j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. & F. <ets>passager</ets>. See <er>Passage</er>, and cf.
<er>Messenger</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A passer or passer-by; a
wayfarer.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A traveler by some established conveyance, as a
coach, steamboat, railroad train, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passenger falcon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
migratory hawk.</cd> <au>Ainsworth.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passenger mile</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sen*ger mile</hw>. <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>A unit of
measurement of the passenger transportation performed by a railroad
during a given period, usually a year, the total of which consists of
the sum of the miles traversed by all the passengers on the road in the
period in question.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passenger mileage</ent><br/
<hw>Passenger mileage</hw>. <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>Passenger miles
collectively; the total number of miles traveled by passengers on a
railroad during a given period.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>passenger pigeon</ent><br/
<hw>passenger pigeon</hw> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <def>A once common wild
pigeon of North America (<spn>Ectopistes migratorius</spn>), now
extinct. It was so called on account of its extensive
migrations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The passenger pigeon presents a striking example of how
dramatic a negative influence man can have on other species. The
population of the passenger pigeon is estimated to have been at one time
as high as five billion in North America, but over a period of about one
hundred years large scale hunting for use as food and killing for
<ldquo/sport<rdquo/ reduced the numbers below that necessary to sustain
existence of the species. At one time over 200,000 birds were shipped to
the food markets in one day. The last wild pigeon is believed to have
died in 1900, and the last bird in captivity died in 1914. It is a rare
example of the date of a man-made extinction being recorded with
certainty.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passe partout</ent><br/
<ent>passe-partout</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>passe`-par`tout"</hw>, \'d8<hw>Passe" par`tout"</hw></mhw>
<pr>(p<aum/s` p<aum/r`t<oomac/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from
<ets>passer</ets> to pass + <ets>partout</ets> everywhere.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That by which one can pass anywhere; a
safe-conduct.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A master key; a latchkey.</def>
<wns>[wns=1]</wns><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A light picture frame or mat of cardboard, wood, or
the like, usually put between the picture and the glass, and sometimes
serving for several pictures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A strip of adhesive paper or gummed tape used to
mount a picture between a piece of glass and a backing.</def>
<wns>[wns=2]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The method of mounting a picture between a piece of
glass and a backing, using a passe-partout{4}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passer</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who passes; a
passenger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passer-by</ent><br/
<ent>passerby</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pas`ser*by"</hw>, <hw>Pass`er-by"</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who passes by, especially casually or by chance;
one not directly involved in some action; a passer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passeres</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas"se*res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>passer</ets> a sparrow.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
order, or suborder, of birds, including more that half of all the known
species. It embraces all singing birds (Oscines), together with many
other small perching birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passeriform</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*ser"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like or belonging to the <ord>Passeres</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passeriformes</ent><br/
<hw>Passeriformes</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The largest order of
birds comprising about half the known species: rooks; finches; sparrows;
tits; warblers; robins; wrens; swallows; etc.; in four suborders:
<subord>Eurylaimi</subord>; <subord>Tyranni</subord>;
<subord>Menurae</subord>; <subord>Oscines</subord> or
<subord>Passeres</subord>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order Passeriformes.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passerina</ent><br/
<hw>Passerina</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of small North
American bush-loving finches; the New World buntings.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Passerina</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passerine</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ser*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>passerinus</ets>, fr. <ets>passer</ets> a sparrow.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<ord>Passeres</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The columbine, gallinaceous, and <qex>passerine</qex> tribes
people the fruit trees.</q> <rj><qau>Sydney Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passerine</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ser*ine</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
<ord>Passeres</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passibility</ent><br/
<hw>Pas`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>passibilitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>passibilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being passible; aptness to feel or suffer;
sensibility.</def> <rj><au>Hakewill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passible</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>passibilis</ets>, fr. <ets>pati</ets>, to suffer: cf. F.
<ets>passible</ets>. See <er>Passion</er>.]</ety> <def>Susceptible of
feeling or suffering, or of impressions from external agents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Apolinarius, which held even deity itself <qex>passible</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passibleness</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"si*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Passibility.</def>
<rj><au>Brerewood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passiflora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas"si*flo"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
L. <ets>passio</ets> passion (fr. <ets>pati</ets>, <ets>passus</ets>, to
suffer) + <ets>flos</ets>, <ets>floris</ets>, flower.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants, including the passion flower.
It is the type of the order <ord>Passiflore<ae/</ord>, which includes
about nineteen genera and two hundred and fifty species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passifloraceae</ent><br/
<hw>Passifloraceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of
tropical woody tendril-climbing vines.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Passifloraceae</fam>, passionflower
family.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passim</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas"sim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>Here and there; everywhere; <as>as, this word occurs
<ex>passim</ex> in the poem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passing</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of one who,
or that which, passes; the act of going by or away.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passing bell</b></col>, <cd>a tolling of a bell to
announce that a soul is passing, or has passed, from its body (formerly
done to invoke prayers for the dying); also, a tolling during the
passing of a funeral procession to the grave, or during funeral
ceremonies.</cd> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au>
<au>Longfellow.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passing</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to the act of
passing or going; going by, beyond, through, or away;
departing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exceeding; surpassing, eminent.</def>
<au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/Her <xex>passing</xex> deformity.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passing note</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a character
including a passing tone.</cd> -- <col><b>Passing tone</b></col>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a tone introduced between two other tones, on an
unaccented portion of a measure, for the sake of smoother melody, but
forming no essential part of the harmony.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passing</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"ing</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Exceedingly; excessively;
surpassingly; <as>as, <ex>passing</ex> fair; <ex>passing</ex>
strange.</as></def> <ldquo/You apprehend <xex>passing</xex>
shrewdly.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Exceedingly.</def>
<rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passion</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>passio</ets>, fr. <ets>pati</ets>, <ets>passus</ets>, to suffer.
See <er>Patient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A suffering or enduring of
imposed or inflicted pain; any suffering or distress (as, a cardiac
<ex>passion</ex>); specifically, the suffering of Christ between the
time of the last supper and his death, esp. in the garden upon the
cross.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>passions</xex> of this time.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Wyclif (Rom. viii. 18).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To whom also he showed himself alive after his <qex>passion</qex>,
by many infallible proofs.</q> <rj><qau>Acts i. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being acted upon; subjection to an
external agent or influence; a passive condition; -- opposed to
<xex>action</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A body at rest affords us no idea of any active power to move,
and, when set in motion, it is rather a <qex>passion</qex> than an
action in it.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Capacity of being affected by external agents;
susceptibility of impressions from external agents.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moldable and not moldable, scissible and not scissible, and many
other <qex>passions</qex> of matter.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The state of the mind when it is powerfully acted
upon and influenced by something external to itself; the state of any
particular faculty which, under such conditions, becomes extremely
sensitive or uncontrollably excited; any emotion or sentiment
(specifically, love or anger) in a state of abnormal or controlling
activity; an extreme or inordinate desire; also, the capacity or
susceptibility of being so affected; <as>as, to be in a
<ex>passion</ex>; the <ex>passions</ex> of love, hate, jealously, wrath,
ambition, avarice, fear, etc.; a <ex>passion</ex> for war, or for drink;
an orator should have <ex>passion</ex> as well as rhetorical
skill.</as></def> <ldquo/A <xex>passion</xex> fond even to
idolatry.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au> <ldquo/Her <xex>passion</xex> is to
seek roses.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lady M. W. Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We also are men of like <qex>passions</qex> with you.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xiv. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The nature of the human mind can not be sufficiently understood,
without considering the affections and <qex>passions</qex>, or those
modifications or actions of the mind consequent upon the apprehension of
certain objects or events in which the mind generally conceives good or
evil.</q> <rj><qau>Hutcheson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The term <qex>passion</qex>, and its adverb
<qex>passionately</qex>, often express a very strong predilection for
any pursuit, or object of taste -- a kind of enthusiastic fondness for
anything.</q> <rj><qau>Cogan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bravery of his grief did put me<br/
Into a towering <qex>passion</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ruling <qex>passion</qex>, be it what it will,<br/
The ruling <qex>passion</qex> conquers reason still.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who walked in every path of human life,<br/
Felt every <qex>passion</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Akenside.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When statesmen are ruled by faction and interest, they can have no
<qex>passion</qex> for the glory of their country.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Disorder of the mind; madness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Passion week. See <cref>Passion week</cref>,
below.</def> <rj><au>R. of Gl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passion flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any
flower or plant of the genus <gen>Passiflora</gen>; -- so named from a
fancied resemblance of parts of the flower to the instruments of the
crucifixion of Christ.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The flowers are showy, and the fruit is sometimes highly
esteemed (see <er>Granadilla</er>, and <er>Maypop</er>). The roots and
leaves are generally more or less noxious, and are used in medicine. The
plants are mostly tendril climbers, and are commonest in the warmer
parts of America, though a few species are Asiatic or
Australian.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passion music</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>,
<cd>originally, music set to the gospel narrative of the passion of our
Lord; after the Reformation, a kind of oratorio, with narrative,
chorals, airs, and choruses, having for its theme the passion and
crucifixion of Christ.</cd> -- <col><b>Passion play</b></col>, <cd>a
mystery play, in which the scenes connected with the passion of our
Savior are represented dramatically.</cd> -- <col><b>Passion
Sunday</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>the fifth Sunday in Lent, or
the second before Easter.</cd> -- <col><b>Passion Week</b></col>,
<cd>the last week but one in Lent, or the second week preceding
Easter.</cd> <ldquo/The name of <xex>Passion week</xex> is frequently,
but improperly, applied to Holy Week.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Passion</er>, <er>Feeling</er>,
<er>Emotion</er>.</syn> <usage> When any <xex>feeling</xex> or
<xex>emotion</xex> completely masters the mind, we call it a
<xex>passion</xex>; as, a <xex>passion</xex> for music, dress, etc.;
especially is anger (when thus extreme) called <xex>passion</xex>. The
mind, in such cases, is considered as having lost its self-control, and
become the passive instrument of the feeling in question.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passion</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Passioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Passioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To give a passionate
character to.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passion</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To suffer pain or sorrow; to
experience a passion; to be extremely agitated.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Dumbly she <xex>passions</xex>, frantically
she doteth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passional</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to passion or the passions; exciting, influenced by, or ministering to,
the passions.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
passionary.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>passionarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>passionaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
book in which are described the sufferings of saints and martyrs.</def>
<rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionate</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>passionatus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>passionn<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable or susceptible of passion, or of different
passions; easily moved, excited or agitated; specifically, easily moved
to anger; irascible; quick-tempered; <as>as, a <ex>passionate</ex>
nature</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Homer's Achilles is haughty and <qex>passionate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characterized by passion; expressing passion; ardent
in feeling or desire; vehement; warm; <as>as, a <ex>passionate</ex>
friendship</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>passionate</xex> Pilgrim.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Suffering; sorrowful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionate</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
affect with passion; to impassion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great pleasure, mixed with pitiful regard,<br/
The godly king and queen did <qex>passionate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To express feelingly or sorrowfully.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionately</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*ate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>In a passionate manner; with strong feeling; ardently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sorrow expresses itself . . . loudly and
<qex>passionately</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Angrily; irascibly.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionateness</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*ate*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of
being passionate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionist</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A
member of a religious order founded in Italy in 1737, and introduced
into the United States in 1852. The members of the order unite the
austerities of the <org>Trappists with</org> the activity and zeal of
the <org>Jesuits and</org> Lazarists. Called also <altname>Barefooted
Clerks of the Most Holy Cross</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passionless</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Void of passion;
without anger or emotion; not easily excited; calm.</def>
<ldquo/Self-contained and <xex>passionless</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passiontide</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sion*tide`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Passion</ets> + <ets>tide</ets> time.]</ety> <def>The last
fortnight of Lent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passive</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>passivus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>passif</ets>. See
<er>Passion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not active, but acted upon;
suffering or receiving impressions or influences; <as>as, they were
<ex>passive</ex> spectators, not actors in the scene</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>passive</qex> air<br/
Upbore their nimble tread.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind is wholly <qex>passive</qex> in the reception of all its
simple ideas.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Receiving or enduring without either active sympathy
or active resistance; without emotion or excitement; patient; not
opposing; unresisting; <as>as, <ex>passive</ex> obedience;
<ex>passive</ex> submission.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The best virtue, <qex>passive</qex> fortitude.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Inactive; inert; unreactive; not
showing strong affinity; <as>as, red phosphorus is comparatively
<ex>passive</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Designating certain morbid
conditions, as hemorrhage or dropsy, characterized by relaxation of the
vessels and tissues, with deficient vitality and lack of reaction in the
affected tissues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Passive congestion</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>congestion due to obstruction to the return of the blood from the
affected part.</cd> -- <col><b>Passive iron</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>iron which has been subjected to the action of
heat, of strong nitric acid, chlorine, etc. It is then not easily acted
upon by acids.</cd> -- <col><b>Passive movement</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a movement of a part, in order to exercise it,
made without the assistance of the muscles which ordinarily move the
part.</cd> -- <col><b>Passive obedience</b></col><cd> (as used by
writers on government), obedience or submission of the subject or
citizen as a duty in all cases to the existing government.</cd> --
<col><b>Passive prayer</b></col>, <cd>among mystic divines, a suspension
of the activity of the soul or intellectual faculties, the soul
remaining quiet, and yielding only to the impulses of grace.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Passive verb</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Passive
voice</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>a verb, or form of a
verb, which expresses the effect of the action of some agent; as, in
Latin, <xex>doceor</xex>, I am taught; in English, she <xex>is
loved</xex>; the picture <xex>is admired</xex> by all; he <xex>is
assailed</xex> by slander.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Inactive; inert; quiescent; unresisting;
unopposing; suffering; enduring; submissive; patient.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passive aeroplane</ent><br/
<ent>Passive balloon</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pas"sive bal*loon"</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>Pas"sive
a"<eum/r*o*plane</hw> }</mhw>. <def>One unprovided with motive
power.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passive flight</ent><br/
<hw>Passive flight</hw>. <def>Flight, such as gliding and soaring,
accomplished without the use of motive power.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passively</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a passive
manner; inertly; unresistingly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>As a passive verb; in the passive voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
passive; unresisting submission.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be an effect implies <qex>passiveness</qex>, or the being
subject to the power and action of its cause.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>passivism</ent><br/
<hw>pas"si*vism`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The doctrine that
all violence is unjustifiable; hence, the principle of passive
resistance.</def> <wns>[wns=1]</wns><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pacifism.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality of being passive.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passivity</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*siv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>passivit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Passiveness; --
opposed to <xex>activity</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>The tendency of a body to
remain in a given state, either of motion or rest, till disturbed by
another body; inertia.</def> <rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The quality or condition of any
substance which has no inclination to chemical activity;
inactivity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pass-key</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"-key`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A key for opening
more locks than one; a master key.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passless</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no pass; impassable.</def>
<rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passman</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Passmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who passes for a
degree, without honors. See <er>Classman</er>, 2.</def> <mark>[Eng.
Univ.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passover</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"o`ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pass</ets> +
<ets>over</ets>. See Pasch.]</ety> <fld>(Jewish Antiq.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A feast of the Jews, instituted to commemorate the
sparing of the Hebrews in Egypt, when God, smiting the firstborn of the
Egyptians, <xex>passed over</xex> the houses of the Israelites which
were marked with the blood of a lamb.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
sacrifice offered at the feast of the passover; the paschal lamb.</def>
<rj><au>Ex. xii.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pass-parole</ent><br/
<hw>Pass`-pa*role"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>passe-parole</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>An order passed
from front to rear by word of mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passport</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"port</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>passeport</ets>, orig., a permission to leave a port or to sail
into it; <ets>passer</ets> to pass + <ets>port</ets> a port, harbor. See
<er>Pass</er>, and <er>Port</er> a harbor.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Permission to pass; a document given by the competent officer of a
state, permitting the person therein named to pass or travel from place
to place, without molestation, by land or by water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Caution in granting <qex>passports</qex> to Ireland.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A document carried by neutral merchant vessels in
time of war, to certify their nationality and protect them from
belligerents; a sea letter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A license granted in time of war for the removal of
persons and effects from a hostile country; a safe-conduct.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Figuratively: Anything which secures advancement and
general acceptance.</def> <rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>passport</qex> is his innocence and grace.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas"sus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Passus</plw>, E. <plw>Passuses</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L., a step, a pace. See <er>Pace</er>.]</ety> <def>A division or
part; a canto; <as>as, the <ex>passus</ex> of Piers Plowman</as>. See 2d
<er>Fit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Password</ent><br/
<hw>Pass"word`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A word to be given
before a person is allowed to pass; a watchword; a countersign.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Passymeasure</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"sy*meas`ure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted fr.
It. <ets>passamezzo</ets>.]</ety> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <def>See
<er>Paspy</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Past</ent><br/
<hw>Past</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pass</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a former time or state;
neither present nor future; gone by; elapsed; ended; spent; <as>as,
<ex>past</ex> troubles; <ex>past</ex> offences.</as></def>
<ldquo/<xex>Past</xex> ages.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Past master</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Master</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Past</ent><br/
<hw>Past</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A former time or state; a state of
things gone by.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>past</xex>, at least, is
secure.<rdquo/ <rj><au>D. Webster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The present is only intelligible in the light of the
<qex>past</qex>, often a very remote <qex>past</qex> indeed.</q>
<rj><qau>Trench.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Past</ent><br/
<hw>Past</hw>, <pos>prep.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Beyond, in position, or
degree; further than; beyond the reach or influence of.</def> <ldquo/Who
being <xex>past</xex> feeling.<rdquo/ <au>Eph. iv. 19.</au>
<ldquo/Galled <xex>past</xex> endurance.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Until we be <qex>past</qex> thy borders.</q> <rj><qau>Num. xxi.
22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Love, when once <qex>past</qex> government, is consequently
<qex>past shame</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1050 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Beyond, in time; after; <as>as, <ex>past</ex> the
hour</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is it not <qex>past</qex> two o'clock?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Above; exceeding; more than.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not <qex>past</qex> three quarters of a mile.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bows not <qex>past</qex> three quarters of a yard long.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Past</ent><br/
<hw>Past</hw> <pr>(p<adot/st)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By; beyond;
<as>as, he ran <ex>past</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The alarum of drums swept <qex>past</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pasta</ent><br/
<hw>pas"ta</hw> <pr>(p<adot/"st<adot/; Brit. p<acr/s"t<adot/)</pr>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Any of a variety of edible unleavened doughey
preparations made from flour, eggs and water, originating in
<country>Italy</country>, and shaped into various forms, such as solid
strings (as spaghetti), hollow tubes, or layered squares (ravioli). They
may be mixed with various sauces, often having a tomato base, or filled
with meat or cheese fillings.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Italian paste, paste.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paste</ent><br/
<hw>Paste</hw> <pr>(p<amac/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>paste</ets>, F. <ets>p<acir/te</ets>, L. <ets>pasta</ets>, fr. Gr.
<?/ barley broth; cf. <?/ barley porridge, <?/ sprinkled with salt, <?/
to sprinkle. Cf. <er>Pasty</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Patty</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A soft composition, as of flour moistened with water or
milk, or of earth moistened to the consistence of dough, as in making
potter's ware.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, in cookery, a dough prepared for the
crust of pies and the like; pastry dough.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A kind of cement made of flour and water, starch and
water, or the like, -- used for uniting paper or other substances, as in
bookbinding, etc., -- also used in calico printing as a vehicle for
mordant or color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A highly refractive vitreous composition, variously
colored, used in making imitations of precious stones or gems. See
<er>Strass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A soft confection made of the inspissated juice of
fruit, licorice, or the like, with sugar, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The mineral substance in which
other minerals are imbedded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paste eel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the vinegar
eel. See under <er>Vinegar</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paste</ent><br/
<hw>Paste</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pasted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pasting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To unite with paste; to fasten or
join by means of paste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteboard</ent><br/
<hw>Paste"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
stiff thick kind of paper board, formed of several single sheets pasted
one upon another, or of paper macerated and pressed into molds,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>A board on which pastry dough
is rolled; a molding board.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pasted</ent><br/
<hw>pasted</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Affixed with glue or
paste.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> glued, stuck to(predicate).</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastel</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.; cf. It.
<ets>pastello</ets>. Cf. <er>Pastil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
crayon made of a paste composed of a color ground with gum water.</def>
<altsp>[Sometimes incorrectly written <asp>pastil</asp>.]</altsp>
<ldquo/Charming heads in <xex>pastel</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>W.
Black.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant affording a blue dye; the
woad (<spn>Isatis tinctoria</spn>); also, the dye itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><--3. a drawing using pastel, or of a pastel shade. 4. the art or
process of drawing with pastels. 5. any of various light or pale colors.
6. a light literary work, as a sketch. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paster</ent><br/
<hw>Past"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pastes; <as>as, a <ex>paster</ex> in a government
department</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slip of paper, usually bearing a name, intended to
be pasted by the voter, as a substitute, over another name on a printed
ballot.</def> <mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastern</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of.
<ets>pasturon</ets>, F. <ets>p<acir/turon</ets>, fr. OF.
<ets>pasture</ets> a tether, for beasts while pasturing; prop., a
pasturing. See <er>Pasture</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The part of the
foot of the horse, and allied animals, between the fetlock and the
coffin joint. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Horse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The upper bone, or phalanx, of the foot is called the
<stype>great pastern bone</stype>; the second, the <stype>small pastern
bone</stype>; and the third, in the hoof, the <stype>coffin
bone</stype>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pastern joint</b></col>, <cd>the joint in the hoof of the
horse, and allied animals, between the great and small pastern
bones.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A shackle for horses while pasturing.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A patten.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paste-up</ent><br/
<hw>paste-up</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A composition of a flat object on a
board or other backing; <as>as, they showed him a <ex>paste-up</ex> of
the book jacket</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pasteurellosis</ent><br/
<hw>pasteurellosis</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>An acute infectious disease
characterized by pneumonia and blood infection.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> hemorrhagic septicemia.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteurian</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*teur"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to <person>Louis <etsep>Pasteur</etsep></person>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteurism</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*teur"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Fr.
<etsep>Pasteur</etsep>, a French scientist.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
method of treatment, devised by <persfn>Pasteur</persfn>, for preventing
certain diseases, as hydrophobia, by successive inoculations with an
attenuated virus of gradually increasing strength.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pasteurization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteurization</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*teur`i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A process
devised by Pasteur for preventing or checking fermentation in fluids,
such as wines, milk, etc., by exposure to a temperature of 140<deg/ F.,
thus destroying the vitality of the contained microorganisms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteurize</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*teur"ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
subject to pasteurization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To treat by pasteurism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteurizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"teur*iz`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One that
Pasteurizes, specif. an apparatus for heating and agitating,
fluid.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasteur's fluid</ent><br/
<hw>Pas`teur's" flu"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>An
artificial nutrient fluid invented by Pasteur for the study of alcoholic
fermentation, but used also for the cultivation of bacteria and other
organisms. It contains all the elements of protoplasm, and was
originally made of the ash of yeast, some ammonia compound, sugar, and
water.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasticcio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas*tic"ci*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr.
<ets>pasta</ets>. See <er>Paste</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A medley;
an olio.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>H. Swinburne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A work of art
imitating directly the work of another artist, or of more artists than
one.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A falsified work of art, as a vase or
statue made up of parts of original works, with missing parts
supplied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pasties</ent><br/
<hw>pas"ties</hw> <pr>(p<amac/s"t<emac/z)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pair
of adhesive patches worn to cover the nipples of exotic dancers and
striptease performers.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastille</ent><br/
<ent>Pastil</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pas"til</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pas*tille"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pastille</ets>, L.
<ets>pastillus</ets>a pastus food. See <er>Pasture</er>, and cf.
<er>Pastel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Pharmacy)</fld> <def>A small
cone or mass made of paste of gum, benzoin, cinnamon, and other
aromatics, -- used for fumigating or scenting the air of a
room.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An aromatic or medicated lozenge, especially one
used to soothe a sore throat; a troche.</def> <wns>[wns=1]</wns><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cough drop, troche.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>See <er>Pastel</er>, a crayon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastime</ent><br/
<hw>pas"time`</hw> <pr>(p<adot/s"t<imac/m`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pass</ets> + <ets>time</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>passetemps</ets>.]</ety> <def>That which amuses, and serves to make
time pass agreeably; sport; amusement; diversion; <as>as, that great
American <ex>pastime</ex>, baseball</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastime</ent><br/
<hw>pas"time`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To sport; to amuse one's
self.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastinaca</ent><br/
<hw>Pastinaca</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus comprising the
parsnips.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus Pastinaca.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastis</ent><br/
<hw>pastis</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*st<emac/s")</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
<fu>ca. 1925</fu>]</ety> <def>An anise-based liqueur similar to absinthe
but yellowish in color and containing no wormwood.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastness</ent><br/
<hw>past"ness</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
past.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastor</ent><br/
<hw>pas"tor</hw> <pr>(p<adot/s"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. <ets>pascere</ets>, <ets>pastum</ets>, to pasture, to feed. Cf.
<er>Pabulum</er>, <er>Pasture</er>, <er>Food</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A shepherd; one who has the care of flocks and herds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A guardian; a keeper; specifically
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, a minister having the charge of a church and
parish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A species of starling
(<spn>Pastor roseus</spn>), native of the plains of Western Asia and
Eastern Europe. Its head is crested and glossy greenish black, and its
back is rosy. It feeds largely upon locusts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorage</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office,
jurisdiction, or duty, of a pastor; pastorate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pastoralis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pastoral</ets>. See
<er>Pastor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to shepherds;
hence, relating to rural life and scenes; <as>as, a <ex>pastoral</ex>
life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to the care of souls, or to the pastor of a
church; <as>as, <ex>pastoral</ex> duties; a <ex>pastoral</ex>
letter.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pastoral staff</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>a staff,
usually of the form of a shepherd's crook, borne as an official emblem
by a bishop, abbot, abbess, or other prelate privileged to carry it. See
<er>Crook</er>, and <er>Crosier</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pastoral
Theology</b></col>, <cd>that part of theology which treats of the duties
of pastors.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A poem
describing the life and manners of shepherds; a poem in which the
speakers assume the character of shepherds; an idyl; a
bucolic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>pastoral</qex> is a poem in which any action or passion is
represented by its effects on a country life.</q>
<rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A cantata relating to rural life;
a composition for instruments characterized by simplicity and sweetness;
a lyrical composition the subject of which is taken from rural
life.</def> <rj><au>Moore (Encyc. of Music).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A letter of a pastor to his
charge; specifically, a letter addressed by a bishop to his diocese;
also <fld>(Prot. Epis. Ch.)</fld>, a letter of the House of Bishops, to
be read in each parish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorale</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pas`to*ra"le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A composition in a soft, rural style,
generally in 6-8 or 12-8 time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of dance; a kind of figure used in a
dance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorally</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In
a pastoral or rural manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the manner of a pastor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorate</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pastorat</ets>. See <er>Pastor</er>.]</ety> <def>The office, state,
or jurisdiction of a pastor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorium</ent><br/
<hw>Pas*to"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pastor</er>; cf. <er>Auditorium</er>.]</ety> <def>A parsonage; -- so
called in some Baptist churches.</def> <mark>[Southern U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorless</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no pastor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorling</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An insignificant
pastor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorly</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Appropriate to a pastor.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tor*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pastorate.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Bull.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastrami</ent><br/
<hw>pas*tra"mi</hw> <pr>(p<aum/s*tr<adot/"m<emac/)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A highly seasoned cut of smoked beef.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastry</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pastries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The place
where pastry is made.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Articles of food made of paste, or having a crust
made of paste, as pies, tarts, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pastry cook</b></col>, <cd>one whose occupation is to
make pastry; <as>as, the <ex>pastry cook</ex> of a
hotel</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasturable</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tur*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Fit for
pasture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasturage</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tur*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pasturage</ets>, F. <ets>p<acir/turage</ets>. See
<er>Pasture</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Grazing ground; grass land
used for pasturing; pasture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Grass growing for feed; grazing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The business of feeding or grazing cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasture</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pasture</ets>, F. <ets>p<acir/ture</ets>, L. <ets>pastura</ets>,
fr. <ets>pascere</ets>, <ets>pastum</ets>, to pasture, to feed. See
<er>Pastor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Food; nourishment.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Toads and frogs his <qex>pasture</qex> poisonous.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically: Grass growing for the food of cattle;
the food of cattle taken by grazing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Grass land for cattle, horses, etc.;
pasturage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He maketh me to lie down in green <qex>pastures</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xxiii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So graze as you find <qex>pasture</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasture</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ture</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pastured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pasturing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To feed, esp. to feed on
growing grass; to supply grass as food for; <as>as, the farmer
<ex>pastures</ex> fifty oxen; the land will <ex>pasture</ex> forty
cows.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasture</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ture</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To feed on growing grass; to
graze.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pastureland</ent><br/
<hw>pas"ture*land`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A field covered with grass or
herbage and suitable for grazing by livestock; pasture.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pasture, grazing land, lea, ley.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pastureless</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ture*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of pasture.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasturer</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"tur*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pastures;
one who takes cattle to graze. See <er>Agister</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasty</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like paste, as in
color, softness, stickness.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pasty</xex>
complexion.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pasty</ent><br/
<hw>Pas"ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pasties</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OF. <ets>past<eacute/</ets>, F.
<ets>p<acir/t<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Paste</er>, and cf.
<er>Patty</er>.]</ety> <def>A pie consisting usually of meat wholly
surrounded with a crust made of a sheet of paste, and often baked
without a dish; a meat pie.</def> <ldquo/If ye pinch me like a
<xex>pasty</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/Apple
<xex>pasties</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Dickens.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A large <qex>pasty</qex> baked in a pewter platter.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pat</ent><br/
<hw>Pat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Patted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Patting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>patschen</ets>,
Prov. G. <ets>patzen</ets>, to strike, tap.]</ety> <def>To strike gently
with the fingers or hand; to stroke lightly; to tap; <as>as, to
<ex>pat</ex> a dog</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gay <qex>pats</qex> my shoulder, and you vanish quite.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pat</ent><br/
<hw>Pat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A light, quik blow or
stroke with the fingers or hand; a tap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small mass, as of butter, shaped by
pats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It looked like a tessellated work of <qex>pats</qex> of
butter.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pat</ent><br/
<hw>Pat</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <ets>pat</ets> a light blow, D.
<ets>te pas</ets> convenient, pat, where <ets>pas</ets> is fr. F.
<ets>passer</ets> to pass.]</ety> <def>Exactly suitable; fit;
convenient; timely.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pat</xex> allusion.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pat</ent><br/
<hw>Pat</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pat manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I foresaw then 't would come in <qex>pat</qex> hereafter.</q>
<rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pataca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ta"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>The Spanish dollar; -- called also
<altname>patacoon</altname>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patache</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`tache"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. & Sp.
<ets>patache</ets>, P. <ets>patacho</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A tender to a fleet, formerly used for conveying men, orders, or
treasure.</def> <mark>[Spain & Portugal]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patacoon</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`ta*coon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Pataca</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patagium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*ta"gi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Patagia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., an edge or
border.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>In bats, an expansion
of the integument uniting the fore limb with the body and extending
between the elongated fingers to form the wing; in birds, the similar
fold of integument uniting the fore limb with the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a pair of small vesicular
organs situated at the bases of the anterior wings of lepidopterous
insects. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Butterfly</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patagonian</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`a*go"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Patagonia.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native of
Patagonia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patamar</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"a*mar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
name.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A vessel resembling a grab, used in
the coasting trade of Bombay and Ceylon.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pattemar</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patas</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tas"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
West African long-tailed monkey (<spn>Cercopithecus ruber</spn>); the
red monkey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patavinity</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`a*vin"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patavinitas</ets>, fr. <ets>Patavium</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>patavinit<eacute/</ets>]</ety> <def>The use of local or provincial
words, as in the peculiar style or diction of Livy, the Roman historian;
-- so called from Patavium, now Padua, the place of Livy's
nativity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patch</ent><br/
<hw>Patch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pacche</ets>;
of uncertain origin, perh. for <ets>placche</ets>; cf. Prov. E.
<ets>platch</ets> patch, LG. <ets>plakk</ets>, <ets>plakke</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece of cloth, or other suitable material, sewed or
otherwise fixed upon a garment to repair or strengthen it, esp. upon an
old garment to cover a hole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Patches</qex> set upon a little breach.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A small piece of anything
used to repair a breach; <as>as, a <ex>patch</ex> on a kettle, a roof,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small piece of black silk stuck on the face, or
neck, to hide a defect, or to heighten beauty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your black <qex>patches</qex> you wear variously.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>A piece of greased cloth or
leather used as wrapping for a rifle ball, to make it fit the
bore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Fig.: Anything regarded as a patch; a small piece of
ground; a tract; a plot; <as>as, scattered <ex>patches</ex> of trees or
growing corn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Employed about this <qex>patch</qex> of ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A block on the muzzle of a gun, to
do away with the effect of dispart, in sighting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A paltry fellow; a rogue; a ninny; a fool.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/Thou scurvy
<xex>patch</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Patch ice</b></col>, <cd>ice in overlapping pieces in the
sea.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft patch</b></col>, <cd>a patch for covering a
crack in a metallic vessel, as a steam boiler, consisting of soft
material, as putty, covered and held in place by a plate bolted or
riveted fast.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patch</ent><br/
<hw>Patch</hw> <pr>(p<acr/ch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Patched</conjf> <pr>(p<acr/cht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Patching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To mend
by sewing on a piece or pieces of cloth, leather, or the like; <as>as,
to <ex>patch</ex> a coat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mend with pieces; to repair with pieces festened
on; to repair clumsily; <as>as, to <ex>patch</ex> the roof of a
house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To adorn, as the face, with a patch or
patches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ladies who <qex>patched</qex> both sides of their faces.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make of pieces or patches; to repair as with
patches; to arrange in a hasty or clumsy manner; -- generally with
<xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>patch</ex> up a truce</as>.</def>
<ldquo/If you'll <xex>patch</xex> a quarrel.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patchboard</ent><br/
<hw>patch"board</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A circuit
board where circuits are completed and modified by making connections
with patchcords.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> switchboard, plugboard.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patchcord</ent><br/
<hw>patch"cord</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A conducting cord with a plug at
each end, used to make connections between circuit terminals at a
patchboard.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patched</ent><br/
<hw>patched</hw> <pr>(p<acr/cht)</pr> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Mended, usually clumsily by covering a hole with a patch; <as>as,
<ex>patched</ex> jeans</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[<pos>p. p.</pos> of <er>patch</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>.]</ety> <def>Partly covered; <as>as, The field was
<ex>patched</ex> with ice and snow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patcher</ent><br/
<hw>Patch"er</hw> <pr>(p<acr/ch"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who patches or botches.</def> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patchery</ent><br/
<hw>Patch"er*y</hw> <pr>(p<acr/ch"<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Botchery; covering of defects; bungling; hypocrisy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patchiness</ent><br/
<hw>patchiness</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Unevenness in quality or
performance.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patching</ent><br/
<hw>patching</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of mending a hole in a
garment by sewing a patch over it.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patchingly</ent><br/
<hw>Patch"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Knavishly;
deceitfully.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patchouly</ent><br/
<ent>Patchouli</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*tchou"li</hw>, <hw>Pa*tchou"ly</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. F. <ets>patchouli</ets>; prob. of East Indian
origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A small shrubby
mintlike plant (<spn>Pogostemon cablin</spn> syn. <spn>Pogostemon
Patchouli</spn>) of the East Indies, yielding an essential oil from
which a highly valued perfume is made.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pachouli, Pogostemon cablin.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The heavy perfume made from the patchouli
plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Patchouly camphor</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
substance homologous with and resembling borneol, found in patchouly
oil.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patch up</ent><br/
<hw>patch` up"</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>To mend by patching; to patch; --
also used figuratively; <as>as, to <ex>patch up</ex> frayed relations
bewteen parties</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patchwork</ent><br/
<hw>Patch"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work composed of
pieces sewed together, esp. pieces of various colors and figures; hence,
anything put together of incongruous or ill-adapted parts; something
irregularly or clumsily composed; a thing patched up.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patchy</ent><br/
<hw>Patch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of, or covered
with, patches; abounding in patches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pate</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`t<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Patt<eacute/</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pate</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`t<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<acir/t<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pie. See
<er>Patty</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A kind of platform with a
parapet, usually of an oval form, and generally erected in marshy
grounds to cover a gate of a fortified place.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pate</ent><br/
<hw>Pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG. & Prov. G.
<ets>pattkopf</ets>, <ets>patzkopf</ets>, scabby head; <ets>patt</ets>,
<ets>patz</ets>, scab + <ets>kopf</ets> head.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The head of a person; the top, or crown, of the head.</def>
<mark>[Now generally used in contempt or ridicule.]</mark><-- esp., bald
pate --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His mischief shall return upon his own head, and his violent
dealing shall come down upon his own <qex>pate</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
vii. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fat paunches have lean <qex>pate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The skin of a calf's head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pated</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a pate; -- used
only in composition; <as>as, long-<ex>pated</ex>;
shallow-<ex>pated</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patee</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pattee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patefaction</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`e*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patefactio</ets>, fr. <ets>patefacere</ets> to open;
<ets>patere</ets> to lie open + <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety>
<def>The act of opening, disclosing, or manifesting; open
declaration.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patela</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pat"e*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>patel<amac/</ets>.]</ety> <def>A large flat-bottomed trading boat
peculiar to the river Ganges; -- called also
<altname>puteli</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*tel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Patell<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a small pan, the
kneepan, dim. of <ets>patina</ets>, <ets>patena</ets>, a pan,
dish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small dish, pan, or vase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The kneecap; the kneepan; the cap
of the knee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of marine gastropods,
including many species of limpets. The shell has the form of a flattened
cone. The common European limpet (<spn>Patella vulgata</spn>) is largely
used for food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of apothecium in lichens,
which is orbicular, flat, and sessile, and has a special rim not a part
of the thallus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patellar</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tel"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the patella, or kneecap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- ##?? patellar tendon --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patelliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tel"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Patella</ets> + <ets>form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pattelliforme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the form of a
patella.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Resembling a limpet of the genus
Patella.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patellula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*tel"lu*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Patellul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>patella</ets>. See <er>Patella</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A cuplike sucker on the feet of certain insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paten</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>patina</ets>,
<ets>patena</ets>, fr. L. <ets>patina</ets>, <ets>patena</ets>, a pan;
cf. L. <ets>patere</ets> to be open, E. <ets>patent</ets>, and Gr.
<grk>pata`nh</grk> a kind of flat dish: cf. F. <ets>pat<egrave/ne</ets>.
Cf. <er>Patina</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A plate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The place on which the
consecrated bread is placed in the Eucharist, or on which the host is
placed during the Mass. It is usually small, and formed as to fit the
chalice, or cup, as a cover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><altsp>[Written also <asp>patin</asp>,
<asp>patine</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patena</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pat"e*na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.]</ety>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A paten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patena</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*te"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Pg.
<ets>patena</ets> a paten.]</ety> <def>A grassy expanse in the hill
region of Ceylon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patency</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"ten*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Patent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The condition of being open,
enlarged, or spread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being patent or evident.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1051 --></p>

<p><ent>Patent</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ent</hw> <pr>(p<acr/t"<it>e</it>nt <or/
p<amac/t"<it>e</it>nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>patens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>patere</ets> to be open: cf. F.
<ets>patent</ets>. Cf. <er>Fathom</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<note>(<xex>Oftener pronounced</xex> <pr>p<amac/t"<it>e</it>nt</pr>
<xex>in this sense</xex>)</note> <def>Open; expanded; evident; apparent;
unconcealed; manifest; public; conspicuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had received instructions, both <qex>patent</qex> and
secret.</q> <rj><qau>Motley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Open to public perusal; -- said of a document
conferring some right or privilege; <as>as, letters
<ex>patent</ex></as>. See <cref>Letters patent</cref>, under 3d
<er>Letter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Appropriated or protected by letters patent; secured
by official authority to the exclusive possession, control, and disposal
of some person or party; patented; <as>as, a <ex>patent</ex> right;
<ex>patent</ex> medicines.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Madder . . . in King Charles the First's time, was made a
<qex>patent</qex> commodity.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Spreading; forming a nearly right
angle with the steam or branch; <as>as, a <ex>patent</ex>
leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Patent leather</b></col>, <cd>a varnished or lacquered
leather, used for boots and shoes, and in carriage and harness
work.</cd> -- <col><b>Patent office</b></col>, <cd>a government bureau
for the examination of inventions and the granting of patents.</cd> --
<col><b>Patent right</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The exclusive right to
an invention, and the control of its manufacture</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>The right, granted by the sovereign, of exclusive
control of some business of manufacture, or of the sale of certain
articles, or of certain offices or prerogatives.</cd> -- <col><b>Patent
rolls</b></col>, <cd>the registers, or records, of
patents.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patent</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>patente</ets>. See
<er>Patent</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A letter patent,
or letters patent; an official document, issued by a sovereign power,
conferring a right or privilege on some person or party.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A writing securing to an
invention.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A document making a grant and
conveyance of public lands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Four other gentlemen of quality remained mentioned in that
<qex>patent</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the United States, by the act of 1870, patents for
inventions are issued for seventeen years, without the privilege of
renewal except by act of Congress.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The right or privilege conferred by such a document;
hence, figuratively, a right, privilege, or license of the nature of a
patent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you are so fond over her iniquity, give her <qex>patent</qex>
to offend.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patent</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ent</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Patented</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Patenting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To grant by patent; to make the
subject of a patent; to secure or protect by patent; <as>as, to
<ex>patent</ex> an invention; to <ex>patent</ex> public
lands.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patentable</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ent*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suitable to be
patented; capable of being patented.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patentee</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`ent*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One to whom a
grant is made, or a privilege secured, by patent.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patent-hammered</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ent-ham"mered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Stone
Cutting)</fld> <def>Having a surface dressed by cutting with a hammer
the head of which consists of broad thin chisels clamped
together.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patently</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ent*ly</hw> <pr>(?; see <er>Patent</er>, <pos>a.</pos>)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>Openly; evidently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pat"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pater<ae/</plw>(<?/).</plu> <ety>[L., fr. <ets>patere</ets> to lie
open.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A saucerlike vessel of earthenware or
metal, used by the Greeks and Romans in libations and
sacrifices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A circular ornament, resembling a
dish, often worked in relief on friezes, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paterero</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`e*re"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pederero</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paterfamilias</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`ter*fa*mil`i*as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pateresfamilias</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L., fr. <ets>pater</ets> father + <ets>familias</ets>, gen. of
<ets>familia</ets> family.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Law)</fld> <def>The head of
a family; in a large sense, the proprietor of an estate; one who is his
own master.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paternal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ter"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paternus</ets>, fr. <ets>pater</ets> a father: cf. F.
<ets>paternel</ets>. See <er>Father</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a father; fatherly; showing the disposition of a father;
guiding or instructing as a father; <as>as, <ex>paternal</ex>
care</as>.</def> <ldquo/Under <xex>paternal</xex> rule.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Received or derived from a father; hereditary;
<as>as, a <ex>paternal</ex> estate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their small <qex>paternal</qex> field of corn.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paternal government</b></col> <fld>(Polit.
Science)</fld>, <cd>the assumption by the governing power of a
quasi-fatherly relation to the people, involving strict and intimate
supervision of their business and social concerns, upon the theory that
they are incapable of managing their own affairs.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paternalism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ter"nal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Polit.
Science)</fld> <def>The theory or practice of paternal government. See
<cref>Paternal government</cref>, under <er>Paternal</er>.</def>
<rj><au>London Times.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paternalistic</ent><br/
<hw>pa*ter`nal*ist"ic</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Benevolent but sometimes
intrusive; -- used often of governments and the administration of large
organizations. See <cref>paternal government</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paternally</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ter"nal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a paternal
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paternity</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*ter"ni*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paternitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>paternit<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Paternal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The relation of a father to
his child; fathership; fatherhood; family headship; <as>as, the divine
<ex>paternity</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The world, while it had scarcity of people, underwent no other
dominion than <qex>paternity</qex> and eldership.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Derivation or descent from a father; male parentage;
<as>as, the <ex>paternity</ex> of a child</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Origin; authorship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>paternity</qex> of these novels was . . . disputed.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paternoster</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"ter*nos`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., Our
Father.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The Lord's prayer, so called from the
first two words of the Latin version.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A beadlike ornament in
moldings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Angling)</fld> <def>A line with a row of hooks and
bead-shaped sinkers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>An elevator of an inclined
endless traveling chain or belt bearing buckets or shelves which ascend
on one side loaded, and empty themselves at the top.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Paternoster pump</b></col>, <col><b>Paternoster
wheel</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a chain pump; a noria.</cd> --
<col><b>Paternoster while</b></col>, <cd>the space of time required for
repeating a paternoster.</cd> <au>Udall.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patesi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa*te"si</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Assyrian.]</ety>
<fld>(Babylonian Antiq.)</fld> <def>A religious as well as a secular
designation applied to rulers of some of the city states of ancient
Chaldea, as Lagash or Shirpurla, who were conceived to be direct
representatives of the tutelary god of the place.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>path</ent><br/
<hw>path</hw> <pr>(p<adot/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>paths</plw> <pr>(p<adot/<th/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[AS.
<ets>p<ae/<edh/</ets>, <ets>pa<edh/</ets>; akin to D. <ets>pad</ets>, G.
<ets>pfad</ets>, of uncertain origin; cf. Gr. <grk>pa`tos</grk>, Skr.
<ets>patha</ets>, <ets>path</ets>. <root/21.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
trodden way; a footway.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dewy <qex>paths</qex> of meadows we will tread.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A way, course, or track, in which anything moves or
has moved; route; passage; an established way; <as>as, the <ex>path</ex>
of a meteor, of a caravan, of a storm, of a pestilence</as>. Also used
figuratively, of a course of life or action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the <qex>paths</qex> of the Lord are mercy and truth.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xxv. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>paths</qex> of glory lead but to the grave.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Path</ent><br/
<hw>Path</hw> <pr>(p<adot/<th/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pathed</conjf>
<pr>(p<adot/<th/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pathing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a path in, or on
(something), or for (some one).</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Pathing</xex> young Henry's unadvised ways.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Path</ent><br/
<hw>Path</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To walk or go.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathan</ent><br/
<hw>Pathan</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Afghanistan, especially of the Pashtun tribes of southern
Afghanistan.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Afghan, Afghanistani, Pashto, pashtun.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pathematic</ent><br/
<hw>path`e*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/th`<esl/*m<acr/t"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paqhmatiko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>pa`qhma</grk>
a suffering, <grk>paqei^n</grk>, to suffer.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining
to, or designating, emotion or suffering.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chalmers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*th<ecr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>patheticus</ets>, Gr. <grk>paqhtiko`s</grk>, fr.
<grk>paqei^n</grk>, <grk>pa`schein</grk>, to suffer: cf. F.
<ets>path<eacute/tique</ets>. See <er>Pathos</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Expressing or showing anger; passionate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Affecting or moving the tender emotions, esp. pity
or grief; full of pathos; <as>as, a <ex>pathetic</ex> song or
story</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pathetic</xex> action.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No theory of the passions can teach a man to be
<qex>pathetic</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>E. Porter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pathetic muscle</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
superior oblique muscle of the eye.</cd> -- <col><b>Pathetic
nerve</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the fourth cranial, or
trochlear, nerve, which supplies the superior oblique, or pathetic,
muscle of the eye.</cd> -- <col><b>The pathetic</b></col>, <cd>a style
or manner adapted to arouse the tender emotions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathetical</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*thet"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pathetic.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> -- <wordforms><wf>Pa*thet"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Pa*thet"ic*al*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathetism</ent><br/
<hw>Path"e*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>path<eacute/tisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Mesmerism</er>.</def>
<rj><au>L. Sunderland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathfinder</ent><br/
<hw>Path"find`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who discovers
a way or path; one who explores untraversed regions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The cow is the true <qex>pathfinder</qex> and pathmaker.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Burroughs.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathic</ent><br/
<hw>Path"ic</hw> <pr>(p<acr/th"<icr/c)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pathicus</ets>, Gr. <grk>paqiko`s</grk>, passive, fr.
<grk>paqei^n</grk>, <grk>pa`schei^n</grk>, to suffer]</ety> <def>A male
who submits to the crime against nature; a catamite.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
<note><hand/ The term <ldquo/crime against nature<rdquo/ to refer to
homosexual activity is now (2002) seldom used except by religious
conservatives. It was in the 1913 Webster, and is left here for
historical purposes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathic</ent><br/
<hw>Path"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paqiko`s</grk>.]</ety>
<def>Passive; suffering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathless</ent><br/
<hw>Path"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no beaten
path or way; untrodden; impenetrable; <as>as, <ex>pathless</ex>
woods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trough the heavens' wide, <qex>pathless</qex> way.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathmaker</ent><br/
<hw>Path"mak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, makes a way or path.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathogene</ent><br/
<ent>Pathogen</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Path"o*gen</hw>, <hw>Path"o*gene</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pathogenic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Any microorganism which causes disease; a pathogenic organism; an
infectious microorganism; a bacterium, virus, or other agent which can
cause disease by infection; -- opposed to <contr>zymogene</contr>. The
spelling <ex>pathogene</ex> is now archaic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathogenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Path`o*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Pathogeny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathogenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Path`o*ge*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Pathogenic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathogenic</ent><br/
<hw>Path`o*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pa`qos</grk> disease + the root of <grk>ge`nos</grk> birth.]</ety>
<fld>(Med. & Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to pathogeny; producting
disease; <as>as, a <ex>pathogenic</ex> organism; a <ex>pathogenic</ex>
bacterium.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathogeny</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*thog"e*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The generation, and method of development, of disease;
<as>as, the <ex>pathogeny</ex> of yellow fever is unsettled</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>That branch of pathology which treats of the
generation and development of disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathognomonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*thog`no*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
skilled in judging of diseases; <grk>pa`qos</grk> a disease + <?/
skilled: cf. F. <ets>pathognomonique</ets>. See <er>Gnomic</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Specially or decisively characteristic of a
disease; indicating with certainty a disease; <as>as, a
<ex>pathognomonic</ex> symptom</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The true <qex>pathognomonic</qex> sign of love jealousy.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathognomy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*thog"no*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ passion
+ <?/ a judgment, fr. <?/, <?/, to know.]</ety> <def>Expression of the
passions; the science of the signs by which human passions are
indicated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathological</ent><br/
<ent>Pathologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Path`o*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Path`o*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>pathologique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
pathology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>caused by or due to disease;
abnormal; morbid; <as>as, <ex>pathological</ex> tissue; a
<ex>pathological</ex> condition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Path`o*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pathologist</ent><br/
<hw>pa*thol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pathologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One skilled in pathology; an
investigator in pathology; <as>as, the <ex>pathologist</ex> of a
hospital, whose duty it is to determine the causes of the
diseases</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pathology</ent><br/
<hw>pa*thol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(-j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>pathologies</plw> <pr>(-j<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>pa`qos</grk> a suffering, disease + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>pathologie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
science which treats of diseases, their nature, causes, progress,
symptoms, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pathology</xex> is <xex>general</xex> or
<xex>special</xex>, according as it treats of disease or morbid
processes in general, or of particular diseases; it is also subdivided
into <xex>internal</xex> and <xex>external</xex>, or <xex>medical</xex>
and <xex>surgical</xex> pathology. Its departments are
<stype>nosology</stype>, <stype><ae/tiology</stype>, <stype>morbid
anatomy</stype>, <stype>symptomatology</stype>, and
<stype>therapeutics</stype>, which treat respectively of the
classification, causation, organic changes, symptoms, and cure of
diseases.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The condition of an organ, tissue,
or fluid produced by disease.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Celluar pathology</b></col>, <cd>a theory that gives
prominence to the vital action of cells in the healthy and diseased
functions of the body.</cd> <rj><au>Virchow.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathopoeia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Path`o*poe"ia</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-ias</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <grk>paqopoii`:a</grk>; <grk>pa`qos</grk> passion +
<grk>poiei^n</grk> to make.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A speech, or
figure of speech, designed to move the passion.</def>
<rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathos</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"thos</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"th<ocr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
from Gr. <grk>pa`qos</grk> a suffering, passion, fr. <grk>paqei^n</grk>,
<grk>pa`schei^n</grk>, to suffer; cf. <grk>po`nos</grk> toil, L.
<ets>pati</ets> to suffer, E. <ets>patient</ets>.]</ety> <def>That
quality or property of anything which touches the feelings or excites
emotions and passions, esp., that which awakens tender emotions, such as
pity, sorrow, and the like; contagious warmth of feeling, action, or
expression; pathetic quality; <as>as, the <ex>pathos</ex> of a picture,
of a poem, or of a cry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The combination of incident, and the <qex>pathos</qex> of
catastrophe.</q> <rj><qau>T. Warton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or character of those emotions, traits,
or experiences which are personal, and therefore restricted and
evanescent; transitory and idiosyncratic dispositions or feelings as
distinguished from those which are universal and deep-seated in
character; -- opposed to <contr>ethos</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suffering; the enduring of active stress or
affliction.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pathway</ent><br/
<hw>Path"way</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A footpath; a beaten
track; any path or course. Also used figuratively.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the way of righteousness is life; and in the <qex>pathway</qex>
thereof is no death.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xii. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We tread the <qex>pathway</qex> arm in arm.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patible</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patibilis</ets>, fr. <ets>pati</ets> to suffer.]</ety>
<def>Sufferable; tolerable; endurable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patibulary</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tib"u*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patibulum</ets> a gallows: cf. F. <ets>patibulaire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to the gallows, or to execution.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patibulated</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tib"u*la`ted</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hanged on a gallows.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patience</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tience</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"sh<eit/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>patience</ets>, fr. L. <ets>patientia</ets>. See
<er>Patient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or quality of being
patient; the power of suffering with fortitude; uncomplaining endurance
of evils or wrongs, as toil, pain, poverty, insult, oppression,
calamity, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strengthened with all might, . . . unto all <qex>patience</qex>
and long-suffering.</q> <rj><qau>Col. i. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I must have <qex>patience</qex> to endure the load.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who hath learned lowliness<br/
From his Lord's cradle, <qex>patience</qex> from his cross.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or power of calmly or contentedly waiting
for something due or hoped for; forbearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have <qex>patience</qex> with me, and I will pay thee all.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xviii. 29.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Constancy in labor or application;
perseverance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He learned with <qex>patience</qex>, and with meekness taught.</q>
<rj><qau>Harte.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sufferance; permission.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They stay upon your <qex>patience</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of dock (<spn>Rumex
Patientia</spn>), less common in America than in Europe; monk's
rhubarb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>Solitaire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Patience</er>, <er>Resignation</er>.</syn>
<usage> <xex>Patience</xex> implies the quietness or self-possession of
one's own spirit under sufferings, provocations, etc.;
<xex>resignation</xex> implies submission to the will of another. The
Stoic may have <xex>patience</xex>; the Christian should have both
<xex>patience</xex> and <xex>resignation</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patient</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tient</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"sh<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. L. <ets>patiens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>pati</ets>
to suffer. Cf. <er>Pathos</er>, <er>Passion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the quality of enduring; physically able to suffer or
bear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Patient</qex> of severest toil and hardship.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Fell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Undergoing pains, trials, or the like, without
murmuring or fretfulness; bearing up with equanimity against trouble;
long-suffering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Constant in pursuit or exertion; persevering; calmly
diligent; <as>as, <ex>patient</ex> endeavor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whatever I have done is due to <qex>patient</qex> thought.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Expectant with calmness, or without discontent; not
hasty; not overeager; composed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not <qex>patient</qex> to expect the turns of fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Forbearing; long-suffering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be <qex>patient</qex> toward all men.</q> <rj><qau>1 Thess. v.
14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patient</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tient</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that
which, is passively affected; a passive recipient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Malice is a passion so impetuous and precipitate that it often
involves the agent and the <qex>patient</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Gov. of
Tongue.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person under medical or surgical treatment; --
correlative to <inv>physician</inv> or <inv>nurse</inv>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a physician, . . . seeing his <qex>patient</qex> in a
pestilent fever.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In patient</b></col>, <cd>a patient who receives lodging
and food, as treatment, in a hospital or an infirmary.</cd> --
<col><b>Out patient</b></col>, <cd>one who receives advice and medicine,
or treatment, from an infirmary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patient</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tient</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To compose, to calm.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Patient</xex> yourself, madam.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patiently</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tient*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a patient manner.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patine</ent><br/
<ent>Patin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pat"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pat"ine</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A plate. See <er>Paten</er>.</def> <ldquo/Inlaid with
<xex>patines</xex> of bright gold.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patina</ent><br/
<hw>pat"ina</hw> <pr>(p<acr/t"<icr/*n<adot/; It.
p<aum/"t<esl/*n<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>patina</ets> a dish, a pan, a kind of cake. Cf.
<er>Paten</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A dish or plate of metal or
earthenware; a patella.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>The color or incrustation
which age gives to works of art; especially, the green rust which covers
ancient bronzes, coins, and medals.</def>
<rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patinate</ent><br/
<hw>patinate</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>to coat with a patina; to
patinize.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> patinize.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patinize</ent><br/
<hw>patinize</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>to coat with a patina.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> patinate.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"ti*o</hw> <pr>(p<aum/"t<esl/*<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Sp., a court]</ety> <fld>(Metal)</fld> <def>A paved yard or floor
where ores are cleaned and sorted, or where ore, salt, mercury, etc.,
are trampled by horses, to effect intermixture and
amalgamation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>patio process</xex> is used to reduce silver
ores by amalgamation.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In Spain, Spanish America, etc., a court or
courtyard of a house or other building; esp., an inner court open to the
sky.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pr>(p<acr/t"<esl/*<omac/`)</pr> <def>A usually paved
area adjacent to a dwelling, used for outdoor lounging, dining,
receptions of guests, etc.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patio furniture</ent><br/
<hw>pa"ti*o fur"ni*ture</hw> <def>Furniture such as chairs, tables,
settees or loungers, suited for use on a patio{3}, i.e. such that will
not be damaged by exposure to rain, sun or other outdoor
elements.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patisserie</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pa`tis`serie"</hw>, \'d8<hw>P<acir/`tis`serie"</hw></mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>p<acir/tisserie</ets>. See
<er>Pate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pastry.</def>
<rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a shop selling mostly pastries; a pastry
shop.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patly</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Fitly;
seasonably.</def> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patness</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Fitness or appropriateness;
striking suitableness; convenience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The description with equal <qex>patness</qex> may suit both.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patois</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tois"</hw> <pr>(p<adot/`tw<aum/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A dialect peculiar to the illiterate classes; a
provincial form of speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The jargon and <qex>patois</qex> of several provinces.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patolli</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tol"li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Mex.
<ets>patolli</ets> dice.]</ety> <def>An American Indian game analogous
to dice, probably originally a method of divination.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patonce</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tonce"</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*t<ocr/ns")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>patte d'once</ets> paw of an ounce.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>Having the arms growing broader and floriated toward the end; --
said of a cross. See <xex>Illust.</xex> 9 of <er>Cross</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrial</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*al</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"tr<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>patria</ets> fatherland, country, fr. <ets>pater</ets>
father.]</ety> <fld>(Lat. Gram.)</fld> <def>Derived from the name of a
country, and designating an inhabitant of the country; gentile; -- said
of a noun.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A patrial noun. Thus
<examp>Romanus</examp>, a Roman, and <examp>Troas</examp>, a woman of
Troy, are <ex>patrial</ex> nouns, or <ex>patrials</ex>.</def></def2>
<rj><au>Andrews.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarch</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*arch</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"tr<icr/*<aum/rk)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>patriarche</ets>, L. <ets>patriarcha</ets>, Gr.
<grk>paria`rchhs</grk>, fr. <grk>paria`</grk> lineage, especially on the
father's side, race; <grk>path`r</grk> father + <grk>'archo`s</grk> a
leader, chief, fr. <grk>'a`rchein</grk> to lead, rule. See
<er>Father</er>, <er>Archaic</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The father
and ruler of a family; one who governs his family or descendants by
paternal right; -- usually applied to heads of families in ancient
history, especially in Biblical and Jewish history to those who lived
before the time of Moses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch. & Gr. Ch.)</fld> <def>A dignitary
superior to the order of archbishops; <as>as, the <ex>patriarch</ex> of
Constantinople, of Alexandria, or of Antioch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A venerable old man; an elder. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>patriarch</qex> hoary, the sage of his kith and the
hamlet.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The monarch oak, the <qex>partiarch</qex> of trees.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryde.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarchal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tri*ar"chal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>patriarcal</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a
patriarch or to patriarchs; possessed by, or subject to, patriarchs;
<as>as, <ex>patriarchal</ex> authority or jurisdiction; a
<ex>patriarchal</ex> see; a <ex>patriarchal</ex> church.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characteristic of a patriarch; venerable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>About whose <qex>patriarchal</qex> knee<br/
Late the little children clung.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>Having an organization of
society and government in which the head of the family exercises
authority over all its generations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Patriarchal cross</b></col> <fld>(Her.)</fld>, <cd>a
cross, the shaft of which is intersected by two transverse beams, the
upper one being the smaller. See <xex>Illust.</xex> (2) of
<er>Cross</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Patriarchal dispensation</b></col>,
<cd>the divine dispensation under which the patriarchs lived before the
law given by Moses.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1052 --></p>

<p><ent>Patriarchate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tri*ar"chate</hw> <pr>(p<amac/`tr<icr/*<aum/r"k<asl/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>patriarcat</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The office, dignity, or jurisdiction of a patriarch.</def>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The residence of an ecclesiastic
patriarch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A patriarchal form of
government or society. See <er>Patriarchal</er>, <pos>a.</pos>,
3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarchdom</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*arch*dom</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"tr<icr/*<aum/rk*d<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or jurisdiction of a patriarch;
patriarchate.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarchic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tri*ar"chic</hw> <pr>(p<amac/'tr<icr/*<aum/r"k<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>patriarchicus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>patriarchiko`s</grk>.]</ety> <def>Patriarchal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarchism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*arch*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Government by
a patriarch, or the head of a family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarchship</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*arch*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A patriarchate.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*arch`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>patriarchi`a</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The jurisdiction of a
patriarch; patriarchship.</def> <rj><au>Brerewood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Government by a patriarch; patriarchism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patricentric</ent><br/
<hw>patricentric</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>centered upon the
father.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrician</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tri"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patricius</ets>, fr. <ets>patres</ets> fathers or senators, pl. of
<ets>pater</ets>: cf. F. <ets>patricien</ets>. See
<er>Paternal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the Roman <xex>patres</xex> (fathers) or senators, or
patricians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of, pertaining to, or appropriate to, a person of
high birth; noble; not plebeian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Born in the <qex>patrician</qex> file of society.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His horse's hoofs wet with <qex>patrician</qex> blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrician</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tri"cian</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>patricius</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>patricien</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld>
<def>Originally, a member of any of the families constituting the
<xex>populus Romanus</xex>, or body of Roman citizens, before the
development of the plebeian order; later, one who, by right of birth or
by special privilege conferred, belonged to the nobility.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person of high birth; a nobleman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One familiar with the works of the Christian
Fathers; one versed in patristic lore.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Colridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patricianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tri"cian*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The rank or
character of patricians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriciate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tri"ci*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The patrician
class; the aristocracy; also, the office of patriarch.</def>
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patricidal</ent><br/
<hw>Pat*ri"ci`dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to patricide; parricidal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patricide</ent><br/
<hw>Pat*ri"cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pater</ets> father + <ets>caedere</ets> to kill. Cf.
<er>Parricide</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The murderer of his
father.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The crime of one who murders his father. Same as
<er>Parricide</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patrikin</ent><br/
<hw>patrikin</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>one related on the father's
side.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> agnate, patrilineal kin, patrisib, patrilineal
sib.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patrilineage</ent><br/
<hw>patrilineage</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The line of descent traced
through the paternal side of the family.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> agnation.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patrilineal</ent><br/
<hw>patrilineal</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>tracing descent through the
male line; <as>as, a <ex>patrilineal</ex> society</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrimonial</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`ri*mo"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patrimonialis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>patrimonial</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to a patrimony; inherited from ancestors; <as>as, a
<ex>patrimonial</ex> estate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrimonially</ent><br/
<hw>Pat`ri*mo"ni*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By
inheritance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrimony</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ri*mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Patrimonies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>patrimonium</ets>, fr. <ets>pater</ets> father: cf. F.
<ets>patrimoine</ets>. See <er>Paternal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
right or estate inherited from one's father; or, in a larger sense, from
any ancestor.</def> <ldquo/'Reave the orphan of his
<xex>patrimony</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Formerly, a church estate or endowment.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriot</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*ot</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"tr<icr/*<ocr/t; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>patriote</ets>; cf. Sp. <ets>patriota</ets>, It.
<ets>patriotto</ets>; all fr. Gr. <grk>patriw`ths</grk> a
fellow-countryman, fr. <grk>pa`trios</grk> established by forefathers,
fr. <grk>path`r</grk> father. See <er>Father</er>.]</ety> <def>One who
loves his country, and zealously supports its authority and
interests.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such tears as <qex>patriots</qex> shed for dying laws.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriot</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*ot</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Becoming to a patriot;
patriotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriotic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tri*ot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>patriotique</ets>, Gr. <grk>patriwtiko`s</grk> belonging to a
fellow-countryman.]</ety> <def>Inspired by patriotism; actuated by love
of one's country; zealously and unselfishly devoted to the service of
one's country; <as>as, a <ex>patriotic</ex> statesman,
vigilance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriotical</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tri*ot"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Patriotic;
that pertains to a patriot.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pa`tri*ot"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriotism</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*ot*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>patriotisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Love of country; devotion to the
welfare of one's country; the virtues and actions of a patriot; the
passion which inspires one to serve one's country.</def>
<rj><au>Berkley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patriots' Day</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*ots' Day</hw>. <def>A legal holiday in the States of
Massachusetts and Maine, April 19, the anniversary of the battle of
Lexington in 1775. It was first observed in 1894.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patripassian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tri*pas"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>Patripassiani</ets>, pl.; L. <ets>pater</ets> father +
<ets>pati</ets>, <ets>passus</ets>, to suffer: cf. F.
<ets>patripassiens</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a
body of believers in the early church who denied the independent
pre<eum/xistent personality of Christ, and who, accordingly, held that
the Father suffered in the Son; a monarchian.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pa`tri*pas"sian*ism</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrist</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"trist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
patristics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patristical</ent><br/
<ent>Patristic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*tris"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*tris"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>patristique</ets>. See
<er>Paternal</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Fathers of the
Christian church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The voluminous editor of Jerome and of tons of
<qex>patristic</qex> theology.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patristics</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*tris"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That departnent
of historical theology which treats of the lives and doctrines of the
Fathers of the church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrizate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tri*zate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patrissare</ets>, <ets>patrizare</ets>; cf. Gr.
<grk>patria`zein</grk>.]</ety> <def>To imitate one's father.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrocinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*troc"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patrocinatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>patrocinari</ets> to patronize,
fr. <ets>patronus</ets> patron.]</ety> <def>To support; to
patronize.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Urquhart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrocination</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*troc`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
patrocinating or patronizing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Patrocinations</xex> of treason.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrociny</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*troc"i*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patrocinium</ets>.]</ety> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <def>See
<er>Patrocination</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrol</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*trol"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Patrolled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Patrolling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>patrouiller</ets>, O. & Prov. F. <ets>patrouiller</ets> to paddle,
paw about, patrol, fr. <ets>patte</ets> a paw; cf. D. <ets>poot</ets>
paw, G. <ets>pfote</ets>, and E. <ets>pat</ets>, v.]</ety> <def>To go
the rounds along a chain of sentinels; to traverse a police district or
beat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrol</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*trol"</hw> <pr>(p<adot/*tr<omac/l")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To go the rounds of, as a sentry, guard, or policeman; <as>as, to
<ex>patrol</ex> a frontier; to <ex>patrol</ex> a beat.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrol</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*trol"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>patrouille</ets>, OF.
<ets>patouille</ets>. See <er>Patrol</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A going of the rounds
along the chain of sentinels and between the posts, by a guard, usually
consisting of three or four men, to insure greater security from attacks
on the outposts.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A movement, by a small body of
troops beyond the line of outposts, to explore the country and gain
intelligence of the enemy's whereabouts.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
guard or men who go the rounds for observation; a detachment whose duty
it is to patrol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any perambulation of a particular line or district
to guard it; also, the men thus guarding; <as>as, a customs
<ex>patrol</ex>; a fire <ex>patrol</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In France there is an army of <qex>patrols</qex> to secure her
fiscal regulations.</q> <rj><qau>A. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>See <er>Boy Scout</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrole</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*trole"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Patrol</er>, <pos>n. & v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patroller</ent><br/
<hw>patroller</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>An individual or a member of a
group that patrols an area.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patrolling</ent><br/
<hw>patrolling</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The activity of going around or
through an area at regular intervals for security purposes.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> patrol.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patrolman</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*trol"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Patrolmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who patrols; a
watchman; especially, a policeman who patrols a particular precinct of a
town or city.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patrol wagon</ent><br/
<hw>patrol wagon</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>An enclosed truck used by
police to transport prisoners.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paddy wagon, Black Maria.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patron</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>patronus</ets>, fr. <ets>pater</ets> a father. See
<er>Paternal</er>, and cf. <er>Patroon</er>, <er>Padrone</er>,
<er>Pattern</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who protects, supports, or
countenances; a defender.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Patron</xex> of my life and
liberty.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>patron</xex> of true
holiness.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A master who
had freed his slave, but still retained some paternal rights over
him.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A man of distinction under whose protection
another person placed himself.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An advocate or
pleader.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let him who works the client wrong<br/
Beware the <qex>patron</qex>'s ire.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who encourages or helps a person, a cause, or a
work; a furtherer; a promoter; <as>as, a <ex>patron</ex> of
art</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Law)</fld> <def>One who has gift and
disposition of a benefice.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A guardian saint. -- called also <altname>patron
saint</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Padrone</er>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Patrons of Husbandry</b></col>, <cd>the grangers. See
<er>Granger</er>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patron</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To be a patron of; to patronize;
to favor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patron</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Doing the duty of a patron; giving
aid or protection; tutelary.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Patron saint</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>, <cd>a
saint regarded as the peculiar protector of a country, community,
church, profession, etc., or of an individual.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronage</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>patronage</ets>. Cf. LL. <ets>patronaticum</ets>, and L.
<ets>patronatus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Special countenance or
support; favor, encouragement, or aid, afforded to a person or a work;
<as>as, the <ex>patronage</ex> of letters; <ex>patronage</ex> given to
an author.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Business custom.</def> <mark>[Commercial
Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Guardianship, as of a saint; tutelary care.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The right of nomination to political office; also,
the offices, contracts, honors, etc., which a public officer may bestow
by favor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>The right of presentation to
church or ecclesiastical benefice; advowson.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronage</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*age</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To act as a patron of; to
maintain; to defend.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronal</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patronalis</ets>; cf. F. <ets>patronal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Patron;
protecting; favoring.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronate</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patronatus</ets>.]</ety> <def>The right or duty of a patron;
patronage.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Westm. Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patroness</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*ess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>patronnesse</ets>.]</ety> <def>A female patron or helper.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Night, best <qex>patroness</qex> of grief.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronization</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tron*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
patronizing; patronage; support.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronize</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Patronized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Patronizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To act as patron toward; to support; to countenance; to favor; to
aid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The idea has been <qex>patronized</qex> by two States only.</q>
<rj><qau>A. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To trade with customarily; to frequent as a
customer.</def> <mark>[Commercial Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To assume the air of a patron, or of a superior and
protector, toward; -- used in an unfavorable sense; <as>as, to
<ex>patronize</ex> one's equals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
patronizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronizing</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*i`zing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Showing
condescending favor; assuming the manner of airs of a superior toward
another.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pat"ron*i`zing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<au>Thackeray.</au></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronless</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"tron*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of a
patron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronomatology</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tro*nom`a*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>path`r</grk>, <grk>patro`s</grk>, a father + E.
<ets>onomatology</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of knowledge which deals
with personal names and their origin; the study of
patronymics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronymic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tro*nym"ic</hw> <pr>(-n<icr/m"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>patronymicus</ets>, Gr. <grk>patrwnymiko`s</grk>;
<grk>path`r</grk> father + <grk>'o`noma</grk> name: cf. F.
<ets>patronymique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Derived from ancestors; <as>as, a
<ex>patronymic</ex> denomination</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronymic</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tro*nym"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>patrwnymiko`n</grk>.]</ety> <def>A modification of the father's
name borne by the son; a name derived from that of a parent or ancestor;
<as>as, <examp>Pelides</examp>, the son of Peleus;
<examp>Johnson</examp>, the son of John; <examp>Macdonald</examp>, the
son of Donald; <examp>Paulowitz</examp>, the son of Paul</as>; also, the
surname of a family; the family name.</def> <rj><au>M. A.
Lower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In <country>Russia</country>, the <ex>patronymic</ex> is
taken routinely as a middle name, and is commonly used together with the
given name in addressing people with whom one is familiar, thus
<examp>Ivan Ivanovich</examp> would be commonly used to address Ivan,
whose father was Ivan; likewise <examp>Boris Michaelovich</examp> would
address Boris the son of Michael, and <examp>Lena Ivanova</examp> would
address Lena, the daughter of Ivan.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patronymical</ent><br/
<hw>Pa`tro*nym"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Patronymic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patroon</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*troon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>patroon</ets> a patron, a protector. See <er>Patron</er>.]</ety>
<def>One of the proprietors of certain tracts of land with manorial
privileges and right of entail, under the old Dutch governments of New
York and New Jersey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patroonship</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*troon"ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
patroon.</def> <rj><au>Irving.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattee</ent><br/
<ent>Patte</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pat`t<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pat*tee"</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>patt<eacute/</ets>,
fem. <ets>patt<eacute/e</ets>, fr. <ets>patte</ets> paw, foot. Cf.
<er>Patten</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Narrow at the inner, and
very broad at the other, end, or having its arms of that shape; -- said
of a cross. See <xex>Illust.</xex> (8) of <er>Cross</er>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pat<eacute/</asp>,
<asp>patee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattemar</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"te*mar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Patamar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patten</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>patin</ets> a
high-heeled shoe, fr. <ets>patte</ets> paw, foot. Cf. <er>Panton</er>,
<er>Patt<eacute/</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A clog or sole of wood,
usually supported by an iron ring, worn to raise the feet from the wet
or the mud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>patten</qex> now supports each frugal dame.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stilt.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattened</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ten*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing
pattens.</def> <ldquo/Some <xex>pattened</xex> girl.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jane
Austen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patter</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pattered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pattering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>pat</ets> to strike gently.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike with
a quick succession of slight, sharp sounds; <as>as, <ex>pattering</ex>
rain or hail; <ex>pattering</ex> feet.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stealing shower is scarce to <qex>patter</qex> heard.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mutter; to mumble; <as>as, to <ex>patter</ex>
with the lips</as>.</def> <au>Tyndale.</au> <ety>[In this sense, and in
the following, perh. from <ets>pater</ets>noster.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To talk glibly; to chatter; to harangue.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I've gone out and <qex>pattered</qex> to get money.</q>
<rj><qau>Mayhew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patter</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To spatter; to
sprinkle.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/And <xex>patter</xex> the water
about the boat.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. R. Drake.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[See <er>Patter</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, 2.]</ety>
<def>To mutter; as prayers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The hooded clouds] <qex>patter</qex> their doleful prayers.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To patter flash</b></col>, <cd>to talk in thieves'
cant.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patter</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A quick succession of
slight sounds; <as>as, the <ex>patter</ex> of rain; the <ex>patter</ex>
of little feet.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Glib and rapid speech; a voluble harangue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The cant of a class; patois; <as>as, thieves's
<ex>patter</ex>; gypsies' <ex>patter</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The language or oratory of a street peddler,
conjurer, or the like, hence, glib talk; a voluble harangue; mere talk;
chatter; also, specif., rapid speech, esp. as sometimes introduced in
songs.</def> <mark>[Cant or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patterer</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who patters, or
talks glibly; specifically, a street peddler.</def> <mark>[Cant,
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattern</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>patron</ets>, F. <ets>patron</ets>, a patron, also, a pattern. See
<er>Patron</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything proposed for
imitation; an archetype; an exemplar; that which is to be, or is worthy
to be, copied or imitated; <as>as, a <ex>pattern</ex> of a
machine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will be the <qex>pattern</qex> of all patience.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part showing the figure or quality of the whole; a
specimen; a sample; an example; an instance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He compares the <qex>pattern</qex> with the whole piece.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Stuff sufficient for a garment; <as>as, a dress
<ex>pattern</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Figure or style of decoration; design; <as>as, wall
paper of a beautiful <ex>pattern</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Something made after a model; a copy.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>patterns</qex> of things in the heavens.</q>
<rj><qau>Heb. ix. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Anything cut or formed to serve as a guide to
cutting or forming objects; <as>as, a dressmaker's
<ex>pattern</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A full-sized model around
which a mold of sand is made, to receive the melted metal. It is usually
made of wood and in several parts, so as to be removed from the mold
without injuring it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>a recognizable characteristic relationship or set of
relationships between the members of any set of objects or actions, or
the properties of the members; also, the set having a definable
relationship between its members.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note> Various collections of objects or markings are spoken of as a
<ex>pattern</ex>. Thus: the distribution of bomb or shell impacts on a
target area, or of bullet holes in a target; a set of traits or actions
that appear to be consistent throughout the members of a group or over
time within a group, as behavioral <ex>pattern</ex>, traffic
<ex>pattern</ex>, dress <ex>pattern</ex>; the wave <ex>pattern</ex> for
a spoken word; the <ex>pattern</ex> of intensities in a spectrum; a
grammatical <ex>pattern</ex>.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>A diagram showing the distribution
of the pellets of a shotgun on a vertical target perpendicular to the
plane of fire.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>the recommended flight path for an airplane to
follow as it approaches an airport for a landing. Same as
<altname>landing pattern</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>an image or diagram containing lines, usually
horizontal, vertical, and diagonal, sometimes of varying widths, used to
test the resolution of an optical instrument or the accuracy of
reproduction of image copying or transmission equipment. Same as
<altname>test pattern</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>pattern box</b></col>, <col><b>pattern
chain</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>pattern cylinder</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Figure Weaving)</fld>, <cd>devices, in a loom, for presenting
several shuttles to the picker in the proper succession for forming the
figure.</cd> -- <col><b>Pattern card</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A set
of samples on a card</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <cd>One of
the perforated cards in a Jacquard apparatus.</cd> -- <col><b>Pattern
reader</b></col>, <cd>one who arranges textile patterns.</cd> --
<col><b>Pattern wheel</b></col> <fld>(Horology)</fld>, <cd>a
count-wheel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattern</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"tern</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Patterned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Patterning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make or design
(anything) by, from, or after, something that serves as a pattern; to
copy; to model; to imitate.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[A temple] <qex>patterned</qex> from that which Adam reared in
Paradise.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To serve as an example for; also, to
parallel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pattern after</b></col>, <cd>to imitate; to
follow.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patterned</ent><br/
<hw>patterned</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Having describable patterns,
especially patterns of colors.</def> [Narrower terms:
<stype>banded</stype>, <stype>blotched, blotchy, splotched</stype>,
<stype>brindled, brindle, brinded, tabby</stype>, <stype>burled</stype>,
<stype>checked, checkered</stype>, <stype>dappled, mottled</stype>,
<stype>dotted, flecked, specked, speckled, stippled</stype>,
<stype>figured</stype>, <stype>floral, flowered</stype>,
<stype>laced</stype>, <stype>marbled, marbleized</stype>, <stype>moire,
watered</stype>, <stype>pinstriped, pinstripe(prenominal)</stype>,
<stype>slashed, streaked</stype>, <stype>spotted</stype>,
<stype>sprigged</stype>, <stype>streaked, streaky</stype>,
<stype>striped, stripy</stype>, <stype>tessellated</stype>,
<stype>veined, venose</stype>] <ant>plain, solid</ant><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patternlike</ent><br/
<hw>patternlike</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>resembling a
pattern or shape or outline.</def> <illu>a gigantic black and
<ex>patternlike</ex> figure against the eastern sky</illu><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>patternmaker</ent><br/
<hw>patternmaker</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Someone who makes patterns (as
for sewing or carpentery or metalworking).</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattern matching</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"tern match"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>pattern</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A technique in
automated data analysis, usually performed on a computer, by which a
group of characteristic properties of an unknown object is compared with
the comparable groups of characteristics of a set of known objects, to
discover the idenity or proper classification of the unknown
object.</def> <note>There are two major types of pattern matching,
<stype>statistical pattern matching</stype> and <stype>syntactic pattern
matching</stype>. In statistical pattern matching, the criteria used to
recognize identity or class membership vary, but in general some
combination of the differences in the groups of characteristics of known
and unknown objects are considered to be a measure of the difference
("distance") between them, and the closest known object or objects are
viewed as presenting the most likely identity or class for the unknown
object. In syntactic pattern matching, a set of known patterns, e.g. as
in the possible order of parts of speech in a language, is defined, and
the unknown pattern is compared to find that known pattern or patterns
which matches the unknown exactly. In general, statistical pattern
matching is used where properties of objects with continuous values are
being compared, and syntactic pattern matching where a complex
arrangement of at least two different objects may be built by
application of a set of rules (a "grammar") for combining the objects in
a specified order. Examples of the latter are natural and formal
languages.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattinson's process</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"tin*son's proc"ess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A
process of desilverizing argentiferous lead by repeated meltings and
skimmings, which concentrate the silver in the molten bath, the final
skimmings being nearly pure lad. The processwas invented in 1833 by Hugh
Lee <etsep>Pattinson</etsep>, an English metallurgist.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patty</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Patties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<acir/t<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Pasty</er>.]</ety> <def>A little
pie.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pattypan</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"ty*pan`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pan
for baking patties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A patty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Patulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pat"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>patulus</ets>, fr. <ets>patere</ets> to be open, extend.]</ety>
<def>Open; expanded; slightly spreading; having the parts loose or
dispersed; <as>as, a <ex>patulous</ex> calyx; a <ex>patulous</ex>
cluster of flowers.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eyes are large and <qex>patulous</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Hill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pau</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pau</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pah</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauciloquent</ent><br/
<hw>Pau*cil"o*quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Uttering few
words; brief in speech.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauciloquy</ent><br/
<hw>Pau*cil"o*quy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pauciloquium</ets>; <ets>paucus</ets> little + <ets>loqui</ets> to
speak.]</ety> <def>Brevity in speech.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paucispiral</ent><br/
<hw>Pau`ci*spi"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paucus</ets> few + E. <ets>spiral</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having few spirals, or whorls; <as>as, a <ex>paucispiral</ex>
operculum or shell</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paucity</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"ci*ty</hw> <pr>(p<add/"s<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>paucitas</ets>, fr. <ets>paucus</ets> few, little: cf. F.
<ets>paucit<eacute/</ets> See <er>Few</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Fewness; smallness of number; scarcity; rarity.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Revelation denies it by the stern reserve, the <qex>paucity</qex>,
and the incompleteness, of its communications.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Smallness of quantity; exiguity; insufficiency;
<as>as, <ex>paucity</ex> of blood</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paugy</ent><br/
<ent>Paugie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pau"gie</hw>, <hw>Pau"gy</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Paugies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Corrupted from Amer. Indian <ets>mishcuppauog</ets>. See
<er>Scup</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The scup. See
<er>Porgy</er>, and <er>Scup</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauhaugen</ent><br/
<hw>Pau*hau"gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[North Amer.
Indian.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The menhaden; -- called also
<altname>poghaden</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paul</ent><br/
<hw>Paul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pawl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paul</ent><br/
<hw>Paul</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An Italian silver coin. See
<er>Paolo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauldron</ent><br/
<hw>Paul"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Powldron</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A piece of armor
covering the shoulder at the junction of the body piece and arm
piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paulianist</ent><br/
<ent>Paulian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pau"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pau"li*an*ist</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>A
follower of <person><etsep>Paul</etsep> of Samosata</person>, a bishop
of <city>Antioch</city> in the third century, who was deposed for
denying the divinity of Christ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paulician</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"li*cian</hw> <pr>(p<add/"l<icr/*sh<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a
sect of Christian dualists originating in <country>Armenia</country> in
the seventh century. They rejected the Old Testament and the part of the
New.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1053 --></p>

<p><ent>Paulin</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"lin</hw> <pr>(p<add/"l<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Tarpaulin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauline</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"line</hw> <pr>(p<add/"l<imac/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Paulinus</ets>, fr. <ets>Paulus</ets> Paul.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the apostle <persfn>Paul</persfn>, or his writings;
resembling, or conforming to, the writings of <persfn>Paul</persfn>;
<as>as, the <ex>Pauline</ex> epistles; <ex>Pauline</ex>
doctrine.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My religion had always been <qex>Pauline</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>J. H.
Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paulist</ent><br/
<hw>Paul"ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>
<def>A member of <org>The Institute of the Missionary Priests of St.
Paul the Apostle</org>, founded in 1858 by the <person>Rev. I. T.
Hecker</person> of <city>New York</city>. The majority of the members
were formerly Protestants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paulownia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pau*low"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So
named from the Russian princess <person>Anna
<etsep>Pavlovna</etsep></person>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus
of trees of the order <ord>Scrophulariace<ae/</ord>, consisting of one
species, <spn>Paulownia imperialis</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The tree is native to Japan, and has immense
heart-shaped leaves, and large purplish flowers in panicles. The
capsules contain many little winged seeds, which are beautiful
microscopic objects. The tree is hardy in America as far north as
Connecticut.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paum</ent><br/
<hw>Paum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Palm</er> to cheat.]</ety> <def>To palm off by fraud; to cheat at
cards.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paunce</ent><br/
<hw>Paunce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pansy</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The pansy.</def>
<ldquo/The pretty <xex>paunce</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paunch</ent><br/
<hw>Paunch</hw> <pr>(p<add/nch <it>or</it> p<aum/nch; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>panch</ets>, <ets>pance</ets>, F.
<ets>panse</ets>, L. <ets>pantex</ets>, <ets>panticis</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The belly and its contents; the
abdomen; also, the first stomach, or rumen, of ruminants. See
<er>Rumen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A paunch mat; -- called also
<altname>panch</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The thickened rim of a bell, struck by the
clapper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A noticeably protruding belly; a potbelly.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paunch mat</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a thick mat
made of strands of rope, used to prevent the yard or rigging from
chafing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paunch</ent><br/
<hw>Paunch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paunched</conjf> <pr>(p<add/ncht <it>or</it> p<aum/ncht)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Paunching</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To pierce or rip the belly of; to eviscerate; to
disembowel.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stuff with food.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Udall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paunchy</ent><br/
<hw>Paunch"y</hw> <pr>(p<add/nch"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pot-bellied.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paune</ent><br/
<hw>Paune</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of bread. See
<er>Pone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauper</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Poor</er>.]</ety> <def>A very poor person; one without any means of
support, especially one dependent on private or public charity. Also
used adjectively; <as>as, <ex>pauper</ex> immigrants, <ex>pauper</ex>
labor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauperism</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"per*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>paup<eacute/risme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of being a pauper;
the state of indigent persons requiring support from the
community.</def> <rj><au>Whatly.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Poverty; indigence; penury; want; need;
destitution. See <er>Poverty</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauperization</ent><br/
<hw>Pau`per*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of reducing to pauperism.</def> <rj><au>C.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauperize</ent><br/
<hw>Pau"per*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pauperized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pauperizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
reduce to pauperism; <as>as, to <ex>pauperize</ex> the
peasantry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauropoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pau*rop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>pay^ros</grk> small + <ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of small myriapods having only nine
pairs of legs and destitute of trache<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pause</ent><br/
<hw>Pause</hw> <pr>(p<add/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pausa</ets>. See <er>Pose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A temporary
stop or rest; an intermission of action; interruption; suspension;
cessation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Temporary inaction or waiting; hesitation; suspence;
doubt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I stand in <qex>pause</qex> where I shall first begin.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In speaking or reading aloud, a brief arrest or
suspension of voice, to indicate the limits and relations of sentences
and their parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In writing and printing, a mark indicating the place
and nature of an arrest of voice in reading; a punctuation point;
<as>as, teach the pupil to mind the <ex>pauses</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A break or paragraph in writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He writes with warmth, which usually neglects method, and those
partitions and <qex>pauses</qex> which men educated in schools
observe.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A hold. See 4th <er>Hold</er>,
7.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Stop; cessation; suspension.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pause</ent><br/
<hw>Pause</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paused</conjf> <pr>(p<add/zd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pausing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pauser</ets>, L.
<ets>pausare</ets>. See <er>Pause</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Pose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make a short stop; to cease
for a time; to intermit speaking or acting; to stop; to wait; to
rest.</def> <ldquo/Tarry, <xex>pause</xex> a day or two.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pausing</qex> a while, thus to herself she mused.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be intermitted; to cease; <as>as, the music
<ex>pauses</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hesitate; to hold back; to delay.</def>
<rj><mark>[R.]</mark></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why doth the Jew <qex>pause</qex>? Take thy forfeiture.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj> <-- is this anti-semitic or what? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stop in order to consider; hence, to consider; to
reflect.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Take time to
<xex>pause</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pause upon</b></col>, <cd>to deliberate
concerning.</cd> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To intermit; stop; stay; wait; delay; tarry;
hesitate; demur.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pause</ent><br/
<hw>Pause</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to stop or rest; -- used
reflexively.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauser</ent><br/
<hw>Paus"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pauses.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pausingly</ent><br/
<hw>Paus"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With pauses; haltingly.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pauxi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Paux"i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
name: cf. Sp. <ets>pauji</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
curassow (<spn>Ourax pauxi</spn>), which, in South America, is often
domesticated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavage</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pavage</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Paviage</er>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavan</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pavane</ets>;
cf. It. & Sp. <ets>pavana</ets>, and Sp. <ets>pavon</ets>,
<ets>pavo</ets>, a peacock, L. <ets>pavo</ets>.]</ety> <def>A stately
and formal Spanish dance for which full state costume is worn; -- so
called from the resemblance of its movements to those of the
peacock.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pavane</asp>, <asp>paven</asp>,
<asp>pavian</asp>, and <asp>pavin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pave</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa`v<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from
<ets>paver</ets> to pave. See <er>Pave</er>.]</ety> <def>The
pavement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Nymphe du pav<eacute/</b></col> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <cd>a
prostitute who solicits in the street; a streetwalker.</cd> <mark>[A low
euphemism.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pave</ent><br/
<hw>Pave</hw> <pr>(p<amac/v)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Paved</conjf> <pr>(p<amac/vd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Paving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>paver</ets> to
pave, LL. <ets>pavare</ets>, from L. <ets>pavire</ets> to beat, ram, or
tread down; cf. Gr. <grk>pai`ein</grk> to beat, strike.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay or cover with stone, brick, or other material,
so as to make a firm, level, or convenient surface for vehicles, horses,
carriages, or persons on foot, to travel on; to floor with brick, stone,
or other solid material; <as>as, to <ex>pave</ex> a street; to
<ex>pave</ex> a court.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With silver <qex>paved</qex>, and all divine with gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>pave</qex> thy realm, and smooth the broken ways.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To make smooth, easy, and safe; to prepare, as
a path or way; <as>as, to <ex>pave</ex> the way to promotion; to
<ex>pave</ex> the way for an enterprise.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It might open and <qex>pave</qex> a prepared way to his own
title.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paved</ent><br/
<hw>paved</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>covered with a firm
surface; -- of pathways or roadways.</def> [Narrower terms:
<stype>asphalt, macadam, macadamized, tarmac, tarmacadam</stype>;
<stype>blacktopped</stype>, <stype>brick</stype>, <stype>cobblestone,
cobblestoned</stype>] [Ant: <ant>unpaved</ant>]<br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> hard-surfaced, surfaced, made-up [British], sealed
[Australian].</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavement</ent><br/
<hw>Pave"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>pavamentum</ets>, L. <ets>pavimentum</ets>. See
<er>Pave</er>.]</ety> <def>That with which anything is paved; a floor or
covering of solid material, laid so as to make a hard and convenient
surface for travel; a paved road or sidewalk; a decorative interior
floor of tiles or colored bricks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The riches of heaven's <qex>pavement</qex>, trodden gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pavement teeth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>flattened teeth which in certain fishes, as the skates and
cestracionts, are arranged side by side, like tiles in a
pavement.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavement</ent><br/
<hw>Pave"ment</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To furnish with a pavement; to
pave.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/How richly
<xex>pavemented</xex>!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paven</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pavan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paver</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who paves; one who
lays a pavement.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pavier</asp> and
<asp>pavior</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavesade</ent><br/
<hw>Pav`e*sade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pavise</er>.]</ety> <def>A canvas screen, formerly sometimes
extended along the side of a vessel in a naval engagement, to conceal
from the enemy the operations on board.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavesse</ent><br/
<ent>Pavese</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pa*vese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pa*vesse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pavise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paviage</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"vi*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A
contribution or a tax for paving streets or highways.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavian</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See Pavan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavid</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pavidus</ets>,
from <ets>pavere</ets> to be afraid.]</ety> <def>Timid; fearful.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavidity</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*vid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Timidity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavier</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"ier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A paver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paviin</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"vi*in</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"v<icr/*<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A glucoside found in species of the genus
<gen>Pavia</gen> of the Horse-chestnut family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavilion</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*vil"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pavillon</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pavilio</ets> a butterfly, also, a
tent, because spread out like a butterfly's wings.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A temporary movable habitation; a large tent; a marquee; esp., a
tent raised on posts.</def> <ldquo/[The] Greeks do pitch their brave
<xex>pavilions</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A single body or mass of
building, contained within simple walls and a single roof, whether
insulated, as in the park or garden of a larger edifice, or united with
other parts, and forming an angle or central feature of a large
pile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A flag, colors, ensign, or
banner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Tent</er>
<fld>(Her.)</fld></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That part of a brilliant which lies between the
girdle and collet. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Brilliant</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The auricle of the ear; also, the
fimbriated extremity of the Fallopian tube.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A covering; a canopy; figuratively, the
sky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pavilion</qex> of heaven is bare.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavilion</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*vil"ion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pavilioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pavilioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To furnish or cover with, or
shelter in, a tent or tents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The field <qex>pavilioned</qex> with his guardians bright.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavin</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pavan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paving</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
process of laying a pavement, or covering some place with a
pavement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pavement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavior</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"ior</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
paves; a paver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A machine for laying pavement; a rammer for driving
paving stones.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paviour, paving machine.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A brick or slab used for paving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paviour</ent><br/
<hw>paviour</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>See pavior{2}.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pavior, paving machine.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavise</ent><br/
<ent>Pavis</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pa*vis</hw>, <hw>Pa*vise</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OF. <ets>pavaix</ets>, F. <ets>pavois</ets>; cf. It.
<ets>pavese</ets>, LL. <ets>pavense</ets>; perh. named from
<ets>Pavia</ets> in Italy.]</ety> <fld>(Mil. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A large
heavy oblong shield covering the whole body, carried by a pavisor, who
sometimes also screened an archer with it. It was originally carried but
sometimes set up in permanent position</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pavis</asp>, <asp>pavais</asp>, <asp>pavese</asp>, and
<asp>pavesse</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavisor</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*vis"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil. Antiq.)</fld>
<def>A soldier who carried a pavise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavlovian</ent><br/
<hw>Pavlovian</hw> <pos>prop. adj.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
<person>Ivan <etsep>Pavlov</etsep></person>, or the principles of
conditioned responses that he investigated; <as>as, a <ex>Pavlovian</ex>
response</as>.</def> <illu>dog</illu><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pa"vo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
peacock. See <er>Peacock</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A genus of birds, including the peacocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The Peacock, a constellation of
the southern hemisphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavon</ent><br/
<hw>Pa"von</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small triangular
flag, esp. one attached to a knight's lance; a pennon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavone</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*vone"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>pavone</ets>, Sp. <ets>pavon</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pavo</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A peacock.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavonian</ent><br/
<hw>Pa*vo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a peacock.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pavonine</ent><br/
<hw>Pav"o*nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pavoninus</ets>, fr. <ets>pavo</ets> a peacock. See
<er>Peacock</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like, or
pertaining to, the genus Pavo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characteristic of a peacock; resembling the tail of
a peacock, as in colors; iridescent.</def> <rj><au>P.
Cleaveland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paw</ent><br/
<hw>Paw</hw> <pr>(p<add/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pawe</ets>,
<ets>poue</ets>, OF. <ets>poe</ets>: cf. <ets>patte</ets>, LG.
<ets>pote</ets>, D. <ets>poot</ets>, G. <ets>pfote</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The foot of a quadruped having claws, as the lion, dog,
cat, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The hand.</def> <mark>[Jocose]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Paw clam</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the tridacna;
-- so called because shaped like an animal's
<xex>paw</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paw</ent><br/
<hw>Paw</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To draw the forefoot along the
ground; to beat or scrape with the forefoot.</def> <rj><au>Job xxxix.
21.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paw</ent><br/
<hw>Paw</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pawed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pawing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass the paw over;
to stroke or handle with the paws; hence, to handle fondly or
rudely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scrape or beat with the forefoot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His hot courser <qex>pawed</qex> the Hungarian plane.</q>
<rj><qau>Tickell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawk</ent><br/
<hw>Pawk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
small lobster.</def> <rj><au>Travis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawky</ent><br/
<hw>Paw"ky</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>p<ae/cean</ets> to deceive.]</ety> <def>Arch; cunning; sly.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawl</ent><br/
<hw>Pawl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[W. <ets>pawl</ets> a
pole, a stake. Cf. <er>Pole</er> a stake.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>
<def>A pivoted tongue, or sliding bolt, on one part of a machine,
adapted to fall into notches, or interdental spaces, on another part, as
a ratchet wheel, in such a manner as to permit motion in one direction
and prevent it in the reverse, as in a windlass; a catch, click, or
detent. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Ratchet Wheel</er>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>paul</asp>, or <asp>pall</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pawl bitt</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a heavy
timber, set abaft the windlass, to receive the strain of the pawls.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Pawl rim</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Pawl
ring</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a stationary metallic ring
surrounding the base of a capstan, having notches for the pawls to catch
in.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawl</ent><br/
<hw>Pawl</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stop with a pawl; to drop the
pawls off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pawl the capstan</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Capstan</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawn</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Pan</er>, the
masticatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawn</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>paune</ets>,
<ets>poun</ets>, OF. <ets>peon</ets>, <ets>poon</ets>, F.
<ets>pion</ets>, LL. <ets>pedo</ets> a foot soldier, fr. L.
<ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot. See <er>Foot</er>, and cf.
<er>Pioneer</er>, <er>Peon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>A man or
piece of the lowest rank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawn</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>pan</ets> pledge, assurance,
skirt, piece, F. <ets>pan</ets> skirt, lappet, piece, from L.
<ets>pannus</ets>. See <er>Pane</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything
delivered or deposited as security, as for the payment of money
borrowed, or of a debt; a pledge. See <er>Pledge</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As for mortgaging or pawning, . . . men will not take
<qex>pawns</qex> without use [<it>i. e.</it>, interest].</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>State of being pledged; a pledge for the fulfillment
of a promise.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Redeem from broking <qex>pawn</qex> the blemish'd crown.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the morning dew is a <qex>pawn</qex> of the evening
fatness.</q> <rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A stake hazarded in a wager.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My life I never held but as a <qex>pawn</qex><br/
To wage against thy enemies.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>In pawn</b></col>, <col><b>At
pawn</b></col></mcol>, <cd>in the state of being pledged.</cd>
<ldquo/Sweet wife, my honor is <xex>at pawn</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
-- <col><b>Pawn shop</b></col>, <cd>a shop where a pawnbroker does
business.</cd> -- <col><b>Pawn ticket</b></col>, <cd>a receipt given by
the pawnbroker for an article pledged.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawn</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pawned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pawning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give or deposit in
pledge, or as security for the payment of money borrowed; to put in
pawn; to pledge; <as>as, to <ex>pawn</ex> one's watch</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> hock [colloq].</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>pawned</qex> the last remaining piece of plate.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pledge for the fulfillment of a promise; to
stake; to risk; to wager; to hazard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pawning</qex> his honor to obtain his lust.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawnable</ent><br/
<hw>Pawna*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
pawned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawnbroker</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn"bro`ker</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes a
business of lending money on the security of personal property pledged
or deposited in his keeping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawnbroking</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn"bro`king</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The business of a
pawnbroker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawnee</ent><br/
<hw>Pawn*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One
or two whom a pledge is delivered as security; one who takes anything in
pawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawnees</ent><br/
<hw>Paw`nees"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Pawnee</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A
tribe of Indians (called also <altname>Loups</altname>) who formerly
occupied the region of the Platte river, but now (1890) live mostly in
the Indian Territory (Oklahoma). The term is often used in a wider sense
to include also the related tribes of Rickarees and Wichitas. Called
also <altname>Pani</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawnor</ent><br/
<ent>Pawner</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pawn"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pawn*or"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who pawns or pledges
anything as security for the payment of borrowed money or of a
debt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pawpaw</ent><br/
<hw>Paw`paw"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Papaya</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pax</ent><br/
<hw>Pax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pax</ets> peace.
See <er>Peace</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The kiss
of peace; also, the embrace in the sanctuary now substituted for it at
High Mass in Roman Catholic churches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A tablet or board, on which
is a representation of Christ, of the Virgin Mary, or of some saint and
which, in the Mass, was kissed by the priest and then by the people, in
medi<ae/val times; an osculatory. It is still used in communities,
confraternities, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Kiss the <qex>pax</qex>, and be quiet like your neighbors.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Friendship, or a friend; -- esp. in the phrases
<xex>to make pax with</xex>, to make friends with, <xex>to be good
pax</xex>, to be good friends; also, truce; -- used esp.
interjectionally.</def> <mark>[Eng. Schoolboy Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paxillose</ent><br/
<hw>Pax"il*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paxillus</ets> a small stake.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Resembling a little stake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paxillus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pax*il"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Paxilli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a peg.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a peculiar kind of spines covering the
surface of certain starfishes. They are pillarlike, with a flattened
summit which is covered with minute spinules or granules. See
<xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paxwax</ent><br/
<hw>Pax"wax`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>faxvax</ets>, fr. AS. <ets>feax</ets> hair (akin to OHG.
<ets>fahs</ets>) + <ets>weaxan</ets> to grow. See <er>Wax</er> to grow,
and cf. <er>Faxed</er>, <er>Pectinate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The strong ligament of the back of the neck in quadrupeds. It
connects the back of the skull with dorsal spines of the cervical
vertebr<ae/, and helps to support the head. Called also
<altname>paxywaxy</altname> and <altname>packwax</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paxywaxy</ent><br/
<hw>Pax"y*wax`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Paxwax</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pay</ent><br/
<hw>Pay</hw> <pr>(p<amac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>peier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>picare</ets> to pitch, <ets>pix</ets>
pitch: cf. OF. <ets>peiz</ets> pitch, F. <ets>poix</ets>. See
<er>Pitch</er> a black substance.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To
cover, as bottom of a vessel, a seam, a spar, etc., with tar or pitch,
or waterproof composition of tallow, resin, etc.; to smear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pay</ent><br/
<hw>Pay</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Paid</conjf> <pr>(p<amac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Paying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>paien</ets>, F.
<ets>payer</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pacare</ets> to pacify, appease, fr.
<ets>pax</ets>, <ets>pacis</ets>, peace. See <er>Peace</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To satisfy, or content; specifically, to satisfy
(another person) for service rendered, property delivered, etc.; to
discharge one's obligation to; to make due return to; to compensate; to
remunerate; to recompense; to requite; <as>as, to <ex>pay</ex> workmen
or servants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>May no penny ale them <qex>pay</qex> [<it>i. e.</it>,
satisfy].</q> <rj><qau>P. Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[She] <qex>pays</qex> me with disdain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively: To compensate justly; to
requite according to merit; to reward; to punish; to retort or retaliate
upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For which, or pay me quickly, or I'll <qex>pay</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To discharge, as a debt, demand, or obligation, by
giving or doing what is due or required; to deliver the amount or value
of to the person to whom it is owing; to discharge a debt by delivering
(money owed).</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pay</xex> me that thou owest.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Matt. xviii. 28.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have patience with me, and I will <qex>pay</qex> thee all.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xviii. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If they <qex>pay</qex> this tax, they starve.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To discharge or fulfill, as a duty; to perform or
render duty, as that which has been promised.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This day have I <qex>paid</qex> my vows.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. vii.
14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To give or offer, without an implied obligation;
<as>as, to <ex>pay</ex> attention; to <ex>pay</ex> a
visit.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not <qex>paying</qex> me a welcome.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pay off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To make
compensation to and discharge; <as>as, <ex>to pay off</ex> the crew of a
ship</as></cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To allow (a thread, cord, etc.) to run
off; to unwind.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>to bribe.</cd> -- <col><b>To pay
one's duty</b></col>, <cd>to render homage, as to a sovereign or other
superior.</cd> -- <col><b>To pay out</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>to pass out; hence, to slacken; to allow to run out; <as>as, <ex>to
pay out</ex> more cable.</as> See under <er>Cable</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>To pay the piper</b></col>, <cd>to bear the cost, expense, or
trouble.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1054 --></p>

<p><ent>Pay</ent><br/
<hw>Pay</hw> <pr>(p<amac/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To give a
recompense; to make payment, requital, or satisfaction; to discharge a
debt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wicked borroweth, and <qex>payeth</qex> not again.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xxxvii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to make or secure suitable return for expense
or trouble; to be remunerative or profitable; to be worth the effort or
pains required; <as>as, it will <ex>pay</ex> to ride; it will
<ex>pay</ex> to wait; politeness always <ex>pays</ex>.</as></def><--
##<col><b>to not pay</b></col> not to be worthwhile; to be inadvisable
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To pay for</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To make amends
for; to atone for; <as>as, men often <ex>pay for</ex> their mistakes
with loss of property or reputation, sometimes with life</as></cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To give an equivalent for; to bear the expense of; to
be mulcted on account of.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T was I <qex>paid for</qex> your sleeps; I watched your
wakings.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>To pay off</b></col>. <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety>
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>To fall to leeward, as the head of a
vessel under sail.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>to repay (a debt).</cd> --
<col><b>To pay on</b></col>. <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety> <cd>To beat
with vigor; to redouble blows.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>To
pay round</b></col> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<cd>To turn the ship's head.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Pay</ent><br/
<hw>Pay</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Satisfaction;
content.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An equivalent or return for money due, goods
purchased, or services performed; salary or wages for work or service;
compensation; recompense; payment; hire; <as>as, the <ex>pay</ex> of a
clerk; the <ex>pay</ex> of a soldier.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where only merit constant <qex>pay</qex> receives.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is neither <qex>pay</qex> nor plunder to be got.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Full pay</b></col>, <cd>the whole amount of wages or
salary; maximum pay; especially, the highest pay or allowance to civil
or military officers of a certain rank, without deductions.</cd> --
<col><b>Half pay</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Half</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pay day</b></col>, <cd>the day of settlement of accounts.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pay dirt</b></col> <fld>(Mining)</fld>, <cd>earth which
yields a profit to the miner.</cd> <mark>[Western U.S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Pay office</b></col>, <cd>a place where payment is made.</cd> --
<col><b>Pay roll</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>a roll or list of persons
entitled to payment, with the amounts due; now usually one word,
<altname>payroll</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>the total sum of money
which is paid to all employees on payday.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payable</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>payable</ets>. Cf. <er>Pacable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
may, can, or should be paid; suitable to be paid; justly due.</def>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thanks are a tribute <qex>payable</qex> by the poorest.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>That may be discharged
or settled by delivery of value.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Matured; now
due.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>payables</ent><br/
<hw>payables</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>money that a person or organization
expects and is obligated to pay on notes and accounts.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>payback</ent><br/
<hw>payback</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>same as <er>retribution</er>.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>same as <er>requital</er>.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pay Cerps</ent><br/
<hw>Pay Cerps</hw>. <def>A staff corps in the United States navy,
consisting of pay directors, pay inspectors, paymasters, passed
assistant paymasters, and assistant paymasters, having relative rank
from captain to ensign, respectively.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pay rock</ent><br/
<ent>Pay dirt</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pay dirt</hw>, <hw>Pay rock</hw>, etc. }</mhw> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Earth, rock, etc., which yields a profit to the
miner.</def> <mark>[Western U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a discovery, especially after an exploratory
process, which yields a profit or sought-after benefit; -- used
especially in the phrase <ldquo/to hit pay dirt<rdquo/.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>PAYE</ent><br/
<hw>PAYE</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>the British system of withholding
tax.</def> <mark>[Acronym]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pay as you earn.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payee</ent><br/
<hw>Pay*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The person to whom
money is to be, or has been, paid; the person named in a bill or note,
to whom, or to whose order, the amount is promised or directed to be
paid. See <cref>Bill of exchange</cref>, under <er>Bill</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payen</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>Pagan.</def>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payer</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pays;
specifically, the person by whom a bill or note has been, or should be,
paid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paying</ent><br/
<hw>paying</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>the act of paying
money.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> payment, defrayal, defrayment.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paying</ent><br/
<hw>paying</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>yielding material gain or profit;
<as>as, <ex>paying</ex> investments</as>.</def> [Ant:
<ant>unprofitable</ant>]<br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> profitable.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>providing a salary for work performed; <as>as, a
<ex>paying</ex> job</as>.</def> <mark>[prenominal]</mark> [Ant:
<ant>unpaid</ant>]<br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> compensable, remunerative, salaried, stipendiary,
gainful.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>payload</ent><br/
<hw>pay"load`</hw> <pr>(p<amac/"l<omac/d`)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
part of a missile or torpedo that carries the explosive
charge.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> warhead, load.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The goods carried by a large vehicle.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cargo, lading, freight, load, loading, shipment,
consignment.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paymaster</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"mas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pays; one
who compensates, rewards, or requites; specifically, an officer or agent
of a government, a corporation, or an employer, whose duty it is to pay
salaries, wages, etc., and keep account of the same.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paymaster-general</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"mas`ter-gen"er*al</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>In the United States army, an officer of the rank
of brigadier general, who commands the pay department, which is charged
with the payment of the officers and men.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Nav.)</fld> <def>In the United States navy, the Chief of the
Bureau of Provisions and Clothing, who has charge of the payment of
officers and men and their clothing and subsistence. He has the rank of
rear admiral.</def><-- ##?? update needed? --><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In Great Britain, an officer of the Treasury who
makes all payments and disbursements, civil as well as military. He is a
member of the ministry, but not of the cabinet.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payment</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>payment</ets>, <ets>paiement</ets>. See <er>Pay</er> to
requite.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of paying, or giving
compensation; the discharge of a debt or an obligation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man envieth the <qex>payment</qex> of a debt.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is paid; the thing given in discharge of
a debt, or an obligation, or in fulfillment of a promise; reward;
recompense; requital; return.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Punishment; chastisement.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payn</ent><br/
<hw>Payn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. & F.
<ets>pain</ets>, fr. L. <ets>panis</ets> bread.]</ety> <def>Bread.
Having</def> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payndemain</ent><br/
<hw>Payn`de*main"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pain</ets> bread + <ets>demaine</ets> manorial, lordly, own,
private. See <er>Payn</er>, and <er>Demesne</er>. Said to be so called
from the figure of our Lord impressed upon it.]</ety> <def>The finest
and whitest bread made in the Middle Ages; -- called also
<altname>paynemain</altname>, <altname>payman</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payne's process</ent><br/
<hw>Payne's process</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A process for preserving
timber and rendering it incombustible by impregnating it successively
with solutions of sulphate of iron and calcium chloride in vacuo.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Payn"ize</wf>, <pos>v. t.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paynim</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"nim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Painim</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paynize</ent><br/
<hw>Payn"ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From Mr.
<etsep>Payne</etsep>, the inventor.]</ety> <def>To treat or preserve, as
wood, by a process resembling kyanizing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>payoff</ent><br/
<hw>payoff</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>profit or gain from an
action; -- used broadly; <as>as, lots of effort with little
<ex>payoff</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>retribution, payback.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of giving or receiving money or other
valuable consideration as a bribe.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bribe.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The money or item given as a bribe.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>same as <er>compensation</er> 2.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> compensation, remuneration, recompense.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The climax or final result of an event or endeavor;
the denouement of a narrative.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payor</ent><br/
<hw>Pay*or"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>See
<er>Payer</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pay-phone</ent><br/
<hw>pay-phone</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>a coin-operated
telephone.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pay-station.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Payse</ent><br/
<hw>Payse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To poise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>paysheet</ent><br/
<hw>pay"sheet`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>the total amount of
money paid in wages; <as>as, the company had a large
<ex>paysheet</ex></as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> payroll.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a list of employees and their salaries; <as>as, the
company had a long <ex>paysheet</ex></as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> payroll.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>payslip</ent><br/
<hw>pay"slip`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>a slip of paper
included with a person's salary payment, that records how much money the
person has earned and how much tax or insurance etc. has been taken
out.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pay-station</ent><br/
<hw>pay-station</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a coin-operated
telephone.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pay-phone.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pay streak</ent><br/
<hw>Pay streak</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The zone,
parallel to the walls of a vein, in which the ore is concentrated, or
any narrow streak of paying ore in less valuable material.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Oil Boring)</fld> <def>A stratum of oil sand thick
enough to make a well pay.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Paytine</ent><br/
<hw>Pay"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An
alkaloid obtained from a white bark resembling that of the cinchona,
first brought from <etsep>Payta</etsep>, in
<country>Peru</country>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>PBS</ent><br/
<hw>PBS</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biochem.)</fld>
<mark>[Initialism]</mark> <def>Phosphate-buffered saline, a <isa>saline
solution</isa> containing a phosphate buffer.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> phosphate-buffered saline.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>[Initialism]</mark> <def>Public Broadcasting
System; a system of non-profit radio and television broadcasting
stations.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>PC</ent><br/
<hw>PC</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A personal
computer; a computer{2} designed for use by one person at a time; --
contrasted with shared-time computers such as <er>mainframes</er> and
<er>minicomputers</er>, which may be accessed by multiple users each
operating from a different input device (in the 1990's, usually a
terminal). A <er>personal computer</er> typically uses a microprocessor
for its CPU.</def> <mark>[Initialism]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> personal computer, microcomputer.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>PC</ent><br/
<hw>PC</hw> <pr>(p<emac/`s<emac/")</pr> <pos>a.</pos> <def>An initialism
for <er>politically correct</er>.</def> <mark>[Initialism]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> P. C., politically correct.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>PCP</ent><br/
<hw>PCP</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A drug originally taken in the form of
powder ("dust") for its hallucinogenic effects.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> phencyclidine hydrochloride, angel dust.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pct</ent><br/
<hw>pct</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbreviation of
<ets>percent</ets>.]</ety> <def>Percent; a fractional proportion,
multiplied by 100.</def> <mark>[abbreviation]</mark> <br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> percentage, percent, per cent.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pd</ent><br/
<hw>Pd</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The chemical symbol for
<altname>palladium</altname>, an element of the platinum group, of
atomic number 46.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> palladium.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pe</ent><br/
<hw>pe</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The 17th letter of the Hebrew
alphabet.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pea</ent><br/
<hw>Pea</hw> <pr>(p<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>peis</ets>. See <er>Poise</er>.]</ety> <def>The sliding weight on a
steelyard.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pea</ent><br/
<hw>Pea</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Peak</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pea</ent><br/
<hw>Pea</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Peas</plw>
<pr>(p<emac/z)</pr> or <plw>Pease</plw> <pr>(p<emac/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[OE. <ets>pese</ets>, fr. AS. <ets>pisa</ets>, or OF.
<ets>peis</ets>, F. <ets>pois</ets>; both fr. L. <ets>pisum</ets>; cf.
Gr. <grk>pi`sos</grk>, <grk>pi`son</grk>. The final <it>s</it> was
misunderstood in English as a plural ending. Cf. <er>Pease</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant, and its fruit, of the genus
<gen>Pisum</gen>, of many varieties, much cultivated for food. It has a
papilionaceous flower, and the pericarp is a legume, popularly called a
<partof>pod</partof>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When a definite number, more than one, is spoken of, the
plural form <xex>peas</xex> is used; as, the pod contained nine
<xex>peas</xex>; but, in a collective sense, the form <xex>pease</xex>
is preferred; as, a bushel of <xex>pease</xex>; they had
<xex>pease</xex> at dinner. This distinction is not always preserved,
the form <xex>peas</xex> being used in both senses.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A name given, especially in the Southern States, to
the seed of several leguminous plants (species of <gen>Dolichos</gen>,
<gen>Cicer</gen>, <gen>Abrus</gen>, etc.) esp. those having a scar
(<xex>hilum</xex>) of a different color from the rest of the
seed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name <xex>pea</xex> is given to many leguminous
plants more or less closely related to the common pea. See the Phrases,
below.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Beach pea</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a seashore
plant, <spn>Lathyrus maritimus</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Black-eyed
pea</b></col>, <cd>a West Indian name for <spn>Dolichos
sph<ae/rospermus</spn> and its seed.</cd> -- <col><b>Butterfly
pea</b></col>, <cd>the American plant <spn>Clitoria Mariana</spn>,
having showy blossoms.</cd> -- <col><b>Chick pea</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Chick-pea</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Egyptian pea</b></col>. <cd>Same as
<er>Chick-pea</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Everlasting pea</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Everlasting</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Glory pea</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Glory</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Hoary pea</b></col>,
<cd>any plant of the genus <gen>Tephrosia</gen>; goat's rue.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Issue pea</b></col>, <col><b>Orris pea</b></col></mcol>.
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Issue</er>, and <er>Orris</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Milk pea</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Milk</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea berry</b></col>, <cd>a kind of a
coffee bean or grain which grows single, and is round or pea-shaped;
often used adjectively; <as>as, <ex>pea-berry</ex> coffee</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pea bug</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <er>Pea
weevil</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea coal</b></col>, <cd>a size of coal
smaller than nut coal.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea crab</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any small crab of the genus
<gen>Pinnotheres</gen>, living as a commensal in bivalves; esp., the
European species (<spn>Pinnotheres pisum</spn>) which lives in the
common mussel and the cockle.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea dove</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the American ground dove.</cd> --
<col><b>Pea-flower tribe</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a suborder
(<ord>Papilionace<ae/</ord>) of leguminous plants having blossoms
essentially like that of the pea.</cd> <au>G. Bentham.</au> --
<col><b>Pea maggot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of a
European moth (<spn>Tortrix pisi</spn>), which is very destructive to
peas.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea ore</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>,
<cd>argillaceous oxide of iron, occurring in round grains of a size of a
pea; pisolitic ore.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea starch</b></col>, <cd>the starch
or flour of the common pea, which is sometimes used in adulterating
wheat flour, pepper, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Pea tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the name of several leguminous shrubs of the
genus <gen>Caragana</gen>, natives of Siberia and China.</cd> --
<col><b>Pea vine</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any plant
which bears peas</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A kind of vetch or tare, common
in the United States (<spn>Lathyrus Americana</spn>, and other similar
species).</cd> -- <col><b>Pea weevil</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
small weevil (<spn>Bruchus pisi</spn>) which destroys peas by eating out
the interior.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon pea</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Pigeon pea</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet pea</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the annual plant <spn>Lathyrus odoratus</spn>;
also, its many-colored, sweet-scented blossoms.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peabird</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The wryneck; -- so called from its note.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peabody bird</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"bod*y bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
American sparrow (<spn>Zonotrichia albicollis</spn>) having a
conspicuous white throat. The name is imitative of its note. Called also
<altname>White-throated sparrow</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peace</ent><br/
<hw>Peace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pees</ets>,
<ets>pais</ets>, OF. pais, paiz, pes, F. <ets>paix</ets>, L.
<ets>pax</ets>, <ets>pacis</ets>, akin to <ets>pacere</ets>,
<ets>paciscere</ets>, <ets>pacisci</ets>, to make an agreement, and
prob. also <ets>pangere</ets> to fasten. Cf. <er>Appease</er>,
<er>Fair</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, <er>Fay</er>, <pos>v.</pos>,
<er>Fang</er>, <er>Pacify</er>, <er>Pact</er>, <er>Pay</er> to
requite.]</ety> <def>A state of quiet or tranquillity; freedom from
disturbance or agitation; calm; repose</def>; specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Exemption from, or cessation of, war with public enemies.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Public quiet, order, and contentment in obedience to
law.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Exemption from, or subjection of, agitating
passions; tranquillity of mind or conscience.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>Reconciliation; agreement after variance; harmony; concord.</def>
<ldquo/The eternal love and <xex>pees</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Peace</xex> is sometimes used as an exclamation in
commanding silence, quiet, or order. <ldquo/<xex>Peace</xex>! foolish
woman.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At peace</b></col>, <cd>in a state of peace.</cd> --
<col><b>Breach of the peace</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Breach</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Justice of the peace</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Justice</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Peace of God</b></col>.
<fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A term used in wills, indictments,
etc., as denoting a state of peace and good conduct.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Theol.)</fld> <cd>The peace of heart which is the gift of
God.</cd> -- <col><b>Peace offering</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Jewish
Antiq.)</fld> <cd>A voluntary offering to God in token of devout homage
and of a sense of friendly communion with Him</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
gift or service offered as satisfaction to an offended person.</cd> --
<col><b>Peace officer</b></col>, <cd>a civil officer whose duty it is to
preserve the public peace, to prevent riots, etc., as a polliceman,
sheriff or constable.</cd> -- <col><b>To hold one's peace</b></col>,
<cd>to be silent; to refrain from speaking.</cd> -- <col><b>To make
one's peace with</b></col>, <cd>to reconcile one with, to plead one's
cause with, or to become reconciled with, another.</cd> <ldquo/I will
<xex>make your peace</xex> with him.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peace</ent><br/
<hw>Peace</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To make or become quiet; to
be silent; to stop.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Peace</xex> your
tattlings.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the thunder would not <qex>peace</qex> at my bidding.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peaceable</ent><br/
<hw>Peace"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>peisible</ets>, F. <ets>paisible</ets>.]</ety> <def>Begin in or at
peace; tranquil; quiet; free from, or not disposed to, war, disorder, or
excitement; not quarrelsome.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Peace"a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Peace"a*bly</wf>, adv.</wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Peaceful; pacific; tranquil; quiet; mild;
undisturbed; serene; still.</syn> -- <usage><er>Peaceable</er>,
<er>Peaceful</er>. <xex>Peaceable</xex> describes the state of an
individual, nation, etc., in reference to external hostility, attack,
etc.; <xex>peaceful</xex>, in respect to internal disturbance. The
former denotes <ldquo/in the spirit of peace;<rdquo/ latter; <ldquo/in
the possession or enjoyment of peace.<rdquo/ A <xex>peaceable</xex>
adjustment of difficulties; a <xex>peaceful</xex> life,
scene.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peacebreaker</ent><br/
<hw>Peace"break`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
disturbs the public peace.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Peace"break`ing</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peaceful</ent><br/
<hw>Peace"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Possessing or enjoying peace; not disturbed by war, tumult,
agitation, anxiety, or commotion; quiet; tranquil; <as>as, a
<ex>peaceful</ex> time; a <ex>peaceful</ex> country; a <ex>peaceful</ex>
end.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not disposed or tending to war, tumult or agitation;
pacific; mild; calm; peaceable; <as>as, <ex>peaceful</ex>
words</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Peaceable</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>--<wordforms><wf>Peace"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>. --
<wf>Peace"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peacekeeper</ent><br/
<hw>peacekeeper</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>a member of a
military force that is assigned (often with international sanction) to
preserve peace in a trouble area.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>someone who keeps peace; <as>as, she's the
<ex>peacekeeper</ex> in that family</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>the pistol of a law officer in the old West.</def>
<mark>[U. S. History]</mark> <br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peaceless</ent><br/
<hw>Peace"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without peace; disturbed.</def>
<rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peace-loving</ent><br/
<hw>peace-loving</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Inclined or disposed to
peace; <as>as, <ex>peace-loving</ex> citizens</as>. Opposed to
<ant>warlike</ant> or <ant>belligerent</ant>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> peaceable, dovish, pacific.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peacemaker</ent><br/
<hw>Peace"mak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes
peace by reconciling parties that are at variance.</def> <rj><au>Matt.
v. 9.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>--<wordforms><wf>Peace"mak`ing</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peacenik</ent><br/
<hw>peacenik</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>someone who prefers negotiations to
armed conflict in the conduct of foreign relations.</def>
<mark>[Derog.]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> dove.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peacetime</ent><br/
<hw>peacetime</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A period of time during which
there is no war; <as>as, civil liberties are valued more highly in
<ex>peacetime</ex> than in war</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peach</ent><br/
<hw>Peach</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Appeach</er>, <er>Impeach</er>.]</ety> <def>To accuse of crime; to
inform against.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peach</ent><br/
<hw>Peach</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To turn informer; to betray one's
accomplice.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If I be ta'en, I'll <qex>peach</qex> for this.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peach</ent><br/
<hw>Peach</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>peche</ets>, <ets>peshe</ets>, OF. <ets>pesche</ets>, F.
<ets>p<ecir/che</ets>, fr. LL. <ets>persia</ets>, L. <ets>Persicum</ets>
(sc. <ets>malum</ets>) a Persian apple, a peach. Cf. <er>Persian</er>,
and <er>Parsee</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
well-known high-flavored juicy fruit, containing one or two seeds in a
hard almond-like endocarp or stone. In the wild stock the fruit is hard
and inedible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The tree (<spn>Prunus Persica</spn> syn.
<spn>Amygdalus Persica</spn>) which bears the peach fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The pale red <isa>color</isa> of the peach blossom,
or the light pinkish yellow of the peach fruit.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Guinea peach</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Sierra
Leone peach</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the large edible berry of the
<spn>Sarcocephalus esculentus</spn>, a rubiaceous climbing shrub of west
tropical Africa.</cd> -- <col><b>Palm peach</b></col>, <cd>the fruit of
a Venezuelan palm tree (<spn>Bactris speciosa</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Peach color</b></col>, <cd>the pale red color of the peach
blossom.</cd> -- <col><b>Peach-tree borer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the larva of a clearwing moth (<spn>Aegeria exitiosa</spn>,
<it>or</it> <spn>Sannina, exitiosa</spn>) of the family
<fam>Aegeriidae</fam>, which is very destructive to peach trees by
boring in the wood, usually near the ground; also, the moth itself. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Borer</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peachblow</ent><br/
<hw>Peach"blow`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Ceramics)</fld> <def>Of the
delicate purplish pink color likened to that of peach blooms; -- applied
esp. to a Chinese porcelain, small specimens of which bring great prices
in the Western countries.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peach-colored</ent><br/
<hw>Peach"-col`ored</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ch"k<ucr/l`<etil/rd)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the color of a peach blossom.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Peach-colored</xex> satin.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peacher</ent><br/
<hw>Peach"er</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ch"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who peaches.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peachick</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"chick`</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"ch<icr/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The chicken of the peacock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peachwood</ent><br/
<hw>peach"wood`</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ch"w<oocr/d`)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A tropical tree <spn>Caesalpinia echinata</spn>, having
a prickly trunk; its heavy red wood yields a red dye and is used for
cabinetry.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Caesalpinia echinata.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The heavy red wood of the <spn>Caesalpinia
echinata</spn>; brazilwood; pernambuco wood.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peachy</ent><br/
<hw>Peach"y</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ch"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Resembling a peach or peaches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pea coat</ent><br/
<ent>peacoat</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pea"coat`</hw>, <hw>pea" coat`</hw></mhw>
<pr>(p<emac/"k<omac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. fr. D.
<ets>pij</ets>, <ets>pije</ets>, a coat of a coarse woolen stuff.]</ety>
<def>A thick loose double-breasted woolen jacket, or coat, commonly worn
by sailors in cold weather; a pea-jacket.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pea coat, peacoat, pea jacket, peajacket,
pea-jacket.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>+ <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peacock</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"cock`</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"k<ocr/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pecok</ets>. <ets>Pea-</ets> in this word is from AS.
<ets>pe<aacute/</ets>, <ets>p<amac/wa</ets>, peacock, fr. L.
<ets>pavo</ets>, prob. of Oriental origin; cf. Gr. <grk>taw`s</grk>,
<grk>taw^s</grk>, Per. <ets>t<amac/us</ets>, <ets>t<amac/wus</ets>, Ar.
<ets>t<amac/w<umac/s</ets>. See <er>Cock</er> the bird.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The male of any <isa>pheasant</isa>
of the genus <gen>Pavo</gen>, of which at least two species are known,
native of Southern Asia and the East Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The upper tail coverts, which are long and capable of
erection, are each marked with a black spot bordered by concentric bands
of brilliant blue, green, and golden colors. The common domesticated
species is <spn>Pavo cristatus</spn>. The Javan peacock (<spn>Pavo
muticus</spn>) is more brilliantly colored than the common
species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In common usage, the species in general or
collectively; a peafowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peacock butterfly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
handsome European butterfly (<spn>Hamadryas Io</spn>) having ocelli like
those of peacock.</cd> -- <col><b>Peacock fish</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the European blue-striped wrasse (<spn>Labrus
variegatus</spn>); -- so called on account of its brilliant colors.
Called also <altname>cook wrasse</altname> and
<altname>cook</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Peacock pheasant</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of handsome Asiatic
pheasants of the genus <gen>Polyplectron</gen>. They resemble the
peacock in color.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peacock-blue</ent><br/
<hw>peacock-blue</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>A bright greenish
blue.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peacock Throne</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"cock` Throne</hw>. <def>A famous throne formerly of the kings of
Delhi, India, but since 1739, when it was carried off by <person>Nadir
Shah</person>, held by the shahs of <country>Persia</country> (now
Iran); -- so called from its bearing a fully expanded peacock's tail
done in gems.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[metonymically named after the throne on which he
sat.]</ety> <def>The office or position of the Shah of Iran; <as>as, to
ascend the <ex>Peacock Throne</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peafowl</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"fowl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Peacock</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The peacock or peahen;
any species of Pavo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peag</ent><br/
<hw>Peag</hw> <pr>(p<emac/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>peage</asp>, <asp>peak</asp>, <asp>peeke</asp>.]</altsp>
<ety>[Prob. of North American Indian origin, by shortening of
<ets>wampumpeag</ets>. <au>RHUD</au>.]</ety> <def>A kind of aboriginal
shell money, or wampum, of the Atlantic coast of the United States; --
originally applied only to polished white cylindrical beads. See also
<er>wampum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peage</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Paage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peagrit</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"grit`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
coarse pisolitic limestone. See <er>Pisolite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peahen</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"hen`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Peacock</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The hen or female
peafowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pea jacket</ent><br/
<ent>Pea jacket</ent><br/
<ent>Pea-jacket</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pea"-jack`et</hw>, <hw>Pea" jack`et</hw> <hw>Pea"
jack`et</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. fr. D.
<ets>pij</ets>, <ets>pije</ets>, a coat of a coarse woolen stuff.]</ety>
<def>A thick loose double-breasted woolen jacket, or coat, commonly worn
by sailors in cold weather.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pea coat, peacoat, pea jacket.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>+ <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peak</ent><br/
<hw>Peak</hw> <pr>(p<emac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pek</ets>, AS. <ets>peac</ets>, perh of Celtic origin; cf. Ir.
<ets>peac</ets> a sharp-pointed thing. Cf. <er>Pike</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A point; the sharp end or top of anything that
terminates in a point; <as>as, the <ex>peak</ex>, or front, of a
cap</as>.</def> <ldquo/Run your beard into a <xex>peak</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The top, or one of the tops, of a hill, mountain, or
range, ending in a point; often, the whole hill or mountain, esp. when
isolated; <as>as, the <ex>Peak</ex> of Teneriffe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Silent upon a <qex>peak</qex> in Darien.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The upper aftermost
corner of a fore-and-aft sail; -- used in many combinations; <as>as,
<ex>peak</ex>-halyards, <ex>peak</ex>-brails, etc.</as></def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The narrow part of a vessel's bow, or the hold within
it.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The extremity of an anchor fluke; the
bill.</def> <altsp>[In the last sense written also <asp>pea</asp> and
<asp>pee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fore peak</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Fore</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peak</ent><br/
<hw>Peak</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Peaked</conjf> <pr>(p<emac/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Peaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rise or extend
into a peak or point; to form, or appear as, a peak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There <qex>peaketh</qex> up a mighty high mount.</q>
<rj><qau>Holand.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To achieve a maximum of
numerical value, intensity of activity, popularity, or other
characteristic, followed by a decline; <as>as, the stock market
<ex>peaked</ex> in January; his performance as a pitcher <ex>peaked</ex>
in 1990; sales of the XTX model <ex>peaked</ex> at 20,000 per
year</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To acquire sharpness of figure or features; hence,
to look thin or sickly.</def> <ldquo/Dwindle, <xex>peak</xex>, and
pine.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Peek</er>.]</ety> <def>To pry; to peep
slyly.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peak arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a pointed or
Gothic arch.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peak</ent><br/
<hw>Peak</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To raise to a
position perpendicular, or more nearly so; <as>as, to <ex>peak</ex>
oars, to hold them upright; to <ex>peak</ex> a gaff or yard, to set it
nearer the perpendicular.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peaked</ent><br/
<hw>Peaked</hw> <pr>(p<emac/kt <it>or</it> p<emac/k"<ecr/d)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pointed; ending in a point; <as>as, a
<ex>peaked</ex> roof</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Oftener p<emac/k"<ecr/d)</fld> <def>Sickly; not
robust.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1055 --></p>

<p><ent>Peaking</ent><br/
<hw>Peak"ing</hw> <pr>(p<emac/k"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Mean; sneaking.</def> <mark>[Vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pining; sickly; peakish.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peakish</ent><br/
<hw>Peak"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or relating to a
peak; or to peaks; belonging to a mountainous region.</def> <ldquo/Her
<xex>peakish</xex> spring.<rdquo/ <au>Drayton.</au> <ldquo/His
<xex>peakish</xex> dialect.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Hall.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having peaks; peaked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having features thin or sharp, as from sickness;
hence, sickly.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peaky</ent><br/
<hw>Peak"y</hw> <pr>(p<emac/k"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having a peak or peaks.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sickly; peaked.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peal</ent><br/
<hw>Peal</hw> <pr>(p<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small salmon; a grilse; a
sewin.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peal</ent><br/
<hw>Peal</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To appeal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peal</ent><br/
<hw>Peal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[An abbrev. of F. <ets>appel</ets> a
call, appeal, ruffle of a drum, fr. <ets>appeller</ets> to call, L.
<ets>appellare</ets>. See <er>Appeal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
loud sound, or a succession of loud sounds, as of bells, thunder,
cannon, shouts, of a multitude, etc.</def> <ldquo/A fair <xex>peal</xex>
of artillery.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hayward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether those <qex>peals</qex> of praise be his or no.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And a deep thunder, <qex>peal</qex> on <qex>peal</qex>, afar.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A set of bells tuned to each other according to the
diatonic scale; also, the changes rung on a set of bells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To ring a peal</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Ring</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peal</ent><br/
<hw>Peal</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pealed</conjf> <pr>(p<emac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pealing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter or give out
loud sounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There let the <qex>pealing</qex> organ blow.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To resound; to echo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the whole air <qex>pealed</qex><br/
<qex>With the cheers of our men</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peal</ent><br/
<hw>Peal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter or give forth
loudly; to cause to give out loud sounds; to noise abroad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The warrior's name,<br/
Though <qex>pealed</qex> and chimed on all the tongues of fame.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Barlow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To assail with noise or loud sounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor was his ear less <qex>pealed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pour out.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peal out</ent><br/
<hw>peal out</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To give off a loud pealing
sound; to peal.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To accelerate quickly from a stop in a vehicle,
especially an automobile, so that the tires grind against the pavement
and make a squealing noise while accelerating; -- sometimes performed by
young drivers for showy display.</def> <mark>[slang]</mark> <br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> drop a patch.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pean</ent><br/
<hw>Pean</hw> <pr>(p<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pene</ets>, F. <ets>panne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>One
of the furs, the ground being <xex>sable</xex>, and the spots or tufts
<xex>or</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pean</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"an</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A song of
praise and triumph. See <er>P<ae/an</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peanism</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"an*ism</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"<ait/n*<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>paianismo`s</grk>, fr. <grk>paiani`zein</grk> to chant
the p<ae/an.]</ety> <def>The song or shout of praise, of battle, or of
triumph.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peanut</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"nut</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"n<ucr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The fruit of a trailing leguminous plant
(<spn>Arachis hypog<ae/a</spn>); also, the plant itself, which is widely
cultivated for its fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The fruit is a hard pod, usually containing two or three
seeds, sometimes but one, which ripen beneath the soil. Called also
<altname>earthnut</altname>, <altname>groundnut</altname>, and
<altname>goober</altname>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peanut butter</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"nut but"ter</hw>. <def>A paste made by mixing ground fresh
roasted peanuts with a small quantity of water or oil, and used chiefly
as a relish on sandwiches, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peanut gallery</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"nut gal"ler*y</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>The cheapest seats in a
theater, being typically those farthest from the stage, as in the
uppermost balcony.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a television show for children, the section where
visiting children sit as guests during the performance.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any group of children, or
the children in a group of people; <as>as, let's have no noise from the
<ex>peanut gallery</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pear</ent><br/
<hw>Pear</hw> <pr>(p<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pere</ets>, AS. <ets>peru</ets>, L. <ets>pirum</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>poire</ets>. Cf. <er>Perry</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
fleshy pome, or fruit, of a rosaceous tree (<spn>Pyrus communis</spn>),
cultivated in many varieties in temperate climates; also, the tree which
bears this fruit. See <cref>Pear family</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pear blight</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>A name of two distinct diseases of pear trees, both causing a
destruction of the branches, viz., that caused by a minute insect
(<spn>Xyleborus pyri</spn>), and that caused by the freezing of the sap
in winter.</cd> <au>A. J. Downing.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>A very small beetle (<spn>Xyleborus pyri</spn>) whose larv<ae/ bore
in the twigs of pear trees and cause them to wither.</cd> --
<col><b>Pear family</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a suborder of
rosaceous plants (<ord>Pome<ae/</ord>), characterized by the calyx tube
becoming fleshy in fruit, and, combined with the ovaries, forming a
pome. It includes the apple, pear, quince, service berry, and
hawthorn.</cd> -- <col><b>Pear gauge</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>,
<cd>a kind of gauge for measuring the exhaustion of an air-pump
receiver; -- so called because consisting in part of a pear-shaped glass
vessel.</cd> -- <col><b>Pear shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
marine gastropod shell of the genus <gen>Pyrula</gen>, native of
tropical seas; -- so called from the shape.</cd> -- <col><b>Pear
slug</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of a sawfly which is
very injurious to the foliage of the pear tree.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Illustr. of pear slug (Celandria cerasi) --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearch</ent><br/
<hw>Pearch</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<def>See <er>Perch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearl</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rl)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fringe or
border.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <def2><pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
fringe; to border.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <see>See
<er>Purl</er>.</see></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pearl stitch</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Purl stitch</cref>,
under <er>Purl</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearl</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>perle</ets>, F.
<ets>perle</ets>, LL. <ets>perla</ets>, <ets>perula</ets>, probably fr.
(assumed) L. <ets>pirulo</ets>, dim. of L. <ets>pirum</ets> a pear. See
<er>Pear</er>, and cf. <er>Purl</er> to mantle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A shelly concretion, usually rounded, and having
a brilliant luster, with varying tints, found in the mantle, or between
the mantle and shell, of certain bivalve mollusks, especially in the
pearl oysters and river mussels, and sometimes in certain univalves. It
is usually due to a secretion of shelly substance around some irritating
foreign particle. Its substance is the same as nacre, or
mother-of-pearl.</def> <note>Pearls which are round, or nearly round,
and of fine luster, are highly esteemed as jewels, and at one time
compared in value with the precious stones. Since development of
cultured pearls, the relative value has diminished somewhat, though the
best pearls are still expensive, and natural pearls even more so.
Artificial pearls may be made of various materials, including material
similar to that of natural pearls; these are less expensive than natural
or cultured pearls. See <cref>cultured pearl</cref>, below.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively, something resembling a pearl;
something very precious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I see thee compassed with thy kingdom's <qex>pearl</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And those <qex>pearls</qex> of dew she wears.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Nacre, or mother-of-pearl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A fish allied to the turbot; the
brill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A light-colored tern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the circle of tubercles
which form the bur on a deer's antler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A whitish speck or film on the eye.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A capsule of gelatin or similar substance containing
some liquid for medicinal application, as ether.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A size of type, between agate
and diamond.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><hand/ <pearltype>This line is printed in the type called
<ex>pearl</ex>.</pearltype><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ground pearl</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Ground</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl barley</b></col>, <cd>kernels of
barley, ground so as to form small, round grains.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl
diver</b></col>, <cd>one who dives for pearl oysters.</cd> --
<col><b>Pearl edge</b></col>, <cd>an edge of small loops on the side of
some kinds of ribbon; also, a narrow kind of thread edging to be sewed
on lace.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl eye</b></col>, <cd>cataract.</cd>
<mark>[R.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pearl gray</b></col>, <cd>a very pale and
delicate blue-gray color.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl millet</b></col>,
<cd>Egyptian millet (<spn>Penicillaria spicata</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Pearl moss</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Carrageen</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pearl moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any moth of the
genus <gen>Margaritia</gen>; -- so called on account of its pearly
color.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl oyster</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of large tropical marine bivalve mollusks
of the genus <gen>Meleagrina</gen>, or <gen>Margaritifera</gen>, found
in the East Indies (especially at Ceylon), in the Persian Gulf, on the
coast of Australia, and on the Pacific coast of America. Called also
<altname>pearl shell</altname>, and <altname>pearl
mussel</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl powder</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Pearl white</cref>, below.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl sago</b></col>,
<cd>sago in the form of small pearly grains.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl
sinter</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>fiorite.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl
spar</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a crystallized variety of
dolomite, having a pearly luster.</cd> -- <col><b>Pearl white</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Basic bismuth nitrate, or bismuth subchloride; -- used
chiefly as a cosmetic</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A variety of white lead
blued with indigo or Berlin blue.</cd> -- <col><b>cultured
pearl</b></col>, <cd>a pearl grown by a pearl oyster into which a round
pellet has been placed, to serve as the seed for more predictable growth
of the pearl. The pellet is usually made from mother-of-pearl, and
additional layers of nacre are deposited onto the seed by the oyster.
Such pearls, being more easily obtained than natural pearls from wild
oysters, are less expensive.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearl</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rl)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to pearl or pearls; made of pearls, or of mother-of-pearl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearl</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To set or adorn with
pearls, or with mother-of-pearl. Used also figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to resemble pearls; to make into small
round grains; <as>as, to <ex>pearl</ex> barley</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearl</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To resemble pearl or
pearls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dive or hunt for pearls; <as>as, to go
<ex>pearling</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearlaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling pearl
or mother-of-pearl; pearly in quality or appearance.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> iridescent, nacreous, opalescent, opaline,
pearlaceous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearlash</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl"ash`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A white amorphous or granular substance which consists principally
of potassium carbonate, and has a strong alkaline reaction. It is
obtained by lixiviating wood ashes, and evaporating the lye, and has
been an important source of potassium compounds. It is used in making
soap, glass, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pearler</ent><br/
<hw>pearl"er</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A diver who searches for molluscs
containing pearls; a pearl diver.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pearl diver.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pearlescent</ent><br/
<hw>pearl*es"cent</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*l<ecr/s"<eit/nt)</pr>
<pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having an iridescent pearl-like
surface, giving a play of lustrous rainbowlike colors; nacreous;
pearlaceous.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> iridescent, nacreous, opalescent, opaline,
pearlaceous.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearl-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl"-eyed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a pearly
speck in the eye; afflicted with a cataract or cataracts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearlfish</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any fish whose scales yield a pearl-like pigment used in
manufacturing artificial pearls, as the bleak, and whitebait.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearlings</ent><br/
<ent>Pearlins</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pearl"ins</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pearl"ings</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Prob. a corruption of
<ets>purflings</ets>. See <er>Purfle</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of lace of
silk or thread.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearlstone</ent><br/
<ent>Pearlite</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pearl"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pearl"stone`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A glassy
volcanic rock of a grayish color and pearly luster, often having a
spherulitic concretionary structure due to the curved cracks produced by
contraction in cooling. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Perlitic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearlwort</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A name given to several species of <gen>Sagina</gen>, low and
inconspicuous herbs of the Chickweed family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearly</ent><br/
<hw>Pearl"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Containing
pearls; abounding with, or yielding, pearls; <as>as, <ex>pearly</ex>
shells</as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resembling pearl or pearls; clear; pure;
transparent; iridescent; <as>as, the <ex>pearly</ex> dew or
flood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pearly everlasting</ent><br/
<hw>pearly everlasting</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>an
American everlasting (<spn>Anaphalis margaritacea</spn>) having foliage
with soft wooly hairs and corymbose heads with pearly-white scarious
involucres.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cottonweed, Anaphalis margaritacea.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+MW10</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pearly-white</ent><br/
<hw>pearly-white</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>White like a pearl; very
white.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pearly.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pearmain</ent><br/
<hw>Pear"main</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The name of several kinds of apples; <as>as, the blue
<ex>pearmain</ex>, winter <ex>pearmain</ex>, and red
<ex>pearmain</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pear-shaped</ent><br/
<hw>Pear"-shaped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the form of a
pear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peart</ent><br/
<hw>Peart</hw> <pr>(p<emac/rt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[A variant of
<ets>pert</ets>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Active; lively; brisk; smart;
-- often applied to convalescents; <as>as, she is quite <ex>peart</ex>
to-day</as>.</def> <mark>[O. Eng. & Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was a tricksy girl, I wot, albeit clad in gray,<br/
As <qex>peart</qex> as bird, as straight as bolt, as fresh as flowers in
May.</q> <rj><qau>Warner (1592).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peasant</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pa<ium/sant</ets> (the <it>i</it> being perh. due to confusion with
the p. pr. of verbs), <ets>pa<ium/san</ets>, F. <ets>paysan</ets>, fr.
OF. & F. <ets>pays</ets> country, fr. L. <ets>pagus</ets> the country.
See <er>Pagan</er>.]</ety> <def>A countryman; a rustic; especially, one
of the lowest class of tillers of the soil in European
countries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Countryman; rustic; swain; hind.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peasant</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"ant</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rustic, rural.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peasantlike</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"ant*like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rude; clownish;
illiterate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peasantly</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"ant*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Peasantlike.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peasantry</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"ant*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Peasants, collectively; the body of rustics.</def> <ldquo/A bold
<xex>peasantry</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rusticity; coarseness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>p. Butler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peascod</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"cod`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The legume or
pericarp, or the pod, of the pea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pease</ent><br/
<hw>Pease</hw> <pr>(p<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu>obs.<xex>pl</xex>. <plw>Peases</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<plw>Peasen</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See <er>Pea</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A pea.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>peose</xex>.<rdquo/ <ldquo/Bread . . . of beans and of
<xex>peses</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A plural form of <er>Pea</er>. See the Note under
<er>Pea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peastone</ent><br/
<hw>Pea"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Pisolite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peasweep</ent><br/
<hw>Peas"weep`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from its
note.]</ety> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The pewit, or lapwing.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
greenfinch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peat</ent><br/
<hw>Peat</hw> <pr>(p<emac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pet</er>
a fondling.]</ety> <def>A small person; a pet; -- sometimes used
contemptuously.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peat</ent><br/
<hw>Peat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. for <ets>beat</ets>, prop.,
material used to make the fire burn <ets>better</ets>, fr. AS.
<ets>b<emac/tan</ets> to better, mend (a fire), <ets>b<omac/t</ets>
advantage. See <er>Better</er>, <er>Boot</er> advantage.]</ety> <def>A
substance of vegetable origin, consisting of roots and fibers, moss,
etc., in various stages of decomposition, and found, as a kind of turf
or bog, usually in low situations, where it is always more or less
saturated with water. It is often dried and used for fuel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peat bog</b></col>, <cd>a bog containing peat; also, peat
as it occurs in such places; peat moss.</cd> -- <col><b>Peat
moss</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The plants which, when decomposed,
become peat</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A fen producing peat</cd>.
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Moss of the genus
<gen>Sphagnum</gen>, which often grows abundantly in boggy or peaty
places.</cd> -- <col><b>Peat reek</b></col>, <cd>the reek or smoke of
peat; hence, also, the peculiar flavor given to whisky by being
distilled with peat as fuel.</cd> <mark>[Scot.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peaty</ent><br/
<hw>Peat"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Composed of peat;
abounding in peat; resembling peat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peavy</ent><br/
<ent>Peavey</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pea"vey</hw>, <hw>Pea"vy</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be from the inventor's name.]</ety>
<fld>(Lumbering)</fld> <def>A cant hook having the end of its lever
armed with a spike; it is used for handling logs.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cant dog.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peba</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ba</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"b<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Pg.
<ets>peba</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An armadillo
(<spn>Tatusia novemcincta</spn>) which is found from Texas to Paraguay;
-- called also <altname>tatouhou</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pebble</ent><br/
<hw>Peb"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>papolst<amac/n</ets>; cf. L. <ets>papula</ets> pimple, mote. See
<er>Stone</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small roundish piece of stone;
especially, a stone worn and rounded by the action of water; a
pebblestone.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>pebbles</xex> on the hungry
beach.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As children gathering <qex>pebbles</qex> on the shore.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Transparent and colorless rock crystal; <as>as,
Brazilian <ex>pebble</ex>; -- so called by opticians</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pebble powder</b></col>, <cd>slow-burning gunpowder, in
large cubical grains.</cd> -- <col><b>Scotch pebble</b></col>,
<cd>varieties of quartz, as agate, chalcedony, etc., obtained from
cavities in amygdaloid.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pebble</ent><br/
<hw>Peb"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pebbled</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pebbling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To grain (leather) so as to
produce a surface covered with small rounded prominences.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pebbled</ent><br/
<hw>Peb"bled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in
pebbles.</def> <rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pebblestone</ent><br/
<hw>Peb"ble*stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A pebble; also, pebbles
collectively.</def> <ldquo/Chains of <xex>pebblestone</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Marlowe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pebbly</ent><br/
<hw>Peb"bly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of pebbles;
pebbled.</def> <ldquo/A hard, <xex>pebbly</xex> bottom.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pebrine</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe`brine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>An epidemic disease of the silkworm, characterized by the presence
of minute vibratory corpuscles in the blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecan</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*can"</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*k<acr/n")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pacane</ets> the nut.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of
hickory (<spn>Carya oliv<ae/formis</spn>), growing in North America,
chiefly in the Mississippi valley and in Texas, where it is one of the
largest of forest trees; also, its fruit, a smooth, oblong nut, an inch
or an inch and a half long, with a thin shell and well-flavored
meat.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pacane</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecary</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Peccary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccability</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`ca*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or
quality of being peccable; liability to sin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The common <qex>peccability</qex> of mankind.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H.
More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccable</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ca*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>peccable</ets>. See <er>Peccant</er>.]</ety> <def>Liable to sin;
subject to transgress the divine law.</def> <ldquo/A frail and
<xex>peccable</xex> mortal.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccadillo</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`ca*dil"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Peccadillos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>pecadillo</ets>, dim. of <ets>pecado</ets> a sin, fr. L.
<ets>peccatum</ets>. See <er>Peccant</er>.]</ety> <def>A slight trespass
or offense; a petty crime; a trifling fault.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"can*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peccantia</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
being peccant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sin; an offense.</def> <rj><au>W.
Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccant</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"cant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peccans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>peccare</ets> to
sin: cf. F. <ets>peccant</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sinning; guilty
of transgression; criminal; <as>as, <ex>peccant</ex> angels</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Morbid; corrupt; <as>as, <ex>peccant</ex>
humors</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Wrong; defective; faulty.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccant</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"cant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An offender.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Whitlock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"cant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a peccant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccary</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ca*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Peccaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[From the native South
American name: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/cari</ets>, Sp.
<ets>pecar</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A pachyderm of the
genus <gen>Dicotyles</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The collared peccary, or tajacu (<spn>Dicotyles
torquatus</spn>), is about the size and shape of a small hog, and has a
white ring aroung the neck. It ranges from <state>Arkansas</state> to
<country>Brazil</country>. A larger species (<spn>Dicotyles
labiatus</spn>), with white cheeks, is found in <geog>South
America</geog>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peccavi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pec*ca"vi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>I have sinned;
-- used colloquially to express confession or acknowledgment of an
offense; -- used rarely, superseded by the approximate equivalent
<altname>mea culpa</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Aubrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecco</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pekoe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peck</ent><br/
<hw>Peck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. akin to <ets>pack</ets>; or,
orig., an indefinite quantity, and fr. <ets>peck</ets>, v. (below): cf.
also F. <ets>picotin</ets> a peak.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The fourth
part of a bushel; a dry measure of eight quarts; <as>as, a <ex>peck</ex>
of wheat</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>peck</xex> of provender.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A great deal; a large or excessive quantity.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>peck</xex> of uncertainties and doubts.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>
` <p><ent>Peck</ent><br/
<hw>Peck</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pecked</conjf> <pr>(p<ecr/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pecking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Pick</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike with the beak; to thrust
the beak into; <as>as, a bird <ex>pecks</ex> a tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: To strike, pick, thrust against, or dig into,
with a pointed instrument; especially, to strike, pick, etc., with
repeated quick movements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To seize and pick up with the beak, or as with the
beak; to bite; to eat; -- often with <xex>up</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This fellow <qex>pecks</qex> up wit as pigeons peas.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make, by striking with the beak or a pointed
instrument; <as>as, to <ex>peck</ex> a hole in a tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peck</ent><br/
<hw>Peck</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make strokes with
the beak, or with a pointed instrument.</def>
<rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pick up food with the beak; hence, to
eat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The hen] went <qex>pecking</qex> by his side.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To peck at</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to attack with
petty and repeated blows; to carp at; to nag; to tease.</cd>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to eat slowly and in small portions, with litle
interest; <as>as, to <ex>peck at</ex> one's food</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1056 --></p>

<p><ent>Peck</ent><br/
<hw>Peck</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A quick, sharp
stroke, as with the beak of a bird or a pointed instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecker</ent><br/
<hw>Peck"er</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, pecks; specif., a bird that pecks holes in
trees; a woodpecker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for pecking; a pick.</def>
<rj><au>Garth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The penis; -- an obscene term for the male sexual
organ.</def> <mark>[Vulgar]</mark> <br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> penis, cock, prick, tool, peter, dick, shaft [all
but the first considered obscene].</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Flower pecker</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Flower</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peckish</ent><br/
<hw>peck"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclined to eat; hungry.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/When shall I feel <xex>peckish</xex>
again?<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beaconsfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peckled</ent><br/
<hw>pec"kled</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"kl'd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speckled;
spotted.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecopteris</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*cop"te*ris</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*k<ocr/p"t<esl/*r<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pe`kein</grk> to comb +
<grk>petri`s</grk> a kind of fern.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An
extensive genus of fossil ferns; -- so named from the regular comblike
arrangement of the leaflets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pec"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>pecus</ets>. See <er>Pecuniary</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An extensive division of ruminants, including the antelopes, deer,
and cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pecs</ent><br/
<hw>pecs</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<ety>[contraction from <ets>pec</ets>toral muscles.]</ety> <def>The
pectoral muscles; -- a contraction used by body-building and health
enthusiasts. Used similarly to <er>abs</er> and <er>delts</er>.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectate</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
salt of pectic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecten</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pecten</ets>,
<ets>-inis</ets>, a comb, a kind of shellfish. See
<er>Pectinate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A vascular pigmented membrane projecting into the vitreous humor
within the globe of the eye in birds, and in many reptiles and fishes;
-- also called <altname>marsupium</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
pubic bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of bivalve mollusks
of the genus <gen>Pecten</gen>, and numerous allied genera (family
<fam>Pectinid<ae/</fam>); a scallop. See <er>Scallop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The comb of a scorpion. See
<er>Comb</er>, 4 <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pectic</ent><br/
<hw>pec"tic</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>phkto`s</grk> curdled.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to pectin; specifically, designating an acid obtained from
ordinary vegetable jelly (pectin) as an amorphous substance, tough and
horny when dry, but gelatinous when moist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pectin</ent><br/
<hw>pec"tin</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>phkto`s</grk> curdled, congealed, from <grk>phgny`nai</grk> to make
fast or stiff: cf. F. <ets>pectine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>One of a series of carbohydrates, commonly called
<altname>vegetable jelly</altname>, found very widely distributed in the
vegetable kingdom, especially in ripe fleshy fruits, as apples,
cranberries, etc. It is extracted as variously colored, translucent
substances, which are soluble in hot water but become viscous on
cooling. It is commonly used in making fruit jelllies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ti*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pecten</ets> comb. See <er>Pectinate</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to a comb; resembling a comb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ti*nal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fish whose bones resemble comb
teeth.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinated</ent><br/
<ent>Pectinate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pec"ti*na`te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pec"ti*na`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pectinatus</ets>, p.
pr. of <ets>pectinare</ets> to comb, from <ets>pecten</ets>,
<ets>-inis</ets>, a comb; cf. Gr. <grk>pe`kein</grk> to comb, AS.
<ets>feax</ets> hair, OHG. <ets>fahs</ets>, E. <ets>paxwax</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Resembling the teeth of a comb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>Having very narrow, close
divisions, in arrangement and regularity resembling those of a comb;
comblike; <as>as, a <ex>pectinate</ex> leaf; <ex>pectinated</ex>
muscles</as>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> <sd>(e)</sd> of
<er>Antenn<ae/</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Interlaced, like two combs.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/Our fingers <xex>pectinated</xex>, or shut together.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pectinate claw</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a claw
having a serrate edge, found in some birds, and supposed to be used in
cleaning the feathers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinately</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ti*nate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
pectinate manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectination</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`ti*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state of being pectinated; that which is pectinated.</def> <rj><au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of combing; the combing of the
head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>Comblike toothing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectineal</ent><br/
<hw>Pec*tin"e*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pecten</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or
pertaining to the pecten.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Relating to, or
connected with, the pubic bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinibranch</ent><br/
<hw>Pec*tin"i*branch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Pectinibranchiata. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinibranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pec`ti*ni*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pecten</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Gastropoda, including those that
have a comblike gill upon the neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectinibranchiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`ti*ni*bran"chi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pecten</ets>, <ets>-inis</ets>, a comb + E.
<ets>branchiate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having pectinated
gills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectiniform</ent><br/
<hw>Pec*tin"i*form</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k*t<icr/n"<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Comblike in form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectize</ent><br/
<hw>Pec*tize"</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k*t<imac/z")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>phkto`s</grk> solid.]</ety> <def>To congeal; to change
into a gelatinous mass.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>H.
Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"to*lite</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<osl/*l<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pecten</ets> a comb + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A whitish mineral occurring in radiated or
fibrous crystalline masses. It is a hydrous silicate of lime and
soda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"to*ral</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<osl/*r<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pectoralis</ets>, fr. <ets>pectus</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>
the breast; cf. F. <ets>pectoral</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the breast, or chest; <as>as, the <ex>pectoral</ex>
muscles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to, or good for, diseases of the chest or
lungs; <as>as, a <ex>pectoral</ex> remedy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the breast conspicuously
colored; <as>as, the <ex>pectoral</ex> sandpiper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pectoral arch</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pectoral girdle</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the two
or more bony or cartilaginous pieces of the vertebrate skeleton to which
the fore limbs are articulated; the shoulder girdle. In man it consists
of two bones, the scapula and clavicle, on each side.</cd> --
<col><b>Pectoral cross</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>a cross worn on
the breast by bishops and abbots, and sometimes also by canons.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Pectoral fins</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pectorals</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>fins situated
on the sides, behind the gills. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Fin</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pectoral rail</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Land rail</cref> <sd>(b)</sd> under
<er>Land</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pectoral sandpiper</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the jacksnipe <sd>(b)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"to*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pectorale</ets> a breastplate, neut. of
<ets>pectoralis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A covering or protecting
for the breast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A breastplate, esp.
that worn by the Jewish high person.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A clasp or
a cross worn on the breast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A medicine for diseases of the chest organs,
especially the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectorally</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"to*ral*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>As connected
with the breast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectoriloquial</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`to*ri*lo"qui*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pectoriloque</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or of the nature of,
pectoriloquy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectoriloquism</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`to*ril"o*quism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Pectoriloquy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectoriloquous</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`to*ril"o*quous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pectoriloquial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectoriloquy</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`to*ril"o*quy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pectus</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>, the breast + <ets>loqui</ets> to
speak: cf. F. <ets>pectoriloquie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The distinct articulation of the sounds of a patient's voice, heard
on applying the ear to the chest in auscultation. It usually indicates
some morbid change in the lungs or pleural cavity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectose</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"tose`</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<omac/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pect</ets>ic + cellul<ets>ose</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An amorphous carbohydrate found in the vegetable
kingdom, esp. in unripe fruits. It is associated with cellulose, and is
converted into substances of the pectin group.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectosic</ent><br/
<hw>Pec*to"sic</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k*t<omac/"s<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, resembling, or derived from,
pectose; specifically, designating an acid supposed to constitute
largely ordinary pectin or vegetable jelly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pectostraca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pec*tos"tra*ca</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k*t<ocr/s"tr<adot/*k<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>phkto`s</grk> fixed +
<grk>'o`strakon</grk> shell of a testacean.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A degenerate order of Crustacea, including the Rhizocephala and
Cirripedia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pectous</ent><br/
<hw>pec"tous</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or consisting of,
pectose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pectus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>pec"tus</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/k"t<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pectora</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., the
breast.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The breast of a bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecul</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"ul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Picul</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculate</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peculated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Peculating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>peculatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>peculari</ets> to peculate, akin to
<ets>peculium</ets> private property. See <er>Peculiar</er>.]</ety>
<def>To appropriate to one's own use the property of the public; to
steal public moneys intrusted to one's care; to embezzle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An oppressive, . . . rapacious, and <qex>peculating</qex>
despotism.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculation</ent><br/
<hw>Pec`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
practice of peculating, or of defrauding the public by appropriating to
one's own use the money or goods intrusted to one's care for management
or disbursement; embezzlement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every British subject . . . active in the discovery of
<qex>peculations</qex> has been ruined.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculator</ent><br/
<hw>Pec"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who peculates.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Peculators</xex> of the public
gold.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculiar</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cul"iar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peculiaris</ets>, fr. <ets>peculium</ets> private property, akin to
<ets>pecunia</ets> money: cf. OF. <ets>peculier</ets>. See
<er>Pecuniary</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One's own; belonging solely
or especially to an individual; not possessed by others; of private,
personal, or characteristic possession and use; not owned in common or
in participation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And purify unto himself a <qex>peculiar</qex> people.</q>
<rj><qau>Titus ii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hymns . . . that Christianity hath <qex>peculiar</qex> unto
itself.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Particular; individual; special;
appropriate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While each <qex>peculiar</qex> power forgoes his wonted seat.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My fate is Juno's most <qex>peculiar</qex> care.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Unusual; singular; rare; strange; <as>as, the sky
had a <ex>peculiar</ex> appearance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Peculiar</er>, <er>Special</er>,
<er>Especial</er>.</syn> <usage> <xex>Peculiar</xex> is from the Roman
<xex>peculium</xex>, which was a thing emphatically and distinctively
one's own, and hence was dear. The former sense always belongs to
<xex>peculiar</xex> (as, a <xex>peculiar</xex> style,
<xex>peculiar</xex> manners, etc.), and usually so much of the latter as
to involve feelings of interest; as, <ex>peculiar</ex> care,
watchfulness, satisfaction, etc. Nothing of this kind belongs to
<xex>special</xex> and <xex>especial</xex>. They mark simply the
relation of <xex>species</xex> to <xex>genus</xex>, and denote that
there is something in this case more than ordinary; as, a
<xex>special</xex> act of Congress; <xex>especial</xex> pains,
etc.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beauty, which, either walking or asleep,<br/
Shot forth <qex>peculiar</qex> graces.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For naught so vile that on the earth doth live,<br/
But to the earth some <qex>special</qex> good doth give.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculiar</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cul"iar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is
peculiar; a sole or exclusive property; a prerogative; a
characteristic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Revenge is . . . the <qex>peculiar</qex> of Heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Canon Law)</fld> <def>A particular parish or
church which is exempt from the jurisdiction of the ordinary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Court of Peculiars</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a
branch of the Court of Arches having cognizance of the affairs of
peculiars.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>Dean of
peculiars</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Dean</er>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculiarity</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cul`iar"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(p<esl/*k<umac/l`y<acr/r"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Peculiarities</plw>
<pr>(p<esl/*k<umac/l`y<acr/r"<icr/*t<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being peculiar; individuality;
singularity.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is peculiar; a special and distinctive
characteristic or habit; particularity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The smallest <qex>peculiarity</qex> of temper or manner.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Exclusive possession or right.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculiarize</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cul"iar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peculiarized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Peculiarizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To make peculiar; to set apart or assign,
as an exclusive possession.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. John
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculiarly</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cul"iar*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a peculiar manner;
particularly; in a rare and striking degree; unusually.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculiarness</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cul"iar*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being peculiar; peculiarity.</def> <rj><au>Mede.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peculium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*cu"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Peculiar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Law)</fld> <def>The
saving of a son or a slave with the father's or master's consent; a
little property or stock of one's own; any exclusive personal or
separate property.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A special fund for private and personal
uses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A slight <qex>peculium</qex> only subtracted to supply his snuff
box and tobacco pouch.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecunial</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cu"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pecuniary.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecuniarily</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cun"ia*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
pecuniary manner; as regards money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecuniary</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cun"ia*ry</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*k<umac/n"y<asl/*r<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pecuniarius</ets>, fr. <ets>pecunia</ets>
money, orig., property in cattle, fr. <ets>pecus</ets> cattle: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/cuniaire</ets>. See <er>Fee</er>, and cf.
<er>Peculiar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to money; monetary;
<as>as, a <ex>pecuniary</ex> penalty; a <ex>pecuniary</ex>
reward.</as></def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pecunious</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*cu"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pecuniosus</ets>, fr. <ets>pecunia</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/cunieux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Abounding in money; wealthy;
rich.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sherwood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ped</ent><br/
<hw>Ped</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. See
<er>Peddler</er>.]</ety> <def>A basket; a hammer; a pannier.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedage</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"age</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<asl/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>pedagium</ets>, for <ets>pedaticum</ets>. See
<er>Paage</er>.]</ety> <def>A toll or tax paid by passengers, entitling
them to safe-conduct and protection.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spelman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagog</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"a*gog</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pedagogue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogic</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`a*gog"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pedagogic</er>, <pos>a.</pos>; cf. G. <ets>pedagogik</ets>.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Pedagogics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogical</ent><br/
<ent>Pedagogic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ped`a*gog"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ped`a*gog"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paidagwgiko`s</grk>:
cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/dagogique</ets>. See <er>Pedagogue</er>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a pedagogue; suited to, or characteristic of, a
pedagogue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogics</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`a*gog"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The science or
art of teaching; the principles and rules of teaching;
pedagogy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogism</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"a*gog*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dagogisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The system, occupation,
character, or manner of pedagogues.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Avocations of pedantry and <qex>pedagogism</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>De
Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogue</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"a*gogue</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<asl/*g<ocr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>p<eacute/dagogue</ets>, L. <ets>paedagogus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>paidagwgo`s</grk>; <grk>pai^s</grk>, <grk>paido`s</grk>, a boy +
<grk>'a`gein</grk> to lead, guide; cf. <grk>'agwgo`s</grk> leading. See
<er>Page</er> a servant, <er>Agent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>A slave who led his master's children to school, and
had the charge of them generally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A teacher of children; one whose occupation is to
teach the young; a schoolmaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who by teaching has become formal, positive, or
pedantic in his ways; one who has the manner of a schoolmaster; a
pedant.</def> <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogue</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"a*gogue</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>paedagogare</ets> to instruct.]</ety> <def>To play the pedagogue
toward.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedagogy</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"a*go`gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paidagwgi`a</grk>: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/dagogie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Pedagogics; pedagogism.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedal</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"dal</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"d<ait/l <it>in def 1</it>; 277)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pedalis</ets>, fr. <ets>pes</ets>,
<ets>pedis</ets>, foot. See <er>Foot</er>, and cf. <er>Pew</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the foot, or to feet, literally or
figuratively; specifically <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, pertaining to the foot of
a mollusk; <as>as, the <ex>pedal</ex> ganglion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<ait/l)</pr> <def>Of or pertaining to a
pedal; having pedals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pedal curve</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Pedal
surface</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>the curve or surface
which is the locus of the feet of perpendiculars let fall from a fixed
point upon the straight lines tangent to a given curve, or upon the
planes tangent to a given surface.</cd> -- <col><b>Pedal note</b></col>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>the note which is held or sustained through an
organ point. See <cref>Organ point</cref>, under <er>Organ</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pedal organ</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>an organ which has
pedals or a range of keys moved by the feet; that portion of a full
organ which is played with the feet.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedal</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"dal</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dale</ets>, It. <ets>pedale</ets>. See <er>Pedal</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A lever or key
acted on by the foot, as in the pianoforte to raise the dampers, or in
the organ to open and close certain pipes; a treadle, as in a lathe or a
bicycle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A pedal curve or
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pedaler</ent><br/
<hw>pedaler</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a person who rides a pedal-driven
vehicle.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pedaller.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedalian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*da"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to the
foot, or to a metrical foot; pedal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Maunder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedality</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
measuring by paces.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Ash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pedaller</ent><br/
<hw>pedaller</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a person who rides a pedal-driven
vehicle; a pedaler.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pedaler.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*da"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pedaneus</ets> of the size of a foot.]</ety> <def>Going on foot;
pedestrian.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedant</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/dant</ets>, It. <ets>pedante</ets>, fr. Gr.
<grk>paidey`ein</grk> to instruct, from <grk>pai^s</grk> boy. See
<er>Pedagogue</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A schoolmaster; a
pedagogue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>pedant</qex> that keeps a school i'th' church.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who puts on an air of learning; one who makes a
vain display of learning; a pretender to superior knowledge.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A scholar, yet surely no <qex>pedant</qex>, was he.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedantical</ent><br/
<ent>Pedantic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe*dan"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pe*dan"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a pedant;
characteristic of, or resembling, a pedant; ostentatious of learning;
<as>as, a <ex>pedantic</ex> writer; a <ex>pedantic</ex> description; a
<ex>pedantical</ex> affectation.</as></def> <ldquo/Figures
<xex>pedantical</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedantically</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dan"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pedantic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedanticly</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dan"tic*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Pedantically.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedantism</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"ant*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office,
disposition, or act of a pedant; pedantry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedantize</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"ant*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dantiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To play the pedant; to use
pedantic expressions.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedantocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`an*toc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pedant</ets> + demo<ets>cracy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The sway of
pedants.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>J. S. Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedantry</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"ant*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/danterie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act, character, or manners
of a pedant; vain ostentation of learning.</def> <ldquo/This
<xex>pedantry</xex> of quotation.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is a practice that savors much of <qex>pedantry</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedanty</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"ant*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An assembly or
clique of pedants.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*da"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pedarius</ets>, fr. <ets>pedarius</ets> belonging to the foot, fr.
<ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld>
<def>One of a class eligible to the office of senator, but not yet
chosen, who could sit and speak in the senate, but could not vote; -- so
called because he might indicate his opinion by walking over to the side
of the party he favored when a vote was taken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedary</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pedaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>pedarius</ets> of
the foot.]</ety> <def>A sandal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*da"ta</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*d<amac/"t<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See <er>Pedate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An order of holothurians, including those that have ambulacral
suckers, or feet, and an internal gill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1057 --></p>

<p><ent>Pedate</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"ate</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pedatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pedare</ets> to furnish with feet, fr.
<ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a foot.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Palmate, with the lateral lobes cleft into two or more segments; --
said of a leaf.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Ped"ate*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedatifid</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dat"i*fid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pedate</ets> + root of L. <ets>findere</ets> to split.]</ety>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <def>Cleft in a pedate manner, but having the
lobes distinctly connected at the base; -- said of a leaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peddle</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Peddler</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To travel about with wares for
sale; to go from place to place, or from house to house, for the purpose
of retailing goods; <as>as, to <ex>peddle</ex> without a
license</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To do a small business; to be busy about trifles; to
piddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peddle</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"dle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Peddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Peddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To sell from place
to place; to retail by carrying around from customer to customer; to
hawk; hence, to retail in very small quantities; <as>as, to
<ex>peddle</ex> vegetables or tinware</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peddler</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"dler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pedlere</ets>, <ets>pedlare</ets>, also <ets>peddare</ets>,
<ets>peoddare</ets>, fr. OE. <ets>ped</ets> a basket, of unknown
origin.]</ety> <def>One who peddles; a traveling trader; one who travels
about, retailing small wares; a hawker.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pedlar</asp> and <asp>pedler</asp>.]</altsp> <ldquo/Some vagabond
huckster or <xex>peddler</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hakluyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peddlery</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"dler*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pedlary</ets> and <ets>pedlery</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
trade, or the goods, of a peddler; hawking; small retail business, like
that of a peddler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Trifling; trickery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Look . . . into these their deceitful
<xex>peddleries</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peddling</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"dling</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Hawking; acting as a
peddler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Petty; insignificant; trifling; paltry; piddling; --
now less common than <altname>piddling</altname>.</def> <ldquo/The
miserable remains of a <xex>peddling</xex> commerce.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> petty; insignificant; trifling; paltry;
piddling.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pederast</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"er*ast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paiderasth`s</grk>; <grk>pai^s</grk>, <grk>paido`s</grk>, a boy +
<grk>'era^n</grk> to love: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/d<eacute/raste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One guilty of pederasty;
a sodomite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pederastic</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`er*as"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paiderastiko`s</grk>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
pederasty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pederasty</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"er*as`ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>paiderasti`a</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/d<eacute/rastie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sexual activity between
two males; sodomy; the <ldquo/crime against nature<rdquo/; -- used
especially when one partner is a boy.</def> <note>The term <ldquo/crime
against nature<rdquo/ to refer to homosexual activity is now (2002)
seldom used except by religious conservatives. It was in the 1913
Webster, and is left here for historical purposes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pederero</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`e*re"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>pedrero</ets>, fr. OSp. <ets>pedra</ets>, Sp. <ets>piedra</ets>, a
stone, L. <ets>petra</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>pe`tra</grk>. So named because
it was at first charged with stones.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A
term formerly applied to a short piece of chambered ordnance.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>paterero</asp> and
<asp>peterero</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*de"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>ph`dhsis</grk> a leaping.]</ety> <def>Same as <cref>Brownian
movement</cref>, under <er>Brownian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestal</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"es*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>pedestal</ets>; cf. F. <ets>pi<eacute/destal</ets>, It.
<ets>piedestallo</ets>; fr. L. <ets>es</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot +
OHG. <ets>stal</ets> standing place, station, place, akin to E.
<ets>stall</ets>. See <er>Foot</er>, and <er>Stall</er>, and
<er>Footstall</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The base
or foot of a column, statue, vase, lamp, or the like; the part on which
an upright work stands. It consists of three parts, the base, the die or
dado, and the cornice or surbase molding. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Column</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Build him a <qex>pedestal</qex>, and say, <ldquo/Stand
there!<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A short free-standing column
or column-like object designed to support a work of art or other object;
a column serving the same function as the base of a statue. It may be
made of wood, marble, or other suitable material.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Furniture)</fld> <def>A part of a desk which
contains a frame and drawers, stands on the floor, and provides support
for the desk surface. There may be zero, one, or two such
<ex>pedestals</ex> in a desk.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Railroad Cars)</fld> <def>A casting
secured to the frame of a truck and forming a jaw for holding a journal
box.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A pillow block; a low
housing.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Bridge Building)</fld> <def>An iron
socket, or support, for the foot of a brace at the end of a truss where
it rests on a pier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pedestal coil</b></col> <fld>(steam Heating)</fld>, <cd>a
group of connected straight pipes arranged side by side and one above
another, -- used in a radiator.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestaled</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"es*taled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Placed on, or
supported by, a pedestal; figuratively, exalted.</def>
<rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pedestaled</qex> haply in a palace court.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrial</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pedester</ets>, <ets>-esteris</ets>, fr. <ets>pes</ets>,
<ets>pedis</ets>, a foot: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/destere</ets>. See
<er>Pedal</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the feet; employing the
foot or feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrially</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pedestrial
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Going on foot; performed on foot; <as>as, a <ex>pedestrian</ex>
journey</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lacking in distinction or imaginativeness; ordinary;
commonplace; dull; insipid; prosaic; <as>as, <ex>pedestrian</ex>
prose</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A walker; one who journeys on
foot; a foot traveler; specif., a professional walker or
runner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act,
art, or practice of a pedestrian; walking or running; traveling or
racing on foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrianize</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*an*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pedestrianized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pedestrianizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To practice walking; to
travel on foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedestrious</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*des"tri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Going on foot;
not winged.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pedestrious</xex>
animals.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedetentous</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`e*ten"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot + <ets>tendere</ets> to stretch
out: cf. L. <ets>tentim</ets> by degrees.]</ety> <def>Proceeding step by
step; advancing cautiously.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>pedetentous</qex> pace and <qex>pedetentous</qex> mind
in which it behooves the wise and virtuous improver to walk.</q>
<rj><qau>Sydney Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedo-</ent><br/
<ent>Pedi-</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ped"i-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ped"o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>. <ety>[See <er>Foot</er>.]</ety> <def>Combining forms from L.
<xex>pes</xex>, <xex>pedis</xex>, foot, <as>as <ex>pedi</ex>palp,
<ex>pedi</ex>reme, <ex>pedo</ex>meter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedial</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
foot, or to any organ called a foot; pedal.</def>
<rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediatric</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`i*at"ric</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d`<icr/*<acr/t"r<icr/k <it>or</it>
p<emac/d`<icr/*<acr/t"r<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pai^s</grk>, <grk>paido`s</grk>, child + <grk>'iatrei`a</grk>
healing.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the care and
medical treatment of children.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediatrics</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`i*at"rics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That branch of
medical science which treats of the hygiene and diseases of infants and
children.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> paediatrics.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pedicab</ent><br/
<hw>ped"i*cab</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<icr/*k<acr/b)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
tricycle (usually propelled by pedalling); used in the Orient for
transporting passengers for hire; <as>as, boys who once pulled rickshaws
now pedal <ex>pedicabs</ex></as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cycle rickshaw.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicel</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/dicelle</ets>. See <er>Pedicle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A stalk which supports one flower or
fruit, whether solitary or one of many ultimate divisions of a common
peduncle. See <er>Peduncle</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Flower</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A slender support of any
special organ, as that of a capsule in mosses, an air vesicle in
alg<ae/, or a sporangium in ferns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A slender stem by which certain
of the lower animals or their eggs are attached. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
of <er>Aphis lion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The ventral part of
each side of the neural arch connecting with the centrum of a
vertebra.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An outgrowth of the frontal bones,
which supports the antlers or horns in deer and allied
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediceled</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*celed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pedicellate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicellaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ped`i*cel*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pedicellari<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pedicel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
peculiar forcepslike organ which occurs in large numbers upon starfishes
and echini. Those of starfishes have two movable jaws, or blades, and
are usually nearly, or quite, sessile; those of echini usually have
three jaws and a pedicel. See <xex>Illustration</xex> in
Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicellate</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*cel`late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
pedicel; supported by a pedicel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicellina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ped`i*cel*li"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pedicel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of Bryozoa,
of the order <ord>Entoprocta</ord>, having a bell-shaped body supported
on a slender pedicel. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Entoprocta</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicle</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pediculus</ets> a little foot, dim. of <ets>pes</ets> foot: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dicule</ets>. See <er>edal</er>, and cf.
<er>Pedicel</er>.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Pedicel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicular</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pedicularis</ets>, fr. <ets>pediculus</ets> a louse: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/diculaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to lice;
having the lousy distemper (phthiriasis); lousy.</def>
<rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediculate</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Pediculati.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediculati</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*dic`u*la"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pedicle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order
of fishes including the anglers. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Angler</er> and <er>Batfish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediculation</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dic`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Phthiriasis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicule</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*cule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pedicle</er>.]</ety> <def>A pedicel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediculina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*dic`u*li"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pediculus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
parasitic hemipterous insects, including the true lice. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediculous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dic"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pediculosus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pedicular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediculus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*dic"u*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pediculi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a
louse.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of wingless parasitic
Hemiptera, including the common lice of man. See
<er>Louse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedicure</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*cure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets> +
L. <ets>cura</ets> care.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Professional care of
the feet, toes, and toenails.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A single treatment of the feet, toes, and
toenails.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who cares for the feet and nails; a
chiropodist.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Ped"i*cure</wf>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
-- <wf>Ped"i*cur*ism</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Ped"i*cur*ist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediform</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*form</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Shaped like a
foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets> + <ets>-gerous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Bearing or having feet or legs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedigree</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*gree</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<icr/*gr<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Of unknown origin; possibly fr. F. <ets>par degr<eacute/s</ets> by
degrees, -- for a pedigree is properly a genealogical table which
records the relationship of families <ets>by degrees</ets>; or, perh.,
fr. F. <ets>pied de grue</ets> crane's foot, from the shape of the
heraldic genealogical trees.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A line of
ancestors; descent; lineage; genealogy; a register or record of a line
of ancestors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Alterations of surnames . . . have obscured the truth of our
<qex>pedigrees</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Camden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His vanity labored to contrive us a <qex>pedigree</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am no herald to inquire of men's <qex>pedigrees</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Jews preserved the <qex>pedigrees</qex> of their tribes.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Stock Breeding)</fld> <def>A record of the lineage
or strain of an animal, as of a horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedigree clause</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*gree clause</hw>. <def>A clause sometimes inserted in
contracts or specifications, requiring that a material of construction,
as cement, must be of a brand that has stood the test of a specified
number of years' use in an important public work.</def> <mark>[Cant, U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediluvy</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*lu`vy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets>
+ L. <ets>luere</ets> to wash: cf. It. & Sp. <ets>pediluvio</ets>, F.
<ets>p<eacute/diluve</ets>.]</ety> <def>The bathing of the feet, a bath
for the feet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedimana</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*dim"a*na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot + <ets>manus</ets>
hand.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of marsupials, including
the opossums.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedimane</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*mane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dimane</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A pedimanous
marsupial; an opossum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedimanous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dim"a*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pedimana</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having feet resembling
hands, or with the first toe opposable, as the opossums and
monkeys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pediment</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pes</ets>,
<ets>pedis</ets>, a foot. See <er>Foot</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Originally, in classical architecture, the triangular space forming
the gable of a simple roof; hence, a similar form used as a decoration
over porticoes, doors, windows, etc.; also, a rounded or broken frontal
having a similar position and use. See <er>Temple</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedimental</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`i*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a pediment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedioecetes</ent><br/
<hw>Pedioecetes</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A genus of fowl including the
<stype>sharp-tailed grouse</stype> (<spn>Pedioecetes phasianellus</spn>,
also called the <stype>prairie chicken</stype>).</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Pedioecetes</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedionomus</ent><br/
<hw>Pedionomus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of birds including
the <stype>plains wanderer</stype> (<spn>Pedionomus torquatus</spn>) of
New South Wales as its only species. It is the only genus of the family
<fam>Pedionomidae</fam> of the order <ord>Passeriformes</ord> and the
plains wanderer is the only species in the family. The total world
population (as of ca. 2000) is estimated to be 5 to 10 thousand.</def>
<note>See also http://www.philipmaher.com/plains.htm.</note><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus Pedionomus.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedipalp</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*palp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dipalpe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
Pedipalpi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedipalpi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ped`i*pal"pi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pedipalpus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
Arachnida, including the whip scorpions (<gen>Thelyphonus</gen>) and
allied forms. Sometimes used in a wider sense to include also the true
scorpions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedipalpous</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`i*pal"pous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or resembling, the pedipalps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedipalpus</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`i*pal"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pedipalpi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Pes</er>, and
<er>Palpus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the second pair
of mouth organs of arachnids. In some they are leglike, but in others,
as the scorpion, they terminate in a claw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedireme</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"i*reme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets> +
L. <ets>remus</ets> oar.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A crustacean,
some of whose feet serve as oars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedler</ent><br/
<ent>Pedlar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ped"lar</hw>, <hw>Ped"ler</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Peddler</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedobaptism</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`do*bap"tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pai^s</grk>, <grk>paido`s</grk>, a child + E.
<ets>baptism</ets>.]</ety> <def>The baptism of infants or of small
children.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>p<ae/dobaptism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedobaptist</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`do*bap"tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
advocates or practices infant baptism.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>p<ae/dobaptist</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedograph</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"o*graph</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/d"<osl/*gr<adot/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>pe`don</grk> ground + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
instrument carried by a pedestrian for automatically making a
topographical record of the ground covered during a journey.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedology</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pai^s</grk>, <grk>paido`s</grk>, child + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pediatrics.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets>
+ <ets>-mancy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by examining the soles of
the feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets>, <ets>pedo-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>An
instrument for including the number of steps in walking, and so
ascertaining the distance passed over. It is usually in the form of a
watch; an oscillating weight by the motion of the body causes the index
to advance a certain distance at each step.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedometrical</ent><br/
<ent>Pedometric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ped`o*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ped`o*met"ric*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or measured by,
a pedometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedomotive</ent><br/
<hw>Ped`o*mo"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pedi-</ets>, <ets>pedo-</ets> + <ets>-motive</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Moved or worked by the action of the foot or feet on a pedal or
treadle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pedophile</ent><br/
<hw>pe"do*phile`</hw> <pr>(P<emac/"d<osl/*f<imac/l)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A person who has pedophilia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pedophiliac.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pedophilia</ent><br/
<hw>pedophilia</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sexual perversion in which
children rather than adults most strongly excite sexual desire, and are
used as sexual partners.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pedophiliac</ent><br/
<hw>pedophiliac</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>pedophile</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pedophile.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedotrophy</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dot"ro*phy</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*d<ocr/t"r<osl/*f<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>paidotrofi`a</grk>, fr. <grk>pai^s</grk>,
<grk>paido`s</grk>, a child + <grk>tre`fein</grk> to nourish: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/dotrophie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art of nourishing
children properly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedrail</ent><br/
<hw>Ped"rail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pedi-</er>;
<er>Rail</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A device
intended to replace the wheel of a self-propelled vehicle for use on
rough roads and to approximate to the smoothness in running of a wheel
on a metal track. The tread consists of a number of rubber shod feet
which are connected by ball-and-socket joints to the ends of sliding
spokes. Each spoke has attached to it a small roller which in its turn
runs under a short pivoted rail controlled by a powerful set of springs.
This arrangement permits the feet to accommodate themselves to obstacles
even such as steps or stairs. The pedrail was invented by one <person>B.
J. Diplock</person> of London, Eng.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A vehicle,
as a traction engine, having such pedrails.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedregal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe`dre*gal"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., a stony
place, fr. <ets>piedra</ets> stone.]</ety> <def>A lava field.</def>
<mark>[Mexico & Western U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedro</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"dro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Sp.
<ets>Pedro</ets> Peter, L. <ets>Petrus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>Pe`tros</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The five of trumps in certain varieties of auction pitch.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A variety of auction pitch in which the five of trumps
counts five.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peduncle</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dun"cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formed fr.
(assumed) L. <ets>pedunculus</ets>, dim. of <ets>pes</ets>,
<ets>pedis</ets>, a foot: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/doncule</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The stem or stalk that supports the
flower or fruit of a plant, or a cluster of flowers or fruits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The ultimate divisions or branches of a peduncle are
called <xex>pedicels</xex>. In the case of a solitary flower, the stalk
would be called a <xex>peduncle</xex> if the flower is large, and a
<xex>pedicel</xex> if it is small or delicate.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sort of stem by which certain
shells and barnacles are attached to other objects. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Barnacle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A band of nervous or fibrous
matter connecting different parts of the brain; <as>as, the
<ex>peduncles</ex> of the cerebellum; the <ex>peduncles</ex> of the
pineal gland.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peduncled</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dun"cled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a peduncle;
supported on a peduncle; pedunculate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peduncular</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*dun"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/donculaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
peduncle; growing from a peduncle; <as>as, a <ex>peduncular</ex>
tendril</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedunculata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*dun`cu*la"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Peduncle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
Cirripedia, including the stalked or goose barnacles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pedunculated</ent><br/
<ent>Pedunculate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe*dun"cu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pe*dun"cu*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having a
peduncle; growing on a peduncle; <as>as, a <ex>pedunculate</ex> flower;
a <ex>pedunculate</ex> eye, as in a lobster.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pee</ent><br/
<hw>Pee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Pea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pee</ent><br/
<hw>Pee</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Bill of
an anchor. See <er>Peak</er>, 3 <sd>(c)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Urine.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of urinating; -- used in the informal
<col>take a pee</col>, meaning, to urinate.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pee</ent><br/
<hw>pee</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To urinate.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peece</ent><br/
<hw>Peece</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<def>See <er>Piece</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peechi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pee"chi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The dauw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peek</ent><br/
<hw>Peek</hw> <pr>(p<emac/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>piken</ets>: cf. F. <ets>piquer</ets> to pierce, prick, E.
<ets>pique</ets>. Cf. <er>Peak</er>.]</ety> <def>To look
surreptitiously, or with the eyes half closed, or through a crevice; to
peep.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peekaboo</ent><br/
<hw>Peek"a*boo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A child's game;
bopeep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peeing</ent><br/
<hw>pee"ing</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Urination; -- an informal term; <as>as, he doesn't like
<ex>peeing</ex> out of doors</as>.</def> <mark>[Inform.]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pee, pissing, urinating.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peel</ent><br/
<hw>Peel</hw> <pr>(p<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pel</ets>. Cf. <er>Pile</er> a heap.]</ety> <def>A small tower,
fort, or castle; a keep.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peel</ent><br/
<hw>Peel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pelle</ets>, L.
<ets>pala</ets>.]</ety> <def>A spadelike implement, variously used, as
for removing loaves of bread from a baker's oven; also, a T-shaped
implement used by printers and bookbinders for hanging wet sheets of
paper on lines or poles to dry. Also, the blade of an oar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peel</ent><br/
<hw>Peel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Confused with <ets>peel</ets> to
strip, but fr. F. <ets>piller</ets> to pillage. See <er>Pill</er> to
rob, <er>Pillage</er>.]</ety> <def>To plunder; to pillage; to rob.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But govern ill the nations under yoke,<br/
<qex>Peeling</qex> their provinces.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peel</ent><br/
<hw>Peel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Peeled</conjf> <pr>(p<emac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Peeling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>peler</ets> to pull out
the hair, to strip, to peel, fr. L. <ets>pilare</ets> to deprive of
hair, fr. <ets>pilus</ets> a hair; or perh. partly fr. F.
<ets>peler</ets> to peel off the skin, perh. fr. L. <ets>pellis</ets>
skin (cf. <er>Fell</er> skin). Cf. <er>Peruke</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To strip off the skin, bark, or rind of; to strip by drawing or
tearing off the skin, bark, husks, etc.; to flay; to decorticate;
<as>as, to <ex>peel</ex> an orange</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The skillful shepherd <qex>peeled</qex> me certain wands.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strip or tear off; to remove by stripping, as the
skin of an animal, the bark of a tree, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peel</ent><br/
<hw>Peel</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lose the skin, bark,
or rind; to come off, as the skin, bark, or rind does; -- often used
with an adverb; <as>as, the bark <ex>peels</ex> easily or
readily</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strip naked; to disrobe. Often used with
<ptcl>down</ptcl> .</def> <mark>[nformal]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peel</ent><br/
<hw>Peel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The skin or rind; <as>as, the
<ex>peel</ex> of an orange</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peele</ent><br/
<hw>Pee"le</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"l<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A graceful and swift South African antelope
(<spn>Pelea capreola</spn>). The hair is woolly, and ash-gray on the
back and sides. The horns are black, long, slender, straight, nearly
smooth, and very sharp. Called also <altname>rheeboc</altname>,
<altname>rhebuck</altname>, <altname>rhebok</altname>, and
<altname>rehboc</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peeled</ent><br/
<hw>peeled</hw> <pr>(p<emac/ld)</pr> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Naked; -- used
informally.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bare-assed, bare-ass, in the altogether, in the
buff, in the raw, raw, naked as a jaybird, stark naked.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peeler</ent><br/
<hw>Peel"er</hw> <pr>(p<emac/l"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
peels or strips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peeler</ent><br/
<hw>Peel"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Peel</er> to
plunder.]</ety> <def>A pillager.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peeler</ent><br/
<hw>Peel"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A nickname for a policeman; -- so
called from <person>Sir Robert <etsep>Peel</etsep></person>.</def>
<mark>[British Slang]</mark> <see>See <er>Bobby</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peelhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Peel"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Peel</er>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peen</ent><br/
<hw>Peen</hw> <pr>(p<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>pinne</ets> pane of a hammer.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
round-edged, or hemispherical, end to the head of a hammer or sledge,
used to stretch or bend metal by indentation.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The sharp-edged end of the head of a mason's hammer.</def>
<altsp>[Spelt also <asp>pane</asp>, <asp>pein</asp>, and
<asp>piend</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peen</ent><br/
<hw>Peen</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To draw, bend, or straighten, as
metal, by blows with the peen of a hammer or sledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peenge</ent><br/
<hw>Peenge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To complain.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peep</ent><br/
<hw>Peep</hw> <pr>(p<emac/p)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peeped</conjf> <pr>(p<emac/pt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Peeping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of imitative
origin; cf. OE. <ets>pipen</ets>, F. <ets>piper</ets>,
<ets>p<eacute/pier</ets>, L. <ets>pipire</ets>, <ets>pipare</ets>,
<ets>pipiare</ets>, D. & G. <ets>piepen</ets>. Senses 2 and 3 perhaps
come from a transfer of sense from the sound which chickens make upon
the first breaking of the shell to the act accompanying it; or perhaps
from the influence of <ets>peek</ets>, or <ets>peak</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pipe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cry, as a chicken hatching or
newly hatched; to chirp; to cheep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was none that moved the wing, or opened the mouth, or
<qex>peeped</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Is. x. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To begin to appear; to look forth from concealment;
to make the first appearance; <as>as, the sun <ex>peeped</ex> over the
eastern hills</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When flowers first <qex>peeped</qex>, and trees did blossoms
bear.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1058 --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To look cautiously or slyly; to peer, as through a
crevice; to pry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Peep</qex> through the blanket of the dark.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From her cabined loophole <qex>peep</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peep sight</b></col>, <cd>an adjustable piece, pierced
with a small hole to peep through in aiming, attached to a rifle or
other firearm near the breech.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peep</ent><br/
<hw>Peep</hw> <pr>(p<emac/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
cry of a young chicken; a chirp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>First outlook or appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Oft have we seen him at the <qex>peep</qex> of dawn.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sly look; a look as through a crevice, or from a
place of concealment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To take t' other <qex>peep</qex> at the stars.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any small sandpiper,
as the least sandpiper (<spn>Trigna minutilla</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The European meadow pipit (<spn>Anthus pratensis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peep show</b></col>, <cd>a small show, or object
exhibited, which is viewed through an orifice or a magnifying
glass.</cd> -- <col><b>Peep-o'-day boys</b></col>, <cd>the Irish
insurgents of 1784; -- so called from their visiting the house of the
loyal Irish at day break in search of arms.</cd>
<mark>[Cant]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peeper</ent><br/
<hw>Peep"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A chicken
just breaking the shell; a young bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who peeps; a prying person; a spy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who's there? <qex>peepers</qex>, . . . eavesdroppers?</q>
<rj><qau>J. Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The eye; <as>as, to close the
<ex>peepers</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peephole</ent><br/
<hw>Peep"hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hole, or crevice,
through which one may peep without being discovered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peeping hole</ent><br/
<hw>Peep"ing hole`</hw>. <def>See <er>Peephole</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peep sight</ent><br/
<hw>Peep sight</hw>. <def>An adjustable piece, pierced with a small hole
to peep through in aiming, attached to a rifle or other firearm near the
breech; -- distinguished from an <contr>open sight</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peepul tree</ent><br/
<ent>peepul</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pee"pul</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"p<ucr/l)</pr>, <hw>Pee"pul
tree`</hw></mhw> <pr>(p<emac/"p<ucr/l tr<emac/`)</pr>. <ety>[Hind.
<ets>p<imac/pal</ets>, Skr. <ets>pippala</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A sacred tree (<spn>Ficus religiosa</spn>) of the Buddhists, a kind
of fig tree which attains great size and venerable age; it lacks the
prop roots of the <contr>banyan</contr>. See <er>Bo tree</er>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pippul tree</asp>, and <asp>pipal
tree</asp>.]</altsp><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pipal, peepul, peepul tree, pipal tree, pipul,
sacred fig, bo tree, <spn>Ficus religiosa</spn>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peer</ent><br/
<hw>Peer</hw> <pr>(p<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Peering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>parir</ets>,
<ets>pareir</ets> equiv. to F. <ets>para<icir/tre</ets> to appear, L.
<ets>parere</ets>. Cf. <er>Appear</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To come
in sight; to appear.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So honor <qex>peereth</qex> in the meanest habit.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>See how his gorget <qex>peers</qex> above his gown!</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Perh. a different word; cf. OE. <ets>piren</ets>,
LG. <ets>piren</ets>. Cf. <er>Pry</er> to peep.]</ety> <def>To look
narrowly or curiously or intently; to peep; <as>as, the <ex>peering</ex>
day</as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Peering</qex> in maps for ports, and piers, and roads.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As if through a dungeon grate he <qex>peered</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peer</ent><br/
<hw>Peer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>per</ets>, OF.
<ets>per</ets>, F. <ets>pair</ets>, fr. L. <ets>par</ets> equal. Cf.
<er>Apparel</er>, <er>Pair</er>, <er>Par</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Umpire</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the same rank, quality,
endowments, character, etc.; an equal; a match; a mate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In song he never had his <qex>peer</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shall they consort only with their <qex>peers</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A comrade; a companion; a fellow; an
associate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He all his <qex>peers</qex> in beauty did surpass.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A nobleman; a member of one of the five degrees of
the British nobility, namely, duke, marquis, earl, viscount, baron;
<as>as, a <ex>peer</ex> of the realm</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A noble <qex>peer</qex> of mickle trust and power.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>House of Peers</b></col>, <col><b>The
Peers</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the British House of Lords. See
<er>Parliament</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spiritual peers</b></col>, <cd>the
bishops and archibishops, or lords spiritual, who sit in the House of
Lords.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peer</ent><br/
<hw>Peer</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make equal in rank.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Heylin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peer</ent><br/
<hw>Peer</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To be, or to assume to be,
equal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peerage</ent><br/
<hw>Peer"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Peer</er> an
equal, and cf. <er>Parage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The rank or
dignity of a peer.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The body of peers; the nobility,
collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Charlemain with all his <qex>peerage</qex> fell.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peerdom</ent><br/
<hw>Peer"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Peerage; also, a
lordship.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peeress</ent><br/
<hw>Peer"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The wife of a peer; a woman
ennobled in her own right, or by right of marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peery</ent><br/
<ent>Peerie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Peer"ie</hw>, <hw>Peer"y</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 1st <er>Peer</er>, 2.]</ety> <def>Inquisitive;
suspicious; sharp.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <ldquo/Two
<xex>peery</xex> gray eyes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peerless</ent><br/
<hw>Peer"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no peer or
equal; matchless; superlative.</def> <ldquo/Her <xex>peerless</xex>
feature.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Unvailed her <qex>peerless</qex> light.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>--<wordforms><wf>Peer"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Peer"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peert</ent><br/
<hw>Peert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Peart</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peerweet</ent><br/
<hw>Peer"weet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pewit</er> (<sd>a</sd> & <sd>b</sd>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peevish</ent><br/
<hw>Pee"vish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pevische</ets>; of uncertain origin, perh. from a word imitative of
the noise made by fretful children + <ets>-ish</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Habitually fretful; easily vexed or fretted; hard to please; apt to
complain; querulous; petulant.</def> <ldquo/Her <xex>peevish</xex>
babe.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She is <qex>peevish</qex>, sullen, froward.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressing fretfulness and discontent, or
unjustifiable dissatisfaction; <as>as, a <ex>peevish</ex>
answer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Silly; childish; trifling.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To send such <qex>peevish</qex> tokens to a king.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Querulous; petulant; cross; ill-tempered; testy;
captious; discontented. See <er>Fretful</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peevishly</ent><br/
<hw>Pee"vish*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a peevish manner.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peevishness</ent><br/
<hw>Pee"vish*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being peevish;
disposition to murmur; sourness of temper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Petulance</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peewit</ent><br/
<ent>Peevit</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pee"vit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pee"wit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pewit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peewit</ent><br/
<hw>peewit</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A large crested Old World plover
having wattles and spurs; the pewit.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> lapwing, green plover, pewit, peevit,
peerwit.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any of several small olive-colored woodland
flycatchers of eastern North America.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pewee, peewee, pewit, wood pewee, <spn>Contopus
virens</spn>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peg</ent><br/
<hw>Peg</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pegge</ets>; cf. Sw. <ets>pigg</ets>, Dan. <ets>pig</ets> a point,
prickle, and E. <ets>peak</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small,
pointed piece of wood, used in fastening boards together, in attaching
the soles of boots or shoes, etc.; <as>as, a shoe
<ex>peg</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wooden pin, or nail, on which to hang things, as
coats, etc. Hence, colloquially and figuratively: A support; a reason; a
pretext; <as>as, a <ex>peg</ex> to hang a claim upon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of the pins of a musical instrument, on which
the strings are strained.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One of the pins used for marking points on a
cribbage board.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A step; a degree; esp. in the slang phrase <ldquo/To
take one down a <xex>peg</xex>.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To screw papal authority to the highest <qex>peg</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And took your grandees down a <qex>peg</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A drink of spirits, usually whisky or brandy diluted
with soda water.</def> <mark>[India]</mark></p>

<p><q>This over, the club will be visited for a
<ldquo/<qex>peg</qex>,<rdquo/ Anglice drink.</q> <rj><qau>Harper's
Mag.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>a hard throw, especially one
made to put out a baserunner; <as>as, the <ex>peg</ex> to the plate went
wild</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>peg board</b></col>, <cd>a board with multiple small
holes into which pegs can be inserted in different arrays so as to form
hooks from which to hang tools or other objects for convenient access;
it is typically hung from a wall in a workshop.</cd> -- <col><b>Peg
ladder</b></col>, <cd>a ladder with but one standard, into which cross
pieces are inserted.</cd> -- <col><b>Peg tankard</b></col>, <cd>an
ancient tankard marked with pegs, so as divide the liquor into equal
portions.</cd> <ldquo/Drink down to your <xex>peg</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Longfellow.</au> -- <col><b>Peg tooth</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Fleam
tooth</cref> under <er>Fleam</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Peg top</b></col>,
<cd>a boy's top which is spun by throwing it.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
peg</b></col>, <cd>a small screw without a head, for fastening
soles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peg</ent><br/
<hw>Peg</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pegged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pegging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To put pegs into; to fasten the parts of with pegs; <as>as, to
<ex>peg</ex> shoes</as>; to confine with pegs; to restrict or limit
closely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will rend an oak<br/
And <qex>peg</qex> thee in his knotty entrails.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cribbage)</fld> <def>To score with a peg, as points
in the game; <as>as, she <ex>pegged</ex> twelve points</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To identify; to recognize; <as>as, she
<ex>pegged</ex> him as a good carpenter; he was <ex>pegged</ex> as a
blowhard as soon as he started speaking; he was <ex>pegged</ex> as a
exceptional player even in high school</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>To throw (a ball); <as>as, he
<ex>pegged</ex> the runner out at second</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peg</ent><br/
<hw>Peg</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To work diligently, as one who pegs
shoes; -- usually with <xex>on</xex>, <xex>at</xex>, or <xex>away</xex>;
<as>as, to <ex>peg</ex> away at a task</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegador</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe`ga*dor"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., a
sticker.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A species of remora
(<spn>Echeneis naucrates</spn>). See <er>Remora</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegasean</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ga"se*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Pegasus, or, figuratively, to poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegasoid</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"a*soid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pegasus</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to
Pegasus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegasus</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"a*sus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>Ph`gasos</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Myth.)</fld> <def>A
winged horse fabled to have sprung from the body of Medusa when she was
slain. He is noted for causing, with a blow of his hoof, Hippocrene, the
inspiring fountain of the Muses, to spring from Mount Helicon. On this
account he is, in modern times, associated with the Muses, and with
ideas of poetic inspiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each spurs his jaded <qex>Pegasus</qex> apace.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A northern constellation near
the vernal equinoctial point. Its three brightest stars, with the
brightest star of Andromeda, form the <haspart>square of
Pegasus</haspart>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of small fishes, having
large pectoral fins, and the body covered with hard, bony plates.
Several species are known from the East Indies and China.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pegged</ent><br/
<hw>pegged</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/gd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tapering toward
the bottom; <as>as, <ex>pegged</ex> pants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>


<p><ent>Pegger</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who fastens with
pegs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegging</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"ging</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process of
fastening with pegs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegm</ent><br/
<hw>Pegm</hw> <pr>(p<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pegma</ets> a movable stage, Gr. <grk>ph^gma</grk>, orig., a
framework.]</ety> <def>A sort of moving machine employed in the old
pageants.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegmatite</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"ma*tite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/
something fastened together, in allusion to the quartz and feldspar in
graphic granite: cf. F. <ets>pegmatite</ets>. See <er>Pegm</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Graphic granite. See under
<er>Granite</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>More generally, a coarse
granite occurring as vein material in other rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegmatitic</ent><br/
<hw>Peg`ma*tit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or resembling, pegmatite; <as>as, the
<ex>pegmatic</ex> structure of certain rocks resembling graphic
granite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegmatoid</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"ma*toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pegmat</ets>ite + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Resembling pegmatite; pegmatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>phgh`</grk> fountain + <ets>-mancy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by
fountains.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pegroots</ent><br/
<hw>Peg"roots`</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/g"r<oomac/ts`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Same as <er>Setterwort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pehlevi</ent><br/
<hw>Peh"le*vi`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Parsee
<ets>Pahlavi</ets>.]</ety> <def>An ancient Persian dialect in which
words were partly represented by their Semitic equivalents. It was in
use from the 3d century (and perhaps earlier) to the middle of the 7th
century, and later in religious writings.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Pahlavi</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peignoir</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pei`gnoir"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>peigner</ets> to comb, L. <ets>pectinare</ets>. See
<er>Pectinate</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman's loose dressing sack; hence, a
loose morning gown or wrapper.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pein</ent><br/
<hw>Pein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Peen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peirameter</ent><br/
<hw>Pei*ram"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pei^ra</grk> a trial + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>A dynamometer
for measuring the force required to draw wheel carriages on roads of
different constructions.</def> <rj><au>G. Francis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peirastic</ent><br/
<hw>Pei*ras"tic</hw> <pr>(p<isl/*r<acr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>peirastiko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>peira^n</grk> to try, fr.
<grk>pei^ra</grk> a trial.]</ety> <def>Fitted for trial or test;
experimental; tentative; treating of attempts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peise</ent><br/
<hw>Peise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Poise</er>.]</ety> <def>A weight; a poise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/To weigh pence with a <xex>peise</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peise</ent><br/
<hw>Peise</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To poise or weigh.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest leaden slumber <qex>peise</qex> me down.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peitrel</ent><br/
<hw>Pei"trel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld>
<def>See <er>Peytrel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pejorative</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*jor"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/joratif</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pejor</ets>, used as compar. of
<ets>malus</ets> evil.]</ety> <def>Implying or imputing evil;
depreciatory; disparaging; unfavorable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pekan</ent><br/
<hw>Pek"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pekan</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Fisher</er>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peke</ent><br/
<hw>Peke</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a Pekingese.</def>
<mark>[Colloquial]</mark> <br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Pekinese, Pekingese.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pekingese</ent><br/
<ent>Pekinese</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pekinese</hw>, <hw>Pekingese</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
Chinese breed of small short-legged dogs with a long silky coat and
broad flat muzzle.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Pekingese, Peke.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pekoe</ent><br/
<hw>Pek"oe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Chin.
<ets>pih-hoau</ets>: cf. F. <ets>peko<eum/</ets>]</ety> <def>A kind of
black tea.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pecco</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pela</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<cref>Wax insect</cref>, under <er>Wax</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelage</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pelage</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>pilus</ets> hair.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
covering, or coat, of a mammal, whether of wool, fur, or hair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelagian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*la"gi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pelagius</ets>, Gr. <grk>pela`gios</grk>, fr. <grk>pe`lagos</grk>
the sea: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/lagien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to the sea; marine; pelagic; <as>as, <ex>pelagian</ex>
shells</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelagian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*la"gi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Pelagianus</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>p<eacute/lagien</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>A
follower of <etsep>Pelagius</etsep>, a British monk, born in the later
part of the 4th century, who denied the doctrines of hereditary sin, of
the connection between sin and death, and of conversion through
grace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelagian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*la"gi*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/lagien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Pelagius, or
to his doctrines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelagianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*la"gi*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/lagianisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrines of
Pelagius.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelagic</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*lag"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pelagicus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the ocean; --
applied especially to animals that live at the surface of the ocean,
away from the coast. Compare <contr>benthic</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelargonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pel`ar*gon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an organic acid (called also
<altname>nonoic acid</altname>) found in the leaves of the geranium
(<gen>Pelargonium</gen>) and allied plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelargonium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel`ar*go"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pelargo`s</grk> a stork.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A large genus of plants of the order
<ord>Geraniace<ae/</ord>, differing from Geranium in having a spurred
calyx and an irregular corolla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ About one hundred and seventy species are known, nearly
all of them natives of South Africa, and many having very beautiful
blossoms. See the Note under <er>Geranium</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelasgic</ent><br/
<ent>Pelasgian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe*las"gi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pe*las"gic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Pelasgus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>Pelasgo`s</grk> a Pelasgian.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the Pelasgians, an ancient people of Greece, of roving
habits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Wandering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelecan</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"e*can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pelican</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelecanidae</ent><br/
<hw>Pelecanidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of birds
consisting of the pelicans.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Pelecanidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelecaniformes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel`e*can`i*for"mes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pelican</er>, and <er>-form</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Those birds that are related to the pelican; the
Totipalmi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelecoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"e*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pe`lekys</grk> a hatchet + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A figure, somewhat hatched-shaped, bounded by a
semicircle and two inverted quadrants, and equal in area to the square
<xex>ABCD</xex> inclosed by the chords of the four quadrants.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pelicoid</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Math.
Dict.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pelecypod</ent><br/
<hw>pelecypod</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A marine or freshwater
mollusk having a soft body with platelike gills enclosed within two
shells hinged together; a member of the Pelecypoda.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bivalve, lamellibranch.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pelecypodous</ent><br/
<ent>pelecypod</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pelecypod</hw> <hw>pelecypodous</hw></mhw> <pos>adj.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>same as <er>lamellibranchiate</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> lamellibranch, lamellibranchiate.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelecypoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel`e*cyp"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>pe`lekys</grk> a hatchet + <ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Lamellibranchia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelegrine</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"e*grine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Peregrine</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelerine</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"er*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<egrave/lerine</ets> a tippet, fr. <ets>p<egrave/lerin</ets> a
pilgrim, fr. L. <ets>peregrinus</ets> foreign, alien. See
<er>Pilgrim</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman's cape; especially, a fur cape
that is longer in front than behind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pele's hair</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"le's hair</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[After a Hawaiian goddess
associated with the crater Kilauea.]</ety> <def>Glass threads or fibers
formed by the wind from bits blown from frothy lava or from the tips of
lava jets or from bits of liquid lava thrown into the air. It often
collects in thick masses resembling tow.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peleus</ent><br/
<hw>Peleus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <fld>(Classical Mythology)</fld>
<def>A king of the Myrmidons and father of Achilles; he was the son of
Aeachus.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelf</ent><br/
<hw>Pelf</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/lf)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pelfir</ets> booty, OF. <ets>pelfre</ets>, akin to
<ets>pelfrer</ets> to plunder, and perh. to E. <ets>pillage</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pilfer</er>.]</ety> <def>Money; riches; lucre; gain; -- generally
conveying the idea of something ill-gotten or worthless. It has no
plural.</def> <ldquo/Mucky <xex>pelf</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au>
<ldquo/Paltry <xex>pelf</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Burke.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can their <qex>pelf</qex> prosper, not got by valor or
industry?</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelfish</ent><br/
<hw>Pelf"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to pelf.</def>
<rj><au>Stanyhurst.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelfry</ent><br/
<ent>Pelfray</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pel"fray</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pel"fry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pelf; also, figuratively, rubbish;
trash.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cranmer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelican</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"i*can</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l"<icr/*k<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>p<eacute/lican</ets>, L. <ets>pelicanus</ets>,
<ets>pelecanus</ets>, Gr. <grk>peleka`n</grk>, <grk>peleka^s</grk>,
<grk>pele`kanos</grk>, the woodpecker, and also a water bird of the
pelican kind, fr. <grk>peleka^n</grk> to hew with an ax, fr.
<grk>pe`lekys</grk> an ax, akin to Skr. <ets>para<cced/u</ets>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pelecan</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any large webfooted bird of the genus
<gen>Pelecanus</gen>, of which about a dozen species are known. They
have an enormous bill, to the lower edge of which is attached a pouch in
which captured fishes are temporarily stored.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The American white pelican (<spn>Pelecanus
erythrorhynchos</spn>) and the brown species (<spn>Pelecanus
fuscus</spn>) are abundant on the Florida coast in winter, but breed
about the lakes in the Rocky Mountains and British America.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>A retort or still having a
curved tube or tubes leading back from the head to the body for
continuous condensation and redistillation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The principle is still employed in certain modern forms
of distilling apparatus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Frigate pelican</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
frigate bird. See under <er>Frigate</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pelican
fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>deep-sea fish (<spn>Eurypharynx
pelecanoides</spn>) of the order <ord>Lyomeri</ord>, remarkable for the
enormous development of the jaws, which support a large gular
pouch.</cd> -- <col><b>Pelican flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the very large and curiously shaped blossom of a climbing plant
(<spn>Aristolochia grandiflora</spn>) of the West Indies; also, the
plant itself.</cd> -- <col><b>Pelican ibis</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a large Asiatic wood ibis (<spn>Tantalus leucocephalus</spn>). The
head and throat are destitute of feathers; the plumage is white, with
the quills and the tail greenish black.</cd> -- <col><b>Pelican in her
piety</b></col> (in heraldry and symbolical art), <cd>a representation
of a pelican in the act of wounding her breast in order to nourish her
young with her blood; -- a practice fabulously attributed to the bird,
on account of which it was adopted as a symbol of the Redeemer, and of
charity.</cd> -- <col><b>Pelican's foot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a marine gastropod shell of the genus <gen>Aporrhais</gen>, esp.
<spn>Aporrhais pes-pelicani</spn> of Europe.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelick</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"ick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
American coot (<gen>Fulica</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelicoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"i*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pelecoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelicosauria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel`i*co*sau"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>peli`ka</grk> a wooden bowl (but taken to mean,
pelvis) + <grk>say^ros</grk> a lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>A suborder of Theromorpha, including terrestrial reptiles from the
Permian formation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peliom</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"li*om</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pelioma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of iolite, of
a smoky blue color; pelioma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelioma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe`li*o"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>peliwmw</grk>, fr. <grk>pelio`s</grk> livid.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A livid ecchymosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>See <er>Peliom</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelisse</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*lisse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pelliceus</ets>, <ets>pellicius</ets>, made of skins, fr.
<ets>pellis</ets> a skin. Cf. <er>Pelt</er> skin, <er>Pilch</er>, and
see 2d <er>Pell</er>.]</ety> <def>An outer garment for men or women,
originally of fur, or lined with fur; a lady's or child's long outer
garment, made of silk or other fabric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pell</ent><br/
<hw>Pell</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pelt</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To pelt; to knock
about.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pell</ent><br/
<hw>Pell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>pel</ets>, F.
<ets>peau</ets>, L. <ets>pellis</ets> a skin. See <er>Fell</er> a
skin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A skin or hide; a pelt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A roll of parchment; a parchment record.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Clerk of the pells</b></col>, <cd>formerly, an officer of
the exchequer who entered accounts on certain parchment rolls, called
<xex>pell rolls</xex>.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellack</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"lack</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l"l<ait/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
Gael. <er>Peileag</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
porpoise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellage</ent><br/
<hw>Pell"age</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l"<asl/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 2d
<er>Pell</er>.]</ety> <def>A customs duty on skins of leather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1059 --></p>

<p><ent>Pellagra</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"la*gra</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l"l<adot/*gr<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[It. <ets>pelle</ets> skin + <ets>agro</ets> rough.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An affection of the skin, characterized by
redness, especially in exposed areas, scaling and shedding of the skin,
and accompanied with severe gastrointestinal disturbance and nervous
symptoms. It is due to a deficiency of niacin (vitamin B3; nicotinic
acid) and protein in the diet, and may be caused by malnutrition, or, in
some cases, by a heavy dependence on maize for food. It was at one time
(ca. 1890) endemic in Northern Italy, and was called <altname>Alpine
scurvy</altname>. It may also be caused by alcoholism or disease causing
an impairment of nutrition. It is also called <altname>St. Ignatius's
itch</altname>, <altname>maidism</altname>, <altname>mal de la
rosa</altname>, <altname>mal rosso</altname>, and
<altname>psychoneurosis maidica</altname>. A variety of
<ex>pellagra</ex> seen in children is called <stype>infantile
pellagra</stype> or <stype>kwashiorkor</stype>.</def>
<au>[Stedman]</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellagrin</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"la*grin</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l"l<adot/*gr<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who is afflicted with pellagra.</def> <rj><au>Chambers's
Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellagrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*lag"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pellagroso</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pellagreux</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or affected with, or attendant on,
pellagra; <as>as, <ex>pellagrous</ex> insanity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellet</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"let</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/l"l<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pelote</ets>, LL. <ets>pelota</ets>, <ets>pilota</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>pila</ets> a ball. Cf. <er>Platoon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
little ball; <as>as, a <ex>pellet</ex> of wax on paper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bullet; a ball for firearms.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As swift as a <qex>pellet</qex> out of a gun.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pellet gun</b></col> <cd>a gun that fires small pellets,
less than 3 mm diameter, usually made of metal.</cd> -- <col><b>Pellet
molding</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a narrow band ornamented with
smalt, flat disks.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellet</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"let</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To form into small balls; to
pelletize.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelletize</ent><br/
<ent>Pelletise</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pel"let*ise</hw>, <hw>Pel"let*ize</hw></mhw>
<pr>(p<ecr/l"l<ecr/t*<imac/z)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To form into
small balls; <as>as, to <ex>pelletize</ex> ore</as>. The spelling
<altname>pelletise</altname> is mostly British.</def> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelleted</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"let*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made of, or like, pellets;
furnished with pellets.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/This
<xex>pelleted</xex> storm.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellibranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel`li*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. <ets>pellis</ets> garment + <ets>branchia</ets> a
gill.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Nudibranchiata, in
which the mantle itself serves as a gill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellicle</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"li*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pellicula</ets>, dim. of <ets>pellis</ets> skin: cf. F.
<ets>pellicule</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thin skin or
film.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A thin film formed on the surface
of an evaporating solution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellicular</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*lic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a pellicle.</def> <rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellile</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*li"le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The redshank; -- so called from its note.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"li*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>paritorie</ets>, OF. <ets>paritoire</ets>, F.
<ets>pari<eacute/taire</ets>; (cf. It. & Sp. <ets>parietaria</ets>), L.
<ets>parietaria</ets> the parietary, or pellitory, the wall plant, fr.
<ets>parietarius</ets> belonging to the walls, fr. <ets>paries</ets>,
<ets>parietis</ets> a wall. Cf. <er>Parietary</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The common name of the several species of the
genus <gen>Parietaria</gen>, low, harmless weeds of the Nettle family;
-- also called <altname>wall pellitory</altname>, and
<altname>lichwort</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <spn>Parietaria officinalis</spn> is common on old walls
in Europe; <spn>Parietaria pennsylvanica</spn> is found in the
<country>United States</country>; and six or seven more species are
found near the <geog>Mediterranean</geog>, or in the
<geog>Orient</geog>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"li*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>pelitre</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>pyrethrum</ets>. See <er>Bertram</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A composite plant (<spn>Anacyclus Pyrethrum</spn>) of
the Mediterranean region, having finely divided leaves and whitish
flowers. The root is the officinal pellitory, and is used as an irritant
and sialogogue. Called also <altname>bertram</altname>, and
<altname>pellitory of Spain</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
feverfew (<spn>Chrysanthemum Parthenium</spn>); -- so called because it
resembles the above.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pell-mell</ent><br/
<hw>Pell`-mell"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pall-mall</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellmell</ent><br/
<hw>Pell`mell"</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<ecir/le-m<ecir/le</ets>, prob. fr. <ets>pelle</ets> a shovel +
<ets>m<ecir/ler</ets> to mix, as when different kinds of grain are
heaped up and mixed with a shovel. See <er>Pell</er> shovel,
<er>Medley</er>.]</ety> <def>In utter confusion; with confused
violence.</def> <ldquo/Men, horses, chariots, crowded
<xex>pellmell</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellucid</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*lu"cid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pellucidus</ets>; <ets>per</ets> (see <er>Per-</er>) +
<ets>lucidus</ets> clear, bright: cf. F. <ets>pellucide</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Transparent; clear; limpid; translucent; not opaque.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Pellucid</xex> crystal.<rdquo/ <au>Dr. H. More.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Pellucid</xex> streams.<rdquo/ <au>Wordsworth.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellucidness</ent><br/
<ent>Pellucidity</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pel`lu*cid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pel*lu"cid*ness</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pellucid</ets>itas.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
pellucid; transparency; translucency; clearness; <as>as, the
<ex>pellucidity</ex> of the air</as>.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pellucidly</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*lu"cid*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pellucid
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pelmata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>pe`lma</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The under surface of
the foot.</def><-- = sole? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelopium</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*lo"pi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>Pelops</ets>, brother of Niobe, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A supposed new metal found in columbite, afterwards shown to be
identical with columbium, or niobium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peloponnesian</ent><br/
<hw>Pel`o*pon*ne"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Peloponnesius</ets>, fr. <ets>Peloponnesus</ets>, Gr. <?/, lit.,
the Island of Pelops; <?/, <?/, Pelops + <?/ an island.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to the Peloponnesus, or southern peninsula of
Greece.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or an inhabitant of
the Peloponnesus.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peloponnesus</ent><br/
<hw>Peloponnesus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>the southern peninsula of
Greece.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Peloponnese, Peloponnesian Peninsula.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peloria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*lo"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ monstrous.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Abnormal regularity; the
state of certain flowers, which, being naturally irregular, have become
regular through a symmetrical repetition of the special
irregularity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peloric</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*lo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Abnormally regular or symmetrical.</def>
<rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelorus</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*lo"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[After
<ets>Pelorus</ets>, said to have been Hannibal's pilot when he left
Italy.]</ety> <fld>(Navig.)</fld> <def>An instrument similar to a
mariner's compass, but without magnetic needles, and having two sight
vanes by which bearings are taken, esp. such as cannot be taken by the
compass.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelota</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*lo"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., lit.,
ball.]</ety> <def>A Basque, Spanish, and Spanish-American game played in
a court, in which a ball is struck with a wickerwork racket.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> jai alai.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelotage</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"o*tage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Packs or bales of Spanish wool.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelt</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/lt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>pelz</ets> a pelt, fur, fr. OF. <ets>pelice</ets>, F.
<ets>pelisse</ets> (see <er>Pelisse</er>); or perh. shortened fr.
<ets>peltry</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The skin of a beast with the
hair on; a raw or undressed hide; a skin preserved with the hairy or
woolly covering on it. See 4th <er>Fell</er>.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Raw <qex>pelts</qex> clapped about them for their clothes.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The human skin.</def> <mark>[Jocose]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>The body of any quarry killed
by the hawk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pelt rot</b></col>, <cd>a disease affecting the hair or
wool of a beast.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelt</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pelted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pelting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pelten</ets>,
<ets>pulten</ets>, <ets>pilten</ets>, to thrust, throw, strike; cf. L.
<ets>pultare</ets>, equiv. to <ets>pulsare</ets> (v. freq. fr.
<ets>pellere</ets> to drive), and E. <ets>pulse</ets> a beating.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike with something thrown or driven; to assail
with pellets or missiles, <as>as, to <ex>pelt</ex> with stones;
<ex>pelted</ex> with hail.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The chidden billows seem to <qex>pelt</qex> the clouds.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw; to use as a missile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My Phillis me with <qex>pelted</qex> apples plies.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelt</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw
missiles.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw out words.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Another smothered seems to <qex>pelt</qex> and swear.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelt</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A blow or stroke from something
thrown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pel"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pelt<ae/</plw>.</plu> <ety>[L., a shield, fr. Gr.
<grk>pe`lth</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Antiq.)</fld> <def>A small
shield, especially one of an approximately elliptic form, or
crescent-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A flat apothecium having no
rim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peltated</ent><br/
<ent>Peltate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pel"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pel"ta*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pelt<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Pelta</er>.]</ety> <def>Shield-shaped; scutiform; <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
having the stem or support attached to the lower surface, instead of at
the base or margin; -- said of a leaf or other organ.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pel"tate*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelter</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pelts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelter</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pinchpenny; a mean,
sordid person; a miser; a skinflint.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Let such <xex>pelters</xex> prate.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gascoigne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peltier effect</ent><br/
<hw>Pel`tier" ef*fect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[After <person>Jean C. A.
<etsep>Peltier</etsep></person>, French physicist, the
discoverer.]</ety> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The production or absorption
of heat at the junction of two metals on the passage of a current. Heat
generated by the passage of the current in one direction will be
absorbed if the current is reversed.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peltier's cross</ent><br/
<hw>Pel`tier's" cross</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A cross
formed of two strips of different metals, to illustrate the Peltier
effect.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peltiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pelta</ets>
+ <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Shieldlike, with the outline nearly
circular; peltate.</def> <rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelting</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Mean; paltry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelton wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"ton wheel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A form of
impulse turbine or water wheel, consisting of a row of double cup-shaped
buckets arranged round the rim of a wheel and actuated by one or more
jets of water playing into the cups at high velocity.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peltry</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pelleterie</ets> peltry, furriery, fr. <ets>pelletier</ets> a
furrier, fr. OF. <ets>pel</ets> skin, F. <ets>peau</ets>, L.
<ets>pelis</ets>. See <er>Pelt</er> a skin, <er>Pell</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <er>Fell</er> a skin.]</ety> <def>Pelts or skins,
collectively; skins with the fur on them; furs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peltryware</ent><br/
<hw>Pelt"ry*ware`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Peltry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peludo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*lu"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>peludo</ets> hairy.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The South
American hairy armadillo (<spn>Dasypus villosus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelure</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*lure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., lit., peel,
fr. <ets>peler</ets> to peel.]</ety> <def>A crisp, hard, thin paper,
sometimes used for postage stamps.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelusiac</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*lu"si*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pelusiacus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Pelusium, an
ancient city of Egypt; <as>as, the <ex>Pelusiac</ex> (or former eastern)
outlet of the Nile</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelvic</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"vic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
in the region of, the pelvis; <as>as, <ex>pelvic</ex>
cellulitis</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pelvic arch</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pelvic
girdle</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the two or more bony or
cartilaginous pieces of the vertebrate skeleton to which the hind limbs
are articulated. When fully ossified, the arch usually consists of three
principal bones on each side, the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which are
often closely united in the adult, forming the innominate bone. See
<cref>Innominate bone</cref>, under <er>Innominate</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelvimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*vim"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pelvis</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.: cf. F.
<ets>pelvim<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for measuring
the dimensions of the pelvis.</def> <rj><au>Coxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelvimetry</ent><br/
<hw>Pel*vim"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pelvis</ets> + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The measurement of the pelvis.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelvis</ent><br/
<hw>Pel"vis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a basin, laver;
cf. Gr. <?/, <?/, bowl.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
pelvic arch, or the pelvic arch together with the sacrum. See
<cref>Pelvic arch</cref>, under <er>Pelvic</er>, and
<er>Sacrum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The calyx of a crinoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pelvis of the kidney</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>,
<cd>the basinlike cavity into which the ureter expands as it joins the
kidney.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pelycosaur</ent><br/
<hw>pelycosaur</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large primitive
reptile having a tall spinal sail; of the Permian or late Paleozoic in
Europe and North America.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pelycosauria</ent><br/
<hw>Pelycosauria</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An order of
extinct reptiles including edaphosaurus and dimetrodon.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order Pelycosauria.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pembroke</ent><br/
<hw>Pembroke</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A breed of dog, the smaller and
straight-legged variety of Welsh corgi having pointed ears and a short
tail.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Pembroke Welsh corgi.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pembroke table</ent><br/
<hw>Pem"broke ta`ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From
<etsep>Pembroke</etsep>, a town and shire in Wales.]</ety> <def>A style
of four-legged drop-leaf table in vogue in England, chiefly in the later
Georgian period.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The characteristic which gives a table the name of
<qex>Pembroke</qex> consists in the drop leaves, which are held up, when
the table is open, by brackets which turn under the top.</q> <rj><qau>F.
C. Morse.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pemmican</ent><br/
<hw>Pem"mi*can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pemican</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Among the North American
Indians, meat cut in thin slices, divested of fat, and dried in the
sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then on <qex>pemican</qex> they feasted.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Meat, without the fat, cut in thin slices, dried in
the sun, pounded, then mixed with melted fat and sometimes dried fruit,
and compressed into cakes or in bags. It contains much nutriment in
small compass, and is of great use in long voyages of
exploration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A treatise of much thought in little
compass.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pemphigus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pem*phi"gus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Nl., fr. Gr.
<?/, <?/, a bubble.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A somewhat rare skin
disease, characterized by the development of blebs upon different parts
of the body.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pen</ent><br/
<hw>Pen</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>penne</ets>, OF. <ets>penne</ets>, <ets>pene</ets>, F.
<ets>penne</ets>, fr. L. <ets>penna</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
feather.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An instrument used for writing with ink, formerly
made of a reed, or of the quill of a goose or other bird, but now also
of other materials, as of steel, gold, etc. Also, originally, a stylus
or other instrument for scratching or graving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Graven with an iron <qex>pen</qex> and lead in the rock.</q>
<rj><qau>Job xix. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: A writer, or his style; <as>as, he has a sharp
<ex>pen</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/Those learned <xex>pens</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The internal shell of a
squid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
female swan; -- contrasted with <contr>cob</contr>, the male swan.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bow pen</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Bow-pen</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Dotting pen</b></col>, <cd>a pen for drawing dotted lines.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Drawing pen</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Ruling
pen</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a pen for ruling lines having a pair of blades
between which the ink is contained.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Fountain
pen</b></col>, <col><b>Geometric pen</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under
<er>Fountain</er>, and <er>Geometric</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Music
pen</b></col>, <cd>a pen having five points for drawing the five lines
of the staff.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Pen and ink</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>pen-and-ink</b></col></mcol>, <cd>executed or done with a pen
and ink; <as>as, a <ex>pen and ink</ex> sketch</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pen
feather</b></col>. <cd>A pin feather.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<col><b>Pen name</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Name</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sea pen</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a pennatula.</cd>
<altsp>[Usually written <asp>sea-pen</asp>.]</altsp></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pen</ent><br/
<hw>Pen</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Penned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Penning</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To write; to compose
and commit to paper; to indite; to compose; <as>as, to <ex>pen</ex> a
sonnet</as>.</def> <ldquo/A prayer elaborately <xex>penned</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pen</ent><br/
<hw>Pen</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Penned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Pent</conjf> (<?/); <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Penning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pennen</ets>, AS. <ets>pennan</ets> in <ets>on-pennan</ets> to
unfasten, prob. from the same source as <ets>pin</ets>, and orig.
meaning, to fasten with a peg.See <er>Pin</er>, <pos>n. &
v.</pos>]</ety> <def>To shut up, as in a pen or cage; to confine in a
small inclosure or narrow space; to coop up, or shut in; to
inclose.</def> <ldquo/Away with her, and <xex>pen</xex> her up.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Watching where shepherds <qex>pen</qex> their flocks at eve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pen</ent><br/
<hw>Pen</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <ety>[From <er>Pen</er> to shut
in.]</ety> <def>A small inclosure; <as>as, a <ex>pen</ex> for sheep or
for pigs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My father stole two geese out of a <qex>pen</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[From <ets>pen</ets>itentiary.]</ety> <def>A
penitentiary[6]; a prison.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penal</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>poenalis</ets>, fr. <ets>poena</ets> punishment: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/nal</ets>. See <er>Pain</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to punishment, to penalties, or to crimes and offenses; pertaining to
criminal jurisprudence</def>: as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Enacting or
threatening punishment; <as>as, a <ex>penal</ex> statue; the
<ex>penal</ex> code</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Incurring punishment;
subject to a penalty; <as>as, a <ex>penal</ex> act or
offense</as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Inflicted as punishment; used as a
means of punishment; <as>as, a <ex>penal</ex> colony or
settlement</as>.</def> <ldquo/Adamantine chains and <xex>penal</xex>
fire.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Penal code</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a code of laws
concerning crimes and offenses and their punishment.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Penal laws</b></col>, <col><b>Penal
statutes</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>laws prohibiting certain
acts, and imposing penalties for committing them.</cd> -- <col><b>Penal
servitude</b></col>, <cd>imprisonment with hard labor, in a prison, in
lieu of transportation.</cd> <mark>[Great Brit.]</mark> --
<mcol><col><b>Penal suit</b></col>, <col><b>Penal
action</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a suit for
penalties.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penality</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>poenalitas</ets>. See <er>Penalty</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or
state of being penal; liability to punishment.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penalization</ent><br/
<hw>penalization</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of punishing.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> punishment, penalty.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penalize</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"nal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
make penal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Sport.)</fld> <def>To put a penalty on. See
<er>Penalty</er>, 3.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penalized</ent><br/
<hw>penalized</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Subjected to a penalty.</def>
[Narrower terms: <stype>fined</stype>, <stype>mulcted</stype>]<br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penally</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"nal*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a penal
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penalty</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"nal*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Penalties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/nalit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Penal</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Penal retribution; punishment for crime or offense; the
suffering in person or property which is annexed by law or judicial
decision to the commission of a crime, offense, or trespass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Death is the <qex>penalty</qex> imposed.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The suffering, or the sum to be forfeited, to which
a person subjects himself by covenant or agreement, in case of
nonfulfillment of stipulations; forfeiture; fine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>penalty</qex> and forfeit of my bond.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A handicap.</def> <mark>[Sporting Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>penalty</xex> is in law mostly applied to
a pecuniary punishment.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bill of pains and penalties</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Bill</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>On penalty of</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Under penalty of</b></col></mcol>, <cd>on pain of;
with exposure to the penalty of, in case of transgression.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penance</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>penance</ets>, <ets>peneance</ets>, L. <ets>paenitentia</ets>
repentance. See <er>Penitence</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Repentance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif (Luke xv.
7).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pain; sorrow; suffering.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Joy or <xex>penance</xex> he feeleth none.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A means of repairing a sin
committed, and obtaining pardon for it, consisting partly in the
performance of expiatory rites, partly in voluntary submission to a
punishment corresponding to the transgression, imposed by a confessor or
other ecclesiastical authority. Penance is the fourth of seven
sacraments in the Roman Catholic Church.</def> <rj><au>Schaff-Herzog
Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And bitter <qex>penance</qex>, with an iron whip.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Quoth he, <ldquo/The man hath <qex>penance</qex> done,<br/
And <qex>penance</qex> more will do.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any act performed by a
person to atone for an offense to another; an act of atonement.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penance</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ance</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Penanced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To impose penance;
to punish.</def> <ldquo/Some <xex>penanced</xex> lady elf.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penanceless</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ance*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from penance.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pen-and-ink</ent><br/
<hw>pen-and-ink</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A drawing executed with pen and
ink.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penang lawyer</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nang" law"yer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Prob. fr. Malay
<ets>p<imac/nang l<imac/ar</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of walking stick
made from the stem of an East Asiatic palm (<spn>Licuala
acutifida</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penang nut</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nang" nut`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From the native name.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The betel nut.</def> <au>Balfour (Cyc. of
India).</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penannular</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*an"nu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pene</ets>, <ets>paene</ets>, almost + E.
<ets>annular</ets>.]</ety> <def>Nearly annular; having nearly the form
of a ring.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Penannular</xex> relics.<rdquo/ <rj><au>D.
Wilson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penary</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Penal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penates</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*na"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The household gods of the ancient Romans.
They presided over the home and the family hearth. See
<er>Lar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penaunt</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"aunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>penant</ets>, <ets>peneant</ets>. See <er>Penitent</er>.]</ety>
<def>A penitent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pence</ent><br/
<hw>Pence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Penny</er>. See <er>Penny</er>.</def> <-- chiefly Brit. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pencel</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pennoncel</er>.]</ety> <def>A small, narrow flag or streamer borne
at the top of a lance; -- called also
<altname>pennoncel</altname>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au> <au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penchant</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen`chant"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>pencher</ets> to bend, fr. (assumed) LL. <ets>pendicare</ets>, L.
<ets>pendere</ets>. See <er>Pendant</er>.]</ety> <def>Inclination;
decided taste; bias; <as>as, a <ex>penchant</ex> for art</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penchant</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen`chant"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Card
Playing)</fld> <def>A game like b<eacute/zique, or, in the game, any
queen and jack of different suits held together.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penchute</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"chute`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Penstock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pencil</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"cil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pincel</ets>, F. <ets>pinceau</ets>, L. <ets>penicillum</ets>,
<ets>penicillus</ets>, equiv. to <ets>peniculus</ets>, dim. of
<ets>penis</ets> a tail. Cf. <er>Penicil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
small, fine brush of hair or bristles used by painters for laying on
colors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With subtile <qex>pencil</qex> depainted was this storie.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slender cylinder or strip of black lead, graphite,
colored chalk, slate etc., or such a cylinder or strip inserted in a
small wooden rod intended to be pointed, or in a case, which forms a
handle, -- used for drawing or writing. See <er>Graphite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively, an artist's ability or peculiar
manner; also, in general, the act or occupation of the artist,
descriptive writer, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>An aggregate or collection of rays
of light, especially when diverging from, or converging to, a
point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A number of lines that intersect
in one point, the point of intersection being called the <xex>pencil
point</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A small medicated
bougie.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pencil case</b></col>, <cd>a holder for pencil lead.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pencil flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an American
perennial leguminous herb (<spn>Stylosanthes elatior</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Pencil lead</b></col>, <cd>a slender rod of black lead, or the
like, adapted for insertion in a holder.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pencil</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"cil</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Penciled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Pencilled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Penciling</conjf> or
<conjf>Pencilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To write or mark with a
pencil; to paint or to draw.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where nature <qex>pencils</qex> butterflies on flowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Harte.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>pencil in</b></col> <cd>to write (a tentative appoinment)
on an appointment calendar, so as to reserve time, but to allow the
appointment to be readily canceled and replaced with another; -- also
used figuratively, with other means of recording appointments. The
notion being that something written in pencil can be more easily changed
than something written in ink. The phrase is commonly used in the early
stages of organizing a meeting of multiple persons, before it is known
whether all attendees will be free at the suggested time.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penciled</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ciled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pencilled</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Painted, drawn, sketched,
or marked with a pencil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Radiated; having pencils of rays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>Marked with parallel or
radiating lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penciling</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"cil*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pencilling</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The work of the pencil or
brush; <as>as, delicate <ex>penciling</ex> in a picture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Brickwork)</fld> <def>Lines of white or black paint
drawn along a mortar joint in a brick wall.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pencillated</ent><br/
<ent>Pencillate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen"cil*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pen"cil*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaped like a pencil;
penicillate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pencraft</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"craft</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Penmanship; skill in writing; chirography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The art of composing or writing;
authorship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would not give a groat for that person's knowledge in
<qex>pencraft</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1060 --></p>

<p><ent>Pend</ent><br/
<hw>Pend</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/nd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Oil cake;
penock.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pend</ent><br/
<hw>Pend</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pended</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>pendere</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To hang; to depend.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pending</qex> upon certain powerful motions.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be undecided, or in process of
adjustment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pend</ent><br/
<hw>Pend</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <ets>pen</ets> to shut in, or
AS. <ets>pyndan</ets>, E. <ets>pound</ets> an inclosure.]</ety> <def>To
pen; to confine.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>ended</qex> within the limits . . . of Greece.</q>
<rj><qau>Udall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendant</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"ant</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/nd"<ait/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
orig. p. pr. of <ets>pendre</ets> to hang, L. <ets>pendere</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pendent</er>, <er>Pansy</er>, <er>Pensive</er>, <er>Poise</er>,
<er>Ponder</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something which hangs or
depends; something suspended; a hanging appendage, especially one of an
ornamental character, as to a chandelier or an eardrop; also, an
appendix or addition, as to a book.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some hang upon the <qex>pendants</qex> of her ear.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many . . . have been pleased with this work and its
<qex>pendant</qex>, the Tales and Popular Fictions.</q>
<rj><qau>Keightley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>An ornamental object or
piece of jewelry with a hook so that it can be hung from a chain around
the neck.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A hanging ornament on roofs,
ceilings, etc., much used in the later styles of Gothic architecture,
where it is of stone, and an important part of the construction. There
are imitations in plaster and wood, which are mere decorative
features.</def> <ldquo/[A bridge] with . . . <xex>pendants</xex> graven
fair.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>One of a pair; a counterpart;
<as>as, one vase is the <ex>pendant</ex> to the other
vase</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A pendulum.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir K.
Digby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The stem and ring of a watch, by which it is
suspended.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pendant post</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a part of
the framing of an open timber roof; a post set close against the wall,
and resting upon a corbel or other solid support, and supporting the
ends of a collar beam or any part of the roof.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendence</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pendent</er>.]</ety> <def>Slope; inclination.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendency</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being pendent or suspended.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being undecided, or in
continuance; suspense; <as>as, the <ex>pendency</ex> of a
suit</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendent</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"ent</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/nd"<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pendens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>pendere</ets> to
hang, to be suspended. Cf. <er>Pendant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Supported from above; suspended; depending; pendulous; hanging;
<as>as, a <ex>pendent</ex> leaf</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>pendent</xex> world.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Often their tresses, when shaken, with <qex>pendent</qex> icicles
tinkle.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Jutting over; projecting; overhanging.</def>
<ldquo/A vapor sometime like a . . . <xex>pendent</xex> rock.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendentive</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*den"tive</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n*d<ecr/nt"<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>pendentif</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pendere</ets> to hang.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The portion of a vault by means of
which the square space in the middle of a building is brought to an
octagon or circle to receive a cupola.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The part
of a groined vault which is supported by, and springs from, one pier or
corbel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendently</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pendent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendice</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"dice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pentice</er>.]</ety> <def>A sloping roof; a lean-to; a
penthouse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendicle</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"di*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Appendicle</er>.]</ety> <def>An appendage; something dependent on
another; an appurtenance; a pendant.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendicler</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*di*cler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An inferior
tenant; one who rents a pendicle or croft.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pending</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pendere</ets> to hang, to be suspended. Cf.
<er>Pendent</er>.]</ety> <def>Not yet decided; in continuance; in
suspension; <as>as, a <ex>pending</ex> suit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pending</ent><br/
<hw>Pend"ing</hw>, <pos>prep.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>During; <as>as,
<ex>pending</ex> the trail</as>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Until; awaiting; <as>as, no decision will be taken
<ex>pending</ex> his arrival</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendragon</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"drag*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chief leader or
a king; a head; a dictator; -- a title assumed by the ancient British
chiefs when called to lead other chiefs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dread <qex>Pendragon</qex>, Britain's king of kings.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendular</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"du*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pendulous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"du*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To swing as a
pendulum.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendule</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"dule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
pendulum.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penduline</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen"du`line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pendulum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A European titmouse
(<spn>Parus pendulinus</spn>, syn. <spn>Aegithalus pendulinus</spn>). It
is noted for its elegant pendulous purselike nest, made of the down of
willow trees and lined with feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendulosity</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`du*los"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pendulous</er>.]</ety> <def>The state or quality of being
pendulous.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"du*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pendulus</ets>, fr. <ets>pendere</ets> to hang. Cf.
<er>Pendant</er>, and cf. <er>Pendulum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Depending; pendent loosely; hanging; swinging.</def> <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/The <xex>pendulous</xex> round earth.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wavering; unstable; doubtful.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>pendulous</xex> state of mind.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Atterbury.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Inclined or hanging downwards, as
a flower on a recurved stalk, or an ovule which hangs from the upper
part of the ovary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendulously</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"du*lous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pendulous
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendulousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"du*lous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being pendulous; the state of hanging loosely; pendulosity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pendulum</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"du*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pendulums</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>pendulus</ets> hanging, swinging. See <er>Pendulous</er>.]</ety>
<def>A body so suspended from a fixed point as to swing freely to and
fro by the alternate action of gravity and momentum. It is used to
regulate the movements of clockwork and other machinery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The time of oscillation of a pendulum is independent of
the arc of vibration, provided this arc be small.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ballistic pendulum</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Ballistic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Compensation pendulum</b></col>,
<cd>a clock pendulum in which the effect of changes of temperature of
the length of the rod is so counteracted, usually by the opposite
expansion of differene metals, that the distance of the center of
oscillation from the center of suspension remains invariable; as, the
<stype>mercurial compensation pendulum</stype>, in which the expansion
of the rod is compensated by the opposite expansion of mercury in a jar
constituting the bob; the <stype>gridiron pendulum</stype>, in which
compensation is effected by the opposite expansion of sets of rods of
different metals.</cd> -- <col><b>Compound pendulum</b></col>, <cd>an
ordinary pendulum; -- so called, as being made up of different parts,
and contrasted with <contr>simple pendulum</contr>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Conical pendulum</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Revolving
pendulum</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a weight connected by a rod with a fixed
point; and revolving in a horizontal circle about the vertical from that
point.</cd> -- <col><b>Pendulum bob</b></col>, <cd>the weight at the
lower end of a pendulum.</cd> -- <col><b>Pendulum level</b></col>, <cd>a
plumb level. See under <er>Level</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pendulum
wheel</b></col>, <cd>the balance of a watch.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Simple pendulum</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Theoretical
pendulum</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an imaginary pendulum having no
dimensions except length, and no weight except at the center of
oscillation; in other words, a material point suspended by an ideal
line.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peneidae</ent><br/
<hw>Peneidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of tropical
prawns.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Peneidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penelope</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*nel"o*pe</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*n<ecr/l"<osl/*p<emac/)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[From. L. <ets>Penelope</ets>, the wife of
<persfn>Ulysses</persfn>, the hero of the Odyssey, Gr.
<grk>Phnelo`ph</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
curassows, including the guans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peneplain</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ne*plain`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>paene</ets>, <ets>pene</ets>, almost + E. <ets>plain</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Phys. Geog.)</fld> <def>A land surface reduced by erosion to the
general condition of a plain, but not wholly devoid of hills; a
base-level plain.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrability</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`e*tra*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/n<eacute/trabilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality
of being penetrable; susceptibility of being penetrated, entered, or
pierced.</def> <rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrable</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*tra*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penetrabilus</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/n<eacute/trable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
penetrated, entered, or pierced. Used also figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And pierce his only <qex>penetrable</qex> part.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am not made of stones,<br/
But <qex>penetrable</qex> to your kind entreats.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pen"e*tra*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pen"e*tra*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrail</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*trail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Penetralia.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetralia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen`e*tra"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. <ets>penetralis</ets> penetrating, internal. See
<er>Penetrate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The recesses, or innermost
parts, of any thing or place, especially of a temple or
palace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hidden things or secrets; privacy; sanctuary;
<as>as, the sacred <ex>penetralia</ex> of the home</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrancy</ent><br/
<ent>Penetrance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen"e*trance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pen"e*tran*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
penetrant; power of entering or piercing; penetrating power or quality;
<as>as, the <ex>penetrancy</ex> of subtile effluvia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrant</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*trant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penetrans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>penetrare</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/n<eacute/trant</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having power to enter or
pierce; penetrating; sharp; subtile; <as>as, <ex>penetrant</ex>
cold</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Penetrant</xex> and powerful
arguments.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Penetrated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Penetrating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>penetratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>penetrare</ets> to penetrate; akin
to <ets>penitus</ets> inward, inwardly, and perh. to <ets>pens</ets>
with, in the power of, <ets>penus</ets> store of food, innermost part of
a temple.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To enter into; to make way into the
interior of; to effect an entrance into; to pierce; <as>as, light
<ex>penetrates</ex> darkness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To affect profoundly through the senses or feelings;
to touch with feeling; to make sensible; to move deeply; <as>as, to
<ex>penetrate</ex> one's heart with pity</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The translator of Homer should <qex>penetrate</qex> himself with a
sense of the plainness and directness of Homer's style.</q> <rj><qau>M.
Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pierce into by the mind; to arrive at the inner
contents or meaning of, as of a mysterious or difficult subject; to
comprehend; to understand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Things which here were too subtile for us to
<qex>penetrate</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*trate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To pass; to make way; to
pierce. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Preparing to <qex>penetrate</qex> to the north and west.</q>
<rj><qau>J. R. Green.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Born where Heaven's influence scarce can <qex>penetrate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sweet of life that <qex>penetrates</qex> so near.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrating</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*tra`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the power of entering, piercing, or pervading; sharp;
subtile; penetrative; <as>as, a <ex>penetrating</ex>
odor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Acute; discerning; sagacious; quick to discover;
<as>as, a <ex>penetrating</ex> mind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetratingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*tra`ting*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a penetrating
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetration</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*tra`tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penetratio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/n<eacute/tration</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
process of penetrating, piercing, or entering; also, the act of mentally
penetrating into, or comprehending, anything difficult.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And to each in ward part,<br/
With gentle <qex>penetration</qex>, though unseen,<br/
Shoots invisible virtue even to the deep.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>penetration</qex> into the difficulties of algebra.</q>
<rj><qau>Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Acuteness; insight; sharp discoverment; sagacity;
<as>as, a person of singular <ex>penetration</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Discernment; sagacity; acuteness; sharpness;
discrimination. See <er>Discernment</er>, and
<er>Sagacity</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrative</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*tra*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/n<eacute/tratif</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Tending to
penetrate; of a penetrating quality; piercing; <as>as, the
<ex>penetrative</ex> sun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His look became keen and <qex>penetrative</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the power to affect or impress the mind or
heart; impressive; <as>as, <ex>penetrative</ex> shame</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Acute; discerning; sagacious; <as>as,
<ex>penetrative</ex> wisdom</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>penetrative</xex> eye.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Led on by skill of <qex>penetrative</qex> soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Grainger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penetrativeness</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"e*tra*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
penetrative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penfish</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
squid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penfold</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"fold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pinfold</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pen-friend</ent><br/
<hw>pen-friend</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person one comes to know by by
frequent friendly correspondence; a pen pal.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pen pal, pen-pal, penfriend.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pengolin</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"go*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The pangolin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penguin</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"guin</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"gw<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh.
orig. the name of another bird, and fr. W. <ets>pen</ets> head +
<ets>gwyn</ets> white; or perh. from a native South American
name.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bird of the order
<ord>Impennes</ord>, or <ord>Ptilopteri</ord>. They are covered with
short, thick feathers, almost scalelike on the wings, which are without
true quills. They are unable to fly, but use their wings to aid in
diving, in which they are very expert. See <cref>King penguin</cref>,
under <er>Jackass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Penguins are found in the south temperate and antarctic
regions. The king penguins (<spn>Aptenodytes Patachonica</spn>, and
<spn>Aptenodytes longirostris</spn>) are the largest; the jackass
penguins (<gen>Spheniscus</gen>) and the rock hoppers
(<gen>Catarractes</gen>) congregate in large numbers at their breeding
grounds.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The egg-shaped fleshy fruit of a
West Indian plant (<spn>Bromelia Pinguin</spn>) of the Pineapple family;
also, the plant itself, which has rigid, pointed, and spiny-toothed
leaves, and is used for hedges.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pinguin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Arctic penguin</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
great auk. See <er>Auk</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penguinery</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"guin*er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A breeding place, or rookery, of penguins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penholder</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"hold`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A handle for a
pen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"house`</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"hous`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
penthouse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penible</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*i"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>penible</ets>. Cf. <er>Painable</er>.]</ety> <def>Painstaking;
assiduous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penicil</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*cil</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"<icr/*s<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>penicillum</ets>, <ets>penicillus</ets>, a painter's
brush, a roil of lint, a tent for wounds.]</ety> <fld>(mented.)</fld>
<def>A tent or pledget for wounds or ulcers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penicillamine</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*cil"lam*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.,
Med.)</fld> <def>A substance (<chform>C5H11NO2S</chform>) which is a
degradation product of the penicillins. Chemically it is
<chname>3-mercapto-D-valine</chname>. It has chelating properties and is
used in medicine as an antirheumatic and to chelate copper in cases of
hepatolenticular degeneration (Wilson's disease).</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penicillate</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*cil"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/nicill<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Penicil</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having the form of a pencil; furnished with a
pencil of fine hairs; ending in a tuft of hairs like a camel's-hair
brush, as the stigmas of some grasses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penicilliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*cil"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Penicillate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penicillin</ent><br/
<hw>pen`i*cil"lin</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n`<icr/*s<icr/l"l<icr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>Penicillium</ets>, the fungus genus from
which it was first isolated.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any of a
variety of substances having a structure containing a beta-lactam ring
fused to a thiirane ring, to which a carboxyl group is attached, but
most commonly interpreted as <stype>benzyl penicillin</stype>. They are
notable as powerful antibacterial agents of relatively low toxicity
which have found extensive use in medicine for treating bacterial
infections. They are categorized as one of the classes of
<isa>beta-lactam antibiotic</isa>. They are produced naturally by some
fungi and bacteria, and industrial production processes almost
invariably start from some form of the penicillin nucleus produced by
fermentation of microorganisms. The fermentation products are then
chemically modified to produce derivatives of enhanced potency, safety,
or antibacterial spectrum. The first <ex>penicillin</ex> to see
extensive use clinically (during World War II) was <stype>penicillin
G</stype>, also called <stype>benzypenicillin</stype>, and commonly
simply <ldquo/penicillin<rdquo/.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note> <hand/The characteristic portion of the structure
characterizing a <ex>penicillin</ex> is the bicyclic structure
<chname>3,3-Dimethyl-7-oxo-4-thia-1-azabicylo[3.2.0]heptane-2-carboxylic
acid</chname>. The different penicillins have varying acyl groups
forming an amide bond with a nitrogen attached to the 6-carbon of this
nucleus. This distinguishes it from the other classes of beta-lactam
antibiotic, the <contr>cephalosporins</contr>,
<contr>thienamycins</contr> and <contr>sulfazecin</contr>.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penicillinase</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*cil"lin*ase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bioch.)</fld> <def>An enzyme which destroys the antibacterial
activity of penicillin by hydrolyzing the amide bond in the beta-lactam
ring. Many penicillinases are known, and are produced by a wide variety
of bacteria. The production of penicillinase is one of the mechanisms by
which bacteria may become resistant to penicillins. Penicillinase
production in various bacterial species may be induced, i.e., it may
occur only when stimulated by the presence of penicillin in the culture
medium, or it may be constitutive, i.e., it may occur whenever the cells
are producing protein. Molecular weights of the various penicillinases
tend to cluster near 50,000.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peninsula</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*in"su*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peninsula</ets> or <ets>paeninsula</ets>; <ets>paene</ets> almost +
<ets>insula</ets> an island. See <er>Isle</er>.]</ety> <def>A portion of
land nearly surrounded by water, and connected with a larger body by a
neck, or isthmus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peninsular</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*in"su*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ninsulaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
peninsula; <as>as, a <ex>peninsular</ex> form; <ex>peninsular</ex>
people; the <ex>peninsular</ex> war.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peninsula State</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*in"su*la State</hw>. <def>Florida; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peninsulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*in"su*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peninsulated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Peninsulating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To form into a
peninsula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>South River . . . <qex>peninsulates</qex> Castle Hill farm.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penis</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"nis</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"n<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The erectile external sexual
organ of males, used in copulation, and in mammals, also for
urination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitence</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*tence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/nitence</ets>, L. <ets>paenitentia</ets>. See
<er>Penitent</er>, and cf. <er>Penance</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or
condition of being penitent; the disposition of a penitent; sorrow for
sins or faults; repentance; contrition.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Penitence</xex> of his old guilt.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Death is deferred, and <qex>penitenance</qex> has room<br/
To mitigate, if not reverse, the doom.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Repentance; contrition; compunction.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitencer</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*ten*cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/nitencier</ets>.]</ety> <def>A priest who heard confession
and enjoined penance in extraordinary cases.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>penitenser</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitency</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*ten*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Penitence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitent</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*tent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/nitent</ets>, L. <ets>paenitens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>,
<ets>poenitens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>paenitere</ets>,
<ets>poenitere</ets>, to cause to repent, to repent; prob. akin to
<ets>poena</ets> punishment. See <er>Pain</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Feeling pain or sorrow on account of sins or offenses; repentant;
contrite; sincerely affected by a sense of guilt, and resolved on
amendment of life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be <qex>penitent</qex>, and for thy fault contrite.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The proud he tamed, the <qex>penitent</qex> he cheered.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Doing penance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitent</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*tent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who repents of
sin; one sorrowful on account of his transgressions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One under church censure, but admitted to penance;
one undergoing penance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One under the direction of a confessor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Penitents</xex> is an appellation given to certain
fraternities in Roman Catholic countries, distinguished by their habit,
and employed in charitable acts.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitential</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*ten"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/nitentiel</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
penitence, or to penance; expressing penitence; of the nature of
penance; <as>as, the <ex>penitential</ex> book; <ex>penitential</ex>
tears.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Penitential</xex> stripes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Guilt that all the <qex>penitential</qex> fires of hereafter can
not cleanse.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitential</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*ten"tial</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A
book formerly used by priests hearing confessions, containing rules for
the imposition of penances; -- called also <altname>penitential
book</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitentially</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*ten"tial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a penitential
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitentiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*ten"tia*ry</hw>
<pr>(p<ecr/n`<icr/*t<ecr/n"sh<adot/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/nitentiaire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Relating to penance, or to the rules and measures of penance.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>penitentiary</xex> tax.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Abp.
Bramhall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressive of penitence; <as>as, a
<ex>penitentiary</ex> letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Used for punishment, discipline, and
reformation.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Penitentiary</xex> houses.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitentiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*ten"tia*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Penitentiaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/nitencier</ets>. See <er>Penitent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who prescribes the rules and measures of penance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who does penance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small building in a monastery where penitents
confessed.</def> <rj><au>Shpiley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That part of a church to which penitents were
admitted.</def> <rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An office of the
papal court which examines cases of conscience, confession, absolution
from vows, etc., and delivers decisions, dispensations, etc. Its chief
is a cardinal, called the <xex>Grand Penitentiary</xex>, appointed by
the pope.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An officer in some dioceses since
<sc>A. D.</sc> 1215, vested with power from the bishop to absolve in
cases reserved to him.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A house of correction, in which offenders are
confined for punishment, discipline, and reformation, and in which they
are generally compelled to labor; a prison; a jail. Colloquially often
shortened to <altname>pen</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitentiaryship</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`i*ten"tia*ry*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
condition of a penitentiary of the papal court.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penitently</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"i*tent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a penitent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1061 --></p>

<p><ent>Penk</ent><br/
<hw>Penk</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A minnow. See
<er>Pink</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penknife</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"knife`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Penknives</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[<ets>Pen</ets> +
<ets>knife</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small pocketknife; formerly, a knife
used for making and mending quill pens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penlight</ent><br/
<hw>pen"light`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small flashlight resembling a
fountain pen, often having a clip to permit firm attachment to a
pocket.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penman</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Penmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who uses
the pen; a writer; esp., one skilled in the use of the pen; a
calligrapher; a writing master.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An author; a composer.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penmanship</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"man*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The use of the pen in writing;
the art of writing; style or manner of writing; chirography; <as>as,
good or bad <ex>penmanship</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penna</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen"na</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"n<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Penn<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A perfect, or normal,
feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n*n<amac/"sh<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to a normal
feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennach</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"nach</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"n<acr/sh)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pennache</ets>. See <er>Panache</er>.]</ety> <def>A bunch of
feathers; a plume.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennached</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"nached</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>pennach<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Panache</er>.]</ety>
<def>Variegated; striped.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennage</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"nage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>penna</ets>
feather.]</ety> <def>Feathery covering; plumage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennant</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"nant</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"n<ait/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>penon</ets>, <ets>penoun</ets>, <ets>pynoun</ets>, OF.
<ets>penon</ets>, F. <ets>pennon</ets>, fr. L. <ets>penna</ets> feather.
See <er>Pen</er> a feather, and cf. <er>Pennon</er>,
<er>Pinion</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small
flag; a pennon. The <stype>narrow pennant</stype>, <it>or</it>
<stype>long pennant</stype> (called also <xex>whip</xex> or <xex>coach
whip</xex>) is a long, narrow piece of bunting, carried at the masthead
of a government vessel in commission. The <stype>board pennant</stype>
is an oblong, nearly square flag, carried at the masthead of a
commodore's vessel.</def> <ldquo/With flags and <xex>pennants</xex>
trimmed.<rdquo/ <au>Drayton.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A rope or strap to
which a purchase is hooked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennated</ent><br/
<ent>Pennate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pen"na*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pennatus</ets> feathered, winged,
from <ets>penna</ets> feather, wing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Winged;
plume-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pinnate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennatula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*nat"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
L. <plw>Pennatul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pennatulas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>penna</ets> a feather.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
<gen>Pennatula</gen>, <gen>Pteroides</gen>, and allied genera of
<ord>Alcyonaria</ord>, having a featherlike form; a sea-pen. The zooids
are situated along one edge of the side branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennatulacea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*nat`u*la"ce*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pennatula</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
alcyonoid corals, including the seapens and related kinds. They are able
to move about by means of the hollow muscular peduncle, which also
serves to support them upright in the mud. See <er>Pennatula</er>, and
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Alcyonaria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penned</ent><br/
<hw>Penned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Winged;
having plumes.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Written with a pen; composed.</def> <ldquo/Their
<xex>penned</xex> speech.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Enclosed in a pen; -- of animals.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penner</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pens; a writer.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A case for holding pens.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penniform</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ni*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penna</ets> feather + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>penniforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form of a feather or
plume.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*nig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penniger</ets>; <ets>penna</ets> feather + <ets>gerere</ets> to
bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Bearing feathers or
quills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penniless</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ni*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Penny</er>.]</ety> <def>Destitute of money; impecunious; poor.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Pen"ni*less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penninerved</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ni*nerved`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penna</ets> feather + E. <ets>nerve</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pinnately
veined or nerved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennipotent</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*nip"o*tent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pennipotens</ets>; <ets>penna</ets> wing + <ets>potens</ets>
strong.]</ety> <def>Strong of wing; strong on the wing.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Davies (Holy Roode).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennon</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pinion</er>.]</ety> <def>A wing; a pinion.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennon</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"non</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pennant</er>.]</ety>
<def>A pennant; a flag or streamer.</def>
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennoncelle</ent><br/
<ent>Pennoncel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen"non*cel`</hw>, <hw>Pen"non*celle`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>penoncel</ets>. See
<er>Pennant</er>.]</ety> <def>A small pennon borne on a lance. See
<er>Pencel</er>.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>penoncel</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennsylvanian</ent><br/
<hw>Pennsylvanian</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The geological period
from 280 million to 310 million years ago; -- it was characterized by a
warm climate and abundance of swampy land.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Pennsylvanian period, Upper Carboniferous, Upper
Carboniferous period.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A resident of the state of Pennsylvania.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penny</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ny</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Perh. a
corruption of <ets>pun</ets>, for <ets>pound</ets>.]</ety> <def>Denoting
the weight in pounds for one thousand; -- used in combination, with
respect to nails; <as>as, ten<ex>penny</ex> nails, nails of which one
thousand weight ten pounds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penny</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*ny</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pennies</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr> or <plw>Pence</plw> <pr>(p<ecr/ns)</pr>. <xex>Pennies</xex>
denotes the number of coins; <xex>pence</xex> the amount of pennies in
value.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>peni</ets>, AS. <ets>penig</ets>,
<ets>pening</ets>, <ets>pending</ets>; akin to D. <ets>penning</ets>,
OHG. <ets>pfenning</ets>, <ets>pfenting</ets>, G. <ets>pfennig</ets>,
Icel. <ets>penningr</ets>; of uncertain origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A former English coin, originally of copper, then of bronze, the
twelfth part of an English shilling in account value, and equal to four
farthings, or about two cents; -- usually indicated by the abbreviation
<abbr>d.</abbr> (the initial of <xex>denarius</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ldquo/The chief Anglo-Saxon coin, and for a long period
the only one, corresponded to the <xex>denarius</xex> of the Continent .
. . [and was] called <xex>penny</xex>, denarius, or denier.<rdquo/
<au>R. S. Poole.</au> The ancient silver <xex>penny</xex> was worth
about three pence sterling (see <er>Pennyweight</er>). The old Scotch
<xex>penny</xex> was only one twelfth the value of the English coin. In
the United States the word <xex>penny</xex> is popularly used for
<xex>cent</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any small sum or coin; a groat; a stiver.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Money, in general; <as>as, to turn an honest
<ex>penny</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>penny</qex> hath Rome borne,<br/
What men provided, what munition sent?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Denarius</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Penny cress</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an annual
herb of the Mustard family, having round, flat pods like silver pennies
(<spn>Thlaspi arvense</spn>). Also spelled <asp>pennycress</asp>.</cd>
<au>Dr. Prior.</au> -- <col><b>Penny dog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a kind of shark found on the South coast of Britain: the tope.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Penny pincher</b></col>, <col><b>Penny
father</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a penurious person; a miser; a niggard. The
latter phrase is now obsolete.</cd> <au>Robinson (More's Utopia).</au>
-- <col><b>Penny grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>pennyroyal.</cd>
<mark>[R.]</mark> -- <col><b>Penny post</b></col>, <cd>a post carrying a
letter for a penny; also, a mail carrier.</cd> -- <col><b>Penny
wise</b></col>, <cd>wise or prudent only in small matters; saving small
sums while losing larger; penny-wise; -- used chiefly in the phrase,
<xex>penny wise</xex> and pound foolish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penny</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Worth or costing one
penny; <as>as, <ex>penny</ex> candy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penny-a-liner</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ny-a-lin"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
furnishes matter to public journals at so much a line; a poor writer for
hire; a hack writer.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pennycress</ent><br/
<hw>pennycress</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any of several plants of the
genus Thlaspi; see <cref>penny cress</cref>.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>penny cress</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penny-pinching</ent><br/
<hw>penny-pinching</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Extreme care in spending
money; reluctance to spend money unnecessarily.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> parsimony, parsimoniousness, thrift.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penny-pinching</ent><br/
<hw>penny-pinching</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Scrimping; reluctant to
spend money; stingy; miserly; same as <er>cheesparing</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cheeseparing, close.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>penny-wise</ent><br/
<hw>penny-wise</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Thrifty in small
matters only. Used mostly in the phrase <ex>penny-wise</ex> and pound
foolish.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennyroyal</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ny*roy"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of
OE. <ets>puliall royal</ets>. OE. <ets>puliall</ets> is ultimately
derived fr. L. <ets>puleium</ets>, or <ets>pulegium</ets> regium (so
called as being good against fleas), fr. <ets>pulex</ets> a flea; and
<ets>royal</ets> is a translation of L. <ets>regium</ets>, in
<ets>puleium regium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An aromatic
herb (<spn>Mentha Pulegium</spn>) of Europe; also, a North American
plant (<spn>Hedeoma pulegioides</spn>) resembling it in
flavor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bastard pennyroyal</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Blue curls</cref>, under <er>Blue</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennyweight</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ny*weight`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A troy weight
containing twenty-four grains, or the twentieth part of a troy ounce;
1.555 grams; <as>as, a <ex>pennyweight</ex> of gold or of arsenic</as>.
It is abbreviated <abbr>dwt</abbr> or <abbr>pwt</abbr>. It was anciently
the weight of a silver penny, whence the name.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennywort</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ny*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A European trailing herb (<spn>Linaria Cymbalaria</spn>) with
roundish, reniform leaves. It is often cultivated in hanging
baskets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>March pennywort</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Water pennywort</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>March</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pennyworth</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ny*worth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
penny's worth; as much as may be bought for a penny.</def> <ldquo/A dear
<xex>pennyworth</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: The full value of one's penny expended; due
return for money laid out; a good bargain; a bargain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The priests sold the better <qex>pennyworths</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small quantity; a trifle.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penock</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pend</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penological</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to penology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in,
or a student of, penology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penology</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>poinh`</grk>, or L. <ets>poena</ets>, punishment +
<ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science or art of punishment.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>p<oe/nology</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penrack</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"rack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rack for pens not
in use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pens</ent><br/
<hw>Pens</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Penny</er>; pence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensative</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sa*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pensive.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensel</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pencel.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensible</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Held aloft.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensile</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sile</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"s<icr/l; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pensilis</ets>, fr. <ets>pendere</ets> to hang: cf. OE.
<ets>pensil</ets>. See <er>Pendant</er>.]</ety> <def>Hanging; suspended;
pendent; pendulous.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The long, <qex>pensile</qex> branches of the birches.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Howitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensileness</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sile*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State or quality of being
pensile; pendulousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pension</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pensio</ets> a paying, payment, fr. <ets>pendere</ets>,
<ets>pensum</ets>, to weight, to pay; akin to <ets>pendere</ets> to
hang. See <er>Pendant</er>, and cf. <er>Spend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A payment; a tribute; something paid or given.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stomach's <qex>pension</qex>, and the time's expense.</q>
<rj><qau>Sylvester.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stated allowance to a person in consideration of
past services; payment made to one retired from service, on account of
age, disability, or other cause; also, a regular stipend paid by a
government to retired public officers, disabled soldiers, the families
of soldiers killed in service, or to meritorious authors, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To all that kept the city <qex>pensions</qex> and wages.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Esd. iv. 56.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A certain sum of money paid to a clergyman in lieu
of tithes.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <ety>[F., pronounced <pr><?/</pr>.]</ety> <def>A boarding
house or boarding school in France, Belgium, Switzerland, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pension</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pensioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pensioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To grant a pension to; to pay
a regular stipend to; in consideration of service already performed; --
sometimes followed by <xex>off</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>pension</ex> off a
servant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One knighted Blackmore, and one <qex>pensioned</qex> Quarles.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pensionable</ent><br/
<hw>pensionable</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Entitled to receive a pension;
<as>as, a <ex>pensionable</ex> employee</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Maintained by a pension; receiving a pension; <as>as,
<ex>pensionary</ex> spies</as>.</def> <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of a pension; <as>as, a
<ex>pensionary</ex> provision for maintenance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pensionaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pensionnaire</ets>. Cf. <er>Pensioner</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who receives a pension; a pensioner.</def> <rj><au>E.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the chief magistrates of towns in
Holland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Grand pensionary</b></col>, <cd>the title of the prime
minister, or or president of the Council, of Holland when a
republic.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One in
receipt of a pension; hence, figuratively, a dependent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fickle <qex>pensioners</qex> of Morpheus' train.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Old <qex>pensioners</qex> . . . of Chelsea Hospital.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of an honorable band of gentlemen who attend the
sovereign of England on state occasions, and receive an annual pension,
or allowance, of <pound/150 and two horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pensionnaire</ets> one who pays for his
board. Cf. <er>Pensionary</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>In the
university of Cambridge, England, one who pays for his living in
commons; -- corresponding to <simto>commoner</simto> at Oxford.</def>
<rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensive</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pensif</ets>, fr. <ets>penser</ets> to think, fr. L.
<ets>pensare</ets> to weigh, ponder, consider, v. intens. fr.
<ets>pendere</ets> to weigh. See <er>Pension</er>,
<er>Poise</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Thoughtful, sober, or sad;
employed in serious reflection; given to, or favorable to, earnest or
melancholy musing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pensive</qex> secrecy of desert cell.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Anxious cares the <qex>pensive</qex> nymph oppressed.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressing or suggesting thoughtfulness with
sadness; <as>as, <ex>pensive</ex> numbers</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensived</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sived</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made pensive.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensively</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sive*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pensive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pensiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"sive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being pensive;
serious thoughtfulness; seriousness.</def> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penstemon</ent><br/
<hw>Penstemon</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A large genus of subshrubs
or herbs having showy blue or purple or red or yellow or white flowers;
found mostly in Western North America.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Penstemon</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penstock</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"stock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain;
perh. fr. <ets>pen</ets> an inclosure + <ets>stock</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A close conduit or pipe for conducting water, as, to a
water wheel, or for emptying a pond, or for domestic uses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The barrel of a wooden pump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pent</ent><br/
<hw>Pent</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/nt)</pr>, <pos>p. p. <or/ a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pen</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Penned or shut up; confined;
-- often with <xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here in the body <qex>pent</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Montgomery.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>pent-up</qex> Utica contracts your powers.</q> <rj><qau>J.
M. Sewall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pent-</ent><br/
<hw>pent-</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/nt)</pr>, <pos>pref.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>penta-</er>; -- used as a combining form before vowels, as in
<ex>pent</ex>oxide.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penta-</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>pref.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>penta-</grk>, a later combining form of <grk>pe`nte</grk> five. See
<er>Five</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A combining form denoting
<xex>five</xex>; <as>as, <ex>penta</ex>capsular;
<ex>penta</ex>gon.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Denoting the degree of
<xex>five</xex>, either as regards quality, property, or composition;
<as>as, <ex>penta</ex>sulphide, <sig>containing five sulfur atoms</sig>;
<ex>pent</ex>oxide, <sig>containing five oxygen atoms</sig>, etc.</as>
Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentabasic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*ba"sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>basic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Capable of uniting with five molecules of a monacid base; having
five acid hydrogen atoms capable of substitution by a basic radical; --
said of certain acids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacapsular</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*cap"su*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>capsular</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having five capsules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentachenium</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*che"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Penta-</er>, and <er>Achenium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
dry fruit composed of five carpels, which are covered by an epigynous
calyx and separate at maturity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentachloride</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*chlo"ride</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>chloride</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A chloride having five atoms of chlorine in each molecule; <as>as,
phosphorus <ex>pentachloride</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentachord</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*chord</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pentachordus</ets> five-stringed, Gr. <grk>penta`chordos</grk>;
<grk>penta-</grk> five + <grk>chordh`</grk> string.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An ancient instrument of music with five strings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An order or system of five sounds.</def>
<rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacid</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tac"id</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pent</ets>a- + <ets>acid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Capable of neutralizing, or combining with, five molecules of a
monobasic acid; having five hydrogen atoms capable of substitution by
acid residues; -- said of certain complex bases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacle</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pe`nte</grk> five.]</ety> <def>A five-pointed star, also called a
pentagram or pentalpha. Sometimes referring to a similar figure, such as
the figure composed of two equilateral triangles intersecting so as to
form a six-pointed star. It was used in early ornamental art, and also
with superstitious import by the astrologers and mystics of the Middle
Ages. The six-pointed star is more commonly called a
<contr>hexagram</contr>, or called Solomon's seal; it resembles the star
of David (Magen David)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacoccous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*coc"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Penta-</er>, <er>Coccus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Composed
of five united carpels with one seed in each, as certain
fruits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaconter</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*con`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>See <er>Penteconter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacrinin</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tac"ri*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A red and purple pigment found in certain crinoids of
the genus Pentacrinus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacrinite</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tac"ri*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <grk>kri`non</grk> a lily.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of Pentacrinus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacrinoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tac"ri*noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pentacrinus</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An immature comatula when it is still attached
by a stem, and thus resembles a Pentacrinus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacrinus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*tac"ri*nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Penta-</er>, and <er>Crinum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
genus of large, stalked crinoids, of which several species occur in deep
water among the West Indies and elsewhere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacron</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*ta"cron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Pentacra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pentacrons</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pe`nte</grk> five +
<grk>'a`kron</grk> a summit.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A solid
having five summits or angular points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentacrostic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*cros"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>acrostic</ets>.]</ety> <def>A set of
verses so disposed that the name forming the subject of the acrostic
occurs five times -- the whole set of verses being divided into five
different parts from top to bottom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentad</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>penta`s</grk> <grk>penta`dos</grk>, a body of five, fr.
<grk>pe`nte</grk> five.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any element,
atom, or radical, having a valence of five, or which can be combined
with, substituted for, or compared with, five atoms of hydrogen or other
monad; <as>as, nitrogen is a <ex>pentad</ex> in the ammonium
compounds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any grouping of five things.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentad</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tad</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Having the
valence of a pentad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentadactyle</ent><br/
<ent>Pentadactyl</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen`ta*dac"tyl</hw>, <hw>Pen`ta*dac"tyle</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>pentada`ktylos</grk> with
five fingers or toes. See <er>Penta-</er>, and <er>Dactyl</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having five digits to the hand or
foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having five appendages resembling fingers or
toes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentadactyloid</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*dac"tyl*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pentadactyl</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having the form of, or a structure modified
from, a pentadactyl limb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentadecane</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*dec"ane</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n`t<adot/*d<ecr/k"<amac/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <grk>de`ka</grk> ten.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hydrocarbon of the paraffin series,
(<chform>C15H32</chform>) found in petroleum, tar oil, etc., and
obtained as a colorless liquid; -- so called from the <xex>fifteen</xex>
carbon atoms in the molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentadecatoic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*dec`a*to"ic</hw>
<pr>(p<ecr/n`t<adot/*d<ecr/k`<adot/*t<omac/"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>decatoic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or derived from, pentadecane, or designating an
acid related to it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentadecylic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*decyl"ic</hw>
<pr>(p<ecr/n`t<adot/*d<esl/*s<icr/l"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>decylic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Quindecylic</er><-- = pentadecyl? -->.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentadelphous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*del"phous</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n`t<adot/*d<ecr/l"f<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'adelfo`s</grk>
brother.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the stamens arranged in
five clusters, those of each cluster having their filaments more or less
united, as the flowers of the linden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1062 --></p>

<p><ent>Pentafid</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*fid</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"t<adot/*f<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + root of L. <ets>findere</ets> to split.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided or cleft into five parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaglot</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*glot</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/n"t<adot/*gl<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>-glot</ets>, as in
<ets>polyglot</ets>.]</ety> <def>A work in five different
tongues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagon</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*gon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>penta`gwnon</grk>; <grk>penta-</grk> (see <er>Penta-</er>) +
<grk>gwni`a</grk> angle: cf. L. <ets>pentagonium</ets>, F.
<ets>pentagone</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A plane figure
having five angles, and, consequently, five sides; any figure having
five angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Regular pentagon</b></col>, <cd>a pentagon in which the
angles are all equal, and the sides all equal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagonal</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tag"o*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pentagonal</ets>, <ets>pentagone</ets>, L. <ets>pentagonus</ets>,
<ets>pentagonius</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Having five corners or
angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pentagonal dodecahedron</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Dodecahedron</er>, and <er>Pyritohedron</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagonally</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tag"o*nal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the form of a
pentagon; with five angles.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagonous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tag"o*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pentagonal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagram</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, neut. of
<?/ having five lines. See <er>Penta-</er>, and <er>-gram</er>.]</ety>
<def>A pentacle or a pentalpha.</def> <ldquo/Like a wizard
<xex>pentagram</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagraphical</ent><br/
<ent>Pentagraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen`ta*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pen`ta*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Corrupted fr. <ets>pantographic</ets>, <ets>-ical</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Pantographic. See <er>Pantograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagynia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen`ta*gyn"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ female.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A Linn<ae/an order of plants, having five styles or
pistils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentagynous</ent><br/
<ent>Pentagynian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen`ta*gyn"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pen*tag"y*nous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to plants of the order Pentagyna; having five
styles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentahedral</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*he"dral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having five
sides; <as>as, a <ex>pentahedral</ex> figure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentahedrical</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*hed"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pentahedral.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentahedron</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*he"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <grk>"e`dra</grk> seat, base.]</ety>
<def>A solid figure having five sides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentahedrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*he"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pentahedral.</def> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentail</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
peculiar insectivore (<spn>Ptilocercus Lowii</spn>) of Borneo; -- so
called from its very long, quill-shaped tail, which is scaly at the base
and plumose at the tip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentalpha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*tal"pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>pentalpha</ets>. See <er>Penta-</er>, and
<er>Alpha</er>.]</ety> <def>A five-pointed star, resembling five alphas
joined at their bases; -- used as a symbol.</def> <-- also called
pentagram and pentacle? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentamera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*tam"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pentamerous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive
division of Coleoptera, including those that normally have five-jointed
tarsi. It embraces about half of all the known species of the
Coleoptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentameran</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tam"er*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Pentamera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentamerous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tam"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <?/ part.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Divided into, or consisting of, five parts;
also, arranged in sets, with five parts in each set, as a flower with
five sepals, five petals, five, or twice five, stamens, and five
pistils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the
Pentamera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentamerus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*tam"e*rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pentamerous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of
extinct Paleozoic brachiopods, often very abundant in the Upper
Silurian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pentamerus limestone</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>a
Silurian limestone composed largely of the shells of
Pentamerus.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentameter</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tam"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/; <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ measure.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. &
L.Pros.)</fld> <def>A verse of five feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The dactylic pentameter consists of two parts separated
by a di<ae/resis. Each part consists of two dactyls and a long syllable.
The spondee may take the place of the dactyl in the first part, but not
in the second. The <xex>elegiac distich</xex> consists of the hexameter
followed by the pentameter.</note> <rj><au>Harkness.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentameter</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tam"e*ter</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having five metrical
feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentamethylene</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*meth"yl*ene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>methylene</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical hydrocarbon,
<chform>C5H10</chform>, metameric with the amylenes, and the nucleus of
a large number of derivatives; -- so named because regarded as composed
of five methylene residues. Cf. <er>Trimethylene</er>, and
<er>Tetramethylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentandria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*tan"dri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/, <?/, man, male.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Linn<ae/an class of plants having five separate
stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentandrous</ent><br/
<ent>Pentandrian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen*tan"dri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pen*tan"drous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to the class Pentadria; having five stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentane</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Penta-</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any one of the three
metameric hydrocarbons, <chform>C5H12</chform>, of the methane or
paraffin series. They are colorless, volatile liquids, two of which
occur in petroleum. So called because of the <xex>five</xex> carbon
atoms in the molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentangle</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tan`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pent</ets>a-
+ <ets>angle</ets>.]</ety> <def>A pentagon.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentangular</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tan"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pent</ets>a- + <ets>angular</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having five
corners or angles.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentapetalous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>petal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having five petals, or flower leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaphyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*taph"yl*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <?/ leaf.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having five leaves or leaflets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentapody</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tap"o*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety> <fld>(Pros.)</fld>
<def>A measure or series consisting of five feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaptote</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tap*tote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
(<ets>pl</ets>.) <ets>pentaptota</ets>. Gr. <?/ with five cases; <?/
(see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ falling.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A
noun having five cases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaptych</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tap*tych</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, a fold.]</ety> <fld>(Fine
Arts)</fld> <def>A picture, or combination of pictures, consisting of a
centerpiece and double folding doors or wings, as for an
altarpiece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tar*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>pentarchie</ets>. See <er>Penta-</er>, and <er>-archy</er>.]</ety>
<def>A government in the hands of five persons; five joint rulers.</def>
<au>P. Fletcher.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>pentarchy</xex> of the
senses.<rdquo/ <au>A. Brewer.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaspast</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*spast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pentaspaston</ets>, Gr. <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ to pull:
cf. F. <ets>pentaspaste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A purchase with five
pulleys.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentaspermous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*sper"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <?/ seed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Containing five seeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentastich</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*stich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ of five
verses; <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ line, verse.]</ety> <def>A
composition consisting of five verses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentastichous</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tas"ti*chous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <grk>sti`chos</grk> a row.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having, or arranged in, five vertical ranks, as
the leaves of an apple tree or a cherry tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentastomida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen`ta*stom"i*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ a mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Linguatulina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentastyle</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a pillar.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Having five columns in front; -- said of a temple or portico in
classical architecture.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A portico
having five columns.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentateuch</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"ta*teuch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pentateuchus</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ (see <er>Penta-</er>) + <?/ a
tool, implement, a book, akin to <?/ to prepare, make ready, and perh.
to E. <ets>text</ets>. See <er>Five</er>, and <er>Text</er>.]</ety>
<def>The first five books of the Old Testament, collectively; -- called
also the <altname>Law of Moses</altname>, <altname>Book of the Law of
Moses</altname>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentateuchal</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*teu"chal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the Pentateuch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentathionic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*thi*on"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>thionic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid of sulphur obtained by
leading hydrogen sulphide into a solution of sulphur dioxide; -- so
called because it contains <xex>five</xex> atoms of sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentathlon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen*tath"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/; <?/ five + <?/ a contest.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A fivefold athletic performance peculiar to the great
national games of the Greeks, including leaping, foot racing, wrestling,
throwing the discus, and throwing the spear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the modern Olympic Games, a composite contest
made up of a running broad jump, throwing the javelin, a 200-meter run,
throwing the discus, and a 1500-meter run.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentatomic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`ta*tom"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + <ets>atomic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having five atoms in the molecule.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Having five hydrogen atoms capable of substitution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentavalent</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tav"a*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> + L. <ets>valens</ets>, p. pr. See
<er>Valence</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Having a valence of
five; -- said of certain atoms and radicals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penteconter</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"te*con`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ (sc.
<?/), fr. <?/ fifty.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A Grecian
vessel with fifty oars.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pentaconter</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentecost</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"te*cost</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pentecoste</ets>, Gr. <?/ (sc. <?/) the fiftieth day, Pentecost,
fr. <?/ fiftieth, fr. <?/ fifty, fr. <?/ five. See <er>Five</er>, and
cf. <er>Pingster</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A solemn festival of the
Jews; -- so called because celebrated on the fiftieth day (seven weeks)
after the second day of the Passover (which fell on the sixteenth of the
Jewish month Nisan); -- hence called, also, the <altname>Feast of
Weeks</altname>. At this festival an offering of the first fruits of the
harvest was made. By the Jews it was generally regarded as commemorative
of the gift of the law on the fiftieth day after the departure from
Egypt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A festival of the Roman Catholic and other churches
in commemoration of the descent of the Holy Spirit on the apostles;
which occurred on the day of Pentecost; -- called also
<altname>Whitsunday</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentecostal</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`te*cos"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Pentecost or to Whitsuntide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentecostals</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`te*cos"tals</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Offerings
formerly made to the parish priest, or to the mother church, at
Pentecost.</def> <rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentecoster</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`te*cos"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ fifty.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An officer in
the Spartan army commanding fifty men.</def>
<rj><au>Mitford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentecosty</ent><br/
<hw>Pen`te*cos"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pentecosties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ the
fiftieth, <?/ fifty.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A troop of
fifty soldiers in the Spartan army; -- called also
<altname>pentecostys</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Jowett (Thucyd.
).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentelican</ent><br/
<ent>Pentelic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen*tel"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pen*tel"i*can</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to Mount
Pentelicus, near Athens, famous for its fine white marble quarries;
obtained from Mount Pentelicus; <as>as, the <ex>Pentelic</ex> marble of
which the Parthenon is built</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentene</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Penta-</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Amylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penthouse</ent><br/
<hw>Pent"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of
<ets>pentice</ets>.]</ety> <def>A shed or roof sloping from the main
wall or building, as over a door or window; a lean-to. Also
figuratively.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>penthouse</xex> of his eyes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. An apartment at the top of a building. It is often the most
luxuriously appointed apartment, and is thus used as a metaphor for
luxurious living. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penthouse</ent><br/
<hw>Pent"house`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Leaning; overhanging.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Penthouse</xex> lid.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/My
<xex>penthouse</xex> eyebrows.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentice</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>appentis</ets> a penthouse. See <er>Append</er>.]</ety> <def>A
penthouse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentile</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tile`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pantile</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentine</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Penta-</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An unsaturated
hydrocarbon, <chform>C5H8</chform>, of the acetylene series. Same as
<er>Valerylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentoic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*to"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Penta-</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or
designating, an acid (called also <xex>valeric acid</xex>) derived from
pentane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentone</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Penta-</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Valylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-sane</ent><br/
<ent>Pentosan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pen"to*san</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> Also
<hw>-sane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>. <ety>[From <er>Pentose</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>One of a class of substances (complex
carbohydrates widely distributed in plants, as in fruits, gums, woods,
hay, etc.) which yield pentoses on hydrolysis.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentose</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets> +
<ets>-ose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any of a group of sugars
of the formula <chform>C5H10O5</chform>, such as as
<stype>arabinose</stype> or <stype>ribose</stype>; -- so called from the
five carbon atoms in the molecule. They are not fermented by
yeast.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentoxide</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Penta-</ets>
+ <ets>oxide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An oxide containing
five atoms of oxygen in each molecule; <as>as, phosphorus
<ex>pentoxide</ex>, <chform>P2O5</chform></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentremite</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tre*mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of Pentremites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentremites</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pen`tre*mi"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ five + L. <ets>remus</ets> an oar.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A genus of crinoids belonging to the Blastoidea. They have five
petal-like ambulacra.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentroof</ent><br/
<hw>Pent"roof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pente</ets> slope + E. <ets>roof</ets>, or from
<ets>pent</ets>house <ets>roof</ets>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Lean-to</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentrough</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"trough`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
penstock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"tyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pent</ets>a +
<ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The hypothetical radical,
<chform>C5H11</chform>, of pentane and certain of its derivatives. Same
as <er>Amyl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pentylic</ent><br/
<hw>Pen*tyl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to,
derived from, or containing, pentyl; as, <xex>pentylic</xex>
alcohol</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinochle</ent><br/
<ent>Pinocle</ent><br/
<ent>Penuchle</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe"nu*chle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pin"o*cle</hw>
<hw>Pin"o*chle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A game at
cards, played with forty-eight cards, being all the cards above the
eight spots in two packs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penult</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"nult</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbreviated fr.
<ets>penultima</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram. & Pros.)</fld> <def>The last
syllable but one of a word; the syllable preceding the final
one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penultima</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nul"ti*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. (sc.
<ets>syllaba</ets>), fr. <ets>penultimus</ets>, <ets>paenultimus</ets>,
the last but one; <ets>paene</ets> almost + <ets>ultimus</ets> the
last.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Penult</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penultimate</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nul"ti*mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Last but one;
<as>as, the <ex>penultimate</ex> syllable, the last syllable but one of
a word</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penultimate</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nul"ti*mate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The penult.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penumbra</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*num"bra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>paene</ets> almost + <ets>umbra</ets> shade.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An incomplete or partial shadow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The shadow cast, in an eclipse,
where the light is partly, but not wholly, cut off by the intervening
body; the space of partial illumination between the umbra, or perfect
shadow, on all sides, and the full light.</def> <rj><au>Sir I.
Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The faint shade surrounding the dark central portion of
a solar spot is also called the <altname>penumbra</altname>, and
sometimes <altname>umbra</altname>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>The part of a picture where the
shade imperceptibly blends with the light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penumbrala</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*num"brala</hw>. <def>Of or pertaining to a penumbra; resembling a
penumbra; partially illuminated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penurious</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*nu"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Penury</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Excessively sparing in the use
of money; sordid; stingy; miserly.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>penurious</xex>
niggard of his wealth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not bountiful or liberal; scanty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here creeps along a poor, <qex>penurious</qex> stream.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Pitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Destitute of money; suffering extreme want.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/My <xex>penurious</xex> band.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Avaricious; covetous; parsimonious; miserly;
niggardly; stingy. See <er>Avaricious</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>--<wordforms><wf>Pe*nu"ri*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pe*nu"ri*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penury</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"u*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>penuria</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/ hunger, <?/ poverty, need, <?/ one who
works for his daily bread, a poor man, <?/ to work for one's daily
bread, to be poor: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/nurie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Absence of resources; want; privation; indigence; extreme poverty;
destitution.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>penury</xex> of military forces.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were exposed to hardship and <qex>penury</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sprat.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It arises in neither from <qex>penury</qex> of thought.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Penuriousness; miserliness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penwiper</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"wip`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cloth, or other
material, for wiping off or cleaning ink from a pen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Penwoman</ent><br/
<hw>Pen"wom`an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Penwomen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A female writer; an
authoress.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peon</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Poon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peon</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"on</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>peon</ets>, or Pg.
<ets>pe<?/o</ets>, one who travels on foot, a foot soldier, a pawn in
chess. See <er>Pawn</er> in chess.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A foot
soldier; a policeman; also, an office attendant; a messenger.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A day laborer; a servant; especially, in some of the
Spanish American countries, debtor held by his creditor in a form of
qualified servitude, to work out a debt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>See 2d <er>Pawn</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peonage</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"on*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The condition of a
peon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peonism</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"on*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Peonage</er>.</def> <rj><au>D. Webster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peony</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"o*ny</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"<osl/*n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Peonies</plw>
<pr>(p<emac/"<osl/*n<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>pione</ets>,
<ets>pioine</ets>, <ets>pioni</ets>, OF. <ets>pione</ets>, F.
<ets>pivoine</ets>, L. <ets>paeonia</ets>, Gr. <grk>paiwni`a</grk>, fr.
<grk>Pai`wn</grk>, <grk>Paia`n</grk>, the god of healing. Cf.
<er>P<ae/an</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant, and its flower,
of the ranunculaceous genus <gen>P<ae/onia</gen>. Of the four or five
species, one is a shrub; the rest are perennial herbs with showy
flowers, often double in cultivation.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>p<ae/ony</asp>, and <asp>piony</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1063 --></p>

<p><ent>People</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"ple</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"p'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>peple</ets>, <ets>people</ets>, OF. <ets>pueple</ets>, F.
<ets>peuple</ets>, fr. L. <ets>populus</ets>. Cf. <er>Populage</er>,
<er>Public</er>, <er>Pueblo</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The body of
persons who compose a community, tribe, nation, or race; an aggregate of
individuals forming a whole; a community; a nation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Unto him shall the gathering of the <qex>people</qex> be.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xlix. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ants are a <qex>people</qex> not strong.</q> <rj><qau>Prov.
xxx. 25.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before many <qex>peoples</qex>, and nations, and tongues.</q>
<rj><qau>Rev. x. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Earth's monarchs are her <qex>peoples</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Whitter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A government of all the <qex>people</qex>, by all the
<qex>people</qex>, for all the <qex>people</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Parker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>People</xex>is a collective noun, generally
construed with a plural verb, and only occasionally used in the plural
form (<xex>peoples</xex>), in the sense of nations or races.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Persons, generally; an indefinite number of men and
women; folks; population, or part of population; <as>as, country
<ex>people</ex></as>; -- sometimes used as an indefinite subject or
verb, like <xex>on</xex> in French, and <xex>man</xex> in German;
<as>as, <ex>people</ex> in adversity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>People</qex> were tempted to lend by great premiums.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>People</qex> have lived twenty-four days upon nothing but
water.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The mass of community as distinguished from a
special class; the commonalty; the populace; the vulgar; the common
crowd; <as>as, nobles and <ex>people</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And strive to gain his pardon from the <qex>people</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> With a possessive pronoun: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One's
ancestors or family; kindred; relations; <as>as, my <ex>people</ex> were
English</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One's subjects; fellow citizens;
companions; followers.</def> <ldquo/You slew great number of his
<xex>people</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>People</er>, <er>Nation</er>.</syn> <usage>
When speaking of a state, we use <xex>people</xex> for the mass of the
community, as distinguished from their rulers, and <xex>nation</xex> for
the entire political body, including the rulers. In another sense of the
term, <xex>nation</xex> describes those who are descended from the same
stock; and in this sense the Germans regard themselves as one
<xex>nation</xex>, though politically subject to different forms of
government.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>People</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"ple</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"p'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peopled</conjf>
<pr>(p<emac/"p'ld)</pr> <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>;
<conjf>Peopling</conjf> <pr>(p<emac/"p'l<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>popler</ets>, <ets>puepler</ets>, F.
<ets>puepler</ets>. Cf. <er>Populate</er>.]</ety> <def>To stock with
people or inhabitants; to fill as with people; to populate.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Peopled</xex> heaven with angels.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the gay motes that <qex>people</qex> the sunbeams.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peopled</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"pled</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"p'ld)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stocked
with, or as with, people; inhabited.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>peopled</xex>
air.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peopleless</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"ple*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of people.</def>
<rj><au>Poe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peopler</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"pler</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"pl<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
settler; an inhabitant.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Peoplers</xex> of the peaceful
glen.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. S. Blackie.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>People's bank</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"ple's bank</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"p'lz)</pr>. <def>A form of
cooperative bank, such as those of Germany; -- a term loosely used for
various forms of cooperative financial institutions.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>People's party</ent><br/
<hw>People's party</hw>. <fld>(U. S. Politics)</fld> <def>A party formed
in 1891, advocating an increase of the currency, public ownership and
operation of railroads, telegraphs, etc., an income tax, limitation in
ownership of land, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peoplish</ent><br/
<hw>Peo"plish</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"pl<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Vulgar.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peorias</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*o"ri*as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Peoria</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>An Algonquin tribe of Indians who formerly inhabited a part of
Illinois.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepastic</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*pas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pepai`nein</grk> to ripen, suppurate: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/pastique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Maturative</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peperino</ent><br/
<ent>Peperine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pep"e*rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pep`e*ri"no</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>peperino</ets>, L.
<ets>piper</ets> pepper. So called on account of its color.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A volcanic rock, formed by the cementing
together of sand, scoria, cinders, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peplis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pep"lis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a kind of
plant, Gr. <grk>pepli`s</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
plants including water purslane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peplum</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl. E.
<plw>-lumus</plw>, L. <plw>-la</plw></plu>. <ety>[L.]</ety> <def> A
peplos. Hence: An overskirt hanging like an ancient peplos; also, a
short fitted skirt attached to a waist or coat.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peplus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pep"lus</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/p"l<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. Gr. <grk>pe`plos</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An upper garment
worn by Grecian and Roman women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of kerchief formerly worn by
Englishwomen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe"po</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"p<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
kind of melon, from Gr. <grk>pe`pwn</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any fleshy fruit with a firm rind, as a pumpkin, melon, or gourd.
See <er>Gourd</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepper</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/p"p<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>peper</ets>, AS. <ets>pipor</ets>, L. <ets>piper</ets>, fr. Gr.
<grk>pe`peri</grk>, <grk>pi`peri</grk>, akin to Skr. <ets>pippala</ets>,
<ets>pippali</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A well-known, pungently
aromatic condiment, the dried berry, either whole or powdered, of the
<spn>Piper nigrum</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <stype>Common pepper</stype>, or <stype>black
pepper</stype>, is made from the whole berry, dried just before
maturity; <stype>white pepper</stype> is made from the ripe berry after
the outer skin has been removed by maceration and friction. It has less
of the peculiar properties of the plant than the black pepper. Pepper is
used in medicine as a carminative stimulant.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The plant which yields pepper, an
East Indian woody climber (<spn>Piper nigrum</spn>), with ovate leaves
and apetalous flowers in spikes opposite the leaves. The berries are red
when ripe. Also, by extension, any one of the several hundred species of
the genus <gen>Piper</gen>, widely dispersed throughout the tropical and
subtropical regions of the earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Capsicum</gen> (of the
<fam>Solanaceae</fam> family, which are unrelated to <gen>Piper</gen>),
and its fruit; red pepper; chili pepper; <as>as, the <stype>bell
<ex>pepper</ex></stype> and the <stype>jalapeno <ex>pepper</ex></stype>
(both <spn>Capsicum annuum</spn>) and the <stype>habanero
<ex>pepper</ex></stype> (<spn>Capsicum chinense</spn>); </as>. These
contain varying levels of the substance <prod>capsaicin</prod>
(<chform>C18H27O3N</chform>), which gives the peppers their hot taste.
The habanero is about 25-50 times hotter than the jalapeno according to
a scale developed by <person>Wilbur Scoville</person> in 1912. See also
<er>Capsicum</er> and
https://web.archive.org/web/20090312013237/http://www.chili-pepper-plants.com.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>pepper</xex> has been extended to various
other fruits and plants, more or less closely resembling the true
pepper, esp. to the common varieties of <gen>Capsicum</gen>. See
<er>Capsicum</er>, and the Phrases, below.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>African pepper</b></col>, <cd>the Guinea pepper. See
under <er>Guinea</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Cayenne pepper</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Cayenne</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Chinese pepper</b></col>,
<cd>the spicy berries of the <spn>Xanthoxylum piperitum</spn>, a species
of prickly ash found in China and Japan.</cd> -- <col><b>Guinea
pepper</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Guinea</er>, and
<er>Capsicum</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Jamaica pepper</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Allspice</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Long pepper</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The spike of berries of <spn>Piper longum</spn>, an East Indian
shrub</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The root of <spn>Piper methysticum</spn>
(syn. <spn>Macropiper methysticum</spn>) of the family
<fam>Piperaceae</fam>. See <er>Kava</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Malaguetta pepper</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Meleguetta pepper</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the aromatic seeds of
the <spn>Amomum Melegueta</spn>, an African plant of the Ginger family.
They are sometimes used to flavor beer, etc., under the name of
<altname>grains of Paradise</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Red
pepper</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Capsicum</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
pepper bush</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an American shrub
(<spn>Clethra alnifolia</spn>), with racemes of fragrant white flowers;
-- called also <altname>white alder</altname>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Pepper box</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Pepper
caster</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a small box or bottle, with a perforated
lid, used for sprinkling ground pepper on food, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Pepper corn</b></col>. <cd>See in the Vocabulary.</cd> --
<col><b>Pepper elder</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a West Indian name
of several plants of the Pepper family, species of <gen>Piper</gen> and
<gen>Peperomia</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pepper moth</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European moth (<spn>Biston betularia</spn>)
having white wings covered with small black specks.</cd> --
<col><b>Pepper pot</b></col>, <cd>a mucilaginous soup or stew of
vegetables and cassareep, much esteemed in the West Indies.</cd> --
<col><b>Pepper root</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>. <cd>See
<er>Coralwort</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>pepper sauce</b></col>, <cd>a
condiment for the table, made of small red peppers steeped in
vinegar.</cd> -- <col><b>Pepper tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an
aromatic tree (<spn>Drimys axillaris</spn>) of the Magnolia family,
common in New Zealand. See <cref>Peruvian mastic tree</cref>, under
<er>Mastic</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepper</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Peppered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Peppering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sprinkle or
season with pepper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: To shower shot or other missiles, or
blows, upon; to pelt; to fill with shot, or cover with bruises or
wounds; <as>as, to <ex>pepper</ex> him with buckshot</as>.</def>
<ldquo/I have <xex>peppered</xex> two of them.<rdquo/ <ldquo/I am
<xex>peppered</xex>, I warrant, for this world.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepper</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fire numerous shots
(at).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepper box</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A buttress on the
left-hand wall of a fives court as the game is played at Eton College,
England.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepperbrand</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*brand`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See 1st <er>Bunt</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peppercorn</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*corn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
dried berry of the black pepper (<spn>Piper nigrum</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything insignificant; a particle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepper dulse</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per dulse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A variety
of edible seaweed (<spn>Laurencia pinnatifida</spn>) distinguished for
its pungency.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepperer</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A grocer; --
formerly so called because he sold pepper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peppergrass</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any herb of the cruciferous genus <gen>Lepidium</gen>,
especially the garden peppergrass, or garden cress, <spn>Lepidium
sativum</spn>; -- called also <altname>pepperwort</altname>. All the
species have a pungent flavor.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The common
pillwort of Europe (<spn>Pilularia globulifera</spn>). See
<er>Pillwort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepperidge</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*idge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. NL.
<ets>berberis</ets>, E. <ets>barberry</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A North American tree (<spn>Nyssa multiflora</spn>) with very tough
wood, handsome oval polished leaves, and very acid berries, -- the sour
gum, or common tupelo. See <er>Tupelo</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>piperidge</asp> and <asp>pipperidge</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pepperidge bush</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
barberry.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peppering</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hot; pungent; peppery.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peppermint</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*mint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pepper</ets> + <ets>mint</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An aromatic and pungent plant of the genus
<gen>Mentha</gen> (<spn>Mentha piperita</spn>), much used in medicine
and confectionery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A volatile oil (oil of peppermint) distilled from
the fresh herb; also, a well-known essence or spirit (essence of
peppermint) obtained from it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A lozenge of sugar flavored with
peppermint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peppermint</b></col><cd> camphor. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> Same
as <er>Menthol</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Peppermint tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name given to several Australian species of gum
tree (<spn>Eucalyptus amygdalina</spn>, <spn>Eucalyptus piperita</spn>,
<spn>E. odorata</spn>, etc.) which have hard and durable wood, and yield
an essential oil.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepperoni</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*o*ni</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a hard sausage of
beef and pork, highly seasoned. </def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peppershaker</ent><br/
<hw>peppershaker</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shaker with a perforated top
for sprinkling ground pepper.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pepper box, pepper pot.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pepperwood</ent><br/
<hw>pepperwood</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Pacific coast tree
(<spn>Umbellularia californica</spn>) having aromatic foliage and small
umbellate flowers followed by olivelike fruit; yields a hard tough
wood.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> California laurel, California bay tree, Oregon
myrtle, spice tree, sassafras laurel, California olive, mountain laurel,
Umbellularia californica.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepperwort</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Peppergrass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peppery</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"per*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to pepper; having the qualities of pepper; hot;
pungent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Hot-tempered; passionate; choleric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>peppy</ent><br/
<hw>pep"py</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Full of pep; spirited; bouncy{2};
<as>as, the <ex>peppy</ex> and interesting talk</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bouncing, bouncy, spirited, zippy.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepsi</ent><br/
<hw>Pepsi</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[See Pepsi Cola]</ety>
<def>Pepsi Cola; -- a familiar contraction; <as>as, I prefer
<ex>Pepsi</ex> to Coke</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Pepsi Cola.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepsi-Cola</ent><br/
<ent>Pepsi Cola</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pepsi Cola</hw>, <hw>Pepsi-Cola</hw></mhw> <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[A trademark of the <org>Pepsi-Cola Corporation</org>.]</ety>
<def>A carbonated soft drink flavored by extract from the cola nut (kola
nut). The nut is found on trees of the species <spn>Cola acuminata</spn>
and <spn>Cola nitida</spn>.</def> <mark>[Trademark]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Pepsi</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>


<p><ent>Pepsin</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"sin</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/p"s<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pe`psis</grk> a cooking, digesting, digestion, fr.
<grk>pe`ptein</grk>, <grk>pe`ssein</grk>, to cook, digest: cf. F.
<ets>pepsine</ets>. Cf. <er>Dyspepsia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A proteolytic enzyme (MW 34,500) contained in the
secretory glands of the stomach. In the gastric juice it is united with
dilute hydrochloric acid (0.2 per cent, approximately) and the two
together constitute the active portion of the digestive fluid. It
degrades proteins to proteoses and peptides, and is notable for having a
very low pH optimum for its activity. It is the active agent in the
gastric juice of all animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ As prepared from the glandular layer of pigs' or calves'
stomachs it constitutes an important article of pharmacy.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepsinhydrochloric</ent><br/
<hw>Pep`sin*hy`dro*chlo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Peptohydrochloric</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pepsinogen</ent><br/
<hw>Pep*sin"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pepsin</ets> + <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A proenzyme, the antecedent of the enzyme pepsin. A
substance contained in the form of granules in the peptic cells of the
gastric glands. It is readily convertible into pepsin. Also called
<altname>propepsin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptic</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"tic</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/p"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pepticus</ets>, Gr. <grk>peptiko`s</grk>. See
<er>Pepsin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to digestion;
promoting digestion; digestive; <as>as, <ex>peptic</ex>
sauces</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Able to digest.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tolerably nutritive for a mind as yet so <qex>peptic</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to pepsin;
resembling pepsin in its power of digesting or dissolving albuminous
matter; containing or yielding pepsin, or a body of like properties;
<as>as, the <ex>peptic</ex> glands</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>peptic ulcer</b></col>, <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>an erosion
of the mucous membrane forming the lining of the stomach, duodenum, or
lower esophagus, originally believed to be due mostly to the action of
gastric juice. Recently, the bacterium <spn>Helicobacter pylori</spn>
has been implicated as a causal agent in many peptic ulcers. In other
cases, the use of non-steroidal antiinflammatory agents (NSAIDs) such as
aspirin are believed to be to blame. Such ulcers may be treated with
antibiotics or antisecretory agents that reduce stomach acidity, or
both. Formerly believed to be a chronic illness that could only be
managed, it is now viewed as a curable disease.</cd></cs> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptic</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An agent that promotes
digestion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The digestive organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is there some magic in the place,<br/
Or do my <qex>peptics</qex> differ?</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptics</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The science of
digestion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptogen</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"to*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pepto</ets>ne
+ <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A substance
convertible into peptone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptogenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pep`to*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Peptogenous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Pep*tog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Capable of yielding, or being converted into,
peptone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptohydrochloric</ent><br/
<hw>Pep`to*hy`dro*chlo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Peptone</er>, and <er>Hydrochloric</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Designating a hypothetical acid (called
<xex>peptohydrochloric acid</xex>, <xex>pepsinhydrochloric acid</xex>,
and <xex>chloropeptic</xex> acid) which is supposed to be formed when
pepsin and dilute (0.1-0.4 per cent) hydrochloric acid are mixed
together.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptone</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pepto`s</grk> cooked.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The soluble polypeptides produced by hydrolysis of
protein; specifically the soluble peptides into which food is
transformed by the action of the gastric and pancreatic juices. Peptones
are also formed from protein matter by the action of boiling water and
boiling dilute acids.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Collectively, in a broader
sense, all the products resulting from the solution of proteinaceous
matter in either gastric or pancreatic juice. In this case, however,
intermediate products (albumose bodies), such as
<xex>antialbumose</xex>, <xex>hemialbumose</xex>, etc., are mixed with
the true peptones. Also termed <xex>albuminose</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Pure peptones are of three kinds,
<xex>amphopeptone</xex>, <xex>antipeptone</xex>, and
<xex>hemipeptone</xex>, and, unlike most proteins, are not precipitated
by saturating their solutions with ammonium sulphate.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptonize</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"to*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To convert into peptone; to digest or
dissolve by means of a proteolytic ferment; <as>as, <ex>peptonized</ex>
food</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptonoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pep"to*noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Peptone</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance related to peptone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptonuria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pep`to*nu"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peptone</er>, and <er>Urine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
presence of peptone, or a peptonelike body, in the urine; now referred
to as <altname>proteinuria</altname>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peptotoxine</ent><br/
<hw>Pep`to*tox"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pepto</ets>ne + <ets>tox</ets>ic + <ets>-ine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A toxic alkaloid found occasionally
associated with the peptones formed from fibrin by pepsinhydrochloric
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pequots</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"quots</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Pequot</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A
tribe of Indians who formerly inhabited Eastern Connecticut.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Pequods</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Per-</ent><br/
<hw>Per-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Per</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A prefix used to signify <xex>through</xex>, <xex>throughout</xex>,
<xex>by</xex>, <xex>for</xex>, or as an <xex>intensive</xex> as
<xex>per</xex>haps, by hap or chance; <xex>per</xex>ennial, that lasts
throughout the year; <xex>per</xex>force, through or by force;
<xex>per</xex>foliate, <xex>per</xex>forate; <xex>per</xex>spicuous,
evident throughout or very evident; <xex>per</xex>plex, literally, to
entangle very much.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Originally, denoting that the
element to the name of which it is prefixed in the respective compounds
exercised its <xex>highest valence</xex>; now, only that the element has
a <xex>higher valence</xex> than in other similar compounds; thus,
barium <xex>per</xex>oxide is the highest oxide of barium; while
nitrogen and manganese <xex>per</xex>oxides, so-called, are not the
highest oxides of those elements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Per</ent><br/
<hw>Per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prep.</pos> <ety>[L. Cf. <er>Far</er>,
<er>For-</er>, <er>Pardon</er>, and cf. <er>Par</er>,
<ets>prep</ets>.]</ety> <def>Through; by means of; through the agency
of; by; for; for each; <as>as, <ex>per</ex> annum; <ex>per</ex> capita,
by heads, or according to individuals; <ex>per</ex> curiam, by the
court; <ex>per</ex> se, by itself, of itself</as>. <ex>Per</ex> is also
sometimes used with English words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Per annum</b></col>, <cd>by the year; in each successive
year; annually.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>per cent</b></col>, <col><b>Per
centum</b></col>, <col><b>percent</b></col></mcol>, <cd>by the hundred;
in the hundred; a proportion multiplied by one hundred; -- used esp. of
proportions of ingredients, rate or amount of interest, and the like;
most commonly used in the shortened form <ex>per cent</ex>; <as>as, 5 is
ten <ex>per cent</ex> of 50</as>. It is commonly symbolized with the per
cent sign, <ldquo/%<rdquo/.</cd> -- <col><b>Per diem</b></col>, <cd>by
the day.</cd> [For other phrases from the Latin, see Quotations,
Phrases, etc., from Foreign Languages, in the Supplement.]</cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peract</ent><br/
<hw>Per*act"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peractus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>peragere</ets>.]</ety> <def>To go
through with; to perform.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peracute</ent><br/
<hw>Per`a*cute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peracutus</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Acute</er>.]</ety>
<def>Very sharp; very violent; <as>as, a <ex>peracute</ex>
fever</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peradventure</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ad*ven"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv. & conj.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>per aventure</ets>, F. <ets>par aventure</ets>. See <er>Per</er>,
and <er>Adventure</er>.]</ety> <def>By chance; perhaps; it may be; if;
supposing.</def> <ldquo/If <xex>peradventure</xex> he speak against
me.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Peradventure</qex> there be fifty righteous within the
city.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xviii. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peradventure</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ad*ven"ture</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Chance; hap; hence, doubt;
question; <as>as, proved beyond <ex>peradventure</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peraeopod</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rae"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>perai^os</grk> on the opposite side + <ets>-pod</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the thoracic legs of a
crustacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peragrate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"a*grate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peragratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>peragrate</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
travel over or through.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peragration</ent><br/
<hw>Per`agra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peragratio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>peragration</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act or state of passing through any space; <as>as, the
<ex>peragration</ex> of the moon in her monthly revolution</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perambulate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*am"bu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perambulated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perambulating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>perambulatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perambulare</ets> to perambulate;
<ets>per</ets> through + <ets>ambulare</ets> to walk. See <er>Per-</er>,
and <er>Amble</er>.]</ety> <def>To walk through or over; especially, to
travel over for the purpose of surveying or examining; to inspect by
traversing; specifically, to inspect officially the boundaries of, as of
a town or parish, by walking over the whole line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perambulate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*am"bu*late</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To walk about; to ramble;
to stroll; <as>as, he <ex>perambulated</ex> in the park</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perambulating</ent><br/
<hw>perambulating</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <ety>[<pos>pr. p.</pos> of
<er>perambulate</er> (definition 2).]</ety> <def>Strolling or walking
around; <as>as, in the field we met some <ex>perambulating</ex>
veterans</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perambulation</ent><br/
<hw>Per*am`bu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of perambulating; traversing.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An annual survey of boundaries, as of town, a
parish, a forest, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A district within which one is authorized to make a
tour of inspection.</def> <ldquo/The . . . bounds of his own
<xex>perambulation</xex>.<rdquo/ <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holyday.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perambulator</ent><br/
<hw>Per*am"bu*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who perambulates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A surveyor's instrument for measuring distances. It
consists of a wheel arranged to roll along over the ground, with an
apparatus of clockwork, and a dial plate upon which the distance
traveled is shown by an index. See <er>Odometer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A low carriage for a child, propelled by pushing; a
baby carriage; -- called also <altname>pram</altname>, in
Britain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perameles</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`a*me"les</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a pouch + L. <ets>meles</ets> a badger.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any marsupial of the genus <gen>Perameles</gen>, which includes
numerous species found in Australia. They somewhat resemble rabbits in
size and form. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Bandicoot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perbend</ent><br/
<hw>Per"bend</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Perpender</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perbreak</ent><br/
<hw>Per"break`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<def>See <er>Parbreak</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perbromate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*bro"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of perbromic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perbromic</ent><br/
<hw>Per*bro"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>bromic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, the highest oxygen acid,
<chform>HBrO4</chform>, of bromine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perbromide</ent><br/
<hw>Per*bro"mide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A bromide having a higher proportion of bromine than any other
bromide of the same substance or series.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
perch.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of fishes, including the
fresh-water perch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percale</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`cale"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A fine cotton fabric, having a linen finish, and often printed on
one side, -- used for women's and children's wear, and for
bedsheets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percaline</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ca*line"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
fine kind of cotton goods, usually of one color, and with a glossy
surface, -- much use for linings.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percarbide</ent><br/
<hw>Per*car"bide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>carbide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
compound containing a relatively large amount of carbon.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percarburet</ent><br/
<hw>Per*car"bu*ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>carburet</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
percarbide.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percarbureted</ent><br/
<hw>Per*car"bu*ret`ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Combined with a relatively large amount of carbon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percase</ent><br/
<hw>Per*case"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>per
cas</ets>. See <er>Parcase</er>.]</ety> <def>Perhaps; perchance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perce</ent><br/
<hw>Perce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pierce.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perceivable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ceiv"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being perceived; perceptible.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*ceiv"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perceivance</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ceiv"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Power of
perceiving.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The senses and common
<xex>perceivance</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perceive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ceive"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<emac/v")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perceived</conjf>
<pr>(p<etil/r*s<emac/vd")</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perceiving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>percevoir</ets>,
<ets>perceveir</ets>, L. <ets>percipere</ets>, <ets>perceptum</ets>;
<ets>per</ets> (see <er>Per-</er>) + <ets>capere</ets> to take, receive.
See <er>Capacious</er>, and cf. <er>Perception</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To obtain knowledge of through the senses; to receive impressions
from by means of the bodily organs; to take cognizance of the existence,
character, or identity of, by means of the senses; to see, hear, or
feel; <as>as, to <ex>perceive</ex> a distant ship; to <ex>perceive</ex>
a discord.</as></def> <rj><au>Reid.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take intellectual cognizance of; to apprehend by
the mind; to be convinced of by direct intuition; to note; to remark; to
discern; to see; to understand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jesus <qex>perceived</qex> their wickedness.</q> <rj><qau>Matt.
xxii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You may, fair lady,<br/
<qex>Perceive</qex> I speak sincerely.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till we ourselves see it with our own eyes, and
<qex>perceive</qex> it by our own understandings, we are still in the
dark.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be affected of influenced by.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The upper regions of the air <qex>perceive</qex> the collection of
the matter of tempests before the air here below.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To discern; distinguish; observe; see; feel;
know; understand.</syn> <usage> -- To <er>Perceive</er>,
<er>Discern</er>. To <xex>perceive</xex> a thing is to apprehend it as
presented to the senses or the intellect; to <xex>discern</xex> is to
mark differences, or to see a thing as distinguished from others around
it. We may <xex>perceive</xex> two persons afar off without being able
to <xex>discern</xex> whether they are men or women. Hence,
<xex>discern</xex> is often used of an act of the senses or the mind
involving close, discriminating, analytical attention. We
<xex>perceive</xex> that which is clear or obvious; we
<xex>discern</xex> that which requires much attention to get an idea of
it. <ldquo/We <xex>perceive</xex> light, darkness, colors, or the truth
or falsehood of anything. We <xex>discern</xex> characters, motives, the
tendency and consequences of actions, etc.<rdquo/</usage>
<rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perceived</ent><br/
<hw>perceived</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<emac/vd")</pr> <pos>adj.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Detected by instinct or inference rather than by
recognized perceptual cues; <as>as, a <ex>perceived</ex>
threat</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> felt, sensed.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Detected by means of the senses; <as>as, a
<ex>perceived</ex> difference in temperature</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1064 --></p>

<p><ent>Perceiver</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ceiv"er</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<emac/v"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who perceives (in any of the senses of the verb).</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percely</ent><br/
<hw>Perce"ly</hw> <pr>(p<aum/rs"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Parsley.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>percent</ent><br/
<hw>per*cent"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<ecr/nt")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
proportion multiplied by 100; <as>as, he receives a <ex>percent</ex> of
the proceeds; 3 is 75 <ex>percent</ex> of 4</as>. Often symbolized by
the character <ldquo/%<rdquo/; <as>as, at 6% interest</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> percentage, per cent, pct.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percentage</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cent"age</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<ecr/nt"<asl/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Per cent</ets> + <ets>-age</ets>, as in average. See
<er>Per</er>, and <er>Cent</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A certain
rate per cent; the allowance, duty, rate of interest, discount, or
commission, on a hundred.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percept</ent><br/
<hw>Per"cept</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"s<ecr/pt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
L. <ets>percipere</ets>, <ets>perceptum</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>That object or phenomenon which is perceived.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The mental result of perceiving; the sensation of
something perceived; that part of the mental state resulting from an act
of perception.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The modern discussion between <qex>percept</qex> and concept, the
one sensuous, the other intellectual.</q> <rj><qau>Max
M<uum/ller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perceptibility</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cep`ti*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perceptibilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being perceptible; <as>as, the <ex>perceptibility</ex> of light
or color</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Perception.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perceptible</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cep"ti*ble</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<ecr/p"t<icr/*b'l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perceptibilis</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>perceptible</ets>. See <er>Perceive</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being perceived; cognizable; discernible; perceivable; large enough to
be perceived; not so small as to be incapable of perception.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a <qex>perceptible</qex> blast of the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per*cep"ti*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Per*cep"ti*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perception</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<ecr/p"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perceptio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>perception</ets>. See <er>Perceive</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of perceiving; cognizance by the senses or intellect;
apprehension by the bodily organs, or by the mind, of what is presented
to them; discernment; apprehension; cognition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>The faculty of perceiving; the
faculty, or peculiar part, of man's constitution by which he has
knowledge through the medium or instrumentality of the bodily organs;
the act of apprehending material objects or qualities through the
senses; -- distinguished from <contr>conception</contr>.</def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Matter hath no life nor <qex>perception</qex>, and is not
conscious of its own existence.</q> <rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The quality, state, or capability, of being affected
by something external; sensation; sensibility.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This experiment discovereth <qex>perception</qex> in plants.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An idea; a notion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ldquo/The word <xex>perception</xex> is, in the
language of philosophers previous to <persfn>Reid</persfn>, used in a
very extensive signification. By <persfn>Descartes</persfn>,
<persfn>Malebranche</persfn>, <persfn>Locke</persfn>,
<persfn>Leibnitz</persfn>, and others, it is employed in a sense almost
as unexclusive as <xex>consciousness</xex>, in its widest signification.
By <persfn>Reid</persfn> this word was limited to our faculty
acquisitive of knowledge, and to that branch of this faculty whereby,
through the senses, we obtain a knowledge of the external world. But his
limitation did not stop here. In the act of external perception he
distinguished two elements, to which he gave the names of
<xex>perception</xex> and <xex>sensation</xex>. He ought perhaps to have
called these <xex>perception proper</xex> and <xex>sensation
proper</xex>, when employed in his special meaning.<rdquo/</note>
<rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perceptive</ent><br/
<hw>per*cep"tive</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*s<ecr/p"t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>perceptif</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the act or power of perceiving; having the faculty or
power of perceiving; used in perception.</def> <ldquo/His
<xex>perceptive</xex> and reflective faculties.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Motley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Possessing or exhibiting a high degree of
understanding, insight, intuition, or analytical skill; <as>as, he gave
a <ex>perceptive</ex> analysis of the situation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perceptivity</ent><br/
<hw>per`cep*tiv"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(p<etil/r`s<ecr/p*t<icr/v"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being perceptive; power of perception.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percesoces</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per*ces"o*ces</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>perca</ets> a perch + <ets>esox</ets>,
<ets>-ocis</ets>, a pike.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of
fishes including the gray mullets (<gen>Mugil</gen>), the barracudas,
the silversides, and other related fishes. So called from their relation
both to perches and to pikes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perch</ent><br/
<hw>perch</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pearch</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[OE. <ets>perche</ets>, F.
<ets>perche</ets>, L. <ets>perca</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>pe`rkh</grk>; cf.
<grk>perkno`s</grk> dark-colored, Skr. <ets>p<rsdot/<cced/ni</ets>
spotted, speckled, and E. <ets>freckle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Any fresh-water fish of the genus <gen>Perca</gen> and
of several other allied genera of the family <fam>Percid<ae/</fam>, as
the common American or yellow perch (<spn>Perca flavescens</spn> syn.
<spn>Perca Americana</spn>), and the European perch (<spn>Perca
fluviatilis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any one of numerous species of spiny-finned fishes
belonging to the <fam>Percid<ae/</fam>, <fam>Serranid<ae/</fam>, and
related families, and resembling, more or less, the true
perches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Black perch</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The black
bass</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The flasher</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The sea
bass.</cd> -- <col><b>Blue perch</b></col>, <cd>the cunner.</cd> --
<col><b>Gray perch</b></col>, <cd>the fresh-water drum.</cd> --
<col><b>Red perch</b></col>, <cd>the rosefish.</cd> --
<col><b>Red-bellied perch</b></col>, <cd>the long-eared pondfish.</cd>
-- <col><b>Perch pest</b></col>, <cd>a small crustacean, parasitic in
the mouth of the perch.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver perch</b></col>, <cd>the
yellowtail.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Stone perch</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Striped perch</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the pope.</cd> --
<col><b>White perch</b></col>, <cd>the <spn>Roccus Americanus</spn>,
<it>or</it> <spn>Morone Americanus</spn>, a small silvery serranoid
market fish of the Atlantic coast.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perch</ent><br/
<hw>Perch</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>perche</ets>, L. <ets>pertica</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
pole; a long staff; a rod; esp., a pole or other support for fowls to
roost on or to rest on; a roost; figuratively, any elevated resting
place or seat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As chauntecleer among his wives all<br/
Sat on his <qex>perche</qex>, that was in his hall.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not making his high place the lawless <qex>perch</qex><br/
Of winged ambitions.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A measure of length containing five and
a half yards; a rod, or pole.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In land or square
measure: A square rod; the 160th part of an acre.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>In solid measure: A mass 16<frac12/ feet long, 1 foot in height,
and 1<frac12/ feet in breadth, or 24<frac34/ cubic feet (in local use,
from 22 to 25 cubic feet); -- used in measuring stonework.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pole connecting the fore gear and hind gear of a
spring carriage; a reach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perch</ent><br/
<hw>Perch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Perched</conjf> <pr>(p<etil/rcht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Perching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>percher</ets>.
See <er>Perch</er> a pole.]</ety> <def>To alight or settle, as a bird;
to sit or roost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wrens make prey where eagles dare not <qex>perch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perch</ent><br/
<hw>Perch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place or to set on,
or as on, a perch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To occupy as a perch.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perchance</ent><br/
<hw>Per*chance"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*ch<adot/ns")</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>par</ets> by (L. <ets>per</ets>) + <ets>chance</ets>. See
<er>Par</er>, and <er>Chance</er>.]</ety> <def>By chance; perhaps;
peradventure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perchant</ent><br/
<hw>Perch"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
bird tied by the foot, to serve as decoy to other birds by its
fluttering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percher</ent><br/
<hw>Perch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Perch</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which,
perches.</def> <rj><au>J. Burroughs.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the Insessores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[From <er>Perch</er> a pole.]</ety> <def>A Paris
candle anciently used in England; also, a large wax candle formerly set
upon the altar.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percheron</ent><br/
<hw>Per"che*ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>One of a breed of draught horses originating in <xex>Perche</xex>,
an old district of France; -- called also
<altname>Percheron-Norman</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perchlorate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*chlo"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of perchloric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perchloric</ent><br/
<hw>Per*chlo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>chloric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, the highest oxygen acid
(<chform>HClO4</chform>), of chlorine; -- called also
<altname>hyperchloric</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perchloride</ent><br/
<hw>Per*chlo"ride</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A chloride having a higher proportion of chlorine than any other
chloride of the same substance or series.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perchromic</ent><br/
<hw>Per*chro"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>chromic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, a certain one of the highly oxidized
compounds of chromium, which has a deep blue color, and is produced by
the action of hydrogen peroxide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perciform</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ci*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL., & L.
<ets>perca</ets> a perch + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the <ord>Perciformes</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perciformes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`ci*for"mes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive order of fishes,
including the true perches (<fam>Percid<ae/</fam>); the pondfishes
(<fam>Centrarchid<ae/</fam>); the sci<ae/noids
(<fam>Sci<ae/nid<ae/</fam>); the sparoids (<fam>Sparid<ae/</fam>); the
serranoids (<fam>Serranid<ae/</fam>), and some other related
families.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percipiency</ent><br/
<ent>Percipience</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*cip"i*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per*cip"i*en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The faculty, act or power of
perceiving; perception.</def> <rj><au>Mrs. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percipient</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cip"i*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>percipiens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>percipere</ets>. See <er>Perceive</er>.]</ety> <def>Having the
faculty of perception; perceiving; <as>as, a <ex>percipient</ex>
being</as>.</def> <au>Bentley.</au> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One who,
or that which, is percipient.</def></def2>
<rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perclose</ent><br/
<hw>Per*close"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>parclose</ets> an inclosed place; L. <ets>per</ets> through +
<ets>claudere</ets>, <ets>clausum</ets>, to shut.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Eccl. Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Parclose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Conclusion; end.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percoid</ent><br/
<hw>Per"coid</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"koid)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perca</ets> a perch + <ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>perco<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Belonging to, or
resembling, the perches, or family <fam>Percid<ae/</fam>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>Any fish of the genus <gen>Perca</gen>, or
allied genera of the family <fam>Percid<ae/</fam>; a
percoidean.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per*coi"de*a</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*koi"d<esl/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Perciformes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>percoidean</ent><br/
<hw>per*coid"e*an</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*koi"d<esl/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Any of numerous spiny-finned fishes of the order
<ord>Perciformes</ord>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> percoid fish, percoid.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percolate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"co*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Percolated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Percolating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>percolatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>percolare</ets> to percolate;
<ets>per</ets> through + <ets>colare</ets> to strain.]</ety> <def>To
cause to pass through fine interstices, as a liquor; to filter; to
strain.</def> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percolate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"co*late</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To pass through fine
interstices; to filter; <as>as, water <ex>percolates</ex> through porous
stone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percolation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`co*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>percolatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of percolating, or
filtering; filtration; straining. Specifically <fld>(Pharm.)</fld>, the
process of exhausting the virtues of a powdered drug by letting a liquid
filter slowly through it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percolator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"co*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, filters.</def> <ldquo/[Tissues] act as
<xex>percolators</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Henfrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A type of coffee pot in which the heated water is
driven by its boiling action up above a porous canister of coffee held
above the level of the water in the pot, and then filters (percolates)
back through the coffee thus extracting its essence.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Pharmacy)</fld> <def>An apparatus for producing an
extract from a drug by percolation.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percomorphi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`co*mor"phi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>perca</ets> perch + Gr. <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of fishes including the perches and
related kinds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perculaced</ent><br/
<hw>Per"cu*laced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Prob. corrupt.
fr. <ets>portcullised</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Latticed. See
<er>Lattice</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percurrent</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cur"rent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>percurrens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>percurrere</ets> to run through;
<ets>per</ets> through + <ets>currere</ets> to run.]</ety> <def>Running
through the entire length.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percursory</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cur"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>percursor</ets> one who runs through, fr. <ets>percurrere</ets>.
See <er>Percurrent</er>.]</ety> <def>Running over slightly or in haste;
cursory.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percuss</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cuss"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*k<ucr/s")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Percussed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Percussing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>percussus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>percutere</ets>; <ets>per +
quatere</ets> to shake, strike. See <er>Quash</er>.]</ety> <def>To
strike smartly; to strike upon or against; <as>as, to <ex>percuss</ex>
the chest in medical examination</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Flame <qex>percussed</qex> by air giveth a noise.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percuss</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cuss"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To strike or
tap in an examination by percussion. See <er>Percussion</er>, 3.</def>
<rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percussion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cus"sion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*k<ucr/sh"<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>percussio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>percussion</ets>. See
<er>Percuss</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of percussing, or
striking one body against another; forcible collision, esp. such as
gives a sound or report.</def> <rj><au>Sir I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: The effect of violent collision; vibratory
shock; impression of sound on the ear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The thunderlike <qex>percussion</qex> of thy sounds.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The act of tapping or striking the
surface of the body in order to learn the condition of the parts beneath
by the sound emitted or the sensation imparted to the fingers.
Percussion is said to be <xex>immediate</xex> if the blow is directly
upon the body; if some intervening substance, as a pleximeter, is, used,
it is called <xex>mediate</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Center of percussion</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Center</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Percussion bullet</b></col>, <cd>a
bullet containing a substance which is exploded by percussion; an
explosive bullet.</cd> -- <col><b>Percussion cap</b></col>, <cd>a small
copper cap or cup, containing fulminating powder, and used with a
percussion lock to explode gunpowder.</cd> -- <col><b>Percussion
fuze</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Fuze</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Percussion
lock</b></col>, <cd>the lock of a gun that is fired by percussion upon
fulminating powder.</cd> -- <col><b>Percussion match</b></col>, <cd>a
match which ignites by percussion.</cd> -- <col><b>Percussion
powder</b></col>, <cd>powder so composed as to ignite by slight
percussion; fulminating powder.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Percussion
sieve</b></col>, <col><b>Percussion table</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a
machine for sorting ores by agitation in running water.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>percussion instrument</ent><br/
<hw>per*cus"sion in"stru*ment</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*k<ucr/sh"<ucr/n
<icr/n"str<ucr/*m<ecr/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A musical instrument
which is played by striking, as a drum, cymbal, or xylophone.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>percussionist</ent><br/
<hw>per*cus"sion*ist</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*k<ucr/sh"<ucr/n*<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A musician who plays percussion
instruments.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><note>The world-renowned deaf <ex>percussionist</ex> <person>Evelyn
Glennie</person> prefers to be thought of as a <ex>percussionist</ex>
who happens to be deaf.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percussive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cuss"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Striking against;
percutient; <as>as, <ex>percussive</ex> force</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Percutient</ent><br/
<hw>Per*cu"tient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>percutiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>percutere</ets>. See
<er>Percuss</er>.]</ety> <def>Striking; having the power of
striking.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That which strikes, or has
power to strike.</def></def2> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdicine</ent><br/
<hw>Per"di*cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Perdix</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
family <fam>Perdicid<ae/</fam>, or partridges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdie</ent><br/
<hw>Per*die"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parde</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Per diem</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per di"em</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>By the day;
substantively (chiefly U. S.), an allowance or amount of so much by the
day. Also used adjectivally; <as>as, a <ex>per diem</ex>
allowance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdifoil</ent><br/
<hw>Per"di*foil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perdere</ets> to lose + <ets>folium</ets> leaf.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A deciduous plant; -- opposed to
<ant>evergreen</ant>.</def> <rj><au>J. Barton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdition</ent><br/
<hw>Per*di"tion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<icr/sh"<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>perditio</ets>, fr. <ets>perdere</ets>,
<ets>perditum</ets>, to ruin, to lose; <ets>per</ets> (cf. Skr.
<ets>par<amac/</ets> away) + <ets>-dere</ets> (only in comp.) to put;
akin to Gr. <grk>tiqe`nai</grk>, E. <ets>do</ets>. See
<er>Do</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Entire loss; utter destruction;
ruin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mere <qex>perdition</qex> of the Turkish fleet.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(religion)</fld> <def>Damnation; the utter loss of
the soul, or of final happiness in a future state; future misery or
eternal death.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If we reject the truth, we seal our own <qex>perdition</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>J. M. Mason.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Loss or diminution.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hell; hades; <as>as, on the path to
<ex>perdition</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perditionable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*di"tion*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being ruined; worthy of perdition.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Pollok.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdix</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per"dix</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"d<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[L., a partridge, Gr. <grk>pe`rdix</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A genus of birds including the common European partridge. Formerly
the word was used in a much wider sense to include many allied
genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdu</ent><br/
<hw>Per*du"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/" <it>or</it>
p<etil/r"d<usl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Perdu</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One placed on watch, or in
ambush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A soldier sent on a forlorn hope.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdue</ent><br/
<ent>Perdu</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*du"</hw>, <hw>Per*due"</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/" <it>or</it> p<etil/r"d<usl/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>perdu</ets>, f. <ets>perdue</ets>, lost, p. p. of
<ets>perdre</ets> to lose, L. <ets>perdere</ets>. See
<er>Perdition</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lost to view; in concealment
or ambush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He should lie <qex>perdue</qex> who is to walk the round.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Accustomed to, or employed in, desperate
enterprises; hence, reckless; hopeless.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>perdue</xex>
captain.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perduellion</ent><br/
<hw>Per`du*el"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perduellio</ets>; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>duellum</ets>,
<ets>bellum</ets>, war.]</ety> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld>
<def>Treason.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdulous</ent><br/
<hw>Per"du*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Perdu</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Lost; thrown away.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Abp. Bramhall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdurability</ent><br/
<hw>Per*dur`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Durability;
lastingness.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdurable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*dur"a*ble</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/r"<adot/*b'l; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>perdurable</ets>, OE.
<ets>pardurable</ets>. See <er>Perdure</er>.]</ety> <def>Very durable;
lasting; continuing long.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per*dur"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perduration</ent><br/
<ent>Perdurance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*dur"ance</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/r"<ait/ns)</pr>,
<hw>Per`du*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`d<usl/*r<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Long continuance.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perdurantism</ent><br/
<hw>per*dur"ant*ism</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/r"<ait/nt*<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Philosophy)</fld> <def>The philosophical view
considering the fundamental objects of the real world as extended in the
direction of time, so that the objects themselves do not change, though
temporal parts may have different properties. Contrasted to
<contr>endurantism</contr>, a view considering objects to be wholly
present at each instant of time.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perdurantist</ent><br/
<hw>per*dur"ant*ist</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/r"<ait/nt*<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Philosophy)</fld> <def>A philosopher who considers
the fundamental objects of the real world as extended in the direction
of time, so that the objects themselves do not change, though temporal
parts may have different properties. Contrasted to
<contr>endurantist</contr>, one who considers objects to be wholly
present at each instant.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdure</ent><br/
<hw>Per*dure"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*d<umac/r")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>perdurare</ets>; <ets>per</ets> through +
<ets>durare</ets> to last.]</ety> <def>To last or endure for a long
time; to be perdurable or lasting.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind <qex>perdures</qex> while its energizing may construct a
thousand lines.</q> <rj><qau>Hickok.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perdy</ent><br/
<hw>Per*dy"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Truly. See
<er>Parde</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ah, dame! <qex>perdy</qex> ye have not done me right.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pere</ent><br/
<hw>Pere</hw> <pr>(p<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A peer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pere</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>P<egrave/re</hw> <pr>(p<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. L. <ets>pater</ets>. See <er>Father</er>.]</ety> <def>Father; --
often used after French proper names to distinguish a father from his
son; <as>as, Dumas <ex>p<egrave/re</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregal</ent><br/
<hw>Per*e"gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>par</ets>
very (L. <ets>per</ets>) + <ets>egal</ets> equal, L.
<ets>aequalis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Fully equal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Peregal</xex> to the best.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregrinate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"e*gri*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peregrinatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>peregrinari</ets> to travel. See
<er>Pilgrim</er>.]</ety> <def>To travel from place to place, or from one
country to another; hence, to sojourn in foreign countries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregrinate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"e*gri*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peregrinatus</ets>, p. p.]</ety> <def>Having traveled;
foreign.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregrination</ent><br/
<hw>Per`e*gri*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peregrinatio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/r<eacute/grination</ets>.]</ety> <def>A traveling from one
country to another; a wandering; sojourn in foreign countries.</def>
<ldquo/His <xex>peregrination</xex> abroad.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregrinator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"e*gri*na`tor</hw>
<pr>(p<ecr/r"<esl/*gr<icr/*n<amac/`t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>One who peregrinates; one who travels
about.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregrine</ent><br/
<hw>Per"e*grine</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r"<esl/*gr<icr/n; 277)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>peregrinus</ets>. See
<er>Pilgrim</er>.]</ety> <def>Foreign; not native; extrinsic or from
without; exotic.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also <asp>pelegrine</asp>.]</altsp>
<ldquo/<xex>Peregrine</xex> and preternatural heat.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peregrine falcon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
courageous and swift falcon (<spn>Falco peregrinus</spn>), remarkable
for its wide distribution over all the continents. The adult plumage is
dark bluish ash on the back, nearly black on the head and cheeks, white
beneath, barred with black below the throat. Called also
<altname>peregrine hawk</altname>, <altname>duck hawk</altname>,
<altname>game hawk</altname>, and <altname>great-footed
hawk</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1065 --></p>

<p><ent>Peregrine</ent><br/
<hw>Per"e*grine</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/"<esl/*gr<icr/n; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The peregrine falcon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peregrinity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`e*grin"i*ty</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r`<esl/*gr<icr/n"<icr/t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>peregrinitas</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/r<eacute/grinit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Foreignness; strangeness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Somewhat
of a <xex>peregrinity</xex> in their dialect.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Travel; wandering.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perel</ent><br/
<hw>Per"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Apparel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perempt</ent><br/
<hw>Per*empt"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peremptus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perimere</ets> to take away
entirely, to destroy; <ets>per</ets> (see <er>Per-</er>) + OL.
<ets>emere</ets> to take. See <er>Redeem</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>To destroy; to defeat.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peremption</ent><br/
<hw>Per*emp"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peremptio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/remption</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A quashing; a defeating.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peremptorily</ent><br/
<hw>Per"emp*to*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
peremptory manner; absolutely; positively.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peremptoriness</ent><br/
<hw>Per"emp*to*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
peremptory; positiveness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peremptory</ent><br/
<hw>Per"emp*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peremptorius</ets> destructive, deadly, decisive, final: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/remptorie</ets>. See <er>Perempt</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Precluding debate or expostulation; not admitting of question or
appeal; positive; absolute; decisive; conclusive; final.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Think of heaven with hearty purposes and <qex>peremptory</qex>
designs to get thither.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Positive in opinion or judgment; decided;
dictatorial; dogmatical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be not too positive and <qex>peremptory</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Briefly, then, for we are <qex>peremptory</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Firmly determined; unawed.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peremptory challenge</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Challenge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Peremptory mandamus</b></col>,
<cd>a final and absolute mandamus.</cd> -- <col><b>Peremptory
plea</b></col>, <cd>a plea by a defendant tending to impeach the
plaintiff's right of action; a plea in bar.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Decisive; positive; absolute; authoritative;
express; arbitrary; dogmatical.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perennate</ent><br/
<hw>perennate</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To survive from season to
season, of plants.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perennial</ent><br/
<hw>Per*en"ni*al</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r*<ecr/n"n<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perennis</ets> that lasts the whole year
through; <ets>per</ets> through + <ets>annus</ets> year. See
<er>Per-</er>, and <er>Annual</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lasting or
continuing through the year; <as>as, <ex>perennial</ex>
fountains</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Continuing without cessation or intermission;
perpetual; unceasing; never failing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>perennial</qex> existence of bodies corporate.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Continuing more than two years;
<as>as, a <ex>perennial</ex> steam, or root, or plant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Perpetual; unceasing; never failing; enduring;
continual; permanent; uninterrupted.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perennial</ent><br/
<hw>Per*en"ni*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A perennial
plant; a plant which lives or continues more than two years, whether it
retains its leaves in winter or not.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perennially</ent><br/
<hw>Per*en"ni*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perennial
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perennibranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per*en`ni*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Perennial</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Those Batrachia which retain their gills through
life, as the menobranchus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perennibranchiate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*en`ni*bran"chi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Perennial</er>, and <er>Branchiate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having branch<ae/, or gills, through life; --
said especially of certain Amphibia, like the menobranchus. Opposed to
<contr>caducibranchiate</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the
Perennibranchiata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perennity</ent><br/
<hw>Per*en"ni*ty</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r*<ecr/n"n<icr/*y<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perennitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality of
being perennial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Derham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pererration</ent><br/
<hw>Per`er*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pererrare</ets>, <ets>pererratum</ets>, to wander through.]</ety>
<def>A wandering, or rambling, through various places.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfect</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fect</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<ecr/kt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>parfit</ets>, OF. <ets>parfit</ets>, <ets>parfet</ets>,
<ets>parfait</ets>, F. <ets>parfait</ets>, L. <ets>perfectus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>perficere</ets> to carry to the end, to perform, finish,
perfect; <ets>per</ets> (see <er>Per-</er>) + <ets>facere</ets> to make,
do. See <er>Fact</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Brought to consummation
or completeness; completed; not defective nor redundant; having all the
properties or qualities requisite to its nature and kind; without flaw,
fault, or blemish; without error; mature; whole; pure; sound; right;
correct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My strength is made <qex>perfect</qex> in weakness.</q> <rj><qau>2
Cor. xii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Three glorious suns, each one a <qex>perfect</qex> sun.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I fear I am not in my <qex>perfect</qex> mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O most entire <qex>perfect</qex> sacrifice!</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God made thee <qex>perfect</qex>, not immutable.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Well informed; certain; sure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am <qex>perfect</qex> that the Pannonians are now in arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Hermaphrodite; having both stamens
and pistils; -- said of a flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Perfect cadence</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a
complete and satisfactory close in the harmony, as upon the tonic
preceded by the dominant.</cd> -- <col><b>Perfect chord</b></col>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a concord or union of sounds which is perfectly
coalescent and agreeable to the ear, as the unison, octave, fifth, and
fourth; a perfect consonance; a common chord in its original position of
keynote, third, fifth, and octave.</cd> -- <col><b>Perfect
number</b></col> <fld>(Arith.)</fld>, <cd>a number equal to the sum of
all its divisors; as, 28, whose aliquot parts, or divisors, are 14, 7,
4, 2, 1. See <cref>Abundant number</cref>, under <er>Abundant</er>.</cd>
<au>Brande & C.</au> -- <col><b>Perfect tense</b></col>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>a tense which expresses an act or state
completed; also called the <altname>perfective
tense</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Finished; consummate; complete; entire;
faultless; blameless; unblemished.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfect</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fect</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<ecr/kt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
perfect tense, or a form in that tense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfect</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fect</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<ecr/kt" or p<etil/r"f<ecr/kt;
277)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Perfected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perfecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>perfectus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>perficere</ets>. See <er>Perfect</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To make perfect; to finish or complete, so as to leave nothing
wanting; to give to anything all that is requisite to its nature and
kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God dwelleth in us, and his love is <qex>perfect</qex> in us.</q>
<rj><qau>1 John iv. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Inquire into the nature and properties of the things, . . . and
thereby <qex>perfect</qex> our ideas of their distinct species.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Perfecting press</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a
press in which the printing on both sides of the paper is completed in
one passage through the machine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To finish; accomplish; complete;
consummate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perfectability</ent><br/
<ent>perfectibility</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>perfectability</hw>, <hw>perfectibility</hw></mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The capability of becoming perfect; <as>as, he
believes in the ultimate <ex>perfectability</ex> of man</as>; -- usually
spelled <ex>perfectibility</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perfected</ent><br/
<hw>perfected</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Brought into final
form; completely formed; -- of plans, ideas, etc.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Refined to a state of excellence; -- of e.g. skills
or the products of skills.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> finished.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfecter</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fect*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, makes perfect.</def> <ldquo/The . . . <xex>perfecter</xex> of our
faith.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectibilian</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fect`i*bil"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
perfectionist.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Ed. Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectibilist</ent><br/
<hw>Per`fec*tib"i*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
perfectionist. See also <er>Illuminati</er>, 2.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectibility</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fect`i*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perfectibilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being perfectible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectible</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fect"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perfectible</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of becoming, or being made,
perfect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfection</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>perfection</ets>, L. <ets>perfectio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being perfect or complete, so that nothing
requisite is wanting; entire development; consummate culture, skill, or
moral excellence; the highest attainable state or degree of excellence;
maturity; <as>as, <ex>perfection</ex> in an art, in a science, or in a
system; <ex>perfection</ex> in form or degree; fruits in
<ex>perfection</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A quality, endowment, or acquirement completely
excellent; an ideal faultlessness; especially, the divine attribute of
complete excellence.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What tongue can her <qex>perfections</qex> tell?</q> <rj><qau>Sir
P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To perfection</b></col>, <cd>in the highest degree of
excellence; perfectly; as, to imitate a model <xex>to
perfection</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfection</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To perfect.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foote.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectional</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to perfection; characterized by perfection.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectionate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
perfect.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectionism</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The doctrine of the Perfectionists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A personal attitude which demands perfection in the
results of actions.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectionist</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One pretending
to perfection; esp., one pretending to moral perfection; one who
believes that persons may and do attain to moral perfection and
sinlessness in this life.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person who insists on perfect results from himself
or from others, in any endeavor.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectionment</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tion*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perfectionnement</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of bringing to
perfection, or the state of having attained to perfection.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>I. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfective</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fect"ive</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<ecr/k"t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Tending or conducing to make perfect, or to bring to
perfection; -- usually followed by <xex>of</xex>.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>perfective</xex> alteration.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Actions <qex>perfective</qex> of their natures.</q>
<rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Denoting an aspect of verbs which
indicates completion of an action, and is sometimes explicitly marked in
verbal inflection, as in the Russian language; <as>as, the
<ex>perfective</ex> form of a verb</as>. The use of a perfective form
rather than the simple past indicates reference to a completed event,
rather than to a process that took place in the past.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perfective</ent><br/
<hw>per*fect"ive</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<ecr/k"t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The perfective case or
aspect.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A verb in the perfective case or
aspect.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectively</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fec"tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perfective
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectly</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fect*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perfect
manner or degree; in or to perfection; completely; wholly; thoroughly;
faultlessly.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Perfectly</xex> divine.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As many as touched were made <qex>perfectly</qex> whole.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xiv. 36.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfectness</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fect*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
perfect; perfection.</def> <ldquo/Charity, which is the bond of
<xex>perfectness</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Col. iii. 14.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfervid</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fer"vid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>fervid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Very fervid; too
fervid; glowing; ardent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perficient</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fi"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perficiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>perficere</ets> to perform. See
<er>Perfect</er>.]</ety> <def>Making or doing thoroughly; efficient;
effectual.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perficient</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fi"cient</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who performs or perfects a
work; especially, one who endows a charity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfidious</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fid"i*ous</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<icr/d"<icr/*<ucr/s; 277)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perfidious</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Guilty of perfidy; violating good faith or vows; false to trust or
confidence reposed; treacherous; faithless; <as>as, a
<ex>perfidious</ex> friend</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Involving, or characterized by, perfidy.</def>
<ldquo/Involved in this <xex>perfidious</xex> fraud.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfidiously</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fid"i*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perfidious
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfidiousness</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fid"i*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
perfidious; perfidy.</def> <rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfidy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fi*dy</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<icr/*d<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Perfidies</plw>
<pr>(p<etil/r"f<icr/*d<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>perfidia</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>perfidus</ets> faithless; <ets>per</ets> (cf. Skr.
<ets>par<amac/</ets> away) + <ets>fides</ets> faith: cf. F.
<ets>perfidie</ets>. See <er>Faith</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
violating faith or allegiance; violation of a promise or vow, or of
trust reposed; faithlessness; treachery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ambition and <qex>perfidy</qex> of tyrants.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>perfidy</qex> to this sacred engagement.</q>
<rj><qau>DeQuincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfit</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fit</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<icr/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Perfect.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfix</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fix"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>per-</ets> + <ets>fix</ets>.]</ety> <def>To fix surely;
to appoint.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perflable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fla*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perflabilis</ets>. See <er>Perflate</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being blown through.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perflate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*flate"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perflatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perflare</ets> to blow
through.]</ety> <def>To blow through.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perflation</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fla"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perflatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of perflating.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfoliate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fo"li*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + L. <ets>folium</ets> leaf.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the basal part produced around the stem;
-- said of leaves which the stem apparently passes directory
through.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Surrounded by a circle of hairs,
or projections of any kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforata</ent><br/
<hw>Per`fo*ra"ta</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`f<osl/*r<amac/"t<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See <er>Perforate</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A division of corals including
those that have a porous texture, as Porites and Madrepora; -- opposed
to <contr>Aporosa</contr>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A division of
Foraminifera, including those having perforated shells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fo*rate</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<osl/*r<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perforated</conjf>
<pr>(p<etil/r"f<osl/*r<amac/`t<ecr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perforating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>perforatus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>perforare</ets> to perforate; <ets>per</ets> through +
<ets>forare</ets> to bore. See <er>Bore</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To bore through; to pierce through with a pointed instrument; to
make a hole or holes through by boring or piercing; to pierce or
penetrate the surface of.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforated</ent><br/
<ent>Perforate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per"fo*rate</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<osl/*r<asl/t)</pr>,
<hw>Per"fo*ra`ted</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<osl/*r<amac/"t<ecr/d)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pierced with a hole or holes, or with pores;
having transparent dots resembling holes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`fo*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perforation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of perforating,
or of boring or piercing through.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hole made by boring or piercing; an
aperture.</def> <ldquo/Slender <xex>perforations</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforative</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fo*ra*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perforatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having power to perforate or
pierce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fo*ra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perforateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, perforates;
esp., a cephalotome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforce</ent><br/
<hw>Per*force"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>par</ets> (L. <ets>per</ets>) + <ets>force</ets>.]</ety> <def>By
force; of necessity; at any rate.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perforce</ent><br/
<hw>Per*force"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To force; to compel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perform</ent><br/
<hw>Per*form"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Performed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Performing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>performen</ets>, <ets>parfourmen</ets>, <ets>parfournen</ets>, OF.
<ets>parfornir</ets>, <ets>parfournir</ets>, to finish, complete; OF. &
F. <ets>par</ets> (see <er>Par</er>) + <ets>fournir</ets> to finish,
complete. The word has been influenced by <ets>form</ets>; cf. L.
<ets>performare</ets> to form thoroughly. See <er>Furnish</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To carry through; to bring to completion; to achieve;
to accomplish; to execute; to do.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will cry unto God most high, unto God that <qex>performeth</qex>
all things for me.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. lvii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great force to <qex>perform</qex> what they did attempt.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To discharge; to fulfill; to act up to; <as>as, to
<ex>perform</ex> a duty; to <ex>perform</ex> a promise or a
vow.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>perform</qex> your father's will.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To represent; to act; to play; as in
drama.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Perform</qex> a part thou hast not done before.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To accomplish; do; act; transact; achieve;
execute; discharge; fulfill; effect; complete; consummate. See
<er>Accomplish</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perform</ent><br/
<hw>Per*form"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To do, execute, or accomplish
something; to acquit one's self in any business; esp., to represent
sometimes by action; to act a part; to play on a musical instrument;
<as>as, the players <ex>perform</ex> poorly; the musician
<ex>performs</ex> on the organ.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Performable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*form"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Admitting of
being performed, done, or executed; practicable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Performance</ent><br/
<hw>Per*form"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
performing; the carrying into execution or action; execution;
achievement; accomplishment; representation by action; <as>as, the
<ex>performance</ex> of an undertaking of a duty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Promises are not binding where the <qex>performance</qex> is
impossible.</q> <rj><qau>Paley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is performed or accomplished; a thing
done or carried through; an achievement; a deed; an act; a feat; esp.,
an action of an elaborate or public character.</def> <ldquo/Her walking
and other actual <xex>performances</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/His musical <xex>performances</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Completion; consummation; execution;
accomplishment; achievement; production; work; act; action; deed;
exploit; feat.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Performer</ent><br/
<hw>Per*form"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who performs,
accomplishes, or fulfills; <as>as, a good promiser, but a bad
<ex>performer</ex></as>; especially, one who shows skill and training in
any art; <as>as, a <ex>performer</ex> of the drama; a <ex>performer</ex>
on the harp.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfricate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fri*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perfricatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perfricare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
rub over.</def> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfumatory</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fu"ma*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Emitting
perfume; perfuming.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir E.
Leigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfume</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fume"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<umac/m")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perfumed</conjf>
<pr>(p<etil/r*f<umac/md")</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perfuming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>parfumer</ets> (cf.
Sp. <ets>perfumar</ets>); <ets>par</ets> (see <er>Par</er>) +
<ets>fumer</ets> to smoke, L. <ets>fumare</ets>, fr. <ets>fumus</ets>
smoke. See <er>Fume</er>.]</ety> <def>To fill or impregnate with a
perfume; to scent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Carmel's flowery top <qex>perfumes</qex> the skies.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfume</ent><br/
<hw>Per"fume</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<umac/m or p<etil/r*f<umac/m";
277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>parfum</ets>; cf. Sp.
<ets>perfume</ets>. See <er>Perfume</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The scent, odor, or odoriferous particles emitted from
a sweet-smelling substance; a pleasant odor; fragrance; aroma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No rich <qex>perfumes</qex> refresh the fruitful
<qex>field</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A substance that emits an agreeable odor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And thou shalt make it a <qex>perfume</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxx.
35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perfumed</ent><br/
<hw>per"fumed</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"f<umac/md)</pr> <pos>adj.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Filled or impregnated with perfume; <as>as,
<ex>perfumed</ex> stationery; a <ex>perfumed</ex>
boudoir</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> scented.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a natural fragrance; <as>as, the
<ex>perfumed</ex> air of June</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> odoriferous, odorous, scented, sweet, sweet-scented,
sweet-smelling.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfumer</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fum"er</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<umac/m"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, perfumes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One whose trade is to make or sell
perfumes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfumery</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fum"er*y</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<umac/m"<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Perfumes, in general.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>parfumerie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art
of preparing perfumes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfunctorily</ent><br/
<hw>Per*func"to*ri*ly</hw>
<pr>(p<etil/r*f<ucr/<nsm/k"t<osl/*r<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a perfunctory manner; formally; carelessly.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfunctoriness</ent><br/
<hw>Per*func"to*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being perfunctory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfunctory</ent><br/
<hw>Per*func"to*ry</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<ucr/<nsm/k"t<osl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perfunctorius</ets>, fr.
<ets>perfunctus</ets> dispatched, p. p. of <ets>perfungi</ets> to
discharge, dispatch; <ets>per</ets> (see <er>Per</er>) +
<ets>fungi</ets> to perform. See <er>Function</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Done merely to get rid of a duty; performed mechanically and as a
thing of rote; done in a careless and superficial manner; characterized
by indifference; <as>as, <ex>perfunctory</ex> admonitions; a
<ex>perfunctory</ex> greeting</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Mechanical; indifferent; listless;
careless.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Perfunctory</xex> in his devotions.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sharp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfuncturate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*func"tu*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To perform
in a perfunctory manner; to do negligently.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfuse</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fuse"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*f<umac/z")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perfused</conjf>
<pr>(p<etil/r*f<umac/zd")</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perfusing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>perfusus</ets>, p. p.
of <ets>perfundere</ets> to pour over; <ets>per + fundere</ets> to
pour.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To suffuse; to fill full or to
excess.</def> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass a liquid through or over; <as>as, the
isolated kidney was <ex>perfused</ex> with saline
solution</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Surgery)</fld> <def>To pass (a liquid) through an
organ.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfusion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perfusio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of perfusing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perfusive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*fu"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a nature to
flow over, or to spread through.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pergamentaceous</ent><br/
<ent>Pergamenous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`ga*me"no*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Per`ga*men*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pergamena</ets> parchment. See <er>Parchment</er>.]</ety>
<def>Like parchment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pergola</ent><br/
<hw>Per"go*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>pergula</ets> shed, shop, vine arbor.]</ety> <def>Lit., an arbor or
bower;</def> <specif>specif.:</specif> <fld>(Italian art)</fld> <def>An
arbor or trellis treated architecturally, as with stone columns or
similar massive structure.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pergolo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per"go*lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>A continuous colonnade or arcade; -- applied to the decorative
groups of windows, as in Venetian palazzi.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perhaps</ent><br/
<hw>Per*haps"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*h<acr/ps")</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Per</ets> + <ets>hap</ets> chance.]</ety> <def>Possibly; by
chance; peradventure; perchance; it may be.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And pray God, if <qex>perhaps</qex> the thought of thine heart may
be forgiven thee.</q> <rj><qau>Acts viii. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peri-</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i-</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r"<icr/-)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>peri`</grk>,
prep.]</ety> <def>A prefix used to signify <sig>around</sig>,
<sig>by</sig>, <sig>near</sig>, <sig>over</sig>, <sig>beyond</sig>, or
to give an <xex>intensive</xex> sense; <as>as, <ex>peri</ex>meter, the
measure around; <ex>peri</ex>gee, point near the earth;
<ex>peri</ex>ergy, work beyond what is needed; <ex>peri</ex>spherical,
quite spherical.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peri</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ri</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"r<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Peris</plw> <pr>(p<emac/"r<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Per. <ets>per<imac/</ets> a female genus, a fairy.]</ety>
<fld>(Persian Myth.)</fld> <def>An imaginary being, male or female, like
an elf or fairy, represented as a descendant of fallen angels, excluded
from paradise till penance is accomplished.</def>
<rj><au>Moore.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1066 --></p>

<p><ent>Periagua</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*a"gua</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r`<icr/*<aum/"gw<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Pirogue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perianth</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*anth</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r"<icr/*<acr/nth)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'anqos</grk> flower: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/rianthe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The leaves of a flower generally, especially when the calyx and
corolla are not readily distinguished.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A saclike
involucre which incloses the young fruit in most hepatic mosses. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Hepatica</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perianthium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*an"thi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The perianth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periapt</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*apt</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r"<icr/*<acr/pt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>peri`apton</grk>, fr. <grk>peri`aptos</grk> hung about,
<grk>peria`ptein</grk> to hang about; <grk>peri`</grk> about +
<grk>'a`ptein</grk> to tie: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/riapte</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A charm worn as a protection against disease or mischief; an
amulet.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now help, ye charming spells and <qex>periapts</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periastral</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*as"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Among or around
the stars.</def> <ldquo/Comets in <xex>periastral</xex> passage.<rdquo/
<rj><au>R. A. Proctor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periastron</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*as"tron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>peri`</grk> about + <grk>'a`stron</grk> a star.]</ety>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>That point, in the real or apparent orbit of
one star revolving around another, at which the former is nearest to the
latter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periauger</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*au"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pirogue</er>.</def> <rj><au>W. Irving.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periblast</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*blast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>periblasta`nein</grk> to grow around. See <er>Peri-</er>, and
<er>-blast</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The protoplasmic matter
which surrounds the entoblast, or cell nucleus, and undergoes
segmentation.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Per`i*blas"tic</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periblem</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*blem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + root of Gr. <?/ to sprout.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Nascent cortex, or immature cellular bark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peribolos</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*rib"o*los</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Nl., fr. Gr.
<grk>peri`bolos</grk>, fr. <grk>peri`bolos</grk>, adj., going round, fr.
<grk>periba`llein</grk> to throw round; cf. L.
<ets>peribolus</ets>.]</ety> <def>In ancient architecture, an inclosed
court, esp., one surrounding a temple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peribranchial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*bran"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Surrounding the branchi<ae/; <as>as, a
<ex>peribranchial</ex> cavity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peribronchial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*bron"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Around the bronchi or bronchial tubes; <as>as,
the <ex>peribronchial</ex> lymphatics</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericambium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*cam"bi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Cambium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
layer of thin-walled young cells in a growing stem, in which layer
certain new vessels originate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericardial</ent><br/
<ent>Pericardiac</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*car"di*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*car"di*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to pericardium; situated around the heart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pericardial fluid</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>a
serous fluid of a pale yellow color contained in the
pericardium.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericardian</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*car"di*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pericardiac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericardic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*car"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pericardiac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericarditus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*car*di"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pericardium</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the pericardium.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericardium</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*car"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>perika`rdion</grk>, fr. <grk>perika`rdios</grk> about or near the
heart; <grk>peri`</grk> about + <grk>kardi`a</grk> heart.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The double baglike fold of serous membrane which
incloses the heart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The inner layer is closely adherent to the outer surface
of the heart, and is called the <part>cardiac pericardium</part>. The
outer layer loosely incloses the heart and the adherent inner layer, and
is called the <part>parietal pericardium</part>. At the base of the
heart the two layers are continuous, and form a narrow closed cavity
filled with fluid, in which the pulsations of the heart cause little
friction.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericarp</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>perika`rpion</grk>; <grk>peri`</grk> around + <grk>karpo`s</grk>
fruit: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/ricarpe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The ripened ovary; the walls of the fruit. See <xex>Illusts.</xex>
of <er>Capsule</er>, <er>Drupe</er>, and <er>Legume</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericarpic</ent><br/
<ent>Pericarpial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*car"pi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*car"pic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to a pericarp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericellular</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*cel"lu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding a cell; <as>as, the <ex>pericellular</ex> lymph spaces
surrounding ganglion cells</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichaeth</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*chaeth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Perichaetium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The leafy involucre
surrounding the fruit stalk of mosses; perichaetium;
perichete.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichaetial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*chae"ti*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the perich<ae/th.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichaetium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*chae"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Perichaetia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. about + <?/ flowing hair, foliage.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Perich<ae/th</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichaetous</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*ch<ae/"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Perich<ae/tium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Surrounded by
set<ae/; -- said of certain earthworms (genus
<gen>Perich<ae/tus</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichete</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*chete</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Perich<ae/th</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichondrial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*chon"dri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the perichondrium; situated
around cartilage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichondritis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*chon*dri"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Perichondrium</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the perichondrium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichondrium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*chon"dri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> around + <grk>cho`ndros</grk> cartilage.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The membrane of fibrous connective tissue which
closely invests cartilage, except where covering articular
surfaces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perichordal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*chor"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Around the
notochord; <as>as, a <ex>perichordal</ex> column</as>. See
<er>Epichordal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periclasite</ent><br/
<ent>Periclase</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per"i*clase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*cla"site</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>peri-</ets> + Gr.
<?/ to break.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A grayish or dark green
mineral, consisting essentially of magnesia (magnesium oxide), occurring
in granular forms or in isometric crystals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periclinium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*clin"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Periclinia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ a bed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The involucre which surrounds the common receptacle in composite
flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periclitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ric"li*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>periclitatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>periclitari</ets>, fr.
<ets>periculum</ets>.]</ety> <def>To endanger.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Periclitating</qex>, pardi! the whole family.</q>
<rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periclitation</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ric`li*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>periclitatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/riclitation</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Trial; experiment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being in peril.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericope</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*ric"o*pe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., section
of a book, Gr. <?/; <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ to cut.]</ety> <def>A
selection or extract from a book; especially <fld>(Theol.)</fld>, a
selection from the Bible, appointed to be read in the churches or used
as a text for a sermon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericranial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*cra"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the pericranium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericranium</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*cra"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The periosteum which covers the cranium
externally; the region around the cranium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periculous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ric"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>periculosus</ets>. See <er>Perilous</er>.]</ety> <def>Dangerous;
full of peril.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periculum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*ric"u*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pericula</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. &
O.Eng. Law)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Danger; risk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a narrower, judicial sense: Accident or
<xex>casus</xex>, as distinguished from <xex>dolus</xex> and
<xex>culpa</xex>, and hence relieving one from the duty of performing an
obligation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pericystitis</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*cys*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Cystitis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the tissues surrounding the bladder.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periderm</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The outer layer of bark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The hard outer covering of
hydroids and other marine animals; the perisarc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peridiastole</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*di*as"to*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The almost inappreciable time which elapses
between the systole and the diastole of the heart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peridium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*rid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Peridia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>peri`</grk> about + <?/, a dim. ending.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The envelope or coat of certain fungi, such as the puffballs and
earthstars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peridot</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*dot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/ridot</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Chrysolite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peridotite</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*do*tite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ridotite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>An eruptive
rock characterized by the presence of chrysolite (peridot). It also
usually contains pyroxene, enstatite, chromite, etc. It is often altered
to serpentine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The chief diamond deposits in South Africa occur in a
more or less altered peridotite.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peridrome</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*drome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
running around, fr. <?/ to run round; <grk>peri`</grk> round + <?/ to
run: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/ridrome</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch<ae/ol.)</fld>
<def>The space between the columns and the wall of the cella, in a Greek
or a Roman temple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periecians</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*e"cians</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Peri<oe/cians</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perienteron</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*en"te*ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Enteron</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
primitive perivisceral cavity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periergy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*er`gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
overcareful; <grk>peri`</grk> about, beyond + <?/ work.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Excessive care or diligence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A bombastic or labored
style.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periganglionic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*gan`gli*on"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Surrounding a ganglion; <as>as, the
<ex>periganglionic</ex> glands of the frog</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigastric</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*gas"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding the stomach; -- applied to the body cavity of Bryozoa
and various other Invertebrata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigean</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*ge"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
perigee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Perigean tides</b></col>, <cd>those spring tides which
occur soon after the moon passes her perigee.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigeum</ent><br/
<ent>Perigee</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per"i*gee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*ge"um</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>perigeum</ets>, fr.
Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> about, near + <grk>gh^</grk> the earth: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/rig<eacute/e</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>That
point, in the orbit of the moon or other body orbiting the earth, which
is nearest to the earth; -- opposed to <ant>apogee</ant>. It is
sometimes, but rarely, used of the nearest points of bodies not orbiting
the earth, such as of a comet, a planet, etc. Called also
<altname>epigee</altname>, <altname>epigeum</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>A theory which explains inheritance by the transmission of the type
of growth force possessed by one generation to another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*gen"e*tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to perigenesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigone</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*gone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/ productive organs.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any organ inclosing the essential
organs of a flower; a perianth.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In mosses, the
involucral bracts of a male flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sac which surrounds the
generative bodies in the gonophore of a hydroid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigonium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*go"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Perigonia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Perigone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigord pie</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*gord pie`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From
<ets>P<eacute/rigord</ets>, a former province of France.]</ety> <def>A
pie made of truffles, much esteemed by epicures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigraph</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ outline;
<?/ round, about + <?/ to write.]</ety> <def>A careless or inaccurate
delineation of anything.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigynium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*gyn"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Perigynia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Perigynous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Some unusual
appendage about the pistil, as the bottle-shaped body in the sedges, and
the bristles or scales in some other genera of the Sedge family, or
<fam>Cyperace<ae/</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perigynous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rig"y*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/ woman.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having
the ovary free, but the petals and stamens borne on the calyx; -- said
of a flower such as that of the cherry or peach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perihelium</ent><br/
<ent>Perihelion</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*hel"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*he"li*um</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Perihelia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>peri`</grk> about, near + <grk>"h`lios</grk> the sun.]</ety>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>That point of the orbit of a planet or comet
which is nearest to the sun; -- opposed to
<ant>aphelion</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peril</ent><br/
<hw>Per"il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/ril</ets>, fr. L. <ets>periculum</ets>,
<ets>periclum</ets>, akin to <ets>peritus</ets> experienced, skilled,
and E. <ets>fare</ets>. See <er>Fare</er>, and cf.
<er>Experience</er>.]</ety> <def>Danger; risk; hazard; jeopardy;
exposure of person or property to injury, loss, or
destruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>perils</qex> of waters, in <qex>perils</qex> of
robbers.</q> <rj><qau>2 Cor. xi. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Adventure hard<br/
With <qex>peril</qex> great achieved.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>At one's peril</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>On
one's peril</b></col></mcol>, <cd>with risk or danger to one; at the
hazard of.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>On thy</xex> soul's <xex>peril</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Hazard; risk; jeopardy. See
<er>Danger</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peril</ent><br/
<hw>Per"il</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Periled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Perilled</conjf>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Periling</conjf> or
<conjf>Perilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To expose to danger; to hazard;
to risk; <as>as, to <ex>peril</ex> one's life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peril</ent><br/
<hw>Per"il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be in
danger.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*ril"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of labiate herbs, of
which one species (<spn>Perilla ocimoides</spn>, or <spn>Perilla
Nankinensis</spn>) is often cultivated for its purple or variegated
foliage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perilous</ent><br/
<hw>Per"il*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>perillous</ets>, <ets>perilleus</ets>, F.
<ets>p<eacute/rilleux</ets>, L. <ets>periculosus</ets>. See
<er>Peril</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>perillous</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of, attended with,
or involving, peril; dangerous; hazardous; <as>as, a <ex>perilous</ex>
undertaking</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Infamous hills, and sandy, <qex>perilous</qex> wilds.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Daring; reckless; dangerous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For I am <qex>perilous</qex> with knife in hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per"il*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per"il*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perilymph</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*lymph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The fluid which surrounds the membranous labyrinth of the internal
ear, and separates it from the walls of the chambers in which the
labyrinth lies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perilymphangial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*lym*phan"gi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Around, or at the side of, a lymphatic
vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perilymphatic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*lym*phat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Pertaining to, or containing,
perilymph.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Perilymphangial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Per*im"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ measure: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/rim<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The outer boundary of a body or figure, or the
sum of all the sides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for determining the extent and shape
of the field of vision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perimetrical</ent><br/
<ent>Perimetric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*met"ric*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
perimeter, or to perimetry; <as>as, a <ex>perimetric</ex> chart of the
eye</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perimetry</ent><br/
<hw>Per*im"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art of using
the perimeter; measurement of the field of vision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perimorph</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*morph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A crystal of one species inclosing one of another species. See
<er>Endomorph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perimysial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*my"sial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Surrounding a muscle or muscles.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Of or pertaining to the perimysium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perimysium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*my"si*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> about + <grk>my^s</grk> muscle.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The connective tissue sheath which surrounds a
muscle, and sends partitions inwards between the bundles of muscular
fibers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perinaeum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*n<ae/"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Perineum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perineal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*ne"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the perineum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perineoplasty</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*ne"o*plas`ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Perineum</ets> + <ets>-plasty</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The act or process of restoring an injured perineum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perineorrhaphy</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*ne*or"rha*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Perineum</ets> + Gr. <?/ to sew.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The operation of sewing up a ruptured perineum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perinephritis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*ne*phri"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Nephritis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the cellular tissue around the kidney.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per`i*ne*phrit"ic</wf>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perineum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*ne"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>peri`naion</grk> about + <grk>peri`neos</grk>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The region which is included within the outlet
of the pelvis, and is traversed by the urinogenital canal and the
rectum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perineurial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*neu"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding nerves or nerve fibers; of or pertaining to the
perineurium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perineurium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*neu"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> about + <grk>ney^ron</grk> a nerve.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The connective tissue sheath which surrounds a
bundle of nerve fibers. See <er>Epineurium</er>, and
<er>Neurilemma</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perinuclear</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*nu"cle*ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a nucleus; situated around a nucleus; <as>as,
the <ex>perinuclear</ex> protoplasm</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Period</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ri*od</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>periodus</ets>, Gr. <grk>peri`odos</grk> a going round, a way
round, a circumference, a period of time; <grk>peri`</grk> round, about
+ <grk>"odo`s</grk> a way: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/riode</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A portion of time as limited and determined by some
recurring or cyclic phenomenon, as by the completion of a revolution of
one of the heavenly bodies; a division of time, as a series of years,
months, or days, in which something is completed, and ready to
recommence and go on in the same order; <as>as, the <ex>period</ex> of
the sun, or the earth, or a comet; the <ex>period</ex> of an
electromagnetic wave is the time interval between maxima</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A stated and recurring
interval of time; more generally, an interval of time specified or left
indefinite; a certain series of years, months, days, or the like; a
time; a cycle; an age; an epoch; <as>as, the <ex>period</ex> of the
Roman republic</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How by art to make plants more lasting than their ordinary
<qex>period</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>One of the great divisions of
geological time; <as>as, the Tertiary <ex>period</ex>; the Glacial
<ex>period</ex></as>. See the <xex>Chart</xex> of
<er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The termination or completion of a revolution,
cycle, series of events, single event, or act; hence, a limit; a bound;
an end; a conclusion.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So spake the archangel Michael; then paused,<br/
As at the world's great <qex>period</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Evils which shall never end till eternity hath a
<qex>period</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is the <qex>period</qex> of my ambition.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A complete sentence, from one
full stop to another; esp., a well-proportioned, harmonious
sentence.</def> <ldquo/Devolved his rounded <xex>periods</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Periods</qex> are beautiful when they are not too long.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Johnson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>period</xex>, according to Heyse, is a compound
sentence consisting of a protasis and apodosis; according to Becker, it
is the appropriate form for the coordinate propositions related by
antithesis or causality.</note> <rj><au>Gibbs.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>The punctuation point [.] that
marks the end of a complete sentence, or of an abbreviated
word.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>One of several similar sets of
figures or terms usually marked by points or commas placed at regular
intervals, as in numeration, in the extraction of roots, and in
circulating decimals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1067 --></p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The time of the exacerbation and
remission of a disease, or of the paroxysm and intermission.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A complete musical
sentence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Sports)</fld> <def>One of the specified time
intervals into which a game is divided; <as>as, there are three
<ex>periods</ex> in a hockey game</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Education)</fld> <def>One of the specified time
intervals into which the academic day is divided; <as>as, my calculus
class is in the first <ex>period</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>The time interval during which a woman is
menstruating, or the event of a single menstruation; <as>as, her
<ex>period</ex> was late this month</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The period</b></col>, <cd>the present or current time, as
distinguished from all other times.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Time; date; epoch; era; age; duration; limit;
bound; end; conclusion; determination.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Period</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ri*od</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"r<icr/*<ucr/d)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To put an end to.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Period</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ri*od</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To come to a period; to
conclude. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/You may <xex>period</xex> upon
this, that,<rdquo/ etc.</def> <rj><au>Felthman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*i"o*date</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*<imac/"<osl/*d<amac/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of periodic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*od"ic</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`<isl/*<ocr/d"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>per-</ets> + <ets>iodic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, the
highest oxygen acid (<chform>HIO4</chform>) of iodine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodical</ent><br/
<ent>Periodic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe`ri*od"ic</hw> <pr>(p<emac/`r<icr/*<ocr/d"<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Pe`ri*od"ic*al</hw> <pr>(p<emac/`r<icr/*<ocr/d"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>periodicus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>periodiko`s</grk>: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/riodique</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a period or periods, or to division
by periods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>periodical</qex> times of all the satellites.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir J. Herschel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Performed in a period, or regular revolution;
proceeding in a series of successive circuits; <as>as, the
<ex>periodical</ex> motion of the planets round the sun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Happening, by revolution, at a stated time;
returning regularly, after a certain period of time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>periodic</qex> return of a plant's flowering.</q>
<rj><qau>Henslow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To influence opinion through the <qex>periodical</qex> press.</q>
<rj><qau>Courthope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Acting, happening, or appearing, at fixed or
somewhat variable intervals; recurring; <as>as, <ex>periodical</ex>
epidemics</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a period;
constituting a complete sentence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Periodic comet</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a
comet that moves about the sun in an elliptic orbit; a comet that has
been seen at two of its approaches to the sun.</cd> -- <col><b>Periodic
function</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>a function whose values recur
at fixed intervals as the variable uniformly increases. The
trigonomertic functions, as sin(<it>x</it>), tan(<it>x</it>), etc., are
periodic functions. Exponential functions are also periodic, having an
imaginary period, and the elliptic functions have not only a real but an
imaginary period, and are hence called <xex>doubly periodic</xex>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Periodic law</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>the
generalization that the properties of the chemical elements are periodic
functions of their atomic weights. <ldquo/In other words, if the
elements are grouped in the order of their atomic weights, it will be
found that nearly the same properties recur periodically throughout the
entire series.<rdquo/ The following tabular arrangement of the atomic
weights shows the regular recurrence of groups (under I., II., III.,
IV., etc.), each consisting of members of the same natural family. The
gaps in the table indicate the probable existence of unknown
elements.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Periodic table</b></col>,
<col><b>Periodic table of the elements</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>A tabular arrangement of the chemical elements,
illustrating the <cref>periodic law</cref>, described above.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/Note: A modern version of the periodic table can be found
at: https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

