<p><note><hand/ A similar relation had been enunciated in a crude way by
<persfn>Newlands</persfn>; but the law in its effective form was
developed and elaborated by <persfn>Mendelejeff</persfn>, whence it is
sometimes called <altname>Mendelejeff's law</altname>. Important
extensions of it were also made by L. Meyer. By this means
<persfn>Mendelejeff</persfn> predicted with remarkable accuracy the
hypothetical elements ekaboron, ekaluminium, and ekasilicon, afterwards
discovered and named respectively scandium, gallium, and
germanium.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Periodic star</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a
variable star whose changes of brightness recur at fixed periods.</cd>
-- <col><b>Periodic time of a heavenly body</b></col>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the time of a complete revolution of the body
about the sun, or of a satellite about its primary.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Periodical</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`ri*od"ic*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A magazine or other
publication which appears at stated or regular intervals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodicalist</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`ri*od"ic*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who publishes, or
writes for, a periodical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodically</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`ri*od"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a periodical{4}
manner; <as>as, flooding occurs <ex>periodically</ex> in the
valley</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodicalness</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`ri*od"ic*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Periodicity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodicity</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`ri*o*dic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Periodicities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riodicit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state
of being periodical, or regularly recurrent; <as>as, the
<ex>periodicity</ex> in the vital phenomena of plants</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Henfrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodide</ent><br/
<hw>Per*i"o*dide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>per-</ets> + <ets>iodide</ets>.]</ety> <def>An iodide containing a
higher proportion of iodine than any other iodide of the same substance
or series.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodontal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*o*don"tal</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r*<icr/*<osl/*d<ocr/n"t<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'odoy`s</grk>,
<grk>'odo`ntos</grk>, tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Surrounding
the teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>periodontitis</ent><br/
<hw>per`i*o*don*ti"tis</hw>
<pr>(p<ecr/r`<emac/*<osl/*d<ocr/n*t<imac/"t<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease that attacks the gum and bone around
the teeth.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> periodontal disease.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periodoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pe`ri*od"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Period</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A table or other means for calculating the periodical functions of
women.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perioecians</ent><br/
<ent>Perioeci</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Per`i*<oe/"ci</hw>, <hw>Per`i*<oe/"cians</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>perioeci</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/;
<grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ house, dwelling.]</ety> <def>Those who
live on the same parallel of latitude but on opposite meridians, so that
it is noon in one place when it is midnight in the other. Compare
<er>Ant<oe/ci</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periople</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*o*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/riople</ets>, from Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> about + <?/ the
hoof of a horse.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The external smooth
horny layer of the hoof of the horse and allied animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perioplic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*op"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the periople; connected with the
periople.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periosteal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*os"te*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated around bone; of or pertaining to the periosteum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periosteum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*os"te*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>perio`steos</grk> round the bones; <grk>peri`</grk> around +
<grk>'oste`on</grk> a bone: cf. L. <ets>periosteon</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The membrane of fibrous connective tissue which
closely invests all bones except at the articular surfaces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periostitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*os*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Periosteum</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the periosteum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periostracum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*os"tra*cum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Periostraca</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ shell of a testacean.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A chitinous membrane covering the exterior of
many shells; -- called also <altname>epidermis</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periotic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*o"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, the ear.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding, or pertaining to the region surrounding, the internal
ear; <as>as, the <ex>periotic</ex> capsule</as>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A periotic bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripatecian</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*pa*te"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
peripatetic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripatetic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*pa*tet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peripateticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to walk about;
<grk>peri`</grk> about + <?/ to walk: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ripat<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Walking
about; itinerant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the philosophy taught by
Aristotle (who gave his instructions while walking in the Lyceum at
Athens), or to his followers.</def> <ldquo/The true
<xex>peripatetic</xex> school.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripatetic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*pa*tet"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who walks
about; a pedestrian; an itinerant.</def> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A disciple of Aristotle; an Aristotelian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripatetical</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*pa*tet"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Peripatetic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Hales.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripateticism</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*pa*tet"i*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ripat<eacute/tisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrines or
philosophical system of the peripatetics. See <er>Peripatetic</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def> <rj><au>Lond. Sat. Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripatus</ent><br/
<ent>Peripatopsis</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pe`ri*pa*top"sis</hw>, <hw>Pe*rip"a*tus</hw></mhw> <pos>prop.
n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>peri`patos</grk> a walking
about.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The type genus of
<fam>Peripatopsidae</fam>, consisting of onychophorans (lowly organized
invertebrates related evolutionarily to the arthropods, also called
<ldquo/walking worms<rdquo/ AND <ldquo/velvet worms<rdquo/) found
chiefly in Asiatic and African tropical regions, in South Africa,
Australia, New Zealand, and tropical America.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Peripatopsis</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><q> The average resident of the Northern Hemisphere is probably not
familiar with the Onychophora; they are restricted to forest regions of
South America, Africa, the Caribbean, and Oceania. Shy creatures, able
to hide in incredibly tight crevices, these <ldquo/velvet worms<rdquo/
(about ninety living species known) are rarely seen even in their
natural habitat. Yet onychophorans are of great interest to biologists,
because they seem to be related to arthropods, and give us an idea of
what the ancestors of the arthropods may have been like. Although they
are rare as fossils, a number that have been found from the Cambrian
period. These fossils show that abundant marine relatives of the
Onychophora flourished in the seas 520 million years ago.</q> <qau>From:
http://www.ucmp.berkeley.edu/onychoph/onychophora.html</qau><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note> In the 1913 Webster <gen>Peripatus</gen> was identified with
the now disused order <ord>Malacopoda</ord> (within the phylum
<phylum>arthropoda</phylum>); however, <ord>Malacopoda</ord> is now
assigned to a group contained within the phylum
<phylum>Onycophora</phylum>. The name <it>Peripatus</it> has also been
used (possibly erroneously) to designate a specific species, as
indicated in the following quotation found on the internet.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q> The onychophora, <ex>peripatus</ex>, is a unique creature, found
in New Zealand. There is no other like peripatus. It is probably a
creature from the dawn of time, as it seems to have a fossil
representative of the most early invertebrates: Aysheaia, as may be
found in some deposits of Burgess Shale.<br/
Aside from a lovely name, Peripatus doesn't look much like an earth
creature. It is also frequently colored blue. It has a mixture of
attributes similar to both annelida and arthopoda. I have also found
interesting the arguments taxonomists have had for years over the
creature; it's taxonomy has been fussed and fought over, and changed
several times. Mostly, there just isn't anything like it.<br/
Its pre-historic relative (which looks just like it), lived at a time
when mother nature was just begining to make complex, multi-cellular
creatures, and most of them (with the exception of the jellyfish) looked
like pure experiments in physical design. No decendants remain of them,
except Peripatus. They were all bizzare in the extreme, like something
from a sci-fi nightmare. And they are all gone.<br/
Except Peripatus. Peripatus still remains. It is totally bizzare, and
totally unique.</q> <qau>Jonathon R. Oglesbee</qau><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripetalous</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding, or situated about, the petals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripheral</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*riph"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to a periphery; constituting a periphery;
peripheric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>External; away from the center;
on or near the surface; <as>as, the <ex>peripheral</ex> portion of the
nervous system</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pertaining to minor, unimportant, superficial, or
irrelevant aspects of the matter in question; <as>as,
<ex>peripheral</ex> issues are dealt with in the
appendix</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripherical</ent><br/
<ent>Peripheric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*pher"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*pher"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riph<eacute/rique</ets>. See <er>Periphery</er>.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Peripheral</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphery</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*riph"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Peripheries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>peripheria</ets>, Gr. <grk>perife`reia</grk>; <grk>peri`</grk>
around + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear, carry: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riph<eacute/rie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The outside
or superficial portions of a body; the surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The circumference of a circle,
ellipse, or other figure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphrase</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*phrase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>periphrasis</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to think about, to be expressed
periphrastically; <?/ + <?/ to speak: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riphrase</ets>. See <er>Phrase</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>The use of more words than are necessary to
express the idea; a roundabout, or indirect, way of speaking;
circumlocution.</def> <ldquo/To describe by enigmatic
<xex>periphrases</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphrase</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*phrase</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Periphrased</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Periphrasing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riphraser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To express by periphrase or
circumlocution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphrase</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*phrase</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To use
circumlocution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphrasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*riph"ra*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Periphrases</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>See <er>Periphrase</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphrastical</ent><br/
<ent>Periphrastic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*phras"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Per`i*phras"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/riphrastique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Expressing, or expressed, in more words than are necessary;
characterized by periphrase; circumlocutory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Periphrastic conjugation</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>,
<cd>a conjugation formed by the use of the simple verb with one or more
auxiliaries.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periphrastically</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*phras"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With
circumlocution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periplast</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*plast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to mold, form.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Periblast</er>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per`i*plas"tic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripneumony</ent><br/
<ent>Peripneumonia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Per`ip*neu*mo"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Per`ip*neu"mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peripneumonia</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ripneumonie</ets>. See <er>Peri-</er>,
<er>Pneumonia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pneumonia.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripneumonic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ip*neu*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peripneumonicus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ripneumonique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to peripneumonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periproct</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*proct</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/ the anus.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
region surrounding the anus, particularly of echinoderms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periproctitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*proc*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Proctitus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the tissues about the rectum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripteral</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rip"ter*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr., fr. <?/ +
<?/ feather, wing, row of columns.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Having
columns on all sides; -- said of an edifice. See
<er>Apteral</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periptery</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rip"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ flying
round about.]</ety> <def>The region surrounding a moving body, such as
the wing of a bird or a gliding a<eum/roplane, within which cyclic or
vortical motion of the air occur.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peripterous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rip"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Peripteral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Feathered all around.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perique</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rique"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Louisiana F.]</ety>
<def>A kind of tobacco with medium-sized leaf, small stem, tough and
gummy fiber, raised in Louisiana, and cured in its own juices, so as to
be very dark colored, usually black. It is marketed in tightly wrapped
rolls called carottes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perisarc</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*sarc</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, flesh.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The outer, hardened integument which covers most
hydroids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periscian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ris"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ shadow: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riscien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the shadow moving all
around.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periscii</ent><br/
<ent>Periscians</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe*ris"cians</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pe*ris"ci*i</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Periscian</er>.]</ety> <def>Those who live within a polar circle,
whose shadows, during some summer days, will move entirely round,
falling toward every point of the compass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periscope</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>A general or
comprehensive view.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An optical instrument of tubular shape containing an
arrangement of lenses and mirrors (or prisms), allowing a person to
observe a field of view otherwise obstructed, as beyond an obstructing
object or (as in submarines) above the surface of the water.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periscopic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riscopique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Viewing all around, or on
all sides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>of or relating to a periscope{2}.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Periscopic spectacles</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>,
<cd>spectacles having concavo-convex or convexo-concave lenses with a
considerable curvature corresponding to that of the eye, to increase the
distinctness of objects viewed obliquely.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perish</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Perished</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Perishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>perissen</ets>, <ets>perisshen</ets>, F. <ets>p<eacute/rir</ets>,
p. pr. <ets>p<eacute/rissant</ets>, L. <ets>perire</ets> to go or run
through, come to nothing, perish; <ets>per</ets> through +
<ets>ire</ets> to go. Cf. <er>Issue</er>, and see <er>-ish</er>.]</ety>
<def>To be destroyed; to pass away; to become nothing; to be lost; to
die; hence, to wither; to waste away.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>perish</qex> with hunger!</q> <rj><qau>Luke xv.
17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grow up and <qex>perish</qex>, as the summer fly.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The thoughts of a soul that <qex>perish</qex> in thinking.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perish</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ish</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause perish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>perish the thought</b></col> <cd>I hope it will never
happen; -- a phrase used after mention of a possible undesirable event,
sometimes facetiously.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perishability</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ish*a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Perishableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perishable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ish*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/rissable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Liable to perish; subject to
decay, destruction, or death; <as>as, <ex>perishable</ex> goods; our
<ex>perishable</ex> bodies.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perishableness</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ish*a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being perishable; liability to decay or destruction.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perishably</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ish*a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perishable degree or
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perishment</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ish*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>perissement</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of perishing.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Udall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perisoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*so"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Perisomata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Perisome</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perisome</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + <ets>-some</ets> body.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The entire covering of an invertebrate animal, as echinoderm or
c<oe/lenterate; the integument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perisperm</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*sperm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/risperme</ets>. See <er>Peri-</er>, and
<er>Sperm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The albumen of a seed,
especially that portion which is formed outside of the embryo sac.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Per`i*sper"mic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perispherical</ent><br/
<ent>Perispheric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per`i*spher"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per`i*spher"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Exactly spherical;
globular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perispomenon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*spom"e*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Perispomena</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/, pr. pass. p. of <?/ to draw around, to circumflex;
<grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ to draw.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Gram.)</fld>
<def>A word which has the circumflex accent on the last syllable.</def>
<rj><au>Goodwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perispore</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*spore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The outer covering of a spore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perissad</ent><br/
<hw>Per"is*sad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ odd, from
<?/ over.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Odd; not even; -- said of
elementary substances and of radicals whose valence is not divisible by
two without a remainder. Contrasted with <contr>artiad</contr>.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perisse</ent><br/
<hw>Per"isse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perissodactyl</ent><br/
<hw>Per`is*so*dac"tyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Perissodactyla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perissodactyla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`is*so*dac"ty*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/ odd (fr. <?/ over) + <?/ finger.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of ungulate mammals, including those
that have an odd number of toes, as the horse, tapir, and rhinoceros; --
opposed to <contr>Artiodactyla</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perissological</ent><br/
<hw>Per`is*so*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/rissologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Redundant or excessive in
words.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perissology</ent><br/
<hw>Per`is*sol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perissologia</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ odd, superfluous + <?/
discourse.]</ety> <def>Superfluity of words.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>G. Campbell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristalsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*stal"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peristaltic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Peristaltic
contraction or action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristaltic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*stal"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
clasping and compressing, fr. <?/ to surround, wrap up; <?/ round + <?/
to place, arrange: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/ristaltique</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to a wormlike wave motion of the
intestines, and by analogy, of other flexible tubular structures. In the
body it is produced by a progressive contraction of the muscular fibers
of their walls, forcing their contents onwards in the direction of the
wave; <as>as, <ex>peristaltic</ex> movement</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per`i*stal"tic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristeria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`is*te"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Peristerion</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
orchidaceous plants. See <er>Dove plant</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristerion</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`is*te"ri*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ a dovecote, a kind of verbena, fr. <grk>peristera`</grk> a dove,
pigeon; cf. L. <ets>peristereon</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
herb vervain (<spn>Verbena officinalis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristerite</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ris"ter*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>peristera`</grk> a pigeon.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety
of albite, whitish and slightly iridescent like a pigeon's
neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristeromorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ris`ter*o*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>peristera`</grk> a pigeon + <ets>-morphous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to the pigeons or
Columb<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristeropodous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ris`ter*op"o*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>peristera`</grk> a pigeon + <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>,
foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having pigeonlike feet; -- said of
those gallinaceous birds that rest on all four toes, as the curassows
and megapods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristole</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ris"to*le</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*r<icr/s"t<osl/*l<esl/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/ristole</ets>. See
<er>Peristaltic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Peristaltic
action, especially of the intestines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*ris"to*ma</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*r<icr/s"t<osl/*m<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Peristomata</plw>
<pr>(p<ecr/r`<icr/*st<ocr/m"<adot/*t<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Peristome</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1068 --></p>

<p><ent>Peristome</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*stome</hw> <pr>(p<esl/r"<icr/*st<omac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <grk>sto`ma</grk>,
<grk>sto`matos</grk>, mouth.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The fringe of teeth around the orifice of the capsule of mosses. It
consists of 4, 8, 16, 32, or 64 teeth, and may be either single or
double.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The lip, or edge of
the aperture, of a spiral shell.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The membrane
surrounding the mouth of an invertebrate animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristomial</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*sto"mi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a peristome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristomium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*sto"mi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Peristome</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristrephic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*streph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
turn round.]</ety> <def>Turning around; rotatory; revolving; <as>as, a
<ex>peristrephic</ex> painting (of a panorama)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peristyle</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peristylum</ets>, Gr. <grk>peri`stylon</grk>,
<grk>peri`stylos</grk>; <grk>peri`</grk> about + <grk>sty^los</grk> a
column: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/ristyle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A range of columns with their entablature, etc.; specifically, a
complete system of columns, whether on all sides of a court, or
surrounding a building, such as the cella of a temple. Used in the
former sense, it gives name to the larger and inner court of a Roman
dwelling, the <ex>peristyle</ex>. See <er>Colonnade</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perisystole</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*sys"to*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + <ets>systole</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/risystole</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The
interval between the diastole and systole of the heart. It is
perceptible only in the dying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perite</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rite"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peritus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Skilled.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perithecium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*the"ci*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ box.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An
organ in certain fungi and lichens, surrounding and enveloping the
masses of fructification.</def> <rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritomous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rit"o*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ cut off
all around. See <er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Tome</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Cleaving in more directions than one, parallel to
the axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritonaeum</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*to*n<ae/"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Peritoneum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritoneal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*to*ne"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/riton<eacute/al</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the peritoneum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritoneum</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*to*ne"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peritoneum</ets>, <ets>peritonaeum</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/, fr. <?/ to
stretch all around or over; <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ to
stretch.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The smooth serous membrane which
lines the cavity of the abdomen, or the whole body cavity when there is
no diaphragm, and, turning back, surrounds the viscera, forming a
closed, or nearly closed, sac.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>periton<ae/um</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritonitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*to*ni"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peritoneum</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the peritoneum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritracheal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*tra"che*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding the trache<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritreme</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*treme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a hole.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>That part of the integument of an insect which
surrounds the spiracles.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The edge of the
aperture of a univalve shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritricha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*rit"ri*cha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>peri`</grk> about + <?/, <?/, hair.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of ciliated Infusoria having a circle
of cilia around the oral disk and sometimes another around the body. It
includes the vorticellas. See <er>Vorticella</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritrochium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*tro"chi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/; <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ a wheel.]</ety>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>The wheel which, together with the axle, forms
the <cref>axis in peritrochio</cref>, which see under
<er>Axis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritropal</ent><br/
<hw>Per*it"ro*pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to turn around; <grk>peri`</grk> around + <?/ to turn: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ritrope</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Rotatory;
circuitous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the axis of the seed perpendicular to the
axis of the pericarp to which it is attached.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peritropous</ent><br/
<hw>Per*it"ro*pous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Peritropal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perityphlitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`i*typh*li"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Peri-</er>, and <er>Typhlitis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the connective tissue about the c<ae/cum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periuterine</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*u"ter*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Surrounding the uterus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perivascular</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*vas"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Around the
blood vessels; <as>as, <ex>perivascular</ex> lymphatics</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perivertebral</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*ver"te*bral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Surrounding the vertebr<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perivisceral</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*vis"cer*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Around the viscera; <as>as, the <ex>perivisceral</ex>
cavity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perivitelline</ent><br/
<hw>Per`i*vi*tel"line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>peri-</ets> + <ets>vitelline</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Situated around the vitellus, or between the vitellus and zona
pellucida of an ovum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periwig</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*wig</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/r"<icr/*w<icr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>perrwige</ets>, <ets>perwicke</ets>, corrupt. fr. F.
<ets>perruque</ets>; cf. OD. <ets>peruyk</ets>, from French. See
<er>Peruke</er>, and cf. <er>Wig</er>.]</ety> <def>A headdress of false
hair, usually covering the whole head, and representing the natural
hair; a wig.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periwig</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*wig</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Perwigged</conjf> <pr>(p<ecr/r"<icr/*w<icr/gd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Perwigging</conjf>
<pr>(p<ecr/r"<icr/*w<icr/g*g<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To dress with
a periwig, or with false hair.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>periwigged</ent><br/
<hw>periwigged</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Wearing a peruke (a style of
wig popular for men in the 17th and 18th centuries).</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> peruked.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periwinkle</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*win`kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From AS.
<ets>pinewincla</ets> a shellfish, in which <ets>pine-</ets> is fr. L.
<ets>pina</ets>, <ets>pinna</ets>, a kind of mussel, akin to Gr. <?/.
Cf. <er>Winkle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small marine
gastropod shell of the genus <gen>Littorina</gen>. The common European
species (<spn>Littorina littorea</spn>), in Europe extensively used as
food, has recently become naturalized abundantly on the American coast.
See <er>Littorina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In America the name is often applied to several large
univalves, as <spn>Fulgur carica</spn>, and <spn>Fulgur
canaliculata</spn>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Periwinkle</ent><br/
<hw>Per"i*win`kle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pervenke</ets>, AS.
<ets>pervince</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pervinca</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A trailing herb of the genus
<gen>Vinca</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common perwinkle (<spn>Vinca minor</spn>) has
opposite evergreen leaves and solitary blue or white flowers in their
axils. In America it is often miscalled <altname>myrtle</altname>. See
under <er>Myrtle</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjenet</ent><br/
<hw>Per"jen*et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pear</er>,
and <er>Jenneting</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of pear.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjure</ent><br/
<hw>Per"jure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Perjured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Perjuring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>parjurer</ets>, L. <ets>perjurare</ets>, <ets>perjerare</ets>;
<ets>per</ets> through, over + <ets>jurare</ets> to swear. See
<er>Jury</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to violate an oath or a
vow; to cause to make oath knowingly to what is untrue; to make guilty
of perjury; to forswear; to corrupt; -- often used reflexively; <as>as,
he <ex>perjured</ex> himself</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Want will <qex>perjure</qex><br/
The ne'er-touched vestal.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a false oath to; to deceive by oaths and
protestations.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And with a virgin innocence did pray<br/
For me, that <qex>perjured</qex> her.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Fletcher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To <er>Perjure</er>, <er>Forswear</er>.</syn>
<usage> These words have been used interchangeably; but there is a
tendency to restrict <xex>perjure</xex> to that species of forswearing
which constitutes the crime of perjury at law, namely, the willful
violation of an oath administered by a magistrate or according to
law.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjure</ent><br/
<hw>Per"jure</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perjurus</ets>: cf. OF.
<ets>parjur</ets>, F. <ets>parjure</ets>.]</ety> <def>A perjured
person.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjured</ent><br/
<hw>Per"jured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Guilty of perjury;
having sworn falsely; forsworn.</def> <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Perjured</xex> persons.<rdquo/ <au>1 Tim. i. 10.</au>
<ldquo/Their <xex>perjured</xex> oath.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjurer</ent><br/
<hw>Per"jur*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is guilty
of perjury; one who perjures or forswears, in any sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjurous</ent><br/
<ent>Perjurious</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*ju"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per"ju*rous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perjuriosus</ets>,
<ets>perjurus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Guilty of perjury; containing
perjury.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Quarles. B.
Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perjury</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ju*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Perjuries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>perjurium</ets>.
See <er>Perjure</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>False
swearing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>At common law, a willfully false
statement in a fact material to the issue, made by a witness under oath
in a competent judicial proceeding. By statute the penalties of perjury
are imposed on the making of willfully false affirmations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ If a man swear falsely in nonjudicial affidavits, it is
made perjury by statute in some jurisdictions in the United
States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perk</ent><br/
<hw>Perk</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rk)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perked</conjf> <pr>(p<etil/rkt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Perking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. W.
<ets>percu</ets> to trim, to make smart.]</ety> <def>To make trim or
smart; to straighten up; to erect; to make a jaunty or saucy display of;
<as>as, to <ex>perk</ex> the ears; to <ex>perk</ex> up one's
head.</as></def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au> <au>Sherburne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>to perk up one's ears</b></col> <cd>to begin to listen
attentively; -- usually used when something of interest is mentioned in
some discourse, after one's attention had lapsed; <as>as, he <ex>perked
up his ears</ex> when he heard his name mentioned</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perk</ent><br/
<hw>Perk</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To exalt one's self; to
bear one's self loftily.</def> <ldquo/To <xex>perk</xex> over
them.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To act in a jaunty or presumptuous manner.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To perk it</b></col>, <cd>to carry one's self proudly or
saucily.</cd> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perk</ent><br/
<hw>Perk</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Smart; trim; spruce; jaunty;
vain.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Perk</xex> as a peacock.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perk</ent><br/
<hw>Perk</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To peer; to look
inquisitively.</def> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>perk</ent><br/
<hw>perk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A perquisite.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perkin</ent><br/
<hw>Per"kin</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"k<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind
of weak perry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perkinism</ent><br/
<hw>Per"kin*ism</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"k<icr/n*<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A remedial treatment, by drawing the pointed
extremities of two rods, each of a different metal, over the affected
part; tractoration, -- first employed by <person>Dr. Elisha
<etsep>Perkins</etsep></person> of Norwich, Conn. See
<er>Metallotherapy</er>.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perky</ent><br/
<hw>Perk"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Perk; pert; jaunty;
trim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There amid <qex>perky</qex> larches and pines.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perlaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Per*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pearl</er>.]</ety> <def>Pearly; resembling pearl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perlid</ent><br/
<hw>Per"lid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
insect of the genus <gen>Perla</gen>, or family <fam>Perlid<ae/</fam>.
See <cref>Stone fly</cref>, under <er>Stone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perlite</ent><br/
<hw>Per"lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pearlite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perlitic</ent><br/
<hw>Per*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Relating to or resembling perlite, or pearlstone; <as>as, the
<ex>perlitic</ex> structure of certain rocks</as>. See
<er>Pearlite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perlous</ent><br/
<hw>Per"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Perilous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perlustration</ent><br/
<hw>Per`lus*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perlustrare</ets> to wander all through, to survey. See 3d
<er>Luster</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of viewing all over.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permanable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ma*na*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Permanent;
durable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Lydgate.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permanency</ent><br/
<ent>Permanence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per"ma*nence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per"ma*nen*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>permanence</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
permanent; continuance in the same state or place; duration; fixedness;
<as>as, the <ex>permanence</ex> of institutions; the <ex>permanence</ex>
of nature.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permanent</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ma*nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>permanens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>permanere</ets>
to stay or remain to the end, to last; <ets>per + manere</ets> to
remain: cf. F. <ets>permanent</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and
<er>Mansion</er>.]</ety> <def>Continuing in the same state, or without
any change that destroys form or character; remaining unaltered or
unremoved; abiding; durable; fixed; stable; lasting; <as>as, a
<ex>permanent</ex> impression</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Eternity stands <qex>permanent</qex> and fixed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Permanent gases</b></col> <fld>(Chem. & Physics)</fld>,
<cd>hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, and carbon monoxide; -- also called
<altname>incondensible gases</altname> or <altname>incoercible
gases</altname>, before their liquefaction in 1877. The term is now
archaic.</cd> -- <col><b>Permanent way</b></col>, <cd>the roadbed and
superstructure of a finished railway; -- so called in distinction from
the contractor's <contr>temporary way</contr>.</cd> -- <col><b>Permanent
white</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>barium sulphate (<altname>heavy
spar</altname>), used as a white pigment or paint, in distinction from
<contr>white lead</contr>, which tarnishes and darkens from the
formation of the sulphide.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Lasting; durable; constant. See
<er>Lasting</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permanent</ent><br/
<hw>permanent</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A wave or curl in the
hair that lasts for months and is made durable by treating the hair with
chemicals when it is curled.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> permanent wave.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permanently</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ma*nent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a permanent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permanent-press</ent><br/
<hw>permanent-press</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fabric that has been
chemically processed to resist wrinkles and hold its shape.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> permanent press, permanent-press fabric, durable
press, durable-press fabric.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permanent-press</ent><br/
<hw>permanent-press</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Having a permanent crease,
or remaining unwrinkled without pressing; -- used of fabrics that do not
require ironing.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> drip-dry.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permanent wave</ent><br/
<hw>permanent wave</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A wave or curl in
the hair that lasts for months and is made durable by treating the hair
with chemicals when it is curled.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> permanent.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The process of treating the hair with chemicals to
create a <er>permanent wave</er>{1}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permanganate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*man"ga*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of permanganic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Potassium permanganate</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<cd>See <cref>Potassium permanganate</cref>, under
<er>Potassium</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permanganic</ent><br/
<hw>Per`man*gan"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, one of the higher acids of
manganese, <chform>HMnO4</chform>, which forms salts called
<xex>permanganates</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permansion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*man"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>permansio</ets>. See <er>Permanent</er>.]</ety>
<def>Continuance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeability</ent><br/
<hw>Per`me*a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perm<eacute/abilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state
of being permeable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Magnetic permeability</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>,
<cd>the specific capacity of a body for magnetic induction, or its
conducting power for lines of magnetic force.</cd> <rj><au>Sir W.
Thomson.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"me*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>permeabilis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perm<eacute/able</ets>. See
<er>Permeate</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being permeated, or passed
through; yielding passage; passable; penetrable; -- used especially of
substances which allow the passage of fluids; <as>as, wood is
<ex>permeable</ex> to oil; glass is <ex>permeable</ex> to
light.</as></def> <rj><au>I. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeably</ent><br/
<hw>Per"me*a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a permeable
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeance</ent><br/
<hw>Per"me*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Permeant</er>.]</ety> <def>Permeation;</def>
<specif>specif.</specif> <fld>(Magnetism)</fld>, <def>the reciprocal of
reluctance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeant</ent><br/
<hw>Per"me*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>permeans</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Passing through;
permeating.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"me*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Permeated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Permeating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>permeatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>permeare</ets> to permeate; <ets>per
+ meare</ets> to go, pass.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass through the
pores or interstices of; to penetrate and pass through without causing
rupture or displacement; -- applied especially to fluids which pass
through substances of loose texture; <as>as, water <ex>permeates</ex>
sand</as>.</def> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To enter and spread through; to pervade; <as>as,
after the first setback, the team became <ex>permeated</ex> with
pessimism</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God was conceived to be diffused throughout the whole world, to
<qex>permeate</qex> and pervade all things.</q>
<rj><qau>Cudworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permeated</ent><br/
<hw>permeated</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>permeate</er>; <as>as, Her poems are <ex>permeated</ex> with
sorrow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permeative</ent><br/
<ent>permeating</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>permeating</hw> <hw>permeative</hw></mhw> <pos>adj.</pos>
<def>Spreading throughout.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> permeant.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permeation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`me*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
permeating, passing through, or spreading throughout, the pores or
interstices of any substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here is not a mere involution only, but a spiritual
<qex>permeation</qex> and inexistence.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>permed</ent><br/
<hw>permed</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <ety>[from <ets>perm</ets>anent
wave]</ety> <def>Treated with chemicals to make it curly; having been
given a <cref>permanent wave</cref>; -- of hair; <as>as, <ex>permed</ex>
hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permian</ent><br/
<hw>Per"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. a.</pos> <ety>[From the
ancient kingdom of <ets>Permia</ets>, where the Permian formation
exists.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Belonging or relating to the
period, and also to the formation, next following the Carboniferous, and
regarded as closing the Carboniferous age and Paleozoic era.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The Permian period. See <xex>Chart</xex> of
<er>Geology</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permians</ent><br/
<hw>Per"mi*ans</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Permian</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>A tribe belonging to the Finnic race, and inhabiting a portion of
Russia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permiscible</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis"ci*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>permiscere</ets> to mingle; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>miscere</ets> to
mix.]</ety> <def>Capable of being mixed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permiss</ent><br/
<hw>Per*miss"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Permit</er>.]</ety> <def>A permitted choice; a rhetorical figure in
which a thing is committed to the decision of one's opponent.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permissibility</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality of being permissible; permissibleness; allowableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permissible</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
permitted; allowable; admissible.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*mis"si*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Per*mis"si*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permission</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis"sion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*m<icr/sh"<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>permissio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>permission</ets>. See
<er>Permit</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of permitting or allowing; formal
consent; authorization; leave; license or liberty granted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>High <qex>permission</qex> of all-ruling Heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have given me your <qex>permission</qex> for this address.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Leave; liberty; license.</syn> <usage> --
<er>Leave</er>, <er>Permission</er>. <xex>Leave</xex> implies that the
recipient may decide whether to use the license granted or not.
<xex>Permission</xex> is the absence on the part of another of anything
preventive, and in general, at least by implication, signifies
approval.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permissive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis"sive</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*m<icr/s"s<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Permitting; granting leave or liberty.</def> <ldquo/By
his <xex>permissive</xex> will.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Permitted; tolerated; suffered.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permissively</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis"sive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a permissive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permistion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mis"tion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*m<icr/s"ch<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>permistio</ets>, <ets>permixtio</ets>, fr.
<ets>permiscere</ets>, <ets>permistum</ets>, and <ets>permixtum</ets>.
See <er>Permiscible</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of mixing; the state of
being mingled; mixture.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>permixtion</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permit</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Permitted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Permitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>permittere</ets>,
<ets>permissum</ets>, to let through, to allow, permit; <ets>per +
mittere</ets> to let go, send. See <er>Per-</er>, and
<er>Mission</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To consent to; to allow or
suffer to be done; to tolerate; to put up with.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What things God doth neither command nor forbid . . . he
<qex>permitteth</qex> with approbation either to be done or left
undone.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To grant (one) express license or liberty to do an
act; to authorize; to give leave; -- followed by an
infinitive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou art <qex>permitted</qex> to speak for thyself.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xxvi. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To give over; to resign; to leave; to
commit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us not aggravate our sorrows,<br/
But to the gods <qex>permit</qex> the event of things.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To allow; let; grant; admit; suffer; tolerate;
endure; consent to.</syn> <usage> -- To <er>Allow</er>, <er>Permit</er>,
<er>Suffer</er>, <er>Tolerate</er>. To <xex>allow</xex> is more
positive, denoting (at least originally and etymologically) a decided
assent, either directly or by implication. To <xex>permit</xex> is more
negative, and imports only acquiescence or an abstinence from
prevention. The distinction, however, is often disregarded by good
writers. To <xex>suffer</xex> has a stronger passive or negative sense
than to <xex>permit</xex>, sometimes implying against the will,
sometimes mere indifference. To <xex>tolerate</xex> is to endure what is
contrary to will or desire. To <xex>suffer</xex> and to
<xex>tolerate</xex> are sometimes used without
discrimination.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permit</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mit"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To grant permission; to
allow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permit</ent><br/
<hw>Per"mit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Warrant; license;
leave; permission; specifically, a written license or permission given
to a person or persons having authority; <as>as, a <ex>permit</ex> to
land goods subject to duty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permit</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sp.
<ets>palamida</ets> a kind of scombroid fish.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
large pompano (<spn>Trachinotus goodei</spn>) of the West Indies,
Florida, etc. It becomes about three feet long.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The round pompano. (<spn>Trachinotus falcatus</spn>).</def>
<mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permittance</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mit"tance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
permitting; allowance; permission; leave.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permittee</ent><br/
<hw>Per`mit*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One to whom a
permission or permit is given.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permitter</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mit"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
permits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>permitter</qex>, or not a hinderer, of sin.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permix</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mix"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To mix; to
mingle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permixtion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mix"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Permission</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permulator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"mu*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>A special form of rotary converter with stationary commutator and
rotating brushes, in which the exciting field is induced by the
alternating current in a short-circuited magnetic core instead of being
produced by an external magnet.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permutable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mut"a*ble</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*m<umac/"t<adot/*b'l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>permutable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being permuted; exchangeable.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*mut"a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Per*mut"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permutation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`mu*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`m<usl/"t<amac/"sn<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>permutatio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>permutation</ets>. See <er>Permute</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of permuting; exchange of the thing for another; mutual
transference; interchange.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The violent convulsions and <qex>permutations</qex> that have been
made in property.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The arrangement of
any determinate number of things, as units, objects, letters, etc., in
all possible orders, one after the other; -- called also
<altname>alternation</altname>. Cf. <er>Combination</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
4.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any one of such possible
arrangements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Barter; exchange.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Permutation lock</b></col>, <cd>a lock in which the parts
can be transposed or shifted, so as to require different arrangements of
the tumblers on different occasions of unlocking.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1069 --></p>

<p><ent>Permute</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mute"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*m<umac/t")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>permutare</ets>, <ets>permutatum</ets>; <ets>per +
mutare</ets> to change: cf. F. <ets>permuter</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To interchange; to transfer reciprocally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exchange; to barter; to traffic.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bought, trucked, <qex>permuted</qex>, or given.</q>
<rj><qau>Hakluyt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Permuter</ent><br/
<hw>Per*mut"er</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*m<umac/t"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who permutes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pern</ent><br/
<hw>Pern</hw> <pr>(p<etil/rn)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pernancy</er>.]</ety> <def>To take profit of; to make
profitable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pern</ent><br/
<hw>Pern</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The honey
buzzard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernancy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"nan*cy</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"n<ait/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OF. <ets>prenance</ets>, fr. <ets>prendre</ets>,
<ets>prenre</ets>, <ets>penre</ets>, to take, L. <ets>prendere</ets>,
<ets>prehendere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A taking or
reception, as the receiving of rents or tithes in kind, the receiving of
profits.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernel</ent><br/
<hw>Per"nel</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"n<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pimpernel</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernicion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ni"cion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 2d
<er>Pernicious</er>.]</ety> <def>Destruction; perdition.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernicious</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ni"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pernix</ets>, <ets>-icis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Quick; swift (to
burn).</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernicious</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ni"cious</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perniciosus</ets>,
from <ets>pernicies</ets> destruction, from <ets>pernecare</ets> to kill
or slay outright; <ets>per + necare</ets> to kill, slay: cf. F.
<ets>pernicieux</ets>. Cf. <er>Nuisance</er>,
<er>Necromancy</er>.]</ety> <def>Having the quality of injuring or
killing; destructive; very mischievous; baleful; malicious;
wicked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let this <qex>pernicious</qex> hour<br/
Stand aye accursed in the calendar.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pernicious</qex> to his health.</q>
<rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Destructive; ruinous; deadly; noxious; injurious;
baneful; deleterious; hurtful; mischievous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per*ni"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>, --
<wf>Per*ni"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernicity</ent><br/
<hw>Per*nic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pernicitas</ets>. See 1st <er>Pernicious</er>.]</ety>
<def>Swiftness; celerity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernicketty</ent><br/
<ent>Pernickety</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*nick"et*y</hw>, <hw>Per*nick"et*ty</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Finical or fussy; full of petty
details.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> finical, finicky, fussy, meticulous, painstaking,
persnickety, punctilious.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per"ni*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A chilblain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernoctalian</ent><br/
<hw>Per`noc*ta"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
watches or keeps awake all night.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernoctation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`noc*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pernoctatio</ets>, fr. <ets>pernoctare</ets> to stay all night;
<ets>per</ets> + <ets>nox</ets>, <ets>noctis</ets>, night.]</ety>
<def>The act or state of passing the whole night; a remaining all
night.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pernoctation</xex> in prayer.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernor</ent><br/
<hw>Per"nor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pern</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who receives the profits,
as of an estate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernot furnace</ent><br/
<hw>Per"not fur"nace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from
<person>Charles <etsep>Pernot</etsep></person>, its inventor.]</ety>
<def>A reverberatory furnace with a circular revolving hearth, -- used
in making steel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pernyi moth</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ny*i moth"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
silk-producing moth (<spn>Attacus Pernyi</spn>) which feeds upon the
oak. It has been introduced into Europe and America from
China.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perofskite</ent><br/
<hw>Per*of"skite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From von
<ets>Perovski</ets>, of St.Petersburg.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Perovskite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perogue</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pirogue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peromyscus</ent><br/
<hw>Peromyscus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of New World wood
mice.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Peromyscus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peronate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"o*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peronatus</ets> rough-booted, fr. <ets>pero</ets>,
<ets>-onis</ets>, a kind of rough boot.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
term applied to the stipes or stalks of certain fungi which are covered
with a woolly substance which at length becomes powdery.</def>
<rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peroneal</ent><br/
<hw>Per`o*ne"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ the
fibula.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the fibula;
in the region of the fibula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peronospora</ent><br/
<hw>Peronospora</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of destructive
downy mildews.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus Peronospora.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peronosporaceae</ent><br/
<hw>Peronosporaceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of
parasitic fungi; the downy mildews.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family Peronosporaceae.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peronosporales</ent><br/
<hw>Peronosporales</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>An order of chiefly
parasitic lower fungi, including the <fam>Albuginaceae</fam> and
<fam>Peronosporaceae</fam> and <fam>Pythiaceae</fam>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order <ord>Peronosporales</ord>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perorate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"o*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Peroration</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a peroration; to
harangue.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peroration</ent><br/
<hw>Per`o*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>peroratio</ets>, fr. <ets>perorate</ets>, <ets>peroratum</ets>, to
speak from beginning to end; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>orate</ets> to speak.
See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Oration</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>The concluding part of an oration; especially, a final summing up
and enforcement of an argument.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perovskite</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ov"skite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From von
<etsep>Perovski</etsep>, of St. Petersburg.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A titanate of lime occurring in octahedral or cubic crystals.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Perofskite</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peroxidation</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ox`i*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act, process,
or result of peroxidizing; oxidation to a peroxide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peroxide</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An oxide containing more oxygen than some other oxide of the same
element. Formerly peroxides were regarded as the highest oxides. Cf.
<er>Per-</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peroxidize</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ox"i*dize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Peroxidized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Peroxidizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To oxidize to the utmost degree, so as to form a
peroxide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpend</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perpendere</ets>, <ets>perpensum</ets>; <ets>per</ets> +
<ets>pendere</ets> to weight.]</ety> <def>To weight carefully in the
mind.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Perpend</xex> my words.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpend</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pend"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To attend; to be
attentive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpender</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pend"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>parpaing</ets>, pierre <ets>parpaigne</ets>; of uncertain
origin.]</ety> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>A large stone reaching through
a wall so as to appear on both sides of it, and acting as a binder; --
called also <altname>perbend</altname>, <altname>perpend
stone</altname>, and <altname>perpent stone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpendicle</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pen"di*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perpendiculum</ets>; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>pendere</ets> to hang:
cf. F. <ets>perpendicule</ets>.]</ety> <def>Something hanging straight
down; a plumb line.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpendicular</ent><br/
<hw>Per`pen*dic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perpendicularis</ets>, <ets>perpendicularius</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>perpendiculaire</ets>. See <er>Perpendicle</er>,
<er>Pension</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Exactly upright or vertical;
pointing to the zenith; at right angles to the plane of the horizon;
extending in a right line from any point toward the center of the
earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>At right angles to a given line
or surface; <as>as, the line <ex>ad</ex> is <ex>perpendicular</ex> to
the line <ex>bc</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Perpendicular style</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a
name given to the latest variety of English Gothic architecture, which
prevailed from the close of the 14th century to the early part of the
16th; -- probably so called from the vertical style of its window
mullions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpendicular</ent><br/
<hw>Per`pen*dic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A line at right angles to the plane of the horizon; a vertical line
or direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A line or plane falling at right
angles on another line or surface, or making equal angles with it on
each side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpendicularity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`pen*dic`u*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perpendicularit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being perpendicular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpendicularly</ent><br/
<hw>Per`pen*dic"u*lar*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
perpendicular manner; vertically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpend stone</ent><br/
<hw>Per"pend stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See
<er>Perpender</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpension</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Perpend</er>.]</ety> <def>Careful consideration; pondering.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpensity</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pen"si*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Perpension.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpent stone</ent><br/
<hw>Per"pent stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See
<er>Perpender</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpession</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pes"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perpessio</ets>, fr. <ets>perpeti</ets>, <ets>perpessus</ets>, to
bear steadfastly; <ets>per + pati</ets> to bear.]</ety> <def>Suffering;
endurance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetrable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"pe*tra"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being perpetrated.</def> <rj><au>R. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetrate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"pe*trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perpetrated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Perpetrating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>perpetratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perpetrare</ets> to effect,
perpetrare; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>patrare</ets> to perform.]</ety>
<def>To do or perform; to carry through; to execute, commonly in a bad
sense; to commit (as a crime, an offense); to be guilty of; <as>as, to
<ex>perpetrate</ex> a foul deed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What the worst <qex>perpetrate</qex>, or best endure.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetration</ent><br/
<hw>Per`pe*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perpetratio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perp<eacute/tration</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of perpetrating; a doing; -- commonly used of
doing something wrong, as a crime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The thing perpetrated; an evil action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetrator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"pe*tra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who perpetrates; esp., one who commits an offense or
crime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetuable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being perpetuated or continued.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Varieties are <qex>perpetuable</qex>, like species.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetual</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>perpetuel</ets>, F. <ets>perp<eacute/tuel</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>perpetualis</ets>, fr. <ets>perpetuus</ets> continuing throughout,
continuous, fr. <ets>perpes</ets>, <ets>-etis</ets>, lasting
throughout.]</ety> <def>Neverceasing; continuing forever or for an
unlimited time; unfailing; everlasting; continuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Unto the kingdom of <qex>perpetual</qex> night.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Perpetual</qex> feast of nectared sweets.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Circle of perpetual apparition</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Circle of perpetual occultation</b></col></mcol>.
<cd>See under <er>Circle</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Perpetual
calendar</b></col>, <cd>a calendar so devised that it may be adjusted
for any month or year.</cd> -- <col><b>Perpetual curacy</b></col>
<fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld>, <cd>a curacy in which all the tithes are
appropriated, and no vicarage is endowed.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au> --
<col><b>Perpetual motion</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Motion</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Perpetual screw</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Endless
screw</cref>, under <er>Screw</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Continual; unceasing; endless; everlasting;
incessant; constant; eternal. See <er>Constant</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetual calendar</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*al cal"en*dar</hw>. <def>A calendar that can be used
perpetually or over a wide range of years. That of <person>Capt.
Herschel</person> covers, as given below, dates from 1750 to 1961 only,
but is capable of indefinite extension.</def></p>

