[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetually</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perpetual manner; constantly; continually.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Bible and Common Prayer Book in the vulgar tongue, being <qex>perpetually</qex> read in churches, have proved a kind of standard for language.</q>
<au>Swift.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetualty</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or condition of being perpetual.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Testament of Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetuance</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Perpetuity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetuate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perpetuated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Perpetuating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>perpetuatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perpetuare</ets> to perpetuate. See <er>Perpetual</er>.]</ety> <def>To make perpetual; to cause to endure, or to be continued, indefinitely; to preserve from extinction or oblivion; to eternize.</def>  <rj><au>Addison.</au>  <au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetuate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perpetuatus</ets>, p. p.]</ety> <def>Made perpetual; perpetuated.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>  <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetuation</ent><br/
<hw>Per*pet`u*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>perp<eacute/tuation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of making perpetual, or of preserving from extinction through an endless existence, or for an indefinite period of time; continuance.</def>  <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpetuity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`pe*tu"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perpetuitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perp<eacute/tuit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being perpetual; <as>as, the <ex>perpetuity</ex> of laws</as>.</def>  <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A path to <qex>perpetuity</qex> of fame.</q> <rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>perpetuity</qex> of a single emotion is insanity.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something that is perpetual.</def>  <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Endless time.</def> <ldquo/And yet we should, for <xex>perpetuity</xex>, go hence in debt.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Annuities)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The number of years in which the simple interest of any sum becomes equal to the principal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The number of years' purchase to be given for an annuity to continue forever.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A perpetual annuity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Duration without limitations as to time.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The quality or condition of an estate by which it becomes inalienable, either perpetually or for a very long period; also, the estate itself so modified or perpetuated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplex</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plex"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perplexed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Perplexing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>perplexari</ets>. See <er>Perplex</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To involve; to entangle; to make intricate or complicated, and difficult to be unraveled or understood; <as>as, to <ex>perplex</ex> one with doubts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No artful wildness to <qex>perplex</qex> the scene.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What was thought obscure, <qex>perplexed</qex>, and too hard for our weak parts, will lie open to the understanding in a fair view.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To embarrass; to puzzle; to distract; to bewilder; to confuse; to trouble with ambiguity, suspense, or anxiety.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Perplexd</xex> beyond self-explication.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are <qex>perplexed</qex>, but not in despair.</q> <rj><qau>2 Cor. iv. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We can distinguish no general truths, or at least shall be apt to <qex>perplex</qex> the mind.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To plague; to vex; to torment.</def>  <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To entangle; involve; complicate; embarrass; puzzle; bewilder; confuse; distract. See <er>Embarrass</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplex</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plex"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perplexus</ets> entangled, intricate; <ets>per + plectere</ets>, <ets>plexum</ets>, to plait, braid: cf. F. <ets>perplexe</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Plait</er>.]</ety> <def>Intricate; difficult.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplexed</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plexed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Entangled, involved, or confused; hence, embarrassd; puzzled; doubtful; anxious.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Per*plex"ed*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Per*plex"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplexing</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plex"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Embarrassing; puzzling; troublesome.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Perplexing</xex> thoughts.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplexity</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plex"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Perplexities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>perplexitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perplexit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being perplexed or puzzled; complication; intricacy; entanglement; distraction of mind through doubt or difficulty; embarrassment; bewilderment; doubt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By their own <qex>perplexities</qex> involved,<br/
They ravel more.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplexiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plex"ive*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being perplexing; tendency to perplex.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perplexly</ent><br/
<hw>Per*plex"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Perplexedly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perpotation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`po*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perpotatio</ets>, fr. <ets>perpotate</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Potation</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of drinking excessively; a drinking bout.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perquisite</ent><br/
<hw>Per"qui*site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perquisitum</ets>, fr. <ets>perquisitus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perquirere</ets> to ask for diligently; <ets>per + quaerere</ets> to seek. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Quest</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something gained from a place or employment over and above the ordinary salary or fixed wages for services rendered; -- examples are, a fee allowed by law to an officer for a specific service; the use of a company automobile or other company property.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> perk.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pillage of a place taken by storm was regarded as the <qex>perquisite</qex> of the soldiers.</q> <rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The best <qex>perquisites</qex> of a place are the advantages it gaves a man of doing good.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Things gotten by a man's own industry, or purchased with his own money, as opposed to things which come to him by descent.</def>  <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perquisited</ent><br/
<hw>Per"qui*sit*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Supplied with perquisites.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Perquisited</xex> varlets frequent stand.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Savage.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perquisition</ent><br/
<hw>Per`qui*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>perquisition</ets>.]</ety> <def>A thorough inquiry of search.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>  <rj><au>Berkeley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perradial</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ra"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated around the radii, or radial tubes, of a radiate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perrie</ent><br/
<hw>Per"rie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pierreries</ets>, pl., fr. <ets>pierre</ets> stone, L. <ets>petra</ets>.]</ety> <def>Precious stones; jewels.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also <asp>perre</asp>, <asp>perrye</asp>, etc.]</altsp>  <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perrier</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ri*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>perriere</ets>, <ets>perrier</ets>, F. <ets>perrier</ets>.  Cf. <er>Pederero</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A short mortar used formerly for throwing stone shot.</def>  <rj><au>Hakluyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perrier</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ri*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>  <def>The name of a brand of bottled mineral water; a glass or bottle of such water; <as>as, I'll have a <ex>Perrier</ex>.</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perron</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An out-of-door flight of steps, as in a garden, leading to a terrace or to an upper story; -- usually applied to medi<ae/vel or later structures of some architectural pretensions.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perroquet</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ro*quet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Paroquet</er>, <er>Parakeet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perruque</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`ruque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>See <er>Peruke</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perruquier</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ru"qui*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A maker of perukes or wigs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perry</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>per<eacute/</ets>, F. <ets>poir<eacute/</ets>, fr. <ets>poire</ets> a pear, L. <ets>pirum</ets>. See <er>Pear</er> the fruit.]</ety> <def>A fermented liquor made from pears; pear cider.</def>  <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perry</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sudden squall. See <er>Pirry</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pers</ent><br/
<hw>Pers</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pers</ets>.]</ety> <def>Light blue; grayish blue; -- a term applied to different shades at different periods.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A cloth of sky-blue color.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A long surcoat of <xex>pers</xex>.<rdquo/</def2>  <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persalt</ent><br/
<hw>Per"salt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A term formerly given to the salts supposed to be formed respectively by neutralizing acids with certain peroxides.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persant</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>per<cced/ant</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>percer</ets> to pierce.]</ety> <def>Piercing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perscrutation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`scru*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perscrutatio</ets>, fr. <ets>perscrutari</ets> to search through.]</ety> <def>A thorough searching; a minute inquiry or scrutiny.</def>  <rj><au>Carlyle</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persecot</ent><br/
<hw>Per"se*cot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Persicot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persecute</ent><br/
<hw>Per"se*cute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Persecuted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Persecuting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>pers<eacute/cueter</ets>, L. <ets>persequi</ets>, <ets>persecutus</ets>, to pursue, prosecute; <ets>per + sequi</ets> to follow, pursue. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Second</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pursue in a manner to injure, grieve, or afflict; to beset with cruelty or malignity; to harass; especially, to afflict, harass, punish, or put to death, for adherence to a particular religious creed or mode of worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and <qex>persecute</qex> you.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. v. 44.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To harass with importunity; to pursue with persistent solicitations; to annoy.</def>  <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To oppress; harass; distress; worry; annoy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>persecuted</ent><br/
<hw>persecuted</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Same as <er>oppressed</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> downtrodden, oppressed.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persecution</ent><br/
<hw>Per`se*cu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pers<eacute/cution</ets>, L. <ets>persecutio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or practice of persecuting; especially, the infliction of loss, pain, or death for adherence to a particular creed or mode of worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Persecution</qex> produces no sincere conviction.</q> <rj><qau>Paley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state or condition of being persecuted.</def>  <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A carrying on; prosecution.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persecutor</ent><br/
<hw>Per"se*cu`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.: cf. F. <ets>pers<eacute/cuteur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who persecutes, or harasses.</def>  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persecutrix</ent><br/
<hw>Per"se*cu`trix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A woman who persecutes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perseid</ent><br/
<hw>Per"se*id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Perseus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>One of a group of shooting stars which appear yearly about the 10th of August, and cross the heavens in paths apparently radiating from the constellation <etsep>Perseus</etsep>.  They are probably fragments of Swift's comet of 1862 (III).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perseus</ent><br/
<hw>Per"se*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[L., from Gr. <grk>Persey`s</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>A Grecian legendary hero, son of Jupiter and Dana<eum/, who slew the Gorgon Medusa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A consellation of the northern hemisphere, near Taurus and Cassiopea. It contains a star cluster visible to the naked eye as a nebula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persever</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sev"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To persevere.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perseverance</ent><br/
<hw>Per`se*ver"ance</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`s<esl/*v<emac/r"<ait/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pers<eacute/v<eacute/rance</ets>, L. <ets>perseverantia</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of persevering; persistence in anything undertaken; continued pursuit or prosecution of any business, or enterprise begun.</def> <ldquo/The king-becoming graces . . . <xex>perseverance</xex>, mercy, lowliness.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whose constant <qex>perseverance</qex> overcame<br/
Whate'er his cruel malice could invent.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Discrimination.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Sir J. Harrington.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>Continuance in a state of grace until it is succeeded by a state of glory; sometimes called <altname>final perseverance</altname>, and <altname>the perseverance of the saints</altname>. See <er>Calvinism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Persistence; steadfastness; constancy; steadiness; pertinacity.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1070 --></p>

<p><ent>Perseverant</ent><br/
<hw>Per`se*ver"ant</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`s<esl/*v<emac/r"<ait/nt)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perseverans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p.
pr.: cf. F. <ets>pers<eacute/v<eacute/rant</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Persevering.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Perseverant</xex>
faith.<rdquo/ <au>Whitby.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Per`se*ver"ant*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persevere</ent><br/
<hw>Per`se*vere"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Persevered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Persevering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pers<eacute/v<eacute/rer</ets>, L. <ets>perseverare</ets>, fr.
<ets>perseverus</ets> very strict; <ets>per + severus</ets> strict,
severe. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Severe</er>.]</ety> <def>To persist
in any business or enterprise undertaken; to pursue steadily any project
or course begun; to maintain a purpose in spite of counter influences,
opposition, or discouragement; not to give or abandon what is
undertaken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thrice happy, if they know<br/
Their happiness, and <qex>persevere</qex> upright.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To <er>Persevere</er>, <er>Continue</er>,
<er>Persist</er>.</syn> <usage> The idea of not laying aside is common
to these words. <xex>Continue</xex> is the generic term, denoting simply
to do as one has done hitherto. To <xex>persevere</xex> is to
<xex>continue</xex> in a given course in spite of discouragements, etc.,
from a desire to obtain our end. To <xex>persist</xex> is to
<xex>continue</xex> from a determination of will not to give up.
<xex>Persist</xex> is frequently used in a bad sense, implying obstinacy
in pursuing an unworthy aim.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persevering</ent><br/
<hw>Per`se*ver"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized
by perseverance; persistent.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per`se*ver"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persian</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Persia</ets>: cf. It. <ets>Persiano</ets>. Cf. <er>Parsee</er>,
<er>Peach</er>, <er>Persic</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Persia,
to the Persians, or to their language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Persian berry</b></col>, <cd>the fruit of <spn>Rhamnus
infectorius</spn>, a kind of buckthorn, used for dyeing yellow, and
imported chiefly from Trebizond.</cd> -- <col><b>Persian cat</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <spn>Angora cat</spn>, under
<er>Angora</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Persian columns</b></col>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>columns of which the shaft represents a Persian
slave; -- called also <altname>Persians</altname>. See
<er>Atlantes</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Persian drill</b></col>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a drill which is turned by pushing a nut back
and forth along a spirally grooved drill holder.</cd> -- <col><b>Persian
fire</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>malignant pustule.</cd> --
<col><b>Persian powder</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Insect powder</cref>,
under <er>Insect</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Persian red</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Indian red</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, under <er>Indian</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Persian wheel</b></col>, <cd>a noria; a tympanum. See
<er>Noria</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persian</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sian</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or inhabitant
of Persia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The language spoken in Persia. Ancient Persian of
the 3rd to 10th centuries is also called <stype>Pahlavi</stype>, and
modern Persian is also called <stype>Farsi</stype>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A thin silk fabric, used formerly for linings.</def>
<rj><au>Beck.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>See
<cref>Persian columns</cref>, under <er>Persian</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persic</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Persicus</ets>. Cf. <er>Persian</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or relating to
Persia.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The Persian
language.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persicaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`si*ca"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
LL. <ets>persicarius</ets> a peach tree. See <er>Peach</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <er>Lady's thumb</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persico</ent><br/
<hw>Per"si*co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>same as
<er>Persicot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persicot</ent><br/
<hw>Per"si*cot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Peach</er>.]</ety> <def>A cordial made of the kernels of apricots,
nectarines, etc., with refined spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persienne</ent><br/
<hw>Per`si*enne"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`s<icr/*<ecr/n";
p<etil/r`sh<icr/*<ecr/n"; <it>F.</it> p<etil/r`sy<ecr/n")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fem. of <ets>presien</ets> Persian.]</ety>
<def>Properly, printed calico, whether Oriental or of fanciful design
with flowers, etc., in Western work. Hence, as extended in English,
material of a similar character.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persiennes</ent><br/
<hw>Per`si*ennes"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`s<icr/*<ecr/nz"; <it>F.</it>
p<etil/r`sy<ecr/n")</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>Window
blinds having movable slats, similar to Venetian blinds.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persiflage</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`si`flage"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>persifler</ets> to quiz, fr. L. <ets>per</ets> + <ets>siffler</ets>
to whistle, hiss, L. <ets>sibilare</ets>, <ets>sifilare</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Frivolous or bantering talk; a frivolous manner of treating any
subject, whether serious or otherwise; light raillery.</def>
<rj><au>Hannah More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persifleur</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`si`fleur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>One who indulges in persiflage; a banterer; a quiz.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persimmon</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sim"mon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Virginia
Indian.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An American tree (<spn>Diospyros
Virginiana</spn>) and its fruit, found from New York southward. The
fruit is like a plum in appearance, but is very harsh and astringent
until it has been exposed to frost, when it becomes palatable and
nutritious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Japanese persimmon</b></col>, <cd><spn>Diospyros
Kaki</spn> and its red or yellow edible fruit, which outwardly resembles
a tomato, but contains a few large seeds.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persis</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A kind of coloring matter obtained from
lichens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persism</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An ancient Persian
religion, esp. as represented by the Magi; a form of
<isa>Zoroastrianism</isa>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>This system we shall call <qex>'Persism'</qex>, in order to free
ourselves of the popular associations still connected with such terms as
magism, Parseeism, and so forth; meaning by <qex>'Persism'</qex> the
teaching of <persfn>Zarathustra</persfn> as it affected the Greek and
Latin world.</q> <rj><qau>E. Vernon Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persism</ent><br/
<hw>Per"sism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Persian
idiom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persist</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sist"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Persisted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Persisting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>persistere</ets>;
<ets>per + sistere</ets> to stand or be fixed, fr. <ets>stare</ets> to
stand: cf. F. <ets>persister</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and
<er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <def>To stand firm; to be fixed and unmoved; to
stay; to continue steadfastly; especially, to continue fixed in a course
of conduct against opposing motives; to persevere; -- sometimes
conveying an unfavorable notion, as of doggedness or
obstinacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If they <qex>persist</qex> in pointing their batteries against
particular persons, no laws of war forbid the making reprisals.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some positive, <qex>persisting</qex> fops we know,<br/
Who, if once wrong, will needs be always so.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That face <qex>persists</qex>.<br/
It floats up; it turns over in my mind.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Persevere</er>, and
<er>Insist</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persistency</ent><br/
<ent>Persistence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*sist"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per*sist"en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Persistent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
being persistent; staying or continuing quality; hence, in an
unfavorable sense, doggedness; obstinacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The continuance of an effect after the cause which
first gave rise to it is removed</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>The <ex>persistence</ex> of motion.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Visual <ex>persistence</ex>, or
<ex>persistence</ex> of the visual impression; auditory
<ex>persistence</ex>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persistent</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sist"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>persistens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>persistere</ets>. See <er>Persist</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Inclined to persist; having staying qualities; tenacious of
position or purpose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Remaining beyond the period when
parts of the same kind sometimes fall off or are absorbed; permanent;
<as>as, <ex>persistent</ex> teeth or gills; a <ex>persistent</ex>
calyx</as>; -- opposed to <contr>deciduous</contr>, and
<contr>caducous</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persistently</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sist"ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a persistent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persisting</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sist"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclined to persist; tenacious
of purpose; persistent.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Per*sist"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persistive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sist"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Persistent</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persnickety</ent><br/
<hw>Per*snick"et*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Finicky or fussy; demanding excessive attention to petty details or
niceties; unduly particular; fastidious; -- of people.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> finical, finicky, fussy, meticulous, painstaking,
pernickety, pernicketty, punctilious.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Snobbish; behaving aloofly.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pernickety, pernicketty.</syn>
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persolve</ent><br/
<hw>Per*solve"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>persolvere</ets>.]</ety> <def>To pay wholly, or fully.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>E. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Person</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"s'n; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>persone</ets>, <ets>persoun</ets>, <ets>person</ets>,
<ets>parson</ets>, OF. <ets>persone</ets>, F. <ets>personne</ets>, L.
<ets>persona</ets> a mask (used by actors), a personage, part, a person,
fr. <ets>personare</ets> to sound through; <ets>per</ets> +
<ets>sonare</ets> to sound. See <er>Per-</er>, and cf.
<er>Parson</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A character or part, as in a
play; a specific kind or manifestation of individual character, whether
in real life, or in literary or dramatic representation; an assumed
character.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His first appearance upon the stage in his new <qex>person</qex>
of a sycophant or juggler.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man can long put on a <qex>person</qex> and act a part.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To bear rule, which was thy part<br/
And <qex>person</qex>, hadst thou known thyself aright.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How different is the same man from himself, as he sustains the
<qex>person</qex> of a magistrate and that of a friend!</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The bodily form of a human being; body; outward
appearance; <as>as, of comely <ex>person</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A fair <qex>persone</qex>, and strong, and young of age.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If it assume my noble father's <qex>person</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Love, sweetness, goodness, in her <qex>person</qex> shined.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A living, self-conscious being, as distinct from an
animal or a thing; a moral agent; a human being; a man, woman, or
child.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Consider what <qex>person</qex> stands for; which, I think, is a
thinking, intelligent being, that has reason and reflection.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A human being spoken of indefinitely; one; a man;
<as>as, any <ex>person</ex> present</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A parson; the parish priest.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>Among Trinitarians, one of the
three subdivisions of the Godhead (the Father, the Son, and the Holy
Ghost); an hypostasis.</def> <ldquo/Three <xex>persons</xex> and one
God.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bk. of Com. Prayer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>One of three relations or
conditions (that of speaking, that of being spoken to, and that of being
spoken of) pertaining to a noun or a pronoun, and thence also to the
verb of which it may be the subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A noun or pronoun, when representing the speaker, is
said to be in the first <xex>person</xex>; when representing what is
spoken to, in the second <xex>person</xex>; when representing what is
spoken of, in the third <xex>person</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A shoot or bud of a plant; a
polyp or zooid of the compound Hydrozoa, Anthozoa, etc.; also, an
individual, in the narrowest sense, among the higher animals.</def>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>True corms, composed of united person<ae/ . . . usually arise by
gemmation, . . . yet in sponges and corals occasionally by fusion of
several originally distinct <qex>persons</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc.
Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Artificial person</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Fictitious person</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a
corporation or body politic; -- this term is used in contrast with
<contr>natural person</contr>, a real human being. See also <cref>legal
person</cref>.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>Legal
person</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an individual or group that is
allowed by law to take legal action, as plaintiff or defendent. It may
include natural persons as well as fictitious persons (such as
corporations).</cd> -- <col><b>Natural person</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a man, woman, or child, in distinction from a
corporation.</cd> -- <col><b>In person</b></col>, <cd>by one's self;
with bodily presence, rather than by remote communication; not by
representative.</cd> <ldquo/The king himself <xex>in person</xex> is set
forth.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>In the person of</b></col>,
<cd>in the place of; acting for.</cd> <au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Person</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To represent as a
person; to personify; to impersonate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persona</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per*so"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Person<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Person</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*a*ble</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"s<ucr/n*<adot/*b'l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a well-formed body, or person;
graceful; comely; of good appearance; presentable; <as>as, a
<ex>personable</ex> man or woman</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wise, warlike, <qex>personable</qex>, courteous, and kind.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king, . . . so visited with sickness, was not
<qex>personable</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>E. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Enabled to maintain
pleas in court.</def> <au>Cowell.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having capacity
to take anything granted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>personableness</ent><br/
<hw>personableness</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The complex of attributes
that make a person socially attractive.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personage</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*age</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"s<ucr/n*<asl/j: 48)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>personnage</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Form, appearance, or belongings of a person; the external
appearance, stature, figure, air, and the like, of a person.</def>
<ldquo/In <xex>personage</xex> stately.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hayward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The damsel well did view his <qex>personage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Character assumed or represented.</def> <ldquo/The
actors and <xex>personages</xex> of this fable.<rdquo/ <au>Broome.</au>
<ldquo/Disguised in a false <xex>personage</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Addison.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A notable or distinguished person; a conspicuous or
peculiar character; <as>as, an illustrious <ex>personage</ex>; a comely
<ex>personage</ex> of stature tall.</as></def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personal</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*al</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"s<ucr/n*<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>personalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>personnel</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to human beings as distinct from
things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every man so termed by way of <qex>personal</qex> difference.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a particular person; relating
to, or affecting, an individual, or each of many individuals; peculiar
or proper to private concerns; not public or general; <as>as,
<ex>personal</ex> comfort; <ex>personal</ex> desire.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The words are conditional, -- If thou doest well, -- and so
<qex>personal</qex> to Cain.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pertaining to the external or bodily appearance;
corporeal; <as>as, <ex>personal</ex> charms</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Done in person; without the intervention of
another.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Personal</xex> communication.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fabyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The immediate and <qex>personal</qex> speaking of God.</q>
<rj><qau>White.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Relating to an individual, his character, conduct,
motives, or private affairs, in an invidious and offensive manner;
<as>as, <ex>personal</ex> reflections or remarks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Denoting person; <as>as, a
<ex>personal</ex> pronoun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Personal action</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a suit or
action by which a man claims a debt or personal duty, or damages in lieu
of it; or wherein he claims satisfaction in damages for an injury to his
person or property, or the specific recovery of goods or chattels; --
opposed to <xex>real action</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Personal
equation</b></col>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Equation</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Personal estate</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Personal property</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>movables; chattels; -- opposed to <contr>real estate</contr> or
<contr>real property</contr>. It usually consists of things temporary
and movable, including all subjects of property not of a freehold
nature.</cd> -- <col><b>Personal identity</b></col>
<fld>(Metaph.)</fld>, <cd>the persistent and continuous unity of the
individual person, which is attested by consciousness.</cd> --
<col><b>Personal pronoun</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>one of the
pronouns <stype>I</stype>, <stype>thou</stype>, <stype>he</stype>,
<stype>she</stype>, <stype>it</stype>, and their plurals.</cd> --
<col><b>Personal representatives</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the
executors or administrators of a person deceased.</cd> --
<col><b>Personal rights</b></col>, <cd>rights appertaining to the
person; <as>as, the rights of a <ex>personal</ex> security,
<ex>personal</ex> liberty, and private property</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Personal tithes</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Tithe</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Personal verb</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>a verb which is
modified or inflected to correspond with the three
persons.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personal</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A movable; a
chattel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>personal computer</ent><br/
<hw>personal computer</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A computer{2} designed for use by one person
at a time; -- contrasted with shared-time computers such as a
<contr>mainframe</contr>, <contr>server</contr> or
<contr>minicomputer</contr>, which may be accessed by multiple users
each operating from a different input device (in the 1990's, usually a
terminal or personal computer). A <er>personal computer</er> typically
uses a microprocessor for its CPU.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> PC, microcomputer.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personalism</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being personal; personality.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personality</ent><br/
<hw>Per`son*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Personalities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>personnalit<eacute/</ets>. Cf. <er>Personality</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That which constitutes distinction of person; the
externally evident aspects of the character or behavior of a person;
individuality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Personality</qex> is individuality existing in itself, but
with a nature as a ground.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something said or written which refers to the
person, conduct, etc., of some individual, especially something of a
disparaging or offensive nature; personal remarks; <as>as, indulgence in
<ex>personalities</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sharp <qex>personalities</qex> were exchanged.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>That quality of a law which
concerns the condition, state, and capacity of persons.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A person who is famous or notable; a
celebrity.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personalize</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Personalized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Personalizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To make personal.</def> <ldquo/They
<xex>personalize</xex> death.<rdquo/ <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>personalized</ent><br/
<hw>personalized</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Made for or
directed or adjusted to a particular individual; modified for a
particular person; <as>as, <ex>personalized</ex> luggage;
<ex>personalized</ex> advice</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> individualized.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pointedly referring to or concerning a person's
individual personality or intimate affairs, especially offensively;
<as>as, <ex>personalized</ex> criticism</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pointed.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personally</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a personal
manner; by bodily presence; in person; not by representative or
substitute; <as>as, to deliver a letter
<ex>personally</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He, being cited, <qex>personally</qex> came not.</q>
<rj><qau>Grafton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With respect to an individual; as regards the
person; individually; particularly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She bore a mortal hatred to the house of Lancaster, and
<qex>personally</qex> to the king.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>With respect to one's individuality; as regards
one's self; <as>as, <ex>personally</ex> I have no feeling in the
matter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personalty</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state of being a person; personality.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Personal property, as distinguished
from realty or real property.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Personated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Personating</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>personare</ets> to cry out, LL., to extol. See
<er>Person</er>.]</ety> <def>To celebrate loudly; to extol; to
praise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In fable, hymn, or song so <qex>personating</qex><br/
Their gods ridiculous.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*ate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>personatus</ets>
masked, assumed, fictitious, fr. <ets>persona</ets> a mask. See
<er>Person</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To assume the character of; to
represent by a fictitious appearance; to act the part of; to
impersonate{3}; hence, to counterfeit; to feign; <as>as, he tried to
<ex>personate</ex> his brother; a <ex>personated</ex>
devotion.</as></def> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set forth in an unreal character; to disguise; to
mask.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>personated</xex>
mate.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To personify; to typify; to describe.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*ate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To play or assume a
character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>personatus</ets> masked.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the
throat of a bilabiate corolla nearly closed by a projection of the base
of the lower lip; masked, as in the flower of the snapdragon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`son*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
personating, or conterfeiting the person or character of another;
impersonation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*a`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
personates.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>personators</xex> of these
actions.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personeity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`son*e"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Personality.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personification</ent><br/
<hw>Per*son`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>personnification</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
personifying; impersonation; embodiment.</def> <rj><au>C.
Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure of speech in which an
inanimate object or abstract idea is represented as animated, or endowed
with personality; prosopop<oe/ia; <as>as, the floods clap their
hands</as>.</def> <ldquo/Confusion heards his voice.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personifier</ent><br/
<hw>Per*son"i*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
personifies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personify</ent><br/
<hw>Per*son"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Personified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Personifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Person</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>personnifier</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To regard, treat, or
represent as a person; to represent as a rational being.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The poets take the liberty of <qex>personifying</qex> inanimate
things.</q> <rj><qau>Chesterfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be the embodiment or personification of; to
impersonate; <as>as, he <ex>personifies</ex> the law</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personize</ent><br/
<hw>Per"son*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
personify.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Milton has <qex>personized</qex> them.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Richardson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Personnel</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per`son`nel"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Personal</er>.]</ety> <def>The body of persons employed in some
public service, as the army, navy, etc.; -- distinguished from
<contr>materiel</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>person to person</ent><br/
<ent>person-to-person</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>person-to-person</hw>, <hw>person to person</hw></mhw>
<pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>By direct contact between
persons.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Without intervening persons.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> face-to-face.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persoonia</ent><br/
<hw>Persoonia</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of Australian
undershrubs to small trees; the geebungs.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Persoonia</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspective</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spec"tive</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*sp<ecr/k"t<icr/v)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perspicere</ets>, <ets>perspectum</ets>, to
look through; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>spicere</ets>, <ets>specere</ets>,
to look: cf. F. <ets>perspectif</ets>; or from E.
<ets>perspective</ets>, n. See <er>Spy</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the science of vision;
optical.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to the art, or in accordance with the
laws, of perspective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Perspective plane</b></col>, <cd>the plane or surface on
which the objects are delineated, or the picture drawn; the plane of
projection; -- distinguished from the <contr>ground plane</contr>, which
is that on which the objects are represented as standing. When this
plane is oblique to the principal face of the object, the perspective is
called <stype>oblique perspective</stype>; when parallel to that face,
<stype>parallel perspective</stype>.</cd> -- <col><b>Perspective
shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any shell of the genus
<gen>Solarium</gen> and allied genera. See
<er>Solarium</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspective</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spec"tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>perspective</ets>,
fr. <ets>perspectif</ets>: cf. It. <ets>perspettiva</ets>. See
<er>Perspective</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A glass
through which objects are viewed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Not a
<xex>perspective</xex>, but a mirror.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is seen through an opening; a view; a
vista.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>perspective</xex> of life.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The effect of distance upon the appearance of
objects, by means of which the eye recognizes them as being at a more or
less measurable distance. Hence, <xex>a<eum/rial perspective</xex>, the
assumed greater vagueness or uncertainty of outline in distant
objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A<eum/rial <qex>perspective</qex> is the expression of space by
any means whatsoever, sharpness of edge, vividness of color, etc.</q>
<rj><qau>Ruskin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The art and the science of so delineating objects
that they shall seem to grow smaller as they recede from the eye; --
called also <altname>linear perspective</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A drawing in linear perspective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Isometrical perspective</b></col>, <cd>an inaccurate term
for a mechanical way of representing objects in the direction of the
diagonal of a cube.</cd> -- <col><b>Perspective glass</b></col>, <cd>a
telescope which shows objects in the right position.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1071 --></p>

<p><ent>Perspectively</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spec"tive*ly</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*sp<ecr/k"t<icr/v*l<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Optically; as through a glass.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You see them <qex>perspectively</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>According to the rules of perspective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspectograph</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spec"to*graph</hw>
<pr>(p<etil/r*sp<ecr/k"t<osl/*gr<adot/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perspectus</ets> (p. p. of <ets>perspicere</ets> to look through) +
<ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for obtaining, and
transferring to a picture, the points and outlines of objects, so as to
represent them in their proper geometrical relations as viewed from some
one point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspectography</ent><br/
<hw>Per`spec*tog"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(p<etil/r`sp<ecr/k*t<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The science or art of delineating objects according to the laws of
perspective; the theory of perspective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicable</ent><br/
<hw>Per"spi*ca*ble</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"sp<icr/*k<adot/*b'l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perspicabilis</ets>, fr.
<ets>perspicere</ets>.]</ety> <def>Discernible.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicacious</ent><br/
<hw>Per`spi*ca"cious</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r`sp<icr/*k<amac/"sh<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perspicax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>, fr.
<ets>perspicere</ets> to look through: cf. F. <ets>perspicace</ets>. See
<er>Perspective</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the power of seeing
clearly; quick-sighted; sharp of sight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Of acute discernment; keen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per`spi*ca"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per`spi*ca"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicacity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`spi*cac"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(p<etil/r`sp<icr/*k<acr/s"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perspicacitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perspicacit<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Perspicacious</er>.]</ety> <def>The state of being perspicacious;
acuteness of sight or of intelligence; acute discernment.</def>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicacy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"spi*ca*cy</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"sp<icr/*k<adot/*s<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Perspicacity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicience</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spi"cience</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*sp<icr/sh"<eit/ns)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>perspicientia</ets>, fr.
<ets>perspiciens</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perspicere</ets>. See
<er>Perspective</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of looking sharply.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicil</ent><br/
<hw>Per"spi*cil</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r"sp<icr/*s<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[LL. <ets>perspicilla</ets>, fr. L. <ets>perspicere</ets> to look
through.]</ety> <def>An optical glass; a telescope.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Crashaw.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicuity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`spi*cu"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(p<etil/r`sp<icr/*k<umac/"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perspicuitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perspicuit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being transparent or
translucent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality of being perspicuous to the
understanding; clearness of expression or thought.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Sagacity; perspicacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Clearness; perspicuousness; plainness;
distinctness; lucidity; transparency. See <er>Clearness</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspicuous</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spic"u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perspicuus</ets>, from <ets>perspicere</ets> to look through. See
<er>Perspective</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being through;
transparent; translucent; not opaque.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Peacham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Clear to the understanding; capable of being clearly
understood; clear in thought or in expression; not obscure or ambiguous;
<as>as, a <ex>perspicuous</ex> writer; <ex>perspicuous</ex>
statements.</as></def> <ldquo/The purpose is
<xex>perspicuous</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per*spic"u*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per*spic"u*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspirability</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spir`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being perspirable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspirable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spir"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perspirable</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being
perspired.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Emitting perspiration; perspiring.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspiration</ent><br/
<hw>Per`spi*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perspiration</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of
perspiring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is excreted through the skin;
sweat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A man of average weight throws off through the skin
during 24 hours about 18 ounces of water, 300 grains of solid matter,
and 400 grains of carbonic acid gas. Ordinarily, this constant
exhalation is not apparent, and the excretion is then termed
<xex>insensible perspiration</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspirative</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spir"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Performing the
act of perspiration; perspiratory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspiratory</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spir"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or producing, perspiration; <as>as, the
<ex>perspiratory</ex> ducts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspire</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spire"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perspired</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Perspiring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>perspirare</ets> to breathe through; <ets>per + spirare</ets>. See
<er>Per-</er>, and <er>Spirit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To excrete matter through the skin; esp., to
excrete fluids through the pores of the skin; to sweat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be evacuated or excreted, or to exude, through
the pores of the skin; <as>as, a fluid
<ex>perspires</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perspire</ent><br/
<hw>Per*spire"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To emit or evacuate through
the pores of the skin; to sweat; to excrete through pores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Firs . . . <qex>perspire</qex> a fine balsam of turpentine.</q>
<rj><qau>Smollett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perstreperous</ent><br/
<hw>Per*strep"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perstrepere</ets> to make a great noise.]</ety> <def>Noisy;
obstreperous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perstringe</ent><br/
<hw>Per*stringe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perstringere</ets>; <ets>per + stringere</ets> to bind up, to touch
upon.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To touch; to graze; to glance on.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To criticise; to touch upon.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuadable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*suad"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
persuaded.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Per*suad"a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos> -- <wf>Per*suad"a*bly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuade</ent><br/
<hw>Per*suade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Persuaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Persuading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>persuadere</ets>,
<ets>persuasum</ets>; <ets>per + suadere</ets> to advise, persuade: cf.
F. <ets>persuader</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Suasion</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To influence or gain over by argument, advice,
entreaty, expostulation, etc.; to draw or incline to a determination by
presenting sufficient motives.</def><-- "gain over" = win over, win to
one's side --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Almost thou <qex>persuadest</qex> me to be a Christian.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xxvi. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We will <qex>persuade</qex> him, be it possible.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To try to influence.</def>
<mark>[Obsolescent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hearken not unto Hezekiah, when he <qex>persuadeth</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Kings xviii. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To convince by argument, or by reasons offered or
suggested from reflection, etc.; to cause to believe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beloved, we are <qex>persuaded</qex> better things of you.</q>
<rj><qau>Heb. vi. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To inculcate by argument or expostulation; to
advise; to recommend.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To convince; induce; prevail on; win over;
allure; entice. See <er>Convince</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuade</ent><br/
<hw>Per*suade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To use
persuasion; to plead; to prevail by persuasion.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuade</ent><br/
<hw>Per*suade"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Persuasion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuaded</ent><br/
<hw>Per*suad"ed</hw>, <pos>p. p. & a.</pos> <def>Prevailed upon;
influenced by argument or entreaty; convinced.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*suad"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per*suad"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuader</ent><br/
<hw>Per*suad"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, persuades or influences.</def> <ldquo/Powerful
<xex>persuaders</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuasibility</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sua`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Capability
of being persuaded.</def> <rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuasible</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sua"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>persuasibilis</ets> persuasive, F. <ets>persuasible</ets>
persuasible.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being persuaded;
persuadable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Persuasive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per*sua"si*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Per*sua"si*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuasion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sua"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>persuasio</ets>; Cf. F. <ets>persuasion</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of persuading; the act of influencing the mind by arguments
or reasons offered, or by anything that moves the mind or passions, or
inclines the will to a determination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For thou hast all the arts of fine <qex>persuasion</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Otway.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being persuaded or convinced; settled
opinion or conviction, which has been induced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the general <qex>persuasion</qex> of all men does so account
it.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My firm <qex>persuasion</qex> is, at least sometimes,<br/
That Heaven will weigh man's virtues and his crimes<br/
With nice attention.</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A creed or belief; a sect or party adhering to a
certain creed or system of opinions; <as>as, of the same
<ex>persuasion</ex>; all <ex>persuasions</ex> are agreed.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of whatever state or <qex>persuasion</qex>, religious or
political.</q> <rj><qau>Jefferson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The power or quality of persuading;
persuasiveness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is 't possible that my deserts to you<br/
Can lack <qex>persuasion</qex>?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which persuades; a persuasive.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Conviction</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuasive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sua"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>persuasif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to persuade; having the power
of persuading; <as>as, <ex>persuasive</ex> eloquence</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Persuasive</xex> words.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuasive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sua"sive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which persuades; an
inducement; an incitement; an exhortation.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*sua"sive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per*sua"sive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persuasory</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sua"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Persuasive.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persulphate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sul"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A sulphate of the peroxide of any base.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persulphide</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sul"phide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A sulphide containing more sulphur than some other compound of the
same elements; <as>as, iron pyrites is a <ex>persulphide</ex></as>; --
formerly called <altname>persulphuret</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persulphocyanate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sul`pho*cy"a*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of persulphocyanic acid.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persulphocyanic</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sul`pho*cy*an"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, a yellow
crystalline substance (called also <xex>perthiocyanic acid</xex>),
analogous to sulphocyanic acid, but containing more sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persulphocyanogen</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sul`pho*cy*an"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An orange-yellow substance, produced by the
action of chlorine or boiling dilute nitric acid and sulphocyanate of
potassium; -- called also <altname>pseudosulphocyanogen</altname>,
<altname>perthiocyanogen</altname>, and formerly
<altname>sulphocyanogen</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Persulphuret</ent><br/
<hw>Per*sul"phu*ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A persulphide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pert</ent><br/
<hw>Pert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[An aphetic form of OE. &
OF. <ets>apert</ets> open, known, true, free, or impudent. See
<er>Apert</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Open; evident; apert.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lively; brisk; sprightly; smart.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Indecorously free, or presuming; saucy; bold;
impertinent.</def> <ldquo/A very <xex>pert</xex> manner.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The squirrel, flippant, <qex>pert</qex>, and full of play.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pert</ent><br/
<hw>Pert</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To behave with pertness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertain</ent><br/
<hw>Per*tain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pertained</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pertaining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>partenen</ets>, OF. <ets>partenir</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>pertinere</ets> to stretch out, reach, pertain; <ets>per +
tenere</ets> to hold, keep. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Tenable</er>, and
cf. <er>Appertain</er>, <er>Pertinent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
belong; to have connection with, or dependence on, something, as an
appurtenance, attribute, etc.; to appertain; <as>as, saltness
<ex>pertains</ex> to the ocean; flowers <ex>pertain</ex> to plant
life.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men hate those who affect that honor by ambition which
<qex>pertaineth</qex> not to them.</q> <rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have relation or reference to
something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These words <qex>pertain</qex> unto us at this time as they
<qex>pertained</qex> to them at their time.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perterebration</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ter`e*bra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perterebratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>perterebrare</ets> to bore
through.]</ety> <def>The act of boring through.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perthiocyanogen</ent><br/
<hw>Per*thi`o*cy*an"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Persulphocyanogen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perthite</ent><br/
<hw>Perth"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
<ets>Perth</ets>, in canada.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of
feldspar consisting of a laminated intertexture of albite and
orthoclase, usually of different colors.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*thit"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinacious</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ti*na"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pertinax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>; <ets>per + tenax</ets> tenacious.
See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Tenacious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Holding or adhering to any opinion, purpose, or design, with
obstinacy; perversely persistent; obstinate; <as>as,
<ex>pertinacious</ex> plotters; a <ex>pertinacious</ex>
beggar.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resolute; persevering; constant; steady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Diligence is a steady, constant, and <qex>pertinacious</qex>
study.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Obstinate; stubborn; inflexible; unyielding;
resolute; determined; firm; constant; steady.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Per`ti*na"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per`ti*na"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinacity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`ti*nac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pertinacit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
pertinacious; obstinacy; perseverance; persistency.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Obstinacy</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinacy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ti*na*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pertinere</ets> to pertain. See <er>Pertinence</er>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being pertinent; pertinence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinacy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ti*na*cy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pertinacia</ets>, fr.
<ets>pertinax</ets>. See <er>Pertinacious</er>.]</ety>
<def>Pertinacity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ti*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertinacious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinately</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ti*nate*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Pertinaciously.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinency</ent><br/
<ent>Pertinence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per"ti*nence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per"ti*nen*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pertinence</ets>.
See <er>Pertinent</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
pertinent; justness of relation to the subject or matter in hand;
fitness; appositeness; relevancy; suitableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fitness and <qex>pertinency</qex> of the apostle's
discourse.</q> <rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertinent</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ti*nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pertinens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>pertinere</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>pertinent</ets>. See <er>Pertain</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Belonging or related to the subject or matter in hand; fit or
appropriate in any way; adapted to the end proposed; apposite; material;
relevant; <as>as, <ex>pertinent</ex> illustrations or arguments;
<ex>pertinent</ex> evidence.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Regarding; concerning; belonging; pertaining.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pertinent</xex> unto faith.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Apposite; relevant; suitable; appropriate;
fit.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Per"ti*nent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per"ti*nent*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertly</ent><br/
<hw>Pert"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pert
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertness</ent><br/
<hw>Pert"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
pert.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertransient</ent><br/
<hw>Per*tran"sient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pertransiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>pertransire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Passing through or over.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturb</ent><br/
<hw>Per*turb"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perturbare</ets>, <ets>perturbatum</ets>; <ets>per + turbare</ets>
to disturb, fr. <ets>turba</ets> a disorder: cf. OF.
<ets>perturber</ets>. See <er>Per-</er>, and <er>Turbid</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To disturb; to agitate; to vex; to trouble; to
disquiet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye that . . . <qex>perturb</qex> so my feast with crying.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To disorder; to confuse.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbability</ent><br/
<hw>Per*turb`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being perturbable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbable</ent><br/
<hw>Per*turb"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Liable to be
perturbed or agitated; liable to be disturbed or disquieted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbance</ent><br/
<hw>Per*turb"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Disturbance;
perturbation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Perturbance</xex> of
the mind.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sharp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"tur*bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>perturbatus</ets>, p. p.]</ety> <def>To perturb.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbate</ent><br/
<hw>Per"tur*bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Perturbed;
agitated.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbation</ent><br/
<hw>Per`tur*ba"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perturbatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perturbation</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of perturbing, or the state of being perturbed;
esp., agitation of mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A disturbance in the regular
elliptic or other motion of a heavenly body, produced by some force
additional to that which causes its regular motion; <as>as, the
<ex>perturbations</ex> of the planets are caused by their attraction on
each other</as>.</def> <rj><au>Newcomb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbational</ent><br/
<hw>Per`tur*ba"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to perturbation, esp. to the perturbations of the
planets.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>perturbational</xex> theory.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir J. Herschel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbative</ent><br/
<hw>Per"tur*ba*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
cause perturbation; disturbing.</def> <rj><au>Sir J.
Herschel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbator</ent><br/
<hw>Per"tur*ba`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
perturber.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturbed</ent><br/
<hw>Per*turbed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Agitated;
disturbed; troubled.</def> <au>Shak.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Per*turb"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perturber</ent><br/
<hw>Per*turb"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, perturbs, or cause perturbation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertusate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*tus"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pertuse</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Pierced at the
apex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertused</ent><br/
<ent>Pertuse</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Per*tuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Per*tused"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pertusus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>pertundere</ets> to beat or thrust through, to bore through;
<ets>per + tundere</ets> to beat: cf. F. <ets>pertus</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pierce</er>.]</ety> <def>Punched; pierced with, or having,
holes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertusion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*tu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pertusio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of punching or piercing with a
pointed instrument; <as>as, <ex>pertusion</ex> of a vein</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A punched hole; a perforation.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pertussis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per*tus"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>per</ets> through, very + <ets>tussis</ets> cough.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The whooping cough.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peruke</ent><br/
<hw>Per"uke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>perruque</ets>, It. <ets>perrucca</ets>, <ets>parrucca</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>pilus</ets> hair. Cf. <er>Periwig</er>, <er>Wig</er>,
<er>Peel</er> to strip off, <er>Plush</er>, <er>Pile</er> a hair.]</ety>
<def>A wig; a periwig.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peruke</ent><br/
<hw>Per"uke</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dress with a peruke.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Per"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Perul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., dim. of
<ets>pera</ets> wallet, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/rule</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the scales of a leaf
bud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A pouchlike portion of the
perianth in certain orchides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perule</ent><br/
<hw>Per"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Perula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perusal</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rus"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Peruse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of carefully viewing or
examining.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of reading, especially of reading through or
with care.</def> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peruse</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ruse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Perused</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perusing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>per-</ets> +
<ets>use</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To observe; to examine with
care.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Myself I then <qex>perused</qex>, and limb by limb<br/
Surveyed.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To read through; to read carefully.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peruser</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*rus"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
peruses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peruvian</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ru"vi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/ruvien</ets>, Sp. <ets>peruviano</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to Peru, in South America.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A native or an inhabitant of Peru.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peruvian balsam</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Balsam of
Peru</cref>, under <er>Balsam</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Peruvian
bark</b></col>, <cd>the bitter bark of trees of various species of
Cinchona. It acts as a powerful tonic, and is a remedy for malarial
diseases. This property is due to several alkaloids, as quinine,
cinchonine, etc., and their compounds; -- called also <altname>Jesuit's
bark</altname>, and <altname>cinchona</altname>. See
<er>Cinchona</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervade</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vade"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*v<amac/d")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pervaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pervading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervadere</ets>, <ets>pervasum</ets>; <ets>per</ets> +
<ets>vadere</ets> to go, to walk. See <er>Per-</er>, and
<er>Wade</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass or flow through, as an
aperture, pore, or interstice; to permeate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That labyrinth is easily <qex>pervaded</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass or spread through the whole extent of; to be
diffused throughout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A spirit of cabal, intrigue, and proselytism <qex>pervaded</qex>
all their thoughts, words, and actions.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pervading</ent><br/
<hw>pervading</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Being spread
throughout.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pervasive.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pervaporation</ent><br/
<hw>pervaporation</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The concentration of a
colloidal solution whose colloid will not pass through a semipermeable
membrane; the solution is placed in a bag of the membrane and the
solvent is evaporated off.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervasion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*va"sion</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*v<amac/"zh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pervasio</ets>. See <er>Pervade</er>.]</ety> <def>The act
of pervading, passing, or spreading through the whole extent of a
thing.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervasive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*va"sive</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*v<amac/"s<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Tending to pervade, or having power to spread throughout; of a
pervading quality.</def> <ldquo/Civilization <xex>pervasive</xex> and
general.<rdquo/ <rj><au>M. Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pervasiveness</ent><br/
<hw>pervasiveness</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of filling or
spreading throughout; <as>as, the <ex>pervasiveness</ex> of the odor of
cabbage in tenement hallways</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1072 --></p>

<p><ent>Perverse</ent><br/
<hw>Per*verse"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*v<etil/rs")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>perversus</ets> turned the wrong way, not right, p. p. of
<ets>pervertere</ets>to turn around, to overturn: cf. F.
<ets>pervers</ets>. See <er>Pervert</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Turned
aside; hence, specifically, turned away from the right; willfully
erring; wicked; perverted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The only righteous in a world <qex>perverse</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Obstinate in the wrong; stubborn; intractable;
hence, wayward; vexing; contrary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To so <qex>perverse</qex> a sex all grace is vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Froward; untoward; wayward; stubborn;
ungovernable; intractable; cross; petulant; vexatious.</syn> <usage> --
<er>Perverse</er>, <er>Froward</er>. One who is <xex>froward</xex> is
capricious, and reluctant to obey. One who is <xex>perverse</xex> has a
settled obstinacy of will, and likes or dislikes by the rule of
contradiction to the will of others.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perversed</ent><br/
<hw>Per*versed"</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*v<etil/rst")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Turned aside.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perversedly</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vers"ed*ly</hw> <pr>(p<etil/r*v<etil/rs"<ecr/d*l<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>Perversely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perversely</ent><br/
<hw>Per*verse"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a perverse
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perverseness</ent><br/
<hw>Per*verse"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being perverse.</def> <ldquo/Virtue hath some
<xex>perverseness</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perversion</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ver"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perversio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perversion</ets>. See
<er>Pervert</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of perverting, or the state of
being perverted; a turning from truth or right; a diverting from the
true intent or object; a change to something worse; a turning or
applying to a wrong end or use.</def> <ldquo/Violations and
<xex>perversions</xex> of the laws.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perversity</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ver"si*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>perversitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>perversit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being perverse; perverseness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perversive</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ver"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><def>Tending to
pervert.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervert</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vert"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Perverted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Perverting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>pervertir</ets>, L.
<ets>pervertere</ets>, <ets>perversum</ets>; <ets>per</ets> +
<ets>vertere</ets> to turn. See <er>Per-</er>, and
<er>Verse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To turn another way; to
divert.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let's follow him, and <qex>pervert</qex> the present wrath.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To turn from truth, rectitude, or propriety; to
divert from a right use, end, or way; to lead astray; to corrupt; also,
to misapply; to misinterpret intentionally; <as>as, to <ex>pervert</ex>
one's words</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He, in the serpent, had <qex>perverted</qex> Eve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervert</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vert"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become perverted; to take
the wrong course.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Testament of
Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervert</ent><br/
<hw>Per"vert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has been
perverted; one who has turned to error, especially in religion; --
opposed to <contr>convert</contr>. See the Synonym of
<er>Convert</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That notorious <qex>pervert</qex>, Henry of Navarre.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perverter</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vert"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who perverts
(a person or thing).</def> <ldquo/His own parents his
<xex>perverters</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>South.</au> <ldquo/A
<xex>perverter</xex> of his law.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Stillingfleet.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervertible</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vert"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being perverted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervestigate</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ves"ti*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervestigatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pervestigare</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To investigate thoroughly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervestigation</ent><br/
<hw>Per*ves`ti*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervestigatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Thorough investigation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chillingworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervial</ent><br/
<hw>Per"vi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pervious</er>.]</ety> <def>Pervious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Per"vi*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervicacious</ent><br/
<hw>Per`vi*ca"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervicax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Obstinate; willful;
refractory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Per`vi*ca"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Per`vi*ca"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervicacity</ent><br/
<hw>Per`vi*cac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Obstinacy;
pervicaciousness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bentley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervicacy</ent><br/
<hw>Per"vi*ca*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervicacia</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pervicacity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervigilation</ent><br/
<hw>Per*vig`i*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervigilatio</ets>, fr. <ets>pervigilare</ets>.]</ety> <def>Careful
watching.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervious</ent><br/
<hw>Per"vi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pervis</ets>; <ets>per</ets> + <ets>via</ets> a way. See
<er>Per-</er>, and <er>Voyage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Admitting
passage; capable of being penetrated by another body or substance;
permeable; <as>as, a <ex>pervious</ex> soil</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Doors] . . . <qex>pervious</qex> to winds, and open every
way.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being penetrated, or seen through, by
physical or mental vision.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God, whose secrets are <qex>pervious</qex> to no eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Capable of penetrating or pervading.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Open; -- used synonymously with
<altname>perforate</altname>, as applied to the nostrils or
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Perviousness</ent><br/
<hw>Per"vi*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being pervious; <as>as, the <ex>perviousness</ex> of glass</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pervis</ent><br/
<hw>Per"vis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parvis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pery</ent><br/
<hw>Per"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pear tree. See
<er>Pirie</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pes</hw> <pr>(p<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pedes</plw> .</plu> <ety>[L., the foot.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The distal segment of the hind limb of vertebrates, including the
tarsus and foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesade</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*sade"</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*s<amac/d"; F. p<eit/*z<adot/d")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>The motion of a
horse when, raising his fore quarters, he keeps his hind feet on the
ground without advancing; rearing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesage</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>peser</ets> to weigh.]</ety> <def>A fee, or toll, paid for the
weighing of merchandise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesane</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anc. Armor.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pusane</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesanted</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ant*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pesant</ets> heavy.]</ety> <def>Made heavy or dull; debased.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pesanted</xex> to each lewd thought's
control.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peschito</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*schit"o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Peshito</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pese</ent><br/
<hw>Pese</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pea</er>.]</ety>
<def>A pea.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peseta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*se"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>A Spanish silver coin, and money of account, equal to about
nineteen cents (ca. 1900), and divided into 100 centesimos. Now replaced
by the euro.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peshitto</ent><br/
<ent>Peshito</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe*shit"o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pe*shit"to</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Syriac
<ets>pesh<icir/t<acir/</ets> simple.]</ety> <def>The earliest Syriac
version of the Old Testament, translated from Hebrew; also, the
incomplete Syriac version of the New Testament.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>peschito</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesky</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ky</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>Pestering; vexatious; troublesome. Used also as
an intensive.</def> <mark>[Colloq. & Low, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Judd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peso</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe"so</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety> <def>A
Spanish dollar; also, an Argentine, Chilian, Colombian, etc., coin,
equal to from 75 cents to a dollar; also, a pound weight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessary</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"sa*ry</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/s"s<adot/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pessaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>pessarium</ets>, <ets>pessum</ets>, <ets>pessus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>pesso`s</grk>: cf. F. <ets>pessaire</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An instrument or device to be introduced into and worn
in the vagina, to support the uterus, or remedy a malposition.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A medicinal substance in the form of a bolus or mass,
designed for introduction into the vagina; a vaginal
suppository.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pessimal</ent><br/
<hw>pes"si*mal</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Least favorable for survival;
-- of an organism's environment.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pessimum(prenominal).</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessimism</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"si*mism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pessimus</ets> worst, superl. of <ets>pejor</ets> worse: cf. F.
<ets>pessimisme</ets>. Cf. <er>Impair</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>The opinion or doctrine that everything in
nature is ordered for or tends to the worst, or that the world is wholly
evil; -- opposed to <ant>optimism</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A disposition to take the least hopeful view of a
situation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessimist</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"si*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pessimus</ets> worst: cf. F. <ets>pessimiste</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>One who advocates the doctrine of
pessimism; -- opposed to <ant>optimist</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who looks on the dark side of things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessimistic</ent><br/
<ent>Pessimist</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pes"si*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pes`si*mis"tic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to pessimism; characterized by pessimism; gloomy;
foreboding.</def> <ldquo/Giving utterance to <xex>pessimistic</xex>
doubt.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessimistical</ent><br/
<hw>Pes`si*mis"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pessimistic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessimize</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"si*mize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To hold or
advocate the doctrine of pessimism.</def> <rj><au>London Sat.
Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pessulus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pes"su*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pessuli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a bolt.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A delicate bar of cartilage connecting the
dorsal and ventral extremities of the first pair of bronchial cartilages
in the syrinx of birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pest</ent><br/
<hw>Pest</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pestis</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>peste</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fatal epidemic
disease; a pestilence; specif., the plague.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>England's sufferings by that scourge, the <qex>pest</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything which resembles a pest; one who, or that
which, is troublesome, noxious, mischievous, or destructive; a
nuisance.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pest</xex> and public enemy.<rdquo/
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestalozzian</ent><br/
<hw>Pes`ta*loz"zi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to,
or characteristic of, a system of elementary education which combined
manual training with other instruction, advocated and practiced by
<person>Jean Henri <etsep>Pestalozzi</etsep></person> (1746-1827), a
Swiss teacher.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>An advocate or follower
of the system of <persfn>Pestalozzi</persfn>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestalozzianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pes`ta*loz"zi*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
system of education introduced by <persfn>Pestalozzi</persfn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pester</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ter</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/s"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pestered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pestering</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Abbrev. fr. <ets>impester</ets>, fr. OF. <ets>empaistrier</ets>,
<ets>empestrer</ets>, to entangle the feet or legs, to embarrass, F.
<ets>emp</ets><ecir/<ets>trer</ets>; pref. <ets>em-</ets>,
<ets>en-</ets> (L. <ets>in</ets> in) + LL. <ets>pastorium</ets>,
<ets>pastoria</ets>, a fetter by which horses are prevented from
wandering in the pastures, fr. L. <ets>pastorius</ets> belonging to a
herdsman or shepherd, <ets>pastor</ets> a herdsman. See <er>In</er>, and
<er>Pasture</er>, <er>Pastor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To trouble;
to disturb; to annoy; to harass with petty vexations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are <qex>pestered</qex> with mice and rats.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H.
More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A multitude of scribblers daily <qex>pester</qex> the world.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To crowd together in an annoying way; to overcrowd;
to infest.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All rivers and pools . . . <qex>pestered</qex> full with
fishes.</q> <rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pestered</ent><br/
<hw>pestered</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Troubled persistently especially
with petty annoyances; <as>as, the exasperation of a <ex>pestered</ex>
animal</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> annoyed, harassed, harried.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesterer</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pesters or
harasses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesterment</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ter*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
pestering, or the state of being pestered; vexation; worry.</def>
<ldquo/The trouble and <xex>pesterment</xex> of children.<rdquo/
<rj><au>B. Franklin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesterous</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclined to
pester. Also, vexatious; encumbering; burdensome.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestful</ent><br/
<hw>Pest"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pestiferous.</def>
<ldquo/After long and <xex>pestful</xex> calms.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pesthouse</ent><br/
<hw>Pest`house"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house or
hospital for persons who are infected with any pestilential
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestiduct</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ti*duct</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pestis</ets> pest + <ets>ductus</ets> a leading, fr.
<ets>ducere</ets> to lead.]</ety> <def>That which conveys contagion or
infection.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pes*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pestiferus</ets>, <ets>pestifer</ets>; <ets>pestis</ets> pest +
<ets>ferre</ets> to bear: cf. F. <ets>pestif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Pest-bearing; pestilential; noxious to health;
malignant; infectious; contagious; <as>as, <ex>pestiferous</ex>
bodies</as>.</def> <ldquo/Poor, <xex>pestiferous</xex> creatures begging
alms.<rdquo/ <au>Evelyn.</au> <ldquo/Unwholesome and
<xex>pestiferous</xex> occupations.<rdquo/ <au>Burke.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Noxious to peace, to morals, or to society; vicious;
hurtful; destructive; <as>as, a <ex>pestiferous</ex>
demagogue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pestiferous</qex> reports of men very nobly held.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestiferously</ent><br/
<hw>Pes*tif"er*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pestiferous
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilence</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ti*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pestilence</ets>, L. <ets>pestilentia</ets>. See
<er>Pestilent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Specifically, the disease
known as the plague; hence, any contagious or infectious epidemic
disease that is virulent and devastating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pestilence</qex> that walketh in darkness.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xci. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: That which is pestilent, noxious, or
pernicious to the moral character of great numbers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll pour this <qex>pestilence</qex> into his ear.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pestilence weed</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
butterbur coltsfoot (<spn>Petasites vulgaris</spn>), so called because
formerly considered a remedy for the plague.</cd> <rj><au>Dr.
Prior.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilent</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ti*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pestilens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, fr. <ets>pestis</ets> pest: cf.
F. <ets>pestilent</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pestilential; noxious; pernicious;
mischievous.</def> <ldquo/Corrupt and <xex>pestilent</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au> <ldquo/What a <xex>pestilent</xex> knave is this
same!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilential</ent><br/
<hw>Pes`ti*len"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pestilentiel</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the nature or
qualities of a pestilence.</def> <ldquo/Sends the
<xex>pestilential</xex> vapors.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Mischievous; noxious; pernicious; morally
destructive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So <qex>pestilential</qex>, so infectious a thing is sin.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilentially</ent><br/
<hw>Pes`ti*len"tial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Pestilently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilentious</ent><br/
<hw>Pes`ti*len"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pestilential.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilently</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ti*lent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
pestilent manner; mischievously; destructively.</def> <ldquo/Above all
measure <xex>pestilently</xex> noisome.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pestilentness</ent><br/
<hw>Pes"ti*lent*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
pestilent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pestillation</ent><br/
<hw>pes`til*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>pestillum</ets>, L. <ets>pistillum</ets>. See
<er>Pestle</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of pounding and bruising with a
pestle in a mortar.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pestle</ent><br/
<hw>pes"tle</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/s"t'l <it>or</it> p<ecr/s"'l; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pestel</ets>, OF. <ets>pestel</ets>, LL.
<ets>pestellum</ets>, L. <ets>pistillum</ets>, <ets>pistillus</ets>, a
pounder, pestle, fr. <ets>pisere</ets>, <ets>pinsere</ets>, to pound,
crush, akin to Gr. <grk>pti`ssein</grk>, Skr. <ets>pish</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pistil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An implement for pounding and
breaking or braying substances in a mortar; <as>as, to grind with a
mortar and <ex>pestle</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A constable's or bailiff's staff; -- so called from
its shape.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The leg and leg bone of an animal, especially of a
pig; <as>as, a <ex>pestle</ex> of pork</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pestle</ent><br/
<hw>pes"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pestled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pestling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To pound,
pulverize, bray, or mix with a pestle, or as with a pestle; to use a
pestle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pesto</ent><br/
<hw>pes"to</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/s"t<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pesto</ets>, pounded, from <ets>pestare</ets> to pound, crush, fr.
LL. <ets>pistare</ets>, fr. <ets>pinsere</ets>. See also
<er>pestle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>A sauce used in
Italian cuisine, typically made by blending olive oil, basil, garlic,
pine nuts, and grated parmesan cheese; it is served hot or cold over
pasta, meat, or fish. Where pine nuts are expensive, sunflower seeds are
sometimes substituted.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pet</ent><br/
<hw>pet</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formerly
<ets>peat</ets>, perhaps from Ir. <ets>peat</ets>, akin to Gael.
<ets>peata</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cade lamb; a lamb brought up
by hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any person especially cherished and indulged; a
fondling; a darling; often, a favorite child; <as>as, a teacher's
<ex>pet</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The love of cronies, <qex>pets</qex>, and favorites.</q>
<rj><qau>Tatler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Prob. fr. <er>Pet</er> a fondling, hence, the
behavior or humor of a spoiled child.]</ety> <def>A slight fit of
peevishness or fretfulness.</def> <ldquo/In a <xex>pet</xex> she started
up.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any animal kept as a companion, usually in or around
one's home, typically domesticated and cared for attentively and often
affectionately. Distinguished from animals raised for food or to perform
useful tasks, as a <contr>draft animal</contr> or a <contr>farm
animal</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pet</ent><br/
<hw>pet</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Petted; indulged; favorite; admired;
cherished; <as>as, a <ex>pet</ex> child; a <ex>pet</ex> lamb; a
<ex>pet</ex> theory; a <ex>pet</ex> animal.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some young lady's <qex>pet</qex> curate.</q> <rj><qau>F.
Harrison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pet cock</b></col>. <ety>[Perh. for <ets>petty
cock</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>A little faucet in a water
pipe or pump, to let air out, or at the end of a steam cylinder, to
drain it.</cd> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>petcock</asp>.]</altsp></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pet</ent><br/
<hw>Pet</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Petted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Petting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To treat as a pet; to fondle; to
indulge; <as>as, she was <ex>petted</ex> and spoiled</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pet</ent><br/
<hw>Pet</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be a pet.</def>
<rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petal</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"al</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<ait/l; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pe`talon</grk> a leaf, a leaf or plate of metal, fr.
<grk>pe`talos</grk> outspread, broad, flat: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/tale</ets>. See <er>Fathom</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the leaves of the corolla, or the colored
leaves of a flower. See <er>Corolla</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Flower</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the expanded ambulacra
which form a rosette on the black of certain Echini.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaled</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"aled</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<ait/ld)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having petals; <as>as, a <ex>petaled</ex>
flower</as>; -- opposed to <ant>apetalous</ant>, and much used in
compounds; <as>as, one-<ex>petaled</ex>, three-<ex>petaled</ex>,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`al*if"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petal</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Bearing
petals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tal"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the form of a petal; petaloid; petal-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaline</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"al*ine</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<ait/l*<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/talin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to a petal; attached to, or resembling, a
petal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petalism</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"al*ism</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<ait/l*<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>petalismo`s</grk>, fr. <grk>pe`talon</grk> a leaf: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/talisme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A form
of sentence among the ancient Syracusans by which they banished for five
years a citizen suspected of having dangerous influence or ambition. It
was similar to the <sim>ostracism</sim> in Athens; but olive leaves were
used instead of shells for ballots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petalite</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"al*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/talite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A rare
mineral, occurring crystallized and in cleavable masses, usually white,
or nearly so, in color. It is a silicate of aluminum and
lithium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petalody</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tal"o*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Petal</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
metamorphosis of various floral organs, usually stamens, into
petals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaloid</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"al*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Petal</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/talo<ium/de</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Petaline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaloideous</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`al*oid"e*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the whole or part of the perianth petaline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petaloideous division</b></col>, <cd>that division of
endogenous plants in which the perianth is wholly or partly petaline,
embracing the <fam>Liliace<ae/</fam>, <fam>Orchidace<ae/</fam>,
<fam>Amaryllide<ae/</fam>, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petalosticha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pet`a*los"ti*cha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>PROP. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>pe`talon</grk> a leaf + <grk>sti`chos</grk> a
row.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of Echini, including the
irregular sea urchins, as the spatangoids. See
<er>Spatangoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petalous</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having petals;
petaled; -- opposed to <ant>apetalous</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petalum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pet"a*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Petala</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>A
petal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petar</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Petard</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Hoist with his own
<xex>petar</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petard</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tard"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/tard</ets>, fr. <ets>p<eacute/ter</ets> to break wind, to
crack, to explode, L. <ets>pedere</ets>, <ets>peditum</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A case containing powder to be exploded, esp. a
conical or cylindrical case of metal filled with powder and attached to
a plank, to be exploded against and break down gates, barricades,
drawbridges, etc. It has been superseded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petardier</ent><br/
<ent>Petardeer</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pet`ar*deer"</hw>, <hw>Pet`ar*dier"</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>p<eacute/tardier</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>One who managed a petard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petasus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pet"a*sus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from Gr.
<grk>pe`tasos</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. & Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The
winged cap of Mercury; also, a broad-brimmed, low-crowned hat worn by
Greeks and Romans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaurist</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tau"rist</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*t<add/"r<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>petaurista</ets> a ropedancer, Gr.
<grk>petayristh`s</grk>, fr. <grk>petayri`zein</grk> to dance on a rope,
fr. <grk>pe`tayron</grk> a pole, a stage for ropedancers: cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/tauriste</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any flying
marsupial of the genera <gen>Petaurus</gen>, <gen>Phalangista</gen>,
<gen>Acrobata</gen>, and allied genera. See <cref>Flying mouse</cref>,
under <er>Flying</er>, and <er>Phalangister</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petaurista</ent><br/
<hw>Petaurista</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of very large
Asiatic flying squirrels.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Petaurista</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petauristidae</ent><br/
<hw>Petauristidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of Old
World flying squirrels.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> subfamily <fam>Petauristidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petcock</ent><br/
<hw>pet"cock`</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"k<ocr/k`)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
small cock or faucet or valve for letting out air or releasing
compression, or for draining a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petechiae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*tech"i*<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Petechia</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <ety>[NL., fr. LL.
<ets>peteccia</ets>; cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/t<eacute/chie</ets>, It.
<ets>petecchia</ets>, Sp. <ets>petequia</ets>, Gr. <grk>pitta`kion</grk>
a label, plaster.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Small crimson, purple,
or livid spots, like flea-bites, due to extravasation of blood, which
appear on the skin in malignant fevers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petechial</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tech"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/t<eacute/chial</ets>, LL. <ets>petecchialis</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Characterized by, or pertaining to, petechi<ae/;
spotted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petechial fever</b></col>, <cd>a malignant fever,
accompanied with livid spots on the skin.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peter</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ter</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A
common baptismal name for a man. The name of one of the twelve apostles
of Christ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Peter boat</b></col>, <cd>a fishing boat, sharp at both
ends, originally of the Baltic Sea, but now common in certain English
rivers.</cd> -- <col><b>Peter Funk</b></col>, <cd>the auctioneer in a
mock auction.</cd> <mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>Peter
pence</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Peter's pence</b></col></mcol>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An annual tax or tribute, formerly paid by the English
people to the pope, being a penny for every house, payable on Lammas or
St. Peter's day; -- called also <altname>Rome scot</altname>, and
<altname>hearth money</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>In modern times,
a voluntary contribution made by Roman Catholics to the private purse of
the pope.</cd> -- <col><b>Peter's fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a haddock; -- so called because the black spots, one on each side,
behind the gills, are traditionally said to have been caused by the
fingers of St. Peter, when he caught the fish to pay the tribute. The
name is applied, also, to other fishes having similar
spots.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1073 --></p>

<p><ent>Peter</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"er</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Petered</conjf>
<pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Petering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety>
<def>To become depleted; to run out; to fail; -- used generally with
<ptcl>out</ptcl>; <as>as, that mine has <ex>petered</ex> out</as>.</def>
<mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peterel</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"er*el</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<etil/*<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Petrel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peterero</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`e*re"ro</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t`<esl/*r<emac/"r<osl/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>See <er>Pederero</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peterman</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ter*man</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r*m<acr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Petermen</plw>
<pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r*m<ecr/n)</pr>.</plu> <def>A fisherman; -- so
called after the apostle Peter.</def> <mark>[An obs. local term in
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petersham</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ter*sham</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r*sh<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Named after Lord <etsep>Petersham</etsep>.]</ety> <def>A rough,
knotted woolen cloth, used chiefly for men's overcoats; also, a coat of
that material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peterwort</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"ter*wort`</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r*w<ucir/t`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Saint
Peter's-wort</cref>, under <er>Saint</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petiolary</ent><br/
<ent>Petiolar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pet"i*o*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pet"i*o*la*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/tiolarie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to petiole, or proceeding from it; <as>as, a
<ex>petiolar</ex> tendril</as>; growing or supported upon a petiole;
<as>as, a <ex>petiolar</ex> gland; a <ex>petiolar</ex>
bud.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petiolated</ent><br/
<ent>Petiolate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pet"i*o*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pet"i*o*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a stalk or petiole; <as>as, a <ex>petioleate</ex> leaf; the
<ex>petiolated</ex> abdomen of certain Hymenoptera.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petiole</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"i*ole</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<icr/*<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>p<eacute/tiole</ets>, fr. L. <ets>petiolus</ets> a little
foot, a fruit stalk; cf. <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a
foot.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A leafstalk; the
footstalk of a leaf, connecting the blade with the stem. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Leaf</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A stalk or peduncle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petioled</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"i*oled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Petiolate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petiolulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`i*ol"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Supported by its own petiolule.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petiolule</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"i*o*lule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/tiolule</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A small
petiole, or the petiole of a leaflet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petit</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"it</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"<ycr/; F. p<eit/*t<esl/")</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. See <er>Petty</er>.]</ety> <def>Small; little;
insignificant; mean; -- Same as <er>Petty</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.,
except in legal language.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By what small, <qex>petit</qex> hints does the mind catch hold of
and recover a vanishing notion.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petit constable</b></col>, <cd>an inferior civil officer,
subordinate to the high constable.</cd> -- <col><b>Petit jury</b></col>,
<cd>a jury of twelve men, impaneled to try causes at the bar of a court;
-- so called in distinction from the <contr>grand jury</contr>.</cd> --
<col><b>Petit larceny</b></col>, <cd>the stealing of goods of, or under,
a certain specified small value; -- opposed to <contr>grand
larceny</contr>. The distinction is abolished in England.</cd> --
<col><b>Petit ma<icir/tre</b></col> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <ety>[F., lit.,
little master.]</ety> <cd>A fop; a coxcomb; a ladies' man.</cd>
<au>Goldsmith.</au> -- <col><b>Petit serjeanty</b></col> <fld>(Eng.
Law)</fld>, <cd>the tenure of lands of the crown, by the service of
rendering annually some implement of war, as a bow, an arrow, a sword, a
flag, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Petit treason</b></col>, <cd>formerly, in
England, the crime of killing a person to whom the offender owed duty or
subjection, as one's husband, master, mistress, etc. The crime is now
not distinguished from murder.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petite</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>pe*tite"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fem. of
<ets>petit</ets>.]</ety> <def>Small, little; -- used especially of a
woman or girl, of small size and trim figure; <as>as, her
<ex>petite</ex> figure</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bantam, diminutive, lilliputian, midget,
tiny.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petite</ent><br/
<hw>pe*tite"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fem. of
<ets>petit</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A size category of women's
clothing, for women with less than average height.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An aricle of women's clothing classified in the
<er>petite</er>{1} size category.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A girl or woman whose figure is best fit by a
<er>petite</er>{1} garment.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petitio</ent><br/
<hw>petitio</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Rhetoric, Logic)</fld> <def>The
fallacy of assuming the conclusion in the premises; begging the
question.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> petitio principii.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petition</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/tition</ets>, L. <ets>petitio</ets>, fr.
<ets>petere</ets>, <ets>petitum</ets>, to beg, ask, seek; perh. akin to
E. <ets>feather</ets>, or <ets>find</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
prayer; a supplication; an imploration; an entreaty; especially, a
request of a solemn or formal kind; a prayer to the Supreme Being, or to
a person of superior power, rank, or authority; also, a single clause in
such a prayer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A house of prayer and <qex>petition</qex> for thy people.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Macc. vii. 37.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This last <qex>petition</qex> heard of all her prayer.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A formal written request addressed to an official
person, or to an organized body, having power to grant it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <def>A
request to government, in either of its branches, for the granting of a
particular grace or right, or for the legislature to take a specific
action; -- in distinction from a <contr>memorial</contr>, which calls
certain facts to mind. The petition may be signed by one or any number
of persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The written document containing a <er>petition</er>
(senses 1 or 2).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petition of right</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a
petition to obtain possession or restitution of property, either real or
personal, from the Crown, which suggests such a title as controverts the
title of the Crown, grounded on facts disclosed in the petition
itself.</cd> <au>Mozley & W.</au> -- <col><b>The Petition of
Right</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld>, <cd>the parliamentary
declaration of the rights of the people, assented to by Charles
I.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petition</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Petitioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Petitioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a prayer or request
to; to ask from; to solicit; to entreat; especially, to make a formal
written supplication, or application to, as to any branch of the
government; <as>as, to <ex>petition</ex> the court; to <ex>petition</ex>
the governor.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have . . . <qex>petitioned</qex> all the gods for my
prosperity.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petition</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a petition or
solicitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitionarily</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion*a*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way of
begging the question; by an assumption.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Supplicatory; making a petition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pardon Rome, and any <qex>petitionary</qex> countrymen.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Containing a petition; of the nature of a petition;
<as>as, a <ex>petitionary</ex> epistle</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitionee</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti`tion*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person cited
to answer, or defend against, a petition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who presents
a petition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitioning</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*ti"tion*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of presenting
apetition; a supplication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petit mal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*tit" mal"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[F., lit., little
sickness.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The mildest form of epilepsy,
with momentary faintness or unconsciousness, but without convulsions; --
opposed to <contr>grand mal</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitor</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>petere</ets> to seek.]</ety> <def>One who seeks or asks; a seeker;
an applicant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"i*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>petitorius</ets>, fr. <ets>petere</ets>, <ets>petitum</ets>, to
beg, ask: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/titore</ets>.]</ety> <def>Petitioning;
soliciting; supplicating.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Petitory suit</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Petitory action</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Admiralty Law)</fld>,
<cd>a suit in which the mere title to property is litigated and sought
to be enforced, as distinguished from a <contr>possessory suit</contr>;
also <fld>(Scots Law)</fld>, a suit wherein the plaintiff claims
something as due him by the defendant.</cd>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petong</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tong"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Packfong</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petralogy</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tral"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Petrology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrary</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ra*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>petra</ets>
stone. Cf. Sp. <ets>petraria</ets>, and E. <er>Pederero</er>.]</ety>
<def>An ancient war engine for hurling stones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petre</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"tre</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saltpeter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrean</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tre"an</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*tr<emac/"<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>petraeus</ets>, Gr. <grk>petrai^os</grk>, fr.
<grk>pe`tra</grk> a rock.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to rock.</def>
<rj><au>G. S. Faber.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrel</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"trel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>p<eacute/trel</ets>; a dim. of the name <ets>Peter</ets>, L.
<ets>Petrus</ets>, Gr. <grk>pe`tros</grk> a stone (<au>John i. 42</au>);
-- probably so called in allusion to St. <ets>Peter's</ets> walking on
the sea. See <er>Petrify</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of longwinged sea birds belonging to the family
<fam>Procellarid<ae/</fam>. The small petrels, or Mother Carey's
chickens, belong to <stype><gen>Oceanites</gen></stype>,
<stype><gen>Oceanodroma</gen></stype>,
<stype><gen>Procellaria</gen></stype>, and several allied
genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Diving petrel</b></col>, <cd>any bird of the genus
<gen>Pelecanoides</gen>. They chiefly inhabit the southern
hemisphere.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Fulmar petrel</b></col>, <col><b>Giant
petrel</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <er>Fulmar</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pintado petrel</b></col>, <cd>the Cape pigeon. See under
<er>Cape</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pintado petrel</b></col>, <cd>any one of
several small petrels, especially <spn>Procellaria pelagica</spn>, or
Mother Carey's chicken, common on both sides of the
Atlantic.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrescence</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tres"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The process of
changing into stone; petrification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrescent</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tres"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>petra</ets> rock, stone, Gr. <grk>pe`tra</grk>.]</ety>
<def>Petrifying; converting into stone; <as>as, <ex>petrescent</ex>
water</as>.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrifaction</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ri*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Petrify</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The process of petrifying, or
changing into stone; conversion of any organic matter (animal or
vegetable) into stone, or a substance of stony hardness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state or condition of being petrified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is petrified; popularly, a body incrusted
with stony matter; an incrustation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: Hardness; callousness; obduracy.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Petrifaction</xex> of the soul.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrifactive</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ri*fac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the quality of converting organic matter into stone;
petrifying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or characterized by,
petrifaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The . . . <qex>petrifactive</qex> mutations of hard bodies.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrific</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/trifique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Petrifying;
petrifactive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Death with his mace <qex>petrific</qex>, cold and dry.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrificate</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ri*fi*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
petrify.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our hearts <qex>petrificated</qex> were.</q> <rj><qau>J. Hall
(1646).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrification</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ri*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<eacute/trification</ets>. See <er>Petrify</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>See <er>Petrifaction</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Obduracy; callousness.</def>
<rj><au>Hallywell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petrified</ent><br/
<hw>petrified</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Converted into
stone.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Converted into a mineral; <as>as, <ex>petrified</ex>
wood</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> mineralized.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Same as <er>terrified</er>; <as>as,
<ex>petrified</ex> by the sight of the approaching bear</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> panicky, panic-stricken, panic-struck, terrified,
frightened.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>rendered unable to act due to intense fear.</def>
<illu><ex>petrified</ex> with fear</illu><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> frozen.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Rendered lifeless or inactive; <as>as, an
imagination <ex>petrified</ex> by habitual television
viewing</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> frozen; ossified.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrify</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ri*fy</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"r<icr/*f<imac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Petrified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Petrifying</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>petra</ets> rock, Gr.
<grk>pe`tra</grk> (akin to <grk>pe`tros</grk> a stone) + <ets>-fy</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/trifier</ets>. Cf. <er>Parrot</er>,
<er>Petrel</er>, <er>Pier</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To convert, as
any animal or vegetable matter, into stone or stony substance; <as>as,
<ex>petrified</ex> wood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A river that <qex>petrifies</qex> any sort of wood or leaves.</q>
<rj><qau>Kirwan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make callous or obdurate; to transform, as by
petrifaction; <as>as, to <ex>petrify</ex> the heart</as>.
<xex>Young</xex>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Petrifying</xex> accuracy.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>petrify</qex> a genius to a dunce.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A hideous fatalism, which ought, logically, to <qex>petrify</qex>
your volition.</q> <rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To paralyze, especially with fear; to stupefy;
<as>as, she was <ex>petrified</ex> by the sight of the bear in her
tent</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The poor, <qex>petrified</qex> journeyman, quite unconscious of
what he was doing.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrify</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ri*fy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become stone,
or of a stony hardness, as organic matter by calcareous
deposits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To become stony, callous, or
obdurate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like Niobe we marble grow,<br/
And <qex>petrify</qex> with grief.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrine</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"trine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
<person>St. Peter</person>; <as>as, the <ex>Petrine</ex>
Epistles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petro-</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ro-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>pe`tra</grk> a <xlati>rock</xlati>, <grk>pe`tros</grk> a
<xlati>stone</xlati>; <as>as, <ex>petro</ex>logy,
<ex>petro</ex>glyphic</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrocoptis</ent><br/
<hw>Petrocoptis</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of perennial
tussock-forming rock plants; native to the Pyrenees and mountains of
Northern Spain; it is similar to and sometimes placed in the genus
<gen>Lychnis</gen>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Petrocoptis</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrogale</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trog"a*le</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*tr<ocr/g"<adot/*l<emac/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pe`tra</grk> a rock +
<grk>galh^</grk> a weasel.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any Australian
kangaroo of the genus <gen>Petrogale</gen>, as the rock wallaby
(<spn>Petrogale penicillata</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petroglyph</ent><br/
<hw>pet"ro*glyph`</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"r<osl/*gl<icr/f`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A carving or drawing on rock, especially one made by
prehistoric humans; called also a
<altname>petrograph</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petroglyphic</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*glyph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to petroglyphs or petroglyphy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petroglyphy</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trog"ly*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + Gr. <grk>gly`fein</grk> to carve.]</ety>
<def>The art or operation of carving figures or inscriptions on rock or
stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petrograph</ent><br/
<hw>pet"ro*graph`</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"r<osl/*gr<acr/f`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A carving or drawing on rock, especially one made by
prehistoric humans; called also a
<altname>petrograph</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrographical</ent><br/
<ent>Petrographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pet`ro*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pet`ro*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertaining to petrography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrography</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
art of writing on stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The scientific description of rocks; that department
of science which investigates the constitution of rocks;
petrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petrohyoid</ent><br/
<hw>pet`ro*hy"oid</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t`r<osl/*h<imac/"oid)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + <ets>hyoid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the petrous, or periotic, portion
of the skull and the hyoid arch; <as>as, the <ex>petrohyoid</ex> muscles
of the frog</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrol</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trol"</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*tr<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Petroleum.</def> <mark>[Archaic and R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pr>(p<ecr/"tr<add/l")</pr>, <def>Same as
<er>gasoline</er>; -- British usage.</def> <mark>[British]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrolatum</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*la"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem. &
Pharm.)</fld> <def>A semisolid unctuous substance, neutral, and without
taste or odor, derived from petroleum by distilling off the lighter
portions and purifying the residue. It is a yellowish, fatlike mass,
transparent in thin layers, and somewhat fluorescent. It is used as a
bland protective dressing, and as a substitute for fatty materials in
ointments.</def> <rj><au>U. S. Pharm.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>Petrolatum</ex> is the official name for the
purified product. <altname>Cosmoline</altname> and
<altname>vaseline</altname> are commercial names for substances
essentially the same, but differing slightly in appearance and
consistency or fusibility.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petroleum</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tro"le*um</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*tr<omac/"l<esl/*<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>petra</ets> a rock +
<ets>oleum</ets> oil: cf. F. <ets>p<eacute/trole</ets>. Cf.
<er>Petrify</er>, and <er>Oil</er>.]</ety> <def>Rock oil, mineral oil,
or natural oil, a dark brown or greenish inflammable liquid, which, at
certain points, exists in the upper strata of the earth, from whence it
is pumped, or forced by pressure of the gas attending it. It consists of
a complex mixture of various hydrocarbons, largely of the methane
series, but may vary much in appearance, composition, and properties. It
is refined by distillation, and the products include kerosene, benzine,
gasoline, paraffin, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petroleum spirit</b></col>, <cd>a volatile liquid
obtained in the distillation of crude petroleum at a temperature of
170<deg/ Fahr., or below. The term is rather loosely applied to a
considerable range of products, including benzine and ligroin. The terms
<xex>petroleum ether</xex>, and <xex>naphtha</xex>, are sometimes
applied to the still more volatile products, including rhigolene,
gasoline, cymogene, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petroleuse</ent><br/
<ent>Petroleur</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>P<eacute/`tro`leur"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. m.</pos>
\'d8<hw>P<eacute/`tro`leuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. f.</pos> }</mhw>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>One who makes use of petroleum for incendiary
purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petroline</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ro*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A paraffin obtained from petroleum from Rangoon in India, and
practically identical with ordinary paraffin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrological</ent><br/
<ent>Petrologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pet`ro*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pet`ro*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
petrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrologically</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*log"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>According to
petrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
versed in petrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrology</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*trol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Petro</ets>
+ <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The department of science
which is concerned with the mineralogical and chemical composition of
rocks, and with their classification: lithology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A treatise on petrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petromastoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*mas"toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + <ets>mastoid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the petrous and mastoid parts of the temporal
bone; periotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petromyzont</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*my"zont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + Gr. <grk>my`zein</grk> to suck in.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A lamprey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petronel</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>petrinal</ets>, fr. <ets>peitrine</ets>, <ets>petrine</ets>, the
breast, F. <ets>poitrine</ets>; so called because it was placed against
the breast in order to fire. See <er>Poitrel</er>.]</ety> <def>A sort of
hand cannon, or portable firearm, used in France in the 15th
century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrosal</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tro"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Petrous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Hard;
stony; petrous; <as>as, the <ex>petrosal</ex> bone; <ex>petrosal</ex>
part of the temporal bone.</as></def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of, pertaining
to, or in the region of, the petrous, or petrosal, bone, or the
corresponding part of the temporal bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petrosal bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a bone
corresponding to the petrous portion of the temporal bone of man; or one
forming more or less of the periotic capsule.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrosal</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tro"sal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A petrosal bone.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The auditory
capsule.</def> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrosilex</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*si"lex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + <ets>silex</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Felsite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrosilicious</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*si*li"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing,
or consisting of, petrosilex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrostearine</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ro*ste"a*rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petro</ets> + <ets>stearine</ets>.]</ety> <def>A solid
unctuous material, of which candles are made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"trous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>petrosus</ets>, fr. <ets>petra</ets> a stone.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Like stone; hard; stony; rocky; <as>as, the <ex>petrous</ex> part
of the temporal bone</as>.</def> <rj><au>Hooper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Petrosal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pe-tsai</ent><br/
<hw>pe-tsai</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A plant with an elongated celerylike
head of broadstalked leaves used as a vegetable in east Asia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Chinese cabbage, celery cabbage, napa, <spn>Brassica
rapa</spn> <varn>pekinensis</varn>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettichaps</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*chaps</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pettychaps</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petticoat</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*coat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<ety>[<ets>Petty</ets> + <ets>coat</ets>.]</ety> <def>A loose
under-garment worn by women, and covering the body below the
waist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petticoat government</b></col>, <cd>government by women,
whether in politics or domestic affairs.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> --
<col><b>Petticoat pipe</b></col> <fld>(Locomotives)</fld>, <cd>a short,
flaring pipe surrounding the blast nozzle in the smoke box, to equalize
the draft.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petticoated</ent><br/
<hw>petticoated</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>wearing or
furnished with a petticoat; <as>as, <ex>petticoated</ex> ladies; a
<ex>petticoated</ex> table</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifog</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*fog</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pettifogged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pettifogging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Petty</ets> + <ets>fog</ets> to pettifog.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To do a petty business as a lawyer; also, to do law business in a
petty or tricky way.</def> <ldquo/He takes no money, but
<xex>pettifogs</xex> gratis.<rdquo/ <rj><au>S. Butler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To debate or quarrel over unimportant, trifling
matters.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To use subterfuge, deception, or flattery for any
purpose; to practice chicanery.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifog</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*fog</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To advocate like a
pettifogger; to argue trickily; <as>as, to <ex>pettifog</ex> a
claim</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifogger</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*fog`ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lawyer who
deals in petty cases; an attorney whose methods are mean and tricky; an
inferior lawyer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>pettifogger</qex> was lord chancellor.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifoggery</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*fog`ger*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>pettifoggeries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The practice or
arts of a pettifogger; disreputable tricks; quibbles;
chicanery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Quirks of law, and <qex>pettifoggeries</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifogging</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*fog`ging</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Paltry;
quibbling; mean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifogging</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*fog`ging</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pettifoggery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettifogulize</ent><br/
<hw>Pet`ti*fog"u*lize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act as
a pettifogger; to use contemptible tricks.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettily</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a petty manner;
frivolously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
petty or paltry; littleness; meanness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>petting</ent><br/
<hw>petting</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sexually titillating foreplay
without contact with the genital organs.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> caressing, cuddling, fondling, hugging, kissing,
necking, smooching, snuggling.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettish</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"tish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pet</er>.]</ety> <def>Fretful; peevish; moody; capricious; inclined
to ill temper.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pettish</xex> kind of humor.<rdquo/
<au>Sterne.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Pet"tish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pet"tish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettitoes</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ti*toes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petty</ets> + <ets>toes</ets>.]</ety> <def>The toes or feet
of a pig, -- often used as food; sometimes, in contempt, the human
feet.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petto</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pet"to</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"t<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.,
fr. L. <ets>pectus</ets>.]</ety> <def>The breast.</def>
<mark>[Obsolescent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In petto</b></col>, <cd>in the breast; hence, in secrecy;
in reserve.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petty</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ty</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Pettier</adjf>
<pr>(p<ecr/t"t<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Pettiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>petit</ets>, F.
<ets>petit</ets>; probably of Celtic origin, and akin to E.
<ets>piece</ets>. Cf. <er>Petit</er>.]</ety> <def>Little; trifling;
inconsiderable; unimportant; also, inferior; subordinate; <as>as, a
<ex>petty</ex> fault; <ex>petty</ex> complaints; a <ex>petty</ex>
prince.</as></def> <rj><au>Denham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a <qex>petty</qex> god<br/
I walked about, admired of all.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Petty averages</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Average</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Petty cash</b></col>, <cd>money
expended or received in small items or amounts.</cd> -- <col><b>Petty
officer</b></col>, <cd>a subofficer in the navy, as a gunner, etc.,
corresponding to a noncommissionned officer in the army.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ For <xex>petty constable</xex>, <xex>petty jury</xex>,
<xex>petty larceny</xex>, <xex>petty treason</xex>, See
<er>Petit</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Little; diminutive; inconsiderable; inferior;
trifling; trivial; unimportant; frivolous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1074 --></p>

<p><ent>Pettychaps</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ty*chaps</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"t<ycr/*ch<ocr/ps)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of small European
singing birds of the subfamily <fam>Sylviin<ae/</fam>, as the willow
warbler, the chiff-chaff, and the golden warbler (<spn>Sylvia
hortensis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pettywhin</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"ty*whin</hw> <pr>(p<ecr/t"t<ycr/*hw<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Petty</ets> + <ets>whin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The needle furze. See under <er>Needle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petulancy</ent><br/
<ent>Petulance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pet"u*lance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pet"u*lan*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>petulania</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>p<eacute/tulance</ets>. See <er>Petulant</er>.]</ety> <def>The
quality or state of being petulant; temporary peevishness; pettishness;
capricious ill humor.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>petulancy</xex> of our
words.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like pride in some, and like <qex>petulance</qex> in others.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lowering eye, the <qex>petulance</qex>, the frown.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Petulance</er>, <er>Peevishness</er>. --
<xex>Peevishness</xex> implies the permanence of a sour, fretful temper;
<xex>petulance</xex> implies temporary or capricious
irritation.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petulant</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"u*lant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>petulans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, prop., making slight attacks
upon, from a lost dim. of <ets>petere</ets> to fall upon, to attack: cf.
F. <ets>p<eacute/tulant</ets>. See <er>Petition</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Forward; pert; insolent; wanton.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capriciously fretful; characterized by ill-natured
freakishness; irritable.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Petulant</xex> moods.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Irritable; ill-humored; peevish; cross; fretful;
querulous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petulantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"u*lant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a petulant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petulcity</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tul"ci*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Petulcous</er>.]</ety> <def>Wantonness; friskiness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petulcous</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tul"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>petulcus</ets>. Cf. <er>Petulant</er>.]</ety> <def>Wanton; frisky;
lustful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>J. V. Cane.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petune</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tune"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Petuned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Petuning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Petunia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>To spray (tobacco) with a liquid intended to
produce flavor or aroma.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petunia</ent><br/
<hw>Pe*tu"ni*a</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*t<umac/"n<icr/*<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Braz. <ets>petun</ets> tobacco.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of solanaceous herbs with funnel-form or
salver-shaped corollas. Two species are common in cultivation,
<spn>Petunia violacea</spn>, with reddish purple flowers, and
<spn>Petunia nyctaginiflora</spn>, with white flowers. There are also
many hybrid forms with variegated corollas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petuntze</ent><br/
<ent>Petuntse</ent><br/
<ent>Petunse</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pe*tunse"</hw>, <hw>Pe*tuntse"</hw>, <hw>Pe*tuntze"</hw>
}</mhw> <pr>(p<esl/*t<ucr/ns")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
Chinese.]</ety> <def>Powdered fledspar, kaolin, or quartz, used in the
manufacture of porcelain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petworth marble</ent><br/
<hw>Pet"worth mar"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A kind of shell marble
occurring in the Wealden clay at Petworth, in Sussex, England; -- called
also <altname>Sussex marble</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Petzite</ent><br/
<hw>Petz"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Petz</ets>, who analyzed it.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
telluride of silver and gold, related to hessite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peucedanin</ent><br/
<hw>Peu*ced"a*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A tasteless white crystalline substance, extracted from the roots
of the sulphurwort (<gen>Peucedanum</gen>), masterwort
(<gen>Imperatoria</gen>), and other related plants; -- called also
<altname>imperatorin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peucil</ent><br/
<hw>Peu"cil</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"s<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pey`kh</grk> pine tree.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A liquid
resembling camphene, obtained by treating turpentine hydrochloride with
lime.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>peucyl</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pew</ent><br/
<hw>Pew</hw> <pr>(p<umac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pewe</ets>, OF. <ets>puie</ets> parapet, balustrade, balcony, fr.
L. <ets>podium</ets> an elevated place, a jutty, balcony, a parapet or
balcony in the circus, where the emperor and other distinguished persons
sat, Gr. <grk>po`dion</grk>, dim. of <grk>poy`s</grk>,
<grk>podo`s</grk>, foot; -- hence the Latin sense of a raised place
(orig. as a rest or support for the foot). See <er>Foot</er>, and cf.
<er>Podium</er>, <er>Poy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the
compartments in a church which are separated by low partitions, and have
long seats upon which several persons may sit; -- sometimes called
<altname>slip</altname>. Pews were originally made square, but are now
usually long and narrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any structure shaped like a church pew, as a stall,
formerly used by money lenders, etc.; a box in theater; a pen; a
sheepfold.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Pepys.</au>
<au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pew opener</b></col>, <cd>an usher in a church.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pew</ent><br/
<hw>Pew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To furnish with pews.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Ash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewee</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"wee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from its
note.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A common American
tyrant flycatcher (<spn>Sayornis ph<oe/be</spn>, or <spn>Sayornis
fuscus</spn>). Called also <altname>pewit</altname>, and
<altname>ph<oe/be</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The woodcock.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Wood pewee</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a bird
(<spn>Contopus virens</spn>) similar to the pewee (See <er>Pewee</er>,
1), but of smaller size.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewet</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"wet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Pewit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewfellow</ent><br/
<hw>Pew"fel`low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who occupies the same pew with another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An intimate associate; a companion.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewit</ent><br/
<hw>Pe"wit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. of imitative
origin; cf. OD. <ets>piewit</ets>, D. <ets>kievit</ets>, G.
<ets>kibitz</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
lapwing.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The European black-headed, or laughing,
gull (<spn>Xema ridibundus</spn>). See under <er>Laughing</er>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The pewee.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>peevit</asp>, <asp>peewit</asp>, <asp>pewet</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewter</ent><br/
<hw>Pew"ter</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pewtyr</ets>, OF. <ets>peutre</ets>, <ets>peautre</ets>,
<ets>piautre</ets>: cf. D. <ets>peauter</ets>, <ets>piauter</ets>, It.
<ets>peltro</ets>, Sp. & Pg. <ets>peltre</ets>, LL. <ets>peutreum</ets>,
<ets>pestrum</ets>. Cf. <er>Spelter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
hard, tough, but easily fusible, alloy, originally consisting of tin
with a little lead, but afterwards modified by the addition of copper,
antimony, or bismuth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Utensils or vessels made of pewter, as dishes,
porringers, drinking vessels, tankards, pots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pewter</xex> was formerly much used for domestic
utensils. Inferior sorts contain a large proportion of lead.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewterer</ent><br/
<hw>Pew"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One whose
occupation is to make utensils of pewter; a pewtersmith.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pewtery</ent><br/
<hw>Pew"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to, or
resembling, pewter; <as>as, a <ex>pewtery</ex> taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pexity</ent><br/
<hw>Pex"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pexitas</ets>, fr. <ets>pexus</ets> woolly, nappy, p. p. of
<ets>pectere</ets> to comb.]</ety> <def>Nap of cloth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peyer's glands</ent><br/
<hw>Pey"er's glands`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from <person>J.
K. <etsep>Peyer</etsep></person>, who described them in 1677.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Patches of lymphoid nodules, in the walls of the
small intestiness; agminated glands; -- called also <altname>Peyer's
patches</altname>. In typhoid fever they become the seat of ulcers which
are regarded as the characteristic organic lesion of that
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peytrel</ent><br/
<hw>Pey"trel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>peitral</ets>. See <er>Poitrel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld>
<def>The breastplate of a horse's armor or harness. <altsp>[Spelt also
<asp>peitrel</asp>.]</altsp> See <er>Poitrel</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Peziza</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pe*zi"za</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
corrupt. from L. <ets>pezica</ets> a sessile mushroom, fr. Gr.
<grk>pe`zis</grk>, fr. <grk>pe`za</grk>, <grk>poy`s</grk>, a
foot.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of fungi embracing a great
number of species, some of which are remarkable for their regular
cuplike form and deep colors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pezizaceae</ent><br/
<hw>Pezizaceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large natural
family comprising many typical cup fungi.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family Pezizaceae.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pezizales</ent><br/
<hw>Pezizales</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An order of
mostly saprophytic fungi having cup-shaped ascocarps.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order Pezizales.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pezizoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pez"i*zoid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Peziza</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Resembling a fungus of
the genus <gen>Peziza</gen>; having a cuplike form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pezophaps</ent><br/
<hw>Pezophaps</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus constituted by the
extinct solitaire.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus Pezophaps.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pfalz</ent><br/
<hw>Pfalz</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <fld>(Geog.)</fld> <def>A territory
in southwestern Germany formerly ruled by the counts palatine.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Palatinate.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pfannkuchen</ent><br/
<hw>pfannkuchen</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[German.]</ety> <def>A puffy
mildly sweet lemon-flavored egg mixture sprinkled with confectioners'
sugar and served with jam or a wine or fruit sauce.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> german pancake.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pfennig</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pfen"nig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pfennigs</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, G. <plw>Pfennige</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[G. See <er>Penny</er>.]</ety> <def>A small
copper coin of Germany. It is the hundredth part of a mark. In 2001 it
was superseded by the euro.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phacellus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*cel"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phacelli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fa`kelos</grk> a bundle of fagots.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the filaments on the inner surface of the gastric cavity of
certain jellyfishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phacochere</ent><br/
<hw>Phac"o*chere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fako`s</grk> a lentil seed, a wart + <grk>choi^ros</grk> a
pig.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The wart hog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phacoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fako`s</grk> a lentil + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a
lentil; lenticular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phacolite</ent><br/
<hw>Phac"o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fako`s</grk> lentil + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A colorless variety of chabazite; the original was from Leipa, in
Bohemia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phacops</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha"cops</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fako`s</grk> a lentil + <grk>'w`ps</grk>, <grk>'wpo`s</grk>, the
eye.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of trilobites found in the
Silurian and Devonian formations. <spn>Phacops bufo</spn> is one of the
most common species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaeacian</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<ae/*a"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the Ph<ae/acians, a fabulous seafaring people fond of the feast, the
lyre, and the dance, mentioned by Homer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaenogam</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<ae/"no*gam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the class <class>Ph<ae/nogamia</class> .</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaenogamia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ph<ae/`no*ga"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fai`nein</grk> to show + <grk>ga`mos</grk>
marriage.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The class of flowering plants
including all which have true flowers with distinct floral organs;
phanerogamia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaenogamic</ent><br/
<ent>Phaenogamian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ph<ae/`no*ga"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ph<ae/`no*gam"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same
as <er>Ph<ae/nogamous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaenogamous</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<ae/*nog"a*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having true flowers with distinct floral organs;
flowering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaenomenon</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<ae/*nom"e*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Phenomenon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaeospore</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<ae/"o*spore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>faio`s</grk> dusky + E. <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A brownish zoospore, characteristic of an order
(<ord>Ph<ae/ospore<ae/</ord>) of dark green or olive-colored
alg<ae/.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Ph<ae/`o*spor"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaethon</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"<eum/*thon</hw> <pr>(f<amac/"<esl/*th<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>prop.
n.</pos> <ety>[L., Pha<eum/thon (in sense 1), fr. Gr.
<grk>Fae`qwn</grk>, fr. <grk>fae`qein</grk>, <grk>fa`ein</grk>, to
shine. See <er>Phantom</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class.
Myth.)</fld> <def>The son of Helios (Ph<oe/bus), that is, the son of
light, or of the sun. He is fabled to have obtained permission to drive
the chariot of the sun, in doing which his want of skill would have set
the world on fire, had he not been struck with a thunderbolt by Jupiter,
and hurled headlong into the river Po.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of oceanic birds
including the tropic birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaeton</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"<eum/*ton</hw> <pr>(f<amac/"<esl/*t<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>pha<eacute/ton</ets> a kind of carriage, fr.
<ets>Pha<eacute/thon</ets> Pha<eum/thon, the son of Helios. See
<er>Pha<eum/thon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A four-wheeled carriage
(with or without a top), open, or having no side pieces, in front of the
seat. It is drawn by one or two horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>See <er>Pha<eum/thon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A handsome American butterfly
(<spn>Euphydryas Pha<eum/ton</spn> syn. <spn>Melit<ae/a
Pha<eum/ton</spn>). The upper side of the wings is black, with
orange-red spots and marginal crescents, and several rows of
cream-colored spots; -- called also
<altname>Baltimore</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phagedena</ent><br/
<hw>Phag`e*de"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phagedaena</ets>, Gr. <grk>fage`daina</grk>, fr. <grk>fagei^n</grk>
to eat.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A canine appetite;
bulimia.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Spreading,
obstinate ulceration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phagedenical</ent><br/
<ent>Phagedenic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phag`e*den"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phag`e*den"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>phagedaenicus</ets>,
Gr. <grk>fagedainiko`s</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>phag<eacute/d<eacute/nique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of,
like, or pertaining to, phagedena; used in the treatment of phagedena;
<as>as, a <ex>phagedenic</ex> ulcer or medicine</as>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A phagedenic medicine.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phagedenous</ent><br/
<hw>Phag`e*de"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Phagedenic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phagocyte</ent><br/
<hw>phag"o*cyte</hw> <pr>(f<acr/g"<osl/*s<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat + <grk>ky`tos</grk> a hollow
vessel.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A leucocyte which plays a part
in immunity and retrogressive processes by taking up (eating), in the
form of fine granules, foreign objects such as bacteria or cellular
parts to be removed. There are two principle subtypes, (1)
<stype>microphages</stype>, polymorphonuclear lymphocytes which
principally digest bacteria; and (2) <stype>macrophages</stype>,
mononucleated cells which primarily scavenge dead tissue and degenerate
cells.</def> <rj><au>Stedman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phainopepla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*i`no*pep"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>faeino`s</grk> shining + <grk>pe`plos</grk> robe.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small crested passerine bird
(<spn>Pha<ium/nopepla nitens</spn>), native of Mexico and the Southern
United States. The adult male is of a uniform glossy blue-black; the
female is brownish. Called also <altname>black
flycatcher</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phakoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Phak"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fako`s</grk> a lentil, or lenticular body +
<ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for
studying the mechanism of accommodation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalaena</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*l<ae/"na</hw> <pr>(f<adot/*l<emac/"n<adot/)</pr>, <pos>prop.
n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fa`laina</grk>, <grk>fa`llaina</grk>, a
kind of moth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A linn<ae/an genus which
included the moths in general.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalaenid</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*l<ae/"nid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`laina</grk>, <grk>fa`llaina</grk>, a kind of moth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any moth of the family
<fam>Phal<ae/nid<ae/</fam>, of which the cankerworms are examples; a
geometrid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalaenopsis</ent><br/
<hw>Phalaenopsis</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of ornamental
epiphytic orchids of Asia and Australia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phalaenopsis</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangal</ent><br/
<ent>Phalangeal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pha*lan"ge*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pha*lan"gal</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
phalanges. See <er>Phalanx</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalanger</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*lan"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phalanger</ets>. See <er>Phalanx</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any marsupial belonging to <gen>Phalangista</gen>,
<gen>Cuscus</gen>, <gen>Petaurus</gen>, and other genera of the family
<fam>Phalangistid<ae/</fam>. They are arboreal, and the species of
<gen>Petaurus</gen> are furnished with lateral parachutes. See
<cref>Flying phalanger</cref>, under <er>Flying</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalanges</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*lan"ges</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<def><pluf>pl.</pluf> of <er>Phalanx</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangian</ent><br/
<ent>Phalangial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pha*lan"gi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pha*lan"gi*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Phalangeal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangid</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*lan"gid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phalangides</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One
of the Phalangoidea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangious</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*lan"gi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phalangium</ets> a kind of venomous spider, Gr.
<grk>fala`ggion</grk>, fr. <grk>fa`lagx</grk> a spider. Cf.
<er>Phalanx</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
Phalangoidea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangist</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*lan"gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any arboreal marsupial of the genus <gen>Phalangista</gen>. The
vulpine phalangist (<spn>Phalangista vulpina</spn>) is the largest
species, the full grown male being about two and a half feet long. It
has a large bushy tail.</def> <-- Spanish history -- member of the
Falange?? Sp.? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangistine</ent><br/
<ent>Phalangister</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phal`an*gis"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phal`an*gis"tine</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Phalangist</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangite</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"an*gite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>falaggi`ths</grk>: cf. F. <ets>phalangite</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
soldier belonging to a phalanx.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalangoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phal`an*goi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from <ets>Phalangium</ets> the daddy longlegs (see <er>Phalangious</er>)
+ Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division
of Arachnoidea, including the <stype>daddy longlegs</stype> or
<stype>harvestman</stype> (<gen>Phalangium</gen>) and many similar
kinds. They have long, slender, many-jointed legs; usually a rounded,
segmented abdomen; and chelate jaws. They breathe by trache<ae/. Called
also <altname><ord>Phalangides</ord></altname>,
<altname><ord>Phalangidea</ord></altname>,
<altname><ord>Phalangiida</ord></altname>, and
<altname><ord>Opilionea</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalanstere</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha`lan`st<eacute/re"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A phalanstery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalansterian</ent><br/
<hw>Phal`an*ste"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>phalanst<eacute/rien</ets>, a. & n.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
phalansterianism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalansterian</ent><br/
<hw>Phal`an*ste"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who favors the system
of phalansteries proposed by <persfn>Fourier</persfn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalansterianism</ent><br/
<ent>Phalansterism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pha*lan"ster*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phal`an*ste"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A system of phalansteries proposed by <persfn>Fourier</persfn>;
Fourierism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalanstery</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"an*ster*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>phalanst<egrave/re</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>fa`lagx</grk> a phalanx +
<grk>stereo`s</grk> firm, solid.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An association
or community organized on the plan of <persfn>Fourier</persfn>. See
<er>Fourierism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The dwelling house of a Fourierite
community.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalanx</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"lanx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phalanxes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Phalanges</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., from Gr. <grk>fa`lagx</grk>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A body of heavy-armed infantry
formed in ranks and files close and deep. There were several different
arrangements, the phalanx varying in depth from four to twenty-five or
more ranks of men.</def> <ldquo/In cubic <xex>phalanx</xex> firm
advanced.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Grecian <qex>phalanx</qex>, moveless as a tower.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any body of troops or men formed in close array, or
any combination of people distinguished for firmness and solidity of a
union.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At present they formed a united <qex>phalanx</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sheep recumbent, and the sheep that grazed,<br/
All huddling into <qex>phalanx</qex>, stood and gazed.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A Fourierite community; a phalanstery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of the digital bones of the
hand or foot, beyond the metacarpus or metatarsus; an
internode.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[<ets>pl</ets>. <er>Phalanges</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A group or bundle of stamens, as in polyadelphous
flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalarope</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"a*rope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>falaro`s</grk> having a patch of white + <grk>poy`s</grk>,
<grk>podo`s</grk>, a foot: cf. F. <ets>phalarope</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of <gen>Phalaropus</gen> and allied
genera of small wading birds (<ord>Grall<ae/</ord>), having lobate toes.
They are often seen far from land, swimming in large flocks. Called also
<altname>sea goose</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalaropidae</ent><br/
<hw>Phalaropidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of
birds, consisting of the phalaropes.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Phalaropidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phalaropus</ent><br/
<hw>Phalaropus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The type genus of the
<fam>Phalaropidae</fam>: the phalaropes.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phalaropus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phallaceae</ent><br/
<hw>Phallaceae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of fungi
consisting of the true stinkhorns.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family Phallaceae.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phallales</ent><br/
<hw>Phallales</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>An order of fungi comprising
the stinkhorns and related forms whose mature hymenium is slimy and
fetid; sometimes placed in the subclass
<subclass>Homobasidiomycetes</subclass>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> order <ord>Phallales</ord>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phallic</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"lic</hw> <pr>(f<acr/l"l<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>falliko`s</grk>]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the phallus, or to
phallism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phallicism</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"li*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Phallism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phallism</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"lism</hw> <pr>(f<acr/l"l<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
worship of the generative principle in nature, symbolized by the
phallus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phallus</ent><br/
<hw>Phal"lus</hw> <pr>(f<acr/l"l<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Phalli</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a
phallus (in sense 1), Gr. <grk>fallo`s</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The emblem of the generative power in nature, carried in procession
in the Bacchic orgies, or worshiped in various ways.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The penis or clitoris, or the
embryonic or primitive organ from which either may be derived.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of fungi which have a
fetid and disgusting odor; the stinkhorn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1075 --></p>

<p><ent>Phanar</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"ar</hw> <pr>(f<acr/n"<dot/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk.
<ets>fanar</ets>, fr. NGr. <grk>fana`ri</grk> lighthouse, Gr.
<grk>fana`rion</grk> lantern; -- from a lighthouse on a point projecting
into the Golden Horn.]</ety> <def>A quarter of Constantinople which,
after the Turkish conquest of the city, became the chief Greek quarter;
hence, the Greek officials of Turkey, or phanariots, as a
class.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanariote</ent><br/
<ent>Phanariot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pha*nar"i*ot</hw> <pr>(f<adot/*n<acr/r"<icr/*<ocr/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> Also <hw>Pha*nar"i*ote</hw>
<pr>(f<adot/*n<acr/r"<icr/*<omac/t)</pr>}</mhw>. <ety>[NGr.
<grk>Fanariw`ths</grk>, fr. <ets>Phanar</ets>. See
<er>Phanar</er>.]</ety> <def>One of the Greeks of Constantinople who
after the Turkish conquest became powerful in clerical and other offices
under Turkish patronage.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phane</ent><br/
<hw>Phane</hw> <pr>(f<amac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Fane</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Joye.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerite</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"er*ite</hw> <pr>(f<acr/n"<etil/r*<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>fanero`s</grk> visible, from <grk>fai`nein</grk> to bring
to light.]</ety> <def>Evident; visible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phanerite series</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>the
uppermost part of the earth's crust, consisting of deposits produced by
causes in obvious operation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerocarpae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phan`er*o*car"p<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>fanero`s</grk> evident + <grk>karpo`s</grk>
fruit (but taken to mean, ovary).]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Acraspeda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerocodonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phan`er*o*co*don"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fanero`s</grk> evident + <grk>kw`dwn</grk> a bell.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having an umbrella-shaped or bell-shaped body,
with a wide, open cavity beneath; -- said of certain
jellyfishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerocrystalline</ent><br/
<hw>Phan`er*o*crys"tal*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fanero`s</grk> visible + E. <ets>crystalline</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Distinctly crystalline; -- used of rocks.
Opposed to <ant>cryptocrystalline</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerodactyla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phan`er*o*dac"ty*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fanero`s</grk> evident + <grk>da`ktylos</grk>
finger.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Saurur<ae/</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerogamia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phan`er*o*ga"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fanero`s</grk> visible (fr. <grk>fai`nein</grk>
to bring to light) + <grk>ga`mos</grk> marriage.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>That one of the two primary divisions of the
vegetable kingdom which contains the phanerogamic, or flowering,
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerogamian</ent><br/
<hw>Phan`er*o*ga"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Phanerogamous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phanerogamous</ent><br/
<ent>Phanerogamic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phan`er*o*gam"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phan`er*og"a*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having visible flowers containing distinct stamens and pistils; --
said of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaneroglossal</ent><br/
<hw>Phan`er*o*glos"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fanero`s</grk> evident + <grk>glw`ssa</grk> tongue.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a conspicious tongue; -- said of certain
reptiles and insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantascope</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"ta*scope</hw> <pr>(f<acr/n"t<adot/*sk<omac/p)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fa`ntasma</grk> image +
<ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>An optical instrument or toy, resembling
the phenakistoscope, and illustrating the same principle; -- called also
<altname>phantasmascope</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasm</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"tasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phantasma</ets>. See <er>Phantom</er>, and cf.
<er>Fantasm</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Spelt also <asp>fantasm</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An image formed by the mind, and supposed to be real or
material; a shadowy or airy appearance; sometimes, an optical illusion;
a phantom; a dream.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They be but <qex>phantasms</qex> or apparitions.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mental image or representation of a real object; a
fancy; a notion.</def> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Figures or little features, of which the description had produced
in you no <qex>phantasm</qex> or expectation.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phan"tas"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>A phantasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmagoria</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas`ma*go"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <grk>fa`ntasma</grk> a phantasm + <grk>'agora`</grk> an assembly,
fr. <grk>'agei`rein</grk> to gather: cf. F.
<ets>phantasmagorie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An optical effect
produced by a magic lantern. The figures are painted in transparent
colors, and all the rest of the glass is opaque black. The screen is
between the spectators and the instrument, and the figures are often
made to appear as in motion, or to merge into one another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The apparatus by which such an effect is
produced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: A medley of figures; illusive images.</def>
<ldquo/This mental <xex>phantasmagoria</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmagorial</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas`ma*go"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of,
relating to, or resembling phantasmagoria; phantasmagoric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmagoric</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas`ma*gor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to phantasmagoria; phantasmagorial.</def>
<rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmagory</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas"ma*go*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Phantasmagoria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmal</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, of
the nature of, or resembling, a phantasm; spectral; illusive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmascope</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas"ma*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Phantascope</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmatical</ent><br/
<hw>Phan`tas*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phantasmaticus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Phantasmal.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasmatography</ent><br/
<hw>Phan*tas`ma*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`ntasma</grk>, <grk>fanta`smatos</grk>, phantasm +
<ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A description of celestial phenomena, as
rainbows, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantastical</ent><br/
<ent>Phantastic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phan*tas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phan*tas"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Fantastic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantasy</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"ta*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Fantasy</er>, and <er>Fancy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantom</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"tom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>fantome</ets>, <ets>fantosme</ets>, <ets>fantesme</ets>, OF.
<ets>fant<ocir/me</ets>, fr. L. <ets>phantasma</ets>, Gr.
<grk>fa`ntasma</grk>, fr. <grk>fai`nein</grk> to show. See
<er>Fancy</er>, and cf. <er>Pha<eum/ton</er>, <er>Phantasm</er>,
<er>Phase</er>.]</ety> <def>That which has only an apparent existence;
an apparition; a specter; a phantasm; a sprite; an airy spirit; an ideal
image.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strange <qex>phantoms</qex> rising as the mists arise.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was a <qex>phantom</qex> of delight.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phantom ship</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Flying
Dutchman</cref>, under <er>Flying</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Phantom
tumor</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a swelling, especially of the
abdomen, due to muscular spasm, accumulation of flatus, etc., simulating
an actual tumor in appearance, but disappearing upon the administration
of an an<ae/sthetic.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantom</ent><br/
<hw>Phan"tom</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being, or of the nature of, a
phantom.</def></p>

<p><q><qex>Phantom</qex> isles are floating in the skies.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantom circuit</ent><br/
<hw>Phantom circuit</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The equivalent of an
additional circuit or wire, in reality not existing, obtained by certain
arrangements of real circuits, as in some multiplex telegraph
systems.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phantomatic</ent><br/
<hw>Phan`tom*at"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Phantasmal.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharaoh</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"raoh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>par<omac/h</ets>; of Egyptian origin: cf. L. <ets>pharao</ets>, Gr.
<grk>faraw`</grk>. Cf. <er>Faro</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A title by
which the sovereigns of ancient Egypt were designated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>See <er>Faro</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pharaoh's chicken</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
gier-eagle, or Egyptian vulture; -- so called because often sculpured on
Egyptian monuments. It is nearly white in color.</cd> --
<col><b>Pharaoh's rat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common
ichneumon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharaon</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"ra*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pharaoh</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharaonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`a*on"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pharaonique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Pharaohs, or
kings of ancient Egypt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phare</ent><br/
<hw>Phare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pharos</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A beacon tower; a
lighthouse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a harbor.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharisaical</ent><br/
<ent>Pharisaic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phar`i*sa"ic</hw> <pr>(f<acr/r`<icr/*s<amac/"<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Phar`i*sa"ic*al</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Pharisaicus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>Farisai:ko`s</grk>: cf. F. <ets>pharisa<ium/que</ets>. See
<er>Pharisee</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Pharisees; resembling the Pharisees.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>Pharisaic</xex> sect among the Jews.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Addicted to external forms
and ceremonies; making a show of religion without the spirit of it;
ceremonial; formal; hypocritical; self-righteous.</def> <ldquo/Excess of
outward and <xex>pharisaical</xex> holiness.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Pharisaical</xex> ostentation.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Phar`i*sa"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Phar`i*sa"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharisaism</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`i*sa"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pharisaisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The notions, doctrines,
and conduct of the Pharisees, as a sect.</def>
<rj><au>Sharp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rigid observance of external forms of religion,
without genuine piety; hypocrisy in religion; a censorious,
self-righteous spirit in matters of morals or manners.</def> <ldquo/A
piece of <xex>pharisaism</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharisean</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`i*se"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pharisaeus</ets>, Gr. <grk>Farisai^os</grk>.]</ety> <def>Following
the practice of Pharisees; Pharisaic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Pharisean</xex> disciples.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharisee</ent><br/
<hw>Phar"i*see</hw> <pr>(f<acr/r"<icr/*s<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>Pharisaeus</ets>, Gr. <grk>Farisai^os</grk>, from Heb.
<ets>p<amac/rash</ets> to separate.]</ety> <def>One of a sect or party
among the Jews, noted for a strict and formal observance of rites and
ceremonies and of the traditions of the elders, and whose pretensions to
superior sanctity led them to separate themselves from the other
Jews.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phariseeism</ent><br/
<hw>Phar"i*see*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pharisaism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmaceutical</ent><br/
<ent>Pharmaceutic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phar`ma*ceu"tic</hw>
<pr>(f<aum/r`m<adot/*s<umac/"t<icr/k)</pr>, <hw>Phar`ma*ceu"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(-t<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pharmaceuticus</ets>, Gr. <grk>farmakeytiko`s</grk>, fr.
<grk>farmakey`ein</grk>: cf. F. <ets>pharmaceutique</ets>. See
<er>Pharmacy</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the knowledge or art
of pharmacy, or to the art of preparing medicines according to the rules
or formulas of pharmacy; <as>as, <ex>pharmaceutical</ex>
preparations</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Phar`ma*ceu"tic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pharmaceutical chemistry</b></col>, <cd>that department
of chemistry which ascertains or regulates the composition of medicinal
substances.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmaceutics</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*ceu"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The science
of preparing medicines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmaceutist</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*ceu"tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in pharmacy; a pharmacist; a druggist. See the Note under
<er>Apothecary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacist</ent><br/
<hw>Phar"ma*cist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in
pharmacy; a pharmaceutist; a druggist; an apothecary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacodymanics</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*co*dy*man"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> medicine + E. <ets>dynamics</ets>.]</ety> <def>That
branch of pharmacology which treats of the action and the effects of
medicines in the body, and also their distribution in various bodily
tissues over time, and their breakdown and excretion.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacognosis</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*cog*no"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> a drug + <grk>gnw^sis</grk> a knowing.]</ety>
<def>That branch of pharmacology which treats of unprepared medicines or
simples; -- usually called <altname>pharmacognosy</altname>, and also
<altname>pharmacography</altname>, and
<altname>pharmacomathy</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacognosy</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*cog"no*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pharmacognosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The study of the distribution of, methods for
finding, and properties of medically useful agents in natural sources,
especially plants.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacography</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*cog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> a drug + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pharmacognosis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacolite</ent><br/
<hw>Phar*mac"o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> drug, poisonous drug + <ets>-lite</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pharmacolithe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous
arsenate of lime, usually occurring in silky fibers of a white or
grayish color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*col"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pharmacologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One skilled in
pharmacology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacology</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*col"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> drug + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pharmacologie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The science dealing
with knowledge of the preparation, use, and effects of drugs or
medicines; the art of preparing medicines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A treatise on the art of preparing
medicines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacomathy</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*com"a*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> a drug + <grk>manqa`nein</grk> to learn.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Pharmacognosis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacon</ent><br/
<hw>Phar"ma*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk>.]</ety> <def>A medicine or drug; also, a
poison.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacopoeia</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*co*p<oe/"ia</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <grk>farmakopoii:`a</grk> the preparation of medicines;
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> medicine + <grk>poiei^n</grk> to make.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A book or treatise describing the drugs, preparations,
etc., used in medicine; especially, one that is issued by official
authority and considered as an authoritative standard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A chemical laboratory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacopolist</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*cop"o*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pharmacopola</ets>, Gr. <grk>farmakopw`lhs</grk>;
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> medicine + <grk>pwlei^n</grk> to sell.]</ety>
<def>One who sells medicines; an apothecary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacosiderite</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`ma*co*sid"er*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> drug, poison + E. <ets>siderite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous arsenate of iron occurring in green or
yellowish green cubic crystals; cube ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharmacy</ent><br/
<hw>Phar"ma*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>fermacie</ets>, OF. <ets>farmacie</ets>, <ets>pharmacie</ets>, F.
<ets>pharmacie</ets>, Gr. <grk>farmakei`a</grk>, fr.
<grk>farmakey`ein</grk> to administer or use medicines, fr.
<grk>fa`rmakon</grk> medicine.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art or
practice of preparing and preserving drugs, and of compounding and
dispensing medicines according to prescriptions of physicians; the
occupation of an apothecary or a pharmaceutical chemist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place where medicines are compounded; a drug
store; an apothecary's shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharo</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pharos; a
lighthouse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>See <er>Faro</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharology</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*rol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fa`ros</grk> a lighthouse + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art
or science which treats of lighthouses and signal lights.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharos</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"ros</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>fa`ros</grk>, fr. <grk>Fa`ros</grk> an island in the Bay of
Alexandria, where king <person>Ptolemy Philadelphus</person> built a
famous lighthouse, one of the seven wonders of the ancient world.]</ety>
<def>A lighthouse or beacon for the guidance of seamen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . built a <qex>pharos</qex>, or lighthouse.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngal</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*ryn"gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pharyngeal.</def>
<rj><au>H. Sweet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngeal</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`yn*ge"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pharynx</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
pharynx; in the region of the pharynx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngeal</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`yn*ge"al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
pharyngeal bone or cartilage; especially, one of the <stype>lower
pharyngeals</stype>, which belong to the rudimentary fifth branchial
arch in many fishes, or one of the <stype>upper pharyngeals</stype>, or
pharyngobranchials, which are the dorsal elements in the complete
branchial arches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phar`yn*gi"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pharynx</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the pharynx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngobranchial</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*ryn`go*bran"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pharynx</ets> + <ets>branchial</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pharynx and the
branchi<ae/; -- applied especially to the dorsal elements in the
branchial arches of fishes. See <er>Pharyngeal</er>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A pharyngobranchial, or upper pharyngeal, bone
or cartilage.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngobranchii</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*ryn`go*bran"chi*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pharynx</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Leptocardia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngognathi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phar`yn*gog"na*thi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pharynx</er>, and <er>Gnathic</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of fishes in which the lower
pharyngeal bones are united. It includes the scaroid, labroid, and
embioticoid fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngolaryngeal</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*ryn`go*lar`yn*ge"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pharynx</ets> + <ets>laryngeal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining both to pharynx and the larynx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngopneusta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*ryn`gop*neus"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fa`rygx</grk> the pharynx + <grk>pnei^n</grk> to
breathe.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of invertebrates
including the Tunicata and Enteropneusta.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pha*ryn`gop*neus"tal</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngotome</ent><br/
<hw>Pha*ryn"go*tome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>
<def>An instrument for incising or scarifying the tonsils,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharyngotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Phar`yn*got"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pharynx</ets> + Gr. <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut: cf. F.
<ets>pharyngotomie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The operation of making an incision into the pharynx, to remove a
tumor or anything that obstructs the passage.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Scarification or incision of the tonsils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pharynx</ent><br/
<hw>Phar"ynx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>pharynges</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fa`rygx</grk>, <grk>fa`ryggos</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>pharynx</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The part of the
alimentary canal between the cavity of the mouth and the esophagus. It
has one or two external openings through the nose in the higher
vertebrates, and lateral branchial openings in fishes and some
amphibias.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phascolarctos</ent><br/
<hw>Phascolarctos</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of animals
consisting of the koalas.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phascolarctos</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phascolome</ent><br/
<hw>Phas"co*lome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ pouch +
<?/ mouse.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marsupial of the genus
<gen>Phascolomys</gen>; a wombat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase</ent><br/
<hw>Phase</hw> <pr>(f<amac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phases</plw> <pr>(f<amac/z"<ecr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
<ets>phasis</ets>, Gr. <grk>fa`sis</grk>, fr. <grk>fai`nein</grk> to
make to appear: cf. F. <ets>phase</ets>. See <er>Phenomenon</er>,
<er>Phantom</er>, and <er>Emphasis</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
which is exhibited to the eye; the appearance which anything manifests,
especially any one among different and varying appearances of the same
object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any appearance or aspect of an object of mental
apprehension or view; <as>as, the problem has many
<ex>phases</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A particular appearance or
state in a regularly recurring cycle of changes with respect to quantity
of illumination or form of enlightened disk; <as>as, the <ex>phases</ex>
of the moon or planets</as>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Moon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Any one point or portion in a
recurring series of changes, as in the changes of motion of one of the
particles constituting a wave or vibration; one portion of a series of
such changes, in distinction from a contrasted portion, as the portion
on one side of a position of equilibrium, in contrast with that on the
opposite side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Phys. Chem.)</fld> <def>A homogenous, physically
distinct portion of matter in a system not homogeneous; <as>as, the
three <ex>phases</ex>, ice, water, and aqueous vapor; in a mixture of
gasoline and water, the gasoline will settle as the upper
<ex>phase</ex></as>. A phase may be either a single chemical substance
or a mixture, as of gases.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>In certain birds and mammals, one
of two or more color variations characteristic of the species, but
independent of the ordinary seasonal and sexual differences, and often
also of age. Some of the herons which appear in white and colored
phases, and certain squirrels which are sometimes uniformly blackish
instead of the usual coloration, furnish examples. Color phases occur
also in other animals, notably in butterflies.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>the relation at any instant of
any cyclically varying physical quantity, such as voltage in an A.C.
circuit, an electromagnetic wave, a sound wave, or a rotating object, to
its initial value as expressed as a fractional part of the complete
cycle. It is usually expressed in angular measure, the complete cycle
being 360<deg/. Such periodic variations are generally well represented
by sine curves; and phase relations are shown by the relative positions
of the crests and hollows of such curves. Magnitudes which have the same
phase are said to be <xex>in phase</xex>.</def> <note>The concept of
phase is also applied generally to any periodically varying phenomenon,
as the cycle of daylight. One person who sleeps during the day and
another who sleeps at night may be said to be <it>out of phase</it> with
each other.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>The relation at any instant of a periodically varying electric
magnitude, as electro-motive force, a current, etc., to its initial
value as expressed in factorial parts of the complete cycle. It is
usually expressed in angular measure, the cycle being four right angles,
or 360<deg/.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase</ent><br/
<hw>Phase</hw> <pr>(f<amac/z)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Feeze</er>.]</ety> <def>To disturb the composure of; to disconcert;
to nonplus; -- an older spelling, now replaced by <asp>faze</asp>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq., Archaic]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> faze.</syn>
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase angle</ent><br/
<hw>Phase angle</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The angle expressing phase
relation.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase converter</ent><br/
<hw>Phase converter</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A machine for
converting an alternating current into an alternating current of a
different number of phases and the same frequency.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase displacement</ent><br/
<hw>Phase displacement</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A charge of phase
whereby an alternating current attains its maximum later or earlier. An
inductance would cause a lag, a capacity would cause an advance, in
phase.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase in</ent><br/
<hw>Phase" in`</hw> <pr>(f<amac/z" <icr/n`)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To begin an activity or to incorporate new elements or replace
older elements of an activity in steps, or by degrees.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasel</ent><br/
<hw>Pha"sel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phaselus</ets>, <ets>phaseolus</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/: cf. F.
<ets>phas<eacute/ole</ets>, <ets>fas<eacute/ole</ets>. Cf.
<er>Fesels</er>.]</ety> <def>The French bean, or kidney bean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaseless</ent><br/
<hw>Phase"less</hw> <pr>(f<amac/z"l<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Without a phase, or visible form.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>phaseless</xex> and increasing gloom.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Poe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasemeter</ent><br/
<ent>Phase meter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phase meter</hw>, <it>or</it> <hw>Phase"me`ter</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A device for measuring the
difference in phase of two alternating currents of electromotive
forces.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaseolus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha*se"o*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of leguminous plants,
including the Lima bean, the kidney bean, the scarlet runner, etc. See
<er>Bean</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phaseomannite</ent><br/
<hw>Pha`se*o*man"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called
because found in the unripe fruit of the bean (<ets>Phaseolus
vulgaris</ets>).]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Inosite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase out</ent><br/
<hw>Phase" out`</hw> <pr>(f<amac/z" out`)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
halt (an activity) in steps, or gradually; <as>as, to <ex>phase out</ex>
use of 386-based PC's</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase rule</ent><br/
<hw>Phase rule</hw>. <fld>(Phys. Chem.)</fld> <def>A generalization with
regard to systems of chemical equilibrium, discovered by Prof.
<person>J. Willard Gibbs</person>. It may be stated thus: <it>The degree
of variableness (number of degrees of freedom) of a system is equal to
the number of components minus the number of phases, plus two</it>.
Thus, if the components be salt and water, and the phases salt, ice,
saturated solution, and vapor, the system is invariant, that is, there
is only one set of conditions under which these four phases can exist in
equilibrium. If only three phases be considered, the system is
univariant, that is, the fixing of one condition, as temperature,
determines the others.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase splitter</ent><br/
<hw>Phase splitter</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A device by which a
single-phase current is split into two or more currents differing in
phase. It is used in starting single-phase induction motors.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phase splitting</ent><br/
<hw>Phase splitting</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The dephasing of the
two parts of a single alternating current in two dissimilar branches of
a given circuit.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasianidae</ent><br/
<hw>Phasianidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of fowl
including the pheasants, quails, and partridges.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Phasianidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasianus</ent><br/
<hw>Phasianus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The type genus of the
<fam>Phasianidae</fam>: the typical pheasants.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phasianus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasing</ent><br/
<hw>Phas"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to phase or differences of phase.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasing current</ent><br/
<hw>Phasing current</hw>. <def>The momentary current between two
alternating-current generators when juxtaposed in parallel and not
agreeing exactly in phase or period.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasing transformer</ent><br/
<hw>Phasing transformer</hw>. <def>Any of several transformers (there
must be at least two) for changing phase.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pha"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phases</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Phase</er>.</def> <rj><au>Creech.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasma</ent><br/
<ent>Phasm</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phas"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>phasma</ets>, Gr. <grk>fa`sma</grk>.
See <er>Phase</er>.]</ety> <def>An apparition; a phantom; an
appearance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Hammond. Sir T.
Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasmatidae</ent><br/
<hw>Phasmatidae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family
consisting of the stick insects.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Phasmidae, family <fam>Phasmidae</fam>, family
<fam>Phasmatidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasmatodea</ent><br/
<hw>Phasmatodea</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>in some
classifications considered a suborder of Orthoptera: stick insects; leaf
insects.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Phasmida, order Phasmida, order
Phasmatodea.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasmid</ent><br/
<hw>Phas"mid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Phasm</er>.
Probably so called from its mimicking, or appearing like, inanimate
objects.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any orthopterous insect of the
family <fam>Phasmid<ae/</fam>, as a leaf insect or a stick
insect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasmidae</ent><br/
<hw>Phasmidae</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Same as
<er>phasmatidae</er>; the stick insects.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Phasmidae</fam>, Phasmatidae, family
<fam>Phasmatidae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phasmidia</ent><br/
<hw>Phasmidia</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>One of two subgroups of
Nematoda used in some classification systems.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> class Phasmidia.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phassachate</ent><br/
<hw>Phas"sa*chate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ the
wood pigeon + <?/ the agate.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The
lead-colored agate; -- so called in reference to its color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phatagin</ent><br/
<hw>Phat"a*gin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/;
perhaps from native name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The long-tailed
pangolin (<spn>Manis tetradactyla</spn>); -- called also
<altname>ipi</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ph.D.</ent><br/
<ent>PhD</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>PhD</hw>, <hw>Ph.D.</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrfev.
from L., <ets>Ph</ets>ilosophae <ets>D</ets>octor.]</ety> <def>A
scholastic degree awarded for study beyond college; the Doctorate degree
in Philosophy. In America it is usually based on at least 3 years
graduate study and a dissertation; the doctorate is the highest degree
awarded by a graduate school, and is awarded in other fields, such as
Doctor of Arts. The <ex>Ph.D.</ex> is broadly applicable, as in most
fields of science and technology; -- the term <it>philosophy</it> being
derived from an earlier label for experimental science as <it>Natural
Philosophy</it>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Doctor of Philosophy.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pheasant</ent><br/
<hw>Pheas"ant</hw> <pr>(f<ecr/z"<ait/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>fesant</ets>, <ets>fesaunt</ets>, OF. <ets>faisant</ets>,
<ets>faisan</ets>, F. <ets>faisan</ets>, L. <ets>phasianus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>fasiano`s</grk> (sc. <grk>'o`rnis</grk>) the Phasian bird,
pheasant, fr. <grk>Fa`sis</grk> a river in Colchis or Pontus.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of large
gallinaceous birds of the genus <gen>Phasianus</gen>, and many other
genera of the family <fam>Phasianid<ae/</fam>, found chiefly in
Asia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- # note collocations in notes have italic font in WEB1913 -->
<note><hand/ The <mcol><col><b>common pheasant</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>English pheasant</b></col></mcol> (<spn>Phasianus
Colchicus</spn>) is now found over most of temperate Europe, but was
introduced from Asia. The <col><b>ring-necked pheasant</b></col>
(<spn>Phasianus torquatus</spn>) and the <col><b>green
pheasant</b></col> (<spn>Phasianus versicolor</spn>) have been
introduced into Oregon. The <col><b>golden pheasant</b></col>
(<spn>Thaumalea picta</spn>) is one of the most beautiful species. The
<col><b>silver pheasant</b></col> (<spn>Euplocamus nychthemerus</spn>)
of China, and several related species from Southern Asia, are very
beautiful.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ruffed grouse.</def>
<mark>[Southern U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Various other birds are locally called
<xex>pheasants</xex>, as the lyre bird, the leipoa, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fireback pheasant</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Fireback</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Gold pheasant</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Golden pheasant</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a Chinese pheasant (<spn>Thaumalea picta</spn>), having rich, varied
colors. The crest is amber-colored, the rump is golden yellow, and the
under parts are scarlet.</cd> -- <col><b>Mountain pheasant</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the ruffed grouse.</cd> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pheasant coucal</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a large Australian cuckoo (<spn>Centropus phasianus</spn>). The
general color is black, with chestnut wings and brown tail. Called also
<altname>pheasant cuckoo</altname>. The name is also applied to other
allied species.</cd> -- <col><b>Pheasant duck</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The pintail</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>The hooded merganser.</cd> -- <col><b>Pheasant parrot</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large and beautiful Australian parrakeet
(<spn>Platycercus Adelaidensis</spn>). The male has the back black, the
feathers margined with yellowish blue and scarlet, the quills deep blue,
the wing coverts and cheeks light blue, the crown, sides of the neck,
breast, and middle of the belly scarlet.</cd> -- <col><b>Pheasant's
eye</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A red-flowered herb
(<spn>Adonis autumnalis</spn>) of the Crowfoot family; -- called also
<altname>pheasant's-eye Adonis</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
garden pink (<spn>Dianthus plumarius</spn>); -- called also
<altname>Pheasant's-eye pink</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pheasant
shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any marine univalve shell of the
genus <gen>Phasianella</gen>, of which numerous species are found in
tropical seas. The shell is smooth and usually richly colored, the
colors often forming blotches like those of a pheasant.</cd> --
<col><b>Pheasant wood</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Partridge wood</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, under <er>Partridge</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sea pheasant</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
pintail.</cd> -- <col><b>Water pheasant</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The sheldrake</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The hooded
merganser.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1076 --></p>

<p><ent>Pheasantry</ent><br/
<hw>Pheas"ant*ry</hw> <pr>(f<ecr/z"<ait/nt*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>faisanderie</ets>.]</ety> <def>A place for keeping and
rearing pheasants.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phebe</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"be</hw> <pr>(f<emac/"b<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Ph<oe/be</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pheer</ent><br/
<hw>Pheer</hw> <pr>(f<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Fere</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pheese</ent><br/
<hw>Pheese</hw> <pr>(f<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To comb;
also, to beat; to worry.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Local]</mark> <see>See
<er>Feaze</er>, <pos>v.</pos></see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pheese</ent><br/
<hw>Pheese</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Fretful excitement.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Local]</mark> <see>See <er>Feaze</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phelloderm</ent><br/
<hw>Phel"lo*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fello`s</grk> cork + <ets>-derm</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A layer of green parenchymatous cells formed on the inner side of
the phellogen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phellogen</ent><br/
<hw>Phel"lo*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fello`s</grk> cork + <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The tissue of young cells which produces cork cells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phelloplastics</ent><br/
<hw>Phel`lo*plas"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fello`s</grk> cork + <grk>pla`ssein</grk> to mold.]</ety> <def>Art
of modeling in cork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenacetine</ent><br/
<ent>Phenacetin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phe*nac"e*tin</hw>, <hw>Phe*nac"e*tine</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phen</ets>yl + <ets>acet</ets>ic
+ <ets>-in</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Pharm.)</fld> <def>A white, crystalline
compound, <chform>C10H13O2N</chform>, once used in medicine principally
as an antipyretic. It is now seldom used because of serious side
effects.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> N-(4-ethoxyphenyl)acetamide, p-acetophenetidide,
p-ethoxyacetanilide, acetophenetidin, para-acetphenetidin,
p-acetophenetide.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenacite</ent><br/
<hw>Phen"a*cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fe`nax</grk>, <grk>fe`nakos</grk>, impostor, deceiver.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A glassy colorless mineral occurring in
rhombohedral crystals, sometimes used as a gem. It is a silicate of
glucina, and receives its name from its deceptive similarity to
quartz.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenakistoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Phen`a*kis"to*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fenakisth`s</grk> a deceiver + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
revolving disk on which figures drawn in different relative attitudes
are seen successively, so as to produce the appearance of an object in
actual motion, as an animal leaping, etc., in consequence of the
persistence of the successive visual impressions of the retina. It is
often arranged so that the figures may be projected upon a
screen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenalgin</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nal"gin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phen</ets>yl
+ an<ets>alg</ets>etic + <ets>-in</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Pharm.)</fld>
<def>An ammoniated compound of phenyl and acetamide, used as an
analgesic and antipyretic. It resembles phenacetin in its therapeutic
action.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenanthrene</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nan"threne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phen</ets>yl + <ets>antr</ets>ac<ets>ene</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A complex hydrocarbon, <chform>C14H10</chform>,
found in coal tar, and obtained as a white crystalline substance with a
bluish fluorescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenanthridine</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nan"thri*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phenanthr</ets>ene + pyr<ets>idine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A nitrogenous hydrocarbon base,
<chform>C13H9N</chform>, analogous to phenanthrene and
quinoline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenanthroline</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nan"thro*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phenanthr</ets>ene + quin<ets>oline</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Either of two metameric nitrogenous hydrocarbon
bases, <chform>C12H8N2</chform>, analogous to phenanthridine, but more
highly nitrogenized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phene</ent><br/
<hw>Phene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Benzene.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenetol</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"ne*tol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phen</ets>yl
+ <ets>et</ets>hyl + L. <ets>ol</ets>eum oil.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The ethyl ether of phenol, obtained as an aromatic liquid,
<chform>C6H5.O.C2H5</chform>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenic</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, derived from, or resembling, phenyl or phenol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phenic acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
phenol.</cd> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenician</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*ni"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Ph<oe/nician</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenicine</ent><br/
<hw>Phen"i*cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>foi^nix</grk> purple red: cf. F.
<ets>ph<eacute/nicine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A purple powder precipitated when a sulphuric solution of indigo is
diluted with water.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A coloring matter produced
by the action of a mixture of strong nitric and sulphuric acids on
phenylic alcohol.</def> <rj><au>Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenicious</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*ni"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phoeniceus</ets>, Gr. <grk>foini`keos</grk>, from
<grk>foi^nix</grk> purple red.]</ety> <def>Of a red color with a slight
mixture of gray.</def> <rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenicopter</ent><br/
<hw>Phen`i*cop"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phoenicopterus</ets>, Gr. <grk>foiniko`pteros</grk>, i. e.,
red-feathered; <grk>foi^nix</grk>, <grk>foi`nikos</grk>, purple red +
<grk>ptero`n</grk> feather: cf. F.
<ets>ph<eacute/nicopt<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
flamingo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenix</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"nix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phenixes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>phoenix</ets>, Gr.
<grk>foi^nix</grk>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>ph<oe/nix</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Myth.)</fld> <def>A
bird fabled to exist single, to be consumed by fire by its own act, and
to rise again from its ashes. Hence, an emblem of immortality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A southern
constellation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A marvelous person or thing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A person or thing that suffered destruction or
defeat and was restored to its former state.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>to rise like a phoenix</b></col>, <cd>to resume an
endeavor after an apparently final defeat.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenocryst</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"no*cryst</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fai`nesqai</grk> to appear + <grk>kry`stallos</grk> crystal.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>One of the prominent embedded crystals of a
porphyry.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenogamia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phen`o*ga"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Ph<ae/nogamia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenogamous</ent><br/
<ent>Phenogamic</ent><br/
<ent>Phenogamian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phen`o*ga"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phen`o*gam"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phe*nog"a*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Ph<ae/nogamian</er>,
<er>Ph<ae/nogamic</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenol</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"nol</hw> <pr>(f<emac/"n<omac/l; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>fai`nein</grk> to show + <ets>-ol</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>ph<eacute/nol</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
white or pinkish crystalline substance, <chform>C6H5OH</chform>,
produced by the destructive distillation of many organic bodies, as
wood, coal, etc., and obtained from the heavy oil from coal
tar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It has a peculiar odor, somewhat resembling creosote,
which is a complex mixture of phenol derivatives. It is of the type of
alcohols, and is called also <altname>phenyl alcohol</altname>, but has
acid properties, and hence is popularly called <altname>carbolic
acid</altname>, and was formerly called <altname>phenic acid</altname>.
It is a powerful caustic poison, and in dilute solution has been used as
an antiseptic.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any one of the series of hydroxyl derivatives of
which phenol proper is the type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Glacial phenol</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>pure
crystallized phenol or carbolic acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Phenol
acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any one of a series of compounds
which are at once a derivative of phenol and an organic acid; thus,
<stype>salicylic acid</stype> is a <ex>phenol acid</ex>.</cd> --
<col><b>Phenol alcohol</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
series of derivatives of phenol and carbinol which have the properties
of both combined; thus, <stype>saligenin</stype> is a <ex>phenol
alcohol</ex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Phenol aldehyde</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any one of a series of compounds having both
phenol and aldehyde properties.</cd> -- <col><b>Phenol
phthalein</b></col>. <cd>See <er>phenolphthalein</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenolate</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"no*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phenol</ets>
+ <ets>-ate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A compound of phenol
analogous to a salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenology</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contracted from
<er>Phenomenology</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The science of
the relations between climate and periodic biological phenomena, as the
migrations and breeding of birds, the flowering and fruiting of plants,
etc.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Phe`no*log"ic*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> -- <wf>Phe`no*log"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Phe*nol"o*gist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenol phthalein</ent><br/
<ent>Phenolphthalein</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Phe`nol*phthal"e*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Phenol
phthalein</hw>.</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white or
yellowish white crystalline substance, <chform>C20H14O4</chform>, formed
by condensation of the anhydride of phthalic acid and phenol. Its
solution in alkalies is brilliant red, but is decolorized by acids. This
reaction, being very delicate, is used as a pH indicator. It is
sometimes used as a laxative in veterinary medicine.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenomenal</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nom"e*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>ph<eacute/nom<eacute/nal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Relating to, or of the
nature of, a phenomenon; hence, extraordinary; wonderful; <as>as, a
<ex>phenomenal</ex> memory</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Phe*nom"e*nal*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenomenalism</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nom"e*nal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>That theory which limits positive or
scientific knowledge to phenomena only, whether material or
spiritual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenomenist</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nom"e*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
believes in the theory of phenomenalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenomenology</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nom`e*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phenomenon</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>ph<eacute/nom<eacute/nologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>A description,
history, or explanation of phenomena.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>phenomenology</xex> of the mind.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenomenon</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nom"e*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phenomena</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>phaenomenon</ets>, Gr. <grk>faino`menon</grk>, fr.
<grk>fai`nesqai</grk> to appear, <grk>fai`nein</grk> to show. See
<er>Phantom</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An appearance; anything
visible; whatever, in matter or spirit, is apparent to, or is
apprehended by, observation; <as>as, the <ex>phenomena</ex> of heat,
light, or electricity; <ex>phenomena</ex> of imagination or
memory.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the <qex>phenomena</qex> of the material world, and in many of
the <qex>phenomena</qex> of mind.</q> <rj><qau>Stewart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which strikes one as strange, unusual, or
unaccountable; an extraordinary or very remarkable person, thing, or
occurrence; <as>as, a musical <ex>phenomenon</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenose</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phen</ets>yl +
dextr<ets>ose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A sweet amorphous
deliquescent substance obtained indirectly from benzene, and isometric
with, and resembling, dextrose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phenotype</ent><br/
<hw>phenotype</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biology)</fld> <def>The
observable characteristics of an organism, resulting from the genetic
characteristics influenced by a particular environment. Contrasted with
<contr>genotype</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phenotypical</ent><br/
<ent>phenotypic</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>phenotypic</hw> <hw>phenotypical</hw></mhw> <pos>adj.</pos>
<fld>(Biology)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or characteristic of a
phenotype; <as>as, <ex>phenotypic</ex> profile</as>. Contrasted with
<contr>genotypic</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenyl</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"nyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>
fai`nein</grk> to bring to light + <ets>-yl</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>ph<eacute/nyle</ets>. So called because it is a by-product of
illuminating gas.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hydrocarbon radical
(<chform>C6H5</chform>) regarded as the essential residue of benzene,
and the basis of an immense number of aromatic derivatives.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phenyl hydrate</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>phenol
or carbolic acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Phenyl hydrazine</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a nitrogenous base
(<chform>C6H5.NH.NH2</chform>) produced artificially as a colorless oil
which unites with acids, ketones, etc., to form easily crystallized
compounds. It is used in organic chemistry, particulary to isolate
compounds in pure form for analysis.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phenylacetamide</ent><br/
<hw>phenylacetamide</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A white crystalline compound
used as an analgesic and also as an antipyretic.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> acetanilide, acetanilid.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenylamine</ent><br/
<hw>Phe`nyl*am"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phenyl</ets> + <ets>amine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Any one of certain class of organic bases regarded as formed from
ammonia by the substitution of phenyl for hydrogen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenylene</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"nyl*ene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A hypothetic radical (<chform>C6H4</chform>) occurring in certain
derivatives of benzene; <as>as, <ex>phenylene</ex>
diamine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phenylic</ent><br/
<hw>Phe*nyl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, derived from, or containing, phenyl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phenylic alcohol</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>phenol.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pheon</ent><br/
<hw>Phe"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. from Old
French.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A bearing representing the head of
a dart or javelin, with long barbs which are engrailed on the inner
edge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phial</ent><br/
<hw>Phi"al</hw> <pr>(f<imac/"<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>fiole</ets>, L. <ets>phiala</ets> a broad, flat, shallow cup or
bowl, Gr. <grk>fia`lh</grk>. cf. <er>Vial</er>.]</ety> <def>A glass
vessel or bottle, especially a small bottle for medicines; a
vial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phial</ent><br/
<hw>Phi"al</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Phialed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Phialing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To put or keep in, or as in, a
phial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Its <qex>phial'd</qex> wrath may fate exhaust.</q>
<rj><qau>Shenstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philabeg</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"a*beg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Filibeg</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philadelphia lawyer</ent><br/
<hw>Philadelphia lawyer</hw> <def>A lawyer knowledgeable about the most
detailed and minute points of law, especially one with an exceptional
propensity and ability to exploit fine technical points of law for the
client's advantage.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philadelphian</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`a*del"phi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>filadelfia</grk> brotherly love, from <grk>fila`delfos</grk>
brotherly; <grk>fi`los</grk> loved, loving, friendly +
<grk>'adelfo`s</grk> brother.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Ptolemy
Philadelphus, or to one of the cities named Philadelphia, esp. the
modern city in Pennsylvania.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philadelphian</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`a*del"phi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or
an inhabitant of Philadelphia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a society of mystics
of the seventeenth century, -- called also the <xex>Family of
Love</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>philadelphus</ent><br/
<hw>philadelphus</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any of various chiefly
deciduous ornamental shrubs of the genus <gen>Philadelphus</gen> having
white sweet-scented flowers, single or in clusters; it is widely grown
in temperate regions.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philalethist</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`a*le"thist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'alh`qeia</grk> truth.]</ety> <def>A
lover of the truth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Brathwait.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philander</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fi`landros</grk> fond of men; <grk>fi`los</grk> loving +
<grk>'anh`r</grk> man.]</ety> <def>To make love to women; to play the
male flirt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You can't go <qex>philandering</qex> after her again.</q>
<rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philander</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lover.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Congreve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philander</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A South American opossum (<spn>Didelphys philander</spn>).</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>An Australian bandicoot (<spn>Perameles
lagotis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanderer</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who hangs
about women; a male flirt.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>C.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthrope</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"an*thrope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A philanthropist.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>R.
North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthropical</ent><br/
<ent>Philanthropic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phil`an*throp"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phil`an*throp"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>philanthropique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
philanthropy; characterized by philanthropy; loving or helping mankind;
<as>as, a <ex>philanthropic</ex> enterprise</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Phil`an*throp"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthropinism</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`an*throp"i*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A system
of education on so-called natural principles, attempted in Germany in
the last century by Basedow, of Dessau.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthropinist</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`an*throp"i*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
advocate of, or believer in, philanthropinism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthropist</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan"thro*pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fila`nqrwpos</grk>; <grk>fi`los</grk> loving +
<grk>'a`nqrwpos</grk> man: cf. F. <ets>philanthrope</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who practices philanthropy; one who loves mankind,
and seeks to promote the good of others; especially, a wealthy
individual who donates large amounts of money to charitable or
philanthropic causes. Opposite of <ant>misanthrope</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthropistic</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan`thro*pis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertaining to, or characteristic of, a philanthropist.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philanthropy</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lan"thro*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>philanthropia</ets>, Gr. <grk>filanqrwpi`a</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>philanthropie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Love to mankind; benevolence
toward the whole human family; universal good will; desire and readiness
to do good to all men; -- opposed to <ant>misanthropy</ant>.</def>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An active effort to promote human welfare;
humanitarian activity. in this sense, it is an action, not merely a
state of mind.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An organization whose purpose is to engage in
philanthropy(2), and is supported by funds from one or a small number of
wealthy individuals; a type of charity, the source of whose funds is
typically from a wealthy individual or a corporation, or a trust fund
established by a wealthy individual. It is distinguished from other
charitable organizations in that the source of funds of other charities
may come from a large number of sources, or from public
solicitation.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philatelic</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`a*tel"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to philately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philatelist</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lat"e*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
philately; one who collects postage stamps, as a hobby or for
investment; a stamp collector.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philately</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lat"e*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'ate`leia</grk> exemption from tax;
cf. <ets>frank</ets> to send free.]</ety> <def>The collection of postage
stamps of various issues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philathea</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*la"the*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Coined from Gr.
<grk>fi`los</grk> loving + <grk>'alh`qeia</grk> truth.]</ety> <def>An
international, interdenominational organization of Bible classes of
young women.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philatory</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>filatiere</ets>, <ets>philatiere</ets>. See
<er>Phylactery</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A kind of
transparent reliquary with an ornamental top.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philauty</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"au*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>filayti`a</grk>; <grk>fi`los</grk> loving + <grk>a'yto`s</grk>
self.]</ety> <def>Self-love; selfishness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Beaumont.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philharmonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`har*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>"armoni`a</grk> harmony: cf. F.
<ets>philharmonique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Loving harmony or
music.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philharmonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`har*mon"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who loves harmony or
music;</def> <specif>also</specif> <mark>(Colloq.)</mark>, <def>short
for <altname>Philharmonic Society</altname>, <altname>Philharmonic
concert</altname>, <altname>Philharmonic assemblage</altname>, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philhellene</ent><br/
<hw>Phil*hel"lene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A friend of
Greece, or of the Greeks; a philhellenist.</def>
<rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philhellenic</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`hel*len"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to philhellenism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philhellenism</ent><br/
<hw>Phil*hel"len*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Love of
Greece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philhellenist</ent><br/
<hw>Phil*hel"len*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + Gr.
<grk>"E`llhn</grk> a Greek: cf. F. <ets>philhell<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A friend of Greece; one who supports the cause of the Greeks;
particularly, one who supported them in their struggle for independence
against the Turks; a philhellene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philibeg</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"i*beg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Filibeg</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philip</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"ip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from their
notes.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The European hedge
sparrow.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The house sparrow. Called also
<altname>phip</altname>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philippian</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lip"pi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to <city>Philippi</city>, a city of ancient Macedonia.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or an inhabitant of
Philippi.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philippic</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lip"pic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Philippiko`s</ets> belonging to Philip, Philippic, Gr.
<grk>filippikos</grk>, fr. <grk>Fi`lippos</grk> Philip,
<grk>fi`lippos</grk> fond of horses: cf. F.
<ets>philippique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any one of the series of
famous orations of Demosthenes, the Grecian orator, denouncing Philip,
king of Macedon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Any discourse or declamation abounding in
acrimonious invective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philippine</ent><br/
<hw>Philippine</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The official language of the
Philippines, based on Tagalog; it draws its lexicon from other
Philippine languages.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Filipino.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philippines</ent><br/
<hw>Philippines</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>An East Asian country
occupying the Phillipine Islands.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Republic of the Philippines.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An archipelago off the eastern coast of
Asia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Philippine Islands.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philippium</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lip"pi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So named
from <etsep>Philippe</etsep> Plantamour, of Geneva, Switzerland.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A rare and doubtful metallic element said to
have been discovered in the mineral samarskite.</def><-- no such element
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philippize</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"ip*pize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Philippized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Philippizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to be on
Philip's side.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To support or advocate the cause
of Philip of Macedon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[See <er>Philippic</er>.]</ety> <def>To write or
speak in the style of a philippic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philister</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lis"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.]</ety> <def>A
Philistine; -- a cant name given to townsmen by students in German
universities.</def><-- in sense 3 --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philistine</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lis"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Philistinus</ets>, Heb. <ets>Phlishth<imac/</ets>, pl.
<ets>Phlishth<imac/m</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or an
inhabitant of ancient Philistia, a coast region of southern
Palestine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bailiff.</def> <mark>[Cant, Eng.]</mark>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A person deficient in liberal culture and
refinement; one without appreciation of the nobler aspirations and
sentiments of humanity; one whose scope is limited to selfish and
material interests.</def> <mark>[Recent]</mark> <rj><au>M.
Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philistine</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lis"tine</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining
to the Philistines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Uncultured; commonplace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philistinism</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lis"tin*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The condition,
character, aims, and habits of the class called Philistines. See
<er>Philistine</er>, 3.</def> <mark>[Recent]</mark>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the side of beauty and taste, vulgarity; on the side of morals
and feeling, coarseness; on the side of mind and spirit, unintelligence,
-- this is <qex>Philistinism</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>M.
Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phillipsite</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"lips*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named after
John <ets>Phillips</ets>, an English mineralogist.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous silicate of aluminia, lime, and soda, a
zeolitic mineral commonly occurring in complex twin crystals, often
cruciform in shape; -- called also
<altname>christianite</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phillygenin</ent><br/
<hw>Phil*lyg"e*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philly</ets>rin + <ets>-gen</ets> + <ets>-in</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A pearly crystalline substance obtained by the
decomposition of phillyrin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phillyrea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phil*lyr"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of evergreen plants
growing along the shores of the Mediterranean, and bearing a fruit
resembling that of the olive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phillyrin</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"ly*rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A glucoside extracted from Phillyrea as a bitter white crystalline
substance. It is sometimes used as a febrifuge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philo-</ent><br/
<hw>Philo-</hw>. <def>A combining form from Gr. <grk>fi`los</grk>
<sig>loving</sig>, <sig>fond of</sig>, <sig>attached to</sig>; <as>as,
<ex>philo</ex>sophy, <ex>philo</ex>technic</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philogynist</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*log"y*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Philogyny</er>.]</ety> <def>A lover or friend of women; one who
esteems woman as the higher type of humanity; -- opposed to
<ant>misogynist</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philogyny</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*log"y*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>filogyni`a</grk>; <grk>fi`los</grk> loving + <grk>gynh`</grk>
woman.]</ety> <def>Fondness for women; uxoriousness; -- opposed to
<ant>misogyny</ant>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philohellenian</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*hel*le"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
philhellenist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philologer</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lol"o*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>philologus</ets> a man of letters, Gr. <grk>filo`logos</grk>,
originally, fond of talking; hence, fond of learning and literature;
<grk>fi`los</grk> loving + <grk>lo`gos</grk> speech, discourse.]</ety>
<def>A philologist.</def> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philologian</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*lo"gi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
philologist.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philologic</ent><br/
<ent>Philological</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phil`o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phil`o*log"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>philologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
philology.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Phil`o*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
philology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philologize</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lol"o*gize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To study, or
make critical comments on, language.</def> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philologue</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"o*logue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>philologue</ets>.]</ety> <def>A philologist.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philology</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>philologia</ets> love of learning,
interpretation, philology, Gr. <grk>filologi`a</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>philologie</ets>. See <er>Philologer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Criticism; grammatical learning.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The study of language, especially in a philosophical
manner and as a science; the investigation of the laws of human speech,
the relation of different tongues to one another, and historical
development of languages; linguistic science.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Philology</xex> comprehends a knowledge of the
etymology, or origin and combination of words; grammar, the construction
of sentences, or use of words in language; criticism, the interpretation
of authors, the affinities of different languages, and whatever relates
to the history or present state of languages. It sometimes includes
rhetoric, poetry, history, and antiquities.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A treatise on the science of language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1077 --></p> ` <p><ent>Philomath</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"o*math</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"<osl/*m<acr/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>filomaqh`s</grk>; <grk>fi`los</grk> loving, a friend +
<grk>ma`qh</grk> learning, fr. <grk>maqei^n</grk>,
<grk>manqa`nein</grk>, to learn.]</ety> <def>A lover of learning; a
scholar.</def> <rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomathematic</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*math`e*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
philomath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomathic</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*math"ic</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l`<osl/*m<acr/th"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>philomathique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to philomathy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having love of learning or letters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomathy</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*lom"a*thy</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/m"<adot/*th<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>filomaqi`a</grk>,
<grk>filoma`qeia</grk>.]</ety> <def>The love of learning or
letters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomel</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"o*mel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Philomela</er>, the nightingale.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton. Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomela</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*me"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>philomela</ets>, Gr. <grk>filomh`la</grk>, according to the legend,
from <grk>Filomh`la</grk> <persfn>Philomela</persfn> (daughter of
<persfn>Pandion</persfn>, king of Athens), who was changed into a
nightingale.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The nightingale; philomel.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of birds including the
nightingales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomene</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"o*mene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
nightingale.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomot</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"o*mot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Filemot</er>.]</ety> <def>Of the color of a dead leaf.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philomusical</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*mu"sic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + <ets>musical</ets>.]</ety> <def>Loving music.
<mark>[R.]</mark>Busby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philopena</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*pe"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably a
corruption fr. G. <ets>vielliebchen</ets>, LG. <ets>vielliebken</ets>,
or D. <ets>veelliebken</ets>, a philopena, literally, much loved; but
influenced by Gr. <grk>fi`los</grk> a friend, and L. <ets>poena</ets>
penalty, from an idea that the gift was a penalty of friendship or
love.]</ety> <def>A present or gift which is made as a forfeit in a
social game that is played in various ways; also, the game itself.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>fillipeen</asp> and
<asp>phillippine</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ One of the ways may be stated as follows: A person
finding a nut with two kernels eats one, and gives the other to a person
of the opposite sex, and then whichever says <xex>philopena</xex> first
at the next meeting wins the present. The name is also applied to the
kernels eaten.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philopolemical</ent><br/
<ent>Philopolemic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phil`o*po*lem"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phil`o*po*lem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>filopo`lemos</grk> fond of war, warlike;
<grk>fi`los</grk> loving + <grk>po`lemos</grk> war.]</ety> <def>Fond of
polemics or controversy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philoprogenitive</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*pro*gen"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
the love of offspring; fond of children.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philoprogenitiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*pro*gen"i*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + L. <ets>progenies</ets> offspring.]</ety>
<fld>(Phren.)</fld> <def>The love of offspring; fondness for
children.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophaster</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*phas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a bad
philosopher, fr. <ets>philosophus</ets>: cf. OF.
<ets>philosophastre</ets>.]</ety> <def>A pretender to philosophy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophate</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>philosophatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>philosophari</ets> to
philosophize.]</ety> <def>To play the philosopher; to moralize.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophation</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los`o*pha"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Philosophical speculation and discussion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Petty.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophe</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"o*sophe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., a
philosopher.]</ety> <def>A philosophaster; a philosopher.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosopheme</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*pheme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>filoso`fhma</grk>, from <grk>filosofei^n</grk> to love
knowledge.]</ety> <def>A philosophical proposition, doctrine, or
principle of reasoning.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This, the most venerable, and perhaps the most ancient, of Grecian
myths, is a <qex>philosopheme</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosopher</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>philosophre</ets>, F. <ets>philosophe</ets>, L.
<ets>philosophus</ets>, Gr. <grk>filo`sofos</grk>; <grk>fi`los</grk>
loving + <grk>sofo`s</grk> wise. Cf. <er>Philosophy</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who philosophizes; one versed in, or devoted to,
philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then certain <qex>philosophers</qex> of the Epicureans, and of the
Stoics, encountered him.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xvii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who reduces the principles of philosophy to
practice in the conduct of life; one who lives according to the rules of
practical wisdom; one who meets or regards all vicissitudes with
calmness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An alchemist.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Philosopher's stone</b></col>, <cd>an imaginary stone
which the alchemists formerly sought as the instrument of converting the
baser metals into gold.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophical</ent><br/
<ent>Philosophic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phil`o*soph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phil`o*soph"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>philosophicus</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>philosophique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
philosophy; versed in, or imbued with, the principles of philosophy;
hence, characterizing a philosopher; rational; wise; temperate; calm;
cool.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Phil`o*soph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophism</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*phism</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>philosophisme</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Spurious philosophy; the love or practice of sophistry.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophist</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*phist</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>philosophiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
pretender in philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophistical</ent><br/
<ent>Philosophistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phi*los`o*phis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phi*los`o*phis"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to the love or practice of sophistry.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>philosophize</ent><br/
<hw>phi*los"o*phize</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<imac/z)</pr>,
<pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Philosophized</conjf> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<imac/zd)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>hilosophizing</conjf>
<pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<imac/`z<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
reason like a philosopher; to search into the reason and nature of
things; to investigate phenomena, and assign rational causes for their
existence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man <qex>philosophizes</qex> as he lives. He may
<qex>philosophize</qex> well or ill, but <qex>philosophize</qex> he
must.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophizer</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*phi`zer</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<imac/`z<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who philosophizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philosophy</ent><br/
<hw>Phi*los"o*phy</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Philosophies</plw>
<pr>(f<icr/*l<ocr/s"<osl/*f<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>philosophie</ets>, F. <ets>philosophie</ets>, L.
<ets>philosophia</ets>, from Gr. <grk>filosofi`a</grk>. See
<er>Philosopher</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Literally, the love of,
inducing the search after, wisdom; in actual usage, the knowledge of
phenomena as explained by, and resolved into, causes and reasons, powers
and laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When applied to any particular department of knowledge,
<xex>philosophy</xex> denotes the general laws or principles under which
all the subordinate phenomena or facts relating to that subject are
comprehended. Thus <ex>philosophy</ex>, when applied to God and the
divine government, is called <xex>theology</xex>; when applied to
material objects, it is called <xex>physics</xex>; when it treats of
man, it is called <xex>anthropology</xex> and <xex>psychology</xex>,
with which are connected <xex>logic</xex> and <xex>ethics</xex>; when it
treats of the necessary conceptions and relations by which
<ex>philosophy</ex> is possible, it is called
<xex>metaphysics</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ldquo/<xex>Philosophy</xex> has been defined: -- the
science of things divine and human, and the causes in which they are
contained; -- the science of effects by their causes; -- the science of
sufficient reasons; -- the science of things possible, inasmuch as they
are possible; -- the science of things evidently deduced from first
principles; -- the science of truths sensible and abstract; -- the
application of reason to its legitimate objects; -- the science of the
relations of all knowledge to the necessary ends of human reason; -- the
science of the original form of the ego, or mental self; -- the science
of science; -- the science of the absolute; -- the science of the
absolute indifference of the ideal and real.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Sir
W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A particular philosophical system or theory; the
hypothesis by which particular phenomena are explained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Books] of Aristotle and his <qex>philosophie</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We shall in vain interpret their words by the notions of our
<qex>philosophy</qex> and the doctrines in our school.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Practical wisdom; calmness of temper and judgment;
equanimity; fortitude; stoicism; <as>as, to meet misfortune with
<ex>philosophy</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then had he spent all his <qex>philosophy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Reasoning; argumentation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of good and evil much they argued then, . . . <br/
Vain wisdom all, and false <qex>philosophy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The course of sciences read in the schools.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A treatise on philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Philosophy of the Academy</b></col>, <cd>that of Plato,
who taught his disciples in a grove in Athens called the Academy.</cd>
-- <col><b>Philosophy of the Garden</b></col>, <cd>that of Epicurus, who
taught in a garden in Athens.</cd> -- <col><b>Philosophy of the
Lyceum</b></col>, <cd>that of Aristotle, the founder of the Peripatetic
school, who delivered his lectures in the Lyceum at Athens.</cd> --
<col><b>Philosophy of the Porch</b></col>, <cd>that of Zeno and the
Stoics; -- so called because Zeno of Citium and his successors taught in
the porch of the Poicile, a great hall in Athens.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philostorgy</ent><br/
<hw>Phil`o*stor"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>filostorgi`a</grk>; <grk>fi`los</grk> loving + <grk>storgh`</grk>
affection.]</ety> <def>Natural affection, as of parents for their
children.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philotechnical</ent><br/
<ent>Philotechnic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phil`o*tech"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phil`o*tech"nic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Philo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>te`chnh</grk> an art: cf. F.
<ets>philotechnique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Fond of the arts.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philter</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>philtre</ets>, L. <ets>philtrum</ets>, Gr. <grk>fi`ltron</grk>, fr.
<grk>filei^n</grk> to love, <grk>fi`los</grk> dear, loving.]</ety>
<def>A potion or charm intended to excite the passion of love.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>philtre</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Philter</ent><br/
<hw>Phil"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Philtered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Philtering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To impregnate or
mix with a love potion; <as>as, to <ex>philter</ex> a
draught</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To charm to love; to excite to love or sexual desire
by a potion.</def> <rj><au>Gov. of Tongue.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phimosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phi*mo"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a muzzling, fr. <?/ muzzle.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
condition of the penis in which the prepuce can not be drawn back so as
to uncover the glans penis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phitoness</ent><br/
<hw>Phi"ton*ess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pythoness;
witch.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phiz</ent><br/
<hw>Phiz</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phizes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Contr. fr.
<ets>physiognomy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The face or visage.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phle*bi"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fle`ps</grk>, <grk>flebo`s</grk>, a vein + <ets>-itis</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammation of a vein.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebogram</ent><br/
<hw>Phleb"o*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fle`ps</grk>, <grk>flebo`s</grk> + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A tracing (with the sphygmograph) of the
movements of a vein, or of the venous pulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebolith</ent><br/
<ent>Phlebolite</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phleb"o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phleb"o*lith</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, a vein +
<ets>-lite</ets>, <ets>-lith</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
small calcareous concretion formed in a vein; a vein stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebology</ent><br/
<hw>Phle*bol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fle`ps</grk>, <grk>flebo`s</grk>, a vein + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A branch of anatomy which treats of the veins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebotomist</ent><br/
<hw>Phle*bot"o*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phl<eacute/botomiste</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>One who
practiced phlebotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebotomize</ent><br/
<hw>Phle*bot"o*mize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Phlebotomized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Phlebotomizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phl<eacute/botomiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To let blood from by
opening a vein; to bleed.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phlebotomus</ent><br/
<hw>phlebotomus</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mild viral disease
transmitted by the bite of the sand fly <spn>Phlebotomus
papatasii</spn>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> sandfly fever, pappataci fever.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>[Capitalized]</mark> <def>A genus of small
blood-sucking mothlike sand flies.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phlebotomus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlebotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Phle*bot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phlebotomia</ets>, Gr. <grk>flebotomi`a</grk>; <grk>fle`ps</grk>,
<grk>flebo`s</grk>, a vein + <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut: cf. F.
<ets>phl<eacute/botomie</ets>. Cf. <er>Fleam</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The act or practice of opening a vein for letting
blood, in the treatment of disease; venesection; bloodletting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegethon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phleg"e*thon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/, prop. p. pr. of <?/ to blaze.]</ety> <fld>(Class Myth.)</fld>
<def>One of the principal rivers of Hades, in the channel of which fire
flowed instead of water.</def></p>

<p><q>Fierce <qex>Phlegethon</qex>,<br/
Whose waves of torrent fire inflame with rage.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegm</ent><br/
<hw>Phlegm</hw> <pr>(fl<ecr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>phlegme</ets>, <ets>flegme</ets>, L. <ets>phlegma</ets>, fr. Gr.
<grk>fle`gma</grk> a flame, inflammation, phlegm, a morbid, clammy humor
in the body, fr. <grk>fle`gein</grk> to burn. Cf. <er>Phlox</er>,
<er>Flagrant</er>, <er>Flame</er>, <er>Bleak</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
<er>Fluminate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the four humors of
which the ancients supposed the blood to be composed. See
<er>Humor</er>.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Viscid mucus secreted in
abnormal quantity in the respiratory and digestive passages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>A watery distilled liquor, in
distinction from a spirituous liquor.</def> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sluggishness of temperament; dullness; want of
interest; indifference; coldness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They judge with fury, but they write with <qex>phlegm</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmagogue</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg"ma*gogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>flegmagwgo`s</grk> carrying of phlegm; <grk>fle`gma</grk> phlegm +
<grk>'a`gein</grk> to lead.]</ety> <fld>(Old Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
supposed to expel phlegm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmasia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phleg*ma"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/. See <er>Phlegm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An
inflammation; more particularly, an inflammation of the internal
organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Phlegmasia dolens</b></col>
<pr>(d<omac/"l<ecr/nz)</pr> <ety>[NL.]</ety>, <cd>milk
leg.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phlegmaticus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>phlegmatique</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Watery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Aqueous and
<xex>phlegmatic</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Abounding in phlegm; <as>as, <ex>phlegmatic</ex>
humors; a <ex>phlegmatic</ex> constitution.</as></def>
<rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Generating or causing phlegm.</def> <ldquo/Cold and
<xex>phlegmatic</xex> habitations.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not easily excited to action or passion; cold; dull;
sluggish; heavy; <as>as, a <ex>phlegmatic</ex> person</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phlegmatic temperament</b></col> <fld>(Old
Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>lymphatic temperament. See under
<er>Lymphatic</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmatical</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Phlegmatic.</def> <rj><au>Ash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmatically</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg*mat"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a phlegmatic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmaticly</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg*mat"ic*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Phlegmatically.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmon</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg"mon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phlegmone</ets>, <ets>phlegmon</ets>, inflammation beneath the
skin, Gr. <grk>flegmonh`</grk>, fr. <grk>fle`gein</grk> to burn: cf. F.
<ets>phlegmon</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Purulent inflammation
of the cellular or areolar tissue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlegmonous</ent><br/
<hw>Phleg"mon*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phlegmoneux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the nature or properties of
phlegmon; <as>as, <ex>phlegmonous</ex> pneumonia</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phleme</ent><br/
<hw>Phleme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Surg. & Far.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Fleam</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phleum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phle"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ a kind of marsh plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
grasses, including the timothy (<spn>Phleum pratense</spn>), which is
highly valued for hay; cat's-tail grass.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phloem</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo"<eum/m</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
bark.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>That portion of fibrovascular
bundles which corresponds to the inner bark; the liber tissue; --
distinguished from <contr>xylem</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogistian</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gis"tian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A believer in
the existence of phlogiston.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogistic</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to phlogiston, or to belief in its
existence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammatory; belonging to
inflammations and fevers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogistical</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gis"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <def>Phlogistic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogisticate</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gis"ti*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Phlogisticated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Phlogisticating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>To
combine phlogiston with; -- usually in the form and sense of the <pos>p.
p.</pos> or the <pos>adj.</pos>; <as>as, highly <ex>phlogisticated</ex>
substances</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogistication</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gis`ti*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <def>The act or process of combining with
phlogiston.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogiston</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gis"ton</hw> <pr>(fl<osl/*j<icr/s"t<ocr/n; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>flogisto`s</grk> burnt, set on
fire, fr. <grk>flogi`zein</grk> to set on fire, to burn, fr.
<grk>flo`x</grk>, <grk>flogo`s</grk>, a flame, blaze. See
<er>Phlox</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>The former
hypothetical principle of fire, or inflammability, regarded by Stahl as
a chemical element; it is now known to be nonexistent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This was supposed to be united with combustible
(<xex>phlogisticated</xex>) bodies and to be separated from
incombustible (<xex>dephlogisticated</xex>) bodies, the phenomena of
flame and burning being the escape of phlogiston. Soot and sulphur were
regarded as nearly pure phlogiston. The essential principle of this
theory was, that combustion was a decomposition rather than the union
and combination which it has since been shown to be. This theory is now
discredited and superseded by the theory of chemical reaction between
oxidizable substances and oxidants as an explanation of
combustion</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*gog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>flo`x</grk>, <grk>flogo`s</grk> fire + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Causing inflammation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogopite</ent><br/
<hw>Phlog"o*pite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>flogwpo`s</grk> firelike.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of
mica having generally a peculiar bronze-red or copperlike color and a
pearly luster. It is a silicate of aluminia, with magnesia, potash, and
some fluorine. It is characteristic of crystalline limestone or dolomite
and serpentine. See <er>Mica</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phlo*go"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>flo`gwsis</grk> burning heat.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of external parts of the body; erysipelatous
inflammation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlogotic</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*got"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to phlogosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phloramine</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*ram"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phlor</ets>lucin + <ets>amine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A basic amido derivative of phloroglucin, having
an astringent taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phloretic</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo*ret"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or derived from, or designating, an organic acid
obtained by the decomposition of phloretin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phloretin</ent><br/
<hw>Phlor"e*tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
Phlorizin.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A bitter white crystalline
substance obtained by the decomposition of phlorizin, and formerly used
to some extent as a substitute for quinine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlorizin</ent><br/
<hw>Phlor"i*zin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
bark + <grk>"ri`za</grk> root.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A bitter
white crystalline glucoside extracted from the root bark of the apple,
pear, cherry, plum, etc.</def> <altsp>[Formerly also written
<asp>phloridzin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phloroglucin</ent><br/
<hw>Phlor`o*glu"cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phlor</ets>etin + Gr. <grk>glyky`s</grk> sweet.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A sweet white crystalline substance, metameric
with pyrogallol, and obtained by the decomposition of phloretin, and
from certain gums, as catechu, kino, etc. It belongs to the class of
phenols. [Called also <altname>phloroglucinol</altname>.]</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlorol</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo"rol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phlor</ets>etic
+ <ets>-ol</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A liquid metameric with
xylenol, belonging to the class of phenols, and obtained by distilling
certain salts of phloretic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlorone</ent><br/
<hw>Phlo"rone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phlor</ets>ol
+ quin<ets>one</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A yellow
crystalline substance having a peculiar unpleasant odor, resembling the
quinones, and obtained from beechwood tar and coal tar, as also by the
oxidation of xylidine; -- called also
<altname>xyloquinone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phlox</ent><br/
<hw>phlox</hw> <pr>(fl<ocr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a kind of
flower, fr. Gr. <grk>flo`x</grk> flame, fr. <grk>fle`gein</grk> to
burn.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of American herbs, having
showy red, white, or purple flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phlox worm</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of
an American moth (<spn>Heliothis phloxiphaga</spn>). It is destructive
to phloxes.</cd> -- <col><b>Phlox subulata</b></col>, <cd>the moss pink.
See under <er>Moss</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phlyctenular</ent><br/
<hw>Phlyc*ten"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
blister or pustule.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Characterized by the
presence of small pustules, or whitish elevations resembling pustules;
<as>as, <ex>phlyctenular</ex> ophthalmia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phobia</ent><br/
<hw>pho"bi*a</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"b<esl/*<adot/)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Any irrational and persistent fear, whether of a situation,
activity, or object; a phobia typically leads to a strong desire to
avoid the object of the phobia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> phobic disorder, phobic neurosis.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-phobia</ent><br/
<hw>-pho"bi*a</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"b<esl/*<adot/)</pr> <pos>suffix</pos>
<def>A combining form used as a suffix to designate specific phobias,
such as agora<ex>phobia</ex> (fear of public places),
acro<ex>phobia</ex> (fear of heights). The first part of the combining
form is usually derived from Greek.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phobic</ent><br/
<hw>pho"bic</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"b<icr/k)</pr> <pos>adj.</pos>
<def>Suffering from irrational fears.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phobic</ent><br/
<hw>pho"bic</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"b<icr/k)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person
suffering from a phobia.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phobophobia</ent><br/
<hw>phobophobia</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A morbid fear of developing a
phobia.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pho"ca</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"k<adot/)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[L., a seal, fr. Gr. <grk>fw`kh</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A genus of seals. It includes the common harbor seal and allied
species. See <er>Seal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phocacean</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*ca"cean</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of Phoca; a seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phocal</ent><br/
<hw>pho"cal</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to seals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phocenic</ent><br/
<hw>pho*cen"ic</hw> <pr>(f<osl/*s<ecr/n"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>fw`kaina</grk> a porpoise.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to dolphin oil or porpoise oil; -- said of an acid
(called also <xex>delphinic</xex> acid) subsequently found to be
identical with valeric acid.</def> <rj><au>Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phocenin</ent><br/
<hw>pho*ce"nin</hw> <pr>(f<osl/*s<emac/"n<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>phoc<eacute/nine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Delphin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1078 --></p>

<p><ent>Phocine</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"cine</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"s<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phoca</ets> a seal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to the seal tribe; phocal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phocodont</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"co*dont</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"k<osl/*d<ocr/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Phocodontia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phocodontia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pho`co*don"ti*a</hw>
<pr>(f<omac/`k<osl/*d<ocr/n"sh<icr/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fw`kh</grk> a seal + <grk>'odoy`s</grk>,
<grk>'odo`ntos</grk>, a tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of
extinct carnivorous whales. Their teeth had compressed and serrated
crowns. It includes <gen>Squalodon</gen> and allied genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoebe</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<oe/"be</hw> <pr>(f<emac/"b<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The pewee, or pewit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoebus</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<oe/"bus</hw> <pr>(f<emac/"b<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. Gr. <grk>Foi^bos</grk>, fr. <grk>foi^bos</grk> pure, bright.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>Apollo; the sun
god.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sun.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Ph<oe/bus</xex> 'gins
arise.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoenician</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<oe/*ni"cian</hw> <pr>(f<esl/*n<icr/sh"<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>prop.
a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to Ph<oe/nica.</def> -- <def2><pos>prop.
n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of Ph<oe/nica.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoenicious</ent><br/
<hw>Ph<oe/*ni"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Phenicious</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoenicopterus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ph<oe/`ni*cop"te*rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Phenicopter</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
birds which includes the flamingoes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoenicurus</ent><br/
<hw>Phoenicurus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of Old World
thrushes.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phoenicurus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoenix</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ph<oe/"nix</hw> <pr>(f<emac/"n<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., a fabulous bird. See <er>Phenix</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Same as <er>Phenix</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <mark>[Capitalized]</mark> <def>A genus
of palms including the date tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pholad</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"lad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
species of <gen>Pholas</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pholadean</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*la"de*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pholad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pholas</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pho"las</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pholades</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fwla`s</grk>, <grk>fwla`dos</grk>, a kind of mollusk.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of marine bivalve
mollusks of the genus <gen>Pholas</gen>, or family
<fam>Pholadid<ae/</fam>. They bore holes for themselves in clay, peat,
and soft rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pholistoma</ent><br/
<hw>Pholistoma</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus of straggling herbs
of the southwestern U.S.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus Pholistoma.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonal</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"nal</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fwnh`</grk> the voice.]</ety> <def>Of or relating to the voice;
<as>as, <ex>phonal</ex> structure</as>.</def> <rj><au>Max
M<uum/ller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonascetics</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`nas*cet"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
practice the voice; <grk>fwnh`</grk> voice + <?/ to practice.]</ety>
<def>Treatment for restoring or improving the voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonation</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fwnh`</grk> the voice.]</ety> <def>The act or process by which
articulate sounds are uttered; the utterance of articulate sounds;
articulate speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonautograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nau"to*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + Gr. <grk>a'yto`s</grk> self +
<ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument by
means of which a sound can be made to produce a visible trace or record
of itself. It consists essentially of a resonant vessel, usually of
paraboloidal form, closed at one end by a flexible membrane. A stylus
attached to some point of the membrane records the movements of the
latter, as it vibrates, upon a moving cylinder or plate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phone</ent><br/
<hw>Phone</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Colloq. for
<er>Telephone</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument that converts
sound into signals that can be transmitted over distances and then
converts received signals back into sounds; a telephone; <as>as, I
talked to him on the phone</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> telephone, telephone set.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>An individual sound unit of
speech without concern as to whether or not it is a phoneme of some
language. A speech sound.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> speech sound, sound.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A device for converting electric signals into
sounds; it is held over or inserted into the ear.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> earearpiece, headphone, phone.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phone</ent><br/
<hw>phone</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To get or try to get into
communication with, using a telephone; <as>as, I <ex>phoned</ex> him
this morning</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> call, telephone, call up, ring.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>E. T. <ex>phone</ex> home.</q> <rj><qau>E. T. (the
movie)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoneidoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nei"do*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form +
<ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument for
studying the motions of sounding bodies by optical means. It consists of
a tube across the end of which is stretched a film of soap solution thin
enough to give colored bands, the form and position of which are
affected by sonorous vibrations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phone-in</ent><br/
<hw>phone-in</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A radio or telephone program, or
part of a program, during which the audience participates by
telephone.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phoneme</ent><br/
<hw>pho"neme</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<esl/m)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Linguistics)</fld> <def>One of a small set of speech sounds that
are used by and distinguished by the speakers of a particular language.
They are combined into <partof>morphemes</partof>,
<partof>words</partof>, and <partof>sentences</partof>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phonemic</ent><br/
<hw>pho*ne"mic</hw> <pr>(f<osl/*n<emac/"m<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>adj.</pos>
<fld>(Linguistics)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a phoneme; <as>as,
<ex>phonemic</ex> analysis</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phonemics</ent><br/
<hw>pho*ne"mics</hw> <pr>(f<osl/*n<emac/"m<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Linguistics)</fld> <def>The study of the sound system of a given
language and the analysis and classification of its phonemes.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> phonology.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*net"ic</hw> <pr>(f<osl/*n<ecr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>fwnhtiko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>fwnh`</grk> a sound, tone;
akin to Gr. <grk>fa`nai</grk> to speak: cf. F.
<ets>phon<eacute/tique</ets>. See <er>Ban</er> a proclamation.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the voice, or its use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Representing sounds; <as>as, <ex>phonetic</ex>
characters</as>; -- opposed to <contr>ideographic</contr>; <as>as, a
<ex>phonetic</ex> notation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phonetic spelling</b></col>, <cd>spelling in phonetic
characters, each representing one sound only; -- contrasted with
<contr>Romanic spelling</contr>, or that by the use of the Roman
alphabet.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetically</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*net"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a phonetic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetician</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`ne*ti"cian</hw> <pr>(f<omac/`n<esl/*t<icr/sh"<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in phonetics; a phonetist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetics</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*net"ics</hw> <pr>(f<osl/*n<ecr/t"<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The doctrine or science of sounds; especially those of
the human voice; phonology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The art of representing vocal sounds by signs and
written characters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetism</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"ne*tism</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<esl/*t<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The science which treats of vocal sounds.</def> <rj><au>J.
Peile.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetist</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"ne*tist</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<esl/*t<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One versed in phonetics; a phonologist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who advocates a phonetic spelling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetization</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`ne*ti*za"tion</hw> <pr>(f<omac/`n<esl/*t<icr/*z<amac/"sh<ucr/n;
277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act, art, or process of representing
sounds by phonetic signs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonetize</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"ne*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To represent by
phonetic signs.</def> <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phoney</ent><br/
<hw>pho"ney</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<emac/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Also
spelled <asp>phony</asp>.]</altsp> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>phonier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>phoniest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Imitating something
superior; intended to deceive; fraudulent; having a misleading
appearance; not genuine; false; counterfeit; fake; <as>as, a
<ex>phoney</ex> diamond; a <ex>phoney</ex> hundred-dollar
bill</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bogus, counterfeit, fake, ersatz; fraudulent;
false.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pretending to be other than one is; putting on false
appearances; insincere; hypocritical; -- of people.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phoney</ent><br/
<hw>pho"ney</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Something or someone that is phoney.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>phony</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phon"ic</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/n"<icr/k; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fwnh`</grk> sound: cf. F. <ets>phonique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to sound; of the nature of sound; acoustic.</def>
<rj><au>Tyndall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonics</ent><br/
<hw>Phon"ics</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/n"<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Same as <er>Phonetics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A method of teaching reading and spelling to
beginning students, emphasizing the sound values of individual letters
and syllables, and the relationship between pronunciation and spelling.
Contrasted to <contr>whole language method</contr> and <contr>sentence
method</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phono-</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"no-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>fwnh`</grk> <sig>sound</sig>, <sig>tone</sig>; <as>as,
<ex>phono</ex>graph, <ex>phono</ex>logy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phono</ent><br/
<hw>Phono</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
South American butterfly (<spn>Ithonia phono</spn>) having nearly
transparent wings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonocamptic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`no*camp"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + Gr. <grk>ka`mptein</grk> to bend: cf. F.
<ets>phonocamptique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Reflecting sound.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Phonocamptic</xex> objects.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Derham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonogram</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"no*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets>
+ <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A letter, character, or mark
used to represent a particular sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Phonograms</qex> are of three kinds: (1) Verbal signs, which
stand for entire words; (2) Syllabic signs, which stand for the
articulations of which words are composed; (3) Alphabetic signs, or
letters, which represent the elementary sounds into which the syllable
can be resolved.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor (The Alphabet).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A record of sounds made by a phonograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phonogramic</ent><br/
<hw>phonogramic</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
phonogram.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"no*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
character or symbol used to represent a sound, esp. one used in
phonography.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument for the
mechanical registration and reproduction of audible sounds, as
articulate speech, etc. An early simple version consisted of a rotating
cylinder or disk covered with some material easily indented, as tinfoil,
wax, paraffin, etc., above which is a thin plate carrying a stylus. As
the plate vibrates under the influence of a sound, the stylus makes
minute indentations or undulations in the soft material, and these, when
the cylinder or disk is again turned, set the plate in vibration, and
reproduce the sound. Modern versions use electronic circuitry and
various more stable recording media to record sound more
accurately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An instrument for reproducing sounds, especially
music, previously recorded on a plastic cylinder or disk as a pattern of
bumps or wiggles in a groove. A needle (stylus) held in the groove is
made to vibrate by motion (rotation) of the recording, and the
vibrations caused by the bumps and wiggles are transmitted directly to a
membrane, or first transduced into electrical impulses and sent to an
electronic amplifier circuit, thereby reproducing with greater or less
fidelity the original sounds. A <ex>phonograph</ex> which is equipped
with electronics enabling the playback of sound with high fidelity to
the original is often called a <stype>hi-fi</stype>.</def> <note>In the
1990's such devices are beginning to be replaced in many homes by
compact disk players; the production of plastic recordings of music for
playback on a phonograph has almost ceased for entertainment
purposes.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonographer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One versed or skilled in phonography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who uses, or is skilled in the use of, the
phonograph. See <er>Phonograph</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonographical</ent><br/
<ent>Phonographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`no*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pho`no*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>phonographique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to phonography; based upon phonography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to phonograph; done by the
phonograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonographically</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`no*graph"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a phonographic
manner; by means of phonograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonographist</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nog"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Phonographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
description of the laws of the human voice, or sounds uttered by the
organs of speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A representation of sounds by distinctive
characters; commonly, a system of shorthand writing invented by
<person>Isaac Pitman</person>, or a modification of his system, much
used by reporters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ The consonants are represented by straight lines and
curves; the vowels by dots and short dashes; but by skilled
phonographers, in rapid work, most vowel marks are omitted, and brief
symbols for common words and combinations of words are extensively
employed. The following line is an example of phonography, in which all
the sounds are indicated: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- illustr. of phonetic transcription of the line below --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They also serve who only stand and wait.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj></p></note>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The art of constructing, or using, the
phonograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"no*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets>
+ <ets>-lite</ets>: cf. F. <ets>phonolithe</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A compact, feldspathic, igneous rock containing
nephelite, ha<uum/ynite, etc. Thin slabs give a ringing sound when
struck; -- called also <altname>clinkstone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonologer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nol"o*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
phonologist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonological</ent><br/
<ent>Phonologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`no*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pho`no*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
phonology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
phonology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonology</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science or
doctrine of the elementary sounds uttered by the human voice in speech,
including the various distinctions, modifications, and combinations of
tones; phonetics. Also, a treatise on sounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>An instrument for measuring sounds, as to their intensity, or the
frequency of the vibrations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonomotor</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`no*mo"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + <ets>-motor</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>An instrument in which motion is produced by the vibrations of a
sounding body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonorganon</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*nor"ga*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Phono-</er>, and <er>Organon</er>.]</ety> <def>A speaking
machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"no*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An instrument for observing or exhibiting the motions
or properties of sounding bodies; especially, an apparatus invented by
K<oum/nig for testing the quality of musical strings.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>An instrument for producing luminous figures by the vibrations of
sounding bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonotype</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"no*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phono-</ets>
+ <ets>-type</ets>.]</ety> <def>A type or character used in
phonotypy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonotypical</ent><br/
<ent>Phonotypic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`no*typ"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pho`no*typ"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to phonotypy;
<as>as, a <ex>phonotypic</ex> alphabet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonotypist</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*not"y*pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
phonotypy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phonotypy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*not"y*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A method of
phonetic printing of the English language, as devised by <person>Mr.
Pitman</person>, in which nearly all the ordinary letters and many new
forms are employed in order to indicate each elementary sound by a
separate character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phony</ent><br/
<hw>pho"ny</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<emac/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Also
spelled <asp>phoney</asp>.]</altsp> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>phonier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>phoniest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>imitating something
superior; intended to deceive; fraudulent; having a misleading
appearance; not genuine; counterfeit; fake; <as>as, a <ex>phony</ex>
diamond; a <ex>phony</ex> hundred-dollar bill</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> phoney, bogus, counterfeit, fake.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pretending to be other than one is; putting on false
appearances; insincere; hypocritical; -- of people.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phony</ent><br/
<hw>pho"ny</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"n<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something
or someone that is phony.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>phoney</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-phore</ent><br/
<hw>-phore</hw> <pr>(f<ocir/r)</pr>, <pos>suff.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fe`rein</grk> to bring, bear, carry.]</ety> <def>Combining form
meaning <sig>bearer of</sig> the element preceding; <as>as,
iono<ex>phore</ex>; sema<ex>phore</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phorminx</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phor"minx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>A kind of lyre used by the Greeks.</def> <rj><au>Mrs.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phormium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phor"mi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. fr. Gr.
<?/ a plaited mat, a kind of plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of liliaceous plants, consisting of one species (<spn>Phormium
tenax</spn>). See <er>Flax-plant</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phorone</ent><br/
<hw>Phor"one</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cam<ets>phor</ets> +
acet<ets>one</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A yellow crystalline
substance, having a geraniumlike odor, regarded as a complex derivative
of acetone, and obtained from certain camphor compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoronis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pho*ro"nis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>Phoronis</ets>, a surname of Io, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A remarkable genus of marine worms having tentacles around the
mouth. It is usually classed with the gephyreans. Its larva
(<it>Actinotrocha</it>) undergoes a peculiar metamorphosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoronomia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phor`o*no"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Phoronomics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phoronomics</ent><br/
<hw>Phor`o*nom"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
carrying, motion + <?/ a law.]</ety> <def>The science of motion;
kinematics.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Weisbach.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosgene</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"gene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fw^s</grk>
light + the root of <grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born: cf. F.
<ets>phosg<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>
<def>Producing, or produced by, the action of light; -- used
specifically to designate a gas also called carbonyl chloride. See
<er>phosgene</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and <er>Carbonyl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phosgene</ent><br/
<hw>phos"gene</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"j<emac/n <it>or</it>
f<ocr/z"j<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A reactive
chemical substance (<chform>COCl2</chform>), also called
<altname>carbonyl choride</altname>, used in synthesis of numerous
substances. In the First World War it was also used as a poisonous gas
in combat.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosgenite</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"gen*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A rare mineral occurring in tetragonal crystals of a white, yellow,
or grayish color and adamantine luster. It is a chlorocarbonate of
lead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phospham</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"pham</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosph</ets>orus + <ets>am</ets>monia.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An inert amorphous white powder,
<chform>PN2H</chform>, obtained by passing ammonia over heated
phosphorus.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also <asp>phosphame</asp>.]</altsp> --
<wordforms><wf>Phos"pham"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphate</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of phosphoric acid or a compound containing the phosphate
radical group <chform>-PO4</chform>; <as>as, trisodium phosphate
(<chform>Na3PO4</chform>); triethyl phosphate
(<chform>(C2H5)3PO4</chform>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphatic</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*phat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or containing, phosphorus, phosphoric acid, or
phosphates; <as>as, <ex>phosphatic</ex> nodules</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phosphatic diathesis</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
habit of body which leads to the undue excretion of phosphates with the
urine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphaturia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phos`pha*tu"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Phosphate</er>, and <er>Urine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The excessive discharge of phosphates in the urine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphene</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phene</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fw^s</grk> light + <grk>fai`nein</grk> to show.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A luminous impression produced through
excitation of the retina by some cause other than the impingement upon
it of rays of light, as by pressure upon the eyeball when the lids are
closed. Cf. <er>After-image</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphide</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phide</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<imac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A binary compound of phosphorus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phosphine</ent><br/
<hw>phos"phine</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<emac/n or f<ocr/s"f<icr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A colorless gas,
<chform>PH3</chform>, analogous to ammonia, and having a disagreeable
odor resembling that of garlic. Called also <altname>hydrogen
phosphide</altname>, and formerly, <altname>phosphureted
hydrogen</altname>. It is postulated as one possible cause of the rare
lights seen in marshes -- see <er>ignis fatuus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is the most important compound of phosphorus and
hydrogen, and is produced by the action of caustic potash on phosphorus.
It is spontaneously inflammable, owing to impurities, and in burning
produces peculiar vortical rings of smoke.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphine</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Dyeing)</fld>
<def>Chrysaniline, often in the form of a salt.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphinic</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*phin"ic</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s*f<icr/n"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, certain acids
analogous to the phosphonic acids, but containing two hydrocarbon
radicals, and derived from the secondary phosphines by
oxidation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphite</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of phosphorous acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s*f<ocr/n"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosph</ets>oric + sulph<ets>onic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, certain
derivatives of phosphorous acid containing a hydrocarbon radical with
the phosphorus directly bonded to a carbon, and analogous to the
sulphonic acids; <as>as, methane<ex>phosphonic</ex> acid,
<chform>CH3.PO.(OH)2</chform></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphonium</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*pho"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosph</ets>orus + amm<ets>onium</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The hypothetical radical <chform>PH4</chform>,
analogous to ammonium, and regarded as the nucleus of certain
derivatives of phosphine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphor</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<ocir/r or f<ocr/s"f<ocr/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>phosphor</ets>. See
<er>Phosphorus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Phosphorus.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The planet Venus, when appearing as the morning
star; Lucifer.</def> <mark>[Poetic and Rare]</mark> <rj><au>Pope.</au>
<au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A phosphorescent substance.</def>
<mark>[archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A substance capable of exhibiting luminescence. This
term is now most commonly used to refer to substances used in
fluorescent lights, image intensifiers, or cathode-ray tubes (CRT's),
which luminesce when excited by ultraviolet radiation or an electron
beam. In color CRT's, three different phosphors, each giving different
colors when excited, are used.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorate</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Phosphorated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Phosphorating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To impregnate, or combine, with phosphorus or its compounds;
<as>as, <ex>phosphorated</ex> oil</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphor-bronze</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor-bronze`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosphor</ets> + <ets>bronze</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A variety of bronze possessing great hardness,
elasticity, and toughness, obtained by melting copper with tin
phosphide. It contains one or two per cent of phosphorus and from five
to fifteen per cent of tin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphoreous</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*pho"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Phosphorescent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphoresce</ent><br/
<hw>Phos`phor*esce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Phosphoresced</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Phosphorescing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shine as phosphorus; to be
phosphorescent; to emit a phosphoric light. Generally, to emit light at
temperatures too low to excite incandescent radiation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To emit light after a source of illumination has
been removed; to exhibit phosphorescence.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorescence</ent><br/
<hw>Phos`phor*es"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phosphorescence</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state
of being phosphorescent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The process of phosphorescing, especially that of
emitting light after a source of excitation has been removed. This
contrasts with the process of <contr>fluorescence</contr>, in which a
substance emits light of a lower wavelength than the illuminating light,
only while the illumination continues.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Light emitted by the process of
phosphorescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorescent</ent><br/
<hw>Phos`phor*es"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phosphorescent</ets>.]</ety> <def>Shining with a phosphoric light;
emitting phosphorescence; luminous without sensible heat.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A phosphorescent substance.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphoric</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phosphorique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to phosphorus; resembling, or containing, phosporus;
specifically, designating those compounds in which phosphorus has a
higher valence as contrasted with the <contr>phosphorous</contr>
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Phosphorescent.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>phosphoric</xex>
sea.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Glacial phosphoric acid</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Metaphosphoric acid in the form of glassy
semitransparent masses or sticks</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Pure normal
phosphoric acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Phosphoric acid</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a white crystalline substance,
<chform>H3PO4</chform>, which is the most highly oxidized acid of
phosphorus, and forms an important and extensive series of compounds,
viz., the phosphates.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Soluble phosphoric
acid</b></col>, <col><b>Insoluble phosphoric acid</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Agric. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>phosphoric acid combined in acid salts,
or in neutral or basic salts, which are respectively soluble and
insoluble in water or in plant juices.</cd> -- <col><b>Reverted
phosphoric acid</b></col> <fld>(Agric. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>phosphoric acid
changed from acid (soluble) salts back to neutral or basic (insoluble)
salts.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorical</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*phor"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <def>Phosphoric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorite</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A massive variety of apatite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphoritic</ent><br/
<hw>Phos`phor*it"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to phosphorite; resembling, or of the nature of,
phosphorite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorize</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
phosphorate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorized</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*ized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing, or
impregnated with, phosphorus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorogenic</ent><br/
<hw>Phos`phor*o*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosphorus</ets> + <ets>-gen</ets> + <ets>-ic</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Generating phosphorescence; <as>as, <ex>phosphorogenic</ex>
rays</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphoroscope</ent><br/
<hw>Phos*phor"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosphorus</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An apparatus for observing the phosphorescence
produced in different bodies by the action of light, and for measuring
its duration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorous</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*ous</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<ocr/r*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>phosphoreux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to phosphorus; resembling or containing phosphorus;
specifically, designating those compounds in which phosphorus has a
lower valence as contrasted with <contr>phosphoric</contr> compounds;
<as>as, <ex>phosphorous</ex> acid,
<chform>H3PO3</chform></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphorus</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*us</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<ocr/r*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Phosphori</plw>
<pr>(f<ocr/s"f<ocr/r*<imac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., the morning star, Gr.
<grk>fwsfo`ros</grk>, lit., light bringer; <grk>fw^s</grk> light +
<grk>fe`rein</grk> to bring.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The morning star;
Phosphor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A poisonous nonmetallic element
of the nitrogen group, obtained as a white, or yellowish, translucent
waxy substance, having a characteristic disagreeable smell; this waxy
allotropic form is also called <stype>yellow phosphorus</stype>, to
distinguish it from another allotropic form, <stype>red
phosphorus</stype>. It is very active chemically, must be preserved
under water, and unites with oxygen even at ordinary temperatures,
giving a faint glow, -- whence its name. It always occurs combined,
usually in phosphates, as in the mineral apatite, in bones, etc. It is
used in the composition on the tips of friction matches, and for many
other purposes. It is an essential component of the genetic material of
all living organisms. The molecule contains four atoms. Symbol P. Atomic
weight 31.0.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Hence, any substance which shines
in the dark like phosphorus, as certain phosphorescent bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bologna phosphorus</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>sulphide of barium, which shines in the dark after exposure to
light; -- so called because this property was discovered by a resident
of <city>Bologna</city>. The term is sometimes applied to other
compounds having similar properties.</cd> -- <col><b>Metallic
phosphorus</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an allotropic modification
of phosphorus, obtained as a gray metallic crystalline substance, having
very inert chemical properties. It is obtained by heating ordinary
phosphorus in a closed vessel at a high temperature.</cd> --
<col><b>Phosphorus disease</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a disease
common among workers in phosphorus, giving rise to necrosis of the
jawbone, and other symptoms.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Red
phosphorus</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Amorphous
phosphorus</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an allotropic
modification of phosphorus, obtained as a dark red powder by heating
ordinary phosphorus in closed vessels. It is not poisonous, is not
phosphorescent, and is only moderately active chemically. It is valuable
as a chemical reagent, and is used in the composition of the friction
surface on which safety matches are ignited.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar
phosphori</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>phosphorescent substances
which shine in the dark after exposure to the sunlight or other intense
light.</cd> -- <col><b>yellow phosphorus</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>the waxy yellow allotropic form of elemental phosphorus. See also
<er>phosphorus</er>{2}.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1079 --></p>

<p><ent>Phosphorus steel</ent><br/
<hw>Phosphorus steel</hw>. <def>A steel in which the amount of
phosphorus exceeds that of carbon.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphoryl</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phor*yl</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<ocr/r*<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phosphor</ets>us + <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The radical <chform>PO</chform>, regarded as the typical nucleus of
certain compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphuret</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phu*ret</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<usl/*r<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A phosphide.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phosphureted</ent><br/
<hw>Phos"phu*ret`ed</hw> <pr>(f<ocr/s"f<usl/*r<ecr/t`<ecr/d)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Impregnated, or combined, with
phosphorus.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>phosphuretted</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phosphureted hydrogen</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Phosphine</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"tic</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fw^s</grk>, <grk>fwto`s</grk>, light.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to light; specifically, relating to the production of light by the lower
animals.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photic region</ent><br/
<hw>Photic region</hw>. <fld>(Phytogeography)</fld> <def>The uppermost
zone of the sea, which receives the most light.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photics</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"tics</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>The science of light; -- a general term
sometimes employed when <contr>optics</contr> is restricted to light as
a producing vision.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photism</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"tism</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fwtismo`s</grk> illumination.]</ety> <fld>(Psycol.)</fld> <def>A
luminous image or appearance of a hallucinatory character.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Photos</plw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<osl/z)</pr>.</plu>
<def>A contraction of <er>Photograph</er>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to-</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<osl/-)</pr>. <def>A combining form from
Gr. <grk>fw^s</grk>, <grk>fwto`s</grk>, <xlati>light</xlati>; <as>as,
<ex>photo</ex>graphy, <ex>photo</ex>type,
<ex>photo</ex>meter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photobacterium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pho`to*bac*te"ri*um</hw>
<pr>(f<omac/`t<osl/*b<acr/k*t<emac/"r<esl/*<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Photo-</er>, and <er>Bacterium</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bacteriol.)</fld> <def>A genus including certain comma-shaped
marine bacteria which emit bluish or greenish phosphorescence. Also, any
microorganism of this group.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photobiotic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*bi*ot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>biotic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Requiring light to live; incapable of living without light; <as>as,
<ex>photobiotic</ex> plant cells</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>photocathode</ent><br/
<hw>pho`to*cath"ode</hw> <pr>(f<omac/`t<osl/*k<acr/th"<omac/d)</pr>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A cathode that emits electrons when
illuminated.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>photocell</ent><br/
<hw>pho"to*cell`</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<osl/*s<ecr/l`)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>An electrical device which changes voltage or resistance upon
exposure to light, used to detect and measure light and other
radiations.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> photoelectric cell, electric eye.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoceramics</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*ce*ram"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Art or
process of decorating pottery with photographically prepared
designs.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*ce*ram"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochemical</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>chemical</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to chemical changes produced by light; <as>as, the
<ex>photochemical</ex> changes of the visual purple of the
retina</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochemistry</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chem"is*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>chemistry</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The branch of chemistry which relates to the
effect of light in producing chemical changes, as in
photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromatic</ent><br/
<ent>Photochromic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`to*chro"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pho`to*chro*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to photochromy; produced by photochromy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chro*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>chrw^ma</grk> color +
<ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Art or process of printing colored
photographs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chro"mo*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>chrw^ma</grk> color +
<ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A device for giving shifting
effects of color to a photograph. The unmounted print, made translucent,
is illuminated from behind with colored light.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A combination of three optical lanterns for
projecting objects on a screen in the colors of nature. The images of
three partial photographs taken through color screens (red, green, and
blue, respectively) are superimposed. Each image is given its own
primary color, and these colors blend and reproduce the colors of the
object.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromotype</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chro"mo*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>chrw^ma</grk> color +
<ets>-type</ets>.]</ety> <def>A colored print made
photomechanically.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromotype</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chro"mo*type</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To represent by a
colored print made by any photomechanical process.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromotypy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chro"mo*typ`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art
of making photochromotypes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochromy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*toch"ro*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>chrw^ma</grk> color.]</ety> <def>The
art or process of reproducing colors by photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochronograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chron"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>chronograph</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument for recording minute intervals
of time. The record is made by the power of a magnetic field, due to an
electric signaling current, to turn the plane of polarization of light.
A flash, coinciding in time and duration with the signal, is thus
produced and is photographed on a moving plate.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>An instrument for the
photographic recording of star transits.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photochronography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*chro*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Art of
recording or measuring intervals of time by the photochronograph.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*chron`o*graph"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<wf>-graph"ic*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Pho`to*chron`o*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>photodrome</ent><br/
<hw>pho"to*drome</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<osl/*dr<omac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>dramei^n</grk> to run.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An apparatus consisting of a large wheel with
spokes, which when turning very rapidly is illuminated by momentary
flashes of light passing through slits in a rotating disk. By properly
timing the succession of flashes the wheel is made to appear to be
motionless, or to rotate more or less slowly in either
direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photodynamics</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*dy*nam"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>dynamics</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Plant
Physiol.)</fld> <def>The relation of light to the movements of plants
and their organs; the study of the phenomena of curvatures induced by
the stimulus of light.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*dy*nam"ic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <wf>Pho`to*dy*nam"ic*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-electric</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-e*lec"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>electric</ets>.]</ety> <def>Acting by the
operation of both light and electricity; -- said of apparatus for
producing pictures by electric light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-electrical</ent><br/
<ent>Photo-electric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`to-e*lec"tric</hw>, <hw>Pho`to-e*lec"tric*al</hw>
}</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or capable of developing,
photo-electricity.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-electric cell</ent><br/
<hw>Photo-electric cell</hw>. <def>A cell (as one of two electrodes
embedded in selenium) which by exposure to light generates an electric
current.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-electricity</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-e*lec*tric"i*ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> +
<ets>electricity</ets>.]</ety> <def>Electricity produced by
light.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-electrograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-e*lec"tro*graph</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Photo-</er>; <er>Electrograph</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Meteor.)</fld>
<def>An electrometer registering by photography.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-electrotype</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-e*lec"tro*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>An electrotype plate formed in a mold made by
photographing on prepared gelatine, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-engraving</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-en*grav"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>engraving</ets>.]</ety> <def>The process
of obtaining an etched or engraved plate from the photographic image, to
be used in printing; also, a picture produced by such a
process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-engrave</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-en*grave"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Photo-engraved</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Photo-engraving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> +
<ets>engrave</ets>.]</ety> <def>To engrave by a photomechanical process;
to make a photo-engraving of.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to-en*grav"er</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-epinasty</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-ep"i*nas`ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Photo-</er>, and <er>Epinastic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
disproportionately rapid growth of the upper surface of dorsiventral
organs, such as leaves, through the stimulus of exposure to light.</def>
<rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-etch</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-etch"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Photo-etched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Photo-etching</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>etch</ets>.]</ety> <def>To engrave, or
make an engraving of, by any photomechanical process involving etching
of the plate.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photo-etching</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to-etch"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A photo-engraving produced
by any process involving the etching of the plate.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogalvanography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*gal`va*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>galvanography</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art
or process of making photo-electrotypes.</def> <rj><au>Sir D.
Brewster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogen</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets>
+ <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A light hydrocarbon
oil resembling kerosene. It is obtained by distilling coal, paraffin,
etc., and is used as a lubricant, illuminant, etc.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>photogene</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogene</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*gene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Photogen</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A photograph.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A more or less continued impression or image on the
retina.</def> <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to photogeny; producing or generating light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Appearing attractive in a photograph; having
features that appear attractive in a photograph.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Caused by light, as for example a
skin abnormality.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogeny</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"e*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Photogen</er>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Photography</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoglyphic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*glyph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>gly`fein</grk> to engrave.]</ety>
<def>Pertaining to the art of engraving by the action of light.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>photoglyptic</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Photoglyphic engraving</b></col>, <cd>a process of
etching on copper, steel, or zinc, by means of the action of light and
certain chemicals, so that from the plate impressions may be taken.</cd>
<rj><au>Sir D. Brewster.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoglyphy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"ly*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Photoglyphic
engraving. See under <er>Photoglyphic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoglyptic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*glyp"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Photoglyphic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogram</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets>
+ <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <def>A photograph.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogrammeter</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*gram"me*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Photogram</er>; <er>-meter</er>.]</ety> <def>A phototheodolite, or a
camera designed for use in photogrammetry.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to*gram*met"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<wf>Pho`to*gram*met"ric*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogrammetry</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*gram"me*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photogram</ets> + <ets>metry</ets>.]</ety> <def>A method of
surveying or map making by photography, used also in determining the
height and motions of clouds, sea waves, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>A picture or
likeness obtained by photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*graph</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Photographed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Photographing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To take a
picture or likeness of by means of photography; <as>as, to
<ex>photograph</ex> a view; to <ex>photograph</ex> a
group.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He makes his pen drawing on white paper, and they are afterwards
<qex>photographed</qex> on wood.</q> <rj><qau>Hamerton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>Also used figuratively.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is <qex>photographed</qex> on my mind.</q> <rj><qau>Lady D.
Hardy.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*graph</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To practice photography; to
take photographs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photographer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
practices, or is skilled in, photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photographical</ent><br/
<ent>Photographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`to*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pho`to*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>photographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
photography; obtained by photography; used ib photography; as a
<xex>photographic</xex> picture; a <xex>photographic</xex> camera.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Photographic printing</b></col>, <cd>the process of
obtaining pictures, as on chemically prepared paper, from photographic
negatives, by exposure to light.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photographist</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
photographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photographometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"ra*phom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photograph</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>An instrument for determining the sensibility
of the plates employed in photographic processes to luminous
rays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photographone</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"ra*phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Photograph</er>; <er>-phone</er>.]</ety> <def>A device, consisting
essentially of an electric arc and a camera, by which a series of
photographs of the variations of the arc due to sound waves are obtained
for reproduction by means of a selenium cell and a telephone.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>photographie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The science which
relates to the action of light on sensitive bodies in the production of
pictures, the fixation of images, and the like. The production of
pictures by the photochemical action of light on films of chemicals
sensitive to light, and also the production of electronic images in
electronic cameras, are both considered types of photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The art or process of producing pictures by this
action of light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In traditional photography, the well-focused optical
image is thrown on a surface of metal, glass, paper, or other suitable
substance, coated with collodion or gelatin, and sensitized with the
chlorides, bromides, or iodides of silver, or other salts sensitive to
light. The exposed plate is then treated with reducing agents, as
pyrogallic acid, ferrous sulphate, etc., to develop the latent image.
The image is then fixed by washing off the excess of unchanged sensitive
salt with sodium hyposulphite (<xex>thiosulphate</xex>) or other
suitable reagents.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>color photography</b></col>, <cd>the production of
colored images by a photographic process. A variety of dyes are used to
produced the colored images in photochemical processes. Such processes
may or may not use silver to produce the colored image. Color
photographs may also be produced by electronic cameras.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photogravure</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*grav"ure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A photoengraving; also, the process by which such a picture is
produced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoheliograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*he"li*o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>heliograph</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A modified kind of telescope adapted to taking
photographs of the sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoheliometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*he`li*om"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>heliometer</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A double-lens instrument for measuring slight
variations of the sun's diameter by photography, utilizing the common
chord of two overlapping images.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photolithograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*lith"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>lithograph</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
lithographic picture or copy from a stone prepared by the aid of
photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photolithograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*lith"o*graph</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To produce (a
picture, a copy) by the process of photolithography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photolithographer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*li*thog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
practices, or one who employs, photolithography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photolithographic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*lith`o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to photolithography; produced by photolithography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photolithography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*li*thog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The art or process of producing photolithographs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The process by which the image of a pattern is
transferred photographically to a sensitive surface, and the surface
subsequently etched; used for printing or in the production of
integrated circuits.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photological</ent><br/
<ent>Photologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`to*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pho`to*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to photology, or the
doctrine of light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who studies or expounds the laws of light.</def>
<mark>[Obsolescent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who studies the science of
photography.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photology</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>photologie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The doctrine or science
of light, explaining its nature and phenomena; optics.</def>
<mark>[Obsolescent]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The science of photography.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoluminescence</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*lu`mi*nes"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>luminescence</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Luminescence induced by exposure to light
waves in the infrared to ultraviolet range.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoluminescent</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*lu`mi*nes"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>luminescent</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Luminescent due to exposure to light
waves.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photomagnetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*mag*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to photomagnetism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photomagnetism</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*mag"net*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The branch
of science which treats of the relation of magnetism to light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photomechanical</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*me*chan"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, any photographic process in which a
printing surface is obtained without the intervention of hand
engraving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>photom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An
instrument for measuring the intensity of light, or, more especially,
for comparing the relative intensities of different lights, or their
relative illuminating power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photometrical</ent><br/
<ent>Photometric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho`to*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pho`to*met"ric*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>photom<eacute/trique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
photometry, or to a photometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photometrist</ent><br/
<ent>Photometrician</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pho*tom`e*tri"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pho*tom"e*trist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
engaged in the scientific measurement of light; a specialist in
<er>photometry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>photom<eacute/trie</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of science which
treats of the measurement of the intensity of light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photomezzotype</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*mez"zo*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + It. <ets>mezzo</ets> middle, half +
<ets>-type</ets>.]</ety> <def>A photomechanical process similar to
collotype.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photomicrograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*mi"cro*graph</hw>
<pr>(f<omac/`t<osl/*m<imac/"kr<osl/*gr<adot/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>micro</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An enlarged or macroscopic photograph of a microscopic
object. See <er>Microphotograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A microscopically small photograph of an
object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photomicrography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*mi*crog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art
of producing photomicrographs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photonephograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*neph"o*graph</hw>
<pr>(f<omac/`t<osl/*n<ecr/f"<osl/*gr<adot/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>ne`fos</grk> a cloud +
<ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <def>A nephoscope
registering by photography, commonly consisting of a pair of cameras
used simultaneously.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photophilous</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*toph"i*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ loving.]</ety> <fld>(Phytogeog.)</fld>
<def>Light-loving; growing in strong light, as many plants.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photophobia</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*pho"bi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fw^s</grk>, <grk>fwto`s</grk>, light + <grk>fo`bos</grk>
fear.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A dread or intolerance of
light.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Watson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photophone</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*phone</hw> <pr>(f<omac/"t<osl/*f<omac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fwnh`</grk> sound.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An apparatus for the production of sound by
the action of rays of light.</def> <rj><au>A. G. Bell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photophonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to photophone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photophony</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*toph"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art or
practice of using the photophone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoplay</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*play`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A play for
representation or exhibition by moving pictures; also, the
moving-picture representation of a play.</def><-- = screenplay? --><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photophore</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A form of endoscope using an electric
light.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A light-emitting organ; specif.,
one of the luminous spots on certain marine (mostly deep-sea)
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoprint</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*print`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any print made
by a photomechanical process.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photopsia</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*top"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fw^s</grk>, <grk>fwto`s</grk>, light + <grk>'o`psis</grk>
sight.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An affection of the eye, in which
the patient perceives luminous rays, flashes, coruscations, etc. See
<er>phosphene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photopsy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*top"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Photopsia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photorelief</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*re*lief"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A printing
surface in relief, obtained by photographic means and subsequent
manipulations.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>Anything employed for the observation of light or luminous
effects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoscopic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the photoscope or its uses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photosculpture</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*sculp"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>sculpture</ets>.]</ety> <def>A process in
which, by means of a number of photographs simultaneously taken from
different points of view on the same level, rough models of the figure
or bust of a person or animal may be made with great
expedition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photosphere</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*sphere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>sphere</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sphere of
light; esp., the luminous envelope of the sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photospheric</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*spher"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the photosphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photosynthesis</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*syn"the*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Plant
Physiol.)</fld> <def>The process of constructive metabolism in which
green plants utilize the energy of sunlight to manufacture carbohydrates
from carbon dioxide and water in the presence of chlorophyll. It was
formerly called <altname>assimilation</altname>, but this is now
commonly used as in animal physiology.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to*syn*thet"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Pho`to*syn*thet"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><note> In green plants water is absorbed by the roots and carried to
the leaves by the xylem, and carbon dioxide is obtained from air that
enters the leaves through the stomata and diffuses to the cells
containing chlorophyll. The green pigment chlorophyll is uniquely
capable of converting the active energy of light into a latent form that
can be stored (in food) and used when needed.<br/
The initial process in photosynthesis is the decomposition of water
(H2O) into oxygen, which is released, and hydrogen; direct light is
required for this process. The hydrogen and the carbon and oxygen of
carbon dioxide (CO2) are then converted into a series of increasingly
complex compounds that result finally in a stable organic compound,
glucose (C6H12O6 ), and water. This phase of photosynthesis utilizes
stored energy and therefore can proceed in the dark. The simplified
equation used to represent this overall process is
6CO2+12H2O+energy=C6H12O6+6O2+6H2 O. In general, the results of this
process are the reverse of those in respiration, in which carbohydrates
are oxidized to release energy, with the production of carbon dioxide
and water.<br/
The intermediary reactions before glucose is formed involve several
enzymes, which react with the coenzyme ATP (see adenosine triphosphate )
to produce various molecules. Studies using radioactive carbon have
indicated that among the intermediate products are three-carbon
molecules from which acids and amino acids, as well as glucose, are
derived.</note><br/
<au>http://www.infoplease.com/ce6/sci/A0860378.html</au><br/
<br/
<note><b>The role of chlorophyll</b><br/
Chlorophyll contains a hydrophyllic head group and a hydrophobic tail
region. A magnesium atom is held in the center of a cyclic, conjugated
double bond porphyrin ring which is responsible for absorbing red light.
(There also is an absorption band in the blue. Thus red and blue are
absorbed and green passes through, giving plants a characteristic green
color.)<br/
Light is absorbed by antenna chlorophyll molecules, then transferred to
the reaction center chlorophylls. Some hundreds of antenna chlorophyll
molecules transfer energy to a reaction center, with transfer times of
about 10<sup>-10</sup> sec from the edge of the unit to the center.<br/
The energy from light is used to pump H+ ions from the stroma into the
thylakoid space and to reduce NADP+ to NADPH. Flow of H+ back into the
stroma releases energy which is used to phosphorylate ADP to ATP. The
chemiosmotic coupling is working here in a similar way to the mechanism
of ATP generation used in mitochondria.<br/
<br/
<b>Carbon Fixation</b> Carbon fixation is catalyzed by ribulose
bisphosphate carboxylase (RuBP carboxylase), the world's most abundent
enzyme.<br/
The<col><b> Calvin cycle</b></col> combines three carbon dioxide
molecules into one molecule of three carbon glyceraldehyde
3-phosphate.<br/
Some plants, particularly many which live in hot, dry climates, have a
mechanism for storing carbon dioxide by combining it with a three carbon
molecule to form a four carbon molecule. This pathway is known as the C4
or Hatch-Slack pathway.</note><br/
<au>https://web.archive.org/web/20011111194455/http://fig.cox.miami.edu/Faculty/Tom/bil255/bil255sum98/17_photo.html</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototaxy</ent><br/
<ent>Phototaxis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pho`to*tax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pho"to*tax`y</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>phototaxis</ets>;
<ets>photo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ an arranging.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>The influence of light on the movements of low organisms, as
various infusorians, the zoospores of certain alg<ae/, etc.; also, the
tendency to follow definite directions of motion or assume definite
positions under such influence. If the migration is toward the source of
light, it is termed <col><b>positive phototaxis</b></col>; if away from
the light, <col><b>negative phototaxis</b></col>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to*tac"tic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Pho`to*tac"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototelegraphy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*te*leg"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Telegraphy by means of light, as by the heliograph or the
photophone. Also, less properly, telephotography.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to*tel"e*graph</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pho`to*tel`e*graph"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototelescope</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*tel"e*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A telescope adapted for taking photographs of
the heavenly bodies.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototheodolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*the*od"o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
arrangement of two photographic cameras, the plates of which may be
brought into exactly the same plane, used in surveying and map making.
From the differences between two pictures taken at the same moment,
measurements in all dimensions of the region may be obtained.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototherapy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*ther"a*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The application of light for therapeutic purposes, esp. for
treating diseases of the skin.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to*the*rap"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<wf>Pho`to*ther`a*peu"tic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photothermic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*ther"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to both light and heat.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototonus</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tot"o*nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Photo-</er>, and <er>Tone</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A motile condition in plants resulting from exposure to
light.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*ton"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An irritable condition of
protoplasm, resulting in movement, due to a certain intensity of
light.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*ton"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototopography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*to*pog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Photogrammetry.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pho`to*top`o*graph"ic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <wf>Pho`to*top`o*graph"ic*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototrichromatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*tri`chro*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>tri-</ets> + <ets>chromatic</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Designating a photomechanical process for making reproductions in
natural colors by three printings.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototropic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*trop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to turn.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>of or pertaining to phototropism; exhibiting phototropism; same as
<er>Heliotropic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototropism</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tot"ro*pism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to turn.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Plant Physiol.)</fld> <def>The tendency of growing plant organs to
move or curve under the influence of light. In ordinary use the term is
practically synonymous with <er>heliotropism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Microbiology)</fld> <def>exhibiting movement in a
direction toward (<stype>positive phototropism</stype>) or away from
(<stype>negative phototropism</stype>) a source of light.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototype</ent><br/
<hw>Pho"to*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets>
+ <ets>-type</ets>.]</ety> <def>A plate or block with a printing surface
(usually in relief) obtained from a photograph; also, any one of the
many methods of processes by which such a printing surface is
obtained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototypic</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*typ"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a phototype or phototypy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototypography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*ty*pog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>typography</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Phototypy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phototypy</ent><br/
<hw>Pho*tot"y*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art or
process of producing phototypes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photovisual</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*vis"u*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Optics)</fld>
<def>Of certain achromatic lenses, having the same focus for the actinic
and for the brightest of the visual rays.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photoxylography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*xy*log"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>xylography</ets>.]</ety> <def>The process
of producing a representation of an object on wood, by photography, for
the use of the wood engraver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photozincograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*zin"co*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A print
made by photozincography.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pho`to*zin`co*graph"ic</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Photozincography</ent><br/
<hw>Pho`to*zin*cog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Photo-</ets> + <ets>zincography</ets>.]</ety> <def>A process,
analogous to photolithography, for reproducing photographed impressions
transferred to zinc plate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phragmocone</ent><br/
<hw>Phrag"mo*cone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, a
fence, an inclosure + <?/ a cone.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
thin chambered shell attached to the anterior end of a belemnite.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>phragmacone</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phragmosiphon</ent><br/
<hw>Phrag`mo*si"phon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The siphon of a phragmocone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrasal</ent><br/
<hw>Phras"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the nature of a
phrase; consisting of a phrase; <as>as, a <ex>phrasal</ex>
adverb</as>.</def> <rj><au>Earlc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrase</ent><br/
<hw>Phrase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>phrasis</ets> diction, phraseology, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
speak.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A brief expression, sometimes a single
word, but usually two or more words forming an expression by themselves,
or being a portion of a sentence; <as>as, an adverbial
<ex>phrase</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Convey<rdquo/ the wise it call. <ldquo/Steal!<rdquo/ foh! a
fico for the <qex>phrase</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A short, pithy expression; especially, one which is
often employed; a peculiar or idiomatic turn of speech; <as>as, <ex>to
err is human</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A mode or form of speech; the manner or style in
which any one expreses himself; diction; expression.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Phrases</xex> of the hearth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou speak'st<br/
In better <qex>phrase</qex> and matter than thou didst.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A short clause or portion of a
period.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A composition consists first of sentences, or
<xex>periods</xex>; these are subdivided into <xex>sections</xex>, and
these into <xex>phrases</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phrase book</b></col>, <cd>a book of idiomatic
phrases.</cd> <rj><au>J. S. Blackie.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrase</ent><br/
<hw>Phrase</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Phrased</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Phrasing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phraser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To express in words, or in peculiar
words; to call; to style.</def> <ldquo/These suns -- for so they
<xex>phrase</xex> 'em.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrase</ent><br/
<hw>Phrase</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use proper or fine
phrases.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To group notes into phrases;
<as>as, he <ex>phrases</ex> well</as>. See <er>Phrase</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phraseless</ent><br/
<hw>Phrase"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Indescribable.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phraseogram</ent><br/
<hw>Phra"se*o*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
phrase + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Phonography)</fld> <def>A symbol
for a phrase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phraseological</ent><br/
<ent>Phraseologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phra`se*o*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phra`se*o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to phraseology; consisting of a peculiar form of
words.</def> <ldquo/This verbal or <xex>phraseological</xex>
answer.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phraseologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phra`se*ol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A collector
or coiner of phrases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phraseology</ent><br/
<hw>Phra`se*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
phrase + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>phras<eacute/ologie</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Manner of expression; peculiarity of diction;
style.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Most completely national in his . . . <qex>phraseology</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A collection of phrases; a phrase book.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Diction; style. See <er>Diction</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrasing</ent><br/
<hw>Phras"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Method
of expression; association of words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The act or method of grouping the
notes so as to form distinct musical phrases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phratry</ent><br/
<hw>Phra"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phratries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A subdivision of a phyle, or tribe, in
Athens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phreatic</ent><br/
<hw>Phre*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>phr<eacute/atique</ets>, from Gr. <?/, <?/, a well.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Subterranean; -- applied to sources supplying
wells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenetical</ent><br/
<ent>Phrenetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phre*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phre*net"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>phreneticus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>frenhtiko`s</grk>, <grk>frenitiko`s</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>phr<eacute/n<eacute/tique</ets>. See <er>Frantic</er>, and cf.
<er>Frenetic</er>.]</ety> <def>Relating to phrenitis; suffering from
frenzy; delirious; mad; frantic; frenetic.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Phre*net"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phrenico-</ent><br/
<ent>phreni-</ent><br/
<ent>phreno-</ent><br/
<ent>phren-</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>phren-</hw>, <hw>phreno-</hw>, <hw>phreni-</hw>,
<hw>phrenico-</hw></mhw> <pr>(fr<ecr/n- or fr<emac/n-)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>frh`n</grk>, <grk>freno`s</grk>, the
midriff, or diaphragm, the heart (as the seat of emotions), the
mind.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A combining form meaning, of or
pertaining to the diaphragm, the mind, or the brain; frenic.</def>
<au>Stedman.</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Phre*net"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is phrenetic.</def>
<rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1080 --></p>

<p><ent>Phrenic</ent><br/
<hw>Phren"ic</hw> <pr>(fr<ecr/n"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>frh`n</grk>, <grk>freno`s</grk>, the midriff, or diaphragm, the
heart, the mind: cf. F. <ets>phr<eacute/nique</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the diaphragm;
diaphragmatic; <as>as, the <ex>phrenic</ex> nerve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenics</ent><br/
<hw>Phren"ics</hw> <pr>(fr<ecr/n"<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That
branch of science which relates to the mind; mental philosophy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenism</ent><br/
<hw>Phre"nism</hw> <pr>(fr<emac/"n<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[See <er>Phrenic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>See
<cref>Vital force</cref>, under <er>Vital</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phre*ni"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>freni^tis</grk>, fr. <grk>frh`n</grk>, <grk>freno`s</grk>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammation of the brain, or of the
meninges of the brain, attended with acute fever and delirium; -- called
also <altname>cephalitis</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>See <er>Frenzy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenograph</ent><br/
<hw>Phre"no*graph</hw> <pr>(fr<emac/"n<osl/*gr<adot/f)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>frh`n</grk>, <grk>freno`s</grk>, the
midriff + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An
instrument for registering the movements of the diaphragm, or midriff,
in respiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenologer</ent><br/
<hw>Phre*nol"o*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
phrenologist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenologic</ent><br/
<hw>Phren`o*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phr<eacute/nologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Phrenological.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenological</ent><br/
<hw>Phren`o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to phrenology.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Phren`o*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phre*nol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phr<eacute/nologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One versed in phrenology; a
craniologist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenology</ent><br/
<hw>Phre*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>frh`n</grk>, <grk>freno`s</grk>, the mind + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>phr<eacute/nologie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The science of
the special functions of the several parts of the brain, or of the
supposed connection between the various faculties of the mind and
particular organs in the brain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In popular usage, the obsolete physiological
hypothesis of <persfn>Gall</persfn>, that the mental faculties, and
traits of character, are shown on the surface of the head or skull;
craniology. it is considered a pseudo-science by all reputable medical
personnel, but is still believed by some people.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> craniognomy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <persfn>Gall</persfn> marked out on his model of the
head the places of twenty-six organs, as round inclosures with vacant
interspaces. <persfn>Spurzheim</persfn> and <persfn>Combe</persfn>
divided the whole scalp into oblong and conterminous patches.</note>
<rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Illustr. of a chart of phrenology, showing the areas of the skull
as "mapped" by Gall. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenomagnetism</ent><br/
<hw>Phre`no*mag"net*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>frh`n</grk>, <grk>freno`s</grk>, the mind + E.
<ets>magnetism</ets>.]</ety> <def>The power of exciting the organs of
the brain by magnetic or mesmeric influence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrenosin</ent><br/
<hw>Phre"no*sin</hw> <pr>(fr<emac/"n<osl/*s<icr/n or
fr<ecr/n"<osl/*s<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Phrenic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A cerebroside,
related to cerebrin, present in white matter of the brain.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cerebron.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrensied</ent><br/
<hw>Phren"sied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. p. & a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Frenzied</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrensy</ent><br/
<hw>Phren"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Violent and
irrational excitement; delirium. See <er>Frenzy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrensy</ent><br/
<hw>Phren"sy</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render frantic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrentic</ent><br/
<hw>Phren"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Phrenetic</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phryganeid</ent><br/
<hw>Phry*ga"ne*id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any insect belonging to the Phryganeides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phryganeides</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phryg`a*ne"i*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. <ets>Phryganea</ets>, the typical genus, fr. Gr. <?/ a dry
stick.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of neuropterous insects
which includes the caddice flies; -- called also
<altname>Trichoptera</altname>. See <er>Trichoptera</er>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Phryganides</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrygian</ent><br/
<hw>Phryg"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Phrygius</ets>, Gr. <grk>Fry`gios</grk>, fr. <grk>Frygi`a</grk>
Phrygia, a country of Asia Minor.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
Phrygia, or to its inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phrygian mode</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>one of the
ancient Greek modes, very bold and vehement in style; -- so called
because fabled to have been invented by the <xex>Phrygian</xex>
<persfn>Marsyas</persfn>.</cd> <au>Moore (Encyc. of Music).</au> --
<col><b>Phrygian stone</b></col>, <cd>a light, spongy stone, resembling
a pumice, -- used by the ancients in dyeing, and said to be drying and
astringent.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrygian</ent><br/
<hw>Phryg"i*an</hw>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Phrygia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>A Montanist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phrygian cap</ent><br/
<hw>Phryg"i*an cap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A close-fitting cap
represented in Greek art as worn by Orientals, assumed to have been
conical in shape. It has been adopted in modern art as the so-called
<altname>liberty cap</altname>, or <altname>cap of
liberty</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalate</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of phthalic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalein</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"e*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Phthalic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>One of a series of
artificial organic dyes made as condensation products of the phenols
with phthalic acid, and well represented by phenolphthalein. Their
alkaline solutions are fluorescent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phenol phthalein</b></col>, <cd>a white or yellowish
white crystalline substance made from phthalic acid and phenol. Same as
<er>phenolphthalein</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalic</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Na<ets>phthal</ets>ene + <ets>-ic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, a dibasic acid obtained by the
oxidation of naphthalene and allied substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Phthalic acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a white
crystalline substance, <chform>C6H4.(CO2H)2</chform>, analogous to
benzoic acid, and employed in the brilliant dyestuffs called the
phthaleins.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalide</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phthal</ets>yl + anhydr<ets>ide</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A lactone (<chform>C8H6O2</chform>) obtained as
a white crystalline substance by reduction of phthalyl chloride and
other processes; hence, by extension, any one of the series of which
phthalide proper is the type.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>phthalid</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>A fungicide
(<chform>C8H2Cl4O2</chform>) which is a tetrachloro derivative of
phthalide{1} proper; in this sense, the spelling <asp>fthalide</asp> is
also used. It has CAS Registry Number 27355-22-2.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. 4,5,6,7-tetrachloro-1(3H)-isobenzofuranone; --</b>
.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalimide</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"i*mide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phthal</ets>ic + <ets>imide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An imido derivative of phthalic acid, obtained as a white
crystalline substance, <chform>C6H4.(CO)2NH</chform>, which has itself
(like succinimide) acid properties, and forms a series of salts. Cf.
<cref>Imido acid</cref>, under <er>Imido</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalin</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
colorless crystalline substance obtained by reduction from phthalein,
into which it is easily converted by oxidation; hence, any one of the
series of which phthalin proper is the type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthalyl</ent><br/
<hw>Phthal"yl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phthal</ets>ic
+ <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The hypothetical
radical of phthalic acid.</def><-- now usu. pthaloyl --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthiriasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phthi*ri"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ louse.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease
(<xex>morbus pediculous</xex>) consisting in the excessive
multiplication of lice on the human body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthisic</ent><br/
<hw>Phthis"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Phthisis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthisical</ent><br/
<hw>Phthis"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phthisicus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>phthisique</ets>. See
<er>Phthisis</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to phthisis; affected
with phthisis; wasting; consumptive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthisicky</ent><br/
<hw>Phthis"ick*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having phthisis,
or some symptom of it, as difficulty in breathing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthisiology</ent><br/
<hw>Phthis`i*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phthisis</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A treatise on phthisis.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthisipneumony</ent><br/
<ent>Phthisipneumonia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Phthis`ip*neu*mo"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phthis`ip*neu"mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Phthisis</er>, <er>Pneumonia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pulmonary consumption; tuberculosis of the
lungs.</def> <mark>[obs.]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phthisis</ent><br/
<hw>phthi"sis</hw> <pr>(th<imac/"s<icr/s; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>fqi`sis</grk>, fr. <grk>fqi`ein</grk> to pass or
waste away: cf. F. <ets>phthisie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
wasting or consumption of the tissues. The term is now obsolete; it was
once applied to many wasting diseases, but in the early 1900's became
restricted to tuberculosis of the lungs (pulmonary phthisis, or
consumption). See <er>Consumption</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fibroid phthisis</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Fibroid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthongal</ent><br/
<hw>Phthon"gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
voice.]</ety> <def>Formed into, or characterized by, voice; vocalized;
-- said of all the vowels and the semivowels, also of the vocal or
sonant consonants <xex>g</xex>, <xex>d</xex>, <xex>b</xex>,
<xex>l</xex>, <xex>r</xex>, <xex>v</xex>, <xex>z</xex>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthongal</ent><br/
<hw>Phthon"gal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vocalized element or
letter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phthongometer</ent><br/
<hw>Phthon*gom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
voice + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for measuring vocal
sounds.</def> <rj><au>Whewell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phtor</ent><br/
<hw>phtor</hw> <pr>(t<ocir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>phthore</ets>, Gr. <grk>fqei`rein</grk> to destroy.]</ety>
<fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Fluorine.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>phthor</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycite</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Erythrite</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycochrome</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"co*chrome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + <grk>chrw^ma</grk> color.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A bluish green coloring matter of certain
alg<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycocyanine</ent><br/
<ent>Phycocyanin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phy`co*cy"a*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phy`co*cy"a*nine</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed
+ E. <ets>cyanin</ets>.]</ety> <def>A blue coloring matter found in
certain algae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycoerythrine</ent><br/
<ent>Phycoerythrin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phy`co*e*ryth"rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phy`co*e*ryth"rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + E. <ets>erythrin</ets>,
<ets>-ine</ets>.]</ety> <def>A red coloring matter found in algae of the
subclass <subclass>Florideae</subclass>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycography</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*cog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
description of seaweeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycology</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*col"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of
algae, or seaweeds; algology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycomater</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy`co*ma"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + L. <ets>mater</ets> mother.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A gelatin in which the algae spores have been
supposed to vegetate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycomycetes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy`co*my*ce"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.;
Gr. <grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + <ets>mycetes</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A large, important class of parasitic or
saprophytic fungi, the algal or algalike fungi. The plant body ranges
from an undifferentiated mass of protoplasm to a well-developed and
much-branched mycelium. Reproduction is mainly sexual, by the formation
of conidia or sporangia; but the group shows every form of transition
from this method through simple conjugation to perfect sexual
reproduction by egg and sperm in the higher forms.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Phy`co*my*ce"tous</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycophaeine</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`co*ph<ae/"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed + (<?/) dusky.]</ety> <def>A brown coloring
matter found in certain alg<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phycoxanthine</ent><br/
<ent>Phycoxanthin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phy`co*xan"thin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phy`co*xan"thine</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fy^kos</grk> seaweed
+ <?/ yellow.]</ety> <def>A yellowish coloring matter found in certain
algae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylacter</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*lac"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
phylactery.</def> <rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylactered</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*lac"tered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing a
phylactery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylacterical</ent><br/
<ent>Phylacteric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phyl`ac*ter"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phyl`ac*ter"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
phylacteries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylactery</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*lac"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phylacteries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>filateri</ets>, OF. <ets>filatire</ets>, <ets>filatiere</ets>, F.
<ets>phylact<egrave/re</ets>, L. <ets>phylacterium</ets>, Gr.
<grk>fylakth`rion</grk>, fr. <grk>fylakth`r</grk> a watcher, guard,
<grk>fyla`ssein</grk> to watch, guard. Cf. <er>Philatory</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Any charm or amulet worn as a preservative from danger
or disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small square box, made either of parchment or of
black calfskin, containing slips of parchment or vellum on which are
written the scriptural passages Exodus xiii. 2-10, and 11-17, Deut. vi.
4-9, 13-22. They are worn by Jews on the head and left arm, on week-day
mornings, during the time of prayer.</def> <rj><au>Schaff-Herzog
Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Among the primitive Christians, a case in which the
relics of the dead were inclosed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylactocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*lac"to*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fyla`ssein</grk> to guard + <grk>karpo`s</grk> fruit.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A branch of a plumularian hydroid specially
modified in structure for the protection of the gonothec<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylactolaemata</ent><br/
<ent>Phylactolaema</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Phy*lac`to*l<ae/"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
\'d8<hw>Phy*lac`to*l<ae/"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fyla`ssein</grk> to guard +
<grk>laimo`s</grk> the gullet.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order
of fresh-water Bryozoa in which the tentacles are arranged on a
horseshoe-shaped lophophore, and the mouth is covered by an epistome.
Called also <altname><ord>Lophopoda</ord></altname>, and
<altname>hippocrepians</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylactolaematous</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*lac`to*l<ae/"ma*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Phylactol<ae/ma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylactolemata</ent><br/
<ent>Phylactolema</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Phy*lac`to*le"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
\'d8<hw>Phy*lac`to*le"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Phylactol<ae/ma</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylarch</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"larch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>phylarchus</ets>, Gr. <?/. See <er>Phyle</er>, and
<er>-arch</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The chief of a
phyle, or tribe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"larch*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fylarchi`a</grk>.]</ety> <def>The office of a phylarch; government
of a class or tribe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyle</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy"le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phyl<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a body of
men united by ties of blood or habitation.]</ety> <def>A local division
of the people in ancient Athens; a clan; a tribe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllite</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Phylo-</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A mineral
related to ottrelite.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Clay slate; argillaceous
schist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllo-</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lo-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>fy`llon</grk> <sig>a leaf</sig>; <as>as, <ex>phyllo</ex>pod,
<ex>phyllo</ex>taxy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllobranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phyl`lo*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Phyllobranci<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Phyllo-</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A crustacean gill composed of
lamellae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phyllocladium</ent><br/
<ent>phylloclade</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>phylloclade</hw>, \'d8<hw>phyl`lo*cla"di*um</hw></mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Phyllocladia</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fy`llon</grk> a leaf +
<grk>kla`dos</grk> a sprout.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A flattened
stem or branch which more or less resembles a leaf, and performs the
function of a leaf as regards respiration and assimilation.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> cladode, cladophyll, phylloclad.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllocladus</ent><br/
<hw>Phyllocladus</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A genus consisting of
celery pine.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Phyllocladus</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllocyanin</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl`lo*cy"a*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + <ets>cyanin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A blue coloring matter extracted from chlorophyll.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>phyllocyanine</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllocyst</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lo*cyst</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + <ets>cyst</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The cavity of a hydrophyllium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllode</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Phyllodium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllodineous</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl`lo*din"eous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having phyllodia; relating to phyllodia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phyl*lo"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Phyllodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ leaflike; <grk>fy`llon</grk> leaf + <grk>e'i^dos</grk>
form.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A petiole dilated into the form of a
blade, and usually with vertical edges, as in the Australian
acacias.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllody</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lo*dy</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*d<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[See <er>Phyllodium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
retrograde metamorphosis of the floral organs to the condition of
leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylloid</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"loid</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"loid)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a
leaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllomania</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl`lo*ma"ni*a</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/l`l<osl/*m<amac/"n<icr/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + <ets>mania</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An abnormal or excessive production of leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phyllome</ent><br/
<hw>phyl"lome</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<omac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy`llwma</grk> foliage, fr. <grk>fy`llon</grk> a leaf.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A foliar part of a plant; any organ homologous
with a leaf, or produced by metamorphosis of a leaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllomorphosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phyl`lo*mor*pho"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Phyllo-</er>, <er>Morphosis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The succession and variation of leaves during different
seasons.</def> <rj><au>R. Brown.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllophagan</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl*loph"a*gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of a group of marsupials
including the phalangists.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of a tribe of
beetles which feed upon the leaves of plants, as the chafers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllophagous</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl*loph"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Substituting on leaves; leaf-eating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllophorous</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl*loph"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Leaf-bearing; producing leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phyllopod</ent><br/
<hw>phyl"lo*pod</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*p<ocr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the <ord>Phyllopoda</ord>. <note>[Also
used adjectively.]</note></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phyl*lop"o*da</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l*l<ocr/p"<osl/*d<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fy`llon</grk> a leaf +
<ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of
Entomostraca including a large number of species, most of which live in
fresh water. They have flattened or leaflike legs, often very numerous,
which they use in swimming. Called also
<altname><ord>Branchiopoda</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In some, the body is covered with a bivalve shell
(<fam>Holostraca</fam>); in others, as Apus, by a shield-shaped carapace
(<fam>Monostraca</fam>); in others, like Artemia, there is no carapace,
and the body is regularly segmented. Sometimes the group is made to
include also the Cladocera.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllopodous</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl*lop"o*dous</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l*l<ocr/p"<osl/*d<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Phyllopoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllorhine</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lo*rhine</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*r<imac/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>"ri`s</grk>, <grk>"rino`s</grk>, the
nose.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
<gen>Phyllorhina</gen> and other related genera of bats that have a
leaflike membrane around the nostrils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllosoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phyl`lo*so"ma</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l`l<osl/*s<omac/"m<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See <er>Phyllo-</er>, and <er>-some</er>
body.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The larva of the spiny lobsters
(<gen>Palinurus</gen> and allied genera). Its body is remarkably thin,
flat, and transparent; the legs are very long. Called also
<altname>glass-crab</altname>, and
<altname>glass-shrimp</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1081 --></p>

<p><ent>Phyllostome</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lo*stome</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*st<omac/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>sto`ma</grk>
mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bat of the genus
<gen>Phyllostoma</gen>, or allied genera, having large membranes around
the mouth and nose; a nose-leaf bat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllostomid</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl*los"to*mid</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l*l<ocr/s"t<omac/*m<icr/d)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A phyllostome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllotactic</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl`lo*tac"tic</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*t<acr/k"t<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
phyllotaxy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllotaxis</ent><br/
<ent>Phyllotaxy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phyl"lo*tax`y</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*t<acr/ks`<ycr/)</pr>,
<hw>Phyl"lo*tax`is</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<osl/*t<acr/ks`<icr/s)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>ta`xis</grk>
order.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The order or arrangement of leaves
on the stem; the science of the relative position of leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl"lous</hw> <pr>(f<icr/l"l<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Homologous with a leaf; <as>as, the sepals,
petals, stamens, and pistils are <ex>phyllous</ex>
organs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylloxanthin</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl`lo*xan"thin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyllo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>xanqo`s</grk> yellow.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A yellow coloring matter extracted from
chlorophyll.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylloxera</ent><br/
<hw>Phyl`lox*e"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fy`llon</grk> leaf + <grk>xhro`s</grk> dry.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small hemipterous insect (<spn>Phylloxera
vastatrix</spn>) allied to the aphids. It attacks the roots and leaves
of the grapevine, doing great damage, especially in Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It exists in several forms, some of which are winged,
other wingless. One form produces galls on the leaves and twigs, another
affects the roots, causing galls or swellings, and often killing the
vine.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The diseased condition of a vine caused by the
insect just described.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylloxeridae</ent><br/
<hw>Phylloxeridae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family
consisting of the plant lice.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Phylloxeridae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>phylogeny</ent><br/
<ent>phylogenesis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>phy`lo*gen"e*sis</hw>
<pr>(f<imac/`l<osl/*j<ecr/n;<esl/*s<icr/s)</pr>, <hw>phy*log"e*ny</hw>
<pr>(f<imac/*l<ocr/j"<esl/*n<ycr/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^lon</grk> race, tribe + E. <ets>genesis</ets>, or root of Gr.
<grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born.]</ety> <def>The history of
genealogical development; the sequence of events involved in the
evolutionary development of a species or taxonomic group of organisms;
the historic exolution of the <xex>phylon</xex> or tribe, in distinction
from <contr>ontogeny</contr>, or the development of the individual
organism, and from <xex>biogenesis</xex>, or life development
generally.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> evolution.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylogenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*lo*ge*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to
phylogenesis, or the evolutionary history of a type of organism.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Phy*lo*ge*net"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phyla</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fy^lon</grk> race, tribe.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
tribe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phylum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phyla</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Phylon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
larger divisions of the animal kingdom; a branch; a grand
division.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A series of animals or plants
genetically connected.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phymata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fy^ma</grk>, fr. <grk>fy`ein</grk> to produce.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A tubercle on any external part of the
body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fy^sa</grk> a bellows.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
fresh-water Pulmonifera, having reversed spiral shells. See <cref>Pond
snail</cref>, under <er>Pond</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physalia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*sa"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>fysalli`s</grk> a bladder, fr. <grk>fy^sa</grk> a
bellows.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of large oceanic
Siphonophora which includes the <member>Portuguese
man-of-war</member>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It has a large air sac, or float, with a sail-like crest
on its upper side. Numerous zooids of different kinds are attached to
the under side of the float. Some of the zooids have very long
tentacles; some have a mouth and digest food; others produce gonophores.
The American species (<spn>Physalia arethusa</spn>) is brilliantly
colored, the float being pink or purple, and bright blue; the zooids
blue. It is noted for its virulent stinging powers, as well as for its
beautiful colors, graceful motions, and its ability to sail to
windward.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physali<ae/</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*sa"li*<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of Siphonophora which
includes <gen>Physalia</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physaria</ent><br/
<hw>Physaria</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A small genus of Western
North American herbs similar to <gen>Lesquerella</gen>; the
bladderpods.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> genus <gen>Physaria</gen>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physemaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phys`e*ma"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>fy^shma</grk> a blowing.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
group of simple marine organisms, usually classed as the lowest of the
sponges. They have inflated hollow bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physeter</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*se"ter</hw> <pr>(f<imac/*s<emac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>fyshth`r</grk>, fr. <grk>fysa^n</grk> to blow:
cf. F. <ets>phys<eacute/t<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety>
<mark>[capitalized]</mark> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The genus
that includes the sperm whale (<spn>Physeter
macrocephalus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A filtering machine operated by air
pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><hw>Physeteridae</hw> <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>A natural family of
<isa>marine mammals</isa> that includes the sperm whales.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> family <fam>Physeteridae</fam>.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physianthropy</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*an"thro*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <grk>'a`nqrwpos</grk> man.]</ety> <def>The
philosophy of human life, or the doctrine of the constitution and
diseases of man, and their remedies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><hw>physiatrics</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Therapy that use physical
agents: exercise and massage and so on.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> physical therapy, physiotherapy.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physic</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"ic</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>phisike</ets>, <ets>fisike</ets>, OF. <ets>phisique</ets>, F.
<ets>physique</ets> knowledge of nature, physics, L. <ets>physica</ets>,
<ets>physice</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>fysikh`</grk>, fr. <grk>fysiko`s</grk>
natural, from <grk>fy`sis</grk> nature, fr. <grk>fy`ein</grk> to
produce, grow, akin to E. <ets>be</ets>. See <er>Be</er>, and cf.
<er>Physics</er>, <er>Physique</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art of
healing diseases; the science of medicine; the theory or practice of
medicine; -- an archaic term, superseded by
<altname>medicine</altname>.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark> <ldquo/A
doctor of <xex>physik</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A specific internal application for the cure or
relief of sickness; a remedy for disease; a medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Specifically, a medicine that purges; a
cathartic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A physician.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Physic nut</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small
tropical American euphorbiaceous tree (<spn>Jatropha Curcas</spn>), and
its seeds, which are well flavored, but contain a drastic oil which
renders them dangerous if eaten in large quantities.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physic</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"ic</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Physiced</conjf>
<pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Physicking</conjf> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/k*<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To treat with physic or medicine; to administer
medicine to, esp. a cathartic; to operate on as a cathartic; to
purge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To work on as a remedy; to heal; to cure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The labor we delight in <qex>physics</qex> pain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A mind diseased no remedy can <qex>physic</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physical</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"ic*al</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to nature (as including all created
existences); in accordance with the laws of nature; also, of or relating
to natural or material things, or to the bodily structure, as opposed to
things mental, moral, spiritual, or imaginary; material; natural;
<as>as, armies and navies are the <ex>physical</ex> force of a nation;
the body is the <ex>physical</ex> part of man.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Labor, in the <qex>physical</qex> world, is . . . employed in
putting objects in motion.</q> <rj><qau>J. S. Mill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A society sunk in ignorance, and ruled by mere <qex>physical</qex>
force.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to physics, or natural philosophy;
treating of, or relating to, the causes and connections of natural
phenomena; <as>as, <ex>physical</ex> science; <ex>physical</ex>
laws.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Physical</xex> philosophy.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Perceptible through a bodily or material
organization; cognizable by the senses; external; <as>as, the
<ex>physical</ex>, opposed to <ex>chemical</ex>, characters of a
mineral</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to physic, or the art of medicine;
medicinal; curative; healing; also, cathartic; purgative.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Physical</xex> herbs.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir
T. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is Brutus sick? and is it <qex>physical</qex><br/
To walk unbraced, and suck up the humors<br/
Of the dank morning?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Physical astronomy</b></col>, <cd>that part of astronomy
which treats of the causes of the celestial motions; specifically, that
which treats of the motions resulting from universal gravitation.</cd>
-- <col><b>Physical education</b></col>, <cd>training of the bodily
organs and powers with a view to the promotion of health and vigor.</cd>
-- <col><b>Physical examination</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>an
examination of the bodily condition of a person.</cd> --
<col><b>Physical geography</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Geography</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Physical point</b></col>, <cd>an
indefinitely small portion of matter; a point conceived as being without
extension, yet having physical properties, as weight, inertia, momentum,
etc.; a material point.</cd> -- <col><b>Physical signs</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>the objective signs of the bodily state afforded
by a physical examination.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>physicalism</ent><br/
<hw>physicalism</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Philosophy)</fld> <def>The
doctrine that matter is the only reality.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> materialism.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>physicality</ent><br/
<hw>physicality</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A preoccupation with
satisfaction of physical drives and appetites; -- of people.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> animalism.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physically</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a physical manner;
according to the laws of nature or physics; by physical force; not
morally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am not now treating <qex>physically</qex> of light or
colors.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>According to the rules of medicine.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that lives <qex>physically</qex> must live miserably.</q>
<rj><qau>Cheyne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>physicalness</ent> <hw>physicalness</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality of being physical; consisting of matter; materiality.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> materiality, corporeality.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physician</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*si"cian</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*z<icr/sh"<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>fisician</ets>, <ets>fisicien</ets>, OF.
<ets>physucien</ets>, a physician, in F., a natural philosopher, an
experimentalist in physics. See <er>Physic</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A person skilled in medicine, or the art of healing; especially,
one trained and licensed to treat illness and prescribe medicines; a
doctor of medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively, one who ministers to moral
diseases; <as>as, a <ex>physician</ex> of the soul</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicianed</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*si"cianed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Licensed as a
physician.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>physicianed</xex>
apothecary.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicism</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"i*cism</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/*s<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The tendency of the mind toward, or its preoccupation with,
physical phenomena; materialism in philosophy and religion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Anthropomorphism grows into theology, while <qex>physicism</qex>
(if I may so call it) develops into science.</q>
<rj><qau>Huxley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicist</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"i*cist</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/*s<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One versed in physics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A believer in the theory that the
fundamental phenomena of life are to be explained upon purely chemical
and physical principles; -- opposed to <xex>vitalist</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicking</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"ick*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> fr.
<er>Physic</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physico-</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"i*co-</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/*k<osl/-)</pr>. <ety>[Fr. Gr.
<grk>fysiko`s</grk> natural, physical.]</ety> <def>A combining form,
denoting <xex>relation to</xex>, or <xex>dependence upon</xex>,
<xex>natural causes</xex>, or <xex>the science of
physics</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicochemical</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*co*chem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Physico-</ets> + <ets>chemical</ets>.]</ety> <def>Involving
the principles of both physics and chemistry; dependent on, or produced
by, the joint action of physical and chemical agencies.</def>
<rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicologic</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*co*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Physico-</ets> + <ets>logic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Logic
illustrated by physics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicological</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*co*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to physicologic.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physicology</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*col"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Physico-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Physics.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> -- <wordforms><wf>Phys`i*col"o*gist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physico-mathematics</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*co-math`e*mat"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Physico-</ets> + <ets>mathematics</ets>.]</ety> <def>Mixed
mathematics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physico-philosophy</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*co-phi*los"o*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Physico-</ets> + <ets>philosophy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
philosophy of nature.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physico-theology</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*co-the*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Physico-</ets> + <ets>theology</ets>.]</ety> <def>Theology or
divinity illustrated or enforced by physics or natural
philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physics</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Physic</er>.]</ety> <def>The science of nature, or of natural
objects; that branch of science which treats of the laws and properties
of matter, and the forces acting upon it; especially, that department of
natural science which treats of the causes (as gravitation, heat, light,
magnetism, electricity, etc.) that modify the general properties of
bodies; natural philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Chemistry</xex>, though a branch of <xex>general
physics</xex>, is commonly treated as a science by itself, and the
application of physical principles which it involves constitute a branch
called <xex>chemical physics</xex>, which treats more especially of
those physical properties of matter which are used by chemists in
defining and distinguishing substances.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiocrat</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"i*o*crat</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z"<icr/*<osl/*kr<acr/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <grk>kratei^n</grk>
to rule.]</ety> <def>One of the followers of Quesnay of France, who, in
the 18th century, founded a system of political economy based upon the
supremacy of natural order.</def> <au>F. A. Walker.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Phys`i*o*crat"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiogeny</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"e*ny</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/j"<esl/*n<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + root of
<grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The
germ history of the functions, or the history of the development of
vital activities, in the individual, being one of the branches of
ontogeny. See <er>Morphogeny</er>.</def> <rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognomer</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"no*mer</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/g"n<osl/*m<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Physiognomist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognomical</ent><br/
<ent>Physiognomic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phys`i*og*nom"ic</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/g*n<ocr/m"<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Phys`i*og*nom"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/g*n<ocr/m"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>fysiognwmoniko`s</grk>: cf. F.
<ets>physiognomonique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
physiognomy; according with the principles of physiognomy.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Phys`i*og*nom"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognomist</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og*nom"ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Physiognomy</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognomist</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"no*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>physiognomiste</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One skilled in
physiognomy.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who tells fortunes by physiognomy.</def>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognomize</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"no*mize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
observe and study the physiognomy of.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognommonic</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og`no*mmon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Physiognomic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiognomy</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"no*my</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/g"n<ocr/*m<ycr/;
277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Physiognomies</plw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/g"n<ocr/*m<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>fisonomie</ets>, <ets>phisonomie</ets>, <ets>fisnamie</ets>, OF.
<ets>phisonomie</ets>, F. <ets>physiognomie</ets>,
<ets>physiognomonie</ets>, from Gr. <grk>fysiognwmoni`a</grk>;
<grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <grk>gnw`mwn</grk> one who knows or examines,
a judge, fr. <grk>gnw^mai</grk>, <grk>gignw`skein</grk>, to know. See
<er>Physic</er>, and <er>Know</er>, and cf. <er>Phiz</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The art and science of discovering the predominant
temper, and other characteristic qualities of the mind, by the outward
appearance, especially by the features of the face.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The face or countenance, especially viewed as an
indication of the temper of the mind; particular configuration, cast, or
expression of countenance, as denoting character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The art telling fortunes by inspection of the
features.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The general appearance or aspect of a thing, without
reference to its scientific characteristics; <as>as, the
<ex>physiognomy</ex> of a plant, or of a meteor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiogony</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <grk>go`nos</grk> birth.]</ety> <def>The
birth of nature.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiographical</ent><br/
<ent>Physiographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phys`i*o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Phys`i*o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>physiographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
physiography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiography</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*og"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <ets>-graphy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>physiographie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The science which
treats of the earth's exterior physical features, climate, life, etc.,
and of the physical movements or changes on the earth's surface, as the
currents of the atmosphere and ocean, the secular variations in heat,
moisture, magnetism, etc.; physical geography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The descriptive part of a natural science as
distinguished from the explanatory or theoretic part; <as>as, mineral
<ex>physiography</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiolatry</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*ol"a*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <?/ service.]</ety> <def>The worship of the
powers or agencies of nature; materialism in religion; nature
worship.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>physiolatry</xex> of the Vedas.<rdquo/
<rj><au>M. Williams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiologer</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*ol"o*ger</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A physiologist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiologic</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*o*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>physiologicus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>physiologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Physiological.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiological</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to physiology; relating to the science of the functions of
living organism; <as>as, <ex>physiological</ex> botany or
chemistry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiologically</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*o*log"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a physiological
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*ol"o*gist</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>physiologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who is versed in the
science of physiology; a student of the properties and functions of
animal and vegetable organs and tissues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiologize</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*ol"o*gize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
speculate in physiology; to make physiological investigations.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>physiology</ent><br/
<hw>phys`i*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Physiologies</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>physiologia</ets>, Gr.
<grk>fysiologi`a</grk>; <grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <grk>lo`gos</grk>
discourse: cf. F. <ets>physiologie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
science which treats of the phenomena of living organisms; the study of
the processes incidental to, and characteristic of, life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is divided into <xex>animal</xex> and <xex>vegetable
physiology</xex>, dealing with animal and vegetable life respectively.
When applied especially to a study of the functions of the organs and
tissues in man, it is called <xex>human physiology</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A treatise on physiology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Mental physiology</b></col>, <cd>the science of the
functions and phenomena of the mind, as distinguished from a
philosophical explanation of the same.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physiophyly</ent><br/
<hw>Phys`i*oph"y*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy`sis</grk> nature + <?/ a clan.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>The tribal history of the functions, or the history of the
paleontological development of vital activities, -- being a branch of
<branchof>phylogeny</branchof>. See <er>Morphophyly</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>physiotherapy</ent><br/
<hw>phys`i*o*ther"a*py</hw>
<pr>(f<icr/z`<icr/*<osl/*th<etil/r"<adot/*p<ycr/)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Therapy that uses physical agents: exercise and massage and so
on.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> physical therapy, physiatrics.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physique</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*sique"</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*z<emac/k")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
See <er>Physic</er>.]</ety> <def>The natural constitution, or physical
structure, of a person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With his white hair and splendid <qex>physique</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. Stowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physnomy</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"no*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Physiogmony.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physoclist</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"o*clist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
Physoclisti.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physoclisti</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phys`o*clis"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fy^sa</grk> a bellows + <?/ to close.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of teleost in which the air bladder has
no opening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physograde</ent><br/
<hw>Phys"o*grade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>fy^sa</grk> a bellows + L. <ets>gradi</ets> to walk, go.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any siphonophore which has an air sac for a
float, as the Physalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physophorae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*soph"o*r<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fy^sa</grk> a bellows + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to
bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of Siphonophora, furnished
with an air sac, or float, and a series of nectocalyces. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Nectocalyx</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physopod</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"so*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Physopoda; a thrips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*sop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>fy^sa</grk> a bellows + <ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Thysanoptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physostigmine</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`so*stig"mine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An alkaloid found in the Calabar bean (the seed of <spn>Physostigma
venenosum</spn>), and extracted as a white, tasteless, substance,
amorphous or crystalline; -- formerly called <altname>eserine</altname>,
with which it was regarded as identical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Physostomi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*sos"to*mi</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*s<ocr/s"t<osl/*m<imac/)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fy^sa</grk> a bellows +
<grk>sto`ma</grk> mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of
fishes in which the air bladder is provided with a duct, and the ventral
fins, when present, are abdominal. It includes the salmons, herrings,
carps, catfishes, and others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1082 --></p>

<p><ent>Physostomous</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*sos"to*mous</hw> <pr>(f<icr/*s<ocr/s"t<osl/*m<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having a duct to the
air bladder.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Pertaining to the
<ord>Physostomi</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytelephas</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*tel"e*phas</hw> <pr>(f<imac/*t<ecr/l"<esl/*f<acr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> a plant +
<grk>ele`fos</grk> the elephant; also, ivory.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A genus of South American palm trees, the seeds of which furnish
the substance called <prod>vegetable ivory</prod>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytivorous</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tiv"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + L. <ets>vorare</ets> to eat greedily.]</ety>
<def>Feeding on plants or herbage; phytophagous; <as>as,
<ex>phytivorous</ex> animals</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyto-</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Physic</er>.]</ety> <def>A
combining form from Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> <xlati>a plant</xlati>;
<as>as, <ex>phyto</ex>chemistry, <ex>phyto</ex>graphy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytochemical</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*chem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to
phytochemistry.</def> <rj><au>R. Hunt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytochemistry</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"to*chem"is*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>chemistry</ets>.]</ety> <def>Chemistry in
its relation to vegetable bodies; vegetable chemistry.</def> <rj><au>R.
Hunt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytochimy</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*toch"i*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>phytochimie</ets>; Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> a plant + F.
<ets>chimie</ets> chemistry.]</ety> <def>Phytochemistry.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytogeny</ent><br/
<ent>Phytogenesis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phy`to*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phy*tog"e*ny</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> +
<ets>genesis</ets>, or root of Gr. <grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be
born.]</ety> <def>The doctrine of the generation of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytogeographical</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*ge"o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to phytogeography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytogeography</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*ge*og"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>geography</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
geographical distribution of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytoglyphic</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*glyph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to
phytoglyphy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytoglyphy</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tog"ly*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + Gr. <grk>gly`fein</grk> to engrave.]</ety>
<def>See <cref>Nature printing</cref>, under <er>Nature</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytographical</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phytographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
phytography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytography</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>phytographie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of describing plants in
a systematic manner; also, a description of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytoid</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a plant; plantlike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytolacca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy`to*lac"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> plant + It. <ets>lacca</ets> lac.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of herbaceous plants, some of them having
berries which abound in intensely red juice; poke, or
pokeweed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytolite</ent><br/
<hw>Phy"to*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets>
+ <ets>-lite</ets>: cf. F. <ets>phytolithe</ets>.]</ety> <def>An old
name for a fossil plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytolithologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*li*thol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
versed in phytolithology; a paleobotanist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytolithology</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*li*thol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>lithology</ets>.]</ety> <def>The branch
of science which treats of fossil plants; -- usually called
<altname>paleobotany</altname>, sometimes
<altname>paleophytology</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytological</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>phytologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to phytology;
botanical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(f<imac/*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in phytology; a writer on plants; a
botanist.</def> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytology</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(f<imac/*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>phytologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of plants; a description
of the kinds and properties of plants; botany.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytomeron</ent><br/
<ent>Phytomer</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Phy"to*mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Phy*tom"e*ron</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>phytomeron</ets>, fr.
Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> plant + <grk>me`ros</grk> share.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An organic element of a flowering plant; a
phyton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyton</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Phytons</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>fyto`n</grk> plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the parts
which by their repetition make up a flowering plant, each being a single
joint of a stem with its leaf or leaves; a phytomer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytonomy</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*ton"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + Gr. <?/ law: cf. F.
<ets>phytonomie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of the origin and growth
of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytopathologist</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*pa*thol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
skilled in diseases of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytopathology</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*pa*thol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>pathology</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science
of diseases to which plants are liable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytophaga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy*toph"a*ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> a plant + <grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Hymenoptera; the
sawflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytophagic</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*phag"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Phytophagous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytophagous</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*toph"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Feeding on plants; herbivorous; <as>as, a
<ex>phytophagous</ex> animal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytophagy</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*toph"a*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The eating of
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytophysiology</ent><br/
<hw>Phy`to*phys`i*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + <ets>physiology</ets>.]</ety> <def>Vegetable
physiology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytotomist</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tot"o*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
phytotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Phy*tot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Phyto-</ets> + Gr. <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut.]</ety>
<def>The dissection of plants; vegetable anatomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytozoaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy`to*zo*a"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Phytozoon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Infusoria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phytozoon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Phy`to*zo"<oum/n</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Phytozoa</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> + <grk>zw^,on</grk> an animal.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A plantlike animal. The term is sometimes
applied to zoophytes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Phyz</ent><br/
<hw>Phyz</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Phiz</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>P<sub>i</sub></ent><br/
<hw>P<sub>i</sub></hw> <pr>(p<emac/` <imac/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The inorganic orthophoshate ion; -- a symbol
used in biochemistry.</def> <mark>[acronym]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pi</ent><br/
<hw>Pi</hw> <pr>(p<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pica</er>,
<er>Pie</er> magpie, service-book.]</ety> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A
mass of type confusedly mixed or unsorted.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pie</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pi</ent><br/
<hw>Pi</hw> <pr>(p<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pi^</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A Greek letter (<PI/, <pi/)
corresponding to the Roman letter <er>P</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>The letter <pi/, <PI/, as used to denote the number or quotient
approximately expressing the ratio of the circumference of a circle to
its diameter; also, the quotient or the ratio itself. The value of the
quotient <ex>pi</ex>, to twenty decimal places, is
3.14159265358979323846 (see note). The number <ex>pi</ex> is an
irrational number, i.e. it cannot be expressed as the quotient of two
integers. It is also a <isa>transcendental number</isa>, i.e. it cannot
be expressed as a root of an algebraic equation with a finite number of
terms; and from this fact follows the impossibility of the quadrature of
the circle by purely algebraic processes, or by the aid of a ruler and
compass.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

