<p><ent>Pi</ent><br/
<hw>Pi</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pieing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Print.)</fld>
<def>To put into a mixed and disordered condition, as type; to mix and
disarrange the type of; <as>as, to <ex>pi</ex> a form</as>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pie</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piacaba</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*a<cced/"a*ba</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Piassava</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piacle</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"a*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piaculum</ets> a propitiatory sacrifice, that which requires
expiation, a wicked action, fr. <ets>piare</ets> to appease, to expiate,
<ets>pius</ets> pious.]</ety> <def>A heinous offense which requires
expiation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piacular</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*ac"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piacularis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>piaculaire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Expiatory; atoning.</def> <rj><au>Sir G. C. Lewis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Requiring expiation; criminal; atrociously
bad.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Piacular</xex> pollution.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piacularity</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*ac`u*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being piacular; criminality; wickedness.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piaculous</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*ac"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Piacular</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pial</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the <er>pia mater</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pia mater</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"a ma"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>pia</ets>
(fem. of <ets>pius</ets> tender, kind) + <ets>mater</ets> mother.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The delicate and highly vascular membrane
immediately investing the brain and spinal cord.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pian</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pg. <ets>pian</ets>,
<ets>epian</ets>, or. Sp. <ets>pian</ets>; from the native name in South
America: cf. F. <ets>pian</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The yaws.
See <er>Yaws</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianet</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"a*net`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pie</er>
magpie.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The magpie.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pianate</asp>, and
<asp>pyenate</asp>.]</altsp> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The lesser
woodpecker.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianette</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*a*nette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>piano</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A small piano; a
pianino.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianino</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`a*ni"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., dim. of
<ets>piano</ets>, adj. See <er>Piano</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>A pianette, or small piano.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianissimo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`a*nis"si*mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.,
superl. of <ets>piano</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Very soft; --
a direction to execute a passage as softly as possible. (Abbrev.
<abbr>pp.</abbr>)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianist</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*an"ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pianiste</ets>, It. <ets>pianista</ets>.]</ety> <def>A performer,
esp. a skilled performer, on the piano.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><hw>pianistic</hw> <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
characteristic of a pianist; <as>as, <ex>pianistic</ex>
abilities</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piano</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*a"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <ety>[It., even,
smooth, soft, fr. L. <ets>planus</ets> even, level.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Soft; -- a direction to the performer to execute
a certain passage softly, and with diminished volume of tone. (Abbrev.
<abbr>p.</abbr>)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piano</ent><br/
<ent>Pianoforte</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pi*an"o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pi*an"o*for`te</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>piano</ets> soft (fr.
L. <ets>planus</ets> even, smooth; see <er>Plain</er>, <pos>a.</pos>) +
It. <ets>forte</ets> strong, fr. L. <ets>fortis</ets> (see
<er>Fort</er>).]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A well-known musical
instrument somewhat resembling the harpsichord, and consisting of a
series of wires of graduated length, thickness, and tension, struck by
hammers moved by keys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dumb piano</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Digitorium</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Grand piano</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Grand</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Square piano</b></col>, <cd>one with a horizontal frame and an
oblong case.</cd> -- <col><b>Upright piano</b></col>, <cd>one with an
upright frame and vertical wires.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*an"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Piano</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>A form of melodiograph applied to a piano.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pianola</ent><br/
<hw>Pianola</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mechanically operated piano that
uses a roll of perforated paper to activate the keys.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> mechanical piano, player piano.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piapec</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"a*pec</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pie</er> a
magpie.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A West African pie
(<spn>Ptilostomus Senegalensis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piarist</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"a*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pius</ets>
pious.]</ety> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>One of a religious order who
are the regular clerks of the <xex>Scuole Pie</xex> (religious schools),
an institute of secondary education, founded at Rome in the last years
of the 16th century.</def> <rj><au>Addis & Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piassava</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*as"sa*va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pg.
<ets>piasaba</ets>.]</ety> <def>A fibrous product of two Brazilian palm
trees (<spn>Attalea funifera</spn> and <spn>Leopoldinia Piassaba</spn>),
-- used in making brooms, and for other purposes. Called also
<altname>pia<cced/aba</altname> and
<altname>piasaba</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piaster</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*as"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>piastre</ets>, It. <ets>piastra</ets> a thin plate of metal, a
dollar, LL. <ets>piastra</ets>, fr. L. <ets>emplastrum</ets>. See
<er>Plaster</er>.]</ety> <def>A silver coin of Spain and various other
countries. See <er>Peso</er>. The Spanish piaster (commonly called
<xex>peso</xex>, or <xex>peso duro</xex>) is of about the value of the
American dollar. The Italian piaster, or scudo, was worth from 80 to 100
cents. The Turkish and Egyptian piasters are now worth about four and a
half cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piastre</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*as"tre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Piaster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piation</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piatio</ets>. See <er>Piacle</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of making
atonement; expiation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piatti</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*at"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[It., prop.,
plates.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Cymbals.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>pyatti</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piazza</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*az"za</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Piazzas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It., place, square, market
place, L. <ets>platea</ets> street, courtyard. See
<er>Place</er>.]</ety> <def>An open square in a European town,
especially an Italian town; hence <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, an arcaded and
roofed gallery; a portico. In the United States the word is popularly
applied to a veranda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We walk by the obelisk, and meditate in <qex>piazzas</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pibcorn</ent><br/
<hw>Pib"corn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[W. <ets>pib</ets>
pipe + <ets>corn</ets> horn.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A wind
instrument or pipe, with a horn at each end, -- used in Wales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pibroch</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"broch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>piobaireachd</ets> pipe music, fr. <ets>piobair</ets> a piper, fr.
<ets>pioba</ets> pipe, bagpipe, from English. See <er>Pipe</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A Highland air, suited to the particular
passion which the musician would either excite or assuage; generally
applied to those airs that are played on the bagpipe before the
Highlanders when they go out to battle.</def>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pic</ent><br/
<hw>Pic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pic</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Turkish cloth measure, varying from 18 to
28 inches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pica</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pica</ets> a
pie, magpie; in sense 3 prob. named from some resemblance to the colors
of the magpie. Cf. <er>Pie</er> magpie.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The genus that includes the magpies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A vitiated appetite that craves
what is unfit for food, such as clay, chalk, ashes, coal, etc.;
chthonophagia.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> allotriophagy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A service-book. See
<er>Pie</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A size of type next larger than
small pica, and smaller than English.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <picatype>This line is printed in
<it>pica</it></picatype></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pica</xex> is twice the size of nonpareil, and is
used as a standard of measurement in casting leads, cutting rules, etc.,
and also as a standard by which to designate several larger kinds of
type, as <xex>double pica</xex>, <xex>two-line pica</xex>,
<xex>four-line pica</xex>, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Small pica</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a size of
type next larger than long primer, and smaller than pica.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <smpicatype>This line is printed in <it>small
pica</it></smpicatype></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picador</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pic`a*dor"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>A horseman armed with a lance, who in a bullfight receives the
first attack of the bull, and excites him by picking him without
attempting to kill him.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picamar</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"a*mar`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pix</ets>,
<ets>picis</ets>, pitch + <ets>amarus</ets> bitter.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An oily liquid hydrocarbon extracted from the
creosote of beechwood tar. It consists essentially of certain
derivatives of pyrogallol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picapare</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"a*pare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The finfoot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picard</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld>
<def>One of a sect of Adamites in the fifteenth century; -- so called
from one <xex>Picard</xex> of Flanders. See <er>Adamite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picaresque</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`a*resque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. Sp.
<ets>picaro</ets> rogue.]</ety> <def>Applied to that class of literature
in which the principal personage is the Spanish <xex>picaro</xex>,
meaning a rascal, a knave, a rogue, an adventurer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picari<ae/</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*ca"ri*<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>picus</ets> a woodpecker.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
extensive division of birds which includes the woodpeckers, toucans,
trogons, hornbills, kingfishers, motmots, rollers, and goatsuckers. By
some writers it is made to include also the cuckoos, swifts, and humming
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*ca"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to Picari<ae/.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>One of the Picari<ae/.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picaroon</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`a*roon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>picaron</ets>, aug. of <ets>picaro</ets> roguish, n., a
rogue.]</ety> <def>One who plunders; especially, a plunderer of wrecks;
a pirate; a corsair; a marauder; a sharper.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picayune</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`a*yune"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the language
of the Caribs.]</ety> <def>A small coin of the value of six and a
quarter cents. See <er>Fippenny bit</er>.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- adj. picayunish; small-minded --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picayunish</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`a*yun"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Petty; paltry;
mean; <as>as, a <ex>picayunish</ex> business</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piccadilly</ent><br/
<ent>Piccadil</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pic"ca*dil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pic`ca*dil"ly</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>piccagilles</ets> the
several divisions of pieces fastened together about the brim of the
collar of a doublet, a dim. fr. Sp. <ets>picado</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>picar</ets> to prick. See <er>Pike</er>.]</ety> <def>A high, stiff
collar for the neck; also, a hem or band about the skirt of a garment,
-- worn by men in the 17th century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piccage</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"cage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>piccadium</ets>, fr. F. <ets>piquer</ets> to prick.]</ety> <fld>(O.
Eng. Law)</fld> <def>Money paid at fairs for leave to break ground for
booths.</def> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piccalilli</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ca*lil`li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pickle of
various vegetables with pungent species, -- originally made in the East
Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piccolo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pic"co*lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.,
small.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A small, shrill flute,
the pitch of which is an octave higher than the ordinary flute; an
octave flute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A small upright piano.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An organ stop, with a high,
piercing tone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pice</ent><br/
<hw>Pice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>pais<amac/</ets>]</ety> <def>A small copper coin of the East
Indies, worth less than a cent.</def> <rj><au>Malcom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pic"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the pitch
pine, from <ets>pix</ets>, <ets>picis</ets>, pitch.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of coniferous trees of the northern
hemisphere, including the Norway spruce and the American black and white
spruces. These trees have pendent cones, which do not readily fall to
pieces, in this and other respects differing from the firs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picene</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Piceous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hydrocarbon
(C<?/H<?/) extracted from the pitchy residue of coal tar and petroleum
as a bluish fluorescent crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"e*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piceus</ets>, fr. <ets>pix</ets>, <ets>picis</ets>, pitch.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to pitch; resembling pitch in color or quality;
pitchy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pichey</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"chey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Native name.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A Brazilian armadillo (<spn>Dasypus
minutus</spn>); the little armadillo.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pichiy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pichiciago</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`chi*ci*a"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Native
name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small, burrowing, South American
edentate (<spn>Chlamyphorus truncatus</spn>), allied to the armadillos.
The shell is attached only along the back.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pichyciego</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pichurim bean</ent><br/
<hw>Pich"u*rim bean`</hw> <pr>(p<icr/ch"<usl/*r<icr/m b<emac/n`)</pr>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The seed of a Brazilian lauraceous tree
(<spn>Nectandra Puchury</spn>) of a taste and smell between those of
nutmeg and of sassafras, -- sometimes used medicinally. Called also
<altname>sassafras nut</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pici</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"ci</hw> <pr>(p<imac/"s<imac/)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>picus</ets> a woodpecker.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of birds including the woodpeckers
and wrynecks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piciform</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ci*form</hw> <pr>(p<imac/"s<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Piciformes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piciformes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pic`i*for"mes</hw> <pr>(p<icr/s`<icr/*f<ocir/r"m<emac/z)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See <er>Picus</er>, and
<er>-form</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of birds
including the woodpeckers, toucans, barbets, colies, kingfishes,
hornbills, and some other related groups.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picine</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the woodpeckers (<ord>Pici</ord>), or to the
Piciformes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pick</ent><br/
<hw>Pick</hw> <pr>(p<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Picked</conjf> <pr>(p<icr/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Picking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>picken</ets>,
<ets>pikken</ets>, to prick, peck; akin to Icel. <ets>pikka</ets>, Sw.
<ets>picka</ets>, Dan. <ets>pikke</ets>, D. <ets>pikken</ets>, G.
<ets>picken</ets>, F. <ets>piquer</ets>, W. <ets>pigo</ets>. Cf.
<er>Peck</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, <er>Pike</er>, <er>Pitch</er> to
throw.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw; to pitch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As high as I could <qex>pick</qex> my lance.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To peck at, as a bird with its beak; to strike at
with anything pointed; to act upon with a pointed instrument; to pierce;
to prick, as with a pin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To separate or open by means of a sharp point or
points; <as>as, to <ex>pick</ex> matted wool, cotton, oakum,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To open (a lock) as by a wire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To pull apart or away, especially with the fingers;
to pluck; to gather, as fruit from a tree, flowers from the stalk,
feathers from a fowl, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To remove something from with a pointed instrument,
with the fingers, or with the teeth; <as>as, to <ex>pick</ex> the teeth;
to <ex>pick</ex> a bone; to <ex>pick</ex> a goose; to <ex>pick</ex> a
pocket.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Did you <qex>pick</qex> Master Slender's purse?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>picks</qex> clean teeth, and, busy as he seems<br/
With an old tavern quill, is hungry yet.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To choose; to select; to separate as choice or
desirable; to cull; <as>as, to <ex>pick</ex> one's company; to
<ex>pick</ex> one's way</as>; -- often with <xex>out</xex>.</def>
<ldquo/One man <xex>picked</xex> out of ten thousand.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To take up; esp., to gather from here and there; to
collect; to bring together; <as>as, to <ex>pick</ex> rags</as>; -- often
with <xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>pick</ex> up a ball or stones; to
<ex>pick</ex> up information.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To trim.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pick at</b></col>, <cd>to tease or vex by pertinacious
annoyance.</cd> -- <col><b>To pick a bone with</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Bone</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To pick a thank</b></col>, <cd>to curry
favor.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Robynson (More's Utopia).</au> --
<col><b>To pick off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To pluck; to remove by
picking</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To shoot or bring down, one by one;
<as>as, sharpshooters <ex>pick off</ex> the enemy</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To pick out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To mark out; to
variegate; <as>as, <ex>to pick out</ex> any dark stuff with lines or
spots of bright colors</as></cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To select from a
number or quantity.</cd> -- <col><b>To pick to pieces</b></col>, <cd>to
pull apart piece by piece; hence <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>, to analyze;
esp., to criticize in detail.</cd> -- <col><b>To pick a
quarrel</b></col>, <cd>to give occasion of quarrel intentionally.</cd>
-- <col><b>To pick up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To take up, as with
the fingers</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To get by repeated efforts; to gather
here and there; <as>as, <ex>to pick up</ex> a livelihood; <ex>to pick
up</ex> news</as>.</cd><-- <sd>(c)</sd> to acquire (an infectious
disease); as, to <ex>pick up</ex> a cold on the airplane. (d) To meet (a
person) and induce to accompany one; as, to pick up a date at the mall.
[See several other defs in MW10] --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1083 --></p>

<p><ent>Pick</ent><br/
<hw>Pick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To eat
slowly, sparingly, or by morsels; to nibble.</def><-- = to pick at
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why stand'st thou <qex>picking</qex>? Is thy palate sore?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To do anything nicely or carefully, or by attending
to small things; to select something with care.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To steal; to pilfer.</def> <ldquo/To keep my hands
from <xex>picking</xex> and stealing.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Book of Com.
Prayer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pick up</b></col>, <cd>to improve by degrees; as, he
is <xex>picking up</xex> in health or business.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><-- or, to increase gradually, as the car picked up speed
rolling downhill --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pick</ent><br/
<hw>Pick</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pic</ets> a pickax, a pick.
See <er>Pick</er>, and cf. <er>Pike</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
sharp-pointed tool for picking; -- often used in composition; <as>as, a
tooth<ex>pick</ex>; a <ex>pick</ex>lock.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining & Mech.)</fld> <def>A heavy iron tool,
curved and sometimes pointed at both ends, wielded by means of a wooden
handle inserted in the middle, -- used for digging into the ground by
quarrymen, roadmakers, etc.; also, a pointed hammer used for dressing
millstones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pike or spike; the sharp point fixed in the center
of a buckler.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Take down my buckler . .
. and grind the <xex>pick</xex> on 't.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Choice; right of selection; <as>as, to have one's
<ex>pick</ex>; in cat breeding, the owner of a stud gets the
<ex>pick</ex> of the litter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>France and Russia have the <qex>pick</qex> of our stables.</q>
<rj><qau>Ld. Lytton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>That which would be picked
or chosen first; the best; <as>as, the <ex>pick</ex> of the
flock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A particle of ink or paper
imbedded in the hollow of a letter, filling up its face, and occasioning
a spot on a printed sheet.</def> <rj><au>MacKellar.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Painting)</fld> <def>That which is picked in, as
with a pointed pencil, to correct an unevenness in a picture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>The blow which drives the
shuttle, -- the rate of speed of a loom being reckoned as so many
<ex>picks</ex> per minute;</def> <specif>hence,</specif> <fld>in
describing the fineness of a fabric</fld>, <def>a weft thread; <as>as,
so many <ex>picks</ex> to an inch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pick dressing</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>in cut
stonework, a facing made by a pointed tool, leaving the surface in
little pits or depressions.</cd> -- <col><b>Pick hammer</b></col>, <cd>a
pick with one end sharp and the other blunt, used by
miners.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickaback</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"a*back`</hw> <pr>(p<icr/k"<adot/*b<acr/k`)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>On the back or shoulders; <as>as, to ride
<ex>pickback</ex></as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pickapack</asp>,
<asp>pickback</asp>, and <asp>pickpack</asp>.]</altsp> <syn><b>Syn.
--</b> piggyback.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>A woman stooping to take a child <qex>pickaback</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>R,Jefferies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickaninny</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"a*nin`ny</hw> <pr>(p<icr/k"<adot/*n<icr/n`n<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pickaninnies</plw>
<pr>(p<icr/k"<adot/*n<icr/n`n<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. Sp.
<ets>peque<ntil/o</ets> little, young.]</ety> <def>A small child;
especially, a negro or mulatto infant. Now (2001) used primarily in the
latter sense, and in that sense often considered derogatory.</def>
<mark>[U.S. & West Indies]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickapack</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"a*pack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Pickaback.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickaxe</ent><br/
<ent>Pickax</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pick"ax`</hw>, <hw>Pick"axe`</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(p<icr/k"<acr/ks`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of OE.
<ets>pikois</ets>, <ets>pikeis</ets>, F. <ets>picois</ets>, fr.
<ets>pic</ets>. See <er>Pick</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A pick with
a point at one end, a transverse edge or blade at the other, and a
handle inserted at the middle; a hammer with a flattened end for driving
wedges and a pointed end for piercing as it strikes.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickback</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>On the
back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picked</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pointed;
sharp.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Picked</xex> and polished.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the stake be made <qex>picked</qex> at the top.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a pike or spine on the
back; -- said of certain fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Carefully selected; chosen; <as>as, <ex>picked</ex>
men</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fine; spruce; smart; precise; dainty.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Picked dogfish</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Dogfish</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Picked out</b></col>,
<cd>ornamented or relieved with lines, or the like, of a different,
usually a lighter, color; as, a carriage body dark green, <xex>picked
out</xex> with red.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickedness</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"ed*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state of being sharpened; pointedness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fineness; spruceness; smartness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Too much <qex>pickedness</qex> is not manly.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickeer</ent><br/
<hw>Pick*eer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pickeered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pickeering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>picorer</ets> to go marauding, orig., to go to steal cattle,
ultimately fr. L. <ets>pecus</ets>, <ets>pecoris</ets>, cattle; cf. F.
<ets>picor<eacute/e</ets>, Sp. <ets>pecorea</ets> robbery committed by
straggling soldiers.]</ety> <def>To make a raid for booty; to maraud;
also, to skirmish in advance of an army. See <er>Picaroon</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickeerer</ent><br/
<hw>Pick*eer"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pickeers.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picker</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pick</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, picks, in
any sense, -- as, one who uses a pick; one who gathers; a thief; a pick;
a pickax; <as>as, a cotton <ex>picker</ex></as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Pickers</xex> and stealers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A machine for picking fibrous
materials to pieces so as to loosen and separate the fiber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>The piece in a loom which
strikes the end of the shuttle, and impels it through the
warp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Ordnance)</fld> <def>A priming wire for cleaning
the vent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickerel</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"er*el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<er>Pike</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pickerell</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A young or small
pike.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bet [better] is, quoth he, a pike than a <qex>pickerel</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of several
species of freshwater fishes of the genus <gen>Esox</gen>, esp. the
smaller species.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The glasseye, or wall-eyed
pike. See <er>Wall-eye</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The federation, or chain, pickerel (<spn>Esox
reticulatus</spn>) and the brook pickerel (<spn>Esox Americanus</spn>)
are the most common American species. They are used for food, and are
noted for their voracity. About the Great Lakes the pike is called
<xex>pickerel</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pickerel weed</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
blue-flowered aquatic plant (<spn>Pontederia cordata</spn>) having large
arrow-shaped leaves. So called because common in slow-moving waters
where pickerel are often found.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickering</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"er*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably a
corruption of <er>Pickerel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
sauger of the St.Lawrence River.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickery</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pick</er>
to steal; or perhaps from <er>Pickeer</er>.]</ety> <def>Petty
theft.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picket</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>piquet</ets>,
properly dim. of <ets>pique</ets> spear, pike. See <er>Pike</er>, and
cf. <er>Piquet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A stake sharpened or
pointed, especially one used in fortification and encampments, to mark
bounds and angles; or one used for tethering horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pointed pale, used in marking fences.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Probably so called from the <ets>picketing</ets> of
the horses.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A detached body of troops
serving to guard an army from surprise, and to oppose reconnoitering
parties of the enemy; -- called also <altname>outlying
picket</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>By extension, men appointed by a trades union, or
other labor organization, to intercept outsiders, and prevent them from
working for employers with whom the organization is at variance.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark><-- any individual standing at the entrance to a
building (typically a business establishement), usually for the purpose
of inhibiting or preventing others from entering that establishment, but
sometimes only for demonstration or protest, and usually bearing a sign
informing others of the nature of the grievance causing the picketing.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A military punishment, formerly resorted to, in
which the offender was forced to stand with one foot on a pointed
stake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A game at cards. See <er>Piquet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Inlying picket</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a
detachment of troops held in camp or quarters, detailed to march if
called upon.</cd> -- <col><b>Picket fence</b></col>, <cd>a fence made of
pickets. See def. 2, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Picket guard</b></col>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a guard of horse and foot, always in readiness in
case of alarm.</cd> -- <col><b>Picket line</b></col>. <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A position held and guarded by small bodies of men
placed at intervals</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A rope to which horses are
secured when groomed.</cd> -- <col><b>Picketpin</b></col>, <cd>an iron
pin for picketing horses.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picket</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"et</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Picketed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Picketing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fortify with
pointed stakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inclose or fence with pickets or pales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To tether to, or as to, a picket; <as>as, to
<ex>picket</ex> a horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To guard, as a camp or road, by an outlying
picket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To torture by compelling to stand with one foot on a
pointed stake.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picketee</ent><br/
<hw>Pick`e*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Picotee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pick-fault</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"-fault`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who seeks out
faults.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picking</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of digging or
breaking up, as with a pick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of choosing, plucking, or
gathering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is, or may be, picked or
gleaned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pilfering; also, that which is pilfered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The pulverized shells of oysters
used in making walks.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Rough sorting of ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Overburned bricks.</def>
<rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picking</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Done or made as with a
pointed tool; <as>as, a <ex>picking</ex> sound</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nice; careful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> was too warm on <qex>picking</qex> work to dwell.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Picking peg</b></col>. <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Picker</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickle</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<def>See <er>Picle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickle</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"kle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>pekel</ets>. Probably
a dim. fr. <er>Pick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, alluding to the cleaning of
the fish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A solution of salt and
water, in which fish, meat, etc., may be preserved or corned;
brine.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Vinegar, plain or spiced, used for
preserving vegetables, fish, eggs, oysters, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any article of food which has been preserved in
brine or in vinegar.</def><-- esp., a cucumber thus preserved --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A bath of dilute sulphuric or
nitric acid, etc., to remove burnt sand, scale rust, etc., from the
surface of castings, or other articles of metal, or to brighten them or
improve their color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A troublesome child; <as>as, a little
<ex>pickle</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To be in a pickle</b></col>, <cd>to be in disagreeable
position; to be in a condition of embarrassment, difficulty, or
disorder.</cd> <ldquo/How cam'st thou in this <xex>pickle</xex>?<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To put a rod in pickle</b></col>, <cd>to
prepare a particular reproof, punishment, or penalty for future
application.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickle</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pickled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pickling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
preserve or season in pickle; to treat with some kind of pickle; <as>as,
to <ex>pickle</ex> herrings or cucumbers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give an antique appearance to; -- said of copies
or imitations of paintings by the old masters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickled</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"kled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Preserved in a
pickle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickle-herring</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"kle-her"ring</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
herring preserved in brine; a pickled herring.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A merry-andrew; a buffoon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickler</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes
pickles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picklock</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"lock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
instrument for picking locks.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who picks locks; a thief.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>picklock</xex> of secrets.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pick-me-up</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"-me-up`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stimulant, restorative, or
tonic; a bracer.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickmire</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"mire`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from its
<ets>picking</ets> its food from the <ets>mire</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The pewit, or black-headed gull.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picknick</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"nick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Picnic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickoff</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"off</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>
<def>a play in which a base runner is picked off. See <er>pick
off</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pick off</ent><br/
<hw>Pick" off</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>to put out a baserunner who is off base by
tagging him/her, especially by a quick throw from the pitcher or
catcher.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to shoot so as to kill or disable, especially one by
one from a position or in a situation where the target is unable to
return fire at the shooter; -- used commonly of sniper fire.</def>
<ldquo/The sniper <ex>picked off</ex> a dozen passersby from the tower
before he was shot down by police.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Football)</fld> <def>to intercept (a
pass).</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickpack</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"pack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Pickaback.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickpenny</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"pen`ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pickpennies</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A miser; also, a
sharper.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickpocket</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"pock`et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who steals
purses or other articles from pockets.</def>
<rj><au>Bentley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickpurse</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"purse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who steals
purses, or money from purses.</def> <rj><au>Latimer. Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picksy</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pixy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pickthank</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"thank`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who strives to
put another under obligation; an officious person; hence, a flatterer.
Used also adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Smiling <qex>pickthanks</qex>, and base newsmongers.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picktooth</ent><br/
<hw>Pick"tooth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A toothpick.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pick-up</ent><br/
<ent>Pickup</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pick"up</hw>, <it>or</it> <hw>Pick"-up`</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Colloq., Cant, or Slang]</mark> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Act of picking up, as, in various games, the fielding or hitting of
a ball just after it strikes the ground.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which picks up;</def> <specif>specif.:</specif>
<fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>same as <er>Brush</er> <b>b</b>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One that is picked up, as a meal hastily got up for
the occasion, a chance acquaintance, an informal game, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>a social companion for the evening who is met
without prior arrangement, as at a singles bar; also, the act of joining
with such a companion in that fashion.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Automobiles)</fld> <def>acceleration; a measure of
the ability of a vehicle to accelerate.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Electronics)</fld> <def>a component of a phonograph
which contains the stylus and also components for converting the
vibrations of the stylus into electrical impulses for subsequent
processing into sound; often referred to as a
<altname>cartridge</altname>; also, the process of converting vibrations
into electrical impulses.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Electronics)</fld> <def>the conversion of sound or
light into electrical signals in a sound or image recording or
transmitting device.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Vehicles)</fld> <def>a small truck having an
enclosed driver's compartment (cab) but an open rear with usually low
sides and a tailgate. Also called <altname>pickup
truck</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>a hitchhiker who has been picked up.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picle</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"cle</hw> <pr>((p<icr/k"'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. fr.
<ets>pightel</ets> or <ets>pingle</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small piece of
land inclosed with a hedge; a close.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pickle</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picnic</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>piquenique</ets>. See <er>Pick</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Knickknack</er>.]</ety> <def>Formerly, an entertainment at which
each person contributed some dish to a common table; now, an excursion
or pleasure party in which the members partake of a collation or repast
(usually in the open air, and from food carried by
themselves).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picnic</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Picnicked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Picnicking</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To go on
a picnic, or pleasure excursion; to eat in public fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picnicker</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"nick*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who takes part
in a picnic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Picus</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to
the Pici.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picoline</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"o*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pix</ets>,
<ets>picis</ets>, pitch + <ets>ol</ets>eum oil + <ets>-ine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any one of three isometric bases
(<chform>C6H7N</chform>) related to pyridine, and obtained from bone
oil, acrolein ammonia, and coal-tar naphtha, as colorless mobile liquids
of strong odor; -- called also <altname>methyl
pyridine</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picot</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`cot"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>One of many small loops, as of thread, forming an ornamental
border, as on a ribbon.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picotine</ent><br/
<ent>Picotee</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pic`o*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pic`o*tine"</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>picot<eacute/</ets>
dotted, picked.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A variety of carnation
having petals of a light color variously dotted and spotted at the
edges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picquet</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"quet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Piquet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picra</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"cra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/ sharp,
bitter.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The powder of aloes with canella,
formerly officinal, employed as a cathartic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"crate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
salt of picric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picric</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"cric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
bitter.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, a
strong organic acid (called <xex>picric acid</xex>), intensely
bitter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Picric acid</xex> is obtained by treating phenol
with strong nitric acid, as a brilliant yellow crystalline substance,
<chform>C6H2(NO2)3.OH</chform>. It is used in dyeing silk and wool, and
also in the manufacture of explosives, as it is very unstable when
heated. Called also <altname>trinitrophenol</altname>, and formerly
<altname>carbazotic acid</altname>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picrite</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/
bitter.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A dark green igneous rock,
consisting largely of chrysolite, with hornblende, augite, biotite,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picrolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ro*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ bitter +
<ets>-lite</ets>.: cf. F. <ets>picrolithe</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A fibrous variety of serpentine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picromel</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ro*mel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ bitter +
<?/ honey: cf. F. <ets>picromel</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>
<def>A colorless viscous substance having a bitter-sweet
taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It was formerly supposed to be the essential principle
of the bile, but is now known to be a mixture, principally of salts of
glycocholic and taurocholic acids.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picrotoxin</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`ro*tox"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ bitter
+ <ets>tox</ets>ic + <ets>-in</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
bitter white crystalline substance found in the cocculus indicus. It is
a peculiar poisonous neurotic and intoxicant, and consists of a mixture
of several neutral substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picryl</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"cryl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Picr</ets>ic +
<ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The hypothetical radical
of picric acid, analogous to phenyl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictish</ent><br/
<hw>Pict"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Picts; resembling the Picts.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>Pictish</xex>
peer.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"to*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Picture</er>, and <er>-graph</er>.]</ety> <def>A picture or
hieroglyph representing and expressing an idea.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pic`to*graph"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pic*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pictorius</ets>, fr. <ets>pictor</ets> a painter, fr.
<ets>pingere</ets> to paint. See <er>Paint</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to pictures; illustrated by pictures; forming pictures;
representing with the clearness of a picture; <as>as, a
<ex>pictorial</ex> dictionary; a <ex>pictorial</ex>
imagination.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Pictorial</xex> rhetoric.<rdquo/
<au>Ruskin.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Pic*to"ri*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictorical</ent><br/
<ent>Pictoric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pic*tor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pic*tor"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pictorial.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picts</ent><br/
<hw>Picts</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Pict</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <ety>[L. <ets>Picti</ets>;
cf. AS. <ets>Peohtas</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A race of
people of uncertain origin, who inhabited Scotland in early
times.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pic*tu"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
painting.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pattern of
coloration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picturable</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"tur*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
pictured, or represented by a picture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictural</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pictorial.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictural</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"tur*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A picture.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picture</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pictura</ets>, fr. <ets>pingere</ets>, <ets>pictum</ets>, to paint:
cf. F. <ets>peinture</ets>. See <er>Paint</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The art of painting; representation by painting.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Any well-expressed image . . . either in <qex>picture</qex> or
sculpture.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A representation of anything (as a person, a
landscape, a building) upon canvas, paper, or other surface, produced by
means of painting, drawing, engraving, photography, etc.; a
representation in colors. By extension, a figure; a model.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pictures</qex> and shapes are but secondary objects.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The young king's <qex>picture</qex> . . . in virgin wax.</q>
<rj><qau>Howell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An image or resemblance; a representation, either to
the eye or to the mind; that which, by its likeness, brings vividly to
mind some other thing; <as>as, a child is the <ex>picture</ex> of his
father; the man is the <ex>picture</ex> of grief.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My eyes make <qex>pictures</qex> when they are shut.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Picture</xex> is often used adjectively, or in
forming self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>picture</xex> book or
<xex>picture</xex>-book, <xex>picture</xex> frame or
<xex>picture</xex>-frame, <xex>picture</xex> seller or
<xex>picture</xex>-seller, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Animated picture</b></col>, <cd>a moving picture.</cd> --
<col><b>Picture gallery</b></col>, <cd>a gallery, or large apartment,
devoted to the exhibition of pictures.</cd> -- <col><b>Picture
red</b></col>, <cd>a rod of metal tube fixed to the walls of a room,
from which pictures are hung.</cd> -- <col><b>Picture writing</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The art of recording events, or of expressing messages,
by means of pictures representing the actions or circumstances in
question</cd>. <au>Tylor.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The record or message so
represented; <as>as, the <ex>picture writing</ex> of the American
Indians</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Picture</er>, <er>Painting</er>.</syn>
<usage> Every kind of representation by drawing or painting is a
<xex>picture</xex>, whether made with oil colors, water colors, pencil,
crayons, or India ink; strictly, a <xex>painting</xex> is a picture made
by means of colored paints, usually applied moist with a
brush.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picture</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ture</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pictured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Picturing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To draw or paint a resemblance
of; to delineate; to represent; to form or present an ideal likeness of;
to bring before the mind.</def> <ldquo/I . . . do <xex>picture</xex> it
in my mind.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have not seen him so <qex>pictured</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pictured</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"tured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with
pictures; represented by a picture or pictures; <as>as, a
<ex>pictured</ex> scene</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1084 --></p>

<p><ent>Picturer</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"tur*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes
pictures; a painter.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picturesque</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`tur*esque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pittoresco</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pittoresque</ets>. See
<er>Pictorial</er>.]</ety> <def>Forming, or fitted to form, a good or
pleasing picture; representing with the clearness or ideal beauty
appropriate to a picture; expressing that peculiar kind of beauty which
is agreeable in a picture, natural or artificial; graphic; vivid;
<as>as, a <ex>picturesque</ex> scene or attitude; <ex>picturesque</ex>
language.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What is <qex>picturesque</qex> as placed in relation to the
beautiful and the sublime? It is . . . the characteristic pushed into a
sensible excess.</q> <au>De Quincey.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pic`tur*esque"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pic`tur*esque"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picturesquish</ent><br/
<hw>Pic`tur*esqu"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
picturesque.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picturize</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"tur*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Picturized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Picturizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To picture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To adorn with pictures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picul</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"ul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Jav. & Malay
<ets>pikul</ets>, fr. <ets>pikul</ets> to carry on the back, to carry a
burden; n., a man's burden.]</ety> <def>A commercial weight varying in
different countries and for different commodities. In Borneo it is
135<frac58/ lbs.; in China and Sumatra, 133<frac12/ lbs.; in Japan,
133<frac13/ lbs.; but sometimes 130 lbs., etc. Called also, by the
Chinese, <altname>tan</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pecul</asp>, and <asp>pecal</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piculet</ent><br/
<hw>Pic"u*let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<er>Picus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of very small
woodpeckers of the genus <gen>Picumnus</gen> and allied genera. Their
tail feathers are not stiff and sharp at the tips, as in ordinary
woodpeckers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Picus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pici</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a woodpecker.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of woodpeckers, including some of the
common American and European species.</def><-- now picoides? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piddle</ent><br/
<hw>Pid"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Piddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw.
<ets>pittla</ets> to keep picking at, Sw. <ets>peta</ets> to
pick.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To deal in trifles; to concern one's self
with trivial matters rather than with those that are important.</def>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be squeamishly nice about one's food.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To urinate; -- child's word.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piddler</ent><br/
<hw>Pid"dler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
piddles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piddling</ent><br/
<hw>Pid"dling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><def>Trifling; trivial;
frivolous; paltry; -- applied to persons and things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ignoble hucksterage of <qex>piddling</qex> tithes.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piddock</ent><br/
<hw>Pid"dock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of Pholas; a
pholad. See <er>Pholas</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pie</ent><br/
<hw>Pie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pie</ets>,
<ets>pye</ets>; cf. Ir. & Gael. <ets>pighe</ets> pie, also Gael.
<ets>pige</ets> an earthen jar or pot. Cf. <er>Piggin</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An article of food consisting of paste baked with
something in it or under it; <as>as, chicken <ex>pie</ex>; venison
<ex>pie</ex>; mince <ex>pie</ex>; apple <ex>pie</ex>; pumpkin
<ex>pie</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>See <er>Camp</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 5.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pie crust</b></col>, <cd>the paste of a pie.</cd></cs>
<-- easy as pie = very easy --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pie</ent><br/
<hw>Pie</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pie</ets>, L. <ets>pica</ets>;
cf. <ets>picus</ets> woodpecker, <ets>pingere</ets> to paint; the bird
being perhaps named from its colors. Cf. <er>Pi</er>, <er>Paint</er>,
<er>Speight</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A magpie.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any other species of the genus
<gen>Pica</gen>, and of several allied genera.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>pye</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>The service book.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Pritn.)</fld> <def>Type confusedly mixed. See
<er>Pi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>By cock and pie</b></col>, <cd>an adjuration equivalent
to <ldquo/by God and the service book.<rdquo/</cd> <au>Shak.</au> --
<col><b>Tree pie</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any Asiatic bird of
the genus <gen>Dendrocitta</gen>, allied to the magpie.</cd> --
<col><b>Wood pie</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>French
pie</cref>, under <er>French</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pie</ent><br/
<hw>Pie</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Pi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piebald</ent><br/
<hw>Pie"bald`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pie</ets> the
party-colored bird + <ets>bald</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having
spots and patches of black and white, or other colors; mottled;
pied.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>piebald</xex> steed of Thracian strain.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Mixed.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Piebald</xex>
languages.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piece</ent><br/
<hw>Piece</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pece</ets>, F.
<ets>pi<egrave/ce</ets>, LL. <ets>pecia</ets>, <ets>petia</ets>,
<ets>petium</ets>, probably of Celtic origin; cf. W. <ets>peth</ets> a
thing, a part, portion, a little, Armor. <ets>pez</ets>, Gael. & Ir.
<ets>cuid</ets> part, share. Cf. <er>Petty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A fragment or part of anything separated from the whole, in any
manner, as by cutting, splitting, breaking, or tearing; a part; a
portion; <as>as, a <ex>piece</ex> of sugar; to break in
<ex>pieces</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bring it out <qex>piece</qex> by <qex>piece</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xxiv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A definite portion or quantity, as of goods or work;
<as>as, a <ex>piece</ex> of broadcloth; a <ex>piece</ex> of wall
paper.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any one thing conceived of as apart from other
things of the same kind; an individual article; a distinct single effort
of a series; a definite performance</def>; especially: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A literary or artistic composition; <as>as, a <ex>piece</ex> of
poetry, music, or statuary</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A musket, gun,
or cannon; <as>as, a battery of six <ex>pieces</ex>; a following
<ex>piece</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A coin; <as>as, a sixpenny
<ex>piece</ex></as>; -- formerly applied specifically to an English gold
coin worth 22 shillings.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A fact; an item;
<as>as, a <ex>piece</ex> of news; a <ex>piece</ex> of
knowledge</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An individual; -- applied to a person as being of a
certain nature or quality; often, but not always, used slightingly or in
contempt.</def> <ldquo/If I had not been a <xex>piece</xex> of a
logician before I came to him.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy mother was a <qex>piece</qex> of virtue.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His own spirit is as unsettled a <qex>piece</qex> as there is in
all the world.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <col><b>a piece of cake</b></col>, <cd>a task easily
accomplished.</cd> -- <col><b>a piece of work</b></col>, <cd>a
disparaging term for a person considered to have an excess of some
undesirable quality; esp. a difficult or eccentric person.</cd> --
<col><b>Piece of ass</b></col> <cd>vulgar term for a woman, considered
as a partner in sexual intercourse.</cd> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>One of the superior men,
distinguished from a pawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A castle; a fortified building.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Of a piece</b></col>, <cd>of the same sort, as if taken
from the same whole; like; -- sometimes followed by
<xex>with</xex>.</cd> <au>Dryden.</au> -- <col><b>Piece of
eight</b></col>, <cd>the Spanish piaster, formerly divided into eight
reals.</cd> -- <col><b>To give a piece of one's mind to</b></col>,
<cd>to speak plainly, bluntly, or severely to (another).</cd>
<au>Thackeray.</au> -- <col><b>Piece broker</b></col>, <cd>one who buys
shreds and remnants of cloth to sell again.</cd> -- <col><b>Piece
goods</b></col>, <cd>goods usually sold by pieces or fixed portions, as
shirtings, calicoes, sheetings, and the like.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piece</ent><br/
<hw>Piece</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pieced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piecing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make,
enlarge, or repair, by the addition of a piece or pieces; to patch;
<as>as, to <ex>piece</ex> a garment</as>; -- often with
<xex>out</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To unite; to join; to combine.</def>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His adversaries . . . <qex>pieced</qex> themselves together in a
joint opposition against him.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piece</ent><br/
<hw>Piece</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To unite by a
coalescence of parts; to fit together; to join.</def> <ldquo/It
<xex>pieced</xex> better.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pieceless</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not made of pieces; whole;
entire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecely</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In pieces; piecemeal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecemeal</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"meal`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pecemele</ets>; <ets>pece</ets> a piece + AS. <ets>m<?/lum</ets>,
dat. pl. of <ets>m<?/l</ets> part. See <er>Meal</er> a portion.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>In pieces; in parts or fragments.</def> <ldquo/On which
it <xex>piecemeal</xex> brake.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The beasts will tear thee <qex>piecemeal</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Piece by piece; by little and little in
succession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Piecemeal</qex> they win, this acre first, than that.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecemeal</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"meal`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made up of parts or pieces;
single; separate.</def> <ldquo/These <xex>piecemeal</xex> guilts.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gov. of Tongue.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecemeal</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"meal`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fragment; a scrap.</def>
<rj><au>R. Vaughan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecemealed</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"mealed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Divided into
pieces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecener</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
supplies rolls of wool to the slubbing machine in woolen
mills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Piecer</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecer</ent><br/
<hw>Pie"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pieces; a patcher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A child employed in spinning mill to tie together
broken threads.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piecework</ent><br/
<hw>Piece"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work done by the
piece or job; work paid for at a rate based on the amount of work done,
rather than on the time employed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The reaping was <qex>piecework</qex>, at so much per acre.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Jefferies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pied</ent><br/
<hw>Pied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of <er>Pi</er>,
or <er>Pie</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pied</ent><br/
<hw>Pied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pie</er> the
party-colored bird.]</ety> <def>Variegated with spots of different
colors; party-colored; spotted; piebald.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pied</xex>
coats.<rdquo/ <au>Burton.</au> <ldquo/Meadows trim with daisies
<xex>pied</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pied antelope</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
bontebok.</cd> -- <col><b>Pied-billed grebe</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the dabchick.</cd> -- <col><b>Pied
blackbird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any Asiatic thrush of the
genus <gen>Turdulus</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pied finch</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The chaffinch.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>The snow bunting</cd>. <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pied
flycatcher</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common European
flycatcher (<spn>Ficedula atricapilla</spn>). The male is black and
white.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piedmont</ent><br/
<hw>Pied"mont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pied</ets>
foot + <ets>mont</ets> mountain.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Noting
the region of foothills near the base of a mountain chain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piedmontite</ent><br/
<hw>Pied"mont*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A manganesian kind of epidote, from <xex>Piedmont</xex>. See
<er>Epidote</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piedness</ent><br/
<hw>Pied"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
pied.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pi<eacute/douche</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi<eacute/`douche"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. It. <ets>peduccio</ets> console, corbel.]</ety> <def>A pedestal of
small size, used to support small objects, as busts, vases, and the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piedstall</ent><br/
<hw>Pied"stall</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pedestal</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pieman</ent><br/
<hw>Pie"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Piemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A man who makes or sells
pies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piend</ent><br/
<hw>Piend</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>pind</ets> a peg.]</ety> <def>See <er>Peen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pieno</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*e"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>plenus</ets> full.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Full; having all
the instruments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pieplant</ent><br/
<hw>Pie"plant`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant (<spn>Rheum Rhaponticum</spn>) the leafstalks of which are acid,
and are used in making pies; the garden rhubarb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piepowder</ent><br/
<ent>Piepoudre</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pie"pou`dre</hw>, <hw>Pie"pow`der</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Lit., dustyfoot, i. e., dusty-footed dealers, fr. F.
<ets>pied</ets> foot + <ets>poudreux</ets> dusty.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng.
Law)</fld> <def>An ancient court of record in England, formerly incident
to every fair and market, of which the steward of him who owned or had
the toll was the judge.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pier</ent><br/
<hw>Pier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pere</ets>, OF.
<ets>piere</ets> a stone, F. <ets>pierre</ets>, fr. L. <ets>petra</ets>,
Gr. <?/. Cf. <er>Petrify</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any detached mass of masonry, whether insulated or
supporting one side of an arch or lintel, as of a bridge; the piece of
wall between two openings.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any additional or
auxiliary mass of masonry used to stiffen a wall. See
<er>Buttress</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A projecting wharf or landing place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Abutment pier</b></col>, <cd>the pier of a bridge next
the shore; a pier which by its strength and stability resists the thrust
of an arch.</cd> -- <col><b>Pier glass</b></col>, <cd>a mirror, of high
and narrow shape, to be put up between windows.</cd> -- <col><b>Pier
table</b></col>, <cd>a table made to stand between
windows.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierage</ent><br/
<hw>Pier"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Wharfage</er>.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierce</ent><br/
<hw>Pierce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pierced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piercing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>percen</ets>, F. <ets>percer</ets>, OF. <ets>percier</ets>,
<ets>perchier</ets>, <ets>parchier</ets>; perh. fr. (assumed) LL.
<ets>pertusiare</ets> for <ets>pertusare</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>pertundere</ets>, <ets>pertusum</ets>, to beat, push, bore through;
<ets>per</ets> through + <ets>tundere</ets> to beat: cf. OF.
<ets>pertuisier</ets> to pierce, F. <ets>pertuis</ets> a hole. Cf.
<er>Contuse</er>, <er>Parch</er>, <er>Pertuse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To thrust into, penetrate, or transfix, with a pointed
instrument.</def> <ldquo/I <xex>pierce</xex> . . . her tender
side.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To penetrate; to enter; to force a way into or
through; to pass into or through; <as>as, to <ex>pierce</ex> the enemy's
line; a shot <ex>pierced</ex> the ship.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: To penetrate; to affect deeply; <as>as, to
<ex>pierce</ex> a mystery</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pierced</xex> with
grief.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can no prayers <qex>pierce</qex> thee?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierce</ent><br/
<hw>Pierce</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To enter; to penetrate; to make a
way into or through something, as a pointed instrument does; -- used
literally and figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>pierced</qex> to the skin, but bit no more.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She would not <qex>pierce</qex> further into his meaning.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierceable</ent><br/
<hw>Pierce"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
pierced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierced</ent><br/
<hw>Pierced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Penetrated; entered;
perforated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piercel</ent><br/
<hw>Pier"cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>perce</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of gimlet for making vents in
casks; -- called also <altname>piercer</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piercer</ent><br/
<hw>Pier"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, pierces or perforates</def>; specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>An instrument used in forming eyelets; a stiletto.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A piercel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The ovipositor, or
sting, of an insect.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An insect provided with an
ovipositor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piercing</ent><br/
<hw>Pier"cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Forcibly entering,
or adapted to enter, at or by a point; perforating; penetrating; keen;
-- used also figuratively; <as>as, a <ex>piercing</ex> instrument, or
thrust</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Piercing</xex> eloquence.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pier"cing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pier"cing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierian</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*e"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pierius</ets>, from Mount <etsep>Pierus</etsep>, in Thessaly,
sacred to the Muses.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Pierides or
Muses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Drink deep, or taste not the <qex>Pierian</qex> spring.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierid</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"er*id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Peirides</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any butterfly of the
genus <gen>Pieris</gen> and related genera. See <cref>Cabbage
butterfly</cref>, under <er>Cabbage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierides</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*er"i*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <?/. See <er>Pierian</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>The
Muses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pierre-perdu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pierre`-per`du"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pierre perdue</ets> lost stone.]</ety> <def>Blocks of stone or
concrete heaped loosely in the water to make a foundation (as for a sea
wall), a breakwater, a mole, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piet</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"et</hw> <pr>(p<imac/"<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<er>Pie</er> a magpie: cf. F. <ets>piette</ets> a smew.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The dipper, or water ouzel.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The magpie.</def>
<mark>[Prov.Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Jay piet</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the European
jay.</cd> <mark>[Prov.Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Sea piet</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the oyster catcher.</cd>
<mark>[Prov.Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pieta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*e*t<agrave/"</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*<asl/*t<aum/")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>A
representation of the dead Christ, attended by the Virgin Mary or by
holy women and angels.</def> <rj><au>Mollett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pietism</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"e*tism</hw> <pr>(p<imac/"<esl/*t<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. G. <ets>pietismus</ets>, F. <ets>pi<eacute/tisme</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The principle or practice of the Pietists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Strict devotion; also, affectation of
devotion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>Sch<oum/ne Seele</qex>, that ideal of gentle
<qex>pietism</qex>, in <ldquo/Wilhelm Meister.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>W.
Pater.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pietist</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"e*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>pietist</ets>, F. <ets>pi<eacute/tiste</ets>. See
<er>Piety</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a class of
religious reformers in Germany in the 17th century who sought to revive
declining piety in the Protestant churches; -- often applied as a term
of reproach to those who make a display of religious feeling. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pietistical</ent><br/
<ent>Pietistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pi`e*tis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pi`e*tis"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Pietists; hence, in contempt, affectedly or demonstratively
religious.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pietra dura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*e"tra du"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[It., hard stone.]</ety>
<fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>Hard and fine stones in general, such as are
used for inlay and the like, as distinguished from the softer stones
used in building; thus, a Florentine mosaic is a familiar instance of
work in <xex>pietra dura</xex>, though the ground may be soft
marble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piety</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pi<eacute/t<eacute/</ets>; cf. It. <ets>piet<agrave/</ets>; both
fr. L. <ets>pietas</ets> piety, fr. <ets>pius</ets> pious. See
<er>Pious</er>, and cf. <er>Pity</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Veneration or reverence of the Supreme Being, and love of his
character; loving obedience to the will of God, and earnest devotion to
his service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Piety</qex> is the only proper and adequate relief of
decaying man.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Duty; dutifulness; filial reverence and devotion;
affectionate reverence and service shown toward parents, relatives,
benefactors, country, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Conferred upon me for the <qex>piety</qex><br/
Which to my country I was judged to have shown.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Religion; sanctity; devotion; godliness;
holiness. See <er>Religion</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piewipe</ent><br/
<hw>Pie"wipe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from its
note.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The lapwing, or pewit.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- piezo- piezo-electric --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piezometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`e*zom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
press + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pi<eacute/zom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring the
compressibility of liquids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A gauge connected with a water
main to show the pressure at that point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piffara</ent><br/
<ent>Piffero</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pif"fe*ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pif"fa*ra</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>piffero</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A fife; also, a rude kind of oboe or a bagpipe
with an inflated skin for reservoir.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piffle</ent><br/
<hw>Pif"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Piffled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piffling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To be sequeamish or
delicate; hence, to act or talk triflingly or ineffectively; to talk
nonsense or about trivial matters; to twaddle; piddle.</def>
<mark>[Dial. or Slang]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> chatter, palaver, prate, tittle-tattle, twaddle,
clack, maunder, prattle, gibber, tattle, blabber, gabble.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To act in a trivial or ineffective way.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piffle</ent><br/
<hw>Pif"fle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of piffling; trifling talk or
action; piddling; twaddle.</def> <mark>[Dial. or Slang]</mark>
<ldquo/Futile <xex>piffle</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Kipling</au>.<br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig</ent><br/
<hw>Pig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A piggin.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pigg</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig</ent><br/
<hw>Pig</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>big</ets>,
<ets>bigge</ets>, LG. <ets>bigge</ets>, also Dan. <ets>pige</ets> girl,
Sw. <ets>piga</ets>, Icel. <ets>p<imac/ka</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The young of swine, male or female; also, any swine; a hog.</def>
<ldquo/Two <xex>pigges</xex> in a poke.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any wild species of the genus
<gen>Sus</gen> and related genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sow</er> a channel for melted iron.]</ety>
<def>An oblong mass of cast iron, lead, or other metal. See <cref>Mine
pig</cref>, under <er>Mine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One who is hoggish; a greedy person.</def>
<mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Masked pig</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Masked</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pig bed</b></col>
<fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>the bed of sand in which the iron from a
smelting furnace is cast into pigs.</cd> -- <col><b>Pig iron</b></col>,
<cd>cast iron in pigs, or oblong blocks or bars, as it comes from the
smelting furnace. See <er>Pig</er>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Pig
yoke</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a nickname for a quadrant or
sextant.</cd> -- <col><b>A pig in a poke</b></col> (that is,
<xex>bag</xex>), <cd>a blind bargain; something bought or bargained for,
without the quality or the value being known.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig</ent><br/
<hw>Pig</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pigged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pigging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring
forth (pigs); to bring forth in the manner of pigs; to farrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To huddle or lie together like pigs, in one
bed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeon</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pipio</ets> a young pipping or chirping bird, fr. <ets>pipire</ets>
to peep, chirp. Cf. <er>Peep</er> to chirp.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bird of the order Columb<ae/, of which
numerous species occur in nearly all parts of the world.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common domestic pigeon, or dove, was derived from
the Old World rock pigeon or rock dove (<spn>Columba livia</spn>),
common in cities. It has given rise to numerous very remarkable
varieties, such as the carrier, fantail, nun, pouter, tumbler, etc. The
common wild pigeon of the Eastern United States is the <cref>Mourning
dove</cref> (<spn>Zenaida macroura</spn>, called also <cref>Carolina
dove</cref>). Before the 19th century, the most common pigeon was the
passenger pigeon, but that species is now extinct. See <er>Passenger
pigeon</er>, and <cref>Carolina dove</cref> under <er>Dove</er>. See,
also, <cref>Fruit pigeon</cref>, <cref>Ground pigeon</cref>, <cref>Queen
pigeon</cref>, <cref>Stock pigeon</cref>, under <er>Fruit</er>,
<er>Ground</er>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An unsuspected victim of sharpers; a gull.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blue pigeon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
Australian passerine bird (<spn>Graucalus melanops</spn>); -- called
also <altname>black-faced crow</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Green
pigeon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of
Old World pigeons belonging to the family <fam>Treronid<ae/</fam>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Imperial pigeon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
the large Asiatic fruit pigeons of the genus <gen>Carpophada</gen>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pigeon berry</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the purplish
black fruit of the pokeweed; also, the plant itself. See
<er>Pokeweed</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon English</b></col>
<ety>[perhaps a corruption of <ets>business English</ets>]</ety>, <cd>an
extraordinary and grotesque dialect, employed in the commercial cities
of China, as the medium of communication between foreign merchants and
the Chinese. Its base is English, with a mixture of Portuguese and
Hindustani.</cd> <au>Johnson's Cyc.</au><-- pidgin English??? --> --
<col><b>Pigeon grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of foxtail
grass (<spn>Setaria glauca</spn>), of some value as fodder. The seeds
are eagerly eaten by pigeons and other birds.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon
hawk</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small American
falcon (<spn>Falco columbarius</spn>). The adult male is dark slate-blue
above, streaked with black on the back; beneath, whitish or buff,
streaked with brown. The tail is banded.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
American sharp-shinned hawk (<spn>Accipiter velox</spn> <it>or</it>
<spn>Accipiter fuscus</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon hole</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A hole for pigeons to enter a pigeon house</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>See <er>Pigeonhole</er>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd>
<pluf>pl.</pluf> <cd>An old English game, in which balls were rolled
through little arches.</cd> <au>Halliwell.</au> -- <col><b>Pigeon
house</b></col>, <cd>a dovecote.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon pea</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the seed of <spn>Cajanus Indicus</spn>; a kind of
pulse used for food in the East and West Indies; also, the plant
itself.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon plum</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
edible drupes of two West African species of <gen>Chrysobalanus</gen>
(<spn>Chrysobalanus ellipticus</spn> and <spn>Chrysobalanus
luteus</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon tremex</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Tremex</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon
wood</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name in the West Indies for the
wood of several very different kinds of trees, species of
<gen>Dipholis</gen>, <gen>Diospyros</gen>, and
<gen>Coccoloba</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pigeon woodpecker</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the flicker.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie
pigeon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The upland
plover</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The golden plover.</cd> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1085 --></p>

<p><ent>Pigeon</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pluck; to
fleece; to swindle by tricks in gambling.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He's <qex>pigeoned</qex> and undone.</q>
<rj><qau>Observer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeon-breasted</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon-breast`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
breast like a pigeon, -- the sternum being so prominent as to constitute
a deformity; chicken-breasted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeonfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon*foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The dove's-foot geranium (<spn>Geranium molle</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeon-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Timid; easily
frightened; chicken-hearted.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeonhole</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon*hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small
compartment in a desk or case for the keeping of letters, documents,
etc.; -- so called from the resemblance of a row of them to the
compartments in a dovecote.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeonhole</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon*hole`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To place in the pigeonhole
of a case or cabinet; hence, to put away; to lay aside indefinitely;
<as>as, to <ex>pigeonhole</ex> a letter or a report</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeon-livered</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon-liv`ered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pigeon-hearted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeonry</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place for
pigeons; a dovecote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeontoed</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon*toed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the toes
turned in.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigeonwing</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"geon*wing`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
wing of a pigeon, or a wing like it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An old mode of dressing men's side hair in a form
likened to a pigeon's wings; also, a wig similarly shaped.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Dancing)</fld> <def>A fancy step executed by
jumping and striking the legs together; <as>as, to cut a
<ex>pigeonwing</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A certain fancy figure in skating.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Tempering)</fld> <def>A color, brown shaded with
purple, coming between dark brown and light blue in the table of colors
in drawing the temper of hardened steel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"-eyed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having small,
deep-set eyes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigfish</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of several species of salt-water grunts; --
called also <altname>hogfish</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
sculpin. The name is also applied locally to several other
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
marine fish (<spn>Scorp<ae/na porcus</spn>), native of Europe. It is
reddish brown, mottled with dark brown and black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigg</ent><br/
<hw>Pigg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A piggin. See 1st
<er>Pig</er>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piggery</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"ger*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Piggeries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A place where swine are
kept.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piggin</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"gin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot.; cf. Gael.
<ets>pigean</ets>, dim. of <ets>pigeadh</ets>, <ets>pige</ets>, an
earthen jar, pitcher, or pot, Ir. <ets>pigin</ets>, <ets>pighead</ets>,
W. <ets>piccyn</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small wooden pail or tub with an
upright stave for a handle, -- often used as a dipper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piggish</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"gish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to, or like,
a pig; greedy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig-headed</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"-head`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a head
like a pig; hence, figuratively: stupidity obstinate; perverse;
stubborn.</def> <au>B. Jonson.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pig"-head`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pight</ent><br/
<hw>Pight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Pitch</er>, to throw; -- used also adjectively</mord>. <def>Pitched;
fixed; determined.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[His horse] <qex>pight</qex> him on the pommel of his head.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I found him <qex>pight</qex> to do it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pightel</ent><br/
<hw>Pigh"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pight</er>,
<er>Picle</er>.]</ety> <def>A small inclosure.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>pightle</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig-jawed</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"-jawed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the upper jaw projecting beyond the lower, with the upper
incisors in advance of the lower; -- said of dogs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigmean</ent><br/
<hw>Pig*me"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pygmean</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigment</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pigmentum</ets>, fr. the root of <ets>pingere</ets> to paint: cf.
F. <ets>pigment</ets>. See <er>Paint</er>, and cf. <er>Pimento</er>,
<er>Orpiment</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any material from which a
dye, a paint, or the like, may be prepared; particularly, the refined
and purified coloring matter ready for mixing with an appropriate
vehicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Any one of the colored
substances found in animal and vegetable tissues and fluids, as
bilirubin, urobilin, chlorophyll, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Wine flavored with species and honey.</def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pigment cell</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>a small
cell containing coloring matter, as the pigmented epithelial cells of
the choroid and iris, or the pigmented connective tissue cells in the
skin of fishes, reptiles, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigmentary</ent><br/
<ent>Pigmental</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pig*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pig"men*ta*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to pigments;
furnished with pigments.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pigmentary degeneration</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>a morbid condition in which an undue amount of pigment is deposited
in the tissues.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigmentation</ent><br/
<hw>Pig`men*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A deposition, esp. an excessive deposition,
of coloring matter; <as>as, <ex>pigmentation</ex> of the
liver</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigmented</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"ment*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Colored;
specifically <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, filled or imbued with pigment; <as>as,
<ex>pigmented</ex> epithelial cells; <ex>pigmented</ex>
granules.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigmentous</ent><br/
<hw>Pig*men"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pigmental.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigmy</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pygmy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pigmy falcon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Falconet</er>, 2 <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pignerate</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"ner*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pigneratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pignerate</ets> to pledge.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To pledge or pawn.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to receive in pawn, as a pawnbroker does.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pignoration</ent><br/
<hw>Pig`no*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>pignoratio</ets>, L. <ets>pigneratio</ets>, fr.
<ets>pignerate</ets> to pledge, fr. <ets>pignus</ets>, gen.
<ets>-ous</ets> and <ets>-eris</ets>, a pledge, a pawn: cf. F.
<ets>pignoration</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pledging or
pawning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>The taking of cattle doing
damage, by way of pledge, till satisfaction is made.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pignorative</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"no*ra*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pignoratif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pledging, pawning.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pignus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pig"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pignora</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Rom.
Law)</fld> <def>A pledge or pawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pignut</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"nut</hw> <pr>(p<icr/g"n<ucr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>See <er>Groundnut</er>
<sd>(d)</sd>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The bitter-flavored nut of a
species of hickory (<spn>Carya glabra</spn> syn. <spn>Carya
porcina</spn>); also, the tree itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigpen</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"pen`</hw> <pr>(p<icr/g"p<ecr/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pen,
or sty, for pigs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigskin</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"skin`</hw> <pr>(p<icr/g"sk<icr/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The skin of a pig, -- used chiefly for making saddles;
hence, a colloquial or slang term for a saddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A football; -- so called because the covering is
often made of pigskin.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigsney</ent><br/
<hw>Pigs"ney</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. a dim. of Dan.
<ets>pige</ets> a girl, or Sw. <ets>piga</ets>; or from E. <ets>pig's
eye</ets>.]</ety> <def>A word of endearment for a girl or woman.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pigsnie</asp>,
<asp>pigsny</asp>, etc.]</altsp> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pig-sticking</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"-stick`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Boar hunting;
-- so called by Anglo-Indians.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigsty</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"sty`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pigsties</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A pigpen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigtail</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The tail
of a pig.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Hair Dressing)</fld> <def>A cue, or queue.</def>
<rj><au>J. & H. Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A kind of twisted chewing tobacco.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tobacco he usually cheweth, called <qex>pigtail</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigtailed</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"tailed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a tail like
a pig's; <as>as, the <ex>pigtailed</ex> baboon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigweed</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
name of several annual weeds. See <er>Goosefoot</er>, and
<er>Lamb's-quarters</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pigwidgeon</ent><br/
<hw>Pig"wid`geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pigwidgin</ets> and <ets>pigwiggen</ets>.]</ety> <def>A cant word
for anything petty or small. It is used by Drayton as the name of a
fairy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pika</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
one of several species of tailless rodents of the genus
<gen>Ochotona</gen> (formerly <gen>Lagomys</gen>), resembling small
rabbits, but with short ears alnd legs. They inhabit the high mountains
of Asia and America. Called also <altname>calling hare</altname>, and
<altname>crying hare</altname> and <altname>rock rabbit</altname>. See
<er>Chief hare</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pike</ent><br/
<hw>Pike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pique</ets>;
perhaps of Celtic origin; cf. W. <ets>pig</ets> a prick, a point, beak,
Arm. <ets>pik</ets> pick. But cf. also L. <ets>picus</ets> woodpecker
(see <er>Pie</er> magpie), and E. <ets>spike</ets>. Cf. <er>Pick</er>,
<pos>n. & v.</pos>, <er>Peak</er>, <er>Pique</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A foot soldier's weapon, consisting of a long
wooden shaft or staff, with a pointed steel head. It is now superseded
by the bayonet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pointed head or spike; esp., one in the center of
a shield or target.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A hayfork.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A pick.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wright. Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A pointed or peaked hill.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A large haycock.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A turnpike; a toll bar.</def>
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <pos>sing. & pl.</pos> <def>A large
fresh-water fish (<spn>Esox lucius</spn>), found in Europe and America,
highly valued as a food fish; -- called also
<altname>pickerel</altname>, <altname>gedd</altname>,
<altname>luce</altname>, and <altname>jack</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Blue pike</xex>, <xex>grass pike</xex>, <xex>green
pike</xex>, <xex>wall-eyed pike</xex>, and <xex>yellow pike</xex>, are
names, not of true pike, but of the wall-eye. See
<er>Wall-eye</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Gar pike</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Gar</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pike perch</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any fresh-water
fish of the genus <gen>Stizostedion</gen> (formerly
<gen>Lucioperca</gen>). See <er>Wall-eye</er>, and <er>Sauger</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pike pole</b></col>, <cd>a long pole with a pike in one end,
used in directing floating logs.</cd> -- <col><b>Pike whale</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a finback whale of the North Atlantic
(<spn>Bal<ae/noptera rostrata</spn>), having an elongated snout; --
called also <altname>piked whale</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
pike</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the lizard fish.</cd> --
<col><b>Sea pike</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the garfish
<sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piked</ent><br/
<hw>Piked</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with a pike;
ending in a point; peaked; pointed.</def> <ldquo/With their
<xex>piked</xex> targets bearing them down.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pike-devant</ent><br/
<hw>Pike`-de*vant"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pike</ets> point (fr. F. <ets>pique</ets>) + F.
<ets>devant</ets> before.]</ety> <def>A pointed beard.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pikelin</ent><br/
<ent>Pikelet</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pike"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pike"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A light, thin cake or muffin.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pikeman</ent><br/
<hw>Pike"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pikeman</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A soldier
armed with a pike.</def> <rj><au>Knolles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A miner who works with a pick.</def>
<rj><au>Beaconsfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A keeper of a turnpike gate.</def> <rj><au>T.
Hughes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pikestaff</ent><br/
<hw>Pike"staff`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
staff, or shaft, of a pike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A staff with a spike in the lower end, to guard
against slipping.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piketail</ent><br/
<hw>Pike"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pintail</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pikrolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pik"ro*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Picrolite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilage</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pelage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilaster</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*las"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pilastre</ets>, It. <ets>pilastro</ets>, LL. <ets>pilastrum</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>pila</ets> a pillar. See <er>Pillar</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An upright architectural member right-angled in
plan, constructionally a pier (See <er>Pier</er>, 1 <sd>(b)</sd>), but
architecturally corresponding to a column, having capital, shaft, and
base to agree with those of the columns of the same order. In most cases
the projection from the wall is one third of its width, or
less.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilastered</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*las"tered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with
pilasters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilau</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*lau"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pillau</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilch</ent><br/
<hw>Pilch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>pylce</ets>,
<ets>pylece</ets>, LL. <ets>pellicia</ets>. See <er>Pelisse</er>, and
<er>Pelt</er> skin.]</ety> <def>A gown or case of skin, or one trimmed
or lined with fur.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilchard</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"chard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>pilseir</ets>, W. <ets>pilcod</ets> minnows.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small European food fish (<spn>Clupea
pilchardus</spn>) resembling the herring, but thicker and rounder. It is
sometimes taken in great numbers on the coast of England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fools are as like husbands as <qex>pilchards</qex> are to
herrings.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilcher</ent><br/
<hw>Pilch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pilch</er>.]</ety> <def>A scabbard, as of a sword.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilcher</ent><br/
<hw>Pilch"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
pilchard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilcrow</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"crow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of
<er>Paragraph</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>a paragraph mark,
<para/.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile</ent><br/
<hw>Pile</hw> <pr>(p<imac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pilus</ets> hair. Cf. <er>Peruke</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
hair; hence, the fiber of wool, cotton, and the like; also, the nap when
thick or heavy, as of carpeting and velvet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Velvet soft, or plush with shaggy <qex>pile</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A covering of hair or
fur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile</ent><br/
<hw>Pile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pilum</ets> javelin. See
<er>Pile</er> a stake.]</ety> <def>The head of an arrow or spear.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile</ent><br/
<hw>Pile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>p<imac/l</ets> arrow, stake,
L. <ets>pilum</ets> javelin; but cf. also L. <ets>pila</ets>
pillar.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large stake, or piece of timber,
pointed and driven into the earth, as at the bottom of a river, or in a
harbor where the ground is soft, for the support of a building, a pier,
or other superstructure, or to form a cofferdam, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Tubular iron piles are now much used.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pile</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>One of the ordinaries or subordinaries having the form of a wedge,
usually placed palewise, with the broadest end uppermost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pile bridge</b></col>, <cd>a bridge of which the roadway
is supported on piles.</cd> -- <col><b>Pile cap</b></col>, <cd>a beam
resting upon and connecting the heads of piles.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Pile driver</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pile
engine</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an apparatus for driving down piles,
consisting usually of a high frame, with suitable appliances for raising
to a height (by animal or steam power, the explosion of gunpowder, etc.)
a heavy mass of iron, which falls upon the pile.</cd> -- <col><b>Pile
dwelling</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Lake dwelling</cref>, under
<er>Lake</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pile plank</b></col> <fld>(Hydraul.
Eng.)</fld>, <cd>a thick plank used as a pile in sheet piling. See
<cref>Sheet piling</cref>, under <er>Piling</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pneumatic pile</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Pneumatic</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Screw pile</b></col>, <cd>one with a screw at the lower end,
and sunk by rotation aided by pressure.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile</ent><br/
<hw>Pile</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To drive piles into; to fill with
piles; to strengthen with piles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sheet-pile</b></col>, <cd>to make sheet piling in or
around. See <cref>Sheet piling</cref>, under 2nd
<er>Piling</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile</ent><br/
<hw>Pile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pile</ets>, L.
<ets>pila</ets> a pillar, a pier or mole of stone. Cf.
<er>Pillar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mass of things heaped
together; a heap; <as>as, a <ex>pile</ex> of stones; a <ex>pile</ex> of
wood.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mass formed in layers; <as>as, a <ex>pile</ex> of
shot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A funeral pile; a pyre.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A large building, or mass of buildings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pile</qex> o'erlooked the town and drew the fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Iron Manuf.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Fagot</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A vertical series of alternate
disks of two dissimilar metals, as copper and zinc, laid up with disks
of cloth or paper moistened with acid water between them, for producing
a current of electricity; -- commonly called <altname>Volta's
pile</altname>, <altname>voltaic pile</altname>, or <altname>galvanic
pile</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term is sometimes applied to other forms of
apparatus designed to produce a current of electricity, or as synonymous
with <xex>battery</xex>; as, for instance, to an apparatus for
generating a current of electricity by the action of heat, usually
called a <xex>thermopile</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>pile</ets> pile, an engraved die, L.
<ets>pila</ets> a pillar.]</ety> <def>The reverse of a coin. See
<er>Reverse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cross and pile</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Cross</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Dry pile</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Dry</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile</ent><br/
<hw>Pile</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Piled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay or throw into a
pile or heap; to heap up; to collect into a mass; to accumulate; to
amass; -- often with <xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>pile</ex> up
wood</as>.</def> <ldquo/Hills <xex>piled</xex> on hills.<rdquo/
<au>Dryden.</au> <ldquo/Life <xex>piled</xex> on life.<rdquo/
<au>Tennyson.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The labor of an age in <qex>piled</qex> stones.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover with heaps; or in great abundance; to fill
or overfill; to load.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To pile arms</b></col> <col><b>To pile
muskets</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>to place three guns
together so that they may stand upright, supporting each other; to stack
arms.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pileated</ent><br/
<ent>Pileate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pi"le*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pi"le*a`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pileatus</ets>, fr.
<ets>pileus</ets> a felt cap or hat.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the
form of a cap for the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a crest covering the
pileus, or whole top of the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pileated woodpecker</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
large American woodpecker (<spn>Ceophloeus pileatus</spn>). It is black,
with a bright red pointed crest. Called also <altname>logcock</altname>,
and <altname>woodcock</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piled</ent><br/
<hw>Piled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 2d
<er>Pile</er>.]</ety> <def>Having a pile or point; pointed.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Magus threw a spear well
<xex>piled</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piled</ent><br/
<hw>Piled</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 1d <er>Pile</er>.]</ety>
<def>Having a pile or nap.</def> <ldquo/Three-<xex>piled</xex>
velvet.<rdquo/ <rj><au>L. Barry (1611).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piled</ent><br/
<hw>Piled</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 6d <er>Pile</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Iron Manuf.)</fld> <def>Formed from a pile or fagot; <as>as,
<ex>piled</ex> iron</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pileiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*le"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pileus</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form
of a pileus or cap; pileate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilement</ent><br/
<hw>Pile"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pile</er>
to lay into a heap.]</ety> <def>An accumulation; a heap.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1086 --></p>

<p><ent>Pilentum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*len"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pilenta</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>An easy chariot or carriage, used by Roman ladies,
and in which the vessels, etc., for sacred rites were carried.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pileorhiza</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`le*o*rhi"za</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pilorhiz<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a cap + <?/ root.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A cap of
cells which covers the growing extremity of a root; a rootcap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pileous</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"le*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pilous</er>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of, or covered with, hair;
hairy; pilose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piler</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who places things
in a pile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piles</ent><br/
<hw>Piles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pila</ets>
a ball. Cf. <er>Pill</er> a medicine.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
small, troublesome tumors or swellings about the anus and lower part of
the rectum which are technically called <altname>hemorrhoids</altname>.
See <er>Hemorrhoids</er>. <note>[The singular
<singw><it>pile</it></singw> is sometimes used.]</note></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blind piles</b></col>, <cd>hemorrhoids which do not
bleed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pileus</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"le*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pilei</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a felt cap.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A kind of skull cap of
felt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The expanded upper portion of many
of the fungi. See <er>Mushroom</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The top of the head of a bird,
from the bill to the nape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pileworm</ent><br/
<hw>Pile"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The teredo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pile-worn</ent><br/
<hw>Pile"-worn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the pile
worn off; threadbare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilewort</ent><br/
<hw>Pile"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant (<spn>Ranunculus Ficaria</spn> of Linn<ae/us) whose tuberous roots
have been used in poultices as a specific for the piles.</def>
<rj><au>Forsyth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilfer</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pilfered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pilfering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pelfrer</ets>. See <er>Pelf</er>.]</ety> <def>To steal in small
quantities, or articles of small value; to practice petty
theft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilfer</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"fer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To take by petty theft; to
filch; to steal little by little.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And not a year but <qex>pilfers</qex> as he goes<br/
Some youthful grace that age would gladly keep.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilferer</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"fer*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pilfers; a
petty thief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilfering</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"fer*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Thieving in a small way.</def>
<au>Shak.</au> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>Petty theft.</def></def2> --
<wordforms><wf>Pil"fer*ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilfery</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"fer*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Petty theft.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilgarlic</ent><br/
<hw>Pil*gar"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>One who has lost his hair by disease; a sneaking
fellow, or one who is hardly used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilgrim</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"grim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pilgrim</ets>, <ets>pelgrim</ets>, <ets>pilegrim</ets>,
<ets>pelegrim</ets>; cf. D. <ets>pelgrim</ets>, OHG.
<ets>piligr<imac/m</ets>, G. <ets>pilger</ets>, F.
<ets>p<egrave/lerin</ets>, It. <ets>pellegrino</ets>; all fr. L.
<ets>peregrinus</ets> a foreigner, fr. <ets>pereger</ets> abroad;
<ets>per</ets> through + <ets>ager</ets> land, field. See <er>Per-</er>,
and <er>Acre</er>, and cf. <er>Pelerine</er>, <er>Peregrine</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A wayfarer; a wanderer; a traveler; a
stranger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strangers and <qex>pilgrims</qex> on the earth.</q> <rj><qau>Heb.
xi. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who travels far, or in strange lands, to visit
some holy place or shrine as a devotee; <as>as, a <ex>pilgrim</ex> to
Loretto; Canterbury <ex>pilgrims</ex></as>. See <er>Palmer</er>.</def>
<rj><au>P. Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilgrim</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"grim</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a pilgrim, or
pilgrims; making pilgrimages.</def> <ldquo/With <xex>pilgrim</xex>
steps.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pilgrim fathers</b></col>, <cd>a name popularly given to
the one hundred and two English colonists who landed from the Mayflower
and made the first settlement in New England at Plymouth in 1620. They
were separatists from the Church of England, and most of them had
sojourned in Holland.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilgrim</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"grim</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To journey; to wander; to
ramble.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Grew. Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilgrimage</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"grim*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pilgrimage</ets>, <ets>pelgrinage</ets>; cf. F.
<ets>p<egrave/lerinage</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The journey of a
pilgrim; a long journey; especially, a journey to a shrine or other
sacred place. Fig., the journey of human life.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The days of the years of my <qex>pilgrimage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xlvii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tedious and wearisome time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In prison hast thou spent a <qex>pilgrimage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Journey; tour; excursion. See
<er>Journey</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilgrimize</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"grim*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To wander as a
pilgrim; to go on a pilgrimage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*lid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pildia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, dim. of
<?/ a cap.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The free-swimming, hat-shaped
larva of certain nemertean worms. It has no resemblance to its parent,
and the young worm develops in its interior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*lif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Piliferous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Mammalia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pilus</ets> hair + <ets>-ferous</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pilif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Bearing a single
slender bristle, or hair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Beset with hairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pilus</ets> hair + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Resembling hairs or down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piligerous</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*lig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pilus</ets> hair + <ets>-gerous</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pilig<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <def>Bearing hair; covered with hair
or down; piliferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piling</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pile</er> a
heap.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of heaping up.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Iron Manuf.)</fld> <def>The process of building up,
heating, and working, fagots, or piles, to form bars, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piling</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pile</er> a stake.]</ety>
<def>A series of piles; piles considered collectively; <as>as, the
<ex>piling</ex> of a bridge</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pug piling</b></col>, <cd>sheet piles connected together
at the edges by dovetailed tongues and grooves.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheet
piling</b></col>, <cd>a series of piles made of planks or half logs
driven edge to edge, -- used to form the walls of cofferdams,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pill</ent><br/
<hw>Pill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Peel</er> skin,
or <er>Pillion</er>.]</ety> <def>The peel or skin.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Some be covered over with crusts, or hard
<xex>pills</xex>, as the locusts.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pill</ent><br/
<hw>Pill</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be peeled; to peel off in
flakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pill</ent><br/
<hw>Pill</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L. <ets>pilare</ets> to
deprive of hair, and E. <ets>pill</ets>, n. (above).]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To deprive of hair; to make bald.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To peel; to make by removing the skin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Jacob] <qex>pilled</qex> white streaks . . . in the rods.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxx. 37.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pill</ent><br/
<hw>Pill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pilled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>piller</ets>,
L. <ets>pilare</ets>; cf. It. <ets>pigliare</ets> to take. Cf.
<er>Peel</er> to plunder.]</ety> <def>To rob; to plunder; to pillage; to
peel. See <er>Peel</er>, to plunder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pillers and robbers were come in to the field to <qex>pill</qex>
and to rob.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Malroy.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pill</ent><br/
<hw>Pill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pilute</ets>, L.
<ets>pilula</ets> a pill, little ball, dim. of L. <ets>pila</ets> a
ball. Cf. <er>Piles</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A medicine in the form
of a little ball, or small round mass, to be swallowed whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively, something offensive or nauseous which
must be accepted or endured.</def><-- esp., as bitter pill -->
<rj><au>Udall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pill beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any small
beetle of the genus <gen>Byrrhus</gen>, having a rounded body, with the
head concealed beneath the thorax.</cd> -- <col><b>Pill bug</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any terrestrial isopod of the genus
<gen>Armadillo</gen>, having the habit of rolling itself into a ball
when disturbed. Called also <altname>pill wood
louse</altname>.</cd></cs><-- poison pill Fig., anything accompanying a
desirable object or action, which makes it deleterious to him who
accepts it; esp. (Finance) a provision in the regulations or financial
structure (as indebtedness) of a company which makes the company
undesirable as a target for a hostile takeover --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillage</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>piller</ets> to plunder. See <er>Pill</er> to plunder.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pillaging; robbery.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is taken from another or others by open
force, particularly and chiefly from enemies in war; plunder; spoil;
booty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which <qex>pillage</qex> they with merry march bring home.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Plunder; rapine; spoil; depredation.</syn>
<usage> -- <er>Pillage</er>, <er>Plunder</er>. <xex>Pillage</xex> refers
particularly to the act of stripping the sufferers of their goods, while
<xex>plunder</xex> refers to the removal of the things thus taken; but
the words are freely interchanged.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillage</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lage</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pillaged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pillaging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To strip of money
or goods by open violence; to plunder; to spoil; to lay waste; <as>as,
to <ex>pillage</ex> the camp of an enemy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mummius . . . took, <qex>pillaged</qex>, and burnt their city.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillage</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lage</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take spoil; to plunder; to
ravage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were suffered to <qex>pillage</qex> wherever they went.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillager</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"la*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pillages.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillar</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>piler</ets>F. <ets>pilier</ets>, LL. <ets>pilare</ets>,
<ets>pilarium</ets>, <ets>pilarius</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pila</ets> a
pillar. See <er>Pile</er> a heap.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The general
and popular term for a firm, upright, insulated support for a
superstructure; a pier, column, or post; also, a column or shaft not
supporting a superstructure, as one erected for a monument or an
ornament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jacob set a <qex>pillar</qex> upon her grave.</q> <rj><qau>Gen.
xxxv. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The place . . . vast and proud,<br/
Supported by a hundred <qex>pillars</qex> stood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively, that which resembles such a pillar in
appearance, character, or office; a supporter or mainstay; <as>as, the
<ex>Pillars</ex> of Hercules; a <ex>pillar</ex> of the state.</as></def>
<ldquo/You are a well-deserving <xex>pillar</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By day a cloud, by night a <qex>pillar</qex> of fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A portable ornamental column,
formerly carried before a cardinal, as emblematic of his support to the
church.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>The center of the volta, ring, or
manege ground, around which a horse turns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>From pillar to post</b></col>, <cd>hither and thither; to
and fro; from one place or predicament to another; backward and
forward.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pillar saint</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Stylite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pillars of the
fauces</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Fauces</er>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillar</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lar</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Having a support
in the form of a pillar, instead of legs; <as>as, a <ex>pillar</ex>
drill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillar-block</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lar-block`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See under
<er>Pillow</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillared</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lared</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Supported or
ornamented by pillars; resembling a pillar, or pillars.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>pillared</xex> arches.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W. Scott.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Pillared</xex> flame.<rdquo/ <au>Thomson.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillaret</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lar*et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little
pillar.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillarist</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lar*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Stylite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillau</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pil*lau"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per. & Turk.
<ets>pilau</ets>.]</ety> <def>An Oriental dish consisting of rice boiled
with mutton, fat, or butter.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pilau</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilled</ent><br/
<hw>Pilled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 3rd
<er>Pill</er>.]</ety> <def>Stripped of hair; scant of hair; bald.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pilled</xex> beard.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilled-garlic</ent><br/
<hw>Pilled"-gar"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pilgarlic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piller</ent><br/
<hw>Pill"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pills or
plunders.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillery</ent><br/
<hw>Pill"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pilleries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Plunder; pillage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillion</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ir.
<ets>pillin</ets>, <ets>pilliun</ets> (akin to Gael. <ets>pillean</ets>,
<ets>pillin</ets>), fr. Ir. & Gael. <ets>pill</ets>, <ets>peall</ets>, a
skin or hide, prob. fr. L. <ets>pellis</ets>. See <er>Pell</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <er>Fell</er> skin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A panel or
cushion saddle; the under pad or cushion of saddle; esp., a pad or
cushion put on behind a man's saddle, on which a woman may
ride.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His [a soldier's] shank <qex>pillion</qex> without stirrups.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A saddle or seat behind the driver's saddle on a
motorcycle, used by passengers.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillorize</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lo*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To set in, or
punish with, the pillory; to pillory.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillory</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lo*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pillories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>pilori</ets>; cf.
Pr. <ets>espitlori</ets>, LL. <ets>piloricum</ets>,
<ets>pilloricum</ets>, <ets>pellericum</ets>, <ets>pellorium</ets>,
<ets>pilorium</ets>, <ets>spilorium</ets>; perhaps from a derivative of
L. <ets>speculari</ets> to look around, observe. Cf.
<er>Speculate</er>.]</ety> <def>A frame of adjustable boards erected on
a post, and having holes through which the head and hands of an offender
were thrust so as to be exposed in front of it.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillory</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lo*ry</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pilloried</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pillorying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pilorier</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To set in, or punish with,
the pillory.</def> <ldquo/Hungering for Puritans to
<xex>pillory</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively, to expose to public scorn.</def>
<rj><au>Gladstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillow</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pilwe</ets>,
AS. <ets>pyle</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pilvinus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Anything used to support the head of a person when reposing;
especially, a sack or case filled with feathers, down, hair, or other
soft material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Resty sloth] finds the down <qex>pillow</qex> hard.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A piece of metal or wood, forming
a support to equalize pressure; a brass; a pillow block.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A block under the inner end of a
bowsprit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A kind of plain, coarse fustian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Lace pillow</b></col>, <cd>a cushion used in making
hand-wrought lace.</cd> -- <col><b>Pillow bier</b></col> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pilwebere</ets>; cf. LG. <ets>b<uum/re</ets> a pillowcase]</ety>,
<cd>a pillowcase; pillow slip.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>Pillow block</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>,
<cd>a block, or standard, for supporting a journal, as of a shaft. It is
usually bolted to the frame or foundation of a machine, and is often
furnished with journal boxes, and a movable cover, or cap, for
tightening the bearings by means of bolts; -- called also
<altname>pillar block</altname>, or <altname>plumber
block</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pillow lace</b></col>, <cd>handmade
lace wrought with bobbins upon a lace pillow.</cd> -- <col><b>Pillow of
a plow</b></col>, <cd>a crosspiece of wood which serves to raise or
lower the beam.</cd> -- <col><b>Pillow sham</b></col>, <cd>an ornamental
covering laid over a pillow when not in use.</cd> -- <col><b>Pillow
slip</b></col>, <cd>a pillowcase.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillow</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pillowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pillowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To rest or lay upon,
or as upon, a pillow; to support; <as>as, to <ex>pillow</ex> the
head</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pillows</qex> his chin upon an orient wave.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillowcase</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"low*case`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A removable case
or covering for a pillow, usually of white linen or cotton
cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillowed</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"lowed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Provided with a
pillow or pillows; having the head resting on, or as on, a
pillow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pillowed</qex>on buckler cold and hard.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillow lace</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"low lace</hw>. <def>Lace made by hand with bobbins on a
pillow.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillowy</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"low*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a pillow.</def>
<rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pill-willet</ent><br/
<hw>Pill"-wil`let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from
its note.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The willet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillworm</ent><br/
<hw>Pill"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any myriapod of the genus <gen>Iulus</gen> and allied genera which
rolls up spirally; a galleyworm. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Myriapod</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pillwort</ent><br/
<hw>Pill"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Pilularia</gen>; minute aquatic
cryptograms, with small pill-shaped fruit; -- sometimes called
<altname>peppergrass</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilocarpine</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`lo*car"pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From NL.
<ets><spn>Pilocarpus pennatifolius</spn></ets> jaborandi; L.
<ets>pilus</ets> hair + Gr. <grk>karpo`s</grk> fruit: cf. F.
<ets>pilocarpine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An alkaloid
extracted from jaborandi (<spn>Pilocarpus pennatifolius</spn>) as a
white amorphous or crystalline substance which has a peculiar effect on
the vasomotor system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*lon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., sugar
loaf.]</ety> <mark>[Sp. Amer.]</mark> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A conical loaf of
sugar.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A gratuity given by tradesmen to customers settling
their accounts.</def> <mark>[Southern U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilonce</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*lon"ce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Amer. Sp. See
<er>Piloncillo</er>.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Pilon</er>.</def>
<mark>[Texas]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piloncillo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`lon*cil"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Amer. Sp.,
dim. of <ets>pilon</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Pilon</er>.</def>
<mark>[Texas]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilose</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*lose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pilosus</ets>, fr. <ets>pilus</ets> hair. See <er>Pile</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Hairy; full of, or made of, hair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heat-retaining property of the <qex>pilose</qex> covering.</q>
<rj><qau>Owen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Clothed thickly with pile or soft
down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Covered with long, slender hairs;
resembling long hairs; hairy; <as>as, <ex>pilose</ex>
pubescence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilosity</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*los"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pilosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
pilose; hairiness.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"lot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pilote</ets>,
prob. from D. <ets>peillood</ets> plummet, sounding lead;
<ets>peilen</ets>, <ets>pegelen</ets>, to sound, measure (fr. D. & G.
<ets>peil</ets>, <ets>pegel</ets>, a sort of measure, water mark) +
<ets>lood</ets> lead, akin to E. <ets>lead</ets>. The pilot, then, is
the <ets>lead</ets> man, i. e., he who throws the lead. See
<er>Pail</er>, and <er>Lead</er> a metal.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>One employed to steer a vessel; a helmsman; a
steersman.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a person duly qualified, and licensed
by authority, to conduct vessels into and out of a port, or in certain
waters, for a fixed rate of fees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Figuratively: A guide; a director of another through
a difficult or unknown course.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An instrument for detecting the compass
error.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The cowcatcher of a locomotive.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(A<eum/ronautics)</fld> <def>One who flies, or is
qualified to fly, an airplane, balloon, or other flying
machine.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A short plug at the end of a
counterbore to guide the tool. Pilots are sometimes made
interchangeable.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The heading or excavation of
relatively small dimensions, first made in the driving of a larger
tunnel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Television)</fld> <def>a filmed or taped episode of
a proposed television series, produced as an example of the series. It
may be shown only to those television broadcast executives who may
decide whether to buy the rights to the series, or aired to test viewer
reaction or to interest sponsors. Also called <altname>pilot
film</altname> or <altname>pilot tape</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pilot balloon</b></col>, <cd>a small balloon sent up in
advance of a large one, to show the direction and force of the
wind.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot bird</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A bird found near the Caribbee Islands; -- so called
because its presence indicates to mariners their approach to these
islands.</cd> <au>Crabb.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The black-bellied
plover.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pilot boat</b></col>,
<cd>a strong, fast-sailing boat used to carry and receive pilots as they
board and leave vessels.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot bread</b></col>, <cd>ship
biscuit.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot cloth</b></col>, <cd>a coarse, stout kind
of cloth for overcoats.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot engine</b></col>, <cd>a
locomotive going in advance of a train to make sure that the way is
clear.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot fish</b></col>. <fld>(Zool)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A pelagic carangoid fish (<spn>Naucrates ductor</spn>);
-- so named because it is often seen in company with a shark, swimming
near a ship, on account of which sailors imagine that it acts as a pilot
to the shark</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The rudder fish (<spn>Seriola
zonata</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot jack</b></col>, <cd>a flag or
signal hoisted by a vessel for a pilot.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot
jacket</b></col>, <cd>a pea jacket.</cd> -- <col><b>Pilot nut</b></col>
<fld>(Bridge Building)</fld>, <cd>a conical nut applied temporarily to
the threaded end of a pin, to protect the thread and guide the pin when
it is driven into a hole.</cd> <au>Waddell.</au> -- <col><b>Pilot
snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A large North
American snake (<spn>Coluber obsoleus</spn>). It is lustrous black, with
white edges to some of the scales. Called also <altname>mountain black
snake</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The pine snake.</cd> --
<col><b>Pilot whale</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Blackfish</er>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"lot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Piloted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piloting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>piloter</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To direct the course of, as
of a ship, where navigation is dangerous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: To guide, as through dangers or
difficulties.</def> <ldquo/The art of <xex>piloting</xex> a
state.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Berkeley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(A<eum/ronautics)</fld> <def>To fly, or act as pilot
of (an aircraft); to operate (an airplane).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilotage</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"lot*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pilotage</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The pilot's skill or
knowledge, as of coasts, rocks, bars, and channels.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The compensation made or allowed to a
pilot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Guidance, as by a pilot.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot balloon</ent><br/
<hw>Pilot balloon</hw>. <def>A small, unmanned balloon sent up to
indicate the direction of air currents.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot flag</ent><br/
<hw>Pilot flag</hw>. <def>The flag hoisted at the fore by a vessel
desiring a pilot, in the United States the union jack, in Great Britain
the British union jack with a white border.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilotry</ent><br/
<ent>Pilotism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pi"lot*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pi"lot*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pilotage; skill in the duties
of a pilot.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1087 --></p>

<p><ent>Pilot light</ent><br/
<ent>Pilot lamp</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pilot lamp</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>Pilot light</hw> }</mhw>.
<fld>(Elec.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small incandescent telltale lamp
on a dynamo or battery circuit to show approximately by its brightness
the voltage of the current.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a small light on an electrical device to indicate
whether the device is in operation or that the power is on.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot light</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"lot light`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a small gas
flame kept burning continuously on a stove, water heater, or other
gas-burning device, so as to allow immediate ignition of the main flame
when the gas flow is turned on.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot valve</ent><br/
<hw>Pilot valve</hw>. <fld>(Hydraulics)</fld> <def>A small hand-operated
valve to admit liquid to operate a valve difficult to turn by
hand.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilot wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Pilot wheel</hw>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A wheel, usually with
radial handles projecting from the rim, for traversing the saddle of a
machine tool, esp. an automatic machine tool, by hand.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilour</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"our</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A piller; a
plunderer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilous</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pilose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilpul</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"pul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Aramaic
<ets>pilp<umac/l</ets>; cf. Heb. <ets>pilpel</ets> to spice, to season,
dispute violently.]</ety> <def>Among the Jews, penetrating
investigation, disputation, and drawing of conclusions, esp. in Talmudic
study.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pil"pul*ist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> -- <wf>Pil`pul*is"tic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilser</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"ser</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An insect that flies
into a flame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilular</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
pills; resembling a pill or pills; <as>as, a <ex>pilular</ex>
mass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pilula</ets> a pill. See <er>Pill</er>.]</ety> <def>Like a pill;
small; insignificant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>G.
Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pilwe</ent><br/
<hw>Pil"we</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pillow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pily</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like
pile or wool.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimaric</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*mar"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>pi</ets>num <ets>mar</ets>itima, an old name for <spn>Pinum
Pinaster</spn>, a pine which yields galipot.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid found in galipot, and
isomeric with abietic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimelic</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*mel"ic</hw> <pr>(p<icr/*m<ecr/l"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>pimelh`</grk> fat.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, a substance obtained from certain
fatty substances, and subsequently shown to be a mixture of suberic and
adipic acids.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Designating the acid proper
(<chform>C5H10(CO2H)2</chform>) which is obtained from camphoric
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimelite</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"e*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pimelh`</grk> fat.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>An apple-green
mineral having a greasy feel. It is a hydrous silicate of nickel,
magnesia, aluminia, and iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piment</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pimento</er>.]</ety> <def>Wine flavored with spice or honey. See
<er>Pigment</er>, 3.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimenta</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*men"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pimento</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimento</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*men"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>pimiento</ets>, <ets>pimienta</ets>; cf. Pg. <ets>pimenta</ets>, F.
<ets>piment</ets>; all fr. L. <ets>pigmentum</ets> a paint, pigment, the
juice of plants; hence, something spicy and aromatic. See
<er>Pigment</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Allspice; --
applied both to the tree and its fruit. See <er>Allspice</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>same as <er>pimiento</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimiento</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*mien"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>The Spanish sweet pepper, the fruit of which is used as a
vegetable, to stuff olives, etc.; also the fruit itself. Also called
<altname>pimento</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimlico</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"li*co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The friar bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimola</ent><br/
<hw>Pim*o"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An olive stuffed with
a kind of sweet red pepper, or pimiento.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimp</ent><br/
<hw>Pimp</hw> <pr>(p<icr/mp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pimpant</ets> smart, sparkish; perh. akin to <ets>piper</ets> to
pipe, formerly also, to excel. Cf. <er>Pipe</er>.]</ety> <def>One who
provides gratification for the lust of others; a procurer; a
pander.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimp</ent><br/
<hw>Pimp</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pimped</conjf> <pr>(p<icr/mt; 215)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pimping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To procure women for the
gratification of others' lusts; to pander.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimpernel</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"per*nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pimprenelle</ets>; cf. Sp. <ets>pimpinela</ets>, It.
<ets>pimpinella</ets>; perh. from LL. <ets>bipinnella</ets>, for
<ets>bipinnula</ets> two-winged, equiv. to L. <ets>bipennis</ets>;
<ets>bis</ets> twice + <ets>penna</ets> feather, wing. Cf. <er>Pen</er>
a feather.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant of the genus
<gen>Anagallis</gen>, of which one species (<spn>Anagallis
arvensis</spn>) has small flowers, usually scarlet, but sometimes
purple, blue, or white, which speedily close at the approach of bad
weather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Water pimpernel</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Brookweed</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimpillo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pim"pil*lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A West Indian name for the prickly pear (<gen>Opuntia</gen>); --
called also <altname>pimploes</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimpinel</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"pi*nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pimpernel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The burnet saxifrage.
See under <er>Saxifrage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimping</ent><br/
<hw>Pimp"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>pimpelig</ets>, <ets>pimpelnd</ets>, sickly, weak.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Little; petty; pitiful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Crabbe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Puny; sickly.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimple</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>p<imac/pelian</ets> to blister; cf. L. <ets>papula</ets>
pimple.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any small acuminated
elevation of the cuticle, whether going on to suppuration or not.</def>
<ldquo/All eyes can see a <xex>pimple</xex> on her nose.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: A swelling or protuberance like a
pimple.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pimple</xex> that portends a future
sprout.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimpled</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"pled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having pimples.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimply</ent><br/
<hw>Pim"ply</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pimpled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pimpship</ent><br/
<hw>Pimp"ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office,
occupation, or persom of a pimp.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin</ent><br/
<hw>Pin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Metal Working)</fld>
<def>To peen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin</ent><br/
<hw>Pin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pen</er> to
confine, or <er>Pinfold</er>.]</ety> <def>To inclose; to confine; to
pen; to pound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin</ent><br/
<hw>Pin</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pinne</ets>, AS.
<ets>pinn</ets> a pin, peg; cf. D. <ets>pin</ets>, G. <ets>pinne</ets>,
Icel. <ets>pinni</ets>, W. <ets>pin</ets>, Gael. & Ir. <ets>pinne</ets>;
all fr. L. <ets>pinna</ets> a pinnacle, pin, feather, perhaps orig. a
different word from <ets>pinna</ets> feather. Cf. <er>Fin</er> of a
fish, <er>Pen</er> a feather.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece of wood,
metal, etc., generally cylindrical, used for fastening separate articles
together, or as a support by which one article may be suspended from
another; a peg; a bolt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With <qex>pins</qex> of adamant<br/
And chains they made all fast.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, a small, pointed and headed piece of
brass or other wire (commonly tinned), largely used for fastening
clothes, attaching papers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, a thing of small value; a trifle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . did not care a <qex>pin</qex> for her.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which resembles a pin in its form or use</def>;
as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A peg in musical instruments, for increasing or
relaxing the tension of the strings.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
linchpin.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A rolling-pin.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>A clothespin.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A short
shaft, sometimes forming a bolt, a part of which serves as a
journal.</def> See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Knuckle joint</cref>,
under <er>Knuckle</er>. <sd>(f)</sd> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>The tenon
of a dovetail joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>One of a row of pegs in the side of an ancient
drinking cup to mark how much each man should drink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The bull's eye, or center, of a target; hence, the
center.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The very <xex>pin</xex> of his
heart cleft.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Mood; humor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/In
merry <xex>pin</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Caligo. See <er>Caligo</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>An ornament, as a brooch or badge, fastened to the
clothing by a pin; <as>as, a Masonic <ex>pin</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>The leg; <as>as, to knock one off his
<ex>pins</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Banking pin</b></col> <fld>(Horol.)</fld>, <cd>a pin
against which a lever strikes, to limit its motion.</cd> -- <col><b>Pin
drill</b></col> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a drill with a central pin or
projection to enter a hole, for enlarging the hole, or for sinking a
recess for the head of a bolt, etc.; a counterbore.</cd> -- <col><b>Pin
grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Alfilaria</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pin hole</b></col>, <cd>a small hole made by a pin; hence, any
very small aperture or perforation.</cd> -- <col><b>Pin lock</b></col>,
<cd>a lock having a cylindrical bolt; a lock in which pins, arranged by
the key, are used instead of tumblers.</cd> -- <col><b>Pin
money</b></col>, <cd>an allowance of money, as that made by a husband to
his wife, for private and personal expenditure.</cd> -- <col><b>Pin
rail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a rail, usually within the
bulwarks, to hold belaying pins. Sometimes applied to the <altname>fife
rail</altname>. Called also <altname>pin rack</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pin wheel</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A contrate wheel in which
the cogs are cylindrical pins</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Fireworks)</fld>
<cd>A small coil which revolves on a common pin and makes a wheel of
yellow or colored fire.</cd></cs> <-- a toy with lightweight, usually
brightly colored vanes, as of plastic, which revolve on a pin at the end
of a stick, when acted on by a wind --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin</ent><br/
<hw>Pin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pinned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pinning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Pin</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To fasten with, or as with, a pin; to join;
<as>as, to <ex>pin</ex> a garment; to <ex>pin</ex> boards
together.</as></def> <ldquo/As if she would <xex>pin</xex> her to her
heart.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pin one's faith upon</b></col>, <cd>to depend upon; to
trust to.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pi<ntil/a</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`<ntil/a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., orig.,
pineapple, pine cone.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
pineapple.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Pi<ntil/a cloth or the fiber of which
it is made.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pina</ent><br/
<sn>2.</sn> Also <hw>Pi"na</hw> <pr>(<it>pron. also</it> <?/)</pr>.
<fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A cone of silver amalgam prepared for
retorting; also, the residuary cone of spongy silver left after the
retorting.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinacate bug</ent><br/
<hw>Pin`a*ca"te bug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Orig. uncert.]</ety>
<def>Any of several clumsy, wingless beetles of the genus
<gen>Eleodes</gen>, found in the Pacific States.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pi<ntil/a cloth</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"<ntil/a cloth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See
<er>Pi<ntil/a</er>.]</ety> <def>A fine fabric for scarfs, handkerchiefs,
embroidery, etc., woven from the fiber obtained from the leaf of the
sterile pineapple plant. It is delicate, soft, and transparent, with a
tinge of pale yellow.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinacoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"a*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, a
tablet + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A plane
parallel to two of the crystalline axes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pina colada</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"<ntil/a co*la"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Sp., strained
pineapple.]</ety> <def>A sweet mixed drink made from rum, pineapple
juice, and cream of coconut, mixed with crushed ice.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinacolin</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*nac"o*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pinac</ets>one + L. <ets>ol</ets>eum oil.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A colorless oily liquid related to the ketones,
and obtained by the decomposition of pinacone; hence, by extension, any
one of the series of which pinacolin proper is the type.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pinacoline</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinacone</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"a*cone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/, <?/,
a tablet. So called because it unites with water so as to form
tablet-shaped crystals.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white
crystalline substance related to the glycols, and made from acetone;
hence, by extension, any one of a series of substances of which pinacone
proper is the type.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pinakone</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinacotheca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pin`a*co*the"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinacotheca</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/; <?/, <?/, a picture + <?/
repisitory.]</ety> <def>A picture gallery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinafore</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"a*fore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pin</ets> +
<ets>afore</ets>.]</ety> <def>An apron for a child to protect the front
part of dress; a tier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinakothek</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pin"a*ko*thek`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.]</ety>
<def>Pinacotheca.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinaster</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*nas"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>pinus</ets> a pine.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of pine
(<spn>Pinus Pinaster</spn>) growing in Southern Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinax</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"nax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pinaces</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/
tablet.]</ety> <def>A tablet; a register; hence, a list or scheme
inscribed on a tablet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pince-nez</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pince`-nez"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pincer</ets> to pinch + <ets>nez</ets> nose.]</ety> <def>Eyeglasses
kept on the nose by a spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pincers</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"cers</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pince</ets> pinchers, fr. <ets>pincer</ets> to pinch. See
<er>Pinch</er>, <er>Pinchers</er>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pinchers</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinch</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pinched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pinching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>pincer</ets>, probably
fr. OD. <ets>pitsen</ets> to pinch; akin to G. <ets>pfetzen</ets> to
cut, pinch; perhaps of Celtic origin. Cf. <er>Piece</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To press hard or squeeze between the ends of the
fingers, between teeth or claws, or between the jaws of an instrument;
to squeeze or compress, as between any two hard bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to seize; to grip; to bite; -- said of
animals.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [the hound] <qex>pinched</qex> and pulled her down.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To plait.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full seemly her wimple <qex>ipinched</qex> was.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Figuratively: To cramp; to straiten; to oppress; to
starve; to distress; <as>as, to be <ex>pinched</ex> for
money</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Want of room . . . <qex>pinching</qex> a whole nation.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To move, as a railroad car, by prying the wheels
with a pinch. See <er>Pinch</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To seize by way of theft; to steal; to lift.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Robert Barr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>to catch; to arrest (a criminal).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinch</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To act with pressing
force; to compress; to squeeze; <as>as, the shoe
<ex>pinches</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Hunt.)</fld> <def>To take hold; to grip, as a dog
does.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To spare; to be niggardly; to be covetous.</def>
<rj><au>Gower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wretch whom avarice bids to <qex>pinch</qex> and spare.</q>
<rj><qau>Franklin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pinch at</b></col>, <cd>to find fault with; to take
exception to.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinch</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A close compression, as
with the ends of the fingers, or with an instrument; a nip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>As much as may be taken between the finger and
thumb; any very small quantity; <as>as, a <ex>pinch</ex> of
snuff</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pian; pang.</def> <ldquo/Necessary's sharp
<xex>pinch</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A lever having a projection at one end, acting as a
fulcrum, -- used chiefly to roll heavy wheels, etc. Called also
<altname>pinch bar</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>At a pinch</b></col>, <col><b>On a
pinch</b></col></mcol>, <cd>in an emergency; <as>as, he could <ex>on a
pinch</ex> read a little Latin</as>.</cd><-- also in a pinch
--></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchbeck</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"beck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be from
the name of the inventor; cf. It. <ets>prencisbecco</ets>.]</ety>
<def>An alloy of copper and zinc, resembling gold; a yellow metal,
composed of about three ounces of zinc to a pound of copper. It is much
used as an imitation of gold in the manufacture of cheap
jewelry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchbeck</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"beck</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made of pinchbeck; sham; cheap;
spurious; unreal.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pinchbeck</xex> throne.<rdquo/
<rj><au>J. A. Symonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchcock</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A clamp on a
flexible pipe to regulate the flow of a fluid through the
pipe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchem</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"chem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The European blue titmouse.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pincher</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, pinches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchers</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"ers</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pinch</er>.]</ety> <def>An instrument having two handles and two
grasping jaws working on a pivot; -- used for griping things to be held
fast, drawing nails, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This spelling is preferable to <xex>pincers</xex>, both
on account of its derivation from the English <xex>pinch</xex>, and
because it represents the common pronunciation.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchfist</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"fist`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A closefisted
person; a miser.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinching</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Compressing; nipping; griping;
niggardly; <as>as, <ex>pinching</ex> cold; a <ex>pinching</ex>
parsimony.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pinching bar</b></col>, <cd>a pinch bar. See
<er>Pinch</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Pinching
nut</b></col>, <cd>a check nut. See under <er>Check</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pinching way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinchpenny</ent><br/
<hw>Pinch"pen`ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A miserly
person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pincoffin</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"coff*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Pincoff</ets>, an English manufacturer.]</ety> <def>A commercial
preparation of garancin, yielding fine violet tints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pincpinc</ent><br/
<hw>Pinc"pinc`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Named from its
note.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An African wren warbler.
(<spn>Drymoica textrix</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pincushion</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"cush`ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small cushion,
in which pins may be stuck for use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pindar</ent><br/
<ent>Pindal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pin"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pin"dar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>piendel</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The peanut (<spn>Arachis hypog<ae/a</spn>); -- so
called in the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pindaric</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*dar"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pindaricus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ (L. <ets>Pindarus</ets>) Pindar:
cf. F. <ets>pindarique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Pindar,
the Greek lyric poet; after the style and manner of Pindar; <as>as,
<ex>Pindaric</ex> odes</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Pindaric ode.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pindarical</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*dar"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pindaric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Too extravagant and <qex>Pindarical</qex> for prose.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pindarism</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"dar*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Imitation of
Pindar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pindarist</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"dar*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who imitates
Pindar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinder</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>pyndan</ets>
to pen up, fr. <ets>pund</ets> a pound.]</ety> <def>One who impounds; a
poundkeeper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pine</ent><br/
<hw>Pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>p<imac/n</ets>,
L. <ets>poena</ets> penalty. See <er>Pain</er>.]</ety> <def>Woe;
torment; pain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pyne</xex> of
hell.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pine</ent><br/
<hw>Pine</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pined</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>p<imac/nan</ets> to
torment, fr. <ets>p<imac/n</ets> torment. See 1st <er>Pine</er>,
<er>Pain</er>, <pos>n.</pos> & <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
inflict pain upon; to torment; to torture; to afflict.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That people that <qex>pyned</qex> him to death.</q> <rj><qau>Piers
Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One is <qex>pined</qex> in prison, another tortured on the
rack.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To grieve or mourn for.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pine</ent><br/
<hw>Pine</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To suffer; to be
afflicted.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To languish; to lose flesh or wear away, under any
distress or anexiety of mind; to droop; -- often used with
<xex>away</xex>.</def> <ldquo/The roses wither and the lilies
<xex>pine</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tickell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To languish with desire; to waste away with longing
for something; -- usually followed by <xex>for</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For whom, and not for Tybalt, Juliet <qex>pined</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To languish; droop; flag; wither;
decay.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pine</ent><br/
<hw>Pine</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>p<imac/n</ets>, L.
<ets>pinus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any tree of
the coniferous genus <gen>Pinus</gen>. See <er>Pinus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ There are about twenty-eight species in the
<country>United States</country>, of which the <stype>white pine</stype>
(<spn>Pinus Strobus</spn>), the <stype>Georgia pine</stype> (<spn>Pinus
australis</spn>), the <stype>red pine</stype> (<spn>Pinus
resinosa</spn>), and the great West Coast <stype>sugar pine</stype>
(<spn>Pinus Lambertiana</spn>) are among the most valuable. The
<stype>Scotch pine</stype> or <stype>fir</stype>, also called
<altname>Norway</altname> or <altname>Riga pine</altname> (<spn>Pinus
sylvestris</spn>), is the only British species. The <stype>nut
pine</stype> is any pine tree, or species of pine, which bears large
edible seeds. See <er>Pinon</er>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> The spruces, firs, larches, and true cedars, though formerly
considered pines, are now commonly assigned to other genera.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The wood of the pine tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pineapple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ground pine</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Ground</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Norfolk Island pine</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a beautiful coniferous tree, the <spn>Araucaria
excelsa</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine barren</b></col>, <cd>a tract of
infertile land which is covered with pines.</cd> <mark>[Southern
U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pine borer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
beetle whose larv<ae/ bore into pine trees.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine
finch</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Pinefinch</er>, in the
Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine grosbeak</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a large grosbeak (<spn>Pinicola enucleator</spn>), which inhabits
the northern parts of both hemispheres. The adult male is more or less
tinged with red.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine lizard</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small, very active, mottled gray lizard
(<spn>Sceloporus undulatus</spn>), native of the Middle States; --
called also <altname>swift</altname>, <altname>brown scorpion</altname>,
and <altname>alligator</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine marten</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A European weasel (<spn>Mustela
martes</spn>), called also <altname>sweet marten</altname>, and
<altname>yellow-breasted marten</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
American sable. See <er>Sable</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine moth</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of small tortricid
moths of the genus <gen>Retinia</gen>, whose larv<ae/ burrow in the ends
of the branchlets of pine trees, often doing great damage.</cd> --
<col><b>Pine mouse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American wild
mouse (<spn>Arvicola pinetorum</spn>), native of the Middle States. It
lives in pine forests.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine needle</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>one of the slender needle-shaped leaves of a pine
tree. See <er>Pinus</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine-needle wool</b></col>.
<cd>See <cref>Pine wool</cref> (below).</cd> -- <col><b>Pine
oil</b></col>, <cd>an oil resembling turpentine, obtained from fir and
pine trees, and used in making varnishes and colors.</cd> --
<col><b>Pine snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large harmless
North American snake (<spn>Pituophis melanoleucus</spn>). It is whitish,
covered with brown blotches having black margins. Called also
<altname>bull snake</altname>. The Western pine snake (<spn>Pituophis
Sayi</spn>) is chestnut-brown, mottled with black and orange.</cd> --
<col><b>Pine tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a tree of the genus
<gen>Pinus</gen>; pine.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine-tree money</b></col>,
<cd>money coined in <state>Massachusetts</state> in the seventeenth
century, and so called from its bearing a figure of a pine tree. The
most noted variety is the <stype>pine tree shilling</stype>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pine weevil</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of weevils whose larv<ae/ bore in the wood of pine
trees. Several species are known in both <geog>Europe</geog> and
<geog>America</geog>, belonging to the genera <gen>Pissodes</gen>,
<gen>Hylobius</gen>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine wool</b></col>, <cd>a
fiber obtained from pine needles by steaming them. It is prepared on a
large scale in some of the <geog>Southern United States</geog>, and has
many uses in the economic arts; -- called also <altname>pine-needle
wool</altname>, and <altname>pine-wood wool</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pineal</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ne*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pinea</ets>
the cone of a pine, from <ets>pineus</ets> of the pine, from
<ets>pinus</ets> a pine: cf. F. <ets>pin<eacute/ale</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a pine cone; resembling a pine cone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pineal gland</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a
glandlike body in the roof of the third ventricle of the vertebrate
brain; -- called also <altname>pineal body</altname>,
<altname>epiphysis</altname>, <altname>conarium</altname>. In some
animals it is connected with a rudimentary eye, the so-called
<xex>pineal eye</xex>, and in other animals it is supposed to be the
remnant of a dorsal median eye.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1088 --></p>

<p><ent>Pineapple</ent><br/
<hw>Pine"ap`ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A tropical plant (<spn>Ananassa sativa</spn>); also, its fruit; --
so called from the resemblance of the latter, in shape and external
appearance, to the cone of the pine tree. Its origin is unknown, though
conjectured to be American.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pineaster</ent><br/
<hw>Pine`as"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pinaster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pine-crowned</ent><br/
<ent>Pine-clad</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pine"-clad`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pine"-crowned`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Clad or crowned with pine
trees; <as>as, <ex>pine-clad</ex> hills</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinedrops</ent><br/
<hw>Pine"drops`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A reddish herb (<spn>Pterospora andromedea</spn>) of the United
States, found parasitic on the roots of pine trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinefinch</ent><br/
<hw>Pine"finch`</hw> <pr>(p<imac/n"f<icr/nch`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small American bird (<spn>Spinus
spinus</spn> syn. <spn>Chrysomitris spinus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>pine siskin</altname>, and <altname>American
siskin</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The pine grosbeak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinenchyma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*nen"chy*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>pi`nax</grk> a tablet + <ets>-enchyma</ets>, as in
<ets>parenchyma</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Tabular parenchyma,
a form of cellular tissue in which the cells are broad and flat, as in
some kinds of epidermis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinery</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pineries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pine
forest; a grove of pines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hothouse in which pineapples are grown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinesap</ent><br/
<hw>Pine"sap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
reddish fleshy herb of the genus <gen>Monotropa</gen> (<spn>Monotropa
hypopitys</spn>), formerly thought to be parasitic on the roots of pine
trees, but more probably saprophytic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pine-tree State</ent><br/
<hw>Pine-tree State</hw>. <def>Maine; -- a nickname alluding to the pine
tree in its coat of arms.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinetum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*ne"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a pine
grove.]</ety> <def>A plantation of pine trees; esp., a collection of
living pine trees made for ornamental or scientific purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pineweed</ent><br/
<hw>Pine"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
low, bushy, nearly leafless herb (<spn>Hypericum Sarothra</spn>), common
in sandy soil in the Eastern United States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piney</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Piny</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piney</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ey</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Of East Indian origin.]</ety>
<def>A term used in designating an East Indian tree (the <spn>Vateria
Indica</spn> or piney tree, of the order <ord>Dipterocarpe<ae/</ord>,
which grows in Malabar, etc.) or its products.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Piney dammar</b></col>, <col><b>Piney
resin</b></col>, <col><b>Piney varnish</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a pellucid,
fragrant, acrid, bitter resin, which exudes from the piney tree
(<spn>Vateria Indica</spn>) when wounded. It is used as a varnish, in
making candles, and as a substitute for incense and for amber. Called
also <altname>liquid copal</altname>, and <altname>white
dammar</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Piney tallow</b></col>, <cd>a solid
fatty substance, resembling tallow, obtained from the roasted seeds of
the <spn>Vateria Indica</spn>; called also <altname>dupada
oil</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Piney thistle</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a plant (<spn>Atractylis gummifera</spn>), from
the bark of which, when wounded, a gummy substance exudes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"-eyed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the stigma visible at the throad of a gamopetalous corolla,
while the stamens are concealed in the tube; -- said of dimorphous
flowers. The opposite of <ant>thrum-eyed</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinfeather</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"feath`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A feather not
fully developed; esp., a rudimentary feather just emerging through the
skin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinfeathered</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"feath`ered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having part, or
all, of the feathers imperfectly developed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin-fire</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"-fire`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>Having a firing pin to explode the cartridge; <as>as, a
<ex>pin-fire</ex> rifle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinfish</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
their sharp dorsal spines.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The sailor's choice (<spn>Diplodus rhomboides</spn> syn.
<spn>Lagodon rhomboides</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The salt-water
bream (<spn>Diplodus Holbrooki</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Both are excellent food fishes, common on the coast of
the United States south of Cape Hatteras. The name is also applied to
other allied species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinfold</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"fold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>pindfold</ets>. See <er>Pinder</er>, <er>Pound</er> an inclosure,
and <er>Fold</er> an inclosure.]</ety> <def>A place in which stray
cattle or domestic animals are confined; a pound; a penfold.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A parish <qex>pinfold</qex> begirt by its high hedge.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ping</ent><br/
<hw>Ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably of imitative
origin.]</ety> <def>The sound made by a bullet in striking a solid
object or in passing through the air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ping</ent><br/
<hw>Ping</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pinged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pinging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make the sound called
<xex>ping</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pingle</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps fr.
<ets>pin</ets> to impound.]</ety> <def>A small piece of inclosed
ground.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>ping-pong</ent><br/
<hw>ping"-pong`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Imitative.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An indoor modification of lawn tennis played with small
bats, or battledores, and a very light, hollow, celluloid ball, on a
large table divided across the middle by a net. Also called
<altname>table tennis</altname>.</def> <mark>[[originally a trade
name]</mark> <br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A size of photograph a little larger than a postage
stamp.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ping-pong</ent><br/
<hw>Ping"-pong`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To play
ping-pong.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to bounce back and forth, in the manner of a
ping-pong ball.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ping-pong ball</ent><br/
<hw>Ping"-pong ball`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>the small hollow
celluloid ball used for the game of ping-pong.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pingster</ent><br/
<hw>Ping"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pinkster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinguefaction</ent><br/
<hw>Pin`gue*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinguefacere</ets>, <ets>pinguefactum</ets>, to fatten;
<ets>pinguis</ets> fat + <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A making of, or turning into, fat.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinguicula</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*guic"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>pinguiculus</ets> somewhat fat, fattish.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Butterwort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinguid</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"guid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinguis</ets> fat.]</ety> <def>Fat; unctuous; greasy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Some clays are more
<xex>pinguid</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinguidinous</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*guid"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinguedo</ets> fatness, fr. <ets>pinguis</ets> fat.]</ety>
<def>Containing fat; fatty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinguitude</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"gui*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinguitudo</ets>, from <ets>pinguis</ets> fat.]</ety> <def>Fatness;
a growing fat; obesity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinhold</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"hold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place where a pin
is fixed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinic</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"nic</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pinus</ets>
pine.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pine;
obtained from the pine; formerly, designating an acid which is the chief
constituent of common resin, -- now called <xex>abietic</xex>, or
<xex>sylvic</xex>, <xex>acid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pining</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Languishing; drooping; wasting away, as with longing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wasting; consuming.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>pining</xex> malady of France.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piningly</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pining manner;
droopingly.</def> <rj><au>Poe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinion</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
moth of the genus <gen>Lithophane</gen>, as <spn>Lithophane
antennata</spn>, whose larva bores large holes in young peaches and
apples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinion</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>pignon</ets> a pen, F.,
gable, pinion (in sense 5); cf. Sp. <ets>pi<ntil/on</ets> pinion; fr. L.
<ets>pinna</ets> pinnacle, feather, wing. See <er>Pin</er> a peg, and
cf. <er>Pen</er> a feather, <er>Pennat</er>, <er>Pennon</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A feather; a quill.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wing, literal or figurative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Swift on his sooty <qex>pinions</qex> flits the gnome.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The joint of bird's wing most remote from the
body.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A fetter for the arm.</def>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A cogwheel with a small number of
teeth, or leaves, adapted to engage with a larger wheel, or rack (see
<er>Rack</er>); esp., such a wheel having its leaves formed of the
substance of the arbor or spindle which is its axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Lantern pinion</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Lantern</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pinion wire</b></col>, <cd>wire fluted
longitudinally, for making the pinions of clocks and watches. It is
formed by being drawn through holes of the shape required for the leaves
or teeth of the pinions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinion</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pinioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pinioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bind or
confine the wings of; to confine by binding the wings.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To disable by cutting off the pinion joint.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To disable or restrain, as a person, by binding the
arms, esp. by binding the arms to the body.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her elbows <qex>pinioned</qex> close upon her hips.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, generally, to confine; to bind; to tie
up.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pinioned</xex> up by formal rules of state.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinioned</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ioned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having wings or
pinions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinionist</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any winged
creature.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinite</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
<ets>Pini</ets>, a mine in Saxony.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
compact granular cryptocrystalline mineral of a dull grayish or greenish
white color. It is a hydrous alkaline silicate, and is derived from the
alteration of other minerals, as iolite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinite</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pinus</ets>
the pine tree.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Any fossil
wood which exhibits traces of having belonged to the Pine
family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A sweet white crystalline
substance extracted from the gum of a species of pine (<spn>Pinus
Lambertina</spn>). It is isomeric with, and resembles,
quercite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>pink</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A vessel with a very
narrow stern; -- called also <altname>pinky</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Sir
W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pink stern</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a narrow
stern.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>pinken</ets>,
<ets>pinkoogen</ets>, to blink, twinkle with the eyes.]</ety> <def>To
wink; to blink.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half-shut; winking.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pinked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pinken</ets> to prick,
probably a nasalized form of <ets>pick</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
pierce with small holes; to cut the edge of, as cloth or paper, in small
scallops or angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stab; to pierce as with a sword.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To choose; to cull; to pick out.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stab.</def>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. akin to <ets>pick</ets>; as if
the edges of the petals were picked out. Cf. <er>Pink</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to
several plants of the caryophyllaceous genus <gen>Dianthus</gen>, and to
their flowers, which are sometimes very fragrant and often double in
cultivated varieties. The species are mostly perennial herbs, with
opposite linear leaves, and handsome five-petaled flowers with a tubular
calyx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A color resulting from the combination of a pure
vivid red with more or less white; -- so called from the common color of
the flower.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything supremely excellent; the embodiment or
perfection of something.</def> <ldquo/The very <xex>pink</xex> of
courtesy.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European minnow; -- so called
from the color of its abdomen in summer.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bunch pink</b></col><cd> is <spn>Dianthus
barbatus</spn>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>China pink</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Indian pink</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>China</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Clove pink</b></col><cd> is <spn>Dianthus Caryophyllus</spn>,
the stock from which carnations are derived.</cd> -- <col><b>Garden
pink</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Pheasant's eye</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Meadow
pink</b></col><cd> is applied to <spn>Dianthus deltoides</spn>; also, to
the ragged robin.</cd> -- <col><b>Maiden pink</b></col>,
<cd><spn>Dianthus deltoides</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Moss pink</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Moss</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pink needle</b></col>,
<cd>the pin grass; -- so called from the long, tapering points of the
carpels. See <er>Alfilaria</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sea pink</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Thrift</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink</ent><br/
<hw>Pink</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling the garden pink in color;
of the color called <xex>pink</xex> (see 6th <er>Pink</er>, 2); <as>as,
a <ex>pink</ex> dress; <ex>pink</ex> ribbons.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pink eye</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a popular name
for an epidemic variety of ophthalmia, associated with early and marked
redness of the eyeball.</cd> -- <col><b>Pink salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.
& Dyeing)</fld>, <cd>the double chlorides of (stannic) tin and ammonium,
formerly much used as a mordant for madder and cochineal.</cd> --
<col><b>Pink saucer</b></col>, <cd>a small saucer, the inner surface of
which is covered with a pink pigment.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinked</ent><br/
<hw>Pinked</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pierced with small
holes; worked in eyelets; scalloped on the edge.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"-eyed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pink</ets>
half-shut + <ets>eye</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having small eyes.</def>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><hw>pinkify</hw><br/
<hw>pinkify</hw> <pos>v.</pos> <def>To make pink.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinking</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of piercing or
stabbing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or method of decorating fabrics or garments
with a pinking iron; also, the style of decoration; scallops made with a
pinking iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pinking iron</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An instrument
for scalloping the edges of ribbons, flounces, etc.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A sword.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinkish</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat pink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinkness</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of
being pink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinkroot</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"root`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The root of <spn>Spigelia Marilandica</spn>, used
as a powerful vermifuge; also, that of <spn>Spigelia Anthelmia</spn>.
See definition 2 (below).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A perennial North
American herb (<spn>Spigelia Marilandica</spn>), sometimes cultivated
for its showy red blossoms. Called also <altname>Carolina
pink</altname>, <altname>Maryland pinkroot</altname>, and <altname>worm
grass</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An annual South American and
West Indian plant (<spn>Spigelia Anthelmia</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinkster</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>pinkster</ets>, <ets>pinksteren</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/. See
<er>Pentecost</er>.]</ety> <def>Whitsuntide.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pingster</asp> and <asp>pinxter</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pinkster flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the rosy
flower of the <spn>Azalea nudiflora</spn>; also, the shrub itself; --
called also <altname>Pinxter blomachee</altname> by the New York
descendants of the Dutch settlers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink stern</ent><br/
<hw>Pink" stern`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See 1st <er>Pink</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Chebacco</er>, and 1st
<er>Pink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pink-sterned</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"-sterned`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 1st
<er>Pink</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Having a very narrow
stern; -- said of a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinky</ent><br/
<hw>Pink"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
1st <er>Pink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinna</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pin"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pinn<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pinnas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a feather.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A leaflet of a pinnate leaf. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Bipinnate leaf</cref>, under
<er>Bipinnate</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the primary divisions
of a decompound leaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the divisions of a pinnate
part or organ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[L. <ets>pinna</ets>, akin to Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of <gen>Pinna</gen>, a genus of
large bivalve mollusks found in all warm seas. The byssus consists of a
large number of long, silky fibers, which have been used in
manufacturing woven fabrics, as a curiosity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The auricle of the ear. See
<er>Ear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnace</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pinasse</ets>; cf. It. <ets>pinassa</ets>, <ets>pinazza</ets>, Sp.
<ets>pinaza</ets>; all from L. <ets>pinus</ets> a pine tree, anything
made of pine, e.g., a ship. Cf. <er>Pine</er> a tree.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small vessel propelled by sails
or oars, formerly employed as a tender, or for coast defence; -- called
originally, <altname>spynace</altname> or
<altname>spyne</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A man-of-war's
boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whilst our <qex>pinnace</qex> anchors in the Downs.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A procuress; a pimp.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnacle</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"na*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pinacle</ets>, F. <ets>pinacle</ets>, L. <ets>pinnaculum</ets>, fr.
<ets>pinna</ets> pinnacle, feather. See <er>Pin</er> a peg.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An architectural member, upright,
and generally ending in a small spire, -- used to finish a buttress, to
constitute a part in a proportion, as where pinnacles flank a gable or
spire, and the like. Pinnacles may be considered primarily as added
weight, where it is necessary to resist the thrust of an arch,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some renowned metropolis<br/
With glistering spires and <qex>pinnacles</qex> around.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything resembling a pinnacle; a lofty peak; a
pointed summit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Three silent <qex>pinnacles</qex> of aged snow.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The slippery tops of human state,<br/
The gilded <qex>pinnacles</qex> of fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnacle</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"na*cle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pinnacled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pinnacling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To build or
furnish with a pinnacle or pinnacles.</def> <rj><au>T.
Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnage</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pinfold</er>.]</ety> <def>Poundage of cattle. See
<er>Pound</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnated</ent><br/
<ent>Pinnate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pin"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pin"na*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pinnatus</ets> feathered, fr.
<ets>pinna</ets> a feather. See <er>Pin</er> a peg, <er>Pen</er>
feather.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Consisting of several
leaflets, or separate portions, arranged on each side of a common
petiole, as the leaves of a rosebush, a hickory, or an ash. See
<cref>Abruptly pinnate</cref>, and <xex>Illust.</xex>, under
<er>Abruptly</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a winglike tuft of long
feathers on each side of the neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pinnated grouse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
prairie chicken.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnately</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pinnate
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnatifid</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*nat"i*fid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinnatus</ets> feathered + root of <ets>findere</ets> to split: cf.
F. <ets>pinnatifide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided in a
pinnate manner, with the divisions not reaching to the midrib.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnatilobate</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*nat`i*lo"bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pinnate</er>, and <er>Lobate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having lobes arranged in a pinnate manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnatiped</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*nat"i*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinnatus</ets> feathered + <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets> foot:
cf. F. <ets>pinnatip<egrave/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the toes bordered by membranes; fin-footed, as certain
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnatiped</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*nat"i*ped</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bird
which has the toes bordered by membranes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinner</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, pins or fastens, as with pins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Costume)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A headdress like a
cap, with long lappets.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An apron with a bib; a
pinafore.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A cloth band for a gown.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With kerchief starched, and <qex>pinners</qex> clean.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pin maker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinner</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ner</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pin</er> to pound.]</ety>
<def>One who pins or impounds cattle. See <er>Pin</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnet</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pinnacle.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinniform</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ni*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinna</ets> feather, fin + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Shaped
like a fin or feather.</def> <rj><au>Sir J. Hill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnigrada</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pin`ni*gra"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. <ets>pinna</ets> a feather + <ets>gradi</ets> to walk, move.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pinnipedia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnigrade</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ni*grade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An animal of the seal tribe, moving by short feet that serve as
paddles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinniped</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ni*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinna</ets> feather, fin + <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a
foot: cf. F. <ets>pinnip<egrave/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the Pinnipedia; a seal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>One of the Pinnipedes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnipedes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pin*nip"e*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Steganopodes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnipedia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pin`ni*pe"di*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. So
called because their webbed feet are used as paddles or fins.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of aquatic carnivorous mammals
including the seals and walruses; -- opposed to
<contr>Fissipedia</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1089 --></p>

<p><ent>Pinnock</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of uncertain
origin.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hedge
sparrow.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
tomtit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnothere</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"no*there</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a pinna
+ <?/ an animal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A crab of the genus
<gen>pinnotheres</gen>. See <cref>Oyster crab</cref>, under
<er>Oyster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnula</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nu*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pinnul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Pinnule</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pinnule</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having each pinna
subdivided; -- said of a leaf, or of its pinn<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnulated</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nu*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having pinnules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnule</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"nule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pinnula</ets>, dim. of <ets>pinna</ets> feather: cf. F.
<ets>pinnule</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the
small divisions of a decompound frond or leaf. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<cref>Bipinnate leaf</cref>, under <er>Bipinnate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of a series of small,
slender organs, or parts, when arranged in rows so as to have a
plumelike appearance; <as>as, a <ex>pinnule</ex> of a gorgonia; the
<ex>pinnules</ex> of a crinoid.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinnywinkles</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ny*win`kles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>An
instrument of torture, consisting of a board with holes into which the
fingers were pressed, and fastened with pegs.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pilliewinkles</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinocle</ent><br/
<ent>Pinochle</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pin"o*chle</hw>, <hw>Pin"o*cle</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A game at cards, played with forty-eight cards, being
all the cards above the eight spots in two packs.</def> <altsp>[Also
spelled <asp>penuchle</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinole</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*nole"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
aromatic powder used in Italy in the manufacture of chocolate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Parched maize, ground, and mixed with sugar, etc.
Mixed with water, it makes a nutritious beverage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pi<ntil/on</ent><br/
<hw>Pi<ntil/"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>pi<ntil/on</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
edible seed of several species of pine; also, the tree producing such
seeds, as <spn>Pinus Pinea</spn> of Southern Europe, and <spn>Pinus
Parryana, cembroides, edulis, and monophylla</spn>, the nut pines of
Western North America.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>See <er>Monkey's
puzzle</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pignon</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinpatch</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"patch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The common English periwinkle.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pint</ent><br/
<hw>Pint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pinte</ets>, F.
<ets>pinte</ets>, fr. Sp. <ets>pinta</ets> spot, mark, pint, fr.
<ets>pintar</ets> to paint; a mark for a pint prob. having been made on
or in a larger measure. See <er>Paint</er>.]</ety> <def>A measure of
capacity, equal to half a quart, or four gills, -- used in liquid and
dry measures. See <er>Quart</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pint</ent><br/
<hw>Pint</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The laughing
gull.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pintado</ent><br/
<hw>Pin*ta"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pintados</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Sp., painted, fr.
<ets>pintar</ets> to paint.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any bird of the genus <gen>Numida</gen>. Several species are found
in Africa. The common pintado, or Guinea fowl, the helmeted, and the
crested pintados, are the best known. See <cref>Guinea fowl</cref>,
under <er>Guinea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fish (<spn>Scomberomorus regalis</spn>) similar
to, but larger than, the Spanish mackerel, and having elongated spots,
common about Florida and the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pintail</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A northern duck (<spn>Dafila acuta</spn>),
native of both continents. The adult male has a long, tapering tail.
Called also <altname>gray duck</altname>, <altname>piketail</altname>,
<altname>piket-tail</altname>, <altname>spike-tail</altname>,
<altname>split-tail</altname>, <altname>springtail</altname>,
<altname>sea pheasant</altname>, and <altname>gray
widgeon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The sharp-tailed grouse of the
great plains and Rocky Mountains (<spn>Pedioc<ae/tes
phasianellus</spn>); -- called also <altname>pintailed grouse</altname>,
<altname>pintailed chicken</altname>, <altname>springtail</altname>, and
<altname>sharptail</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pin-tailed</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"-tailed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a tapered tail, with the middle feathers longest; -- said of
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pintle</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A diminutive of
<er>Pin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A little pin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>An upright pivot pin</def>; as:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The pivot pin of a hinge.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
hook or pin on which a rudder hangs and turns.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A
pivot about which the chassis swings, in some kinds of gun
carriages.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A kingbolt of a wagon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinto</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Sp., painted.]</ety>
<def>Lit., painted; hence, piebald; mottled; pied.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinto</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"to</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any pied animal; esp., a pied or
<ldquo/painted<rdquo/ horse.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pintos</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"tos</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Pinto</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</sing> <ety>[Sp., painted,
mottled.]</ety> <fld>(Eyhnol.)</fld> <def>A mountain tribe of Mexican
Indians living near Acapulco. They are remarkable for having the dark
skin of the face irregularly spotted with white. Called also
<altname>speckled Indians</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pintsch gas</ent><br/
<hw>Pintsch gas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[After Richard
<etsep>Pintsch</etsep>, German inventor.]</ety> <def>A kind of oil gas
extensively used for lighting railroad cars, which carry it in
compressed form.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinule</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pinnule</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>One of the sights of
an astrolabe.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a pine
tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A large genus of evergreen
coniferous trees, mostly found in the northern hemisphere. The genus
formerly included the firs, spruces, larches, and hemlocks, but is now
limited to those trees which have the primary leaves of the branchlets
reduced to mere scales, and the secondary ones (<xex>pine needles</xex>)
acicular, and usually in fascicles of two to seven. See
<er>Pine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinweed</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Lechea</gen>, low North American herbs
with branching stems, and very small and abundant leaves and
flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinworm</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
small nematoid worm (<spn>Oxyurus vermicularis</spn>), which is
parasitic chiefly in the rectum of man. It is most common in children
and aged persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinxit</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pinx"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., perfect indicative 3d sing.
of <ets>pingere</ets> to paint.]</ety> <def>A word appended to the
artist's name or initials on a painting, or engraved copy of a painting;
<as>as, <ex>Rubens pinxit</ex>, Rubens painted (this)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pinxter</ent><br/
<hw>Pinx"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pinkster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piny</ent><br/
<hw>Pin"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding with
pines.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>piney</asp>.]</altsp> <ldquo/The
<xex>piny</xex> wood.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pioned</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"o*ned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>A Shakespearean word
of disputed meaning; perh., <ldquo/abounding in marsh
marigolds.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy banks with <qex>pioned</qex> and twilled brims.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pioneer</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`o*neer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pionier</ets>, orig., a foot soldier, OF. <ets>peonier</ets>, fr.
OF. <ets>peon</ets> a foot soldier, F. <ets>pion</ets>. See
<er>Pawn</er> in chess.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A
soldier detailed or employed to form roads, dig trenches, and make
bridges, as an army advances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who goes before, as into the wilderness,
preparing the way for others to follow; <as>as, <ex>pioneers</ex> of
civilization; <ex>pioneers</ex> of reform.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pioneer</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`o*neer"</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pioneered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pioneering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go
before, and prepare or open a way for; to act as pioneer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to take part in the early development of; to break
ground in; to invent or originate.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pioneering</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`o*neer"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>groundbreaking; originating;
-- of efforts that begin work in a field or on a topic not previously
widely known.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pioneers' Day</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`o*neers'" Day</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>In Utah, a legal holiday,
July 24, commemorated the arrival, in 1847, of Brigham Young and his
followers at the present site of Salt Lake City.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`o*ner"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pioneer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piony</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Peony</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piot</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Piet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The magpie.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pious</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pius</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>pieux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
piety; exhibiting piety; reverential; dutiful; religious; devout;
godly.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pious</xex> hearts.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Pious</xex> poetry.<rdquo/ <au>Johnson.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where was the martial brother's <qex>pious</qex> care?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Practiced under the pretext of religion; prompted by
mistaken piety; <as>as, <ex>pious</ex> errors; <ex>pious</ex>
frauds.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Godly; devout; religious; righteous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piously</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pious manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pip</ent><br/
<hw>Pip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pippe</ets>, D.
<ets>pip</ets>, or F. <ets>p<eacute/pie</ets>; from LL.
<ets>pipita</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pituita</ets> slime, phlegm, rheum, in
fowls, the pip. Cf. <er>Pituite</er>.]</ety> <def>A contagious disease
of fowls, characterized by hoarseness, discharge from the nostrils and
eyes, and an accumulation of mucus in the mouth, forming a
<ldquo/scale<rdquo/ on the tongue. By some the term <xex>pip</xex> is
restricted to this last symptom, the disease being called
<xex>roup</xex> by them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pip</ent><br/
<hw>Pip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formerly <ets>pippin</ets>,
<ets>pepin</ets>. Cf. <er>Pippin</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
seed, as of an apple or orange.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pip</ent><br/
<hw>Pip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. for <ets>pick</ets>, F.
<ets>pique</ets> a spade at cards, a pike. Cf. <er>Pique</er>.]</ety>
<def>One of the conventional figures or <ldquo/spots<rdquo/ on playing
cards, dominoes, etc.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pip</ent><br/
<hw>Pip</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pipped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pipping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Peep</er>.]</ety>
<def>To cry or chirp, as a chicken; to peep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To hear the chick <qex>pip</qex> and cry in the egg.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipa</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*pa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pipas</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
Surinam toad (<spn>Pipa Americana</spn>), noted for its peculiar
breeding habits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The male places the eggs on the back of the female,
where they soon become inclosed in capsules formed by the thickening of
the skin. The incubation of the eggs takes place in the capsules, and
the young, when hatched, come forth with well developed legs.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipage</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Transportation, as of
petroleum oil, by means of a pipe conduit; also, the charge for such
transportation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipal tree</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"pal tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Same as <er>Peepul
tree</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>p<imac/pe</ets>, probably fr. L. <ets>pipare</ets>,
<ets>pipire</ets>, to chirp; of imitative origin. Cf. <er>Peep</er>,
<er>Pibroch</er>, <er>Fife</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A wind
instrument of music, consisting of a tube or tubes of straw, reed, wood,
or metal; any tube which produces musical sounds; <as>as, a shepherd's
<ex>pipe</ex>; the <ex>pipe</ex> of an organ.</as></def> <ldquo/Tunable
as sylvan <xex>pipe</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now had he rather hear the tabor and the <qex>pipe</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any long tube or hollow body of wood, metal,
earthenware, or the like: especially, one used as a conductor of water,
steam, gas, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small bowl with a hollow stem, -- used in smoking
tobacco, and, sometimes, other substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A passageway for the air in speaking and breathing;
the windpipe, or one of its divisions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The key or sound of the voice.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The peeping whistle, call, or note of a
bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The earliest <qex>pipe</qex> of half-awakened birds.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The bagpipe; <as>as, the
<ex>pipes</ex> of Lucknow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>An elongated body or vein of
ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A roll formerly used in the English exchequer,
otherwise called the <xex>Great Roll</xex>, on which were taken down the
accounts of debts to the king; -- so called because put together like a
pipe.</def> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A boatswain's whistle, used to
call the crew to their duties; also, the sound of it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pipe</ets>, fr. <ets>pipe</ets> a wind
instrument, a tube, fr. L. <ets>pipare</ets> to chirp. See Etymol.
above.]</ety> <def>A cask usually containing two hogsheads, or 126 wine
gallons; also, the quantity which it contains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pipe fitter</b></col>, <cd>one who fits pipes together,
or applies pipes, as to an engine or a building.</cd> -- <col><b>Pipe
fitting</b></col>, <cd>a piece, as a coupling, an elbow, a valve, etc.,
used for connecting lengths of pipe or as accessory to a pipe.</cd> --
<col><b>Pipe office</b></col>, <cd>an ancient office in the Court of
Exchequer, in which the clerk of the pipe made out leases of crown
lands, accounts of cheriffs, etc.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Pipe tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the lilac and the
mock orange; -- so called because their were formerly used to make pipe
stems; -- called also <altname>pipe privet</altname>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Pipe wrench</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pipe
tongs</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a jawed tool for gripping a pipe, in turning
or holding it.</cd> -- <col><b>To smoke the pipe of peace</b></col>,
<cd>to smoke from the same pipe in token of amity or preparatory to
making a treaty of peace, -- a custom of the American
Indians.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To play on a pipe,
fife, flute, or other tubular wind instrument of music.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have <qex>piped</qex> unto you, and ye have not danced.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xi. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To call, convey orders, etc., by
means of signals on a pipe or whistle carried by a boatswain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To emit or have a shrill sound like that of a pipe;
to whistle.</def> <ldquo/Oft in the <xex>piping</xex> shrouds.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>To become hollow in the process
of solodifying; -- said of an ingot, as of steel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Piped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To perform, as a tune,
by playing on a pipe, flute, fife, etc.; to utter in the shrill tone of
a pipe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A robin . . . was <qex>piping</qex> a few querulous notes.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To call or direct, as a crew, by
the boatswain's whistle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As fine a ship's company as was ever <qex>piped</qex> aloft.</q>
<rj><qau>Marryat.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To furnish or equip with pipes; <as>as, to
<ex>pipe</ex> an engine, or a building</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe clay</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe" clay`</hw> <pr>(kl<amac/`)</pr>. <def>A plastic, unctuous clay
of a grayish white color, -- used in making tobacco pipes and various
kinds of earthenware, in scouring cloth, and in cleansing soldiers'
equipments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipeclay</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"clay`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To whiten or
clean with pipe clay, as a soldier's accouterments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To clear off; <as>as, to <ex>pipeclay</ex>
accounts</as>.</def> <mark>[Slang, Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piped</ent><br/
<hw>Piped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Formed with a pipe;
having pipe or pipes; tubular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipefish</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any lophobranch fish of the genus <gen>Siphostoma</gen>, or
<gen>Syngnathus</gen>, and allied genera, having a long and very slender
angular body, covered with bony plates. The mouth is small, at the end
of a long, tubular snout. The male has a pouch on his belly, in which
the incubation of the eggs takes place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe layer</ent><br/
<ent>Pipelayer</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pipe"lay`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Pipe" lay`er</hw></mhw>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who lays conducting
pipes in the ground, as for water, gas, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Polit. Cant)</fld> <def>A politician who works in
secret; -- in this sense, usually written as one word.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe laying</ent><br/
<ent>Pipelaying</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pipe"lay`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>, <it>or</it> <hw>Pipe"
lay`ing</hw></mhw>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>The laying of conducting pipes
underground, as for water, gas, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Polit. Cant)</fld> <def>The act or method of making
combinations for personal advantage secretly or slyly; -- in this sense,
usually written as one word.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pipe line</ent><br/
<ent>pipeline</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pipeline</hw>, <hw>pipe line</hw></mhw>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
line of pipe with pumping machinery and apparatus for conveying liquids,
gases, or finely divided solids, such as petroleum or natural gas,
between distant points.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def><mark>fig.</mark> an information channel direct from
the source.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>the set of stages and processes from the invention
or design of a product to its ultimate use, production, or commercial
sale. Used commonly in the phrase <col><b>in the pipeline</b></col>,
<it>i. e.</it> still in preparation or under development.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipe-line</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"-line`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To convey by a pipe line; to
furnish with a pipe line or pipe lines.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipemouth</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"mouth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any fish of the genus <gen>Fistularia</gen>; -- called also
<altname>tobacco pipefish</altname>. See <er>Fistularia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piper</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pepper</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piper</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>One who plays on a pipe, or the like, esp. on a
bagpipe.</def> <ldquo/The hereditary <xex>piper</xex> and his
sons.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A common European
gurnard (<spn>Trigla lyra</spn>), having a large head, with prominent
nasal projection, and with large, sharp, opercular spines.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A sea urchin (<spn>Goniocidaris hystrix</spn>) having
very long spines, native of both the American and European
coasts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pay the piper</b></col>, <cd>to bear the cost,
expense, or trouble.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pip`er*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piper</ets> pepper.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to the order of plants (<ord>Piperace<ae/</ord>) of which the pepper
(<spn>Piper nigrum</spn>) is the type. There are about a dozen genera
and a thousand species, mostly tropical plants with pungent and aromatic
qualities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-zin</ent><br/
<ent>Piperazine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pi*per"a*zine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> Also
<hw>-zin</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[<ets>Piper</ets>idine + <ets>az</ets>ote +
<ets>-ine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A crystalline substance,
<chform>(C2H4NH)2</chform>, formed by action of ammonia on ethylene
bromide, by reduction of pyrazine, etc. It is a strong base, and is used
as a remedy for gout.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperic</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*per"ic</hw> <pr>(p<isl/*p<ecr/r"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or derived from, or designating,
a complex organic acid found in the products of different members of the
Pepper family, and extracted as a yellowish crystalline
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperidge</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"er*idge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pepperidge</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperidine</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*per"i*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An oily liquid alkaloid, <chform>C5H11N</chform>, having a hot,
peppery, ammoniacal odor. It is related to pyridine, and is obtained by
the decomposition of piperine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperine</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"er*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piper</ets> pepper: cf. F. <ets>piperin</ets>,
<ets>piperine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white crystalline
compound of piperidine and piperic acid. It is obtained from the black
pepper (<spn>Piper nigrum</spn>) and other species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperonal</ent><br/
<hw>Pip`er*o"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A white crystalline substance obtained by oxidation of piperic
acid, and regarded as a complex aldehyde.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piperylene</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*per"y*lene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Piper</ets>idine + acet<ets>ylene</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hydrocarbon obtained by decomposition of
certain piperidine derivatives.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipestem</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"stem`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The hollow stem or
tube of a pipe used for smoking tobacco, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Took a long reed for a <qex>pipestem</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipestone</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of clay
slate, carved by the Indians into tobacco pipes. Cf.
<er>Catlinite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipette</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*pette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>pipe</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small glass tube, often with an
enlargement or bulb in the middle, and usually graduated, -- used for
transferring or delivering measured quantities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipevine</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"vine`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any climbing species of <gen>Aristolochia</gen>; esp., the
Dutchman's pipe (<spn>Aristolochia sipho</spn>). See under
<er>Dutchman</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipewood</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An ericaceous shrub
(<spn>Leucotho<eum/ acuminata</spn>) of the southern United States, from
the wood of which pipe bowls are made.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipewort</ent><br/
<hw>Pipe"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of a genus (<gen>Eriocaulon</gen>) of aquatic or marsh
herbs with soft grass-like leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piping</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"ing</hw> <pr>(p<imac/p"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pipe</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Playing on a
musical pipe.</def> <ldquo/Lowing herds and <xex>piping
swains</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Peaceful; favorable to, or characterized by, the
music of the pipe rather than of the drum and fife.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Emitting a high, shrill sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Simmering; boiling; sizzling; hissing; -- from the
sound of boiling fluids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Piping crow</b></col>, <col><b>Piping crow
shrike</b></col>, <col><b>Piping roller</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any Australian bird of the genus
<gen>Gymnorhina</gen>, esp. <spn>Gymnorhina tibicen</spn>, which is
black and white, and the size of a small crow. Called also
<altname>caruck</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Piping frog</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small American tree frog (<spn>Hyla
Pickeringii</spn>) which utters a high, shrill note in early
spring.</cd> -- <col><b>Piping hot</b></col>, <cd>boiling hot; hissing
hot; very hot.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piping</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small cord covered
with cloth, -- used as trimming for women's dresses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pipes, collectively; <as>as, the <ex>piping</ex> of
a house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of playing on a pipe; the shrill noted of
birds, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A piece cut off to be set or planted; a cutting;
also, propagation by cuttings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1090 --></p>

<p><ent>Pipistrelle</ent><br/
<ent>Pipistrel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pi*pis"trel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pip`i*strelle"</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pipistrelle</ets>, It.
<ets>pipistrello</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small European
bat (<spn>Vesperugo pipistrellus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>flittermouse</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipit</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from its call
note.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
small singing birds belonging to <gen>Anthus</gen> and allied genera, of
the family <fam>Motacillid<ae/</fam>. They strongly resemble the true
larks in habits, colors, and the great length of the hind claw. They
are, therefore, often called <altname>titlarks</altname>, and
<altname>pipit larks</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <stype>meadow pipit</stype> (<spn>Anthus
pratensis</spn>); the <stype>tree pipit</stype>, or tree lark
(<spn>Anthus trivialis</spn>); and the <stype>rock pipit</stype>, or sea
lark (<spn>Anthus obscurus</spn>) are well-known European species. The
common American <ex>pipit</ex>, or brown lark, is <spn>Anthus
Pensilvanicus</spn>. The Western species (<spn>Anthus Spraguei</spn>) is
called the <stype>American skylark</stype>, on account of its musical
powers.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipkin</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"kin</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of <er>Pipe</er>.]</ety>
<def>A small earthen boiler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pippin</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"pin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr. OE.
<ets>pippin</ets> a seed, as being raised from the seed. See
<er>Pip</er> a seed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An apple
from a tree raised from the seed and not grafted; a seedling
apple.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A name given to apples of several
different kinds, as <xex>Newtown pippin</xex>, <xex>summer pippin</xex>,
<xex>fall pippin</xex>, <xex>golden pippin</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We will eat a last year's <qex>pippin</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Normandy pippins</b></col>, <cd>sun-dried apples for
winter use.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pippul tree</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"pul tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Same as <er>Peepul
tree</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipra</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"pra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pipras</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
woodpecker.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species
of small clamatorial birds belonging to <gen>Pipra</gen> and allied
genera, of the family <fam>Piprid<ae/</fam>. The male is usually glossy
black, varied with scarlet, yellow, or sky blue. They chiefly inhabit
South America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piprine</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"prine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the pipras, or the family
<fam>Piprid<ae/</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipsissewa</ent><br/
<hw>Pip*sis"se*wa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From American
Indian.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A low evergreen plant
(<spn>Chimaphila umbellata</spn>), with narrow, wedge-lanceolate leaves,
and an umbel of pretty nodding fragrant blossoms. It has been used in
nephritic diseases. Called also <altname>prince's
pine</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pipy</ent><br/
<hw>Pip"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a pipe;
hollow-stemmed.</def> <rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piquancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"quan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Piquant</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
piquant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piquant</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"quant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., p. pr. of
<ets>piquer</ets> to prick or sting. See <er>Pike</er>.]</ety>
<def>Stimulating to the taste; giving zest; tart; sharp; pungent;
<as>as, a <ex>piquant</ex> anecdote</as>.</def> <ldquo/As
<xex>piquant</xex> to the tongue as salt.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Piquant</xex> railleries.<rdquo/ <au>Gov. of
Tongue.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piquantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"quant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a piquant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pique</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`qu<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., p. p.
of <ets>piquer</ets> to prick.]</ety> <def>A cotton fabric, figured in
the loom, -- used as a dress goods for women and children, and for
vestings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pique</ent><br/
<hw>Pique</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
jigger. See <er>Jigger</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pique</ent><br/
<hw>Pique</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>piquer</ets>. See <er>Pike</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A feeling
of hurt, vexation, or resentment, awakened by a social slight or injury;
irritation of the feelings, as through wounded pride; stinging
vexation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men take up <qex>piques</qex> and displeasures.</q> <rj><qau>Dr.
H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wars had arisen . . . upon a personal <qex>pique</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Keenly felt desire; a longing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though it have the <qex>pique</qex>, and long,<br/
'Tis still for something in the wrong.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>In piquet, the right of
the elder hand to count thirty in hand, or to play before the adversary
counts one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Displeasure; irritation; grudge; spite.</syn>
<usage> <er>Pique</er>, <er>Spite</er>, <er>Grudge</er>.
<xex>Pique</xex> denotes a quick and often transient sense of resentment
for some supposed neglect or injury, but it is not marked by
malevolence. <xex>Spite</xex> is a stronger term, denoting settled ill
will or malice, with a desire to injure, as the result of extreme
irritation. <xex>Grudge</xex> goes still further, denoting cherished and
secret enmity, with an unforgiving spirit. A <xex>pique</xex> is usually
of recent date; a <xex>grudge</xex> is that which has long subsisted;
<xex>spite</xex> implies a disposition to cross or vex
others.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pique</ent><br/
<hw>Pique</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Piqued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Piquing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>piquer</ets>. See <er>Pike</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wound
the pride of; to sting; to nettle; to irritate; to fret; to offend; to
excite to anger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pique</qex> her, and soothe in turn.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To excite to action by causing resentment or
jealousy; to stimulate; to prick; <as>as, to <ex>pique</ex> ambition, or
curiosity</as>.</def> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pride or value; -- used reflexively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men . . . <qex>pique</qex> themselves upon their skill.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To offend; displease; irritate; provoke; fret;
nettle; sting; goad; stimulate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pique</ent><br/
<hw>Pique</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To cause annoyance or
irritation.</def> <ldquo/Every verse hath something in it that
<xex>piques</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piqueer</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*queer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pickeer</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piqueerer</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*queer"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pickeerer</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piquet</ent><br/
<hw>Piqu"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Picket</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piquet</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*quet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., prob. fr.
<ets>pique</ets>. See <er>Pique</er>, <er>Pike</er>, and
<er>Picket</er>.]</ety> <def>A game at cards played between two persons,
with thirty-two cards, all the deuces, threes, fours, fives, and sixes,
being set aside.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>picket</asp> and
<asp>picquet</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piracy</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Piracies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>piratia</ets>, Gr. <?/. See <er>Pirate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or crime of a pirate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Common Law)</fld> <def>Robbery on the high seas;
the taking of property from others on the open sea by open violence;
without lawful authority, and with intent to steal; -- a crime answering
to <xex>robbery</xex> on land.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- air piracy -- to commandeer or hijack an airplane --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ By statute law several other offenses committed on the
seas (as trading with known pirates, or engaging in the slave trade)
have been made <xex>piracy</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def></def><ldquo/Sometimes used, in a quasi-figurative
sense, of violation of copyright; but for this, <xex>infringement</xex>
is the correct and preferable term.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Abbott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piragua</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*ra"gua</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pirogue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirai</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*rai"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Piraya</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirameter</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*ram"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ trial +
<ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>A dynamometer for ascertaining the power
required to draw carriages over roads.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirarucu</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`ra*ru"cu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
South American name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Arapaima</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirate</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pirata</ets>,
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to attempt, undertake, from making attempts or attacks
on ships, <?/ an attempt, trial; akin to E. <ets>peril</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pirate</ets>. See <er>Peril</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A robber
on the high seas; one who by open violence takes the property of another
on the high seas; especially, one who makes it his business to cruise
for robbery or plunder; a freebooter on the seas; also, one who steals
in a harbor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An armed ship or vessel which sails without a legal
commission, for the purpose of plundering other vessels on the high
seas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who infringes the law of copyright, or publishes
the work of an author without permission.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pirate perch</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
fresh-water percoid fish of the United States (<spn>Aphredoderus
Sayanus</spn>). It is of a dark olive color, speckled with blackish
spots.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirate</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"rate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pirated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pirating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pirater</ets>.]</ety> <def>To play the pirate; to practice robbery
on the high seas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirate</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"rate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To publish, as books or
writings, without the permission of the author.</def><-- or other
copyrighted material; see also the similar "knock off", to manufacture
an object with a brand name, without permission of the brand owner, and
usually of inferior quality --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They advertised they would <qex>pirate</qex> his edition.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piratic</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*rat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Piratical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piratical</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*rat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piraticus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>piratique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a pirate; acquired by, or practicing, piracy;
<as>as, a <ex>piratical</ex> undertaking</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Piratical</xex> printers.<rdquo/ <au>Pope.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pi*rat"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piraya</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*ra"ya</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large voracious fresh-water fish
(<spn>Serrasalmo piraya</spn>) of South America, having lancet-shaped
teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirie</ent><br/
<hw>Pir"ie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pirry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirie</ent><br/
<hw>Pir"ie</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pear</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A pear tree.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pery</asp>, <asp>pyrie</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piririgua</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`ri*ri"gua</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the
native name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A South American bird
(<spn>Guira guira</spn>) allied to the cuckoos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirl</ent><br/
<hw>Pirl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Purl</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To spin, as a top.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To twist or twine, as hair in making fishing
lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirn</ent><br/
<hw>Pirn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A quill or reed on which thread or yarn is wound;
a bobbin; also, the wound yarn on a weaver's shuttle; also, the reel of
a fishing rod.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirogue</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*rogue"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Originally an
American Indian word: cf. F. <ets>pirogue</ets>, Sp. <ets>piroga</ets>,
<ets>piragua</ets>.]</ety> <def>A dugout canoe; by extension, any small
boat.</def> <altsp>[Written variously <asp>periauger</asp>,
<asp>perogue</asp>, <asp>piragua</asp>, <asp>periagua</asp>,
etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirouette</ent><br/
<hw>Pir`ou*ette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.; of uncertain
origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A whirling or turning on the toes in
dancing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>The whirling about of a
horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirouette</ent><br/
<hw>Pir`ou*ette"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pirouetted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pirouetting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pirouetter</ets>.]</ety> <def>To perform a pirouette; to whirl,
like a dancer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pirrie</ent><br/
<ent>Pirry</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pir"ry</hw>, <hw>Pir"rie</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Scot. <ets>pirr</ets> a gentle breeze, Icel.
<ets>byrr</ets> a prosperous wind, <ets>bylr</ets> a blast of
wind.]</ety> <def>A rough gale of wind.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Elyot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisasphaltum</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`as*phal"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pissasphalt</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisay</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"say</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pis<eacute/</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscary</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ca*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscarius</ets> relating to fishes or to fishing, fr.
<ets>piscis</ets> a fish.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The right or
privilege of fishing in another man's waters.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscation</ent><br/
<hw>Pis*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscatio</ets>, fr. <ets>piscari</ets> to fish.]</ety>
<def>Fishing; fishery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscator</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pis*ca"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>A fisherman; an angler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscatory</ent><br/
<ent>Piscatorial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pis`ca*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pis"ca*to*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>piscatorius</ets>, fr.
<ets>piscator</ets> a fisherman, fr. <ets>piscari</ets> to fish, fr.
<ets>piscis</ets> a fish. See <er>Fish</er> the animal.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to fishes or fishing.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisces</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pis"ces</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscis</ets> a fish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The twelfth sign of the zodiac, marked <Pisces/ in
almanacs.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A zodiacal constellation, including
the first point of Aries, which is the vernal equinoctial point; the
Fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The class of Vertebrata that
includes the fishes. The principal divisions are Elasmobranchii,
Ganoidei, and Teleostei.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscicapture</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ci*cap`ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Capture of
fishes, as by angling.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>W. H.
Russell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscicultural</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`ci*cul"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to
pisciculture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisciculture</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`ci*cul"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscis</ets> a fish + E. <ets>culture</ets>.]</ety> <def>Fish
culture. See under <er>Fish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisciculturist</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`ci*cul"tur*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who breeds
fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisciform</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ci*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscis</ets> fish + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form
of a fish; resembling a fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pis*ci"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a certain,
fishpond, fr. <ets>piscis</ets> a fish.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A
niche near the altar in a church, containing a small basin for rinsing
altar vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ci*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscinalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>piscinal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging
to a fishpond or a piscina.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscine</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>piscis</ets>
a fish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a fish or
fishes; <as>as, <ex>piscine</ex> remains</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piscivorous</ent><br/
<hw>Pis*civ"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>piscis</ets> a fish + <ets>vorare</ets> to devour: cf. F.
<ets>piscivore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Feeding or
subsisting on fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pise</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi`s<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pis<eacute/</ets>, from <ets>piser</ets> to stamp, pound, L.
<ets>pisare</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A species of wall made
of stiff earth or clay rammed in between molds which are carried up as
the wall rises; -- called also <altname>pis<eacute/
work</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pish</ent><br/
<hw>Pish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>An exclamation of
contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pish</ent><br/
<hw>Pish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To express
contempt.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pishu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"shu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The Canada lynx.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>peeshoo</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"si*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pisum</ets> a pea + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pisiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a pea or peas in size and
shape; <as>as, a <ex>pisiform</ex> iron ore</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"si*form</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A small bone
on the ulnar side of the carpus in man and many mammals. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Artiodactyla</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pismire</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"mire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Piss</ets> +
<ets>mire</ets>; so called because it discharges a moisture vulgarly
considered urine. See <er>Mire</er> an ant.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An ant, or emmet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pi"so*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a pea +
<ets>-lite</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pisolithe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A variety of calcite, or calcium carbonate, consisting of
aggregated globular concretions about the size of a pea; -- called also
<altname>peastone</altname>, <altname>peagrit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Oolite is similar in structure, but the concretions are
as small as the roe of a fish.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisolitic</ent><br/
<hw>Pi`so*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pisolithique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Composed of,
containing, or resembling, pisolite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pisophalt</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"o*phalt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>pissasphalt</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Pissasphalt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piss</ent><br/
<hw>Piss</hw> <pr>(p<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pissen</ets>, F. <ets>pisser</ets>; akin to It.
<ets>pisciare</ets>, D. & G. <ets>pissen</ets>, Dan. <ets>pisse</ets>,
Icel. <ets>pissa</ets>.]</ety> <def>To discharge urine, to urinate; --
usually considered vulgar.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piss</ent><br/
<hw>Piss</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Urine.</def> <mark>[vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>take a piss</b></col> <cd>urinate.</cd>
<mark>[vulgar]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pissabed</ent><br/
<hw>Piss"a*bed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A name locally applied to various wild plants, as dandelion, bluet,
oxeye daisy, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pissasphalt</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"sas*phalt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pissasphaltus</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ pitch + <?/ asphalt: cf. F.
<ets>pissasphalte</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Earth pitch; a
soft, black bitumen of the consistency of tar, and of a strong smell. It
is inflammable, and intermediate between petroleum and asphalt.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pisasphaltum</asp>, <asp>pisasphalt</asp>,
etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pissant</ent><br/
<hw>piss"ant`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Worthless or of no
significance.</def> <mark>[vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>piss away</ent><br/
<hw>piss away</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To waste; to squander; to
fritter away; <as>as, he <ex>pissed away</ex> his opportunity to get the
job by arriving late for the interview</as>.</def>
<mark>[vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pissed off</ent><br/
<ent>pissed</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pissed</hw>, <hw>pissed off</hw></mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Angered or very annoyed.</def> <mark>[vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pisser</ent><br/
<hw>pis"ser</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something very difficult or
troublesome.</def> <mark>[vulgar slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>piss off</ent><br/
<hw>piss off</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To anger or strongly annoy;
<as>as, his flippant attitude <ex>pissed off</ex> the
teacher</as>.</def> <mark>[vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>piss off</ent><br/
<hw>piss off</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To leave immediately; to scram;
-- chiefly British, and sometimes considered vulgar.</def> <mark>[Brit.,
vulgar]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pissoir</ent><br/
<hw>pissoir</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a public urinal, in European
countries.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pist</ent><br/
<hw>Pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Piste</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistache</ent><br/
<hw>Pis*tache"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pistace</ets>, fr. F. <ets>pistache</ets>. See
<er>Pistachio</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The anacardiaceous
tree <spn>Pistacia vera</spn>, which yields the pistachio nut; also, the
nut itself and the flavoring extract prepared from it. Called also
<altname>pistachio</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistachio</ent><br/
<hw>Pis*ta"chio</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pistacchio</ets> (cf. Sp. <ets>pistacho</ets>, F.
<ets>pistache</ets>), fr. L. <ets>pistacium</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/, fr.
Per. <ets>pistah</ets>. Cf. <er>Fistinut</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The nut of the <spn>Pistacia vera</spn>, a tree of the order
<ord>Anacardiace<ae/</ord>, containing a kernel of a pale greenish
color, which has a pleasant taste, resembling that of the almond, and
yields an oil of agreeable taste and odor; -- called also
<altname>pistachio nut</altname>. It is wholesome and nutritive. The
tree grows in Arabia, Persia, Syria, and Sicily.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>pistachia</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The small anacardiaceous tree (<spn>Pistacia
vera</spn>), of southern Europe and Asia Minor, which bears the
pistachio nut. Called also <altname>pistache</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>The flavor of the pistachio
nut, or an ice or confection flavored with it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pistachio green.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistachio green</ent><br/
<hw>Pistachio green</hw>. <def>A light yellowish green color resembling
that of the pistachio nut.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistacia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pis*ta"ci*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pistachio</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The name of a genus of
trees, including the tree which bears the pistachio (<spn>Pistacia
vera</spn>), the Mediterranean mastic tree (<spn>Pistacia
Lentiscus</spn>), and the species (<spn>Pistacia Terebinthus</spn>)
which yields Chian or Cyprus turpentine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistacite</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ta*cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pistacite</ets>. So called from its green color. See
<er>Pistachio</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Epidote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistareen</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`ta*reen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An old Spanish
silver coin of the value of about twenty cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistazite</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ta*zite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pistacite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piste</ent><br/
<hw>Piste</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pisere</ets>, <ets>pinsere</ets>, <ets>pistum</ets>, to
pound.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The track or tread a horseman makes
upon the ground he goes over.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistil</ent><br/
<ent>Pistel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pis"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pis"til</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An epistle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistic</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pisticus</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Pure; genuine.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistil</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"til</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pistillum</ets>, <ets>pistillus</ets>, a pestle: cf. F.
<ets>pistil</ets>. See <er>Pestle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The seed-bearing organ of a flower. It consists of an ovary,
containing the ovules or rudimentary seeds, and a stigma, which is
commonly raised on an elongated portion called a <xex>style</xex>. When
composed of one carpel a pistil is simple; when composed of several, it
is compound. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Flower</er>, and
<er>Ovary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistillaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`til*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Growing on, or having nature of, the pistil; of or pertaining to a
pistil.</def> <rj><au>Barton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistillate</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"til*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having a pistil or pistils; -- usually said of flowers having
pistils but no stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistillation</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`til*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pistillum</ets> a pestle.]</ety> <def>The act of pounding or
breaking in a mortar; pestillation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistillidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pis`til*lid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pistillida</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. E. <ets>pistil</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Archegonium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistilliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`til*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pistil</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pistillif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Pistillate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistillody</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"til*lo*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pistil</ets> + Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The metamorphosis of other organs into
pistils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistol</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"tol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pistole</ets>, <ets>pistolet</ets>, It. <ets>pistola</ets>; prob.
from a form <ets>Pistola</ets>, for <ets>Pistoja</ets>, a town in Italy
where pistols were first made. Cf. <er>Pistole</er>.]</ety> <def>The
smallest firearm used, intended to be fired from one hand, -- now of
many patterns, and bearing a great variety of names. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Revolver</er>.</def><-- generically, also
called handgun --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pistol carbine</b></col>, <cd>a firearm with a removable
but-piece, and thus capable of being used either as a pistol or a
carbine.</cd> -- <col><b>Pistol pipe</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>,
<cd>a pipe in which the blast for a furnace is heated, resembling a
pistol in form.</cd> -- <col><b>Pistol shot</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The discharge of a pistol</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The distance to
which a pistol can propel a ball.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistol</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"tol</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pistoled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pistoling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pistoler</ets>.]</ety> <def>To shoot with a pistol.</def> <ldquo/To
<xex>pistol</xex> a poacher.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sydney Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistolade</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"to*lade`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
pistol shot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistole</ent><br/
<hw>Pis*tole"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., probably a name
given in jest in France to a Spanish coin. Cf. <er>Pistol</er>.]</ety>
<def>The name of certain gold coins of various values formerly coined in
some countries of Europe. In Spain it was equivalent to a quarter
doubloon, or about $3.90, and in Germany and Italy nearly the same.
There was an old Italian pistole worth about $5.40.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistoleer</ent><br/
<hw>Pis`to*leer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pistolier</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who uses a pistol.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pistolet</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"to*let`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., a dim. of
<ets>pistole</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small pistol.</def> <rj><au>Donne.
Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- pistol-whip, to beat with a pistol --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1091 --></p>

<p><ent>Piston</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>piston</ets>;
cf. It. <ets>pistone</ets> piston, also <ets>pestone</ets> a large
pestle; all fr. L. <ets>pinsere</ets>, <ets>pistum</ets>, to pound, to
stamp. See <er>Pestle</er>, <er>Pistil</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>
<def>A sliding piece which either is moved by, or moves against, fluid
pressure. It usually consists of a short cylinder fitting within a
cylindrical vessel along which it moves, back and forth. It is used in
steam engines to receive motion from the steam, and in pumps to transmit
motion to a fluid; also for other purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Piston head</b></col> <fld>(Steam Eng.)</fld>, <cd>that
part of a piston which is made fast to the piston rod.</cd> --
<col><b>Piston rod</b></col>, <cd>a rod by which a piston is moved, or
by which it communicates motion.</cd> -- <col><b>Piston valve</b></col>
<fld>(Steam Eng.)</fld>, <cd>a slide valve, consisting of a piston, or
connected pistons, working in a cylindrical case which is provided with
ports that are traversed by the valve.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piston ring</ent><br/
<hw>Pis"ton ring</hw>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A spring packing ring, or
any of several such rings, for a piston.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pit</ent><br/
<hw>Pit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pit</ets>,
<ets>put</ets>, AS. <ets>pytt</ets> a pit, hole, L. <ets>puteus</ets> a
well, pit.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large cavity or hole in the ground,
either natural or artificial; a cavity in the surface of a body; an
indentation</def>; specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The shaft of a coal
mine; a coal pit.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A large hole in the ground
from which material is dug or quarried; <as>as, a stone <ex>pit</ex>; a
gravel <ex>pit</ex></as>; or in which material is made by burning;
<as>as, a lime <ex>pit</ex>; a charcoal <ex>pit</ex></as>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A vat sunk in the ground; <as>as, a tan
<ex>pit</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tumble me into some loathsome <qex>pit</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any abyss; especially, the grave, or
hades.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Back to the infernal <qex>pit</qex> I drag thee chained.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He keepth back his soul from the <qex>pit</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Job
xxxiii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A covered deep hole for entrapping wild beasts; a
pitfall; hence, a trap; a snare. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The anointed of the Lord was taken in their <qex>pits</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Lam. iv. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A depression or hollow in the surface of the human
body</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hollow place under the shoulder or
arm; the axilla, or armpit.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>See <cref>Pit of the
stomach</cref> (below).</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The indentation or mark
left by a pustule, as in smallpox.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Formerly, that part of a theater, on the floor of
the house, below the level of the stage and behind the orchestra; now,
in England, commonly the part behind the stalls; in the United States,
the parquet; also, the occupants of such a part of a theater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An inclosed area into which gamecocks, dogs, and
other animals are brought to fight, or where dogs are trained to kill
rats.</def> <ldquo/As fiercely as two gamecocks in the
<xex>pit</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>pit</ets>, akin to E.
<ets>pith</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The endocarp
of a drupe, and its contained seed or seeds; a stone; <as>as, a peach
<ex>pit</ex>; a cherry <ex>pit</ex>, etc.</as></def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
depression or thin spot in the wall of a duct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cold pit</b></col> <fld>(Hort.)</fld>, <cd>an excavation
in the earth, lined with masonry or boards, and covered with glass, but
not artificially heated, -- used in winter for the storing and
protection of half-hardly plants, and sometimes in the spring as a
forcing bed.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit coal</b></col>, <cd>coal dug from the
earth; mineral coal.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit frame</b></col>, <cd>the
framework over the shaft of a coal mine.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit
head</b></col>, <cd>the surface of the ground at the mouth of a pit or
mine.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit kiln</b></col>, <cd>an oven for coking
coal.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit martin</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
bank swallow.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pit of the
stomach</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the depression on the middle
line of the epigastric region of the abdomen at the lower end of the
sternum; the infrasternal depression.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit saw</b></col>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a saw worked by two men, one of whom stands on
the log and the other beneath it. The place of the latter is often in a
pit, whence the name.</cd> -- <col><b>pit stop</b></col>, <cd>See
<er>pit stop</er> in the vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Pit viper</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any viperine snake having a deep pit on each
side of the snout. The rattlesnake and copperhead are examples.</cd> --
<col><b>Working pit</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a shaft in which
the ore is hoisted and the workmen carried; -- in distinction from a
shaft used for the pumps.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pit</ent><br/
<hw>Pit</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pitted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place or put into
a pit or hole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They lived like beasts, and were <qex>pitted</qex> like beasts,
tumbled into the grave.</q> <rj><qau>T. Grander.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mark with little hollows, as by various pustules;
<as>as, a face <ex>pitted</ex> by smallpox</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To introduce as an antagonist; to set forward for or
in a contest; <as>as, to <ex>pit</ex> one dog against
another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A fiber obtained from the <spn>Agave
Americana</spn> and other related species, -- used for making cordage
and paper. Called also <altname>pita fiber</altname>, and <altname>pita
thread</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The plant which yields the
fiber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pita</ent><br/
<mhw>\'d8<hw>Pi"ta</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<adot/)</pr>, <hw>Pi"ta
bread`</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety> <def>A
thin flat bread that is formed in two layers, to make a pocket into
which other foods may be placed.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitahaya</ent><br/
<hw>Pit`a*ha"ya</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., prob. from
the native name.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A cactaceous shrub
(<spn>Cereus Pitajaya</spn>) of tropical America, which yields a
delicious fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitapat</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"a*pat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[An onomatopoetic
reduplication of <ets>pat</ets> a light, quick blow.]</ety> <def>In a
flutter; with palpitation or quick succession of beats.</def>
<au>Lowell.</au> <ldquo/The fox's heart went <xex>pitapat</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>L'Estrange.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitapat</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"a*pat`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A light, repeated sound; a
pattering, as of the rain.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>pitapat</xex> of a
pretty foot.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pich</ets>,
AS. <ets>pic</ets>, L. <ets>pix</ets>; akin to Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A thick, black, lustrous, and sticky substance obtained
by boiling down tar. It is used in calking the seams of ships; also in
coating rope, canvas, wood, ironwork, etc., to preserve them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that toucheth <qex>pitch</qex> shall be defiled therewith.</q>
<rj><qau>Ecclus. xiii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pitchstone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Amboyna pitch</b></col>, <cd>the resin of <spn>Dammara
australis</spn>. See <er>Kauri</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Burgundy
pitch</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Burgundy</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Canada
pitch</b></col>, <cd>the resinous exudation of the hemlock tree
(<spn>Abies Canadensis</spn>); hemlock gum.</cd> -- <col><b>Jew's
pitch</b></col>, <cd>bitumen.</cd> -- <col><b>Mineral pitch</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Bitumen</er> and <er>Asphalt</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch
coal</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>bituminous coal.</cd> --
<col><b>Pitch peat</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a black homogeneous
peat, with a waxy luster.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch pine</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of pine, yielding
pitch, esp. the <spn>Pinus rigida</spn> of North America.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pitched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pitching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Pitch</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover over or smear with
pitch.</def> <rj><au>Gen. vi. 14.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To darken; to blacken; to obscure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The welkin <qex>pitched</qex> with sullen could.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>picchen</ets>; akin to E. <ets>pick</ets>, <ets>pike</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw, generally with a definite aim or purpose; to
cast; to hurl; to toss; <as>as, to <ex>pitch</ex> quoits; to
<ex>pitch</ex> hay; to <ex>pitch</ex> a ball.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To thrust or plant in the ground, as stakes or
poles; hence, to fix firmly, as by means of poles; to establish; to
arrange; <as>as, to <ex>pitch</ex> a tent; to <ex>pitch</ex> a
camp.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To set, face, or pave with rubble or undressed
stones, as an embankment or a roadway.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To fix or set the tone of; <as>as, to <ex>pitch</ex>
a tune</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To set or fix, as a price or value.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pitched battle</b></col>, <cd>a general battle; a battle
in which the hostile forces have fixed positions; -- in distinction from
a <xex>skirmish</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>To pitch into</b></col>, <cd>to
attack; to assault; to abuse.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fix or place a tent
or temporary habitation; to encamp.</def> <ldquo/Laban with his brethren
<xex>pitched</xex> in the Mount of Gilead.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Gen. xxxi.
25.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To light; to settle; to come to rest from
flight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tree whereon they [the bees] <qex>pitch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fix one's choice; -- with <xex>on</xex> or
<xex>upon</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pitch</qex> upon the best course of life, and custom will
render it the more easy.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To plunge or fall; esp., to fall forward; to decline
or slope; <as>as, to <ex>pitch</ex> from a precipice; the vessel
<ex>pitches</ex> in a heavy sea; the field <ex>pitches</ex> toward the
east.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pitch and pay</b></col>, <cd>an old aphorism which
inculcates ready-money payment, or payment on delivery of goods.</cd>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A throw; a toss; a cast,
as of something from the hand; <as>as, a good <ex>pitch</ex> in
quoits</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pitch and toss</b></col>, <cd>a game played by tossing up
a coin, and calling <ldquo/Heads or tails;<rdquo/</cd> hence: <col><b>To
play pitch and toss with <plain>(anything)</plain></b></col>, <cd>to be
careless or trust to luck about it.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>To play pitch and
toss with</xex> the property of the country.<rdquo/ <au>G. Eliot.</au>
-- <col><b>Pitch farthing</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Chuck
farthing</cref>, under 5th <er>Chuck</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>That point of the ground on
which the ball pitches or lights when bowled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A point or peak; the extreme point or degree of
elevation or depression; hence, a limit or bound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Driven headlong from the <qex>pitch</qex> of heaven, down<br/
Into this deep.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enterprises of great <qex>pitch</qex> and moment.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To lowest <qex>pitch</qex> of abject fortune.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He lived when learning was at its highest <qex>pitch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The exact <qex>pitch</qex>, or limits, where temperance ends.</q>
<rj><qau>Sharp.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Height; stature.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A descent; a fall; a thrusting down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The point where a declivity begins; hence, the
declivity itself; a descending slope; the degree or rate of descent or
slope; slant; <as>as, a steep <ex>pitch</ex> in the road; the
<ex>pitch</ex> of a roof.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The relative acuteness or gravity
of a tone, determined by the number of vibrations which produce it; the
place of any tone upon a scale of high and low.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Musical tones with reference to <xex>absolute
pitch</xex>, are named after the first seven letters of the alphabet;
with reference to <xex>relative pitch</xex>, in a series of tones called
the <xex>scale</xex>, they are called <xex>one</xex>, <xex>two</xex>,
<xex>three</xex>, <xex>four</xex>, <xex>five</xex>, <xex>six</xex>,
<xex>seven</xex>, <xex>eight</xex>. <xex>Eight</xex> is also
<xex>one</xex> of a new scale an octave higher, as <xex>one</xex> is
<xex>eight</xex> of a scale an octave lower.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The limit of ground set to a
miner who receives a share of the ore taken out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The distance from
center to center of any two adjacent teeth of gearing, measured on the
<xex>pitch line</xex>; -- called also <xex>circular pitch</xex>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The length, measured along the axis, of a complete
turn of the thread of a screw, or of the helical lines of the blades of
a screw propeller.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The distance between the
centers of holes, as of rivet holes in boiler plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The distance between
symmetrically arranged or corresponding parts of an armature, measured
along a line, called the <xex>pitch line</xex>, drawn around its length.
Sometimes half of this distance is called the pitch.</def></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Concert pitch</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>the
standard of pitch used by orchestras, as in concerts, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Diametral pitch</b></col> <fld>(Gearing)</fld>, <cd>the distance
which bears the same relation to the pitch proper, or circular pitch,
that the diameter of a circle bears to its circumference; it is
sometimes described by the number expressing the quotient obtained by
dividing the number of teeth in a wheel by the diameter of its pitch
circle in inches; as, 4 pitch, 8 pitch, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch
chain</b></col>, <cd>a chain, as one made of metallic plates, adapted
for working with a sprocket wheel.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Pitch
line</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pitch circle</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Gearing)</fld>, <cd>an ideal line, in a toothed gear or rack,
bearing such a relation to a corresponding line in another gear, with
which the former works, that the two lines will have a common velocity
as in rolling contact; it usually cuts the teeth at about the middle of
their height, and, in a circular gear, is a circle concentric with the
axis of the gear; the line, or circle, on which the pitch of teeth is
measured.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch of a roof</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>,
<cd>the inclination or slope of the sides expressed by the height in
parts of the span; as, one half <xex>pitch</xex>; whole
<xex>pitch</xex>; or by the height in parts of the half span, especially
among engineers; or by degrees, as a <xex>pitch</xex> of 30<deg/, of
45<deg/, etc.; or by the <xex>rise</xex> and <xex>run</xex>, that is,
the ratio of the height to the half span; as, a <xex>pitch</xex> of six
rise to ten run. <xex>Equilateral pitch</xex> is where the two sloping
sides with the span form an equilateral triangle.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch
of a plane</b></col> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>the slant of the cutting
iron.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch of poles</b></col> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>,
<cd>the distance between a pair of poles of opposite sign.</cd> --
<col><b>Pitch pipe</b></col>, <cd>a wind instrument used by choristers
in regulating the pitch of a tune.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitch point</b></col>
<fld>(Gearing)</fld>, <cd>the point of contact of the pitch lines of two
gears, or of a rack and pinion, which work together.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch-black</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"-black`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Black as pitch
or tar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchblende</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"blende`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[1st <ets>pitch
+ blende</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A pitch-black mineral
consisting chiefly of the oxide of uranium; uraninite. See
<er>Uraninite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch-dark</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"-dark`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dark as a pitch;
pitch-black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitcher</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pitches anything, as hay, quoits, a ball, etc.; specifically
<fld>(Baseball)</fld>, the player who delivers the ball to the
batsman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sort of crowbar for digging.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitcher</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>picher</ets>, OF. <ets>pichier</ets>, OHG. <ets>pehhar</ets>,
<ets>pehh<amac/ri</ets>; prob. of the same origin as E.
<ets>beaker</ets>. Cf. <er>Beaker</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
wide-mouthed, deep vessel for holding liquids, with a spout or
protruding lip and a handle; a water jug or jar with a large ear or
handle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tubular or cuplike appendage or
expansion of the leaves of certain plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>American pitcher plants</b></col>, <cd>the species of
Sarracenia. See <er>Sarracenia</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Australian pitcher
plant</b></col>, <cd>the <spn>Cephalotus follicularis</spn>, a low
saxifragaceous herb having two kinds of radical leaves, some
oblanceolate and entire, others transformed into little ovoid pitchers,
longitudinally triple-winged and ciliated, the mouth covered with a lid
shaped like a cockleshell.</cd> -- <col><b>California pitcher
plant</b></col>, <cd>the <spn>Darlingtonia California</spn>. See
<er>Darlingtonia</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pitcher plant</b></col>, <cd>any
plant with the whole or a part of the leaves transformed into pitchers
or cuplike organs, especially the species of <gen>Nepenthes</gen>. See
<er>Nepenthes</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitcherful</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"er*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pitcherfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The quantity a pitcher
will hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch-faced</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"-faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Stone
Cutting)</fld> <def>Having the arris defined by a line beyond which the
rock is cut away, so as to give nearly true edges; -- said of squared
stones that are otherwise quarry-faced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchfork</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"fork`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fork, or farming
utensil, used in pitching hay, sheaves of grain, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchfork</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"fork`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pitch or throw with, or
as with, a pitchfork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He has been <qex>pitchforked</qex> into the footguards.</q>
<rj><qau>G. A. Sala.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pitchy</er>.]</ety> <def>Blackness, as of pitch; darkness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitching</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of throwing
or casting; a cast; a pitch; <as>as, wild <ex>pitching</ex> in
baseball</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The rough paving of a street to a grade with blocks
of stone.</def> <rj><au>Mayhew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Hydraul. Eng.)</fld> <def>A facing of stone laid
upon a bank to prevent wear by tides or currents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pitching piece</b></col> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>the
horizontal timber supporting the floor of a platform of a stairway, and
against which the stringpieces of the sloping parts are
supported.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitch-ore</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"-ore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Pitchblende.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchstone</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>An igneous rock of semiglassy nature, having a luster like
pitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchwork</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The work of a coal
miner who is paid by a share of his product.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitchy</ent><br/
<hw>Pitch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 1st
<er>Pitch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Partaking of the qualities of
pitch; resembling pitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Smeared with pitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Black; pitch-dark; dismal.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Pitchy</xex> night.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piteous</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"e*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pitous</ets>, OF. <ets>pitos</ets>, F. <ets>piteux</ets>. See
<er>Pity</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pious; devout.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord can deliver <qex>piteous</qex> men from temptation.</q>
<rj><qau>Wyclif.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Evincing pity, compassion, or sympathy;
compassionate; tender.</def> <ldquo/[She] <xex>piteous</xex> of his
case.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was so charitable and so <qex>pitous</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fitted to excite pity or sympathy; wretched;
miserable; lamentable; sad; <as>as, a <ex>piteous</ex> case</as>.</def>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The most <qex>piteous</qex> tale of Lear.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Paltry; mean; pitiful.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Piteous</xex> amends.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sorrowful; mournful; affecting; doleful; woeful;
rueful; sad; wretched; miserable; pitiable; pitiful;
compassionate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pit"e*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pit"e*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitfall</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"fall`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pit deceitfully
covered to entrap wild beasts or men; a trap of any kind.</def>
<rj><au>Sir T. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitfalling</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"fall`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Entrapping; insnaring.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Full of . . . contradiction and
<xex>pitfalling</xex> dispenses.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pith</ent><br/
<hw>Pith</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>pi<?/a</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>pit</ets> pith, kernel, LG. <ets>peddik</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pit</er> a kernel.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
soft spongy substance in the center of the stems of many plants and
trees, especially those of the dicotyledonous or exogenous classes. It
consists of cellular tissue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The spongy interior
substance of a feather.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
spinal cord; the marrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence: The which contains the strength of life; the
vital or essential part; concentrated force; vigor; strength;
importance; <as>as, the speech lacked <ex>pith</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enterprises of great <qex>pith</qex> and moment.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pith paper</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Rice
paper</cref>, under <er>Rice</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pith</ent><br/
<hw>Pith</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To destroy
the central nervous system of (an animal, as a frog), as by passing a
stout wire or needle up and down the vertebral canal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithecanthropus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pith`e*can*thro"pus</hw>
<pr>(p<icr/th`<esl/*k<acr/n*thr<omac/"p<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.; Gr. <grk>pi`qhkos</grk> ape + <grk>'a`nqrwpos</grk>
man.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A hypothetical genus of primates
intermediate between man and the anthropoid apes.</def>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A genus consisting of an extinct primate
(<spn>Pithecanthropus erectus</spn>) apparently intermediate between man
and the existing anthropoid apes, known from bones first found in
<geog>Java</geog> (hence called <altname>Java man</altname>) in 1891-92,
and other bones found later. The species was renamed <altname><spn>Homo
erectus</spn></altname> around 1960. The Javan bones are believed to be
from 1.6 to 1.9 million years old, and include a thigh bone of the human
type, two molar teeth intermediate between those of man and the
anthropoids, and the calvaria of the skull, indicating a brain capacity
of about 900 cubic centimeters, and resembling in form that of the
Neanderthal man. Additional specimens of what are considered as variants
of the species have been found in <country>China</country>,
<geog>Africa</geog>, and <geog>Europe</geog>. <spn>Homo erectus</spn> is
currently believed to have evolved in Africa from <spn>Homo
habilis</spn>, the first member of the genus <gen>Homo</gen>.
Anatomically and physiologically, <spn>Homo erectus</spn> resembles
contemporary humans except for having a stouter bone structure.</def>
<def2>Also <plu>[<it>pl.</it> <plw>-thropi</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>]</plu>,
<def>an animal of this genus.</def></def2> --
<wordforms><wf>Pith`e*can"thrope</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pith`e*can"thro*poid</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitheci</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi*the"ci</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>pi`qhkos</grk> an ape.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
division of mammals including the apes and monkeys. Sometimes used in
the sense of <xex>Primates</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithecoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"e*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ an ape +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the genus <gen>Pithecia</gen>, or subfamily
<fam>Pithecin<ae/</fam>, which includes the saki, ouakari, and other
allied South American monkeys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the anthropoid apes in
particular, or to the higher apes of the Old World,
collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithful</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of pith.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>W. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithily</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pithy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
pithy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithless</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of pith, or of
strength; feeble.</def> <au>Dryden.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Pithless</xex>
argumentation.<rdquo/ <au>Glandstone.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pit-hole</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"-hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pit; a
pockmark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithsome</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pithy; robust.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pithsome</xex> health and vigor.<rdquo/
<rj><au>R. D. Blackmore.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pithy</ent><br/
<hw>Pith"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Pithier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Pithiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting wholly, or
in part, of pith; abounding in pith; <as>as, a <ex>pithy</ex> stem; a
<ex>pithy</ex> fruit.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having nervous energy; forceful; cogent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>pithy</qex> speech prevailed, and all agreed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In all these Goodman Fact was very short, but
<qex>pithy</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pithy gall</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large,
rough, furrowed, oblong gall, formed on blackberry canes by a small
gallfly (<spn>Diastrophus nebulosus</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1092 --></p>

<p><ent>Pitiable</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"i*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>pitiable</ets>, F. <ets>pitoyable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Deserving
pity; worthy of, or exciting, compassion; miserable; lamentable;
piteous; <as>as, <ex>pitiable</ex> persons; a <ex>pitiable</ex>
condition; <ex>pitiable</ex> wretchedness.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sorrowful; woeful; sad. See
<er>Piteous</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pit"i*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pit"i*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitier</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"i*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pities.</def>
<rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitiful</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"i*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
pity; tender-hearted; compassionate; kind; merciful;
sympathetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord is very <qex>pitiful</qex>, and of tender mercy.</q>
<rj><qau>James v. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Piteous; lamentable; eliciting compassion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thing, indeed, very <qex>pitiful</qex> and horrible.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be pitied for littleness or meanness; miserable;
paltry; contemptible; despicable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That's villainous, and shows a most <qex>pitiful</qex> ambition in
the fool that uses it.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Despicable; mean; paltry. See
<er>Contemptible</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pit"i*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pit"i*ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitiless</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"i*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Destitute of pity;
hard-hearted; merciless; <as>as, a <ex>pitiless</ex>master;
<ex>pitiless</ex> elements.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exciting no pity; <as>as, a <ex>pitiless</ex>
condition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pit"i*less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pit"i*less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitman</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pitmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who works
in a pit, as in mining, in sawing timber, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>The connecting rod in a sawmill;
also, sometimes, a connecting rod in other machinery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>piton</ent><br/
<hw>pi"ton</hw> <pr>(p<emac/"t<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mountain
Climbing)</fld> <def>A metal spike having a sharpened point on one end,
and a hole through which a rope can be passed on the other; it is driven
into the face of a rock cliff during climbing, and used as an anchor
point for a rope.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitot's tube</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*tot's" tube`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Hydraul.)</fld> <def>A bent
tube used to determine the velocity of running water, by placing the
curved end under water, and observing the height to which the fluid
rises in the tube; a kind of current meter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitpan</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"pan`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A long, flat-bottomed
canoe, used for the navigation of rivers and lagoons in Central
America.</def> <rj><au>Squier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitpat</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"pat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & adv.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pitapat</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pitprop</ent><br/
<hw>pit"prop</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wooden prop used to support the
roof of a mine temporarily.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pitprop</ent><br/
<hw>pit"prop</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wooden prop used to support the
roof of a mine temporarily.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>the pits</ent><br/
<ent>pits</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pits</hw>, <hw>the pits</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The worst
possible situation, person, or thing; something extremely bad, boring,
or depressing; -- always used with <it>the</it>; <as>as, cleaning the
house is <ex>the pits</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pitsaw</ent><br/
<hw>pit"saw</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A large two-handed saw formerly used
to cut logs into planks; one man stood above the log and the other in a
pit below.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pit stop</ent><br/
<hw>pit" stop`</hw> <pr>(p<icr/t" st<ocr/p`)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Auto Racing)</fld> <def>A stop by one of the competing
cars in a <it>pit</it> by the side of the racetrack, to take on
gasoline, change tires, or perform other maintenance.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any pause in a journey, to
eat or drink, or to use a rest room.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place where one may make a <er>pit stop</er>[1 or
2].</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitt diamond</ent><br/
<hw>Pitt diamond</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>regent
diamond</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitta</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ta</hw> <pr>(p<icr/t"t<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of a large group of bright-colored
clamatorial birds belonging to <gen>Pitta</gen>, and allied genera of
the family <fam>Pittid<ae/</fam>. Most of the species are varied with
three or more colors, such as blue, green, crimson, yellow, purple, and
black. They are called also <altname>ground thrushes</altname>, and
<altname>Old World ant thrushes</altname>; but they are not related to
the true thrushes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The pittas are most abundant in the East Indies, but
some inhabit Southern Asia, Africa, and Australia. They live mostly upon
the ground, and feed upon insects of various kinds.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pittacal</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ta*cal</hw> <pr>(p<icr/t"t<adot/*k<acr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>pi`tta</grk>, <grk>pi`ssa</grk>, pitch +
<grk>kalo`s</grk> beautiful: cf. F. <ets>pittacale</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A dark blue substance obtained from wood tar. It
consists of hydrocarbons which when oxidized form the orange-yellow
eupittonic compounds, the salts of which are dark blue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pittance</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"tance</hw> <pr>(p<icr/t"t<ait/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pitance</ets>, <ets>pitaunce</ets>, F. <ets>pitance</ets>; cf. It.
<ets>pietanza</ets>, LL. <ets>pitancia</ets>, <ets>pittantia</ets>,
<ets>pictantia</ets>; perh. fr. L. <ets>pietas</ets> pity, piety, or
perhaps akin to E. <ets>petty</ets>. Cf. <er>Petty</er>, and
<er>Pity</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An allowance of food bestowed in
charity; a mess of victuals; hence, a small charity gift; a dole.</def>
<ldquo/A good <xex>pitaunce</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One half only of this <qex>pittance</qex> was ever given him in
money.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A meager portion, quantity, or allowance; an
inconsiderable salary or compensation.</def> <ldquo/The small
<xex>pittance</xex> of learning they received.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The inconsiderable <qex>pittance</qex> of faithful professors.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitted</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ted</hw> <pr>(p<icr/t"t<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Marked with little pits, as in smallpox. See <er>Pit</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having minute thin spots; <as>as,
<ex>pitted</ex> ducts in the vascular parts of vegetable
tissue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitter</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A contrivance for
removing the pits from peaches, plums, and other stone fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitter</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a pattering sound; to
murmur; <as>as, <ex>pittering</ex> streams</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>R. Greene.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitter-patter</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ter-pat`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sound like
that of alternating light beats. Also, a pattering of words.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitter-patter</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"ter-pat`ter</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With, or with the sound
of, alternating light beats; <as>as, his heart went
<ex>pitter-patter</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pittle-pattle</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"tle-pat`tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To talk
unmeaningly; to chatter or prattle.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pituitary</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*tu"i*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pituita</ets> phlegm, pituite: cf. F. <ets>pituitarie</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Secreting mucus or phlegm; <as>as,
the <ex>pituitary</ex> membrane, or the mucous membrane which lines the
nasal cavities</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the
pituitary body; <as>as, the <ex>pituitary</ex> fossa</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pituitary body</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pituitary gland</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a a
small, somewhat cherry-shaped endocrine gland, situated in the pituitary
fossa, and suspended from the base of the hypothalamus; the hypophysis;
-- called also <altname>glandula pituitaria</altname>, and
<altname>basilaris</altname>. It secretes th pituitary hormones:
oxytocin; vasopresin; antidiuretic hormone; luteinizing hormone;
somatotropins; prolactin; thyroid stimulating hormone; gonadotropins;
adrenal corticotropin and other peptide hormones. It affects all
hormonal functions, and is thus called the <ldquo/master
gland<rdquo/.</cd> -- <col><b>Pituitary fossa</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the ephippium.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pituite</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"u*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pituita</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pituite</ets>. Cf. <er>Pip</er> a
disease of fowls.]</ety> <def>Mucus, phlegm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pituitous</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*tu"i*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pituitosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pituiteux</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Consisting of, or resembling, pituite or mucus; full of mucus;
discharging mucus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pituitous fever</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>typhoid
fever; enteric fever.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pituitrin</ent><br/
<hw>Pi*tu"i*trin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance or extract from the pituitary
body.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pity</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>pite</ets>, OF.
<ets>pit<eacute/</ets>, <ets>piti<eacute/</ets>, F.
<ets>piti<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>pietas</ets> piety, kindness, pity. See
<er>Pious</er>, and cf. <er>Piety</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Piety.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A feeling for the sufferings or distresses of
another or others; sympathy with the grief or misery of another;
compassion; fellow-feeling; commiseration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that hath <qex>pity</qex> upon the poor lendeth unto the
Lord.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xix. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . has no more <qex>pity</qex> in him than a dog.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A reason or cause of pity, grief, or regret; a thing
to be regretted.</def> <ldquo/The more the <xex>pity</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>pity</qex> is it<br/
That we can die but once to serve our country!</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense, sometimes used in the plural, especially
in the colloquialism: <ldquo/It is a thousand
<xex>pities</xex>.<rdquo/</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Compassion; mercy; commiseration; condolence;
sympathy, fellow-suffering; fellow-feeling. -- <er>Pity</er>,
<er>Sympathy</er>, <er>Compassion</er>. <xex>Sympathy</xex> is literally
<xex>fellow-feeling</xex>, and therefore requiers a certain degree of
equality in situation, circumstances, etc., to its fullest exercise.
<xex>Compassion</xex> is deep tenderness for another under severe or
inevitable misfortune. <xex>Pity</xex> regards its object not only as
suffering, but <xex>weak</xex>, and hence as inferior.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pity</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pitied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pitying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To feel pity or
compassion for; to have sympathy with; to compassionate; to commiserate;
to have tender feelings toward (any one), awakened by a knowledge of
suffering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like as a father <qex>pitieth</qex> his children, so the Lord
<qex>pitieth</qex> them that fear him.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. ciii.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move to pity; -- used impersonally.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It <qex>pitieth</qex> them to see her in the dust.</q>
<rj><qau>Bk. of Com. Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pity</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"y</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be compassionate; to show
pity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will not <qex>pity</qex>, nor spare, nor have mercy.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. xiii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pitying</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"y*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Expressing pity; <as>as, a
<ex>pitying</ex> eye, glance, or word</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pit"y*ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pityriasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pit`y*ri"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/, lit., bran.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A superficial affection of the skin, characterized by irregular
patches of thin scales which are shed in branlike particles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Veter.)</fld> <def>A disease of domestic animals
characterized by dry epithelial scales, and due to digestive
disturbances and alteration of the function of the sebaceous
glands.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Pityriasis versicolor</b></col><cd> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, a parasitic disease of the skin, characterized by the
development of reddish or brownish patches.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pityroid</ent><br/
<hw>Pit"y*roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ bran +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form of, or resembling,
bran.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Piu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pi"<ugrave/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>plus</ets>. See <er>Plus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
little more; <as>as, <ex>pi<ugrave/</ex> allegro, a little more
briskly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pivot</ent><br/
<hw>Piv"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.; prob. akin to It.
<ets>piva</ets> pipe, F. <ets>pipe</ets>. See <er>Pipe</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A fixed pin or short axis, on the end of which a wheel
or other body turns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The end of a shaft or arbor which rests and turns in
a support; <as>as, the <ex>pivot</ex> of an arbor in a
watch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively: A turning point or condition;
that on which important results depend; <as>as, the <ex>pivot</ex> of an
enterprise</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>The officer or soldier who simply
turns in his place while the company or line moves around him in
wheeling; -- called also <altname>pivot man</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pivot bridge</b></col>, <cd>a form of drawbridge in which
one span, called the <xex>pivot span</xex>, turns about a central
vertical axis.</cd> -- <col><b>Pivot gun</b></col>, <cd>a gun mounted on
a pivot or revolving carriage, so as to turn in any direction.</cd> --
<col><b>Pivot tooth</b></col> <fld>(Dentistry)</fld>, <cd>an artificial
crown attached to the root of a natural tooth by a pin or
peg.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pivot</ent><br/
<hw>Piv"ot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pivoted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pivoting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To place on a pivot.</def>
<rj><au>Clarke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pivotal</ent><br/
<hw>Piv"ot*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a pivot or turning point; belonging to, or constituting, a pivot; of the
nature of a pivot; <as>as, the <ex>pivotal</ex>opportunity of a career;
the <ex>pivotal</ex> position in a battle.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pix</ent><br/
<hw>Pix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pyx</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pixie</ent><br/
<ent>Pixy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pix"y</hw>, <hw>Pix"ie</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pixies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[For <ets>Pucksy</ets>, from <ets>Puck</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An old English name for a fairy; an elf.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>picksy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A low creeping evergreen plant
(<spn>Pyxidanthera barbulata</spn>), with mosslike leaves and little
white blossoms, found in New Jersey and southward, where it flowers in
earliest spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pixy ring</b></col>, <cd>a fairy ring or circle.</cd>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pixy stool</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a toadstool or mushroom.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pixy-led</ent><br/
<hw>Pix"y-led`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Led by pixies;
bewildered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pizzicato</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Piz`zi*ca"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[It., pinched.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A direction to violinists to pluck the string
with the finger, instead of using the bow. (Abbrev.
<abbr>pizz.</abbr>)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pizzle</ent><br/
<hw>Piz"zle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. G.
<ets>pissel</ets>, <ets>pesel</ets>, <ets>peisel</ets>,
<ets>peserich</ets>, D. <ets>pees</ets> a tendon or spring.]</ety>
<def>The penis; -- so called in some animals, as the bull.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placability</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`ca*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placabilitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>placabilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being placable or appeasable; placable
disposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placable</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"ca*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>placare</ets> to quiet, pacify: cf. F.
<ets>placable</ets>. See <er>Placate</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
appeased or pacified; ready or willing to be pacified; willing to
forgive or condone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Methought I saw him <qex>placable</qex> and mild.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placableness</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"ca*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
placable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placard</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*card"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>plaquer</ets> to lay or clap on, <ets>plaque</ets> plate, tablet;
probably from Dutch, cf. D. <ets>plakken</ets> to paste, post up,
<ets>plak</ets> a flat piece of wood.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A public
proclamation; a manifesto or edict issued by authority.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>placards</qex> or edicts are published in his name.</q>
<rj><qau>Howell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Permission given by authority; a license; <as>as, to
give a <ex>placard</ex> to do something</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>ller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A written or printed paper, as an advertisement or a
declaration, posted, or to be posted, in a public place; a
poster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld> <def>An extra plate on the lower
part of the breastplate or backplate.</def>
<rj><au>Planch<eacute/.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Placket</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of
stomacher, often adorned with jewels, worn in the fifteenth century and
later.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placard</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*card"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Placarded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Placarding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To post placards
upon or within; <as>as, to <ex>placard</ex> a wall, to <ex>placard</ex>
the city</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To announce by placards; <as>as, to <ex>placard</ex>
a sale</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placate</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Placard</er>, 4 & 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placate</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Placated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Placating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>placatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>placare</ets> to placate, akin to
<ets>placere</ets> to please. See <er>Please</er>.]</ety> <def>To
appease; to pacify; to conciliate.</def> <ldquo/Therefore is he always
propitiated and <xex>placated</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placation</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of placating.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Puttenham (1589).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Place</ent><br/
<hw>Place</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>platea</ets> a street, an area, a courtyard, from Gr.
<grk>platei^a</grk> a street, properly fem. of <grk>platy`s</grk>, flat,
broad; akin to Skr. <ets>p<rsdot/thu</ets>, Lith. <ets>platus</ets>. Cf.
<er>Flawn</er>, <er>Piazza</er>, <er>Plate</er>, <er>Plaza</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Any portion of space regarded as measured off or
distinct from all other space, or appropriated to some definite object
or use; position; ground; site; spot; rarely, unbounded space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here is the <qex>place</qex> appointed.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>place</qex> can be for us<br/
Within heaven's bound?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The word <qex>place</qex> has sometimes a more confused sense, and
stands for that space which any body takes up; and so the universe is a
<qex>place</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A broad way in a city; an open space; an area; a
court or short part of a street open only at one end.</def>
<ldquo/Hangman boys in the market <xex>place</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A position which is occupied and held; a dwelling; a
mansion; a village, town, or city; a fortified town or post; a
stronghold; a region or country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Are you native of this <qex>place</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Rank; degree; grade; order of priority, advancement,
dignity, or importance; especially, social rank or position; condition;
also, official station; occupation; calling.</def> <ldquo/The enervating
magic of <xex>place</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men in great <qex>place</qex> are thrice servants.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I know my <qex>place</qex> as I would they should do theirs.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Vacated or relinquished space; room; stead (the
departure or removal of another being or thing being implied).</def>
<ldquo/In <xex>place</xex> of Lord Bassanio.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A definite position or passage of a
document.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>place</qex> of the scripture which he read was this.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts viii. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Ordinal relation; position in the order of
proceeding; <as>as, he said in the first <ex>place</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Reception; effect; -- implying the making room
for.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My word hath no <qex>place</qex> in you.</q> <rj><qau>John viii.
37.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Position in the heavens, as of
a heavenly body; -- usually defined by its right ascension and
declination, or by its latitude and longitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Racing)</fld> <def>The position of first, second,
or third at the finish, esp. the second position. In betting, to win a
bet on a horse for <xex>place</xex> it must, in the United States,
finish first or second, in England, usually, first, second, or
third.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Place of arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a place
calculated for the rendezvous of men in arms, etc., as a fort which
affords a safe retreat for hospitals, magazines, etc.</cd>
<au>Wilhelm.</au> -- <col><b>High place</b></col> <fld>(Script.)</fld>,
<cd>a mount on which sacrifices were offered.</cd> <ldquo/Him that
offereth in the <xex>high place</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Jer. xlviii. 35.</au>
-- <col><b>In place</b></col>, <cd>in proper position; timely.</cd> --
<col><b>Out of place</b></col>, <cd>inappropriate; ill-timed; <as>as,
his remarks were <ex>out of place</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Place
kick</b></col> <fld>(Football)</fld>, <cd>the act of kicking the ball
after it has been placed on the ground.</cd> -- <col><b>Place
name</b></col>, <cd>the name of a place or locality.</cd> <au>London
Academy.</au> -- <col><b>To give place</b></col>, <cd>to make room; to
yield; to give way; to give advantage.</cd> <ldquo/Neither <xex>give
place</xex> to the devil.<rdquo/ <au>Eph. iv. 27.</au> <ldquo/Let all
the rest <xex>give place</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To have
place</b></col>, <cd>to have a station, room, or seat; as, such desires
can <xex>have</xex> no <xex>place</xex> in a good heart.</cd> --
<col><b>To take place</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To come to pass; to
occur; as, the ceremony will not <xex>take place</xex>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To take precedence or priority.</cd> <au>Addison.</au>
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To take effect; to prevail.</cd> <ldquo/If your
doctrine <xex>takes place</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Berkeley.</au> <ldquo/But
none of these excuses would <xex>take place</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Spenser.</au> -- <col><b>To take the place of</b></col>, <cd>to be
substituted for.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Situation; seat; abode; position; locality;
location; site; spot; office; employment; charge; function; trust;
ground; room; stead.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Place</ent><br/
<hw>Place</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Placed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Placing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>placer</ets>. See <er>Place</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To assign a place to; to put in a particular spot or place, or in a
certain relative position; to direct to a particular place; to fix; to
settle; to locate; <as>as, to <ex>place</ex> a book on a shelf; to
<ex>place</ex> balls in tennis.</as></def><br/
<syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Put.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Upon my head they <qex>placed</qex> a fruitless crown.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put or set in a particular rank, office, or
position; to surround with particular circumstances or relations in
life; to appoint to certain station or condition of life; <as>as, in
whatever sphere one is <ex>placed</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Place</qex> such over them to be rulers.</q> <rj><qau>Ex.
xviii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put out at interest; to invest; to loan; <as>as,
to <ex>place</ex> money in a bank</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To set; to fix; to repose; <as>as, to <ex>place</ex>
confidence in a friend</as>.</def> <ldquo/My resolution 's
<xex>placed</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To attribute; to ascribe; to set down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Place</qex> it for her chief virtue.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Racing)</fld> <def>To determine or announce the
place of at the finish. Usually, in horse racing only the first three
horses are <xex>placed</xex> officially.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Rugby Football)</fld> <def>To place-kick ( a
goal).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>to recognize or identify (a person).</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placebo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*ce"bo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., I shall
please, fut. of <ets>placere</ets> to please.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>The first antiphon of the vespers for the
dead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A prescription with no
pharmacological activity given to a patient to humor or satisfy the
desire for medical treatment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>a dose of a compound having no
pharmacological activity given to a subject in a medical experiment as
part of a control experiment in a test of the effectiveness of another,
active pharmacological agent.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sing placebo</b></col>, <cd>to agree with one in his
opinion; to be complaisant to.</cd> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placebo effect</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*ce"bo ef*fect`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>a reaction by a patient who receives a
placebo{2}, in which the symptoms of illness are lessened or an
anticipated effect is experienced. Because the placebo{2} itself has no
pharmacological activity, this reaction is mediated by the expectations
of the patient receiving the placebo{2}; the reaction is considered as
an example of the power of suggestion.</def> <note>Dramatic subjective
effects such as relief of discomfort or pain are sometimes observed due
to administration of a placebo, but in some cases measurable
physiological effects may also be observed.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placeful</ent><br/
<hw>Place"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>In the appointed
place.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Place-kick</ent><br/
<hw>Place"-kick`</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To make a place kick;
to make (a goal) by a place kick.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Place"-kick`er</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placeless</ent><br/
<hw>Place"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no place or
office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placeman</ent><br/
<hw>Place"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Placemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who holds or occupies
a place; one who has office under government.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placement</ent><br/

<hw>Place"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>placement</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of placing, or the
state of being placed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Position; place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placenta</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cen"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Placent<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Placentas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a cake, Gr. <?/ a flat cake, from <?/
flat, fr. <?/, <?/, anything flat and broad.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The vascular appendage which connects the fetus
with the parent, and is cast off in parturition with the
afterbirth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In most mammals the placenta is principally developed
from the allantois and chorion, and tufts of vascular villi on its
surface penetrate the blood vessels of the parental uterus, and thus
establish a nutritive and excretory connection between the blood of the
fetus and that of the parent, though the blood itself does not flow from
one to the other.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The part of a pistil or fruit to
which the ovules or seeds are attached.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placental</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cen"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the placenta; having, or characterized by having, a
placenta; <as>as, a <ex>placental</ex> mammal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Placentalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placental</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cen"tal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
Placentalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1093 --></p>

<p><ent>Placentalia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plac`en*ta"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Mammalia
including those that have a placenta, or all the orders above the
marsupials.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placentary</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cen"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having reference
to the placenta; <as>as, the <ex>placentary</ex> system of
classification</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placentation</ent><br/
<hw>Plac`en*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The mode of formation of the placenta in
different animals; <as>as, the <ex>placentation</ex> of
mammals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The mode in which the placenta is
arranged or composed; <as>as, axile <ex>placentation</ex>; parietal
<ex>placentation</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placentiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Plac`en*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Placenta</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot. &
Zool.)</fld> <def>Having or producing a placenta.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placentiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cen"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Placenta</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the shape of a placenta, or circular thickened disk somewhat
thinner about the middle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placentious</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cen"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Please</er>.]</ety> <def>Pleasing; amiable.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>placentious</xex> person.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Place-proud</ent><br/
<hw>Place"-proud`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Proud of rank or
office.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placer</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who places or
sets.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placer</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety> <def>A
deposit of earth, sand, or gravel, containing valuable mineral in
particles, especially by the side of a river, or in the bed of a
mountain torrent.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placet</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla"cet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placet</ets> it pleases.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A vote of assent,
as of the governing body of a university, of an ecclesiastical council,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The assent of the civil power to the promulgation of
an ecclesiastical ordinance.</def> <rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king . . . annulled the royal <qex>placet</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>J. P. Peters.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placid</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placidus</ets>, originally, pleasing, mild, from <ets>placere</ets>
to please: cf. F. <ets>placide</ets>. See <er>Please</er>.]</ety>
<def>Pleased; contented; unruffled; undisturbed; serene; peaceful;
tranquil; quiet; gentle.</def> <ldquo/That <xex>placid</xex> aspect and
meek regard.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> <ldquo/Sleeping . . . the
<xex>placid</xex> sleep of infancy.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placidity</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*cid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placiditas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>placidit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being placid; calmness; serenity.</def>
<rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placidly</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"id*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a placid
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placidness</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"id*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
placid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placit</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>placitum</ets>. See <er>Plea</er>.]</ety> <def>A decree or
determination; a dictum.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>placits</xex> and opinions of other philosophers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placitory</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"i*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Placit</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to pleas or pleading, in
courts of law.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Clayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placitum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plac"i*tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Placita</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL. See
<er>Placit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A public court or assembly in
the Middle Ages, over which the sovereign president when a consultation
was held upon affairs of state.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Old Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A court, or cause in
court.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A plea; a pleading; a judicial
proceeding; a suit.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plack</ent><br/
<hw>Plack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>plaque</ets> a
plate of metal. Cf. <er>Plaque</er>.]</ety> <def>A small copper coin
formerly current in Scotland, worth less than a cent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With not a <qex>plack</qex> in the pocket of the poet.</q>
<rj><qau>Prof. Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placket</ent><br/
<hw>Plack"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaquer</ets> to lay or clap on. See <er>Placard</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A petticoat, esp. an under petticoat; hence, a cant
term for a woman.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The opening or slit left in a petticoat or skirt for
convenience in putting it on; -- called also <altname>placket
hole</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A woman's pocket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placoderm</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"o*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`x</grk>, <grk>plako`s</grk>, tablet + <grk>de`rma</grk>
skin.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>One of the Placodermi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placodermal</ent><br/
<hw>Plac`o*der"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the placoderms; like the placoderms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placodermata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plac`o*der"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Placodermi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placodermi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plac`o*der"mi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>pla`x</grk>, <grk>plako`s</grk>, a tablet +
<grk>de`rma</grk> skin.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct
group of fishes, supposed to be ganoids. The body and head were covered
with large bony plates. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Pterichthys</er>, and <er>Coccosteus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placoganoid</ent><br/
<hw>Plac`o*ga"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the Placoganoidei.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placoganoidei</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plac`o*ga*noi"de*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pla`x</grk>, <grk>plako`s</grk>, a tablet + NL.
<ets>ganoidei</ets>. See <er>Ganoidei</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of ganoid fishes including those that have large
external bony plates and a cartilaginous skeleton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placoid</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`x</grk>, <grk>plako`s</grk>, a tablet + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Platelike; having irregular, platelike, bony
scales, often bearing spines; pertaining to the placoids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placoid</ent><br/
<hw>Plac"oid</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any fish having placoid scales, as the sharks.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>One of the Placoides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placoides</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*coi"des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of fishes including the
sharks and rays; the Elasmobranchii; -- called also
<altname>Placoidei</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>placoidian</ent><br/
<hw>pla*coid"i*an</hw> <pr>(pl<adot/*koid"<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the placoids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Placophora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*coph"o*ra</hw> <pr>(pl<adot/*k<ocr/f"<osl/*r<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>pla`x</grk>,
<grk>plako`s</grk>, tablet + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of gastropod Mollusca, including the
chitons. The back is covered by eight shelly plates. Called also
<altname><subclass>Polyplacophora</subclass></altname>. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Chiton</er>, and
<er>Isopleura</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla"ga</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"g<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Plag<ae/</plw> <pr>(pl<amac/"j<esl/)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L. <ets>pl<amac/ga</ets> a blow, a welt, a stripe.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A stripe of color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagal</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gal</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"g<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.,
from Gr. <grk>pla`gios</grk> sidewise, slanting.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Having a scale running from the dominant to its
octave; -- said of certain old church modes or tunes, as opposed to
those called <contr>authentic</contr>, which ran from the tonic to its
octave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plagal cadence</b></col>, <cd>a cadence in which the
final chord on the tonic is preceded by the chord on the
subdominant.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagate</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having plag<ae/, or irregular enlongated color spots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plage</ent><br/
<hw>Plage</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>plaga</ets>.]</ety> <def>A region; country.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>plages</xex> of the north.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>plagiarise</ent><br/
<hw>pla"gia*rise</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>plagiarize</er>.</def> <mark>[mostly Brit.]</mark> <br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> plagiarize, lift.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiarism</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gia*rism</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"j<adot/*r<icr/z'm <it>or</it>
pl<amac/"j<icr/*<adot/*r<icr/z'm; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plagiarisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or practice of
plagiarizing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is plagiarized; a work which has been
plagiarized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiarist</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gia*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
plagiarizes, or purloins the words, writings, or ideas of another, and
passes them off as his own; a literary thief; a plagiary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiarize</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gia*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Plagiarized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Plagiarizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To steal or purloin
from the writings of another; to appropriate without due acknowledgment
(the ideas or expressions of another).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gia*ry</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To commit
plagiarism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gia*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Plagiaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>plagiarius</ets>
a kidnaper, a literary thief, fr. <ets>plagium</ets> kidnaping; cf.
<ets>plaga</ets> a net, perh. akin to E. <ets>plait</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>plagiaire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A manstealer; a
kidnapper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who purloins another's expressions or ideas, and
offers them as his own; a plagiarist.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Plagiarism; literary theft.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gia*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Kidnapping.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>E. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Practicing plagiarism.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagihedral</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`gi*he"dral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`gios</grk> oblique + <?/ base, seat.]</ety>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Having an oblique spiral arrangement of
planes, as levogyrate and dextrogyrate crystals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiocephalic</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`gi*o*ce*phal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`gios</grk> oblique + <?/ the head.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Having an oblique lateral deformity of the skull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiocephaly</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`gi*o*ceph"a*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Oblique lateral deformity of the
skull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagioclase</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gi*o*clase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`gios</grk> oblique + <grk>kla^n</grk> to break.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A general term used of any triclinic feldspar.
See the Note under <er>Feldspar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagionite</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gi*o*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`gios</grk> oblique. So called in allusion to its usually
oblique crystallization.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A sulphide of
lead and antimony, of a blackish lead-gray color and metallic
luster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiostomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`gi*o*stom"a*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Plagiostomous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiostome</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gi*o*stome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Plagiostomi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiostomi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla`gi*os"to*mi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>pla`gios</grk> slanting + <?/, <?/, mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of fishes including the sharks and
rays; -- called also
<altname><ord>Plagiostomata</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiostomous</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`gi*os"to*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Plagiostomi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiotremata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla`gi*o*trem"a*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>;
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pla`gios</grk> slanting + <?/, <?/, a
hole.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Lepidosauria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagiotropic</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`gi*o*trop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pla`gios</grk> aslant + <?/ to turn.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the longer axis inclined away from the vertical
line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla"gi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>Manstealing; kidnapping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagose</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*gose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plagosus</ets>. See <er>Plague</er>.]</ety> <def>Fond of flogging;
<as>as, a <ex>plagose</ex> master</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plague</ent><br/
<hw>Plague</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plaga</ets> a
blow, stroke, plague; akin to Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to strike; cf. L.
<ets>plangere</ets> to strike, beat. Cf. <er>Plaint</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That which smites, wounds, or troubles; a blow; a
calamity; any afflictive evil or torment; a great trail or
vexation.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And men blasphemed God for the <qex>plague</qex> of hail.</q>
<rj><qau>Wyclif.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The different <qex>plague</qex> of each calamity.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An acute malignant contagious
fever, that often prevails in Egypt, Syria, and Turkey, and has at times
visited the large cities of Europe with frightful mortality; hence, any
pestilence; <as>as, the great London <ex>plague</ex></as>.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>plague</xex> upon the people fell.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cattle plague</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Rinderpest</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Plague mark</b></col>,
<col><b>Plague spot</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a spot or mark of the plague;
hence, a token of something incurable.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plague</ent><br/
<hw>Plague</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plagued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plaguing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To infest or afflict
with disease, calamity, or natural evil of any kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus were they <qex>plagued</qex><br/
And worn with famine.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To vex; to tease; to harass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She will <qex>plague</qex> the man that loves her most.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To vex; torment; distress; afflict; harass;
annoy; tease; tantalize; trouble; molest; embarrass; perplex.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagueful</ent><br/
<hw>Plague"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding, or
infecting, with plagues; pestilential; <as>as, <ex>plagueful</ex>
exhalations</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plagueless</ent><br/
<hw>Plague"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from plagues or the
plague.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaguer</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"guer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plagues or
annoys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaguily</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"gui*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plaguing
manner; vexatiously; extremely.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<ldquo/Ronsard is so <xex>plaguily</xex> stiff and stately.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Landor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaguy</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"guy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Vexatious;
troublesome; tormenting; <as>as, a <ex>plaguy</ex> horse</as>.
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> Also used adverbially; <as>as, <ldquo/He is so
<ex>plaguy</ex> proud</as>.<rdquo/</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaice</ent><br/
<hw>Plaice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>plaise</ets>,
<ets>plais</ets>, prob. fr. L. <ets>platessa</ets> flatish, plaice. See
<er>Place</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A European
food fish (<spn>Pleuronectes platessa</spn>), allied to the flounder,
and growing to the weight of eight or ten pounds or more.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A large American flounder (<spn>Paralichthys
dentatus</spn>; called also <altname>brail</altname>,
<altname>puckermouth</altname>, and <altname>summer flounder</altname>.
The name is sometimes applied to other allied species.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>plaise</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plaice mouth</b></col>, <cd>a mouth like that of a
plaice; a small or wry mouth.</cd> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaid</ent><br/
<hw>Plaid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael. <ets>plaide</ets>
a blanket or plaid, contr. fr. <ets>peallaid</ets> a sheepskin, fr.
<ets>peall</ets> a skin or hide. CF. <er>Pillion</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A rectangular garment or piece of cloth, usually made
of the checkered material called tartan, but sometimes of plain gray, or
gray with black stripes. It is worn by both sexes in Scotland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Goods of any quality or material of the pattern of a
plaid or tartan; a checkered cloth or pattern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaid</ent><br/
<hw>Plaid</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a pattern or colors which
resemble a Scotch plaid; checkered or marked with bars or stripes at
right angles to one another; <as>as, <ex>plaid</ex>
muslin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaided</ent><br/
<hw>Plaid"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the material of
which plaids are made; tartan.</def> <ldquo/In <xex>plaided</xex>
vest.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wearing a plaid.</def>
<rj><au>Campbell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaiding</ent><br/
<hw>Plaid"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Plaid
cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain</ent><br/
<hw>Plain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>playne</ets>, <ets>pleyne</ets>, fr. F. <ets>plaindre</ets>. See
<er>Plaint</er>.]</ety> <def>To lament; to bewail; to complain.</def>
<mark>[Archaic & Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We with piteous heart unto you <qex>pleyne</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain</ent><br/
<hw>Plain</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lament; to mourn over; <as>as,
to <ex>plain</ex> a loss</as>.</def> <mark>[Archaic & Poetic]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir J. Harrington.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain</ent><br/
<hw>Plain</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Plainer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Plainest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[F., level, flat, fr. L.
<ets>planus</ets>, perhaps akin to E. <ets>floor</ets>. Cf.
<er>Llano</er>, <er>Piano</er>, <er>Plan</er>, <er>Plane</er> level, a
level surface.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Without elevations or
depressions; flat; level; smooth; even. See <er>Plane</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The crooked shall be made straight, and the rough places
<qex>plain</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. xl. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Open; clear; unencumbered; equal; fair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our troops beat an army in <qex>plain</qex> fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Felton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not intricate or difficult; evident; manifest;
obvious; clear; unmistakable.</def> <ldquo/'T is a <xex>plain</xex>
case.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Void of extraneous beauty or ornament;
without conspicuous embellishment; not rich; simple.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Not highly cultivated; unsophisticated; free from show or
pretension; simple; natural; homely; common.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Plain</xex> yet pious Christians.<rdquo/ <au>Hammond.</au>
<ldquo/The <xex>plain</xex> people.<rdquo/ <au>A. Lincoln.</au>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>Free from affectation or disguise; candid; sincere;
artless; honest; frank.</def> <ldquo/An honest mind, and
<xex>plain</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>Not luxurious;
not highly seasoned; simple; <as>as, <ex>plain</ex> food</as>.</def>
<sd>(e)</sd> <def>Without beauty; not handsome; homely; <as>as, a
<ex>plain</ex> woman</as>.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>Not variegated, dyed,
or figured; <as>as, <ex>plain</ex> muslin</as>.</def> <sd>(g)</sd>
<def>Not much varied by modulations; <as>as, a <ex>plain</ex>
tune</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plain battle</b></col>, <cd>open battle; pitched
battle.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>Plain
chant</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Plain song</cref>,
below.</cd> -- <col><b>Plain chart</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a
chart laid down on Mercator's projection.</cd> -- <col><b>Plain
dealer</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>One who practices plain dealing</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A simpleton</cd>. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> --
<col><b>Plain dealing</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Dealing</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Plain molding</b></col> <fld>(Join.)</fld>, <cd>molding of which
the surfaces are plain figures.</cd> -- <col><b>Plain sewing</b></col>,
<cd>sewing of seams by simple and common stitches, in distinct from
fancy work, embroidery, etc.; -- distinguished also from designing and
fitting garments.</cd> -- <col><b>Plain song</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The Gregorian chant, or <altname>canto fermo</altname>; the
prescribed melody of the Roman Catholic service, sung in unison, in
tones of equal length, and rarely extending beyond the compass of an
octave</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A simple melody.</cd> -- <col><b>Plain
speaking</b></col>, <cd>plainness or bluntness of speech.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Level; flat; smooth; open; artless; unaffected;
undisguised; frank; sincere; honest; candid; ingenuous; unembellished;
downright; blunt; clear; simple; distinct; manifest; obvious; apparent.
See <er>Manifest</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain</ent><br/
<hw>Plain</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plain manner; plainly.</def>
<ldquo/To speak short and <xex>pleyn</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au>
<ldquo/To tell you <xex>plain</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain</ent><br/
<hw>Plain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>plaigne</ets>, F.
<ets>plaine</ets>. See <er>Plain</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Level land; usually, an open field or a broad stretch of land with
an even surface, or a surface little varied by inequalities; <as>as, the
<ex>plain</ex> of Jordan; the American <ex>plains</ex>, or
prairies.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Descending fro the mountain into <qex>playn</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him the Ammonite<br/
Worshiped in Rabba and her watery <qex>plain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A field of battle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lead forth my soldiers to the <qex>plain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain</ent><br/
<hw>Plain</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plained</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plaining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. <er>Plane</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To plane or level; to make plain
or even on the surface.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We would rake Europe rather, <qex>plain</qex> the East.</q>
<rj><qau>Wither.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make plain or manifest; to explain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What's dumb in show, I'll <qex>plain</qex> in speech.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plainant</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 1st
<er>Plain</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who makes complaint;
the plaintiff.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain-dealing</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"-deal`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Practicing
plain dealing; artless. See <cref>Plain dealing</cref>, under
<er>Dealing</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Frank;
sincere; artless.</def> <au>Milton.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Plain"-heart`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaining</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Complaint.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaining</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Complaining.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Bryant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain-laid</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"-laid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>Consisting of strands twisted together in the ordinary way; <as>as,
a <ex>plain-laid</ex> rope</as>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Cordage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plainly</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plain manner;
clearly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plainness</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
plain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plainsman</ent><br/
<hw>Plains"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-men</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who lives in the
plains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plain-spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"-spo`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speaking with
plain, unreserved sincerity; also, spoken sincerely; <as>as,
<ex>plain-spoken</ex> words</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>plains wanderer</ent><br/
<hw>plains wanderer</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bird of the genus
<gen>Pedionomus</gen> (<spn>Pedionomus torquatus</spn>) of New South
Wales, the only species in the genus. <gen>Pedionomus</gen> is the only
genus of the family <fam>Pedionomidae</fam> of the order
<ord>Passeriformes</ord> and the plains wanderer is thus the only
species in the family. The total world population (ca. 2000) is
estimated to be 5 to 10 thousand.</def> <note>See also
http://www.philipmaher.com/plains.htm.</note><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> plain wanderer.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaint</ent><br/
<hw>Plaint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>plainte</ets>, <ets>pleynte</ets>, F. <ets>plainte</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>plangere</ets>, <ets>planctum</ets> (<ets>plancta</ets>, fem. p.
p.), to beat, beat the breast, lament. Cf. <er>Complain</er>,
<er>Plague</er>, <er>Plangent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Audible
expression of sorrow; lamentation; complaint; hence, a mournful song; a
lament.</def> <au>Chaucer.</au><ldquo/The Psalmist's mournful
<xex>plaint</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Wordsworth.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An accusation or protest on account of an
injury.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are three just grounds of war with Spain: one of
<qex>plaint</qex>, two upon defense.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A private memorial tendered to a
court, in which a person sets forth his cause of action; the exhibiting
of an action in writing.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1094 --></p>

<p><ent>Plaintful</ent><br/
<hw>Plaint"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing a
plaint; complaining; expressing sorrow with an audible voice.</def>
<ldquo/My <xex>plaintful</xex> tongue.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaintiff</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"tiff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaintif</ets> making complaint, plaintive; in Old French equiv. to
<ets>plaignant</ets> complainant, prosecutor, fr. <ets>plaindre</ets>.
See <er>Plaint</er>, and cf. <er>Plaintive</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>One who commences a personal action or suit to obtain a remedy for
an injury to his rights; -- opposed to <ant>defendant</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaintiff</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"tiff</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Plaintive</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaintive</ent><br/
<hw>Plain"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaintif</ets>. See <er>Plaintiff</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Repining; complaining; lamenting.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressive of sorrow or melancholy; mournful;
sad.</def> <ldquo/The most <xex>plaintive</xex> ditty.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Landor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Plain"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Plain"tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaintless</ent><br/
<hw>Plaint"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without complaint;
unrepining.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plaintless</xex> patience.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Savage.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaisance</ent><br/
<hw>Plai`sance"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Pleasance</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaise</ent><br/
<hw>Plaise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Plaice</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaister</ent><br/
<hw>Plais"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<def>See <er>Plaster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plait</ent><br/
<hw>Plait</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>playte</ets>,
OF. <ets>pleit</ets>, L. <ets>plicatum</ets>, <ets>plicitum</ets>, p. p.
of <ets>plicare</ets> to fold, akin to <ets>plectere</ets> to plait. See
<er>Ply</er>, and cf. <er>Plat</er> to weave, <er>Pleat</er>,
<er>Plight</er> fold.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A flat fold; a doubling,
as of cloth; a pleat; <as>as, a box <ex>plait</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>plaits</qex> and foldings of the drapery.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A braid, as of hair or straw; a plat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polish plait</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Plica</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plait</ent><br/
<hw>Plait</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plaited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plaiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fold; to double
in narrow folds; to pleat; <as>as, to <ex>plait</ex> a
ruffle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To interweave the strands or locks of; to braid; to
plat; <as>as, to <ex>plait</ex> hair; to <ex>plait</ex>
rope.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaited</ent><br/
<hw>Plait"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Folded; doubled over; braided;
figuratively, involved; intricate; artful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Time shall unfold what <qex>plaited</qex> cunning hides.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaiter</ent><br/
<hw>Plait"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, plaits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plan</ent><br/
<hw>Plan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>planus</ets> flat, level. See <er>Plain</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A draught or form; properly, a representation drawn on
a plane, as a map or a chart; especially, a top view, as of a machine,
or the representation or delineation of a horizontal section of
anything, as of a building; a graphic representation; a
diagram.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A scheme devised; a method of action or procedure
expressed or described in language; a project; <as>as, the <ex>plan</ex>
of a constitution; the <ex>plan</ex> of an expedition.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God's <qex>plans</qex> like lines pure and white unfold.</q>
<rj><qau>M. R. Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A method; a way of procedure; a custom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The simple <qex>plan</qex>,<br/
That they should take who have the power,<br/
And they should keep who can.</q> <rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Body plan</b></col>, <col><b>Floor
plan</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Body</er>, <er>Floor</er>,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Scheme; draught; delineation; plot; sketch;
project; design; contrivance; device. See <er>Scheme</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plan</ent><br/
<hw>Plan</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Planned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Planning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form a
delineation of; to draught; to represent, as by a diagram.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scheme; to devise; to contrive; to form in
design; <as>as, to <ex>plan</ex> the conquest of a
country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even in penance, <qex>planning</qex> sins anew.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*na"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
L. <plw>Planari<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>-rias</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Planary</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of turbellarian worms belonging to
<gen>Planaria</gen>, and many allied genera. The body is usually flat,
thin, and smooth. Some species, in warm countries, are
terrestrial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Planarida, or Dendroc<oe/la; any turbellarian
worm.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pla*na"ri*an</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planarida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*nar"i*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Turbellaria; the
Dendroc<oe/la.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planarioid</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*na"ri*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Planaria</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like the planarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planary</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>planarius</ets> level. See <er>Plane</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a plane.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planch</ent><br/
<hw>Planch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>planche</ets>.]</ety> <def>A plank.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ld. Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planch</ent><br/
<hw>Planch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Planched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Planching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>planche</ets> a board,
plank. See <er>Plank</er>.]</ety> <def>To make or cover with planks or
boards; to plank.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To that vineyard is a
<xex>planched</xex> gate.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plancher</ent><br/
<hw>Planch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
<ets>planche</ets>. See <er>Planch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A floor
of wood; also, a plank.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The under side of a cornice; a
soffit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plancher</ent><br/
<hw>Planch"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To form of planks.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Golding.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planchet</ent><br/
<hw>Planch"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>planchette</ets> a small board, dim. of <ets>planche</ets>. See
<er>Planch</er>.]</ety> <def>A flat piece of metal; especially, a disk
of metal ready to be stamped as a coin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planchette</ent><br/
<hw>Plan`chette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Planchet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A circumferentor. See
<er>Circumferentor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small tablet of wood supported on casters and
having a pencil attached. The characters produced by the pencil on
paper, while the hand rests on the instrument and it is allowed to move,
are sometimes translated as of oracular or supernatural
import.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planching</ent><br/
<hw>Planch"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The laying of
floors in a building; also, a floor of boards or planks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane</ent><br/
<hw>Plane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>platanus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ broad; -- so called on account of
its broad leaves and spreading form. See <er>Place</er>, and cf.
<er>Platane</er>, <er>Plantain</er> the tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any tree of the genus <gen>Platanus</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The Oriental plane (<stype><spn>Platanus
orientalis</spn></stype>) is a native of Asia. It rises with a straight,
smooth, branching stem to a great height, with palmated leaves, and long
pendulous peduncles, sustaining several heads of small close-sitting
flowers. The seeds are downy, and collected into round, rough, hard
balls. The Occidental plane (<stype><spn>Platanus
occidentalis</spn></stype>), which grows to a great height, is a native
of North America, where it is popularly called
<altname>sycamore</altname>, <altname>buttonwood</altname>, and
<altname>buttonball</altname>, names also applied to the California
species (<spn>Platanus racemosa</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane</ent><br/
<hw>Plane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>planus</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>plan</ets>. See <er>Plan</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Without elevations or depressions; even; level; flat; lying in, or
constituting, a plane; <as>as, a <ex>plane</ex> surface</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In science, this word (instead of <xex>plain</xex>) is
almost exclusively used to designate a flat or level surface.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plane angle</b></col>, <cd>the angle included between two
straight lines in a plane.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Plane chart</b></col>,
<col><b>Plane curve</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Chart</er> and
<er>Curve</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane figure</b></col>, <cd>a figure all
points of which lie in the same plane. If bounded by straight lines it
is a <xex>rectilinear plane figure</xex>, if by curved lines it is a
<xex>curvilinear plane figure</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane
geometry</b></col>, <cd>that part of geometry which treats of the
relations and properties of plane figures.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane
problem</b></col>, <cd>a problem which can be solved geometrically by
the aid of the right line and circle only.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane
sailing</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the method of computing a
ship's place and course on the supposition that the earth's surface is a
plane.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane scale</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a
scale for the use of navigators, on which are graduated chords, sines,
tangents, secants, rhumbs, geographical miles, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Plane surveying</b></col>, <cd>surveying in which the curvature
of the earth is disregarded; ordinary field and topographical surveying
of tracts of moderate extent.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane table</b></col>,
<cd>an instrument used for plotting the lines of a survey on paper in
the field.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane trigonometry</b></col>, <cd>the branch
of trigonometry in which its principles are applied to plane
triangles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane</ent><br/
<hw>Plane</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>plane</ets>, L.
<ets>plana</ets>. See <er>Plane</er>, <pos>v.</pos> &
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A surface, real
or imaginary, in which, if any two points are taken, the straight line
which joins them lies wholly in that surface; or a surface, any section
of which by a like surface is a straight line; a surface without
curvature.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>An ideal surface, conceived as
coinciding with, or containing, some designated astronomical line,
circle, or other curve; <as>as, the <ex>plane</ex> of an orbit; the
<ex>plane</ex> of the ecliptic, or of the equator.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A block or plate having a
perfectly flat surface, used as a standard of flatness; a surface
plate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>A tool for smoothing boards or
other surfaces of wood, for forming moldings, etc. It consists of a
smooth-soled stock, usually of wood, from the under side or face of
which projects slightly the steel cutting edge of a chisel, called the
<xex>iron</xex>, which inclines backward, with an aperture in front for
the escape of shavings; <as>as, the jack <ex>plane</ex>; the smoothing
<ex>plane</ex>; the molding <ex>plane</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Objective plane</b></col> <fld>(Surv.)</fld>, <cd>the
horizontal plane upon which the object which is to be delineated, or
whose place is to be determined, is supposed to stand.</cd> --
<col><b>Perspective plane</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Perspective</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Plane at infinity</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a plane
in which points infinitely distant are conceived as situated.</cd> --
<col><b>Plane iron</b></col>, <cd>the cutting chisel of a joiner's
plane.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane of polarization</b></col>.
<fld>(Opt.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Polarization</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane
of projection</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The plane on which the
projection is made, corresponding to the <xex>perspective plane</xex> in
perspective; -- called also <xex>principal plane</xex>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Descriptive Geom.)</fld> <cd>One of the planes to
which points are referred for the purpose of determining their relative
position in space.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Plane of refraction</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Plane of reflection</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>the plane in which lie both the incident ray and
the refracted or reflected ray.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane</ent><br/
<hw>Plane</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Planed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Planing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>planer</ets>, L.
<ets>planare</ets>, fr. <ets>planus</ets>. See <er>Plane</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, <er>Plain</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Planish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make smooth; to level; to
pare off the inequalities of the surface of, as of a board or other
piece of wood, by the use of a plane; <as>as, to <ex>plane</ex> a
plank</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To efface or remove.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>planed</qex> away the names . . . written on his
tables.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Figuratively, to make plain or smooth.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What student came but that you <qex>planed</qex> her path.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane</ent><br/
<hw>Plane</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>Of a boat, to lift more or less
out of the water while in motion, after the manner of a hydroplane; to
hydroplane.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane-parallel</ent><br/
<hw>Plane`-par"al*lel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Optics)</fld> <def>Having opposite surfaces exactly plane and
parallel, as a piece of glass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planer</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, planes; a planing machine; esp., a machine for planing
wood or metals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A wooden block used for forcing
down the type in a form, and making the surface even.</def>
<rj><au>Hansard.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Planer centers</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Center</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planer tree</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"er tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From <person>J. S.
<etsep>Planer</etsep></person>, a German botanist.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A small-leaved North American tree (<spn>Planera
aquatica</spn>) related to the elm, but having a wingless, nutlike
fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planet</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>planete</ets>, F. <ets>plan<egrave/te</ets>, L. <ets>planeta</ets>,
fr. Gr. <?/, and <?/ a planet; prop. wandering, fr. <?/ to wander, fr.
<?/ a wandering.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A
celestial body which revolves about the sun in an orbit of a moderate
degree of eccentricity. It is distinguished from a <xex>comet</xex> by
the absence of a coma, and by having a less eccentric orbit. See
<er>Solar system</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>planet</xex> was first used to distinguish
those stars which have an apparent motion through the constellations
from the <xex>fixed</xex> stars, which retain their relative places
unchanged. The <xex>inferior planets</xex> are Mercury and Venus, which
are nearer to the sun than is the earth; the <xex>superior planets</xex>
are Mars, the asteroids, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune, which are
farther from the sun than is the earth. <xex>Primary planets</xex> are
those which revolve about the sun; <xex>secondary planets</xex>, or
<xex>moons</xex>, are those which revolve around the primary planets as
satellites, and at the same time revolve with them about the
sun.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A star, as influencing the fate of a men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There's some ill <qex>planet</qex> reigns.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Planet gear</b></col>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Epicyclic train</cref>, under <er>Epicyclic</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Planet wheel</b></col>, <cd>a gear wheel which revolves around
the wheel with which it meshes, in an epicyclic train.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane table</ent><br/
<hw>Plane" ta`ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See under <er>Plane</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planetarium</ent><br/
<hw>Plan`e*ta"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.: cf. F.
<ets>plan<eacute/taire</ets>. See <er>Planetary</er>.]</ety> <def>An
orrery. See <er>Orrery</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ Since the 1930's, orrery has retained its original
meaning, but planetarium now refers primarily to a room with a
dome-shaped roof, in which images of the night sky are projected from a
central projector. The projector itself is also called a planetarium.
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><ent>Planetary</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"et*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>planetarius</ets> an astrologer, F. <ets>plan<eacute/taire</ets>
planetary. See <er>Planet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining
to the planets; <as>as, <ex>planetary</ex> inhabitants;
<ex>planetary</ex> motions; <ex>planetary</ex> year.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of planets; <as>as, a <ex>planetary</ex>
system</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>Under the dominion or influence
of a planet.</def> <ldquo/Skilled in the <xex>planetary</xex>
hours.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Caused by planets.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>planetary</xex> plague.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Having the nature of a planet; erratic; revolving;
wandering.</def> <ldquo/Erratical and <xex>planetary</xex> life.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Planetary days</b></col>, <cd>the days of the week as
shared among the planets known to the ancients, each having its
day.</cd> <au>Hutton.</au> -- <col><b>Planetary nebula</b></col>, <cd>a
nebula exhibiting a uniform disk, like that of a planet.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planeted</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"et*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to planets.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planetical</ent><br/
<ent>Planetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pla*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pla*net"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>planeticus</ets>, Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to planets.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planetoid</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"et*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Planet</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A body resembling a
planet; an asteroid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planetoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"et*oid*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a
planetoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plane tree</ent><br/
<hw>Plane" tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as 1st
<er>Plane</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planet-struck</ent><br/
<ent>Planet-stricken</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plan"et-strick`en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Plan"et-struck`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected by the influence of
planets; blasted.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like <qex>planet-stricken</qex> men of yore<br/
He trembles, smitten to the core<br/
By strong compunction and remorse.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planetule</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"et*ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little
planet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Conybeare.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plangency</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"gen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being plangent; a beating sound.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plangent</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plangens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, fr. <ets>plangere</ets> to beat.
See <er>Plaint</er>.]</ety> <def>Beating; dashing, as a wave.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>plangent</xex> wave.<rdquo/ <rj><au>H.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-</ent><br/
<ent>Plani-</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plan"i-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Plan"o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>. <ety>[L. <ets>planus</ets>. See <er>Plane</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Combining forms signifying <xex>flat</xex>,
<xex>level</xex>, <xex>plane</xex>; as <xex>plani</xex>folious,
<xex>plani</xex>metry, <xex>plano</xex>-concave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planifolious</ent><br/
<hw>Plan`i*fo"li*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plani-</ets> + L. <ets>folium</ets> leaf.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Flat-leaved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planiform</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Having a plane surface; <as>as, a <ex>planiform</ex>, gliding, or
arthrodial articulation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*nim"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plani-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>. Cf.
<er>Planometer</er>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for measuring the area of
any plane figure, however irregular, by passing a tracer around the
bounding line; a platometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planimetrical</ent><br/
<ent>Planimetric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plan`i*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>,
<hw>Plan`i*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>planim<eacute/trique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
planimetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planimetry</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*nim"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>planim<eacute/trie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The mensuration of plane
surfaces; -- distinguished from <xex>stereometry</xex>, or the
mensuration of volumes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planing</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & vb. n.</pos> fr.
<er>Plane</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Planing machine</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>See
<er>Planer</er>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A complex machine for planing
wood, especially boards, containing usually a rapidly revolving cutter,
which chips off the surface in small shavings as the piece to be planed
is passed under it by feeding apparatus.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planipennate</ent><br/
<hw>Pla`ni*pen"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Planipennia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planipennia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla`ni*pen"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>planus</ets> plane + <ets>penna</ets> wing.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of Neuroptera, including those that
have broad, flat wings, as the ant-lion, lacewing, etc. Called also
<altname>Planipennes</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planipetalous</ent><br/
<hw>Plan`i*pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plani-</ets> + <ets>petal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having flat petals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planish</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Planished</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Planishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>planir</ets>, F. <ets>planer</ets>. See <er>Plane</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>, and <er>-ish</er>.]</ety> <def>To make smooth or plane,
as a metallic surface; to condense, toughen, and polish by light blows
with a hammer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planisher</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ish*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, planishes.</def> <rj><au>Weale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planishing</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ish*ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & vb. n.</pos> from
<er>Planish</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Planishing rolls</b></col> <fld>(Coining)</fld>,
<cd>rolls between which metal strips are passed while cold, to bring
them to exactly the required thickness.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planisphere</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"i*sphere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plani-</ets> + <ets>sphere</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>planisph<egrave/re</ets>. See <er>Plain</er>, and
<er>Sphere</er>.]</ety> <def>The representation of the circles of the
sphere upon a plane; especially, a representation of the celestial
sphere upon a plane with adjustable circles, or other appendages, for
showing the position of the heavens, the time of rising and setting of
stars, etc., for any given date or hour.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planispheric</ent><br/
<hw>Plan`i*spher"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a planisphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plank</ent><br/
<hw>Plank</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>planke</ets>,
OF. <ets>planque</ets>, <ets>planche</ets>, F. <ets>planche</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>planca</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/, <?/, anything flat and broad. Cf.
<er>Planch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A broad piece of sawed timber,
differing from a board only in being thicker. See
<er>Board</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: That which supports or upholds, as a board
does a swimmer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His charity is a better <qex>plank</qex> than the faith of an
intolerant and bitter-minded bigot.</q> <rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of the separate articles in a declaration of the
principles of a party or cause; <as>as, a <ex>plank</ex> in the national
platform</as>.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Plank road</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Plank
way</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a road surface formed of planks.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>To walk the plank</b></col>, <cd>to walk
along a plank laid across the bulwark of a ship, until one overbalances
it and falls into the sea; -- a method of disposing of captives
practiced by pirates.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1095 --></p>

<p><ent>Plank</ent><br/
<hw>Plank</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Planked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Planking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover or lay with
planks; <as>as, to <ex>plank</ex> a floor or a ship</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Planked</xex> with pine.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lay down, as on a plank or table; to stake or pay
cash; <as>as, to <ex>plank</ex> money in a wager</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To harden, as hat bodies, by felting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Wooden Manuf.)</fld> <def>To splice together the
ends of slivers of wool, for subsequent drawing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Planked shad</b></col>, <cd>shad split open, fastened to
a plank, and roasted before a wood fire.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planking</ent><br/
<hw>Plank"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of laying
planks; also, planks, collectively; a series of planks in place, as the
wooden covering of the frame of a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of splicing slivers. See <er>Plank</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plank-sheer</ent><br/
<hw>Plank"-sheer`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>The course of plank laid horizontally
over the timberheads of a vessel's frame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plankton</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plank"ton</hw> <pr>(pl<acr/<nsm/k"t<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>plagto`n</grk>, neut. of <grk>plagto`s</grk>
wandering, <grk>pla`zesqai</grk> to wander.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>All the animals and plants, taken collectively, which live at or
near the surface of salt or fresh waters.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Plank*ton"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planless</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
plan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planner</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plans; a
projector.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Plani-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planoblast</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"o*blast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
wander + <ets>-blast</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
free-swimming gonophore of a hydroid; a hydroid medusa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-concave</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-con"cave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>concave</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plane or flat
on one side, and concave on the other; <as>as, a <ex>plano-concave</ex>
lens</as>. See <er>Lens</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-conical</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-con"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>conical</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plane or flat
on one side, and conical on the other.</def> <rj><au>Grew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-convex</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-con"vex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>convex</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plane or flat
on one side, and convex on the other; <as>as, a <ex>plano-convex</ex>
lens</as>. See <er>Convex</er>, and <er>Lens</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planogamete</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"o*ga*mete`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
wandering + E. <ets>gamete</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of
the motile ciliated gametes, or zoogametes, found in isogamous plants,
as many green alg<ae/ (Chlorophyce<ae/).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-horizontal</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-hor`i*zon"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>horizontal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having a
level horizontal surface or position.</def> <rj><au>Lee.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>. Cf.
<er>Planimeter</er>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for gauging or testing a
plane surface. See <cref>Surface gauge</cref>, under
<er>Surface</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*nom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>
<def>The art or process of producing or gauging a plane
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-orbicular</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-or*bic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>orbicular</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plane or
flat on one side, and spherical on the other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planorbis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*nor"bis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>planus</ets> flat + <ets>orbis</ets> a circle.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any fresh-water air-breathing mollusk belonging
to <gen>Planorbis</gen> and other allied genera, having shells of a
discoidal form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plano-subulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"no-su"bu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plano-</ets> + <ets>subulate</ets>.]</ety> <def>Smooth and
awl-shaped. See <er>Subulate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plant</ent><br/
<hw>Plant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>plante</ets>,
L. <ets>planta</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A vegetable; an organized
living being, generally without feeling and voluntary motion, and
having, when complete, a root, stem, and leaves, though consisting
sometimes only of a single leafy expansion, or a series of cellules, or
even a single cellule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ Plants are divided by their structure and methods of
reproduction into two series, <xex>ph<ae/nogamous</xex> or
<xex>flowering plants</xex>, which have true flowers and seeds, and
<xex>cryptogamous</xex> or <xex>flowerless plants</xex>, which have no
flowers, and reproduce by minute one-celled spores. In both series are
minute and simple forms and others of great size and complexity.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> As to their mode of nutrition, plants may be considered as
<xex>self-supporting</xex> and <xex>dependent</xex>.
<xex>Self-supporting plants</xex> always contain chlorophyll, and
subsist on air and moisture and the matter dissolved in moisture, and as
a general rule they excrete oxygen, and use the carbonic acid to combine
with water and form the material for their tissues. <xex>Dependent
plants</xex> comprise all fungi and many flowering plants of a parasitic
or saprophytic nature. As a rule, they have no chlorophyll, and subsist
mainly or wholly on matter already organized, thus utilizing carbon
compounds already existing, and not excreting oxygen. But there are
plants which are partly dependent and partly self-supporting.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> The movements of climbing plants, of some insectivorous plants, of
leaves, stamens, or pistils in certain plants, and the ciliary motion of
zoospores, etc., may be considered a kind of voluntary
motion.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bush, or young tree; a sapling; hence, a stick or
staff.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>plant</xex> of stubborn oak.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The sole of the foot.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/Knotty legs and <xex>plants</xex> of clay.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>The whole machinery and apparatus
employed in carrying on a trade or mechanical business; also, sometimes
including real estate, and whatever represents investment of capital in
the means of carrying on a business, but not including material worked
upon or finished products; <as>as, the <ex>plant</ex> of a foundry, a
mill, or a railroad</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A plan; an artifice; a swindle; a trick.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was n't a bad <qex>plant</qex>, that of mine, on Fikey.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An oyster which has
been bedded, in distinction from one of natural growth.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A young oyster suitable for transplanting.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- a person who joins a group, to spy on them on behalf of another
person or group --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plant bug</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous hemipterous insects which injure the foliage of plants, as
<spn>Lygus lineolaris</spn>, which damages wheat and trees.</cd> --
<col><b>Plant cutter</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a South American
passerine bird of the genus <gen>Phytotoma</gen>, family
<fam>Phytotomid<ae/</fam>. It has a serrated bill with which it cuts off
the young shoots and buds of plants, often doing much injury.</cd> --
<col><b>Plant louse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any small
hemipterous insect which infests plants, especially those of the
families <fam>Aphid<ae/</fam> and <fam>Psyllid<ae/</fam>; an
aphid.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plant</ent><br/
<hw>Plant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Planted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Planting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>plantian</ets>, L.
<ets>plantare</ets>. See <er>Plant</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To put in the ground and cover, as seed for growth;
<as>as, to <ex>plant</ex> maize</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set in the ground for growth, as a young tree, or
a vegetable with roots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt not <qex>plant</qex> thee a grove of any trees.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. xvi. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To furnish, or fit out, with plants; <as>as, to
<ex>plant</ex> a garden, an orchard, or a forest</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To engender; to generate; to set the germ
of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It engenders choler, <qex>planteth</qex> anger.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To furnish with a fixed and organized population; to
settle; to establish; <as>as, to <ex>plant</ex> a colony</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Planting</qex> of countries like planting of woods.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To introduce and establish the principles or seeds
of; <as>as, to <ex>plant</ex> Christianity among the
heathen</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To set firmly; to fix; to set and direct, or point;
<as>as, to <ex>plant</ex> cannon against a fort; to <ex>plant</ex> a
standard in any place; to <ex>plant</ex> one's feet on solid ground; to
<ex>plant</ex> one's fist in another's face.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To set up; to install; to instate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We will <qex>plant</qex> some other in the throne.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plant</ent><br/
<hw>Plant</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perform the act of
planting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have <qex>planted</qex>; Apollos watered.</q> <rj><qau>1 Cor.
iii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantable</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
planted; fit to be planted.</def> <rj><au>B. Edwards.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantage</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A word used once by
Shakespeare to designate plants in general, or anything that is
planted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As true as steel, as <qex>plantage</qex> to the moon.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak. (Troil. iii. sc. 2).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantain</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"tain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plantain-arbre</ets>, <ets>plantanier</ets>, Sp.
<ets>pl<aacute/ntano</ets>, <ets>pl<aacute/tano</ets>; prob. same word
as <ets>plane</ets> tree.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
treelike perennial herb (<spn>Musa paradisiaca</spn>) of tropical
regions, bearing immense leaves and large clusters of the fruits called
<xex>plantains</xex>. See <er>Musa</er>.</def> <-- a type of banana
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The fruit of this plant. It is long and somewhat
cylindrical, slightly curved, and, when ripe, soft, fleshy, and covered
with a thick but tender yellowish skin. The plantain is a staple article
of food in most tropical countries, especially when cooked.</def><--
resembling the banana of commerce, but smaller --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Plantain cutter</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Plantain eater</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one
of several large African birds of the genus <gen>Musophaga</gen>, or
family <fam>Musophagid<ae/</fam>, especially <spn>Musophaga
violacea</spn>. See <er>Turaco</er>. They are allied to the
cuckoos.</cd> -- <col><b>Plantain squirrel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a Java squirrel (<spn>Sciurus plantani</spn>) which feeds upon
plantains.</cd> -- <col><b>Plantain tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the treelike herb <spn>Musa paradisiaca</spn>. See def. 1
(above).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantain</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"tain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>plantago</ets>.
Cf. <er>Plant</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the genus
<gen>Plantago</gen>, but especially the <spn>Plantago major</spn>, a low
herb with broad spreading radical leaves, and slender spikes of minute
flowers. It is a native of Europe, but now found near the abode of
civilized man in nearly all parts of the world.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Indian plantain</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Indian</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Mud plantain</b></col>, <cd>a
homely North American aquatic plant (<spn>Heteranthera
reniformis</spn>), having broad, reniform leaves.</cd> --
<col><b>Rattlesnake plantain</b></col>, <cd>an orchidaceous plant
(<spn>Goodyera pubescens</spn>), with the leaves blotched and spotted
with white.</cd> -- <col><b>Ribwort plantain</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Ribwort</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Robin's plantain</b></col>, <cd>the
<spn>Erigeron bellidifolium</spn>, a common daisylike plant of North
America.</cd> -- <col><b>Water plantain</b></col>, <cd>a plant of the
genus <gen>Alisma</gen>, having acrid leaves, and formerly regarded as a
specific against hydrophobia.</cd> <au>Loudon.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantal</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>planta</ets>
a plant.]</ety> <def>Belonging to plants; <as>as, <ex>plantal</ex>
life</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantar</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"tar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plantaris</ets>, fr. <ets>planta</ets> the sole of the foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sole of the foot;
<as>as, the <ex>plantar</ex> arteries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantation</ent><br/
<hw>Plan*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plantatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>plantation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or practice of planting, or setting in the earth for
growth.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The place planted; land brought under cultivation; a
piece of ground planted with trees or useful plants; esp., in the United
States and West Indies, a large estate appropriated to the production of
the more important crops, and cultivated by laborers who live on the
estate; <as>as, a cotton <ex>plantation</ex>; a coffee
<ex>plantation</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An original settlement in a new country; a
colony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While these <qex>plantations</qex> were forming in
Connecticut.</q> <rj><qau>B. Trumbull.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plant-cane</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"-cane`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stalk or shoot
of sugar cane of the first growth from the cutting. The growth of the
second and following years is of inferior quality, and is called
<xex>rattoon</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plant-eating</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"-eat`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Eating, or
subsisting on, plants; <as>as, a <ex>plant-eating</ex>
beetle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planted</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Joinery)</fld>
<def>Fixed in place, as a projecting member wrought on a separate piece
of stuff; <as>as, a <ex>planted</ex> molding</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planter</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, plants or sows; <as>as, a <ex>planter</ex>of corn; a
machine <ex>planter</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who owns or cultivates a plantation; <as>as, a
sugar <ex>planter</ex>; a coffee <ex>planter</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A colonist in a new or uncultivated territory;
<as>as, the first <ex>planters</ex> in Virginia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>a movable box or a fixed low, open structure, as of
brick, in which plants are grown for decorative purposes.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantership</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The occupation or position of
a planter, or the management of a plantation, as in the United States or
the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planticle</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<er>Plant</er>.]</ety> <def>A young plant, or plant in embryo.</def>
<rj><au>E. Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantigrada</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plan`ti*gra"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A subdivision of Carnivora
having plantigrade feet. It includes the bears, raccoons, and allied
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantigrade</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ti*grade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>planta</ets> sole of the foot + <ets>gradi</ets> to walk: cf. F.
<ets>plantigrade</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Walking on the sole of the foot; pertaining to the
plantigrades.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having the foot so formed that the
heel touches the ground when the leg is upright.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantigrade</ent><br/
<hw>Plan"ti*grade</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
plantigrade animal, or one that walks or steps on the sole of the foot,
as man, and the bears.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planting</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
or operation of setting in the ground for propagation, as seeds, trees,
shrubs, etc.; the forming of plantations, as of trees; the carrying on
of plantations, as of sugar, coffee, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is planted; a plantation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trees of righteousness, the <qex>planting</qex> of the Lord.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. lxi. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The laying of the first courses
of stone in a foundation.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantless</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without plants; barren of
vegetation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantlet</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"let</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Plan*toc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Planter</ets> + <ets>-cracy</ets>, as in
<ets>democracy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Government by planters; planters,
collectively.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plantule</ent><br/
<hw>Plant"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>plante</ets> a plant, L. <ets>planta</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The embryo which has begun its development in the
act of germination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plan"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Planul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a little
plane.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>In embryonic
development, a vesicle filled with fluid, formed from the morula by the
divergence of its cells in such a manner as to give rise to a central
space, around which the cells arrange themselves as an envelope; an
embryonic form intermediate between the morula and gastrula. Sometimes
used as synonymous with <altname>gastrula</altname>.</def><-- now usu.
gastrula --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The very young, free-swimming
larva of the c<oe/lenterates. It usually has a flattened oval or oblong
form, and is entirely covered with cilia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Planxty</ent><br/
<hw>Planx"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>plangere</ets> to mourn aloud.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An
Irish or Welsh melody for the harp, sometimes of a mournful
character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaque</ent><br/
<hw>Plaque</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. Cf. <er>Plack</er>,
and see <er>Placard</er>.]</ety> <def>Any flat, thin piece of metal,
clay, ivory, or the like, used for ornament, or for painting pictures
upon, as a slab, plate, dish, or the like, hung upon a wall; also, a
smaller decoration worn on the person, as a brooch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaquette</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla`quette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>plaque</ets> plate, plaque. See <er>Plaque</er>.]</ety> <def>A
small plaque, esp., in modern medal engraving, a small and delicate
bas-relief, whether cast or struck from a die, or of form other than
circular.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plash</ent><br/
<hw>Plash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OD. <ets>plasch</ets>.
See <er>Plash</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small pool
of standing water; a puddle.</def> <au>Bacon.</au> <ldquo/These shallow
<xex>plashes</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Barrow.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A dash of water; a splash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plash</ent><br/
<hw>Plash</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plashed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>plassen</ets>, G.
<ets>platschen</ets>. Cf. <er>Splash</er>.]</ety> <def>To dabble in
water; to splash.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plashing</xex> among bedded
pebbles.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Far below him <qex>plashed</qex> the waters.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plash</ent><br/
<hw>Plash</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To splash, as
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To splash or sprinkle with coloring matter; <as>as,
to <ex>plash</ex> a wall in imitation of granite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plash</ent><br/
<hw>Plash</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plashed</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>plaissier</ets>,
<ets>plessier</ets>, to bend. Cf. <er>Pleach</er>.]</ety> <def>To cut
partly, or to bend and intertwine the branches of; <as>as, to
<ex>plash</ex> a hedge</as>.</def> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plash</ent><br/
<hw>Plash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The branch of a tree partly cut or
bent, and bound to, or intertwined with, other branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plashet</ent><br/
<hw>Plash"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Plash</ets> +
<ets>-et</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small pond or pool; a puddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plashing</ent><br/
<hw>Plash"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The cutting or
bending and intertwining the branches of small trees, as in
hedges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The dashing or sprinkling of coloring matter on the
walls of buildings, to imitate granite, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plashoot</ent><br/
<hw>Plash"oot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hedge or fence
formed of branches of trees interlaced, or <xex>plashed</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plashy</ent><br/
<hw>Plash"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 1st
<er>Plash</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Watery; abounding with puddles;
splashy.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plashy</xex> fens.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au>
<ldquo/The <xex>plashy</xex> earth.<rdquo/ <au>Wordsworth.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specked, as if plashed with color.</def>
<rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasm</ent><br/
<hw>Plasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plasma</ets>
anything formed or molded, that which is molded, Gr. <?/, <?/, from <?/
to form, mold: cf. F. <ets>plasme</ets>. Cf. <er>Plasma</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A mold or matrix in which anything is cast or formed to
a particular shape.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Plasma</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasma</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Plasm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of
quartz, of a color between grass green and leek green, which is found
associated with common chalcedony. It was much esteemed by the ancients
for making engraved ornaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The viscous material of an animal
or vegetable cell, out of which the various tissues are formed by a
process of differentiation; protoplasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Unorganized material; elementary matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A mixture of starch and glycerin,
used as a substitute for ointments.</def> <rj><au>U. S.
Disp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>same as <cref>blood plasma</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(physics)</fld> <def>a state of matter in which
charged particles such as electrons and atomi nuclei have sufficiently
high energy to move freely, rather than be bound in atoms as in ordinary
matter; it has some of the properties of a gas, but is a conductor of
electricity.</def> <note>In a typical plasma, the number of positive and
negative particles are approximately equal. Plasmas are found naturally
in the atmosphere of stars, and can be created in special laboratory
apparatus.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blood plasma</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>the
colorless fluid of the blood, in which the red and white blood
corpuscles are suspended. It may be obtained by centrifuation of blood
to remove the blood cells. It is distinguished from <er>serum</er> in
that plasma still has the fibrin of blood, and may be clotted, while in
serum the fibrin has been removed.</cd> -- <col><b>Muscle
plasma</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>the fundamental part of
muscle fibers, a thick, viscid, albuminous fluid contained within the
sarcolemma, which on the death of the muscle coagulates to a semisolid
mass.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1096 --></p>

<p><ent>Plasmatical</ent><br/
<ent>Plasmatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plas*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Plas*mat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Forming; shaping; molding.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to plasma;
having the character of plasma; containing, or conveying,
plasma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmation</ent><br/
<hw>Plas*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plasmatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of forming or molding.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Grafton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmator</ent><br/
<hw>Plas*ma"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A
former; a fashioner.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The sovereign
<xex>plasmator</xex>, God Almighty.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Urquhart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmature</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ma*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Form; mold.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmic</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
connected with, plasma; plasmatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmid</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"mid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Molecular
genetics)</fld> <def>A piece of DNA, usually circular, functioning as
part of the genetic material of a cell, not integrated with the
chromosome and replicating independently of the chromosome, but
transferred, like the chromosome, to subsequent generations of daughter
cells. In bacteria, plasmids often carry the genes for antibiotic
resistance; they are exploited in genetic engineering as the vehicles
for introduction of extraneous DNA into cells, to alter the genetic
makeup of the cell. The cells thus altered may produce desirable
proteins which are extracted and used; in the case of genetically
altered plant cells, the altered cells may grow into complete plants
with changed properties, as for example, increased resistance to
disease.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmin</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"min</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A proteid body, separated by some physiologists from
blood plasma. It is probably identical with fibrinogen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmodial</ent><br/
<hw>Plas*mo"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to, or like, a plasmodium; <as>as, the
<ex>plasmodial</ex> form of a life cycle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plas*mo"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Plasmodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Plasma</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A jellylike
mass of free protoplasm, without any union of am<oe/boid cells, and
endowed with life and power of motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A naked mobile mass of
protoplasm, formed by the union of several am<oe/balike young, and
constituting one of the stages in the life cycle of Mycetozoa and other
low organisms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- (a) a genus of microorganisms (b) an individual malarial parasite
cell. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmogen</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"mo*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Plasma</ets>
+ <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The important living
portion of protoplasm, considered a chemical substance of the highest
elaboration. Germ plasm and idioplasm are forms of plasmogen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasmon butter</ent><br/
<ent>Plasmon</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"mon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Plasma</er>.]</ety> <def>A flourlike food preparation made from skim
milk, and consisting essentially of the unaltered proteid of milk. It is
also used in making biscuits and crackers, for mixing with cocoa, etc. A
mixture of this with butter, water, and salt is called <hw>Plasmon
butter</hw>, and resembles clotted cream in appearance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasson</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plas"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/
to form.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The albuminous material
composing the body of a cytode.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is considered simpler than protoplasm of an ordinary
cell in that it has not undergone differentiation into the inner cell
nucleus and the outer cell substance.</note>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaster</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS., a plaster (in
sense 1), fr. L. <ets>emplastrum</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/, fr. <?/ to daub
on, stuff in; <?/ in + <?/ to mold: cf. OF. <ets>plastre</ets> a plaster
(in sense 2), F. <ets>pl<acir/tre</ets>. Cf. <er>Plastic</er>,
<er>Emplaster</er>, <er>Piaster</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written
also <asp>plaister</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An
external application of a consistency harder than ointment, prepared for
use by spreading it on linen, leather, silk, or other material. It is
adhesive at the ordinary temperature of the body, and is used, according
to its composition, to produce a medicinal effect, to bind parts
together, etc.; <as>as, a porous <ex>plaster</ex>; sticking
<ex>plaster</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A composition of lime, water, and sand, with or
without hair as a bond, for coating walls, ceilings, and partitions of
houses. See <er>Mortar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Calcined gypsum, or plaster of Paris, especially
when ground, as used for making ornaments, figures, moldings, etc.; or
calcined gypsum used as a fertilizer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plaster cast</b></col>, <cd>a copy of an object obtained
by pouring plaster of Paris mixed with water into a mold.</cd> --
<col><b>Plaster of Paris</b></col>. <ety>[So called because originally
brought from a suburb of Paris.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>Anhydrous
calcium sulphate, or calcined gypsum, which forms with water a paste
which soon sets or hardens, and is used for casts, moldings, etc. The
term is loosely applied to any plaster stone or species of gypsum.</cd>
-- <col><b>Plaster of Paris bandage</b></col> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>a
bandage saturated with a paste of plaster of Paris, which on drying
forms a perfectly fitting splint.</cd><-- = plaster cast, cast --> --
<col><b>Plaster stone</b></col>, <cd>any species of gypsum. See
<er>Gypsum</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaster</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plastered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plastering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>plastrer</ets>
to plaster (in sense 2), F. <ets>pl<acir/trer</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cover with a plaster, as a wound or sore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To overlay or cover with plaster, as the ceilings
and walls of a house.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: To smooth over; to cover or conceal the
defects of; to hide, as with a covering of plaster.</def>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasterer</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who applies plaster or mortar.</def> <ldquo/Thy father was a
<xex>plasterer</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who makes plaster casts.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>plasterer</xex> doth make his figures by addition.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastering</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Same as
<er>Plaster</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or process of overlaying with
plaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A covering of plaster; plasterwork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasterly</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling plaster of
Paris.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Out of gypseous or
<xex>plasterly</xex> ground.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasterwork</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter*work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Plastering used
to finish architectural constructions, exterior or interior, especially
that used for the lining of rooms. Ordinarly, mortar is used for the
greater part of the work, and pure plaster of Paris for the moldings and
ornaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastery</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ter*y</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the nature of
plaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stone . . . is a poor <qex>plastery</qex> material.</q>
<rj><qau>Clough.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-plastic</ent><br/
<hw>-plas"tic</hw> <pr>(-pl<acr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <?/ fit for
molding, plastic, fr. <?/ to mold, to form.]</ety> <def>A combining form
signifying <xex>developing</xex>, <xex>forming</xex>,
<xex>growing</xex>; <as>as, hetero<ex>plastic</ex>,
mono<ex>plastic</ex>, poly<ex>plastic</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastic</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"tic</hw> <pr>(pl<acr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plasticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to form, mold: cf. F.
<ets>plastique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the power to give
form or fashion to a mass of matter; <as>as, the <ex>plastic</ex> hand
of the Creator</as>.</def> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>See <qex>plastic</qex> Nature working to his end.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being molded, formed, or modeled, as clay
or plaster; -- used also figuratively; <as>as, the <ex>plastic</ex> mind
of a child</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pertaining or appropriate to, or characteristic of,
molding or modeling; produced by, or appearing as if produced by,
molding or modeling; -- said of sculpture and the kindred arts, in
distinction from painting and the graphic arts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Medallions . . . fraught with the <qex>plastic</qex> beauty and
grace of the palmy days of Italian art.</q> <rj><qau>J. S.
Harford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- composed of a plastic substance --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plastic clay</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>one of the
beds of the Eocene period; -- so called because used in making
pottery.</cd> <au>Lyell.</au> -- <col><b>Plastic element</b></col>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>one that bears within the germs of a higher
form.</cd> -- <col><b>Plastic exudation</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>an exudation thrown out upon a wounded surface and constituting the
material of repair by which the process of healing is effected.</cd> --
<col><b>Plastic foods</b></col>. <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <cd>See the
second Note under <er>Food</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plastic
force</b></col>. <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Force</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plastic operation</b></col>, <cd>an
operation in plastic surgery.</cd> -- <col><b>Plastic surgery</b></col>,
<cd>that branch of surgery which is concerned with the repair or
restoration of lost, injured, or deformed parts of the
body.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>plastic</ent><br/
<hw>plastic</hw> <pr>(pl<acr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
substance composed predominantly of a synthetic organic high polymer
capable of being cast or molded; many varieties of plastic are used to
produce articles of commerce (after 1900). [MW10 gives origin of word as
1905]</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastical</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Plastic</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastically</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plastic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plasticity</ent><br/
<hw>Plas*tic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plasticit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being plastic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Plastic force.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastide</ent><br/
<ent>Plastid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plas"tid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Plas"tide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, a creator.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A formative particle of albuminous matter; a
monad; a cytode. See the Note under <er>Morphon</er>.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>any of several types of minute
granules found in the protoplasm of vegetable cells, having their own
membrane, robosomes, and DNA. Among plant cells the most common are
<stype>chloroplasts</stype>, which contain the chlorophyll and the
photosynthetic machinery of the cell. They are divided by their colors
into three classes, chloroplastids, chromoplastids, and
leucoplastids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastidozoa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plas`ti*do*zo"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, creator + <?/ animal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Protoza</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastidule</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"ti*dule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. fr.
<er>Plastid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>One of the small
particles or organic molecules of protoplasm.</def>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastin</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to form,
mold.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A substance associated with nuclein
in cell nuclei, and by some considered as the fundamental substance of
the nucleus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastography</ent><br/
<hw>Plas*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/
fored, molded + <?/ to write.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art of forming
figures in any plastic material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Imitation of handwriting; forgery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plastron</ent><br/
<hw>Plas"tron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plastron</ets> breastplate, plastron, LL. <ets>plastra</ets> a thin
plate of metal. See <er>Plaster</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece of
leather stuffed or padded, worn by fencers to protect the breast.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld> <def>An iron breastplate, worn
under the hauberk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The ventral shield or shell of
tortoises and turtles. See <er>Testudinata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A trimming for the front of a woman's dress, made of
a different material, and narrowing from the shoulders to the
waist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-plasty</ent><br/
<hw>-plas"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <?/ to mold, form.]</ety>
<def>A combining form denoting <xex>the act</xex> or <xex>process of
forming</xex>, <xex>development</xex>, <xex>growth</xex>; <as>as,
auto<ex>plasty</ex>, perineo<ex>plasty</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Platted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Platting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Plait</er>.]</ety>
<def>To form by interlaying interweaving; to braid; to plait.</def>
<ldquo/They had <xex>platted</xex> a crown of thorns.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Matt. xxvii. 29.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work done by platting or braiding; a
plait.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her hair, nor loose, nor tied in formal <qex>plat</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Plat</er> flat, which perh.
caused this spelling, and <er>Plot</er> a piece of ground.]</ety> <def>A
small piece or plot of ground laid out with some design, or for a
special use; usually, a portion of flat, even ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This flowery <qex>plat</qex>, the sweet recess of Eve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I keep smooth <qex>plat</qex> of fruitful ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lay out in plats or plots, as
ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>plat</ets>. See
<er>Plate</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Plain; flat; level.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Plainly; flatly;
downright.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But, sir, ye lie, I tell you <qex>plat</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. of
R.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Flatly; smoothly; evenly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Drant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plat</ent><br/
<hw>Plat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The flat or broad side of
a sword.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A plot; a plan; a design; a diagram; a map; a
chart.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/To note all the
islands, and to set them down in <xex>plat</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hakluyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platan</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>platanus</ets>. See <er>Plane</er> the tree.]</ety> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>platane</asp>.]</altsp> <def>The plane tree.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platanist</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"a*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>platanista</ets> a sort of fish, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>plataniste</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
soosoo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platanus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plat"a*nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Plane</er> the tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of trees;
the plane tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platband</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"band`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plate-bande</ets>; <ets>plat</ets>, <ets>plate</ets>, flat, level +
<ets>bande</ets> a band.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A border of flowers in
a garden, along a wall or a parterre; hence, a border.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A flat molding, or
group of moldings, the width of which much exceeds its projection, as
the face of an architrave.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A list or fillet
between the flutings of a column.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plate</ent><br/
<hw>Plate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>plate</ets> a
plate of metal, a cuirsas, F. <ets>plat</ets> a plate, a shallow vessel
of silver, other metal, or earth, fr. <ets>plat</ets> flat, Gr. <?/. See
<er>Place</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A flat, or nearly
flat, piece of metal, the thickness of which is small in comparison with
the other dimensions; a thick sheet of metal; <as>as, a steel
<ex>plate</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Metallic armor composed of broad pieces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mangled . . . through <qex>plate</qex> and mail.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Domestic vessels and utensils, as flagons, dishes,
cups, etc., wrought in gold or silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Metallic ware which is plated, in distinction from
that which is silver or gold throughout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A small, shallow, and usually circular, vessel of
metal or wood, or of earth glazed and baked, from which food is eaten at
table.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[Cf. Sp. <ets>plata</ets> silver.]</ety> <def>A
piece of money, usually silver money.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Realms and islands were as <xex>plates</xex> dropp'd from his
pocket.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A piece of metal on which anything is engraved for
the purpose of being printed; hence, an impression from the engraved
metal; <as>as, a book illustrated with <ex>plates</ex>; a fashion
<ex>plate</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A page of stereotype, electrotype, or the like, for
printing from; <as>as, publisher's <ex>plates</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>That part of an artificial set of teeth which fits
to the mouth, and holds the teeth in place. It may be of gold, platinum,
silver, rubber, celluloid, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A horizontal timber laid upon a
wall, or upon corbels projecting from a wall, and supporting the ends of
other timbers; also used specifically of the <xex>roof plate</xex> which
supports the ends of the roof trusses or, in simple work, the feet of
the rafters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A roundel of silver or tinctured
argent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>A sheet of glass, porcelain,
metal, etc., with a coating that is sensitive to light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>A prize giving to the winner in a
contest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>A small five-sided area
(enveloping a diamond-shaped area one foot square) beside which the
batter stands and which must be touched by some part of a player on
completing a run; -- called also <altname>home base</altname>, or
<altname>home plate</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <def>One of the thin parts of the bricket of an
animal.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <def>A very light steel racing horsehoe.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <def>Loosely, a sporting contest for a prize; specif.,
in horse racing, a race for a prize, the contestants not making a
stake.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>18.</sn> <def>Skins for fur linings of garments, sewed together
and roughly shaped, but not finally cut or fitted.</def>
<mark>[Furrier's Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>19.</sn> <fld>(Hat Making)</fld> <def>The fine nap (as of beaver,
hare's wool, musquash, nutria, or English black wool) on a hat the body
of which is of an inferior substance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>20.</sn> <def>a quantity sufficient to fill a plate; a
<er>plateful</er>; a dish containing that quantity; <as>a <ex>plate</ex>
of spaghetti</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>21.</sn> <def>the food and service supplied to a customer at a
restaurant; <as>as, the turkey dinner is $9 a <ex>plate</ex></as>; I'll
have a <ex>plate</ex> of spaghetti.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>22.</sn> <def>a flat dish of glass or plastic with a fitted
cover, used for culturing microorganisms in a laboratory.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>23.</sn> <def>the identification tag required to be displayed on
the outside of a vehicle; same as <er>license plate</er>; -- often used
in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>24.</sn> <def>an agenda or schedule of tasks to be performed;
<as>I have a lot on my <ex>plate</ex> today</as>.</def>
<mark>[colloq.]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Plate</xex> is sometimes used in an adjectival
sense or in combination, the phrase or compound being in most cases of
obvious signification; as, <xex>plate</xex> basket or
<xex>plate</xex>-basket, <xex>plate</xex> rack or
<xex>plate</xex>-rack.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Home plate</b></col>. <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Home base</cref>, under <er>Home</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate
armor</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>See <er>Plate</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
2.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Strong metal plates for protecting war vessels,
fortifications, and the like.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate bone</b></col>,
<cd>the shoulder blade, or scapula.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate
girder</b></col>, <cd>a girder, the web of which is formed of a single
vertical plate, or of a series of such plates riveted together.</cd> --
<col><b>Plate glass</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Glass</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Plate iron</b></col>, <cd>wrought iron plates.</cd> --
<col><b>Plate layer</b></col>, <cd>a workman who lays down the rails of
a railway and fixes them to the sleepers or ties.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate
mark</b></col>, <cd>a special mark or emblematic figure stamped upon
gold or silver plate, to indicate the place of manufacture, the degree
of purity, and the like; thus, the local mark for London is a lion.</cd>
-- <col><b>Plate paper</b></col>, <cd>a heavy spongy paper, for printing
from engraved plates.</cd> <au>Fairholt.</au> -- <col><b>Plate
press</b></col>, <cd>a press with a flat carriage and a roller, -- used
for printing from engraved steel or copper plates.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate
printer</b></col>, <cd>one who prints from engraved plates.</cd> --
<col><b>Plate printing</b></col>, <cd>the act or process of printing
from an engraved plate or plates.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate
tracery</b></col>. <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Tracery</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plate wheel</b></col>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a wheel, the rim and hub of which are connected
by a continuous plate of metal, instead of by arms or
spokes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plate</ent><br/
<hw>Plate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover or overlay
with gold, silver, or other metals, either by a mechanical process, as
hammering, or by a chemical process, as electrotyping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover or overlay with plates of metal; to arm
with metal for defense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus <qex>plated</qex> in habiliments of war.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To adorn with plated metal; <as>as, a
<ex>plated</ex> harness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To beat into thin, flat pieces, or
lamin<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To calender; <as>as, to <ex>plate</ex>
paper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plateau</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*teau"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> F.
<plw>Plateaux</plw> <pr>(F. <?/; E. <?/)</pr>, E.</plu>
<plw>Plateaus</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>. <ety>[F., fr. OF. <ets>platel</ets>,
properly a little plate. See <er>Plate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
flat surface; especially, a broad, level, elevated area of land; a
table-land.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An ornamental dish for the table; a tray or
salver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plateful</ent><br/
<hw>Plate"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Platefuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Enough to fill a plate;
as much as a plate will hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plate-gilled</ent><br/
<hw>Plate"-gilled`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having flat, or leaflike, gills, as the bivalve mollusks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platel</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. See
<er>Plateau</er>.]</ety> <def>A small dish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platen</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>platine</ets>, fr. <ets>plat</ets> flat. See <er>Plate</er>, and
cf. <er>Platin</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
part of a printing press which presses the paper against the type and by
which the impression is made.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Hence, an
analogous part of a typewriter, on which the paper rests to receive an
impression.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The movable table of a machine tool,
as a planer, on which the work is fastened, and presented to the action
of the tool; -- also called <altname>table</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plater</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plates or
coats articles with gold or silver; <as>as, a silver
<ex>plater</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A machine for calendering paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plater</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Horse Racing)</fld>
<def>A horse that runs chiefly in plate, esp. selling-plate, races;
hence, an inferior race horse.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plateresque</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`er*esque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>resco</ets>, from <ets>plata</ets> silver.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Resembling silver plate; -- said of certain
architectural ornaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platetrope</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"e*trope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ breadth
+ <?/ to turn.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of a pair of a paired
organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>plate tectonics</ent><br/
<hw>plate tectonics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>A geological theory which holds that the crust of the earth (the
lithosphere) is divided into a small number of large separate plates
which float and move slowly around on the more plastic asthenosphere,
breaking apart and moving away from each other at points where magma
upwells from below, and, driven by such upwellings and other currents on
the athenosphere, sliding past each other, colliding with each other,
and in some cases being submerged (subducted) one below the other. This
theory is now widely accepted, and explains many geological phenomena
such as the clustered locations of earthquakes, mountain building,
volcanism, and the similarities observed between the geology of
continents, such as South America and Africa which are now far apart,
but, according to the theory, were once joined together. The motions of
such tectonic plates are very slow, typically only several centimeters
per year, but over tens and hundreds of millions of years, cause very
large changes in the relative positions of the continents. The
consequence of such movement of plates is called <er>continental
drift</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platform</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"form`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Plat</ets>,
a. + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>plateforme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A plat; a plan; a sketch; a model; a pattern. Used also
figuratively.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place laid out after a model.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>lf the <qex>platform</qex> just reflects the order.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any flat or horizontal surface; especially, one that
is raised above some particular level, as a framework of timber or
boards horizontally joined so as to form a roof, or a raised floor, or
portion of a floor; a landing; a dais; a stage, for speakers,
performers, or workmen; a standing place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A declaration of the principles upon which a person,
a sect, or a party proposes to stand; a declared policy or system;
<as>as, the Saybrook <ex>platform</ex>; a political
<ex>platform</ex>.</as></def> <ldquo/The <xex>platform</xex> of
Geneva.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A light deck, usually placed in a
section of the hold or over the floor of the magazine. See
<er>Orlop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Platform car</b></col>, <cd>a railway car without
permanent raised sides or covering; a flat.</cd> -- <col><b>Platform
scale</b></col>, <cd>a weighing machine, with a flat platform on which
objects are weighed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platform</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"form`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place on a
platform.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form a plan of; to model; to lay out.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Church discipline is <qex>platformed</qex> in the Bible.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plathelminth</ent><br/
<hw>Plat*hel"minth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A member of the <phylum>Platyhelminthes</phylum>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plathelminthes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plat`hel*min"thes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Platyelminthes</er>; -- an older term.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platin</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Platen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platina</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. or NL. See
<er>Platinum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Platinum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Platina mohr</b></col>, <cd>platinum black.</cd> --
<col><b>Platina yellow</b></col>, <cd>a pigment prepared from
platinum.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plating</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art
or process of covering anything with a plate or plates, or with metal,
particularly of overlaying a base or dull metal with a thin plate of
precious or bright metal, as by mechanical means or by electro-magnetic
deposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thin coating of metal laid upon another
metal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A coating or defensive armor of metal (usually
steel) plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinic</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*tin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or containing, platinum; -- used specifically to
designate those compounds in which the element has a higher valence, as
contrasted with the <xex>platinous</xex> compounds; <as>as,
<ex>platinic</ex> chloride (<chform>PtCl4</chform>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1097 --></p>

<p><ent>Platinichloric</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*ni*chlo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an acid
consisting of platinic chloride and hydrochloric acid, and obtained as a
brownish red crystalline substance, called <xex>platinichloric</xex>, or
<xex>chloroplatinic</xex>, <xex>acid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platiniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platinum</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Yielding
platinum; <as>as, <ex>platiniferous</ex> sand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platiniridium</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*ni*rid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem. &
Min.)</fld> <def>A natural alloy of platinum and iridium occurring in
grayish metallic rounded or cubical grains with platinum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinize</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Platinized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Platinizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
cover or combine with platinum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinochloric</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*no*chlo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, an
acid consisting of platinous chloride and hydrochloric acid, called
<xex>platinochloric acid</xex>, or <xex>chloroplatinous,
acid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinochloride</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*no*chlo"ride</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A double chloride of platinum and some other
metal or radical; a salt of platinochloric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinocyanic</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*no*cy*an"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, an
acid compound of platinous cyanide and hydrocyanic acid. It is obtained
as a cinnaber-red crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinocyanide</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*no*cy"a*nide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A double cyanide of platinum and some other
metal or radical; a salt of platinocyanic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinode</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*node</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platinum</ets> + Gr. <?/ a way.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>A cathode.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinoid</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platinum</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling
platinum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinoid</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*noid</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An alloy of
German silver containing tungsten; -- used for forming electrical
resistance coils and standards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinotype</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*no*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platinum</ets> + <ets>-type</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Photog.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A permanent photographic picture
or print in platinum black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The process by which such pictures are
produced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinous</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or containing, platinum; -- used specifically to
designate those compounds in which the element has a lower valence, as
contrasted with the <contr>platinic</contr> compounds; <as>as,
<ex>platinous</ex> chloride (<chform>PtCl2</chform>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platinum</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Sp.
<ets>platina</ets>, from <ets>plata</ets> silver, LL. <ets>plata</ets> a
thin plate of metal. See <er>Plate</er>, and cf.
<er>Platina</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A metallic element of
atomic number 78, one of the noble metals, classed with silver and gold
as a <isa>precious metal</isa>, occurring native or alloyed with other
metals and also as the platinum arsenide (sperrylite). It is a heavy
tin-white metal which is ductile and malleable, but very infusible
(melting point 1772<deg/ C), and characterized by its resistance to
strong chemical reagents. It is used for crucibles in laboratory
operations, as a catalyst, in jewelry, for stills for sulphuric acid,
rarely for coin, and in the form of foil and wire for many purposes.
Specific gravity 21.5. Atomic weight 195.1. Symbol Pt. Formerly called
<altname>platina</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Platinum black</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a soft,
dull black powder, consisting of finely divided metallic platinum
obtained by reduction and precipitation from its solutions. It absorbs
oxygen to a high degree, and is employed as an oxidizer.</cd> --
<col><b>Platinum lamp</b></col> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of
incandescent lamp of which the luminous medium is platinum. See under
<er>Incandescent</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Platinum metals</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>the group of metallic elements which in their
chemical and physical properties resemble platinum. These consist of the
<xex>light platinum group</xex>, viz., rhodium, ruthenium, and
palladium, whose specific gravities are about 12; and the <xex>heavy
platinum group</xex>, viz., osmium, iridium, and platinum, whose
specific gravities are over 21.</cd> -- <col><b>Platinum
sponge</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>metallic platinum in a gray,
porous, spongy form, obtained by reducing the double chloride of
platinum and ammonium. It absorbs oxygen, hydrogen, and certain other
gases, to a high degree, and is employed as an agent in
oxidizing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platitude</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from
<ets>plat</ets> flat. See <er>Plate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being flat, thin, or insipid; flat commonness;
triteness; staleness of ideas of language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To hammer one golden grain of wit into a sheet of infinite
<qex>platitude</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Motley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thought or remark which is flat, dull, trite, or
weak; a truism; a commonplace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platitudinarian</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*tu`di*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
addicted to uttering platitudes, or stale and insipid truisms.</def>
<ldquo/A political <xex>platitudinarian</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G.
Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platitudinize</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*tu"di*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To utter
platitudes or truisms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platitudinous</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`i*tu"di*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in
platitudes; of the nature of platitudes; uttering platitudes.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Plat`i*tu"di*nous*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platly</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Flatly. See
<er>Plat</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platness</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Flatness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Palsgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ flat +
<ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Planimeter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonical</ent><br/
<ent>Platonic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pla*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pla*ton"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Platonicus</ets>, Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>platonique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to Plato, or his philosophy, school, or opinions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pure, passionless; nonsexual;
philosophical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Platonic bodies</b></col>, <cd>the five regular
geometrical solids; namely, the tetrahedron, hexahedron or cube,
octahedron, dodecahedron, and icosahedron.</cd> -- <col><b>Platonic
love</b></col>, <cd>a pure, spiritual affection, subsisting between
persons of opposite sex, unmixed with carnal desires, and regarding the
mind only and its excellences; -- a species of love for which Plato was
a warm advocate.</cd><-- = Platonic relation --> -- <col><b>Platonic
year</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a period of time determined by
the revolution of the equinoxes, or the space of time in which the stars
and constellations return to their former places in respect to the
equinoxes; -- called also <altname>great year</altname>. This
revolution, which is caused by the precession of the equinoxes, is
accomplished in about 26,000 years.</cd> <au>Barlow.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*ton"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A follower of Plato; a
Platonist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonically</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*ton"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a Platonic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonism</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"to*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>Platonisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The doctrines or
philosophy by Plato or of his followers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Plato believed God to be an infinitely wise, just, and
powerful Spirit; and also that he formed the visible universe out of
pre<eum/xistent amorphous matter, according to perfect patterns of ideas
eternally existent in his own mind. Philosophy he considered as being a
knowledge of the true nature of things, as discoverable in those eternal
ideas after which all things were fashioned. In other words, it is the
knowledge of what is eternal, exists necessarily, and is unchangeable;
not of the temporary, the dependent, and changeable; and of course it is
not obtained through the senses; neither is it the product of the
understanding, which concerns itself only with the variable and
transitory; nor is it the result of experience and observation; but it
is the product of our <xex>reason</xex>, which, as partaking of the
divine nature, has innate ideas resembling the eternal ideas of God. By
contemplating these innate ideas, reasoning about them, and comparing
them with their copies in the visible universe, reason can attain that
true knowledge of things which is called <xex>philosophy</xex>. Plato's
professed followers, the Academics, and the New Platonists, differed
considerably from him, yet are called <xex>Platonists</xex>.</note>
<rj><au>Murdock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An elevated rational and ethical conception of the
laws and forces of the universe; sometimes, imaginative or fantastic
philosophical notions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonist</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"to*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who adheres to
the philosophy of Plato; a follower of Plato.</def>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonize</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"to*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Platonized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Platonizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To adopt the opinion
of Plato or his followers.</def> <rj><au>Milner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonize</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"to*nize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To explain by, or accomodate
to, the Platonic philosophy.</def> <rj><au>Enfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platonizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pla"to*ni`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
Platonizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platoon</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*toon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>peloton</ets> a ball of thread, a knot or group of men, a platoon,
from <ets>pelote</ets> a ball formed of things wound round. See
<er>Pellet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Formerly, a
body of men who fired together; also, a small square body of soldiers to
strengthen the angles of a hollow square.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Now,
in the United States service, half of a company.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platt</ent><br/
<hw>Platt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>See
<er>Lodge</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plattdeutsch</ent><br/
<hw>Platt"deutsch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The modern
dialects spoken in the north of Germany, taken collectively; modern Low
German. See <cref>Low German</cref>, under <er>German</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platten</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Plat</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Glass Making)</fld> <def>To
flatten and make into sheets or plates; <as>as, to <ex>platten</ex>
cylinder glass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platter</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Plat</er>
to braid.]</ety> <def>One who plats or braids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platter</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr. OF.
<ets>platel</ets>, F. <ets>plateau</ets>. See <er>Plateau</er>.]</ety>
<def>A large plate or shallow dish on which meat or other food is
brought to the table.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The attendants . . . speedly brought in several large, smoking
<qex>platters</qex>, filled with huge pieces of beef.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platter-faced</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ter-faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
broad, flat face.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platting</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Plaited strips or
bark, cane, straw, etc., used for making hats or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platy</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a plate;
consisting of plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platy-</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"y-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>platy`s</grk> <xex>broad</xex>, <xex>wide</xex>, <xex>flat</xex>;
<as>as, <ex>platy</ex>pus, <ex>platy</ex>cephalous</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platycephalous</ent><br/
<ent>Platycephalic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plat`y*ce*phal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Plat`y*ceph"a*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platy</ets> + Gr. <?/ head.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Broad-headed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platycnemic</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`yc*ne"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platy</ets> + Gr. <?/ leg: cf. F.
<ets>platycn<eacute/mique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of,
relating to, or characterized by, platycnemism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platycnemism</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*tyc"ne*mism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Lateral flattening of the tibia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platycoelian</ent><br/
<hw>Plat`y*c<oe/"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platy</ets> + Gr. <?/ hollow.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Flat at the anterior and concave at the posterior end; -- said of
the centra of the vertebr<ae/ of some extinct dinouaurs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platyelminthes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plat`y*el*min"thes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Platy-</er>, and <er>Helminthes</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A class of helminthes including the cestodes, or
tapeworms, the trematodes, and the turbellarians. Called also
<altname>flatworms</altname>.</def><-- now = platyhelminthes --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platyhelmia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plat`y*hel"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Platyelminthes</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Platyelmia</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platymeter</ent><br/
<hw>Pla*tym"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Platy</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>An apparatus for measuring the capacity of condensers, or the
inductive capacity of dielectrics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platypod</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"y*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Platy</ets> +
<ets>-pod</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An animal having broad
feet, or a broad foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platypoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*typ"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Prosobranchiata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platyptera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla*typ"te*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ broad + <?/ a wing.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
division of Pseudoneuroptera including the species which have four
broad, flat wings, as the termites, or white-ants, and the stone flies
(<gen>Perla</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platypus</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"y*pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ +
<?/ foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The <cref>duck mole</cref>. See
under <er>Duck</er>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> Duck Mole, duckbill, duck-bill platypus, duck-billed
platypus.</syn>
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platyrhine</ent><br/
<hw>Plat"y*rhine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Platy</ets>
+ Gr. <?/, <?/, nose.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having the nose
broad; -- opposed to <ant>leptorhine</ant>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Platyrhini.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Platyrhini</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plat`y*rhi"ni</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ broad + <?/, <?/, nose.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
division of monkeys, including the American species, which have a broad
nasal septum, thirty-six teeth, and usually a prehensile tail. See
<er>Monkey</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Platyrrhini</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaud</ent><br/
<hw>Plaud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To applaud.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaudit</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"dit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>plaudite</ets> do ye praise (which was said by players at the end
of a performance), 2d pers. pl. imperative of <ets>plaudere</ets>. Cf.
<er>Plausible</er>.]</ety> <def>A mark or expression of applause; praise
bestowed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not in the shouts and <qex>plaudits</qex> of the throng.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Acclamation; applause; encomium; commendation;
approbation; approval.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plauditory</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"di*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Applauding;
commending.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plausibility</ent><br/
<hw>Plau`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plausibilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something worthy
of praise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Integrity, fidelity, and other gracious
<qex>plausibilities</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>E. Vaughan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality of being plausible;
speciousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To give any <qex>plausibility</qex> to a scheme.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything plausible or specious.</def> <rj><au>R.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plausible</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plausibilis</ets> praiseworthy, from <ets>plaudere</ets>,
<ets>plausum</ets>, to applaud, clap the hands, strike, beat.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Worthy of being applauded; praiseworthy; commendable;
ready.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hacket.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Obtaining approbation; specifically pleasing;
apparently right; specious; <as>as, a <ex>plausible</ex> pretext;
<ex>plausible</ex> manners; a <ex>plausible</ex> delusion.</as></def>
<ldquo/<xex>Plausible</xex> and popular arguments.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Using specious arguments or discourse; <as>as, a
<ex>plausible</ex> speaker</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 4 appearing worthy of belief [MW10]. Now the most common sense,
and a good sense, rather than the traditional bad sense. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Plausible</er>, <er>Specious</er>.</syn>
<usage> <xex>Plausible</xex> denotes that which seems reasonable, yet
leaves distrust in the judgment. <xex>Specious</xex> describes that
which presents a fair appearance to the view and yet covers something
false. <xex>Specious</xex> refers more definitely to the act or purpose
of false representation; <xex>plausible</xex> has more reference to the
effect on the beholder or hearer. An argument may by <xex>specious</xex>
when it is not <xex>plausible</xex> because its sophistry is so easily
discovered.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plausibleize</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"si*ble*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render
plausible.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plausibleness</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"si*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
plausible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plausibly</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"si*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a plausible
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Contentedly, readily.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Romans <qex>plausibly</qex> did give consent.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plausive</ent><br/
<hw>Plau"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plaudere</ets>, <ets>plausum</ets>, to applaud.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Applauding; manifesting praise.</def> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plausible, specious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Play</ent><br/
<hw>Play</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Played</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Playing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pleien</ets>, AS.
<ets>plegian</ets>, <ets>plegan</ets>, to play, akin to <ets>plega</ets>
play, game, quick motion, and probably to OS. <ets>plegan</ets> to
promise, pledge, D. <ets>plegen</ets> to care for, attend to, be wont,
G. <ets>pflegen</ets>; of unknown origin. <root/28. Cf. <er>Plight</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To engage in sport or lively
recreation; to exercise for the sake of amusement; to frolic; to
spot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As Cannace was <qex>playing</qex> in her walk.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lamb thy riot dooms to bleed to-day,<br/
Had he thy reason, would he skip and <qex>play</qex>!</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And some, the darlings of their Lord,<br/
<qex>Play</qex> smiling with the flame and sword.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To act with levity or thoughtlessness; to trifle; to
be careless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Nay,<rdquo/ quod this monk, <ldquo/I have no lust to
<qex>pleye</qex>.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men are apt to <qex>play</qex> with their healths.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To contend, or take part, in a game; <as>as, to
<ex>play</ex> ball</as>; hence, to gamble; <as>as, he <ex>played</ex>
for heavy stakes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To perform on an instrument of music; <as>as, to
<ex>play</ex> on a flute</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One that . . . can <qex>play</qex> well on an instrument.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xxxiii. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Play</qex>, my friend, and charm the charmer.</q>
<rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To act; to behave; to practice deception.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His mother <qex>played</qex> false with a smith.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To move in any manner; especially, to move regularly
with alternate or reciprocating motion; to operate; to act; <as>as, the
fountain <ex>plays</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heart beats, the blood circulates, the lungs
<qex>play</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Cheyne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To move gayly; to wanton; to disport.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even as the waving sedges <qex>play</qex> with wind.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The setting sun<br/
<qex>Plays</qex> on their shining arms and burnished helmets.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All fame is foreign but of true desert,<br/
<qex>Plays</qex> round the head, but comes not to the heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To act on the stage; to personate a
character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lord will hear your <qex>play</qex> to-night.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Courts are theaters where some men <qex>play</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To play into a person's hands</b></col>, <cd>to act, or
to manage matters, to his advantage or benefit.</cd> -- <col><b>To play
off</b></col>, <cd>to affect; to feign; to practice artifice.</cd> --
<col><b>To play upon</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To make sport of; to
deceive.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Art thou alive?<br/
Or is it fantasy that <qex>plays upon</qex> our eyesight.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To use in a droll manner; to give a droll expression
or application to; as, <xex>to play upon</xex> words.</cd> <-- play
around --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Play</ent><br/
<hw>Play</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put in action or
motion; <as>as, to <ex>play</ex> cannon upon a fortification; to
<ex>play</ex> a trump.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>First Peace and Silence all disputes control,<br/
Then Order <qex>plays</qex> the soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perform music upon; <as>as, to <ex>play</ex> the
flute or the organ</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To perform, as a piece of music, on an instrument;
<as>as, to <ex>play</ex> a waltz on the violin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bring into sportive or wanton action; to exhibit
in action; to execute; <as>as, to <ex>play</ex> tricks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature here<br/
Wantoned as in her prime, and <qex>played</qex> at will<br/
Her virgin fancies.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To act or perform (a play); to represent in music
action; <as>as, to <ex>play</ex> a comedy</as>; also, to act in the
character of; to represent by acting; to simulate; to behave like;
<as>as, to <ex>play</ex> King Lear; to <ex>play</ex> the
woman.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou canst <qex>play</qex> the rational if thou wilt.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To engage in, or go together with, as a contest for
amusement or for a wager or prize; <as>as, to <ex>play</ex> a game at
baseball</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To keep in play, as a hooked fish, in order to land
it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To play hob</b></col>, <cd>to play the part of a
mischievous spirit; to work mischief.</cd> -- <col><b>To play
off</b></col>, <cd>to display; to show; to put in exercise; as, <xex>to
play off</xex> tricks.</cd> -- <col><b>To play one's cards</b></col>,
<cd>to manage one's means or opportunities; to contrive.</cd> --
<col><b>Played out</b></col>, <cd>tired out; exhausted; at the end of
one's resources.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Play</ent><br/
<hw>Play</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Amusement; sport; frolic;
gambols.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any exercise, or series of actions, intended for
amusement or diversion; a game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>John naturally loved rough <qex>play</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act or practice of contending for victory,
amusement, or a prize, as at dice, cards, or billiards; gaming; <as>as,
to lose a fortune in <ex>play</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Action; use; employment; exercise; practice; <as>as,
fair <ex>play</ex>; sword <ex>play</ex>; a <ex>play</ex> of
wit.</as></def> <ldquo/The next who comes in <xex>play</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A dramatic composition; a comedy or tragedy; a
composition in which characters are represented by dialogue and
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>play</qex> ought to be a just image of human nature.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The representation or exhibition of a comedy or
tragedy; <as>as, he attends ever <ex>play</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Performance on an instrument of music.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Motion; movement, regular or irregular; <as>as, the
<ex>play</ex> of a wheel or piston</as>; hence, also, room for motion;
free and easy action.</def> <ldquo/To give them <xex>play</xex>, front
and rear.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The joints are let exactly into one another, that they have no
<qex>play</qex> between them.</q> <rj><qau>Moxon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Hence, liberty of acting; room for enlargement or
display; scope; <as>as, to give full <ex>play</ex> to
mirth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Play actor</b></col>, <cd>an actor of dramas.</cd>
<au>Prynne.</au> -- <col><b>Play debt</b></col>, <cd>a gambling
debt.</cd> <au>Arbuthnot.</au> -- <col><b>Play pleasure</b></col>,
<cd>idle amusement.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bacon.</au> --
<col><b>A play upon words</b></col>, <cd>the use of a word in such a way
as to be capable of double meaning; punning.</cd> -- <col><b>Play of
colors</b></col>, <cd>prismatic variation of colors.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>To bring into play</b></col>, <col><b>To come into
play</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to bring or come into use or exercise.</cd>
-- <col><b>To hold in play</b></col>, <cd>to keep occupied or
employed.</cd></cs> <-- in play. (a) (baseball, football) [of a ball] to
still be subject to action so as to affect the game, true as long as the
specific play has not been completed. Opposite of out of play, out of
bounds. (b) (Corporate Finance) subject to acquisition or merger; said
of companies which have been discussed as potential acquisitions by
potentially acquiring companies. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I, with two more to help me,<br/
Will <qex>hold</qex> the foe <qex>in play</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1098 --></p>

<p><ent>Playa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla"ya</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety> <def>A
beach; a strand; in the plains and deserts of Texas, New Mexico, and
Arizona, a broad, level spot, on which subsequently becomes dry by
evaporation.</def> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>playact</ent><br/
<hw>playact</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perform on a stage or
theater.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> act, play, roleplay.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playbill</ent><br/
<hw>Play"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A printed programme
of a play, with the parts assigned to the actors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playbook</ent><br/
<hw>Play"book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A book of dramatic
compositions; a book of the play.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playday</ent><br/
<hw>Play"day`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A day given to play
or diversion; a holiday.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Player</ent><br/
<hw>Play"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
plays, or amuses himself; one without serious aims; an idler; a
trifler.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who plays any game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A dramatic actor.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One who plays on an instrument of music.</def>
<ldquo/A cunning <xex>player</xex> on a harp.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Sam. xvi.
16.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A gamester; a gambler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>player piano</ent><br/
<hw>player piano</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mechanically operated piano
that uses a roll of perforated paper to activate the keys.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> mechanical piano, Pianola.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playfellow</ent><br/
<hw>Play"fel`low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A companion in
amusements or sports; a playmate.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playfere</ent><br/
<hw>Play"fere`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Play</ets> +
1st <ets>fere</ets>.]</ety> <def>A playfellow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<altsp>[Also, <asp>playfeer</asp>, <asp>playphere</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Holinsheld.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playful</ent><br/
<hw>Play"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sportive; gamboling;
frolicsome; indulging a sportive fancy; humorous; merry; <as>as, a
<ex>playful</ex> child; a <ex>playful</ex> writer.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Play"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Play"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playgame</ent><br/
<hw>Play"game`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Play of
children.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playgoer</ent><br/
<hw>Play"go`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who frequents
playhouses, or attends dramatic performances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playgoing</ent><br/
<hw>Play"go`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Frequenting playhouses; <as>as,
the <ex>playgoing</ex> public</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>The practice of going to plays.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playground</ent><br/
<hw>Play"ground`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A piece of ground
used for recreation; <as>as, the <ex>playground</ex> of a
school</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Play"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>plegh<umac/s</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A building used for
dramatic exhibitions; a theater.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A house for children to play in; a
toyhouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playing</ent><br/
<hw>Play"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & vb. n.</pos> of
<er>Play</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Playing cards</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Card</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playmaker</ent><br/
<hw>Play"mak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
playwright.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playmate</ent><br/
<hw>Play"mate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A companion in
diversions; a playfellow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playsome</ent><br/
<hw>Play"some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Playful; wanton;
sportive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <au>R. Browning.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Play"some*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playte</ent><br/
<hw>Playte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pleyt</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaything</ent><br/
<hw>Play"thing`</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"th<icr/ng`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A thing to play with; a toy; anything that serves to
amuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A child knows his nurse, and by degrees the <qex>playthings</qex>
of a little more advanced age.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playtime</ent><br/
<hw>Play"time`</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"t<imac/m`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Time for play or diversion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playwright</ent><br/
<hw>Play"wright`</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"r<imac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A maker or adapter of plays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Playwriter</ent><br/
<hw>Play"writ`er</hw> <pr>(pl<amac/"r<imac/t`<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A writer of plays; a dramatist; a playwright.</def>
<rj><au>Lecky.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plaza</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pla"za</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. See
<er>Place</er>.]</ety> <def>A public square in a city or town.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plea</ent><br/
<hw>Plea</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>plee</ets>, <ets>plai</ets>, <ets>plait</ets>, fr. OF.
<ets>plait</ets>, <ets>plaid</ets>, <ets>plet</ets>, LL.
<ets>placitum</ets> judgment, decision, assembly, court, fr. L.
<ets>placitum</ets> that which is pleasing, an opinion, sentiment, from
<ets>placere</ets> to please. See <er>Please</er>, and cf.
<er>Placit</er>, <er>Plead</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>That which is alleged by a party in support of his cause; in a
stricter sense, an allegation of fact in a cause, as distinguished from
a <xex>demurrer</xex>; in a still more limited sense, and in modern
practice, the defendant's answer to the plaintiff's declaration and
demand. That which the plaintiff alleges in his declaration is answered
and repelled or justified by the defendant's <xex>plea</xex>. In
chancery practice, a <xex>plea</xex> is a special answer showing or
relying upon one or more things as a cause why the suit should be either
dismissed, delayed, or barred. In criminal practice, the <xex>plea</xex>
is the defendant's formal answer to the indictment or information
presented against him.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A cause in court; a lawsuit;
<as>as, the Court of Common <ex>Pleas</ex></as>. See under
<er>Common</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Supreme Judicial Court shall have cognizance of
<qex>pleas</qex> real, personal, and mixed.</q> <rj><qau>Laws of
Massachusetts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is alleged or pleaded, in defense or in
justification; an excuse; an apology.</def> <ldquo/Necessity, the
tyrant's <xex>plea</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>plea</qex> must serve; 't is cruelty to spare.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An urgent prayer or entreaty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pleas of the crown</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>,
<cd>criminal actions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleach</ent><br/
<hw>Pleach</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/ch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pleached</conjf>
<pr>(pl<emac/cht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pleaching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>plaissier</ets>
to bend, and also F. <ets>plisser</ets> to plait, L. <ets>plicare</ets>,
<ets>plicitum</ets>, to fold, lay, or wind together. Cf. <er>Plash</er>
to pleach.]</ety> <def>To unite by interweaving, as branches of trees;
to plash; to interlock.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>pleached</xex>
bower.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plead</ent><br/
<hw>Plead</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/d)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pleaded</conjf> (<mark>colloq.</mark>
<conjf>Plead</conjf> <pr>(pl<ecr/d)</pr> or <conjf>Pled</conjf>);
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pleading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pleden</ets>, <ets>plaiden</ets>, OF. plaidier, F.
<ets>plaider</ets>, fr. LL. <ets>placitare</ets>, fr.
<ets>placitum</ets>. See <er>Plea</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To argue
in support of a claim, or in defense against the claim of another; to
urge reasons for or against a thing; to attempt to persuade one by
argument or supplication; to speak by way of persuasion; <as>as, to
<ex>plead</ex> for the life of a criminal; to <ex>plead</ex> with a
judge or with a father.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O that one might <qex>plead</qex> for a man with God, as a man
<qex>pleadeth</qex> for his neighbor!</q> <rj><qau>Job xvi.
21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To present an answer, by allegation
of fact, to the declaration of a plaintiff; to deny the plaintiff's
declaration and demand, or to allege facts which show that he ought not
to recover in the suit; in a less strict sense, to make an allegation of
fact in a cause; to carry on the allegations of the respective parties
in a cause; to carry on a suit or plea.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au>
<au>Burrill.</au> <au>Stephen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To contend; to struggle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plead</ent><br/
<hw>Plead</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
discuss, defend, and attempt to maintain by arguments or reasons
presented to a tribunal or person having uthority to determine; to argue
at the bar; <as>as, to <ex>plead</ex> a cause before a court or
jury</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every man should <qex>plead</qex> his own matter.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
T. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense, <xex>argue</xex> is more generally used
by lawyers.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To allege or cite in a legal plea or defense, or for
repelling a demand in law; to answer to an indictment; <as>as, to
<ex>plead</ex> usury; to <ex>plead</ex> statute of limitations; to
<ex>plead</ex> not guilty.</as></def> <rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To allege or adduce in proof, support, or
vendication; to offer in excuse; <as>as, the law of nations may be
<ex>pleaded</ex> in favor of the rights of ambassadors</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will neither <qex>plead</qex> my age nor sickness, in excuse of
faults.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleadable</ent><br/
<hw>Plead"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
pleaded; capable of being alleged in proof, defense, or vindication;
<as>as, a right or privilege <ex>pleadable</ex> at law</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleader</ent><br/
<hw>Plead"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaideur</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who pleads; one who
argues for or against; an advotate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So fair a <qex>pleader</qex> any cause may gain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who draws up or forms pleas;
the draughtsman of pleas or pleadings in the widest sense; <as>as, a
special <ex>pleader</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleading</ent><br/
<hw>Plead"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of advocating, defending,
or supporting, a cause by arguments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleadingly</ent><br/
<hw>Plead"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pleading
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleadings</ent><br/
<hw>Plead"ings</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>The mutual pleas and replies of the plaintiff and defendant, or
written statements of the parties in support of their claims, proceeding
from the declaration of the plaintiff, until issue is joined, and the
question made to rest on some single point.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasance</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaisance</ets>. See <er>Please</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pleasure; merriment; gayety; delight; kindness.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/Full great
<xex>pleasance</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/A realm of
<xex>pleasance</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Tennyson.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A secluded part of a garden.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pleasances</qex> of old Elizabethan houses.</q>
<rj><qau>Ruskin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasant</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaisant</ets>. See <er>Please</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pleasing; grateful to the mind or to the senses; agreeable; <as>as,
a <ex>pleasant</ex> journey; <ex>pleasant</ex> weather.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behold, how good and <qex>pleasant</qex> it is for brethren to
dwell together in unity!</q> <rj><qau>Ps. cxxxiii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cheerful; enlivening; gay; sprightly; humorous;
sportive; <as>as, <ex>pleasant</ex> company; a <ex>pleasant</ex>
fellow.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From grave to light, from <qex>pleasant</qex> to serve.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pleasing; gratifying; agreeable; cheerful;
good-humored; enlivening; gay; lively; merry; sportive; humorous;
jocose; amusing; witty.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Pleasant</er>,
<er>Pleasing</er>, <er>Agreeable</er>. <xex>Agreeable</xex> is applied
to that which agrees with, or is in harmony with, one's tastes,
character, etc. <xex>Pleasant</xex> and <xex>pleasing</xex> denote a
stronger degree of the agreeable. <xex>Pleasant</xex> refers rather to
the state or condition; <xex>pleasing</xex>, to the act or effect. Where
they are applied to the same object, <xex>pleasing</xex> is more
energetic than <xex>pleasant</xex>; <as>as, she is always
<ex>pleasant</ex> and always <ex>pleasing</ex></as>. The distinction,
however, is not radical and not rightly observed.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasant</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wit; a humorist; a
buffoon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pleasant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasantness</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ant*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of
being pleasant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasantry</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ant*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pleasantries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaisanterie</ets>. See <er>Pleasant</er>.]</ety> <def>That which
denotes or promotes pleasure or good humor; cheerfulness; gayety;
merriment; especially, an agreeable playfulness in conversation; a
jocose or humorous remark; badinage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The grave abound in <qex>pleasantries</qex>, the dull in repartees
and points of wit.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The keen observation and ironical <qex>pleasantry</qex> of a
finished man of the world.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasant-tongued</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ant-tongued`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of pleasing
speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Please</ent><br/
<hw>Please</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pleased</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pleasing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>plesen</ets>, OF.
<ets>plaisir</ets>, fr. L. <ets>placere</ets>, akin to
<ets>placare</ets> to reconcile. Cf. <er>Complacent</er>,
<er>Placable</er>, <er>Placid</er>, <er>Plea</er>, <er>Plead</er>,
<er>Pleasure</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give pleasure to; to
excite agreeable sensations or emotions in; to make glad; to gratify; to
content; to satisfy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I pray to God that it may <qex>plesen</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What next I bring shall <qex>please</qex> thee, be assured.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have or take pleasure in; hence, to choose; to
wish; to desire; to will.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whatsoever the Lord <qex>pleased</qex>, that did he.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. cxxxv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man doing as he wills, and doing as he <qex>pleases</qex>, are
the same things in common speech.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be the will or pleasure of; to seem good to; --
used impersonally.</def> <ldquo/It <xex>pleased</xex> the Father that in
him should all fullness dwell.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Col. i. 19.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To-morrow, may it <qex>please</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To be pleased in</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To
be pleased with</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to have complacency in; to take
pleasure in.</cd> -- <col><b>To be pleased to do a thing</b></col>,
<cd>to take pleasure in doing it; to have the will to do it; to think
proper to do it.</cd> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Please</ent><br/
<hw>Please</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
afford or impart pleasure; to excite agreeable emotions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>pleasing</qex> scemed, for her now <qex>pleases</qex>
more.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For we that live to <qex>please</qex>, must <qex>please</qex> to
live.</q> <rj><qau>Johnson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have pleasure; to be willing, as a matter of
affording pleasure or showing favor; to vouchsafe; to consent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heavenly stranger, <qex>please</qex> to taste<br/
These bounties.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That he would <qex>please</qex> 8give me my liberty.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleased</ent><br/
<hw>Pleased</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Experiencing
pleasure.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pleas"ed*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Pleas"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleaseman</ent><br/
<hw>Please"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An officious person
who courts favor servilely; a pickthank.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleaser</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pleases or
gratifies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasing</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Giving pleasure or satisfaction;
causing agreeable emotion; agreeable; delightful; <as>as, a
<ex>pleasing</ex> prospect; <ex>pleasing</ex> manners.</as></def>
<ldquo/<xex>Pleasing</xex> harmony.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Pleasing</xex> features.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pleas"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pleas"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Gratifying; delightful; agreeable. See
<er>Pleasant</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasing</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An object of pleasure.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasurable</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ur*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
affording pleasure or satisfaction; gratifying; abounding in
pleasantness or pleasantry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Planting of orchards is very . . . <qex>pleasurable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, sir, you are very <qex>pleasurable</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pleas"ur*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pleas"ur*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasure</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plaisir</ets>, originally an infinitive. See
<er>Please</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The gratification of the senses
or of the mind; agreeable sensations or emotions; the excitement,
relish, or happiness produced by the expectation or the enjoyment of
something good, delightful, or satisfying; -- opposed to
<ant>pain</ant>, <ant>sorrow</ant>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At thy right hand there are <qex>pleasures</qex> for evermore.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xvi. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Amusement; sport; diversion; self-indulgence;
frivolous or dissipating enjoyment; hence, sensual gratification; --
opposed to <xex>labor</xex>, <xex>service</xex>, <xex>duty</xex>,
<xex>self-denial</xex>, etc.</def> <ldquo/Not sunk in carnal
<xex>pleasure</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that loveth <qex>pleasure</qex> shall be a poor man.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xxi. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lovers of <qex>pleasures</qex> more than lovers of God.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Tim. iii. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>What the will dictates or prefers as gratifying or
satisfying; hence, will; choice; wish; purpose.</def> <ldquo/He will do
his <xex>pleasure</xex> on Babylon.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Isa. xlviii.
14.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Use your <qex>pleasure</qex>; if your love do not presuade you to
come, let not my letter.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which pleases; a favor; a gratification.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Festus, willing to do the Jews a <qex>pleasure</qex></q>
<rj><qau>Acts xxv. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At pleasure</b></col>, <cd>by arbitrary will or
choice.</cd> <au>Dryden.</au> -- <col><b>To take pleasure in</b></col>,
<cd>to have enjoyment in.</cd> <au>Ps. cxlvii. 11.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pleasure</xex> is used adjectively, or in the
formation of self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>pleasure</xex> boat,
<xex>pleasure</xex> ground; <xex>pleasure</xex> house, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Enjoyment; gratification; satisfaction; comfort;
solace; joy; gladness; delight; will; choice; preference; purpose;
command; favor; kindness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasure</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ure</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pleasured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pleasuring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To give or afford pleasure to;
to please; to gratify.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Rolled] his hoop to <qex>pleasure</qex> Edith.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasure</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ure</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take pleasure; to seek
pursue pleasure; <as>as, to go <ex>pleasuring</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasureful</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ure*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affording
pleasure.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasureless</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ure*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Devoid of pleasure.</def>
<rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasurer</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ur*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pleasure
seeker.</def> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleasurist</ent><br/
<hw>Pleas"ur*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person devoted to worldly
pleasure.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleat</ent><br/
<hw>Pleat</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/t)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Plait</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebe</ent><br/
<hw>Plebe</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pl<egrave/be</ets>, fr. L. <ets>plebs</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The common people; the mob.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>plebe</qex> with thirst and fury prest.</q>
<rj><qau>Sylvester.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Plebeian</er>.]</ety> <def>A member of the
lowest class in the military academy at West Point; also, a freshman at
any military or naval academy.</def> <mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebeian</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*be"ian</hw> <pr>(pl<esl/*b<emac/"y<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>plebeius</ets>, from <ets>plebs</ets>, <ets>plebis</ets>,
the common people: cf. F. <ets>pl<eacute/b<eacute/ien</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Roman <xex>plebs</xex>, or
common people.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the common people; vulgar;
common; <as>as, <ex>plebeian</ex> sports; a <ex>plebeian</ex>
throng.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebeian</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*be"ian</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the
<membof>plebs</membof>, or common people of ancient Rome, in distinction
from <contr>patrician</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the common people, or lower rank of
men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebeiance</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*be"iance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Plebeianism.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plebeians, collectively.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebeianism</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*be"ian*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pl<eacute/b<eacute/ianisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being plebeian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The conduct or manners of plebeians;
vulgarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebeianize</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*be"ian*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Plebeianized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plebeianizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To render plebeian, common,
or vulgar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebicolist</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*bic"o*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plebs</ets> the common people + <ets>colere</ets> to
cultivate.]</ety> <def>One who flatters, or courts the favor of, the
common people; a demagogue.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebification</ent><br/
<hw>Pleb`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plebs</ets> the common people + <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to
make. See <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <def>A rendering plebeian; the act of
vulgarizing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You begin with the attempt to popularize learning . . . but you
will end in the <qex>plebification</qex> of knowledge.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebiscitary</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*bis"ci*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to plebiscite.</def> <rj><au>The Century.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebiscite</ent><br/
<hw>Pleb"i*scite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pl<eacute/biscite</ets>, fr. L. <ets>plebiscitum</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A vote by universal male suffrage; especially, in France, a popular
vote, as first sanctioned by the National Constitution of 1791.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>plebiscit</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Plebiscite</qex> we have lately taken, in popular use, from
the French.</q> <rj><qau>Fitzed. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebiscitum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ple`bis*ci"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>plebs</ets>, <ets>plebis</ets>, common people + <ets>scitum</ets>
decree.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A law enacted by the common
people, under the superintendence of a tribune or some subordinate
plebeian magistrate, without the intervention of the senate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plebs</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plebs</hw> <pr>(pl<ecr/bz)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. Cf.
<er>Plebe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The commonalty of ancient Rome
who were citizens without the usual political rights; the plebeians; --
distinguished from the <contr>patricians</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the common people; the populace; -- construed
as a pl.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plectile</ent><br/
<hw>Plec"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plectilis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Woven; plaited.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1099 --></p>

<p><ent>Plectognath</ent><br/
<hw>Plec"tog*nath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Plectognathi.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>One of the Plectognathi.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plectognathi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plec*to"gna*thi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/ twisted (fr. <?/ to plait, twist) + <?/ jaw.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of fishes generally having the
maxillary bone united with the premaxillary, and the articular united
with the dentary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The upper jaw is immovably joined to the skull; the
ventral fins are rudimentary or wanting; and the body is covered with
bony plates, spines, or small rough ossicles, like shagreen. The order
includes the diodons, filefishes, globefishes, and
trunkfishes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plec-tognathous</ent><br/
<ent>Plectognathic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plec`tog*nath"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Plec-tog"na*thous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Plectognathi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plectospondyli</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plec`to*spon"dy*li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ plaited + <?/, <?/, a vertebra.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive suborder of fresh-water
physostomous fishes having the anterior vertebr<ae/ united and much
modified; the Eventognathi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plectospondylous</ent><br/
<hw>Plec`to*spon"dy*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Plectospondyli.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plectrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plec"trum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Plectra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. Plectrums <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/ anything to strike with, fr.<?/ to strike.]</ety>
<def>A small instrument of ivory, wood, metal, or quill, used in playing
upon the lyre and other stringed instruments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pled</ent><br/
<hw>Pled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Plead</er></def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledge</ent><br/
<hw>Pledge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>plege</ets>,
<ets>pleige</ets>, pledge, guaranty, LL. <ets>plegium</ets>,
<ets>plivium</ets>; akin to OF. <ets>plevir</ets> to bail, guaranty,
perhaps fr. L. <ets>praebere</ets> to proffer, offer (<ets>sc.</ets>
<ets>fidem</ets> a trust, a promise of security), but cf. also E.
<ets>play</ets>. <root/28. Cf. <er>Prebend</er>,
<er>Replevin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The transfer
of possession of personal property from a debtor to a creditor as
security for a debt or engagement; also, the contract created between
the debtor and creditor by a thing being so delivered or deposited,
forming a species of bailment; also, that which is so delivered or
deposited; something put in pawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pledge</xex> is ordinarily confined to personal
property; the title or ownership does not pass by it; possession is
essential to it. In all these points it differs from a mortgage [see
<er>Mortgage</er>]; and in the last, from the <xex>hypotheca</xex> of
the Roman law. See <er>Hypotheca</er>.</note> <rj><au>Story.
Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Old Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A person who undertook, or
became responsible, for another; a bail; a surety; a hostage.</def>
<ldquo/I am Grumio's <xex>pledge</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A hypothecation without transfer of
possession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Anything given or considered as a security for the
performance of an act; a guarantee; <as>as, mutual interest is the best
<ex>pledge</ex> for the performance of treaties</as>.</def> <ldquo/That
voice, their liveliest <xex>pledge</xex> of hope.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A promise or agreement by which one binds one's self
to do, or to refrain from doing, something; especially, a solemn promise
in writing to refrain from using intoxicating liquors or the like;
<as>as, to sign the <ex>pledge</ex>; the mayor had made no
<ex>pledges</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- esp. in "take the pledge" --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A sentiment to which assent is given by drinking
one's health; a toast; a health.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dead pledge</b></col>. <ety>[A translation of
<er>LL</er>. <ets>mortuum vadium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>A
mortgage. See <er>Mortgage</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Living
pledge</b></col>. <ety>[A translation of LL. <ets>vivum
vadium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>The conveyance of an estate to
another for money borrowed, to be held by him until the debt is paid out
of the rents and profits.</cd> -- <col><b>To hold in pledge</b></col>,
<cd>to keep as security.</cd> -- <col><b>To put in pledge</b></col>,
<cd>to pawn; to give as security.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Earnest</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledge</ent><br/
<hw>Pledge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pledged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pledging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>pleiger</ets> to
give security. See <er>Pledge</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To deposit, as a chattel, in pledge or pawn; to leave in possession
of another as security; <as>as, <ex>to pledge</ex> one's
watch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give or pass as a security; to guarantee; to
engage; to plight; <as>as, to <ex>pledge</ex> one's word and
honor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We mutually <qex>pledge</qex> to each other our lives, our
fortunes, and our sacred honor.</q> <rj><qau>The Declaration of
Independence.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To secure performance of, as by a pledge.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>pledge</qex> my vow, I give my hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bind or engage by promise or declaration; to
engage solemnly; <as>as, to <ex>pledge</ex> one's self</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To invite another to drink, by drinking of the cup
first, and then handing it to him, as a pledge of good will; hence, to
drink the health of; to toast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pledge</qex> me, my friend, and drink till thou be'st
wise.</q> <rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledgee</ent><br/
<hw>Pledg*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The one to whom a
pledge is given, or to whom property pledged is delivered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledgeless</ent><br/
<hw>Pledge"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
pledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledgor</ent><br/
<ent>Pledgeor</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pledge*or"</hw>, <hw>Pledg*or"</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who pledges, or delivers
anything in pledge; a pledger; -- opposed to
<ant>pledgee</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word analogically requires the <xex>e</xex> after
<xex>g</xex>, but the spelling <xex>pledgor</xex> is perhaps
commoner.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledger</ent><br/
<hw>Pledg"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pledges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledgery</ent><br/
<hw>Pledg"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>pleigerie</ets>.]</ety> <def>A pledging; suretyship.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pledget</ent><br/
<hw>Pledg"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E., a small
plug.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small plug.</def> <mark>[Prov.
End.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A string of oakum used in
calking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A compress, or small flat tent of
lint, laid over a wound, ulcer, or the like, to exclude air, retain
dressings, or absorb the matter discharged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plegepoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ple*gep"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. (<?/) a stroke + <ets>-poda</ets>. In allusion to the rapid
strokes of the vibrating cilia.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Infusoria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleiad</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"iad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the
Pleiades.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleiades</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"ia*des</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. (<?/)]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Myth.)</fld> <def>The seven daughters
of Atlas and the nymph Pleione, fabled to have been made by Jupiter a
constellation in the sky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A group of small stars in the
neck of the constellation <partof>Taurus</partof>; -- called also
<altname>the seven sisters</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Job xxxviii.
31.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Alcyone, the brightest of these, a star of the third
magnitude, was considered by <person>M<aum/dler</person> the central
point around which our universe is revolving, but such a notion has been
thoroughly discounted by modern observations. Only six pleiads are
distinctly visible to the naked eye, whence the ancients supposed that a
sister had concealed herself out of shame for having loved a mortal,
Sisyphus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plein</ent><br/
<hw>Plein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Plan.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plein</ent><br/
<hw>Plein</hw>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To complain. See
<er>Plain</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plein</ent><br/
<hw>Plein</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. & F., fr. L.
<ets>plenus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Full; complete.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Plein</xex> remission.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Plein"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleiocene</ent><br/
<hw>Plei"o*cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pliocene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleiophyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Plei*oph"yl*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ more
+ <?/ leaf.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having several leaves; -- used
especially when several leaves or leaflets appear where normally there
should be only one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleiosaurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plei`o*sau"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pliosaurus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleistocene</ent><br/
<hw>Pleis"to*cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ most +
<?/ new.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the epoch,
or the deposits, following the Tertiary, and immediately preceding
man.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The Pleistocene epoch, or
deposits.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenal</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plenus</ets>
full. Cf. <er>Plenary</er>.]</ety> <def>Full; complete; <as>as, a
<ex>plenal</ex> view or act</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenarily</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"na*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plenary
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenariness</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"na*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
plenary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenarty</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"ar*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of a
benefice when occupied.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenary</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>plenarius</ets>, fr. L. <ets>plenus</ets> full. See
<er>Plenty</er>.]</ety> <def>Full; entire; complete; absolute; <as>as, a
<ex>plenary</ex> license; <ex>plenary</ex> authority.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A treatise on a subject should be <qex>plenary</qex> or full.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plenary indulgence</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>,
<cd>an entire remission of temporal punishment due to, or canonical
penance for, all sins.</cd> -- <col><b>Plenary inspiration</b></col>.
<fld>(Theol.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Inspiration</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenary</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Decisive
procedure.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plene</ent><br/
<hw>Plene</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plenus</ets> full.]</ety> <def>Full; complete; plenary.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenicorn</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"ni*corn</hw> <pr>(pl<emac/"n<icr/*k<ocir/rn)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>plenus</ets> full + <ets>cornu</ets> horn.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A ruminant having solid horns or antlers, as the
deer.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenilunary</ent><br/
<hw>Plen`i*lu"na*ry</hw>
<pr>(pl<ecr/n`<icr/*l<umac/"n<adot/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to the full moon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenilune</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"i*lune</hw> <pr>(pl<ecr/n"<icr/*l<umac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>plenilunium</ets>; <ets>plenus</ets> full +
<ets>luna</ets> the moon.]</ety> <def>The full moon.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenipotency</ent><br/
<ent>Plenipotence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ple*nip"o*tence</hw>
<pr>(pl<esl/*n<icr/p"<osl/*t<eit/ns)</pr>, <hw>Ple*nip"o*ten*cy</hw>
<pr>(pl<esl/*n<icr/p"<osl/*t<eit/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or state of being plenipotent.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenipotent</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*nip"o*tent</hw> <pr>(pl<esl/*n<icr/p"<osl/*t<eit/nt)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plenus</ets> full + <ets>potens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, potent.]</ety> <def>Possessing full power.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenipotentiary</ent><br/
<hw>Plen`i*po*ten"ti*a*ry</hw>
<pr>(pl<ecr/n"<icr/*p<osl/*t<ecr/n"sh<icr/*<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Plenipotentiaries</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL. <ets>plenipotentiarius</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pl<eacute/nipotentiaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>A person invested with
full power to transact any business; especially, an ambassador or envoy
to a foreign court, with full power to negotiate a treaty, or to
transact other business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenipotentiary</ent><br/
<hw>Plen`i*po*ten"ti*a*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing or
conferring full power; invested with full power; <as>as,
<ex>plenipotentiary</ex> license; <ex>plenipotentiary</ex>
ministers.</as></def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenish</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Replenish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To replenish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Reeve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To furnish; to stock, as a house or farm.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenishing</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"ish*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Household furniture;
stock.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenist</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plenus</ets>
full; cf. F. <ets>pl<eacute/niste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who holds that
all space is full of matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenitude</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plenitudo</ets>, fr. <ets>plenus</ets> full; cf. F.
<ets>plenitude</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
being full or complete; fullness; completeness; abundance; <as>as, the
<ex>plenitude</ex> of space or power</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Animal fullness; repletion; plethora.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenitudinarian</ent><br/
<hw>Plen`i*tu`di*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
plenist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenitudinary</ent><br/
<hw>Plen`i*tu"di*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
plenitude; full; complete; thorough.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenteous</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"te*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Plenty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Containing plenty; abundant;
copious; plentiful; sufficient for every purpose; <as>as, a
<ex>plenteous</ex> supply</as>.</def> <ldquo/Reaping
<xex>plenteous</xex> crop.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Yielding abundance; productive; fruitful.</def>
<ldquo/The seven <xex>plenteous</xex> years.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Gen. xli.
34.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having plenty; abounding; rich.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord shall make thee <qex>plenteous</qex> in goods.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. xxviii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Plentiful; copious; full. <er>See
Ample</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Plen"te*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Plen"te*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plentevous</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"te*vous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Plenteous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plentiful</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"ti*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Containing plenty; copious; abundant; ample; <as>as, a
<ex>plentiful</ex> harvest; a <ex>plentiful</ex> supply of
water.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Yielding abundance; prolific; fruitful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If it be a long winter, it is commonly a more <qex>plentiful</qex>
year.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Lavish; profuse; prodigal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that is <qex>plentiful</qex> in expenses will hardly be
preserved from</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Plen"ti*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Plen"ti*ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenty</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Plenties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, in <au>Shak.</au></plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>plentee</ets>, <ets>plente</ets>, OF. <ets>plent<eacute/</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>plenitas</ets>, fr. <ets>plenus</ets> full. See <er>Full</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Complete</er>.]</ety> <def>Full or adequate
supply; enough and to spare; sufficiency; specifically, abundant
productiveness of the earth; ample supply for human wants; abundance;
copiousness.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plenty</xex> of corn and wine.<rdquo/
<au>Gen. xxvii. 28.</au> <ldquo/Promises Britain peace and
<xex>plenty</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Houses of office stuffed with <qex>plentee</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The teeming clouds Descend in gladsome <qex>plenty</qex> o'er the
world.</q> <rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Abundance; exuberance. See
<er>Abundance</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenty</ent><br/
<hw>Plen"ty</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Plentiful; abundant.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If reasons were as <qex>plenty</qex> as blackberries.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak. (Folio ed.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those countries where shrubs are <qex>plenty</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plenum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ple"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>plenus</ets> full.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That state in which
every part of space is supposed to be full of matter; -- opposed to
<contr>vacuum</contr>.</def> <rj><au>G. Francis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Ventilation)</fld> <def>A condition, as in an
occupied room, in which the pressure of the air is greater than that of
the outside atmosphere; <as>as, a <ex>plenum</ex> may exist in a hall
ventilated by a fan blower</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleochroic</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`o*chro"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
property of pleochroism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleochroism</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*och"ro*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.<?/ mor<?/
+ <?/ color.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>The property possessed
by some crystals, of showing different colors when viewed in the
direction of different axes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleochromatic</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*och`ro*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pleochroic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleochromatism</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`o*chro"ma*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Pleochroism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleochroous</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*och"ro*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pleochroic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleomorphic</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`o*mor"phic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
pleomorphism; <as>as, the <ex>pleomorphic</ex> character of
bacteria</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleomorphism</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`o*mor"phism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ more
+ <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld>
<def>The property of crystallizing under two or more distinct
fundamental forms, including <xex>dimorphism</xex> and
<xex>trimorphism</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The theory that the various
genera of bacteria are phases or variations of growth of a number of
Protean species, each of which may exhibit, according to undetermined
conditions, all or some of the forms characteristic of the different
genera and species.</def><-- thoroughly discredited, except for a few
apparent examples of such a phenomenon. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleomorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`o*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
property of pleomorphism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleonasm</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"o*nasm</hw>, <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pleonasmus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to be more than enough, to
abound, fr.<?/, neut. of <?/, more, compar. of <?/ much. See
<er>Full</er>, a., and cf. <er>Poly-</er>, <er>Plus</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>Redundancy of language in speaking or writing;
the use of more words than are necessary to express the idea; <as>as, I
saw it <ex>with my own eyes</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleonast</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"o*nast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is addicted
to pleonasm.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>C. Reade.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleonaste</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"o*naste</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.<?/ abundant, rich; cf. F.
<ets>pl</ets><eacute/<ets>onaste</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
black variety of spinel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleonastical</ent><br/
<ent>Pleonastic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ple`o*nas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ple`o*nas"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pl<eacute/onastique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
pleonasm; of the nature of pleonasm; redundant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleonastically</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`o*nas"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pleonastic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleopod</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Pleopods</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Pleopoda</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to swim + <ets>-pod</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the abdominal legs of a crustacean. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Crustacea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plerome</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"rome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ that which
fills up, fr. <?/ to fill.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The central
column of parenchyma in a growing stem or root.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plerophory</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*roph"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/
full + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety> <def>Fullness; full
persuasion.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>plerophory</xex> of assurance.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesance</ent><br/
<hw>Ples"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pleasance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesh</ent><br/
<hw>Plesh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pool; a plash.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Spenser.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesimorphism</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`si*mor"phism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ near
+ <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>The
property possessed by some substances of crystallizing in closely
similar forms while unlike in chemical composition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesiomorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`si*o*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
alike in form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesiosaur</ent><br/
<hw>Ple"si*o*saur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>One of the Plesiosauria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesiosauria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ple`si*o*sau"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Plesiosaurus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct
order of Mesozoic marine reptiles including the genera Plesiosaurus, and
allied forms; -- called also
<altname><ord>Sauropterygia</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesiosaurian</ent><br/
<hw>Ple`si*o*sau"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A plesiosaur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plesiosaurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ple`si*o*sau"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Plesiosauri</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr <?/ near + <?/ a lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus
of large extinct marine reptiles, having a very long neck, a small head,
and paddles for swimming. It lived in the Mesozoic age.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plessimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Ples*sim"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pleximeter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plete</ent><br/
<hw>Plete</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To plead.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>P. Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethora</ent><br/
<hw>Pleth"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ to be or become full. Cf. <er>Pleonasm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Overfullness; especially, excessive fullness of the blood vessels;
repletion; that state of the blood vessels or of the system when the
blood exceeds a healthy standard in quantity; hyper<ae/mia; -- opposed
to <xex>an<ae/mia</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>State of being overfull; excess;
superabundance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He labors under a <qex>plethora</qex> of wit and imagination.</q>
<rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethoretic</ent><br/
<hw>Pleth`o*ret"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Plethoric.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethoric</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*thor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; cf. F.
<ets>pl<eacute/thorique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having a full habit of body;
characterized by plethora or excess of blood; <as>as, a
<ex>plethoric</ex> constitution</as>; -- used also metaphorically.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Plethoric</xex> phrases.<rdquo/ <au>Sydney Smith.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Plethoric</xex> fullness of thought.<rdquo/ <au>De
Quincey.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethorical</ent><br/
<hw>Ple*thor"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Plethoric.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Ple*thor"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethory</ent><br/
<hw>Pleth"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Plethora.</def>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethrum</ent><br/
<ent>Plethron</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pleth"ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pleth"rum</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Plethra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A long measure of 100 Greek, or 101
English, feet; also, a square measure of 10,000 Greek feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plethysmograph</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleth"ys*mo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
an enlargement + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An
instrument for determining and registering the variations in the size or
volume of a limb, as the arm or leg, and hence the variations in the
amount of blood in the limb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pleth`ys*mo*graph"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1100 --></p>

<p><ent>Plethysmography</ent><br/
<hw>Pleth`ys*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The study, by means of the plethysmograph, of
the variations in size of a limb, and hence of its blood
supply.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Pleuron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleura</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ra</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Pleur<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pleuras</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., <pos>n. fem.</pos>, fr. Gr. <?/ a rib,
the side.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
smooth serous membrane which closely covers the lungs and the adjacent
surfaces of the thorax; the pleural membrane.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The closed sac formed by the pleural membrane about each lung, or
the fold of membrane connecting each lung with the body wall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pleuron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleural</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the pleura or pleur<ae/, or to the sides of the
thorax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuralgia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu*ral"gi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ rib + <?/ pain.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pain in the side
or region of the ribs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurapophysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ra*poph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pleurapophyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pleura</er>, and <er>Apophysis</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of the ventral processes of a vertebra, or
the dorsal element in each half of a hemal arch, forming, or
corresponding to, a vertebral rib.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pleu*rap`o*phys"i*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurenchyma</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu*ren"chy*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ side
+ <?/, as in <ets>parenchyma</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
tissue consisting of long and slender tubular cells, of which wood is
mainly composed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuric</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Pleural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurisy</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ri*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pleur<eacute/sie</ets>, L. <ets>pleurisis</ets>,
<ets>pleuritis</ets>, Gr <grk>pleyri^tis</grk> (sc. <grk>no`sos</grk>),
fr. <grk>pleyra`</grk> rib, side.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An
inflammation of the pleura, usually accompanied with fever, pain,
difficult respiration, and cough, and with exudation into the pleural
cavity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pleurisy root</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The large tuberous root of a kind of milkweed (<spn>Asclepias
tuberosa</spn>) which is used as a remedy for pleuritic and other
diseases</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The plant itself, which has deep
orange-colored flowers; -- called also <altname>butterfly
weed</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurite</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pleuron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuritical</ent><br/
<ent>Pleuritic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pleu*rit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pleu*rit"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pleuriticus</ets>, Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>pleur<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to pleurisy; <as>as,
<ex>pleuritic</ex> symptoms</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Suffering from
pleurisy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuritis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu*ri"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pleurisy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuro-</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ro-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Pleura</er>.]</ety> <def>A
combining form denoting <xex>relation to a side</xex>; specif.,
<xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>situation in</xex> or
<xex>near</xex>, <xex>the pleura</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>pleuro</ex>peritoneum</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurobrachia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ro*brach"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pleuro-</er>, and <er>Brachium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A genus of ctenophores having an ovate body and two long plumose
tentacles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurobranch</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ro*branch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pleuro-</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of the gills of a crustacean that is attached to the side
of the thorax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurobranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ro*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pleuroeranchi<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pleurobranch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ro*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pleuro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any pleurocarpic moss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurocarpous</ent><br/
<ent>Pleurocarpic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pleu`ro*car"pic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pleu`ro*car"pous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Side-fruited;
-- said of those true mosses in which the pedicels or the capsules are
from lateral archegonia; -- opposed to <ant>acrocarpous</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurocentrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ro*cen"trum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. see
<er>Pleuro-</er>, and <er>Centrum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>One of the lateral elements in the centra of the vertebr<ae/ in
some fossil batrachians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuroderes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu*rod"e*res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ the side + <?/ the neck.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
group of fresh-water turtles in which the neck can not be retracted, but
is bent to one side, for protection. The <stype>matamata</stype> is an
example.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurodont</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ro*dont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pleuro-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, a tooth.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having the teeth consolidated with the inner
edge of the jaw, as in some lizards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurodont</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu"ro*dont</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any lizard
having pleurodont teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurodynia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ro*dyn"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ side + <?/ pain.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A painful
affection of the side, simulating pleurisy, usually due to
rheumatism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuron</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu"ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pleura</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
rib.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the sides of an
animal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the lateral pieces of a somite of
an insect.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of lateral processes of a somite
of a crustacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuronectoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu`ro*nec"toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Pleuronectes</ets>, name of a genus (fr. Gr. <?/ rib + <?/ a
swimmer) + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to
the <fam>Pleuronectid<ae/</fam>, or Flounder family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuropericardial</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu`ro*per`i*car"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pleura and
pericardium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuroperipneumony</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu`ro*per`ip*neu"mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pleuro-</ets> + <ets>peripneumony</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pleuropneumonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuroperitoneal</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu`ro*per`i*to*ne"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pleural and peritoneal
membranes or cavities, or to the pleuroperitoneum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuroperitoneum</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu`ro*per`i*to*ne"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pleuro-</ets> + <ets>peritoneum</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The pleural and peritoneal membranes, or the
membrane lining the body cavity and covering the surface of the inclosed
viscera; the peritoneum; -- used especially in the case of those animals
in which the body cavity is not divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Peritoneum</xex> is now often used in the sense of
<xex>pleuroperitoneum</xex>, the pleur<ae/ being regarded as a part of
the peritoneum, when the body cavity is undivided.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuropneumonia</ent><br/
<hw>Pleu`ro*pneu*mo"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pleuro-</ets> + <ets>pneumonia</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammation of the pleura and lungs; a
combination of pleurisy and pneumonia, esp. a kind of contagions and
fatal lung plague of cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleuroptera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu*rop"te*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ side + <?/ wing.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of
Isectivora, including the colugo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurosigma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ro*sig"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pleuro-</er>, and <er>Sigma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of diatoms of elongated elliptical shape, but having the sides
slightly curved in the form of a letter <universbold>S</universbold>.
<spn>Pleurosigma angulatum</spn> has very fine striations, and is a
favorite object for testing the high powers of microscopes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurosteon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu*ros"te*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> L. <plw>Pleurostea</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E.
<plw>-ons</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a rib + <?/ a
bone.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The antero-lateral piece which
articulates the sternum of birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurothotonus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu`ro*thot"o*nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>pleyro`qen</grk> from the side + <grk>to`nos</grk> a
stretching.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A species of tetanus, in which
the body is curved laterally.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au>
<au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleurotoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pleu*rot"o*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> L. <plw>Pleurotom<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E.
<plw>Pleurotomas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ the
side + <grk>tomh`</grk> a cut.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any marine
gastropod belonging to <gen>Pleurotoma</gen>, and ether allied genera of
the family <fam>Pleurotmid<ae/</fam>. The species are very numerous,
especially in tropical seas. The outer lip has usually a posterior notch
or slit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plevin</ent><br/
<hw>Plev"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>plevine</ets>. See <er>Replevin</er>.]</ety> <def>A warrant or
assurance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plexiform</ent><br/
<hw>Plex"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Plexus</ets>
+ <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>Plexiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Like
network; complicated.</def><-- sic. no det. -->
<rj><au>Quincy.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleximeter</ent><br/
<hw>Plex*im"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ stroke,
percussion (from <?/ to strike) + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A small, hard, elastic plate, as of ivory, bone,
or rubber, placed in contact with body to receive the blow, in
examination by mediate percussion.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>plexometer</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plexure</ent><br/
<hw>Plex"ure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Plexus</er>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of weaving together, or
interweaving; that which is woven together.</def> <rj><au>H.
Brooke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plexus</ent><br/
<hw>Plex"us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Plexus</plw>, E. <plw>Plexuses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.,
a twining, braid, fr. <ets>plectere</ets>, <ets>plexum</ets>, to twine,
braid.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A network of vessels,
nerves, or fibers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The system of equations required
for the complete expression of the relations which exist between a set
of quantities.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A network; an intricate or interwoven combination of
elements or parts in a coherent structure.</def></p>

<p><q>In the perception of a tree the reference to an object is
circumscribed and directed by a <qex>plexus</qex> of visual and other
presentations.</q> <rj><qau>G. F. Stout.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pley</ent><br/
<hw>Pley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Play</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pley</ent><br/
<hw>Pley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full See
<er>Plein</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pleyt</ent><br/
<hw>Pleyt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An
old term for a river boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliability</ent><br/
<hw>Pli`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being pliable; flexibility; <as>as, <ex>pliability</ex> of
disposition</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pliability</xex> of movement.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliable</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>plier</ets> to bend, to fold. See <er>Ply</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being plied, turned, or
bent; easy to be bent; flexible; pliant; supple; limber; yielding;
<as>as, willow is a <ex>pliable</ex> plant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Flexible in disposition; readily yielding to
influence, arguments, persuasion, or discipline; easy to be persuaded;
-- sometimes in a bad sense; <as>as, a <ex>pliable</ex>
youth</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pliable</xex> she promised to be.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pli"a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Pli"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"an*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being pliant in sense; <as>as, the <ex>pliancy</ex> of a
rod</as>.</def> <ldquo/Avaunt all specious <xex>pliancy</xex> of
mind.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliant</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pliant</ets>,
p. pr. of <ets>plier</ets> to bend. See <er>Ply</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of plying or bending;
readily yielding to force or pressure without breaking; flexible;
pliable; lithe; limber; plastic; <as>as, a <ex>pliant</ex> thread;
<ex>pliant</ex> wax</as>. Also used figuratively: Easily influenced for
good or evil; tractable; <as>as, a <ex>pliant</ex> heart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The will was then ductile and <qex>pliant</qex> to right
reason.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Favorable to pliancy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/A <xex>pliant</xex> hour.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pli"ant*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pli"ant*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plica</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pli"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., a fold, fr.
L. <ets>plicare</ets> to fold. See <er>Ply</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease of the hair (<xex>Plica
polonica</xex>), in which it becomes twisted and matted together. The
disease is of Polish origin, and is hence called also <altname>Polish
plait</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A diseased state in plants in
which there is an excessive development of small entangled twigs,
instead of ordinary branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The bend of the wing of a
bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plicated</ent><br/
<ent>Plicate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pli"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pli"ca*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plicatus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>plicare</ets> to fold.]</ety> <def>Plaited; folded like a fan;
<as>as, a <ex>plicate</ex> leaf</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pli"cate*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plication</ent><br/
<hw>Pli*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A folding or fold;
a plait.</def> <rj><au>Richardson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plicature</ent><br/
<hw>Plic"a*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plicatura</ets>, fr. <ets>plicare</ets> to fold.]</ety> <def>A
fold; a doubling; a plication.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plicidentine</ent><br/
<hw>Plic`i*den"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>plica</ets> fold + E. <ets>dentine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A form of dentine which shows sinuous lines of structure in a
transverse section of the tooth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plied</ent><br/
<hw>Plied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Ply</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliers</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"ers</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Ply</er>
to bend, fold.]</ety> <def>A kind of small pinchers with long jaws, --
used for bending or cutting metal rods or wire, for handling small
objects such as the parts of a watch, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliform</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Ply</ets> a
fold + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>In the form of a ply, fold, or
doubling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plight</ent><br/
<hw>Plight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> of <er>Plight</er>, to pledge.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plight</ent><br/
<hw>Plight</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Pluck</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plight</ent><br/
<hw>Plight</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pliten</ets>; probably
through Old French, fr. LL. <ets>plectare</ets>, L. <ets>plectere</ets>.
See <er>Plait</er>, <er>Ply</er>.]</ety> <def>To weave; to braid; to
fold; to plait.</def><mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To sew and
<xex>plight</xex>.<rdquo/<-- in the sense of fold, = pleat [plait 2 in
MW10]--> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>plighted</qex> garment of divers colors.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plight</ent><br/
<hw>Plight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A network; a plait; a
fold; rarely a garment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Many a folded
<xex>plight</xex>.<rdquo/<-- = pleat --> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plight</ent><br/
<hw>Plight</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pliht</ets> danger,
engagement, AS. <ets>pliht</ets> danger, fr. <ets>ple<oacute/n</ets> to
risk; akin to D. <ets>plicht</ets> duty, G. <ets>pflicht</ets>, Dan.
<ets>pligt</ets>. <root/28. Cf. <er>Play</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>That which is exposed to risk; that which is plighted or pledged;
security; a gage; a pledge.</def> <ldquo/That lord whose hand must take
my <xex>plight</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Perh. the same word as <ets>plight</ets> a pledge,
but at least influenced by OF. <ets>plite</ets>, <ets>pliste</ets>,
<ets>ploit</ets>, <ets>ploi</ets>, a condition, state; cf. E.
<ets>plight</ets> to fold, and F. <ets>pli</ets> a fold, habit,
<ets>plier</ets> to fold, E. <ets>ply</ets>.]</ety> <def>Condition;
state; -- risk, or exposure to danger, often being implied; <as>as, a
luckless <ex>plight</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/Your <xex>plight</xex> is
pitied.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To bring our craft all in another <qex>plight</qex></q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plight</ent><br/
<hw>Plight</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plighted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plighting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>plihtan</ets> to
expose to danger, <ets>pliht</ets> danger;cf. D. ver<ets>plichten</ets>
to oblige, engage, impose a duty, G. ver<ets>pflichten</ets>, Sw.
f<oum/r<ets>plikta</ets>, Dan. for<ets>pligte</ets>. See
<er>Plight</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pledge; to
give as a pledge for the performance of some act; as, to
<xex>plight</xex> faith, honor, word; -- never applied to property or
goods.</def> <ldquo/ To do them <xex>plighte</xex> their troth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>plighted</qex> his right hand<br/
Unto another love, and to another land.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here my inviolable faith I <qex>plight</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To promise; to engage; to betroth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before its setting hour, divide<br/
The bridegroom from the <qex>plighted</qex> bride.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plighter</ent><br/
<hw>Plight"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, plights.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plim</ent><br/
<hw>Plim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Plump</er>.]</ety> <def>To swell, as grain or wood with water.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plimsoll's mark</ent><br/
<hw>Plim"soll's mark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A mark
conspicuously painted on the port side of all British sea-going merchant
vessels, to indicate the limit of submergence allowed by law; -- so
called from Samuel <ets>Plimsoll</ets>, by whose efforts the act of
Parliament to prevent overloading was procured.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plinth</ent><br/
<hw>Plinth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plinthus</ets>, Gr. <?/ a brick or tile, a plinth, perh. akin to E.
<ets>flint</ets>: cf. F. <ets>plinthe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>In classical architecture, a vertically faced member immediately
below the circular base of a column; also, the lowest member of a
pedestal; hence, in general, the lowest member of a base; a sub-base; a
block upon which the moldings of an architrave or trim are stopped at
the bottom. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Column</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliocene</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"o*cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<ets>pleiocene</ets>.]</altsp> <ety>[Gr. <?/ more + <?/ new,
recent.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
characterizing, the most recent division of the Tertiary age.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliocene</ent><br/
<hw>Pli"o*cene</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The Pliocene
period or deposits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliohippus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pli`o*hip"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. E.
<ets>plio</ets>cene + Gr. <?/ horse.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An
extinct genus of horses from the Pliocene deposits. Each foot had a
single toe (or hoof), as in the common horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pliosaurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pli`o*sau"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ greater + <?/ lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An
extinct genus of marine reptiles allied to Plesiosaurus, but having a
much shorter neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plitt</ent><br/
<hw>Plitt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>plete</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument of punishment or torture
resembling the knout, used in Russia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploc</ent><br/
<hw>Ploc</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A mixture of hair and tar for covering the
bottom of a ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploce</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plo"ce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/
complication, fr. <?/ to entwine.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A
figure in which a word is separated or repeated by way of emphasis, so
as not only to signify the individual thing denoted by it, but also its
peculiar attribute or quality; <as>as, <ldquo/His <ex>wife</ex>'s a
<ex>wife</ex> indeed</as>.<rdquo/</def> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plod</ent><br/
<hw>Plod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plodded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plodding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gf. Gael. <ets>plod</ets> a
clod, a pool; also, to strike or pelt with a clod or clods.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To travel slowly but steadily; to trudge.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To toil; to drudge; especially, to study laboriously
and patiently.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plodding</xex> schoolmen.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plod</ent><br/
<hw>Plod</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To walk on slowly or
heavily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ploughman homeward <qex>plods</qex> his weary way.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plodder</ent><br/
<hw>Plod"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plods; a
drudge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plodding</ent><br/
<hw>Plod"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Progressing in a
slow, toilsome manner; characterized by laborious diligence; <as>as, a
<ex>plodding</ex> peddler; a <ex>plodding</ex> student; a man of
<ex>plodding</ex> habits.</as></def> --<wordforms><wf>Plod"ding*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plonge</ent><br/
<hw>Plonge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Plunge</er>.]</ety> <def>To cleanse, as open drains which are
entered by the tide, by stirring up the sediment when the tide
ebbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plongee</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plon`g<eacute/e"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Plunge</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A slope or sloping toward
the front; <as>as, the <ex>plong<eacute/e</ex> of a parapet; the
<ex>plong<eacute/e</ex> of a shell in its course.</as></def>
<altsp>[Sometimes written <asp>plonge</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plop</ent><br/
<hw>Plop</hw> <pr>(pl<ocr/p)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Plopped</conjf> <pr>(pl<ocr/pt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Plopping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Imitative.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall, drop, or move in any way, with a sudden splash
or slap, as on the surface of water.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q>The body <qex>plopped</qex> up, turning on its side.</q>
<rj><qau>Kipling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>plop</ent><br/
<hw>plop</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to drop (something) so
that it makes a plopping sound; especially, to drop with the sound of
something falling into water.</def> <wns>[wns=1, 2]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set down quickly, so as to make a sound; -- used
often with the reflexive; <as>as, He <ex>plopped</ex> himself into the
sofa</as>.</def> <wns>[wns=3]</wns><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> plank, flump, plonk, plunk, plump down, plunk down,
plump.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plop</ent><br/
<hw>Plop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of plopping; the sound made in
plopping.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plot</ent><br/
<hw>Plot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>plot</ets>; cf.
Goth. <ets>plats</ets> a patch. Cf. <er>Plat</er> a piece of
ground.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small extent of ground; a plat;
<as>as, a garden <ex>plot</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A plantation laid out.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Surv.)</fld> <def>A plan or draught of a field,
farm, estate, etc., drawn to a scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plot</ent><br/
<hw>Plot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plotted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plotting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a plot, map, pr plan,
of; to mark the position of on a plan; to delineate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> This treatise <qex>plotteth</qex> down Cornwall as it now
standeth.</q> <rj><qau>Carew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plot</ent><br/
<hw>Plot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. from
<ets>complot</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any scheme, stratagem,
secret design, or plan, of a complicated nature, adapted to the
accomplishment of some purpose, usually a treacherous and mischievous
one; a conspiracy; an intrigue; <as>as, the Rye-house
<ex>Plot</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have overheard a <qex>plot</qex> of death.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, think what anxious moments pass between<br/
The birth of <qex>plots</qex> and their last fatal periods!</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A share in such a plot or scheme; a participation in
any stratagem or conspiracy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And when Christ saith, Who marries the divorced commits adultery,
it is to be understood, if he had any <qex>plot</qex> in the
divorce.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Contrivance; deep reach of thought; ability to plot
or intrigue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A man of much
<xex>plot</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Denham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A plan; a purpose.</def> <ldquo/No other
<xex>plot</xex> in their religion but serve God and save their
souls.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>In fiction, the story of a play, novel, romance, or
poem, comprising a complication of incidents which are gradually
unfolded, sometimes by unexpected means.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the <qex>plot</qex> or intrigue must be natural, and such as
springs from the subject, then the winding up of the <qex>plot</qex>
must be a probable consequence of all that went before.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Intrigue; stratagem; conspiracy; cabal;
combination; contrivance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1101 --></p>

<p><ent>Plot</ent><br/
<hw>Plot</hw> <pr>(pl<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
form a scheme of mischief against another, especially against a
government or those who administer it; to conspire.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wicked <qex>plotteth</qex> against the just.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
xxxvii. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To contrive a plan or stratagem; to
scheme.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The prince did <qex>plot</qex> to be secretly gone.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plot</ent><br/
<hw>Plot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To plan; to scheme; to devise; to
contrive secretly.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plotting</xex> an unprofitable
crime.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Plotting</xex> now the fall
of others.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plotful</ent><br/
<hw>Plot"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding with
plots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plotinian</ent><br/
<hw>Plo*tin"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><def>Of pertaining to
the Plotinists or their doctrines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plotinist</ent><br/
<hw>Plo*ti"nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>A disciple of <persfn>Plotinus</persfn>, a celebrated
Platonic philosopher of the third century, who taught that the human
soul emanates from the divine Being, to whom it reunited at
death.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plot-proof</ent><br/
<hw>Plot"-proof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Secure against
harm by plots.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plotter</ent><br/
<hw>Plot"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plots or
schemes; a contriver; a conspirator; a schemer.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plough</ent><br/
<hw>Plough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Plow</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plouter</ent><br/
<hw>Plout"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <altsp>[Also
<asp>plowter</asp>, <asp>plotter</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Perh.
imitative.]</ety> <def>To wade or move about with splashing; to dabble;
also, to potter; trifle; idle.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Dial.
Eng.]</mark></p>

<p><q>I did not want to <qex>plowter</qex> about any more.</q>
<rj><qau>Kipling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plouter</ent><br/
<hw>Plout"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Also <ets>plowter</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Act of ploutering; floundering; act or sound of splashing.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Dial.Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plover</ent><br/
<hw>Plov"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>plovier</ets>, F. <ets>pluvier</ets>, prop., the rain bird, fr. LL.
(assumed) <ets>pluviarius</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pluvia</ets> rain, from
<ets>pluere</ets> to rain; akin to E. <ets>float</ets>, G.
<ets>fliessen</ets> to flow. See <er>Float</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of limicoline birds
belonging to the family <fam>Charadrid<ae/</fam>, and especially those
belonging to the subfamily <fam>Charadrins<ae/</fam>. They are prized as
game birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any grallatorial bird allied to,
or resembling, the true plovers, as the crab plover (<spn>Dromas
ardeola</spn>); the American upland, plover (<spn>Bartramia
longicauda</spn>); and other species of sandpipers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Among the more important species are the
<stype>blackbellied plover</stype> or <stype>blackbreasted
plover</stype> (<spn>Charadrius squatarola</spn>) of America and Europe;
-- called also <altname>gray plover</altname>, <altname>bull-head
plover</altname>, <altname>Swiss plover</altname>, <altname>sea
plover</altname>, and <altname>oxeye</altname>; the <stype>golden
plover</stype> (see under <er>Golden</er>); the <stype>ring
plover</stype> or <stype>ringed plover</stype> (<spn>Aegialitis
hiaticula</spn>). See <er>Ringneck</er>. The <stype>piping
plover</stype> (<spn>Aegialitis meloda</spn>); <stype>Wilson's
plover</stype> (<spn>Aegialitis Wilsonia</spn>); the <stype>mountain
plover</stype> (<spn>Aegialitis montana</spn>); and the
<stype>semipalmated plover</stype> (<spn>Aegialitis semipalmata</spn>),
are all small American species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bastard plover</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
lapwing.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Long-legged plover</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>yellow-legged plover</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See
<er>Tattler</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plover's page</b></col>, <cd>the
dunlin.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>Rock
plover</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Stone plover</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>the black-bellied plover.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Whistling plover</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The golden
plover</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The black-bellied plover.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plough</ent><br/
<ent>Plow</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow</hw>, <hw>Plough</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(plou)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>plouh</ets>, <ets>plou</ets>, AS.
<ets>pl<omac/h</ets>; akin to D. <ets>ploeg</ets>, G. <ets>pflug</ets>,
OHG. <ets>pfluog</ets>, <ets>pfluoh</ets>, Icel. <ets>pl<omac/gr</ets>,
Sw. <ets>plog</ets>, Dan. <ets>ploug</ets>, <ets>plov</ets>, Russ.
<ets>plug'</ets>, Lith. <ets>plugas</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
well-known implement, drawn by horses, mules, oxen, or other power, for
turning up the soil to prepare it for bearing crops; also used to furrow
or break up the soil for other purposes; <as>as, the subsoil
<ex>plow</ex>; the draining <ex>plow</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where fern succeeds ungrateful to the <qex>plow</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Agriculture; husbandry.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A carucate of land; a plowland.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Johan, mine eldest son, shall have <qex>plowes</qex> five.</q>
<rj><qau>Tale of Gamelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A joiner's plane for making grooves; a grooving
plane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bookbinding)</fld> <def>An implement for trimming
or shaving off the edges of books.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Charles's
Wain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ice plow</b></col>, <cd>a plow used for cutting ice on
rivers, ponds, etc.</cd>, into cakes suitable for storing. <mark>[U.
S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Mackerel plow</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Mackerel</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plow alms</b></col>, <cd>a penny
formerly paid by every plowland to the church.</cd> <au>Cowell.</au> --
<col><b>Plow beam</b></col>, <cd>that part of the frame of a plow to
which the draught is applied. See <er>Beam</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 9.</cd>
-- <col><b>Plow Monday</b></col>, <cd>the Monday after Twelth Day, or
the end of Christmas holidays.</cd> -- <col><b>Plow staff</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A kind of long-handled spade or paddle for cleaning the
plowshare; a paddle staff</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A plow handle.</cd> --
<col><b>Snow plow</b></col>, <cd>a structure, usually <LAMBDA/-shaped,
for removing snow from sidewalks, railroads, etc., -- drawn or driven by
a horse or a locomotive.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plough</ent><br/
<ent>Plow</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow</hw>, <hw>Plough</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Plowed</conjf> <pr>(ploud)</pr>
or <conjf>Ploughed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plowing</conjf> or <conjf>Ploughing</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To turn up, break up, or trench, with a plow; to till
with, or as with, a plow; <as>as, to <ex>plow</ex> the ground; to
<ex>plow</ex> a field.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To furrow; to make furrows, grooves, or ridges in;
to run through, as in sailing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let patient Octavia <qex>plow</qex> thy visage up<br/
With her prepared nails.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With speed we <qex>plow</qex> the watery way.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bookbinding)</fld> <def>To trim, or shave off the
edges of, as a book or paper, with a plow. See <er>Plow</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>To cut a groove in, as in a
plank, or the edge of a board; especially, a rectangular groove to
receive the end of a shelf or tread, the edge of a panel, a tongue,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To plow in</b></col>, <cd>to cover by plowing; <as>as,
<ex>to plow in</ex> wheat</as>.</cd><-- ## = plow under --> --
<col><b>To plow up</b></col>, <cd>to turn out of the ground by
plowing.</cd></cs> <-- <col><b>plow ahead</b></col>, <cd>to continue in
spite of obstacles or resistence by others. Often used in a bad sense,
meaning to continue obstinately in spite of the contrary advice of
others.</cd> -- <col><b>plow through</b></col>, <cd>to execute a
difficult or laborious task steadily, esp. one containing many parts;
as, he plowed through the stack of correspondence until all had been
answered.</cd> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plough</ent><br/
<ent>Plow</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow</hw>, <hw>Plough</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(plou)</pr>, <pos>v.
i.</pos> <def>To labor with, or as with, a plow; to till or turn up the
soil with a plow; to prepare the soil or bed for anything.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Doth the plowman <qex>plow</qex> all day to sow ?</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxviii. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughable</ent><br/
<ent>Plowable</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"a*ble</hw>, <hw>Plough"a*ble</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being plowed; arable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughbote</ent><br/
<ent>Plowbote</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"bote`</hw>, <hw>Plough"bote`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>Wood or timber allowed to a
tenant for the repair of instruments of husbandry. See
<er>Bote</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughboy</ent><br/
<ent>Plowboy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"boy`</hw>, <hw>Plough"boy`</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A boy that drives or guides a team in plowing; a young
rustic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plougher</ent><br/
<ent>Plower</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"er</hw>, <hw>Plough"er</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plows; a plowman; a cultivator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughfoot</ent><br/
<ent>Plowfoot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"foot`</hw>, <hw>Plough"foot`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>An adjustable staff formerly attached to the plow
beam to determine the depth of the furrow.</def> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughgang</ent><br/
<ent>Plowgang</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"gang`</hw>, <hw>Plough"gang`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Plowgate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughgate</ent><br/
<ent>Plowgate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"gate`</hw>, <hw>Plough"gate`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The Scotch equivalent of the English word
<altname>plowland</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not having one <qex>plowgate</qex> of land.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughhead</ent><br/
<ent>Plowhead</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"head`</hw>, <hw>Plough"head`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The clevis or draught iron of a plow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plougland</ent><br/
<ent>Plowland</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"land`</hw>, <hw>Ploug"land`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Land that is plowed, or suitable for
tillage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>the quantity of land
allotted for the work of one plow; a hide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughman</ent><br/
<ent>Plowman</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"man</hw>, <hw>Plough"man</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-men</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who plows, or who holds and guides a plow; hence, a
husbandman.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer. Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rustic; a countryman; a field laborer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plowman's spikenard</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
European composite weed (<spn>Conyza squarrosa</spn>), having fragrant
roots.</cd> <rj><au>Dr. Prior.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughpoint</ent><br/
<ent>Plowpoint</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"point`</hw>, <hw>Plough"point`</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A detachable share at the extreme front
end of the plow body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughshare</ent><br/
<ent>Plowshare</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"share`</hw>, <hw>Plough"share"</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The share of a plow, or that part which
cuts the slice of earth or sod at the bottom of the furrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plowshare bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
pygostyle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughtail</ent><br/
<ent>Plowtail</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"tail`</hw>, <hw>Plough"tail`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The hind part or handle of a plow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploughwright</ent><br/
<ent>Plowwright</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plow"wright`</hw>, <hw>Plough"wright`</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes or repairs
plows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploy</ent><br/
<hw>Ploy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sport; frolic.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ploy</ent><br/
<hw>Ploy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Prob. abbrev. fr.
<ets>deploy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To form a column from a
line of troops on some designated subdivision; -- the opposite of
<xex>deploy</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Wilhelm.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ployment</ent><br/
<hw>Ploy"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>The act or movement of forming a column from a line of troops on
some designated subdivision; -- the opposite of
<xex>deployment</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluck</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plucked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plucking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>pluccian</ets>; akin
to LG. & D. <ets>plukken</ets>, G. <ets>pfl<uum/cken</ets>, Icel.
<ets>plokka</ets>, <ets>plukka</ets>, Dan. <ets>plukke</ets>, Sw.
<ets>plocka</ets>. <?/27.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pull; to
draw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Its own nature . . . <qex>plucks</qex> on its own dissolution.</q>
<rj><qau>Je<?/. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, to pull with sudden force or effort, or
to pull off or out from something, with a twitch; to twitch; also, to
gather, to pick; <as>as, to <ex>pluck</ex> feathers from a fowl; to
<ex>pluck</ex> hair or wool from a skin; to <ex>pluck</ex>
grapes.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I come to <qex>pluck</qex> your berries harsh and crude.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>E'en children followed, with endearing wile,<br/
And <qex>plucked</qex> his gown to share the good man's smile.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To strip of, or as of, feathers; <as>as, to
<ex>pluck</ex> a fowl</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They which pass by the way do <qex>pluck</qex> her.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. lxxx.<?/2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Universities)</fld> <def>To reject at an
examination for degrees.</def> <rj><au>C. Bront<eacute/.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pluck away</b></col>, <cd>to pull away, or to separate
by pulling; to tear away.</cd> -- <col><b>To pluck down</b></col>,
<cd>to pull down; to demolish; to reduce to a lower state.</cd> --
<col><b>to pluck off</b></col>, <cd>to pull or tear off; <as>as, <ex>to
pluck off</ex> the skin</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>to pluck up</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To tear up by the roots or from the foundation; to
eradicate; to exterminate; to destroy; <as>as, <ex>to pluck up</ex> a
plant; <ex>to pluck up</ex> a nation</as></cd>. <au>Jer. xii. 17.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To gather up; to summon; <as>as, <ex>to pluck up</ex>
courage</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluck</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a motion of pulling or
twitching; -- usually with <xex>at</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>pluck</ex> at
one's gown</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluck</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of plucking; a
pull; a twitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Prob. so called as being <ets>plucked</ets> out
after the animal is killed; or cf. Gael. & Ir. <ets>pluc</ets> a lump, a
knot, a bunch.]</ety> <def>The heart, liver, and lights of an
animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Spirit; courage; indomitable resolution;
fortitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Decay of English spirit, decay of manly <qex>pluck</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of plucking, or the state of being plucked,
at college. See <er>Pluck</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The lyrie.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plucked</ent><br/
<hw>Plucked</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having courage and
spirit.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plucker</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that
which, plucks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou setter up and <qex>plucker</qex> down of kings.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A machine for straightening and cleaning
wool.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plucker tube</ent><br/
<ent>Pluecker tube</ent><br/
<hw>Pl<uum/ck"er tube</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So named after
<person>Julius <etsep>Pl<uum/cker</etsep></person>, a German
physicist.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A vacuum tube,
used in spectrum analysis, in which the part through which the discharge
takes place is a capillary tube, thus producing intense incandescence of
the contained gases.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Crookes tube.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluckily</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plucky
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluckiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
plucky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluckless</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without pluck; timid;
faint-hearted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plucky</ent><br/
<hw>Pluck"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Pluckier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Pluckiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Having pluck or courage;
characterized by pluck; displaying pluck; courageous; spirited; <as>as,
a <ex>plucky</ex> race</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you're <qex>plucky</qex>, and not over subject to fright.</q>
<rj><qau>Barham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluff</ent><br/
<hw>Pluff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Prob. of imitative
origin.]</ety> <def>To throw out, as smoke, dust, etc., in puffs.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluff</ent><br/
<hw>Pluff</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A puff, as of smoke from
a pipe, or of dust from a puffball; a slight explosion, as of a small
quantity of gunpowder.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hairdresser's powder puff; also, the act of using
it.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plug</ent><br/
<hw>Plug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to D.
<ets>plug</ets>, G. <ets>pflock</ets>, Dan. <ets>pl<oum/k</ets>,
<ets>plug</ets>, Sw. <ets>plugg</ets>; cf. W. <ets>ploc</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Any piece of wood, metal, or other substance used to
stop or fill a hole; a stopple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A flat oblong cake of pressed tobacco.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A high, tapering silk hat.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A worthless horse.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Building)</fld> <def>A block of wood let into a
wall, to afford a hold for nails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An act of plugging{6}; a brief mention for the sake
of publicity or advertisement, especially during a public event not
specifically intended for advertising purposes; <as>as, he put in a
<ex>plug</ex> for his favorite charity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Breech plug</b></col> <fld>(Gun.)</fld>, <cd>in
breech-loading guns, the metal plug or cylinder which closes the
aperture in the breech, through which the gun is loaded.</cd> --
<col><b>Fire plug</b></col>, <cd>a street hydrant to which hose may be
attached.</cd> <mark>[U. S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Hawse plug</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a plug to stop a hawse hole.</cd> --
<col><b>Plug and feather</b></col>. <fld>(Stone Working)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Feather</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 7.</cd> -- <col><b>Plug
centerbit</b></col>, <cd>a centerbit ending in a small cylinder instead
of a point, so as to follow and enlarge a hole previously made, or to
form a counterbore around it.</cd> -- <col><b>Plug rod</b></col>
<fld>(Steam Eng.)</fld> <cd>, a rod attached to the beam for working the
valves, as in the Cornish engine.</cd> -- <col><b>Plug valve</b></col>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a tapering valve, which turns in a case like the
<xex>plug</xex> of a faucet.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plug</ent><br/
<hw>Plug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plugged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plugging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To stop with a
plug; to make tight by stopping a hole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To briefly publicize or advertise, especially during
a public event not specifically intended for advertising purposes;
<as>as, during the interview he <ex>plugged</ex> his new
book</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> put in a plug for.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plug board</ent><br/
<hw>Plug board</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A switchboard in which
connections are made by means of plugs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plugger</ent><br/
<hw>Plug"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, plugs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plugging</ent><br/
<hw>Plug"ging</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stopping
with a plug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The material of which a plug or stopple is
made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plum</ent><br/
<hw>Plum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>pl<umac/me</ets>, fr. L. <ets>prunum</ets>; akin to Gr. <?/, <?/.
Cf. <er>Prune</er> a dried plum.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The edible drupaceous fruit of the
<spn>Prunus domestica</spn>, and of several other species of
<gen>Prunus</gen>; also, the tree itself, usually called <altname>plum
tree</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bullace, the damson, and the numerous varieties of
<qex>plum</qex>, of our gardens, although growing into thornless trees,
are believed to be varieties of the blackthorn, produced by long
cultivation.</q> <rj><qau>G. Bentham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- the types marked <col><b>...</b></col> are in bold format, like
collocations. --> <note><hand/ Two or three hundred varieties of plums
derived from the <spn>Prunus domestica</spn> are described; among them
the <stype>greengage</stype>, the <stype>Orleans</stype>, the
<stype>purple gage</stype>, or <stype>Reine Claude Violette</stype>, and
the <stype>German prune</stype>, are some of the best known.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ Among the true plums are; <col><b>Beach plum</b></col>,
the <spn>Prunus maritima</spn>, and its crimson or purple globular
drupes, -- <col><b>Bullace plum</b></col>. See <er>Bullace</er>. --
<col><b>Chickasaw plum</b></col>, the American <spn>Prunus
Chicasa</spn>, and its round red drupes. -- <col><b>Orleans
plum</b></col>, a dark reddish purple plum of medium size, much grown in
England for sale in the markets. -- <col><b>Wild plum of
America</b></col>, <spn>Prunus Americana</spn>, with red or yellow
fruit, the original of the <stype>Iowa plum</stype> and several other
varieties.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>Among plants called <xex>plum</xex>, but of other genera than
<gen>Prunus</gen>, are; <col><b>Australian plum</b></col>,
<spn>Cargillia arborea</spn> and <spn>Cargillia australis</spn>, of the
same family with the persimmon. -- <col><b>Blood plum</b></col>, the
West African <spn>H<ae/matostaphes Barteri</spn>. -- <col><b>Cocoa
plum</b></col>, the Spanish nectarine. See under <er>Nectarine</er>. --
<col><b>Date plum</b></col>. See under <er>Date</er>. --
<col><b>Gingerbread plum</b></col>, the West African <spn>Parinarium
macrophyllum</spn>. -- <col><b>Gopher plum</b></col>, the Ogeechee lime.
-- <mcol><col><b>Gray plum</b></col>, <col><b>Guinea
plum</b></col></mcol>. See under <er>Guinea</er>. -- <col><b>Indian
plum</b></col>, several species of <gen>Flacourtia</gen>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A grape dried in the sun; a raisin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A handsome fortune or property; formerly, in cant
language, the sum of <pound/100,000 sterling; also, the person
possessing it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Something likened to a plum in desirableness; a good
or choice thing of its kind, as among appointments, positions, parts of
a book, etc.; <as>as, the mayor rewarded his cronies with cushy
<ex>plums</ex>, requiring little work for handsome pay</as></def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A color resembling that of a plum; a slightly
grayish deep purple, varying somewhat in its red or blue tint.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Plum bird</b></col>, <col><b>Plum
budder</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the European
bullfinch.</cd> -- <col><b>Plum gouger</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a weevil, or curculio (<spn>Coccotorus scutellaris</spn>), which
destroys plums. It makes round holes in the pulp, for the reception of
its eggs. The larva bores into the stone and eats the kernel.</cd> --
<col><b>Plum weevil</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American weevil
which is very destructive to plums, nectarines, cherries, and many other
stone fruits. It lays its eggs in crescent-shaped incisions made with
its jaws. The larva lives upon the pulp around the stone. Called also
<altname>turk</altname>, and <altname>plum curculio</altname>. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Curculio</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu"ma</hw> <pr>(pl<umac/"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Plum<ae/</plw> <pr>(pl<umac/"m<emac/)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumage</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"age</hw> <pr>(pl<umac/m"<asl/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
from <ets>plume</ets> a feather.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
entire clothing of a bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It consist of the <xex>contour feathers</xex>, or the
ordinary feathers covering the head, neck, and body; the <xex>tail
feathers</xex>, with their upper and lower coverts; the <xex>wing
feathers</xex>, including primaries, secondaries, and tertiaries, with
their coverts; and the <xex>down</xex> which lies beneath the contour
feathers. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Bird</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumassary</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*mas"sa*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plumasseau</ets>.]</ety> <def>A plume or collection of ornamental
feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumassier</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu`mas`sier"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>One who prepares or deals in ornamental plumes or
feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumb</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb</hw> <pr>(pl<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plomb</ets>, L. <ets>plumbum</ets> lead, a leaden ball or bullet;
cf. Gr. <grk>mo`lybos</grk>, <grk>mo`libos</grk>, <grk>mo`lybdos</grk>.
Cf. <er>Plummet</er>, <er>Plunge</er>.]</ety> <def>A little mass or
weight of lead, or the like, attached to a line, and used by builders,
etc., to indicate a vertical direction; a plummet; a plumb bob. See
<cref>Plumb line</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plumb bob</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Bob</er>, 4.</cd> --
<col><b>Plumb joint</b></col>, <cd>in sheet-metal work, a lap joint,
fastened by solder.</cd> -- <col><b>Plumb level</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Level</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plumb line</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The cord by which a plumb bob is suspended; a plummet</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A line directed to the center of gravity of the
earth.</cd> -- <col><b>Plumb rule</b></col>, <cd>a narrow board with a
plumb line, used by builders and carpenters.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumb</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Perpendicular; vertical; conforming
the direction of a line attached to a plumb; <as>as, the wall is
<ex>plumb</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumb</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plumb direction;
perpendicularly.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plumb</xex> down he falls.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumb</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plumbed</conjf> <pr>(pl<ucr/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plumbing</conjf> <pr>(pl<ucr/m"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To adjust by a plumb line; to cause to be
perpendicular; <as>as, to <ex>plumb</ex> a building or a
wall</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sound with a plumb or plummet, as the depth of
water; hence, to examine by test; to ascertain the depth, quality,
dimension, etc.; to sound; to fathom; to test.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He did not attempt to <qex>plumb</qex> his intellect.</q>
<rj><qau>Ld. Lytton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To seal with lead; <as>as, to <ex>plumb</ex> a
drainpipe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To supply, as a building, with a system of
plumbing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbage</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb"age</hw> <pr>(pl<ucr/m"<asl/j; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Leadwork</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbagin</ent><br/
<hw>Plum*ba"gin</hw> <pr>(pl<ucr/m*b<amac/"j<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>plumbago</ets> leadwort, fr. <ets>plumbum</ets> lead; cf.
F. <ets>plombagin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A crystalline
substance said to be found in the root of a certain plant of the
Leadwort (<gen>Plumbago</gen>) family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1102 --></p>

<p><ent>Plumbagineous</ent><br/
<hw>Plum`ba*gin"e*ous</hw>
<pr>(pl<ucr/m`b<adot/*j<icr/n"<esl/*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to natural order
(<ord>Plumbagine<ae/</ord>) of gamopetalous herbs, of which Plumbago is
the type. The order includes also the marsh rosemary, the thrift, and a
few other genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbaginous</ent><br/
<hw>Plum*bag"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling
plumbago; consisting of, or containing, plumbago; <as>as, a
<ex>plumbaginous</ex> slate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbago</ent><br/
<hw>Plum*ba"go</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from <ets>plumbum</ets>
lead.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Graphite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of herbaceous plants with
pretty salver-shaped corollas, usually blue or violet;
leadwort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbeous</ent><br/
<ent>Plumbean</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plum"be*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Plum"be*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plumbeus</ets>, from
<ets>plumbum</ets> the metal lead.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting
of, or resembling, lead.</def> <rj><au>J. Ellis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dull; heavy; stupid.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>J. P. Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumber</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plombier</ets>. See <er>Plumb</er>.]</ety> <def>One who works in
lead; esp., one who furnishes, fits, and repairs lead pipes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any worker who installs or
repairs piping and related equipment for conveyance of water, gas, or
drainage into or out of buildings, or within buildings to fixtures or
equipment using water. The type of material used for the conduits varies
with the application, and may be may be of lead, iron, copper, glass,
palstic, or other material.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumber block</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb"er block`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A pillow block.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbery</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>plomberie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The business of a
plumber.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place where plumbing is carried on; lead
works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbic</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"bic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Plumbum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to,
resembling, or containing, lead; -- used specifically to designate those
compounds in which it has a higher valence as contrasted with
<xex>plumbous</xex> compounds; <as>as, <ex>plumbic</ex>
oxide</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Plum*bif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Plumbum</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing or
containing lead.</def> <rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbing</ent><br/
<hw>Plumb"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art
of casting and working in lead, and applying it to building purposes;
especially, the business of furnishing, fitting, and repairing pipes for
conducting water, sewage, etc.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The lead or iron pipes, and other apparatus, used in
conveying water, sewage, etc., in a building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbism</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"bism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Plumbum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A diseased condition,
produced by the absorption of lead, common among workers in this metal
or in its compounds, as among painters, typesetters, etc. It is
characterized by various symptoms, as lead colic, lead line, and wrist
drop. See under <er>Colic</er>, <er>Lead</er>, and
<er>Wrist</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbous</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"bous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Plumbum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
containing, lead; -- used specifically to designate those compounds in
which it has a lower valence as contrasted with <xex>plumbic</xex>
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumbum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plum"bum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The technical name of lead. See
<er>Lead</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumcot</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"cot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Plum</ets> +
apri<ets>cot</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>A cross between the
plum and apricot.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plume</ent><br/
<hw>Plume</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>pluma</ets>. Cf. <er>Fly</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A feather; esp., a soft, downy feather, or a long,
conspicuous, or handsome feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wings . . . of many a colored <qex>plume</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An ornamental tuft of
feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A feather, or group of feathers, worn as an
ornament; a waving ornament of hair, or other material resembling
feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His high <qex>plume</qex>, that nodded o'er his head.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A token of honor or prowess; that on which one
prides himself; a prize or reward.</def> <ldquo/Ambitious to win from me
some <xex>plume</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A large and flexible panicle of
inflorescence resembling a feather, such as is seen in certain large
ornamental grasses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plume bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any bird
that yields ornamental plumes, especially the species of Epimarchus from
New Guinea, and some of the herons and egrets, as the white heron of
Florida (<spn>Ardea candidissima</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Plume
grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A kind of grass
(<spn>Erianthus saccharoides</spn>) with the spikelets arranged in great
silky plumes, growing in swamps in the Southern United States</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The still finer <spn>Erianthus Ravenn<ae/</spn> from
the Mediterranean region. The name is sometimes extended to the whole
genus.</cd> -- <col><b>Plume moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
one of numerous small, slender moths, belonging to the family
<fam>Pterophorid<ae/</fam>. Most of them have the wings deeply divided
into two or more plumelike lobes. Some species are injurious to the
grapevine.</cd> -- <col><b>Plume nutmeg</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>an aromatic Australian tree (<spn>Atherosperma moschata</spn>),
whose numerous carpels are tipped with long plumose persistent
styles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plume</ent><br/
<hw>Plume</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plumed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pluming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>plumer</ets> to
pluck, to strip, L. <ets>plumare</ets> to cover with feathers.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To pick and adjust the plumes or feathers of; to dress
or prink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pluming</qex> her wings among the breezy bowers.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strip of feathers; to pluck; to strip; to
pillage; also, to peel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.
Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To adorn with feathers or plumes.</def>
<ldquo/Farewell the <xex>plumed</xex> troop.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pride; to vaunt; to boast; -- used reflexively;
<as>as, he <ex>plumes</ex> himself on his skill</as>.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plumed adder</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an African
viper (<spn>Vipera cornuta</spn>, syn. <spn>Clotho cornuta</spn>),
having a plumelike structure over each eye. It is venomous, and is
related to the African puff adder. Called also <altname>horned
viper</altname> and <altname>hornsman</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Plumed
partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the California mountain
quail (<spn>Oreortyx pictus</spn>). See <cref>Mountain quail</cref>,
under <er>Mountain</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumeless</ent><br/
<hw>Plume"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without
plumes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumelet</ent><br/
<hw>Plume"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Plume</ets> +
<ets>-let</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small plume.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When rosy <qex>plumelets</qex> tuft the larch.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumery</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Plumes, collectively
or in general; plumage.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumicorn</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"mi*corn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluma</ets> feather + <ets>cornu</ets> horn.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An ear tuft of feathers, as in the horned
owls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*mig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plumiger</ets>; <ets>pluma</ets> a feather + <ets>gerere</ets> to
bear.]</ety> <def>Feathered; having feathers.</def>
<rj><au>Bailey</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumiliform</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*mil"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plumula</ets>, or <ets>plumella</ets> a little feather (dim. of
<ets>pluma</ets> feather) + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the of
a plume or feather.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumiped</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"mi*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plumipes</ets>, <ets>-edis</ets>; <ets>pluma</ets> a feather +
<ets>pes</ets>: cf. F. <ets>plumip<egrave/de</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having feet covered with feathers.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A plumiped bird.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plummet</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"met</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>plommet</ets>, OF. <ets>plommet</ets>, fr. <ets>plom</ets>,
<ets>plum</ets>, lead, F. <ets>plomb</ets>. See <er>Plumb</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece of lead attached to a line, used in sounding
the depth of water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll sink him deeper than e'er <qex>plummet</qex> sounded.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A plumb bob or a plumb line. See under
<er>Plumb</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, any weight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A piece of lead formerly used by school children to
rule paper for writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plummet line</b></col>, <cd>a line with a plummet; a
sounding line.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumming</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"ming</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Plumb</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The operation of finding,
by means of a mine dial, the place where to sink an air shaft, or to
bring an adit to the work, or to find which way the lode
inclines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plummy</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Plum</er>.]</ety> <def>Of the nature of a plum; desirable;
profitable; advantageous.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/For the
sake of getting something <xex>plummy</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G.
Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumous</ent><br/
<ent>Plumose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Plu*mose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Plu"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plumosus</ets>, fr. <ets>pluma</ets>
feather: cf. F. <ets>plumeux</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Having feathers or plumes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having hairs, or other parts, arranged along an axis
like a feather; feathery; plumelike; <as>as, a <ex>plumose</ex> leaf;
<ex>plumose</ex> tentacles.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumosite</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"mo*site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Jamesonite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumosity</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*mos"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being plumose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plump</ent><br/
<hw>Plump</hw> <pr>(pl<ucr/mp)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Plumper</adjf>
<pr>(pl<ucr/mp"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Plumpest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>plomp</ets> rude,
clumsy; akin to D. <ets>plomp</ets>, G., Dan., & Sw. <ets>plump</ets>;
probably of imitative origin. Cf. <er>Plump</er>, <pos>adv.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Well rounded or filled out; full; fleshy; fat; <as>as,
a <ex>plump</ex> baby; <ex>plump</ex> cheeks.</as></def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The god of wine did his <qex>plump</qex> clusters bring.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Carew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Done or made plump, or suddenly and without
reservation; blunt; unreserved; direct; downright.</def></p>

<p><q>After the <qex>plump</qex> statement that the author was at
Erceldoune and spake with Thomas.</q>
<rj><qau>Saintsbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plump</ent><br/
<hw>Plump</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A knot; a cluster; a group; a crowd;
a flock; <as>as, a <ex>plump</ex> of trees, fowls, or spears</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To visit islands and the <qex>plumps</qex> of men.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plump</ent><br/
<hw>Plump</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>plompen</ets>, G.
<ets>plumpen</ets>, Sw. <ets>plumpa</ets>, Dan. <ets>plumpe</ets>. See
<er>Plump</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To grow plump; to
swell out; <as>as, her cheeks have <ex>plumped</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To drop or fall suddenly or heavily, all at
once.</def><ldquo/Dulcissa <xex>plumps</xex> into a chair.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To <er>give</er> a plumper. See <er>Plumper</er>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plump</ent><br/
<hw>Plump</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Plumped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plumping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make plump; to
fill (out) or support; -- often with <xex>up</xex>.</def><-- as, to
<ex>plump up</ex> the pillows --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>plump</qex> up the hollowness of their history with
improbable miracles.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cast or let drop all at once, suddenly and
heavily; <as>as, to <ex>plump</ex> a stone into water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To give (a vote), as a plumper. See
<er>Plumper</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plump</ent><br/
<hw>Plump</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>plomp</ets>, interj.,
G. <ets>plump</ets>, <ets>plumps</ets>. Cf. <er>Plump</er>, <pos>a. &
v.</pos>]</ety> <def>Directly; suddenly; perpendicularly.</def>
<ldquo/Fall <xex>plump</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumper</ent><br/
<hw>Plump"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, plumps or swells out something else; hence, something
carried in the mouth to distend the cheeks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(English Elections)</fld> <def>A vote given to one
candidate only, when two or more are to be elected, thus giving him the
advantage over the others. A person who gives his vote thus is said to
<xex>plump</xex>, or to <xex>plump</xex> his vote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A voter who plumps his vote.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A downright, unqualified lie.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
or Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumply</ent><br/
<hw>Plump"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Fully; roundly; plainly; without
reserve.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumpness</ent><br/
<hw>Plump"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
plump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumpy</ent><br/
<hw>Plump"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Plump; fat;
sleek.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Plumpy</xex> Bacchus.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu"mu*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Plumule</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E.<plw>-las</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L. See <er>Plumule</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A plumule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A down feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumulaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`mu*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Downy; bearing down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumular</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"mu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Relating to a plumule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumularia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu`mu*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> L. <plw>Plumularl<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E.
<plw>Plumularias</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any hydroid belonging to <gen>Plumularia</gen>
and other genera of the family <fam>Plumularid<ae/</fam>. They generally
grow in plumelike forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumularian</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`mu*la"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any Plumularia. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumule</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"mule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plumula</ets>, dim. of <ets>pluma</ets> a feather; cf. F.
<ets>plumule</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The first
bud, or gemmule, of a young plant; the bud, or growing point, of the
embryo, above the cotyledons. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Radicle</er>.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A down
feather.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The aftershaft of a feather. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Feather</er>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One
of the featherlike scales of certain male butterflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumulose</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"mu*lose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having hairs
branching out laterally, like the parts of a feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plumy</ent><br/
<hw>Plum"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered or adorned with
plumes, or as with plumes; feathery.</def> <ldquo/His <xex>plumy</xex>
crest.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>plumy</xex>
trees.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. S. Blackie.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunder</ent><br/
<hw>Plun"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plundered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Plundering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[G.
<ets>pl<uum/ndern</ets> to plunder, <ets>plunder</ets> frippery,
baggage.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take the goods of by force, or
without right; to pillage; to spoil; to sack; to strip; to rob; <as>as,
to <ex>plunder</ex> travelers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nebuchadnezzar <qex>plunders</qex> the temple of God.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take by pillage; to appropriate forcibly; <as>as,
the enemy <ex>plundered</ex> all the goods they found</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To pillage; despoil; sack; rifle; strip;
rob.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunder</ent><br/
<hw>Plun"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of plundering or pillaging; robbery. See <xex>Syn.</xex> of
<er>Pillage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Inroads and <qex>plunders</qex> of the Saracens.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
T. North.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is taken by open force from an enemy;
pillage; spoil; booty; also, that which is taken by theft or
fraud.</def> <ldquo/He shared in the <xex>plunder</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Personal property and effects; baggage or
luggage.</def> <mark>[Slang, Southwestern U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunderage</ent><br/
<hw>Plun"der*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mar. Law)</fld>
<def>The embezzlement of goods on shipboard.</def>
<rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunderer</ent><br/
<hw>Plun"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who plunders
or pillages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunge</ent><br/
<hw>Plunge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plunged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plunging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>ploungen</ets>, OF. <ets>plongier</ets>, F. <ets>plonger</ets>, fr.
(assumed) LL. <ets>plumbicare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>plumbum</ets> lead. See
<er>Plumb</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust into water, or into
any substance that is penetrable; to immerse; to cause to penetrate or
enter quickly and forcibly; to thrust; <as>as, to <ex>plunge</ex> the
body into water; to <ex>plunge</ex> a dagger into the breast</as>. Also
used figuratively; <as>as, to <ex>plunge</ex> a nation into
war</as>.</def> <ldquo/To <xex>plunge</xex> the boy in pleasing
sleep.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bound and <qex>plunged</qex> him into a cell.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We shall be <qex>plunged</qex> into perpetual errors.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To baptize by immersion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To entangle; to embarrass; to overcome.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Plunged</qex> and graveled with three lines of Seneca.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunge</ent><br/
<hw>Plunge</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust or cast
one's self into water or other fluid; to submerge one's self; to dive,
or to rush in; <as>as, he <ex>plunged</ex> into the river</as>. Also
used figuratively; <as>as, to <ex>plunge</ex> into debt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forced to <qex>plunge</qex> naked in the raging sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>plunge</qex> into guilt of a murther.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pitch or throw one's self headlong or violently
forward, as a horse does.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some wild colt, which . . . flings and <qex>plunges</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To bet heavily and with seeming recklessness on a
race, or other contest; in an extended sense, to risk large sums in
hazardous speculations.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plunging fire</b></col> <fld>(Gun.)</fld>, <cd>firing
directed upon an enemy from an elevated position.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunge</ent><br/
<hw>Plunge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of thrusting
into or submerging; a dive, leap, rush, or pitch into, or as into,
water; <as>as, to take the water with a <ex>plunge</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a desperate hazard or act; a state of being
submerged or overwhelmed with difficulties.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was brought to that <qex>plunge</qex>, to conceal her
husband's murder or accuse her son.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And with thou not reach out a friendly arm,<br/
To raise me from amidst this <qex>plunge</qex> of sorrows?</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of pitching or throwing one's self headlong
or violently forward, like an unruly horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Heavy and reckless betting in horse racing;
hazardous speculation.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plunge bath</b></col>, <cd>an immersion by plunging;
also, a large bath in which the bather can wholly immerse himself.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Plunge battery</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>plunging
battery</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>, <cd>a voltaic battery so
arranged that the plates can be plunged into, or withdrawn from, the
exciting liquid at pleasure.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunger</ent><br/
<hw>Plun"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, plunges; a diver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A long solid cylinder, used, instead of a piston or
bucket, as a forcer in pumps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who bets heavily and recklessly on a race; a
reckless speculator.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Pottery)</fld> <def>A boiler in which clay is
beaten by a wheel to a creamy consistence.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>The firing pin of a
breechloader.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plunger bucket</b></col>, <cd>a piston, without a valve,
in a pump.</cd> -- <col><b>Plunger pole</b></col>, <cd>the pump rod of a
pumping engine.</cd> -- <col><b>Plunger pump</b></col>, <cd>a pump, as
for water, having a plunger, instead of a piston, to act upon the water.
It may be single-acting or double-acting</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunk</ent><br/
<hw>Plunk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Imitative.]</ety>
<mark>[Chiefly Colloq.]</mark> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pluck and release
quickly (a musical string); to twang.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw, push, drive heavily, plumply, or suddenly;
<as>as, to <ex>plunk</ex> down a dollar</as>; also, to hit or
strike.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be a truant from (school).</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunk</ent><br/
<hw>Plunk</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <mark>[Chiefly Colloq.]</mark>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make a quick, hollow, metallic, or harsh sound, as
by pulling hard on a taut string and quickly releasing it; of a raven,
to croak.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To drop or sink down suddenly or heavily; to
plump.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To play truant, or <ldquo/hooky<rdquo/.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunk</ent><br/
<hw>Plunk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act or sound of
plunking.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A large sum of
money.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A dollar.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plunket</ent><br/
<hw>Plun"ket</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of blue color;
also, anciently, a kind of cloth, generally blue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluperfect</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"per`fect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plus</ets> more + <ets>perfectus</ets> perfect; cf. F.
<ets>plus-que-parfait</ets>, L. <ets>plusquamperfectum</ets>.]</ety>
<def>More than perfect; past perfect; -- said of the tense which denotes
that an action or event was completed at or before the time of another
past action or event.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The pluperfect
tense; also, a verb in the pluperfect tense.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plural</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluralis</ets>, from <ets>plus</ets>, <ets>pluris</ets>, more; cf.
F. <ets>pluriel</ets>, OF. <ets>plurel</ets>. See <er>Plus</er>.]</ety>
<def>Relating to, or containing, more than one; designating two or more;
<as>as, a <ex>plural</ex> word</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Plural</qex> faith, which is too much by one.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plural number</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>the
number which designates more than one. See <er>Number</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 8.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plural</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The plural
number; that form of a word which expresses or denotes more than one; a
word in the plural form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluralism</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being plural, or in the plural number.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The state of a pluralist; the
holding of more than one ecclesiastical living at a time.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluralist</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A clerk or
clergyman who holds more than one ecclesiastical benefice.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of the parochial clergy, a large proportion were
<qex>pluralists</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plurality</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*ral"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>pluralities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluralitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pluralit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being plural, or consisting of more than
one; a number consisting of two or more of the same kind; <as>as, a
<ex>plurality</ex> of worlds; the <ex>plurality</ex> of a
verb.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The greater number; a majority; also, the greatest
of several numbers; in elections, the excess of the votes given for one
candidate over those given for another, or for any other, candidate.
When there are more than two candidates, the one who receives the
<xex>plurality</xex> of votes may have less than a majority. See
<er>Majority</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Take the <qex>plurality</qex> of the world, and they are neither
wise nor good.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Plurality of
benefices</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plurality of benefices</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>,
<cd>the possession by one clergyman of more than one benefice or living.
Each benefice thus held is called a <xex>plurality</xex>.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1103 --></p>

<p><ent>Pluralization</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ral*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
pluralizing.</def> <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluralize</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pluralized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pluralizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To make plural by using the plural termination; to attribute
plurality to; to express in the plural form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To multiply; to make manifold.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluralize</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take a
plural; to assume a plural form; <as>as, a noun
<ex>pluralizes</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>To hold more than one benefice at
the same time.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluralizer</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>A pluralist.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plurally</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ral*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a plural manner or
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluri-</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ri-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Plus</er>.]</ety> <def>A
combining form from L. <xex>plus</xex>, <xex>pluris</xex>, more, many;
as <xex>pluri</xex>literal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluries</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu"ri*es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
L. <ets>pluries</ets> many times, often, which occurs in the first
clause.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A writ issued in the third place,
after two former writs have been disregarded.</def> <rj><au>Mozley &
W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plurifarious</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ri*fa"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>plurifarius</ets>, fr. L. <ets>plus</ets>, <ets>pluris</ets>, many.
Cf. <er>Bifarious</er>.]</ety> <def>Of many kinds or fashions;
multifarious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plurifoliolate</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ri*fo"li*o*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pluri-</ets> + <ets>foliolate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having several or many leaflets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluriliteral</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ri*lit"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pluri-</ets> + <ets>literal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of
more letters than three.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
pluriliteral word.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plurilocular</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ri*loc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pluri-</ets> + <ets>locular</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having several
cells or loculi</def>; specifically <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <def>having
several divisions containing seeds; <as>as, the lemon and the orange are
<ex>plurilocular</ex> fruits</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plurilocular sporangia</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>many-celled sporangia, each cell containing a single spore, as in
many alg<ae/.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluriparous</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*rip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pluri-</ets> + L. <ets>parere</ets> to bring forth.]</ety>
<def>Producing several young at a birth; <as>as, a <ex>pluriparous</ex>
animal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluripartite</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ri*par"tite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pluri-</ets> + <ets>partite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Deeply divided into several portions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluripresence</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`ri*pres"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pluri-</ets> + <ets>presence</ets>.]</ety> <def>Presence in
more places than one.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plurisy</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"ri*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>plus</ets>,
<ets>pluris</ets>, more.]</ety> <def>Superabundance; excess;
plethora.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plus</ent><br/
<hw>Plus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L., more; akin to Gr.
<?/, <?/, and E. <ets>full</ets>. See <er>Full</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Pi<ugrave/</er>, <er>Pleonasm</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>More, required to be added;
positive, as distinguished from negative; -- opposed to
<ant>minus</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, in a literary sense, additional; real;
actual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Success goes invariably with a certain <qex>plus</qex> or positive
power.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plus sign</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>the sign (+)
which denotes addition, or a positive quantity.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plush</ent><br/
<hw>Plush</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pluche</ets>,
<ets>peluche</ets> (cf. It. <ets>peluzzo</ets>), fr. L. <ets>pilus</ets>
hair. See <er>pile</er> hair, and cf. <er>Peruke</er>.]</ety> <def>A
textile fabric with a nap or shag on one side, longer and softer than
the nap of velvet.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plushy</ent><br/
<hw>Plush"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like plush; soft and
shaggy.</def> <rj><au>H. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"tar*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ wealth +
<ets>-archy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plutocracy; the rule of wealth.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluteal</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"te*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a pluteus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluteus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu"te*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Plutei</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pluteuses</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a shed.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
free-swimming larva of sea urchins and ophiurans, having several long
stiff processes inclosing calcareous rods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluto</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"to</hw> <pr>(pl<umac/"t<osl/)</pr>, <pos>pr. n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. Gr. <grk>Plou`twn</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class.
Myth.)</fld> <def>The son of Saturn and Rhea, brother of Jupiter and
Neptune; the dark and gloomy god of the Lower World.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The ninth planet of the Solar System, the smallest
(5700 km radius) and most distant from the sun. The suggestion has been
made that it more closely resembles a large close comet than a planet.
Its orbit has an eccentricity of 0.248, larger than that of any other
planet; it varies from 4.44 to 7.37 billion km distance from the
sun.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pluto</qex> is an oddball among its eight sister planets.
It's the smallest in both size and mass, and has the most elliptical
orbit. It moves in a plane tilted markedly away from the other planets'
orbits. Moreover, <qex>Pluto</qex> is the only planet made almost
entirely of ice.</q> <rj><qau>Ron Cohen (Science News, Feb. 27, 1999, p.
139)</qau></rj></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pluto monkey</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
long-tailed African monkey (<spn>Cercopithecus pluto</spn>), having side
whiskers. The general color is black, more or less grizzled; the frontal
band is white.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*toc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(pl<usl/*t<ocr/k"r<adot/*s<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>ploutokrati`a</grk>; <grk>plou^tos</grk>
wealth + <grk>kratei^n</grk> to be strong, to rule, fr.
<grk>krato`s</grk> strength: cf. F. <ets>plutocratie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A form of government in which the supreme power is lodged in the
hands of the wealthy classes; government by the rich; also, a
controlling or influential class of rich men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutocrat</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"to*crat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One whose wealth
gives him power or influence; one of the plutocracy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutocratic</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`to*crat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to plutocracy; <as>as, <ex>plutocratic</ex> ideas</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Bagehot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutology</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ wealth +
<ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science which treats of
wealth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutonian</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*to"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Plutonius</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>plutonien</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Plutonic.</def> <rj><au>Poe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutonian</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*to"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>A Plutonist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutonic</ent><br/
<hw>Plu*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plutonique</ets>. See <er>Pluto</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to Pluto; Plutonian; hence, pertaining to the interior of the
earth; subterranean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, the system of the
Plutonists; igneous; <as>as, the <ex>Plutonic</ex>
theory</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plutonic action</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>the
influence of volcanic heat and other subterranean forces under
pressure.</cd> -- <col><b>Plutonic rocks</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>,
<cd>granite, porphyry, and some other igneous rocks, supposed to have
consolidated from a melted state at a great depth from the surface. Cf.
<cref>Intrusive rocks</cref>, under <er>Intrusive</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Plutonic theory</b></col>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Plutonism</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutonism</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"to*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plutonisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The theory, early advanced in
geology, that the successive rocks of the earth's crust were formed by
igneous fusion; -- opposed to the <xex>Neptunian theory</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutonist</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"to*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>plutoniste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who adopts the geological theory
of igneous fusion; a Plutonian. See <er>Plutonism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plutus</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>The son of Jason and Ceres,
and the god of wealth. He was represented as bearing a cornucopia, and
as blind, because his gifts were bestowed without discrimination of
merit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluvial</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"vi*al</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pluvialis</ets>, fr.
<ets>pluvia</ets> rain: cf. F. <ets>pluvial</ets>. See
<er>Plover</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to rain;
rainy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Produced by the action of
rain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluvial</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"vi*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>pluviale</ets> a
garment which keeps off the rain: cf. F. <ets>pluvial</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A priest's cope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviameter</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`vi*am"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pluviometer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviametrical</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`vi*a*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pluviometrical</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluvian</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"vi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The crocodile bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviograph</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"vi*o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluvia</ets> rain + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
self-registering rain gauge.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviography</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`vi*og"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluvia</ets> rain + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The branch of
meteorology treating of the automatic registration of the precipitation
of rain, snow, etc.; also, the graphic presentation of precipitation
data.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviometer</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`vi*om"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluvia</ets> rain + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pluviom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for
ascertaining the amount of rainfall at any place in a given time; a rain
gauge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviometrical</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`vi*o*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pluviom<eacute/trique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
pluviometer; determined by a pluviometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviometry</ent><br/
<hw>Plu`vi*om"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluvia</ets> rain + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of
meteorology that treats of the measurement of the precipitation of rain,
snow, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluvioscope</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"vi*o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluvia</ets> rain + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>A rain
gauge.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluviose</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Plu`vi`<ocir/se"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pluvious</er>.]</ety> <def>The fifth month of the French republican
calendar adopted in 1793. It began January 20, and ended February 18.
See <er>Vend<eacute/miaire</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pluvious</ent><br/
<hw>Plu"vi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pluviosus</ets>, <ets>pluvius</ets>, fr. <ets>pluvia</ets> rain:
cf. F. <ets>pluvieux</ets>. See <er>Pluvial</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Abounding in rain; rainy; pluvial.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ply</ent><br/
<hw>Ply</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Plied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Plying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>plien</ets>, F. <ets>plier</ets> to fold, to bend, fr. L.
<ets>plicare</ets>; akin to Gr. <?/, G. <ets>flechten</ets>. Cf.
<er>Apply</er>, <er>Complex</er>, <er>Display</er>, <er>Duplicity</er>,
<er>Employ</er>, <er>Exploit</er>, <er>Implicate</er>, <er>Plait</er>,
<er>Pliant</er>, <er>Flax</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As men may warm wax with handes <qex>plie</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lay on closely, or in folds; to work upon
steadily, or with repeated acts; to press upon; to urge importunately;
<as>as, to <ex>ply</ex> one with questions, with solicitations, or with
drink</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>plies</qex> him with redoubled strokes</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>plies</qex> the duke at morning and at night.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To employ diligently; to use steadily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go <qex>ply</qex> thy needle; meddle not.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To practice or perform with diligence; to work
at.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their bloody task, unwearied, still they <qex>ply</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ply</ent><br/
<hw>Ply</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bend; to yield.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It would rather burst atwo than <qex>plye</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The willow <qex>plied</qex>, and gave way to the gust.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To act, go, or work diligently and steadily;
especially, to do something by repeated actions; to go back and forth;
<as>as, a steamer <ex>plies</ex> between certain ports</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ere half these authors be read (which will soon be with
<qex>plying</qex> hard and daily).</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was forced to <qex>ply</qex> in the streets as a porter.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heavy hammers and mallets <qex>plied</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To work to windward; to
beat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ply</ent><br/
<hw>Ply</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pli</ets>, fr.
<ets>plier</ets>. See <er>Ply</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A fold; a plait; a turn or twist, as of a cord.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bent; turn; direction; bias.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The late learners can not so well take the <qex>ply</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Boswell, and others of Goldsmith's contemporaries, . . . did not
understand the secret <qex>plies</qex> of his character.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The czar's mind had taken a strange <qex>ply</qex>, which it
retained to the last.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Ply</xex> is used in composition to designate
folds, or the number of webs interwoven; as, a three-<xex>ply</xex>
carpet.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plyer</ent><br/
<hw>Ply"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which,
plies</def>; specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A kind of
balance used in raising and letting down a drawbridge. It consists of
timbers joined in the form of a St. Andrew's cross.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>See <er>Pliers</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plyght</ent><br/
<hw>Plyght</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Plight</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Plymouth Brethren</ent><br/
<hw>Plym"outh Breth"ren</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The members of a
religious sect which first appeared at Plymouth, England, about 1830.
They protest against sectarianism, and reject all official ministry or
clergy. Also called <altname>Brethren</altname>, <altname>Christian
Brethren</altname>, <altname>Plymouthists</altname>, etc. The
<stype>Darbyites</stype> are a division of the Brethren.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pne*om"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
breathe + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A
spirometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vehicle, as a
bicycle, the wheels of which are fitted with pneumatic tires.</def>
<mark>[archaic]</mark> <br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatical</ent><br/
<ent>Pneumatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pneu*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pneu*mat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pneumaticus</ets>, Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/, <?/, wind, air, <?/ to blow, breathe; cf. OHG.
<ets>fnehan</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pneumatique</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pneumonia</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of, or
resembling, air; having the properties of an elastic fluid; gaseous;
opposed to <xex>dense</xex> or <xex>solid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pneumatical</qex> substance being, in some bodies, the
native spirit of the body.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to air, or to elastic fluids or
their properties; pertaining to pneumatics; <as>as, <ex>pneumatic</ex>
experiments</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pneumatical</xex>
discoveries.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Stewart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Moved or worked by pressure or flow of air; <as>as,
a <ex>pneumatic</ex> instrument; a <ex>pneumatic</ex>
engine.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Fitted to contain air; Having
cavities filled with air; <as>as, <ex>pneumatic</ex> cells;
<ex>pneumatic</ex> bones.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Adapted for containing compressed air; inflated with
air; <as>as, a <ex>pneumatic</ex> cushion; a <ex>pneumatic</ex> tire, a
tire formed of an annular tube of flexible fabric, as India rubber,
suitable for being inflated with air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pneumatic action</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pneumatic lever</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a
contrivance for overcoming the resistance of the keys and other movable
parts in an organ, by causing compressed air from the wind chest to move
them.</cd> -- <col><b>Pneumatic dispatch</b></col>, <cd>a system of
tubes, leading to various points, through which letters, packages, etc.,
are sent, by the flow and pressure of air.</cd> -- <col><b>Pneumatic
elevator</b></col>, <cd>a hoisting machine worked by compressed
air.</cd> -- <col><b>Pneumatic pile</b></col>, <cd>a tubular pile or
cylinder of large diameter sunk by atmospheric pressure.</cd> --
<col><b>Pneumatic pump</b></col>, <cd>an air-exhausting or forcing
pump.</cd> -- <col><b>Pneumatic railway</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Atmospheric railway</cref>, under <er>Atmospheric</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Pneumatic syringe</b></col>, <cd>a stout tube closed at one end,
and provided with a piston, for showing that the heat produced by
compressing a gas will ignite substances.</cd> -- <col><b>Pneumatic
trough</b></col>, <cd>a trough, generally made of wood or sheet metal,
having a perforated shelf, and used, when filled with water or mercury,
for collecting gases in chemical operations.</cd> -- <col><b>Pneumatic
tube</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Pneumatic dispatch</cref>,
above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumaticity</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*tic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>The state of being pneumatic, or of having a cavity or cavities
filled with air; <as>as, the <ex>pneumaticity</ex> of the bones of
birds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatics</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pneumatique</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>That branch of science which treats of the
mechanical properties of air and other elastic fluids, as of their
weight, pressure, elasticity, etc. See <er>Mechanics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Philos. & Theol.)</fld> <def>The scientific study
or knowledge of spiritual beings and their relations to God, angels, and
men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocket</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any hollow place
suggestive of a pocket in form or use;</def> <specif>specif.</specif>:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A bin for strong coal, grain, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A socket for receiving the foot of a post, stake, etc.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A bright on a lee shore.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumato-</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu"ma*to-</hw> <pr>(n<umac/"m<adot/*t<osl/- <it>or</it>
n<usl/*m<acr/t"<osl/-)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>pney^ma</grk>, <grk>pney`matos</grk>, <xlati>wind</xlati>,
<xlati>air</xlati>, <xlati>breath</xlati>, <xlati>respiration</xlati>;
<as>as, <ex>pneumato</ex>graph, <ex>pneumato</ex>logy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatocele</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"o*cele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a tumor; cf. F.
<ets>pneumatoc<egrave/le</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
distention of the scrotum by air; also, hernia of the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatocyst</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"o*cyst</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + <ets>cyst</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A cyst or sac of a siphonophore, containing air, and serving as a
float, as in Physalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatogarm</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"o*garm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A tracing of the respiratory movements,
obtained by a pneumatograph or stethograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for recording the movements of
the thorax or chest wall during respiration; -- also called
<altname>stethograph</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatological</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*to*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pneumatologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
pneumatology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*tol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pneumatologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One versed in
pneumatology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatology</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pneumatologie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The doctrine of, or a
treatise on, air and other elastic fluids. See <er>Pneumatics</er>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Philos. & Theol.)</fld> <def>The science of
spiritual being or phenomena of any description.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring the amount of
force exerted by the lungs in respiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*tom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spirometry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatophore</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mat"o*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Pneumonophora.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumatothorax</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`ma*to*tho"rax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumato-</ets> + <ets>thorax</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pneumothorax</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu"mo-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr. <?/, <?/,
<xex>a lung</xex>; <as>as, <ex>pneumo</ex>gastric,
<ex>pneumo</ex>logy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumococcus</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*coc"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pneumo-</er>, and <er>Coccus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
form of micrococcus found in the sputum (and elsewhere) of persons
suffering with pneumonia, and thought to be the cause of this
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumogastric</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*gas"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumo-</ets> + <ets>gastric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the lungs and the stomach.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The pneumogastric nerve.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pneumogastric nerve</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>one
of the tenth pair of cranial nerves which are distributed to the
pharynx, esophagus, larynx, lungs, heart, stomach, liver, and spleen,
and, in fishes and many amphibia, to the branchial apparatus and also to
the sides of the body.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu"mo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pneumatograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumography</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumo-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A description
of the lungs.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumology</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumo-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The science which treats of the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumo-</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A spirometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Measurement of
the capacity of the lungs for air.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumonia</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mo"ni*a</hw> <pr>(n<usl/*m<omac/"n<icr/*<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pneymoni`a</grk>, fr.
<grk>pney`mwn</grk>, pl. <grk>pney`mones</grk> the lungs, also,
<grk>pley`mwn</grk>, which is perh. the original form. Cf.
<er>Pneumatio</er>, <er>Pulmonary</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <mcol><col><b>Catarrhal pneumonia</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Broncho-pneumonia</b></col></mcol>, is inflammation of the lung
tissue, associated with catarrh and with marked evidences of
inflammation of bronchial membranes, often chronic; -- also called
<altname>lobular pneumonia</altname>, from its affecting single lobules
at a time. -- <col><b>Croupous pneumonia</b></col>, or ordinary
pneumonia, is an acute affection characterized by sudden onset with a
chill, high fever, rapid course, and sudden decline; -- also called
<altname>lobar pneumonia</altname>, from its affecting a whole lobe of
the lung at once. See under <er>Croupous</er>. -- <col><b>Fibroid
pneumonia</b></col> is an inflammation of the interstitial connective
tissue lying between the lobules of the lungs, and is very slow in its
course, producing shrinking and atrophy of the lungs.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>pneumonique</ets>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the
lungs; pulmonic.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to pneumonia;
<as>as, <ex>pneumonic</ex> symptoms</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu*mon"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
for affections of the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumonitic</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*nit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to pneumonitis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumonitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pneu`mo*ni"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pneumo-</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the lungs; pneumonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1104 --></p>

<p><ent>Pneumonometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pneumo-</er>, and <er>-meter</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>A spirometer; a pneumometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumonophora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pneu`mo*noph"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a lung + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The division of Siphonophora which includes the
Physalia and allied genera; -- called also
<fam>Pneumatophor<ae/</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumony</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu"mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pneumonie</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Pneumonia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumootoka</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pneu`mo*<oum/t"o*ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pneumo-</er>, and <er>Ooticoid</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Sauropsida</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumophora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pneu*moph"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pneumonophora</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of holothurians having an internal gill, or respiratory
tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumoskeleton</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*skel"e*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pneumo-</ets> + <ets>skeleton</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A chitinous structure which supports the gill in
some invertebrates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumotherapy</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*ther"a*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ air
+ <ets>therapy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The treatment of
disease by inhalations of compressed or rarefied air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pneumothorax</ent><br/
<hw>Pneu`mo*tho"rax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ air +
E. <ets>thorax</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A condition in which
air or other gas is present in the cavity of the chest; -- called also
<altname>pneumatothorax</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pnigalion</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pni*ga"li*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ nightmare, fr. <?/ to throttle.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Nightmare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pnyx</ent><br/
<hw>Pnyx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The place at Athens where the meetings of
the people were held for making decrees, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poa</ent><br/
<hw>Po"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/
grass.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of grasses, including a
great number of species, as the kinds called <stype>meadow
grass</stype>, <stype>Kentucky blue grass</stype>, <stype>June
grass</stype>, and <stype>spear grass</stype> (which see).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poach</ent><br/
<hw>Poach</hw> <pr>(p<omac/ch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Poached</conjf> <pr>(p<omac/cht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Poaching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pocher</ets> to place in a pocket, to poach eggs (the yolk of the
egg being as it were <ets>pouched</ets> in the white), from
<ets>poche</ets> pocket, pouch. See <er>Pouch</er>, <pos>v. &
n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cook, as eggs, by breaking them into
boiling water; also, to cook with butter after breaking in a
vessel.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To rob of game; to pocket and convey away by
stealth, as game; hence, to plunder.</def> <rj><au>Garth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poach</ent><br/
<hw>Poach</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To steal or pocket game, or to
carry it away privately, as in a bag; to kill or destroy game contrary
to law, especially by night; to hunt or fish unlawfully; <as>as, to
<ex>poach</ex> for rabbits or for salmon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poach</ent><br/
<hw>Poach</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>pocher</ets> to
thrust or dig out with the fingers, to bruise (the eyes), F.
<ets>pouce</ets> thumb, L. <ets>pollex</ets>, and also E.
<ets>poach</ets> to cook eggs, to plunder, and <ets>poke</ets> to thrust
against.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To stab; to pierce; to spear, as
fish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To force, drive, or plunge into anything.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His horse <qex>poching</qex> one of his legs into some hollow
ground.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make soft or muddy by trampling.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To begin and not complete.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poach</ent><br/
<hw>Poach</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become soft or muddy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chalky and clay lands . . . chap in summer, and <qex>poach</qex>
in winter.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poachard</ent><br/
<hw>Poach"ard</hw> <pr>(p<omac/ch"<etil/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[From <er>Poach</er> to stab.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pocard</asp>, <asp>pochard</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A common European duck (<spn>Aythya ferina</spn>); --
called also <altname>goldhead</altname>, <altname>poker</altname>, and
<altname>fresh-water widgeon</altname>, <it>or</it> <altname>red-headed
widgeon</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The American redhead, which
is closely allied to the European poachard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Red-crested poachard</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
Old World duck (<spn>Branta rufina</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Scaup
poachard</b></col>, <cd>the scaup duck.</cd> -- <col><b>Tufted
poachard</b></col>, <cd>a scaup duck (<spn>Aythya cristata</spn>,
<it>or</it> <spn>Fuligula cristata</spn>), native of Europe and
Asia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poacher</ent><br/
<hw>Poach"er</hw> <pr>(p<omac/ch"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who poaches; one who kills or catches game or fish
contrary to law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The American widgeon.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sea poacher</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
lyrie.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poachiness</ent><br/
<hw>Poach"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being poachy; marshiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poachy</ent><br/
<hw>Poach"y</hw> <pr>(p<omac/ch"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Poach</er> to stab.]</ety> <def>Wet and soft; easily penetrated by
the feet of cattle; -- said of land</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poake</ent><br/
<ent>Poak</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Poak</hw>, <hw>Poake</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Waste matter from the preparation of skins, consisting of hair,
lime, oil, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocan</ent><br/
<hw>Po"can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
poke (<spn>Phytolacca decandra</spn>); -- called also <altname>pocan
bush</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pochard</ent><br/
<hw>Po"chard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Poachard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pock</ent><br/
<hw>Pock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pokke</ets>,
AS. <ets>pocc</ets>, <ets>poc</ets>; akin to D. <ets>pok</ets>, G.
<ets>pocke</ets>, and perh. to E. <ets>poke</ets> a pocket. Cf.
<er>Pox</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A pustule raised on the
surface of the body in variolous and vaccine diseases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of <qex>pokkes</qex> and of scab every sore.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pockarred</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"arred</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pockmarked</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pock-broken</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"-bro`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Broken out, or
marked, with smallpox; pock-fretten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocket</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"et</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/k"<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>poket</ets>, Prov. F. & OF. <ets>poquette</ets>, F.
<ets>pochette</ets>, dim. fr. <ets>poque</ets>, <ets>pouque</ets>, F.
<ets>poche</ets>; probably of Teutonic origin. See <er>Poke</er> a
pocket, and cf. <er>Poach</er> to cook eggs, to plunder, and
<er>Pouch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bag or pouch; especially; a
small bag inserted in a garment for carrying small articles,
particularly money; hence, figuratively, money; wealth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of several bags attached to a billiard table,
into which the balls are driven.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A large bag or sack used in packing various
articles, as ginger, hops, cowries, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the wool or hop trade, the <xex>pocket</xex> contains
half a sack, or about 168 Ibs.; but it is a variable quantity, the
articles being sold by actual weight.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A hole or space covered by a
movable piece of board, as in a floor, boxing, partitions, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mining.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A cavity in a rock
containing a nugget of gold, or other mineral; a small body of ore
contained in such a cavity.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A hole containing
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Nat.)</fld> <def>A strip of canvas, sewn upon a
sail so that a batten or a light spar can placed in the
interspace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pouch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Any hollow place suggestive of a pocket in form or
use;</def> <specif>specif.</specif>: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A bin for storing
coal, grain, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A socket for receiving the
foot of a post, stake, etc.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A bight on a lee
shore.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>a small cavity in the body, especially
one abnormally filled with a fluid; <as>as, a <ex>pocket</ex> of
pus</as>.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Dentistry)</fld> <def>a small space
between a tooth and the adjoining gum, formed by an abnormal separation
of the gum from the tooth.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>An isolated group or area which has properties in
contrast to the surrounding area; <as>as, a <ex>pocket</ex> of poverty
in an affluent region; <ex>pockets</ex> of resistance in a conquered
territory; a <ex>pocket</ex> of unemployment in a booming
ecomony</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Football)</fld> <def>The area from which a
quarterback throws a pass, behind the line of scrimmage, delineated by
the defensive players of his own team who protect him from attacking
opponents; <as>as, he had ample time in the <ex>pocket</ex> to choose an
open receiver</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>The part of a baseball glove
covering the palm of the wearer's hand.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Bowling)</fld> <def>the space between the head pin
and one of the pins in the second row, considered as the optimal point
at which to aim the bowling ball in order to get a strike.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pocket</xex> is often used adjectively in the sense
of <it>small</it>, or in the formation of compound words usually of
obvious signification; as, <xex>pocket</xex> knife, <xex>pocket</xex>
comb, <xex>pocket</xex> compass, <xex>pocket</xex> edition,
<xex>pocket</xex> handkerchief, <xex>pocket</xex> money,
<xex>pocket</xex> picking, or <xex>pocket</xex>-picking, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>deep pocket</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>deep
pockets</b></col>, <cd>wealth or substantial financial assets.</cd>
<note>Used esp. in legal actions, where plaintiffs desire to find a
defendant with "deep pockets", so as to be able to actually obtain the
sum of damages which may be judged due to him. This contrasts with a
"judgment-proof" defendant, one who has neither assets nor insurance,
and against whom a judgment for monetary damages would be uncollectable
and worthless.</note> --<col><b>Out of pocket</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Out</er>, <pos>prep.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Pocket borough</b></col>,
<cd>a borough <ldquo/owned<rdquo/ by some person.</cd> See under
<er>Borough</er>. <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pocket gopher</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of American rodents
of the genera <gen>Geomys</gen>, and <gen>Thomomys</gen>, family
<fam>Geomyd<ae/</fam>. They have large external cheek pouches, and are
fossorial in their habits. they inhabit North America, from the
Mississippi Valley west to the Pacific. Called also <altname>pouched
gopher</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pocket mouse</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any species of American mice of the family
<fam>Saccomyid<ae/</fam>. They have external cheek pouches. Some of them
are adapted for leaping (genus <gen>Dipadomys</gen>), and are called
<stype>kangaroo mice</stype>. They are native of the Southwestern United
States, Mexico, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Pocket piece</b></col>, <cd>a piece
of money kept in the pocket and not spent.</cd> -- <col><b>Pocket
pistol</b></col>, <cd>a pistol to be carried in the pocket.</cd> --
<col><b>Pocket sheriff</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a sheriff
appointed by the sole authority of the crown, without a nomination by
the judges in the exchequer.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocket</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pocketed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pocketing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put, or conceal,
in the pocket; <as>as, to <ex>pocket</ex> the change</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He would <qex>pocket</qex> the expense of the license.</q>
<rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take clandestinely or fraudulently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>pocketed</qex> pay in the names of men who had long been
dead.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pocket a ball</b></col> <fld>(Billiards)</fld>, <cd>to
drive a ball into a pocket of the table.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To pocket
an insult</b></col>, <col><b>affront</b></col>, etc.</mcol>, <cd>to
receive an affront without open resentment, or without seeking
redress.</cd> <ldquo/I must <xex>pocket</xex> up these
<xex>wrongs</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocketbook</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"et*book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small book or
case for carrying papers, money, etc., in the pocket; also, a notebook
for the pocket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocketful</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"et*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pocketfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>As much as a pocket
will hold; enough to fill a pocket; <as>as, <ex>pocketfuls</ex> of
chestnuts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocketknife</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"et*knife`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-knives</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A knife with one or more
blades, which fold into the handle so as to admit of being carried in
the pocket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocket veto</ent><br/
<hw>Pocket veto</hw>. <def>The retention by the President of the United
States of a bill unsigned so that it does not become a law, in virtue of
the following constitutional provision (<au>Const. Art. I., sec. 7, cl.
2</au>): <ldquo/If any bill shall not be returned by the President
within ten days (Sundays excepted) after it shall have been presented to
him, the same shall be a law, in like manner as if he had signed it,
unless the Congress by their adjournment prevent its return, in which
case it shall not be a law.<rdquo/ Also, an analogous retention of a
bill by a State governor.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pock-fretten</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"-fret`ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pockmarked</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pockiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
pocky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pockmark</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"mark</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mark or pit made
by smallpox.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pockmarked</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"marked`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Marked by
smallpox; pitted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pock-pitted</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"-pit`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pockmarked;
pitted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pock-pudding</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"-pud`ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bag pudding;
a name of reproach or ridicule formerly applied by the Scotch to the
English.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pockwood</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called because
formerly used as a specific for the pock.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Lignum-vit<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocky</ent><br/
<hw>Pock"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Pockier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Pockiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Full of pocks; affected with
smallpox or other eruptive disease.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poco</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A little; -- used chiefly in phrases indicating
the time or movement; <as>as, <ex>poco</ex> pi<ugrave/ allegro, a little
faster; <ex>poco</ex> largo, rather slow.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Poco a poco</b></col> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <cd>Little by little; as, <xex>poco a poco</xex>
crescendo, gradually increasing in loudness.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocock</ent><br/
<hw>Po"cock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Peacock.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pococurante</ent><br/
<hw>Po`co*cu*ran"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>poco
curante</ets> caring little.]</ety> <def>A careless person; a
trifler.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pococurantism</ent><br/
<hw>Po`co*cu*ran"tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Carelessness; apathy; indifference.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pocoson</ent><br/
<hw>Po*co"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Low, wooded grounds
or swamps in Eastern Maryland and Virginia.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>poquoson</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Washington.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poculent</ent><br/
<hw>Poc"u*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>poculentus</ets>, fr. <ets>poculum</ets> a cup.]</ety> <def>Fit for
drink.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Some those herbs which are not
esculent, are . . . <xex>poculent</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poculiform</ent><br/
<hw>Poc"u*li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>poculum</ets> a cup + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>poculiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the shape of a goblet or
drinking cup.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-pod</ent><br/
<hw>-pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Foot</er>.]</ety> <def>A
combining form or suffix from Gr. <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>,
<xex>foot</xex>; <as>as, deca<ex>pod</ex>, an animal having ten feet;
phyllo<ex>pod</ex>, an animal having leaflike feet; myria<ex>pod</ex>,
hexa<ex>pod</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pod</ent><br/
<hw>Pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably akin to
<ets>pudding</ets>, and perhaps the same word as <ets>pad</ets> a
cushion; cf. also Dan. <ets>pude</ets> pillow, cushion, and also E.
<ets>cod</ets> a husk, pod.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bag; a
pouch.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A capsule of plant, especially a
legume; a dry dehiscent fruit. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Angiospermous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A considerable number of animals
closely clustered together; -- said of seals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pod auger</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>pod
bit</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an auger or bit the channel of which is
straight instead of twisted.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pod</ent><br/
<hw>Pod</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Podded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Podding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To swell; to fill; also, to
produce pods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-poda</ent><br/
<hw>-po*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A New Latin plural combining form or
suffix from Gr. <?/, <?/, <sig>foot</sig>; <as>as, hexa<ex>poda</ex>,
myria<ex>poda</ex></as>. See <er>-pod</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podagra</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"a*gra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Podagric</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Gout in the joints of
the foot; -- applied also to gout in other parts of body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podagrical</ent><br/
<ent>Podagric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Po*dag"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Po*dag"ric*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>podagricus</ets>, Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ gout in the feet; <?/, <?/, Foot + <?/ a
catching.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to the gout; gouty; caused by
gout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Afflicted with gout.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podagrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"a*grous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Gouty;
podagric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podalgia</ent><br/
<hw>Po*dal"gi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/,
<?/, foot + <?/ pain.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>pain in the foot,
due to gout, rheumatism, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podarthrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*dar"thrum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Podarthra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, <?/,
foot + <?/ joint.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The foot joint; in
birds, the joint between the metatarsus and the toes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podded</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"ded</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having pods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podder</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who collects pods
or pulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podesta</ent><br/
<hw>Po*des"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>podest<agrave/</ets>, fr. L. <ets>potestas</ets> power, magistracy.
See <er>Potent</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the chief magistrates of the Italian
republics in the Middle Ages.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mayor, alderman, or other magistrate, in some
towns of Italy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podetium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*de"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Podetia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Podetiums</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A stalk which bears the fructification in some
lichens, as in the so-called reindeer moss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podge</ent><br/
<hw>Podge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>patsche</ets> puddle, mire.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A puddle; a
plash.</def> <rj><au>Skinner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Porridge.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podgy</ent><br/
<hw>Podg"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Fat and short;
pudgy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podical</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>podex</ets>, <ets>podicis</ets>, the anus.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Anal; -- applied to certain organs of
insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podiceps</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod"i*ceps</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>podex</ets>, <ets>podicis</ets>, anus + <ets>pes</ets> foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Grebe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Podia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/, dim. of <?/,
<?/, foot. See <er>Pew</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A low wall, serving as a foundation, a substructure, or a terrace
wall.</def> It is especially employed by arch<ae/ologists in two senses:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The dwarf wall surrounding the arena of an
amphitheater, from the top of which the seats began.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The masonry under the stylobate of a temple, sometimes a mere
foundation, sometimes containing chambers.</def> See <xex>Illust.</xex>
of <er>Column</er>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podley</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"ley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
young coalfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Foot</er>.]</ety> <def>A
combining form or prefix from Gr. <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>,
<xex>foot</xex>; <as>as, <ex>podo</ex>carp, <ex>podo</ex>cephalous,
<ex>podo</ex>logy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podobranch</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"o*branch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Podo-</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the branchi<ae/ attached to the bases of the legs in
Crustacea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podobranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod`o*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Podobranchi<ae/</plw> <pr>(-<emac/)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Podobranch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"o*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Podo-</ets> +
Gr. <grk>karpo`s</grk> fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A stem, or
footstalk, supporting the fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podocephalous</ent><br/
<hw>Pod`o*ceph"a*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Podo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ head.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having a head of flowers on a long peduncle, or
footstalk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podogynium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod`o*gyn"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>, foot + <grk>gynh`</grk>
woman.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Basigynium</er></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podophthalmia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod`oph*thal"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Podophthalmic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
stalk-eyed Crustacea, -- an order of Crustacea having the eyes supported
on movable stalks. It includes the crabs, lobsters, and prawns. Called
also <altname>Podophthalmata</altname>, and
<altname>Decapoda</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podophthalmous</ent><br/
<ent>Podophthalmic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pod`oph*thal"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pod`oph*thal"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Podo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ an eye.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having the eyes on movable footstalks, or
pedicels.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Podophthalmia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podophthalmite</ent><br/
<hw>Pod`oph*thal"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The eyestalk of a crustacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podophyllin</ent><br/
<hw>Pod`o*phyl"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Podophyllum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A brown bitter gum
extracted from the rootstalk of the May apple (<spn>Podophyllum
peltatum</spn>). It is a complex mixture of several
substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podophyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Pod`o*phyl"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having thin, flat, leaflike locomotive
organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or composing, the
layer of tissue, made up of lamin<ae/, beneath a horse's hoof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podophyllum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod`o*phyl"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>, foot + <grk>fy`llon</grk>
leaf.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of herbs of the
Barberry family, having large palmately lobed peltate leaves and
solitary flower. There are two species, the American <spn>Podophyllum
peltatum</spn>, or May apple, the Himalayan <spn>Podophyllum
Emodi</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The rhizome and rootlet of the May
apple (<spn>Podophyllum peltatum</spn>), -- used as a cathartic
drug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podoscaph</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"o*scaph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Podo-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ boat.]</ety> <def>A canoe-shaped float attached to the foot,
for walking on water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podosperm</ent><br/
<hw>Pod"o*sperm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Podo-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ seed: cf. F. <ets>podosperme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The stalk of a seed or ovule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podostomata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod`o*stom"a*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>, foot + <?/, <?/,
mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of Bryozoa of which
Rhabdopleura is the type. See <er>Rhabdopleura</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podotheca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pod`o*the"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Podothec<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>, foot + <?/ case.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The scaly covering of the foot of a bird or
reptile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podrida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*dri"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.,
rotten.]</ety> <def>A miscellaneous dish of meats. See
<er>Olla-podrida</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Podura</ent><br/
<hw>Po*du"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Podur<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Poduras</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.; Gr. <grk>poy`s</grk>, <grk>podo`s</grk>,
foot + <?/ tail.]</ety> <def>Any small leaping thysanurous insect of the
genus <gen>Podura</gen> and related genera; a springtail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Podura scale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one of the
minute scales with which the body of a podura is covered. They are used
as test objects for the microscope.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1105 --></p>

<p><ent>Podurid</ent><br/
<hw>Po*du"rid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of <gen>Podura</gen> or allied genera.</def> --
<def2><pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the poduras.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poe</ent><br/
<hw>Po"e</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Poi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poebird</ent><br/
<hw>Po"e*bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The parson bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poecile</ent><br/
<hw>P<oe/"ci*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Poicile</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poecilitic</ent><br/
<hw>P<oe/`ci*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>poiki`los</grk> many-colored, variegated.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Mottled with various colors; variegated; spotted; --
said of certain rocks.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Specifically: Of or
pertaining to, or characterizing, Triassic and Permian sandstones of red
and other colors.</def> <altsp>[Also written
<asp>poikilitic</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poecilopod</ent><br/
<hw>P<oe/*cil"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>p<oe/cilopode</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
P<oe/cilopoda. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poecilopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>P<oe/`ci*lop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ variegated, manifold + <ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Originally, an artificial group
including many parasitic Entomostraca, together with the horseshoe crabs
(<ord>Limuloidea</ord>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>By some recent writers
applied to the Merostomata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poem</ent><br/
<hw>Po"em</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>po<eum/ma</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to make, to compose, to write,
especially in verse: cf. F. <ets>po<eum/me</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A metrical composition; a composition in verse written in certain
measures, whether in blank verse or in rhyme, and characterized by
imagination and poetic diction; -- contradistinguished from
<xex>prose</xex>; <as>as, the <ex>poems</ex> of Homer or of
Milton</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A composition, not in verse, of which the language
is highly imaginative or impassioned; <as>as, a prose <ex>poem</ex>; the
<ex>poems</ex> of Ossian.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poematic</ent><br/
<hw>Po`em*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<def>Pertaining to a poem, or to poetry; poetical.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poenamu</ent><br/
<hw>Po*e"na*mu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
variety of jade or nephrite, -- used in New Zealand for the manufacture
of axes and weapons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poenology</ent><br/
<hw>P<oe/*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(p<esl/*n<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Penology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poephaga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*eph"a*ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>pohfa`gos</grk> grass eating; <grk>po`a</grk> grass +
<grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of
herbivorous marsupials including the kangaroos and their allies.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Po*eph"a*gous</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poesy</ent><br/
<hw>Po"e*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>po<eacute/sie</ets> (cf. It. <ets>poesia</ets>), L.
<ets>poesis</ets>, from Gr. <?/. from <?/ to make. Cf.
<er>Posy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The art of composing poems; poetical skill or
faculty; <as>as, the heavenly gift of <ex>poesy</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Poetry; metrical composition; poems.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Music and <qex>poesy</qex> used to quicken you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A short conceit or motto engraved on a ring or other
thing; a posy.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poet</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>po<eum/te</ets>, L. <ets>po<eum/ta</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
make. Cf. <er>Poem</er>.]</ety> <def>One skilled in making poetry; one
who has a particular genius for metrical composition; the author of a
poem; an imaginative thinker or writer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>poet's</qex> eye, in a fine frenzy rolling,<br/
Doth glance from heaven to earth, from earth to heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>poet</qex> is a maker, as the word signifies.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poet laureate</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Laureate</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetaster</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et*as`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An inferior
rhymer, or writer of verses; a dabbler in poetic art.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The talk of forgotten <qex>poetasters</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetastry</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et*as`try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The works of a
poetaster.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetess</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>po<eacute/tesse</ets>.]</ety> <def>A female poet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetical</ent><br/
<ent>Poetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Po*et"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Po*et"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>po<eum/ticus</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>po<eacute/tiquee</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to poetry; suitable for poetry, or for writing poetry;
<as>as, <ex>poetic</ex> talent, theme, work, sentiments</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressed in metrical form; exhibiting the
imaginative or the rhythmical quality of poetry; <as>as, a
<ex>poetical</ex> composition; <ex>poetical</ex> prose.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poetic license</b></col>. <cd>See <er>License</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 4.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetically</ent><br/
<hw>Po*et"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a poetic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetics</ent><br/
<hw>Po*et"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>po<eacute/tique</ets>, L. <ets>po<eum/tica</ets>,
<ets>po<eum/tice</ets>, Gr. <?/ (sc. <?/.]</ety> <def>The principles and
rules of the art of poetry.</def> <rj><au>J. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poeticule</ent><br/
<hw>Po*et"i*cule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
poetaster.</def> <rj><au>Swinburne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetize</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Poetized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Poetizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>po<eacute/tiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To write as a poet; to compose
verse; to idealize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I versify the truth, not <qex>poetize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetry</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>poeterie</ets>. See <er>Poet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art
of apprehending and interpreting ideas by the faculty of imagination;
the art of idealizing in thought and in expression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For <qex>poetry</qex> is the blossom and the fragrance of all
human knowledge, human thoughts, human passions, emotions, language.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Imaginative language or composition, whether
expressed rhythmically or in prose. Specifically: Metrical composition;
verse; rhyme; poems collectively; <as>as, heroic <ex>poetry</ex>;
dramatic <ex>poetry</ex>; lyric or Pindaric <ex>poetry</ex>.</as></def>
<ldquo/The planetlike music of <xex>poetry</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She taketh most delight<br/
In music, instruments, and <qex>poetry</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poets' Corner</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ets' Cor"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>An angle in the south
transept of Westminster Abbey, London; -- so called because it contains
the tombs of Chaucer, Spenser, Dryden, Ben Jonson, Gray, Tennyson,
Browning, and other English poets, and memorials to many buried
elsewhere.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poetship</ent><br/
<hw>Po"et*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or personality of a
poet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pogamoggan</ent><br/
<hw>Pog`a*mog"gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[North Amer.
Indian.]</ety> <def>An aboriginal weapon consisting of a stone or piece
of antler fastened to the end of a slender wooden handle, used by
American Indians from the Great Plains to the Mackenzie River.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poggy</ent><br/
<hw>Pog"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>See <er>Porgy</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A small
whale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pogy</ent><br/
<hw>Po"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
menhaden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pogy</xex> is often confounded with
<xex>porgy</xex>, and therefore incorrectly applied to various
fishes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poh</ent><br/
<hw>Poh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>An exclamation
expressing contempt or disgust; bah !</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pohagen</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ha"gen</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pauhaugen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poi</ent><br/
<hw>Po"i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A national food of the
Hawaiians, made by baking and pounding the kalo (or taro) root, and
reducing it to a thin paste, which is allowed to ferment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>
<-- also spelled <asp>poe</asp> -->

<p><ent>Poecile</ent><br/
<ent>Poicile</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Poi"ci*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>P<oe/"ci*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ (sc. <?/); cf. L. <ets>poecile</ets>.]</ety> <def>The frescoed porch or gallery in <city>Athens</city> where <persfn>Zeno</persfn> taught.</def>  <rj><au>R. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poignancy</ent><br/
<hw>Poign"an*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being poignant; <as>as, the <ex>poignancy</ex> of satire; the <ex>poignancy</ex> of grief.</as></def>  <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poignant</ent><br/
<hw>Poign"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., p. pr. of <ets>poindre</ets> to sting, fr. L. <ets>pungere</ets> to prick, sting. See <er>Pungent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pricking; piercing; sharp; pungent.</def> <ldquo/His <xex>poignant</xex> spear.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Poynaunt</xex> sauce.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Pointed; keen; satirical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His wit . . . became more lively and <qex>poignant</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poignantly</ent><br/
<hw>Poign"ant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a poignant manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poikilitic</ent><br/
<hw>Poi`ki*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>See <er>P<oe/cilitic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poikilocyte</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"ki*lo*cyte</hw> <pr>(poi"k<icr/*l<osl/*s<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>poiki`los</grk> diversified, changeable + <grk>ky`tos</grk> hollow vessel.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An irregular form of corpuscle found in the blood in cases of profound an<ae/mia, probably a degenerated red blood corpuscle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poikilothermic</ent><br/
<ent>Poikilothermal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Poi`ki*lo*ther"mal</hw> <pr>(-th<etil/r"m<ait/l)</pr>, <hw>Poi`ki*lo*ther"mic</hw> <pr>(-th<etil/r"m<icr/k)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>poiki`los</grk> changeable + E. <ets>thermal</ets>, <ets>thermic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Having a varying body temperature. See <er>Homoiothermal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poikilothermous</ent><br/
<hw>Poi`ki*lo*ther"mous</hw> <pr>(-m<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Poikilothermal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poinciana</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Poin`ci*a"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. Named after M. de <etsep>Poinci</etsep>, a governor of the French West Indies.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A prickly tropical shrub (<spn>C<ae/salpinia, <plain>formerly</plain> Poinciana, pulcherrima</spn>), with bipinnate leaves, and racemes of showy orange-red flowers with long crimson filaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The genus <gen>Poinciana</gen> is kept up for three trees of Eastern Africa, the Mascarene Islands, and India.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poind</ent><br/
<hw>Poind</hw> <pr>(poind)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pound</er> to confine.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To impound, as cattle.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark>  <rj><au>Flavel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To distrain.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>  <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poinder</ent><br/
<hw>Poind"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The keeper of a cattle pound; a pinder.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark>  <rj><au>T. Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who distrains property.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>  <rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poinsettia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Poin*set"ti*a</hw> <pr>(poin*s<ecr/t"t<icr/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. Named after Joel R. <etsep>Poinsett</etsep> of South Carolina.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Mexican shrub (<spn>Euphorbia pulcherrima</spn>) with very large and conspicuous vermilion bracts below the yellowish flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point</ent><br/
<hw>Point</hw> <pr>(point)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To appoint.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point</ent><br/
<hw>Point</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>point</ets>, and probably also <ets>pointe</ets>, L. <ets>punctum</ets>, <ets>puncta</ets>, fr. <ets>pungere</ets>, <ets>punctum</ets>, to prick. See <er>Pungent</er>, and cf. <er>Puncto</er>, <er>Puncture</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which pricks or pierces; the sharp end of anything, esp. the sharp end of a piercing instrument, as a needle or a pin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument which pricks or pierces, as a sort of needle used by engravers, etchers, lace workers, and others; also, a pointed cutting tool, as a stone cutter's <xex>point</xex>; -- called also <altname>pointer</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything which tapers to a sharp, well-defined termination. Specifically: A small promontory or cape; a tract of land extending into the water beyond the common shore line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The mark made by the end of a sharp, piercing instrument, as a needle; a prick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An indefinitely small space; a mere spot indicated or supposed. Specifically: <fld>(Geom.)</fld> That which has neither parts nor magnitude; that which has position, but has neither length, breadth, nor thickness, -- sometimes conceived of as the limit of a line; that by the motion of which a line is conceived to be produced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An indivisible portion of time; a moment; an instant; hence, the verge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When time's first <qex>point</qex> begun<br/
Made he all souls.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A mark of punctuation; a character used to mark the divisions of a composition, or the pauses to be observed in reading, or to point off groups of figures, etc.; a stop, as a comma, a semicolon, and esp. a period; hence, figuratively, an end, or conclusion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And there a <qex>point</qex>, for ended is my tale.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Commas and <qex>points</qex> they set exactly right.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Whatever serves to mark progress, rank, or relative position, or to indicate a transition from one state or position to another, degree; step; stage; hence, position or condition attained; <as>as, a <ex>point</ex> of elevation, or of depression; the stock fell off five <ex>points</ex>; he won by ten<ex>points</ex>.</as></def> <ldquo/A <xex>point</xex> of precedence.<rdquo/ <au>Selden.</au>  <ldquo/Creeping on from <xex>point</xex> to <xex>point</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Tennyson.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lord full fat and in good <qex>point</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>That which arrests attention, or indicates qualities or character; a salient feature; a characteristic; a peculiarity; hence, a particular; an item; a detail; <as>as, the good or bad <ex>points</ex> of a man, a horse, a book, a story, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He told him, <qex>point</qex> for <qex>point</qex>, in short and plain.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>point</qex> of religion and in <qex>point</qex> of honor.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shalt thou dispute<br/
With Him the <qex>points</qex> of liberty ?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Hence, the most prominent or important feature, as of an argument, discourse, etc.; the essential matter; esp., the proposition to be established; <as>as, the <ex>point</ex> of an anecdote</as>.</def> <ldquo/Here lies the <xex>point</xex>.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They will hardly prove his <qex>point</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A small matter; a trifle; a least consideration; a punctilio.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This fellow doth not stand upon <qex>points</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] cared not for God or man a <qex>point</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A dot or mark used to designate certain tones or time</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Anc. Mus.)</fld> <def>A dot or mark distinguishing or characterizing certain tones or styles; <as>as, <ex>points</ex> of perfection, of augmentation, etc.</as>; hence, a note; a tune.</def>  <ldquo/Sound the trumpet -- not a levant, or a flourish, but a <xex>point</xex> of war.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W. Scott.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mod. Mus.)</fld> <def>A dot placed at the right hand of a note, to raise its value, or prolong its time, by one half, as to make a whole note equal to three half notes, a half note equal to three quarter notes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A fixed conventional place for reference, or zero of reckoning, in the heavens, usually the intersection of two or more great circles of the sphere, and named specifically in each case according to the position intended; <as>as, the equinoctial <ex>points</ex>; the solstitial <ex>points</ex>; the nodal <ex>points</ex>; vertical <ex>points</ex>, etc. See <er>Equinoctial Nodal</er>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>One of the several different parts of the escutcheon. See <er>Escutcheon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the points of the compass (see <cref>Points of the compass</cref>, below); also, the difference between two points of the compass; <as>as, to fall off a <ex>point</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A short piece of cordage used in reefing sails. See <cref>Reef point</cref>, under <er>Reef</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Costume)</fld> <def>A a string or lace used to tie together certain parts of the dress.</def>  <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <def>Lace wrought the needle; <as>as, <ex>point</ex> de Venise; Brussels <ex>point</ex></as>. See <ex>Point lace</ex>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>18.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Railways)</fld> <def>A switch.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>19.</sn> <def>An item of private information; a hint; a tip; a pointer.</def> <mark>[Cant, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>20.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>A fielder who is stationed on the off side, about twelve or fifteen yards from, and a little in advance of, the batsman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>21.</sn> <def>The attitude assumed by a pointer dog when he finds game; <as>as, the dog came to a <ex>point</ex></as>. See <er>Pointer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>22.</sn> <fld>(Type Making)</fld> <def>A standard unit of measure for the size of type bodies, being one twelfth of the thickness of pica type. See <cref>Point system of type</cref>, under <er>Type</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>23.</sn> <def>A tyne or snag of an antler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>24.</sn> <def>One of the spaces on a backgammon board.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>25.</sn> <fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>A movement executed with the saber or foil; <as>as, tierce <ex>point</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>26.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A pointed piece of quill or bone covered at one end with vaccine matter; -- called also <altname>vaccine point</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>27.</sn>  <def>One of the raised dots used in certain systems of printing and writing for the blind. The first practical system was that devised by <person>Louis Braille</person> in 1829, and still used in Europe (see <er>Braille</er>). Two modifications of this are current in the United States: <col><b>New York point</b></col> founded on three bases of equidistant points arranged in two lines (viz., : :: :::), and a later improvement, <col><b>American Braille</b></col>, embodying the Braille base (:::) and the New-York-point principle of using the characters of few points for the commonest letters.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>28.</sn>  <specif>In technical senses:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In various games, a position of a certain player, or, by extension, the player himself;</def> as: (1) <fld>(Lacrosse & Ice Hockey)</fld> <def>The position of the player of each side who stands a short distance in front of the goal keeper; also, the player himself.</def> (2) <fld>(Baseball)</fld> (<pluf>pl.</pluf>) <def>The position of the pitcher and catcher.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Hunting)</fld> <def>A spot to which a straight run is made; hence, a straight run from point to point; a cross-country run.</def> <mark>[Colloq. Oxf. E. D.]</mark> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>The perpendicular rising of a hawk over the place where its prey has gone into cover.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>Act of pointing, as of the foot downward in certain dance positions.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The word <ex>point</ex> is a general term, much used in the sciences, particularly in mathematics, mechanics, perspective, and physics, but generally either in the geometrical sense, or in that of degree, or condition of change, and with some accompanying descriptive or qualifying term, under which, in the vocabulary, the specific uses are explained; as, boiling <ex>point</ex>, carbon <ex>point</ex>, dry <ex>point</ex>, freezing <ex>point</ex>, melting <ex>point</ex>, vanishing <ex>point</ex>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At all points</b></col>, <cd>in every particular, completely; perfectly.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>At point</b></col>, <col><b>In point</b></col>, <col><b>At the point</b></col>, <col><b>In the point</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>On the point</b></col></mcol>, <cd>as near as can be; on the verge; about (see <er>About</er>, <pos>prep.</pos>, 6); <as>as, <ex>at the point</ex> of death; he was <ex>on the point</ex> of speaking</as>.</cd>  <ldquo/<xex>In point</xex> to fall down.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/Caius Sidius Geta, <xex>at point</xex> to have been taken, recovered himself so valiantly as brought day on his side.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> -- <col><b>Dead point</b></col>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Dead center</cref>, under <er>Dead</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Far point</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>in ophthalmology, the farthest point at which objects are seen distinctly. In normal eyes the nearest point at which objects are seen distinctly; either with the two eyes together (<xex>binocular near point</xex>), or with each eye separately (<xex>monocular near point</xex>).</cd> -- <col><b>Nine points of the law</b></col>, <cd>all but the tenth point; the greater weight of authority.</cd> -- <col><b>On the point</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>At point</cref>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Point lace</b></col>, <cd>lace wrought with the needle, as distinguished from that made on the pillow.</cd> -- <col><b>Point net</b></col>, <cd>a machine-made lace imitating a kind of Brussels lace (Brussels ground).</cd> -- <col><b>Point of concurrence</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a point common to two lines, but not a point of tangency or of intersection, as, for instance, that in which a cycloid meets its base.</cd> -- <col><b>Point of contrary flexure</b></col>, <cd>a point at which a curve changes its direction of curvature, or at which its convexity and concavity change sides.</cd> -- <col><b>Point of order</b></col>, <cd>in parliamentary practice, a question of order or propriety under the rules.</cd> -- <col><b>Point of sight</b></col> <fld>(Persp.)</fld>, <cd>in a perspective drawing, the point assumed as that occupied by the eye of the spectator.</cd> -- <col><b>Point of view</b></col>, <cd>the relative position from which anything is seen or any subject is considered.</cd> -- <col><b>Points of the compass</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the thirty-two points of division of the compass card in the mariner's compass; the corresponding points by which the circle of the horizon is supposed to be divided, of which the four marking the directions of east, west, north, and south, are called <xex>cardinal points</xex>, and the rest are named from their respective directions, as N. by E., N. N. E., N. E. by N., N. E., etc. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Compass</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Point paper</b></col>, <cd>paper pricked through so as to form a stencil for transferring a design.</cd> -- <col><b>Point system of type</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Type</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Singular point</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a point of a curve which possesses some property not possessed by points in general on the curve, as a cusp, a point of inflection, a node, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>To carry one's point</b></col>, <cd>to accomplish one's object, as in a controversy.</cd> -- <col><b>To make a point of</b></col>, <cd>to attach special importance to.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To make a point</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To gain a point</b></col></mcol>, <cd>accomplish that which was proposed; also, to make advance by a step, grade, or position.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To mark a point</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To score a point</b></col></mcol>, <cd>as in billiards, cricket, etc., to note down, or to make, a successful hit, run, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>To strain a point</b></col>, <cd>to go beyond the proper limit or rule; to stretch one's authority or conscience.</cd> -- <col><b>Vowel point</b></col>, <cd>in Arabic, Hebrew, and certain other Eastern and ancient languages, a mark placed above or below the consonant, or attached to it, representing the vowel, or vocal sound, which precedes or follows the consonant.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point</ent><br/
<hw>Point</hw> <pr>(point)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pointed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pointing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pointer</ets>. See <er>Point</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give a point to; to sharpen; to cut, forge, grind, or file to an acute end; <as>as, to <ex>point</ex> a dart, or a pencil</as>. Used also figuratively; <as>as, to <ex>point</ex> a moral</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To direct toward an abject; to aim; <as>as, to <ex>point</ex> a gun at a wolf, or a cannon at a fort</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, to direct the attention or notice of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever should be guided through his battles by Minerva, and <qex>pointed</qex> to every scene of them.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To supply with punctuation marks; to punctuate; <as>as, to <ex>point</ex> a composition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To mark (a text, as in Arabic or Hebrew) with <cref>vowel points</cref>; -- also called <altname>vocalize</altname>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> vocalize.</syn>
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>RP</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To give particular prominence to; to designate in a special manner; to indicate, as if by pointing; <as>as, the error was <ex>pointed</ex> out</as>.</def>  <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>points</qex> it, however, by no deviation from his straightforward manner of speech.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To indicate or discover by a fixed look, as game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>To fill up and finish the joints of (a wall), by introducing additional cement or mortar, and bringing it to a smooth surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Stone Cutting)</fld> <def>To cut, as a surface, with a pointed tool.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To point a rope</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to taper and neatly finish off the end by interweaving the nettles.</cd> -- <col><b>To point a sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to affix points through the eyelet holes of the reefs.</cd> -- <col><b>To point off</b></col>, <cd>to divide into periods or groups, or to separate, by pointing, as figures.</cd> -- <col><b>To point the yards</b></col> (of a vessel) <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to brace them so that the wind shall strike the sails obliquely.</cd> <au>Totten.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1106  --></p>

<p><ent>Point</ent><br/
<hw>Point</hw> <pr>(point)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
direct the point of something, as of a finger, for the purpose of
designating an object, and attracting attention to it; -- with
<xex>at</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now must the world <qex>point</qex> at poor Katharine.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Point</qex> at the tattered coat and ragged shoe.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To indicate the presence of game by fixed and steady
look, as certain hunting dogs do.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He treads with caution, and he <qex>points</qex> with fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To approximate to the surface; to
head; -- said of an abscess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To point at</b></col>, <cd>to treat with scorn or
contempt by pointing or directing attention to.</cd> -- <col><b>To point
well</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to sail close to the wind; --
said of a vessel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointal</ent><br/
<hw>Point"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Point</er>:
cf. F. <ets>pointal</ets> an upright wooden prop, OF.
<ets>pointille</ets> a prick or prickle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The pistil of a plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of pencil or style used with the tablets of
the Middle Ages.</def> <ldquo/A pair of tablets [<it>i. e.</it>,
tablets] . . . and a <xex>pointel</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>See <er>Poyntel</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point alphabet</ent><br/
<hw>Point alphabet</hw>. <def>An alphabet for the blind with a system of
raised points corresponding to letters.</def><-- = Braille? --><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point applique</ent><br/
<hw>Point appliqu<eacute/</hw>. <def>Lace having a needle-made design
applied to a net ground, this ground often being machine-made.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point-blank</ent><br/
<hw>Point`-blank"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>point</ets> point + <ets>blanc</ets> white.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The white spot on a target, at which an arrow or other missile is
aimed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>With all small arms,
the second point in which the natural line of sight, when horizontal,
cuts the trajectory.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>With artillery, the point
where the projectile first strikes the horizontal plane on which the gun
stands, the axis of the piece being horizontal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point-blank</ent><br/
<hw>Point`-blank"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Directed in a
line toward the object aimed at; aimed directly toward the
mark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, direct; plain; unqualified; -- said of
language; <as>as, a <ex>point-blank</ex> assertion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Point-blank range</b></col>, <cd>the extent of the
apparent right line of a ball discharged.</cd> -- <col><b>Point-blank
shot</b></col>, <cd>the shot of a gun pointed directly toward the object
to be hit.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point-blank</ent><br/
<hw>Point`-blank"</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a point-blank
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To sin <qex>point-blank</qex> against God's word.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point d'appui</ent><br/
<-- the following foreign words had no mark \'d8 in front, in the original. Why? -->
<hw>Point` d'ap`pui"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[F.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>See under <er>Appui</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point-devise</ent><br/
<ent>Point-device</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Point`-de*vice"</hw>, <hw>Point`-de*vise"</hw>  }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>at point devis</ets>; <ets>at</ets> at + <ets>point</ets> point, condition + <ets>devis</ets> exact, careful, OF. <ets>devis</ets> fixed, set. See <er>Device</er>.]</ety> <def>Uncommonly nice and exact; precise; particular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You are rather <qex>point-devise</qex> in your accouterments.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus he grew up, in logic <qex>point-devise</qex>,<br/
Perfect in grammar, and in rhetoric nice.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point-devise</ent><br/
<ent>Point-device</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Point`-de*vice"</hw>, <hw>Point`-de*vise"</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Exactly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointed</ent><br/
<hw>Point"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sharp; having a sharp point; <as>as, a <ex>pointed</ex> rock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characterized by sharpness, directness, or pithiness of expression; terse; epigrammatic; especially, directed to a particular person or thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His moral pleases, not his <qex>pointed</qex> wit.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pointed arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>an arch with a pointed crown.</cd> -- <col><b>Pointed style</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a name given to that style of architecture in which the pointed arch is the predominant feature; -- more commonly called <altname>Gothic</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Point"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Point"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointel</ent><br/
<hw>Point"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Point</er>.  Cf. <er>Pointal</er>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Pointal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointer</ent><br/
<hw>Point"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which, points.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hand of a timepiece.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a breed of dogs trained to stop at scent of game, and with the nose point it out to sportsmen.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The two stars (Merak and Dubhe) in the Great Bear, the line between which points nearly in the direction of the north star.</def> See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Ursa Major</er>. <sd>(b)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Diagonal braces sometimes fixed across the hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointing</ent><br/
<hw>Point"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sharpening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of designating, as a position or direction, by means of something pointed, as a finger or a rod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act or art of punctuating; punctuation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of filling and finishing the joints in masonry with mortar, cement, etc.; also, the material so used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The rubbing off of the point of the wheat grain in the first process of high milling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Sculpt.)</fld> <def>The act or process of measuring, at the various distances from the surface of a block of marble, the surface of a future piece of statuary; also, a process used in cutting the statue from the artist's model.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointingstock</ent><br/
<hw>Point`ing*stock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An object of ridicule or scorn; a laughingstock.</def>  <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointless</ent><br/
<hw>Point"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no point; blunt; wanting keenness; obtuse; <as>as, a <ex>pointless</ex> sword; a <ex>pointless</ex> remark.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Blunt; obtuse, dull; stupid.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointlessly</ent><br/
<hw>Point"less*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Without point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointleted</ent><br/
<hw>Point"let*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having a small, distinct point; apiculate.</def>  <rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point man</ent><br/
<hw>Point" man`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-men</plw> <pr>(-m<it>e</it>n)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>the lead soldier in a foot patrol under combat conditions.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a person who takes a conspicuous public position in proposing a new idea or initiating a new policy, who may become a target of criticism for those opposed.</def>  <ldquo/The Secretary of State served as <ex>point man</ex> for the administration's new China policy.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointrel</ent><br/
<hw>Poin"trel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A graving tool.</def>  <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pointsman</ent><br/
<hw>Points"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-men</plw> <pr>(-m<it>e</it>n)</pr>.</plu> <def>A man who has charge of railroad points or switches.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Point switch</ent><br/
<hw>Point switch</hw>. <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>A switch made up of a rail from each track, both rails being tapered far back and connected to throw alongside the through rail of either track.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poise</ent><br/
<hw>Poise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pois</ets>, <ets>peis</ets>, OF. <ets>pois</ets>, <ets>peis</ets>, F. <ets>poids</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pensum</ets> a portion weighed out, <ets>pendere</ets> to weigh, weigh out.  Cf. <er>Avoirdupois</er>, <er>Pendant</er>, <er>Poise</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written also <asp>peise</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Weight; gravity; that which causes a body to descend; heaviness.</def> <ldquo/Weights of an extraordinary <xex>poise</xex>.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj>
<-- Obsolete? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The weight, or mass of metal, used in weighing, to
balance the substance weighed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The state of being balanced by equal weight or
power; equipoise; balance; equilibrium; rest.</def>
<rj><au>Bentley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which causes a balance; a
counterweight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men of unbounded imagination often want the <qex>poise</qex> of
judgment.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>a dignified and self-confident manner; graceful
composure and tact in handling difficult social situations.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poise</ent><br/
<hw>Poise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Poised</conjf>, <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Poising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>poisen</ets>,
<ets>peisen</ets>, OF. & F. <ets>peser</ets>, to weigh, balance, OF. il
<ets>peise</ets>, il <ets>poise</ets>, he weighs, F. il
<ets>p<egrave/se</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pensare</ets>, v. intens. fr.
<ets>pendere</ets> to weigh. See <er>Poise</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Pensive</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written also
<asp>peise</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To balance; to make of equal
weight; <as>as, to <ex>poise</ex> the scales of a
balance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hold or place in equilibrium or
equiponderance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor yet was earth suspended in the sky;<br/
Nor <qex>poised</qex>, did on her own foundation lie.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To counterpoise; to counterbalance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One scale of reason to <qex>poise</qex> another of sensuality.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>poise</qex> with solid sense a sprightly wit.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To ascertain, as by the balance; to weigh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He can not sincerely consider the strength, <qex>poise</qex> the
weight, and discern the evidence.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To weigh (down); to oppress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest leaden slumber <qex>peise</qex> me down to-morrow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poise</ent><br/
<hw>Poise</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To hang in equilibrium; to be
balanced or suspended; hence, to be in suspense or doubt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The slender, graceful spars<br/
<qex>Poise</qex> aloft in air.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poiser</ent><br/
<hw>Pois"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
balancer of dipterous insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poison</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poison</ets>,
in Old French also, a potion, fr. L. <ets>potio</ets> a drink, draught,
potion, a poisonous draught, fr. <ets>potare</ets> to drink. See
<er>Potable</er>, and cf. <er>Potion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any
agent which, when introduced into the animal organism, is capable of
producing a morbid, noxious, or deadly effect upon it; <as>as, morphine
is a deadly <ex>poison</ex>; the <ex>poison</ex> of pestilential
diseases.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which taints or destroys moral purity or
health; <as>as, the <ex>poison</ex> of evil example; the <ex>poison</ex>
of sin.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poison ash</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>A tree of the genus <gen>Amyris</gen> (<spn>Amyris
balsamifera</spn>) found in the West Indies, from the trunk of which a
black liquor distills, supposed to have poisonous qualities.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The poison sumac (<spn>Rhus venenata</spn>)</cd>.
<mark>[U. S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Poison dogwood</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>poison sumac.</cd> -- <col><b>Poison
fang</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one of the superior maxillary
teeth of some species of serpents, which, besides having the cavity for
the pulp, is either perforated or grooved by a longitudinal canal, at
the lower end of which the duct of the poison gland terminates. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Fang</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Poison
gland</b></col> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>a gland, in animals or plants,
which secretes an acrid or venomous matter, that is conveyed along an
organ capable of inflicting a wound.</cd> -- <col><b>Poison
hemlock</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a poisonous umbelliferous plant
(<spn>Conium maculatum</spn>). See <er>Hemlock</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Poison ivy</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a poisonous climbing
plant (formerly <spn>Rhus Toxicodendron</spn>, or <spn>Rhus
radicans</spn>, now classified as <spn>Toxicodendron radicans</spn>) of
North America. It is common as a climbing vine, especially found on tree
trunks, or walls, or as a low, spreading vine, or as a shrub. As a low
vine it grows well in lightly shaded areas, recognizable by growing in
clusters of three leaves. Its leaves are trifoliate, rhombic-ovate, and
variously notched. Its form varies slightly from location to location,
leading to some speculation that it may consist of more than one
species. Many people are poisoned by it, though some appear resistant to
its effects. Touching the leaves may leave a residue of an oil on the
skin, and if not washed off quickly, sensitive areas of skin become
reddened and develop multiple small blisters, lasting for several days
to several weeks, and causing a persistent itch. The toxic reaction is
due to an oil, present in all parts of the plant except the pollen,
called <prod>urushiol</prod>, the active component of which is the
compound <prod>pentadecylacatechol</prod> (according to <a
href="http://www.jaxmed.com/articles/Diseases/poison_ivy_dermatitis.htm">Charles
H. Booras</a>). See <er>Poison sumac</er>. It is related to
<altname>poison oak</altname>, and is also called
<altname>mercury</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Poison nut</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Nux vomica</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
tree which yields this seed (<spn>Strychnos Nuxvomica</spn>). It is
found on the Malabar and Coromandel coasts.</cd> -- <col><b>Poison
oak</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a dermatitis-producing plant often
lumped together with the poison ivy (<spn>Toxicodendron radicans</spn>)
in common terminology, but more properly distinguished as the more
shrubby <spn>Toxicodendron quercifolium</spn> (syn. <spn>Toxicodendron
diversilobum</spn>), common in California and Oregon. Opinion varies as
to whether the poison oak and poison ivy are only variants of a single
species. See <cref>poison ivy</cref>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Poison
sac</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Poison gland</cref>,
above. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Fang</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Poison sumac</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a poisonous shrub
formerly considered to be of the genus <gen>Rhus</gen> (<spn>Rhus
venenata</spn>), but now classified as <spn>Toxicodendron vernix</spn>;
-- also called <altname>poison ash</altname>, <altname>poison
dogwood</altname>, and <altname>poison elder</altname>. It has pinnate
leaves on graceful and slender common petioles, and usually grows in
swampy places. Both this plant and the poison ivy (<spn>Toxicodendron
radicans</spn>, formerly <spn>Rhus Toxicodendron</spn>) have clusters of
smooth greenish white berries, while the red-fruited species of this
genus are harmless. The tree (<spn>Rhus vernicifera</spn>) which yields
the celebrated Japan lacquer is almost identical with the poison sumac,
and is also very poisonous. The juice of the poison sumac also forms a
lacquer similar to that of Japan.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Venom; virus; bane; pest; malignity.</syn>
<usage> -- <er>Poison</er>, <er>Venom</er>. <xex>Poison</xex> usually
denotes something received into the system by the mouth, breath, etc.
<xex>Venom</xex> is something discharged from animals and received by
means of a wound, as by the bite or sting of serpents, scorpions, etc.
Hence, <xex>venom</xex> specifically implies some malignity of nature or
purpose.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poison</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Poisoned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poisoning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>poisonner</ets>,
F. <ets>empoissoner</ets>, L. <ets>potionare</ets> to give to drink. See
<er>Poison</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To put poison upon or into; to infect with poison;
<as>as, to <ex>poison</ex> an arrow; to <ex>poison</ex> food or
drink.</as></def> <ldquo/The ingredients of our <xex>poisoned</xex>
chalice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To injure or kill by poison; to administer poison
to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you <qex>poison</qex> us, do we not die ?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To taint; to corrupt; to vitiate; <as>as, vice
<ex>poisons</ex> happiness; slander <ex>poisoned</ex> his
mind.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whispering tongues can <qex>poison</qex> truth.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poison</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act as, or convey, a
poison.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tooth that <qex>poisons</qex> if it bite.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poisonable</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of poisoning; poisonous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Poisonable</xex> heresies.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being poisoned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poison bush</ent><br/
<hw>Poison bush</hw>. <mark>In Australia:</mark> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any
fabaceous shrub of the genus <gen>Gastrolobium</gen>, the herbage of
which is poisonous to stock; also, any species of several related
genera, as <gen>Oxylobium</gen>, <gen>Gompholobium</gen>, etc.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The plant <spn>Myoporum deserti</spn>, often
distinguished as <altname>Ellangowan poison bush</altname> or
<altname>dogwood poison bush</altname>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
ulmaceous plant <spn>Trema cannabina</spn>, which, though not poisonous,
is injurious to stock because of its large amount of fiber.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poison cup</ent><br/
<hw>Poison cup</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cup containing poison.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A cup that was supposed to break on having poison
put into it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poisoner</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
poisons.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poisonous</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
qualities or effects of poison; venomous; baneful; corrupting;
noxious.</def> <au>Shak.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Poi"son*ous*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Poi"son*ous*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poisonsome</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"son*some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Poisonous.<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <xex>Holland</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poisure</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Poise</er>.]</ety> <def>Weight.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poitrel</ent><br/
<hw>Poi"trel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>poitrel</ets>, F. <ets>poitrail</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pectorale</ets>
a breastplate, fr. <ets>pectoralis</ets>, a. See <er>Pectoral</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld> <def>The breastplate of the
armor of a horse. See <er>Peytrel</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poize</ent><br/
<hw>Poize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Poise</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pokal</ent><br/
<hw>Po*kal"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.]</ety> <def>A tall
drinking cup.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poke</ent><br/
<hw>Poke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A large
North American herb of the genus <gen>Phytolacca</gen> (<spn>Phytolacca
decandra</spn>), bearing dark purple juicy berries; -- called also
<altname>garget</altname>, <altname>pigeon berry</altname>,
<altname>pocan</altname>, and <altname>pokeweed</altname>. The root and
berries have emetic and purgative properties, and are used in medicine.
The young shoots are sometimes eaten as a substitute for asparagus, and
the berries are said to be used in Europe to color wine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poke</ent><br/
<hw>Poke</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>poca</ets>, <ets>poha</ets>,
<ets>pohha</ets>; akin to Icel. <ets>poki</ets>, OD. <ets>poke</ets>,
and perh. to E. <ets>pock</ets>; cf. also Gael. <ets>poca</ets>, and OF.
<ets>poque</ets>. Cf. <er>Pock</er>, <er>Pocket</er>,
<er>Pouch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bag; a sack; a pocket.</def>
<ldquo/He drew a dial from his <xex>poke</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They wallowed as pigs in a <qex>poke</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A long, wide sleeve; -- called also <altname>poke
sleeve</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To boy a pig a poke</b></col><cd> (that is, in a bag), to
buy a thing without knowledge or examination of it.</cd>
<rj><au>Camden.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poke</ent><br/
<hw>Poke</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Poked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. LG. <ets>poken</ets> to
prick, pierce, thrust, <ets>pok</ets> a dagger, knife, D.
<ets>pook</ets>, G. <ets>pocken</ets> to beat, also Ir. <ets>poc</ets> a
blow, Gael. <ets>puc</ets> to push.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust or
push against or into with anything pointed; hence, to stir up; to
excite; <as>as, to <ex>poke</ex> a fire</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>poked</qex> John, and said <ldquo/Sleepest thou
?<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To thrust with the horns; to gore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[From 5th <er>Poke</er>, 3.]</ety> <def>To put a
poke on; <as>as, to <ex>poke</ex> an ox</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To poke fun</b></col>, <cd>to excite fun; to joke; to
jest.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>To poke fun at</b></col>,
<cd>to make a butt of; to ridicule.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poke</ent><br/
<hw>Poke</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To search; to feel one's way, as in
the dark; to grope; <as>as, to <ex>poke</ex> about</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man must have <qex>poked</qex> into Latin and Greek.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poke</ent><br/
<hw>Poke</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of poking; a
thrust; a jog; <as>as, a <ex>poke</ex> in the ribs</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A lazy person; a dawdler; also, a stupid or
uninteresting person.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A contrivance to prevent an animal from leaping or
breaking through fences. It consists of a yoke with a pole inserted,
pointed forward.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poke bonnet</b></col>, <cd>a bonnet with a straight,
projecting front.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pokebag</ent><br/
<hw>Poke"bag`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called in
allusion to its baglike nest.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
European long-tailed titmouse; -- called also
<altname>poke-pudding</altname>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poker</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Poke</er> to
push.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who pokes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which pokes or is used in poking, especially a
metal bar or rod used in stirring a fire of coals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A poking-stick.</def> <rj><au>Decker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The poachard.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poker picture</b></col>, <cd>a picture formed in
imitation of bisterwashed drawings, by singeing the surface of wood with
a heated poker or other iron.</cd></cs><-- wood burning? -->
<rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poker</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of uncertain etymol.]</ety> <def>A
game at cards derived from brag, and first played about 1835 in the
Southwestern United States.</def> <rj><au>Johnson's Cyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>A poker hand is played with a poker deck, composed of fifty-two
cards, of thirteeen values, each card value being represented once in
each of four "suits", namely spades, hearts, diamonds, and clubs. The
game is played in many variations, but almost invariably the stage of
decision as to who wins occurs when each player has five cards (or
chooses five cards from some larger number available to him). The winner
usually is the player with the highest-valued hand, but, in some
variations, the winner may be the player with the lowest-valued hand.
The value of a hand is ranked by hand types, representing the
relationships of the cards to each other. [The hand types are ranked by
the probability of receiving such a hand when dealt five cards.] Within
each hand type the value is also ranked by the values of the cards. The
hand types are labeled, in decreasing value: five of a kind; royal
flush; straight flush; four of a kind; full house (coll. full boat, or
boat); flush; straight; three of a kind; two pairs; one pair; and, when
the contending players have no hands of any of the above types, the
player with the highest-valued card wins -- if there is a tie, the
next-highest-valued card of the tied players determines the winner, and
so on. If two players have the same type of hand, the value of the cards
within each type determines the winner; thus, if two players both have
three of a kind (and no other player has a higher type of hand), the
player whose three matched cards have the highest card value is the
winner.</note></p>

<p><ent>Poker</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan. <ets>pokker</ets> the
deuce, devil, also W. <ets>pwci</ets>, a hobgoblin, bugbear, and E.
<ets>puck</ets>.]</ety> <def>Any imagined frightful object, especially
one supposed to haunt the darkness; a bugbear.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pokerish</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"er*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Infested by pokers; adapted to
excite fear; <as>as, a <ex>pokerish</ex> place</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is something <qex>pokerish</qex> about a deserted
dwelling.</q> <rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pokerish</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"er*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stiff like a poker.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poket</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pocket.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pokeweed</ent><br/
<hw>Poke"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Poke</er>, the plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pokey</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"ey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Poky</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poking</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drudging;
servile.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bred to some <qex>poking</qex> profession.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poking-stick</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"ing-stick`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small stick
or rod of steel, formerly used in adjusting the plaits of ruffs.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poky</ent><br/
<hw>Pok"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>pokey</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Confined; cramped.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dull; tedious; uninteresting.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polacca</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lac"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>polacca</ets>, <ets>polaccra</ets>, <ets>polacra</ets>; cf. F.
<ets>polaque</ets>, <ets>polacre</ets>, Sp. <ets>polacre</ets>,]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>polacre</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A vessel with two or three masts, used in the
Mediterranean. The masts are usually of one piece, and without tops,
caps, or crosstrees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>See <er>Polonaise</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polack</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Polander.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polacre</ent><br/
<hw>Po*la"cre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Polacca</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polander</ent><br/
<hw>Po"land*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Poland; a Pole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polar</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polaire</ets>. See <er>Pole</er> of the earth.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to one of the poles of the earth, or of a sphere;
situated near, or proceeding from, one of the poles; <as>as,
<ex>polar</ex> regions; <ex>polar</ex> seas; <ex>polar</ex>
winds.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the magnetic pole, or to the
point to which the magnetic needle is directed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, reckoned from, or
having a common radiating point; <as>as, <ex>polar</ex>
coordinates</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polar axis</b></col>, <cd>that axis of an astronomical
instrument, as an equatorial, which is parallel to the earths axis.</cd>
-- <col><b>Polar bear</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large bear
(<spn>Ursus maritimus</spn> syn. <spn>Thalarctos maritimus</spn>)
inhabiting the arctic regions. It sometimes measures nearly nine feet in
length and weighs 1,600 pounds. It is partially amphibious, very
powerful, and the most carnivorous of all the bears. The fur is white,
tinged with yellow. Called also <altname>White bear</altname>. See
<er>Bear</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Polar body</b></col>, <col><b>Polar
cell</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Polar globule</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>a minute cell which separates by karyokinesis
from the ovum during its maturation. In the maturation of ordinary ova
two polar bodies are formed, but in parthogenetic ova only one. The
first polar body formed is usually larger than the second one, and often
divides into two after its separation from the ovum. Each of the polar
bodies removes maternal chromatin from the ovum to make room for the
chromatin of the fertilizing spermatozoon; but their functions are not
fully understood.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar circles</b></col> <fld>(Astron.
& Geog.)</fld>, <cd>two circles, each at a distance from a pole of the
earth equal to the obliquity of the ecliptic, or about 23<deg/
28<prime/, the northern called the <xex>arctic circle</xex>, and the
southern the <xex>antarctic circle</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar
clock</b></col>, <cd>a tube, containing a polarizing apparatus, turning
on an axis parallel to that of the earth, and indicating the hour of the
day on an hour circle, by being turned toward the plane of maximum
polarization of the light of the sky, which is always 90<deg/ from the
sun.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar coordinates</b></col>. <cd>See under 3d
<er>Coordinate</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar dial</b></col>, <cd>a dial
whose plane is parallel to a great circle passing through the poles of
the earth.</cd> <au>Math. Dict.</au> -- <col><b>Polar
distance</b></col>, <cd>the angular distance of any point on a sphere
from one of its poles, particularly of a heavenly body from the north
pole of the heavens.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Polar equation of a
line</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Polar equation of a
surface</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an equation which expresses the relation
between the polar coordinates of every point of the line or
surface.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar forces</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>,
<cd>forces that are developed and act in pairs, with opposite tendencies
or properties in the two elements, as magnetism, electricity, etc.</cd>
-- <col><b>Polar hare</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large hare of
Arctic America (<spn>Lepus arcticus</spn>), which turns pure white in
winter. It is probably a variety of the common European hare (<spn>Lepus
timidus</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Polar lights</b></col>, <cd>the aurora
borealis or australis.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Polar opposition</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Polaric opposition</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Polar contrast</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Polaric
contrast</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Logic)</fld>, <cd>an opposition or
contrast made by the existence of two opposite conceptions which are the
extremes in a species, as white and black in colors; hence, as great an
opposition or contrast as possible.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar
projection</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Projection</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Polar spherical triangle</b></col> <fld>(Spherics)</fld>, <cd>a
spherical triangle whose three angular points are poles of the sides of
a given triangle. See 4th <er>Pole</er>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Polar
whale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the right whale, or bowhead. See
<er>Whale</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1107 --></p>

<p><ent>Polar</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Conic Sections)</fld>
<def>The right line drawn through the two points of contact of the two
tangents drawn from a given point to a given conic section. The given
point is called the <xex>pole</xex> of the line. If the given point lies
within the curve so that the two tangents become imaginary, there is
still a real polar line which does not meet the curve, but which
possesses other properties of the polar. Thus the focus and directrix
are pole and polar. There are also poles and polar curves to curves of
higher degree than the second, and poles and polar planes to surfaces of
the second degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ar*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polyarchy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polaric</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lar"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polar</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarily</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar*i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a polary
manner; with polarity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lar*im"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polar</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>An instrument for determining the amount of polarization of light,
or the proportion of polarized light, in a partially polarized
ray.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarimetry</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lar*im"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>The art or process of measuring the polarization of
light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polaris</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*la"ris</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Polar</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The polestar. See
<cref>North star</cref>, under <er>North</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polariscope</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lar"i*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polar</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>An instrument consisting essentially of a polarizer and an
analyzer, used for polarizing light, and analyzing its
properties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polariscopic</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lar`i*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the polariscope; obtained by the use of a
polariscope; <as>as, <ex>polariscopic</ex> observations</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polariscopy</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lar*is"co*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>The art or rocess of making observations with the
polariscope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polaristic</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lar*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to,
or exhibiting, poles; having a polar arrangement or disposition; arising
from, or dependent upon, the possession of poles or polar
characteristics; <as>as, <ex>polaristic</ex> antagonism</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarity</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polarit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>That quality or condition of a body in virtue of which it exhibits
opposite, or contrasted, properties or powers, in opposite, or
contrasted, parts or directions; or a condition giving rise to a
contrast of properties corresponding to a contrast of positions, <as>as,
for example, attraction and repulsion in the opposite parts of a magnet,
the dissimilar phenomena corresponding to the different sides of a
polarized ray of light, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A property of the conic sections
by virtue of which a given point determines a corresponding right line
and a given right line determines a corresponding point. See
<er>Polar</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarizable</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar*i`za*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Susceptible of
polarization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarization</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lar*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polarisation</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of polarizing; the state of being polarized,
or of having polarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>A peculiar affection or condition
of the rays of light or heat, in consequence of which they exhibit
different properties in different directions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ If a beam of light, which has been reflected from a
plate of unsilvered glass at an angle of about 56<deg/, be received upon
a second plate of glass similar to the former, and at the same angle of
incidence, the light will be readily reflected when the two planes of
incidence are parallel to each other, but will not be reflected when the
two planes of incidence are perpendicular to each other. The light has,
therefore, acquired new properties by reflection from the first plate of
glass, and is called <xex>polarized light</xex>, while the modification
which the light has experienced by this reflection is called
<xex>polarization</xex>. The plane in which the beam of light is
reflected from the first mirror is called the <xex>plane of
polarization</xex>. The <xex>angle of polarization</xex> is the angle at
which a beam of light must be reflected, in order that the polarization
may be the most complete. The term <xex>polarization</xex> was derived
from the theory of emission, and it was conceived that each luminous
molecule has two poles analogous to the poles of a magnet; but this view
is not now held. According to the undulatory theory, ordinary light is
produced by vibrations transverse or perpendicular to the direction of
the ray, and distributed as to show no distinction as to any particular
direction. But when, by any means, these, vibrations are made to take
place in one plane, the light is said to be <xex>plane polarized</xex>.
If only a portion of the vibrations lie in one plane the ray is said to
be <xex>partially polarized</xex>. Light may be polarized by several
methods other than by reflection, as by refraction through most
crystalline media, or by being transmitted obliquely through several
plates of glass with parallel faces. If a beam of polarized light be
transmitted through a crystal of quartz in the direction of its axis,
the plane of polarization will be changed by an angle proportional to
the thickness of the crystal. This phenomenon is called <xex>rotatory
polarization</xex>. A beam of light reflected from a metallic surface,
or from glass surfaces under certain peculiar conditions, acquires
properties still more complex, its vibrations being no longer
rectilinear, but circular, or elliptical. This phenomenon is called
<xex>circular</xex> or <xex>elliptical polarization</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>An effect produced upon the
plates of a voltaic battery, or the electrodes in an electrolytic cell,
by the deposition upon them of the gases liberated by the action of the
current. It is chiefly due to the hydrogen, and results in an increase
of the resistance, and the setting up of an opposing electro-motive
force, both of which tend materially to weaken the current of the
battery, or that passing through the cell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarize</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Polarized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Polarizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polariser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To communicate polarity to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polarizer</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>That which polarizes; especially, the part of a polariscope which
receives and polarizes the light. It is usually a reflecting plate, or a
plate of some crystal, as tourmaline, or a doubly refracting
crystal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Polaroid. [A trademark of the Polaroid company] 1. polarizing
light; as, a polaroid lens. Used to refer to a smitransparent material
which permits transmission of only plane-polarized light. 2. Relating
to, or able to take, instant photographs; as, a polaroid camera; a
polaroid photo. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polary</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lar*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to a pole;
having a direction toward a pole.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polatouche</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po`la`touche"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A flying squirrel (<spn>Sciuropterus
volans</spn>) native of Northern Europe and Siberia; -- called also
<altname>minene</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polder</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.]</ety> <def>A
tract of low land reclaimed from the sea by of high embankments.</def>
<mark>[Holland & Belgium]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poldway</ent><br/
<hw>Pold"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Poledavy</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of coarse bagging, -- used for
coal sacks.</def> <rj><au>Weale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pole</ent><br/
<hw>Pole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>Pole</ets> a
Pole, <ets>Polen</ets> Poland.]</ety> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Poland; a Polander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pole</ent><br/
<hw>Pole</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[As. <ets>p<amac/l</ets>, L.
<ets>palus</ets>, akin to <ets>pangere</ets> to make fast. Cf.
<er>Pale</er> a stake, <er>Pact</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long,
slender piece of wood; a tall, slender piece of timber; the stem of a
small tree whose branches have been removed; <as>as, specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> A carriage pole, a wooden bar extending from the front axle
of a carriage between the wheel horses, by which the carriage is guided
and held back</as>. <sd>(b)</sd> A flag pole, a pole on which a flag is
supported. <sd>(c)</sd> A Maypole. See <er>Maypole</er>. <sd>(d)</sd> A
barber's pole, a pole painted in stripes, used as a sign by barbers and
hairdressers. <sd>(e)</sd> A pole on which climbing beans, hops, or
other vines, are trained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A measuring stick; also, a measure of length equal
to 5<?/ yards, or a square measure equal to 30<?/ square yards; a rod; a
perch.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pole bean</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any kind of
bean which is customarily trained on poles, as the scarlet runner or the
Lima bean.</cd> -- <col><b>Pole flounder</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a large deep-water flounder (<spn>Glyptocephalus cynoglossus</spn>),
native of the northern coasts of Europe and America, and much esteemed
as a food fish; -- called also <altname>craig flounder</altname>, and
<altname>pole fluke</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pole lathe</b></col>,
<cd>a simple form of lathe, or a substitute for a lathe, in which the
work is turned by means of a cord passing around it, one end being
fastened to the treadle, and the other to an elastic pole above.</cd> --
<col><b>Pole mast</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a mast formed from a
single piece or from a single tree.</cd> -- <col><b>Pole of a
lens</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>the point where the principal axis
meets the surface.</cd> -- <col><b>Pole plate</b></col>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a horizontal timber resting on the tiebeams of a
roof and receiving the ends of the rafters. It differs from the
<xex>plate</xex> in not resting on the wall.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pole</ent><br/
<hw>Pole</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Poled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To furnish with poles
for support; <as>as, to <ex>pole</ex> beans or hops</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To convey on poles; <as>as, to <ex>pole</ex> hay
into a barn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To impel by a pole or poles, as a boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stir, as molten glass, with a pole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pole</ent><br/
<hw>Pole</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>polus</ets>, Gr. <?/ a pivot
or hinge on which anything turns, an axis, a pole; akin to <?/ to move:
cf. F. <ets>p<ocir/le</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Either extremity of
an axis of a sphere; especially, one of the extremities of the earth's
axis; <as>as, the north <ex>pole</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Spherics)</fld> <def>A point upon the surface of a
sphere equally distant from every part of the circumference of a great
circle; or the point in which a diameter of the sphere perpendicular to
the plane of such circle meets the surface. Such a point is called the
<xex>pole</xex> of that circle; <as>as, the <ex>pole</ex> of the
horizon; the <ex>pole</ex> of the ecliptic; the <ex>pole</ex> of a given
meridian.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>One of the opposite or
contrasted parts or directions in which a polar force is manifested; a
point of maximum intensity of a force which has two such points, or
which has polarity; <as>as, the <ex>poles</ex> of a magnet; the north
<ex>pole</ex> of a needle.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The firmament; the sky.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shoots against the dusky <qex>pole</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>See <er>Polarity</er>, and
<er>Polar</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Magnetic pole</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Magnetic</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Poles of the earth</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Terrestrial poles</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Geog.)</fld>, <cd>the two opposite points on the earth's surface
through which its axis passes.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Poles of the
heavens</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Celestial poles</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>the two opposite points in the celestial sphere which coincide with
the earth's axis produced, and about which the heavens appear to
revolve.</cd><-- sic. something seems missing. is "produced"
superfluous? --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poleaxe</ent><br/
<ent>Poleax</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pole"ax`</hw>, <hw>Pole"axe`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pollax</ets>; cf. OD. <ets>pollexe</ets>.
See <er>Poll</er> head, and Ax.]</ety> <def>Anciently, a kind of
battle-ax with a long handle; later, an ax or hatchet with a short
handle, and a head variously patterned; -- used by soldiers, and also by
sailors in boarding a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>poleax</ent><br/
<ent>poleaxe</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>poleax</hw>, <hw>poleaxe</hw></mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To fell with or as if with a poleax; -- often used
figuratively; <as>as, the entire department was <ex>poleaxed</ex> after
the takeover</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Stunned; astonished;; <as>as, we couldn't speak,
<ex>poleaxed</ex> by the sight in front of us</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polecat</ent><br/
<hw>Pole"cat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr. F.
<ets>poule</ets> hen, and originally, a poultry cat, because it feeds on
poultry. See <er>Poultry</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A small European carnivore of the Weasel family (<spn>Putorius
f<oe/tidus</spn>). Its scent glands secrete a substance of an
exceedingly disagreeable odor. Called also <altname>fitchet</altname>,
<altname>foulmart</altname>, and <altname>European
ferret</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The zorilla. The name is also
applied to other allied species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poledavy</ent><br/
<hw>Pole"da`vy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymology
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A sort of coarse canvas; poldway.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poleless</ent><br/
<hw>Pole"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a pole; <as>as, a
<ex>poleless</ex> chariot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemarch</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"e*march</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/ war
+ <?/ leader, from <?/ to be first.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld>
<def>In Athens, originally, the military commander-in-chief; but,
afterward, a civil magistrate who had jurisdiction in respect of
strangers and sojourners. In other Grecian cities, a high military and
civil officer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemic</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lem"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ warlike,
fr.<?/ war: cf. F. <ets>pol<eacute/mique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to controversy; maintaining, or involving,
controversy; controversial; disputative; <as>as, a <ex>polemic</ex>
discourse or essay; <ex>polemic</ex> theology.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Engaged in, or addicted to, polemics, or to
controversy; disputations; <as>as, a <ex>polemic</ex> writer</as>.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemic</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lem"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who writes in
support of one opinion, doctrine, or system, in opposition to another;
one skilled in polemics; a controversialist; a disputant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sarcasms and invectives of the young <qex>polemic</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A polemic argument or controversy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemical</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Polemic;
controversial; disputatious.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Po*lem"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Polemical</qex> and impertinent disputations.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemicist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lem"i*cist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A polemic.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemics</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lem"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pol<eacute/mique</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or practice of
disputation or controversy, especially on religious subjects; that
branch of theological science which pertains to the history or conduct
of ecclesiastical controversy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemist</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"e*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A polemic.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemoniaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`e*mo`ni*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a natural order of plants
(<ord>Polemoniace<ae/</ord>), which includes <gen>Polemonium</gen>,
<gen>Phlox</gen>, <gen>Gilia</gen>, and a few other genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemonium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`e*mo"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr.<?/ a kind of plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
gamopetalous perennial herbs, including the Jacob's ladder and the Greek
valerian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lem"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ war +
<ets>-scope</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pol<eacute/moscope</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
opera glass or field glass with an oblique mirror arranged for seeing
objects that do not lie directly before the eye; -- called also
<altname>diagonal opera glass</altname>, or <altname>side opera
glass</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polemy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"e*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polemic</er>.]</ety> <def>Warfare; war; hence, contention;
opposition.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polenta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*len"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>polenta</ets> peeled barley.]</ety> <def>Pudding made of Indian
meal; also, porridge made of chestnut meal.</def>
<mark>[Italy]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poler</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
poles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poler</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An extortioner. See
<er>Poller</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bacon.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polestar</ent><br/
<hw>Pole"star`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Polaris, or the north star. See <cref>North star</cref>, under
<er>North</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A guide or director.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polewards</ent><br/
<hw>Pole"wards</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward a pole of
the earth.</def> <ldquo/The regions further <xex>polewards</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Whewell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polewig</ent><br/
<hw>Pole"wig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Polliwig</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European spotted
goby (<spn>Gobius minutus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>pollybait</altname>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poley</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Poly</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poley</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ley</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without horns; polled.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/That <xex>poley</xex> heifer.<rdquo/
<rj><au>H. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polianite</ent><br/
<hw>Po"li*a*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
become gray.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Manganese dioxide, occurring
in tetragonal crystals nearly as hard as quartz.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pollicate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Police</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lice"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>politia</ets> the condition of a state, government, administration,
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to be a citizen, to govern or administer a state, fr.
<?/ citizen, fr. <?/ city; akin to Skr. <ets>pur</ets>, <ets>puri</ets>.
Cf. <er>Policy</er> polity, <er>Polity</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
judicial and executive system, for the government of a city, town, or
district, for the preservation of rights, order, cleanliness, health,
etc., and for the enforcement of the laws and prevention of crime; the
administration of the laws and regulations of a city, incorporated town,
or borough.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which concerns the order of the community; the
internal regulation of a state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The organized body of civil officers in a city,
town, or district, whose particular duties are the preservation of good
order, the prevention and detection of crime, and the enforcement of the
laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>Military police, the body of
soldiers detailed to preserve civil order and attend to sanitary
arrangements in a camp or garrison.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The cleaning of a camp or garrison, or the state <?/
a camp as to cleanliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Police commissioner</b></col>, <cd>a civil officer,
usually one of a board, commissioned to regulate and control the
appointment, duties, and discipline of the police.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Police constable</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Police
officer</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a policeman.</cd> -- <col><b>Police
court</b></col>, <cd>a minor court to try persons brought before it by
the police.</cd> -- <col><b>Police inspector</b></col>, <cd>an officer
of police ranking next below a superintendent.</cd> -- <col><b>Police
jury</b></col>, <cd>a body of officers who collectively exercise
jurisdiction in certain cases of police, as levying taxes, etc.; -- so
called in Louisiana.</cd> <au>Bouvier.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Police
justice</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Police
magistrate</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a judge of a police court.</cd> --
<col><b>Police offenses</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>minor offenses
against the order of the community, of which a police court may have
final jurisdiction.</cd> -- <col><b>Police station</b></col>, <cd>the
headquarters of the police, or of a section of them; the place where the
police assemble for orders, and to which they take arrested
persons.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Police</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lice"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Policed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Policing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To keep in order by
police.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To make clean; <as>as, to
<ex>police</ex> a camp</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policed</ent><br/
<hw>Po*liced"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Regulated by laws
for the maintenance of peace and order, enforced by organized
administration.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>policed</xex> kingdom.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policeman</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lice"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Policemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A member of a body of
police; a constable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Police power</ent><br/
<hw>Police power</hw>. <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The inherent power of a
government to regulate its police affairs.</def> <note>The term
<xex>police power</xex> is not definitely fixed in meaning. In the
earlier cases in the United States it was used as including the whole
power of internal government, or the powers of government inherent in
every sovereignty to the extent of its dominions (<au>11 Peters (U. S.)
102</au>). The later cases have excepted from its domain the development
and administration of private law. Modern political science defines the
power as a branch of internal administration in the exercise of which
the executive should move within the lines of general principles
prescribed by the constitution or the legislature, and in the exercise
of which the most local governmental organizations should participate as
far as possible (<au>Burgess</au>). Under this limitation the police
power, as affecting persons, is the power of the state to protect the
public against the abuse of individual liberty, that is, to restrain the
individual in the exercise of his rights when such exercise becomes a
danger to the community. The tendency of judicial and popular usage is
towards this narrower definition.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policial</ent><br/
<hw>Po*li"cial</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to the
police.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1108 --></p>

<p><ent>Policied</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*cied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Policed.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Policies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>politia</ets>, Gr.
<?/; cf. F. <ets>police</ets>, Of. <ets>police</ets>. See
<er>Police</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Civil
polity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The settled method by which the government and
affairs of a nation are, or may be, administered; a system of public or
official administration, as designed to promote the external or internal
prosperity of a state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The method by which any institution is administered;
system of management; course.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Management or administration based on temporal or
material interest, rather than on principles of equity or honor; hence,
worldly wisdom; dexterity of management; cunning; stratagem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Prudence or wisdom in the management of public and
private affairs; wisdom; sagacity; wit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The very <qex>policy</qex> of a hostess, finding his purse so far
above his clothes, did detect him.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Motive; object; inducement.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>policy</qex> have you to bestow a benefit where it is
counted an injury?</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Polity</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*cy</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Policied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Policying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To regulate by laws; to reduce
to order.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Policying</xex> of
cities.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Policy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*cy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>police</ets>; cf. Pr.
<ets>polissia</ets>, Sp. <ets>p<oacute/lizia</ets>, It.
<ets>p<oacute/lizza</ets>; of uncertain origin; cf. L. <ets>pollex</ets>
thumb (as being used in pressing the seal), in LL. also, seal; or cf.
LL. <ets>politicum</ets>, <ets>poleticum</ets>, <ets>polecticum</ets>,
L. <ets>polyptychum</ets>, account book, register, fr. Gr. <?/ having
many folds or leaves; <?/ many + <?/ fold, leaf, from <?/ to fold; or
cf. LL. <ets>apodixa</ets> a receipt.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A ticket
or warrant for money in the public funds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The writing or instrument in which a contract of
insurance is embodied; an instrument in writing containing the terms and
conditions on which one party engages to indemnify another against loss
arising from certain hazards, perils, or risks to which his person or
property may be exposed. See <er>Insurance</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A method of gambling by betting as to what numbers
will be drawn in a lottery; <as>as, to play
<ex>policy</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Interest policy</b></col>, <cd>a policy that shows by its
form that the assured has a real, substantial interest in the matter
insured.</cd> -- <col><b>Open policy</b></col>, <cd>one in which the
value of the goods or property insured is not mentioned.</cd> --
<col><b>Policy book</b></col>, <cd>a book to contain a record of
insurance policies.</cd> -- <col><b>Policy holder</b></col>, <cd>one to
whom an insurance policy has been granted.</cd> -- <col><b>Policy
shop</b></col>, <cd>a gambling place where one may bet on the numbers
which will be drawn in lotteries.</cd> -- <col><b>Valued
policy</b></col>, <cd>one in which the value of the goods, property, or
interest insured is specified.</cd> -- <col><b>Wager policy</b></col>,
<cd>a policy that shows on the face of it that the contract it embodies
is a pretended insurance, founded on an ideal risk, where the insured
has no interest in anything insured.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poling</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pole</er> a
stick.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of supporting or of propelling by
means of a pole or poles; <as>as, the <ex>poling</ex> of beans; the
<ex>poling</ex> of a boat.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gardening)</fld> <def>The operation of dispersing
worm casts over the walks with poles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of the poles or planks used in upholding the
side earth in excavating a tunnel, ditch, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polish</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Pole</er> a
Polander.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Poland or its
inhabitants.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The language of the
Poles.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polish</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Polished</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Polishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>polir</ets>,
L. <ets>polire</ets>. Cf. <er>Polite</er>, <er>-ish</er>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make smooth and glossy, usually by friction; to
burnish; to overspread with luster; <as>as, to <ex>polish</ex> glass,
marble, metals, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to refine; to wear off the rudeness,
coarseness, or rusticity of; to make elegant and polite; <as>as, to
<ex>polish</ex> life or manners</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To polish off</b></col>, <cd>to finish completely, as an
adversary.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>W. H.
Russell.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polish</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become smooth, as from
friction; to receive a gloss; to take a smooth and glossy surface;
<as>as, steel <ex>polishes</ex> well</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polish</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A smooth, glossy
surface, usually produced by friction; a gloss or luster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Another prism of clearer glass and better <qex>polish</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything used to produce a gloss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Refinement; elegance of manners.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This Roman <qex>polish</qex> and this smooth behavior.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polishable</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
polished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polished</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ished</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made smooth and
glossy, as by friction; hence, highly finished; refined; polite; <as>as,
<ex>polished</ex> plate; <ex>polished</ex> manners; <ex>polished</ex>
verse.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polishedness</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ished*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
polished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polisher</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, polishes; also, that which is used in polishing.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polishing</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish*ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Polish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polishing iron</b></col>, <cd>an iron burnisher; esp., a
small smoothing iron used in laundries.</cd> -- <col><b>Polishing
slate</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A gray or yellow slate, found in
Bohemia and Auvergne, and used for polishing glass, marble, and
metals</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A kind of hone or whetstone; hone
slate.</cd> -- <col><b>Polishing snake</b></col>, <cd>a tool used in
cleaning lithographic stones.</cd> -- <col><b>Polishing wheel</b></col>,
<cd>a wheel or disk coated with, or composed of, abrading material, for
polishing a surface.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polishment</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ish*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
polishing, or the state of being polished.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polissoir</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po`lis`soir"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A polishing or grinding implement or
instrument.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Glass Making)</fld> <def>A tool consisting of a
flat wooden block with a long iron handle, used for flattening out split
cylinders of blown glass.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polite</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lite"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Politer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Politest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>politus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>polire</ets> to polish: cf. F.
<ets>poli</ets>. See <er>Polish</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Smooth; polished.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rays of light falling on a <qex>polite</qex> surface.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Smooth and refined in behavior or manners; well
bred; courteous; complaisant; obliging; civil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He marries, bows at court, and grows <qex>polite</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Characterized by refinement, or a high degree of
finish; <as>as, <ex>polite</ex> literature</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Polished; refined; well bred; courteous; affable;
urbane; civil; courtly; elegant; genteel.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polite</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lite"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To polish; to refine; to render
polite.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politely</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lite"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
polished manner; so as to be smooth or glossy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a polite manner; with politeness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politeness</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lite"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>High finish;
smoothness; burnished elegance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being polite; refinement of
manners; urbanity; courteous behavior; complaisance; obliging
attentions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Courtesy; good breeding; refinement; urbanity;
courteousness; affability; complaisance; civility; gentility;
courtliness.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Politeness</er>, <er>Courtesy</er>.
<xex>Politeness</xex> denotes that ease and gracefulness of manners
which first sprung up in cities, connected with a desire to please
others by anticipating their wants and wishes, and studiously avoiding
whatever might give them pain. <xex>Courtesy</xex> is, etymologically,
the <xex>politeness</xex> of courts. It displays itself in the address
and manners; it is shown more especially in receiving and entertaining
others, and is a union of dignified complaisance and
kindness.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politesse</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`i*tesse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Politeness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>politicus</ets> political, Gr. <?/ belonging to the citizens or to
the state, fr.<?/ citizen: cf. F. <ets>politique</ets>. See
<er>Police</er>, and cf. <er>ePolitical</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to polity, or civil government; political; <as>as, the
body <ex>politic</ex></as>. See under <er>Body</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He with his people made all but one <qex>politic</qex> body.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or promoting, a policy, especially a
national policy; well-devised; adapted to its end, whether right or
wrong; -- said of things; <as>as, a <ex>politic</ex> treaty</as>.</def>
<ldquo/Enrich'd with <xex>politic</xex> grave counsel.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Sagacious in promoting a policy; ingenious in
devising and advancing a system of management; devoted to a scheme or
system rather than to a principle; hence, in a good sense, wise;
prudent; sagacious; and in a bad sense, artful; unscrupulous; cunning;
-- said of persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Politic</qex> with my friend, smooth with mine enemy.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wise; prudent; sagacious; discreet; provident;
wary; artful; cunning.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`i*tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A politician.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Swiftly the <qex>politic</qex> goes; is it dark? he borrows a
lantern;<br/
Slowly the statesman and sure, guiding his feet by the stars.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Political</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lit"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having, or conforming to, a settled system of administration.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>political</xex> government.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to public policy, or to politics;
relating to affairs of state or administration; <as>as, a
<ex>political</ex> writer</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>political</xex>
state of Europe.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Paley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a party, or to parties, in the
state; <as>as, his <ex>political</ex> relations were with the
Whigs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Politic; wise; also, artful.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Political economy</b></col>, <cd>that branch of political
science or philosophy which treats of the sources, and methods of
production and preservation, of the material wealth and prosperity of
nations.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politicalism</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lit"i*cal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Zeal or party
spirit in politics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politically</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lit"i*cal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
political manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Politicly; artfully.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Knolles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politicaster</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lit"i*cas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>politicastro</ets>.]</ety> <def>A petty politician; a pretender in
politics.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politician</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`i*ti"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>politicien</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One versed or experienced in the science of
government; one devoted to politics; a statesman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While empiric <qex>politicians</qex> use deceit.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One primarily devoted to his own advancement in
public office, or to the success of a political party; -- used in a
depreciatory sense; one addicted or attached to politics as managed by
parties (see <er>Politics</er>, 2); a schemer; an intriguer; <as>as, a
mere <ex>politician</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a scurvy <qex>politician</qex>, seem<br/
To see the things thou dost not.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>politician</qex> . . . ready to do anything that he
apprehends for his advantage.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politician</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`i*ti"cian</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cunning; using artifice;
politic; artful.</def> <ldquo/Ill-meaning <xex>politician</xex>
lords.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politicist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lit"i*cist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A political
writer.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politicly</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*tic*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a politic
manner; sagaciously; shrewdly; artfully.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politics</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>politique</ets>, Gr. <?/ (sc.<?/). See <er>Politic</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The science of government; that part of ethics which
has to do with the regulation and government of a nation or state, the
preservation of its safety, peace, and prosperity, the defense of its
existence and rights against foreign control or conquest, the
augmentation of its strength and resources, and the protection of its
citizens in their rights, with the preservation and improvement of their
morals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The management of a political party; the conduct and
contests of parties with reference to political measures or the
administration of public affairs; the advancement of candidates to
office; in a bad sense, artful or dishonest management to secure the
success of political candidates or parties; political
trickery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When we say that two men are talking <qex>politics</qex>, we often
mean that they are wrangling about some mere party question.</q>
<rj><qau>F. W. Robertson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politize</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To play the
politician; to dispute as politicians do.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politure</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>politura</ets>, fr. <ets>polire</ets> to polish. See
<er>Polish</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>Polish; gloss.
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <xex>Donne</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polity</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Polities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>politia</ets>, Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>politie</ets>. See 1st <er>Policy</er>,
<er>Police</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The form or constitution of the
civil government of a nation or state; the framework or organization by
which the various departments of government are combined into a
systematic whole.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone. Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: The form or constitution by which any
institution is organized; the recognized principles which lie at the
foundation of any human institution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor is possible that any form of <qex>polity</qex>, much less
<qex>polity</qex> ecclesiastical, should be good, unless God himself be
author of it.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Policy; art; management.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Policy.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Polity</er>,
<er>Policy</er>. These two words were originally the same.
<xex>Polity</xex> is now confined to the structure of a government; as,
civil or ecclesiastical <xex>polity</xex>; while <xex>policy</xex> is
applied to the scheme of management of public affairs with reference to
some aim or result; as, foreign or domestic <xex>policy</xex>.
<xex>Policy</xex> has the further sense of skillful or cunning
management.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Politzerization</ent><br/
<hw>Po*litz`er*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The act of inflating the middle ear by blowing
air up the nose during the act of swallowing; -- so called from
<person>Prof. <etsep>Politzer</etsep></person> of <city>Vienna</city>,
who first practiced it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polive</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pulley.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polka</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pol. <ets>Polka</ets>
a Polish woman: cf. F. & G. <ets>polka</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
dance of Polish origin, but now common everywhere. It is performed by
two persons in common time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A lively Bohemian or Polish dance
tune in 2-4 measure, with the third quaver accented.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polka jacket</b></col>, <cd>a kind of knit jacket worn by
women.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poll</ent><br/
<hw>Poll</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>Polly</ets>,
The proper name.]</ety> <def>A parrot; -- familiarly so
called.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poll</ent><br/
<hw>Poll</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ the many, the rabble.]</ety>
<def>One who does not try for honors, but is content to take a degree
merely; a passman.</def> <mark>[Cambridge Univ., Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poll</ent><br/
<hw>Poll</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG.
<ets>polle</ets> the head, the crest of a bird, the top of a tree, OD.
<ets>pol</ets>, <ets>polle</ets>, Dan. <ets>puld</ets> the crown of a
hat.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The head; the back part of the head.</def>
<ldquo/All flaxen was his <xex>poll</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A number or aggregate of heads; a list or register
of heads or individuals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are the greater <qex>poll</qex>, and in true fear<br/
They gave us our demands.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The muster file, rotten and sound, upon my life, amounts not to
fifteen thousand <qex>poll</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Specifically, the register of the names of electors
who may vote in an election.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The casting or recording of the votes of registered
electors; <as>as, the close of the <ex>poll</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All soldiers quartered in place are to remove . . . and not to
return till one day after the <qex>poll</qex> is ended.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The place where the votes are cast
or recorded; <as>as, to go to the <ex>polls</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The broad end of a hammer; the but of an
ax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European chub. See
<er>Pollard</er>, 3 <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poll book</b></col>, <cd>a register of persons entitled
to vote at an election.</cd> -- <col><b>Poll evil</b></col>
<fld>(Far.)</fld>, <cd>an inflammatory swelling or abscess on a horse's
head, confined beneath the great ligament of the neck.</cd> --
<col><b>Poll pick</b></col> <fld>(Mining)</fld>, <cd>a pole having a
heavy spike on the end, forming a kind of crowbar.</cd> -- <col><b>Poll
tax</b></col>, <cd>a tax levied by the head, or poll; a capitation
tax.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poll</ent><br/
<hw>Poll</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Polled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Polling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To remove the poll or
head of; hence, to remove the top or end of; to clip; to lop; to shear;
<as>as, to <ex>poll</ex> the head; to <ex>poll</ex> a
tree.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he [Absalom] <qex>pollled</qex> his head.</q> <rj><qau>2 Sam.
xiv. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His death did so grieve them that they <qex>polled</qex>
themselves; they clipped off their horse and mule's hairs.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. North.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut off; to remove by clipping, shearing, etc.;
to mow or crop; -- sometimes with <xex>off</xex>; <as>as, to
<ex>poll</ex> the hair; to <ex>poll</ex> wool; to <ex>poll</ex>
grass.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who, as he <qex>polled</qex> off his dart's head, so sure he had
decreed<br/
That all the counsels of their war he would <qex>poll</qex> off like
it.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To extort from; to plunder; to strip.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which <qex>polls</qex> and pills the poor in piteous wise.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To impose a tax upon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To pay as one's personal tax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The man that <qex>polled</qex> but twelve pence for his head.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To enter, as polls or persons, in a list or
register; to enroll, esp. for purposes of taxation; to enumerate one by
one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Polling</qex> the reformed churches whether they equalize in
number those of his three kingdoms.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To register or deposit, as a vote; to elicit or call
forth, as votes or voters; <as>as, he <ex>polled</ex> a hundred votes
more than his opponent</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>poll</qex> for points of faith his trusty vote.</q>
<rj><qau>Tickell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To cut or shave smooth or even; to
cut in a straight line without indentation; <as>as, a <ex>polled</ex>
deed</as>. See <er>Dee<?/ poll</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 9. to inquire (of a group of persons) to learn their opinion on
some subject. Similar to poll a jury, but often used to determine the
opinions of a group by polling a portion of that group. Thus, opinion
poll. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To poll a jury</b></col>, <cd>to call upon each member of
the jury to answer individually as to his concurrence in a verdict which
has been rendered.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poll</ent><br/
<hw>Poll</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To vote at an election.</def>
<rj><au>Beaconsfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollack</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. & D.
<ets>pollack</ets>, and Gael. <ets>pollag</ets> a little pool, a sort of
fish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A marine gadoid food
fish of Europe (<spn>Pollachius virens</spn>). Called also
<altname>greenfish</altname>, <altname>greenling</altname>,
<altname>lait</altname>, <altname>leet</altname>,
<altname>lob</altname>, <altname>lythe</altname>, and <altname>whiting
pollack</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The American pollock; the
coalfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollage</ent><br/
<hw>Poll"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A head or poll tax;
hence, extortion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollan</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gael.
<ets>pollag</ets> a kind of fish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A lake
whitefish (<spn>Coregonus pollan</spn>), native of Ireland. In
appearance it resembles a herring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollard</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Poll</er>
the head.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A tree having its top cut off at some
height above the ground, that may throw out branches.</def>
<rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A clipped coin; also, a counterfeit.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A fish, the
chub.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A stag that has cast its antlers.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A hornless animal (cow or sheep).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollard</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lard</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pollarded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pollarding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To lop the tops of, as trees;
to poll; <as>as, to <ex>pollard</ex> willows</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollax</ent><br/
<hw>Poll"ax`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A poleax.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polled</ent><br/
<hw>Polled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deprived of a poll, or
of something belonging to the poll. Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> Lopped;
-- said of trees having their tops cut off. <sd>(b)</sd> Cropped; hence,
bald; -- said of a person. <ldquo/The <xex>polled</xex> bachelor.<rdquo/
<au>Beau. & Fl.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> Having cast the antlers; -- said of a
stag. <sd>(d)</sd> Without horns; <as>as, <ex>polled</ex> cattle;
<ex>polled</ex> sheep.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollen</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"len</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pollen</ets>
fine flour, fine dust; cf. Gr. <?/]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Fine bran or
flour.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The fecundating dustlike cells of
the anthers of flowers. See <er>Flower</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Filament</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pollen grain</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a particle
or call of pollen.</cd> -- <col><b>Pollen mass</b></col>, <cd>a
pollinium.</cd> <au>Gray.</au> -- <col><b>Pollen sac</b></col>, <cd>a
compartment of an anther containing pollen, -- usually there are four in
each anther.</cd> -- <col><b>Pollen tube</b></col>, <cd>a slender tube
which issues from the pollen grain on its contact with the stigma, which
it penetrates, thus conveying, it is supposed, the fecundating matter of
the grain to the ovule.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1109 --></p>

<p><ent>Pollenarious</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`len*a"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of
meal or pollen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollened</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lened</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered with
pollen.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polleniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`len*if"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pollen</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Producing pollen; polliniferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollenin</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"len*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>poll<eacute/nine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance
found in the pollen of certain plants.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollenize</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"len*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pollenized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pollenizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
supply with pollen; to impregnate with pollen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poller</ent><br/
<hw>Poll"er</hw> <pr>(p<omac/l"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Poll</er>]</ety> <def>One who polls;</def>
<specif>specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One who polls or lops
trees.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who polls or cuts hair; a
barber.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One who extorts or
plunders.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bacon.</au> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>One who registers voters, or one who enters his name as a
voter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollex</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol"lex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pollices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[the thumb.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The first, or preaxial, digit of the fore limb,
corresponding to the hallux in the hind limb; the thumb. In birds, the
pollex is the joint which bears the bastard wing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"li*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pollex</ets>, <ets>pollicis</ets>, a thumb.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a curved projection or spine on the inner
side of a leg joint; -- said of insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollicitation</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lic`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pollicitatio</ets>, fr. <ets>pollicitari</ets> to promise, v.
intens. fr. <ets>polliceri</ets> to promise: cf. F.
<ets>pollicitation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A voluntary
engagement, or a paper containing it; a promise.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Roman Law)</fld> <def>A promise without mutuality;
a promise which has not been accepted by the person to whom it is
made.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"li*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pollinose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"li*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>To apply pollen to (a stigma).</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pol`li*na"tion</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollinctor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol*linc"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>pollingere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>One who
prepared corpses for the funeral.</def><-- undertaker, funeral director
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polling</ent><br/
<hw>Poll"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Poll</er>
the head.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of topping, lopping, or
cropping, as trees or hedges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plunder, or extortion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>E. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of voting, or of registering a
vote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polling booth</b></col>, <cd>a temporary structure where
the voting at an election is done; a polling place.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polliniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`li*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pollen</ets>, <ets>-inis</ets>, pollen + <ets>-ferous</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pollinif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Producing
pollen; polleniferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollinium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol*lin"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pollinia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[<er>NL</er>. See
<er>Pollen</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A coherent mass of
pollen, as in the milkweed and most orchids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollinose</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"li*nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pollen</ets>, <ets>-inis</ets>, dust.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the surface covered with a fine yellow dust, like
pollen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polliwog</ent><br/
<ent>Polliwig</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol"li*wig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pol"li*wog</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>polwigle</ets>. Cf.
<er>Poll</er> head, and <er>Wiggle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A tadpole; -- called also <altname>purwiggy</altname> and
<altname>porwigle</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollock</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pollack</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marine gadoid fish
(<spn>Pollachius carbonarius</spn>), native both of the European and
American coasts. It is allied to the cod, and like it is salted and
dried. In England it is called <altname>coalfish</altname>,
<altname>lob</altname>, <altname>podley</altname>,
<altname>podling</altname>, <altname>pollack</altname>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollucite</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"lu*cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pollux</er>, and 4th <er>Castor</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A colorless transparent mineral, resembling quartz, occurring with
castor or castorite on the island of Elba. It is a silicate of alumina
and c<ae/sia. Called also <altname>pollux</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollute</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Polluted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Polluting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>pollutus</ets>, p. p.
of <ets>polluere</ets> to defile, to pollute, from a prep. appearing
only in comp. + <ets>luere</ets> to wash. See <er>Position</er>,
<er>Lave</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make foul, impure, or unclean;
to defile; to taint; to soil; to desecrate; -- used of physical or moral
defilement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The land was <qex>polluted</qex> with blood.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. cvi.
38</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wickedness . . . hath <qex>polluted</qex> the whole earth.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Esd. xv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To violate sexually; to debauch; to
dishonor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Jewish Law)</fld> <def>To render ceremonially
unclean; to disqualify or unfit for sacred use or service, or for social
intercourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Neither shall ye <qex>pollute</qex> the holy things of the
children of Israel, lest ye die.</q> <rj><qau>Num. xviii.
32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They have <qex>polluted</qex> themselves with blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Lam. iv. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To defile; soil; contaminate; corrupt; taint;
vitiate; debauch; dishonor; ravish.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollute</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lute"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pollutus</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Polluted.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polluted</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lut"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Defiled; made unclean or impure;
debauched.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pol*lut"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Pol*lut"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polluter</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lut"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pollutes.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polluting</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lut"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adapted or tending to pollute;
causing defilement or pollution.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pol*lut"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollution</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*lu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pollutio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pollution</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of polluting, or the state of being polluted (in any sense
of the verb); defilement; uncleanness; impurity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The emission of semen, or sperm,
at other times than in sexual intercourse.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollux</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol"lux</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the twin
brother of castor; also, the constellation.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A fixed star of the second magnitude, in the
constellation Gemini. Cf. 3d <er>Castor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pollucite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polly</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman's name; also, a
popular name for a parrot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pollywog</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ly*wog</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A polliwig.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polo</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of Eastern origin; --
properly, the ball used in the game.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A game of
ball of Eastern origin, resembling hockey, with the players on
horseback.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A similar game played on the ice, or on a prepared
floor, by players wearing skates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A game similar to hockey played by
swimmers.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polo</ent><br/
<hw>Po"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., an air or popular
song in Andalucia.]</ety> <def>A Spanish gypsy dance characterized by
energetic movements of the body while the feet merely shuffle or glide,
with unison singing and rhythmic clapping of hands.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polonaise</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lo*naise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>polonais</ets>, <ets>polonaise</ets>, Polish.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the Poles, or to Poland.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Polonese</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polonaise</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lo*naise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>Polonese</ets> and <ets>Polonoise</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The Polish language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An article of dress for women, consisting of a body
and an outer skirt in one piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A stately Polish dance tune, in
3-4 measure, beginning always on the beat with a quaver followed by a
crotchet, and closing on the beat after a strong accent on the second
beat; also, a dance adapted to such music; a polacca.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polonese</ent><br/
<hw>Po`lo*nese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polonaise</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polonium</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lo"ni*um</hw> <pr>(p<osl/*l<omac/"n<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So named after Poland, in L. form
<ets>Polonia</ets>, one of the discoverers being a Pole.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A radioactive chemical element, discovered by M.
and MMe. Curie in pitchblende, and originally called <altname>radium
F</altname>. It has atomic number 84 and an atomic weight of 210. It is
a very rare natural element, having an abundance in uranium ores only
0.2% that of radium. It is closely related chemically to bismuth. It
emits only alpha rays, and has a half-life of 138 days. It is thus more
unstable than radium, and a milligram of polonium emits as many alpha
particles as 5 grams of radium. Twenty-seven isotopes are known, with
atomic masses from 192 to 218. At present a more practical method of
preparation than isolation from ores is the preparation by neutron
bombardment of bismuth in a nuclear reactor, and it may be obtained
commercially by users having an appropriate permit.</def>
<au>[HBCF61]</au><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polony</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lo"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. corrupt. fr.
<ets>Bologna</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of sausage made of meat partly
cooked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polron</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pauldron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polt</ent><br/
<hw>Polt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. E. <ets>pelt</ets>,
L. <ets>pultare</ets> to beat, strike.]</ety> <def>A blow or
thump.</def> <au>Halliwell.</au> -- <def2><pos>a.</pos>
<def>Distorted.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pot foot</b></col>, <cd>a distorted foot.</cd>
<rj><au>Sir T. Herbert.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polt-footed</ent><br/
<ent>Polt-foot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Polt"-foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Polt"-foot`ed</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a distorted foot, or a
clubfoot or clubfeet.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poltroon</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*troon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poltron</ets>, from It. <ets>poltrone</ets> an idle fellow,
sluggard, coward, <ets>poltro</ets> idle, lazy, also, bed, fr. OHG.
<ets>polstar</ets>, <ets>bolstar</ets>, cushion, G. <ets>polster</ets>,
akin to E. <ets>bolster</ets>. See <er>Bolster</er>.]</ety> <def>An
arrant coward; a dastard; a craven; a mean-spirited wretch.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poltroon</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*troon"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Base; vile; contemptible;
cowardly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poltroonery</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*troon"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poltronnerie</ets>; cf. It. <ets>poltroneria</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Cowardice; want of spirit; pusillanimity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poltroonish</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*troon"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a poltroon;
cowardly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polverine</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ve*rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>polverino</ets>, fr. <ets>polvere <?/</ets>ust, L.
<ets>pulvis</ets>, <ets>-veris</ets>. See <er>Powder</er>.]</ety>
<def>Glassmaker's ashes; a kind of potash or pearlash, brought from the
Levant and Syria, -- used in the manufacture of fine glass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polwig</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"wig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
polliwig. <xex>Holland</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poly-</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See <er>Full</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A combining form or prefix from Gr.
<grk>poly`s</grk>, <xex>many</xex>; <as>as, <ex>poly</ex>gon, a figure
of many angles; <ex>poly</ex>atomic, having many atoms;
<ex>poly</ex>chord, <ex>poly</ex>conic.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poly</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>polium</ets>,
the name of a plant, perhaps <ets>Teucrium polium</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A whitish woolly plant (<spn>Teucrium
Polium</spn>) of the order <ord>Labiat<ae/</ord>, found throughout the
Mediterranean region. The name, with sundry prefixes, is sometimes given
to other related species of the same genus.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also
<asp>poley</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poly mountain</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Poly-mountain</er>,
in Vocabulary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyacid</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ac"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>acid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Capable of
neutralizing, or of combining with, several molecules of a monobasic
acid; having more than one hydrogen atom capable of being replaced by
acid radicals; -- said of certain bases; <as>as, calcium hydrate and
glycerin are <ex>polyacid</ex> bases</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyacoustic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*a*cous"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>acoustic</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>polyacoustique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Multiplying or magnifying
sound.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A polyacoustic
instrument.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyacoustics</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*a*cous"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art of
multiplying or magnifying sounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyacron</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*a"cron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Polyacra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Polyacrons</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many +
<grk>'a`kron</grk> summit.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A solid having
many summits or angular points; a polyhedron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyactinia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*ac*tin"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Actinia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An old name for those Anthozoa which, like the actinias, have
numerous simple tentacles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyadelphia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*a*del"phi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ brother.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Linn<ae/an class of plants having stamens
united in three or more bodies or bundles by the filaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyadelphous</ent><br/
<ent>Polyadelphian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*a*del"phi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pol`y*a*del"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the class Polyadelphia; having
stamens united in three or more bundles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyandria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*an"dri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Polyandry</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Linn<ae/an class
of monoclinous or hermaphrodite plants, having many stamens, or any
number above twenty, inserted in the receptacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyandrian</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*an"dri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Polyandrous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyandric</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*an"dric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<ets>polyandrique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or characterized by,
polyandry; mating with several males.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Polyandric</xex>
societies.<rdquo/ <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyandrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*an"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Belonging to the class Polyandria; having many stamens, or any
number above twenty, inserted in the receptacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyandry</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*an"dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>'anh`r</grk>, <grk>'andro`s</grk>, man, male: cf. F.
<ets>polyandrie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The possession by a woman of more
than one husband at the same time; -- contrasted with
<ant>monandry</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In law, this falls under the head of
polygamy.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyanthus</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*an"thus</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*<acr/n"th<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Polyanthuses</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>poly`anqos</grk> rich in
flowers; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <grk>'a`nqos</grk> flower.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>polyanthos</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The oxlip. So called because the peduncle bears a
many-flowered umbel. See <er>Oxlip</er>. <sd>(b)</sd> A bulbous
flowering plant of the genus <gen>Narcissus</gen> (<spn>Narcissus
Tazetta</spn>, or <spn>Narcissus polyanthus</spn> of some authors). See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Narcissus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyarchist</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*ar`chist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
advocates polyarchy; -- opposed to <contr>monarchist</contr>.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*ar`chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>-archy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>polyarchie.</ets> Cf.
<er>Polarchy</er>.]</ety> <def>A government by many persons, of whatever
order or class.</def> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyatomic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*a*tom"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>atomic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having more than one atom in the molecule; consisting
of several atoms.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having a valence greater than
one.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyautography</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*au*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>autography</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or
practice of multiplying copies of one's own handwriting, or of
manuscripts, by printing from stone, -- a species of
lithography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polybasic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ba"sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>basic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Capable of
neutralizing, or of combining with, several molecules of a monacid base;
having several hydrogen atoms capable of being replaced by basic
radicals; -- said of certain acids; <as>as, sulphuric acid is
<ex>polybasic</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polybasite</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ba"site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polybasic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>An iron-black ore of
silver, consisting of silver, sulphur, and antimony, with some copper
and arsenic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polybranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of Nudibranchiata including those which have numerous
branchi<ae/ on the back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polybromide</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*bro"mide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>bromide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A bromide containing more than one atom of bromine in the
molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycarpellary</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*car"pel*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Composed of several or numerous carpels; -- said
of such fruits as the orange.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycarpous</ent><br/
<ent>Polycarpic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*car"pic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pol`y*car"pous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/
fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Bearing fruit
repeatedly, or year after year.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having several
pistils in one flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychaeta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*ch<ae/"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ hair.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the two principal groups of Ch<ae/topoda. It includes those
that have prominent parapodia and fascicles of set<ae/. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Parapodia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychloride</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chlo"ride</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>chloride</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A chloride containing more than one atom of chlorine in the
molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychoerany</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ch<oe/r"a*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ wide-ruling.]</ety> <def>A government by many chiefs, princes,
or rules.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychord</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chord</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ string, cord.]</ety> <def>Having many
strings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychord</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chord</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A musical instrument of ten strings.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An
apparatus for coupling two octave notes, capable of being attached to a
keyed instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychrest</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chrest</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ useful
for many purposes; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ useful, fr. <?/ to use:
cf. F. <ets>polychreste</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
that serves for many uses, or that cures many diseases.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polychrest salt</b></col> <fld>(Old Med. Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>potassium sulphate, specifically obtained by fusing niter with
sulphur.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychroism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chro*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ color.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Pleochroism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychroite</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chro*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ color: cf. F.
<ets>polychro<ium/te</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The coloring
matter of saffron; -- formerly so called because of the change of color
on treatment with certain acids; -- called also
<altname>crocin</altname>, and <altname>safranin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychromate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chro"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polychromic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of a
polychromic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychromate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chro"mate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polychromatic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A compound which
exhibits, or from which may be prepared, a variety of colors, as certain
solutions derived from vegetables, which display colors by
fluorescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychromatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chro*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>chromatic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Showing a
variety, or a change, of colors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polychromatic acid</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>a substance obtained by the action of nitric acid on
aloes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychrome</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chrome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ color.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Esculin; -- so called in
allusion to its fluorescent solutions.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychrome</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chrome</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polychrome</ets>.]</ety> <def>Executed in the manner of polychromy;
<as>as, <ex>polychrome</ex> printing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychromic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chro"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + (sense 1) Gr. <?/, or (sense 2)
<ets>chromic</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Polychromatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating,
any one of several acids (known only in their salts) which contain more
than one atom of chromium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychromous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chro"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to polychromy; many-colored; polychromatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychromy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*chro`my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ color.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Art)</fld>
<def>The art or practice of combining different colors, especially
brilliant ones, in an artistic way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polychronious</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*chro"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ for a long time, <?/ time.]</ety>
<def>Enduring through a long time; chronic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyclinic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*clin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>clinic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A clinic in which diseases of many sorts are treated; especially,
an institution in which clinical instruction is given in all kinds of
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyconic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*con"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>conic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or based upon, many
cones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polyconic projection</b></col> <fld>(Map Making)</fld>,
<cd>a projection of the earth's surface, or any portion thereof, by
which each narrow zone is projected upon a conical surface that touches
the sphere along this zone, the conical surface being then unrolled.
This projection differs from <xex>conic projection</xex> in that latter
assumes but one cone for the whole map. Polyconic projection is that in
use in the United States coast and geodetic survey.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>polycosmic</ent><br/
<hw>pol`y*cos"mic</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/l`<esl/*k<ocr/z"m<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>cosmic</ets>, containing
many universes. (<fu>2003</fu>).]</ety> <fld>(Computers)</fld>
<def>Representing multiple possible worlds; -- used of a computational
ontology that includes logically inconsistent assertions (theories),
structured as different possible worlds; <as>as, the only way to
accommodate different theories of physics in one ontology is for that
ontology to be <ex>polycosmic</ex></as>.</def> <note>A computational
ontology that includes both the existence of one God and nonexistence of
God as alternative and inconsistent theories would be
<ex>polycosmic</ex></note>.<br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycotyledon</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*cot`y*le"don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>cotyledon</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>polycotyl<eacute/done</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant
that has many, or more than two, cotyledons in the seed.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pol`y*cot`y*led"on*ous</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycotyledonary</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*cot`y*led"on*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>cotyledonary</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having the villi of the placenta collected into
definite patches, or cotyledons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycracy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>-cracy</ets>, as in <ets>democracy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Government
by many rulers; polyarchy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1110 --></p>

<p><ent>Polycrotic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*crot"ic</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*kr<ocr/t"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <grk>krotei^n</grk> to
beat.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to polycrotism;
manifesting polycrotism; <as>as, a <ex>polycrotic</ex> pulse; a
<ex>polycrotic</ex> pulse curve.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycrotism</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyc"ro*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>That state or condition of the pulse in which
the pulse curve, or sphygmogram, shows several secondary crests or
elevations; -- contrasted with <contr>monocrotism</contr> and
<contr>dicrotism</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycystid</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*cys"tid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the Polycystidea.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One
of the Polycystina.</def> -- <def2><pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
Polycystidea, or the Polycystina.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycystidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*cys*tid"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Cystidea</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of Gregarin<ae/ including those that have two or more
internal divisions of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycystina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*cys*ti"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Cyst</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
division of Radiolaria including numerous minute marine species. The
skeleton is composed of silica, and is often very elegant in form and
sculpture. Many have been found in the fossil state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycystine</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*cys"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the Polycystina.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>One of the Polycystina.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. a homopolymer of the amino acid cystine. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polycyttaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*cyt*ta"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/, dim. fr. <?/ a hollow
vessel.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Radiolaria. It
includes those having one more central capsules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- polydactyl. = polydactylous. having more that the normal number
of digits; e.g. a polydactylous cat may have six or more toes on its
paw. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polydactylism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*dac"tyl*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ finger: cf. F.
<ets>polydactylisme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The possession
of more that the normal number of digits.</def> <-- also polydactyly.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polydipsia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*dip"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> much + <?/ thirst.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Excessive and constant thirst occasioned by disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyedron</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*e"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polyhedron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyedrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*e"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polyhedral</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyeidic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ei"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Passing
through several distinct larval forms; -- having several distinct kinds
of young.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyeidism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ei"dism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The quality or state of being polyeidic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyembryonate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*em"bry*o*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>embryonate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Consisting of, or having, several embryos; polyembryonic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyembryonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*em`bry*on"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>embryonic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Polyembryonate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyembryony</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*em"bry*o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Embryo</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
production of two or more embryos in one seed, due either to the
existence and fertilization of more than one embryonic sac or to the
origination of embryos outside of the embryonic sac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyfoil</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*foil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> +
<ets>foil</ets>, n.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Multifoil</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygala</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*lyg"a*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., milkwort,
fr. Gr. <?/; <grk>poly`s</grk> much + <?/ milk.]</ety> <def>A genus of
bitter herbs or shrubs having eight stamens and a two-celled ovary (as
the Seneca snakeroot, the flowering wintergreen, etc.);
milkwort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygalaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ga*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a natural order of plants (<ord>Polygalace<ae/</ord>) of
which Polygala is the type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygalic</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"a*lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or obtained from, Polygala; specifically,
designating an acrid glucoside (called <xex>polygalic acid</xex>,
<xex>senegin</xex>, etc.), resembling, or possibly identical with,
<xex>saponin</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygamia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*ga"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Polygamous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
Linn<ae/an class of plants, characterized by having both hermaphrodite
and unisexual flowers on the same plant.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A name
given by Linn<ae/us to file orders of plants having syngenesious
flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygamian</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ga"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Polygamous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygamist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"a*mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polygamiste</ets>, <ets>polygame</ets>, Gr. <?/,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>One who practices polygamy, or maintains its
lawfulness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygamize</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"a*mize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To practice
polygamy; to marry several wives.</def> <rj><au>Sylvester.
Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygamous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"a*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ living
<?/ polygamy; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ marriage. Cf.
<er>Bigamy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to polygamy; characterized by, or
involving, polygamy; having a plurality of wives; <as>as,
<ex>polygamous</ex> marriages</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>monogamous</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pairing with more than one
female.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Most deer, cattle, and sheep are <qex>polygamous</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the Polygamia;
bearing both hermaphrodite and unisexual flowers on the same
plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygamy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"a*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; cf. F.
<ets>polygamie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The having of a plurality
of wives or husbands at the same time; usually, the marriage of a man to
more than one woman, or the practice of having several wives, at the
same time; -- opposed to <xex>monogamy</xex>; <as>as, the nations of the
East practiced <ex>polygamy</ex></as>. See the Note under
<er>Bigamy</er>, and cf. <er>Polyandry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The state or habit of having more
than one mate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The condition or state of a plant
which bears both perfect and unisexual flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygastrian</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*gas"tri*an</hw>
<pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*g<acr/s"tr<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Polygastrica.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygastric</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*gas"tric</hw> <pr>(-tr<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>gastric</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>polygastrique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Having several bellies; -- applied to muscles which are made up of
several bellies separated by short tendons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the
Polygastrica.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygastric</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*gas"tric</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*g<acr/s"tr<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Polygastrica.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygastrica</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*gas"tri*ca</hw> <pr>(-tr<icr/*k<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. So called because they were supposed to have several
stomachs, or digestive cavities.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
Infusoria.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygeny</ent><br/
<ent>Polygenesis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(-j<ecr/n"<esl/*s<icr/s)</pr>,
<hw>Po*lyg"e*ny</hw> <pr>(p<osl/*l<icr/j"<esl/*n<ycr/)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>genesis</ets>, or root of
Gr. <grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The
theory that living organisms originate in cells or embryos of different
kinds, instead of coming from a single cell; -- opposed to
<contr>monogenesis</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ge*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having many distinct sources; originating at various places or
times.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to polygenesis;
polyphyletic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polygenetic mountain range</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>,
<cd>one which is composite, or consists of two or more monogenetic
ranges, each having had its own history of development.</cd>
<rj><au>Dana.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(-j<ecr/n"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or relating to polygeny;
polygenetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygenism</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"e*nism</hw> <pr>(p<osl/*l<icr/j"<esl/*n<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>polyg<eacute/nisme</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine that animals of the same species
have sprung from more than one original pair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygenist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"e*nist</hw> <pr>(-n<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>One who maintains that animals of the same
species have sprung from more than one original pair; -- opposed to
<contr>monogenist</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygenous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>-genous</ets>: cf. Gr. <?/ of many
families.]</ety> <def>Consisting of, or containing, many kinds; <as>as,
a <ex>polygenous</ex> mountain</as>.</def> <rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyglot</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*glot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>poly`glwttos</grk> many-tongued; <grk>poly`s</grk> many +
<grk>glw^tta</grk>, <grk>glw^ssa</grk>, tongue, language: cf. F.
<ets>polyglotte</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Containing, or made up,
of, several languages; <as>as, a <ex>polyglot</ex> lexicon,
Bible</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Versed in, or speaking, many languages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyglot</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*glot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who speaks
several languages.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>polyglot</xex>,
or good linguist.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A book containing several versions of the same text,
or containing the same subject matter in several languages; esp., the
Scriptures in several languages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enriched by the publication of <qex>polyglots</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Abp. Newcome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyglottous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*glot"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polyglot</er>.]</ety> <def>Speaking many languages; polyglot.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>polyglottous</xex> tribes of
America.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Max M<uum/ller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygon</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*gon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>poly`gwnos</grk> polygonal; <grk>poly`s</grk> many +
<grk>gwni`a</grk> angle: cf. F. <ets>polygone</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A plane figure having many angles, and
consequently many sides; esp., one whose perimeter consists of more than
four sides; any figure having many angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polygon of forces</b></col> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a
polygonal figure, the sides of which, taken successively, represent, in
length and direction, several forces acting simultaneously upon one
point, so that the side necessary to complete the figure represents the
resultant of those forces. Cf. <cref>Parallelogram of forces</cref>,
under <er>Parallelogram</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygonaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*go*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polygonum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a
natural order of apetalous plants (<ord>Polygonace<ae/</ord>), of which
the knotweeds (species of <gen>Polygonum</gen>) are the type, and which
includes also the docks (<gen>Rumex</gen>), the buckwheat, rhubarb, sea
grape (<gen>Coccoloba</gen>), and several other genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygonal</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"o*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having many
angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polygonal numbers</b></col>, <cd>certain figurate
numbers. See under <er>Figurate</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygoneutic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*go*neu"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ offspring.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having two or more broods in a season.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygonometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*go*nom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polygon</ets> + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrine
of polygons; an extension of some of the principles of trigonometry to
the case of polygons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygonous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"o*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Polygonal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygonum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*lyg"o*num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a kind of plant; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <grk>go`ny</grk> the knee,
a joint of a plant. So called in allusion to the numerous joints.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants embracing a large number of
species, including bistort, knotweed, smartweed, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygony</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus Polygonum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygordius</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*gor"di*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Gordius</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
genus of marine annelids, believed to be an ancient or ancestral type.
It is remarkable for its simplicity of structure and want of parapodia.
It is the type of the order Archiannelida, or Gymnotoma. See
<er>Loeven's larva</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygram</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ marked
with many stripes; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ a line.]</ety> <def>A
figure consisting of many lines.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barlow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygraph</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ writing
much; <grk>poly`s</grk> much, many + <?/ to write: cf. F.
<ets>polygraphe</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument for
multiplying copies of a writing; a manifold writer; a copying
machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In bibliography, a collection of different works,
either by one or several authors.</def> <rj><au>Brande &
C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><--<sn>3.</sn> <def>An instrument for detecting deceptive statements
by a subject, by measuring several physiological states of the subject,
such as pulse, heartbeat, and sweating. The instrument records these
parameters on a strip of paper while the subject is asked questions
designed to elicit emotional responses when the subject tries to deceive
the interrogator. Also called <altname>lie
detector</altname></def>--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygraphical</ent><br/
<ent>Polygraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pol`y*graph"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polygraphique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or employed in,
polygraphy; <as>as, a <ex>polygraphic</ex> instrument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Done with a polygraph; <as>as, a
<ex>polygraphic</ex> copy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygraphy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> much + <grk>gra`fein</grk> to write: cf. F.
<ets>polygraphie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Much writing; writing of
many books.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The art of writing in various ciphers, and of
deciphering the same.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The art or practice of using a polygraph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygrooved</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*grooved`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>groove</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having many
grooves; <as>as, a <ex>polygrooved</ex> rifle or gun (referring to the
rifling)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygyn</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*gyn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polygyne</ets>. See <er>Polygyny</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A plant of the order Polygynia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygynia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*gyn"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Polygyny</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Linn<ae/an order of
plants having many styles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygynous</ent><br/
<ent>Polygynian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*gyn"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Po*lyg"y*nous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having many
styles; belonging to the order Polygynia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygynist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"y*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
practices or advocates polygyny.</def> <rj><au>H. Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polygyny</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyg"y*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ woman, wife.]</ety> <def>The state or practice of having
several wives at the same time; marriage to several wives.</def>
<rj><au>H. Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyhalite</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ha"lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ salt.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
mineral usually occurring in fibrous masses, of a brick-red color, being
tinged with iron, and consisting chiefly of the sulphates of lime,
magnesia, and soda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyhedrical</ent><br/
<ent>Polyhedral</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*he"dral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pol`y*hed"ric*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polyhedron</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Having many sides,
as a solid body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polyhedral angle</b></col>, <cd>an angle bounded by three
or more plane angles having a common vertex.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyhedron</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*he"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Polyhedrons</plw>. <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Polyhedra</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ with many seats or sides;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ a seat or side: cf. F.
<ets>poly<egrave/dre</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A
body or solid contained by many sides or planes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>A polyscope, or multiplying
glass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyhedrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*he"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Polyhedral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyhistor</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*his"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ very
learned.]</ety> <def>One versed in various learning.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyhymnia</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*hym"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from Gr.
<?/; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ hymn.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Myth.)</fld>
<def>The Muse of lyric poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyiodide</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*i"o*dide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A iodide having more than one atom of iodine in the
molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polylogy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyl"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> much + <?/ discourse.]</ety> <def>Talkativeness.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyloquent</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyl"o*quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + L. <ets>loquens</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>logui</ets> to speak.]</ety> <def>Garrulous; loquacious.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymastism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*mas"tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a breast.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The condition of having more than two mamm<ae/, or
breasts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- polymath = polymathist --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymathic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*math"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polymathique</ets>. See <er>Polymathy</er>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining
to polymathy; acquainted with many branches of learning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymathist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lym"a*thist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
many sciences; a person of various learning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymathy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lym"a*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> much + <?/, <?/, to learn.]</ety> <def>The knowledge
of many arts and sciences; variety of learning.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymeniscous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*me*nis"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Meniscus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having numerous facets; -- said of the compound eyes of insects and
crustaceans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymer</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polymeric</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any one of two or
more substances related to each other by polymerism; specifically, a
substance produced from another substance by chemical
polymerization.</def> <mark>[Formerly also written polymere.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymeric</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*mer"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ part.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Having the same
percentage composition (that is, having the same elements united in the
same proportion by weight), but different molecular weights; -- often
used with <xex>with</xex>; thus, cyanic acid (<chform>CNOH</chform>),
fulminic acid (<chform>C2N2O2H2</chform>), and cyanuric acid
(<chform>C3N3O3H3</chform>), are <xex>polymeric</xex> with each
other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The figures expressing the number of atoms of each
element in a number of polymeric substances are respectively multiples
and factors of each other, or have some simple common divisor. The
relation may be merely a numerical one, as in the example given above,
or a chemical one, as in the case of aldehyde, paraldehyde, and
metaldehyde.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. consisting of multiple units linked together by covalent bonds
to form a larger molecule --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymerism</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lym"er*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The state, quality, or relation of two or more
polymeric substances.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The act or process of
forming polymers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymerization</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*mer`i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act or process of changing to a polymeric
form; the condition resulting from such change.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymerize</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*mer*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To cause polymerization of; to produce polymers from; to increase
the molecular weight of, without changing the atomic proportions; thus,
certain acids <xex>polymerize</xex> aldehyde.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymerize</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*mer*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To
change into another substance having the same atomic proportions, but a
higher molecular weight; to undergo polymerization; thus, aldehyde
<xex>polymerizes</xex> in forming paraldehyde.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymerous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lym"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having many parts or members in each set.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Polymeric.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymnia</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lym"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polyhymnia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymnite</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ym*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ full of
moss; <grk>poly`s</grk> much + <?/ moss.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
stone marked with dendrites and black lines, and so disposed as to
represent rivers, marshes, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymorph</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*morph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
multiform; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <grk>morfh`</grk> form: cf. F.
<ets>polymorphe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A substance
capable of crystallizing in several distinct forms; also, any one of
these forms. Cf. <er>Allomorph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymorphic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*mor"phic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Polymorphous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymorphism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*mor"phism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pleomorphism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The capability of
assuming different forms; the capability of widely varying in
form.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Existence in many forms; the coexistence,
in the same locality, of two or more distinct forms independent of sex,
not connected by intermediate gradations, but produced from common
parents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymorphosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*mor*pho"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Morphosis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The assumption of several structural forms without a corresponding
difference in function; -- said of sponges, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having, or assuming, a variety of forms, characters, or styles;
<as>as, a <ex>polymorphous</ex> author</as>.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having, or occurring in, several
distinct forms; -- opposed to <xex>monomorphic</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1111 --></p>

<p><ent>Polymorphy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*mor`phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Existence in
many forms; polymorphism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poly-mountain</ent><br/
<hw>Po`ly-moun"tain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Same as <er>Poly</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The closely related <spn>Teucrium montanum</spn>,
formerly called <spn>Polium montanum</spn>, a plant of Southern
Europe.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The <spn>Bartsia alpina</spn>, a low
purple-flowered herb of Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymyodae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*my"o*d<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Polymyoid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Oscines</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymyodous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*my"o*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Polymyoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polymyoid</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lym"y*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/, <?/, muscle + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having numerous vocal muscles; of or pertaining to the
Polymyod<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyneme</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*neme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> +
Gr. <?/ thread.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of tropical food fishes of the family <fam>Polynemid<ae/</fam>.
They have several slender filaments, often very long, below the pectoral
fin. Some of them yield isinglass of good quality. Called also
<altname>threadfish</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynemoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ne"moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polyneme</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the polynemes, or the family
<fam>Polynemid<ae/</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynesian</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ne"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Polynesia (the islands of the eastern and central Pacific), or to the
Polynesians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynesians</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*ne"sians</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Polynesian</singw>.</sing> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>The race of
men native in Polynesia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynia</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyn"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>poluineia</ets> a warm place in water, i. e., a place which does
not freeze.]</ety> <def>The open sea supposed to surround the north
pole.</def> <rj><au>Kane.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynomial</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*no"mi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>-nomial</ets>, as in <ets>monomial</ets>,
<ets>binomial</ets>: cf. F. <ets>polyn<ocir/me</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Alg.)</fld> <def>An expression composed of two or more terms,
connected by the signs <it>plus</it> or <it>minus</it>; as,
<mathex>a<exp>2</exp> - 2ab + b<exp>2</exp></mathex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynomial</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*no"mi*al</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Containing many
names or terms; multinominal; <as>as, the <ex>polynomial</ex>
theorem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of two or more words; having names
consisting of two or more words; <as>as, a <ex>polynomial</ex> name;
<ex>polynomial</ex> nomenclature.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynuclear</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*nu"cle*ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>nuclear</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Containing many nuclei.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polynucleolar</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*nu*cle"o*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>nucleolar</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Having more than one nucleolus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyommatous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*om"ma*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, the eye.]</ety> <def>Having many
eyes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyonomous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*on"o*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, name: cf. Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<def>Having many names or titles; polyonymous.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Jones.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyonomy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*on"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/ a
multitude of names.]</ety> <def>The use of a variety of names for the
same object.</def> <rj><au>G. S. Faber.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyonym</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*o*nym</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
object which has a variety of names.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A polynomial name or term.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyonymous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*on"y*mous</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Polyonomous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyoptrum</ent><br/
<ent>Polyoptron</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*op"tron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pol`y*op"trum</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ seen.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>A glass
through which objects appear multiplied, but diminished in size.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyorama</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*o*ra"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a sight, view.]</ety> <def>A view of
many objects; also, a sort of panorama with dissolving views.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyp</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"yp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>polypus</ets>,
Gr. <?/, <?/, literally, many-footed; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/, <?/,
foot: cf. F. <ets>polype</ets>. See <er>Poly-</er> and <er>Foot</er>,
and cf. <er>Polypode</er>, <er>Polypody</er>, <er>Poulp</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the feeding or nutritive
zooids of a hydroid or coral.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the
Anthozoa.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Same as
<er>Anthozoa</er>. See <er>Anthozoa</er>, <er>Madreporaria</er>,
<er>Hydroid</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>polype</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fresh-water polyp</b></col>, <cd>the hydra.</cd> --
<col><b>Polyp stem</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>that portion of the
stem of a siphonophore which bears the polypites, or feeding
zooids.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyparous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyp"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + L. <ets>parere</ets> to produce.]</ety>
<def>Producing or bearing a great number; bringing forth many.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypary</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*pa*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Polyparies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See
<er>Polyp</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Polypidom</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polype</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"ype</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Polyp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypean</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*pe"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a polyp, or polyps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyperythrin</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*pe*ryth"rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polyp</ets> + Gr. <?/ red.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>
<def>A coloring matter found in many simple Anthozoa and some
hydroids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypetalous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>petal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Consisting of, or having, several or many separate petals; <as>as,
a <ex>polypetalous</ex> corolla, flower, or plant</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Martyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphagous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>polyphagus</ets>, Gr. <?/; <grk>poly`s</grk> much, many + <?/ to
eat: cf. F. <ets>polyphage</ets>.]</ety> <def>Eating, or subsisting on,
many kinds of food; <as>as, <ex>polyphagous</ex> animals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphagy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph""a*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The practice or
faculty of subsisting on many kinds of food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypharmacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*phar"ma*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ the using of medicine, fr. <?/
medicine: cf. F. <ets>polypharmacie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The act or practice of prescribing too many
medicines.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A prescription made up of many
medicines or ingredients.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphase</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*phase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>phase</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>Having or producing
two or more phases; multiphase; <as>as, a <ex>polyphase</ex> machine, a
machine producing two or more pressure waves of electro-motive force,
differing in phase; a <ex>polyphase</ex> current</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphaser</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*phas`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>A machine generating more than one pressure wave; a
multiphaser.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphemus</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*phe"mus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Polyphemus</ets> the one-eyed Cyclops who was blinded by
Ulysses.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A very large American moth
(<spn>Telea polyphemus</spn>) belonging to the Silkworm family
(<fam>Bombycid<ae/</fam>). Its larva, which is very large, bright green,
with silvery tubercles, and with oblique white stripes on the sides,
feeds on the oak, chestnut, willow, cherry, apple, and other trees. It
produces a large amount of strong silk. Called also <altname>American
silkworm</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphone</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A character or
vocal sign representing more than one sound, as <xex>read</xex>, which
is pronounced <xex>r<emac/d</xex> or <xex>r<ecr/d</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ sound: cf. F. <ets>polyphone</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a multiplicity of sounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characterized by polyphony; <as>as, Assyrian
<ex>polyphonic</ex> characters</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Consisting of several tone series,
or melodic parts, progressing simultaneously according to the laws of
counterpoint; contrapuntal; <as>as, a <ex>polyphonic</ex>
composition</as>; -- opposed to <contr>homophonic</contr>, or
<contr>monodic</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphonism</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph"o*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Polyphony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphonist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph"o*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
proficient in the art of multiplying sounds; a ventriloquist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A master of polyphony; a
contrapuntist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphonous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph"o*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Polyphonic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphony</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Multiplicity of sounds, as in the reverberations of an
echo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plurality of sounds and articulations expressed by
the same vocal sign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Composition in mutually related,
equally important parts which share the melody among them; contrapuntal
composition; -- opposed to <xex>homophony</xex>, in which the melody is
given to one part only, the others filling out the harmony. See
<er>Counterpoint</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphore</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
receptacle which bears many ovaries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphote</ent><br/
<ent>Polyphotal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*pho"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pol"y*phote</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/,
<?/, light.]</ety> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to or designating
arc lamps so constructed that more than one can be used on a single
circuit.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphyletic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*phy*let"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ clan.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or characterized by, descent from more than one root
form, or from many different root forms; polygenetic; -- opposed to
<ant>monophyletic</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyphyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyph"yl*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ leaf.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Many-leaved; <as>as, a <ex>polyphyllous</ex> calyx or
perianth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol"y*pi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Anthozoa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypide</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*pide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the ordinary zooids of the Bryozoa.</def> <altsp>[Spellt
also <asp>polypid</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypidom</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyp"i*dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polypus</ets> + L. <ets>domus</ets> house.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A coral, or corallum; also, one of the
coral-like structure made by bryozoans and hydroids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypier</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po`ly`pier"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A polypidom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*pif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Anthozoa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol*y*pif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polypus</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Bearing polyps, or polypites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypiparous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*pip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Polypus</ets> + L. <ets>parere</ets> to produce.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Producing polyps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypite</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*pite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the feeding zooids, or
polyps, of a coral, hydroid, or siphonophore; a hydranth. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Campanularian</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Sometimes, the manubrium of a hydroid medusa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A fossil coral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyplacophora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*pla*coph"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Placophora</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Placophora</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyplastic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*plas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>-plastic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Assuming, or having the power of assuming, many forms; <as>as, a
<ex>polyplastic</ex> element which does not preserve its original
shape</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypode</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*pode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polypode</ets>. See <er>Polypody</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A plant of the genus <gen>Polypodium</gen>; polypody.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>polypod</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypode</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*pode</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, the wood louse,
milleped: cf. F. <ets>polypode</ets>. See <er>Polyp</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An animal having many feet; a
myriapod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypodium</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*po`di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/, dim. of <?/. See <er>Polyp</er>, and cf. 2d
<er>Polypode</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants of
the order <ord>Filices</ord> or ferns. The fructifications are in
uncovered roundish points, called <xex>sori</xex>, scattered over the
inferior surface of the frond or leaf. There are numerous
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypody</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*po`dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Polypodium</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*poid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Polyp</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like a
polyp; having the nature of a polyp, but lacking the tentacles or other
parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Resembling a polypus in
appearance; having a character like that of a polypus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypomedusae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*po*me*du"s<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Polyp</er>, and <er>Medusa</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Hydrozoa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyporous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyp"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>porous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having many
pores.</def> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyporus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*lyp"o*rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Polypori</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ a pore.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of fungi having the under surface full of minute pores; also, any
fungus of this genus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <spn>Polyporus fomentarius</spn> was formerly dried and
cut in slices for tinder, called <xex>amadou</xex>. <spn>Polyporus
betulinus</spn> is common in America, and forms very large thick white
semicircular excrescences on birch trees. Several species of
<gen>Polyporous</gen> are considered edible.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*pous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polypeux</ets>. See <er>Polyp</er>.]</ety> <def>Of the nature of a
polypus; having many feet or roots, like the polypus; affected with
polypus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypragmatical</ent><br/
<ent>Polypragmatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*prag*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pol`y*prag*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>pragmatic</ets>, <ets>-ical</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Overbusy; officious.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Heywood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypragmaty</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*prag"ma*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ business.]</ety> <def>The state of
being overbusy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyprotodonta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*pro`to*don"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ first + <?/, <?/,
tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of marsupials in which
there are more fore incisor teeth in each jaw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypteroidei</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*lyp`te*roi"de*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Polypterus</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of existing ganoid fishes having
numerous fins along the back. The bichir, or Polypterus, is the type.
See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Crossopterygian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypterus</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyp`te*rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ feather, wing.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An African genus of ganoid fishes including the bichir.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyptoton</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`yp*to"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/ having, or being in, many cases; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/
case.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure by which a word is
repeated in different forms, cases, numbers, genders, etc., as in
Tennyson's line, -- <ldquo/My own heart's heart, and ownest own,
farewell.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polypus</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Polypuses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Polypi</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See <er>Polyp</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Polyp</er>.</def><-- polyp is the
normal term now --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A tumor, usually with a narrow
base, somewhat resembling a pear, -- found in the nose, uterus, etc.,
and produced by hypertrophy of some portion of the mucous
membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyrhizous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*rhi"zous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ root.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having
numerous roots, or rootlets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyschematist</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*sche"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ form, manner.]</ety> <def>Having, or
existing in, many different forms or fashions; multiform.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
farseeing; <grk>poly`s</grk> much, many + <?/ to view: cf. F.
<ets>polyscope</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>A glass
which makes a single object appear as many; a multiplying glass.</def>
<rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An apparatus for affording a view
of the different cavities of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysepalous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*sep"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>sepal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the sepals separate from each other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysilicic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*si*lic"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>silicic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to compounds formed by the condensation of two or
more molecules of silicic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polysilicic acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any
one of a series of acids formed by the condensation of two or more
molecules of silicic acid, with elimination of water.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyspast</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*spast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>polyspaston</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ drawn by several cords;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ to draw: cf. F.
<ets>polyspaste</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A machine
consisting of many pulleys; specifically, an apparatus formerly used for
reducing luxations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyspermous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*sper"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ seed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Containing many seeds; <as>as, a <ex>polyspermous</ex> capsule or
berry</as>.</def> <rj><au>Martyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyspermy</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*sper`my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Fullness of sperm, or seed; the passage of more than one
spermatozoon into the vitellus in the impregnation of the
ovum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysporous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*spor"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Containing many spores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polystomata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*stom"a*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/, <?/, mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of trematode worms having more two
suckers. Called also <altname>Polystomea</altname> and
<altname>Polystoma</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polystome</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*stome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
many-mouthed; <grk>poly`s</grk> + <grk>sto`ma</grk> mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having many mouths.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polystome</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*stome</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An animal
having many mouths; -- applied to Protozoa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polystyle</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ with many
columns; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ column: cf. F.
<ets>polystyle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Having many
columns; -- said of a building, especially of an interior part or court;
<as>as, a <ex>polystyle</ex> hall</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A polystyle hall or edifice.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysulphide</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*sul"phide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>sulphide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A sulphide having more than one atom of sulphur in the molecule; --
contrasted with <xex>monosulphide</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysulphuret</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*sul"phu*ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A polysulphide.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyllabical</ent><br/
<ent>Polysyllabic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*syl*lab"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pol`y*syl*lab"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/; <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ syllable: cf. F.
<ets>polysyllabique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to a polysyllable;
containing, or characterized by, polysyllables; consisting of more than
three syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyllabicism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syl*lab"i*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Polysyllabism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyllabicity</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syl`la*bic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Polysyllabism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyllabism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syl"la*bism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being polysyllabic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyllable</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*syl`la*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>syllable</ets>.]</ety> <def>A word of many
syllables, or consisting of more syllables than three; -- words of less
than four syllables being called <contr>monosyllables</contr>,
<contr>dissyllables</contr>, and <contr>trisyllables</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyndetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syn*det"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized
by polysyndeton, or the multiplication of conjunctions.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pol`y*syn*det"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyndeton</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*syn"de*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ bound together, fr. <?/ to bind
together; <?/ with + <?/ to bind.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A
figure by which the conjunction is often repeated, as in the sentence,
<ldquo/We have ships and men and money and stores.<rdquo/ Opposed to
<contr>asyndeton</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysynthesis</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syn"the*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>synthesis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or process of combining many separate elements into a
whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Philol.)</fld> <def>The formation of a word by the
combination of several simple words, as in the aboriginal languages of
America; agglutination.</def> <rj><au>Latham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysynthetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syn*thet"ic</hw>
<pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*s<icr/n*th<ecr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + <ets>synthetic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Characterized
by polysynthesis; agglutinative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polysynthetic twinning</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>,
<cd>repeated twinning, like that of the triclinic feldspar, producing
fine parallel bands in alternately reversed positions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polysyntheticism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*syn*thet"i*cism</hw>
<pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*s<icr/n*th<ecr/t"<icr/*s<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Polysynthesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1112 --></p>

<p><ent>Polytechnic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*tech"nic</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/l`<icr/*t<ecr/k"n<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>poly`technos</grk>; <grk>poly`s</grk> many
+ <grk>te`chnh</grk> an art: cf. F. <ets>polytechnique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Comprehending, or relating to, many arts and sciences; -- applied
particularly to schools in which many branches of art and science are
taught with especial reference to their practical application; <as>as, a
<ex>polytechnic</ex> institute</as>; also to exhibitions of machinery
and industrial products.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytechnical</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*tech"nic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Polytechnic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytechnics</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*tech"nics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The science of
the mechanic arts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polythalamia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*tha*la"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Polythalamous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
Foraminifera including those having a manychambered shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polythalamous</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*thal"a*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a chamber.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Many-chambered; -- applied to shells of Foraminifera and
cephalopods. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Nautilus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytheism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*the*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ cf. F.
<ets>polyth<eacute/isme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrine of, or belief
in, a plurality of gods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the Old Testament, the gradual development of
<qex>polytheism</qex> from the primitive monotheism may be learned.</q>
<rj><qau>Shaff-Herzog.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytheist</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*the*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>polyth<eacute/iste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who believes in, or
maintains the doctrine of, a plurality of gods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytheistical</ent><br/
<ent>Polytheistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pol`y*the*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pol`y*the*is"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to polytheism; characterized by polytheism; professing or
advocating polytheism; <as>as, <ex>polytheistic</ex> worship; a
<ex>polytheistic</ex> author, or nation.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pol`y*the*is"tic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytheize</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*the*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To adhere to,
advocate, or inculcate, the doctrine of polytheism.</def>
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polythelism</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*the"lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <grk>qhlh`</grk> a nipple.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The condition of having more than two teats, or
nipples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytocous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyt"o*cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>poly`s</grk> many + <?/ offspring.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing fruit repeatedly, as most perennial
plants; polycarpic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Producing many or
young.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytomous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyt"o*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a cutting, fr. <?/ to cut.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Subdivided into many distinct subordinate parts,
which, however, not being jointed to the petiole, are not true leaflets;
-- said of leaves.</def> <rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytomy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyt"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Logic)</fld>
<def>A division into many members.</def> <rj><au>F. Bowen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytungstate</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*tung"state</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A salt of
polytungstic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytungstic</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*tung"stic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Containing several tungsten atoms or radicals; <as>as,
<ex>polytungstic</ex> acid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polytungstic acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any
one of several complex acids of tungsten containing more than one atom
of tungsten.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytype</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> +
<ets>-type</ets>: cf. F. <ets>polytype</ets>, a.]</ety>
<fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A cast, or facsimile copy, of an engraved
block, matter in type, etc.; <as>as, a <ex>polytype</ex> in
relief</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pol"y*type</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q>By pressing the wood cut into semifluid metal, an intaglio matrix
is produced: and from this matrix, in a similar way, a
<qex>polytype</qex> in relief is obtained.</q>
<rj><qau>Hansard.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytype</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*type</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to polytypes; obtained by polytyping; <as>as, a
<ex>polytype</ex> plate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polytype</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*type</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Polytyped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Polytyping</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Print.)</fld>
<def>To produce a polytype of; <as>as, to <ex>polytype</ex> an
engraving</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyurethane</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"y*ur`eth*ane</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>any
polymer containing <chform>[-NH.CO.O-]</chform> linkages; such polymers
are much used as the basis of light but rigid foams for packaging
(<col><b>polyurethane foam</b></col>) and for hard coatings, as on
floors.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyuria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*u"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Poly-</er>, and <er>Urine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
persistently excessive flow of watery urine, with low specific gravity
and without the presence of either protein or sugar. It is generally
accompanied with more or less thirst.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyvalent</ent><br/
<hw>Po*lyv"a*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets> + L. <ets>valens</ets>, p. pr. See
<er>Valent</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Multivalent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyve</ent><br/
<hw>Pol"yve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Polive</er>.]</ety> <def>A pulley.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyzoa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*zo"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>poly`s</grk> many + <grk>zw^,on</grk> an animal.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Bryozoa</er>. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
under <er>Bryozoa</er>, and <er>Phylactol<ae/mata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyzoan</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*zo"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any species of Polyzoa; one of the Polyzoa.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A polyzoon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyzoarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*zo*a"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Polyzoaria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Polyzoary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyzoary</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*zo"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The compound organism of a polyzoan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyzonal</ent><br/
<hw>Pol`y*zon"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Poly-</ets>
+ <ets>zonal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of many zones or
rings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Polyzonal lens</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>a lens
made up of pieces arranged zones or rings, -- used in the lanterns of
lighthouses.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Polyzoon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pol`y*zo"<oum/n</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Polyzoa</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Polyzoan</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the individual
zooids forming the compound organism of a polyzoan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomace</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"ace</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponum</ets> a fruit, LL., an apple: cf. LL. <ets>pomagium</ets>,
<ets>pomacium</ets>.]</ety> <def>The substance of apples, or of similar
fruit, crushed by grinding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomacentroid</ent><br/
<hw>Po`ma*cen"troid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
cover + <?/ a prickle + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the <fam>Pomacentrid<ae/</fam>, a family of
bright-colored tropical fishes having spiny opercula; -- often called
<altname>coral fishes</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ma"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>ponum</ets> an apple.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Like an apple or pear; producing pomes.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to a suborder
(<subord>Pome<ae/</subord>) of rosaceous plants, which includes the true
thorn trees, the quinces, service berries, medlars, and loquats, as well
as the apples, pears, crabs, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Like pomace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomade</ent><br/
<hw>Po*made"</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pommade</ets> pomatum, OF. <ets>pomade</ets> cider (cf. Sp.
<ets>pomada</ets>, It. <ets>pomata</ets>, LL. <ets>pomata</ets> a drink
made of apples), from L. <ets>pomum</ets> fruit, LL., an apple. Cf.
<er>Pomatum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Cider.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Perfumed ointment; esp., a fragrant unguent for the
hair; pomatum; -- originally made from apples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomander</ent><br/
<hw>Po*man"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>poma</ets>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A perfume to be carried with
one, often in the form of a ball.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A box to
contain such perfume, formerly carried by ladies, as at the end of a
chain; -- more properly <altname>pomander box</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomarine</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ma*rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a lid +
<?/, <?/, nose.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the nostril
covered with a scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pomarine jager</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a North
Atlantic jager (<spn>Stercorarius pomarinus</spn>) having the elongated
middle tail feathers obtuse. The adult is black.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomatum</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ma"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pomade</er>.]</ety> <def>A perfumed unguent or composition, chiefly
used in dressing the hair; pomade.</def> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomatum</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ma"tum</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dress with
pomatum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pome</ent><br/
<hw>Pome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pomum</ets> a
fruit: cf. F. <ets>pomme</ets> apple. Cf. <er>Pomade</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A fruit composed of several
cartilaginous or bony carpels inclosed in an adherent fleshy mass, which
is partly receptacle and partly calyx, as an apple, quince, or
pear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A ball of silver or other
metal, which is filled with hot water, and used by the priest in cold
weather to warm his hands during the service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pome</ent><br/
<hw>Pome</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pommer</ets>. See
<er>Pome</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To grow to a head, or form a
head in growing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomegranate</ent><br/
<hw>Pome"gran`ate</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pomgarnet</ets>, OF. <ets>pome de grenate</ets>, F.
<ets>grenade</ets>, L. <ets>pomum</ets> a fruit + <ets>granatus</ets>
grained, having many grains or seeds. See <er>Pome</er>, and
<er>Garnet</er>, <er>Grain</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The fruit of the tree <spn>Punica Granatum</spn>; also, the tree
itself (see <er>Balaustine</er>), which is native in the Orient, but is
successfully cultivated in many warm countries, and as a house plant in
colder climates. The fruit is as large as an orange, and has a hard rind
containing many rather large seeds, each one separately covered with
crimson, acid pulp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A carved or embroidered ornament resembling a
pomegranate.</def> <rj><au>Ex. xxviii. 33.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomel</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pommel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomelo</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"e*lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pompelmous</er>.]</ety> <def>A variety of shaddock, called also
<altname>grape fruit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomely</ent><br/
<hw>Pome"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pomel<eacute/</ets>, F. <ets>pommel<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Pome</er>.]</ety> <def>Dappled.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Pomely</xex> gray.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomeranian</ent><br/
<hw>Pom`e*ra"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Pomerania, a province of Prussia on the Baltic Sea.</def>
-- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Pomerania.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pomeranian dog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
loup-loup, or Spitz dog.</cd><-- also just Pomeranian--></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomewater</ent><br/
<hw>Pome"wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of sweet,
juicy apple.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pomwater</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomey</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"ey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pomeys</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>pomm<eacute/</ets>
grown round, or like an apple, p. p. of <ets>pommer</ets> to
pome.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A figure supposed to resemble an
apple; a roundel, -- always of a green color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomfret</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"fret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps corrupt. fr.
Pg. <ets>pampano</ets> a kind of fish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of two or more species of marine food fishes of
the genus <gen>Stromateus</gen> (<spn>Stromateus niger</spn>,
<spn>Stromateus argenteus</spn>) native of Southern Europe and
Asia.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A marine food fish of Bermuda (<spn>Brama
Raji</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomiculture</ent><br/
<hw>Po"mi*cul`ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pomum</ets> fruit + <ets>cultura</ets> culture.]</ety>
<fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>The culture of fruit; pomology as an
art.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Po*mif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pomifer</ets>; <ets>pomum</ets> fruit + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear:
cf. F. <ets>pomif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Bearing pomes, or applelike fruits.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Bearing
fruits, or excrescences, more or less resembling an apple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pommage</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"mage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pomage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomme</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pom`m<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pomey</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Having the ends
terminating in rounded protuberances or single balls; -- said of a
cross.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomme blanche</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pomme` blanche"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[F., literally, white
apple.]</ety> <def>The prairie turnip. See under
<er>Prairie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pommel</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"mel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pomel</ets>,
OF. <ets>pomel</ets>, F. <ets>pommeau</ets>, LL. <ets>pomellus</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>pomum</ets> fruit, LL. also, an apple. See
<er>Pome</er>.]</ety> <def>A knob or ball; an object resembling a ball
in form</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The knob on the hilt of a
sword.</def> <au>Macaulay.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The knob or
protuberant part of a saddlebow.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The top (of the
head).</def> <au>Chaucer.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A knob forming the
finial of a turret or pavilion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pommel</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"mel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pommeled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Pommelled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pommeling</conjf> or
<conjf>Pommelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To beat soundly, as with the
pommel of a sword, or with something knoblike; hence, to beat with the
fists.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pummel</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pommelion</ent><br/
<hw>Pom*mel"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pommel</er>: cf. LL. <ets>pomilio</ets> pygmy.]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>The cascabel, or hindmost knob, of a
cannon.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pommette</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pom`met`t<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>Having two balls or protuberances at each end; --
said of a cross.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomological</ent><br/
<hw>Po`mo*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pomologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
pomology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomologist</ent><br/
<hw>Po*mol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
pomology; one who cultivates fruit trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomology</ent><br/
<hw>Po*mol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pomum</ets> fruit + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pomologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of fruits; a treatise on
fruits; the cultivation of fruits and fruit trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomona</ent><br/
<hw>Po*mo"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>pomum</ets> fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>The
goddess of fruits and fruit trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomp</ent><br/
<hw>Pomp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pompe</ets>, F.
<ets>pompe</ets>, L. <ets>pompa</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ a sending, a solemn
procession, pomp, fr. <?/ to send. Cf. <er>Pump</er> a shoe.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A procession distinguished by ostentation and splendor;
a pageant.</def> <ldquo/All the <xex>pomps</xex> of a Roman
triumph.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Show of magnificence; parade; display;
power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Display; parade; pageant; pageantry; splendor;
state; magnificence; ostentation; grandeur; pride.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomp</ent><br/
<hw>Pomp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a pompous
display; to conduct.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompadour</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pa*dour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A crimson or pink
color; also, a style of dress cut low and square in the neck; also, a
mode of dressing the hair by drawing it straight back from the forehead
over a roll; -- so called after the Marchioness de <xex>Pompadour</xex>
of France. Also much used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompano</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pa*no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>p<aacute/mpano</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pampano</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any one
of several species of marine fishes of the genus <gen>Trachynotus</gen>,
of which four species are found on the Atlantic coast of the United
States; -- called also <altname>palometa</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They have a brilliant silvery or golden luster, and are
highly esteemed as food fishes. The round pompano (<spn>Trachynotus
thomboides</spn>) and the Carolina pompano (<spn>Trachynotus
Carolinus</spn>) are the most common. Other species occur on the Pacific
coast.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A California harvest fish (<spn>Stromateus
simillimus</spn>), highly valued as a food fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pompano shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small
bivalve shell of the genus <gen>Donax</gen>; -- so called because eaten
by the pompano.</cd> <mark>[Florida]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pom*pat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pompaticus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pompous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompeian</ent><br/
<hw>Pom*pe"ian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pompeianus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to, or characteristic
of, <city>Pompeii</city>, an ancient city of Italy, buried by an
eruption of Vesuvius in 79 <sc>a. d.</sc>, and partly uncovered by
modern excavations.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompeian red</ent><br/
<hw>Pompeian red</hw>. <fld>(Art)</fld> <def>A brownish red approaching
maroon, supposed to be imitated from the color of the wall panels of
houses in Pompeii, which were decorated during the last age of the
Republic.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompelmous</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pel*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pompelmouses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[D.
<ets>pompelmoes</ets>; cf. G. <ets>pompelmuse</ets>, F.
<ets>pamplemousse</ets>, and F. <ets>pompol<eacute/on</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A shaddock, esp. one of large size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompet</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pompette</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>The ball formerly
used to ink the type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompholyx</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pho*lyx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/ a
bubble, the slag on the surface of smelted ore, from <?/ a
blister.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Impure zinc
oxide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A skin disease in which there is
an eruption of bull<ae/, without inflammation or fever.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompillion</ent><br/
<hw>Pom*pil"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An ointment or
pomatum made of black poplar buds.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompion</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pi*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pompon</ets>. See <er>Pumpkin</er>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pumpion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompire</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pomum</ets>
a fruit, LL. also, an apple + <ets>pirum</ets> a pear.]</ety> <def>A
pearmain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompoleon</ent><br/
<hw>Pom*po"le*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pompelmous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pom-pom</ent><br/
<ent>Pompom</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pom"pom`</hw>, <hw>Pom"-pom`</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Imitative.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>originally,
a Vickers-Maxim one-pounder automatic machine cannon using metallic
ammunition fed from a lopped belt attached to the gun; -- popularly so
called from its peculiar drumming sound in action. More recently, the
term is applied mostly to automatic antiaircraft cannons.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompom</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pom`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>an ornamental ball or
tuft of wool, feathers, or other fluffy material used as a decoration on
clothing such as hats and slippers.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a <er>pompon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompom girl</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pom girl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a female
cheerleader, especially one who waves a pompon{5} while performing a
cheering routine.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompon</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"pon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Any trifling ornament for a woman's dress or bonnet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A tuft or ball of wool, or the
like, sometimes worn by soldiers on the front of the hat (such as a
shako), instead of a feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A hardy garden chrysanthemum having
buttonlike heads of flowers.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any of several
dwarf varieties of the Provence rose.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>the globe-shaped flower head characteristic of
certain plants such as dahlias and chrysanthemums.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>a ball-shaped cluster of ribbons or streamers held
in the hand and waved by some cheerleaders at team sports contests. See
<er>pompom girl</er>. Called also <altname>pompom</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomposity</ent><br/
<hw>Pom*pos"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pomposities</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The quality or state
of being pompous; pompousness.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomposo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pom*po"so</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos>
<ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Grand and dignified; in grand
style.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pompous</ent><br/
<hw>Pomp"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pompeux</ets>, L. <ets>pomposus</ets>. See <er>Pomp</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Displaying pomp; stately; showy with grandeur;
magnificent; <as>as, a <ex>pompous</ex> procession</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ostentatious; pretentious; boastful; vainglorious;
<as>as, <ex>pompous</ex> manners; a <ex>pompous</ex> style.</as></def>
<ldquo/<xex>Pompous</xex> in high presumption.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>he <qex>pompous</qex> vanity of the old schoolmistress.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pom"ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pomp"ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomptine</ent><br/
<hw>Pomp"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pontine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pomwater</ent><br/
<hw>Pom"wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pomewater</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poncelet</ent><br/
<hw>Ponce"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[After Jean Victor
<etsep>Poncelet</etsep>, French engineer.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>A unit of power, being the power obtained from an expenditure of
one hundred kilogram-meters of energy per second. One poncelet equals
<it>g</it> watts, when <it>g</it> is the value of the acceleration of
gravity in centimeters.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poncho</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"cho</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Ponchos</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Sp.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A kind of cloak worn by the Spanish Americans, having the form of a
blanket, with a slit in the middle for the head to pass through. A kind
of <xex>poncho</xex> made of rubber or painted cloth is used by the
mounted troops in the United States service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A trade name for camlets, or stout
worsteds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pond</ent><br/
<hw>Pond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably originally, an
inclosed body of water, and the same word as <ets>pound</ets>. See
<er>Pound</er> an inclosure.]</ety> <def>A body of water, naturally or
artificially confined, and usually of less extent than a lake.</def>
<ldquo/Through <xex>pond</xex> or pool.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pond hen</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the American
coot. See <er>Coot</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pond
lily</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the water lily. See under
<er>Water</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Nymph<ae/a</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pond snail</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any gastropod
living in fresh-water ponds or lakes. The most common kinds are
air-breathing snails (<subclass>Pulmonifera</subclass>) belonging to
Limn<ae/a, Physa, Planorbis, and allied genera. The operculated species
are pectinibranchs, belonging to <gen>Melantho</gen>,
<gen>Valvata</gen>, and various other genera.</cd> -- <col><b>Pond
spice</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an American shrub
(<spn>Tetranthera geniculata</spn>) of the Laurel family, with small
oval leaves, and axillary clusters of little yellow flowers. The whole
plant is spicy. It grows in ponds and swamps from Virginia to
Florida.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Pond tortoise</b></col>, <col><b>Pond
turtle</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any freshwater tortoise
of the family <fam>Emydid<ae/</fam>. Numerous species are found in North
America.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1113 --></p>

<p><ent>Pond</ent><br/
<hw>Pond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make into a pond;
to collect, as water, in a pond by damming.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pond</ent><br/
<hw>Pond</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Ponder</er>.]</ety>
<def>To ponder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pleaseth you, <qex>pond</qex> your suppliant's plaint.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponder</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pondered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pondering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponderare</ets>, fr. <ets>pondus</ets>, <ets>ponderis</ets>, a
weight, fr. <ets>pendere</ets> to weigh: cf. F.
<ets>pond<eacute/rer</ets>. See <er>Pendant</er>, and cf. <er>Pound</er>
a weight.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To weigh.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To weigh in the mind; to view with deliberation; to
examine carefully; to consider attentively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Ponder</qex> the path of thy feet.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. iv.
26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To <er>Ponder</er>, <er>Consider</er>,
<er>Muse</er>.</syn> <usage> To <xex>consider</xex> means to view or
contemplate with fixed thought. To <xex>ponder</xex> is to dwell upon
with long and anxious attention, with a view to some practical result or
decision. To <xex>muse</xex> is simply to think upon continuously with
no definite object, or for the pleasure it gives. We <xex>consider</xex>
any subject which is fairly brought before us; we <xex>ponder</xex> a
concern involving great interests; we <xex>muse</xex> on the events of
childhood.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponder</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To think; to deliberate; to
muse; -- usually followed by <xex>on</xex> or <xex>over</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderability</ent><br/
<hw>Pon`der*a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pond<eacute/rabilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state
of being ponderable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderable</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponderabilis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pond<eacute/rable</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Capable of being weighed; having appreciable weight.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pon"der*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderal</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pond<eacute/ral</ets>.]</ety> <def>Estimated or ascertained by
weight; -- distinguished from <xex>numeral</xex>; <as>as, a
<ex>ponderal</ex> drachma</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderance</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponderans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>ponderare</ets> to weigh: cf. OF.
<ets>ponderant</ets> of weight.]</ety> <def>Weight; gravity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Gregory.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderary</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to weight; <as>as, a <ex>ponderary</ex> system</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>M'Culloch.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderate</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponderatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>ponderare</ets>. See
<er>Ponder</er>.]</ety> <def>To consider; to ponder.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderate</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To have weight or
influence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderation</ent><br/
<hw>Pon`der*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponderatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pond<eacute/ration</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act of weighing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderer</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
ponders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pondering</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deliberating.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pon"der*ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderosity</ent><br/
<hw>Pon`der*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Ponderosities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OF.
<ets>ponderosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
ponderous; weight; gravity; heaviness, ponderousness; <as>as, the
<ex>ponderosity</ex> of gold</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderous</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponderosus</ets>, from <ets>pondus</ets>, <ets>-eris</ets>, a
weight: cf. F. <ets>pond<eacute/reux</ets>. See <er>Ponder</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Very heavy; weighty; <as>as, a <ex>ponderous</ex>
shield; a <ex>ponderous</ex> load; the <ex>ponderous</ex>
elephant.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sepulcher . . . <br/
Hath oped his <qex>ponderous</qex> and marble jaws.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Important; momentous; forcible.</def> <ldquo/Your
more <xex>ponderous</xex> and settled project.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Heavy; dull; wanting; lightless or spirit; <as>as, a
<ex>ponderous</ex> style; a <ex>ponderous</ex> joke.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ponderous spar</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>heavy
spar, or barytes. See <er>Barite</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderously</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a ponderous
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponderousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"der*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being ponderous; ponderosity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pondfish</ent><br/
<hw>Pond"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of American fresh-water fishes
belonging to the family <fam>Centrarchid<ae/</fam>; -- called also
<altname>pond perch</altname>, and <altname>sunfish</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common pondfish of New England (<spn>Lepomis
gibbosus</spn>) is called also <altname>bream</altname>,
<altname>pumpkin seed</altname>, and <altname>sunny</altname>. See
<er>Sunfish</er>. The long-eared pondfish (<spn>Lepomis auritus</spn>)
of the Eastern United States is distinguished by its very long opercular
flap.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pondweed</ent><br/
<hw>Pond"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any aquatic plant of the genus <gen>Potamogeton</gen>, of which
many species are found in ponds or slow-moving rivers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Choke pondweed</b></col>, <cd>an American water weed
(<spn>Anarcharis Canadensis</spn>, syn. <spn>Elodea Canadensis</spn>.)
See <er>Anacharis</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Horned pondweed</b></col>,
<cd>the <spn>Zannichellia palustris</spn>, a slender, branching aquatic
plant, having pointed nutlets.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pone</ent><br/
<hw>Pone</hw> <pr>(p<omac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of Amer. Indian
origin.]</ety> <def>A kind of johnnycake.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>paune</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Southern U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pone</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ne</hw> <pr>(p<omac/"n<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pone</ets>, imper. of <ets>ponere</ets> to place.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An original writ, now superseded by the
writ of certiorari, for removing a case from an inferior court into the
Court of Exchequer.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An obsolete writ to enforce
appearance in court by attaching goods or requiring
securities.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pr>(<it>pron.</it> p<omac/n)</pr> <fld>(Card
Playing)</fld> <def>The player who cuts the cards, being usually the
player on the dealer's right.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponent</ent><br/
<hw>Po"nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF., fr. It.
<ets>ponente</ets>, properly, setting (applied to the setting sun), fr.
L. <ets>ponens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>ponere</ets> to set, put.]</ety>
<def>Western; occidental.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forth rush the levant and the <qex>ponent</qex> winds.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pongee</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*gee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of East Indian
origin.]</ety> <def>A fabric of undyed silk from India and
China.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponghee</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*ghee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
name.]</ety> <def>A Buddhist priest of the higher orders in
Burmah.</def> <rj><au>Malcom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pongo</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
large ape; especially, the chimpanzee and the orang-outang.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poniard</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"iard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poignard</ets> (cf. It. <ets>pugnale</ets>, Sp.
<ets>pu<ntil/al</ets>), fr. L. <ets>pugio</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>;
probably akin to <ets>pugnus</ets> fist, or fr. <ets>pugnus</ets> fist,
as held in the fist. See <er>Pugnacious</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of
dagger, -- usually a slender one with a triangular or square
blade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She speaks <qex>poniards</qex>, and every word stabs.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poniard</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"iard</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Poniarded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poniarding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To pierce with a poniard; to
stab.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponibility</ent><br/
<hw>Po`ni*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>ponere</ets> to place.]</ety> <def>The capability of being placed
or located.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pons</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pons</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pontes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a bridge.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A bridge; -- applied to several parts which
connect others, but especially to the <xex>pons Varolii</xex>, a
prominent band of nervous tissue situated on the ventral side of the
medulla oblongata and connected at each side with the hemispheres of the
cerebellum; the mesocephalon. See <er>Brain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Pons asinorum</b></col>. <ety>[L., literally, bridge
of asses.]</ety> <cd>See <cref>Asses' bridge</cref>, under
<er>Ass</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontage</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"tage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>pontagium</ets>, from L. <ets>pons</ets>, <ets>pontis</ets>, a
bridge: cf. F. <ets>pontage</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>A duty or tax paid for repairing bridges.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontee</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pontil</ets>, <ets>pontis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Glass Making)</fld>
<def>An iron rod used by glass makers for manipulating the hot glass; --
called also, <altname>puntil</altname>, <altname>puntel</altname>,
<altname>punty</altname>, and <altname>ponty</altname>. See
<er>Fascet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontic</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Ponticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ the sea, especially, the Black
Sea.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Pontus, Euxine, or Black
Sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifex</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pon"ti*fex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pontifices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A high
priest; a pontiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontiff</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"tiff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pontife</ets>, L. <ets>pontifex</ets>, <ets>-ficis</ets>;
<ets>pons</ets>, <ets>pontis</ets>, a bridge (perhaps originally, a way,
path) + <ets>facere</ets> to make. Cf. <er>Pontoon</er>.]</ety> <def>A
high priest.</def> Especially: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the sacred
college, in ancient Rome, which had the supreme jurisdiction over all
matters of religion, at the head of which was the <xex>Pontifex
Maximus</xex>.</def> <au>Dr. W. Smith.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Jewish
Antiq.)</fld> <def>The chief priest.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(R. C.
Ch.)</fld> <def>The pope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontific</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>pontificius</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to, or
consisting of, pontiffs or priests.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>pontific</xex>
college with their augurs and flamens.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the pope; papal.</def>
<rj><au>Shenstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifical</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pontificalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pontifical</ets>. See
<er>Pontiff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a pontiff,
or high priest; <as>as, <ex>pontifical</ex> authority</as>; hence,
belonging to the pope; papal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the building of bridges.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now had they brought the work by wondrous art<br/
<qex>Pontifical</qex>, a ridge of pendent rock<br/
Over the vexed abyss.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifical</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif"ic*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
book containing the offices, or formulas, used by a pontiff.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The dress and ornaments of a
pontiff.</def> <ldquo/Dressed in full <xex>pontificals</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontificality</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif`i*cal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
and government of the pope; the papacy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifically</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pontifical
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontificate</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pontificatus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pontificat</ets>. See
<er>Pontiff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or dignity of a high
priest; specifically, the office of the pope.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The term of office of a pontiff.</def>
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontificate</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*tif"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(R. C.
Ch.)</fld> <def>To perform the duty of a pontiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifice</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"ti*fice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pons</ets>, <ets>pontis</ets>, a bridge + <ets>facere</ets> to
make. Cf. <er>Pontiff</er>.]</ety> <def>Bridgework; structure or edifice
of a bridge.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontificial</ent><br/
<hw>Pon`ti*fi"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pontificius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Papal; pontifical.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pontificial</xex> writers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifician</ent><br/
<hw>Pon`ti*fi"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the pontiff or pope.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontifician</ent><br/
<hw>Pon`ti*fi"cian</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who adheres to the pope
or papacy; a papist.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontil</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"til</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pontee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontile</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pontilis</ets> pertaining to a bridge.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the pons Varolii. See <er>Pons</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontine</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pontinus</ets> or <ets>Pomptinus</ets>, an appellation given to a
district in Latium, near <ets>Pometia</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to an extensive marshy district between Rome and
Naples.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>Pomptine</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontlevis</ent><br/
<hw>Pont"le*vis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., properly, a
drawbridge.]</ety> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>The action of a horse in
rearing repeatedly and dangerously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponton</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*ton"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pontoon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontoon</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*toon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>ponton</ets> (cf. It. <ets>pontone</ets>), from L.
<ets>ponto</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, fr. <ets>pons</ets>,
<ets>pontis</ets>, a bridge, perhaps originally, a way, path: cf. Gr.
<?/ path, Skr. <ets>path</ets>, <ets>pathi</ets>, <ets>panthan</ets>.
Cf. <er>Punt</er> a boat.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A
wooden flat-bottomed boat, a metallic cylinder, or a frame covered with
canvas, India rubber, etc., forming a portable float, used in building
bridges quickly for the passage of troops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A low, flat vessel, resembling a
barge, furnished with cranes, capstans, and other machinery, used in
careening ships, raising weights, drawing piles, etc., chiefly in the
Mediterranean; a lighter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pontoon bridge</b></col>, <cd>a bridge formed with
pontoons.</cd> -- <col><b>Pontoon train</b></col>, <cd>the carriages of
the pontoons, and the materials they carry for making a pontoon
bridge.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The French spelling <xex>ponton</xex> often appears in
scientific works, but <xex>pontoon</xex> is more common form.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontooning</ent><br/
<hw>Pon*toon"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act, art, or process of
constructing pontoon bridges.</def> <ldquo/Army instruction in
<xex>pontooning</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Gen. W. T. Shermah.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pontvolant</ent><br/
<hw>Pont`vo*lant"</hw> <pr>(?; F. ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pont</ets> bridge + <ets>volant</ets> flying.]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A kind of light bridge, used in sieges, for
surprising a post or outwork which has but a narrow moat; a flying
bridge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ponty</ent><br/
<hw>Pon"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Class Making)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pontee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pony</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Ponies</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>poney</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Gael. <ets>ponaidh</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A small horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Twenty-five pounds sterling.</def> <mark>[Slang,
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A translation or a key used to avoid study in
getting lessons; a crib; a trot.</def> <mark>[College Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A small glass of beer.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pony chaise</b></col>, <cd>a light, low chaise, drawn by
a pony or a pair of ponies.</cd> -- <col><b>Pony engine</b></col>, <cd>a
small locomotive for switching cars from one track to another.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pony truck</b></col> <fld>(Locomotive
Engine)</fld>, <cd>a truck which has only two wheels.</cd> --
<col><b>Pony truss</b></col> <fld>(Bridge Building)</fld>, <cd>a truss
which has so little height that overhead bracing can not be
used.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>poo</ent><br/
<ent>poop</ent><br/
<ent>poopoo</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>poo</hw>, <hw>poop</hw>, <hw>poopoo</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Excrement; feces; -- informal baby talk sometimes used as a
euphemism.</def> <mark>[slang]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pooch</ent><br/
<hw>pooch</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dog.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pooch</ent><br/
<hw>pooch</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To round one's lips as if intending
to kiss.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> pooch out.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pood</ent><br/
<hw>Pood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>pud'</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Russian weight, equal to forty Russian
pounds or about thirty-six English pounds avoirdupois.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poodle</ent><br/
<hw>Poo"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>pudel</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A breed of dogs having
curly hair, and often showing remarkable intelligence in the performance
of tricks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pooh</ent><br/
<hw>Pooh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[Of. imitative
origin; cf. Icel. <ets>p<umac/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pshaw! pish! nonsense!
-- an expression of scorn, dislike, or contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pooh-pooh</ent><br/
<hw>Pooh`-pooh"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make light
of; to treat with derision or contempt, <as>as if by saying
<ex>pooh</ex>! <ex>pooh</ex>!</as></def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pookoo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Poo"koo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A red African antelope (<spn>Kobus
Vardoni</spn>) allied to the water buck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pool</ent><br/
<hw>Pool</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>p<omac/l</ets>;
akin to LG. <ets>pool</ets>, <ets>pohl</ets>, D. <ets>poel</ets>, G.
<ets>pfuhl</ets>; cf. Icel. <ets>pollr</ets>, also W. <ets>pwll</ets>,
Gael. <ets>poll</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small and rather deep
collection of (usually) fresh water, as one supplied by a spring, or
occurring in the course of a stream; a reservoir for water; <as>as, the
<ex>pools</ex> of Solomon</as>.</def> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Charity will hardly water the ground where it must first fill a
<qex>pool</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sleepy <qex>pool</qex> above the dam.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small body of standing or stagnant water; a
puddle.</def> <ldquo/The filthy mantled <xex>pool</xex> beyond your
cell.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pool</ent><br/
<hw>Pool</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poule</ets>, properly, a hen.
See <er>Pullet</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>poule</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The stake played for in
certain games of cards, billiards, etc.; an aggregated stake to which
each player has contributed a snare; also, the receptacle for the
stakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A game at billiards, in which each of the players
stakes a certain sum, the winner taking the whole; also, in public
billiard rooms, a game in which the loser pays the entrance fee for all
who engage in the game; a game of skill in pocketing the balls on a pool
table.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This game is played variously, but commonly with fifteen
balls, besides one cue ball, the contest being to drive the most balls
into the pockets.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He plays <qex>pool</qex> at the billiard houses.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In rifle shooting, a contest in which each
competitor pays a certain sum for every shot he makes, the net proceeds
being divided among the winners.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any gambling or commercial venture in which several
persons join.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A combination of persons contributing money to be
used for the purpose of increasing or depressing the market price of
stocks, grain, or other commodities; also, the aggregate of the sums so
contributed; <as>as, the <ex>pool</ex> took all the wheat offered below
the limit; he put $10,000 into the <ex>pool</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>A mutual arrangement between
competing lines, by which the receipts of all are aggregated, and then
distributed <xex>pro rata</xex> according to agreement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An aggregation of properties or
rights, belonging to different people in a community, in a common fund,
to be charged with common liabilities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pin pool</b></col>, <cd>a variety of the game of
billiards in which small wooden pins are set up to be knocked down by
the balls.</cd> -- <col><b>Pool ball</b></col>, <cd>one of the colored
ivory balls used in playing the game at billiards called
<xex>pool</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pool snipe</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the European redshank.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pool table</b></col>, <cd>a billiard table with
pockets.</cd></cs><-- pool hall, a commercial establishment where
customers may play pool for a fee. pool room, (a) a room containing a
pool table as its most prominent feature. (b) pool hall. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pool</ent><br/
<hw>Pool</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pooled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pooling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To put together; to contribute to
a common fund, on the basis of a mutual division of profits or losses;
to make a common interest of; <as>as, the companies <ex>pooled</ex>
their traffic</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Finally, it favors the <qex>pooling</qex>of all issues.</q>
<rj><qau>U. S. Grant.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pool</ent><br/
<hw>Pool</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To combine or contribute with
others, as for a commercial, speculative, or gambling
transaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pooler</ent><br/
<hw>Pool"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stick for stirring a
tan vat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pooling</ent><br/
<hw>Pool"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The act of
uniting, or an agreement to unite, an aggregation of properties
belonging to different persons, with a view to common liabilities or
profits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poon</ent><br/
<hw>Poon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Canarese
<ets>ponne</ets>.]</ety> <def>A name for several East Indian, or their
wood, used for the masts and spars of vessels, as <spn>Calophyllum
angustifolium</spn>, <spn>Calophyllum inophullum</spn>, and
<spn>Sterculia f<oe/tida</spn>; -- called also
<altname>peon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poonac</ent><br/
<hw>Poo"nac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of oil cake
prepared from the cocoanut. See <cref>Oil cake</cref>, under
<er>Cake</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poonah painting</ent><br/
<hw>Poo"nah paint`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From
<etsep>Poona</etsep>, in Bombay Province, India.]</ety> <def>A style of
painting, popular in England in the 19th century, in which a thick
opaque color is applied without background and with scarcely any
shading, to thin paper, producing flowers, birds, etc., in imitation of
Oriental work.</def> <note>Hence: <col><b>Poonah brush</b></col>,
<col><b>paper</b></col>, <col><b>painter</b></col>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poonga oil</ent><br/
<hw>Poon"ga oil`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A kind of oil used in India for
lamps, and for boiling with dammar for pitching vessels. It is pressed
from the seeds of a leguminous tree (<spn>Pongamia
glabra</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poop</ent><br/
<hw>Poop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>See 2d
<er>Poppy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poop</ent><br/
<hw>Poop</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pooped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pooping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>poepen</ets>. See
<er>Pop</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a noise; to pop; also, to break
wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poop</ent><br/
<hw>Poop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poupe</ets>; cf. Sp. & Pg.
<ets>popa</ets>, It. <ets>poppa</ets>; all fr. L.
<ets>puppis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A deck raised above
the after part of a vessel; the hindmost or after part of a vessel's
hull; also, a cabin covered by such a deck. See <cref>Poop deck</cref>,
under <er>Deck</er>. See also <er>Roundhouse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With wind in <qex>poop</qex>, the vessel plows the sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>poop</qex> was beaten gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poop</ent><br/
<hw>Poop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To
break over the poop or stern, as a wave.</def> <ldquo/A sea which he
thought was going to <xex>poop</xex> her.<rdquo/ <au>Lord Dufferin.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To strike in the stern, as by collision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pooped</ent><br/
<hw>Pooped</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/pt)</pr>, <pos>p. p. & a.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a poop; furnished with a
poop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Struck on the poop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pooped</ent><br/
<ent>pooped out</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pooped</hw>, <hw>Pooped out</hw></mhw> <pr>(p<oomac/pt`
out")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tired; exhausted; fatigued.</def>
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pooping</ent><br/
<hw>Poop"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The act or shock of striking a vessel's stern by a following wave
or vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1114 --></p>

<p><ent>Poor</ent><br/
<hw>Poor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Poorer</adjf> <pr>(?; 254)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Poorest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>poure</ets> or
<ets>povre</ets>, OF. <ets>povre</ets>, F. <ets>pauvre</ets>, L.
<ets>pauper</ets>; the first syllable of which is probably akin to
<ets>paucus</ets> few (see <er>Paucity</er>, <er>Few</er>), and the
second to <ets>parare</ets> to prepare, procure. See <er>Few</er>, and
cf. <er>Parade</er>, <er>Pauper</er>, <er>Poverty</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Destitute of property; wanting in material riches or
goods; needy; indigent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is often synonymous with <xex>indigent</xex> and with
<xex>necessitous</xex> denoting extreme want. It is also applied to
persons who are not entirely destitute of property, but who are not
rich; as, a <xex>poor</xex> man or woman; <xex>poor</xex>
people.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>So completely destitute of property
as to be entitled to maintenance from the public.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> Hence, in very various applications: <def>Destitute of
such qualities as are desirable, or might naturally be expected</def>;
as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Wanting in fat, plumpness, or fleshiness; lean;
emaciated; meager; <as>as, a <ex>poor</ex> horse, ox, dog,
etc.</as></def> <ldquo/Seven other kine came up after them,
<xex>poor</xex> and very ill-favored and lean-fleshed.<rdquo/ <au>Gen.
xli. 19.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Wanting in strength or vigor; feeble;
dejected; <as>as, <ex>poor</ex> health; <ex>poor</ex>
spirits</as>.</def> <ldquo/His genius . . . <xex>poor</xex> and
cowardly.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Of little value or
worth; not good; inferior; shabby; mean; <as>as, <ex>poor</ex> clothes;
<ex>poor</ex> lodgings</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>poor</xex>
vessel.<rdquo/ <au>Clarendon.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>Destitute of
fertility; exhausted; barren; sterile; -- said of land; <as>as,
<ex>poor</ex> soil</as>.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>Destitute of beauty,
fitness, or merit; <as>as, a <ex>poor</ex> discourse; a <ex>poor</ex>
picture</as>.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>Without prosperous conditions or
good results; unfavorable; unfortunate; unconformable; <as>as, a
<ex>poor</ex> business; the sick man had a <ex>poor</ex>
night</as>.</def> <sd>(g)</sd> <def>Inadequate; insufficient;
insignificant; <as>as, a <ex>poor</ex> excuse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That I have wronged no man will be a <qex>poor</qex> plea or
apology at the last day.</q> <rj><qau>Calamy.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Worthy of pity or sympathy; -- used also sometimes
as a term of endearment, or as an expression of modesty, and sometimes
as a word of contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And for mine own <qex>poor</qex> part,<br/
Look you, I'll go pray.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Poor</qex>, little, pretty, fluttering thing.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Free from self-assertion; not proud or arrogant;
meek.</def> <ldquo/Blessed are the <xex>poor</xex> in spirit.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Matt. v. 3.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poor law</b></col>, <cd>a law providing for, or
regulating, the relief or support of the poor.</cd> -- <col><b>Poor
man's treacle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>garlic; -- so called
because it was thought to be an antidote to animal poison.</cd>
<mark>[Eng]</mark> <au>Dr. Prior.</au> -- <col><b>Poor man's
weatherglass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the red-flowered pimpernel
(<spn>Anagallis arvensis</spn>), which opens its blossoms only in fair
weather.</cd> -- <col><b>Poor rate</b></col>, <cd>an assessment or tax,
as in an English parish, for the relief or support of the poor.</cd> --
<col><b>Poor soldier</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the friar
bird.</cd> -- <col><b>The poor</b></col>, <cd>those who are destitute of
property; the indigent; the needy. In a legal sense, those who depend on
charity or maintenance by the public.</cd> <ldquo/I have observed the
more public provisions are made for <xex>the poor</xex>, the less they
provide for themselves.<rdquo/ <au>Franklin.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poor</ent><br/
<hw>Poor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
small European codfish (<spn>Gadus minutus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>power cod</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poorbox</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A receptacle in
which money given for the poor is placed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poorhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dwelling for a
number of paupers maintained at public expense; an almshouse; a
workhouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poor-john</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"-john`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A small European fish, similar to the cod, but of inferior
quality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Poor-john</qex> and apple pies are all our fare.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir J. Harrington.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poorliness</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being poorly; ill health.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poorly</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a poor manner or
condition; without plenty, or sufficiency, or suitable provision for
comfort; <as>as, to live <ex>poorly</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With little or no success; indifferently; with
little profit or advantage; <as>as, to do <ex>poorly</ex> in
business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Meanly; without spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor is their courage or their wealth so low,<br/
That from his wars they <qex>poorly</qex> would retire.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Without skill or merit; <as>as, he performs
<ex>poorly</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poorly off</b></col>, <cd>not well off; not
rich.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poorly</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat ill; indisposed; not in
health.</def> <ldquo/Having been <xex>poorly</xex> in health.<rdquo/
<rj><au>T. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poorness</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
poor (in any of the senses of the adjective).</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poor-spirited</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"-spir`it*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a mean
spirit; cowardly; base.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Poor"-spir`it*ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poor-will</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"-will`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called in
imitation of its note.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A bird of the
Western United States (<spn>Phal<ae/noptilus Nutalli</spn>) allied to
the whip-poor-will.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poor-willie</ent><br/
<hw>Poor"-wil`lie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called in
imitation of its note.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The bar-tailed
godwit.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pop</ent><br/
<hw>Pop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of imitative origin. Cf.
<er>Poop</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small, sharp, quick explosive
sound or report; <as>as, to go off with a <ex>pop</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A nonalcoholic carbonated beverage; -- so called
because it expels the cork with a pop from the bottle containing it;
<as>as, ginger <ex>pop</ex>; lemon <ex>pop</ex>, etc.</as></def>
<rj><au>Hood.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> soda, soda pop, minerals.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European redwing.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pop corn</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Corn, or maize, of
peculiar excellence for popping; especially, a kind the grains of which
are small and compact</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Popped corn; corn which has
been popped.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pop</ent><br/
<hw>Pop</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Popped</conjf> <pr>(p<ocr/pt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Popping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make a pop, or
sharp, quick sound; <as>as, the muskets <ex>popped</ex> away on all
sides</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To enter, or issue forth, with a quick, sudden
movement; to move from place to place suddenly; to dart; -- with
<xex>in</xex>, <xex>out</xex>, <xex>upon</xex>, <xex>off</xex>,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that killed my king . . . <br/
<qex>Popp'd</qex> in between the election and my hopes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A trick of <qex>popping</qex> up and down every moment.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To burst open with a pop, when heated over a fire;
<as>as, this corn <ex>pops</ex> well</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pop</ent><br/
<hw>Pop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust or push
suddenly; to offer suddenly; to bring suddenly and unexpectedly to
notice; <as>as, to <ex>pop</ex> one's head in at the
door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>popped</qex> a paper into his hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to pop; to cause to burst open by heat, as
grains of Indian corn; <as>as, to <ex>pop</ex> corn or
chestnuts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To eat or swallow; -- of food, especially snacks, in
small pieces; <as>as, he <ex>popped</ex> a whole can of peanuts while
watching the movie</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pop off</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to thrust away, or
put off promptly; as, to <xex>pop</xex> one off with a denial.</cd>
<au>Locke.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>to make a statement, or series of
statements, forcefully and in an opinionated manner; <as>as, he
<ex>popped off</ex> about his dislike of modern art</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To pop the question</b></col>, <cd>to make an offer of marriage
to a lady.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <au>Dickens.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pop</ent><br/
<hw>Pop</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/p)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Like a pop;
suddenly; unexpectedly.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pop</xex> goes his
plate.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pop corn</ent><br/
<ent>popcorn</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pop"corn`</hw>, <hw>pop" corn`</hw></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>See <cref>pop corn</cref> under <er>pop</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pope</ent><br/
<hw>Pope</hw> <pr>(p<omac/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>p<amac/pa</ets>, L. <ets>papa</ets> father, bishop. Cf.
<er>Papa</er>, <er>Papal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any ecclesiastic,
esp. a bishop.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The bishop of Rome, the head of the Roman Catholic
Church. See <er>Note</er> under <er>Cardinal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A parish priest, or a chaplain, of the Greek
Church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A fish; the ruff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pope Joan</b></col>, <cd>a game at cards played on a
round board with compartments.</cd> -- <col><b>Pope's eye</b></col>,
<cd>the gland surrounded with fat in the middle of the thigh of an ox or
sheep.</cd> <au>R. D. Blackmore.</au> -- <col><b>Pope's nose</b></col>,
<cd>the rump, or uropygium, of a bird. See <er>Uropygium</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>to be more Catholic than the Pope</b></col> <cd>to adhere more
stringently to Roman Catholic practices and doctrine than is required by
church doctrine; -- usually used in a negative sense to mean, to be
excessively pious.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popedom</ent><br/
<hw>Pope"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>p<amac/ped<omac/m</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The place, office,
or dignity of the pope; papal dignity.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The jurisdiction of the pope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popeling</ent><br/
<hw>Pope"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A petty
or deputy pope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An adherent of the pope.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Marlowe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popelote</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"e*lote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A word variously
explained as <ldquo/a little puppet,<rdquo/ <ldquo/a little doll,<rdquo/
or <ldquo/a young butterfly.<rdquo/ Cf. <er>Popet</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So gay a <qex>popelote</qex>, so sweet a wench.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popery</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The religion of the
Roman Catholic Church, comprehending doctrines and practices; --
generally used in an opprobrious sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pope's head</ent><br/
<hw>Pope's head</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A long-handled brush for dusting
ceilings, etc., also for washing windows.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popet</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A puppet.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popgun</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"gun`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A child's gun; a tube
and rammer for shooting pellets, with a popping noise, by compression of
air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popinjay</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"in*jay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>popingay</ets>, <ets>papejay</ets>, OF. <ets>papegai</ets>,
<ets>papegaut</ets>; cf. Pr. <ets>papagai</ets>, Sp. & Pg.
<ets>papagayo</ets>, It. <ets>pappagallo</ets>, LGr. <?/, NGr. <?/; in
which the first syllables are perhaps imitative of the bird's chatter,
and the last either fr. L. <ets>gallus</ets> cock, or the same word as
E. <ets>jay</ets>, F. <ets>geai</ets>. Cf. <er>Papagay</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The green
woodpecker.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A parrot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pye and <qex>popyngay</qex> speak they know not what.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A target in the form of a parrot.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A trifling, chattering, fop or coxcomb.</def>
<ldquo/To be so pestered with a <xex>popinjay</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popish</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
the pope; taught or ordained by the pope; hence, of or pertaining to the
Roman Catholic Church; -- often used opprobriously.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pop"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pop"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poplar</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>popler</ets>, OF. <ets>poplier</ets>, F. <ets>peuplier</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>populus</ets> poplar.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Any tree of the genus <gen>Populus</gen>; also, the timber, which
is soft, and capable of many uses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The aspen poplar is <spn>Populus tremula</spn> and
<spn>Populus tremuloides</spn>; Balsam poplar is <spn>Populus
balsamifera</spn>; Lombardy poplar (<spn>Populus dilatata</spn>) is a
tall, spiry tree; white poplar is <spn>Populus alba</spn>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The timber of the tulip tree; -- called also
<altname>white poplar</altname>.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poplexy</ent><br/
<hw>Po*plex"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Apoplexy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poplin</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>popeline</ets>, <ets>papeline</ets>.]</ety> <def>A fabric of many
varieties, usually made of silk and worsted, -- used especially for
women's dresses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Irish poplin</b></col>, <cd>a fabric with silk warp and
worsted weft, made in Ireland.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popliteal</ent><br/
<hw>Pop*lit"e*al</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>poples</ets>, <ets>-itis</ets>, the ham.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the ham; in the region of the ham, or behind
the knee joint; <as>as, the <ex>popliteal</ex> space</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poplitic</ent><br/
<hw>Pop*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Popliteal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popovtsy</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*pov"tsy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Russ.,
prop., those having popes or priests.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Raskolnik</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popper</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A utensil for popping
corn, usually a wire basket with a long handle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popper</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"per</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dagger.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poppet</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>See
<er>Puppet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>One of certain upright timbers on
the bilge ways, used to support a vessel in launching.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>An upright support or guide
fastened at the bottom only.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>same as <er>poppit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Poppet head</b></col>, <col><b>Puppet
head</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <er>Headstock</er>
<sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poppied</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"pied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 1st
<er>Poppy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Mingled or interspersed with
poppies.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Poppied</xex> corn.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Affected with poppy juice; hence, figuratively,
drugged; drowsy; listless; inactive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>poppied</qex> sails doze on the yard.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popping</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Pop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Popping crease</b></col>. <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Crease</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poppit</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"pit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a small plastic bead
with opposed holes and protuberant knobs, so shaped that the beads may
be fastened to each other in chains and detached easily, and can be used
to form variable-length chains for use as necklaces, bracelets, or other
ornamental objects. Also called <altname>poppet</altname> and
<altname>poppit bead</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popple</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pop</er>.]</ety> <def>To move quickly up and down; to bob up and
down, as a cork on rough water; also, to bubble.</def>
<rj><au>Cotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popple</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"ple</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The poplar.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tares.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To sow
<xex>popple</xex> among wheat.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poppy</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Poppies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>popy</ets>, AS.
<ets>popig</ets>, L. <ets>papaver</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant or species of the genus <gen>Papaver</gen>, herbs with
showy polypetalous flowers and a milky juice. From one species
(<spn>Papaver somniferum</spn>) opium is obtained, though all the
species contain it to some extent; also, a flower of the plant. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Capsule</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>California poppy</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any
yellow-flowered plant of the genus <gen>Eschscholtzia</gen>.</cd> --
<col><b>Corn poppy</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Corn</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Horn poppy</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Horned
poppy</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Horn</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Poppy bee</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a leaf-cutting bee
(<spn>Anthocopa papaveris</spn>) which uses pieces cut from poppy petals
for the lining of its cells; -- called also <altname>upholsterer
bee</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prickly poppy</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd><spn>Argemone Mexicana</spn>, a yellow-flowered
plant of the Poppy family, but as prickly as a thistle.</cd> --
<col><b>Poppy seed</b></col>, <cd>the seed the opium poppy (<spn>Papaver
somniferum</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Spatling poppy</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a species of Silene (<spn>Silene inflata</spn>).
See <er>Catchfly</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poppyhead</ent><br/
<ent>Poppy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pop"py*head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poup<eacute/e</ets> doll, puppet.
See <er>Puppet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A raised ornament
frequently having the form of a final. It is generally used on the tops
of the upright ends or elbows which terminate seats, etc., in Gothic
churches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populace</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>populace</ets>, fr. It. <ets>popolaccio</ets>,
<ets>popolazzo</ets>, fr. <ets>popolo</ets> people, L.
<ets>populus</ets>. See <er>People</er>.]</ety> <def>The common people;
the vulgar; the multitude, -- comprehending all persons not
distinguished by rank, office, education, or profession.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To . . . calm the peers and please the <qex>populace</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They . . . call us Britain's barbarous <qex>populaces</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Mob; people; commonalty.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*la*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Populace.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popular</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>popularis</ets>, fr. <ets>populus</ets> people: cf. F.
<ets>populaire</ets>. See <er>People</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the common people, or to the whole body of the people, as
distinguished from a select portion; <as>as, the <ex>popular</ex> voice;
<ex>popular</ex> elections.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Popular</xex>
states.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au> <ldquo/So the <xex>popular</xex> vote
inclines.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The men commonly held in <qex>popular</qex> estimation are
greatest at a distance.</q> <rj><qau>J. H. Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suitable to common people; easy to be comprehended;
not abstruse; familiar; plain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Homilies are plain <qex>popular</qex> instructions.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Adapted to the means of the common people; possessed
or obtainable by the many; hence, cheap; common; ordinary; inferior;
<as>as, <ex>popular</ex> prices; <ex>popular</ex>
amusements.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The smallest figs, called <qex>popular</qex> figs, . . . are, of
all others, the basest and of least account.</q>
<rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Beloved or approved by the people; pleasing to
people in general, or to many people; <as>as, a <ex>popular</ex>
preacher; a <ex>popular</ex> law; a <ex>popular</ex>
administration.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Devoted to the common people; studious of the favor
of the populace.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such <qex>popular</qex> humanity is treason.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Prevailing among the people; epidemic; <as>as, a
<ex>popular</ex> disease</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Popular action</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd> an action
in which any person may sue for penalty imposed by statute.</cd>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populares</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pop`u*la"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>The people or the people's party, in ancient Rome,
as opposed to the <xex>optimates</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popularity</ent><br/
<hw>Pop`u*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Popularities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>popularitas</ets> an effort to please the people: cf. F.
<ets>popularit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being popular; especially, the state of being esteemed by, or
of being in favor with, the people at large; good will or favor
proceeding from the people; <as>as, the <ex>popularity</ex> of a law,
statesman, or a book</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>popularity</qex> which has lasted down to our time.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being adapted or pleasing to
common, poor, or vulgar people; hence, cheapness; inferiority;
vulgarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This gallant laboring to avoid <qex>popularity</qex> falls into a
habit of affectation.</q> <rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something which obtains, or is intended to obtain,
the favor of the vulgar; claptrap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Popularities</qex>, and circumstances which . . . sway the
ordinary judgment.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of courting the favor of the people.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Indicted . . . for <xex>popularity</xex> and
ambition.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Public sentiment; general passion.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A little time be allowed for the madness of <qex>popularity</qex>
to cease.</q> <rj><qau>Bancroft.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popularization</ent><br/
<hw>Pop`u*lar*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
making popular, or of introducing among the people.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popularize</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Popularized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Popularizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>populariser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make popular; to make suitable
or acceptable to the common people; to make generally known; <as>as, to
<ex>popularize</ex> philosophy</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>popularizing</xex> of religious teaching.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popularizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lar*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
popularizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popularly</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lar*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a popular manner; so as
to be generally favored or accepted by the people; commonly; currently;
<as>as, the story was <ex>popularity</ex> reported</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The victor knight,<br/
Bareheaded, <qex>popularly</qex> low had bowed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Popularness</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lar*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being popular; popularity.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populate</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populus</ets> people. See <er>People</er>.]</ety>
<def>Populous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populate</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Populated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Populating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To furnish with
inhabitants, either by natural increase or by immigration or
colonization; to cause to be inhabited; to people.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populate</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*late</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To propagate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great shoals of people which go on to <qex>populate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Population</ent><br/
<hw>Pop`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>population</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or process of populating; multiplication of
inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The whole number of people, or inhabitants, in a
country, or portion of a country; <as>as, a <ex>population</ex> of ten
millions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populator</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
populates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populicide</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*li*cide`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populus</ets> people + <ets>caedere</ets> to kill.]</ety>
<def>Slaughter of the people.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populin</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populus</ets> poplar: cf. F. <ets>populine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A glycoside, related to salicin, found in the
bark of certain species of the poplar (<gen>Populus</gen>), and
extracted as a sweet white crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1115 --></p>

<p><ent>Populism</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(U. S.
Politics)</fld> <def>The political doctrines advocated by the People's
party.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populist</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populus</ets> people + <ets>-ist</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(U. S.
Politics)</fld> <def>A member of the People's party.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pop`u*lis"tic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populosity</ent><br/
<hw>Pop`u*los"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populositas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>populosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Populousness.</def><mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Populous</ent><br/
<hw>Pop"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>populosus</ets>, fr. <ets>populus</ets> people: cf. F.
<ets>populeux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding in people; full
of inhabitants; containing many inhabitants in proportion to the extent
of the country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heaven, yet <qex>populous</qex>, retains<br/
Number sufficient to possess her realms.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Popular; famous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>J. Webster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Common; vulgar.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arden of Feversham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Numerous; in large number.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/The dust . . . raised by your <xex>populous</xex> troops.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pop"u*lous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pop"u*lous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poraille</ent><br/
<hw>Po*raille"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pouraille</ets>. See <er>Poor</er>.]</ety> <def>Poor people; the
poor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porbeagle</ent><br/
<hw>Por"bea`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A species of shark (<spn>Lamna cornubica</spn>), about eight feet
long, having a pointed nose and a crescent-shaped tail; -- called also
<altname>mackerel shark</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written <asp>also
probeagle</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcate</ent><br/
<hw>Por"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>porca</ets>
a ridge between two furrows.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having
grooves or furrows broader than the intervening ridges;
furrowed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcelain</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ce*lain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Purslain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcelain</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ce*lain</hw> <pr>(277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>porcelaine</ets>, It. <ets>porcellana</ets>, orig., the porcelain
shell, or Venus shell (<ets>Cypr<ae/a porcellana</ets>), from a dim. fr.
L. <ets>porcus</ets> pig, probably from the resemblance of the shell in
shape to a pig's back. Porcelain was called after this shell, either on
account of its smoothness and whiteness, or because it was believed to
be made from it. See <er>Pork</er>.]</ety> <def>A fine translucent or
semitransculent kind of earthenware, made first in China and Japan, but
now also in Europe and America; -- called also <altname>China</altname>,
or <altname>China ware</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Porcelain</qex>, by being pure, is apt to break.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ivory porcelain</b></col>, <cd>porcelain with a surface
like ivory, produced by depolishing. See <er>Depolishing</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Porcelain clay</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Clay</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Porcelain crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any crab of the
genus <gen>Porcellana</gen> and allied genera (family
<fam>Porcellanid<ae/</fam>). They have a smooth, polished carapace.</cd>
-- <col><b>Porcelain jasper</b></col>. <fld>(Min.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Porcelanite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Porcelain printing</b></col>,
<cd>the transferring of an impression of an engraving to porcelain.</cd>
-- <col><b>Porcelain shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
cowry.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcelainized</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ce*lain*ized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Baked like potter's lay; -- applied to clay shales that have been
converted by heat into a substance resembling porcelain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcellaneous</ent><br/
<ent>Porcelaneous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Por`ce*la"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Por`cel*la"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to porcelain; resembling porcelain;
<as>as, <ex>porcelaneous</ex> shells</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a smooth, compact shell
without pores; -- said of certain Foraminifera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcelanite</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ce*la*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>porcelanite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A semivitrified
clay or shale, somewhat resembling jasper; -- called also
<altname>porcelain jasper</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcellanous</ent><br/
<ent>Porcelanous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Por"ce*la`nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Por"cel*la`nous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Porcelaneous.</def>
<rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porch</ent><br/
<hw>Porch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>porche</ets>,
L. <ets>porticus</ets>, fr. <ets>porta</ets> a gate, entrance, or
passage. See <er>Port</er> a gate, and cf. <er>Portico</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A covered and inclosed entrance to a
building, whether taken from the interior, and forming a sort of
vestibule within the main wall, or projecting without and with a
separate roof. Sometimes the porch is large enough to serve as a covered
walk. See also <cref>Carriage porch</cref>, under <er>Carriage</er>, and
<er>Loggia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The graceless Helen in the <qex>porch</qex> I spied<br/
Of Vesta's temple.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A portico; a covered walk.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Repair to Pompey's <qex>porch</qex>, where you shall find find
us.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The Porch</b></col>, <cd>a public portico, or great hall,
in Athens, where Zeno, the philosopher, taught his disciples; hence,
sometimes used as equivalent to <xex>the school of the Stoics</xex>. It
was called <grk>"h poiki`lh stoa`</grk>. [See
<er>Poicile</er>.]</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcine</ent><br/
<hw>Por"cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>porcinus</ets>, from <ets>porcus</ets> a swine. See
<er>Pork</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to swine; characteristic of
the hog.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Porcine</xex> cheeks.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G.
Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porcupine</ent><br/
<hw>Por"cu*pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>porkepyn</ets>, <ets>porpentine</ets>, OF. <ets>porc-espi</ets>, F.
<ets>porc-<eacute/pic</ets> (cf. It. <ets>porco spino</ets>, <ets>porco
spinoso</ets>, Sp. <ets>puerco espino</ets>, <ets>puerco espin</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>porcus</ets> swine + <ets>spina</ets> thorn, spine). The
last part of the French word is perhaps a corruption from the It. or
Sp.; cf. F. <ets><eacute/pi</ets> ear, a spike of grain, L.
<ets>spica</ets>. See <er>Pork</er>, <er>Spike</er> a large nail,
<er>Spine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any Old Word
rodent of the genus <gen>Hystrix</gen>, having the back covered with
long, sharp, erectile spines or quills, sometimes a foot long. The
common species of Europe and Asia (<spn>Hystrix cristata</spn>) is the
best known.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of
<gen>Erethizon</gen> and related genera, native of America. They are
related to the true porcupines, but have shorter spines, and are
arboreal in their habits. The Canada porcupine (<spn>Erethizon
dorsatus</spn>) is a well known species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Porcupine ant-eater</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
echidna.</cd> -- <col><b>Porcupine crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a large spiny Japanese crab (<spn>Acantholithodes
hystrix</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Porcupine disease</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>. <cd>See <er>Ichthyosis</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Porcupine fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any plectognath
fish having the body covered with spines which become erect when the
body is inflated. See <er>Diodon</er>, and <er>Globefish</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Porcupine grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a grass
(<spn>Stipa spartea</spn>) with grains bearing a stout twisted awn,
which, by coiling and uncoiling through changes in moisture, propels the
sharp-pointed and barbellate grain into the wool and flesh of sheep. It
is found from Illinois westward. See <xex>Illustration</xex> in
Appendix.</cd> -- <col><b>Porcupine wood</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the hard outer wood of the cocoa palm; -- so called because, when
cut horizontally, the markings of the wood resemble the quills of a
porcupine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pore</ent><br/
<hw>Pore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>porus</ets>, Gr. <?/ a passage, a pore. See <er>Fare</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the minute orifices in an
animal or vegetable membrane, for transpiration, absorption,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A minute opening or passageway; an interstice
between the constituent particles or molecules of a body; <as>as, the
<ex>pores</ex> of stones</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pore</ent><br/
<hw>Pore</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pored</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>poren</ets>, of
uncertain origin; cf. D. <ets>porren</ets> to poke, thrust, Gael.
<ets>purr</ets>.]</ety> <def>To look or gaze steadily in reading or
studying; to fix the attention; to be absorbed; -- often with
<xex>on</xex> or <xex>upon</xex>, and now usually with
<xex>over</xex>.</def><ldquo/Painfully to <xex>pore</xex> upon a
book.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eye grows weary with <qex>poring</qex> perpetually on the same
thing.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poreblind</ent><br/
<hw>Pore"blind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Probably
influenced by <ets>pore</ets>, v. See <er>Purblind</er>.]</ety>
<def>Nearsighted; shortsighted; purblind.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porer</ent><br/
<hw>Por"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porgy</ent><br/
<hw>Por"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Porgies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See <er>Paugie</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The scup.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sailor's choice, or pinfish.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>The margate fish.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The spadefish.</def>
<sd>(e)</sd> <def>Any one of several species of embiotocoids, or surf
fishes, of the Pacific coast. The name is also given locally to several
other fishes, as the bur fish.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>porgee</asp>, <asp>porgie</asp>, and <asp>paugy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous sparoid food
fishes, as the <styp>jolthead <ex>porgy</ex></styp>, the
<styp>sheepshead <ex>porgy</ex></styp> (<spn>Calamus penna</spn>) of the
West Indies, the <styp>grass <ex>porgy</ex></styp> (<spn>Calamus
arctifrons</spn>) of Florida, and the <styp>red <ex>porgy</ex></styp>
(<spn>Pagrus pagrus</spn>) of Europe.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*rif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>porus</ets> pore + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A grand division of the Invertebrata, including
the sponges; -- called also <ord>Spongi<ae/</ord>, <ord>Spongida</ord>,
and <ord>Spongiozoa</ord>. The principal divisions are Calcispongi<ae/,
Keratosa or Fibrospongi<ae/, and Silicea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poriferan</ent><br/
<hw>Po*rif"er*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Polifera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poriferata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*rif`e*ra"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>The Polifera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poriform</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ri*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>porus</ets> pore + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>poriforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a pore, or small
puncture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porime</ent><br/
<hw>Po"rime</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
practicable.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A theorem or proposition so
easy of demonstration as to be almost self-evident.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poriness</ent><br/
<hw>Por"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Porosity.</def>
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porism</ent><br/
<hw>Po"rism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a thing
procured, a deduction from a demonstration, fr. <?/ to bring, provide:
cf. F. <ets>porisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A
proposition affirming the possibility of finding such conditions as will
render a certain determinate problem indeterminate or capable of
innumerable solutions.</def> <rj><au>Playfair.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Geom.)</fld> <def>A corollary.</def>
<rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Three books of <xex>porisms</xex> of Euclid have been
lost, but several attempts to determine the nature of these propositions
and to restore them have been made by modern geometers.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porismatical</ent><br/
<ent>Porismatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Po`ris*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Po`ris*mat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a porism;
poristic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poristical</ent><br/
<ent>Poristic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Po*ris"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Po*ris"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ for providing, <?/
provided.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a porism; of the nature of a
porism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porite</ent><br/
<hw>Po"rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>porite</ets>. See <er>Pore</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any coral of the genus Porites, or family
<fam>Poritid<ae/</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porites</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*ri"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>po`ros</grk> a pore.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An important
genus of reef-building corals having small twelve-rayed calicles, and a
very porous coral. Some species are branched, others grow in large
massive or globular forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pork</ent><br/
<hw>Pork</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>porc</ets>, L.
<ets>porcus</ets> hog, pig. See <er>Farrow</er> a litter of pigs, and
cf. <er>Porcelain</er>, <er>Porpoise</er>.]</ety> <def>The flesh of
swine, fresh or salted, used for food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porker</ent><br/
<hw>Pork"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hog.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porket</ent><br/
<hw>Pork"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of F.
<ets>porc</ets>. See <er>Pork</er>.]</ety> <def>A young hog; a
pig.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden. W. Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porkling</ent><br/
<hw>Pork"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pig; a
porket.</def> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porkwood</ent><br/
<hw>Pork"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The coarse-grained brownish yellow wood of a small tree
(<spn>Pisonia obtusata</spn>) of Florida and the West Indies. Also
called <altname>pigeon wood</altname>, <altname>beefwood</altname>, and
<altname>corkwood</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pornerastic</ent><br/
<hw>Por`ne*ras"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ harlot
+ <?/ to love.]</ety> <def>Lascivious; licentious.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>F. Harrison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pornographic</ent><br/
<hw>Por`no*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to pornography; lascivious; licentious; <as>as,
<ex>pornographic</ex> writing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pornography</ent><br/
<hw>Por*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
harlot + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Licentious painting
or literature; especially, the painting anciently employed to decorate
the walls of rooms devoted to bacchanalian orgies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A treatise on prostitutes, or
prostitution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>obscene pictures, writings, drawings, motion
pictures, videos, or the like intended primarily to cause sexual arousal
and having little or no artistic merit; also, the content of such
materials.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porosity</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ros"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>porosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
porous; -- opposed to <xex>density</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porotic</ent><br/
<hw>Po*rot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
callus.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine supposed to promote the
formation of callus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porotype</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ro*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pore</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and <er>-type</er>.]</ety> <def>A copy of a print,
writing, etc., made by placing it upon a chemically prepared paper which
is acted upon by a gas which permeates the paper of the print, writing,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porous</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>poreux</ets>. See <er>Pore</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Full of
pores; having interstices in the skin or in the substance of the body;
having spiracles or passages for fluids; permeable by liquids; <as>as, a
<ex>porous</ex> skin; <ex>porous</ex> wood.</as></def> <ldquo/The veins
of <xex>porous</xex> earth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porously</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a porous manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porousness</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of
being porous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The open parts; the interstices of anything.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They will forcibly get into the <qex>porousness</qex> of it.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir K. Digby.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porpentine</ent><br/
<hw>Por"pen*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Porcupine.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porpesse</ent><br/
<hw>Por"pesse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A porpoise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyraceous</ent><br/
<hw>Por`phy*ra"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Porphyritic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyre</ent><br/
<hw>Por"phyre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Porphyry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyrite</ent><br/
<hw>Por"phy*rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A rock with a porphyritic structure; <as>as, augite
<ex>porphyrite</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyritic</ent><br/
<hw>Por`phy*rit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>porphyritique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Relating to, or
resembling, porphyry, that is, characterized by the presence of distinct
crystals, as of feldspar, quartz, or augite, in a relatively
fine-grained base, often aphanitic or cryptocrystalline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyrization</ent><br/
<hw>Por`phy*ri*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
porphyrizing, or the state of being porphyrized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyrize</ent><br/
<hw>Por`phy*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>porphyriser</ets>, Gr. <?/ to purplish.]</ety> <def>To cause to
resemble porphyry; to make spotted in composition, like
porphyry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyrogenitism</ent><br/
<hw>Por`phy*ro*gen"i*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>porphyro genitus</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/; <?/ purple + root of
<grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born.]</ety> <def>The principle of
succession in royal families, especially among the Eastern Roman
emperors, by which a younger son, if born after the accession of his
father to the throne, was preferred to an elder son who was not so
born.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Palgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porphyry</ent><br/
<hw>Por"phy*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Porphyries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>porphyre</ets>,
L. <ets>porphyrites</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ like purple, fr. <?/ purple. See
<er>Purple</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A term used somewhat
loosely to designate a rock consisting of a fine-grained base (usually
feldspathic) through which crystals, as of feldspar or quartz, are
disseminated. There are red, purple, and green varieties, which are
highly esteemed as marbles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Porphyry shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
handsome marine gastropod shell (<spn>Oliva porphyria</spn>), having a
dark red or brown polished surface, marked with light spots, like
porphyry.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porpita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Por"pi*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ brooch.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of bright-colored
Siphonophora found floating in the warmer parts of the ocean. The
individuals are round and disk-shaped, with a large zooid in the center
of the under side, surrounded by smaller nutritive and reproductive
zooids, and by slender dactylozooids near the margin. The disk contains
a central float, or pneumatocyst.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porpoise</ent><br/
<hw>Por"poise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>porpeys</ets>, OF. <ets>porpeis</ets>, literally, hog fish, from L.
<ets>porcus</ets> swine + <ets>piscis</ets> fish. See <er>Pork</er>, and
<er>Fish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small
cetacean of the genus <gen>Phoc<ae/na</gen>, especially <spn>Phoc<ae/na
communis</spn>, or <spn>Phoc<ae/na phoc<ae/na</spn>, of Europe, and the
closely allied American species (<spn>Phoc<ae/na Americana</spn>). The
color is dusky or blackish above, paler beneath. They are closely allied
to the dolphins, but have a shorter snout. Called also <altname>harbor
porpoise</altname>, <altname>herring hag</altname>, <altname>puffing
pig</altname>, and <altname>snuffer</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A true dolphin
(<gen>Delphinus</gen>); -- often so called by sailors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Skunk porpoise</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Bay
porpoise</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a North American
porpoise (<spn>Lagenorhynchus acutus</spn>), larger than the common
species, and with broad stripes of white and yellow on the sides. See
<xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porporino</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Por`po*ri"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>A composition of quicksilver, tin, and sulphur, forming a yellow
powder, sometimes used by medi<ae/val artists, for the sake of economy,
instead of gold.</def> <rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porpus</ent><br/
<hw>Por"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A porpoise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porraceous</ent><br/
<hw>Por*ra"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>porraceus</ets>, from <ets>porrum</ets>, <ets>porrus</ets>, a
leek.]</ety> <def>Resembling the leek in color; greenish.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Porraceous</xex> vomiting.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porrect</ent><br/
<hw>Por*rect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>porrectus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>porrigere</ets> to stretch out
before one's self, to but forth.]</ety> <def>Extended horizontally;
stretched out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porrection</ent><br/
<hw>Por*rec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>porrectio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>porrection</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act
of stretching forth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porret</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>porrette</ets>, fr. L. <ets>porrum</ets>, <ets>porrus</ets>, leek.
See <er>Porraceous</er>.]</ety> <def>A scallion; a leek or small
onion.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porridge</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ridge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably corrupted
fr. <ets>pottage</ets>; perh. influenced by OE. <ets>porree</ets> a kind
of pottage, OF. <ets>porr<eacute/e</ets>, fr. L. <ets>porrum</ets>,
<ets>porrus</ets>, leek. See <er>Pottage</er>, and cf.
<er>Porringer</er>.]</ety> <def>A food made by boiling some leguminous
or farinaceous substance, or the meal of it, in water or in milk, making
of broth or thin pudding; <as>as, barley <ex>porridge</ex>, milk
<ex>porridge</ex>, bean <ex>porridge</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porringer</ent><br/
<hw>Por"rin*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pottanger</ets>, for <ets>pottager</ets>; cf. F. <ets>potager</ets>
a soup basin. See <er>Porridge</er>.]</ety> <def>A porridge dish; esp.,
a bowl or cup from which children eat or are fed; <as>as, a silver
<ex>porringer</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>Oporto</ets>,
in Portugal, i. e., <ets><?/ porto</ets> the port, L. <ets>portus</ets>.
See <er>Port</er> harbor.]</ety> <def>A dark red or purple astringent
wine made in Portugal. It contains a large percentage of
alcohol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>port</ets>, L.
<ets>portus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>port</ets>. See <er>Farm</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>, <er>Ford</er>, and 1st, 3d, & 4h <er>Port</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A place where ships may ride secure from storms; a
sheltered inlet, bay, or cove; a harbor; a haven. Used also
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1116 --></p>

<p><q>Peering in maps for <qex>ports</qex> and piers and roads.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are in <qex>port</qex> if we have Thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In law and commercial usage, a harbor where vessels
are admitted to discharge and receive cargoes, from whence they depart
and where they finish their voyages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Free port</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Free</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Port bar</b></col>. <fld>(Naut,)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
boom</cd>. See <er>Boom</er>, 4, also <er>Bar</er>, 3. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A bar, as of sand, at the mouth of, or in, a port.</cd> --
<col><b>Port charges</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>charges, as
wharfage, etc., to which a ship or its cargo is subjected in a
harbor.</cd> -- <col><b>Port of entry</b></col>, <cd>a harbor where a
customhouse is established for the legal entry of merchandise.</cd> --
<col><b>Port toll</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a payment made for the
privilege of bringing goods into port.</cd> -- <col><b>Port
warden</b></col>, <cd>the officer in charge of a port; a harbor
master.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>porte</ets>, L.
<ets>porta</ets>, akin to <ets>portus</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>porte</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>porta</ets>. See <er>Port</er> a harbor, and cf.
<er>Porte</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A passageway; an opening or
entrance to an inclosed place; a gate; a door; a portal.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him I accuse<br/
The city <qex>ports</qex> by this hath entered.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Form their ivory <qex>port</qex> the cherubim<br/
Forth issuing.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An opening in the side of a
vessel; an embrasure through which cannon may be discharged; a porthole;
also, the shutters which close such an opening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her <qex>ports</qex> being within sixteen inches of the water.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A passageway in a machine,
through which a fluid, as steam, water, etc., may pass, as from a valve
to the interior of the cylinder of a steam engine; an opening in a valve
seat, or valve face.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Air port</b></col>, <col><b>Bridle
port</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Air</er>, <er>Bridle</er>,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Port bar</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a bar to
secure the ports of a ship in a gale.</cd> -- <col><b>Port lid</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a lid or hanging for closing the portholes of a
vessel.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Steam port</b></col>, <and/
<col><b>Exhaust port</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Steam Engine)</fld>, <cd>the
ports of the cylinder communicating with the valve or valves, for the
entrance or exit of the steam, respectively.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Ported</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Porting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>porter</ets>, L.
<ets>portare</ets> to carry. See <er>Port</er> demeanor.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To carry; to bear; to transport.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are easily <qex>ported</qex> by boat into other shires.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To throw, as a musket, diagonally
across the body, with the lock in front, the right hand grasping the
small of the stock, and the barrel sloping upward and crossing the point
of the left shoulder; <as>as, to <ex>port</ex> arms</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Began to hem him round with <qex>ported</qex> spears.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Port arms</b></col>, <cd>a position in the manual of
arms, executed as above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>port</ets>, fr.
<ets>porter</ets> to carry, L. <ets>portare</ets>, prob. akin to E.
<ets>fare</ets>, v. See <er>Port</er> harbor, and cf. <er>Comport</er>,
<er>Export</er>, <er>Sport</er>.]</ety> <def>The manner in which a
person bears himself; deportment; carriage; bearing; demeanor; hence,
manner or style of living; <as>as, a proud <ex>port</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And of his <qex>port</qex> as meek as is a maid.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The necessities of pomp, grandeur, and a suitable <qex>port</qex>
in the world.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymology uncertain.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The larboard or left side of a ship (looking
from the stern toward the bow); <as>as, a vessel heels to
<ex>port</ex></as>. See <er>Note</er> under <er>Larboard</er>. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port</ent><br/
<hw>Port</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To turn or put
to the left or larboard side of a ship; -- said of the helm, and used
chiefly in the imperative, as a command; <as>as, <ex>port</ex> your
helm</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Por"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Port<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a gate. See
<er>Port</er> a hole.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
part of the liver or other organ where its vessels and nerves enter; the
hilus.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The foramen of Monro.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portability</ent><br/
<hw>Port`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being portable; fitness to be carried.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portable</ent><br/
<hw>Port"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>portabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>portare</ets> to carry: cf. F.
<ets>portable</ets>. See <er>Port</er> demeanor.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being borne or carried; easily transported; conveyed
without difficulty; <as>as, a <ex>portable</ex> bed, desk,
engine</as>.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Possible to be endured; supportable.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How light and <qex>portable</qex> my pain seems now!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Portable forge</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Forge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Portable steam engine</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Steam engine</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portableness</ent><br/
<hw>Port"a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being portable; portability.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portace</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tace</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portass</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portage</ent><br/
<hw>Port"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From 2d
<er>Port</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A sailor's
wages when in port.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The amount of a sailor's
wages for a voyage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portage</ent><br/
<hw>Port"age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[3d <er>Port</er>.]</ety> <def>A
porthole.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portage</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from
<ets>porter</ets> to carry. See <er>Port</er> to carry.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of carrying or transporting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The price of carriage; porterage.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Fell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Capacity for carrying; tonnage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hakluyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A carry between navigable waters. See 3d
<er>Carry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portage</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To carry
(goods, boats, etc.) overland between navigable waters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portage group</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tage group`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from the township
of <ets>Portage</ets> in New York.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A
subdivision of the Chemung period in American geology. See
<xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portague</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ta*gue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Portuguese</er>.]</ety> <def>A Portuguese gold coin formerly
current, and variously estimated to be worth from three and one half to
four and one half pounds sterling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>portegue</asp> and
<asp>portigue</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ten thousand <qex>portagues</qex>, besides great pearls.</q>
<rj><qau>Marlowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portal</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>portal</ets>, F. <ets>portail</ets>, LL. <ets>portale</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>porta</ets> a gate. See <er>Port</er> a gate.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A door or gate; hence, a way of entrance or exit, especially one
that is grand and imposing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thick with sparkling orient gems<br/
The <qex>portal</qex> shone.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From out the fiery <qex>portal</qex> of the east.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The lesser gate,
where there are two of different dimensions.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Formerly, a small square corner in a room separated from the rest
of the apartment by wainscoting, forming a short passage to another
apartment.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>By analogy with the French
<xex>portail</xex>, used by recent writers for the whole architectural
composition which surrounds and includes the doorways and porches of a
church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bridge Building)</fld> <def>The space, at one end,
between opposite trusses when these are terminated by inclined
braces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A prayer book or breviary; a portass.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Portal bracing</b></col> <fld>(Bridge Building)</fld>,
<cd>a combination of struts and ties which lie in the plane of the
inclined braces at a portal, serving to transfer wind pressure from the
upper parts of the trusses to an abutment or pier of the
bridge.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portal</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to a porta, especially the porta of the liver; <as>as, the
<ex>portal</ex> vein, which enters the liver at the porta, and divides
into capillaries after the manner of an artery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>Portal</ex> is applied to other veins which break up
into capillaries; <as>as, the renal <ex>portal</ex> veins in the
frog</as>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portamento</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Por`ta*men"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr.
<ets>portare</ets> to carry.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>In singing,
or in the use of the bow, a gradual carrying or lifting of the voice or
sound very smoothly from one note to another; a gliding from tone to
tone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portance</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Port</er>,
carriage, demeanor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.
Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portass</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tass</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>porte-hors</ets> a kind of prayer book, so called from being
portable; cf. LL. <ets>portiforium</ets>.]</ety> <def>A breviary; a
prayer book.</def> <altsp>[Written variously <asp>portace</asp>,
<asp>portasse</asp>, <asp>portesse</asp>, <asp>portise</asp>,
<asp>porthose</asp>, <asp>portos</asp>, <asp>portus</asp>,
<asp>portuse</asp>, etc.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.
Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By God and by this <qex>porthors</qex> I you swear.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portate</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>portatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>portare</ets> to carry.]</ety>
<fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Borne not erect, but diagonally athwart an
escutcheon; <as>as, a cross <ex>portate</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portative</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ta*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>portatif</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Portable.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Capable of holding up or
carrying; <as>as, the <ex>portative</ex> force of a magnet, of
atmospheric pressure, or of capillarity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portcluse</ent><br/
<hw>Port"cluse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A portcullis.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portcrayon</ent><br/
<hw>Port`cray"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>porte-crayon</ets>; <ets>porter</ets> to carry + <ets>crayon</ets>
a crayon.]</ety> <def>A metallic handle with a clasp for holding a
crayon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portcullis</ent><br/
<hw>Port*cul"lis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>porte
coulisse</ets>, <ets>cole<ium/ce</ets>, a sliding door, fr. L.
<ets>colare</ets>, <ets>colatum</ets>, to filter, to strain: cf. F.
<ets>couler</ets> to glide. See <er>Port</er> a gate, and cf.
<er>Cullis</er>, <er>Colander</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A grating of iron or of timbers pointed with
iron, hung over the gateway of a fortress, to be let down to prevent the
entrance of an enemy.</def> <ldquo/Let the <xex>portcullis</xex>
fall.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She . . . the huge <qex>portcullis</qex> high updrew.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An English coin of the reign of Elizabeth, struck
for the use of the East India Company; -- so called from its bearing the
figure of a portcullis on the reverse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portcullis</ent><br/
<hw>Port*cul"lis</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Portcullised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Portcullising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To obstruct with, or as
with, a portcullis; to shut; to bar.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porte</ent><br/
<hw>Porte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>porte</ets> a
gate, L. <ets>porta</ets>. See <er>Port</er> a gate.]</ety> <def>The
Ottoman court; the government of the Turkish empire, officially called
the <altname>Sublime Porte</altname>, from the gate (<xex>port</xex>) of
the sultan's palace at which justice was administered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porte-cochere</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Porte"-co`ch<egrave/re"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. See <er>Port</er> a gate, and <er>Coach</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A large doorway allowing vehicles to drive into
or through a building. It is common to have the entrance door open upon
the passage of the <xex>porte-coch<egrave/re</xex>. Also, a porch over a
driveway before an entrance door.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ported</ent><br/
<hw>Port"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having gates.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We took the sevenfold-<qex>ported</qex> Thebes.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portegue</ent><br/
<hw>Por"te*gue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portague</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portemonnaie</ent><br/
<hw>Porte"mon*naie`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>porter</ets> to carry + <ets>monnaie</ets> money.]</ety> <def>A
small pocketbook or wallet for carrying money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portend</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Portended</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Portending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>portendre</ets>,
<ets>portentum</ets>, to foretell, to predict, to impend, from an old
preposition used in comp. + <ets>tendere</ets> to stretch. See
<er>Position</er>, <er>Tend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To indicate
(events, misfortunes, etc.) as in future; to foreshow; to foretoken; to
bode; -- now used esp. of unpropitious signs.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many signs <qex>portended</qex> a dark and stormy day.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stretch out before.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/Doomed to feel the great Idomeneus' <xex>portended</xex>
steel.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To foreshow; foretoken; betoken; forebode; augur;
presage; foreshadow; threaten.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portension</ent><br/
<hw>Por*ten"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
foreshowing; foreboding.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portent</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tent"</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>portentum</ets>. See <er>Portend</er>.]</ety> <def>That which
portends, or foretoken; esp., that which portends evil; a sign of coming
calamity; an omen; a sign.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My loss by dire <qex>portents</qex> the god foretold.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portentive</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tent"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Presaging;
foreshadowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portentous</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tent"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>portentosus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a
portent; containing portents; foreshadowing, esp. foreshadowing ill;
ominous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For, I believe, they are <qex>portentous</qex> things.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Victories of strange and almost <qex>portentous</qex>
splendor.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Monstrous; prodigious; wonderful; dreadful;
<as>as, a beast of <ex>portentous</ex> size</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Roscommon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Por*tent"ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Por*tent"ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porter</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>portier</ets>, L. <ets>portarius</ets>, from <ets>porta</ets> a
gate, door. See <er>Port</er> a gate.]</ety> <def>A man who has charge
of a door or gate; a doorkeeper; one who waits at the door to receive
messages.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To him the <qex>porter</qex> openeth.</q> <rj><qau>John x.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porter</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>porteur</ets>, fr.
<ets>porter</ets> to carry, L. <ets>portare</ets>. See <er>Port</er> to
carry.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A carrier; one who carries or conveys
burdens, luggage, etc.; for hire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Forging)</fld> <def>A bar of iron or steel at the
end of which a forging is made; esp., a long, large bar, to the end of
which a heavy forging is attached, and by means of which the forging is
lifted and handled in hammering and heating; -- called also
<altname>porter bar</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A malt liquor, of a dark color and moderately bitter
taste, possessing tonic and intoxicating qualities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Porter is said to be so called as having been first used
chiefly by the London <xex>porters</xex>, and this application of the
word is supposed to be not older than 1750.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porterage</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ter*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
work of a porter; the occupation of a carrier or of a
doorkeeper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Money charged or paid for the carriage of burdens or
parcels by a porter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porteress</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ter*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portress</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porterhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ter*house</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house where porter is
sold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Porterhouse steak</b></col>, <cd>a steak cut from a
sirloin of beet, including the upper and under part.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portesse</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tesse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Porteass</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tyndale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portfire</ent><br/
<hw>Port"fire`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A case of strong
paper filled with a composition of niter, sulphur, and mealed powder, --
used principally to ignite the priming in proving guns, and as an
incendiary material in shells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portfolio</ent><br/
<hw>Port*fol"io</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>portefeuille</ets>; <ets>porter</ets> to carry + <ets>feuille</ets>
a leaf. See <er>Port</er> to carry, and <er>Folio</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A portable case for holding loose papers, prints,
drawings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: The office and functions of a minister of
state or member of the cabinet; <as>as, to receive the
<ex>portfolio</ex> of war; to resign the
<ex>portfolio</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portglave</ent><br/
<hw>Port"glave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>porte-glaive</ets>; <ets>porter</ets> to carry + <ets>glaive</ets>
a sword.]</ety> <def>A sword bearer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portgrave</ent><br/
<ent>Portgreve</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Port"greve`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Port"grave`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <ety>[AS. <ets>portger<emac/fa</ets>;
<ets>port</ets> a harbor + <ets>ger<emac/fa</ets> a reeve or sheriff.
See <er>Reeve</er> a steward, and cf. <er>Portreeve</er>.]</ety> <def>In
old English law, the chief magistrate of a port or maritime town.; a
portreeve.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fabyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porthole</ent><br/
<hw>Port"hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>An embrasure in a ship's side. See 3d <er>Port</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porthook</ent><br/
<hw>Port"hook`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>One of the iron hooks to which the port hinges are attached.</def>
<rj><au>J. Knowles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porthors</ent><br/
<hw>Port"hors`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portass</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portico</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ti*co</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Porticoes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr> <xex>or</xex>
<plw>Porticos</plw>.</plu> <ety>[It., L. <ets>porticus</ets>. See
<er>Porch</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A colonnade or covered
ambulatory, especially in classical styles of architecture; usually, a
colonnade at the entrance of a building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porticoed</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ti*coed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with a
portico.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portiere</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Por`ti<egrave/re""</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. <ets>porte</ets> gate, door. See <er>Port</er> a gate.]</ety> <def>A
curtain hanging across a doorway.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portigue</ent><br/
<hw>Por"ti*gue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portague</er>.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portingal</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tin*gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Portugal; Portuguese.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A Portuguese.</def></def2>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portion</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>portio</ets>, akin to <ets>pars</ets>, <ets>partis</ets>, a part.
See <er>Part</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is
divided off or separated, as a part from a whole; a separated part of
anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part considered by itself, though not actually cut
off or separated from the whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These are parts of his ways; but how little a <qex>portion</qex>
is heard of him!</q> <rj><qau>Job xxvi. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Portions</qex> and parcels of the dreadful past.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A part assigned; allotment; share; fate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lord of that servant . . . will appoint him his
<qex>portion</qex> with the unbelievers.</q> <rj><qau>Luke xii.
46.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man's <qex>portion</qex> is to die and rise again.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The part of an estate given to a child or heir, or
descending to him by law, and distributed to him in the settlement of
the estate; an inheritance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give me the <qex>portion</qex> of goods that falleth to me.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke xv. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A wife's fortune; a dowry.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Division; share; parcel; quantity; allotment;
dividend.</syn> -- <usage><er>Portion</er>, <er>Part</er>.
<xex>Part</xex> is generic, having a simple reference to some
<xex>whole</xex>. <xex>Portion</xex> has the additional idea of such a
division as bears reference to an individual, or is allotted to some
object; <as>as, a <ex>portion</ex> of one's time; a <ex>portion</ex> of
Scripture</as>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portion</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Portioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Portioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate or
divide into portions or shares; to parcel; to distribute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>portion</qex> to his tribes the wide domain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To endow with a portion or inheritance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him <qex>portioned</qex> maids, apprenticed orphans, blest.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1117 --></p>

<p><ent>Portioner</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who portions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>See <er>Portionist</er>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portionist</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tion*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
scholar at Merton College, Oxford, who has a certain academical
allowance or portion; -- corrupted into <xex>postmaster</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>One of the incumbents of a
benefice which has two or more rectors or vicars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portionless</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tion*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no portion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portise</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portass</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portland cement</ent><br/
<hw>Port"land ce*ment"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A cement having the color
of the Portland stone of England, made by calcining an artificial
mixture of carbonate of lime and clay, or sometimes certain natural
limestones or chalky clays. It contains a large proportion of clay, and
hardens under water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portland stone</ent><br/
<hw>Port"land stone"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A yellowish-white
calcareous freestone from the Isle of Portland in England, much used in
building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portland vase</ent><br/
<hw>Port"land vase`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A celebrated cinerary urn or
vase found in the tomb of the Emperor Alexander Severus. It is owned by
the Duke of Portland, and kept in the British Museum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portlast</ent><br/
<hw>Port"last</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The portoise. See <er>Portoise</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portliness</ent><br/
<hw>Port"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being portly; dignity of mien or of personal
appearance; stateliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such pride is praise; such <qex>portliness</qex> is honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bulkiness; corpulence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portly</ent><br/
<hw>Port"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Port</er>
demeanor.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a dignified port or mien; of a
noble appearance; imposing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bulky; corpulent.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>portly</xex>
personage.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portman</ent><br/
<hw>Port"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Portmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>An inhabitant or burgess
of a port, esp. of one of the Cinque Ports.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portmanteau</ent><br/
<hw>Port*man"teau</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Portmanteaus</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>porte-manteau</ets>; <ets>porter</ets> to carry +
<ets>manteau</ets> a cloak, mantle. See <er>Port</er> to carry, and
<er>Mantle</er>.]</ety> <def>A bag or case, usually of leather, for
carrying wearing apparel, etc., on journeys.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>portmanteau word</ent><br/
<hw>port*man"teau word`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>portmanteau</er>.]</ety> <def>A word formed by joining two others;
-- as, <it>smog</it> is formed from <er>smoke</er> and
<er>fog</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Wordnet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portmantle</ent><br/
<hw>Port*man"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
portmanteau.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portmote</ent><br/
<hw>Port"mote`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>In old English law,
a court, or mote, held in a port town.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portoir</ent><br/
<hw>Por"toir</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF., fr.
<ets>porter</ets> to bear.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, bears;
hence, one who, or that which, produces.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Branches . . . which were <qex>portoirs</qex>, and bare
grapes.</q> <rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portoise</ent><br/
<hw>Por"toise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps fr. OF.
<ets>porteis</ets> portative, portable.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The gunwale of a ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To lower the yards a-portoise</b></col>, <cd>to lower
them to the gunwale.</cd> -- <col><b>To ride a portoise</b></col>,
<cd>to ride an anchor with the lower yards and topmasts struck or
lowered, as in a gale of wind.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portos</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tos</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portass</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portpane</ent><br/
<hw>Port"pane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>portare</ets> to carry + <ets>panis</ets> bread; prob. through
French.]</ety> <def>A cloth for carrying bread, so as not to touch it
with the hands.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portrait</ent><br/
<hw>Por"trait</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., originally p.
p. of <ets>portraire</ets> to portray. See <er>Portray</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The likeness of a person, painted, drawn, or engraved;
commonly, a representation of the human face painted from real
life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>portraits</qex>, the grace, and, we may add, the likeness,
consists more in the general air than in the exact similitude of every
feature.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Reynolds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The meaning of the word is sometimes extended so as to
include a photographic likeness.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, any graphic or vivid delineation or
description of a person; <as>as, a <ex>portrait</ex> in
words</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Portrait bust</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Portrait statue</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a bust or statue
representing the actual features or person of an individual; -- in
distinction from an <xex>ideal bust</xex> or
<xex>statue</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portrait</ent><br/
<hw>Por"trait</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To portray; to draw.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portraitist</ent><br/
<hw>Por"trait*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A portrait painter.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Hamerton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portraiture</ent><br/
<hw>Por"trai*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>portraiture</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A portrait; a likeness;
a painted resemblance; hence, that which is copied from some example or
model.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For, by the image of my cause, I see<br/
The <qex>portraiture</qex> of his.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Divinity maketh the love of ourselves the pattern; the love of our
neighbors but the <qex>portraiture</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pictures, collectively; painting.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The art or practice of making portraits.</def>
<rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portraiture</ent><br/
<hw>Por"trai*ture</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To represent by a
portrait, or as by a portrait; to portray.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shaftesbury.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portray</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tray"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pourtray</asp>.]</altsp> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>portrayed</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Portraying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pourtraien</ets>,
OF. <ets>portraire</ets>, <ets>pourtraire</ets>, F.
<ets>portraire</ets>, fr. L. <ets>protrahere</ets>,
<ets>protractum</ets>, to draw or drag forth; <ets>pro</ets> forward,
forth + <ets>trahere</ets> to draw. See <er>Trace</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Protract</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To paint or
draw the likeness of; <as>as, to <ex>portray</ex> a king on
horseback</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Take a tile, and lay it before thee, and <qex>portray</qex> upon
it the city, even Jerusalem.</q> <rj><qau>Ezek. iv. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively, to describe in words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To adorn with pictures.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spear and helmets thronged, and shields<br/
Various with boastful arguments <qex>potrayed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portrayal</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tray"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process
of portraying; description; delineation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portrayer</ent><br/
<hw>Por*tray"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
portrays.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portreeve</ent><br/
<hw>Port"reeve`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A port
warden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portress</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
porter.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Port-royalist</ent><br/
<hw>Port-roy"al*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>One of the dwellers in the Cistercian convent of Port
Royal des Champs, near Paris, when it was the home of the Jansenists in
the 17th century, among them being Arnauld, Pascal, and other famous
scholars. Cf. <er>Jansenist</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portsale</ent><br/
<hw>Port"sale`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Port</ets>
gate + <ets>sale</ets>.]</ety> <def>Public or open sale; auction.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portuary</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tu*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Portass</er>.]</ety> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A breviary.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portuguese</ent><br/
<hw>Por"tu*guese</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>portugais</ets>, Sp. <ets>portugues</ets>, Pg.
<ets>portuguez</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Portugal, or its
inhabitants.</def> -- <def2><pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Portugal; people of Portugal.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Portuguese man-of-war</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Physalia</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portulaca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Por`tu*la"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
purslane.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of polypetalous plants;
also, any plant of the genus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <spn>Portulaca oleracea</spn> is the common purslane.
<spn>Portulaca grandiflora</spn> is a South American herb, widely
cultivated for its showy crimson, scarlet, yellow, or white, ephemeral
blossoms.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Portulacaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Por`tu*la*ca"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a natural order of plants
(<ord>Portulacace<ae/</ord>), of which Portulaca is the type, and which
includes also the spring beauty (<gen>Claytonia</gen>) and other
genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Porwigle</ent><br/
<hw>Por"wi`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Polliwig</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pory</ent><br/
<hw>Por"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Porous; <as>as,
<ex>pory</ex> stone</as>. <mark>[R.]</mark> Dryden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pose</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po`s<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., placed,
posed.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Standing still, with all the feet
on the ground; -- said of the attitude of a lion, horse, or other
beast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pose</ent><br/
<hw>Pose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>gepose</ets>;
of uncertain origin; cf. W. <ets>pas</ets> a cough, Skr.
<ets>k<amac/s</ets> to cough, and E. <ets>wheeze</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
cold in the head; catarrh.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pose</ent><br/
<hw>Pose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pose</ets>, fr.
<ets>poser</ets>. See <er>Pose</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The
attitude or position of a person; the position of the body or of any
member of the body; especially, a position formally assumed for the sake
of effect; an artificial position; <as>as, the <ex>pose</ex> of an
actor; the <ex>pose</ex> of an artist's model or of a
statue.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pose</ent><br/
<hw>Pose</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Posed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Posing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>poser</ets> to place, to
put, L. <ets>pausare</ets> to pause, in LL. also, to place, put, fr. L.
<ets>pausa</ets> a pause, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to make to cease, prob. akin
to E. <ets>few</ets>. In compounds, this word appears corresponding to
L. <ets>ponere</ets> to put, place, the substitution in French having
been probably due to confusion of this word with L. <ets>positio</ets>
position, fr. <ets>ponere</ets>. See <er>Few</er>, and cf.
<er>Appose</er>, <er>Dispose</er>, <er>Oppose</er>, <er>Pause</er>,
<er>Repose</er>, <er>Position</er>.]</ety> <def>To place in an attitude
or fixed position, for the sake of effect; to arrange the posture and
drapery of (a person) in a studied manner; <as>as, to <ex>pose</ex> a
model for a picture; to <ex>pose</ex> a sitter for a
portrait.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pose</ent><br/
<hw>Pose</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To assume and maintain a studied
attitude, with studied arrangement of drapery; to strike an attitude; to
attitudinize; figuratively, to assume or affect a certain character;
<as>as, she <ex>poses</ex> as a prude</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . <qex>posed</qex> before her as a hero.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pose</ent><br/
<hw>Pose</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Shortened from <ets>appose</ets>,
for <ets>oppose</ets>. See 2d <er>Appose</er>, <er>Oppose</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To interrogate; to question.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/She . . . <xex>posed</xex> him and sifted him.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To question with a view to puzzling; to embarrass by
questioning or scrutiny; to bring to a stand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A question wherewith a learned Pharisee thought to <qex>pose</qex>
and puzzle him.</q> <rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posed</ent><br/
<hw>Posed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Firm; determined;
fixed.</def> <ldquo/A most <xex>posed</xex> . . . and grave
behavior.<rdquo/ <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Urquhart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poser</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which,
puzzles; a difficult or inexplicable question or fact.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poseuse</ent><br/
<ent>Poseur</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Po`seur"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. masc.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Poseurs</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></plu>,
\'d8<hw>Po`seuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. fem.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Poseuses</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></plu> }</mhw>,
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A person who poses or attitudizes, esp.
mentally.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posied</ent><br/
<hw>Po"sied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inscribed with a
posy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>poised</qex> lockets bribe the fair.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>So as to pose or
puzzle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posit</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Posited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Positing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>ponere</ets>,
<ets>positum</ets>, to place. See <er>Position</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To dispose or set firmly or fixedly; to place or dispose in
relation to other objects.</def> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>To assume as real or conceded;
<as>as, to <ex>posit</ex> a principle</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Position</ent><br/
<hw>Po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>position</ets>, L. <ets>positio</ets>, fr. <ets>ponere</ets>,
<ets>positum</ets>, to put, place; prob. for <ets>posino</ets>, fr. an
old preposition used only in comp. (akin to Gr. <?/) + <ets>sinere</ets>
to leave, let, permit, place. See <er>Site</er>, and cf.
<er>Composite</er>, <er>Compound</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, <er>Depone</er>,
<er>Deposit</er>, <er>Expound</er>, <er>Impostor</er>,
<er>Opposite</er>, <er>Propound</er>, <er>Pose</er>, <pos>v.</pos>,
<er>Posit</er>, <er>Post</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being posited, or placed; the manner in
which anything is placed; attitude; condition; <as>as, a firm, an
inclined, or an upright <ex>position</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have different prospects of the same thing, according to our
different <qex>positions</qex> to it.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The spot where a person or thing is placed or takes
a place; site; place; station; situation; <as>as, the <ex>position</ex>
of man in creation; the fleet changed its
<ex>position</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence: The ground which any one takes in an argument
or controversy; the point of view from which any one proceeds to a
discussion; also, a principle laid down as the basis of reasoning; a
proposition; a thesis; <as>as, to define one's <ex>position</ex>; to
appear in a false <ex>position</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not the proof of any <qex>position</qex> depend on the
<qex>positions</qex> that follow, but always on those which go
before.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Relative place or standing; social or official rank;
<as>as, a person of <ex>position</ex></as>; hence, office; post; <as>as,
to lose one's <ex>position</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>A method of solving a problem by
one or two suppositions; -- called also the <altname>rule of trial and
error</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Angle of position</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the
angle which any line (as that joining two stars) makes with another
fixed line, specifically with a circle of declination.</cd> --
<col><b>Double position</b></col> <fld>(Arith.)</fld>, <cd>the method of
solving problems by proceeding with each of two assumed numbers,
according to the conditions of the problem, and by comparing the
difference of the results with those of the numbers, deducing the
correction to be applied to one of them to obtain the true result.</cd>
-- <col><b>Guns of position</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>heavy
fieldpieces, not designed for quick movements.</cd> -- <col><b>Position
finder</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a range finder. See under
<er>Range</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Position micrometer</b></col>, <cd>a
micrometer applied to the tube of an astronomical telescope for
measuring angles of position in the field of view.</cd> --
<col><b>Single position</b></col> <fld>(Arith.)</fld>, <cd>the method of
solving problems, in which the result obtained by operating with an
assumed number is to the true result as the number assumed is to the
number required.</cd> -- <col><b>Strategic position</b></col>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a position taken up by an army or a large
detachment of troops for the purpose of checking or observing an
opposing force.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Situation; station; place; condition; attitude;
posture; proposition; assertion; thesis.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Position</ent><br/
<hw>Po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To indicate the
position of; to place.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positional</ent><br/
<hw>Po*si"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ascribing unto plants <qex>positional</qex> operations.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positive</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>positif</ets>, F. <ets>positif</ets>, L. <ets>positivus</ets>. See
<er>Position</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a real position,
existence, or energy; existing in fact; real; actual; -- opposed to
<xex>negative</xex>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Positive</xex> good.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Derived from an object by itself; not dependent on
changing circumstances or relations; absolute; -- opposed to
<xex>relative</xex>; <as>as, the idea of beauty is not
<ex>positive</ex>, but depends on the different tastes
individuals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Definitely laid down; explicitly stated; clearly
expressed; -- opposed to <xex>implied</xex>; <as>as, a <ex>positive</ex>
declaration or promise</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Positive</qex> words, that he would not bear arms against
King Edward's son.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence: Not admitting of any doubt, condition,
qualification, or discretion; not dependent on circumstances or
probabilities; not speculative; compelling assent or obedience;
peremptory; indisputable; decisive; <as>as, <ex>positive</ex>
instructions; <ex>positive</ex> truth; <ex>positive</ex>
proof.</as></def> <ldquo/'T is <xex>positive</xex> 'gainst all
exceptions.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Prescribed by express enactment or institution;
settled by arbitrary appointment; said of laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In laws, that which is natural bindeth universally; that which is
<qex>positive</qex>, not so.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Fully assured; confident; certain; sometimes,
overconfident; dogmatic; overbearing; -- said of persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some <qex>positive</qex>, persisting fops we know,<br/
That, if once wrong, will needs be always.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Having the power of direct action or influence;
<as>as, a <ex>positive</ex> voice in legislation</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>Corresponding with the original
in respect to the position of lights and shades, instead of having the
lights and shades reversed; <as>as, a <ex>positive</ex>
picture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Electro-positive.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Hence, basic; metallic;
not acid; -- opposed to <ant>negative</ant>, and said of metals, bases,
and basic radicals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Mach. & Mech.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Designating, or pertaining to, a motion or device in which the
movement derived from a driver, or the grip or hold of a restraining
piece, is communicated through an unyielding intermediate piece or
pieces; <as>as, a claw clutch is a <ex>positive</ex> clutch, while a
friction clutch is not</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Designating, or
pertaining to, a device giving a to-and-fro motion; <as>as, a
<ex>positive</ex> dobby</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Vehicles)</fld> <def>Designating a method of
steering or turning in which the steering wheels move so that they
describe concentric arcs in making a turn, to insure freedom from side
slip or harmful resistance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Positive crystals</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>a
doubly refracting crystal in which the index of refraction for the
extraordinary ray is greater than for the ordinary ray, and the former
is refracted nearer to the axis than the latter, as quartz and ice; --
opposed to <xex>negative crystal</xex>, or one in which this
characteristic is reversed, as Iceland spar, tourmaline, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Positive degree</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>that state of
an adjective or adverb which denotes simple quality, without comparison
or relation to increase or diminution; as, <xex>wise</xex>,
<xex>noble</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Positive electricity</b></col>
<fld>(Elec)</fld>, <cd>the kind of electricity which is developed when
glass is rubbed with silk, or which appears at that pole of a voltaic
battery attached to the plate that is not attacked by the exciting
liquid; -- formerly called <altname>vitreous electricity</altname>; --
opposed to <ant>negative electricity</ant>.</cd> -- <col><b>Positive
eyepiece</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Eyepiece</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Positive law</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Municipal law</cref>,
under <er>Law</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Positive motion</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>motion which is derived from a driver through
unyielding intermediate pieces, or by direct contact, and not through
elastic connections, nor by means of friction, gravity, etc.; definite
motion.</cd> -- <col><b>Positive philosophy</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Positivism</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Positive pole</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <cd>The pole of a battery or pile which
yields positive or vitreous electricity; -- opposed to <ant>negative
pole</ant>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Magnetism)</fld> <cd>The north
pole</cd>. <mark>[R.]</mark> -- <col><b>Positive quantity</b></col>
<fld>(Alg.)</fld>, <cd>an affirmative quantity, or one affected by the
sign <xex>plus</xex> [+].</cd> -- <col><b>Positive rotation</b></col>
<fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>left-handed rotation.</cd> -- <col><b>Positive
sign</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>the sign [+] denoting
<xex>plus</xex>, or <xex>more</xex>, or addition.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positive</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is
capable of being affirmed; reality.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which settles by absolute
appointment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The positive degree or
form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>A picture in which the lights
and shades correspond in position with those of the original, instead of
being reversed, as in a <xex>negative</xex>.</def> <rj><au>R.
Hunt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The positive plate of a voltaic
or electrolytic cell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positively</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a positive manner;
absolutely; really; expressly; with certainty; indubitably;
peremptorily; dogmatically; -- opposed to
<xex>negatively</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good and evil which is removed may be esteemed good or evil
comparatively, and <qex>positively</qex> simply.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give me some breath, some little pause, my lord,<br/
Before I <qex>positively</qex> speak herein.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would ask . . . whether . . . the divine law does not
<qex>positively</qex> require humility and meekness.</q>
<rj><qau>Sprat.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Positively charged</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Positively electrified</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>,
<cd>having a charge of positive electricity; -- opposed to
<ant>negatively charged</ant>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being positive; reality; actualness; certainty; confidence;
peremptoriness; dogmatism. See <er>Positive</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Positiveness</qex>, pedantry, and ill manners.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>positiveness</qex> of sins of commission lies both in the
habitude of the will and in the executed act too; the
<qex>positiveness</qex> of sins of omission is in the habitude of the
will only.</q> <rj><qau>Norris.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positivism</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*tiv*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A system of
philosophy originated by M. Auguste Comte, which deals only with
<xex>positives</xex>. It excludes from philosophy everything but the
natural phenomena or properties of knowable things, together with their
invariable relations of coexistence and succession, as occurring in time
and space. Such relations are denominated <xex>laws</xex>, which are to
be discovered by observation, experiment, and comparison. This
philosophy holds all inquiry into <xex>causes</xex>, both efficient and
final, to be useless and unprofitable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positivist</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*tiv*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A believer in
positivism.</def> -- <def2><pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to
positivism.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positivity</ent><br/
<hw>Pos`i*tiv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Positiveness.</def> <rj><au>J. Morley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Positure</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Posture</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posnet</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>po<cced/onet</ets>, dim. of <ets>po<cced/on</ets> a pot, a
vessel.]</ety> <def>A little basin; a porringer; a skillet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posological</ent><br/
<ent>Posologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pos`o*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pos`o*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>posologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to posology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posology</ent><br/
<hw>Po*sol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ how much
+ <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>posologie</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The science or doctrine of doses;
dosology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1118 --></p>

<p><ent>Pospolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"po*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pol.
<ets>pospolite ruszenie</ets> a general summons to arms, an arriere-ban;
<ets>pospolity</ets> general + <ets>ruszenie</ets> a stirring.]</ety>
<def>A kind of militia in Poland, consisting of the gentry, which, in
case of invasion, was summoned to the defense of the country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poss</ent><br/
<hw>Poss</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Push</er>.]</ety> <def>To push; to dash; to throw.</def> <mark>[Obs.
or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A cat . . . <qex>possed</qex> them [the rats] about.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posse</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"se</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Posse
comitatus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In posse</b></col>. <cd>See <er>In posse</er> in the
Vocabulary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posse comitatus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pos"se com`i*ta"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>posse</ets>
to be able, to have power + LL. <ets>comitatus</ets> a county, from
<ets>comes</ets>, <ets>comitis</ets>, a count. See <er>County</er>, and
<er>Power</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The power of the county, or the
citizens who may be summoned by the sheriff to assist the authorities in
suppressing a riot, or executing any legal precept which is forcibly
opposed.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A collection of people; a throng; a rabble.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The word <xex>comitatus</xex> is often omitted, and
<xex>posse</xex> alone used. <ldquo/A whole <xex>posse</xex> of
enthusiasts.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As if the passion that rules were the sheriff of the place, and
came off with all the <qex>posse</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possess</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*sess"</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Possessed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Possessing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>possessus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>possidere</ets> to have, possess,
from an inseparable prep. (cf. <er>Position</er>) + <ets>sedere</ets> to
sit. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To occupy in person; to
hold or actually have in one's own keeping; to have and to
hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Houses and fields and vineyards shall be <qex>possessed</qex>
again in this land.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. xxxii. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet beauty, though injurious, hath strange power,<br/
After offense returning, to regain<br/
Love once <qex>possessed</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have the legal title to; to have a just right to;
to be master of; to own; to have; <as>as, to <ex>possess</ex> property,
an estate, a book</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am yours, and all that I <qex>possess</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To obtain occupation or possession of; to
accomplish; to gain; to seize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How . . . to <qex>possess</qex> the purpose they desired.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To enter into and influence; to control the will of;
to fill; to affect; -- said especially of evil spirits, passions,
etc.</def> <ldquo/Weakness <xex>possesseth</xex> me.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those which were <qex>possessed</qex> with devils.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. iv. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For ten inspired, ten thousand are <qex>possessed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To put in possession; to make the owner or holder of
property, power, knowledge, etc.; to acquaint; to inform; -- followed by
<xex>of</xex> or <xex>with</xex> before the thing possessed, and now
commonly used reflexively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have <qex>possessed</qex> your grace of what I purpose.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Record a gift . . . of all he dies <qex>possessed</qex><br/
Unto his son.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>possessed</qex> our selves of the kingdom of Naples.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>possess</qex> our minds with an habitual good
intention.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To have; hold; occupy; control; own.</syn>
<usage> -- <er>Possess</er>, <er>Have</er>. <xex>Have</xex> is the more
general word. To <xex>possess</xex> denotes to <xex>have as a
property</xex>. It usually implies more permanence or definiteness of
control or ownership than is involved in <xex>having</xex>. A man does
not <xex>possess</xex> his wife and children: they are (so to speak)
part of himself. For the same reason, we <xex>have</xex> the faculties
of reason, understanding, will, sound judgment, etc.: they are exercises
of the mind, not <xex>possessions</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possession</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*ses"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>possession</ets>, L. <ets>possessio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or state of possessing, or holding as one's own.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The having, holding, or detention
of property in one's power or command; actual seizin or occupancy;
ownership, whether rightful or wrongful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Possession may be either actual or constructive; actual,
when a party has the immediate occupancy; constructive, when he has only
the right to such occupancy.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The thing possessed; that which any one occupies,
owns, or controls; in the plural, property in the aggregate; wealth;
dominion; <as>as, foreign <ex>possessions</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the young man heard that saying, he went away sorrowful, for
he had great <qex>possessions</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. xix.
22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ananias, with Sapphira his wife, sold a <qex>possession</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts v. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The house of Jacob shall possess their <qex>possessions</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ob. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The state of being possessed or controlled, as by an
evil spirit, or violent passions; madness; frenzy; <as>as, demoniacal
<ex>possession</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How long hath this <qex>possession</qex> held the man?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To give possession</b></col>, <cd>to put in another's
power or occupancy.</cd> -- <col><b>To put in possession</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To invest with ownership or occupancy; to provide or
furnish with; as, <xex>to put</xex> one <xex>in possession</xex> of
facts or information</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>To place
one in charge of property recovered in ejectment or writ of entry.</cd>
-- <col><b>To take possession</b></col>, <cd>to enter upon, or to bring
within one's power or occupancy.</cd> -- <col><b>Writ of
possession</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a precept directing a sheriff
to put a person in peaceable possession of property recovered in
ejectment or writ of entry.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possession</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*ses"sion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To invest with
property.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*ses"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to possession; arising from possession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*ses"sion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
possessor; a property holder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Possessioners</xex> of riches.<rdquo/ <rj><au>E.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Having been of old freemen and <qex>possessioners</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An invidious name for a member of any religious
community endowed with property in lands, buildings, etc., as contrasted
with mendicant friars.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessival</ent><br/
<hw>Pos`ses*si"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the possessive case; <as>as, a <ex>possessival</ex>
termination</as>.</def> <rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessive</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*sess"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>possessivus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>possessif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to possession; having or indicating possession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Possessive case</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Gram.)</fld>,
<cd>the genitive case; the case of nouns and pronouns which expresses
ownership, origin, or some possessive relation of one thing to another;
as, <xex>Homer's</xex> admirers; the <xex>pear's</xex> flavor; the
<xex>dog's</xex> faithfulness.</cd> -- <col><b>Possessive
pronoun</b></col>, <cd>a pronoun denoting ownership; as, <xex>his</xex>
name; <xex>her</xex> home; <xex>my</xex> book.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessive</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*sess"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The possessive case.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A possessive pronoun, or a word
in the possessive case.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessively</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*sess"ive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a possessive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessor</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*sess"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.: cf. F.
<ets>possesseur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who possesses; one who occupies,
holds, owns, or controls; one who has actual participation or enjoyment,
generally of that which is desirable; a proprietor.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Possessors</xex> of eternal glory.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Law.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As if he had been <qex>possessor</qex> of the whole world.</q>
<rj><qau>Sharp.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Owner; proprietor; master; holder;
occupant.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possessory</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*sess"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>possessorius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>possessoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to possession, either as a fact or a right; of the nature
of possession; <as>as, a <ex>possessory</ex> interest; a
<ex>possessory</ex> lord.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Possessory action</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Possessory suit</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an action
to regain or obtain possession of something. See under
<er>Petitory</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posset</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"set</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[W. <ets>posel</ets>
curdled milk, posset.]</ety> <def>A beverage composed of hot milk
curdled by some strong infusion, as by wine, etc., -- much in favor
formerly.</def> <ldquo/I have drugged their <xex>posset</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posset</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"set</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Posseted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Posseting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To curdle; to turn,
as milk; to coagulate; <as>as, to <ex>posset</ex> the blood</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To treat with possets; to pamper.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/She was cosseted and
<xex>posseted</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>O. W. Holmes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possibility</ent><br/
<hw>Pos`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Possibilities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>possibilit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>possibilitas</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being possible; the power of
happening, being, or existing.</def> <ldquo/All <xex>possibility</xex>
of error.<rdquo/ <au>Hooker.</au> <ldquo/Latent <xex>possibilities</xex>
of excellence.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is possible; a contingency; a thing or
event that may not happen; a contingent interest, as in real or personal
estate.</def> <rj><au>South. Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possible</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>possibilis</ets>, fr. <ets>posse</ets> to be able, to have power;
<ets>potis</ets> able, capable + <ets>esse</ets> to be. See
<er>Potent</er>, <er>Am</er>, and cf. <er>Host</er> a landlord.]</ety>
<def>Capable of existing or occurring, or of being conceived or thought
of; able to happen; capable of being done; not contrary to the nature of
things; -- sometimes used to express extreme improbability; barely able
to be, or to come to pass; <as>as, possibly he is honest, as it is
<ex>possible</ex> that Judas meant no wrong</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With God all things are <qex>possible</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Matt.
xix. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Practicable; likely. See
<er>Practicable</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possibly</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"si*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a possible manner; by
possible means; especially, by extreme, remote, or improbable
intervention, change, or exercise of power; by a chance; perhaps;
<as>as, <ex>possibly</ex> he may recover</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can we . . . <qex>possibly</qex> his love desert?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When <qex>possibly</qex> I can, I will return.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Possum</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"sum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Shortened from
<ets>opossum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An opossum.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To play possum</b></col>, <col><b>To act
possum</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to feign ignorance, indifference or
inattention, with the intent to deceive; to dissemble; -- in allusion to
the habit of the opossum, which feigns death when attacked or
alarmed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-</ent><br/
<hw>Post-</hw> <pr>(p<omac/st)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>post</ets> behind,
after; cf. Skr. <ets>pa<cced/c<amac/</ets>behind, afterwards.]</ety>
<def>A prefix signifying <xex>behind</xex>, <xex>back</xex>,
<xex>after</xex>; <as>as, <ex>post</ex>commissure, <ex>post</ex>dot,
<ex>post</ex>script</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post</ent><br/
<hw>Post</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>aposter</ets> to place in a
post or position, generally for a bad purpose.]</ety> <def>Hired to do
what is wrong; suborned.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir E.
Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post</ent><br/
<hw>Post</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS., fr. L. <ets>postis</ets>, akin
to <ets>ponere</ets>, <ets>positum</ets>, to place. See
<er>Position</er>, and cf. 4th <er>Post</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
piece of timber, metal, or other solid substance, fixed, or to be fixed,
firmly in an upright position, especially when intended as a stay or
support to something else; a pillar; <as>as, a hitching <ex>post</ex>; a
fence <ex>post</ex>; the <ex>posts</ex> of a house.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They shall take of the blood, and strike it on the two side
<qex>posts</qex> and on the upper doorpost of the houses.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then by main force pulled up, and on his shoulders bore,<br/
The gates of Azza, <qex>post</qex> and massy bar.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Unto his order he was a noble <qex>post</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Post</xex>, in the sense of an upright timber or
strut, is used in composition, in such words as king-<xex>post</xex>,
queen-<xex>post</xex>, crown-<xex>post</xex>, gate<xex>post</xex>,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The doorpost of a victualer's shop or inn, on which
were chalked the scores of customers; hence, a score; a debt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When God sends coin<br/
I will discharge your <qex>post</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>S.
Rowlands.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>From pillar to post</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Pillar</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Knight of the post</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Knight</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Post hanger</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a bearing for a revolving shaft, adapted to be
fastened to a post.</cd> -- <col><b>Post hole</b></col>, <cd>a hole in
the ground to set the foot of a post in.</cd> -- <col><b>Post
mill</b></col>, <cd>a form of windmill so constructed that the whole
fabric rests on a vertical axis firmly fastened to the ground, and
capable of being turned as the direction of the wind varies.</cd> --
<col><b>Post and stall</b></col> <fld>(Coal Mining)</fld>, <cd>a mode of
working in which pillars of coal are left to support the roof of the
mine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post</ent><br/
<hw>Post</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poste</ets>, LL.
<ets>posta</ets> station, post (where horses were kept), properly, a
fixed or set place, fem. fr. L. <ets>positus</ets> placed, p. p. of
<ets>ponere</ets>. See <er>Position</er>, and cf. <er>Post</er> a
pillar.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The place at which anything is stopped,
placed, or fixed; a station.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
station, or one of a series of stations, established for the refreshment
and accommodation of travelers on some recognized route; <as>as, a stage
or railway <ex>post</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A military
station; the place at which a soldier or a body of troops is stationed;
also, the troops at such a station.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The piece of
ground to which a sentinel's walk is limited.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A messenger who goes from station; an express;
especially, one who is employed by the government to carry letters and
parcels regularly from one place to another; a letter carrier; a
postman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In certain places there be always fresh <qex>posts</qex>, to carry
that further which is brought unto them by the other.</q> <rj><qau>Abp.
Abbot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I fear my Julia would not deign my lines,<br/
Receiving them from such a worthless <qex>post</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An established conveyance for letters from one place
or station to another; especially, the governmental system in any
country for carrying and distributing letters and parcels; the post
office; the mail; hence, the carriage by which the mail is
transported.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I send you the fair copy of the poem on dullness, which I should
not care to hazard by the common <qex>post</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Haste or speed, like that of a messenger or mail
carrier.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/In <xex>post</xex> he
came.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>One who has charge of a station, especially of a
postal station.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He held office of postmaster, or, as it was then called,
<qex>post</qex>, for several years.</q> <rj><qau>Palfrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A station, office, or position of service, trust, or
emolument; <as>as, the <ex>post</ex> of duty; the <ex>post</ex> of
danger.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>post</qex> of honor is a private station.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A size of printing and writing paper. See the Table
under <er>Paper</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Post and pair</b></col>, <cd>an old game at cards, in
which each player a hand of three cards.</cd> <au>B. Jonson.</au> --
<col><b>Post bag</b></col>, <cd>a mail bag.</cd> -- <col><b>Post
bill</b></col>, <cd>a bill of letters mailed by a postmaster.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Post chaise</b></col>, or <col><b>Post
coach</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a carriage usually with four wheels, for the
conveyance of travelers who travel post.</cd> -- <col><b>Post
day</b></col>, <cd>a day on which the mall arrives or departs.</cd> --
<col><b>Post hackney</b></col>, <cd>a hired post horse.</cd> <au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au> -- <col><b>Post horn</b></col>, <cd>a horn, or trumpet,
carried and blown by a carrier of the public mail, or by a
coachman.</cd> -- <col><b>Post horse</b></col>, <cd>a horse stationed,
intended, or used for the post.</cd> -- <col><b>Post hour</b></col>,
<cd>hour for posting letters.</cd> <au>Dickens.</au> -- <col><b>Post
office</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An office under governmental
superintendence, where letters, papers, and other mailable matter, are
received and distributed; a place appointed for attending to all
business connected with the mail</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The governmental
system for forwarding mail matter.</cd> -- <col><b>Postoffice
order</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Money order</cref>, under
<er>Money</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Post road</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Post route</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a road or way over which the
mail is carried.</cd> -- <col><b>Post town</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
town in which post horses are kept</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A town in
which a post office is established by law.</cd> -- <col><b>To ride
post</b></col>, <cd>to ride, as a carrier of dispatches, from place to
place; hence, to ride rapidly, with as little delay as possible.</cd> --
<col><b>To travel post</b></col>, <cd>to travel, as a post does, by
relays of horses, or by keeping one carriage to which fresh horses are
attached at each stopping place.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post</ent><br/
<hw>Post</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Posted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Posting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To attach to a post,
a wall, or other usual place of affixing public notices; to placard;
<as>as, to <ex>post</ex> a notice; to <ex>post</ex>
playbills.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Formerly, a large post was erected before the sheriff's
office, or in some public place, upon which legal notices were
displayed. This way of advertisement has not entirely gone of
use.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hold up to public blame or reproach; to advertise
opprobriously; to denounce by public proclamation; <as>as, to
<ex>post</ex> one for cowardice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On pain of being <qex>posted</qex> to your sorrow<br/
Fail not, at four, to meet me.</q> <rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To enter (a name) on a list, as for service,
promotion, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To assign to a station; to set; to place; <as>as, to
<ex>post</ex> a sentinel</as>.</def> <ldquo/It might be to obtain a ship
for a lieutenant, . . . or to get him <xex>posted</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bookkeeping)</fld> <def>To carry, as an account,
from the journal to the ledger; <as>as, to <ex>post</ex> an
account</as>; to transfer, as accounts, to the ledger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have not <qex>posted</qex> your books these ten years.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To place in the care of the post; to mail; <as>as,
to <ex>post</ex> a letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To inform; to give the news to; to make (one)
acquainted with the details of a subject; -- often with
<xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thoroughly <qex>posted</qex> up in the politics and literature of
the day.</q> <rj><qau>Lond. Sat. Rev.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To post off</b></col>, <cd>to put off; to delay.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Why did I, venturously, <xex>post off</xex>
so great a business?<rdquo/ <au>Baxter.</au> -- <col><b>To post
over</b></col>, <cd>to hurry over.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Fuller.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post</ent><br/
<hw>Post</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>poster</ets>. See 4th
<er>Post</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To travel with post horses;
figuratively, to travel in haste.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Post</xex> seedily
to my lord your husband.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>post</qex> o'er land and ocean without rest.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>To rise and sink in the saddle, in
accordance with the motion of the horse, esp. in trotting.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post</ent><br/
<hw>Post</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With post horses; hence, in haste;
<as>as, to travel <ex>post</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-abdomen</ent><br/
<hw>Post`-ab*do"men</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>abdomen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>That part of a crustacean behind the cephalothorax; -- more
commonly called <altname>abdomen</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postable</ent><br/
<hw>Post"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
carried by, or as by, post.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>W.
Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postact</ent><br/
<hw>Post"act`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An act done
afterward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postage</ent><br/
<hw>Post"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The price established
by law to be paid for the conveyance of a letter or other mailable
matter by a public post.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Postage stamp</b></col>, <cd>a government stamp required
to be put upon articles sent by mail in payment of the postage, esp. an
adhesive stamp issued and sold for that purpose.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postal</ent><br/
<hw>Post"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>postal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging to the post office or mail
service; <as>as, <ex>postal</ex> arrangements; <ex>postal</ex>
authorities.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Postal card</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Post
card</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a card used for transmission of messages
through the mails, at a lower rate of postage than a sealed letter; also
called <altname>postcard</altname>. Such cards are sold by the
government with postage already paid, or by private vendors without a
postage stamp. The message is written on one side of the card, and the
address on the other.</cd> -- <col><b>Postal money order</b></col>.
<cd>See <cref>Money order</cref>, under <er>Money</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Postal note</b></col>, <cd>an order payable to bearer, for a sum
of money (in the United States less than five dollars under existing
law), issued from one post office and payable at another specified
office.</cd> -- <col><b>Postal Union</b></col>, <cd>a union for postal
purposes entered into by the most important powers, or governments,
which have agreed to transport mail matter through their several
territories at a stipulated rate.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postanal</ent><br/
<hw>Post*a"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>anal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind, or posterior to, the anus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postaxial</ent><br/
<hw>Post*ax"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>axial</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind any transverse axis in the body of an animal;
caudal; posterior; especially, behind, or on the caudal or posterior
(that is, ulnar or fibular) side of, the axis of a vertebrate
limb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1119 --></p>

<p><ent>Postboy</ent><br/
<hw>Post"boy`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
rides post horses; a position; a courier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A boy who carries letters from the post.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-captain</ent><br/
<hw>Post"-cap`tain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A captain of a
war vessel whose name appeared, or was <ldquo/posted,<rdquo/ in the
seniority list of the British navy, as distinguished from a commander
whose name was not so posted. The term was also used in the United
States navy; but no such commission as <xex>post-captain</xex> was ever
recognized in either service, and the term has fallen into
disuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postcava</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post"ca`va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Postcav<ae/</plw> .</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Post-</er>, and
<er>Cave</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The inferior
vena cava.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Post"ca`val</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>B. G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postclavicle</ent><br/
<hw>Post*clav"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>clavicle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
bone in the pectoral girdle of many fishes projecting backward from the
clavicle.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Post`*cla*vic"u*lar</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postcommissure</ent><br/
<hw>Post*com"mis*sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>commisure</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A transverse commissure in the posterior part of the roof of the
third ventricle of the brain; the posterior cerebral commissure.</def>
<rj><au>B. G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postcommunion</ent><br/
<hw>Post`com*mun"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>communion</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Ch. of
Eng. & Prot. Epis. Ch.)</fld> <def>The concluding portion of the
communion service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A prayer or prayers which the
priest says at Mass, after the ablutions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postcornu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*cor"nu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Postcornua</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Post-</er>,
and <er>Cornu</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The posterior horn of
each lateral ventricle of the brain.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postdate</ent><br/
<hw>Post"date`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Postdated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Postdating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>post-</ets> +
<ets>date</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To date after the real time;
<as>as, to <ex>postdate</ex> a contract, that is, to date it later than
the time when it was in fact made</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To affix a date to after the event.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postdate</ent><br/
<hw>Post"date`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made or done after the date
assigned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of these [predictions] some were <qex>postdate</qex>; cunningly
made after the thing came to pass.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postdate</ent><br/
<hw>Post"date`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A date put to a bill of exchange
or other paper, later than that when it was actually made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postdiluvian</ent><br/
<ent>Postdiluvial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Post`di*lu"vi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Post`di*lu"vi*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>post-</ets> +
<ets>diluvial</ets>, <ets>diluvian</ets>.]</ety> <def>Being or happening
after the flood in Noah's days.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postdiluvian</ent><br/
<hw>Post`di*lu"vi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lived after the
flood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-disseizin</ent><br/
<hw>Post"-dis*sei"zin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>disseizin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>A subsequent disseizin committed by one of lands which the
disseizee had before recovered of the same disseizor; a writ founded on
such subsequent disseizin, now abolished.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.
Tomlins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-disseizor</ent><br/
<hw>Post`-dis*sei"zor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>disseizor</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>A person who disseizes another of lands which the disseizee had
before recovered of the same disseizor.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., after these
or those (things), afterward.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The return of
the judge before whom a cause was tried, after a verdict, of what was
done in the cause, which is indorsed on the <xex>nisi prius</xex>
record.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postel</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Apostle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postencephalon</ent><br/
<hw>Post`en*ceph"a*lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The metencephalon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postentry</ent><br/
<hw>Post"en*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>entry</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A second or
subsequent, at the customhouse, of goods which had been omitted by
mistake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bookkeeping)</fld> <def>An additional or subsequent
entry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poster</ent><br/
<hw>Post"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large
bill or placard intended to be posted in public places.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who posts bills; a billposter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poster</ent><br/
<hw>Post"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who posts, or
travels expeditiously; a courier.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Posters</xex> of the
sea and land.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A post horse.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Posters</xex> at
full gallop.<rdquo/ <rj><au>C. Lever.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posterior</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*te"ri*or</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/s*t<emac/"r<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>posterior</ets>, compar. of
<ets>posterus</ets> coming after, from <ets>post</ets> after. See
<er>Post-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Later in time; hence, later in
the order of proceeding or moving; coming after; -- opposed to
<contr>prior</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hesiod was <qex>posterior</qex> to Homer.</q>
<rj><qau>Broome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Situated behind; hinder; -- opposed to
<contr>anterior</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>At or toward the caudal
extremity; caudal; -- in human anatomy often used for
<altname>dorsal</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>On the side next the axis of
inflorescence; -- said of an axillary flower.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posteriority</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*te`ri*or"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>post<eacute/riorit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of being
later or subsequent; <as>as, <ex>posteriority</ex> of time, or of an
event</as>; -- opposed to <contr>priority</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posteriorly</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*te"ri*or*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Subsequently
in time; also, behind in position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posteriors</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*te"ri*ors</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>The hinder
parts, as of an animal's body.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posterity</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*ter"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>posteritas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>post<eacute/rit<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Posterior</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The race that proceeds from
a progenitor; offspring to the furthest generation; the aggregate number
of persons who are descended from an ancestor of a generation;
descendants; -- contrasted with <xex>ancestry</xex>; <as>as, the
<ex>posterity</ex> of Abraham</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If [the crown] should not stand in thy <qex>posterity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Succeeding generations; future times.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their names shall be transmitted to <qex>posterity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their names shall be transmitted to <qex>posterity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Smalridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postern</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>posterne</ets>, <ets>posterle</ets>, F. <ets>poterne</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>posterula</ets>, fr. <ets>posterus</ets> coming after. See
<er>Posterior</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Originally, a back door or
gate; a private entrance; hence, any small door or gate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He by a privy <qex>postern</qex> took his flight.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Out at the <qex>postern</qex>, by the abbey wall.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A subterraneous passage
communicating between the parade and the main ditch, or between the
ditches and the interior of the outworks.</def>
<rj><au>Mahan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postern</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tern</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Back; being behind; private.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>postern</xex> door.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postero</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"te*ro</hw><def>- (<?/). A combining form meaning
<xex>posterior</xex>, <xex>back</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>postero</ex>-inferior, situated back and below;
<ex>postero</ex>-lateral, situated back and at the side.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postexilic</ent><br/
<ent>Postexilian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Post`ex*il"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Post`ex*il"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>} <def>After the exile;</def>
<specif>specif.</specif> <fld>(Jewish Hist.)</fld>, <def>belonging to a
period subsequent to the Babylonian captivity or exile (<sc>b. c.</sc>
597 or about 586-about 537).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postexist</ent><br/
<hw>Post`ex*ist"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>exist</ets>.]</ety> <def>To exist after; to live
subsequently.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postexistence</ent><br/
<hw>Post`ex*ist"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Subsequent
existence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postexistent</ent><br/
<hw>Post`ex*ist"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Existing or
living after.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Postexistent</xex>
atoms.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfact</ent><br/
<hw>Post"fact`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Post-</er>, and <er>Fact</er>.]</ety> <def>Relating to a fact that
occurs after another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfact</ent><br/
<hw>Post"fact`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fact that occurs after
another.</def> <ldquo/Confirmed upon the <xex>postfact</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfactum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post`fac"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.]</ety>
<fld>(Rom. & Eng. Law)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Postfact</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-fine</ent><br/
<hw>Post"-fine`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>fine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>A duty paid to the king by the cognizee in a fine of lands, when
the same was fully passed; -- called also the <altname>king's
silver</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfix</ent><br/
<hw>Post"fix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Postfixes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Pref. <ets>post-</ets> +
<ets>-fix</ets>, as in <ets>prefix</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>postfixe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A letter, syllable,
or word, added to the end of another word; a suffix.</def>
<rj><au>Parkhurst.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfix</ent><br/
<hw>Post*fix"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To annex;
specifically <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, to add or annex, as a letter, syllable,
or word, to the end of another or principal word; to suffix.</def>
<rj><au>Parkhurst.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfrontal</ent><br/
<hw>Post*fron"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>frontal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind the frontal bone or the frontal region of the
skull; -- applied especially to a bone back of and below the frontal in
many animals.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A postfrontal
bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postfurca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*fur"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Postfurc<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr.
<ets>post</ets> behind + <ets>furca</ets> a fork.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the internal thoracic processes of the
sternum of an insect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postgeniture</ent><br/
<hw>Post*gen"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + L. <ets>genitura</ets> birth, geniture.]</ety>
<def>The condition of being born after another in the same family; --
distinguished from <xex>primogeniture</xex>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postglacial</ent><br/
<hw>Post*gla"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Formed or occurring after the last glacial epoch of
the Pleistocene period, or at a locality within the area of Pleistocene
glaciation after the final disappearance of the glacier from the
locality.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an
epoch after the last Glacial and before the Terrace epoch.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postglenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Post*gle"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>glenoid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind the glenoid fossa of the temporal bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postgraduate</ent><br/
<hw>Post*grad"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>graduate</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to,
or designating, the studies pursued after graduation, esp., after
receiving the bachelor's degree at a college; graduate.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A student who pursues such
studies.</def></def2></p>

<p><note><hand/ Most careful writers consider the word
<xex>graduate</xex> to be the proper word to use in this
sense.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthaste</ent><br/
<hw>Post`haste"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Haste or speed in
traveling, like that of a post or courier.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthaste</ent><br/
<hw>Post`haste</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With speed or expedition;
<as>as, he traveled <ex>posthaste</ex>; to send
<ex>posthaste</ex>.</as></def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthetomy</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*thet"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>po`sqh</grk> prepuce + <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Circumcision.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthouse</ent><br/
<hw>Post"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
house established for the convenience of the post, where relays of
horses can be obtained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A house for distributing the malls; a post
office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthumed</ent><br/
<ent>Posthume</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Post"hume</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Post"humed</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Posthumous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>I. Watts.</au> <au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthumous</ent><br/
<hw>Post"hu*mous</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>posthumus</ets>, <ets>postumus</ets>, properly, last; hence, late
born (applied to children born after the father's death, or after he had
made his will), superl. of <ets>posterus</ets>, <ets>posterior</ets>.
See <er>Posterior</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Born after the death of
the father, or taken from the dead body of the mother; <as>as, a
<ex>posthumous</ex> son or daughter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Published after the death of the author; <as>as,
<ex>posthumous</ex> works; a <ex>posthumous</ex> edition.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being or continuing after one's death; <as>as, a
<ex>posthumous</ex> reputation</as>.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au> <au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posthumously</ent><br/
<hw>Post"hu*mous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a posthumous manner;
after one's decease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postic</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>posticus</ets>, fr. post after, behind.]</ety> <def>Backward.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posticous</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"ti*cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>posticus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Posterior.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Situated on the outer side of a
filament; -- said of an extrorse anther.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postil</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>postille</ets>, <ets>apostille</ets>, LL. <ets>postilla</ets>,
probably from L. <ets>post illa</ets> (sc. <ets>verba</ets>) after those
(words). Cf. <er>Apostil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Originally, an
explanatory note in the margin of the Bible, so called because written
after the text; hence, a marginal note; a comment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Langton also made <qex>postils</qex> upon the whole Bible.</q>
<rj><qau>Foxe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch. & Luth. Ch.)</fld> <def>A short homily or
commentary on a passage of Scripture; <as>as, the first <ex>postils</ex>
were composed by order of Charlemagne</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postil</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>postillare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To write marginal or explanatory
notes on; to gloss.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postil</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Postiled</conjf> (<?/) or <conjf>Postilled</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Postiling</conjf> or
<conjf>Postilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To write postils, or marginal
notes; to comment; to postillate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Postiling</qex> and allegorizing on Scripture.</q>
<rj><qau>J. H. Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postiler</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>postiller</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who writers marginal notes; one
who illustrates the text of a book by notes in the margin.</def>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postilion</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*til"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>postillon</ets>, It. <ets>postiglione</ets>, fr. <ets>posta</ets>
post. See <er>Post</er> a postman.]</ety> <def>One who rides and guides
the first pair of horses of a coach or post chaise; also, one who rides
one of the horses when one pair only is used.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>postillion</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postillate</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>postillatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>postillare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
explain by marginal notes; to postil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tracts . . . <qex>postillated</qex> by his own hand.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Knight.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postillate</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til*late</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To write
postils; to comment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To preach by expounding Scripture verse by verse, in
regular order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postillation</ent><br/
<hw>Pos`til*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>postillatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of postillating; exposition
of Scripture in preaching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postillator</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"til*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.]</ety>
<def>One who postillates; one who expounds the Scriptures verse by
verse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posttiller</ent><br/
<hw>Post"til*ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Postiler</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-impressionism</ent><br/
<hw>Post`-im*pres"sion*ism</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Painting)</fld>
<def>In the broadest sense, the theory or practice of any of several
groups of painters of the early 1900's, or of these groups taken
collectively, whose work and theories have in common a tendency to
reaction against the scientific and naturalistic character of
impressionism and neo-impressionism. In a strict sense the term
post-impressionism is used to denote the effort at self-expression,
rather than representation, shown in the work of C<eacute/zanne,
Matisse, etc.; but it is more broadly used to include
<colf>cubism</colf>, the theory or practice of a movement in both
painting and sculpture which lays stress upon volume as the important
attribute of objects and attempts its expression by the use of
geometrical figures or solids only; and <colf>futurism</colf>, a theory
or practice which attempts to place the observer within the picture and
to represent simultaneously a number of consecutive movements and
impressions. In practice these theories and methods of the
post-impressionists change with great rapidity and shade into one
another, so that a picture may be both cubist and futurist in character.
They tend to, and sometimes reach, a condition in which both
representation and traditional decoration are entirely abolished and a
work of art becomes a purely subjective expression in an arbitrary and
personal language.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posting</ent><br/
<hw>Post"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of traveling post.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bookkeeping)</fld> <def>The act of transferring an
account, as from the journal to the ledger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Posting house</b></col>, <cd>a post house.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-it note</ent><br/
<hw>Post"-it note</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the
trademark <ets>Post-it</ets> registered by the <company>3M
Corporation</company>.]</ety> <def>A small sheet of paper having the
back part partly covered with a non-permanent gum which allows the note
to be attached temporarily to another object, and easily removed without
leaving any trace of glue on the object to which it was affixed. Such
note papers are sold in pads of varying sizes.</def>
<mark>[Trademark]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postliminiar</ent><br/
<hw>Post`li*min"i*ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Postliminium</er>.]</ety> <def>Contrived, done, or existing
subsequently.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Postliminious</xex> after applications
of them to their purposes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postliminiary</ent><br/
<hw>Post`li*min"i*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to, or involving, the right of postliminium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postliminy</ent><br/
<ent>Postliminium</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Post`li*min"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Post*lim"i*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postliminium</ets>, <ets>post</ets> after + <ets>limen</ets>,
<ets>liminis</ets>, a threshold.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>The return to his own country, and his former
privileges, of a person who had gone to sojourn in a foreign country, or
had been banished, or taken by an enemy.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Internat. Law)</fld> <def>The right by virtue of
which persons and things taken by an enemy in war are restored to their
former state when coming again under the power of the nation to which
they belonged.</def> <rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postlude</ent><br/
<hw>Post"lude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>-lude</ets>, as in <ets>prelude</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A voluntary at the end of a service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postman</ent><br/
<hw>Post"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Postmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A post or
courier; a letter carrier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>One of the two most
experienced barristers in the Court of Exchequer, who have precedence in
motions; -- so called from the place where he sits. The other of the two
is called the <xex>tubman</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Whishaw.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postmark</ent><br/
<hw>Post"mark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The mark, or stamp,
of a post office on a letter, giving the place and date of mailing or of
arrival.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postmark</ent><br/
<hw>Post"mark`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Postmarked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Postmarking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To mark with a post-office
stamp; <as>as, to <ex>postmark</ex> a letter or parcel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postmaster</ent><br/
<hw>Post"mas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who has charge of a station for the accommodation of travelers; one who
supplies post horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who has charge of a post office, and the
distribution and forwarding of mails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postmaster-general</ent><br/
<hw>Post"mas`ter-gen"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Postmasters-general</plw>.</plu> <def>The chief
officer of the post-office department of a government. In the United
States the postmaster-general is a member of the cabinet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postmastership</ent><br/
<hw>Post"mas`ter*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of
postmaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postmeridian</ent><br/
<hw>Post`me*rid"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postmeridianus</ets>; <ets>post</ets> after +
<ets>meridianus</ets>. See <er>Meridian</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Coming after the sun has passed the meridian; being in, or
belonging to, the afternoon. (Abbrev. <abbr><sc>P.
M.</sc></abbr>)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig., belonging to the after portion of life;
late.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-mortem</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post-mor"tem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L., after
death.]</ety> <def>After death; <as>as, <ex>post-mortem</ex>
rigidity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Post-mortem examination</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>an examination of the body made after the death of the patient; an
autopsy.</cd></cs><-- also, simply post-mortem.; (Fig.) any inquiry
after the failure of an enterprise to determine the casue of failure
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postnares</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*na"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Post-</er>, and <er>Nares</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
posterior nares. See <er>Nares</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postnatal</ent><br/
<hw>Post*na"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>natal</ets>.]</ety> <def>After birth; subsequent
to birth; <as>as, <ex>postnatal</ex> infanticide; <ex>postnatal</ex>
diseases.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postnate</ent><br/
<hw>Post"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>postnatus</ets> second or subsequently born; L. <ets>post</ets>
after + <ets>natus</ets> born.]</ety> <def>Subsequent.</def> <ldquo/The
graces and gifts of the spirit are <xex>postnate</xex>.<rdquo/
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post note</ent><br/
<hw>Post" note`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A note issued
by a bank, payable at some future specified time, as distinguished from
a note payable on demand.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postnuptial</ent><br/
<hw>Post*nup"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>nuptial</ets>.]</ety> <def>Being or happening
after marriage; <as>as, a <ex>postnuptial</ex> settlement on a
wife</as>.</def> <rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-obit bond</ent><br/
<ent>Post-obit</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Post-o"bit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Post-o"bit bond`</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[Pref. <ets>post-</ets> +
<ets>obit</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A bond in which the
obligor, in consideration of having received a certain sum of money,
binds himself to pay a larger sum, on unusual interest, on the death of
some specified individual from whom he has expectations.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postoblongata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*ob`lon*ga"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Post-</er>, and <er>Oblongata</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The posterior part of the medulla oblongata.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postocular</ent><br/
<hw>Post*oc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>ocular</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Postorbital</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post office</ent><br/
<hw>Post" of`fice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See under 4th
<er>Post</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postoral</ent><br/
<hw>Post*o"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>oral</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind, or posterior to, the mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postorbital</ent><br/
<hw>Post*or"bit*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>orbital</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat. & Zool.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind the orbit; <as>as, the <ex>postorbital</ex> scales
of some fishes and reptiles</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
postorbital bone or scale.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpaid</ent><br/
<hw>Post"paid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the postage
prepaid, as a letter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpalatine</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pal"a*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>palatine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind the palate, or behind the palatine bones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpliocene</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pli"o*cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>post-</ets> + <ets>pliocene</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the period immediately following the Pliocene;
Pleistocene. Also used as a noun. See <er>Quaternary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpone</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pone"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Postponed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Postponing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>postponere</ets>, <ets>postpositum</ets>; <ets>post</ets> after +
<ets>ponere</ets> to place, put. See <er>Post-</er>, and
<er>Position</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To defer to a future or later
time; to put off; also, to cause to be deferred or put off; to delay; to
adjourn; <as>as, to <ex>postpone</ex> the consideration of a bill to the
following day, or indefinitely</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His praise <qex>postponed</qex>, and never to be paid.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To place after, behind, or below something, in
respect to precedence, preference, value, or importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All other considerations should give way and be
<qex>postponed</qex> to this.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To adjourn; defer; delay;
procrastinate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postponement</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pone"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
postponing; a deferring, or putting off, to a future time; a temporary
delay.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postponence</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pon"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>postponens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>The act of postponing, in
sense 2.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postponer</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pon"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
postpones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpose</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Postposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Postposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>postposer</ets>. See <er>Post-</er>, and <er>Pose</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To postpone.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postposit</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pos"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postpositus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Postpone</er>.]</ety> <def>To
postpone.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postposition</ent><br/
<hw>Post`po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>postposition</ets>. See <er>Postpone</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of placing after, or the state of being placed after.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>postposition</xex> of the nominative case to the
verb.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mede.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1120 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A word or particle placed after, or at the end of,
another word; -- distinguished from <xex>preposition</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpositional</ent><br/
<hw>Post`po*si"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to postposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postpositive</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pos"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Postpone</er>.]</ety> <def>Placed after another word; <as>as, a
<ex>postpositive</ex> conjunction; a <ex>postpositive</ex>
letter.</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Post*pos"i*tive*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postprandial</ent><br/
<hw>Post*pran"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>prandial</ets>.]</ety> <def>Happening, or done,
after dinner; after-dinner; <as>as, <ex>postprandial</ex>
speeches</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postremogeniture</ent><br/
<hw>Pos*tre`mo*gen"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postremus</ets> last + <ets>genitura</ets> birth, geniture.]</ety>
<def>The right of the youngest born.</def> <rj><au>Mozley &
W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postremote</ent><br/
<hw>Post`re*mote"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>remote</ets>.]</ety> <def>More remote in
subsequent time or order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postrider</ent><br/
<hw>Post"rid`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who rides over
a post road to carry the mails.</def> <rj><au>Bancroft.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscapula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*scap"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Post-</er>, and <er>Scapula</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
part of the scapula behind or below the spine, or mesoscapula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscapular</ent><br/
<hw>Post*scap"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the postscapula; infraspinous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscenium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*sce"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>post + scena</ets> a scene.]</ety> <def>The part of a theater
behind the scenes; the back part of the stage of a theater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscribe</ent><br/
<hw>Post*scribe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postscribere</ets>. See <er>Postscript</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a
postscript.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscript</ent><br/
<hw>Post"script</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postscriptus</ets>, (assumed) p. p. of <ets>postscribere</ets> to
write after; <ets>post</ets> after + <ets>scribere</ets> to write: cf.
F. <ets>postscriptum</ets>. See <er>Post-</er>, and
<er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <def>A paragraph added to a letter after it is
concluded and signed by the writer; an addition made to a book or
composition after the main body of the work has been finished,
containing something omitted, or something new occurring to the
writer.</def> <altsp>[Abbrev. <abbr>P. S.</abbr>]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscripted</ent><br/
<hw>Post"script*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a postscript; added
in a postscript.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>J. Q.
Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postscutellum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post`scu*tel"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Post-</er>, and <er>Scutellum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The hindermost dorsal piece of a thoracic somite of an insect; the
plate behind the scutellum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postsphenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Post*sphe"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>sphenoid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the posterior part of the sphenoid
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-temporal</ent><br/
<hw>Post-tem"po*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>temporal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated back of the temporal bone or the temporal region of the
skull; -- applied especially to a bone which usually connects the
supraclavicle with the skull in the pectoral arch of fishes.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A post-temporal bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posttertiary</ent><br/
<hw>Post*ter"ti*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>tertiary</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Following, or more recent than, the Tertiary; Quaternary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-tragus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post"-tra`gus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Post-</er>, and <er>Tragus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
ridge within and behind the tragus in the ear of some animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Post-tympanic</ent><br/
<hw>Post`-tym*pan"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>post-</ets> + <ets>tympanic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated behind the tympanum, or in the skull, behind the auditory
meatus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulant</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*lant</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>postulans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>postulare</ets>. See
<er>Postulate</er>.]</ety> <def>One who makes a request or demand;
hence, a candidate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postulatum</ets> a demand, request, prop. p. p. of
<ets>postulare</ets> to demand, prob. a dim. of <ets>poscere</ets> to
demand, prob. for <ets>porcscere</ets>; akin to G. <ets>forschen</ets>
to search, investigate, Skr. <ets>prach</ets> to ask, and L.
<ets>precari</ets> to pray: cf. F. <ets>postulat</ets>. See
<er>Pray</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something demanded or asserted;
especially, a position or supposition assumed without proof, or one
which is considered as self-evident; a truth to which assent may be
demanded or challenged, without argument or evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The enunciation of a self-evident
problem, in distinction from an <xex>axiom</xex>, which is the
enunciation of a self-evident theorem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The distinction between a <qex>postulate</qex> and an axiom lies
in this, -- that the latter is admitted to be self-evident, while the
former may be agreed upon between two reasoners, and admitted by both,
but not as proposition which it would be impossible to deny.</q>
<rj><qau>Eng. Cyc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*late</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Postulated.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Postulated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Postulating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To beg,
or assume without proof; <as>as, to <ex>postulate</ex>
conclusions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take without express consent; to
assume.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Byzantine emperors appear to have . . . <qex>postulated</qex>
a sort of paramount supremacy over this nation.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Tooke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To invite earnestly; to solicit.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulated</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Assumed without
proof; <as>as, a <ex>postulated</ex> inference</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulation</ent><br/
<hw>Pos`tu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postulatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>postulation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act of postulating, or that which is postulated; assumption;
solicitation; suit; cause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*la*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>postulatorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of the nature of a
postulate.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postulatum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pos`tu*la"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Postulata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See
<er>Postulate</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A postulate.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postumous</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tu*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Posthumous</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postural</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to posture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posture</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>positura</ets>, fr. <ets>ponere</ets>, <ets>positum</ets>, to
place. See <er>Position</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The position of
the body; the situation or disposition of the several parts of the body
with respect to each other, or for a particular purpose; especially
<fld>(Fine Arts)</fld>, the position of a figure with regard to the
several principal members by which action is expressed;
attitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Atalanta, the <qex>posture</qex> of whose limbs was so lively
expressed . . . one would have sworn the very picture had run.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In most strange <qex>postures</qex><br/
We have seen him set himself.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>posture</qex> of a poetic figure is a description of his
heroes in the performance of such or such an action.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Place; position; situation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His [man's] noblest <qex>posture</qex> and station in this
world.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>State or condition, whether of external
circumstances, or of internal feeling and will; disposition; mood;
<as>as, a <ex>posture</ex> of defense; the <ex>posture</ex> of
affairs.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The several <qex>postures</qex> of his devout soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Attitude; position. See
<er>Attitude</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posture</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Postured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Posturing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To place in a
particular position or attitude; to dispose the parts of, with reference
to a particular purpose; <as>as, to <ex>posture</ex> one's self; to
<ex>posture</ex> a model.</as></def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posture</ent><br/
<hw>Pos"ture</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To assume a
particular posture or attitude; to contort the body into artificial
attitudes, as an acrobat or contortionist; also, to pose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To assume a character; <as>as, to
<ex>posture</ex> as a saint</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posturer</ent><br/
<hw>Pos`tur*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
postures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Postzygapophysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Post*zyg`a*poph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Postzygapophyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Post-</er>, and <er>Zygapophysis</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A posterior zygapophysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Posy</ent><br/
<hw>Po"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Posies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Contr. fr.
<ets>poesy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A brief poetical sentiment;
hence, any brief sentiment, motto, or legend; especially, one inscribed
on a ring.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>posy</xex> of a ring.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Probably so called from the use of flowers as
having an enigmatical significance. <ets>Wedgwood</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
flower; a bouquet; a nosegay.</def> <ldquo/Bridegroom's
<xex>posies</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We make a difference between suffering thistles to grow among us,
and wearing them for <qex>posies</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot</ent><br/
<hw>Pot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG.
<ets>pott</ets>, D. <ets>pot</ets>, Dan. <ets>potte</ets>, Sw.
<ets>potta</ets>, Icel. <ets>pottr</ets>, F. <ets>pot</ets>; of unknown
origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A metallic or earthen vessel,
appropriated to any of a great variety of uses, as for boiling meat or
vegetables, for holding liquids, for plants, etc.; <as>as, a quart
<ex>pot</ex>; a flower <ex>pot</ex>; a bean <ex>pot</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An earthen or pewter cup for liquors; a
mug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The quantity contained in a pot; a potful; <as>as, a
<ex>pot</ex> of ale</as>.</def> <ldquo/Give her a <xex>pot</xex> and a
cake.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De Foe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A metal or earthenware extension of a flue above the
top of a chimney; a chimney pot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A crucible; <as>as, a graphite <ex>pot</ex>; a
melting <ex>pot</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A wicker vessel for catching fish, eels,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A perforated cask for draining sugar.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A size of paper. See <er>Pott</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>marijuana.</def> <mark>[slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>The total of the bets at stake at one time, as in
racing or card playing; the pool;</def> <specif>also</specif>
<fld>(Racing, Eng.)</fld> <def>a horse heavily backed; a favorite.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Armor)</fld> <def>A plain defensive headpiece;
later, and perhaps in a jocose sense, any helmet; -- called also
<altname>pot helmet</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>The total of the bets at
one time; the pool.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Jack pot</b></col>. <cd>See under 2d <er>Jack</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Pot cheese</b></col>, <cd>cottage cheese. See under
<er>Cottage</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pot companion</b></col>, <cd>a
companion in drinking.</cd> -- <col><b>Pot hanger</b></col>, <cd>a
pothook.</cd> -- <col><b>Pot herb</b></col>, <cd>any plant, the leaves
or stems of which are boiled for food, as spinach, lamb's-quarters,
purslane, and many others.</cd> -- <col><b>Pot hunter</b></col>, <cd>one
who kills anything and everything that will help to fill has bag; also,
a hunter who shoots game for the table or for the market.</cd> --
<col><b>Pot metal</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The metal from which iron
pots are made, different from common pig iron</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An
alloy of copper with lead used for making large vessels for various
purposes in the arts</cd>. <au>Ure.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>A kind of
stained glass, the colors of which are incorporated with the melted
glass in the pot.</cd> <au>Knight.</au> -- <col><b>Pot plant</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>either of the trees which bear the
monkey-pot.</cd> -- <col><b>Pot wheel</b></col> <fld>(Hydraul.)</fld>,
<cd>a noria.</cd> -- <col><b>To go to pot</b></col>, <cd>to go to
destruction; to come to an end of usefulness; to become refuse.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au> <au>J. G.
Saxe.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot</ent><br/
<hw>Pot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Potted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Potting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place or inclose
in pots</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To preserve seasoned in pots.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Potted</xex> fowl and fish.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To set out or cover in pots; <as>as, <ex>potted</ex>
plants or bulbs</as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>To drain; <as>as, to
<ex>pot</ex> sugar, by taking it from the cooler, and placing it in
hogsheads, etc., having perforated heads, through which the molasses
drains off</as>.</def> <au>B. Edwards.</au> <sd>(d)</sd>
<fld>(Billiards)</fld> <def>To pocket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shoot for the pot, i.e., cooking; to secure or
hit by a pot shot; to shoot when no special skill is needed.</def></p>

<p><q>When hunted, it [the jaguar] takes refuge in trees, and this habit
is well known to hunters, who pursue it with dogs and <qex>pot</qex> it
when treed.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc. of Sport.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To secure; gain; win; bag.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot</ent><br/
<hw>Pot</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To tipple; to
drink.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is less labor to plow than to <qex>pot</qex> it.</q>
<rj><qau>Feltham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take a pot shot or shots, as at game or an
enemy.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potable</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ta*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>potabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>potare</ets> to drink; akin to Gr.
<grk>po`tos</grk> a drinking, <grk>po`sis</grk> a drink, Skr.
<ets>p<amac/</ets> to drink, OIr. <ets>ibim</ets> I drink. Cf.
<er>Poison</er>, <er>Bib</er>, <er>Imbibe</er>.]</ety> <def>Fit to be
drunk; drinkable.</def> <ldquo/Water fresh and
<xex>potable</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
potable liquid; a beverage.</def> <ldquo/Useful in
<xex>potables</xex>.<rdquo/</def2> <rj><au>J. Philips.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potableness</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ta*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
drinkable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potage</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pottage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potager</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"a*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. fr.
<ets>potage</ets> soup, porridge. See <er>Pottage</er>.]</ety> <def>A
porringer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Grew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potagro</ent><br/
<hw>Po*tag"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Potargo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potale</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ale`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The refuse from a
grain distillery, used to fatten swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potamian</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ta"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
river.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A river tortoise; one of a group
of tortoises (<fam>Potamites</fam>, or <fam>Trionychoidea</fam>) having
a soft shell, webbed feet, and a sharp beak. See
<er>Trionyx</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potamography</ent><br/
<hw>Pot`a*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
river + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>An account or description of
rivers; potamology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potamology</ent><br/
<hw>Pot`a*mol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ river
+ <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A scientific account or discussion of
rivers; a treatise on rivers; potamography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potamospongiae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pot`a*mo*spon"gi*<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ river + <?/ a sponge.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The fresh-water sponges. See <er>Spongilla</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potance</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>potence</ets>. See <er>Potence</er>, <er>Potency</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Watch Making)</fld> <def>The stud in which the bearing for the
lower pivot of the verge is made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potargo</ent><br/
<hw>Po*tar"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Botargo</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of sauce or pickle.</def>
<rj><au>King.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potash</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ash`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pot</ets> +
<ets>ash</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
hydroxide of potassium hydrate, a hard white brittle substance,
<chform>KOH</chform>, having strong caustic and alkaline properties; --
hence called also <altname>caustic potash</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The impure potassium carbonate obtained by leaching wood ashes,
either as a strong solution (<xex>lye</xex>), or as a white crystalline
(<xex>pearlash</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potashes</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ash`es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Potash.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potassa</ent><br/
<hw>Po*tas"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. E.
<ets>potash</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Potassium
oxide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Potassium hydroxide,
commonly called <altname>caustic potash</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potassamide</ent><br/
<hw>Pot`ass*am"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Potass</ets>ium + <ets>amide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A yellowish brown substance obtained by heating potassium in
ammonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pottassic</ent><br/
<hw>Pot*tas"sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or containing, potassium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potassium</ent><br/
<hw>Po*tas"si*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Potassa</er>, <er>Potash</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An
Alkali element, occurring abundantly but always combined, as in the
chloride, sulphate, carbonate, or silicate, in the minerals sylvite,
kainite, orthoclase, muscovite, etc. Atomic weight 39.0. Symbol K
(<xex>Kalium</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is reduced from the carbonate as a soft white metal,
lighter than water, which oxidizes with the greatest readiness, and, to
be preserved, must be kept under liquid hydrocarbons, as naphtha or
kerosene. Its compounds are very important, being used in glass making,
soap making, in fertilizers, and in many drugs and chemicals.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Potassium permanganate</b></col>, <cd>the salt
<chform>KMnO4</chform>, crystallizing in dark red prisms having a
greenish surface color, and dissolving in water with a beautiful purple
red color; -- used as an oxidizer and disinfectant. The name
<altname>chameleon mineral</altname> is applied to this salt and also to
potassium manganate.</cd> -- <col><b>Potassium bitartrate</b></col>.
<cd>See <cref>Cream of tartar</cref>, under
<er>Cream</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potassoxyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pot`ass*ox"yl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Potass</ets>ium + <ets>ox</ets>ygen + <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The radical <chform>KO</chform>, derived from,
and supposed to exist in, potassium hydroxide and other
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potation</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>potatio</ets>, fr. <ets>potare</ets>. See <er>Potable</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of drinking.</def> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A draught.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Potations</xex> pottle
deep.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Drink; beverage.</def> <ldquo/Thin
<xex>potations</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potato</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ta"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Potatoes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Sp. <ets>patata</ets>
potato, <ets>batata</ets> sweet potato, from the native American name
(probably <ets>batata</ets>) in Hayti.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A plant (<spn>Solanum tuberosum</spn>) of the
Nightshade family, and its esculent farinaceous tuber, of which there
are numerous varieties used for food. It is native of South America, but
a form of the species is found native as far north as New Mexico.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sweet potato (see below).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Potato beetle</b></col>, <col><b>Potato
bug</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A beetle
(<spn>Doryphora decemlineata</spn>) which feeds, both in the larval and
adult stages, upon the leaves of the potato, often doing great damage.
Called also <altname>Colorado potato beetle</altname>, and
<altname>Doryphora</altname>.</cd> See <er>Colorado beetle</er>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The <spn>Lema trilineata</spn>, a smaller and more
slender striped beetle which feeds upon the potato plant, bur does less
injury than the preceding species.</cd> -- <col><b>Potato fly</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of blister beetles
infesting the potato vine. The black species (<spn>Lytta atrata</spn>),
the striped (<spn>Lytta vittata</spn>), and the gray (<spn>Lytta
Fabricii</spn> syn. <spn>Lytta cinerea</spn>) are the most common. See
<cref>Blister beetle</cref>, under <er>Blister</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Potato rot</b></col>, <cd>a disease of the tubers of the potato,
supposed to be caused by a kind of mold (<spn>Peronospora
infestans</spn>), which is first seen upon the leaves and stems.</cd> --
<col><b>Potato weevil</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American
weevil (<spn>Baridius trinotatus</spn>) whose larva lives in and kills
the stalks of potato vines, often causing serious damage to the
crop.</cd> -- <col><b>Potato whisky</b></col>, <cd>a strong, fiery
liquor, having a hot, smoky taste, and rich in amyl alcohol (<xex>fusel
oil</xex>); it is made from potatoes or potato starch.</cd> --
<col><b>Potato worm</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the large green
larva of a sphinx, or hawk moth (<spn>Macrosila quinquemaculata</spn>);
-- called also <altname>tomato worm</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
under <er>Tomato</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Seaside potato</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd><spn>Ipom<oe/a Pes-Capr<ae/</spn>, a kind of
morning-glory with rounded and emarginate or bilobed leaves.</cd>
<mark>[West Indies]</mark> -- <col><b>Sweet potato</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a climbing plant (<spn>Ipom<oe/a Balatas</spn>)
allied to the morning-glory. Its farinaceous tubers have a sweetish
taste, and are used, when cooked, for food. It is probably a native of
Brazil, but is cultivated extensively in the warmer parts of every
continent, and even as far north as New Jersey. The name
<xex>potato</xex> was applied to this plant before it was to the
<spn>Solanum tuberosum</spn>, and this is the <ldquo/potato<rdquo/ of
the Southern United States.</cd> -- <col><b>Wild potato</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A vine (<spn>Ipom<oe/a
pandurata</spn>) having a pale purplish flower and an enormous root. It
is common in sandy places in the United States.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
similar tropical American plant (<spn>Ipom<oe/a fastigiata</spn>) which
it is thought may have been the original stock of the sweet
potato.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potator</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ta"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A
drinker.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potatory</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ta*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>potatorius</ets>, from <ets>potare</ets> to drink.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to drinking.</def> <rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot-au-feu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pot`-au`-feu"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., lit.,
pot on the fire.]</ety> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>A dish of broth, meat,
and vegetables prepared by boiling in a pot, -- a dish esp. common among
the French.</def> <rj><au>Grant Allen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot-bellied</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"-bel`lied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
protuberant belly, like the bottom of a pot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot-belly</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"-bel`ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A protuberant
belly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potboiler</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"boil`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A term applied
derisively to any literary or artistic work, and esp. a painting, done
simply for money and the means of living.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potboy</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"boy`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boy who carries
pots of ale, beer, etc.; a menial in a public house.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potch</ent><br/
<hw>Potch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Poach</er>
to stab.]</ety> <def>To thrust; to push.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/I 'll <xex>potch</xex> at him some way.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potch</ent><br/
<hw>Potch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Poach</er>, to cook.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potcher</ent><br/
<hw>Potch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, potches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Potcher engine</b></col> <fld>(Paper Making)</fld>, <cd>a
machine in which washed rags are stirred in a bleaching
solution.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potecary</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"e*ca*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
apothecary.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potheen</ent><br/
<ent>Poteen</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Po*teen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Po*theen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ir. <ets>poitin</ets> a small pot, whisky
made in private stills; cf. <ets>pota</ets> pot, fr. E.
<ets>pot</ets>.]</ety> <def>Whisky; especially whisky distilled in a
small way privately or illicitly by the Irish peasantry.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>potteen</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poteen</ent><br/
<hw>Po*teen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[, <ets>poitin</ets>
a small pot.]</ety> <def>especially, whisky illicitly distilled by the
Irish peasantry.</def> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potelot</ent><br/
<hw>Po"te*lot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,; cf. G.
<ets>pottloth</ets> black lead.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem. & Min.)</fld>
<def>Molybdenum sulphide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potence</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>potentia</ets> staff, crutch, L., might, power. See
<er>Potency</er>.]</ety> <def>Potency; capacity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1121 --></p>

<p><ent>Potency</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ten*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>potentia</ets>, from <ets>potens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, potent.
See <er>Potent</er>, and cf. <er>Potance</er>, <er>Potence</er>,
<er>Puissance</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being potent;
physical or moral power; inherent strength; energy; ability to effect a
purpose; capability; efficacy; influence.</def> <ldquo/Drugs of
<xex>potency</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A place of <qex>potency</qex> and away o' the state.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potent</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>potens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>posse</ets> to be able, to have power,
fr. <ets>potis</ets> able, capable (akin to Skr. <ets>pati</ets> master,
lord) + <ets>esse</ets> to be. See <er>Host</er> a landlord,
<er>Am</er>, and cf. <er>Despot</er>, <er>Podesta</er>,
<er>Possible</er>, <er>Power</er>, <er>Puissant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Producing great physical effects; forcible; powerful' efficacious;
<as>as, a <ex>potent</ex> medicine</as>.</def> <ldquo/Harsh and
<xex>potent</xex> injuries.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moses once more his <qex>potent</qex> rod extends.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having great authority, control, or dominion;
puissant; mighty; influential; <as>as, a <ex>potent</ex>
prince</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>potent</xex> dukedom.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Most <qex>potent</qex>, grave, and reverend signiors.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Powerful, in an intellectual or moral sense; having
great influence; <as>as, <ex>potent</ex> interest; a <ex>potent</ex>
argument.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cross potent</b></col>. <fld>(Her.)</fld> <cd>See
<xex>Illust.</xex> (7) of <er>Cross</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Powerful; mighty; puissant; strong; able;
efficient; forcible; efficacious; cogent; influential.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potent</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A prince; a
potentate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[See <er>Potence</er>.]</ety> <def>A staff or
crutch.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>One of the furs; a surface
composed of patches which are supposed to represent crutch heads; they
are always alternately argent and azure, unless otherwise specially
mentioned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Counter potent</b></col> <fld>(Her.)</fld>, <cd>a fur
differing from potent in the arrangement of the patches.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentacy</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ten*ta*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Potentate</er>.]</ety> <def>Sovereignty.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentate</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ten*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>potentatus</ets>, fr. <ets>potentare</ets> to exercise power: cf.
F. <ets>potentat</ets>. See <er>Potent</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>One who is potent; one who possesses great power or sway; a prince,
sovereign, or monarch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The blessed and only <qex>potentate</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>1 Tim. vi.
15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cherub and seraph, <qex>potentates</qex> and thrones.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potential</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ten"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>potentiel</ets>. See <er>Potency</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Being potent; endowed with energy adequate to a result;
efficacious; influential.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/And hath in
his effect a voice <xex>potential</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Existing in possibility, not in actuality.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>potential</xex> hero.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Potential</qex> existence means merely that the thing may be
at ome time; <qex>actual</qex> existence, that it now is.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Potential cautery</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Cautery</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Potential energy</b></col>.
<fld>(Mech.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under <er>Energy</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Potential mood</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Potential
mode</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>that form of the verb
which is used to express possibility, liberty, power, will, obligation,
or necessity, by the use of <xex>may</xex>, <xex>can</xex>,
<xex>must</xex>, <xex>might</xex>, <xex>could</xex>, <xex>would</xex>,
or <xex>should</xex>; as, I <xex>may go</xex>; he <xex>can
write</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potential</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ten"tial</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything that may
be possible; a possibility; potentially.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>In the theory of gravitation, or
of other forces acting in space, a function of the rectangular
coordinates which determine the position of a point, such that its
differential coefficients with respect to the coordinates are equal to
the components of the force at the point considered; -- also called
<altname>potential function</altname>, or <altname>force
function</altname>. It is called also <stype>Newtonian potential</stype>
when the force is directed to a fixed center and is inversely as the
square of the distance from the center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The energy of an electrical
charge measured by its power to do work; hence, the degree of
electrification as referred to some standard, as that of the earth;
electro-motive force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentiality</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ten`ti*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being potential; possibility, not actuality; inherent
capability or disposition, not actually exhibited.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentially</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ten"tial*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>With power; potently.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a potential manner; possibly, not
positively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The duration of human souls is only <qex>potentially</qex>
infinite.</q> <rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentiate</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ten"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Potentiated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Potentiating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To render active or
potent.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentiometer</ent><br/
<hw>Po*ten`ti*om"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Potential</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring or comparing
electrial potentials or electro-motive forces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentize</ent><br/
<hw>Po"ten*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Potentized</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Potentizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To render the latent power of
(anything) available.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potently</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With great force
or energy; powerfully; efficaciously.</def> <ldquo/You are
<xex>potently</xex> opposed.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potentness</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tent*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
potent; powerfulness; potency; efficacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potestate</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tes*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chief ruler; a
potentate. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <xex>Wyclif</xex>.</def> <ldquo/An irous
<xex>potestate</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potestative</ent><br/
<hw>Po*tes"ta*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>potestativus</ets>, fr. <ets>potestas</ets> power: cf. F.
<ets>potestatif</ets>. See <er>Potent</er>.]</ety>
<def>Authoritative.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potgun</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"gun`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
pot-shaped cannon; a mortar.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Twelve
<xex>potguns</xex> of brass.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hakluyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A popgun.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pothecary</ent><br/
<hw>Poth"e*ca*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
apothecary.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potheen</ent><br/
<hw>Po*theen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Poteen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pother</ent><br/
<hw>Poth"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>peuteren</ets> to rummage, poke. Cf. <er>Potter</er>,
<er>Pudder</er>.]</ety> <def>Bustle; confusion; tumult; flutter;
bother.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>potter</asp>, and
<asp>pudder</asp>.]</altsp> <ldquo/What a <xex>pother</xex> and
stir!<rdquo/ <au>Oldham.</au> <ldquo/Coming on with a terrible
<xex>pother</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Wordsworth.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pother</ent><br/
<hw>Poth"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a bustle or stir; to be
fussy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pother</ent><br/
<hw>Poth"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pothered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pothering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To harass and perplex; to
worry.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pothers</xex> and wearies himself.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pothole</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A circular hole
formed in the rocky beds of rivers by the grinding action of stones or
gravel whirled round by the water in what was at first a natural
depression of the rock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pothook</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"hook`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
<universbold>S</universbold>-shaped hook on which pots and kettles are
hung over an open fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A written character curved like a pothook;
(<pluf>pl.</pluf>) a scrawled writing.</def> <ldquo/I long to be
spelling her Arabic scrawls and <xex>pothooks</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pothouse</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An alehouse.</def>
<rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potiche</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po`tiche"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl.
<plw>-tiches</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <ety>[F., fr. <ets>pot</ets> a
pot.]</ety> <fld>(Ceramics)</fld> <def>A vase with a separate cover, the
body usually rounded or polygonal in plan with nearly vertical sides, a
neck of smaller size, and a rounded shoulder.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potichomanie</ent><br/
<ent>Potichomania</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Po`ti*cho*ma"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
\'d8<hw>Po`ti*cho*ma"nie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>potichomanie</ets>; <ets>potiche</ets> a porcelain vase +
<ets>manie</ets> mania.]</ety> <def>The art or process of coating the
inside of glass vessels with engravings or paintings, so as to give them
the appearance of painted ware.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potion</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>potio</ets>,
from <ets>potare</ets> to drink: cf. F. <ets>potion</ets>. See
<er>Poison</er>.]</ety> <def>A draught; a dose; usually, a draught or
dose of a liquid medicine.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potion</ent><br/
<hw>Po"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To drug.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Speed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot lace</ent><br/
<hw>Pot lace</hw>. <def>Lace whose pattern includes one or more
representations of baskets or bowls from which flowers spring.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potlatch</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"latch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Chinook
<ets>potlatch</ets>, <ets>pahtlatch</ets>, fr. Nootka
<ets>pahchilt</ets>, <ets>pachalt</ets>, a gift.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Among the Kwakiutl, Chimmesyan, and other Indians of the
northwestern coast of North America, a ceremonial distribution by a man
of gifts to his own and neighboring tribesmen, often, formerly, to his
own impoverishment. Feasting, dancing, and public ceremonies accompany
it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a feast given to a large number of persons,
often accompanied by gifts.</def> <mark>[Colloq., Northwestern
America]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot lead</ent><br/
<hw>Pot lead</hw>. <def>Graphite, or black lead, often used on the
bottoms of racing vessels to diminish friction.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potlid</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"lid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The lid or cover of a
pot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Potlid valve</b></col>, <cd>a valve covering a round hole
or the end of a pipe or pump barrel, resembling a potlid in
form.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potluck</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"luck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Whatever may chance
to be in the pot, or may be provided for a meal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A woman whose <qex>potluck</qex> was always to be relied on.</q>
<rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To take potluck</b></col>, <cd>to take what food may
chance to be provided.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potman</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Potmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pot
companion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Life of A. Wood
(1663).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A servant in a public house; a potboy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potoo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po*too"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A large South American goatsucker (<spn>Nyctibius
grandis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potoroo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Po`to*roo"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any small kangaroo belonging to <gen>Hypsiprymnus</gen>,
<gen>Bettongia</gen>, and allied genera, native of Australia and
Tasmania. Called also <altname>kangaroo rat</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potpie</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"pie`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A meat pie which is
boiled instead of being baked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potpourri</ent><br/
<hw>Pot`pour`ri"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>pot</ets> pot + <ets>pourri</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pourrir</ets> to
rot, L. <ets>putrere</ets>. Cf. <er>Olla-podrida</er>.]</ety> <def>A
medley or mixture.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A ragout
composed of different sorts of meats, vegetables, etc., cooked
together.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A jar or packet of flower leaves,
perfumes, and spices, used to scent a room.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A
piece of music made up of different airs strung together; a
medley.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A literary production composed of parts
brought together without order or bond of connection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potsdam group</ent><br/
<hw>Pots"dam group`</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A
subdivision of the Primordial or Cambrian period in American geology; --
so named from the sandstone of <etsep>Potsdam</etsep>, New York. See
<xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potshare</ent><br/
<ent>Potshard</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pot"shard`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pot"share`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A potsherd.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potsherd</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"sherd`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pot</ets> +
<ets>sherd</ets> or <ets>shard</ets>.]</ety> <def>A piece or fragment of
a broken pot.</def> <rj><au>Job ii. 8.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot shot</ent><br/
<hw>Pot shot</hw>. <def>Lit., a shot fired simply to fill the pot;
hence, a shot fired at an animal or person when at rest or within easy
range, or fired simply to kill, without reference to the rules of sport;
a shot needling no special skill.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potstone</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
variety of steatite sometimes manufactured into culinary
vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot-sure</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"-sure`</hw> <pr>(-sh<udd/r)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made
confident by drink.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pott</ent><br/
<hw>Pott</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A size of paper. See
under <er>Paper</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pottage</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"tage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>potage</ets>, fr. <ets>pot</ets> pot. See <er>Pot</er>, and cf.
<er>Porridge</er>, <er>Porringer</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of food made
by boiling vegetables or meat, or both together, in water, until soft; a
thick soup or porridge.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>potage</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then Jacob gave Esau bread and <qex>pottage</qex> of lentils.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxv. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pottain</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"tain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Old pot metal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potteen</ent><br/
<hw>Pot*teen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Poteen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potter</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>potier</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One whose occupation is to
make earthen vessels.</def> <rj><au>Ps. ii. 9.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>potter</qex> heard, and stopped his wheel.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who hawks crockery or earthenware.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who pots meats or other eatables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The red-bellied terrapin. See
<er>Terrapin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Potter's asthma</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>emphysema of the lungs; -- so called because very prevalent among
potters.</cd> <au>Parkers.</au> -- <col><b>Potter's clay</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Clay</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Potter's field</b></col>,
<cd>a public burial place, especially in a city, for paupers, unknown
persons, and criminals; -- so named from the field south of Jerusalem,
mentioned in <au>Matt. xxvii. 7.</au></cd> -- <col><b>Potter's
ore</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Alquifou</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Potter's
wheel</b></col>, <cd>a horizontal revolving disk on which the clay is
molded into form with the hands or tools.</cd> <ldquo/My thoughts are
whirled like a <xex>potter's wheel</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> --
<col><b>Potter wasp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small solitary
wasp (<spn>Eumenes fraternal</spn>) which constructs a globular nest of
mud and sand in which it deposits insect larv<ae/, such as cankerworms,
as food for its young.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potter</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pottered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pottering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. W. <ets>pwtio</ets> to
poke, or OD. <ets>poteren</ets> to search one thoroughly, Sw.
<ets>p<aring/ta</ets>, <ets>peta</ets>, to pick, E. <ets>pother</ets>,
<ets>put</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To busy one's self with trifles;
to labor with little purpose, energy, of effect; to trifle; to putter;
to pother.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> putter; pother.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pottering</qex> about the Mile End cottages.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. Humphry Ward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To walk lazily or idly; to saunter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potter</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To poke; to push; also, to
disturb; to confuse; to bother.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pottern</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
potters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pottern ore</b></col>, <cd>a species of ore which, from
its aptness to vitrify like the glazing of potter's wares, the miners
call by this name.</cd> <au>Boyle.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pottery</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Potteries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>poterie</ets>,
fr. <ets>pot</ets>. See <er>Pot</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
vessels or ware made by potters; earthenware, glazed and
baked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The place where earthen vessels are made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potting</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Tippling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of placing in a pot; <as>as, the
<ex>potting</ex> of plants; the <ex>potting</ex> of meats for
preservation.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The process of putting sugar in casks for cleansing
and draining.</def> <mark>[West Indies]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Edwards.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pottle</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>potel</ets>,
OF. <ets>potel</ets>, dim. of <ets>pot</ets>. See <er>Pot</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A liquid measure of four pints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pot or tankard.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A dry <qex>pottle</qex> of sack before him.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A vessel or small basket for holding
fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had a . . . <qex>pottle</qex> of strawberries in one hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pottle draught</b></col>, <cd>taking a pottle of liquor
at one draught.</cd> <mark>[ Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potto</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pot"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A nocturnal mammal (<spn>Perodictius potto</spn>) of
the Lemur family, found in West Africa. It has rudimentary forefingers.
Called also <altname>aposoro</altname>, and <altname>bush
dog</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The kinkajou.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pott's disease</ent><br/
<hw>Pott's" dis*ease"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Caries
of the vertebr<ae/, frequently resulting in curvature of the spine and
paralysis of the lower extremities; -- so named from Percival
<xex>Pott</xex>, an English surgeon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pott's fracture</b></col>, <cd>a fracture of the lower
end of the fibula, with displacement of the tibia.</cd>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Potulent</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"u*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>potulentus</ets>, fr. <ets>potus</ets> a drinking, drink, fr.
<ets>potare</ets> to drink.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Fit to drink;
potable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nearly drunk; tipsy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot-valiant</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"-val`iant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
courage given by drink.</def> <rj><au>Smollett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pot-walloper</ent><br/
<hw>Pot"-wal`lop*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
voter in certain boroughs of England, where, before the passage of the
reform bill of 1832, the qualification for suffrage was to have boiled
(walloped) his own pot in the parish for six months.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who cleans pots; a scullion.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouch</ent><br/
<hw>Pouch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poche</ets> a
pocket, pouch, bag; probably of Teutonic origin. See <er>Poke</er> a
bag, and cf. <er>Poach</er> to cook eggs, to plunder.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A small bag; usually, a leathern bag; <as>as, a <ex>pouch</ex> for
money; a shot <ex>pouch</ex>; a mail <ex>pouch</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is shaped like, or used as, a
pouch</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A protuberant belly; a paunch; -- so
called in ridicule.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sac or
bag for carrying food or young; <as>as, the cheek <ex>pouches</ex> of
certain rodents, and the <ex>pouch</ex> of marsupials</as>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A cyst or sac containing
fluid.</def> <au>S. Sharp.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
silicle, or short pod, as of the shepherd's purse.</def> <sd>(e)</sd>
<def>A bulkhead in the hold of a vessel, to prevent grain, etc., from
shifting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pouch mouth</b></col>, <cd>a mouth with blubbered or
swollen lips.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouch</ent><br/
<hw>Pouch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pouched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pouching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put or take into
a pouch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To swallow; -- said of fowls.</def>
<rj><au>Derham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pout.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pocket; to put up with.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouched</ent><br/
<hw>Pouched</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having a marsupial pouch; <as>as, the <ex>pouched</ex>
badger, or the wombat</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having external
cheek pouches; <as>as, the <ex>pouched</ex> gopher</as>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>Having internal cheek pouches; <as>as, the
<ex>pouched</ex> squirrels</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pouched dog</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Zebra wolf</cref>, under <er>Zebra</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pouched
frog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the nototrema, the female of
which has a dorsal pouch in which the eggs are hatched, and in which the
young pass through their brief tadpole stage.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Pouched gopher</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pouched
rat</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Pocket
gopher</cref>, under <er>Pocket</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pouched
mouse</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Pocket mouse</cref>,
under <er>Pocket</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouchet box</ent><br/
<hw>Pou"chet box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Pouncet
box</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouch-mouthed</ent><br/
<hw>Pouch"-mouthed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a pouch
mouth; blobber-lipped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouchong</ent><br/
<hw>Pou*chong"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A superior kind of
souchong tea.</def> <rj><au>De Colange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouch-shell</ent><br/
<hw>Pouch"-shell`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A small British and American pond snail (<spn>Bulinus
hypnorum</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poudre</ent><br/
<hw>Pou"dre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Powder</er>.]</ety> <def>Dust; powder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poudre marchant</b></col> <ety>[see
<er>Merchant</er>]</ety>, <cd>a kind of flavoring powder used in the
Middle Ages.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poudrette</ent><br/
<hw>Pou*drette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>poudre</ets> dust, powder. See <er>Powder</er>.]</ety> <def>A
manure made from night soil, dried and mixed with charcoal, gypsum,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouffe</ent><br/
<ent>Pouf</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pouf</hw>, <hw>Pouffe</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(p<oomac/f)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pouff</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[F.
<ets>pouf</ets>. Cf. <er>Puff</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Lit., a
puff;</def> <specif>specif.:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A soft cushion,
esp. one circular in shape and not, like a pillow, of bag form, or thin
at the edges.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A piece of furniture like an
ottoman, generally circular and affording cushion seats on all
sides.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poulaine</ent><br/
<hw>Pou*laine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. soulier
<agrave/ la <ets>poulaine</ets>.]</ety> <def>A long pointed shoe. See
<er>Cracowes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poulard</ent><br/
<hw>Pou*lard"</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/*l<aum/rd")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poularde</ets> pullet, fr. <ets>poule</ets> hen. See
<er>Pullet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A pullet from which the
ovaries have been removed to produce fattening; hence, a fat
pullet.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouldavis</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"da`vis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Poledavy</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poulder</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>Powder.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouldron</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pauldron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poulpe</ent><br/
<ent>Poulp</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Poulp</hw>, <hw>Poulpe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>poulpe</ets>, fr. L. <ets>polypus</ets>. See
<er>Polyp</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Octopus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Musk poulp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
Mediterranean octopod (<spn>Eledone moschata</spn>) which emits a strong
odor of musk.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poult</ent><br/
<hw>Poult</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>pulte</ets>,
F. <ets>poulet</ets>, dim. of <ets>poule</ets> fowl. See
<er>Pullet</er>.]</ety> <def>A young chicken, partridge, grouse, or the
like.</def> <rj><au>King. Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Starling the heath <qex>poults</qex> or black game.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Jefferise.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poulter</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>pulter</ets>. See <ets>Poult</ets>.]</ety> <def>A poulterer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poulterer</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who deals in
poultry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poultice</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"tice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>puls</ets>,
pl. <ets>pultes</ets>, a thick pap; akin to Gr. <grk>po`ltos</grk>. Cf.
<er>Pulse</er> seeds.]</ety> <def>A soft composition, as of bread, bran,
or a mucilaginous substance, to be applied to sores, inflamed parts of
the body, etc.; a cataplasm.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Poultice</xex> relaxeth
the pores.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poultice</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"tice</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Poulticed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poulticing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To apply a
poultice to; to dress with a poultice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poultive</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A poultice.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>W. Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poultry</ent><br/
<hw>Poul"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Poult</er>.]</ety> <def>Domestic fowls reared for the table, or for
their eggs or feathers, such as cocks and hens, capons, turkeys, ducks,
and geese.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pounce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>ponce</ets>
pumice, pounce, fr. L. <ets>pumex</ets>, <ets>-icis</ets>, pumice. See
<er>Pumice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fine powder, as of sandarac,
or cuttlefish bone, -- formerly used to prevent ink from spreading on
manuscript.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1122 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Charcoal dust, or some other colored powder for
making patterns through perforated designs, -- used by embroiderers,
lace makers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pounce box</b></col>, <cd>a box for sprinkling
pounce.</cd> -- <col><b>Pounce paper</b></col>, <cd>a transparent paper
for tracing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pounce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pounded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pouncing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To sprinkle or rub
with pounce; <as>as, to <ex>pounce</ex> paper, or a
pattern</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pounce</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. through French, from an
assumed LL. <ets>punctiare</ets> to prick, L. <ets>pungere</ets>,
<ets>punctum</ets>. See <er>Puncheon</er>, <er>Punch</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The claw or talon of a bird of
prey.</def> <rj><au>Spenser. Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A punch or stamp.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>pounce</xex> to print money with.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Withals.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Cloth worked in eyelet holes.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Homilies.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pounce</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike or seize
with the talons; to pierce, as with the talons.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Stooped from his highest pitch to <qex>pounce</qex> a wren.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>pounce</qex> him lightly,<br/
And as he roars and rages, let's go deeper.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Fletcher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To punch; to perforate; to stamp holes in, or dots
on, by way of ornament.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Elyot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pounce</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fall suddenly and seize with
the claws; -- with <xex>on</xex> or <xex>upon</xex>; <as>as, a hawk
<ex>pounces</ex> upon a chicken</as>. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Derision is never so agonizing as when it <qex>pounces</qex> on
the wanderings of misguided sensibility.</q>
<rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounced</ent><br/
<hw>Pounced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Furnished
with claws or talons; <as>as, the <ex>pounced</ex> young of the
eagle</as>.</def> <rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ornamented with perforations or dots.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Gilt bowls <xex>pounced</xex> and
pierced.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouncet box</ent><br/
<hw>Poun"cet box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>poncette</ets>,
fr. <ets>ponce</ets> pounce. See <er>Pounce</er> a powder.]</ety> <def>A
box with a perforated lid, for sprinkling pounce, or for holding
perfumes.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouncing</ent><br/
<hw>Poun"cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art
or practice of transferring a design by means of pounce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Decorative perforation of cloth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pound</ent><br/
<hw>Pound</hw> <pr>(pound)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pounded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pounding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pounen</ets>, AS.
<ets>punian</ets> to bruise. Cf. <er>Pun</er> a play on words.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike repeatedly with some heavy instrument; to
beat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With cruel blows she <qex>pounds</qex> her blubbered cheeks.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To comminute and pulverize by beating; to bruise or
break into fine particles with a pestle or other heavy instrument;
<as>as, to <ex>pound</ex> spice or salt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pound</ent><br/
<hw>Pound</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike heavy blows;
to beat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>To make a jarring noise, as in
running; <as>as, the engine <ex>pounds</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pound</ent><br/
<hw>Pound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>pund</ets> an inclosure:
cf. <ets>forpyndan</ets> to turn away, or to repress, also Icel.
<ets>pynda</ets> to extort, torment, Ir. <ets>pont</ets>, pond, pound.
Cf. <er>Pinder</er>, <er>Pinfold</er>, <er>Pin</er> to inclose,
<er>Pond</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An inclosure, maintained by
public authority, in which cattle or other animals are confined when
taken in trespassing, or when going at large in violation of law; a
pinfold.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A level stretch in a canal between locks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Fishing)</fld> <def>A kind of net, having a large
inclosure with a narrow entrance into which fish are directed by wings
spreading outward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pound covert</b></col>, <cd>a pound that is close or
covered over, as a shed.</cd> -- <col><b>Pound overt</b></col>, <cd>a
pound that is open overhead.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pound</ent><br/
<hw>Pound</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To confine in, or as in, a pound;
to impound.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pound</ent><br/
<hw>Pound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pounds</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>, collectively <plw>Pound</plw> or <plw>Pounds</plw>.</plu>
<ety>[AS. <ets>pund</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pondo</ets>, akin to
<ets>pondus</ets> a weight, <ets>pendere</ets> to weigh. See
<er>Pendant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A certain specified measure of
mass or weight; especially, a legal standard consisting of an
established number of ounces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The pound in general use in the <country>United
States</country> and in England is the <stype>pound avoirdupois</stype>,
which is divided into sixteen ounces, and contains 7,000 grains (0.453
kilogram). The <stype>pound troy</stype> is divided into twelve ounces,
and contains 5,760 grains. 144 pounds avoirdupois are equal to 175
pounds troy weight. See <er>Avoirdupois</er>, and
<er>Troy</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A British denomination of money of account,
equivalent to twenty shillings sterling, and equal in value to about
$4.86 in 1900 and $1.50 in 2002. The modern pound coin was introduced in
1983. Formerly there was a gold sovereign of the same value.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <ex>pound</ex> sterling was in Saxon times, about
<sc>a. d.</sc> 671, a <ex>pound</ex> troy of silver, and a shilling was
its twentieth part; consequently the latter was three times as large as
it is at present.</note> <rj><au>Peacham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundage</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sum
deducted from a pound, or a certain sum paid for each pound; a
commission.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A subsidy of twelve pence in the pound, formerly
granted to the crown on all goods exported or imported, and if by
aliens, more.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The sum allowed to a sheriff or
other officer upon the amount realized by an execution; -- estimated in
England, and formerly in the United States, at so much of the
pound.</def> <rj><au>Burrill. Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundage</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"age</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To collect, as poundage; to
assess, or rate, by poundage.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundage</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 3d <er>Pound</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Confinement of cattle, or other animals, in a public
pound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A charge paid for the release of impounded
cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundal</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From 5th
<er>Pound</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics & Mech.)</fld> <def>A unit of force
based upon the pound, foot, and second, being the force which, acting on
a pound avoirdupois for one second, causes it to acquire by the of that
time a velocity of one foot per second. It is about equal to the weight
of half an ounce, and is 13,825 dynes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pound-breach</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"-breach`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The breaking of
a public pound for releasing impounded animals.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundcake</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"cake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of rich,
sweet cake; -- so called from the ingredients being used by pounds, or
in equal quantities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounder</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, pounds, as a stamp in an ore mill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument used for pounding; a pestle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A person or thing, so called with reference to a
certain number of pounds in value, weight, capacity, etc.; <as>as, a
cannon carrying a twelve-pound ball is called a twelve
<ex>pounder</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Before the English reform act of 1867, one who was an
elector by virtue of paying ten pounds rent was called a ten
<xex>pounder</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pounding</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of beating, bruising, or breaking up; a beating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pounded or pulverized substance.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Covered with the <xex>poundings</xex> of these
rocks.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. S. Blackie.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundkeeper</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"keep`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The keeper of a
pound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poundrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pound"*rate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rate or
proportion estimated at a certain amount for each pound;
poundage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poup</ent><br/
<hw>Poup</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Powp</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poupart's ligament</ent><br/
<hw>Pou*part's" lig"a*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
ligament, of fascia, extending, in most mammals, from the ventral side
of the ilium to near the symphysis of the pubic bones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poupeton</ent><br/
<hw>Pou"pe*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Puppet</er>.]</ety> <def>A puppet, or little baby.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Palsgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pour</ent><br/
<hw>Pour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Poor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pour</ent><br/
<hw>Pour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To pore.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pour</ent><br/
<hw>Pour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Poured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pouring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pouren</ets>, of
uncertain origin; cf. W. <ets>bwrw</ets> to cast, throw, shed, <ets>bwrw
gwlaw</ets> to rain.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to flow in a
stream, as a liquid or anything flowing like a liquid, either out of a
vessel or into it; <as>as, to <ex>pour</ex> water from a pail; to
<ex>pour</ex> wine into a decanter; to <ex>pour</ex> oil upon the
waters; to <ex>pour</ex> out sand or dust.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To send forth as in a stream or a flood; to emit; to
let escape freely or wholly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I . . . have <qex>poured</qex> out my soul before the Lord.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Sam. i. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now will I shortly <qex>pour</qex> out my fury upon thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. vii. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>London doth <qex>pour</qex> out her citizens !</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherefore did Nature <qex>pour</qex> her bounties forth<br/
With such a full and unwithdrawing hand ?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To send forth from, as in a stream; to discharge
uninterruptedly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is it for thee the linnet <qex>pours</qex> his throat ?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pour</ent><br/
<hw>Pour</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To flow, pass, or issue in a
stream, or as a stream; to fall continuously and abundantly; <as>as, the
rain <ex>pours</ex>; the people <ex>poured</ex> out of the
theater.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the rude throng <qex>pour</qex> on with furious pace.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pour</ent><br/
<hw>Pour</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stream, or something like a stream;
a flood.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>pour</xex> of
rain.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Miss Ferrier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poureliche</ent><br/
<hw>Poure"liche`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Poorly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourer</ent><br/
<hw>Pour"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pours.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourlieu</ent><br/
<hw>Pour"lieu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Purlieu</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourparler</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pour`par`ler"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Diplomacy)</fld> <def>A consultation preliminary to a
treaty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourparty</ent><br/
<hw>Pour`par"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pourparties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See
<er>Purparty</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A division; a divided
share.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To make pourparty</b></col>, <cd>to divide and apportion
lands previously held in common.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourpoint</ent><br/
<hw>Pour"point</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
quilted military doublet or gambeson worn in the 14th and 15th
centuries; also, a name for the doublet of the 16th and 17th centuries
worn by civilians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourpresture</ent><br/
<hw>Pour*pres"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <def>See <er>Purpresture</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poursuivant</ent><br/
<hw>Pour"sui*vant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pursuivant</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourtray</ent><br/
<hw>Pour*tray"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Portray</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pourveyance</ent><br/
<hw>Pour*vey"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Purveyance</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pousse</ent><br/
<hw>Pousse</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pulse;
pease.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pousse-cafe</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pousse"-ca`f<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. <ets>pousser</ets> to push + <ets>caf<eacute/</ets> coffee.]</ety>
<def>A drink served after coffee at dinner, usually one of several
liqueurs, or cordials, of different specific gravities poured so as to
remain separate in layers; hence, such a drink of cordials served at any
time.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poussette</ent><br/
<hw>Pous*sette"</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/*s<ecr/t")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F., pushpin, fr. <ets>pousser</ets> to push. See
<er>Push</er>.]</ety> <def>A movement, or part of a figure, in the
contradance.</def> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poussette</ent><br/
<hw>Pous*sette"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perform a certain
movement in a dance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Down the middle, up again, <qex>poussette</qex>, and cross.</q>
<rj><qau>J. & H. Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pou sto</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pou sto</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/ st<omac/; pou st<omac/)</pr>.
<ety>[Gr. <grk>poy^ stw^</grk> where I may stand; -- from the reputed
saying of Archimedes, <ldquo/Give me where I may stand and I will move
the whole world with my steelyard.<rdquo/]</ety> <def>A place to stand
upon; a locus standi; hence, a foundation or basis for
operations.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pout</ent><br/
<hw>Pout</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poulet</ets>. See <er>Poult</er>.]</ety> <def>The young of some
birds, as grouse; a young fowl.</def> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pout</ent><br/
<hw>Pout</hw> <pr>(p<oomac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To shoot
pouts.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pout</ent><br/
<hw>Pout</hw> <pr>(pout)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pouted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pouting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pouten</ets>, of
uncertain origin; cf. Prov. <ets>pot</ets> lip, Prov. F.
<ets>potte</ets>, faire la <ets>potte</ets> to pout, W. <ets>pwdu</ets>
to pout, be sullen, <ets>poten</ets>, <ets>potten</ets>, a paunch,
belly.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust out the lips, as in sullenness
or displeasure; hence, to look sullen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>poutest</qex> upon thy fortune and thy love.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To protrude.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pouting</xex>
lips.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pout</ent><br/
<hw>Pout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sullen protrusion of the lips; a fit
of sullenness.</def> <ldquo/Jack's in the <xex>pouts</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>J. & H. Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pout</ent><br/
<hw>Pout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Eelpout</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European whiting pout or bib.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Eel pout</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Eelpout</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Horn pout</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Horned pout</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Bullhead</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouter</ent><br/
<hw>Pout"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, pouts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Cf. E. <ets>pout</ets>, and G. <ets>puter</ets>
turkey.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A variety of the domestic pigeon
remarkable for the extent to which it is able to dilate its throat and
breast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pouting</ent><br/
<hw>Pout"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Childish sullenness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poutingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pout"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pouting, or a sullen,
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Povert</ent><br/
<hw>Pov"ert</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/v"<etil/rt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Poverty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poverty</ent><br/
<hw>Pov"er*ty</hw> <pr>(p<ocr/v"<etil/r*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>poverte</ets>, OF. <ets>povert<eacute/</ets>, F.
<ets>pauvret<eacute/</ets>, fr. L. <ets>paupertas</ets>, fr.
<ets>pauper</ets> poor. See <er>Poor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being poor or indigent; want or scarcity of means of
subsistence; indigence; need.</def> <ldquo/Swathed in numblest
<xex>poverty</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The drunkard and the glutton shall come to <qex>poverty</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xxiii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any deficiency of elements or resources that are
needed or desired, or that constitute richness; <as>as, <ex>poverty</ex>
of soil; <ex>poverty</ex> of the blood; <ex>poverty</ex> of
ideas.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poverty grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name
given to several slender grasses (as <spn>Aristida dichotoma</spn>, and
<spn>Danthonia spicata</spn>) which often spring up on old and worn-out
fields.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Indigence; penury; beggary; need; lack; want;
scantiness; sparingness; meagerness; jejuneness.</syn> <usage>
<er>Poverty</er>, <er>Indigence</er>, <er>Pauperism</er>.
<xex>Poverty</xex> is a relative term; what is <xex>poverty</xex> to a
monarch, would be competence for a day laborer. <xex>Indigence</xex>
implies extreme distress, and almost absolute destitution.
<xex>Pauperism</xex> denotes entire dependence upon public charity, and,
therefore, often a hopeless and degraded state.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powen</ent><br/
<ent>Powan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pow"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pow"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small British lake
whitefish (<spn>Coregonus clupeoides</spn>, or <spn>Coregonus
ferus</spn>); -- called also <altname>gwyniad</altname> and
<altname>lake herring</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powder</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>poudre</ets>, <ets>pouldre</ets>, F. <ets>poudre</ets>, OF. also
<ets>poldre</ets>, <ets>puldre</ets>, L. <ets>pulvis</ets>,
<ets>pulveris</ets>: cf. <ets>pollen</ets> fine flour, mill dust, E.
<ets>pollen</ets>. Cf. <er>Polverine</er>, <er>Pulverize</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The fine particles to which any dry substance is
reduced by pounding, grinding, or triturating, or into which it falls by
decay; dust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grind their bones to <qex>powder</qex> small.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An explosive mixture used in gunnery, blasting,
etc.; gunpowder. See <er>Gunpowder</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Atlas powder</b></col>, <col><b>Baking
powder</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Atlas</er>,
<er>Baking</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder down</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the peculiar dust, or exfoliation, of
powder-down feathers.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder-down feather</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one of a peculiar kind of modified feathers
which sometimes form patches on certain parts of some birds. They have a
greasy texture and a scaly exfoliation.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder-down
patch</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a tuft or patch of powder-down
feathers.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder hose</b></col>, <cd>a tube of strong
linen, about an inch in diameter, filled with powder and used in firing
mines.</cd> <au>Farrow.</au> -- <col><b>Powder hoy</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a vessel specially fitted to carry powder for
the supply of war ships. They are usually painted red and carry a red
flag.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Powder magazine</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Powder room</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <er>Magazine</er>, 2.</cd>
-- <col><b>Powder mine</b></col>, <cd>a mine exploded by gunpowder. See
<er>Mine</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder monkey</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a boy formerly employed on war vessels to carry
powder; a powder boy.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder post</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Dry rot</cref>, under <er>Dry</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder
puff</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Puff</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powder</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Powdered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Powdering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>poudrer</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To reduce to fine particles; to pound, grind, or rub
into a powder; to comminute; to pulverize; to triturate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sprinkle with powder, or as with powder; to be
sprinkle; <as>as, to <ex>powder</ex> the hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A circling zone thou seest<br/
<qex>Powdered</qex> with stars.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To sprinkle with salt; to corn, as meat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powder</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be reduced to
powder; to become like powder; <as>as, some salts <ex>powder</ex>
easily</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To use powder on the hair or skin; <as>as, she
paints and <ex>powders</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powdered</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"dered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Reduced
to a powder; sprinkled with, or as with, powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sprinkled with salt; salted; corned.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Powdered</qex> beef, pickled meats.</q>
<rj><qau>Harvey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Sem<eacute/</er>.</def> <rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powderflask</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der*flask`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A flask in
which gunpowder is carried, having a charging tube at the end.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powderhorn</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der*horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A horn in which
gunpowder is carried.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powdering</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der*ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Powder</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Powdering tub</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A tub or vessel
in which meat is corned or salted</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A heated tub in
which an infected lecher was placed for cure.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powdermill</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der*mill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mill in which
gunpowder is made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powder-posted</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der-post`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
dry rot; reduced to dust by rot. See <cref>Dry rot</cref>, under
<er>Dry</er>.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powdery</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"der*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Easily
crumbling to pieces; friable; loose; <as>as, a <ex>powdery</ex>
spar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sprinkled or covered with powder; dusty; <as>as, the
<ex>powdery</ex> bloom on plums</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Resembling powder; consisting of powder.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>powdery</xex> snow.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powdike</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"dike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot.
<ets>pow</ets>, <ets>pou</ets>, a pool, a watery or marshy place, fr. E.
<ets>pool</ets>.]</ety> <def>A dike a marsh or fen.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powdry</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Powdery</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Power</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Poor</er>, the fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Power</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pouer</ets>,
<ets>poer</ets>, OF. <ets>poeir</ets>, <ets>pooir</ets>, F.
<ets>pouvoir</ets>, n. & v., fr. LL. <ets>potere</ets>, for L.
<ets>posse</ets>, <ets>potesse</ets>, to be able, to have power. See
<er>Possible</er>, <er>Potent</er>, and cf. <er>Posse
comitatus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Ability to act, regarded as
latent or inherent; the faculty of doing or performing something;
capacity for action or performance; capability of producing an effect,
whether physical or moral: potency; might; <as>as, a man of great
<ex>power</ex>; the <ex>power</ex> of capillary attraction; money gives
<ex>power</ex>.</as></def> <ldquo/One next himself in <xex>power</xex>,
and next in crime.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ability, regarded as put forth or exerted; strength,
force, or energy in action; <as>as, the <ex>power</ex> of steam in
moving an engine; the <ex>power</ex> of truth, or of argument, in
producing conviction; the <ex>power</ex> of enthusiasm.</as></def>
<ldquo/The <xex>power</xex> of fancy.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Capacity of undergoing or suffering; fitness to be
acted upon; susceptibility; -- called also <altname>passive
power</altname>; <as>as, great <ex>power</ex> of
endurance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Power</qex>, then, is active and passive; faculty is active
<qex>power</qex> or capacity; capacity is passive <qex>power</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The exercise of a faculty; the employment of
strength; the exercise of any kind of control; influence; dominion;
sway; command; government.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Power</qex> is no blessing in itself but when it is employed
to protect the innocent.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The agent exercising an ability to act; an
individual invested with authority; an institution, or government, which
exercises control; <as>as, the great <ex>powers</ex> of Europe</as>;
hence, often, a superhuman agent; a spirit; a divinity.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>powers</xex> of darkness.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the <qex>powers</qex> of the heavens shall be shaken.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xxiv. 29.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A military or naval force; an army or navy; a great
host.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never such a <qex>power</qex> . . . <br/
Was levied in the body of a land.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1123 --></p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A large quantity; a great number; <as>as, a
<ex>power</ex> o<?/ good things</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Richardson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The rate at which
mechanical energy is exerted or mechanical work performed, as by an
engine or other machine, or an animal, working continuously; <as>as, an
engine of twenty horse <ex>power</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The English unit of power used most commonly is the
<xex>horse power</xex>. See <er>Horse power</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A mechanical agent; that from which useful
mechanical energy is derived; <as>as, water <ex>power</ex>; steam
<ex>power</ex>; hand <ex>power</ex>, etc.</as></def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>Applied force; force producing motion or pressure; as, the
<xex>power</xex> applied at one and of a lever to lift a weight at the
other end.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This use in mechanics, of <xex>power</xex> as a synonym
for <xex>force</xex>, is improper and is becoming obsolete.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>A machine acted upon by an animal, and serving as a
motor to drive other machinery; <as>as, a dog
<ex>power</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Power</xex> is used adjectively, denoting, driven,
or adapted to be driven, by machinery, and not actuated directly by the
hand or foot; as, a <xex>power</xex> lathe; a <xex>power</xex> loom; a
<xex>power</xex> press.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The product arising from the
multiplication of a number into itself; <as>as, a square is the second
<ex>power</ex>, and a cube is third <ex>power</ex>, of a
number</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Mental or moral ability to
act; one of the faculties which are possessed by the mind or soul;
<as>as, the <ex>power</ex> of thinking, reasoning, judging, willing,
fearing, hoping, etc.</as></def> <rj><au>I. Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The guiltiness of my mind, the sudden surprise of my
<qex>powers</qex>, drove the grossness . . . into a received belief.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Optics)</fld> <def>The degree to which a lens,
mirror, or any optical instrument, magnifies; in the telescope, and
usually in the microscope, the number of times it multiplies, or
augments, the apparent diameter of an object; sometimes, in microscopes,
the number of times it multiplies the apparent surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An authority enabling a person to
dispose of an interest vested either in himself or in another person;
ownership by appointment.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>Hence, vested authority to act in a given case;
<as>as, the business was referred to a committee with
<ex>power</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Power</xex> may be predicated of inanimate agents,
like the winds and waves, electricity and magnetism, gravitation, etc.,
or of animal and intelligent beings; and when predicated of these
beings, it may indicate physical, mental, or moral ability or
capacity.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Mechanical powers</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Mechanical</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Power loom</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Power press</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See Def. 8
<sd>(d)</sd>, note.</cd> -- <col><b>Power of attorney</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Attorney</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Power of a point</b></col>
(relative to a given curve) <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>the result of
substituting the coordinates of any point in that expression which being
put equal to zero forms the equation of the curve; as,
<mathex>x<exp>2</exp> + y<exp>2</exp> - 100</mathex> is the <xex>power
of the point</xex> <it>x, y</it>, relative to the circle
<mathex>x<exp>2</exp> + y<exp>2</exp> - 100 = 0</mathex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powerable</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"er*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being effected or accomplished by the application of
power; possible.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>J. Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of exerting power; powerful.</def>
<rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>power-dive</ent><br/
<ent>power dive</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>power-dive</hw>, <hw>power dive</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>An airplane maneuver in which the pilot deads downward at a steep
angle while the engines are operating at or near full power; -- a method
to descend rapidly.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>power-dive</ent><br/
<ent>power dive</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>power-dive</hw>, <hw>power dive</hw></mhw> <pos>v. i.</pos>
<def>To make a power dive, as of an airplane.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>power-dive</ent><br/
<ent>power dive</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>power-dive</hw>, <hw>power dive</hw></mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To cause (an airplane) to make a power dive; <as>as, he
<ex>power-dived</ex> his plane to escape the attackers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powerful</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"er*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
power; capable of producing great effects of any kind; potent; mighty;
efficacious; intense; <as>as, a <ex>powerful</ex> man or beast; a
<ex>powerful</ex> engine; a <ex>powerful</ex> argument; a
<ex>powerful</ex> light; a <ex>powerful</ex> vessel.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>powerful</qex> grace that lies<br/
In herbs, plants, stones, and their true qualities.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Large; capacious; -- said of
veins of ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Mighty; strong; potent; forcible; efficacious;
energetic; intense.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pow"er*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pow"er*ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powerless</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"er*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of power, force, or
energy; weak; impotent; not able to produce any effect.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pow"er*less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pow"er*less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powldron</ent><br/
<hw>Powl"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espauleron</ets>, from <ets>espaule</ets> shoulder, F.
<ets><eacute/paule</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Pauldron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powp</ent><br/
<hw>Powp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See <er>Poop</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powter</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"ter</hw> <pr>(pou"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Pouter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powwow</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"wow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A priest,
or conjurer, among the North American Indians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be it sagamore, sachem, or <qex>powwow</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Conjuration attended with great noise and confusion,
and often with feasting, dancing, etc., performed by Indians for the
cure of diseases, to procure success in hunting or in war, and for other
purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Any assembly characterized
by noise and confusion; a noisy frolic or gathering.</def>
<mark>[Archaic, formerly Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any meeting assembled to discuss an issue; a
parley.</def> <mark>[Informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Powwow</ent><br/
<hw>Pow"wow`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use conjuration,
with noise and confusion, for the cure of disease, etc., as among the
North American Indians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To hold a noisy, disorderly
meeting.</def> <mark>[Archaic, formerly Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hold a meeting to discuss an issue.</def>
<mark>[Informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pox</ent><br/
<hw>Pox</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>pocks</ets>, OE.
<ets>pokkes</ets>. See <er>Pock</er>. It is plural in form but is used
as a singular.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Strictly, a disease by
pustules or eruptions of any kind, but chiefly or wholly restricted to
three or four diseases, -- the smallpox, the chicken pox, and the
vaccine and the venereal diseases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Pox</xex>, when used without an epithet, as in
imprecations, formerly signified <xex>smallpox</xex>; but it now
signifies <xex>syphilis</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pox</ent><br/
<hw>Pox</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Poxed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Poxing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To infect with the pox, or
syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poy</ent><br/
<hw>Poy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>apui</ets>,
<ets>apoi</ets>, a support, prop., staff, F. <ets>appui</ets>, fr. OF.
<ets>apuier</ets>, <ets>apoier</ets>, to support, F. <ets>appuyer</ets>,
fr. <ets><agrave/</ets> to (L. <ets>ad</ets>) + OF. <ets>pui</ets>,
<ets>poi</ets>, a rising ground, hill, L. <ets>podium</ets>. See
<er>Podium</er>, <er>Pew</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A support; --
used in composition; <as>as, tea<ex>poy</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A ropedancer's balancing pole.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A long boat hook by which barges are propelled
against the stream.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poynado</ent><br/
<hw>Poy*na"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A poniard.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Lyly.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poynder</ent><br/
<ent>Poynd</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Poynd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v.</pos>, <hw>Poynd"er</hw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></mhw> <def>See <er>Poind</er>,
<er>Poinder</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poy nette</ent><br/
<hw>Poy nette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Point</er>.]</ety> <def>A bodkin.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poyntel</ent><br/
<hw>Poyn"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pointal</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Paving or flooring made
of small squares or lozenges set diagonally.</def> <altsp>[Formerly
written <asp>pointal</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poyou</ent><br/
<hw>Poy"ou</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
South American armadillo (<spn>Dasypus sexcinctus</spn>). Called also
<altname>sixbanded armadillo</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Poze</ent><br/
<hw>Poze</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See 5th
<er>Pose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pozzolana</ent><br/
<ent>Pozzuolana</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Poz`zu*o*la"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Poz`zo*la"*na</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety> <def>Volcanic ashes
from Pozzuoli, in Italy, used in the manufacture of a kind of mortar
which hardens under water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praam</ent><br/
<hw>Praam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>praam</ets>;
cf. G. <ets>prahm</ets>, F. <ets>prame</ets>; all of Slavonic origin,
from a word akin to E. <ets>fare</ets>. See <er>Fare</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A flat-bottomed boat or lighter, -- used in
Holland and the Baltic, and sometimes armed in case of war.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pram</asp>, and <asp>prame</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practic</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Practical</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Practical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Artful; deceitful; skillful.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Cunning sleights and <xex>practick</xex>
knavery.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practicability</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*ca*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being practicable; practicableness;
feasibility.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>practicability</xex> of such a
project.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Stewart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practicable</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*ca*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>practicare</ets> to act, transact, fr. L. <ets>practicus</ets>
active, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>practicable</ets>, <ets>pratiquer</ets> to
practice. See <er>Practical</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That may be
practiced or performed; capable of being done or accomplished with
available means or resources; feasible; <as>as, a <ex>practicable</ex>
method; a <ex>practicable</ex> aim; a <ex>practicable</ex>
good.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being used; passable; <as>as, a
<ex>practicable</ex> weapon; a <ex>practicable</ex> road.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Practicable breach</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a
breach which admits of approach and entrance by an assailing
party.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Possible; feasible. -- <er>Practicable</er>,
<er>Possible</er>. A thing may be <xex>possible</xex>, i. e., not
forbidden by any law of nature, and yet may not now be
<xex>practicable</xex> for want of the means requisite to its
performance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Prac"ti*ca*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Prac"ti*ca*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practical</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>practicus</ets> active, Gr. <?/ fit for doing or performing,
practical, active, fr. <?/ to do, work, effect: cf. F.
<ets>pratique</ets>, formerly also <ets>practique</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pragmatic</er>, <er>Practice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to practice or action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being turned to use or account; useful,
in distinction from <contr>ideal</contr> or <contr>theoretical</contr>;
<as>as, <ex>practical</ex> chemistry</as>.</def> <ldquo/Man's
<xex>practical</xex> understanding.<rdquo/ <au>South.</au> <ldquo/For
all <xex>practical</xex> purposes.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Evincing practice or skill; capable of applying
knowledge to some useful end; <as>as, a <ex>practical</ex> man; a
<ex>practical</ex> mind.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Derived from practice; <as>as, <ex>practical</ex>
skill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Practical joke</b></col>, <cd>a joke put in practice; a
joke the fun of which consists in something done, in distinction from
something said; esp., a trick played upon a person.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practicality</ent><br/
<hw>Prac`ti*cal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being practical; practicalness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practicalize</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*cal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render
practical.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Practicalizing</xex>
influences.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. S. Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practically</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*cal*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>In a practical way; not theoretically; really; <as>as, to look at
things <ex>practically</ex>; <ex>practically</ex>
worthless.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>By means of practice or use; by experience or
experiment; <as>as, <ex>practically</ex> wise or skillful;
<ex>practically</ex> acquainted with a subject.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In practice or use; <as>as, a medicine
<ex>practically</ex> safe; theoretically wrong, but <ex>practically</ex>
right.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Almost.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practicalness</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*cal*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Practicality</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practice</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>praktike</ets>, <ets>practique</ets>, F. <ets>pratique</ets>,
formerly also, <ets>practique</ets>, LL. <ets>practica</ets>, fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ practical. See <er>Practical</er>, and cf.
<er>Pratique</er>, <er>Pretty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Frequently
repeated or customary action; habitual performance; a succession of acts
of a similar kind; usage; habit; custom; <as>as, the <ex>practice</ex>
of rising early; the <ex>practice</ex> of making regular entries of
accounts; the <ex>practice</ex> of daily exercise.</as></def> <-- also
commonly practise --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A heart . . . exercised with covetous <qex>practices</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Pet. ii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Customary or constant use; state of being
used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Obsolete words may be revived when they are more sounding or more
significant than those in <qex>practice</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Skill or dexterity acquired by use;
expertness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/His nice fence and his active
<xex>practice</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Actual performance; application of knowledge; --
opposed to <xex>theory</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are two functions of the soul, -- contemplation and
<qex>practice</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a distinction, but no opposition, between theory and
<qex>practice</qex>; each, to a certain extent, supposes the other;
theory is dependent on <qex>practice</qex>; <qex>practice</qex> must
have preceded theory.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Systematic exercise for instruction or discipline;
<as>as, the troops are called out for <ex>practice</ex>; she neglected
<ex>practice</ex> in music.</as></def> <-- practice makes perfect. MW10
2a. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Application of science to the wants of men; the
exercise of any profession; professional business; <as>as, the
<ex>practice</ex> of medicine or law; a large or lucrative
<ex>practice</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Practice</qex> is exercise of an art, or the application of a
science in life, which application is itself an art.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Skillful or artful management; dexterity in
contrivance or the use of means; art; stratagem; artifice; plot; --
usually in a bad sense.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He sought to have that by <qex>practice</qex> which he could not
by prayer.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A easy and concise method of
applying the rules of arithmetic to questions which occur in trade and
business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The form, manner, and order of
conducting and carrying on suits and prosecutions through their various
stages, according to the principles of law and the rules laid down by
the courts.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Custom; usage; habit; manner.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practice</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Practiced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Practicing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Often
written <ets>practise</ets>, <ets>practised</ets>,
<ets>practising</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To do or perform
frequently, customarily, or habitually; to make a practice of; <as>as,
to <ex>practice</ex> gaming</as>.</def> <ldquo/Incline not my heart . .
. <xex>practice</xex> wicked works.<rdquo/ <-- also commonly practise
--> <rj><au>Ps. cxli. 4.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exercise, or follow, as a profession, trade, art,
etc., <as>as, to <ex>practice</ex> law or medicine</as>.</def><-- = MW10
1c. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exercise one's self in, for instruction or
improvement, or to acquire discipline or dexterity; <as>as, to
<ex>practice</ex> gunnery; to <ex>practice</ex> music.</as></def> <-- =
MW10 2a --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To put into practice; to carry out; to act upon; to
commit; to execute; to do.</def> <ldquo/Aught but Talbot's shadow
whereon to <xex>practice</xex> your severity.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As this advice ye <qex>practice</qex> or neglect.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To make use of; to employ.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In malice to this good knight's wife, I <qex>practiced</qex>
Ubaldo and Ricardo to corrupt her.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To teach or accustom by practice; to
train.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In church they are taught to love God; after church they are
<qex>practiced</qex> to love their neighbor.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practice</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tice</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Often written
<ets>practise</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To perform certain acts
frequently or customarily, either for instruction, profit, or amusement;
<as>as, to <ex>practice</ex> with the broadsword or with the rifle; to
<ex>practice</ex> on the piano.</as></def> <-- also commonly
<asp>practise</asp> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To learn by practice; to form a habit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They shall <qex>practice</qex> how to live secure.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Practice</qex> first over yourself to reign.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To try artifices or stratagems.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He will <qex>practice</qex> against thee by poison.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To apply theoretical science or knowledge, esp. by
way of experiment; to exercise or pursue an employment or profession,
esp. that of medicine or of law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[I am] little inclined to <qex>practice</qex> on others, and as
little that others should <qex>practice</qex> on me.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practiced</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ticed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Often written
<ets>practised</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Experienced; expert;
skilled; <as>as, a <ex>practiced</ex> marksman</as>.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>practiced</xex> picklock.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Used habitually; learned by practice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practicer</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Often written
<ets>practiser</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who practices, or puts
in practice; one who customarily performs certain acts.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who exercises a profession; a
practitioner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who uses art or stratagem.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practician</ent><br/
<hw>Prac*ti"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>praticien</ets>, OF. also <ets>practicien</ets>.]</ety> <def>One
who is acquainted with, or skilled in, anything by practice; a
practitioner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practick</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Practice.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practico</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prac"ti*co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl.
<plw>Practicos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <ety>[Sp., lit., experienced,
skilled. Cf. <er>Practical</er>.]</ety> <def>A guide.</def> <mark>[Cuba
& Phil. Islands]</mark> <rj><au>D. C. Worcester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practisant</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*sant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An agent or
confederate in treachery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practise</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Practice</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The analogy of the English language requires that the
noun and verb which are pronounced alike should agree in spelling. Thus
we have <xex>notice</xex> (n. & v.), <xex>noticed</xex>,
<xex>noticing</xex>, <xex>noticer</xex>; <xex>poultice</xex> (n. & v.);
<xex>apprentice</xex> (n. & v.); <xex>office</xex> (n. & v.),
<xex>officer</xex> (n.); <xex>lattice</xex> (n.), <xex>latticed</xex>
(a.); <xex>benefice</xex> (n.), <xex>beneficed</xex> (a.), etc. Cf.
<xex>sacrifice</xex> (<?/; n. & v.), <xex>surmise</xex> (<?/; n. & v.),
<xex>promise</xex> (<?/; n. & v.); <xex>compromise</xex> (<?/; n. & v.),
etc. Contrast <xex>advice</xex> (<?/; n.), and <xex>advise</xex> (<?/);
<xex>device</xex> (<?/), and <xex>devise</xex> (<?/), etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practisour</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"ti*sour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
practitioner.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practitioner</ent><br/
<hw>Prac*ti"tion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Practician</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is engaged in the
actual use or exercise of any art or profession, particularly that of
law or medicine.</def> <rj><au>Crabbe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who does anything customarily or
habitually.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sly or artful person.</def>
<rj><au>Whitgift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>General practitioner</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>General</er>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Practive</ent><br/
<hw>Prac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Doing; active.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Sylvester.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Prac"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The preacher and the people both,<br/
Then <qex>practively</qex> did thrive.</q>
<rj><qau>Warner.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prad</ent><br/
<hw>Prad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>paard</ets>.]</ety> <def>A horse.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prae-</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A prefix. See
<er>Pre-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecava</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/"ca`va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and 1st <er>Cave</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
superior vena cava.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pr<ae/"ca`val</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecipe</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/c"i*pe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., imperative
of <ets>praecipere</ets> to give rules or precepts. See
<er>Precept</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A writ
commanding something to be done, or requiring a reason for neglecting
it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A paper containing the particulars of a
writ, lodged in the office out of which the writ is to be issued.</def>
<rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecoces</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/"co*ces</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Precocious</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of birds
including those whose young are able to run about when first
hatched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecocial</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*co"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Pr<ae/coces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecognita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/*cog"ni*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecognitus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praecognoscere</ets> to foreknow.
See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Cognition</er>.]</ety> <def>This previously
known, or which should be known in order to understand something
else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecommissure</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*com"mis*sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pr<ae/ + commissure</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
transverse commissure in the anterior part of the third ventricle of the
brain; the anterior cerebral commissure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecoracoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*cor"a*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>See <er>Precoracoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecordia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/*cor"di*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>cor</ets>, <ets>cordis</ets>, the
heart.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The front part of the thoracic
region; the epigastrium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecordial</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*cor"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Precordial</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praecornu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/*cor"nu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pr<ae/cornua</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Cornu</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The anterior horn of each lateral ventricle of the brain.</def>
<rj><au>B. G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praedial</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Predial</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praefloration</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/`flo*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Prefloration</er>.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1124 --></p>

<p><ent>Praefoliation</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*fo`li*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Prefoliation</er>.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praemaxilla</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/`max*il"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Premaxilla</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praemolar</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*mo"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Premolar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praemorse</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*morse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Premorse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praemunire</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/m`u*ni"re</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted
from L. <ets>praemonere</ets> to forewarn, cite. See
<er>Admonish</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
offense of introducing foreign authority into England, the penalties for
which were originally intended to depress the civil power of the pope in
the kingdom.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The writ grounded on that
offense.</def> <au>Wharton.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The penalty ascribed
for the offense of <xex>pr<ae/munire</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wolsey incurred a <qex>pr<ae/munire</qex>, and forfeited his
honor, estate, and life.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The penalties of <xex>pr<ae/munire</xex> were
subsequently applied to many other offenses; but prosecutions upon a
<xex>pr<ae/munire</xex> are at this day unheard of in the English
courts.</note> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praemunire</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/m`u*ni"re</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To subject
to the penalties of pr<ae/munire.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T.
Ward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praemunitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*mu"ni*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Premunitory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praenares</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/*na"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pre-</er>, <er>Nares</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
anterior nares. See <er>Nares</er>.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praenasal</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*na"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Prenasal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praenomen</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*no"men</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pr<ae/nomina</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>nomen</ets> name.]</ety> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>The first name of a person, by which individuals of
the same family were distinguished, answering to our <xex>Christian
name</xex>, as Caius, Lucius, Marcus, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praenominical</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/`no*min"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a pr<ae/nomen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>M. A.
Lower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praeoperculum</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/`o*per"cu*lum</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Preoperculum</er>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pr<ae/`o*per"cu*lar</wf>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praesternum</ent><br/
<ent>Praescutum</ent><br/
<ent>Praescapula</ent><br/
<ent>Praepubis</ent><br/
<ent>Praeoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*o"ral</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Pr<ae/*pu"bis</hw>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Pr<ae/*scap"u*la</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<hw>Pr<ae/*scu"tum</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Pr<ae/*ster"num</hw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Preoral</er>, <er>Prepubis</er>,
<er>Prescapula</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praeter-</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/"ter-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A prefix. See
<er>Preter-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praeterist</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/t"er*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Theol.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Preterist</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praetermit</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/`ter*mit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pretermit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praetexta</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*tex"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pr<ae/text<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pr<ae/textas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. (sc. <ets>toga</ets>), fr.
<ets>praetextus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praetexere</ets> to weave before,
to fringe, border; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>texere</ets> to
weave.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A white robe with a purple
border, worn by a Roman boy before he was entitled to wear the <xex>toga
virilis</xex>, or until about the completion of his fourteenth year, and
by girls until their marriage. It was also worn by magistrates and
priests.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praetor</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pretor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praetores</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/*to"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pretor</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
butterflies including the satyrs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praetorian</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*to"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pretorian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praetorium</ent><br/
<hw>Pr<ae/*to"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pretorium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praezygapophysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<ae/*zyg`a*poph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Prezygapophysis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmatical</ent><br/
<ent>Pragmatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prag*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Prag*mat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pragmaticus</ets>
busy, active, skilled in business, especially in law and state affairs,
systematic, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ a thing done, business, fr. <?/ to do: cf.
F. <ets>pragmatique</ets>. See <er>Practical</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to business or to affairs; of the nature of
business; practical; material; businesslike in habit or
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The next day . . . I began to be very <qex>pragmatical</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We can not always be contemplative, diligent, or
<qex>pragmatical</qex>, abroad; but have need of some delightful
intermissions.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Low, <qex>pragmatical</qex>, earthly views of the gospel.</q>
<rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Busy; specifically, busy in an objectionable way;
officious; fussy and positive; meddlesome.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Pragmatical</xex> officers of justice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fellow grew so <qex>pragmatical</qex> that he took upon him
the government of my whole family.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Philosophical; dealing with causes, reasons, and
effects, rather than with details and circumstances; -- said of
literature.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pragmatic</xex> history.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Pragmatic</xex> poetry.<rdquo/ <au>M.
Arnold.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pragmatic sanction</b></col>, <cd>a solemn ordinance or
decree issued by the head or legislature of a state upon weighty
matters; -- a term derived from the Byzantine empire. In European
history, two decrees under this name are particularly celebrated. One of
these, issued by Charles VII. of France, <sc>A. D.</sc> 1438, was the
foundation of the liberties of the Gallican church; the other, issued by
Charles VI. of Germany, <sc>A. D.</sc> 1724, settled his hereditary
dominions on his eldest daughter, the Archduchess Maria
Theresa.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Prag*mat"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One skilled in
affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My attorney and solicitor too; a fine <qex>pragmatic</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A solemn public ordinance or decree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A royal <qex>pragmatic</qex> was accordingly passed.</q>
<rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmatically</ent><br/
<hw>Prag*mat"ic*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
pragmatical manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmaticalness</ent><br/
<hw>Prag*mat"ic*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being pragmatical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmatism</ent><br/
<hw>Prag"ma*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being pragmatic; in literature, the pragmatic, or
philosophical, method.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The narration of this apparently trifling circumstance belongs to
the <qex>pragmatism</qex> of the history.</q> <rj><qau>A.
Murphy.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmatist</ent><br/
<hw>Prag"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
pragmatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pragmatize</ent><br/
<hw>Prag"ma*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To consider,
represent, or embody (something unreal) as fact; to materialize.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>pragmatized</xex> metaphor.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prairial</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prai`ri`al"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>prairie</ets> meadow.]</ety> <def>The ninth month of the French
Republican calendar, which dated from September 22, 1792. It began May,
20, and ended June 18. See <er>Vendemiaire</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prairie</ent><br/
<hw>Prai"rie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., an extensive
meadow, OF. <ets>praerie</ets>, LL. <ets>prataria</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>pratum</ets> a meadow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An extensive tract
of level or rolling land, destitute of trees, covered with coarse grass,
and usually characterized by a deep, fertile soil. They abound
throughout the Mississippi valley, between the Alleghanies and the Rocky
mountains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From the forests and the <qex>prairies</qex>,<br/
From the great lakes of the northland.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A meadow or tract of grass; especially, a so called
natural meadow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prairie chicken</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
American grouse of the genus <gen>Tympanuchus</gen>, especially
<spn>Tympanuchus Americanus</spn> (formerly <spn>Tympanuchus
cupido</spn>), which inhabits the prairies of the central United States.
Applied also to the sharp-tailed grouse.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie
clover</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the leguminous
genus <gen>Petalostemon</gen>, having small rosy or white flowers in
dense terminal heads or spikes. Several species occur in the prairies of
the United States.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie dock</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a coarse composite plant (<spn>Silphium
terebinthaceum</spn>) with large rough leaves and yellow flowers, found
in the Western prairies.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie dog</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small American rodent (<spn>Cynomys
Ludovicianus</spn>) allied to the marmots. It inhabits the plains west
of the Mississippi. The prairie dogs burrow in the ground in large
warrens, and have a sharp bark like that of a dog. Called also
<altname>prairie marmot</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie
grouse</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Prairie chicken</cref>, above.</cd>
-- <col><b>Prairie hare</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large
long-eared Western hare (<spn>Lepus campestris</spn>). See <cref>Jack
rabbit</cref>, under 2d <er>Jack</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Prairie
hawk</b></col>, <col><b>Prairie falcon</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a falcon of Western North America (<spn>Falco
Mexicanus</spn>). The upper parts are brown. The tail has transverse
bands of white; the under parts, longitudinal streaks and spots of
brown.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie hen</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <cref>Prairie chicken</cref>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie
itch</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>an affection of the skin attended
with intense itching, which is observed in the Northern and Western
United States; -- also called <altname>swamp itch</altname>,
<altname>winter itch</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie
marmot</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Prairie
dog</cref>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie mole</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large American mole (<spn>Scalops
argentatus</spn>), native of the Western prairies.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Prairie pigeon</b></col>, <col><b>Prairie
plover</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Prairie snipe</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the upland plover. See <er>Plover</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie rattlesnake</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the massasauga.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie
snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large harmless American snake
(<spn>Masticophis flavigularis</spn>). It is pale yellow, tinged with
brown above.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie squirrel</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any American ground squirrel of the genus
<gen>Spermophilus</gen>, inhabiting prairies; -- called also
<altname>gopher</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie turnip</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the edible turnip-shaped farinaceous root of a
leguminous plant (<spn>Psoralea esculenta</spn>) of the Upper Missouri
region; also, the plant itself. Called also <altname>pomme
blanche</altname>, and <altname>pomme de prairie</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Prairie warbler</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
bright-colored American warbler (<spn>Dendroica discolor</spn>). The
back is olive yellow, with a group of reddish spots in the middle; the
under parts and the parts around the eyes are bright yellow; the sides
of the throat and spots along the sides, black; three outer tail
feathers partly white.</cd> -- <col><b>Prairie wolf</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Coyote</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prairie State</ent><br/
<hw>Prai"rie State</hw>. <def>Illinois; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praisable</ent><br/
<hw>Prais"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Fit to be praised;
praise-worthy; laudable; commendable.</def> <rj><au>Wyclif (2 Tim. ii.
15).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praisably</ent><br/
<hw>Prais"a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a praisable
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praise</ent><br/
<hw>Praise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Praised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Praising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>preisen</ets>, OF.
<ets>preisier</ets>, <ets>prisier</ets>, F. <ets>priser</ets>, L.
<ets>pretiare</ets> to prize, fr. <ets>pretium</ets> price. See
<er>Price</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Appreciate</er>,
<er>Praise</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Prize</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To commend; to applaud; to express approbation of; to
laud; -- applied to a person or his acts.</def> <ldquo/I
<xex>praise</xex> well thy wit.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let her own works <qex>praise</qex> her in the gates.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xxxi. 31.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>praise</qex> not Hector, though his name, we know,<br/
Is great in arms; 't is hard to <qex>praise</qex> a foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To extol in words or song; to magnify; to glorify on
account of perfections or excellent works; to do honor to; to display
the excellence of; -- applied especially to the Divine Being.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Praise</qex> ye him, all his angels; <qex>praise</qex> ye
him, all his hosts!</q> <rj><qau>Ps. cxlviii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To value; to appraise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To commend; laud; eulogize; celebrate; glorify;
magnify.</syn> <usage> -- To <er>Praise</er>, <er>Applaud</er>,
<er>Extol</er>. To <xex>praise</xex> is to set at high price; to
<xex>applaud</xex> is to greet with clapping; to <xex>extol</xex> is to
bear aloft, to exalt. We may <xex>praise</xex> in the exercise of calm
judgment; we usually <xex>applaud</xex> from impulse, and on account of
some specific act; we <xex>extol</xex> under the influence of high
admiration, and usually in strong, if not extravagant,
language.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praise</ent><br/
<hw>Praise</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>preis</ets>, OF.
<ets>preis</ets> price, worth, value, estimation. See <er>Praise</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>, <er>Price</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Commendation for
worth; approval expressed; honor rendered because of excellence or
worth; laudation; approbation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are men who always confound the <qex>praise</qex> of
goodness with the practice.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Praise</xex> may be expressed by an individual, and
thus differs from <xex>fame</xex>, <xex>renown</xex>, and
<xex>celebrity</xex>, which are always the expression of the approbation
of numbers, or public commendation.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, the joyful tribute of gratitude or
homage rendered to the Divine Being; the act of glorifying or extolling
the Creator; worship, particularly worship by song, distinction from
prayer and other acts of worship; <as>as, a service of
<ex>praise</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The object, ground, or reason of praise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is thy <qex>praise</qex>, and he is thy God.</q> <rj><qau>Deut.
x. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Encomium; honor; eulogy; panegyric; plaudit;
applause; acclaim; eclat; commendation; laudation.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiseful</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Praiseworthy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiseful</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Praiseworthy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiseless</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without praise or
approbation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praise-meeting</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"-meet`*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A religious
service mainly in song.</def> <mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praisement</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Appraisement.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiser</ent><br/
<hw>Prais"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
praises.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Praisers</xex> of men.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An appraiser; a valuator.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiseworthily</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"wor`thi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
praiseworthy manner.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiseworthiness</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"wor`thi*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being praiseworthy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praiseworthy</ent><br/
<hw>Praise"wor`thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Worthy of
praise or applause; commendable; <as>as, <ex>praiseworthy</ex> action;
he was <ex>praiseworthy</ex>.</as></def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prakrit</ent><br/
<hw>Pra"krit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>pr<amac/k<rsdot/ta</ets> original, natural, usual, common,
vulgar.]</ety> <def>Any one of the popular dialects descended from, or
akin to, Sanskrit; -- in distinction from the Sanskrit, which was used
as a literary and learned language when no longer spoken by the people.
Pali is one of the <ex>Prakrit</ex> dialects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prakritic</ent><br/
<hw>Pra*krit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
Prakrit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praline</ent><br/
<hw>Pra"line</hw> <pr>(pr<aum/"l<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A confection made of nut kernels, usually of
almonds, roasted in boiling sugar until brown and crisp.</def></p>

<p><q>Bonbons, <qex>pralines</qex>, . . . saccharine, crystalline
substances of all kinds and colors.</q> <rj><qau>Du
Maurier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pralltriller</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prall"tril`ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>G.
pl.</it> <plw>Prall"triller</plw></plu>. <ety>[G.]</ety>
<fld>(Music)</fld> <def>A melodic embellishment consisting of the quick
alternation of a principal tone with an auxiliary tone above it, usually
the next of the scale; -- called also the <altname>inverted
mordente</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prame</ent><br/
<ent>Pram</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pram</hw> <pr>(pr<acr/m)</pr>, <hw>Prame</hw>
<pr>(pr<amac/m)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Praam</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pram</ent><br/
<hw>Pram</hw> <pr>(pr<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
<er>perambulator{3}</er>; -- British informal shortened form.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prance</ent><br/
<hw>Prance</hw> <pr>(pr<adot/ns)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pranced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Prancing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>prauncen</ets>; probably akin to
<ets>prank</ets>, v. t. See Prank.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To spring or
bound, as a horse in high mettle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now rule thy <qex>prancing</qex> steed.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To ride on a prancing horse; to ride in an
ostentatious manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The insulting tyrant <qex>prancing</qex> o'er the field.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To walk or strut about in a pompous, showy manner,
or with warlike parade.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prancer</ent><br/
<hw>Pran"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A horse which
prances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then came the captain . . . upon a brave <qex>prancer</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prandial</ent><br/
<hw>Pran"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prandium</ets> a repast.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a repast,
especially to dinner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prangos</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pran"gos</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[From the
native name in <country>Afghanistan</country>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A genus of umbelliferous plants, one species of which (<spn>Prangos
pabularia</spn>), found in <geog>Tibet</geog>, <geog>Cashmere</geog>,
<country>Afghanistan</country>, etc., has been used as fodder for
cattle. It has decompound leaves with very long narrow divisions, and a
highly fragrant smell resembling that of new clover hay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prank</ent><br/
<hw>Prank</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pranked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pranking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. E. <ets>prink</ets>, also
G. <ets>prangen</ets>, <ets>prunken</ets>, to shine, to make a show,
Dan. <ets>prange</ets>, <ets>prunke</ets>, Sw. <ets>prunka</ets>, D.
<ets>pronken</ets>.]</ety> <def>To adorn in a showy manner; to dress or
equip ostentatiously; -- often followed by <xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to
<ex>prank</ex> up the body</as>. See <er>Prink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In sumptuous tire she joyed herself to <qex>prank</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prank</ent><br/
<hw>Prank</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make ostentatious
show.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>White houses <qex>prank</qex> where once were huts.</q>
<rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prank</ent><br/
<hw>Prank</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A gay or sportive action; a
ludicrous, merry, or mischievous trick; a caper; a frolic.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The harpies . . . played their accustomed <qex>pranks</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>pranks</qex> have been too broad to bear with.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prank</ent><br/
<hw>Prank</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of gambols or tricks.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pranker</ent><br/
<hw>Prank"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who dresses
showily; a prinker.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pranker</xex> or a
dancer.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prankish</ent><br/
<hw>Prank"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of pranks;
frolicsome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prase</ent><br/
<hw>Prase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prasius</ets>,
fr. Gr. <?/ of a leek-green, fr. Gr. <?/ a leek: cf. F.
<ets>prase</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of
cryptocrystalline of a leek-green color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praseo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pra"se*o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <?/ leek-green, green, fr.
<?/ a leek.]</ety> <def>A combining form signifying <xex>green</xex>;
<as>as, <ex>praseo</ex>cobalt, a green variety of cobalt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praseodymium</ent><br/
<hw>Pra`se*o*dym"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Praseo-</ets> + di<ets>dymium</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An elementary substance, one of the constituents
of didymium; -- so called from the green color of its salts. Symbol Ps.
Atomic weight 143.6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praseolite</ent><br/
<hw>Pra"se*o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Praseo-</ets> + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A variety of altered iolite of a green color and greasy
luster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prasinous</ent><br/
<hw>Pras"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prasinus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ a leek.]</ety> <def>Grass-green;
clear, lively green, without any mixture.</def>
<rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prasoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pra"soid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ leek +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Resembling
prase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prate</ent><br/
<hw>Prate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to LG. & D.
<ets>praten</ets>, Dan. <ets>prate</ets>, Sw. & Icel.
<ets>prata</ets>.]</ety> <def>To talk much and to little purpose; to be
loquacious; to speak foolishly; to babble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>prate</qex> and talk for life and honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And make a fool presume to <qex>prate</qex> of love.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prate</ent><br/
<hw>Prate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To utter foolishly; to speak
without reason or purpose; to chatter, or babble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What nonsense would the fool, thy master, <qex>prate</qex>,<br/
When thou, his knave, canst talk at such a rate !</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prate</ent><br/
<hw>Prate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG. & D. <ets>praat</ets>,
Sw. <ets>prat</ets>.]</ety> <def>Talk to little purpose; trifling talk;
unmeaning loquacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sick of tops, and poetry, and <qex>prate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prateful</ent><br/
<hw>Prate"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Talkative.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>W. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prater</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who prates.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pratic</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pratique</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pratincole</ent><br/
<hw>Pra"tin*cole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any bird of the Old World genus <gen>Glareola</gen>, or family
<fam>Glareolid<ae/</fam>, allied to the plovers. They have long, pointed
wings and a forked tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pratingly</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With idle talk;
with loquacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pratique</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"ique</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.; cf. It.
<ets>pratica</ets>, Sp. <ets>practica</ets>. See
<er>Practice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>Primarily,
liberty of converse; intercourse; hence, a certificate, given after
compliance with quarantine regulations, permitting a ship to land
passengers and crew; -- a term used particularly in the south of
Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1125 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Practice; habits.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/One of English education and <xex>pratique</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>R. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prattle</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prattled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prattling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq.
<ets>of prate</ets>.]</ety> <def>To talk much and idly; to prate; hence,
to talk lightly and artlessly, like a child; to utter child's
talk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prattle</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"tle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To utter as prattle; to babble;
<as>as, to <ex>prattle</ex> treason</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prattle</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Trifling or childish tattle; empty
talk; loquacity on trivial subjects; prate; babble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mere <qex>prattle</qex>, without practice.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prattlement</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"tle*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prattle.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jeffrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prattler</ent><br/
<hw>Prat"tler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prattles.</def> <rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pravity</ent><br/
<hw>Prav"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pravitas</ets>, from <ets>pravus</ets> crooked, perverse.]</ety>
<def>Deterioration; degeneracy; corruption; especially, moral
crookedness; moral perversion; perverseness; depravity; <as>as, the
<ex>pravity</ex> of human nature</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>pravity</xex> of the will.<rdquo/ <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prawn</ent><br/
<hw>Prawn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>prane</ets>,
of unknown origin; cf. L. <ets>perna</ets> a sea mussel.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of large shrimplike
Crustacea having slender legs and long antenn<ae/. They mostly belong to
the genera <gen>Pandalus</gen>, <gen>Pal<ae/mon</gen>,
<gen>Pal<ae/monetes</gen>, and <gen>Peneus</gen>, and are much used as
food. The common English prawn is <spn>Pal<ae/mon
serratus</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is often applied to any large
shrimp.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praxinoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Prax*in"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
action + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>An instrument,
similar to the phenakistoscope, for presenting to view, or projecting
upon a screen, images the natural motions of real objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praxis</ent><br/
<hw>Prax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to do. See <er>Practice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Use; practice;
especially, exercise or discipline for a specific purpose or
object.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>praxis</xex> and theory of music.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An example or form of exercise, or a collection of
such examples, for practice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pray</ent><br/
<hw>Pray</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pry</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pray</ent><br/
<hw>Pray</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prayed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Praying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>preien</ets>, OF.
<ets>preier</ets>, F. <ets>prier</ets>, L. <ets>precari</ets>, fr.
<ets>prex</ets>, <ets>precis</ets>, a prayer, a request; akin to Skr.
<ets>prach</ets> to ask, AS. <ets>frignan</ets>, <ets>fr<imac/nan</ets>,
<ets>fricgan</ets>, G. <ets>fragen</ets>, Goth.
<ets>fra<iacute/hnan</ets>. Cf. <er>Deprecate</er>, <er>Imprecate</er>,
<er>Precarious</er>.]</ety> <def>To make request with earnestness or
zeal, as for something desired; to make entreaty or supplication; to
offer prayer to a deity or divine being as a religious act;
specifically, to address the Supreme Being with adoration, confession,
supplication, and thanksgiving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> And to his goddess pitously he <qex>preyde</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When thou <qex>prayest</qex>, enter into thy closet, and when thou
hast shut thy door, <qex>pray</qex> to thy Father which is in secret;
and thy Father which seeth in secret shall reward thee openly.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. vi. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>I pray</b></col>, <it>or</it> (by ellipsis)
<col><b>Pray</b></col></mcol>, <cd>I beg; I request; I entreat you; --
used in asking a question, making a request, introducing a petition,
etc.; <as>as, <ex>Pray</ex>, allow me to go</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>pray</qex>, sir. why am I beaten?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To entreat; supplicate; beg; implore; invoke;
beseech; petition.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pray</ent><br/
<hw>Pray</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To address earnest
request to; to supplicate; to entreat; to implore; to beseech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And as this earl was <qex>preyed</qex>, so did he.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>pray</qex> you . . . by ye reconciled to God.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Cor. v. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To ask earnestly for; to seek to obtain by
supplication; to entreat for.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I know not how to <qex>pray</qex> your patience.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To effect or accomplish by praying; <as>as, to
<ex>pray</ex> a soul out of purgatory</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pray in aid</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To call in as a helper one who has an interest in the cause</cd>.
<au>Bacon.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A phrase often used to signify claiming
the benefit of an argument. See under <er>Aid</er>.</cd> <rj><au>Mozley
& W.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prayer</ent><br/
<hw>Pray"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who prays; a
supplicant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prayer</ent><br/
<hw>Prayer</hw> <pr>(<?/; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>preiere</ets>, OF. <ets>preiere</ets>, F. <ets>pri<egrave/re</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>precarius</ets> obtained by prayer, fr. <ets>precari</ets>
to pray. See <er>Pray</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of praying, or of asking a favor; earnest request or entreaty;
hence, a petition or memorial addressed to a court or a legislative
body.</def> <ldquo/Their meek <xex>preyere</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of addressing supplication to a divinity,
especially to the true God; the offering of adoration, confession,
supplication, and thanksgiving to the Supreme Being; <as>as, public
<ex>prayer</ex>; secret <ex>prayer</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As he is famed for mildness, peace, and <qex>prayer</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The form of words used in praying; a formula of
supplication; an expressed petition; especially, a supplication
addressed to God; <as>as, a written or extemporaneous <ex>prayer</ex>;
to repeat one's <ex>prayers</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He made those excellent <qex>prayers</qex> which were published
immediately after his death.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Fell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prayer book</b></col>, <cd>a book containing devotional
prayers.</cd> -- <col><b>Prayer meeting</b></col>, <cd>a meeting or
gathering for prayer to God.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Petition; orison; supplication; entreaty;
suit.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prayerful</ent><br/
<hw>Prayer"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Given to prayer;
praying much or often; devotional.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>prayerful</xex>
man.<rdquo/ <au>J. S. Blackie.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Prayer"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Prayer"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prayerless</ent><br/
<hw>Prayer"less</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not using
prayer; habitually neglecting prayer to God; without prayer.</def>
<ldquo/The next time you go <xex>prayerless</xex> to bed.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Prayer"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Prayer"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Praying</ent><br/
<hw>Pray"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Pray</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Praying insect</b></col>, <col><b>Praying
locust</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Praying mantis</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a mantis, especially <spn>Mantis
religiosa</spn>. See <er>Mantis</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Praying
machine</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Praying wheel</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>a wheel on which prayers are pasted by Buddhist priests, who then
put the wheel in rapid revolution. Each turn in supposed to have the
efficacy of an oral repetition of all the prayers on the wheel.
Sometimes it is moved by a stream.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prayingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pray"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With supplication to
God.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pre-</ent><br/
<hw>Pre-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>prae</ets>, adv. & prep.,
before, akin to <ets>pro</ets>, and to E. <ets>for</ets>, prep.: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/-</ets>. See <er>Pro-</er>, and cf.
<er>Prior</er>.]</ety> <def>A prefix denoting <xex>priority</xex> (of
time, place, or rank); <as>as, <ex>precede</ex>, to go before;
<ex>pre</ex>cursor, a forerunner; <ex>pre</ex>fix, to fix or place
before</as>; <ex>pre</ex><eum/minent eminent before or above others.
<ex>Pre-</ex> is sometimes used intensively, <as>as in
<ex>pre</ex>potent, very potent</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pr<ae/-</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preaccusation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ac`cu*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
accusation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preace</ent><br/
<hw>Preace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>Press.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preach</ent><br/
<hw>Preach</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Preached</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Preaching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>prechen</ets>, OF. <ets>preechier</ets>, F.
<ets>pr<ecir/cher</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praedicare</ets> to cry in public,
to proclaim; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>dicare</ets> to make known,
<ets>dicere</ets> to say; or perhaps from (assumed) LL.
<ets>praedictare</ets>. See <er>Diction</er>, and cf.
<er>Predicate</er>, <er>Predict</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
proclaim or publish tidings; specifically, to proclaim the gospel; to
discourse publicly on a religious subject, or from a text of Scripture;
to deliver a sermon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How shall they <qex>preach</qex>, except they be sent?</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. x. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From that time Jesus began to <qex>preach</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. iv. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give serious advice on morals or religion; to
discourse in the manner of a preacher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preach</ent><br/
<hw>Preach</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To proclaim by public
discourse; to utter in a sermon or a formal religious
harangue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That Cristes gospel truly wolde <qex>preche</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord hath anointed me to <qex>preach</qex> good tidings unto
the meek.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. lxi. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inculcate in public discourse; to urge with
earnestness by public teaching.</def> <ldquo/I have <xex>preached</xex>
righteousness in the great congregation.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ps. xl.
9.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To deliver or pronounce; <as>as, to <ex>preach</ex>
a sermon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To teach or instruct by preaching; to inform by
preaching.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/As ye are
<xex>preached</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To advise or recommend earnestly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My master <qex>preaches</qex> patience to him.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To preach down</b></col>, <cd>to oppress, or humiliate by
preaching.</cd> <au>Tennyson.</au> -- <col><b>To preach up</b></col>,
<cd>to exalt by preaching; to preach in support of; <as>as, <ex>to
preach up</ex> equality</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preach</ent><br/
<hw>Preach</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pr<ecir/che</ets>, fr.
<ets>pr<ecir/cher</ets>. See <er>Preach</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A religious discourse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preacher</ent><br/
<hw>Preach"er</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/ch"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>preeschierre</ets>, <ets>prescheur</ets>, F.
<ets>pr<ecir/cheur</ets>, L. <ets>praedicator</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who preaches; one who discourses publicly on religious
subjects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How shall they hear without a <qex>preacher</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. x. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who inculcates anything with
earnestness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>preacher</qex> is listened to but Time.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Preacher bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
toucan.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preachership</ent><br/
<hw>Preach"er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
preacher.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>preachership</xex> of the Rolls.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preachify</ent><br/
<hw>Preach"i*fy</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/ch"<icr/*f<imac/)</pr>, <pos>v.
i.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Preach</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
discourse in the manner of a preacher.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preaching</ent><br/
<hw>Preach"ing</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/ch"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The act of delivering a religious discourse; the art of
sermonizing; also, a sermon; a public religious discourse; serious,
earnest advice.</def> <rj><au>Milner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Preaching cross</b></col>, <cd>a cross, sometimes
surmounting a pulpit, erected out of doors to designate a preaching
place.</cd> -- <col><b>Preaching friars</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Dominican</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preachman</ent><br/
<hw>Preach"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Preachmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A preacher; -- so called
in contempt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preachment</ent><br/
<hw>Preach"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A religious
harangue; a sermon; -- used derogatively.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preacquaint</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ac*quaint"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To acquaint
previously or beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Fielding.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preacquaintance</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ac*quaint"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
acquaintance or knowledge.</def> <rj><au>Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preact</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*act"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To act beforehand;
to perform previously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preaction</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadamic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`a*dam"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Prior to
Adam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadamite</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ad"am*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/adamite</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An inhabitant of
the earth before Adam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who holds that men existed before
Adam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadamitic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ad`am*it"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Existing or
occurring before Adam; preadamic; <as>as, <ex>preadamitic</ex>
periods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadjustment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ad*just"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
adjustment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadministration</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ad*min`is*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Previous administration.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadmission</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ad*mis"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Lit., previous
admission;</def> <specif>specif.</specif> <fld>(Engin.)</fld>,
<def>admission, as of steam, to the engine cylinder before the back
stroke is completed, thus increasing the cushioning.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadmonish</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ad*mon"ish</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To admonish
previously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadmonition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ad`mo*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
warning or admonition; forewarning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preadvertise</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ad`ver*tise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
advertise beforehand; to preannounce publicly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preamble</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"am`ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>praeambulum</ets>, from L. <ets>praeambulus</ets> walking before,
fr. <ets>praeambulare</ets> to walk before; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>ambulare</ets> to walk: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/ambule</ets>. See
<er>Amble</er>.]</ety> <def>A introductory portion; an introduction or
preface, as to a book, document, etc.; specifically, the introductory
part of a statute, which states the reasons and intent of the
law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preamble</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"am`ble</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To make a preamble to;
to preface; to serve as a preamble.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Feltham. Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preambulary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*am"bu*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>preambulaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a preamble;
introductory; contained or provided for in a preamble.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>preambulary</xex> tax.<rdquo/ <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preambulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*am"bu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeambulare</ets>. See <er>Preamble</er>.]</ety> <def>To walk
before.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jordan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preambulation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*am`bu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A walking or going before; precedence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A preamble.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preambulatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*am"bu*la*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Preceding;
going before; introductory.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Simon Magus had <qex>preambulatory</qex> impieties.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preambulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*am"bu*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Preamble</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>See
<er>Perambulatory</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preannounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`an*nounce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To announce
beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preantenultimate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*an`te*nul"ti*mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being or
indicating the fourth syllable from the end of a word, or that before
the antepenult.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preaortic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`a*or"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>In front, or on the ventral side, of the aorta.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preappoint</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ap*point"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To appoint
previously, or beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preappointment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ap*point"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
appointment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preapprehension</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ap`pre*hen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
apprehension or opinion formed before examination or knowledge.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prearm</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*arm"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To forearm.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prearrange</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ar*range"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To arrange
beforehand.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>pre-arrange</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prease</ent><br/
<hw>Prease</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To press; to
crowd.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A press; a
crowd.</def></def2> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preassurance</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`as*sur"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
assurance.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preataxic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`a*tax"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Occurring before the symptom ataxia has developed; -- applied to
the early symptoms of locomotor ataxia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preaudience</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*au"di*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng.
Law)</fld> <def>Precedence of rank at the bar among lawyers.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preaxial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ax"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of any transverse axis in the body of an animal;
anterior; cephalic; esp., in front, or on the anterior, or cephalic
(that is, radial or tibial) side of the axis of a limb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebend</ent><br/
<hw>Preb"end</hw> <pr>(pr<ecr/b"<ecr/nd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/bende</ets> (cf. It. & Sp. <ets>prebenda</ets>), from L.
<ets>praebenda</ets>, from L. <ets>praebere</ets> to hold forth, afford,
contr. fr. <ets>praehibere</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>habere</ets> to have, hold. See <er>Habit</er>, and cf.
<er>Provender</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A payment or stipend; esp.,
the stipend or maintenance granted to a prebendary out of the estate of
a cathedral or collegiate church with which he is connected. See
<er>Note</er> under <er>Benefice</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A prebendary.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dignitary prebend</b></col>, <cd>one having jurisdiction
annexed to it.</cd> -- <col><b>Simple prebend</b></col>, <cd>one without
jurisdiction.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebendal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ben"dal</hw> <pr>(pr<esl/*b<ecr/n"d<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to a prebend; holding a prebend; <as>as, a
<ex>prebendal</ex> priest or stall</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebendary</ent><br/
<hw>Preb"en*da*ry</hw> <pr>(pr<ecr/b"<ecr/n*d<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>praebendarius</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/bendaire</ets>. See <er>Prebend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A clergyman attached to a collegiate or cathedral church who enjoys
a prebend in consideration of his officiating at stated times in the
church. See <er>Note</er> under <er>Benefice</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
3.</def> <rj><au>Hook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A prebendaryship.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebendaryship</ent><br/
<hw>Preb"en*da*ry*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
prebendary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebendate</ent><br/
<hw>Preb"en*date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>praebendatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praebendari</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
invest with the office of prebendary; to present to a prebend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Grafton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebendship</ent><br/
<hw>Preb"end*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
prebendaryship.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prebronchial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*bron"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the bronchus; -- applied especially to an air
sac on either side of the esophagus of birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precalculate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cal"cu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
calculate or determine beforehand; to prearrange.</def>
<rj><au>Masson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>precans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>precari</ets> to
pray.]</ety> <def>One who prays.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precarious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ca"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>precarius</ets> obtained by begging or prayer, depending on request
or on the will of another, fr. <ets>precari</ets> to pray, beg. See
<er>Pray</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Depending on the will or pleasure
of another; held by courtesy; liable to be changed or lost at the
pleasure of another; <as>as, <ex>precarious</ex> privileges</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Held by a doubtful tenure; depending on unknown
causes or events; exposed to constant risk; not to be depended on for
certainty or stability; uncertain; <as>as, a <ex>precarious</ex> state
of health; <ex>precarious</ex> fortunes.</as></def> <ldquo/Intervals of
partial and <xex>precarious</xex> liberty.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Uncertain; unsettled; unsteady; doubtful;
dubious; equivocal.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Precarious</er>,
<er>Uncertain</er>. <xex>Precarious</xex> in stronger than
<xex>uncertain</xex>. Derived originally from the Latin
<xex>precari</xex>, it first signified <ldquo/granted to
entreaty,<rdquo/ and, hence, <ldquo/wholly dependent on the will of
another.<rdquo/ Thus it came to express the highest species of
uncertainty, and is applied to such things as depend wholly on future
casualties.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre*ca"ri*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre*ca"ri*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>precatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of praying; supplication;
entreaty.</def> <rj><au>Cotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precatory</ent><br/
<ent>Precative</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prec"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Prec"a*to*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>precativus</ets>,
<ets>precatorius</ets>, fr. <ets>precari</ets> to pray. See
<er>Precarious</er>.]</ety> <def>Suppliant; beseeching.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hopkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Precatory words</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>words of
recommendation, request, entreaty, wish, or expectation, employed in
wills, as distinguished from <xex>express directions</xex>; -- in some
cases creating a trust.</cd> <rj><au>Jarman.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precaution</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cau"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cation</ets>, L. <ets>praecautio</ets>, fr.
<ets>praecavere</ets>, <ets>praecautum</ets>, to guard against
beforehand; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>cavere</ets> be on one's
guard. See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Caution</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Previous caution or care; caution previously employed to prevent
mischief or secure good; <as>as, his life was saved by
<ex>precaution</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They [ancient philosophers] treasured up their supposed
discoveries with miserable <qex>precaution</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>J. H.
Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A measure taken beforehand to ward off evil or
secure good or success; a precautionary act; <as>as, to take
<ex>precautions</ex> against accident</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precaution</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cau"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cautionner</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To warn or
caution beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1126 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take precaution against.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precautional</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cau"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Precautionary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precautionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cau"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to precaution, or precautions; <as>as, <ex>precautionary</ex>
signals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precautious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cau"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Taking or using
precaution; precautionary.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre*cau"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre*cau"*tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ce*da"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Preceding;
antecedent; previous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precede</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cede"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preceded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preceding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praecedere</ets>,
<ets>praecessum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>cedere</ets> to go,
to be in motion: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/ceder</ets>. See <er>Pre-</er>,
and <er>Cede</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go before in order of
time; to occur first with relation to anything.</def> <ldquo/Harm
<xex>precedes</xex> not sin.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To go before in place, rank, or
importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to be preceded; to preface; to introduce;
-- used with <xex>by</xex> or <xex>with</xex> before the instrumental
object.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is usual to <qex>precede</qex> hostilities by a public
declaration.</q> <rj><qau>Kent.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedency</ent><br/
<ent>Precedence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*ced"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre*ced"en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/c<eacute/dence</ets>. See <er>Precede</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or state of preceding or going before in order
of time; priority; <as>as, one event has <ex>precedence</ex> of
another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or state of going or being before in rank or
dignity, or the place of honor; right to a more honorable place;
superior rank; <as>as, barons have <ex>precedence</ex> of
commoners</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which of them [the different desires] has the
<qex>precedency</qex> in determining the will to the next action?</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Antecedence; priority; pre<eum/minence;
preference; superiority.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ced"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecedens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>praecedere</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/c<eacute/dent</ets>. See
<er>Precede</er>.]</ety> <def>Going before; anterior; preceding;
antecedent; <as>as, <ex>precedent</ex> services</as>.</def>
<au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>precedent</xex> injury.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Condition precedent</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a
condition which precede the vesting of an estate, or the accruing of a
right.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedent</ent><br/
<hw>Prec"e*dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Something done or said that may serve as an example to authorize a
subsequent act of the same kind; an authoritative example.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Examples for cases can but direct as <qex>precedents</qex>
only.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A preceding circumstance or condition; an
antecedent; hence, a prognostic; a token; a sign.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A rough draught of a writing which precedes a
finished copy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A judicial decision which serves as
a rule for future determinations in similar or analogous cases; an
authority to be followed in courts of justice; forms of proceeding to be
followed in similar cases.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Example; antecedent.</syn> <usage> --
<er>Precedent</er>, <er>Example</er>. An <xex>example</xex> in a similar
case which may serve as a rule or guide, but has no authority out of
itself. A <xex>precedent</xex> is something which comes down to us from
the past with the sanction of usage and of common consent. We quote
<xex>examples</xex> in literature, and <xex>precedents</xex> in
law.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedented</ent><br/
<hw>Prec"e*dent*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a precedent;
authorized or sanctioned by an example of a like kind.</def>
<rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedential</ent><br/
<hw>Prec`e*den"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the nature
of a precedent; having force as an example for imitation; <as>as,
<ex>precedential</ex> transactions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All their actions in that time are not <qex>precedential</qex> to
warrant posterity.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precedently</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ced"ent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Beforehand;
antecedently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceding</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ced"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Going before; --
opposed to <xex>following</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>In the direction toward which
stars appear to move. See <er>Following</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precel</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cel"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Precellence</er>.]</ety> <def>To surpass; to excel; to exceed.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precellency</ent><br/
<ent>Precellence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*cel"lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre*cel"len*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praecellentia</ets>,
from <ets>praecellens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>praecellere</ets> to excel,
surpass: cf. OF. <ets>precellence</ets>.]</ety> <def>Excellence;
superiority.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sheldon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precellent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cel"lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecellens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Excellent; surpassing.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precentor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cen"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecentor</ets>, fr. <ets>praecinere</ets> to sing before;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>canere</ets> to sing. See
<er>Chant</er>.]</ety> <def>A leader of a choir; a directing
singer.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The leader of the choir in
a cathedral; -- called also the <altname>chanter</altname> or
<xex>master</xex> of the choir.</def> <au>Hook.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The leader of the congregational singing in Scottish and other
churches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precentorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cen"tor*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
precentor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precept</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cept</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeceptum</ets>, from <ets>praecipere</ets> to take beforehand, to
instruct, teach; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>capere</ets> to take: cf.
F. <ets>pr<eacute/cepte</ets>. See <er>Pre-</er>, and
<er>Capacious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any commandment,
instruction, or order intended as an authoritative rule of action; esp.,
a command respecting moral conduct; an injunction; a rule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For <qex>precept</qex> must be upon <qex>precept</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxviii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No arts are without their <qex>precepts</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A command in writing; a species of
writ or process.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Commandment; injunction; mandate; law; rule;
direction; principle; maxim. See <er>Doctrine</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precept</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cept</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To teach by precepts.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Preceptive.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Passion] would give <qex>preceptial</qex> medicine to rage.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preception</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeceptio</ets>.]</ety> <def>A precept.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeceptivus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Containing or giving precepts; of
the nature of precepts; didactic; <as>as, the <ex>preceptive</ex> parts
of the Scriptures</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lesson given us here is <qex>preceptive</qex> to us.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeceptor</ets>, fr. <ets>praecipere</ets> to teach: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cepteur</ets>. See <er>Precept</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who gives commands, or makes rules; specifically, the master or
principal of a school; a teacher; an instructor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The head of a preceptory among the Knights
Templars.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`cep*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a preceptor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"to*ry</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Preceptive.</def> <ldquo/A law <xex>preceptory</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Anderson (1573).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Preceptories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL.
<ets>praeceptoria</ets> an estate assigned to a preceptor, from L.
<ets>praeceptor</ets> a commander, ruler, teacher, in LL., procurator,
administrator among the Knights Templars. See <er>Preceptor</er>.]</ety>
<def>A religious house of the Knights Templars, subordinate to the
temple or principal house of the order in London. See
<er>Commandery</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preceptress</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cep"tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who is
the principal of a school; a female teacher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precession</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ces"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecedere</ets>, <ets>praecessum</ets>, to go before: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cession</ets>. See <er>Precede</er>.]</ety> <def>The act
of going before, or forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Lunisolar precession</b></col>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Lunisolar</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Planetary
precession</b></col>, <cd>that part of the precession of the equinoxes
which depends on the action of the planets alone.</cd> --
<col><b>Precession of the equinoxes</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>,
<cd>the slow backward motion of the equinoctial points along the
ecliptic, at the rate of 50.2<sec/ annually, caused by the action of the
sun, moon, and planets, upon the protuberant matter about the earth's
equator, in connection with its diurnal rotation; -- so called because
either equinox, owing to its westerly motion, comes to the meridian
sooner each day than the point it would have occupied without the motion
of precession, and thus <xex>precedes</xex> that point continually with
reference to the time of transit and motion.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precessional</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ces"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to pression; <as>as, the <ex>precessional</ex> movement of
the equinoxes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precessor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ces"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecessor</ets>.]</ety> <def>A predecessor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precieuse</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<eacute/`cieuse"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An affected woman of
polite society, esp. one of the literary women of the French salons of
the 17th century. See aslo <er>preciosity</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precinct</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cinct</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>praecinctum</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praecingere</ets>,
<ets>praecinctum</ets>, to gird about, to encompass; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>cingere</ets> to gird, surround. See <er>Pre-</er>, and
<er>Cincture</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The limit or exterior line
encompassing a place; a boundary; a confine; limit of jurisdiction or
authority; -- often in the plural; <as>as, the <ex>precincts</ex> of a
state</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>precincts</xex> of light.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A district within certain boundaries; a minor
territorial or jurisdictional division; <as>as, an election
<ex>precinct</ex>; a school <ex>precinct</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A parish or prescribed territory attached to a
church, and taxed for its support.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The parish, or <qex>precinct</qex>, shall proceed to a new
choice.</q> <rj><qau>Laws of Massachusetts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preciosity</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ci*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Preciousness;
something precious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preciosity</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ci*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl.
<plw>-ties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/ciosit<eacute/</ets>, OF. also
<ets>precieuset<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Fastidious refinement, esp.
in language; specif., the affected purism and sententiousness
characteristic of the French pr<eacute/cieuses of the 17th
century.</def></p>

<p><q>He had the fastidiousness, the <qex>preciosity</qex>, the love of
archaisms, of your true decadent.</q> <rj><qau>L.
Douglas.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>precious</ets>, <ets>precius</ets>, <ets>precios</ets>, F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cieux</ets>, L. <ets>pretiosus</ets>, fr.
<ets>pretium</ets> price, worth, value. See <er>Price</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of great price; costly; <as>as, a <ex>precious</ex>
stone</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>precious</xex> bane.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of great value or worth; very valuable; highly
esteemed; dear; beloved; <as>as, <ex>precious</ex>
recollections</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She is more <qex>precious</qex> than rules.</q> <rj><qau>Prov.
iii. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many things which are most <qex>precious</qex> are neglected only
because the value of them lieth hid.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>Also used ironically; as, a <xex>precious</xex>
rascal.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Particular; fastidious; overnice; overrefined. Cf.
<er>Pr<eacute/cieuse</er>, <er>Preciosity</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest that <qex>precious</qex> folk be with me wroth.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Elaborate embroidery of <qex>precious</qex> language.</q>
<rj><qau>Saintsbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Precious metals</b></col>, <cd>the uncommon and highly
valuable metals, esp. gold and silver.</cd> -- <col><b>Precious
stones</b></col>, <cd>gems; jewels.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preciously</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cious*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a precious manner;
expensively; extremely; dearly. Also used ironically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preciousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"cious*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being precious; costliness; dearness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipe</ent><br/
<hw>Prec"i*pe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pr<ae/cipe</er>, and <er>Precept</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipice</ent><br/
<hw>Prec"i*pice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cipice</ets>, L. <ets>praecipitium</ets>, fr.
<ets>praeceps</ets>, <ets>-cipitis</ets>, headlong; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>caput</ets>, <ets>capitis</ets>, the head. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Chief</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden or
headlong fall.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A headlong steep; a very steep, perpendicular, or
overhanging place; an abrupt declivity; a cliff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where wealth like fruit on <qex>precipices</qex> grew.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipient</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecipiens</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Precept</er>.]</ety>
<def>Commanding; directing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitability</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip`i*ta*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being precipitable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*ta*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being precipitated, or cast to the bottom, as a substance in solution.
See <er>Precipitate</er>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitancy</ent><br/
<ent>Precipitance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*cip"i*tance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre*cip"i*tan*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Precipitant</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
precipitant, or precipitate; headlong hurry; excessive or rash haste in
resolving, forming an opinion, or executing a purpose; precipitation;
<as>as, the <ex>precipitancy</ex> of youth</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Precipitance</xex> of judgment.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I.
Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecipitans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>praecipitare</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/cipitant</ets>. See
<er>Precipitate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Falling or rushing
headlong; rushing swiftly, violently, or recklessly; moving
precipitately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They leave their little lives<br/
Above the clouds, <qex>precipitant</qex> to earth.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Should he return, that troop so blithe and bold,<br/
<qex>Precipitant</qex> in fear would wing their flight.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unexpectedly or foolishly brought on or hastened;
rashly hurried; hasty; sudden; reckless.</def> <au>Jer. Taylor.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Precipitant</xex> rebellion.<rdquo/ <au>Eikon
Basilike.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any force
or reagent which causes the formation of a precipitate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With rash or foolish
haste; in headlong manner.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitantness</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tant*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being precipitant; precipitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecipitatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praecipitare</ets> to
precipitate, fr. <ets>praeceps</ets> headlong. See
<er>Precipice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Overhasty; rash; <as>as, the
king was too <ex>precipitate</ex> in declaring war</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lacking due deliberation or care; hurried; said or
done before the time; <as>as, a <ex>precipitate</ex> measure</as>.</def>
<ldquo/The rapidity of our too <xex>precipitate</xex> course.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Landor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Falling, flowing, or rushing, with steep descent;
headlong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Precipitate</qex> the furious torrent flows.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Ending quickly in death; brief and fatal; <as>as, a
<ex>precipitate</ex> case of disease</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>praecipitatum</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cipit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An
insoluble substance separated from a solution in a concrete state by the
action of some reagent added to the solution, or of some force, such as
heat or cold. The precipitate may fall to the bottom (whence the name),
may be diffused through the solution, or may float at or near the
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>atmospheric moisture condensed as rain or snow,
etc.; same as <er>precipitation{5}</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Red precipitate</b></col> <fld>(Old. Chem)</fld>,
<cd>mercuric oxide (<chform>HgO</chform>) a heavy red crystalline powder
obtained by heating mercuric nitrate, or by heating mercury in the air.
Prepared in the latter manner, it was the <altname>precipitate per
se</altname> of the alchemists.</cd> -- <col><b>White
precipitate</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A heavy
white amorphous powder (<chform>NH2.HgCl</chform>) obtained by adding
ammonia to a solution of mercuric chloride or corrosive sublimate; --
formerly called also <altname>infusible white precipitate</altname>, and
now <altname>amido-mercuric chloride</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
white crystalline substance obtained by adding a solution of corrosive
sublimate to a solution of sal ammoniac (ammonium chloride); -- formerly
called also <altname>fusible white precipitate</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Precipitated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Precipitating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw
headlong; to cast down from a precipice or height.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She and her horse had been <qex>precipitated</qex> to the pebbled
region of the river.</q> <rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To urge or press on with eager haste or violence; to
cause to happen, or come to a crisis, suddenly or too soon; <as>as,
<ex>precipitate</ex> a journey, or a conflict</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Back to his sight <qex>precipitates</qex> her steps.</q>
<rj><qau>Glover.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If they be daring, it may <qex>precipitate</qex> their designs,
and prove dangerous.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To separate from a solution, or
other medium, in the form of a precipitate; <as>as, water
<ex>precipitates</ex> camphor when in solution with
alcohol</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The light vapor of the preceding evening had been
<qex>precipitated</qex> by the cold.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To dash or
fall headlong.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So many fathom down <qex>precipitating</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hasten without preparation.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To separate from a solution as a
precipitate. See <er>Precipitate</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitately</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
precipitate manner; headlong; hastily; rashly.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecipitatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/cipitation</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of precipitating, or the state of being
precipitated, or thrown headlong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In peril of <qex>precipitation</qex><br/
From off rock Tarpeian.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A falling, flowing, or rushing downward with
violence and rapidity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The hurry, <qex>precipitation</qex>, and rapid motion of the
water, returning . . . towards the sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Great hurry; rash, tumultuous haste;
impetuosity.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>precipitation</xex> of
inexperience.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Rambler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act or process of
precipitating from a solution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Meteorology)</fld> <def>A deposit on the earth of
hail, mist, rain, sleet, or snow; also, the quantity of water
deposited.</def></p>

<p><note><hand/ Deposits of dew, fog, and frost are not regarded by the
United States Weather Bureau as <xex>precipitation</xex>. Sleet and snow
are melted, and the record of <xex>precipitation</xex> shows the depth
of the horizontal layers of water in hundredths of an inch or in
millimeters.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitator</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*ta`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecipitator</ets> an overthrower.]</ety> <def>One who
precipitates, or urges on with vehemence or rashness.</def>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitious</ent><br/
<hw>Prec`i*pi"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Precipitous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Prec`i*pi"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precipitous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cip"i*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeceps</ets>, <ets>-cipitis</ets>: cf. OF.
<ets>precipiteux</ets>. See <er>Precipice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Steep, like a precipice; <as>as, a <ex>precipitous</ex> cliff or
mountain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Headlong; <as>as, <ex>precipitous</ex>
fall</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hasty; rash; quick; sudden; precipitate; <as>as,
<ex>precipitous</ex> attempts</as>.</def> <au>Sir T. Browne.</au>
<ldquo/Marian's low, <xex>precipitous</xex> <lsquo/Hush!'<rdquo/
<rj><au>Mrs. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre*cip"i*tous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre*cip"i*tous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pr<eacute/`cis"</hw> <pr>(pr<asl/`s<emac/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. See <er>Precise</er>.]</ety> <def>A concise or abridged
statement or view; an abstract; a summary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>precis</ent><br/
<hw>precis</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make a precis of.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precise</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecisus</ets> cut off, brief, concise, p. p. of
<ets>praecidere</ets> to cut off in front, to cut off; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>caedere</ets> to cut: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/cis</ets>. Cf.
<er>Concise</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having determinate
limitations; exactly or sharply defined or stated; definite; exact;
nice; not vague or equivocal; <as>as, <ex>precise</ex> rules of
morality</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law in this point is not <qex>precise</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the hour <qex>precise</qex><br/
Exacts our parting hence.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Strictly adhering or conforming to rule; very nice
or exact; punctilious in conduct or ceremony; formal; ceremonious.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was ever <qex>precise</qex> in promise-keeping.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Accurate; exact; definite; correct; scrupulous;
punctilious; particular; nice; formal. See <er>Accurate</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre*cise"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre*cise"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precisian</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ci"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who limits, or restrains.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An overprecise person; one rigidly or ceremoniously
exact in the observance of rules; a formalist; -- formerly applied to
the English Puritans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The most dissolute cavaliers stood aghast at the dissoluteness of
the emancipated <qex>precisian</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precisianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ci"sian*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being a precisian; the practice of a precisian.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precisianist</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ci"sian*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A precisian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precision</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ci"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cision</ets>, L. <ets>praecisio</ets> a cutting off. See
<er>Precise</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being precise;
exact limitation; exactness; accuracy; strict conformity to a rule or a
standard; definiteness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have left out the utmost <qex>precisions</qex> of fractions.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Preciseness; exactness; accuracy; nicety.</syn>
<usage> -- <er>Precision</er>, <er>Preciseness</er>.
<xex>Precision</xex> is always used in a good sense; <as>as,
<ex>precision</ex> of thought or language; <ex>precision</ex> in
military evolutions</as>. <xex>Preciseness</xex> is sometimes applied to
persons or their conduct in a disparaging sense, and <xex>precise</xex>
is often used in the same way.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precisive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ci"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cutting off;
<fld>(Logic)</fld> exactly limiting by cutting off all that is not
absolutely relative to the purpose; <as>as, <ex>precisive</ex> censure;
<ex>precisive</ex> abstraction.</as></def> <rj><au>I.
Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1127 --></p>

<p><ent>Preclude</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*clude"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Precluded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Precluding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praecludere</ets>,
<ets>praeclusum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>claudere</ets> to
shut. See <er>Close</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put a
barrier before; hence, to shut out; to hinder; to stop; to
impede.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The valves <qex>preclude</qex> the blood from entering the
veins.</q> <rj><qau>E. Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shut out by anticipative action; to prevent or
hinder by necessary consequence or implication; to deter action of,
access to, employment of, etc.; to render ineffectual; to obviate by
anticipation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This much will obviate and <qex>preclude</qex> the objections.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preclusion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*clu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeclusio</ets>. See <er>Preclude</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
precluding, or the state of being precluded; a shutting out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preclusive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*clu"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shutting out;
precluding, or tending to preclude; hindering.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre*clu"sive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precoce</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*coce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/coce</ets>.]</ety> <def>Precocious.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precoces</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre"co*ces</hw>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pr<ae/coces</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precocious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*co"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecox</ets>, <ets>-ocis</ets>, and <ets>praecoquus</ets>, fr.
<ets>praecoquere</ets> to cook or ripen beforehand; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>coquere</ets> to cook. See 3d <er>Cook</er>, and cf.
<er>Apricot</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Ripe or mature before the
proper or natural time; early or prematurely ripe or developed; <as>as,
<ex>precocious</ex> trees</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Developed more than is natural or usual at a given
age; exceeding what is to be expected of one's years; too forward; --
used especially of mental forwardness; <as>as, a <ex>precocious</ex>
child; <ex>precocious</ex> talents.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precociously</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*co"cious*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a precocious
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precocity</ent><br/
<ent>Precociousness</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*co"cious*ness</hw>, <hw>Pre*coc"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cocit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being precocious; untimely ripeness; premature development, especially
of the mental powers; forwardness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Saucy <qex>precociousness</qex> in learning.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Mannyngham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>precocity</qex> which sometimes distinguishes uncommon
genius.</q> <rj><qau>Wirt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precoetanean</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*co`e*ta"ne*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
contemporary with, but older than, another.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precogitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cog"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecogitatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praecogitare</ets>. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Cogitate</er>.]</ety> <def>To cogitate
beforehand.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sherwood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precogitation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cog`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecogitatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Previous cogitation.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precognition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`cog*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecognitio</ets>, fr. <ets>praecognoscere</ets> to foreknow. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Cognition</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Previous
cognition.</def> <rj><au>Fotherby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>A preliminary examination of
a criminal case with reference to a prosecution.</def>
<rj><au>Erskine.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precognizable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cog"ni*za*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cognizable
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precognosce</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cog"nosce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecognoscere</ets> to foreknow.]</ety> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld>
<def>To examine beforehand, as witnesses or evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A committee of nine <qex>precognoscing</qex> the chances.</q>
<rj><qau>Masson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precollection</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`col*lec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A collection
previously made.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precompose</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`com*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Precomposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Precomposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To compose
beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconceit</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*ceit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An opinion or
notion formed beforehand; a preconception.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconceive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*ceive"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preconceived</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preconceiving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To conceive, or form an
opinion of, beforehand; to form a previous notion or idea of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In a dead plain the way seemeth the longer, because the eye hath
<qex>preconceived</qex> it shorter than the truth.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconception</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
preconceiving; conception or opinion previously formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconcert</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*cert"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preconcerted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preconcerting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To concert or arrange
beforehand; to settle by previous agreement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconcert</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con"cert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something
concerted or arranged beforehand; a previous agreement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconcerted</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*cert"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Previously
arranged; agreed upon beforehand.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre`con*cert"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre`con*cert"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconcertion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*cer"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
preconcerting; preconcert.</def> <rj><au>Dr. T. Dwight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precondemn</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*demn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To condemn
beforehand.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pre*con`dem*na"tion</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precondition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*di"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A previous or
antecedent condition; a preliminary condition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconform</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*form"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
conform by way anticipation.</def> <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconformity</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*form"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Anticipative
or antecedent conformity.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconizate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con"i*zate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/coniser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To proclaim; to publish; also,
to summon; to call.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconization</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con`i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeconium</ets> a crying out in public, fr. <ets>praeco</ets>,
<ets>-onis</ets>, a crier, a herald: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/conisation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A publishing by
proclamation; a public proclamation.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A formal approbation by the pope
of a person nominated to an ecclesiastical dignity.</def> <rj><au>Addis
& Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconize</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"con*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>To approve by preconization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconquer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con"quer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To conquer in
anticipation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconscious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con""scious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a state before consciousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconsent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*sent"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A previous
consent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconsign</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*sign"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preconsigned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Preconsigning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To consign
beforehand; to make a previous consignment of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconsolidated</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*sol"i*da`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Consolidated beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preconstitute</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con"sti*tute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
constitute or establish beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precontract</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*tract"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Precontracted</conjf>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Precontracting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To
contract, engage, or stipulate previously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precontract</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*tract"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a
previous contract or agreement.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precontract</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*con"tract</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A contract
preceding another</def>; especially <fld>(Law)</fld>, <def>a contract of
marriage which, according to the ancient law, rendered void a subsequent
marriage solemnized in violation of it.</def>
<rj><au>Abbott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precontrive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`con*trive"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
contrive or plan beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precoracoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cor"a*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The anterior part of the coracoid (often closely united with the
clavicle) in the shoulder girdle of many reptiles and
amphibians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precordial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cor"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + L. <ets>cor</ets>, <ets>cordis</ets>, heart: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/cordial</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated in
front of the heart; of or pertaining to the pr<ae/cordia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precrural</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cru"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the leg or thigh; <as>as, the
<ex>precrural</ex> glands of the horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precurrer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cur"rer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A precursor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precurse</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*curse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecursus</ets>.]</ety> <def>A forerunning.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precursive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cur"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Preceding;
introductory; precursory.</def> <ldquo/A deep <xex>precursive</xex>
sound.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precursor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cur"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecursor</ets>, fr. <ets>praecurrere</ets> to run before;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>currere</ets> to run. See
<er>Course</er>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, precedes an event,
and indicates its approach; a forerunner; a harbinger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Evil thoughts are the invisible, airy <qex>precursors</qex> of all
the storms and tempests of the soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Predecessor; forerunner; harbinger; messenger;
omen; sign.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precursorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cur"sor*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The position or condition
of a precursor.</def> <rj><au>Ruskin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precursory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cur"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praecursorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Preceding as a precursor or
harbinger; indicating something to follow; <as>as, <ex>precursory</ex>
symptoms of a fever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Precursory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*cur"so*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An introduction.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predacean</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*da"cean</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeda</ets> prey.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A carnivorous
animal.</def> <rj><au>Kirby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*da"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeda</ets> prey. See <er>Prey</er>.]</ety> <def>Living by prey;
predatory.</def> <rj><au>Derham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praeda</ets>
prey.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to prey; plundering; predatory.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Boyse.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*date"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To date
anticipation; to affix to (a document) an earlier than the actual date;
to antedate; <as>as, a <ex>predated</ex> deed or letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedatio</ets>, fr. <ets>praedari</ets> to plunder.]</ety>
<def>The act of pillaging.</def> <rj><au>E. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predatorily</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"a*to*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
predatory manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedatorius</ets>, fr. <ets>praedari</ets> to plunder, fr.
<ets>praeda</ets> prey. See <er>Prey</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Characterized by plundering; practicing rapine; plundering;
pillaging; <as>as, a <ex>predatory</ex> excursion; a <ex>predatory</ex>
party.</as></def> <ldquo/A <xex>predatory</xex> war.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hungry; ravenous; <as>as, <ex>predatory</ex>
spirits</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Exercise . . . maketh the spirits more hot and
<qex>predatory</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Living by preying upon other
animals; carnivorous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prede</ent><br/
<hw>Prede</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedari</ets>. See <er>Prey</er>.]</ety> <def>To prey; to
plunder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prede</ent><br/
<hw>Prede</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prey; plunder; booty.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predecay</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"de*cay`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Premature
decay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predecease</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*cease</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To die sooner
than.</def> <ldquo/If children <xex>predecease</xex> progenitors.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predecease</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"de*cease`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The death of one
person or thing before another.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Brougham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predecessive</ent><br/
<hw>Pred`e*ces"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Going before;
preceding.</def> <ldquo/Our <xex>predecessive</xex> students.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predecessor</ent><br/
<hw>Pred`e*ces"sor</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedecessor</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>decessor</ets>
one who withdraws from the province he has governed, a retiring officer
(with reference to his successor), a predecessor, fr.
<ets>decedere</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/d<eacute/cesseur</ets>. See
<er>Decease</er>.]</ety> <def>One who precedes; one who has preceded
another in any state, position, office, etc.; one whom another follows
or comes after, in any office or position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A prince who was as watchful as his <qex>predecessor</qex> had
been over the interests of the state.</q>
<rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predeclare</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*clare"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To declare or
announce beforehand; to preannounce.</def> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prededication</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ded`i*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dedication
made previously or beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predefine</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*fine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To define
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predeliberation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*lib`er*a"tion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
deliberation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predelineation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*lin`e*a"tion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
delineation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*del"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>The step, or raised secondary part, of an altar; a superaltar;
hence, in Italian painting, a band or frieze of several pictures running
along the front of a superaltar, or forming a border or frame at the
foot of an altarpiece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predesign</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*sign"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To design or
purpose beforehand; to predetermine.</def>
<rj><au>Mitford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predesignate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ig*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Logic)</fld>
<def>A term used by Sir William Hamilton to define propositions having
their quantity indicated by a verbal sign; <as>as, <ex>all</ex>,
<ex>none</ex>, etc.</as>; -- contrasted with <xex>preindesignate</xex>,
defining propositions of which the quantity is not so
indicated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des`ti*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to predestination; <as>as, the <ex>predestinarian</ex>
controversy</as>.</def> <rj><au>Waterland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des`ti*na"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who believes in or
supports the doctrine of predestination.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinarianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des`ti*na"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
system or doctrine of the predestinarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ti*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Predestinarian.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Heylin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ti*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedestinatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praedestinare</ets> to
predestine; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>destinare</ets> to determine.
See <er>Destine</er>.]</ety> <def>Predestinated; foreordained;
fated.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>predestinate</xex> scratched face.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ti*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predestinated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Predestinating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Predestine</er>.]</ety> <def>To predetermine or foreordain; to
appoint or ordain beforehand by an unchangeable purpose or decree; to
pre<eum/lect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whom he did foreknow, he also did <qex>predestinate</qex> to be
conformed to the image of his Son.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. viii.
29.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To predetermine; foreordain; preordain; decree;
predestine; foredoom.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestination</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des`ti*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedestinatio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/destination</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
predestinating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Predestination</qex> had overruled their will.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The purpose of Good from
eternity respecting all events; especially, the preordination of men to
everlasting happiness or misery. See <er>Calvinism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ti*na*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Determining
beforehand; predestinating.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestinator</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ti*na`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/destinateur</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
predestinates, or foreordains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who holds to the doctrine of predestination; a
predestinarian.</def> <rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestine</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predestined</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Predestining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/destiner</ets>. See <er>Predestinate</er>.]</ety> <def>To
decree beforehand; to foreordain; to predestinate.</def>
<rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predestiny</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*des"ti*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Predestination.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predeterminable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*ter"mi*na*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable
of being determined beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predeterminate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*ter"mi*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Determined
beforehand; <as>as, the <ex>predeterminate</ex> counsel of
God</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predetermination</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*ter`mi*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/d<eacute/termination</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of
previous determination; a purpose formed beforehand; <as>as, the
<ex>predetermination</ex> of God's will</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predetermine</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*ter"mine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predetermined</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Predermining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>pre-</ets> +
<ets>determine</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/d<eacute/terminer</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
determine (something) beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To doom by previous decree; to foredoom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predetermine</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`de*ter"mine</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To determine
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedium</ets> a farm, estate: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/dial</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of land or
farms; landed; <as>as, <ex>predial</ex> estate</as>; that is, real
estate.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Attached to land or farms; <as>as, <ex>predial</ex>
slaves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Issuing or derived from land; <as>as,
<ex>predial</ex> tithes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prediastolic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*di`as*tol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Preceding the diastole of the heart; <as>as,
a <ex>prediastolic</ex> friction sound</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicability</ent><br/
<hw>Pred`i*ca*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being predicable, or affirmable of something, or attributed
to something.</def> <rj><au>Reid.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicable</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*ca*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/dicable</ets>, L. <ets>praedicabilis</ets> praiseworthy.
See <er>Predicate</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being predicated or
affirmed of something; affirmable; attributable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicable</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*ca*ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything
affirmable of another; especially, a general attribute or notion as
affirmable of, or applicable to, many individuals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>One of the five most general
relations of attributes involved in logical arrangements, namely, genus,
species, difference, property, and accident.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicament</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dic"a*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/dicament</ets>, L. <ets>praedicamentum</ets>. See
<er>Predicate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A class or kind described by
any definite marks; hence, condition; particular situation or state;
especially, an unfortunate or trying position or condition.</def>
<ldquo/O woeful sympathy; piteous <xex>predicament</xex>!<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>See <er>Category</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Category; condition; state; plight.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicamental</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dic`a*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a predicament.</def> <rj><au>John Hall
(1646).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicant</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*cant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedicans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>praedicare</ets>. See <er>Predicate</er>.]</ety> <def>Predicating;
affirming; declaring; proclaiming; hence; preaching.</def> <ldquo/The
Roman <xex>predicant</xex> orders.<rdquo/ <rj><au>N. Brit.
Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicant</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*cant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who predicates, affirms, or
proclaims; specifically, a preaching friar; a Dominican.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predicated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Predicating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedicatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praedicare</ets> to cry in public,
to proclaim. See <er>Preach</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To assert to
belong to something; to affirm (one thing of another); <as>as, to
<ex>predicate</ex> whiteness of snow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To found; to base.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Predicate</xex> is sometimes used in the United
States for <xex>found</xex> or <xex>base</xex>; as, to
<xex>predicate</xex> an argument <xex>on</xex> certain principles; to
<xex>predicate</xex> a statement <xex>on</xex> information received.
<xex>Predicate</xex> is a term in logic, and used only in a single case,
namely, when we affirm one thing <xex>of</xex> another.
<ldquo/Similitude is not <xex>predicated</xex> of essences or
substances, but of figures and qualities only.<rdquo/</note>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*cate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To affirm something of
another thing; to make an affirmation.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedicatum</ets>, neut. of <ets>praedicatus</ets>, p. p.
<ets>praedicare</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/dicat</ets>. See
<er>Predicate</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>That which is affirmed or denied of the subject.
In these propositions, <ldquo/<xex>Paper is white</xex>,<rdquo/
<ldquo/<xex>Ink is not white</xex>,<rdquo/ <xex>whiteness</xex> is the
<xex>predicate</xex> affirmed of paper and denied of ink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The word or words in a
proposition which express what is affirmed of the subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Affirmation; declaration.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*cate</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praedicatus</ets>, p.
p.]</ety> <def>Predicated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predication</ent><br/
<hw>Pred`i*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedicatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/dication</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of predicating, or of affirming one thing of
another; affirmation; assertion.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Preaching.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicative</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedicativus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Expressing affirmation or
predication; affirming; predicating, <as>as, a <ex>predicative</ex>
term</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pred"i*ca*tive*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predicatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pred"i*ca*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>praedicatorius</ets> praising.]</ety> <def>Affirmative;
positive.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1128 --></p>

<p><ent>Predicrotic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`di*crot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A term applied to the pulse wave sometimes
seen in a pulse curve or sphygmogram, between the apex of the curve and
the dicrotic wave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>predicrotic</qex> or tidal wave is best marked in a hard
pulse, <it>i. e.</it>, where the blood pressure is high.</q>
<rj><qau>Landois & Stirling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predict</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dict"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predicted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Predicting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praedictus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>praedicere</ets> to predict; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>dicere</ets> to say, tell. See <er>Diction</er>, and cf.
<er>Preach</er>.]</ety> <def>To tell or declare beforehand; to foretell;
to prophesy; to presage; <as>as, to <ex>predict</ex> misfortune; to
<ex>predict</ex> the return of a comet.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To foretell; prophesy; prognosticate; presage;
forebode; foreshow; bode.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predict</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dict"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A prediction.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predictable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dict"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
predicted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prediction</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dic"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedictio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/diction</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act of foretelling; also, that which is foretold;
prophecy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>predictions</qex> of cold and long winters.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prophecy; prognostication; foreboding; augury;
divination; soothsaying; vaticination.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predictional</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dic"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Prophetic;
prognostic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predictive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dict"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praedictivus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Foretelling; prophetic;
foreboding.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pre*dict"ive*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predictor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dict"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who predicts;
a foreteller.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predictory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dict"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Predictive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predigest</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`di*gest"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>To subject (food) to predigestion or artificial
digestion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predigestion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`di*ges"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Digestion too soon performed; hasty digestion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Artificial digestion of food for
use in illness or impaired digestion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predilect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`di*lect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To elect or
choose beforehand.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Walter
Harte.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predilection</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`di*lec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + L. <ets>dilectus</ets>, p. p. <ets>diligere</ets> to
prefer: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/dilection</ets>. See
<er>Diligent</er>.]</ety> <def>A previous liking; a prepossession of
mind in favor of something; predisposition to choose or like;
partiality.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prediscover</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`dis*cov"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To discover
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prediscovery</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`dis*cov"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A previous
discovery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predisponency</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`dis*po"nen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being predisposed; predisposition.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predisponent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`dis*po"nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disposing
beforehand; predisposing.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
predisposes.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Predisponent causes</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Predisposing causes</cref>, under <er>Predispose</er>.</cd>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predispose</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`dis*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predisposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Predisposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>dispose</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/disposer</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To dispose or
incline beforehand; to give a predisposition or bias to; <as>as, to
<ex>predispose</ex> the mind to friendship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make fit or susceptible beforehand; to give a
tendency to; <as>as, debility <ex>predisposes</ex> the body to
disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Predisposing causes</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>causes which render the body liable to disease; predisponent
causes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predisposition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dis`po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>disposition</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/disposition</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
predisposing, or the state of being predisposed; previous inclination,
tendency, or propensity; predilection; -- applied to the mind; <as>as, a
<ex>predisposition</ex> to anger</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> inclination; tendency; predilection;
propensity.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Previous fitness or adaptation to any change,
impression, or purpose; susceptibility; -- applied to material things;
<as>as, the <ex>predisposition</ex> of the body to
disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predominance</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom"i*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/dominance</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being predominant; superiority; ascendency; prevalence;
predomination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>predominance</qex> of conscience over interest.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>The superior influence of a
planet.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predominancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom"i*nan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Predominance.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predominant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom"i*nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/dominant</ets>. See <er>Predominante</er>.]</ety>
<def>Having the ascendency over others; superior in strength, influence,
or authority; prevailing; <as>as, a <ex>predominant</ex> color;
<ex>predominant</ex> excellence.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those help . . . were <qex>predominant</qex> in the king's
mind.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Foul subordination is <qex>predominant</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prevalent; superior; prevailing; ascendant;
ruling; reigning; controlling; overruling.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predominantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom"i*nant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a predominant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predominate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Predominated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Predominating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>pre-</ets> +
<ets>dominate</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/dominer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
be superior in number, strength, influence, or authority; to have
controlling power or influence; to prevail; to rule; to have the
mastery; <as>as, love <ex>predominated</ex> in her heart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Certain] rays may <qex>predominate</qex> over the rest.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir. I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predominate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom"i*nate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To rule over; to
overpower.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predomination</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dom`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/domination</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or state of
predominating; ascendency; predominance.</def> <rj><au>W.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predoom</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*doom"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
foredoom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predorsal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*dor"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the back; immediately in front, or on the
ventral side the dorsal part of the vertebral column.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Predy</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<ecir/t</ets> ready.]</ety> <def>Cleared and ready for
engagement, as a ship.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preedy</ent><br/
<hw>Preed"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With ease.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preef</ent><br/
<hw>Preef</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Proof.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preelect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/*lect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To elect
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preelection</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/*lec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Election
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preeminence</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/m"i*nence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/<eacute/minence</ets>, L. <ets>praeeminentia</ets>. See
<er>Pre<eum/minent</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
pre<eum/minent; superiority in prominence or in excellence; distinction
above others in quality, rank, etc.; rarely, in a bad sense, superiority
or notoriety in evil; <as>as, <ex>pre<eum/minence</ex> in
honor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pre<eum/minence</qex> of Christianity to any other
religious scheme.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Painful <qex>pre<eum/minence</qex>! yourself to view<br/
Above life's weakness, and its comforts too.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beneath the forehead's walled <qex>pre<eum/minence</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preeminent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/m"i*nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeminens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. <ets>praeminere</ets>
to be prominent, to surpass: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/<eacute/minent</ets>.
See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Eminent</er>.]</ety> <def>Eminent above
others; prominent among those who are eminent; superior in excellence;
surpassing, or taking precedence of, others; rarely, surpassing others
in evil, or in bad qualities; <as>as, <ex>pre<eum/minent</ex> in
guilt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In goodness and in power <qex>pre<eum/minent</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preeminently</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/m"i*nent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pre<eum/minent
degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preemploy</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/m*ploy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To employ
beforehand.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pre<eum/mployed</xex> by him.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preempt</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/mpt"</hw> <pr>(pr<esl/*<ecr/mt"; 215)</pr>, <pos>v. t. &
i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/mpted</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/mpting</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[See <er>Pre<eum/mption</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To settle
upon (public land) with a right of preemption, as under the laws of the
United States; to take by preemption.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>pre-empt</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To acquire or take for oneself first (an object or
right), thereby preventing others from acquiring; to appropriate; to
arrogate.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To supplant or take the place of (an action), due to
changes in circumstances or priorities; <as>as, the regular meeting was
<ex>preempted</ex> by an emergency conference</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To prevent or delay (an event) by acting beforehand;
to head off; to preclude.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preemption</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/mp"tion</hw> <pr>(?; 215)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>emption</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/emption</ets>. See <er>Redeem</er>.]</ety> <def>The act
or right of preemption.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The act or
right of purchasing before others.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The privilege
or prerogative formerly enjoyed by the king of buying provisions for his
household in preference to others.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The right of an actual settler upon public lands
(particularly those of the United States) to purchase a certain portion
at a fixed price in preference to all other applicants.</def>
<au>Abbott.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preemptioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/mp"tion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
holds a prior right to purchase certain public land.</def>
<rj><au>Abbott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preemptive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/mp"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to pre<eum/mption; having power to pre<eum/mpt;
pre<eum/mpting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preemptor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/mpt"or</hw> <pr>(?; 215)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>praeemptor</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who pre<eum/mpts; esp., one who
pre<eum/mpts public land.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preemptory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/mpt"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to pre<eum/mption.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preen</ent><br/
<hw>Preen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>pre<oacute/n</ets> a clasp, bodkin; akin to D. <ets>priem</ets>
punch, bodkin, awl, G. <ets>pfriem</ets>, Icel. <ets>prj<omac/nn</ets> a
knitting needle, pin, Dan. <ets>preen</ets> a bodkin, punch.]</ety>
<def>A forked tool used by clothiers in dressing cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preen</ent><br/
<hw>Preen</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Preened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Preen</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>; or cf. <er>Prune</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To dress
with, or as with, a preen; to trim or dress with the beak, as the
feathers; -- said of birds.</def> <rj><au>Derham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To trim up, as trees.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To dress (oneself) carefully
or stylishly; to primp.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pride (oneself) on one's accomplishments; to
congratulate (oneself).</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preen</ent><br/
<hw>Preen</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dress up neatly and smartly; to
make oneself well-groomed and well-dressed.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To feel proud of one's achievement; to swell or
gloat.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preengage</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/n*gage"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/ngaged</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/ngaging</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To engage by previous contract; to bind or
attach previously; to preoccupy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But he was <qex>pre<eum/ngaged</qex> by former ties.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preengagement</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/n*gage"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prior
engagement, obligation, or attachment, as by contract, promise, or
affection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My <qex>pre<eum/ngagements</qex> to other themes were not unknown
to those for whom I was to write.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preerect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/*rect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To erect
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prees</ent><br/
<hw>Prees</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Press; throng.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preestablish</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/s*tab"lish</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To establish
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preestablishment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/s*tab"lish*ment</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Settlement
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preeternity</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/*ter"ni*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Infinite
previous duration.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The world's
<xex>pre<eum/ternity</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexamination</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*am`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Previous examination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexamine</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*am"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/xamined</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pre<eum/xamining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To examine
beforehand.</def> <-- in orig, "perexamined" had no dieresis over the
second "e" because it was broken by a hyphen at the end of a line. Thus
this transcription is in such cases style of the original rather than
typographically identical. Similarly, above at "preengaged" and below,
at "preexist" --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexist</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*ist"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/xisted</conjf>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pre<eum/xisting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To
exist previously; to exist before something else; to exist before the
current activity commenced; <as>as, <ex>preexisting</ex> illnesses are
not covered by this insurance policy</as>.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>pre-exist</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexistence</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*ist"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Existence in a former state, or previous to something
else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wisdom declares her antiquity and <qex>pre<eum/xistence</qex> to
all the works of this earth.</q> <rj><qau>T. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Existence of the soul before its union with the
body; -- a doctrine held by certain philosophers.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexistency</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*ist"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Pre<eum/xistence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexistent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*ist"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Existing
previously; preceding existence; <as>as, a <ex>pre<eum/xistent</ex>
state</as>.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexistentism</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*ist"ent*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>The theory of a pre<eum/xistence of souls
before their association with human bodies.</def>
<rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexistimation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`<eum/x*is`ti*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Previous esteem or estimation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preexpectation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*<eum/x`pec*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Previous expectation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preface</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"ace</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/face</ets>; cf. Sp. <ets>prefacio</ets>,
<ets>prefacion</ets>, It. <ets>prefazio</ets>, <ets>prefazione</ets>;
all fr. L. <ets>praefatio</ets>, fr. <ets>praefari</ets> to speak or say
beforehand; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>fari</ets>, <ets>fatus</ets>,
to speak. See <er>Fate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something spoken as
introductory to a discourse, or written as introductory to a book or
essay; a proem; an introduction, or series of preliminary
remarks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This superficial tale<br/
Is but a <qex>preface</qex> of her worthy praise.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heaven's high behest no <qex>preface</qex> needs.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>The prelude or introduction
to the canon of the Mass.</def> <rj><au>Addis & Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proper preface</b></col> <fld>(Ch. of Eng. & Prot. Epis.
Ch.)</fld>, <cd>a portion of the communion service, preceding the prayer
of consecration, appointed for certain seasons.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Introduction; preliminary; preamble; proem;
prelude; prologue.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preface</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"ace</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prefaced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prefacing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To introduce by a preface; to
give a preface to; <as>as, to <ex>preface</ex> a book
discourse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preface</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"ace</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a preface.</def>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefacer</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"a*cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The writer of a
preface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefatorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pref`a*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Prefatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefatorily</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"a*to*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
prefatory manner; by way of preface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or
of the nature of, a preface; introductory to a book, essay, or
discourse; <as>as, <ex>prefatory</ex> remarks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>prefatory</qex> addition to the Creed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"fect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefectus</ets>, fr. <ets>praefectus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>praeficere</ets> to set over; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>facere</ets> to make: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/fet</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A Roman officer who controlled or superintended a
particular command, charge, department, etc.; <as>as, the
<ex>prefect</ex> of the aqueducts; the <ex>prefect</ex> of a camp, of a
fleet, of the city guard, of provisions; the pretorian <ex>prefect</ex>,
who was commander of the troops guarding the emperor's
person.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A superintendent of a department who has control of
its police establishment, together with extensive powers of municipal
regulation.</def> <mark>[France]</mark> <rj><au>Brande &
C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In the Greek and Roman Catholic churches, a title of
certain dignitaries below the rank of bishop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Apostolic prefect</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>,
<cd>the head of a mission, not of episcopal rank.</cd>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefectorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`fec*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a prefect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefectship</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"fect*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
jurisdiction of a prefect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefecture</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"fec*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefectura</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/fecture</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The office, position, or jurisdiction of a prefect; also, his
official residence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefecundation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fec`un*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A term collectively applied to the changes or
conditions preceding fecundation, especially to the changes which the
ovum undergoes before fecundation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefecundatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`fe*cun"da*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to prefecundation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Preferred</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Preferring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/f<eacute/rer</ets>, L. <ets>praeferre</ets>;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear or carry. See 1st
<er>Bear</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To carry or bring (something)
forward, or before one; hence, to bring for consideration, acceptance,
judgment, etc.; to offer; to present; to proffer; to address; -- said
especially of a request, prayer, petition, claim, charge, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He spake, and to her hand <qex>preferred</qex> the bowl.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Presently <qex>prefer</qex> his suit to C<ae/sar.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Three tongues <qex>prefer</qex> strange orisons on high.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To go before, or be before, in estimation; to
outrank; to surpass.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Though maidenhood
<xex>prefer</xex> bigamy.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to go before; hence, to advance before
others, as to an office or dignity; to raise; to exalt; to promote;
<as>as, to <ex>prefer</ex> an officer to the rank of
general</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would <qex>prefer</qex> him to a better place.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To set above or before something else in estimation,
favor, or liking; to regard or honor before another; to hold in greater
favor; to choose rather; -- often followed by <xex>to</xex>,
<xex>before</xex>, or <xex>above</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If I <qex>prefer</qex> not Jerusalem above my chief joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. cxxxvii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Preferred</qex> an infamous peace before a most just war.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Preferred stock</b></col>, <cd>stock which takes a
dividend before other capital stock; -- called also <altname>preference
stock</altname> and <altname>preferential stock</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To choose; elect. See <er>Choose</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferability</ent><br/
<hw>Pref`er*a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being preferable; preferableness.</def> <rj><au>J. S.
Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferable</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"er*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/f<eacute/rable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Worthy to be preferred
or chosen before something else; more desirable; <as>as, a
<ex>preferable</ex> scheme</as>.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferableness</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"er*a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being preferable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferably</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"er*a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In preference; by
choice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To choose Plautus <qex>preferably</qex> to Terence.</q>
<rj><qau>Dennis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preference</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"er*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/f<eacute/rence</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
Preferring, or the state of being preferred; the setting of one thing
before another; precedence; higher estimation; predilection; choice;
also, the power or opportunity of choosing; <as>as, to give him his
<ex>preference</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Leave the critics on either side to contend about the
<qex>preference</qex> due to this or that sort of poetry.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Knowledge of things alone gives a value to our reasonings, and
<qex>preference</qex> of one man's knowledge over another's.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is preferred; the object of choice or
superior favor; as, which is your <xex>preference</xex>?</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferential</ent><br/
<hw>Pref`er*en"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Giving,
indicating, or having a preference or precedence; <as>as, a
<ex>preferential</ex> claim; <ex>preferential</ex>
shares.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferential voting</ent><br/
<hw>Preferential voting</hw>. <fld>(Political Science)</fld> <def>A
system of voting, as at primaries, in which the voters are allowed to
indicate on their ballots their preference (usually their first and
second choices) between two or more candidates for an office, so that if
no candidate receives a majority of first choices the one receiving the
greatest number of first and second choices together in nominated or
elected.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fer"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of choosing, or the state of being chosen; preference.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Natural <qex>preferment</qex> of the one . . . before the
other.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of preferring, or advancing in dignity or
office; the state of being advanced; promotion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Neither royal blandishments nor promises of valuable
<qex>preferment</qex> had been spared.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A position or office of honor or profit; <as>as, the
<ex>preferments</ex> of the church</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preferrer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fer"rer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prefers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefidence</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"i*dence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being prefident.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefident</ent><br/
<hw>Pref"i*dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>praefidens</ets> overconfident. See <er>Pre-</er>, and
<er>Confident</er>.]</ety> <def>Trusting beforehand; hence,
overconfident.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefigurate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fig"u*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefiguratus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Prefigure</er>.]</ety> <def>To
prefigure.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Grafton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefiguration</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fig`u*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefiguratio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of prefiguring, or the
state of being prefigured.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A variety of prophecies and <qex>prefigurations</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Norris.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefigurative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fig"ur*a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Showing by
prefiguration.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>prefigurative</xex>
atonement.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp. Horne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefigure</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fig"ure</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prefigured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Prefiguring</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[F. <ets>pr<eacute/figurer</ets>, or L. <ets>praefigurare</ets>,
<ets>praefiguratum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>figurare</ets>
to figure. See <er>Figure</er>, and cf. <er>Prefigurate</er>.]</ety>
<def>To show, suggest, or announce, by antecedent types and similitudes;
to foreshadow.</def> <ldquo/Whom all the various types
<xex>prefigured</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefigurement</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fig"ure*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
prefiguring; prefiguration; also, that which is prefigured.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefine</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefinire</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>finire</ets> to
limit, determine: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/finer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
limit beforehand.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Knolles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1129 --></p>

<p><ent>Prefinite</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fi"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefinitus</ets>, p. p.]</ety> <def>Prearranged.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/ Set and <xex>prefinite</xex> time.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefinition</ent><br/
<hw>Pref`i*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefinitio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Previous limitation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fotherby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefix</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fix"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prefixed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prefixing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefixus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praefigere</ets> to fix or fasten
before; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>figere</ets> to fix: cf. F.
<ets>pr</ets><eacute/<ets>fix</ets> fixed beforehand, determined,
<ets>pr</ets><eacute/<ets>fixer</ets> to prefix. See Fix.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To put or fix before, or at the beginning of,
another thing; <as>as, to <ex>prefix</ex> a syllable to a word, or a
condition to an agreement</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set or appoint beforehand; to settle or establish
antecedently.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/ <xex>Prefixed</xex>
bounds. <rdquo/ <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And now he hath to her <qex>prefixt</qex> a day.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefix</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"fix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr</ets><eacute/<ets>fixe</ets>.]</ety> <def>That which is
prefixed; esp., one or more letters or syllables combined or united with
the beginning of a word to modify its signification; <as>as,
<ex>pre-</ex> in <ex>pre</ex>fix, <ex>con-</ex> in
<ex>con</ex>jure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefixion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fix"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>prefixion</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of prefixing.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefloration</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`flo*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + L. <ets>flos</ets>, <ets>floris</ets>, flower.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Aestivation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefoliation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fo`li*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + L. <ets>folium</ets> leaf.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Vernation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preform</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*form"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeformare</ets>. See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Form</er>.]</ety>
<def>To form beforehand, or for special ends.</def> <ldquo/Their natures
and <xex>preformed</xex> faculties. <rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preformation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`for*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>An old theory of the pre<eum/xistence of germs. Cf.
<er>Embo<icir/tement</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preformative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*form"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A formative
letter at the beginning of a word.</def> <rj><au>M.
Stuart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefrontal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*fron"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat. &
Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated in front of the frontal bone, or the frontal
region of the skull; ectethmoid, as a certain bone in the nasal capsule
of many animals, and certain scales of reptiles and fishes.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A prefrontal bone or scale.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prefulgency</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ful"gen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praefulgens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>praefulgere</ets> to shine forth.
See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Fulgent</er>.]</ety> <def>Superior brightness
or effulgency.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregage</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*gage"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
pre<eum/ngage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preglacial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*gla"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Prior to the glacial or drift period.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnable</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"na*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>, <ety>[F.
<ets>prenable</ets>. See <er>Impregnable</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being entered, taken, or captured; expugnable; <as>as, a
<ex>pregnable</ex> fort</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnance</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pregnancy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnancy</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
condition of being pregnant; the state of being with young.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: The quality of being heavy with
important contents, issue, significance, etc.; unusual consequence or
capacity; fertility.</def> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnant</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praegnans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>genere</ets>, <ets>gignere</ets>, to beget: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/gnant</ets>. See <er>Gender</er>, 2d
<er>Kin</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Being with young, as a female; having conceived;
great with young; breeding; teeming; gravid; preparing to bring
forth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Heavy with important contents, significance, or
issue; full of consequence or results; weighty; <as>as,
<ex>pregnant</ex> replies</as>.</def> <ldquo/ A <xex>pregnant</xex>
argument.<rdquo/ <au>Prynne.</au> <ldquo/ A <xex>pregnant</xex>
brevity.<rdquo/<-- pregnant silence --> <rj><au>E.
Everett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Full of promise; abounding in ability, resources,
etc.; <as>as, a <ex>pregnant</ex> youth</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherein the <qex>pregnant</qex> enemy does much.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pregnant construction</b></col> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>,
<cd>one in which more is implied than is said; as, the beasts
<xex>trembled forth</xex> from their dens, that is, came forth trembling
with fright.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnant</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pregnant woman.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnant</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nant</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>prenant</ets> taking.
Cf. <er>Pregnable</er>.]</ety> <def>Affording entrance; receptive;
yielding; willing; open; prompt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/
<xex>Pregnant</xex> to good pity.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnantly</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pregnant manner;
fruitfully; significantly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregnantly</ent><br/
<hw>Preg"nant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Unresistingly; openly;
hence, clearly; evidently.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregravate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"gra*vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praegravatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praegravare</ets> to be heavy
upon, fr. <ets>praegravis</ets> very heavy.]</ety> <def>To bear down; to
depress.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregravitate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*grav"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To descend
by gravity; to sink.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregustant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*gus"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praegustans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>praegustare</ets> to taste
beforehand; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>gustare</ets> to taste.]</ety>
<def>Tasting beforehand; having a foretaste.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ed. Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pregustation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`gus*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
tasting beforehand; foretaste.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr.
Walker (1678).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehallux</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*hal"lux</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Hallux</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>An
extra first toe, or rudiment of a toe, on the preaxial side of the
hallux.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehend</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*hend"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prehendere</ets>. See <er>Prehensile</er>.]</ety> <def>To lay hold
of; to seize.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Middleton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehensi-ble</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*hen"si-ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/hensible</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
seized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehensile</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*hen"sile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prehensus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>prehendere</ets> to lay hold of,
seize; <ets>pre-</ets> (equiv. to <ets>prae</ets> before) +
<ets>hendere</ets> (in comp.), akin to E. <ets>get</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/hensile</ets>. See <er>Get</er>, and cf. <er>Prison</er>,
<er>Prize</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Adapted to seize or grasp;
seizing; grasping; <as>as, the <ex>prehensile</ex> tail of a
monkey</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehension</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*hen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prehensio</ets>; cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/hension</ets>. See
<er>Prehensile</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of taking hold, seizing, or
grasping, as with the hand or other member.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehensory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*hen"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adapted to seize
or grasp; prehensile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehistoric</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`his*tor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a period before written history begins; <as>as, the
<ex>prehistoric</ex> ages; <ex>prehistoric</ex> man.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehnite</ent><br/
<hw>Prehn"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from the
German Colonel <ets>Prehn</ets>, who first found it.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A pale green mineral occurring in crystalline
aggregates having a botryoidal or mammillary structure, and rarely in
distinct crystals. It is a hydrous silicate of alumina and
lime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prehnitic</ent><br/
<hw>Prehn*it"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, a tetrabasic acid of benzene
obtained as a white crystalline substance; -- probably so called from
the resemblance of the wartlike crystals to the mammill<ae/ on the
surface of prehnite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preignition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ig*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Engin.)</fld>
<def>Ignition in an internal-combustion engine while the inlet valve is
open or before compression is completed.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preindesignate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`in*des"ig*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Logic.)</fld> <def>Having no sign expressive of quantity;
indefinite. See <er>Predesignate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preindispose</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*in`dis*pose"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
render indisposed beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preinstruct</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`in*struct"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preinstructed</conjf>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Preinstructing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To
instruct previously or beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preintimation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*in`ti*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
intimation; a suggestion beforehand.</def> <rj><au>T.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pre-isolate</ent><br/
<ent>preisolate</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pre-isolate</hw>, <hw>preisolate</hw></mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos>
<fld>(chemistry)</fld> <def>To isolate{3} (a substance)
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudge</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*judge"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prejudged</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prejudging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>pre</ets>
+ <ets>judge</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/juger</ets>. Cf.
<er>Prejudicate</er>, <er>Prejudice</er>.]</ety> <def>To judge before
hearing, or before full and sufficient examination; to decide or
sentence by anticipation; to condemn beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The committee of council hath <qex>prejudged</qex> the whole case,
by calling the united sense of both houses of Parliament<ldquo/ a
universal clamor.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudgment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*judg"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
prejudging; decision before sufficient examination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*ca*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prejudice;
prepossession.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir. H.
Blount.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudical</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the determination of some matter not previously decided; <as>as, a
<ex>prejudical</ex> inquiry or action at law</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*cant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praejudicans</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Influenced by prejudice;
biased.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/ With not too hasty and
<xex>prejudicant</xex> ears.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praejudicatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praejudicare</ets> to prejudge;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>judicare</ets> to judge. See
<er>Judge</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Formed before due
examination.</def> <ldquo/Ignorance and <xex>prejudicate</xex>
opinions.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Biased by opinions formed prematurely;
prejudiced.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Prejudicate</xex> readers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prejudicated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prejudicating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Prejudge</er>.]</ety> <def>To determine beforehand, especially to
disadvantage; to prejudge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our dearest friend<br/
<qex>Prejudicates</qex> the business.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*cate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To prejudge.</def>
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicately</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*cate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With
prejudice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudication</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju`di*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of prejudicating, or of judging without due examination of
facts and evidence; prejudgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A preliminary
inquiry and determination about something which belongs to a matter in
dispute.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A previous treatment and decision of a
point; a precedent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ju"di*ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Forming a
judgment without due examination; prejudging.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudice</ent><br/
<hw>Prej"u*dice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/judice</ets>, L. <ets>praejudicium</ets>; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>judicium</ets> judgment. See <er>Prejudicate</er>,
<er>Judicial</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Foresight.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Naught might hinder his quick <qex>prejudize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An opinion or judgment formed without due
examination; prejudgment; a leaning toward one side of a question from
other considerations than those belonging to it; an unreasonable
predilection for, or objection against, anything; especially, an opinion
or leaning adverse to anything, without just grounds, or before
sufficient knowledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though often misled by <qex>prejudice</qex> and passion, he was
emphatically an honest man.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A bias on the part of judge, juror,
or witness which interferes with fairness of judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Mischief; hurt; damage; injury; detriment.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>England and France might, through their amity,<br/
Breed him some <qex>prejudice</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prejudgment; prepossession; bias; harm; hurt;
damage; detriment; mischief; disadvantage.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudice</ent><br/
<hw>Prej"u*dice</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prejudiced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prejudicing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/judicier</ets>. See <er>Prejudice</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to have prejudice; to
prepossess with opinions formed without due knowledge or examination; to
bias the mind of, by hasty and incorrect notions; to give an
unreasonable bent to, as to one side or the other of a cause; <as>as, to
<ex>prejudice</ex> a critic or a juryman</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Suffer not any beloved study to <qex>prejudice</qex> your mind so
far as to despise all other learning.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Watts</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To obstruct or injure by prejudices, or by previous
bias of the mind; hence, generally, to hurt; to damage; to injure; to
impair; <as>as, to <ex>prejudice</ex> a good cause</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Seek how may <qex>prejudice</qex> the foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prejudicial</ent><br/
<hw>Prej`u*di"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praejudicialis</ets> belonging to a preceding judgment: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/judiciel</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Biased, possessed, or blinded by prejudices; <as>as,
to look with a <ex>prejudicial</ex> eye</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holyday.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tending to obstruct or impair; hurtful; injurious;
disadvantageous; detrimental.</def> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His going away . . . was most <qex>prejudicial</qex> and most
ruinous to the king's affairs.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Prej`u*di"cial*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Prej`u*di"cial*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preknowledge</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*knowl"edge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prior
knowledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelacy</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prelacies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL. <ets>praelatia</ets>.
See <er>Prelate</er>; cf. <er>Prelaty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
office or dignity of a prelate; church government by prelates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prelacies may be termed the greater benefices.</q>
<rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The order of prelates, taken collectively; the body
of ecclesiastical dignitaries.</def> <ldquo/Divers of the reverend
<xex>prelacy</xex>, and other most judicious men.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"lal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prelum</ets>
a press.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to printing; typographical.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelate</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"ate</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/lat</ets>, LL. <ets>praelatus</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>praelatus</ets>, used as p. p. of <ets>praeferre</ets> to prefer,
but from a different root. See <er>Elate</er>.]</ety> <def>A clergyman
of a superior order, as an archbishop or a bishop, having authority over
the lower clergy; a dignitary of the church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word and the words derived from it are often used
invidiously, in English ecclesiastical history, by dissenters,
respecting the Established Church system.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hear him but reason in divinity, . . . <br/
You would desire the king were made a <qex>prelate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelate</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act as a
prelate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Right <qex>prelating</qex> is busy laboring, and not lording.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelateity</ent><br/
<hw>Prel`a*te"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prelacy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelateship</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"ate*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
prelate.</def> <rj><au>Harmar.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatess</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*tess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who is a
prelate; the wife of a prelate.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*la"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Prelatical.</def>
<rj><au>Beaconsfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatical</ent><br/
<ent>Prelatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*lat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre*lat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to prelates or
prelacy; <as>as, <ex>prelatical</ex> authority</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatically</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lat"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prelatical manner;
with reference to prelates.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The last Georgic was a good <qex>prelude</qex> to the
Aeneis.</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praelatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/lation</ets>. See
<er>Prelate</er>, and cf. <er>Prefer</er>.]</ety> <def>The setting of
one above another; preference.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatism</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prelacy;
episcopacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatist</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who supports
of advocates prelacy, or the government of the church by prelates;
hence, a high-churchman.</def> <rj><au>Hume.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am an Episcopalian, but not a <qex>prelatist</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatize</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prelatized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prelatizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
bring under the influence of prelacy.</def>
<rj><au>Palfrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatize</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*tize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To uphold or encourage
prelacy; to exercise prelatical functions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An episcopacy that began then to <qex>prelatize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatry</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prelaty;
prelacy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelatureship</ent><br/
<ent>Prelature</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prel"a*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <hw>Prel"a*ture*ship</hw>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pr<eacute/lature</ets>, or LL.
<ets>praelatura</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state or dignity of a prelate;
prelacy.</def> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelaty</ent><br/
<hw>Prel"a*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prelacy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prelected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prelecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praelectus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>praelegere</ets> to read before. See <er>Pre-</er>, and
<er>Lection</er>.]</ety> <def>To read publicly, as a lecture or
discourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lect"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To discourse publicly; to
lecture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spitting . . . was publicly <qex>prelected</qex> upon.</q>
<rj><qau>De. Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>prelect</qex> upon the military art.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Horsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelection</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praelectio</ets>.]</ety> <def>A lecture or discourse read in public
or to a select company.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>prelections</xex> of
Faber.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelector</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lec"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praelector</ets>.]</ety> <def>A reader of lectures or discourses; a
lecturer.</def> <rj><au>Sheldon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelibation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`li*ba"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praelibatio</ets>, fr. <ets>praelibare</ets> to taste beforehand:
cf. F. <ets>prelibation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A tasting
beforehand, or by anticipation; a foretaste; <as>as, a
<ex>prelibation</ex> of heavenly bliss</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pouring out, or libation, before
tasting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preliminarily</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lim"i*na*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
preliminary manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preliminary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lim"i*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre</ets> + L. <ets>liminaris</ets> belonging to a threshold, fr.
<ets>limen</ets>, <ets>liminis</ets>, threshold, entrance: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/liminaire</ets>. Cf. <er>Limit</er>.]</ety>
<def>Introductory; previous; preceding the main discourse or business;
prefatory; <as>as, <ex>preliminary</ex> observations to a discourse or
book; <ex>preliminary</ex> articles to a treaty; <ex>preliminary</ex>
measures; <ex>preliminary</ex> examinations.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Introductory; preparatory; prefatory; proemial;
previous; prior; precedent; antecedent.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preliminary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lim"i*na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Preliminaries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>That which precedes
the main discourse, work, design, or business; something introductory or
preparatory; <as>as, the <ex>preliminaries</ex> to a negotiation or
duel; to take one's <ex>preliminaries</ex> the year before entering
college.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Introduction; preface; prelude.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelimit</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lim"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To limit
previously.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelook</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*look"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To look forward.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Surrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelude</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"lude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/lude</ets> (cf. It. <ets>preludio</ets>, LL.
<ets>praeludium</ets>), fr. L. <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>ludus</ets>
play. See <er>Prelude</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>An introductory
performance, preceding and preparing for the principal matter; a
preliminary part, movement, strain, etc.; especially <fld>(Mus.)</fld>,
a strain introducing the theme or chief subject; a movement introductory
to a fugue, yet independent; -- with recent composers often synonymous
with <xex>overture</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The last Georgic was a good <qex>prelude</qex> to the Aenis</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The cause is more than the <qex>prelude</qex>, the effect is more
than the sequel, of the fact.</q> <rj><qau>Whewell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Preface; introduction; preliminary; preamble;
forerunner; harbinger; precursor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelude</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lude"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preluded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preluding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praeludere</ets>,
<ets>praelusum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>ludere</ets> to
play: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/luder</ets>. See <er>Ludicrous</er>.]</ety>
<def>To play an introduction or prelude; to give a prefatory
performance; to serve as prelude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The musicians <qex>preluded</qex> on their instruments.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir. W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are <qex>preluding</qex> too largely, and must come at once to
the point.</q> <rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelude</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lude"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To introduce with
a previous performance; to play or perform a prelude to; <as>as, to
<ex>prelude</ex> a concert with a lively air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To serve as prelude to; to precede as
introductory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Music] <qex>preluding</qex> some great tragedy.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preluder</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lud"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, preludes; one who plays a prelude.</def>
<rj><au>Mason.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preludial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lud"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a prelude; of the nature of a prelude; introductory.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preludious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lud"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Preludial.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelumbar</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lum"bar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated immediately in front of the loins; -- applied to the
dorsal part of the abdomen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelusive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lu"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prelude</er>.]</ety> <def>Of the nature of a prelude; introductory;
indicating that something of a like kind is to follow.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Prelusive</xex> drops.<rdquo/ <au>Thomson.</au>
--<wordforms><wf>Pre*lu"sive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelusorily</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lu"so*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
prelusory way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prelusory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*lu"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Introductory;
prelusive.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1130 --></p>

<p><ent>Premature</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ma*ture"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praematurus</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>maturus</ets>
ripe. See <er>Mature</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Mature or ripe before
the proper time; <as>as, the <ex>premature</ex> fruits of a
hotbed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Happening, arriving, existing, or performed before
the proper or usual time; adopted too soon; too early; untimely; <as>as,
a <ex>premature</ex> fall of snow; a <ex>premature</ex> birth; a
<ex>premature</ex> opinion; <ex>premature</ex> decay.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Arriving or received without due authentication or
evidence; <as>as, a <ex>premature</ex> report</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre`ma*ture"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre`ma*ture"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prematurity</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ma*tu"ri*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/maturit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state
of being premature; early, or untimely, ripeness; <as>as, the
<ex>prematurity</ex> of genius</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premaxilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre"max*il"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Premaxill<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Maxilla</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A bone on either side of the middle line between the nose and
mouth, forming the anterior part of each half of the upper jawbone; the
intermaxilla. In man the premaxill<ae/ become united and form the
incisor part of the maxillary bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the maxillary bones; pertaining to the
premaxill<ae/; intermaxillary.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
premaxilla.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premediate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*me"di*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
advocate.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premeditate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*med"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Premeditated</conjf>
<pr>(-t<amac/`t?d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Premeditating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemeditatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praemeditari</ets>;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>meditari</ets> to meditate. See
<er>Meditate</er>.]</ety> <def>To think on, and revolve in the mind,
beforehand; to contrive and design previously; <as>as, to
<ex>premeditate</ex> robbery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With words <qex>premeditated</qex> thus he said.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premeditate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*med"i*tate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To think, consider,
deliberate, or revolve in the mind, beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premeditate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*med"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemeditatus</ets>, p. p.]</ety> <def>Premeditated;
deliberate.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premeditately</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*med"i*tate*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With
premeditation.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premeditation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*med`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemeditatio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/m<eacute/ditation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of
meditating or contriving beforehand; previous deliberation;
forethought.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premerit</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mer"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To merit or
deserve beforehand.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Eikon
Basilike.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premiant</ent><br/
<ent>Premial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre"mi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre"mi*ant</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praemialis</ets>. See
<er>Premium</er>.]</ety> <def>Serving to reward; rewarding.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premices</ent><br/
<hw>Prem"i*ces</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/mices</ets>, L. <ets>primitiae</ets>. See
<er>Primitia</er>.]</ety> <def>First fruits.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premier</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"mi*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>premier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>primarius</ets> of the first rank,
principal, fr. <ets>primus</ets> the first. See <er>Primary</er>,
<er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>First; chief;
principal; <as>as, the <ex>premier</ex> place; <ex>premier</ex>
minister.</as></def> <rj><au>Camden. Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Most ancient; -- said of the peer bearing the oldest
title of his degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premier</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"mi*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The first minister
of state; the prime minister.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premiere</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*mi<egrave/re"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. fem.</pos>
<ety>[F., prop. fem. of <ets>premier</ets> first. See <er>Premier</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>First; chief; <as>as, a
<ex>premi<egrave/re</ex> danseuse</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.
fem.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-mi<egrave/res</plw> <pr>(<it>F.</it>
pr<eit/*my<acir/r")</pr></plu>. <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The leading woman of a
group, esp. in a theatrical cast.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A first
performance, as of a play; a first night.</def></def2><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>premiere</ent><br/
<hw>premiere</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to be performed for
the first time; of a play, ballet, composition, movie, or other literary
work.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> premier.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>premiere</ent><br/
<hw>premiere</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to perform (a play,
ballet, or composition) for the first time.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> premier.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premiership</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"mi*er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of the
premier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premillennial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`mil*len"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Previous to
the millennium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"mi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemiosus</ets>, fr. <ets>praemium</ets> a premium.]</ety>
<def>Rich in gifts.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Clarke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premise</ent><br/
<hw>Prem"ise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Premises</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <altsp>[Written also, less
properly, <asp>premiss</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/misse</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praemissus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>praemittere</ets> to send before; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>mittere</ets> to send. See <er>Mission</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A proposition antecedently supposed or proved; something previously
stated or assumed as the basis of further argument; a condition; a
supposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>premises</qex> observed,<br/
Thy will by my performance shall be served.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>Either of the first two
propositions of a syllogism, from which the conclusion is
drawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><ldquo/All sinners deserve punishment: A B is a
sinner.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>These propositions, which are the <xex>premises</xex>, being true or
admitted, the conclusion follows, that A B deserves
punishment.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><q>While the <qex>premises</qex> stand firm, it is impossible to
shake the conclusion.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Matters previously
stated or set forth; esp., that part in the beginning of a deed, the
office of which is to express the grantor and grantee, and the land or
thing granted or conveyed, and all that precedes the
<xex>habendum</xex>; the thing demised or granted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A piece of real estate; a building
and its adjuncts; <as>as, to lease <ex>premises</ex>; to trespass on
another's <ex>premises</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premise</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Premised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Premising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From L.
<ets>praemissus</ets>, p. p., or E. <ets>premise</ets>, n. See
<er>Premise</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To send before
the time, or beforehand; hence, to cause to be before something else; to
employ previously.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>premised</qex> flames of the last day.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If venesection and a cathartic be <qex>premised</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>E. Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set forth beforehand, or as introductory to the
main subject; to offer previously, as something to explain or aid in
understanding what follows; especially, to lay down premises or first
propositions, on which rest the subsequent reasonings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>premise</qex> these particulars that the reader may know
that I enter upon it as a very ungrateful task.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premise</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a
premise; to set forth something as a premise.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premiss</ent><br/
<hw>Prem"iss</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Premise.</def>
<rj><au>Whately. I. Watts</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premit</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To premise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premium</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"mi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Premiums</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>praemium</ets>,
originally, what one has got before or better than others;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>emere</ets> to take, buy. See
<er>Redeem</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A reward or recompense; a prize
to be won by being before another, or others, in a competition; reward
or prize to be adjudged; a bounty; <as>as, a <ex>premium</ex> for good
behavior or scholarship, for discoveries, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> To think it not the necessity, but the <qex>premium</qex> and
privilege of life, to eat and sleep without any regard to glory.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law that obliges parishes to support the poor offers a
<qex>premium</qex> for the encouragement of idleness.</q>
<rj><qau>Franklin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something offered or given for the loan of money;
bonus; -- sometimes synonymous with <xex>interest</xex>, but generally
signifying a sum in addition to the capital.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>People were tempted to lend, by great <qex>premiums</qex> and
large interest.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sum of money paid to underwriters for insurance,
or for undertaking to indemnify for losses of any kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sum in advance of, or in addition to, the nominal
or par value of anything; <as>as, gold was at a <ex>premium</ex>; he
sold his stock at a <ex>premium</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premolar</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mo"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the molar teeth.</def> --<def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>An anterior molar tooth which has replaced a deciduous molar. See
<er>Tooth</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonish</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Premonished</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Premonishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>monish</ets>: cf. L.
<ets>praemonere</ets>.]</ety> <def>To forewarn; to admonish
beforehand.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Herrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To teach, and to <qex>premonish</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bk. of Com.
Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonishment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"ish*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
warning or admonition; forewarning.</def> <rj><au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`mo*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemonitio</ets>. See <er>Premonish</er>.]</ety> <def>Previous
warning, notice, or information; forewarning; <as>as, a
<ex>premonition</ex> of danger</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonitor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemonitor</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, gives
premonition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"i*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemonitorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Giving previous warning or
notice; <as>as, <ex>premonitory</ex> symptoms of disease</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre*mon"i*to*ri*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonstrant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"strant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Premonstratensian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonstrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"strate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemonstratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praemonstrare</ets>;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>monstrate</ets> to show.]</ety> <def>To
show beforehand; to foreshow.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonstratensian</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon`stra*ten"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/montr<eacute/</ets>, fr.
<ets>Pr<eacute/montr<eacute/</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pratum
monstratum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>One of a religious
order of regular canons founded by St. Norbert at
Pr<eacute/montr<eacute/, in France, in 1119. The members of the order
are called also <altnpluf>White Canons</altnpluf>,
<altnpluf>Norbertines</altnpluf>, and
<altnpluf>Premonstrants</altnpluf>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonstration</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`mon*stra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemonstratio</ets>.]</ety> <def>A showing beforehand;
foreshowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premonstrator</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mon"stra*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemonstrator</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which,
premonstrates.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premorse</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*morse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemorsus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praemordere</ets> to bite off;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>mordere</ets> to bite.]</ety>
<def>Terminated abruptly, or as it bitten off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Premorse root</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Premorse leaves</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>such as
have an abrupt, ragged, and irregular termination, as if bitten off
short.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premosaic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`mo*sa"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to the
time before Moses; <as>as, <ex>premosaic</ex> history</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premotion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mo"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>motion</ets>.]</ety> <def>Previous motion or
excitement to action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premunire</ent><br/
<hw>Prem`u*ni"re</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pr<ae/munire</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premunite</ent><br/
<hw>Prem`u*nite"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemunitus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praemunire</ets> to fortify in
front; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>munire</ets> to fortify.]</ety>
<def>To fortify beforehand; to guard against objection.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fotherby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premunition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`mu*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praemunitio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/munition</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act of fortifying or guarding against objections.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Premunitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*mu"ni*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a premunire; <as>as, a <ex>premunitory</ex>
process</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenasal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*na"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the nose, or in front of the nasal
chambers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenatal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*na"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being or happening
before birth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prender</ent><br/
<hw>Pren"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prendre</ets> to take, fr. L. <ets>prehendere</ets> to take.]</ety>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The power or right of taking a thing before it is
offered.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenomen</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*no"men</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pr<ae/nomen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenominal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nom"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Serving as a
prefix in a compound name.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenominate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nom"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praenominatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praenominare</ets> to give the
prenomen to, to prenominate, fr. <ets>praenomen</ets> prenomen.]</ety>
<def>Forenamed; named beforehand.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Prenominate</xex> crimes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenominate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nom"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To forename;
to name beforehand; to tell by name beforehand.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenomination</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nom`i*na*tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
prenominating; privilege of being named first.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenostic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nos"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praenoscere</ets> to foreknow; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>noscere</ets>, <ets>notum</ets>, to know.]</ety> <def>A prognostic;
an omen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenote</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*note"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praenotare</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>notare</ets> to
note.]</ety> <def>To note or designate beforehand.</def>
<rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenotion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*no"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praenotio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/notion</ets>. See
<er>Prenostic</er>.]</ety> <def>A notice or notion which precedes
something else in time; previous notion or thought; foreknowledge.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prensation</ent><br/
<hw>Pren*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prensatio</ets>, from <ets>prensare</ets>, <ets>prehensare</ets>,
v. freq. from <ets>prehendere</ets> to seize.]</ety> <def>The act of
seizing with violence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow
.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prentice</ent><br/
<hw>Pren"tice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>apprentice</ets>.]</ety> <def>An apprentice.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Colloq.]</mark> <au>Piers Plowman.</au> <ldquo/My accuser is my
<xex>prentice</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenticehood</ent><br/
<hw>Pren"tice*hood</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Apprenticehood.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This jolly prentice with his master bode<br/
Till he was out nigh of his <qex>prenticehood</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenticeship</ent><br/
<hw>Pren"tice*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Apprenticeship.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He served a <qex>prenticeship</qex> who sets up shop.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenunciation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nun`ci*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praenunciatio</ets>, fr. <ets>praenunciare</ets> to announce
beforehand. See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Announce</er>.]</ety> <def>The
act of announcing or proclaiming beforehand.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prenuncious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*nun"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praenuncius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Announcing beforehand;
presaging.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Blount.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoblongata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*ob`lon*ga"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Oblongata</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The anterior part of the medulla oblongata.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preobtain</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ob*tain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To obtain
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoccupancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*oc"cu*pan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Preoccupate</er>.]</ety> <def>The act or right of taking possession
before another; <as>as, the <ex>preoccupancy</ex> of wild
land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoccupate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*oc"cu*pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeoccupatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praeoccupare</ets> to preoccupy.
See <er>Preoccupy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To anticipate; to take before.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Fear <xex>preoccupateth</xex> it
[death].<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To prepossess; to prejudice.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoccupation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*oc`cu*pa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeoccupatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/occupation</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of preoccupying, or taking possession of
beforehand; the state of being preoccupied; prepossession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anticipation of objections.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoccupy</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*oc"cu*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preoccupied</conjf> <pr>(-p<imac/d)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Preoccupying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/occuper</ets>. See <er>Preoccupate</er>,
<er>Occupy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take possession of before
another; <as>as, to <ex>preoccupy</ex> a country not before
held</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To prepossess; to engage, occupy, or engross the
attention of, beforehand; hence, to prejudice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I Think it more respectful to the reader to leave something to
reflections than to <qex>preoccupy</qex> his judgment.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preocular</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*oc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Placed just in front of the eyes, as the antenn<ae/ of certain
insects.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the scales just in
front of the eye of a reptile or fish.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preominate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*om"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To ominate
beforehand; to portend.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preopercular</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`o*per"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the operculum; pertaining to the
preoperculum.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The
preoperculum.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoperculum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre`o*per"cu*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The anterior opercular bone in
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preopinion</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`o*pin"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Opinion
previously formed; prepossession; prejudice.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoption</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*op"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Right of first
choice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*o"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of, or anterior to, the mouth; <as>as,
<ex>preoral</ex> bands</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preorbital</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*or"bit*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front or the orbit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preordain</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`or*dain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre + ordain</ets>: cf. L. <ets>praeordinare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
ordain or appoint beforehand: to predetermine: to foreordain.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preorder</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*or"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To order to
arrange beforehand; to foreordain.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preordinance</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*or"di*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Antecedent
decree or determination.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preordinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*or"di*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeordinatus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Preordain</er>.]</ety>
<def>Preordained.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Elyot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preordination</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*or`di*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/ordination</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of foreordaining:
previous determination.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>preordination</xex> of
God.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
prepared.</def> <ldquo/Medicine <xex>preparable</xex> by art.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparation</ent><br/
<hw>Prep`a*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/paration</ets>, L. <ets>praeparatio</ets>. See
<er>Prepare</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of preparing or fitting beforehand for a
particular purpose, use, service, or condition; previous arrangement or
adaptation; a making ready; <as>as, the <ex>preparation</ex> of land for
a crop of wheat; the <ex>preparation</ex> of troops for a
campaign.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being prepared or made ready;
preparedness; readiness; fitness; <as>as, a nation in good
<ex>preparation</ex> for war</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which makes ready, prepares the way, or
introduces; a preparatory act or measure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will show what <qex>preparations</qex> there were in nature for
this dissolution.</q> <rj><qau>T. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which is prepared, made, or compounded by a
certain process or for a particular purpose; a combination.
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> Any medicinal substance fitted for use.
<sd>(b)</sd> Anything treated for preservation or examination as a
specimen. <sd>(c)</sd> Something prepared for use in cookery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I wish the chemists had been more sparing who magnify their
<qex>preparations</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the <qex>preparations</qex> of cookery, the most volatile parts
of vegetables are destroyed.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An army or fleet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The holding over of a note from
one chord into the next chord, where it forms a temporary discord, until
resolved in the chord that follows; the anticipation of a discordant
note in the preceding concord, so that the ear is prepared for the
shock. See <er>Suspension</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Accomplishment; qualification.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/paratif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to prepare or make
ready; having the power of preparing, qualifying, or fitting;
preparatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Laborious quest of knowledge <qex>preparative</qex> to this
work.</q> <rj><qau> South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"a*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>That which has the power of preparing, or previously
fitting for a purpose; that which prepares.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>preparative</xex> unto sermons.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is done in the way of preparation.</def>
<ldquo/Necessary <xex>preparatives</xex> for our voyage.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparatively</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"a*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way of
preparation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparator</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"a*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeparator</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who prepares beforehand, as
subjects for dissection, specimens for preservation in collections,
etc.</def> <rj><au>Agassiz.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeparatorius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/paratoire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Preparing the way for anything by previous measures of adaptation;
antecedent and adapted to what follows; introductory; preparative;
<as>as, a <ex>preparatory</ex> school; a <ex>preparatory</ex>
condition.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepare</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pare"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prepared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Preparing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/parer</ets>, L. <ets>praeparare</ets>; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>parare</ets> to make ready. See <er>Pare</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To fit, adapt, or qualify for a particular purpose
or condition; to make ready; to put into a state for use or application;
<as>as, to <ex>prepare</ex> ground for seed; to <ex>prepare</ex> a
lesson.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our souls, not yet <qex>prepared</qex> for upper light.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To procure as suitable or necessary; to get ready;
to provide; <as>as, to <ex>prepare</ex> ammunition and provisions for
troops; to <ex>prepare</ex> ships for defence; to <ex>prepare</ex> an
entertainment.</as></def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That they may <qex>prepare</qex> a city for habitation.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. cvii. 36</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To fit; adjust; adapt; qualify; equip; provide;
form; make; make; ready.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1131 --></p>

<p><ent>Prepare</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pare"</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
make all things ready; to put things in order; <as>as, to
<ex>prepare</ex> for a hostile invasion</as>.</def> <ldquo/Bid them
<xex>prepare</xex> for dinner.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make one's self ready; to get ready; to take the
necessary previous measures; <as>as, to <ex>prepare</ex> for
death</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepare</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pare"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Preparation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepared</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pared"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made fit or
suitable; adapted; ready; <as>as, <ex>prepared</ex> food;
<ex>prepared</ex> questions.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre*par"ed*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<au>Shak.</au> -- <wf>Pre*par"ed*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preparer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*par"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, prepares, fits, or makes ready.</def> <rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepay</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pay"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prepaid</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prepaying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To pay in advance, or
beforehand; <as>as, to <ex>prepay</ex> postage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepayment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pay"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Payment in
advance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepenial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pe"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of, or anterior to, the penis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepense</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pense"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre</ets> + F. <ets>penser</ets> to think. See
<er>Pansy</er>.]</ety> <def>To weigh or consider beforehand; to
premeditate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser. Sir T.
Elyot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepense</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pense"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To deliberate
beforehand.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepense</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pense"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pansy</er>, and cf.
<er>Prepense</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Devised, contrived, or
planned beforehand; preconceived; premeditated; aforethought; -- usually
placed after the word it qualifies; <as>as, malice
<ex>prepense</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This has not arisen from any misrepresentation or error
<qex>prepense</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepensely</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pense"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a premeditated
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepollency</ent><br/
<ent>Prepollence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*pol"lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre*pol"len*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepollentia</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
prepollent; superiority of power; predominance; prevalence.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coventry.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepollent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pol"lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepollens</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praepollere</ets> to surpass in
power; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>pollere</ets> to be
powerful.]</ety> <def>Having superior influence or power; prevailing;
predominant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepollent</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*pol"lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prepollices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Pre-</er>,
<er>Pollex</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>An extra first digit, or
rudiment of a digit, on the preaxial side of the pollex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponder</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pon"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
preponderate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponderancy</ent><br/
<ent>Preponderance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*pon"der*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pre*pon"der*an*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>pr<eacute/pond<eacute/rance</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being preponderant; superiority or excess of weight,
influence, or power, etc.; an outweighing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind should . . . reject or receive proportionably to the
<qex>preponderancy</qex> of the greater grounds of probability.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In a few weeks he had changed the relative position of all the
states in Europe, and had restored the equilibrium which the
<qex>preponderance</qex> of one power had destroyed.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>The excess of weight of that part
of a canon behind the trunnions over that in front of them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponderant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pon"der*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeponderans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/pond<eacute/rant</ets>. See <er>Preponderate</er>.]</ety>
<def>Preponderating; outweighing; overbalancing; -- used literally and
figuratively; <as>as, a <ex>preponderant</ex> weight; of
<ex>preponderant</ex> importance.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre*pon"der*ant*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponderate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pon"der*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preponderated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preponderating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeponderatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praeponderare</ets>;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>ponderare</ets> to weigh, fr.,
<ets>pondus</ets>, <ets>ponderis</ets>, a weight. See
<er>Ponder</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To outweigh; to overpower by
weight; to exceed in weight; to overbalance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An inconsiderable weight, by distance from the center of the
balance, will <qex>preponderate</qex> greater magnitudes.</q>
<rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To overpower by stronger or moral power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to prefer; to incline; to decide.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The desire to spare Christian blood <qex>preponderates</qex> him
for peace.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponderate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pon"der*ate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To exceed in weight;
hence, to incline or descend, as the scale of a balance; figuratively,
to exceed in influence, power, etc.; hence; to incline to one side;
<as>as, the affirmative side <ex>preponderated</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That is no just balance in which the heaviest side will not
<qex>preponderate</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Wilkins.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponderatingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pon"der*a`ting*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
preponderating manner; preponderantly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preponderation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pon`der*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeponderatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or state of
preponderating; preponderance; <as>as, a <ex>preponderation</ex> of
reasons</as>.</def> <rj><au>I. Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepose</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/poser</ets>; pref. <ets>pr<eacute/-</ets> (L.
<ets>prae</ets> before) + <ets>poser</ets>. See <er>Pose</er>.]</ety>
<def>To place or set before; to prefix.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preposition</ent><br/
<hw>Prep`o*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepositio</ets>, fr. <ets>praeponere</ets> to place before;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>ponere</ets> to put, place: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/position</ets>. See <er>Position</er>, and cf.
<er>Provost</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A word employed to connect a noun
or a pronoun, in an adjectival or adverbial sense, with some other word;
a particle used with a noun or pronoun (in English always in the
objective case) to make a phrase limiting some other word; -- so called
because usually placed before the word with which it is phrased; <as>as,
a bridge <xex>of</xex> iron; he comes <xex>from</xex> town; it is good
<xex>for</xex> food; he escaped <xex>by</xex> running.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A proposition; an exposition; a discourse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He made a long <qex>preposition</qex> and oration.</q>
<rj><qau>Fabyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepositional</ent><br/
<hw>Prep`o*si"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/positionnel</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
preposition; of the nature of a preposition.</def> <au>Early.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Prep`o*si"tion*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepositive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pos"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepositivus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/positif</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Put before; prefixed; <as>as, a
<ex>prepositive</ex> particle</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
prepositive word.</def> <rj><au>Tooke.</au></rj></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepositor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*pos"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>A scholar appointed to inspect other scholars; a monitor.</def>
<rj><au>Todd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepositure</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pos"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepositura</ets>. See <er>Preposition</er>, and cf.
<er>Provost</er>.]</ety> <def>The office or dignity of a provost; a
provostship.</def> <rj><au>Lowth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepossess</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`pos*sess"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prepossessed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Prepossessing</conjf>.]</vmorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To preoccupy, as ground or land; to take previous
possession of.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To preoccupy, as the mind or heart, so as to
preclude other things; hence, to bias or prejudice; to give a previous
inclination to, for or against anything; esp., to induce a favorable
opinion beforehand, or at the outset.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It created him enemies, and <qex>prepossessed</qex> the lord
general.</q> <rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepossessing</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`pos*sess"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
invite favor; attracting confidence, favor, esteem, or love; attractive;
<as>as, a <ex>prepossessing</ex> manner</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre`pos*sess"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepossession</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`pos*ses"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Preoccupation; prior possession.</def>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Preoccupation of the mind by an opinion, or
impression, already formed; preconceived opinion; previous impression;
bias; -- generally, but not always, used in a favorable sense; <as>as,
the <ex>prepossessions</ex> of childhood</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
prejudices and <xex>prepossessions</xex> of the country.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Bent; bias; inclination; preoccupancy;
prejudgment. See <er>Bent</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepossessor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`*pos*sess"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
possesses, or occupies, previously.</def> <rj><au>R.
Brady.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preposterous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pos"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeposterus</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>posterus</ets>
coming after, latter. See <er>Posterior</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Having that first which ought to be last; inverted
in order.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The method I take may be censured as <qex>preposterous</qex>,
because I thus treat last of the antediluvian earth, which was first in
the order of nature.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Contrary to nature or reason; not adapted to the
end; utterly and glaringly foolish; unreasonably absurd;
perverted.</def> <ldquo/Most <xex>preposterous</xex> conclusions.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Preposterous</qex> ass, that never read so far!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Absurd; perverted; wrong; irrational; foolish;
monstrous. See <er>Absurd</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre*pos"ter*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-<wf>Pre*pos"ter*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepostor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pos"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Prepositor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepotency</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*po"ten*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepotentia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/potence</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or condition of being prepotent;
predominance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> <sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The capacity, on the part of one
of the parents, as compared with the other, to transmit more than his or
her own share of characteristics to their offspring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepotent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*po"tent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praepotens</ets>. See <er>Pre-</er>, and
<er>Potent</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Very powerful; superior in force, influence, or
authority; predominant.</def> <rj><au>Plaifere.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Characterized by
prepotency.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preprovide</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`pro*vide"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To provide
beforehand.</def> <ldquo/The materials <xex>preprovided</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepubic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pu"bic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of, or anterior to, the pubis; pertaining to the
prepubis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepubis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*pu"bis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Pubis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A bone
or cartilage, of some animals, situated in the middle line in front of
the pubic bones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prepuce</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"puce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/puce</ets>, L. <ets>praeputium</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The foreskin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preputial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*pu"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the prepuce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preraphaelitism</ent><br/
<ent>Preraphaelism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*raph"a*el*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pre*raph"a*el*i`tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>The doctrine or practice of a school of
modern painters who profess to be followers of the painters before
Raphael. Its adherents advocate careful study from nature, delicacy and
minuteness of workmanship, and an exalted and delicate conception of the
subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preraphaelite</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*raph"a*el*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the style called preraphaelitism; <as>as, a
<ex>preraphaelite</ex> figure; a <ex>preraphaelite</ex>
landscape.</as></def> <rj><au>Ruskin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preraphaelite</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*raph"a*el*ite</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
favors or practices art as it was before Raphael; one who favors or
advocates preraphaelitism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Popularly, any modern artist thought to be a
would-be restorer of early ideas or methods, as one of the German
painters often called <stype>Nazarenes</stype>, or one who paints and
draws with extreme minuteness of detail.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preregnant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*reg"nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who reigns
before another; a sovereign predecessor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Warner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preremote</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`re*mote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>More remote in
previous time or prior order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In some cases two more links of causation may be introduced; one
of them may be termed the <qex>preremote</qex> cause, the other the
postremote effect.</q> <rj><qau>E. Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prerequire</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`re*quire"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To require
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some things are <qex>prerequired</qex> of us.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prerequisite</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*req"ui*site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Previously
required; necessary as a preliminary to any proposed effect or end;
<as>as, <ex>prerequisite</ex> conditions of success</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prerequisite</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*req"ui*site</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something previously
required, or necessary to an end or effect proposed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The necessary <qex>prerequisites</qex> of freedom.</q> <rj><qau>
Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preresolve</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`re*solve"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preresolved</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preresolving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To resolve beforehand; to
predetermine.</def> <rj><au>Sir E. Dering.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prerogative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*rog"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/rogative</ets>, from L. <ets>praerogativa</ets>
precedence in voting, preference, privilege, fr.
<ets>praerogativus</ets> that is asked before others for his opinion,
that votes before or first, fr. <ets>praerogare</ets> to ask before
another; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>rogare</ets> to ask. See
<er>Rogation</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>An exclusive or peculiar privilege; prior and
indefeasible right; fundamental and essential possession; -- used
generally of an official and hereditary right which may be asserted
without question, and for the exercise of which there is no
responsibility or accountability as to the fact and the manner of its
exercise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The two faculties that are the <qex>prerogative</qex> of man --
the powers of abstraction and imagination.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An unconstitutional exercise of his <qex>prerogative</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Precedence; pre<eum/minence; first rank.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then give me leave to have <qex>prerogative</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term came into general use in the conflicts between
the Crown and Parliaments of Great Britain, especially in the time of
the Stuarts.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prerogative Court</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a
court which formerly had authority in the matter of wills and
administrations, where the deceased left <xex>bona notabilia</xex>, or
effects of the value of five pounds, in two or more different
dioceses.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>Prerogative
office</b></col>, <cd>the office in which wills proved in the
Prerogative Court were registered.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Privilege; right. See
<er>Privilege</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prerogatived</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*rog"a*tived</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Endowed with a
prerogative, or exclusive privilege.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prerogatively</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*rog"a*tive*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By
prerogative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presage</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"sage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sage</ets>, L. <ets>praesagium</ets>, from
<ets>praesagire</ets>. See <er>Presage</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Something which foreshows or portends a future
event; a prognostic; an omen; an augury.</def> <ldquo/Joy and shout --
<xex>presage</xex> of victory.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Power to look the future, or the exercise of that
power; foreknowledge; presentiment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If there be aught of <qex>presage</qex> in the mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prognostic; omen; token; sign;
presentiment.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presage</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sage"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Presaged</conjf> <pr>(-s<amac/jd")</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Presaging</conjf>. ]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sager</ets>, L. <ets>praesagire</ets>: <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>sagire</ets> to perceive acutely or sharply. See
<er>Sagacious</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To have a presentiment of; to feel beforehand; to
foreknow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To foretell; to predict; to foreshow; to
indicate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My dreams <qex>presage</qex> some joyful news at hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presage</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sage"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To form or utter a prediction;
-- sometimes used with <xex>of</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presageful</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sage"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of presages;
ominous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dark in the glass of some <qex>presageful</qex> mood.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presagement</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sage"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or art of presaging; a foreboding.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is presaged, or foretold.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Ominous <xex>presagement</xex> before his end.
<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presager</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sa"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, presages; a foreteller; a foreboder.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presagious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sa"gious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Foreboding;
ominous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyope</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"by*ope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>One who has presbyopia; a farsighted person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyopia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pres`by*o"pi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/ old,
n., an old man + <?/, <?/, the eye.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
defect of vision consequent upon advancing age. It is due to rigidity of
the crystalline lens, which produces difficulty of accommodation and
recession of the near point of vision, so that objects very near the
eyes can not be seen distinctly without the use of convex glasses.
Called also <altname>presbytia</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyopic</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`by*op"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected by
presbyopia; also, remedying presbyopia; farsighted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyopy</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"by*o`py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>presbyopie</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Presbyopia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyte</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"byte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ an old
man.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Presbyope</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyter</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"by*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. an elder, fr.
Gr. <?/. See <er>Priest</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>An elder in the early Christian church. See 2d
Citation under <er>Bishop</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Ch. of Eng. & Prot. Epis. Ch.)</fld> <def>One
ordained to the second order in the ministry; -- called also
<altname>priest</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I rather term the one sort <qex>presbyter</qex> than priest.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>New <qex>presbyter</qex> is but old priest writ large.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Presbyterian Ch.)</fld> <def>A member of a
presbytery whether lay or clerical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A Presbyterian.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyteral</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*byt"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a presbyter or presbytery; presbyterial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyterate</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*byt"er*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>presbyteratus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>presbyt<eacute/rat</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A presbytery; also, presbytership.</def>
<rj><au>Heber.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyteress</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"by*ter*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female presbyter.</def>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyterial</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`by*te"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>presbyt<eacute/ral</ets>.]</ety> <def>Presbyterian.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Presbyterial</xex> government.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyterian</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`by*te"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>presbyt<eacute/rien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
presbyter, or to ecclesiastical government by presbyters; relating to
those who uphold church government by presbyters; also, to the doctrine,
discipline, and worship of a communion so governed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyterian</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`by*te"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>presbyt<eacute/rien</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who maintains the
validity of ordination and government by presbyters; a member of the
Presbyterian church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Reformed Presbyterians</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Cameronian</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyterianism</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`by*te"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>presbyt<eacute/rianisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>That form of church
government which invests presbyters with all spiritual power, and admits
no prelates over them; also, the faith and polity of the Presbyterian
churches, taken collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbyterium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pres`by*te"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Presbytery</er>,
4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbytership</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"by*ter*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
station of a presbyter; presbyterate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbytery</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"by*ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Presbyteries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>presbyterium</ets>, Gr. <?/. See <er>Presbyter</er>, and cf.
<er>Presbyterium</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A body of elders in the early Christian
church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Presbyterian Ch.)</fld> <def>A judicatory
consisting of all the ministers within a certain district, and one
layman, who is a ruling elder, from each parish or church, commissioned
to represent the church in conjunction with the pastor. This body has a
general jurisdiction over the churches under its care, and next below
the provincial synod in authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The Presbyterian religion of polity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>That part of the
church reserved for the officiating priest.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
residence of a priest or clergyman.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbytia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pres*byt"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Presbyte</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Presbyopia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbytic</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*byt"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Presbyopic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presbytism</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"byt*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Presbyopia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescapula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*scap"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The part of the scapula in front of, or above,
the spine, or mesoscapula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescapular</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scap"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the prescapula; supraspinous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescience</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"sci*ence</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/"sh<icr/*<eit/ns <it>or</it>
pr<emac/"sh<eit/ns; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prescience</ets>, L. <ets>praescientia</ets>. See
<er>Prescient</er>.]</ety> <def>Knowledge of events before they take
place; foresight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God's certain <qex>prescience</qex> of the volitions of moral
agents.</q> <rj><qau>J. Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1132 --></p>

<p><ent>Prescient</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"sci*ent</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/"sh<icr/*<eit/nt <it>or</it>
-sh<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praesciens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>praescire</ets> to foreknow;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>scire</ets> to know: cf. F.
<ets>prescient</ets>. See <er>Science</er>.]</ety> <def>Having knowledge
of coming events; foreseeing; conscious beforehand.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Henry . . . had shown himself sensible, and almost
<qex>prescient</qex>, of this event.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presciently</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"sci*ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With prescience or
foresight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescind</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scind"</hw> <pr>(pr<esl/*s<icr/nd")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>praescindere</ets> to cut off in front; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>scindere</ets> to cut asunder: cf. F.
<ets>prescinder</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut off; to abstract.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>To consider by a separate act
of attention or analysis.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescindent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scind"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praescius</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>scius</ets> knowing,
fr. <ets>scire</ets> to know.]</ety> <def>Cutting off;
abstracting.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"scious</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/"sh<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praescius</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>scius</ets> knowing,
fr. <ets>scire</ets> to know.]</ety> <def>Foreknowing; having
foreknowledge; <as>as, <ex>prescious</ex> of ills</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescribe</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scribe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prescribed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prescribing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praescribere</ets>, <ets>praescriptum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before
+ <ets>scriebe</ets> to write. See <er>Scribe</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay down authoritatively as a guide, direction,
or rule of action; to impose as a peremptory order; to dictate; to
appoint; to direct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prescribe</qex> not us our duties.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let streams <qex>prescribe</qex> their fountains where to run.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To direct, as a remedy to be used
by a patient; as, the doctor <xex>prescribed</xex> quinine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To appoint; order; command; dictate; ordain;
institute; establish.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescribe</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scribe"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give
directions; to dictate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A forwardness to <qex>prescribe</qex> to their opinions.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To influence by long use</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To write or to give medical
directions; to indicate remedies; as, to <xex>prescribe</xex> for a
patient in a fever.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To claim by prescription; to claim
a title to a thing on the ground of immemorial use and enjoyment, that
is, by a custom having the force of law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescriber</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scrib"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prescribes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescript</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"script</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praescriptus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praescribere</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>prescrit</ets>. See <er>Prescribe</er>.]</ety> <def>Directed;
prescribed.</def> <ldquo/ A <xex>prescript</xex> from of words.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescript</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"script</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praescriptum</ets>: cf.
OF. <ets>prescript</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Direction; precept; model prescribed.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A medical prescription.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Fell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescriptibility</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scrip`ti*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being prescriptible.</def>
<rj><au>Story.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescriptible</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scrip"ti*ble</hw> <pr>(pr<esl/*skr<icr/p"t<icr/*b'l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>prescriptible</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Depending on, or derived from, prescription; proper to be
prescribed.</def> <rj><au>Grafton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescription</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scrip"tion</hw> <pr>(pr<esl/*skr<icr/p"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>prescription</ets>, L.
<ets>praescriptio</ets>, an inscription, preface, precept, demurrer,
prescription (in sense 3), fr. <ets>praescribere</ets>. See
<er>Prescribe</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of prescribing, directing, or dictating;
direction; precept; also, that which is prescribed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A direction of a remedy or of
remedies for a disease, and the manner of using them; a medical recipe;
also, a prescribed remedy.</def> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>a written
order from a physician for a medication, which allows a patient to
legally obtain medication which is required by law to be dispensed only
on authorization from a physician or other qualified medical
practitioner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A prescribing for title; the claim
of title to a thing by virtue of immemorial use and enjoyment; the right
or title acquired by possession had during the time and in the manner
fixed by law.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That profound reverence for law and <qex>prescription</qex> which
has long been characteristic of Englishmen.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Prescription</xex> differs from <xex>custom</xex>,
which is a local usage, while <xex>prescription</xex> is personal,
annexed to the person only. <xex>Prescription</xex> only extends to
incorporeal rights, such as a right of way, or of common. What the law
gives of <xex>common rights</xex> is not the subject of
<xex>prescription</xex>. <au>Blackstone</au>. <au>Cruise</au>.
<au>Kent</au>. In Scotch law, <xex>prescription</xex> is employed in the
sense in which <xex>limitation</xex> is used in England and America,
namely, to express that operation of the lapse of time by which
obligations are extinguished or title protected. <au>Sir T. Craig.</au>
<au>Erskine</au>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescriptive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scrip"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praescriptivus</ets> of a demurrer or legal exception.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Consisting in, or acquired by,
immemorial or long-continued use and enjoyment; <as>as, a
<ex>prescriptive</ex> right of title</as>; pleading the continuance and
authority of long custom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The right to be drowsy in protracted toil has become
<qex>prescriptive</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>J. M. Mason.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the doctrine that acceptable
grammatical rules should be prescribed by authority, rather than be
determined by common usage.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>prescriptivism</ent><br/
<hw>pre*scrip"tiv*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrine
that acceptable grammatical rules should be prescribed by authority,
rather than be determined by common usage.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>prescriptivist</ent><br/
<hw>pre*scrip"tiv*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person who
believes that acceptable practices should be prescribed by an authority
rather than be determined by the usage of the general public;
especially, a supporter of prescriptive{2} rules of grammar; -- also
used attributively, as <ex>prescriptivist</ex> grammar.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescriptively</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*scrip"tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By
prescription.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prescutum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*scu"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prescuta</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pr<ae/-</er>, and <er>Scutum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The first of the four pieces composing the dorsal part, or tergum,
of a thoracic segment of an insect. It is usually small and
inconspicuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preseance</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"se*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/s<eacute/ance</ets>. See <er>Preside</er>.]</ety>
<def>Priority of place in sitting.</def><mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preselect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`se*lect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To select
beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presence</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sence</ets>, L. <ets>praesentia</ets>. See
<er>Present</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being present, or
of being within sight or call, or at hand; -- opposed to
<xex>absence</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The place in which one is present; the part of space
within one's ken, call, influence, etc.; neighborhood without the
intervention of anything that forbids intercourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wrath shell be no more<br/
Thenceforth, but in thy <qex>presence</qex> joy entire.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Specifically, neighborhood to the person of one of
superior of exalted rank; also, presence chamber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In such a <qex>presence</qex> here to plead my thoughts.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An't please your grace, the two great cardinals.<br/
Wait in the <qex>presence</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The whole of the personal qualities of an
individual; person; personality; especially, the person of a superior,
as a sovereign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Sovran <qex>Presence</qex> thus replied.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An assembly, especially of person of rank or
nobility; noble company.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Odmar, of all this <qex>presence</qex> does contain,<br/
Give her your wreath whom you esteem most fair.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Port, mien; air; personal appearence.</def>
<ldquo/Rather dignity of presence than beauty of aspect.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A graceful <qex>presence</qex> bespeaks acceptance.</q> <rj><qau>
Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Presence chamber</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Presence room</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the room in which a great
personage receives company.</cd> <au>Addison.</au> <ldquo/ <xex>Chambers
of presence</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au> -- <col><b>Presence of
mind</b></col>, <cd>that state of the mind in which all its faculties
are alert, prompt, and acting harmoniously in obedience to the will,
enabling one to reach, as it were spontaneously or by intuition, just
conclusions in sudden emergencies.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presensation</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`sen*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Previous
sensation, notion, or idea.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presension</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesensio</ets>, fr. <ets>praesentire</ets> to perceive
beforehand. See <er>Presentient</er>.]</ety> <def>Previous
perception.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sent</ets>, L. <ets>praesens</ets>,<ets>-entis</ets>,
that is before one, in sight or at hand, p. p. of <ets>praeesse</ets> to
be before; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>esse</ets> to be. See
<er>Essence</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Being at hand, within reach or call, within certain
contemplated limits; -- opposed to <xex>absent</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These things have I spoken unto you, being yet <qex>present</qex>
with you.</q> <rj><qau>John xiv. 25.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Now existing, or in process; begun but not ended;
now in view, or under consideration; being at this time; not past or
future; <as>as, the <ex>present</ex> session of Congress; the
<ex>present</ex> state of affairs; the <ex>present</ex>
instance.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll bring thee to the <qex>present</qex> business</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not delayed; immediate; instant; coincident.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>present</xex> recompense.<rdquo/ <ldquo/A
<xex>present</xex> pardon.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An ambassador . . . desires a <qex>present</qex> audience.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Ready; quick in emergency; as a <xex>present</xex>
wit.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Favorably attentive; propitious.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To find a god so <qex>present</qex> to my prayer.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Present tense</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>the tense
or form of a verb which expresses action or being in the present time;
as, I <xex>am writing</xex>, I <xex>write</xex>, or I <xex>do
write</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/sent</ets>.
See <er>Present</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Present
time; the time being; time in progress now, or at the moment
contemplated; as, at this <xex>present</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Past and <qex>present</qex>, wound in one.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Present letters or
instrument, as a deed of conveyance, a lease, letter of attorney, or
other writing; as in the phrase, <ldquo/ Know all men by these
<xex>presents</xex>,<rdquo/ that is, by the writing itself, <ldquo/ per
has literas <xex>praesentes</xex>; <rdquo/ -- in this sense, rarely used
in the singular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A present tense, or the form of
the verb denoting the present tense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At present</b></col>, <cd>at the present time; now.</cd>
-- <col><b>For the present</b></col>, <cd>for the tine being;
temporarily.</cd> -- <col><b>In present</b></col>, <cd>at once, without
delay.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/With them, <xex>in present</xex>,
half his kingdom; the rest to follow at his death.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Presented</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Presenting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/senter</ets>, L. <ets>praesentare</ets>, fr.
<ets>praesens</ets>, a. See <er>Present</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring or introduce into the presence of some one,
especially of a superior; to introduce formally; to offer for
acquaintance; <as>as, to <ex>present</ex> an envoy to the king</as>;
(with the reciprocal pronoun) to come into the presence of a
superior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now there was a day when the sons of God came to
<qex>present</qex> themselves before the lord.</q> <rj><qau>Job i.
6</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exhibit or offer to view or notice; to lay before
one's perception or cognizance; to set forth; to <xex>present</xex> a
fine appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lectorides's memory is ever . . . <qex>presenting</qex> him with
the thoughts of other persons.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pass over, esp. in a ceremonious manner; to give
in charge or possession; to deliver; to make over.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So ladies in romance assist their knight,<br/
<qex>Present</qex> the spear, and arm him for the fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make a gift of; to bestow; to give, generally in
a formal or ceremonious manner; to grant; to confer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My last, least offering, I <qex>present</qex> thee now.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Hence: To endow; to bestow a gift upon; to favor, as
with a donation; also, to court by gifts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Octavia <qex>presented</qex> the poet for him admirable elegy on
her son Marcellus.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To present; to personate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> In specific uses; <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To nominate to an
ecclesiastical benefice; to offer to the bishop or ordinary as a
candidate for institution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The patron of a church may <qex>present</qex> his clerk to a
parsonage or vicarage; that is, may offer him to the bishop of the
diocese to be instituted.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To nominate for support at a public school or other
institution .</def> <au>Lamb.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>To lay before a
public body, or an official, for consideration, as before a legislature,
a court of judicature, a corporation, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>present</ex>
a memorial, petition, remonstrance, or indictment</as>.</def>
<sd>(d)</sd> <def>To lay before a court as an object of inquiry; to give
notice officially of, as a crime of offence; to find or represent
judicially; <as>as, a grand jury <ex>present</ex> certain offenses or
nuisances, or whatever they think to be public injuries</as>.</def>
<sd>(e)</sd> <def>To bring an indictment against .</def>
<mark>[U.S]</mark> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>To aim, point, or direct, as a
weapon; <as>as, to <ex>present</ex> a pistol or the point of a sword to
the breast of another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pesent arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>the command
in response to which the gun is carried perpendicularly in front of the
center of the body, and held there with the left hand grasping it at the
lower band, and the right hand grasping the small of the stock, in token
of respect, as in saluting a superior officer; also, the position taken
at such a command.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To appear at
the mouth of the uterus so as to be perceptible to the finger in vaginal
examination; -- said of a part of an infant during labor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sent</ets> .]</ety> <def>Anything presented or given; a
gift; a donative; <as>as, a Christmas <ex>present</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Gift; donation; donative; benefaction. See
<er>Gift</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>The position of a soldier in presenting arms; <as>as, to stand at
<ex>present</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sentable</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable or
admitting of being presented; suitable to be exhibited, represented, or
offered; fit to be brought forward or set forth; hence, fitted to be
introduced to another, or to go into society; <as>as, ideas that are
<ex>presentable</ex> in simple language; she is not <ex>presentable</ex>
in such a gown.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Admitting of the presentation of a clergiman;
<as>as, a church <ex>presentable</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`en*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesentaneus</ets>. See <er>Present</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Ready; quick; immediate in effect; <as>as, <ex>presentaneous</ex>
poison</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentation</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`en*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesentatio</ets> a showing, representation: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sentation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
presenting, or the state of being presented; a setting forth; an
offering; bestowal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prayers are sometimes a <qex>presentation</qex> of mere
desires.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Hence, <def>exhibition; representation; display;
appearance; semblance; show.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Under the <qex>presentation</qex> of the shoots his wit.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is presented or given; a present; a gift,
<as>as, the picture was a <ex>presentation</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The act of offering a clergyman
to the bishop or ordinary for institution in a benefice; the right of
presenting a clergyman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the bishop admits the patron's <qex>presentation</qex>, the
clerk so admitted is next to be instituted by him.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The particular position of the
child during labor relatively to the passage though which it is to be
brought forth; -- specifically designated by the part which first
appears at the mouth of the uterus; <as>as, a breech
<ex>presentation</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Presentation copy</b></col>, <cd>a copy of a book,
engraving, etc., presented to some one by the author or artist, as a
token of regard.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>Having the right of presentation, or offering a
clergyman to the bishop for institution; <as>as, advowsons are
<ex>presentative</ex>, collative, or donative</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Admitting the presentation of a clergyman; <as>as, a
<ex>presentative</ex> parsonage</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Spelman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Capable of being directly known
by, or presented to, the mind; intuitive; directly apprehensible, as
objects; capable of apprehending, as faculties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The latter term, <qex>presentative</qex> faculty, I use . . . in
contrast and correlation to a <ldquo/representative faculty.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentee</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`en*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sent<eacute/</ets>, p. p. See <er>Present</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> ]</ety> <def>One to whom something is presented; also, one who
is presented; specifically <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, one presented to
benefice.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presenter</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
presents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presential</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>praesentialis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Implying actual presence; present,
immediate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God's mercy is made <qex>presential</qex> to us.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre*sen"tial*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentiality</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen`ti*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State of
being actually present.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make
present.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentient</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen"tient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesentiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>praesentire</ets> to perceive
beforehand; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>sentire</ets> to feel.]</ety>
<def>Feeling or perceiving beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentific</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`en*tif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, present + <ets>facere</ets> to
make.]</ety> <def>Making present.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pres`en*tif"ic*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentifical</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`en*tif"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Presentific.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentiment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen"ti*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>sentiment</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pressentiment</ets>.
See <er>Presentient</er>.]</ety> <def>Previous sentiment, conception, or
opinion; previous apprehension; especially, an antecedent impression or
conviction of something unpleasant, distressing, or calamitous, about to
happen; anticipation of evil; foreboding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentimental</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen`ti*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of nature
of a presentiment; foreboding.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presention</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sen"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Presension</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Philol.)</fld>
<def>Bringing a conception or notion directly before the mind;
presenting an object to the memory of imagination; -- distinguished from
<xex>symbolic</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How greatly the word <ldquo/will<rdquo/ is felt to have lost
<qex>presentive</qex> power in the last three centuries.</q>
<rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pre*sent"ive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pre*sent"ive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presently</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>At
present; at this time; now.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The towns and forts you <qex>presently</qex> have.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>At once; without delay; forthwith; also, less
definitely, soon; shortly; before long; after a little while; by and
by.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>presently</qex> the fig tree withered away.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xxi. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>With actual presence; actually .</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His precious body and blood <qex>presently three</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Gardiner.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sent"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of presenting, or the state of being presented; presentation.</def>
<ldquo/ Upon the heels of my <xex>presentment</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Setting forth to view; delineation; appearance;
representation; exhibition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Power to cheat the eye with blear illusion,<br/
And give it false <qex>presentment</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The notice taken by a
grand jury of any offence from their own knowledge or observation,
without any bill of indictment laid before them, <as>as, the
<ex>presentment</ex> of a nuisance, a libel, or the like</as>; also, an
inquisition of office and indictment by a grand jury; an official
accusation presented to a tribunal by the grand jury in an indictment,
or the act of offering an indictment; also, the indictment itself.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The official notice (formerly required to be given in
court) of the surrender of a copyhold estate.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Presentment of a bill of exchange</b></col>, <cd>the
offering of a bill to the drawee for acceptance, or to the acceptor for
payment. See <cref>Bill of exchange</cref>, under
<er>Bill</er>.</cd></cs> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1133 --></p>

<p><ent>Presentness</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ent*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being present; presence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Presentness</xex> of mind in danger.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presentoir</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`en*toir"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formed after
analogy of French.]</ety> <def>An ornamental tray, dish, or the like,
used as a salver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Present worth</ent><br/
<ent>Present value</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pres"ent value</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>Pres"ent worth</hw>
}</mhw> (of money payable at a future date). <def>The principal which,
drawing interest at a given rate, will amount to the given sum at the
date on which this is to be paid; thus, interest being at 6%, the
present value of $106 due one year hence is $100.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preservable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serv"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being preserved; admitting of preservation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preservation</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`er*va"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/servation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of
preserving, or keeping safe; the state of being preserved, or kept from
injury, destruction, or decay; security; safety; <as>as,
<ex>preservation</ex> of life, fruit, game, etc.</as>; a picture in good
<xex>preservation</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give us particulars of thy <qex>preservation</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preservative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serv"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/servatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the power or quality
of preserving; tending to preserve, or to keep from injury, decay,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preservative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serv"a*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which preserves, or
has the power of preserving; a presevative agent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To wear tablets as <qex>preservatives</qex> against the
plague.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preservatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serv"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Preservative.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preservatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serv"a*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Preservatories</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
preservative.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Whitlock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A room, or apparatus, in which perishable things, as
fruit, vegetables, etc., can be preserved without decay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preserve</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serve"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Preserved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Preserving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/server</ets>, from L. <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>servare</ets> to save, preserve; cf. L. <ets>praeservare</ets> to
observe beforehand. See <er>Serve</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To keep
or save from injury or destruction; to guard or defend from evil, harm,
danger, etc.; to protect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O Lord, thou <qex>preserved</qex> man and beast.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
xxxvi. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now, good angels <qex>preserve</qex> the king.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To save from decay by the use of some preservative
substance, as sugar, salt, etc.; to season and prepare for remaining in
a good state, as fruits, meat, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>preserve</ex>
peaches or grapes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You can not <qex>preserve</qex> it from tainting.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To maintain throughout; to keep intact; <as>as, to
<ex>preserve</ex> appearances; to <ex>preserve</ex>
silence.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To preserve game</b></col>, <cd>to protect it from
extermination.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To keep; save; secure; uphold; sustain; defend;
spare; protect; guard; shield. See <er>Keep</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preserve</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serve"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make
preserves.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To protect game for purposes of sport.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preserve</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serve"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is
preserved; fruit, etc., seasoned and kept by suitable preparation; esp.,
fruit cooked with sugar; -- commonly in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place in which game, fish, etc., are preserved for
purposes of sport, or for food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preserver</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*serv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, preserves, saves, or defends, from destruction,
injury, or decay; esp., one who saves the life or character of
another.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who makes preserves of fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Game preserver</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Game</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preshow</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*show"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
foreshow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preside</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*side"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Presided</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Presiding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praesidere</ets>;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>sedere</ets> to sit: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sider</ets>. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
be set, or to sit, in the place of authority; to occupy the place of
president, chairman, moderator, director, etc.; to direct, control, and
regulate, as chief officer; <as>as, to <ex>preside</ex> at a public
meeting; to <ex>preside</ex> over the senate.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exercise superintendence; to watch
over.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some o'er the public magazines <qex>preside</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidence</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"i*dence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Presidency</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidency</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"i*den*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Presidencies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sidence</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The function or
condition of one who presides; superintendence; control and
care.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The office of president; <as>as, Washington was
elected to the <ex>presidency</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The term during which a president holds his office;
<as>as, during the <ex>presidency</ex> of Madison</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One of the three great divisions of British India,
the Bengal, Madras, and Bombay Presidencies, each of which had a council
of which its governor was president.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>President</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"i*dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Precedent.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>President</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"i*dent</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Occupying the first rank or
chief place; having the highest authority; presiding.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His angels <qex>president</qex><br/
In every province.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>President</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"i*dent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sident</ets>, L. <ets>praesidens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>praesidere</ets>. See
<er>Preside</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is elected or
appointed to preside; a presiding officer, as of a legislative
body.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The chief officer of a
corporation, company, institution, society, or the like.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The chief executive officer of the government in
certain republics; <as>as, the <ex>president</ex> of the United
States</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A protector; a guardian; a presiding genius.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Just Apollo, <qex>president</qex> of verse.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidential</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`i*den"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Presiding or watching over.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Presidential</xex>
angels.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a president; <as>as, the
<ex>presidential</ex> chair; a <ex>presidential</ex>
election.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidentship</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"i*dent*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office
and dignity of president; presidency.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presider</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sid"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
presides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidiary</ent><br/
<ent>Presidial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*sid"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pre*sid"i*a*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>praesidialis</ets> and
<ets>praesidiarius</ets>, fr. <ets>praesidium</ets> a presiding over,
defense, guard. See <er>Preside</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
garrison; having a garrison.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are three <qex>presidial</qex> castles in this city.</q>
<rj><qau>Howell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sid"i*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesidiarium</ets>.]</ety> <def>A guard.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Heavenly <xex>presidiaries</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presiding</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sid"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Preside</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Presiding elder</b></col>. <cd>See under 2d
<er>Elder</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presidio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*si"di*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>A place of defense; a fortress; a garrison; a fortress; a garrison
or guardhouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presignification</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sig`ni*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<?/.
<ets>praesignificatio</ets>. See <er>Presignify</er>.]</ety> <def>The
act of signifying or showing beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presignify</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sig"ni*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Presignified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Presignifying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesignificare</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>significare</ets> to signify.]</ety> <def>To intimate or signify
beforehand; to presage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presphenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sphe"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the sphenoid bone; of or pertaining to the
anterior part of the sphenoid bone (<it>i. e.</it>, the presphenoid
bone).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Presphenoid bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
anterior part of the body of the sphenoid bone in front of the
basisphenoid. It is usually a separate bone in the young or fetus, but
becomes a part of the sphenoid in the adult.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presphenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sphe"noid</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
presphenoid bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presphenoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`sphe*noid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the presphenoid bone; presphenoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prespinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*spi"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Prevertebral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press</ent><br/
<hw>Press</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
East Indian insectivore (<spn>Tupaia ferruginea</spn>). It is arboreal
in its habits, and has a bushy tail. The fur is soft, and varies from
rusty red to maroon and to brownish black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press</ent><br/
<hw>Press</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Corrupt. fr. <ets>prest</ets>
ready money advanced, a loan; hence, earnest money given soldiers on
entering service. See <er>Prest</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To force
into service, particularly into naval service; to impress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To peaceful peasant to the wars is <qex>pressed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press</ent><br/
<hw>Press</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>prest</ets>, confused with
<ets>press</ets>.]</ety> <def>A commission to force men into public
service, particularly into the navy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have misused the king's <qex>press</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Press gang</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pressgang</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a detachment of seamen under the
command of an officer empowered to force men into the naval service. See
<cref>Impress gang</cref>, under <er>Impress</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Press
money</b></col>, <cd>money paid to a man enlisted into public service.
See <cref>Prest money</cref>, under <er>Prest</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press</ent><br/
<hw>Press</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pressed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pressing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>presser</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>pressare</ets> to press, fr. <ets>premere</ets>,
<ets>pressum</ets>, to press. Cf. <er>Print</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To urge, or act upon, with force, as weight; to act
upon by pushing or thrusting, in distinction from pulling; to crowd or
compel by a gradual and continued exertion; to bear upon; to squeeze; to
compress; <as>as, we <ex>press</ex> the ground with the feet when we
walk; we <ex>press</ex> the couch on which we repose; we <ex>press</ex>
substances with the hands, fingers, or arms; we are <ex>pressed</ex> in
a crowd.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good measure, <qex>pressed</qex> down, and shaken together.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke vi. 38.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To squeeze, in order to extract the juice or
contents of; to squeeze out, or express, from something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From sweet kernels <qex>pressed</qex>,<br/
She tempers dulcet creams.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And I took the grapes, and <qex>pressed</qex> them into Pharaoh's
cup, and I gave the cup into Pharaoh's hand.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xl.
11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To squeeze in or with suitable instruments or
apparatus, in order to compact, make dense, or smooth; <as>as, to
<ex>press</ex> cotton bales, paper, etc.</as>; to smooth by ironing;
<as>as, to <ex>press</ex> clothes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To embrace closely; to hug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Leucothoe shook at these alarms,<br/
And <qex>pressed</qex> Palemon closer in her arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To oppress; to bear hard upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Press</qex> not a falling man too far.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To straiten; to distress; <as>as, to be
<ex>pressed</ex> with want or hunger</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To exercise very powerful or irresistible influence
upon or over; to constrain; to force; to compel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Paul was <qex>pressed</qex> in the spirit, and testified to the
Jews that Jesus was Christ.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xviii. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To try to force (something upon some one); to urge
or inculcate with earnestness or importunity; to enforce; <as>as, to
<ex>press</ex> divine truth on an audience</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>pressed</qex> a letter upon me within this hour.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be sure to <qex>press</qex> upon him every motive.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To drive with violence; to hurry; to urge on; to ply
hard; <as>as, to <ex>press</ex> a horse in a race</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The posts . . . went cut, being hastened and <qex>pressed</qex>
on, by the king's commandment.</q> <rj><qau>Esther viii.
14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Press</xex> differs from <xex>drive</xex> and
<xex>strike</xex> in usually denoting a slow or continued application of
force; whereas <xex>drive</xex> and <xex>strike</xex> denote a sudden
impulse of force.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pressed brick</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Brick</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press</ent><br/
<hw>Press</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To exert pressure; to
bear heavily; to push, crowd, or urge with steady force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move on with urging and crowding; to make one's
way with violence or effort; to bear onward forcibly; to crowd; to
throng; to encroach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>pressed</qex> upon him for to touch him.</q>
<rj><qau>Mark iii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To urge with vehemence or importunity; to exert a
strong or compelling influence; <as>as, an argument <ex>presses</ex>
upon the judgment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press</ent><br/
<hw>Press</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>presse</ets>. See 4th
<er>Press</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An apparatus or machine by which
any substance or body is pressed, squeezed, stamped, or shaped, or by
which an impression of a body is taken; sometimes, the place or building
containing a press or presses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Presses are differently constructed for various purposes
in the arts, their specific uses being commonly designated; <as>as, a
cotton <ex>press</ex>, a wine <ex>press</ex>, a cider <ex>press</ex>, a
copying <ex>press</ex>, etc.</as> See <er>Drill press</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a printing press.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The art or business of printing and publishing;
hence, printed publications, taken collectively, more especially
newspapers or the persons employed in writing for them; <as>as, a free
<ex>press</ex> is a blessing, a licentious <ex>press</ex> is a
curse</as>.</def><-- "the press" usually refers to newspaper reporters
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An upright case or closet for the safe keeping of
articles; <as>as, a clothes <ex>press</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The act of pressing or thronging forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In their throng and <qex>press</qex> to that last hold.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Urgent demands of business or affairs; urgency;
<as>as, a <ex>press</ex> of engagements</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A multitude of individuals crowded together; <?/
crowd of single things; a throng.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They could not come nigh unto him for the <qex>press</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mark ii. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cylinder press</b></col>, <cd>a printing press in which
the impression is produced by a revolving cylinder under which the form
passes; also, one in which the form of type or plates is curved around a
cylinder, instead of resting on a flat bed.</cd> -- <col><b>Hydrostatic
press</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Hydrostatic</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Liberty of the press</b></col>, <cd>the free right of publishing
books, pamphlets, or papers, without previous restraint or censorship,
subject only to punishment for libelous, seditious, or morally
pernicious matters.</cd> -- <col><b>Press bed</b></col>, <cd>a bed that
may be folded, and inclosed, in a press or closet.</cd>
<au>Boswell.</au> -- <col><b>Press of sail</b></col>,
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>as much sail as the state of the wind will
permit.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressboard</ent><br/
<hw>Press"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of highly
sized rag paper or board, sometimes containing a small admixture of wood
pulp; -- so called because used originally, as now, in presses for
pressing and finishing knit underwear.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press cake</ent><br/
<hw>Press cake</hw>. <def>A cake of compressed substance, as: in
gunpowder manufacture, the cake resulting from compressing the meal
powder; in the treatment of coal tar, the pressed product at various
stages of the process; or, in beet-sugar manufacture, the vegetable
residue after the sugar juice has been expressed.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presser</ent><br/
<hw>Press"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, presses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Presser bar</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Presser
wheel</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Knitting machine)</fld>, <cd>a bar or wheel
which closes the barbs of the needles to enable the loops of the yarn to
pass over them.</cd> -- <col><b>Presser foot</b></col>, <cd>the part of
a sewing machine which rests on the cloth and presses it down upon the
table of the machine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressgang</ent><br/
<hw>Press"gang`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <cref>Press
gang</cref>, under <er>Press</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressing</ent><br/
<hw>Press"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Urgent; exacting; importunate;
<as>as, a <ex>pressing</ex> necessity</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Press"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pression</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pressio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pression</ets>. See 4th
<er>Press</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pressing;
pressure.</def> <rj><au>Sir I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cartesian Philos.)</fld> <def>An endeavor to
move.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressiroster</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`si*ros"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>presssus</ets> pressed (p. p. of <ets>premere</ets>) +
<ets>rostrum</ets> beak: cf. F. <ets>pressirostre</ets>. See 4th
<er>Press</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a tribe of wading
birds (<fam>Pressirostres</fam>) including those which have a compressed
beak, as the plovers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressirostral</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`si*ros"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the pressirosters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressitant</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"si*tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 4th
<er>Press</er>.]</ety> <def>Gravitating; heavy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressive</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pressing; urgent;
also, oppressive; <as>as, <ex>pressive</ex> taxation</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressly</ent><br/
<hw>Press"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Closely;
concisely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressman</ent><br/
<hw>Press"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pressmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
manages, or attends to, a press, esp. a printing press.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who presses clothes; <as>as, a tailor's
<ex>pressman</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressman</ent><br/
<hw>Press"man</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 2d <er>Press</er>.]</ety>
<def>One of a press gang, who aids in forcing men into the naval
service; also, one forced into the service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressor</ent><br/
<hw>Press"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>Causing, or giving rise to, pressure or to an increase of pressure;
<as>as, <ex>pressor</ex> nerve fibers, stimulation of which excites the
vasomotor center, thus causing a stronger contraction of the arteries
and consequently an increase of the arterial blood pressure</as>; --
opposed to <contr>depressor</contr>.</def> <rj><au>Landois &
Stirling.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presspack</ent><br/
<hw>Press"pack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pack, or
prepare for packing, by means of a press.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press proof</ent><br/
<hw>Press proof</hw>. <fld>(Print.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The last
proof for correction before sending to press.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
proof taken on a press, esp. to show impression, margins, color,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Press revise</ent><br/
<hw>Press revise</hw>. <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A proof for final
revision.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressurage</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"sur*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The juice of the grape extracted by the press; also,
a fee paid for the use of a wine press.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressure</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"sure</hw> <pr>(?; 138)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF., fr. L.
<ets>pressura</ets>, fr. <ets>premere</ets>. See 4th
<er>Press</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pressing, or the
condition of being pressed; compression; a squeezing; a crushing;
<as>as, a <ex>pressure</ex> of the hand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A contrasting force or impulse of any kind; <as>as,
the <ex>pressure</ex> of poverty; the <ex>pressure</ex> of taxes; the
<ex>pressure</ex> of motives on the mind; the <ex>pressure</ex> of
civilization.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where the <qex>pressure</qex> of danger was not felt.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Affliction; distress; grievance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My people's <qex>pressures</qex> are grievous.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon
Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the midst of his great troubles and <qex>pressures</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Urgency; <as>as, the <ex>pressure</ex> of
business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Impression; stamp; character impressed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All saws of books, all forms, all <qex>pressures</qex> past.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>The action of a force against
some obstacle or opposing force; a force in the nature of a thrust,
distributed over a surface, often estimated with reference to the amount
upon a unit's area.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Electro-motive force.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Atmospheric pressure</b></col>, <col><b>Center of
pressure</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Atmospheric</er>,
<er>Center</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Back pressure</b></col>
<fld>(Steam engine)</fld>, <cd>pressure which resists the motion of the
piston, as the pressure of exhaust steam which does not find free
outlet.</cd> -- <col><b>Fluid pressure</b></col>, <cd>pressure like that
exerted by a fluid. It is a thrust which is normal and equally intense
in all directions around a point.</cd> <au>Rankine.</au> --
<col><b>Pressure gauge</b></col>, <cd>a gauge for indicating fluid
pressure; a manometer.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressure cooker</ent><br/
<hw>Pressure cooker</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>a pot
with a lid which may be tightly bound to the bottom part, forming a
vessel which can withstand some gas pressure from within. It is used to
heat foods in water or steam to a temperature somewhat above the boiling
point of water, allowing the cooking process to be accomplished more
quickly than otherwise.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig., a situation involving intense psychological
pressure due to multiple urgent demands, as in certain job
situations.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pressure wires</ent><br/
<hw>Pressure wires</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>Wires leading from
various points of an electric system to a central station, where a
voltmeter indicates the potential of the system at those
points.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pressurize</ent><br/
<hw>pressurize</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To increase the pressure on (a
gas or liquid); <as>as, <ex>pressurized</ex> oxygen</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> supercharge, apply pressure to.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To increase and maintain a pressure in (a
compartment or container), above that of the ambient pressure; -- of
airplanes, space suits, and diving bells, for example; <as>as, the
airplane cabin was <ex>pressurized</ex> for the comfort of the
passengers</as>; the sterile room was <ex>pressurized</ex> to prevent
contamination.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>negatively pressurized</b></col> <cd>maintained at a
pressure somewhat below that of ambient pressure; -- a technique
sometimes used to keep airborn materials in a room from escaping out;
<as>as, the biohazard lab was <ex>negatively pressurized</ex> to keep
hazardous biological agents from escaping into the
environment</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presswork</ent><br/
<ent>Press work</ent><br/
<hw>Press"work`</hw> <pr>(pr<ecr/s"w<ucir/rk`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The art of printing from the surface of type, plates,
or engravings in relief, by means of a press; the work so done.</def>
<rj><au>MacKellar.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Work done on or by a press.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Metal Work)</fld> <def>Act or process of pressing
or drawing with dies or presses; also, the product of such
work.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Cabinetmaking)</fld> <def>Work consisting of a
series of cross-grained veneers united by glue, heat, and
pressure.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Pottery produced by pressing clay into
molds.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presswork</ent><br/
<ent>Press work</ent><br/
<hw>Press"work`</hw> <pr>(pr<ecr/s"w<ucir/rk`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The work of a press agent.</def> <altsp>[Usually <asp>Press
work</asp>]</altsp> <mark>[Chiefly Theat. Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prest</ent><br/
<hw>Prest</hw> <pr>(pr<ecr/st)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Press</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prest</ent><br/
<hw>Prest</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>prest</ets>, F.
<ets>pr<ecir/t</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praestus</ets> ready. Cf.
<er>Presto</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Ready; prompt; prepared.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>prest</qex> to such battle he was.</q> <rj><qau>R. of
Gloucester.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Neat; tidy; proper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prest money</b></col>, <cd>money formerly paid to men
when they enlisted into the British service; -- so called because it
bound those that received it to be ready for service when called
upon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prest</ent><br/
<hw>Prest</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>prest</ets>, F.
<ets>pr<ecir/t</ets>, fr. OF. <ets>prester</ets> to lend, F.
<ets>pr<ecir/ter</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praestare</ets> to stand before, to
become surety for, to fulfill, offer, supply; <ets>prae</ets> before +
<ets>stare</ets> to stand. See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Stand</er>, and
cf. <er>Press</er> to force into service.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Ready
money; a loan of money.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Requiring of the city a <qex>prest</qex> of six thousand
marks.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A duty in money formerly paid by
the sheriff on his account in the exchequer, or for money left or
remaining in his hands.</def> <rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prest</ent><br/
<hw>Prest</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To give as a loan; to lend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sums of money . . . <qex>prested</qex> out in loan.</q>
<rj><qau>E. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestable</ent><br/
<hw>Prest"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Payable.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestation</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praestatio</ets> a performing, paying, fr. <ets>praestare</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>prestation</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A
payment of money; a toll or duty; also, the rendering of a
service.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1134 --></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prestation money</b></col>, <cd>a sum of money paid
yearly by archdeacons and other dignitaries to their
bishop.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prester</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/,
from <?/ to kindle or burn, and <?/ to blow up, swell out by
blowing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A meteor or exhalation formerly
supposed to be thrown from the clouds with such violence that by
collision it is set on fire.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>One of the veins of the neck when
swollen with anger or other excitement.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prester</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>prestre</ets>. See
<er>Priest</er>.]</ety> <def>A priest or presbyter; <as>as,
<ex>Prester</ex> John</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presternum</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ster"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The anterior segment of the sternum; the
manubrium.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pre*ster"nal</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestidigital</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`ti*dig"i*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Nimble-fingered; having fingers fit for prestidigitation, or
juggling.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/His <xex>prestidigital</xex>
hand.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Charles Reade.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestidigitation</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`ti*dig`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Legerdemain; sleight of hand; juggling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestidigitator</ent><br/
<hw>Pres`ti*dig"i*ta`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesto</ets> ready + <ets>digitus</ets> finger: cf. F.
<ets>prestidigitateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One skilled in legerdemain or
sleight of hand; a juggler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestige</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"tige</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>praestigum</ets> delusion, illusion, <ets>praestigae</ets>
deceptions, jugglers' tricks, prob. fr. <ets>prae</ets> before + the
root of <ets>stinguere</ets> to extinguish, originally, to prick. See
<er>Stick</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Delusion;
illusion; trick.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sophisms of infidelity, and the <qex>prestiges</qex> of
imposture.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Weight or influence derived from past success;
expectation of future achievements founded on those already
accomplished; force or charm derived from acknowledged character or
reputation.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>prestige</xex> of his name must go for
something.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir G. C. Lewis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestigiation</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*tig`i*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praestigiare</ets> to deceive by juggling tricks, fr.
<ets>praestigae</ets>. See <er>Prestige</er>.]</ety> <def>Legerdemain;
prestidigitation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestigiator</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*tig"i*a`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praestigiator</ets>.]</ety> <def>A juggler; prestidigitator.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestigiatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*tig"i*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting
of impostures; juggling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestigious</ent><br/
<hw>Pres*tig"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praestigiosus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Practicing tricks; juggling.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cotton Mather.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestimony</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"ti*mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>praestimonium</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praestare</ets> to furnish,
supply: cf. F. <ets>prestimonie</ets>. See <er>Prest</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Canon Law)</fld> <def>A fund for the support
of a priest, without the title of a benefice. The patron in the
collator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestissimo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pres*tis"si*mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It.,
superl. of <ets>presto</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Very
quickly; with great rapidity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presto</ent><br/
<hw>Pres"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It. or Sp.
<ets>presto</ets> quick, quickly. See <er>Prest</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Quickly; immediately; in haste;
suddenly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Presto</qex>! begone! 'tis here again.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Quickly; rapidly; -- a direction
for a quick, lively movement or performance; quicker than allegro, or
any rate of time except prestissimo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prestriction</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*stric"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praestrictio</ets> a binding fast, fr. <ets>praestringere</ets>.
See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Stringent</er>.]</ety> <def>Obstruction,
dimness, or defect of sight.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presultor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sul"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesultor</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>salire</ets> to
dance.]</ety> <def>A leader in the dance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sum"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sumable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Such as may be presumed or
supposed to be true; that seems entitled to belief without direct
evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumably</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sum"a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a presumable manner; by,
or according to, presumption.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presume</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sume"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Presumed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Presuming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/sumer</ets>, L. <ets>praesumere</ets>,
<ets>praesumptum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>sumere</ets> to
take. See <er>Assume</er>, <er>Redeem</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
assume or take beforehand; esp., to do or undertake without leave or
authority previously obtained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dare he <qex>presume</qex> to scorn us in this manner?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bold deed thou hast <qex>presumed</qex>, adventurous Eve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take or suppose to be true, or entitled to
belief, without examination or proof, or on the strength of probability;
to take for granted; to infer; to suppose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every man is to be <qex>presumed</qex> innocent till he is proved
to be guilty.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What rests but that the mortal sentence pass, . . . <br/
Which he <qex>presumes</qex> already vain and void,<br/
Because not yet inflicted?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presume</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sume"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To suppose or
assume something to be, or to be true, on grounds deemed valid, though
not amounting to proof; to believe by anticipation; to infer; <as>as, we
may <ex>presume</ex> too far</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To venture, go, or act, by an assumption of leave or
authority not granted; to go beyond what is warranted by the
circumstances of the case; to venture beyond license; to take liberties;
-- often with <xex>on</xex> or <xex>upon</xex> before the ground of
confidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do not <qex>presume</qex> too much upon my love.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This man <qex>presumes</qex> upon his parts.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumedly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sum"ed*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By presumption.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sum"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who presumes;
also, an arrogant person.</def> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sum"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Confidently;
arrogantly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumption</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sump"tion</hw> <pr>(?; 215)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesumptio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/somption</ets>, OF. also
<ets>presumpcion</ets>. See <er>Presume</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of presuming, or believing upon probable evidence; the act
of assuming or taking for granted; belief upon incomplete
proof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ground for presuming; evidence probable, but not
conclusive; strong probability; reasonable supposition; <as>as, the
<ex>presumption</ex> is that an event has taken place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is presumed or assumed; that which is
supposed or believed to be real or true, on evidence that is probable
but not conclusive.</def> <ldquo/In contradiction to these very
plausible <xex>presumptions</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of venturing beyond due beyond due bounds;
an overstepping of the bounds of reverence, respect, or courtesy;
forward, overconfident, or arrogant opinion or conduct;
presumptuousness; arrogance; effrontery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy son I killed for his <qex>presumption</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had the <qex>presumption</qex> to dedicate to you a very
unfinished piece.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Conclusive presumption</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Conclusive</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Presumption of fact</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an argument of a fact from a fact; an inference as
to the existence of one fact not certainly known, from the existence of
some other fact known or proved, founded on a previous experience of
their connection; supposition of the truth or real existence of
something, without direct or positive proof of the fact, but grounded on
circumstantial or probable evidence which entitles it to belief.</cd>
<au>Burrill.</au> <au>Best.</au> <au>Wharton.</au> --
<col><b>Presumption of law</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a postulate
applied in advance to all cases of a particular class; <it>e. g.</it>,
the presumption of innocence and of regularity of records. Such a
presumption is rebuttable or irrebuttable.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumptive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sump"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/somptif</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Based on
presumption or probability; grounded on probable evidence; probable;
<as>as, <ex>presumptive</ex> proof</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Presumptuous; arrogant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Presumptive evidence</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>that
which is derived from circumstances which necessarily or usually attend
a fact, as distinct from direct evidence or positive proof; indirect or
circumstantial evidence. <ldquo/<xex>Presumptive</xex> evidence of
felony should be cautiously admitted.<rdquo/ <au>Blackstone.</au> The
distinction, however, between direct and presumptive (or circumstantial)
evidence is now generally abandoned; all evidence being now more or less
direct and more or less presumptive.</cd> -- <col><b>Presumptive
heir</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Heir presumptive</cref>, under
<er>Heir</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumptively</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sump"tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By presumption, or
supposition grounded or probability; presumably.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumptuous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sump"tu*ous</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praesumptuosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/somptueux</ets>, OF.
also <ets>presumptuous</ets>. See <er>Presumption</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of presumption; presuming; overconfident or
venturesome; audacious; rash; taking liberties unduly; arrogant;
insolent; <as>as, a <ex>presumptuous</ex> commander;
<ex>presumptuous</ex> conduct.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A class of <qex>presumptuous</qex> men, whom age has not made
cautious, nor adversity wise.</q> <rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Founded on presumption; <as>as, a
<ex>presumptuous</ex> idea</as>.</def> <ldquo/False,
<xex>presumptuous</xex> hope.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Done with hold design, rash confidence, or in
violation of known duty; willful.</def> <ldquo/Keep back the servant
also from <xex>presumptuous</xex> sins.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ps. xix.
13.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Overconfident; foolhardy; rash; presuming;
forward; arrogant; insolent.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumptuously</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sump"tu*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a presumptuous
manner; arrogantly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presumptuousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sump"tu*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being presumptuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presupposal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`sup*pos"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Presupposition.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Presupposal</xex> of knowledge.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presuppose</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`sup*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Presupposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Presupposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>suppose</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/supposer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To suppose beforehand; to
imply as antecedent; to take for granted; to assume; <as>as, creation
<ex>presupposes</ex> a creator</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each [kind of knowledge] <qex>presupposes</qex> many necessary
things learned in other sciences, and known beforehand.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presupposition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*sup`po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> +<ets> supposition</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/supposition</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
presupposing; an antecedent implication; presumption.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is presupposed; a previous supposition or
surmise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presurmise</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`sur*mise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A surmise
previously formed.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Presystolic</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`sys*tol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Preceding the systole or contraction of the
heart; <as>as, the <ex>presystolic</ex> friction sound</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretemporal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tem"po*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the temporal bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretenceless</ent><br/
<ent>Pretenceful</ent><br/
<ent>Pretence</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Pre*tence"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<hw>Pre*tence"ful</hw>, <pos>a.</pos>, <hw>Pre*tence"*less</hw>,
<pos>a.</pos></mhw> <def>See <er>Pretense</er>, <er>Pretenseful</er>,
<er>Pretenseless</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretend</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pretended</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pretending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pretenden</ets> to
lay claim to, F. <ets>pr<eacute/tendre</ets>, L. <ets>praetendere</ets>,
<ets>praetentum</ets>, to stretch forward, pretend, simulate, assert;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>tendere</ets> to stretch. See
<er>Tend</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos> ]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay a claim
to; to allege a title to; to claim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chiefs shall be grudged the part which they
<qex>pretend</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hold before, or put forward, as a cloak or
disguise for something else; to exhibit as a veil for something
hidden.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest that too heavenly form, <qex>pretended</qex><br/
To hellish falsehood, snare them.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hold out, or represent, falsely; to put forward,
or offer, as true or real (something untrue or unreal); to show
hypocritically, or for the purpose of deceiving; to simulate; to feign;
<as>as, to <ex>pretend</ex> friendship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This let him know,<br/
Lest, willfully transgressing, he <qex>pretend</qex><br/
Surprisal.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To intend; to design; to plot; to attempt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such as shall <qex>pretend</qex><br/
Malicious practices against his state.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To hold before one; to extend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/His target always over her
<xex>pretended</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretend</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put in, or
make, a claim, truly or falsely; to allege a title; to lay claim to, or
strive after, something; -- usually with <xex>to</xex>.</def>
<ldquo/Countries that <xex>pretend</xex> to freedom.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For to what fine he would anon <qex>pretend</qex>,<br/
That know I well.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hold out the appearance of being, possessing, or
performing; to profess; to make believe; to feign; to sham; <as>as, to
<ex>pretend</ex> to be asleep</as>.</def> <ldquo/[He]
<xex>pretended</xex> to drink the waters.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretendant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pretender; a
claimant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretended</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making a false appearance;
unreal; false; <as>as, <ex>pretended</ex> friend</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pre*tend"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretendence</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
pretending; pretense.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretender</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who lays claim, or asserts a title (to something); a claimant.</def>
Specifically, <def><xex>The pretender</xex> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld>, the
son or the grandson of James II., the heir of the royal family of
Stuart, who laid claim to the throne of Great Britain, from which the
house was excluded by law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is the shallow, unimproved intellects that are the confident
<qex>pretenders</qex> to certainty.</q>
<rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who pretends, simulates, or feigns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretendership</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The character, right, or
claim of a pretender.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretendingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tend"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>As by right or title;
arrogantly; presumptuously.</def> <rj><au>Collier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretence</ent><br/
<ent>Pretense</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pre*tense"</hw>, <hw>Pre*tence</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>praetensus</ets>, for L.
<ets>praetentus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praetendere</ets>. See
<er>Pretend</er>, and cf. <er>Tension</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of laying claim; the claim laid; assumption; pretension.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Primogeniture can not have any <qex>pretense</qex> to a right of
solely inheriting property or power.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I went to Lambeth with Sir R. Brown's <qex>pretense</qex> to the
wardenship of Merton College, Oxford.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of holding out, or offering, to others
something false or feigned; presentation of what is deceptive or
hypocritical; deception by showing what is unreal and concealing what is
real; false show; simulation; <as>as, <ex>pretense</ex> of illness;
under <ex>pretense</ex> of patriotism; on <ex>pretense</ex> of revenging
C<ae/sar's death.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is pretended; false, deceptive, or
hypocritical show, argument, or reason; pretext; feint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not the Trojans, with a feigned <qex>pretense</qex><br/
Of proffered peace, delude the Latian prince.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Intention; design.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A very <qex>pretense</qex> and purpose of unkindness.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ See the <er>Note</er> under <er>Offense</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Mask; appearance; color; show; pretext;
excuse.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Pretense</er>, <er>Pretext</er>. A
<xex>pretense</xex> is something held out as real when it is not so,
thus falsifying the truth. A <xex>pretext</xex> is something woven up in
order to cover or conceal one's true motives, feelings, or reasons.
<xex>Pretext</xex> is often, but not always, used in a bad
sense.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretensed</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tensed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pretended;
feigned.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <wordforms><wf>Pre*tens"ed*ly</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretenseful</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tense"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in
pretenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretenseless</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tense"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not having or making
pretenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretension</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ten"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/tention</ets>. See <er>Pretend</er>,
<er>Tension</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pretending, or
laying claim; the act of asserting right or title.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The arrogant <qex>pretensions</qex> of Glengarry contributed to
protract the discussion.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A claim made, whether true or false; a right alleged
or assumed; a holding out the appearance of possessing a certain
character; <as>as, <ex>pretensions</ex> to scholarship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This was but an invention and <qex>pretension</qex> given out by
the Spaniards.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men indulge those opinions and practices that favor their
<qex>pretensions</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretentative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ten"ta*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pre-</ets> + <ets>tentative</ets>: cf. L. <ets>praetentare</ets> to
try beforehand.]</ety> <def>Fitted for trial beforehand;
experimental.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretentious</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ten"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/tentieux</ets>. See <er>Pretend</er>.]</ety> <def>Full of
pretension; disposed to lay claim to more than is one's; presuming;
assuming.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pre*ten"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Pre*ten"tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preter-</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"ter-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>praeter</ets> past,
beyond, originally a compar. of <ets>prae</ets> before. See
<er>For</er>, <ets>prep</ets>.]</ety> <def>A prefix signifying
<xex>past</xex>, <xex>by</xex>, <xex>beyond</xex>, <xex>more than</xex>;
<as>as, <ex>preter-</ex> mission, a permitting to go by;
<ex>preter</ex>natural, beyond or more than is natural.</as></def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pr<ae/ter</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterhuman</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*hu"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>preter-</ets> + <ets>human</ets>.]</ety> <def>More than
human.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterient</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*te"ri*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeteriens</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Preterit</er>.]</ety> <def>Passed
through; antecedent; previous; <as>as, <ex>preterient</ex>
states</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterimperfect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*im*per"fect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>preter-</ets> + <ets>imperfect</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Old name of the tense also called
<altname>imperfect</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterist</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"er*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>preter-</ets> + <ets>-ist</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One whose
chief interest is in the past; one who regards the past with most
pleasure or favor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>One who believes the prophecies
of the Apocalypse to have been already fulfilled.</def>
<rj><au>Farrar.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterit</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"er*it</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeteritus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praeterire</ets> to go or pass by;
<ets>praeter</ets> beyond, by + <ets>ire</ets> to go: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/t<eacute/rit</ets>. See <er>Issue</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>preterite</asp> and
<asp>pr<ae/terite</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>Past; -- applied to a tense which expresses an action or state as
past.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Belonging wholly to the past; passed by.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Things and persons as thoroughly <qex>preterite</qex> as Romulus
or Numa.</q> <rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterit</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"er*it</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The preterit;
also, a word in the preterit tense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterite</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"er*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Preterit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preteriteness</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"er*ite*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Preteritness</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterition</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*i"tion</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeteritio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/t<eacute/rition</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
passing, or going past; the state of being past.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure by which, in pretending
to pass over anything, a summary mention of it is made; <as>as, <ldquo/I
will not say, he is valiant, he is learned, he is just</as>.<rdquo/
Called also <altname>paraleipsis</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The omission by a testator of some
one of his heirs who is entitled to a portion.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preteritive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ter"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>Used only or chiefly in the preterit or past tenses, as certain
verbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preteritness</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"er*it*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being past.</def> <rj><au>Bentley. Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterlapsed</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*lapsed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeterlapsus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praeterlabi</ets> to glide by.
See <er>Preter-</er>, <er>Lapse</er>.]</ety> <def>Past; <as>as,
<ex>preterlapsed</ex> ages</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterlegal</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*le"gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>preter-</ets> + <ets>legal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Exceeding the limits
of law.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1135 --></p>

<p><ent>Pretermission</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*mis"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetermissio</ets>. See <er>Pretermit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of passing by or omitting; omission.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Preterition</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretermit</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*mit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pretermitted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pretermitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetermittere</ets>, <ets>praetermissum</ets>; <ets>praeter</ets>
beyond + <ets>mittere</ets> to send. See <er>Mission</er>.]</ety>
<def>To pass by; to omit; to disregard.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preternatural</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*nat"u*ral</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>preter + natural</ets>.]</ety> <def>Beyond of different from what
is natural, or according to the regular course of things, but not
clearly supernatural or miraculous; strange; inexplicable;
extraordinary; uncommon; irregular; abnormal; <as>as, a
<ex>preternatural</ex> appearance; a <ex>preternatural</ex> stillness; a
<ex>preternatural</ex> presentation (in childbirth) or
labor.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This vile and <qex>preternatural</qex> temper of mind.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Supernatural</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preternaturalism</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*nat"u*ral*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
state of being preternatural; a preternatural condition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preternaturality</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*nat`u*ral"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Preternaturalness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. John
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preternaturally</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*nat"u*ral*ly</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In
a preternatural manner or degree.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preternaturalness</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*nat"u*ral*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being preternatural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterperfect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*per"fect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>preter-</ets> + <ets>perfect</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>Old name of the tense also called
<altname>preterit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preterpluperfect</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*plu"per`fect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>preter-</ets> + <ets>pluperfect</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Old name of the tense also called
<altname>pluperfect</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretertiary</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ter"ti*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Earlier than Tertiary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretervection</ent><br/
<hw>Pre`ter*vec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetervectio</ets>, fr. <ets>praetervehere</ets> to carry beyond.
See <er>Invection</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of carrying past or
beyond.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Abp. Potter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretex</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tex"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetexere</ets>. See <er>Pretext</er>.]</ety> <def>To frame; to
devise; to disguise or excuse; hence, to pretend; to declare
falsely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretext</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"text</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/texte</ets>, L. <ets>praetextum</ets>, fr.
<ets>praetextus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praetexere</ets> to weave before,
allege as an excuse; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>texere</ets> to
weave. See <er>Text</er>.]</ety> <def>Ostensible reason or motive
assigned or assumed as a color or cover for the real reason or motive;
pretense; disguise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They suck the blood of those they depend on, under a
<qex>pretext</qex> of service and kindness.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With how much or how little <qex>pretext</qex> of reason.</q>
<rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pretense; excuse; semblance; disguise;
appearance. See <er>Pretense</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretexture</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tex"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
pretext.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretibial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tib"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the tibia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretor</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetor</ets>, for <ets>praeitor</ets>, fr. <ets>praeire</ets> to
go before; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>ire</ets> to go. See
<er>Issue</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A civil
officer or magistrate among the ancient Romans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Originally the <xex>pretor</xex> was a kind of third
consul; but at an early period two pretors were appointed, the first of
whom (<xex>praetor urbanus</xex>) was a kind of mayor or city judge; the
other (<xex>praetor peregrinus</xex>) was a judge of cases in which one
or both of the parties were foreigners. Still later, the number of
pretors, or judges, was further increased.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a mayor or magistrate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pretorian.</def>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretorian</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*to"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetorians</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/torien</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a pretor or magistrate; judicial; exercised by,
or belonging to, a pretor; <as>as, <ex>pretorian</ex> power or
authority</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pretorian bands</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pretorian guards</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Pretorians</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Rom. Hist.)</fld>, <cd>the
emperor's bodyguards, instituted by the Emperor Augustus in nine cohorts
of 1,000 men each.</cd> -- <col><b>Pretorian gate</b></col> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld>, <cd>that one of the four gates in a camp which lay next
the enemy.</cd> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretorian</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*to"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A soldier of the pretorian
guard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretorium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*to"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praetorium</ets>, fr. <ets>praetor</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The general's tent in a Roman camp; hence, a council of war,
because held in the general's tent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The official residence of a governor of a province;
hence, a place; a splendid country seat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"tor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
dignity of a pretor.</def> <rj><au>J. Warton</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretorture</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*tor"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
torture beforehand.</def> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prettily</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ti*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pretty
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prettiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ti*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
pretty; -- used sometimes in a disparaging sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A style . . . without sententious pretension or antithetical
<qex>prettiness</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretty</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Prettier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Prettiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>prati</ets>, AS.
<ets>pr<ae/ttig</ets>, <ets>pr<ae/tig</ets>, crafty, sly, akin to
<ets>pr<ae/t</ets>, <ets>pr<ae/tt</ets>, deceit, trickery, Icel.
<ets>prettugr</ets> tricky, <ets>prettr</ets> a trick; probably fr.
Latin, perhaps through Celtic; cf. W. <ets>praith</ets> act, deed,
practice, LL. <ets>practica</ets> execution, practice, plot. See
<er>Practice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pleasing by delicacy or
grace; attracting, but not striking or impressing; of a pleasing and
attractive form a color; having slight or diminutive beauty; neat or
elegant without elevation or grandeur; pleasingly, but not grandly,
conceived or expressed; <as>as, a <ex>pretty</ex> face; a
<ex>pretty</ex> flower; a <ex>pretty</ex> poem.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is the <qex>prettiest</qex> lowborn lass that ever<br/
Ran on the greensward.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Moderately large; considerable; <as>as, he had saved
a <ex>pretty</ex> fortune</as>.</def> <ldquo/Wavering a
<xex>pretty</xex> while.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Affectedly nice; foppish; -- used in an ill
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pretty</qex> gentleman is the most complaisant in the
world.</q> <rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Mean; despicable; contemptible; -- used ironically;
<as>as, a <ex>pretty</ex> trick; a <ex>pretty</ex>
fellow.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Stout; strong and brave; intrepid; valiant.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] observed they were <qex>pretty</qex> men, meaning not
handsome.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Elegant; neat; fine. See
<er>Handsome</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretty</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In some degree;
moderately; considerably; rather; almost; -- less emphatic than
<xex>very</xex>; <as>as, I am <ex>pretty</ex> sure of the fact;
<ex>pretty</ex> cold weather.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pretty</qex> plainly professes himself a sincere
Christian.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prettyish</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ty*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat pretty.</def>
<rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prettyism</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ty*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Affectation of a
pretty style, manner, etc.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Ed.
Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretty-spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"ty-spo`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spoken or
speaking prettily.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretypify</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*typ"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pretypified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pretypifying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To prefigure; to
exhibit previously in a type.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pretzel</ent><br/
<hw>Pret"zel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>pretzel</ets>, <ets>bretzel</ets>. Cf. <er>Bretzel</er>.]</ety>
<def>A kind of German biscuit or cake in the form of a twisted ring,
salted on the outside.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevail</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vail"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prevailed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prevailing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/valoir</ets>, OF. <ets>prevaleir</ets>, L.
<ets>praevalere</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>valere</ets> to be
strong, able, or worth. See <er>Valiant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
overcome; to gain the victory or superiority; to gain the advantage; to
have the upper hand, or the mastery; to succeed; -- sometimes with
<xex>over</xex> or <xex>against</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Moses held up his hand, Israel <qex>prevailed</qex>, and when
he let down his hand, Amalek <qex>prevailed</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex.
xvii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So David <qex>prevailed</qex> over the Philistine.</q> <rj><qau>1
Sam. xvii. 50.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This kingdom could never <qex>prevail</qex> against the united
power of <qex>England</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be in force; to have effect, power, or influence;
to be predominant; to have currency or prevalence; to obtain; <as>as,
the practice <ex>prevails</ex> this day</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This custom makes the short-sighted bigots, and the warier
skeptics, as far as it <qex>prevails</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To persuade or induce; -- with <xex>on</xex>,
<xex>upon</xex>, or <xex>with</xex>; <as>as, I <ex>prevailed</ex>on him
to wait</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was <qex>prevailed</qex> with to restrain the Earl.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prevail</qex> upon some judicious friend to be your constant
hearer, and allow him the utmost freedom.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevailing</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vail"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having superior
force or influence; efficacious; persuasive.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Saints shall assist thee with <qex>prevailing</qex> prayers.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Predominant; prevalent; most general; <as>as, the
<ex>prevailing</ex> disease of a climate; a <ex>prevailing</ex>
opinion.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> See <er>Prevalent</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevailingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vail"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>So as to
prevail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevailment</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vail"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prevalence;
superior influence; efficacy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevalence</ent><br/
<hw>Prev"a*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevalentia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/valence</ets>. See
<er>Prevail</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or condition of being
prevalent; superior strength, force, or influence; general existence,
reception, or practice; wide extension; <as>as, the <ex>prevalence</ex>
of virtue, of a fashion, or of a disease; the <ex>prevalence</ex> of a
rumor.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The duke better knew what kind of argument were of
<qex>prevalence</qex> with him.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevalency</ent><br/
<hw>Prev"a*len*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Prevalence</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevalent</ent><br/
<hw>Prev"a*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevalens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>praevalere</ets>. See <er>Prevail</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Gaining advantage or superiority; having superior force, influence,
or efficacy; prevailing; predominant; successful; victorious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Brennus told the Roman embassadors, that <qex>prevalent</qex> arms
were as good as any title.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Most generally received or current; most widely
adopted or practiced; also, generally or extensively existing;
widespread; prevailing; <as>as, a <ex>prevalent</ex> observance;
<ex>prevalent</ex> disease.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This was the most received and <qex>prevalent</qex> opinion.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prevailing; predominant; successful; efficacious;
powerful.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Prevalent</er>, <er>Prevailing</er>. What
customarily prevails is <xex>prevalent</xex>; <as>as, a
<ex>prevalent</ex> fashion</as>. What actually prevails is
<xex>prevailing</xex>; <as>as, the <ex>prevailing</ex> winds are
west</as>. Hence, <xex>prevailing</xex> is the livelier and more pointed
word, since it represents a thing in action. It is sometimes the
stronger word, since a thing may prevail sufficiently to be called
<xex>prevalent</xex>, and yet require greater strength to make it
actually <xex>prevailing</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevalently</ent><br/
<hw>Prev"a*lent"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prevalent
manner.</def> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevaricate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*var"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prevaricated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prevaricating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevaricatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praevaricari</ets> to walk
crookedly, to collude; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>varicare</ets> to
straddle, fr. <ets>varicus</ets> straddling, <ets>varus</ets> bent. See
<er>Varicose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shift or turn from one
side to the other, from the direct course, or from truth; to speak with
equivocation; to shuffle; to quibble; <as>as, he <ex>prevaricates</ex>
in his statement</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>prevaricates</qex> with his own understanding.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>To collude, as where an
informer colludes with the defendant, and makes a sham
prosecution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>To undertake a thing falsely
and deceitfully, with the purpose of defeating or destroying
it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To evade; equivocate; quibble; shuffle.</syn>
<usage> -- <er>Prevaricate</er>, <er>Evade</er>, <er>Equivocate</er>.
One who <xex>evades</xex> a question ostensibly answers it, but really
turns aside to some other point. He who <xex>equivocate</xex> uses words
which have a double meaning, so that in one sense he can claim to have
said the truth, though he does in fact deceive, and intends to do it. He
who <xex>prevaricates</xex> talks all round the question, hoping to
<ldquo/dodge<rdquo/ it, and disclose nothing.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevaricate</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*var"i*cate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To evade by a quibble; to
transgress; to pervert.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevarication</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*var`i*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevaricatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/varication</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of prevaricating, shuffling, or quibbling, to
evade the truth or the disclosure of truth; a deviation from the truth
and fair dealing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The august tribunal of the skies, where no
<qex>prevarication</qex> shall avail.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A secret abuse in the exercise of a public
office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Roman Law)</fld>
<def>The collusion of an informer with the defendant, for the purpose of
making a sham prosecution.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Common Law)</fld>
<def>A false or deceitful seeming to undertake a thing for the purpose
of defeating or destroying it.</def> <rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevaricator</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*var"i*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevaricator</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pr<eacute/varicateur</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who prevaricates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Roman Law)</fld> <def>A sham dealer; one who
colludes with a defendant in a sham prosecution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who betrays or abuses a trust.</def>
<rj><au>Prynne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preve</ent><br/
<hw>Preve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & i.</pos> <def>To prove.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preve</ent><br/
<hw>Preve</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Proof.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevenance</ent><br/
<hw>Prev"e*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/venance</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>A going
before; anticipation in sequence or order.</def> <ldquo/The law of
<xex>prevenance</xex> is simply the well-known law of phenomenal
sequence.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevenancy</ent><br/
<hw>Prev"e*nan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
anticipating another's wishes, desires, etc., in the way of favor or
courtesy; hence, civility; obligingness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevene</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/venir</ets>, L. <ets>praevenire</ets>. See
<er>Prevent</er>.]</ety> <def>To come before; to anticipate; hence, to
hinder; to prevent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Philips.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevenience</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ven"i*ence</hw> <pr>(?; 106)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
going before; anticipation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevenient</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ven"i*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praeveniens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Going before; preceding;
hence, preventive.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Prevenient</xex> grace
descending.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prevented</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preventing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>praevenire</ets>,
<ets>praeventum</ets>; <ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>venire</ets> to
come. See <er>Come</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go before; to
precede; hence, to go before as a guide; to direct.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We which are alive and remain unto the coming of the Lord shall
not <qex>prevent</qex> them which are asleep.</q> <rj><qau>1 Thess. iv.
15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We pray thee that thy grace may always <qex>prevent</qex> and
follow us.</q> <rj><qau>Bk. of Common Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then had I come, <qex>preventing</qex> Sheba's queen.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be beforehand with; to anticipate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their ready guilt <qex>preventing</qex> thy commands.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To intercept; to hinder; to frustrate; to stop; to
thwart.</def> <ldquo/This vile purpose to <xex>prevent</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perhaps forestalling night <qex>prevented</qex> them.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevent</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To come before the usual
time.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strawberries . . . will <qex>prevent</qex> and come early.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventability</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being preventable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventable</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being prevented or hindered; <as>as, <ex>preventable</ex>
diseases</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventative</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
prevents; -- incorrectly used instead of
<xex>preventive</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventer</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who goes before; one who forestalls or anticipates another.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who prevents or obstructs; a hinderer; that
which hinders; <as>as, a <ex>preventer</ex> of evils or of
disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An auxiliary rope to strengthen a
mast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Preventer bolts</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Preventer plates</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>fixtures connected with preventers to re<eum/nforce other
rigging.</cd> -- <col><b>Preventer stay</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <er>Preventer</er>, 3.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>So as to prevent or
hinder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevention</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ven"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/vention</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of going,
or state of being, before.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The greater the distance, the greater the
<qex>prevention</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anticipation; esp., anticipation of needs or wishes;
hence, precaution; forethought.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond. Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of preventing or hindering; obstruction of
action, access, or approach; thwarting.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Casca, be sudden, for we fear <qex>prevention</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Prejudice; prepossession.</def> <mark>[A
Gallicism]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventional</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ven"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
prevent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/ventif</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Going before;
preceding.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Any previous counsel or <qex>preventive</qex> understanding.</q>
<rj><qau>Cudworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tending to defeat or hinder; obviating; preventing
the access of; <as>as, a medicine <ex>preventive</ex> of
disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Physic is either curative or <qex>preventive</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Preventive service</b></col>, <cd>the duty performed by
the armed police in guarding the coast against smuggling.</cd>
<mark>[Eng]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventive</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"ive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which prevents, hinders,
or obstructs; that which intercepts access; in medicine, something to
prevent disease; a prophylactic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preventively</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vent"ive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a preventive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevertebral</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*ver"te*bral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated immediately in front, or on the ventral side, of the
vertebral column; prespinal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Previous</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"vi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevius</ets> going before, leading the way; <ets>prae</ets>
before + <ets>via</ets> the way. See <er>Voyage</er>.]</ety> <def>Going
before in time; being or happening before something else; antecedent;
prior; <as>as, <ex>previous</ex> arrangements; a <ex>previous</ex>
illness.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dull sound . . . <qex>previous</qex> to the storm,<br/
Rolls o'er the muttering earth.</q> <rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Previous question</b></col>. <fld>(Parliamentary
Practice)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Question</er>, and compare
<er>Closure</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Previous to</b></col>, <cd>before; --
often used adverbially for <xex>previously</xex>.</cd>
<ldquo/<xex>Previous to</xex> publication.<rdquo/ <au>M. Arnold.</au>
<ldquo/A policy . . . his friends had advised <xex>previous to</xex>
1710.<rdquo/ <au>J. H. Newman.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Antecedent; preceding; anterior; prior;
foregoing; former.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Previously</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"vi*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Beforehand; antecedently;
<as>as, a plan <ex>previously</ex> formed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Previousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pre"vi*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being previous; priority or antecedence in time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Previse</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>praevisus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>praevidere</ets> to foresee;
<ets>prae</ets> before + <ets>videre</ets> to see. See
<er>Vision</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To foresee.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inform beforehand; to warn.</def> <rj><au>Ld.
Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevision</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*vi"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pr<eacute/vision</ets>.]</ety> <def>Foresight; foreknowledge;
prescience.</def> <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prevoyant</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*voy"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pr<eacute/voyant</ets>.]</ety> <def>Foreseeing; prescient.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Mrs. Oliphant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prewarn</ent><br/
<hw>Pre*warn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prewarned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Prewarning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To
warn beforehand; to forewarn.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1136 --></p>

<p><ent>Prey</ent><br/
<hw>Prey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>preie</ets>, F.
<ets>proie</ets>, L. <ets>praeda</ets>, probably for
<ets>praeheda</ets>. See <er>Prehensile</er>, and cf.
<er>Depredate</er>, <er>Predatory</er>.]</ety> <def>Anything, as goods,
etc., taken or got by violence; anything taken by force from an enemy in
war; spoil; booty; plunder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And they brought the captives, and the <qex>prey</qex>, and the
spoil, unto Moses, and Eleazar the priest.</q> <rj><qau>Num. xxxi.
12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is or may be seized by animals or birds
to be devoured; hence, a person given up as a victim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The old lion perisheth for lack of <qex>prey</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Job iv. ii.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Already sees herself the monster's <qex>prey</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of devouring other creatures;
ravage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hog in sloth, fox in stealth, . . . lion in <qex>prey</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Beast of prey</b></col>, <cd>a carnivorous animal; one
that feeds on the flesh of other animals.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prey</ent><br/
<hw>Prey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Preyed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Preying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>preier</ets>,
<ets>preer</ets>, L. <ets>praedari</ets>, fr. <ets>praeda</ets>. See
<er>Prey</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To take booty; to gather spoil;
to ravage; to take food by violence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>More pity that the eagle should be mewed,<br/
While kites and buzzards <qex>prey</qex> at liberty.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To prey on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To prey
upon</b></col></mcol>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To take prey from; to despoil;
to pillage; to rob</cd>. <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To seize as
prey; to take for food by violence; to seize and devour.</cd>
<au>Shak.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To wear away gradually; to cause to
waste or pine away; <as>as, the trouble <ex>preyed upon</ex> his
mind</as>.</cd> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preyer</ent><br/
<hw>Prey"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, preys; a plunderer; a waster; a devourer.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Preyful</ent><br/
<hw>Prey"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Disposed
to take prey.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>preyful</qex> brood of savage beasts.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rich in prey.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prezygapophysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pre*zyg`a*poph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Prezygapophyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pre-</er>, and <er>Zygapophysis</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>An anterior zygapophysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A corruption of
<xex>pair royal</xex>. See under <er>Pair</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prian</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cornish, clayey
ground, from <ets>pri</ets> clay.]</ety> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A
fine, white, somewhat friable clay; also, the ore contained in a mixture
of clay and pebbles.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pryan</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priapean</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`a*pe"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>Priapeius</ets> pertaining to Priapus.]</ety> <fld>(Lat.
Pros.)</fld> <def>A species of hexameter verse so constructed as to be
divisible into two portions of three feet each, having generally a
trochee in the first and the fourth foot, and an amphimacer in the
third; -- applied also to a regular hexameter verse when so constructed
as to be divisible into two portions of three feet each.</def>
<rj><au>Andrews.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priapism</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"a*pism</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>priapismus</ets>, Gr.
<?/, from <ets>Priapus</ets> the god of procreation, the penis, Gr. <?/:
cf. F. <ets>priapisme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>More or less
permanent erection and rigidity of the penis, with or without sexual
desire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priapulacea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri*ap`u*la"ce*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Priapism</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of
Gephyr<ae/a, having a cylindrical body with a terminal anal opening, and
usually with one or two caudal gills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricasour</ent><br/
<hw>Pric"a*sour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hard
rider.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Price</ent><br/
<hw>Price</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pris</ets>,
OF. <ets>pris</ets>, F. <ets>prix</ets>, L. <ets>pretium</ets>; cf. Gr.
<?/ I sell <?/ to buy, Skr. <ets>pa<?/</ets> to buy, OI.
<ets>renim</ets> I sell. Cf. <er>Appreciate</er>, <er>Depreciate</er>,
<er>Interpret</er>, <er>Praise</er>, <pos>n. & v.</pos>,
<er>Precious</er>, <er>Prize</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The sum or
amount of money at which a thing is valued, or the value which a seller
sets on his goods in market; that for which something is bought or sold,
or offered for sale; equivalent in money or other means of exchange;
current value or rate paid or demanded in market or in barter;
cost.</def> <ldquo/Buy wine and milk without money and without
<xex>price</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Isa. lv. 1.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We can afford no more at such a <qex>price</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Value; estimation; excellence; worth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her <qex>price</qex> is far above rubies.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xxxi.
10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>New treasures still, of countless <qex>price</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Reward; recompense; <as>as, the <ex>price</ex> of
industry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is the <qex>price</qex> of toil,<br/
The knave deserves it when he tills the soil.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Price current</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Price
list</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a statement or list of the prevailing prices
of merchandise, stocks, specie, bills of exchange, etc., published
statedly or occasionally.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Price</ent><br/
<hw>Price</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Priced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pricing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pay the price
of.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With thine own blood to <qex>price</qex> his blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set a price on; to value. See
<er>Prize</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To ask the price of; <as>as, to <ex>price</ex>
eggs</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priced</ent><br/
<hw>Priced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rated in price; valued;
<as>as, high-<ex>priced</ex> goods; low-<ex>priced</ex>
labor.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priceite</ent><br/
<hw>Price"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Thomas
<ets>Price</ets> of San Francisco.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
hydrous borate of lime, from Oregon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priceless</ent><br/
<hw>Price"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Too valuable to
admit of being appraised; of inestimable worth; invaluable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of no value; worthless.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>J. Barlow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prick</ent><br/
<hw>Prick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>prica</ets>,
<ets>pricca</ets>, <ets>pricu</ets>; akin to LG. <ets>prick</ets>,
<ets>pricke</ets>, D. <ets>prik</ets>, Dan. <ets>prik</ets>,
<ets>prikke</ets>, Sw. <ets>prick</ets>. Cf. <er>Prick</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which pricks, penetrates, or
punctures; a sharp and slender thing; a pointed instrument; a goad; a
spur, etc.; a point; a skewer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pins, wooden <qex>pricks</qex>, nails, sprigs of rosemary.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is hard for thee to kick against the <qex>pricks</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts ix. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of pricking, or the sensation of being
pricked; a sharp, stinging pain; figuratively, remorse.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>pricks</xex> of conscience.<rdquo/ <rj><au>A. Tucker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A mark made by a pointed instrument; a puncture; a
point.</def> Hence: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A point or mark on the dial,
noting the hour.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>prick</xex>
of noon.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The point on a target
at which an archer aims; the mark; the pin.</def> <ldquo/They that
shooten nearest the <xex>prick</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A mark denoting degree; degree; pitch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To <xex>prick</xex> of highest praise forth
to advance.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A mathematical
point; -- regularly used in old English translations of Euclid.</def>
<sd>(e)</sd> <def>The footprint of a hare.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small roll; <as>as, a
<ex>prick</ex> of spun yarn; a <ex>prick</ex> of tobacco.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prick</ent><br/
<hw>Prick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pricked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pricking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>prician</ets>; akin to
LG. <ets>pricken</ets>, D. <ets>prikken</ets>, Dan. <ets>prikke</ets>,
Sw. <ets>pricka</ets>. See <er>Prick</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Prink</er>, <er>Prig</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pierce
slightly with a sharp-pointed instrument or substance; to make a
puncture in, or to make by puncturing; to drive a fine point into;
<as>as, to <ex>prick</ex> one with a pin, needle, etc.; to
<ex>prick</ex> a card; to <ex>prick</ex> holes in paper.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fix by the point; to attach or hang by
puncturing; <as>as, to <ex>prick</ex> a knife into a board</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Sir I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The cooks <qex>prick</qex> it [a slice] on a prong of iron.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To mark or denote by a puncture; to designate by
pricking; to choose; to mark; -- sometimes with
<xex>off</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some who are <qex>pricked</qex> for sheriffs.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the soldiers for duty be carefully <qex>pricked</qex> off.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those many, then, shall die: their names are
<qex>pricked</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To mark the outline of by puncturing; to trace or
form by pricking; to mark by punctured dots; <as>as, to <ex>prick</ex> a
pattern for embroidery; to <ex>prick</ex> the notes of a musical
composition.</as></def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To ride or guide with spurs; to spur; to goad; to
incite; to urge on; -- sometimes with <xex>on</xex>, or
<xex>off</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who <qex>pricketh</qex> his blind horse over the fallows.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The season <qex>pricketh</qex> every gentle heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My duty <qex>pricks</qex> me on to utter that.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To affect with sharp pain; to sting, as with
remorse.</def> <ldquo/I was <xex>pricked</xex> with some reproof.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now when they heard this, they were <qex>pricked</qex> in their
heart.</q> <rj><qau>Acts ii. 37.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To make sharp; to erect into a point; to raise, as
something pointed; -- said especially of the ears of an animal, as a
horse or dog; and usually followed by <xex>up</xex>; -- hence, <xex>to
prick up the ears</xex>, to listen sharply; to have the attention and
interest strongly engaged.</def> <ldquo/The courser . . .
<xex>pricks</xex> up his ears.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To render acid or pungent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To dress; to prink; -- usually with
<xex>up</xex>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Naut)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To run a middle seam
through, as the cloth of a sail.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To trace on a
chart, as a ship's course.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To drive a nail into
(a horse's foot), so as to cause lameness.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To
nick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prick</ent><br/
<hw>Prick</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be punctured; to
suffer or feel a sharp pain, as by puncture; <as>as, a sore finger
<ex>pricks</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spur onward; to ride on horseback.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gentle knight was <qex>pricking</qex> on the plain.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To become sharp or acid; to turn sour, as
wine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To aim at a point or mark.</def>
<rj><au>Hawkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prick-eared</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"-eared`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having erect, pointed ears; -- said of certain dogs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>prick-eared</qex> cur of Iceland.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricker</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, pricks; a pointed instrument; a sharp point; a
prickle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who spurs forward; a light horseman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>prickers</qex>, who rode foremost, . . . halted.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A priming wire; a priming needle, -- used in
blasting and gunnery.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small marline spike having
generally a wooden handle, -- used in sailmaking.</def> <rj><au>R. H.
Dana, Ir.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricket</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps so called
from the state of his horns. See <er>Prick</er>, and cf.
<er>Brocket</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A buck in his second
year. See <er>Note</er> under 3d <er>Buck</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricking</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of piercing
or puncturing with a sharp point.</def> <ldquo/There is that speaketh
like the <xex>prickings</xex> of a sword.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Prov. xii. 18
[1583].</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The driving of a nail
into a horse's foot so as to produce lameness.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Same as <er>Nicking</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sensation of being pricked.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The mark or trace left by a hare's foot; a prick;
also, the act of tracing a hare by its footmarks.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Dressing one's self for show; prinking.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricking-up</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"ing-up</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>The first coating of plaster in work of three coats upon laths. Its
surface is scratched once to form a better key for the next coat. In the
United States called <altname>scratch coat</altname>.</def>
<rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickle</ent><br/
<hw>Pric"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>pricele</ets>, <ets>pricle</ets>; akin to LG. <ets>prickel</ets>,
D. <ets>prikkel</ets>. See <er>Prick</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A little prick; a small, sharp point; a fine, sharp
process or projection, as from the skin of an animal, the bark of a
plant, etc.; a spine.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of willow basket; -- a term still used in
some branches of trade.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sieve of filberts, -- about fifty pounds.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickle</ent><br/
<hw>Pric"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prick slightly, as with
prickles, or fine, sharp points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Felt a horror over me creep,<br/
<qex>Prickle</qex> skin, and catch my breath.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricklefish</ent><br/
<ent>Prickleback</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pric"kle*back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pric"kle*fish`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
stickleback.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickliness</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Prickly</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality of being prickly, or of having
many prickles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickling</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Prickly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricklouse</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"louse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A tailor; -- so
called in contempt.</def> <mark>[Old slang]</mark>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickly</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of sharp points or prickles;
armed or covered with prickles; <as>as, a <ex>prickly</ex>
shrub</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prickly heat</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
noncontagious cutaneous eruption of red pimples, attended with intense
itching and tingling of the parts affected. It is due to inflammation of
the sweat glands, and is often brought on by overheating the skin in hot
weather.</cd> -- <col><b>Prickly pear</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
name given to several plants of the cactaceous genus <gen>Opuntia</gen>,
American plants consisting of fleshy, leafless, usually flattened, and
often prickly joints inserted upon each other. The sessile flowers have
many petals and numerous stamens. The edible fruit is a large
pear-shaped berry containing many flattish seeds. The common species of
the Northern Atlantic States is <spn>Opuntia vulgaris</spn>. In the
South and West are many others, and in tropical America more than a
hundred more. <spn>Opuntia vulgaris</spn>, <spn>Opuntia
Ficus-Indica</spn>, and <spn>Opuntia Tuna</spn> are abundantly
introduced in the Mediterranean region, and <spn>Opuntia Dillenii</spn>
has become common in India.</cd> -- <col><b>Prickly pole</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a West Indian palm (<spn>Bactris
Plumierana</spn>), the slender trunk of which bears many rings of long
black prickles.</cd> -- <col><b>Prickly withe</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a West Indian cactaceous plant (<spn>Cereus
triangularis</spn>) having prickly, slender, climbing, triangular
stems.</cd> -- <col><b>Prickly rat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
one of several species of South American burrowing rodents belonging to
<gen>Ctenomys</gen> and allied genera. The hair is usually intermingled
with sharp spines.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickly ash</ent><br/
<hw>Prickly ash</hw> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <sn>1.</sn> <def>A prickly shrub
(<spn>Xanthoxylum Americanum</spn>) with yellowish flowers appearing
with the leaves; also called <altname>toothache tree</altname>. All
parts of the plant are pungent and aromatic. The southern species is
<spn>Xanthoxylum Carolinianum</spn>.</def> <au>Gray.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <def><er>Hercules'-club</er>, also
called the <altname>Angelica tree</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickmadam</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"mad`am</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>trique-madame</ets>. Cf. <er>Tripmadam</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to several species of stonecrop,
used as ingredients of vermifuge medicines. See
<er>Stonecrop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickpunch</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"punch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pointed steel
punch, to prick a mark on metal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickshaft</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"shaft`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An arrow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricksong</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"song`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, 4.]</ety> <def>Music written, or
noted, with dots or points; -- so called from the points or dots with
which it is noted down.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He fights as you sing <qex>pricksong</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prickwood</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A shrub (<spn>Euonymus Europ<ae/us</spn>); -- so named from the use
of its wood for goads, skewers, and shoe pegs. Called also
<altname>spindle tree</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pricky</ent><br/
<hw>Prick"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stiff and sharp;
prickly.</def> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pride</ent><br/
<hw>Pride</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>lamprede</ets>, LL. <ets>lampreda</ets>, E.
<ets>lamprey</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small European
lamprey (<spn>Petromyzon branchialis</spn>); -- called also
<altname>prid</altname>, and <altname>sandpiper</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pride</ent><br/
<hw>Pride</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>pr<ymac/te</ets>; akin to
Icel. <ets>pr<ymac/<edh/i</ets> honor, ornament, <ets>pr<?/<?/a</ets> to
adorn, Dan. <ets>pryde</ets>, Sw. <ets>pryda</ets>; cf. W.
<ets>prydus</ets> comely. See <er>Proud</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being proud; inordinate self-esteem; an
unreasonable conceit of one's own superiority in talents, beauty,
wealth, rank, etc., which manifests itself in lofty airs, distance,
reserve, and often in contempt of others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those that walk in <qex>pride</qex> he is able to abase.</q>
<rj><qau>Dan. iv. 37.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pride</qex> that dines on vanity sups on contempt.</q>
<rj><qau>Franklin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sense of one's own worth, and abhorrence of what
is beneath or unworthy of one; lofty self-respect; noble self-esteem;
elevation of character; dignified bearing; proud delight; -- in a good
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus to relieve the wretched was his <qex>pride</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A people which takes no <qex>pride</qex> in the noble achievements
of remote ancestors will never achieve anything worthy to be remembered
with <qex>pride</qex> by remote descendants.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Proud or disdainful behavior or treatment; insolence
or arrogance of demeanor; haughty bearing and conduct; insolent
exultation; disdain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not the foot of <qex>pride</qex> come against me.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xxxvi. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That hardly we escaped the <qex>pride</qex> of France.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That of which one is proud; that which excites
boasting or self-gratulation; the occasion or ground of self-esteem, or
of arrogant and presumptuous confidence, as beauty, ornament, noble
character, children, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lofty trees yclad with summer's <qex>pride</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will cut off the <qex>pride</qex> of the Philistines.</q>
<rj><qau>Zech. ix. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A bold peasantry, their country's <qex>pride</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Show; ostentation; glory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pride</qex>, pomp, and circumstance of glorious war.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Highest pitch; elevation reached; loftiness; prime;
glory; <as>as, to be in the <ex>pride</ex> of one's life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A falcon, towering in her <qex>pride</qex> of place.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Consciousness of power; fullness of animal spirits;
mettle; wantonness; hence, lust; sexual desire; esp., an excitement of
sexual appetite in a female beast.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pride of India</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pride
of China</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Margosa</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pride of the desert</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the camel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Self-exaltation; conceit; hauteur; haughtiness;
lordliness; loftiness.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Pride</er>, <er>Vanity</er>.
<xex>Pride</xex> is a high or an excessive esteem of one's self for some
real or imagined superiority, as rank, wealth, talents, character, etc.
<xex>Vanity</xex> is the love of being admired, praised, exalted, etc.,
by others. <xex>Vanity</xex> is an ostentation of <xex>pride</xex>; but
one may have great <xex>pride</xex> without displaying it.
<xex>Vanity</xex>, which is etymologically <ldquo/emptiness,<rdquo/ is
applied especially to the exhibition of pride in superficialities, as
beauty, dress, wealth, etc.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pride</ent><br/
<hw>Pride</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prided</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Priding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To indulge in pride, or
self-esteem; to rate highly; to plume; -- used reflexively.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pluming and <qex>priding</qex> himself in all his services.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pride</ent><br/
<hw>Pride</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be proud; to glory.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prideful</ent><br/
<hw>Pride"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of pride;
haughty.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pride"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pride"ful-ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prideless</ent><br/
<hw>Pride"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without pride.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pridian</ent><br/
<hw>Prid"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pridianus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the day before, or
yesterday.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pridingly</ent><br/
<hw>Prid"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Proudly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prie</ent><br/
<hw>Prie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
plant privet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prie</ent><br/
<hw>Prie</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To pry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pried</ent><br/
<hw>Pried</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Pry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priedieu</ent><br/
<hw>Prie`dieu"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., literally,
pray God.]</ety> <def>A kneeling desk for prayers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prief</ent><br/
<hw>Prief</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Proof.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser. Lydgate.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prier</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pry</er>.]</ety> <def>One who pries; one who inquires narrowly and
searches, or is inquisitive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So pragmatical a <qex>prier</qex> he is into divine secrets.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priest</ent><br/
<hw>Priest</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>prest</ets>,
<ets>preost</ets>, AS. <ets>pre<oacute/st</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>presbyter</ets>, Gr. <?/ elder, older, n., an elder, compar. of <?/
an old man, the first syllable of which is probably akin to L.
<ets>pristinus</ets>. Cf. <er>Pristine</er>,
<er>Presbyter</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1137 --></p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Christian Church)</fld> <def>A presbyter elder; a
minister</def>; specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(R. C. Ch. & Gr.
Ch.)</fld> <def>One who is authorized to consecrate the host and to say
Mass; but especially, one of the lowest order possessing this
power.</def> <au>Murdock.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Ch. of Eng. & Prot.
Epis. Ch.)</fld> <def>A presbyter; one who belongs to the intermediate
order between bishop and deacon. He is authorized to perform all
ministerial services except those of ordination and
confirmation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who officiates at the altar, or performs the
rites of sacrifice; one who acts as a mediator between men and the
divinity or the gods in any form of religion; <as>as, Buddhist
<ex>priests</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>priests</xex> of
Dagon.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Sam. v. 5.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then the <qex>priest</qex> of Jupiter . . . brought oxen and
garlands . . . and would have done sacrifice with the people.</q>
<au>Acts xiv. 13.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every <qex>priest</qex> taken from among men is ordained for men
in things pertaining to God, that he may offer both gifts and sacrifices
for sins.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. v. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the New Testament presbyters are not called
<xex>priests</xex>; but Christ is designated as a <xex>priest</xex>, and
as a high <xex>priest</xex>, and all Christians are designated
<xex>priests</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priest</ent><br/
<hw>Priest</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To ordain as
priest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestcap</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"cap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Fort.)</fld>
<def>A form of redan, so named from its shape; -- called also
<altname>swallowtail</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestcraft</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"craft`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Priestly policy;
the policy of a priesthood; esp., in an ill sense, fraud or imposition
in religious concerns; management by priests to gain wealth and power by
working upon the religious motives or credulity of others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is better that men should be governed by <qex>priestcraft</qex>
than by violence.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestery</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Priests,
collectively; the priesthood; -- so called in contempt.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestess</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who officiated in sacred
rites among pagans.</def> <rj><au>Abp. Potter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priesthood</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
office or character of a priest; the priestly function.</def>
<rj><au>Bk. of Com. Prayer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Priests, taken collectively; the order of men set
apart for sacred offices; the order of priests.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priesting</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a priest.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestism</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The influence,
doctrines, principles, etc., of priests or the priesthood.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestless</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a priest.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestlike</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Priestly.</def>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestliness</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being priestly.</def> <rj><au>R. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priestly</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a priest or
the priesthood; sacerdotal; befitting or becoming a priest; <as>as, the
<ex>priestly</ex> office; a <ex>priestly</ex> farewell.</as></def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priest-ridden</ent><br/
<hw>Priest"-rid`den</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Controlled or
oppressed by priests; <as>as, a <ex>priest-ridden</ex>
people</as>.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prieve</ent><br/
<hw>Prieve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prove.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prig</ent><br/
<hw>Prig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prigged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prigging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[A modification of
<ets>prick</ets>.]</ety> <def>To haggle about the price of a commodity;
to bargain hard.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prig</ent><br/
<hw>Prig</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cheapen.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Perhaps orig., to ride off with. See
<er>Prick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To filch or steal; <as>as,
to <ex>prig</ex> a handkerchief</as>.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prig</ent><br/
<hw>Prig</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pert, conceited,
pragmatical fellow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The queer <qex>prig</qex> of a doctor.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thief; a filcher.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priggery</ent><br/
<hw>Prig"ger*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Priggism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priggish</ent><br/
<hw>Prig"gish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a prig;
conceited; pragmatical.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Prig"gish*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Prig"gish-ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priggism</ent><br/
<hw>Prig"gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being priggish; the manners of a prig.</def>
<rj><au>Ed. Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Roguery; thievery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fielding.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prighte</ent><br/
<hw>Prigh"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos>
of <er>Prick</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prill</ent><br/
<hw>Prill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Brill</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The brill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prill</ent><br/
<hw>Prill</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To flow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Stow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prill</ent><br/
<hw>Prill</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stream.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Davies (Microcosmos).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prill</ent><br/
<hw>Prill</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A nugget of virgin
metal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Ore selected for excellence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The button of metal from an assay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prillion</ent><br/
<hw>Pril"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Tin extracted from
the slag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prim</ent><br/
<hw>Prim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Privet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot)</fld> <def>The privet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prim</ent><br/
<hw>Prim</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>prim</ets>, <ets>prin</ets>,
prime, first, principal. sharp, thin, piercing, fr. L. <ets>primus</ets>
first. See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Formal; precise;
affectedly neat or nice; <as>as, <ex>prim</ex> regularity; a
<ex>prim</ex> person.</as></def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prim</ent><br/
<hw>Prim</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Primmed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Primming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To deck with great nicety; to
arrange with affected preciseness; to prink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prim</ent><br/
<hw>Prim</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dress or act smartly.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"ma*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>primatia</ets>, fr. L. <ets>primas</ets>, <ets>-atis</ets>, one of
the first or principal, chief, fr. <ets>primus</ets> first: cf. F.
<ets>primatie</ets>. See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or condition of being prime or first, as in
time, place, rank, etc., hence, excellency; supremacy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The office, rank, or character of a primate; the
chief ecclesiastical station or dignity in a national church; the office
or dignity of an archbishop; <as>as, the <ex>primacy</ex> of
England</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prima donna</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri"ma don"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E. <plw>Prima
donnas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, It. <plw>Prime <pr>(#)</pr> Donne
<pr>(#)</pr></plw>.</plu> <ety>[It., fr. <ets>primo</ets>,
<ets>prima</ets>, the first + <ets>donna</ets> lady, mistress. See
<er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and <er>Donna</er>.]</ety> <def>The first
or chief female singer in an opera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prima facie</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri"ma fa"ci*e</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., from abl. of
<ets>primus</ets> first + abl. of <ets>facies</ets> appearance.]</ety>
<def>At first view; on the first appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prima facie evidence</b></col><cd> (of a fact)
<fld>(Law)</fld>, evidence which is sufficient to establish the fact
unless rebutted.</cd> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primage</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"mage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A charge in addition to the freight; originally,
a gratuity to the captain for his particular care of the goods
(sometimes called <altname>hat money</altname>), but now belonging to
the owners or freighters of the vessel, unless by special agreement the
whole or part is assigned to the captain.</def>
<rj><au>Homans.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primal</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>primalis</ets>, fr. L. <ets>primus</ets> the first. See
<er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>First; primary; original;
chief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It hath the <qex>primal</qex> eldest curse upon it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>primal</qex> duties shine aloft like stars.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primality</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being primal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primarily</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"ma*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a primary
manner; in the first place; in the first place; in the first intention;
originally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primariness</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"ma*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being primary, or first in time, in act, or in intention.</def>
<rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primary</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"ma*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>primarius</ets>, fr. <ets>primus</ets> first: cf. F.
<ets>primaire</ets>. See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Premier</er>, <er>Primero</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>First in
order of time or development or in intention; primitive; fundamental;
original.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The church of Christ, in its <qex>primary</qex> institution.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Pearson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These I call original, or <qex>primary</qex>, qualities of
body.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>First in order, as being preparatory to something
higher; <as>as, <ex>primary</ex> assemblies; <ex>primary</ex>
schools.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>First in dignity or importance; chief; principal;
<as>as, <ex>primary</ex> planets</as>; a matter of <xex>primary</xex>
importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Earliest formed;
fundamental.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Illustrating, possessing, or
characterized by, some quality or property in the first degree; having
undergone the first stage of substitution or replacement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Primary alcohol</b></col> <fld>(Organic Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>any alcohol which possess the group <chform>CH2.OH</chform>, and can
be oxidized so as to form a corresponding aldehyde and acid having the
same number of carbon atoms; -- distinguished from <contr>secondary
<and/ tertiary alcohols</contr>.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary amine</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an amine containing the amido group, or a
derivative of ammonia in which only one atom of hydrogen has been
replaced by a basic radical; -- distinguished from <contr>secondary
<and/ tertiary amines</contr>.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary
amputation</b></col> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>an amputation for injury
performed as soon as the shock due to the injury has passed away, and
before symptoms of inflammation supervene.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary
axis</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the main stalk which bears a whole
cluster of flowers.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary colors</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Color</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary meeting</b></col>, <cd>a
meeting of citizens at which the first steps are taken towards the
nomination of candidates, etc. See <er>Caucus</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Primary pinna</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>one of those
portions of a compound leaf or frond which branch off directly from the
main rhachis or stem, whether simple or compounded.</cd> --
<col><b>Primary planets</b></col>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>See the Note
under <er>Planet</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary qualities of
bodies</b></col>, <cd>such are essential to and inseparable from
them.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary quills</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the largest feathers of the wing of a bird; primaries.</cd> --
<col><b>Primary rocks</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>a term early
used for rocks supposed to have been first formed, being crystalline and
containing no organic remains, as granite, gneiss, etc.; -- called also
<altname>primitive rocks</altname>. The terms <xex>Secondary</xex>,
<xex>Tertiary</xex>, and <xex>Quaternary rocks</xex> have also been used
in like manner, but of these the last two only are now in use.</cd> --
<col><b>Primary salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt derived
from a polybasic acid in which only one acid hydrogen atom has been
replaced by a base or basic radical.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary
syphilis</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>the initial stage of syphilis,
including the period from the development of the original lesion or
chancre to the first manifestation of symptoms indicative of general
constitutional infection.</cd> -- <col><b>Primary union</b></col>
<fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>union without suppuration; union by the first
intention.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primary</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"ma*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Primaries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which
stands first in order, rank, or importance; a chief matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A primary meeting; a caucus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the large feathers on the
distal joint of a bird's wing. See <er>Plumage</er>, and
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Bird</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A primary planet; the brighter
component of a double star. See under <er>Planet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primate</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>primat</ets>, F. <ets>primat</ets>, L. <ets>primas</ets>,
<ets>-atis</ets> one of the first, chief, fr. <ets>primus</ets> the
first. See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
chief ecclesiastic in a national church; one who presides over other
bishops in a province; an archbishop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Primates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primates</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri*ma"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The highest order of mammals. It includes man,
together with the apes and monkeys. Cf. <er>Pitheci</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primateship</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"mate*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office,
dignity, or position of a primate; primacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primatial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*ma"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>primatial</ets>.]</ety> <def>Primatical.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>D'Anville (Trans. ).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primatical</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a primate.</def> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prime</ent><br/
<hw>Prime</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>primus</ets> first, a superl. corresponding to the compar.
<ets>prior</ets> former. See <er>Prior</er>, <pos>a.</pos>,
<er>Foremost</er>, <er>Former</er>, and cf. <er>Prim</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, <er>Primary</er>, <er>Prince</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>First in order of time; original; primeval; primitive;
primary.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Prime</xex> forests.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was not the <qex>prime</qex> cause, but I myself.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense the word is nearly superseded by
<xex>primitive</xex>, except in the phrase <xex>prime
cost</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>First in rank, degree, dignity, authority, or
importance; <as>as, <ex>prime</ex> minister</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Prime</xex> virtues.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>First in excellence; of highest quality; <as>as,
<ex>prime</ex> wheat; a <ex>prime</ex> quality of cloth.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Early; blooming; being in the first stage.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His starry helm, unbuckled, showed him <qex>prime</qex><br/
In manhood where youth ended.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Lecherous; lustful; lewd.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Marked or distinguished by a mark (<prime/) called a
<xex>prime mark</xex>.</def><br/
<note> In this dictionary the same typographic mark is used to indicate
a weak accent in headwords, and minutes of a degree in angle
measurements.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Divisible by no
number except itself or unity; <as>as, 7 is a <ex>prime</ex>
number</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having no common factor; -- used
with <xex>to</xex>; <as>as, 12 is <ex>prime</ex> to 25</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prime and ultimate ratio</b></col>. <fld>(Math.)</fld>.
<cd>See <er>Ultimate</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prime conductor</b></col>.
<fld>(Elec.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Conductor</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Prime factor</b></col> <fld>(Arith.)</fld>, <cd>a factor which
is a prime number.</cd> -- <col><b>Prime figure</b></col>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a figure which can not be divided into any other
figure more simple than itself, as a triangle, a pyramid, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Prime meridian</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the meridian
from which longitude is reckoned, as the meridian of Greenwich or
Washington.</cd> -- <col><b>Prime minister</b></col>, <cd>the
responsible head of a ministry or executive government; applied
particularly to that of England.</cd> -- <col><b>Prime mover</b></col>.
<fld>(Mech.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A natural agency applied by man to
the production of power. Especially: Muscular force; the weight and
motion of fluids, as water and air; heat obtained by chemical
combination, and applied to produce changes in the volume and pressure
of steam, air, or other fluids; and electricity, obtained by chemical
action, and applied to produce alternation of magnetic force.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An engine, or machine, the object of which is to
receive and modify force and motion as supplied by some natural source,
and apply them to drive other machines; as a water wheel, a
water-pressure engine, a steam engine, a hot-air engine, etc.</cd>
<sd>(c)</sd> Fig.: <cd>The original or the most effective force in any
undertaking or work; <as>as, Clarkson was the <ex>prime</ex> mover in
English antislavery agitation</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prime
number</b></col> <fld>(Arith.)</fld>, <cd>a number which is exactly
divisible by no number except itself or unity, as 5, 7, 11.</cd> --
<col><b>Prime vertical</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the vertical
circle which passes through the east and west points of the
horizon.</cd> -- <col><b>Prime-vertical dial</b></col>, <cd>a dial in
which the shadow is projected on the plane of the prime vertical.</cd>
-- <col><b>Prime-vertical transit instrument</b></col>, <cd>a transit
instrument the telescope of which revolves in the plane of the prime
vertical, -- used for observing the transit of stars over this
circle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prime</ent><br/
<hw>Prime</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The first
part; the earliest stage; the beginning or opening, as of the day, the
year, etc.; hence, the dawn; the spring.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the very <qex>prime</qex> of the world.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hope waits upon the flowery <qex>prime</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The spring of life; youth; hence, full health,
strength, or beauty; perfection.</def> <ldquo/Cut off in their
<xex>prime</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Eustace.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>prime</xex>
of youth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is first in quantity; the most excellent
portion; the best part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give him always of the <qex>prime</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>prime</ets>, LL. <ets>prima</ets> (sc.
<ets>hora</ets>). See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>The
morning; specifically <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>, the first canonical hour,
succeeding to lauds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Early and late it rung, at evening and at <qex>prime</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Originally, <xex>prime</xex> denoted the first quarter
of the artificial day, reckoned from 6 <sc>a. m.</sc> to 6 <sc>p.
m.</sc> Afterwards, it denoted the end of the first quarter, that is, 9
<sc>a. m.</sc> Specifically, it denoted the first canonical hour, as
now. Chaucer uses it in all these senses, and also in the sense of def.
1, above.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They sleep till that it was <qex>pryme</qex> large.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>The first of the chief
guards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any number expressing the
combining weight or equivalent of any particular element; -- so called
because these numbers were respectively reduced to their lowest relative
terms on the fixed standard of hydrogen as 1.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>A prime number. See under
<er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>An inch, as composed of twelve seconds in the
duodecimal system; -- denoted by [<prime/]. See 2d <er>Inch</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prime of the moon</b></col>, <cd>the new moon at its
first appearance.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prime</ent><br/
<hw>Prime</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Primed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Priming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Prime</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To apply priming to, as a musket
or a cannon; to apply a primer to, as a metallic cartridge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lay the first color, coating, or preparation upon
(a surface), as in painting; <as>as, to <ex>prime</ex> a canvas, a
wall</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To prepare; to make ready; to instruct beforehand;
to post; to coach; <as>as, to <ex>prime</ex> a witness; the boys are
<ex>primed</ex> for mischief.</as></def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To trim or prune, as trees.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>To mark with a prime
mark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To prime a pump</b></col>, <cd>to charge a pump with
water, in order to put it in working condition.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prime</ent><br/
<hw>Prime</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be renewed, or as
at first.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Night's bashful empress, though she often wane,<br/
As oft repeats her darkness, <qex>primes</qex> again.</q>
<rj><qau>Quarles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To serve as priming for the charge of a
gun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To work so that foaming occurs from too violent
ebullition, which causes water to become mixed with, and be carried
along with, the steam that is formed; -- said of a steam
boiler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primely</ent><br/
<hw>Prime"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>At first;
primarily.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a prime manner; excellently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primeness</ent><br/
<hw>Prime"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state
of being first.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being prime, or
excellent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primer</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, primes</def>; specifically, <def>an instrument or device for
priming; esp., a cap, tube, or water containing percussion powder or
other compound for igniting a charge of gunpowder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primer</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"er</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>primer</ets>,
<ets>primier</ets>, <ets>premier</ets>, F. <ets>premier</ets>. See
<er>Premier</er>.]</ety> <def>First; original; primary.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>primer</xex> English kings.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Primer fine</b></col> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a
fine due to the king on the writ or commencement of a suit by fine.</cd>
<au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>Primer seizin</b></col> <fld>(Feudal
Law)</fld>, <cd>the right of the king, when a tenant <xex>in
capite</xex> died seized of a knight's fee, to receive of the heir, if
of full age, one year's profits of the land if in possession, and half a
year's profits if the land was in reversion expectant on an estate for
life; -- now abolished.</cd> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primer</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Originally, the book
read at <ets>prime</ets>, the first canonical hour. LL. <ets>primae
liber</ets>. See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Originally, a small prayer book for church service, containing the
little office of the Virgin Mary; also, a work of elementary religious
instruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>primer</qex>, or office of the Blessed Virgin.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Stillingfleet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small elementary book for teaching children to
read; a reading or spelling book for a beginner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As he sat in the school at his <qex>prymer</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A kind of type, of which there
are two species; one, called <stype>long primer</stype>, intermediate in
size between bourgeois and small pica [see <er>Long primer</er>]; the
other, called <stype>great primer</stype>, larger than pica.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><centered><point18><greatprimertype>Great primer
type.</greatprimertype></point18></centered></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primero</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*me"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>primera</ets>, fr. <ets>primero</ets> first, from L.
<ets>primarius</ets>. See <er>Premier</er>.]</ety> <def>A game at cards,
now unknown.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primerole</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"er*ole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Primrose</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/She was a
<xex>primerole</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primeval</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*me"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>primaevus</ets>; <ets>primus</ets> first + <ets>aevum</ets> age.
See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, <ets>and</ets> <er>Age</er>.]</ety>
<def>Belonging to the first ages; pristine; original; primitive;
primary; <as>as, the <ex>primeval</ex> innocence of man</as>.</def>
<ldquo/This is the forest <xex>primeval</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From chaos, and <qex>primeval</qex> darkness, came Light.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primevally</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*me"val*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a primeval manner; in or
from the earliest times; originally.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primevous</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*me"vous</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Primeval.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1138 --></p>

<p><ent>Primigenial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mi*ge"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>First born, or
first of all; original; primary. See <er>Primogenial</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primigenous</ent><br/
<ent>Primigenious</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pri`mi*ge"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pri*mig"e*nous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>primigenus</ets>,
<ets>primigenius</ets>. See <er>Primogeniture</er>.]</ety> <def>First
formed or generated; original; primigenial.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primine</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"mine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>primus</ets>
first: cf. F. <ets>primine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
outermost of the two integuments of an ovule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word has been used by some writers to denote the
inner integument, which is formed earlier than the outer. Cf.
<er>Secundine</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priming</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
powder or other combustible used to communicate fire to a charge of
gunpowder, as in a firearm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>The first coating of color,
size, or the like, laid on canvas, or on a building, or other
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Steam Eng.)</fld> <def>The carrying over of water,
with the steam, from the boiler, as into the cylinder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Priming of the tide</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Lag of the
tide</cref>, under 2d <er>Lag</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Priming
tube</b></col>, <cd>a small pipe, filled with a combustible composition
for firing cannon.</cd> -- <col><b>Priming valve</b></col> <fld>(Steam
Eng.)</fld>, <cd>a spring safety valve applied to the cylinder of a
steam engine for discharging water carried into the cylinder by
priming.</cd> -- <col><b>Priming wire</b></col>, <cd>a pointed wire used
to penetrate the vent of a piece, for piercing the cartridge before
priming.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primipara</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri*mip"a*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>primus</ets> first + <ets>parere</ets> to bring forth.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A woman who bears a child for the first
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primiparous</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Primipara</er>.]</ety> <def>Belonging to a first birth; bearing
young for the first time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primipilar</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mip"i*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>primipilaris</ets>, fr. <ets>primipilus</ets> the centurion of the
first cohort of a Roman legion, fr. <ets>primus pilus</ets> the division
made up of the triarii in the Roman army.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to the captain of the vanguard of a Roman army.</def>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primitia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri*mi"ti*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Primiti<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr> (<plw>Primitias</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<mark>obs.</mark>).</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>primitiae</ets>, pl., fr.
<ets>primus</ets> first. Cf. <er>Premices</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eng.
Law)</fld> <def>The first fruit; the first year's whole profit of an
ecclesiastical preferment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>primitias</qex> of your parsonage.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primitial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mi"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being of the first
production; primitive; original.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primitive</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>primitivus</ets>, fr. <ets>primus</ets> the first: cf. F.
<ets>primitif</ets>. See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the beginning or origin, or to
early times; original; primordial; primeval; first; <as>as,
<ex>primitive</ex> innocence; the <ex>primitive</ex> church.</as></def>
<ldquo/Our <xex>primitive</xex> great sire.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a former time; old-fashioned;
characterized by simplicity; <as>as, a <ex>primitive</ex> style of
dress</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Original; primary; radical; not derived; <as>as,
<ex>primitive</ex> verb in grammar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Primitive axes of coordinate</b></col>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>that system of axes to which the points of a
magnitude are first referred, with reference to a second set or system,
to which they are afterward referred.</cd> -- <col><b>Primitive
chord</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>that chord, the lowest note of
which is of the same literal denomination as the fundamental base of the
harmony; -- opposed to <xex>derivative</xex>.</cd> <au>Moore (Encyc. of
Music).</au> -- <col><b>Primitive circle</b></col> <fld>(Spherical
Projection)</fld>, <cd>the circle cut from the sphere to be projected,
by the primitive plane.</cd> -- <col><b>Primitive colors</b></col>
<fld>(Paint.)</fld>, <cd>primary colors. See under <er>Color</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Primitive Fathers</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>the
acknowledged Christian writers who flourished before the Council of
Nice, <sc>A. D.</sc> 325.</cd> <au>Shipley.</au> -- <col><b>Primitive
groove</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a depression or groove in the
epiblast of the primitive streak. It is not connected with the medullary
groove, which appears later and in front of it.</cd> --
<col><b>Primitive plane</b></col> <fld>(Spherical Projection)</fld>,
<cd>the plane upon which the projections are made, generally coinciding
with some principal circle of the sphere, as the equator or a
meridian.</cd> -- <col><b>Primitive rocks</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>,
<cd>primary rocks. See under <er>Primary</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Primitive
sheath</b></col>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Neurilemma</er>.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Primitive streak</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Primitive trace</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>an
opaque and thickened band where the mesoblast first appears in the
vertebrate blastoderm.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- First; original; radical; pristine; ancient;
primeval; antiquated; old-fashioned.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primitive</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"i*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An original or primary word; a
word not derived from another; -- opposed to
<xex>derivative</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primitively</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"i*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Originally; at
first.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Primarily; not derivatively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>According to the original rule or ancient practice;
in the ancient style.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primitiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"i*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being primitive; conformity to primitive style or practice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primity</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
first; primitiveness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primly</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prim or precise
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primness</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
prim; affected formality or niceness; preciseness; stiffness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri"mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>First; chief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primogenial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mo*ge"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Primigenial</er>.]</ety> <def>First born, made, or generated;
original; primary; elemental; <as>as, <ex>primogenial</ex>
light</as>.</def> <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primogenitive</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mo*gen"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Primogeniture</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
primogeniture.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primogenitive</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mo*gen"i*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Primogeniture.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>primogenitive</qex> and due of birth.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primogenitor</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mo*gen"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., fr. L.
<ets>primus</ets> first + <ets>genitor</ets> a begetter.]</ety> <def>The
first ancestor; a forefather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primogeniture</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mo*gen"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.,
fr. L. <ets>primus</ets> first + <ets>genitura</ets> a begetting, birth,
generation, fr. <ets>genere</ets>, <ets>gignere</ets>, to beget: cf. F.
<ets>primog<eacute/niture</ets>, L. <ets>primogenitus</ets> firstborn.
See <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and <er>Genus</er>,
<er>Kin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being the firstborn
of the same parents; seniority by birth among children of the same
family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>The exclusive right of
inheritance which belongs to the eldest son. Thus in England the right
of inheriting the estate of the father belongs to the eldest son, and in
the royal family the eldest son of the sovereign is entitled to the
throne by <xex>primogeniture</xex>. In exceptional cases, among the
female children, the crown descends by right of <xex>primogeniture</xex>
to the eldest daughter only and her issue.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primogenitureship</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`mo*gen"i*ture*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
state or privileges of the firstborn.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primordial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mor"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>primordialis</ets>, from <ets>primordium</ets> the first beginning;
<ets>primus</ets> first + <ets>ordiri</ets> to begin a web, to begin:
cf. F. <ets>primordial</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>First in order;
primary; original; of earliest origin; <as>as, <ex>primordial</ex>
condition</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>primordial</xex> facts of our
intelligent nature.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the lowest
beds of the Silurian age, corresponding to the Acadian and Potsdam
periods in American geology. It is called also
<altname>Cambrian</altname>, and by many geologists is separated from
the Silurian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Originally or earliest formed in
the growth of an individual or organ; <as>as, a <ex>primordial</ex>
leaf; a <ex>primordial</ex> cell.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Primordial utricle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
interior lining of a young vegetable cell.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primordial</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mor"di*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A first principle or
element.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primordialism</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mor"di*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Devotion to,
or persistence in, conditions of the primordial state.</def> <rj><au>H.
Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primordially</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mor"di*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>At the beginning; under
the first order of things; originally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primordian</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mor"di*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>primordius</ets> first of all, fr. <ets>primordium</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to several kinds of plums; <as>as,
red <ex>primordian</ex>, amber <ex>primordian</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primordiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*mor"di*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Primordial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primp</ent><br/
<hw>Primp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Prim</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To be formal or affected in
dress or manners; -- often with <xex>up</xex>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primrose</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"rose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>primerole</ets>, F. <ets>primerole</ets>, a derivative fr. LL.
<ets>primula</ets>, from L. <ets>primus</ets> first. See <er>Prime</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An early
flowering plant of the genus <gen>Primula</gen> (<spn>Primula
vulgaris</spn>) closely allied to the cowslip. There are several
varieties, as the white-, the red-, the yellow-flowered, etc. Formerly
called also <altname>primerole</altname>,
<altname>primerolles</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any plant of the
genus <gen>Primula</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Evening primrose</b></col>, <cd>an erect biennial herb
(<spn>Enothera biennis</spn>), with yellow vespertine flowers, common in
the United States. The name is sometimes extended to other species of
the same genus.</cd> -- <col><b>Primrose peerless</b></col>, <cd>the
two-flowered Narcissus (<spn>Narcissus biflorus</spn>).</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primrose</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"rose`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
primrose; of the color of a primrose; -- hence, flowery; gay.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>primrose</xex> path of dalliance.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primrose League</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"rose` League</hw>. <fld>(Eng. Politics)</fld> <def>A league of
both sexes among the Conservatives, founded in 1883. So called because
<xex>primrose</xex> was (erroneously, it is said) taken to be the
favorite flower of the Conservative statesman <person>Benjamin
Disraeli</person>, Earl of Beaconsfield.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prim"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. See
<er>Primrose</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The genus of plants
including the primrose (<spn>Primula vera</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primulaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Prim`u*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to an order of herbaceous plants
(<ord>Primulace<ae/</ord>), of which the primrose is the type, and the
pimpernel, the cyclamen, and the water violet are other
examples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primum mobile</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri"mum mob"i*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., first cause of
motion.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>In the Ptolemaic system, the
outermost of the revolving concentric spheres constituting the universe,
the motion of which was supposed to carry with it all the inclosed
spheres with their planets in a daily revolution from east to west. See
<cref>Crystalline heavens</cref>, under <er>Crystalline</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The motions of the greatest persons in a government ought to be,
as the motions of the planets, under <qex>primum mobile</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri"mus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the
first.]</ety> <def>One of the bishops of the Episcopal Church of
Scotland, who presides at the meetings of the bishops, and has certain
privileges but no metropolitan authority.</def> <rj><au>Internat.
Cyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Primy</ent><br/
<hw>Prim"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Prime</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Being in its prime.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/The youth of <xex>primy</xex> nature.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prince</ent><br/
<hw>Prince</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>princeps</ets>, <ets>-cipis</ets>, the first, chief;
<ets>primus</ets> first + <ets>capere</ets> to take. See <er>Prime</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and <er>Capacious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The one
of highest rank; one holding the highest place and authority; a
sovereign; a monarch; -- originally applied to either sex, but now
rarely applied to a female.</def> <rj><au>Wyclif (Rev. i.
5).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go, Michael, of celestial armies <qex>prince</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Queen Elizabeth, a <qex>prince</qex> admirable above her sex.</q>
<rj><qau>Camden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The son of a king or emperor, or the issue of a
royal family; <as>as, <ex>princes</ex> of the blood</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A title belonging to persons of high rank, differing
in different countries. In England it belongs to dukes, marquises, and
earls, but is given to members of the royal family only. In Italy a
prince is inferior to a duke as a member of a particular order of
nobility; in Spain he is always one of the royal family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The chief of any body of men; one at the head of a
class or profession; one who is pre<eum/minent; <as>as, a merchant
<ex>prince</ex>; a <ex>prince</ex> of players.</as></def> <ldquo/The
<xex>prince</xex> of learning.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Peacham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prince-Albert coat</b></col>, <cd>a long double-breasted
frock coat for men.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Prince of the blood</b></col>,
<col><b>Prince consort</b></col>, <col><b>Prince of
darkness</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Blood</er>,
<er>Consort</er>, and <er>Darkness</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prince of
Wales</b></col>, <cd>the oldest son of the English sovereign.</cd> --
<col><b>Prince's feather</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name given
to two annual herbs (<spn>Amarantus caudatus</spn> and <spn>Polygonum
orientale</spn>), with apetalous reddish flowers arranged in long
recurved panicled spikes.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Prince's
metal</b></col>, <col><b>Prince Rupert's metal</b></col></mcol>. See
under <er>Metal</er>. <col><b>Prince's pine</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Pipsissewa</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prince</ent><br/
<hw>Prince</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To play the prince.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princedom</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The jurisdiction,
sovereignty, rank, or estate of a prince.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thrones, <qex>princedoms</qex>, powers, dominions, I reduce.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princehood</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Princeliness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>E.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princekin</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A petty prince; a
princeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>princekins</qex> of private life.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princeless</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a prince.</def>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princelet</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A petty
prince.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princelike</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Princely.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princeliness</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being princely; the state, manner, or dignity of a prince.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princeling</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A petty prince; a
young prince.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princely</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or relating to a
prince; regal; royal; of highest rank or authority; <as>as,
<ex>princely</ex> birth, character, fortune, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suitable for, or becoming to, a prince; grand;
august; munificent; magnificent; <as>as, <ex>princely</ex> virtues; a
<ex>princely</ex> fortune.</as></def> <ldquo/Most <xex>princely</xex>
gifts.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princely</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a princely
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My appetite was not <qex>princely</qex> got.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princess</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"cess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>princesse</ets>. See <er>Prince</er>, and cf.
<er>Princesse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A female prince; a woman
having sovereign power, or the rank of a prince.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So excellent a <qex>princess</qex> as the present queen.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The daughter of a sovereign; a female member of a
royal family.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The consort of a prince; <as>as, the
<ex>princess</ex> of Wales</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Princess royal</b></col>, <cd>the eldest daughter of a
sovereign.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princesse</ent><br/
<hw>Prin*cesse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., a
princess.]</ety> <def>A term applied to a lady's long, close-fitting
dress made with waist and skirt in one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princesslike</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"cess*like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a
princess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princewood</ent><br/
<hw>Prince"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The wood of two small tropical American trees (<spn>Hamelia
ventricosa</spn>, and <spn>Cordia gerascanthoides</spn>). It is
brownish, veined with lighter color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princified</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Prince</ets> + L. <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.).]</ety>
<def>Imitative of a prince.</def> <mark>[R. & Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principal</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>principalis</ets>. See <er>Prince</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Highest in rank, authority, character, importance, or degree; most
considerable or important; chief; main; <as>as, the <ex>principal</ex>
officers of a Government; the <ex>principal</ex> men of a state; the
<ex>principal</ex> productions of a country; the <ex>principal</ex>
arguments in a case.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wisdom is the <qex>principal</qex> thing.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. iv.
7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a prince; princely.</def>
<mark>[A Latinism]</mark> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Principal axis</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Axis of a
curve</cref>, under <er>Axis</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Principal axes of a
quadric</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>three lines in which the
principal planes of the solid intersect two and two, as in an
ellipsoid.</cd> -- <col><b>Principal challenge</b></col>.
<fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Challenge</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Principal plane</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Plane of
projection</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, under <er>Plane</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Principal of a quadric</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>three
planes each of which is at right angles to the other two, and bisects
all chords of the quadric perpendicular to the plane, as in an
ellipsoid.</cd> -- <col><b>Principal point</b></col>
<fld>(Persp.)</fld>, <cd>the projection of the point of sight upon the
plane of projection.</cd> -- <col><b>Principal ray</b></col>
<fld>(Persp.)</fld>, <cd>the line drawn through the point of sight
perpendicular to the perspective plane.</cd> -- <col><b>Principal
section</b></col> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld>, <cd>a plane passing through
the optical axis of a crystal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principal</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*pal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A leader, chief, or
head; one who takes the lead; one who acts independently, or who has
controlling authority or influence; <as>as, the <ex>principal</ex> of a
faction, a school, a firm, etc.</as>; -- distinguished from a
<contr>subordinate</contr>, <contr>abettor</contr>,
<contr>auxiliary</contr>, or <contr>assistant</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Hence: <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The chief actor
in a crime, or an abettor who is present at it, -- as distinguished from
an <xex>accessory</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A chief obligor,
promisor, or debtor, -- as distinguished from a <xex>surety</xex>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>One who employs another to act for him, -- as
distinguished from an <xex>agent</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.</au>
<au>Bouvier.</au> <au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A thing of chief or prime importance; something
fundamental or especially conspicuous.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A capital sum of money, placed out at interest,
due as a debt or used as a fund; -- so called in distinction from
<xex>interest</xex> or <xex>profit</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Arch.
& Engin.)</fld> <def>The construction which gives shape and strength to
a roof, -- generally a truss of timber or iron, but there are roofs with
stone <xex>principals</xex>. Also, loosely, the most important member of
a piece of framing.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>In English
organs the chief open metallic stop, an octave above the open diapason.
On the manual it is four feet long, on the pedal eight feet. In Germany
this term corresponds to the English open diapason.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A heirloom; a mortuary.</def>
<au>Cowell.</au> <sd>(e)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The first two long
feathers of a hawk's wing.</def> <au>Spenser.</au> <au>J. H. Walsh.</au>
<sd>(f)</sd> <def>One of turrets or pinnacles of waxwork and tapers with
which the posts and center of a funeral hearse were formerly
crowned.</def> <au>Oxf. Gloss.</au> <sd>(g)</sd> <def>A principal or
essential point or rule; a principle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principality</ent><br/
<hw>Prin`ci*pal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Principalities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>principalitas</ets> pre<eum/minence, excellence: cf. F.
<ets>principalit<eacute/</ets>, <ets>principaut<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Principal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sovereignty; supreme power;
hence, superiority; predominance; high, or the highest, station.</def>
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>principalities</qex> shall come down, even the crown of
your glory.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. xiii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The prerogative and <qex>principality</qex> above everything
else.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A prince; one invested with sovereignty.</def>
<ldquo/Next upstood Nisroch, of <xex>principalities</xex> the
prime.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The territory or jurisdiction of a prince; or the
country which gives title to a prince; <as>as, the <ex>principality</ex>
of Wales</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principally</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*pal*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
principal manner; primarily; above all; chiefly; mainly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principalness</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*pal*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
principal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principate</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>principatus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>principat</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Principality; supreme rule.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prin*cip"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>principium</ets>. See <er>Principle</er>.]</ety> <def>First
principles; fundamental beginnings; elements; as. Newton's
<xex>Principia</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principial</ent><br/
<hw>Prin*cip"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Elementary.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principiant</ent><br/
<hw>Prin*cip"i*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>principians</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>principiare</ets> to begin, fr.
<ets>principium</ets>. See <er>Principle</er>.]</ety> <def>Relating to
principles or beginnings.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principiate</ent><br/
<hw>Prin*cip"i*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Principiant</er>.]</ety> <def>To begin; to initiate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principiation</ent><br/
<hw>Prin*cip`i*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Analysis
into primary or elemental parts.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principle</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>principe</ets>, L. <ets>principium</ets> beginning, foundation, fr.
<ets>princeps</ets>, <ets>-cipis</ets>. See <er>Prince</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Beginning; commencement.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Doubting sad end of <qex>principle</qex> unsound.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A source, or origin; that from which anything
proceeds; fundamental substance or energy; primordial substance;
ultimate element, or cause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1139 --></p>

<p><q>The soul of man is an active <qex>principle</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An original faculty or endowment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature in your <qex>principles</qex> hath set [benignity].</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those active <qex>principles</qex> whose direct and ultimate
object is the communication either of enjoyment or suffering.</q>
<rj><qau>Stewart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A fundamental truth; a comprehensive law or
doctrine, from which others are derived, or on which others are founded;
a general truth; an elementary proposition; a maxim; an axiom; a
postulate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Therefore, leaving the <qex>principles</qex> of the doctrine of
Christ, let us go on unto perfection.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. vi.
1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A good <qex>principle</qex>, not rightly understood, may prove as
hurtful as a bad.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A settled rule of action; a governing law of
conduct; an opinion or belief which exercises a directing influence on
the life and behavior; a rule (usually, a right rule) of conduct
consistently directing one's actions; <as>as, a person of no
<ex>principle</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All kinds of dishonesty destroy our pretenses to an honest
<qex>principle</qex> of mind.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any original inherent constituent
which characterizes a substance, or gives it its essential properties,
and which can usually be separated by analysis; -- applied especially to
drugs, plant extracts, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cathartine is the bitter, purgative <qex>principle</qex> of
senna.</q> <rj><qau>Gregory.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Bitter principle</b></col>, <col><b>Principle of
contradiction</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Bitter</er>,
<er>Contradiction</er>, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Principle</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"ci*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Principled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Principling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
equip with principles; to establish, or fix, in certain principles; to
impress with any tenet, or rule of conduct, good or ill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Governors should be well <qex>principled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let an enthusiast be <qex>principled</qex> that he or his teacher
is inspired.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Princox</ent><br/
<ent>Princock</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prin"cock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Prin"cox</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Prim</ets> + <ets>cock</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A coxcomb; a pert boy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prink</ent><br/
<hw>Prink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prinked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably a nasalized form of
<ets>prick</ets>. See <er>Prick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Prig</er>, <er>Prank</er>.]</ety> <def>To dress or adjust one's self
for show; to prank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prink</ent><br/
<hw>Prink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prank or dress up; to deck
fantastically.</def> <ldquo/And <xex>prink</xex> their hair with
daisies.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prinker</ent><br/
<hw>Prink"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prinks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prinpriddle</ent><br/
<hw>Prin"prid`dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The long-tailed titmouse.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Print</ent><br/
<hw>Print</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Printed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Printing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Abbrev. fr. <ets>imprint</ets>.
See <er>Imprint</er>, and <er>Press</er> to squeeze.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To fix or impress, as a stamp, mark, character, idea, etc., into or
upon something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A look will <qex>print</qex> a thought that never may remove.</q>
<rj><qau>Surrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Upon his breastplate he beholds a dint,<br/
Which in that field young Edward's sword did <qex>print</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir John Beaumont.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perhaps some footsteps <qex>printed</qex> in the clay.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stamp something in or upon; to make an impression
or mark upon by pressure, or as by pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forth on his fiery steed betimes he rode,<br/
That scarcely <qex>prints</qex> the turf on which he trod.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> Specifically: <def>To strike off an impression or
impressions of, from type, or from stereotype, electrotype, or engraved
plates, or the like; in a wider sense, to do the typesetting, presswork,
etc., of (a book or other publication); <as>as, to <ex>print</ex> books,
newspapers, pictures; to <ex>print</ex> an edition of a
book.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stamp or impress with colored figures or
patterns; <as>as, to <ex>print</ex> calico</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>To take (a copy, a positive
picture, etc.), from a negative, a transparent drawing, or the like, by
the action of light upon a sensitized surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Printed goods</b></col>, <cd>textile fabrics printed in
patterns, especially cotton cloths, or calicoes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Print</ent><br/
<hw>Print</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use or practice the
art of typography; to take impressions of letters, figures, or
electrotypes, engraved plates, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To publish a book or an article.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From the moment he <qex>prints</qex>, he must except to hear no
more truth.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Print</ent><br/
<hw>Print</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Print</er>, <pos>v.</pos>,
<er>Imprint</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mark made by
impression; a line, character, figure, or indentation, made by the
pressure of one thing on another; <as>as, the <ex>print</ex> of teeth or
nails in flesh; the <ex>print</ex> of the foot in sand or
snow.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where <qex>print</qex> of human feet was never seen.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stamp or die for molding or impressing an
ornamental design upon an object; <as>as, a butter
<ex>print</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which receives an impression, as from a stamp
or mold; <as>as, a <ex>print</ex> of butter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Printed letters; the impression taken from type, as
to excellence, form, size, etc.; <as>as, small <ex>print</ex>; large
<ex>print</ex>; this line is in <ex>print</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which is produced by printing.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An impression taken from anything, as
from an engraved plate.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>prints</xex> which we see
of antiquities.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A printed
publication, more especially a newspaper or other periodical.</def>
<au>Addison.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A printed cloth; a fabric figured by
stamping, especially calico or cotton cloth.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A
photographic copy, or positive picture, on prepared paper, as from a
negative, or from a drawing on transparent paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A core print. See under
<er>Core</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blue print</b></col>, <cd>a copy in white lines on a blue
ground, of a drawing, plan, tracing, etc., or a positive picture in blue
and white, from a negative, produced by photographic printing on
peculiarly prepared paper.</cd><-- also blueprint. Long used for
reproduction of architectural drawings, now also applied to an
architectural plan of any color, and thus (Fig.) a plan, or outline of a
plan of action; as, <ex>blueprint</ex> for action --> -- <col><b>In
print</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>In a printed form; issued from the
press; published</cd>. <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To the letter;
with accurateness.</cd> <ldquo/All this I speak <xex>in
print</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Out of print</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Out</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Print works</b></col>, <cd>a
factory where cloth, as calico, is printed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printa-ble</ent><br/
<hw>Print"a-ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Worthy to be
published.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printer</ent><br/
<hw>Print"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who prints;
especially, one who prints books, newspapers, engravings, etc., a
compositor; a typesetter; a pressman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Printer's devil</b></col>, <col><b>Printer's
gauge</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Devil</er>, and
<er>Gauge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Printer's ink</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Printing ink</cref>, below.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printery</ent><br/
<hw>Print"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place where cloth
is printed; print works; also, a printing office.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printing</ent><br/
<hw>Print"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act, art, or practice of
impressing letters, characters, or figures on paper, cloth, or other
material; the business of a printer, including typesetting and
presswork, with their adjuncts; typography; also, the act of producing
photographic prints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Block printing</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Block</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Printing frame</b></col>
<fld>(Photog.)</fld>, <cd>a shallow box, usually having a glass front,
in which prints are made by exposure to light.</cd> -- <col><b>Printing
house</b></col>, <cd>a printing office.</cd> -- <col><b>Printing
ink</b></col>, <cd>ink used in printing books, newspapers, etc. It is
composed of lampblack or ivory black mingled with linseed or nut oil,
made thick by boiling and burning. Other ingredients are employed for
the finer qualities.</cd> <au>Ure.</au> -- <col><b>Printing
office</b></col>, <cd>a place where books, pamphlets, or newspapers,
etc., are printed.</cd> -- <col><b>Printing paper</b></col>, <cd>paper
used in the printing of books, pamphlets, newspapers, and the like, as
distinguished from writing paper, wrapping paper, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Printing press</b></col>, <cd>a press for printing, books,
newspaper, handbills, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Printing wheel</b></col>,
<cd>a wheel with letters or figures on its periphery, used in machines
for paging or numbering, or in ticket-printing machines, typewriters,
etc.; a type wheel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printing in</ent><br/
<hw>Print"ing in</hw>. <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>A process by which
cloud effects or other features not in the original negative are
introduced into a photograph. Portions, such as the sky, are covered
while printing and the blank space thus reserved is filled in by
printing from another negative.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printing out</ent><br/
<hw>Printing out</hw>. <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>A method of printing,
in which the image is fully brought out by the direct actinic action of
light without subsequent development by means of chemicals.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printless</ent><br/
<hw>Print"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making no imprint.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printless</ent><br/
<hw>Print"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making no imprint.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Printshop</ent><br/
<hw>Print"shop`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shop where prints are
sold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prion</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"on</hw> <pr>(pr<emac/"<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>any of
several types of protein particle lacking nucleic acid, believed to be
the cause of certain slow-developing infectious diseases such as
<causes>scapie</causes> in sheep, and <causes>Creutzfeldt-Jakob
disease</causes> and <causes>Kuru</causes> in humans.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prior</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prior</ets>
former, previous, better, superior; compar. corresponding to
<ets>primus</ets> first, and <ets>pro</ets> for. See <er>Former</er>,
and cf. <er>Prime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and <er>Pre-</er>,
<er>Pro-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Preceding in the order of time;
former; antecedent; anterior; previous; <as>as, a <ex>prior</ex>
discovery; <ex>prior</ex> obligation; -- used elliptically in cases like
the following: he lived alone [in the time] <ex>prior</ex> to his
marriage.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>First, precedent, or superior in the order of
cognition, reason or generality, origin, development, rank,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prior</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>priour</ets>, OF.
<ets>priour</ets>, <ets>prior</ets>, <ets>priur</ets>, F.
<ets>prieur</ets>, from L. <ets>prior</ets> former, superior. See
<er>Prior</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>The superior of a priory, and next below an abbot in
dignity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a chief magistrate, as in the republic of Florence
in the middle ages.</def> <au>[RHUD]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Conventical prior</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Conventual prior</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a prior who is at the
head of his own house. See the Note under <er>Priory</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Claustral prior</b></col>, <cd>an official next in rank to the
abbot in a monastery; prior of the cloisters.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prior</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a prior conviction; -- said of an
accused criminal.</def> <mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priorate</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>prioratus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>priorat</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
dignity, office, or government, of a prior.</def> <rj><au>T.
Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prioress</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>prioresse</ets>.]</ety> <def>A lady superior of a priory of nuns,
and next in dignity to an abbess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>prioritize</ent><br/
<hw>prioritize</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To order or rank (a list of
tasks) according to priority; to assign a priorities to.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priority</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*or"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>priorit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Prior</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being prior or antecedent in
time, or of preceding something else; <as>as, <ex>priority</ex> of
application</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Precedence; superior rank.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Priority of debts</b></col>, <cd>a superior claim to
payment, or a claim to payment before others.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Antecedence; precedence;
pre<eum/minence.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priorly</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Previously.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Geddes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"or*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or office of prior;
priorate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priory</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Priories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>prioria</ets>. See <er>Prior</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A
religious house presided over by a prior or prioress; -- sometimes an
offshoot of, an subordinate to, an abbey, and called also
<altname>cell</altname>, and <altname>obedience</altname>. See
<er>Cell</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Of such houses there were two sorts: one where the prior
was chosen by the inmates, and governed as independently as an abbot in
an abbey; the other where the priory was subordinate to an abbey, and
the prior was placed or displaced at the will of the abbot.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Alien priory</b></col>, <cd>a small religious house
dependent on a large monastery in some other country.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Cloister</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pris</ent><br/
<hw>Pris</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Price</er>, and
1st <er>Prize</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prisage</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>prisage</ets> a praising, valuing, taxing; cf. LL.
<ets>prisagium</ets> prisage; or from F. <ets>prise</ets> a taking,
capture, prize. See <er>Prize</er>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A right belonging to the crown of England, of taking
two tuns of wine from every ship importing twenty tuns or more, -- one
before and one behind the mast. By charter of Edward I. butlerage was
substituted for this.</def> <au>Blackstone.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
share of merchandise taken as lawful prize at sea which belongs to the
king or admiral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priscillianist</ent><br/
<hw>Pris*cil"lian*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>A follower of <ets>Priscillian</ets>, bishop of Avila
in Spain, in the fourth century, who mixed various elements of
Gnosticism and Manicheism with Christianity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prise</ent><br/
<hw>Prise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An enterprise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prise</ent><br/
<hw>Prise</hw>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See <er>Prize</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 5. Also <er>Prize</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Priser</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Prizer</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prism</ent><br/
<hw>Prism</hw> <pr>(pr<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prisma</ets>, Gr. <grk>pri`sma</grk>, fr. <grk>pri`zein</grk>,
<grk>pri`ein</grk>, to saw: cf. F. <ets>prisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A solid whose bases or ends are any similar,
equal, and parallel plane figures, and whose sides are
parallelograms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Prisms</xex> of different forms are often named
from the figure of their bases; as, a triangular <xex>prism</xex>, a
quadrangular <xex>prism</xex>, a rhombic <xex>prism</xex>,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>A transparent body, with usually
three rectangular plane faces or sides, and two equal and parallel
triangular ends or bases; -- used in experiments on refraction,
dispersion, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A form the planes of which
are parallel to the vertical axis. See <er>Form</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
13.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Achromatic prism</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>a prism
composed usually of two prisms of different transparent substances which
have unequal dispersive powers, as two different kinds of glass,
especially flint glass and crown glass, the difference of dispersive
power being compensated by giving them different refracting angles, so
that, when placed together so as to have opposite relative positions, a
ray of light passed through them is refracted or bent into a new
position, but is free from color.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Nicol's
prism</b></col>, <col><b>Nicol prism</b></col></mcol>. <ety>[So called
from Wm. <ets>Nicol</ets>, of Edinburgh, who first proposed it.]</ety>
<fld>(Opt.)</fld> <cd>An instrument for experiments in polarization,
consisting of a rhomb of Iceland spar, which has been bisected obliquely
at a certain angle, and the two parts again joined with transparent
cement, so that the ordinary image produced by double refraction is
thrown out of the field by total reflection from the internal cemented
surface, and the extraordinary, or polarized, image alone is
transmitted.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prismatical</ent><br/
<ent>Prismatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pris*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pris*mat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prismatique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Resembling, or
pertaining to, a prism; <as>as, a <ex>prismatic</ex> form or
cleavage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Separated or distributed by a prism; formed by a
prism; <as>as, <ex>prismatic</ex> colors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Orthorhombic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prismatic borax</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>borax
crystallized in the form of oblique prisms, with ten molecules of water;
-- distinguished from <xex>octahedral borax</xex>.</cd> --
<col><b>Prismatic colors</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>the seven
colors into which light is resolved when passed through a prism; primary
colors. See <cref>Primary colors</cref>, under <er>Color</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Prismatic compass</b></col> <fld>(Surv.)</fld>, <cd>a compass
having a prism for viewing a distant object and the compass card at the
same time.</cd> -- <col><b>Prismatic spectrum</b></col>
<fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>the spectrum produced by the passage of light
through a prism.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prismatically</ent><br/
<hw>Pris*mat"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the form or manner
of a prism; by means of a prism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prismatoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Pris`ma*toid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
prism + <ets>-oid</ets>: F. <ets>prismato<ium/de</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Having a prismlike form.</def> <rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prism glass</ent><br/
<hw>Prism glass</hw>. <def>Glass with one side smooth and the other side
formed into sharp-edged ridges so as to reflect the light that passes
through, used at windows to throw the light into the interior.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prismoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"moid</hw> <pr>(pr<icr/z"moid)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prismto<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <def>A body that approaches to the
form of a prism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prismoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Pris*moid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the form
of a prismoid; <as>as, <ex>prismoidal</ex> solids</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prismy</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a
prism.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prison</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"on</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>prehensio</ets>, <ets>prensio</ets>, a seizing, arresting, fr.
<ets>prehendre</ets>, <ets>prendere</ets>, to lay hold of, to seize. See
<er>Prehensile</er>, and cf. <er>Prize</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Misprision</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place where persons are
confined, or restrained of personal liberty; hence, a place or state
o<?/ confinement, restraint, or safe custody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bring my soul out of <qex>prison</qex>, that I may praise thy
name.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. cxlii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tyrant Aeolus, . . . <br/
With power imperial, curbs the struggling winds,<br/
And sounding tempests in dark <qex>prisons</qex> binds.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a building for the safe custody or
confinement of criminals and others committed by lawful
authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Prison bars</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Prison
base</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <er>Base</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 24.</cd> --
<col><b>Prison breach</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>See Note under 3d
<er>Escape</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Prison
house</b></col>, <cd>a prison.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Prison
ship</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a ship fitted up for the
confinement of prisoners.</cd> -- <col><b>Prison van</b></col>, <cd>a
carriage in which prisoners are conveyed to and from
prison.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prison</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"on</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prisoned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prisoning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To imprison; to
shut up in, or as in, a prison; to confine; to restrain from
liberty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>prisoned</qex> eagle dies for rage.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His true respect will <qex>prison</qex> false desire.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bind (together); to enchain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sir William Crispyn with the duke was led<br/
Together <qex>prisoned</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Robert of
Brunne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prisoner</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"on*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prisonnier</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is confined in a
prison.</def> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person under arrest, or in custody, whether in
prison or not; a person held in involuntary restraint; a captive;
<as>as, a <ex>prisoner</ex> at the bar of a court</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prisoner</qex> of Hope thou art, -- look up and sing.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prisoner's base</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Base</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 24.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prisonment</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"on*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Imprisonment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pristinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"tin*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pristine;
primitive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Pristinate</xex>
idolatry.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pristine</ent><br/
<hw>Pris"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pristinus</ets>, akin to <ets>prior</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pristin</ets>. See <er>Prior</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Belonging to the earliest period or state; original; primitive;
primeval; <as>as, the <ex>pristine</ex> state of innocence; the
<ex>pristine</ex> manners of a people; <ex>pristine</ex>
vigor.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pritch</ent><br/
<hw>Pritch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prick</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sharp-pointed instrument;
also, an eelspear.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pique; offense.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>D.
Rogers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pritchel</ent><br/
<hw>Pritch"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A tool employed by
blacksmiths for punching or enlarging the nail holes in a
horseshoe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prithee</ent><br/
<hw>Prith"ee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>A corruption of
<xex>pray thee</xex>; <as>as, I <ex>prithee</ex></as>; generally used
without <it>I</it>.</def><-- = pray (interj.). See I pray, under pray,
v.i. --> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What was that scream for, I <qex>prithee</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prithee</qex>, tell me, Dimple-chin.</q> <rj><qau>E. C.
Stedman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prittle-prattle</ent><br/
<hw>Prit"tle-prat`tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prattle</er>.]</ety> <def>Empty talk; trifling loquacity; prattle;
-- used in contempt or ridicule.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Abp. Bramhall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"va*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Privacies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See
<er>Private</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being in
retirement from the company or observation of others;
seclusion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place of seclusion from company or observation;
retreat; solitude; retirement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her sacred <qex>privacies</qex> all open lie.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Concealment of what is said or done.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A private matter; a secret.</def>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>See <er>Privity</er>, 2.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privado</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*va"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., fr. L.
<ets>privatus</ets>. See <er>Private</er>.]</ety> <def>A private friend;
a confidential friend; a confidant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privatdocent</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pri*vat"do*cent`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>G.
pl.</it> <plw>-docenten</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <altsp>[Also
<asp>Privatdozent</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[G.; <ets>privat</ets> private +
<ets>docent</ets> teacher. See <er>Docent</er>.]</ety> <def>In the
universities of Germany and some other European countries, a licensed
teacher or lecturer having no share in the university government and
dependent upon fees for remuneration.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Private</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"vate</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>privatus</ets> apart from the state, peculiar to an individual,
private, properly p. p. of <ets>privare</ets> to bereave, deprive,
originally, to separate, fr. <ets>privus</ets> single, private, perhaps
originally, put forward (hence, alone, single) and akin to
<ets>prae</ets> before. See <er>Prior</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Deprive</er>, <er>Privy</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Belonging to, or concerning, an individual person, company, or
interest; peculiar to one's self; unconnected with others; personal;
one's own; not public; not general; separate; <as>as, a man's
<ex>private</ex> opinion; <ex>private</ex> property; a <ex>private</ex>
purse; <ex>private</ex> expenses or interests; a <ex>private</ex>
secretary.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sequestered from company or observation;
appropriated to an individual; secret; secluded; lonely; solitary;
<as>as, a <ex>private</ex> room or apartment; <ex>private</ex>
prayer.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Reason . . . then retires<br/
Into her <qex>private</qex> cell when nature rests.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1140 pr=SKB --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not invested with, or engaged in, public office or
employment; <as>as, a <ex>private</ex> citizen; <ex>private</ex>
life.</as></def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>private</qex> person may arrest a felon.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not publicly known; not open; secret; <as>as, a
<ex>private</ex> negotiation; a <ex>private</ex>
understanding.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Having secret or private knowledge; privy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Private act</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Private
statute</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a statute exclusively for the settlement
of private and personal interests, of which courts do not take judicial
notice; -- opposed to a <contr>general law</contr>, which operates on
the whole community. In the United States Congress, similar private acts
are referred to as <altname>private law</altname> and a general law as a
<contr>public law</contr>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Private
nuisance</b></col> <or/ <col><b>wrong</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See
<er>Nuisance</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Private soldier</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Private</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 5.</cd> -- <col><b>Private
way</b></col>, <cd>a right of private passage over another man's ground;
also, a road on private land, contrasted with <contr>public
road</contr>, which is on a public right of way.</cd></cs>
<rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Private</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"vate</hw> <pr>(pr<imac/"v<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A secret message; a personal unofficial communication.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Personal interest; particular
business.</def><mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor must I be unmindful of my <qex>private</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Privacy; retirement.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>
<ldquo/Go off; I discard you; let me enjoy my <xex>private</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One not invested with a public office.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What have kings, that <qex>privates</qex> have not too?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A common soldier; a soldier below
the grade of a noncommissioned officer.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The private parts; the
genitals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In private</b></col>, <cd>secretly; not openly or
publicly.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privateer</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`va*teer"</hw> <pr>(pr<imac/`v<adot/*t<emac/r")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Private</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
armed private vessel which bears the commission of the sovereign power
to cruise against the enemy. See <cref>Letters of marque</cref>, under
<er>Marque</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The commander of a privateer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Kidd soon threw off the character of a <qex>privateer</qex> and
became a pirate.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privateer</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`va*teer"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Privateered</conjf> <pr>(pr<imac/`v<adot/*t<emac/rd")</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Privateering</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<def>To cruise in a privateer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privateering</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`va*teer"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Cruising in a
privateer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privateersman</ent><br/
<hw>Pri`va*teers"man</hw> <pr>(pr<imac/`v<adot/*t<emac/rz"m<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Privateersmen</plw>
<pr>(pr<imac/`v<adot/*t<emac/rz"m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu> <def>An officer or
seaman of a privateer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privately</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"vate*ly</hw> <pr>(pr<imac/"v<asl/t*l<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>In a private manner; not openly; without the
presence of others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a manner affecting an individual; personally; not
officially; <as>as, he is not <ex>privately</ex>
benefited</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privateness</ent><br/
<hw>Pri"vate*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Seclusion from company or society; retirement;
privacy; secrecy.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of one not invested with public
office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privation</ent><br/
<hw>Pri*va"tion</hw> <pr>(pr<isl/*v<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>privatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>privation</ets>. See
<er>Private</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of depriving, or
taking away; hence, the depriving of rank or office; degradation in
rank; deprivation.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being deprived or destitute of
something, especially of something required or desired; destitution;
need; <as>as, to undergo severe <ex>privations</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The condition of being absent; absence;
negation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Evil will be known by consequence, as being only a
<qex>privation</qex>, or absence, of good.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Privation</qex> mere of light and absent day.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privative</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>privativus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>privatif</ets>. See
<er>Private</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Causing privation;
depriving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting in the absence of something; not
positive; negative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Privative</qex> blessings, blessings of immunity, safeguard,
liberty, and integrity.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Implying privation or negation;
giving a negative force to a word; <as>as, alpha <ex>privative</ex>;
<ex>privative</ex> particles</as>; -- applied to such prefixes and
suffixes as <xex>a-</xex> (Gr. <?/), <xex>un-</xex>, <xex>non-</xex>,
<xex>-less</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privative</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"a*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>That of which the essence is the absence of
something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Blackness and darkness are indeed but <qex>privatives</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A term indicating the absence of
any quality which might be naturally or rationally expected; -- called
also <altname>privative term</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A privative prefix or suffix. See
<er>Privative</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privatively</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"a*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a privative manner; by
the absence of something; negatively.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privativeness</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"a*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
privative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privet</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Scot.
<ets>privie</ets>, Prov. E. <ets>prim-print</ets>, <ets>primwort</ets>.
Prob. for <ets>primet</ets>, and perh. named from being cut and trimmed.
See, <er>Prim</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Prime</er> to prune,
<er>Prim</er>, n., <er>Prie</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An ornamental European shrub (<spn>Ligustrum vulgare</spn>), much
used in hedges; -- called also <altname>prim</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Egyptian privet</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Lawsonia</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Evergreen privet</b></col>, <cd>a
plant of the genus <gen>Rhamnus</gen>. See <er>Alatern</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Mock privet</b></col>, <cd>any one of several evergreen shrubs
of the genus <gen>Phillyrea</gen>. They are from the Mediterranean
region, and have been much cultivated for hedges and for fancifully
clipped shrubberies.</cd><-- i. e., topiary plants --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privilege</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"i*lege</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>privil<egrave/ge</ets>, L. <ets>privilegium</ets> an ordinance or
law against or in favor of an individual; <ets>privus</ets> private +
<ets>lex</ets>, <ets>legis</ets>, law. See <er>Private</er>, and
<er>Legal</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A peculiar benefit, advantage, or favor; a right or
immunity not enjoyed by others or by all; special enjoyment of a good,
or exemption from an evil or burden; a prerogative; advantage;
franchise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He pleads the legal <qex>privilege</qex> of a Roman.</q>
<rj><qau>Kettlewell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>privilege</qex> birthright was a double portion.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A people inheriting <qex>privileges</qex>, franchises, and
liberties.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Stockbroker's Cant)</fld> <def>See <er>Call</er>,
<er>Put</er>, <er>Spread</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Breach of privilege</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Breach</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Question of privilege</b></col>
<fld>(Parliamentary practice)</fld>, <cd>a question which concerns the
security of a member of a legislative body in his special privileges as
such.</cd> -- <col><b>Water privilege</b></col>, <cd>the advantage of
having machinery driven by a stream, or a place affording such
advantage.</cd> <mark>[ U. S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Writ of
privilege</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a writ to deliver a privileged
person from custody when arrested in a civil suit.</cd>
<au>Blackstone.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prerogative; immunity; franchise; right; claim;
liberty.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Privilege</er>, <er>Prerogative</er>.
<xex>Privilege</xex>, among the Romans, was something conferred upon an
individual by a private law; and hence, it denotes some peculiar benefit
or advantage, some right or immunity, not enjoyed by the world at large.
<xex>Prerogative</xex>, among the Romans, was the right of voting first;
and, hence, it denotes a right of precedence, or of doing certain acts,
or enjoying certain privileges, to the exclusion of others. It is the
<xex>privilege</xex> of a member of Congress not to be called in
question elsewhere for words uttered in debate. It is the
<xex>prerogative</xex> of the president to nominate judges and executive
officers. It is the <xex>privilege</xex> of a Christian child to be
instructed in the true religion. It is the <xex>prerogative</xex> of a
parent to govern and direct his children.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privilege</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"i*lege</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Privileged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Privileging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>privil<eacute/gier</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To grant some particular right or exemption to; to
invest with a peculiar right or immunity; to authorize; <as>as, to
<ex>privilege</ex> representatives from arrest</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>privilege</qex> dishonor in thy name.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring or put into a condition of privilege or
exemption from evil or danger; to exempt; to deliver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He took this place for sanctuary, And it shall
<qex>privilege</qex> him from your hands.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privileged</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"i*leged</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Invested with a
privilege; enjoying a peculiar right, advantage, or immunity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Privileged communication</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A communication which can not be disclosed without the
consent of the party making it, -- such as those made by a client to his
legal adviser, or by persons to their religious or medical
advisers</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A communication which does not expose
the party making it to indictment for libel, -- such as those made by
persons communicating confidentially with a government, persons
consulted confidentially as to the character of servants, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Privileged debts</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>those to which
a preference in payment is given out of the estate of a deceased person,
or out of the estate of an insolvent.</cd> <au>Wharton.</au>
<au>Burrill.</au> -- <col><b>Privileged witnesses</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>witnesses who are not obliged to testify as to
certain things, as lawyers in relation to their dealings with their
clients, and officers of state as to state secrets; also, by statute,
clergymen and physicans are placed in the same category, so far as
concerns information received by them professionally.</cd></cs><-- also
called attorney-client privilege, doctor-patient privilege --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privily</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"i*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a privy manner; privately;
secretly.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>2 Pet. ii. 1.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privity</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Privities</plw> <pr>(-t<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[From
<er>Privy</er>, <pos>a.</pos>: cf. F. <ets>privaut<eacute/</ets> extreme
familiarity.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Privacy; secrecy; confidence.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will unto you, in <qex>privity</qex>, discover . . . my
purpose.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Private knowledge; joint knowledge with another of a
private concern; cognizance implying consent or concurrence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the doors were laid open for his departure, not without the
<qex>privity</qex> of the Prince of Orange.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A private matter or business; a secret.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The genitals; the
privates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A connection, or bond of union,
between parties, as to some particular transaction; mutual or successive
relationship to the same rights of property.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privy</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>priv<eacute/</ets>, fr. L. <ets>privatus</ets>. See
<er>Private</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to some person exclusively;
assigned to private uses; not public; private; <as>as, the
<ex>privy</ex> purse</as>.</def> <ldquo/ <xex>Privee</xex> knights and
squires.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Secret; clandestine.</def> <ldquo/ A
<xex>privee</xex> thief.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Appropriated to retirement; private; not open to the
public.</def> <ldquo/ <xex>Privy</xex> chambers.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ezek.
xxi. 14.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Admitted to knowledge of a secret transaction;
secretly cognizant; privately knowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His wife also being <qex>privy</qex> to it.</q> <rj><qau>Acts v.
2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Myself am one made <qex>privy</qex> to the plot.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Privy chamber</b></col>, <cd>a private apartment in a
royal residence.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Privy
council</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>the principal council of
the sovereign, composed of the cabinet ministers and other persons
chosen by the king or queen.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> -- <col><b>Privy
councilor</b></col>, <cd>a member of the privy council.</cd> --
<col><b>Privy purse</b></col>, <cd>moneys set apart for the personal use
of the monarch; also, the title of the person having charge of these
moneys.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Macaulay.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>Privy seal</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Privy
signet</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the seal which the king uses in grants,
etc., which are to pass the great seal, or which he uses in matters of
subordinate consequence which do not require the great seal; also,
<specif>elliptically,</specif> the principal secretary of state, or
person intrusted with the privy seal.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Privy verdict</b></col>, <cd>a verdict given privily to the
judge out of court; -- now disused.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Privy</ent><br/
<hw>Priv"y</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Privies</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A partaker; a
person having an interest in any action or thing; one who has an
interest in an estate created by another; a person having an interest
derived from a contract or conveyance to which he is not himself a
party. The term, in its proper sense, is distinguished from
<xex>party</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au> <au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A necessary house or place for performing excretory
functions in private; an outhouse; a backhouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prizable</ent><br/
<hw>Priz"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Valuable.</def>
<rj><au>H. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prize</ent><br/
<hw>Prize</hw> <pr>(pr<imac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prise</ets> a seizing, hold, grasp, fr. <ets>pris</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>prendre</ets> to take, L. <ets>prendere</ets>,
<ets>prehendere</ets>; in some senses, as 2 <sd>(b)</sd>, either from,
or influenced by, F. <ets>prix</ets> price. See <er>Prison</er>,
<er>Prehensile</er>, and cf. <er>Pry</er>, and also
<er>Price</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is taken from another; something
captured; a thing seized by force, stratagem, or superior
power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will depart my <qex>pris</qex>, or my prey, by deliberation.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His own <qex>prize</qex>,<br/
Whom formerly he had in battle won.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Hence, specifically; <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>Anything captured by a belligerent using the rights of war; esp.,
property captured at sea in virtue of the rights of war, as a
vessel.</def> <au>Kent.</au> <au>Brande & C.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An
honor or reward striven for in a competitive contest; anything offered
to be competed for, or as an inducement to, or reward of,
effort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll never wrestle for <qex>prize</qex> more.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I fought and conquered, yet have lost the <qex>prize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>That which may be won by chance, as in a
lottery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything worth striving for; a valuable possession
held or in prospect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I press toward the mark for the <qex>prize</qex> of the high
calling of God in Christ Jesus.</q> <rj><qau>Phil. iii.
14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A contest for a reward; competition.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A lever; a pry; also, the hold of a lever.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>prise</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prize court</b></col>, <cd>a court having jurisdiction of
all captures made in war on the high seas.</cd> <au>Bouvier.</au> --
<col><b>Prize fight</b></col>, <cd>an exhibition contest, esp. one of
pugilists, for a stake or wager.</cd> -- <col><b>Prize
fighter</b></col>, <cd>one who fights publicly for a reward; -- applied
esp. to a professional boxer or pugilist.</cd> <au>Pope.</au> --
<col><b>Prize fighting</b></col>, <cd>fighting, especially boxing, in
public for a reward or wager.</cd> -- <col><b>Prize master</b></col>,
<cd>an officer put in charge or command of a captured vessel.</cd> --
<col><b>Prize medal</b></col>, <cd>a medal given as a prize.</cd> --
<col><b>Prize money</b></col>, <cd>a dividend from the proceeds of a
captured vessel, etc., paid to the captors.</cd> -- <col><b>Prize
ring</b></col>, <cd>the ring or inclosure for a prize fight; the system
and practice of prize fighting.</cd> -- <col><b>To make prize
of</b></col>, <cd>to capture.</cd> <au>Hawthorne.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prize</ent><br/
<hw>Prize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To move with a lever;
to force up or open; to pry.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>prise</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prize</ent><br/
<hw>Prize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>priser</ets>, OF.
<ets>prisier</ets>, <ets>preisier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pretiare</ets>, fr.
<ets>pretium</ets> worth, value, price. See <er>Price</er>, and cf.
<er>Praise</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written also <asp>prise</asp>.
]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To set or estimate the value of; to appraise; to
price; to rate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A goodly price that I was <qex>prized</qex> at.</q> <rj><qau>Zech.
xi. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>prize</qex> it [life] not a straw, but for mine honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To value highly; to estimate to be of great worth;
to esteem.</def> <ldquo/[I] do love, <xex>prize</xex>, honor you.
<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>prized</qex> your person, but your crown disdain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prize</ent><br/
<hw>Prize</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>prix</ets> price. See 3d
<er>Prize</er>. ]</ety> <def>Estimation; valuation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prizeman</ent><br/
<hw>Prize"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prizemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The winner of a
prize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prizer</ent><br/
<hw>Priz"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 3d
<er>Prize</er>.]</ety> <def>One who estimates or sets the value of a
thing; an appraiser.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prizer</ent><br/
<hw>Priz"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 1st <er>Prize</er>.]</ety>
<def>One who contends for a prize; a prize fighter; a challenger.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Appeareth no man yet to answer the <qex>prizer</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prizing</ent><br/
<hw>Priz"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 2d <er>Prize</er>.]</ety>
<def>The application of a lever to move any weighty body, as a cask,
anchor, cannon, car, etc. See <er>Prize</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pro-</ent><br/
<hw>Pro-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>pro</ets>, or Gr. <?/. See
<er>Pro</er>.]</ety> <def>A prefix signifying <xex>before</xex>, <xex>in
front</xex>, <xex>forth</xex>, <xex>for</xex>, <xex>in behalf of</xex>,
<xex>in place of</xex>, <xex>according to</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>pro</ex>pose, to place before; <ex>pro</ex>ceed, to go before or
forward; <ex>pro</ex>ject, to throw forward; <ex>pro</ex>logue, part
spoken before (the main piece); <ex>pro</ex>pel, <ex>pro</ex>gnathous;
<ex>pro</ex>vide, to look out for; <ex>pro</ex>noun, a word instead of a
noun; <ex>pro</ex>consul, a person acting in place of a consul;
<ex>pro</ex>portion, arrangement according to parts.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pro</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro</hw>, <pos>prep.</pos> <ety>[L.; akin to <ets>prae</ets>
before, Gr. <?/, and E. <ets>for</ets>. See <er>For</er>,
<ets>prep</ets>., and cf. <er>Prior</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A
Latin preposition signifying <xex>for</xex>, <xex>before</xex>,
<xex>forth</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pro confesso</b></col> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>taken as confessed. The action of a court of equity on that portion
of the pleading in a particular case which the pleading on the other
side does not deny.</cd> -- <col><b>Pro rata</b></col>. <ety>[L. See
<er>Prorate</er>.]</ety> <cd>In proportion; proportion.</cd> --
<col><b>Pro re nata</b></col> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>for
the existing occasion; as matters are.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pro</ent><br/
<hw>Pro</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>For, on, or in behalf of, the
affirmative side; -- in contrast with <ant>con</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pro and con</b></col>, <cd>for and against, on the
affirmative and on the negative side; <as>as, they debated the question
<ex>pro and con</ex></as>; -- formerly used also as a verb.</cd> --
<col><b>Pros and cons</b></col>, <cd>the arguments or reasons on either
side.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proa</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Malay
<ets>pr<amac/</ets><?/, pr<ets><amac/h<?/</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A sailing canoe of the Ladrone Islands and Malay
Archipelago, having its lee side flat and its weather side like that of
an ordinary boat. The ends are alike. The canoe is long and narrow, and
is kept from overturning by a cigar-shaped log attached to a frame
extending several feet to windward. It has been called the
<altname>flying proa</altname>, and is the swiftest sailing craft
known.</def><-- having an outrigger --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proach</ent><br/
<hw>Proach</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Approach</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proatlas</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*at"las</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>atlas</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
vertebral rudiment in front of the atlas in some reptiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probabiliorism</ent><br/
<hw>Prob`a*bil"i*o*rism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
doctrine of the probabiliorists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probabiliorist</ent><br/
<hw>Prob`a*bil"i*o*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>probabilior</ets>, compar. of <ets>probabilis</ets>
probable.]</ety> <fld>(Casuistry)</fld> <def>One who holds, in
opposition to the probabilists, that a man is bound to do that which is
most probably right.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probabilism</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"a*bil*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>probabilisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrine of the
probabilists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probabilist</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"a*bil*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>probabiliste</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who maintains that certainty is impossible, and
that probability alone is to govern our faith and actions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Casuistry)</fld> <def>One who maintains that a man
may do that which has a probability of being right, or which is
inculcated by teachers of authority, although other opinions may seem to
him still more probable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probability</ent><br/
<hw>Prob`a*bil"i*ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Probabilities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>probabilitas</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>probabilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being probable; appearance
of reality or truth; reasonable ground of presumption;
likelihood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Probability</qex> is the appearance of the agreement or
disagreement of two ideas, by the intervention of proofs whose
connection is not constant, but appears for the most part to be so.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is or appears probable; anything that has
the appearance of reality or truth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whole life of man is a perpetual comparison of evidence and
balancing of <qex>probabilities</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We do not call for evidence till antecedent
<qex>probabilities</qex> fail.</q> <rj><qau>J. H. Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Likelihood of the occurrence of
any event in the doctrine of chances, or the ratio of the number of
favorable chances to the whole number of chances, favorable and
unfavorable. See 1st <er>Chance</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Likeliness; credibleness; likelihood;
chance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probable</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>probabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>probare</ets> to try, approve, prove:
cf. F. <ets>probable</ets>. See <er>Prove</er>, and cf.
<er>Provable</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being proved.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having more evidence for than against; supported by
evidence which inclines the mind to believe, but leaves some room for
doubt; likely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That is accounted <qex>probable</qex> which has better arguments
producible for it than can be brought against it.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do not say that the principles of religion are merely
<qex>probable</qex>; I have before asserted them to be morally
certain.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Wilkins.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1141 --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Rendering probable; supporting, or giving ground
for, belief, but not demonstrating; <as>as, <ex>probable</ex> evidence;
<ex>probable</ex> presumption.</as></def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Probable cause</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a
reasonable ground of presumption that a charge is, or my be, well
founded.</cd> -- <col><b>Probable error</b></col><cd> (of an
observation, or of the mean of a number), that within which, taken
positively and negatively, there is an even chance that the real error
shall lie. Thus, if 3<sec/ is the probable error in a given case, the
chances that the real error is greater than 3<sec/ are equal to the
chances that it is less. The probable error is computed from the
observations made, and is used to express their degree of
accuracy.</cd><-- now, usually standard deviation is used --> --
<col><b>The probable</b></col>, <cd>that which is within the bounds of
probability; that which is not unnatural or preternatural; -- opposed to
<xex>the marvelous</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probably</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"a*bly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a probable
manner; in likelihood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Distinguish between what may possibly and what will
<qex>probably</qex> be done.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ba*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Probate</er>.]</ety> <def>Proof; trial.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"bal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Approved;
probable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probality</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*bal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Probability.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/With as great
<xex>probality</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probang</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"bang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Probe</er>.]</ety> <def>A slender elastic rod, as of whalebone, with
a sponge on the end, for removing obstructions from the esophagus,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>probatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>probare</ets> to prove. See
<er>Prove</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Proof.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Official proof;
especially, the proof before a competent officer or tribunal that an
instrument offered, purporting to be the last will and testament of a
person deceased, is indeed his lawful act; the copy of a will proved,
under the seal of the Court of Probate, delivered to the executors with
a certificate of its having been proved.</def> <au>Bouvier.</au>
<au>Burrill.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The right or jurisdiction of proving
wills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"bate</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or belonging to a probate, or
court of probate; <as>as, a <ex>probate</ex> record</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Probate Court</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Court
of Probate</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a court for the probate of wills.</cd>
-- <col><b>Probate duty</b></col>, <cd>a government tax on property
passing by will.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To obtain the
official approval of, as of an instrument purporting to be the last will
and testament; <as>as, the executor has <ex>probated</ex> the
will</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>probatio</ets>, fr. <ets>probare</ets> to try, examine, prove: cf.
F. <ets>probation</ets>. See <er>Prove</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of proving; also, that which proves
anything; proof.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When by miracle God dispensed great gifts to the laity, . . . he
gave <qex>probation</qex> that he intended that all should prophesy and
preach.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any proceeding designed to ascertain truth, to
determine character, qualification, etc.; examination; trial; <as>as, to
engage a person on <ex>probation</ex></as>.</def> Hence, specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The novitiate which a person must pass in a convent,
to probe his or her virtue and ability to bear the severities of the
rule.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The trial of a ministerial candidate's
qualifications prior to his ordination, or to his settlement as a
pastor.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Moral trial; the state of man in the
present life, in which he has the opportunity of proving his character,
and becoming qualified for a happier state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No [view of human life] seems so reasonable as that which regards
it as a state of <qex>probation</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Paley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probational</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Probationary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probationary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to probation; serving for trial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To consider this life . . . as a <qex>probationary</qex>
state.</q> <rj><qau>Paley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probationer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is undergoing probation; one who is on
trial; a novice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While yet a young <qex>probationer</qex>,<br/
And candidate of heaven.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A student in divinity, who, having received
certificates of good morals and qualifications from his university, is
admitted to several trials by a presbytery, and, on acquitting himself
well, is licensed to preach.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probationership</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tion*er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being a
probationer; novitiate.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probationship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tion*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A state of
probation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probative</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ba*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>probativus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>probatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Serving
for trial or proof; probationary; <as>as, <ex>probative</ex> judgments;
<ex>probative</ex> evidence.</as></def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ba"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An examiner; an approver.</def>
<rj><au>Maydman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>One who, when indicted for
crime, confessed it, and accused others, his accomplices, in order to
obtain pardon; a state's evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ba*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>probatoire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Serving for trial;
probationary.</def> <rj><au>Abp. Bramhall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or serving for, proof.</def>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Probatory term</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a time for
taking testimony.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probe</ent><br/
<hw>Probe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Probed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Probing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>probare</ets> to try,
examine. See <er>Prove</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To examine, as a wound, an ulcer, or some cavity of
the body, with a probe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: to search to the bottom; to scrutinize or
examine thoroughly.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The growing disposition to <qex>probe</qex> the legality of all
acts, of the crown.</q> <rj><qau>Hallam.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probe</ent><br/
<hw>Probe</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>An instrument for
examining the depth or other circumstances of a wound, ulcer, or cavity,
or the direction of a sinus, of for exploring for bullets, for stones in
the bladder, etc.</def> <rj><au>Parr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Probe scissors</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Probe-pointed scissors</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>,
<cd>scissors used to open wounds, the blade of which, to be thrust into
the orifice, has a button at the end.</cd>
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probeagle</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"bea`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Porbeagle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probe-pointed</ent><br/
<hw>Probe"-point`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>
<def>Having a blunt or button-shaped extremity; -- said of cutting
instruments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Probity</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>probit<eacute/</ets>, fr. L. <ets>probitas</ets>, fr.
<ets>probus</ets> good, proper, honest. Cf. <er>Prove</er>.]</ety>
<def>Tried virtue or integrity; approved moral excellence; honesty;
rectitude; uprightness.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Probity</xex> of mind.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Probity</er>, <er>Integrity</er>.</syn>
<usage> <xex>Probity</xex> denotes unimpeachable honesty and virtue,
shown especially by the performance of those obligations, called
<xex>imperfect</xex>, which the laws of the state do not reach, and can
not enforce. <xex>Integrity</xex> denotes a <xex>whole</xex>-hearted
honesty, and especially that which excludes all injustice that might
favor one's self. It has a peculiar reference to uprightness in mutual
dealings, transfer of property, and the execution of trusts for
others.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Problem</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"lem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>probl<egrave/me</ets>, L. <ets>problema</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ anything
thrown forward, a question proposed for solution, fr. <?/ to throw or
lay before; <?/ before, forward + <?/ to throw. Cf. <er>Parable</er>.
]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A question proposed for solution; a matter stated
for examination or proof; hence, a matter difficult of solution or
settlement; a doubtful case; a question involving doubt.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Anything which is required to be
done; as, in geometry, to bisect a line, to draw a perpendicular; or, in
algebra, to find an unknown quantity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Problem</xex> differs from <xex>theorem</xex> in
this, that a problem is something to be done, as to bisect a triangle,
to describe a circle, etc.; a theorem is something to be proved, as that
all the angles of a triangle are equal to two right angles.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Plane problem</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a problem
that can be solved by the use of the rule and compass.</cd> --
<col><b>Solid problem</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a problem
requiring in its geometric solution the use of a conic section or higher
curve.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Problematical</ent><br/
<ent>Problematic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prob`lem*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Prob`lem*at"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>problematicus</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>probl<eacute/matique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the nature of a problem; not shown in fact; questionable;
uncertain; unsettled; doubtful; <as>as, his theory is
<ex>problematic</ex> because it fails to explain several
facts</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Prob`lem*at"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Diligent inquiries into remote and <qex>problematical</qex> guilt
leave a gate wide open to . . . informers.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having characteristics which will create
difficulties or undesirable consequences; -- of a proposed action;
<as>as, the proposed law is <ex>problematic</ex> because it will cause
many people to lose their jobs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Problematist</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"lem*a*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
proposes problems.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Problematize</ent><br/
<hw>Prob"lem*a*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To propose
problems.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Hear him
<xex>problematize</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*bos"ci*date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Proboscis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a proboscis;
proboscidial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`bos*cid"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Proboscis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of large
mammals including the elephants and mastodons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidean</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`bos*cid"e*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Proboscidian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`bos*cid"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Proboscidate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidian</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`bos*cid"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the Proboscidea.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>One of the Proboscidea.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*bos`ci*dif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Proboscis</er>, and <er>-ferous</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive division of
pectinibranchiate gastropods, including those that have a long
retractile proboscis, with the mouth at the end, as the cones, whelks,
tritons, and cowries. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Gastropoda</er>, and
of <er>Winkle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A subdivision of the
t<ae/nioglossate gastropods, including the fig-shells
(<gen>Pyrula</gen>), the helmet shells (<gen>Cassis</gen>), the tritons,
and allied genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscidiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`bos*cid"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
form or uses of a proboscis; <as>as, a <ex>proboscidiform</ex>
mouth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proboscis</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*bos"cis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proboscides</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. fr. Gr. <?/; <?/
before + <?/ to feed, graze.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A hollow organ or tube attached to the head, or connected with the
mouth, of various animals, and generally used in taking food or drink; a
snout; a trunk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The proboscis of an elephant is a flexible muscular
elongation of the nose. The proboscis of insects is usually a chitinous
tube formed by the modified maxill<ae/, or by the labium. See
<xex>Illusts</xex>. of <er>Hemiptera</er> and
<er>Lepidoptera</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>By extension, applied to various
tubelike mouth organs of the lower animals that can be everted or
protruded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The proboscis of annelids and of mollusks is usually a
portion of the pharynx that can be everted or protruded. That of
nemerteans is a special long internal organ, not connected with the
mouth, and not used in feeding, but capable of being protruded from a
pore in the head. See <xex>Illust.</xex> in Appendix.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The nose.</def> <mark>[Jocose]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proboscis monkey</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Kahau</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procacious</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ca"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>, fr. <ets>procare</ets> to ask,
demand.]</ety> <def>Pert; petulant; forward; saucy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procacity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procacitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Forwardness; pertness;
petulance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procambium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*cam"bi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Cambium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
young tissue of a fibrovascular bundle before its component cells have
begun to be differentiated.</def> <rj><au>Sachs.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procatarctic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`cat*arc"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
beginning beforehand. fr. <?/ to begin first; <?/ before + <?/ to begin;
<?/ intens. + <?/ to begin: cf. F. <ets>procatarctique</ets>. ]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Beginning; predisposing; exciting; initial.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The words <xex>procatarctic causes</xex> have been used
with different significations. Thus they have been employed synonymously
with <xex>prime causes</xex>, <xex>exciting causes</xex>, and
<xex>predisposing</xex> or <xex>remote causes</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The physician inquires into the <qex>procatarctic</qex>
causes.</q> <rj><qau>Harvey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procatarxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`cat*arx"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ first beginning.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The kindling of a
disease into action; also, the procatarctic cause.</def>
<rj><au>Quincy.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procedendo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`ce*den"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abl. of the
gerundive of L. <ets>procedere</ets>. see <er>Proceed</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A writ by which a cause which has
been removed on insufficient grounds from an inferior to a superior
court by <xex>certiorari</xex>, or otherwise, is sent down again to the
same court, <xex>to be proceeded in</xex> there.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>In English practice, a writ issuing out of chancery in cases where
the judges of subordinate courts delay giving judgment, commanding them
to proceed to judgment.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A writ by which the
commission of the justice of the peace is revived, after having been
suspended.</def> <rj><au>Tomlins. Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procedure</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ce"dure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>proc<eacute/dure</ets>. See <er>Proceed</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or manner of proceeding or moving forward; progress;
process; operation; conduct.</def> <ldquo/The true <xex>procedure</xex>
of conscience.<rdquo/ <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A step taken; an act performed; a proceeding; the
steps taken in an action or other legal proceeding.</def>
<ldquo/Gracious <xex>procedures</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which results; issue; product.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceed</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ceed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Proceeded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Proceeding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>proc<eacute/der</ets>. fr. L. <ets>procedere</ets>,
<ets>processum</ets>, to go before, to proceed; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>cedere</ets> to move. See <er>Cede</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
move, pass, or go forward or onward; to advance; to continue or renew
motion begun; <as>as, to <ex>proceed</ex> on a journey</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If thou <qex>proceed</qex> in this thy insolence.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass from one point, topic, or stage, to another;
<as>as, to <ex>proceed</ex> with a story or argument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To issue or come forth as from a source or origin;
to come from; <as>as, light <ex>proceeds</ex> from the
sun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>proceeded</qex> forth and came from God.</q> <rj><qau>John
viii. 42.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It <qex>proceeds</qex> from policy, not love.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To go on in an orderly or regulated manner; to begin
and carry on a series of acts or measures; to act by method; to
prosecute a design.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that <qex>proceeds</qex> upon other principles in his
inquiry.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be transacted; to take place; to occur.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He will, after his sour fashion, tell you<br/
What hath <qex>proceeded</qex> worthy note to-day.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To have application or effect; to operate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This rule only <qex>proceeds</qex> and takes place when a person
can not of common law condemn another by his sentence.</q>
<rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To begin and carry on a legal
process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To advance; go on; continue; progress; issue;
arise; emanate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceed</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ceed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Proceeds</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceeder</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ceed"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
proceeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceeding</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ceed"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who
proceeds, or who prosecutes a design or transaction; progress or
movement from one thing to another; a measure or step taken in a course
of business; a transaction; <as>as, an illegal <ex>proceeding</ex>; a
cautious or a violent <ex>proceeding</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>proceedings</qex> of the high commission.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The course of
procedure in the prosecution of an action at law.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proceedings of a society</b></col>, <cd>the published
record of its action, or of things done at its meetings.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Procedure; measure; step, See
<er>Transaction</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceeds</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ceeds</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>That which comes
forth or results; effect; yield; issue; product; sum accruing from a
sale, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceleusmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Proc`e*leus*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proceleusmaticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to rouse to action
beforehand; <?/ + <?/ to incite; cf. F.
<ets>proc<eacute/leusmatique</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Inciting; animating; encouraging.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>Consisting of four short
syllables; composed of feet of four short syllables each.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceleusmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Proc`e*leus*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>A foot consisting of four short
syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procellarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`cel*la"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procella</ets> a storm.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a
family of oceanic birds (<fam>Procellarid<ae/</fam>) including the
petrels, fulmars, and shearwaters. They are often seen in great
abundance in stormy weather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procellous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cel"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procellosus</ets>, fr. <ets>procella</ets> a storm.]</ety>
<def>Stormy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procephalic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`ce*phal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>cephalic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or forming, the front of the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Procephalic lobe</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>that
part of the head of an invertebrate animal which is in front of the
mouth.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proception</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + L. <ets>capere</ets> to take.]</ety>
<def>Preoccupation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Eikon
Basilik<?/.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procere</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cere"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procerus</ets> tall.]</ety> <def>Of high stature; tall.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procerebrum</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cer"e*brum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>cerebrum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The prosencephalon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proceres</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proc"e*res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>procer</ets> <?/ chief.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order
of large birds; the Ratit<ae/; -- called also
<altname><ord>Proceri</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procerite</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"er*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ <?/ horn.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
segment next to the flagellum of the antenn<ae/ of Crustacea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procerity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cer"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proceritas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Height of stature; tallness.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Process</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>proc<egrave/s</ets>, L.
<ets>processus</ets>. See <er>Proceed</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of proceeding; continued forward movement;
procedure; progress; advance.</def> <ldquo/Long <xex>process</xex> of
time.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The thoughts of men are widened with the <qex>process</qex> of the
suns.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><-- = advance of time? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A series of actions, motions, or occurrences;
progressive act or transaction; continuous operation; normal or actual
course or procedure; regular proceeding; <as>as, the <ex>process</ex> of
vegetation or decomposition; a chemical <ex>process</ex>;
<ex>processes</ex> of nature.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tell her the <qex>process</qex> of Antonio's end.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A statement of events; a narrative.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat. & Zool.)</fld> <def>Any marked prominence or
projecting part, especially of a bone; anapophysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The whole course of proceedings in
a cause real or personal, civil or criminal, from the beginning to the
end of the suit; strictly, the means used for bringing the defendant
into court to answer to the action; -- a generic term for writs of the
class called <xex>judicial</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Deacon's process</b></col> <ety>[from H.
<ets>Deacon</ets>, who introduced it]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
method of obtaining chlorine gas by passing hydrochloric acid gas over
heated slag which has been previously saturated with a solution of some
metallic salt, as sulphate of copper.</cd> -- <col><b>Final
process</b></col> <fld>(Practice)</fld>, <cd>a writ of execution in an
action at law.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> -- <col><b>In process</b></col>,
<cd>in the condition of advance, accomplishment, transaction, or the
like; begun, and not completed.</cd> -- <col><b>Jury process</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the process by which a jury is summoned in a
cause, and by which their attendance is enforced.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au>
-- <col><b>Leblanc's process</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>the
process of manufacturing soda by treating salt with sulphuric acid,
reducing the sodium sulphate so formed to sodium sulphide by roasting
with charcoal, and converting the sodium sulphide to sodium carbonate by
roasting with lime.</cd> -- <col><b>Mesne process</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Mesne</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Process milling</b></col>, <cd>the
process of high milling for grinding flour. See under
<er>Milling</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Reversible process</b></col>
<fld>(Thermodynamics)</fld>, <cd>any process consisting of a cycle of
operations such that the different operations of the cycle can be
performed in reverse order with a reversal of their
effects.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1142 --></p>

<p><ent>Procession</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>processio</ets>. See <er>Proceed</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of proceeding, moving on, advancing, or
issuing; regular, orderly, or ceremonious progress; continuous
course.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That the <qex>procession</qex> of their life might be</q> <q>More
equable, majestic, pure, and free.</q> <rj><qau>Trench.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is moving onward in an orderly, stately,
or solemn manner; a train of persons advancing in order; a ceremonious
train; a retinue; <as>as, a <ex>procession</ex> of mourners; the Lord
Mayor's <ex>procession</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here comes the townsmen on <qex>procession</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>An orderly and ceremonial
progress of persons, either from the sacristy to the choir, or from the
choir around the church, within or without.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>An old term for
litanies which were said in procession and not kneeling.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Procession of the Holy Ghost</b></col>, <cd>a theological
term applied to the relation of the Holy Spirit to the Father and the
Son, the Eastern Church affirming that the Spirit proceeds from the
Father only, and the Western Church that the Spirit proceeds from the
Father and the Son.</cd> <au>Shipley.</au> -- <col><b>Procession
week</b></col>, <cd>a name for Rogation week, when processions were
made; Cross-week.</cd> <au>Shipley.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procession</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To
ascertain, mark, and establish the boundary lines of, as lands.</def>
<mark>[Local, U. S. (North Carolina and Tennessee).]</mark> <ldquo/To
<xex>procession</xex> the lands of such persons as desire it.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procession</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To march in
procession.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procession</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To honor with a
procession.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a procession; consisting in a procession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>processional</qex> services became more frequent.</q>
<rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>processionnal</ets>, LL. <ets>processionale</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A service book relating to
ecclesiastical processions.</def> <rj><au>J. Gregory.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hymn, or other selection, sung during a church
procession; <as>as, the <ex>processional</ex> was the 202d
hymn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processionalist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who goes or marches
in a procession.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>processionarius</ets>, F. <ets>processionnaire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Pertaining to a procession; consisting in processions; <as>as,
<ex>processionary</ex> service</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Processionary moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
moth of the genus <gen>Cnethocampa</gen>, especially <spn>Cnethocampa
processionea</spn> of Europe, whose larv<ae/ make large webs on oak
trees, and go out to feed in regular order. They are covered with
stinging hairs.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processioner</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who takes part in a procession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A manual of processions; a processional.</def>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An officer appointed to procession lands.</def>
<mark>[Local, U. S. (North Carolina and Tennessee).]</mark>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processioning</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sion*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A proceeding prescribed by
statute for ascertaining and fixing the boundaries of land. See 2d
<er>Procession</er>.</def> <mark>[ Local, U. S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Processive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ces"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Proceeding;
advancing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Because it is language, -- ergo, <qex>processive</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- (Biochemistry) acting from one end of a polymer, and continuing
to act on the same polymer, rather than detaching and re-binding
randomly to other molecules. Said of polymerases or hydrolytic enzymes.
Opposed to "distributive". Colloquially, (laboratory slang) processive
and distributive enzymes may be referred to as "clingers" and "hoppers",
respectively. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Process plate</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"ess plate</hw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A plate prepared by a
mechanical process, esp. a photomechanical process.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A very slow photographic plate, giving good contrasts between high
lights and shadows, used esp. for making lantern slides.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proces verbal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`c<egrave/s" ver`bal"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[ F.]</ety>
<fld>(French Law)</fld> <def>An authentic minute of an official act, or
statement of facts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prochein</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"chein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prochain</ets>, fr. L. (assumed) <ets>proximanus</ets>, fr.
<ets>proximus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Next; nearest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Prochein ami</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Prochein
amy</b></col></mcol> <pr>(<?/)</pr> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the next
friend. See under <er>Next</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prochordal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*chor"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>pro +
chordal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated in front of the
notochord; -- applied especially to parts of the cartilaginous rudiments
in the base of the skull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prochronism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"chro*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
preceding in time; <?/ before + <?/ time: cf. F.
<ets>prochronisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The dating of an event before the
time it happened; an antedating; -- opposed to
<ant>metachronism</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prochronize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"chro*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
antedate.</def> <rj><au>Fitzed. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procidentia</ent><br/
<ent>Procidence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Proc"i*dence</hw>, \'d8<hw>Proc*i*den"ti*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>procidentia</ets>, fr.
<ets>procidens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>procidere</ets> to fall down
forward.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A falling down; a
prolapsus.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Parr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prociduous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cid"u*ous</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[ L.
<ets>prociduus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Falling from its proper
place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procinct</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cinct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procinctus</ets>, fr. <ets>procingere</ets>, <ets>procinctum</ets>,
to gird up.]</ety> <def>A state of complete readiness for action.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/War in <xex>procinct</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclaim</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*claim"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Proclaimed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Proclaiming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>proclamen</ets>, L. <ets>proclamare</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before,
forward + <ets>clamare</ets> to call or cry out: cf. F.
<ets>proclamer</ets>. See <er>Claim</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To make known by public announcement; to give wide
publicity to; to publish abroad; to promulgate; to declare; <as>as, to
<ex>proclaim</ex> war or peace</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>proclaim</qex> liberty to the captives.</q> <rj><qau>Isa.
lxi. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the apparel oft <qex>proclaims</qex> the man.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Throughout the host <qex>proclaim</qex><br/
A solemn council forthwith to be held.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To outlaw by public proclamation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I heard myself <qex>proclaimed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To publish; promulgate; declare; announce. See
<er>Announce</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclaimer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*claim"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
proclaims.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclamation</ent><br/
<hw>Proc`la*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>proclamation</ets>, L. <ets>proclamatio</ets>. See
<er>Proclaim</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of proclaiming; official or general notice;
publication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>King Asa made a <qex>proclamation</qex> throughout all Judah; none
was exempted.</q> <rj><qau>1 Kings xv. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is proclaimed, publicly announced, or
officially declared; a published ordinance; <as>as, the
<ex>proclamation</ex> of a king; a Thanksgiving
<ex>proclamation</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclitic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*clit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to lean
forward; <?/ forward + <?/ to lean or incline. Cf.
<er>Enclitic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Gram.)</fld> <def>Leaning forward;
-- said of certain monosyllabic words which are so closely attached to
the following word as not to have a separate accent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*clive"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proclivis</ets> sloping, inclined; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>clivus</ets> hill: cf. F. <ets>proclive</ets>. See
<er>Declivity</er>, and cf. <er>Proclivous</er>.]</ety> <def>Having a
tendency by nature; prone; proclivous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Mrs. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclivity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cliv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proclivitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proclivit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Inclination; propensity; proneness; tendency.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>proclivity</xex> to steal.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Abp.
Bramhall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Readiness; facility; aptitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had such a dexterous <qex>proclivity</qex> as his teachers were
fain to restrain his forwardness.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H.
Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proclivous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cli"vous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proclivus</ets>. See <er>Proclive</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Inclined; tending by nature.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the incisor teeth directed
forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procoele</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*c<oe/le"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro</ets> + Gr. <?/ hollow.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
lateral cavity of the prosencephalon; a lateral ventricle of the
brain.</def> <rj><au>B. G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procoelia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*c<oe/"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Proc<oe/li<ae/</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[
NL.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Proc<oe/le</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procoelia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*c<oe/"li*a</hw>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Crocodilia, including the true
crocodiles and alligators, in which the dorsal vertebr<ae/ are concave
in front.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procoelian</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*c<oe/"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Proc<oe/le</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat & Zool.)</fld> <def>Concave in
front; <as>as, <ex>proc<oe/lian</ex> vertebr<ae/, which have the
anterior end of the centra concave and the posterior
convex</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procoelian</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*c<oe/"li*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
reptile having proc<oe/lian vertebr<ae/; one of the
Proc<oe/lia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procoelous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*c<oe/"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><def>Same as
<er>Proc<oe/lian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proconsul</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*con"sul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>pro</ets> for + <ets>consul</ets> consul.]</ety> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>An officer who discharged the duties of a consul
without being himself consul; a governor of, or a military commander in,
a province. He was usually one who had previously been consul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proconsulary</ent><br/
<ent>Proconsular</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*con"su*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro*con"su*la*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>proconsularis</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>proconsulaire</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining of a proconsul; <as>as,
<ex>proconsular</ex> powers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Under the government of a proconsul; <as>as, a
<ex>proconsular</ex> province</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proconsulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*con"su*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proconsulatus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proconsulat</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
office jurisdiction of a proconsul, or the term of his office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proconsulship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*con"sul*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Proconsulate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrastinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cras"ti*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Procrastinated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Procrastinating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>procrastinatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>procrastinare</ets> to
procrastinate; <ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>crastinus</ets> of
to-morrow, fr. <ets>cras</ets> to-morrow.]</ety> <def>To put off till
to-morrow, or from day to day; to defer; to postpone; to delay; <as>as,
to <ex>procrastinate</ex> repentance</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hopeless and helpless Aegeon wend,<br/
But to <qex>procrastinate</qex> his lifeless end.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To postpone; adjourn; defer; delay; retard;
protract; prolong.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrastinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cras"ti*nate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To delay; to be
dilatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>procrastinate</qex> more than I did twenty years ago.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrastination</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cras`ti*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procrastinatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>procrastination</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act or habit of procrastinating, or putting off to a future
time; delay; dilatoriness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Procrastination</qex> is the thief of time.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrastinator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cras"ti*na`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
procrastinates, or defers the performance of anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrastinatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cras"ti*na*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to procrastination; dilatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrastine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cras"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
procrastinate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreant</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cre*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procreans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>procreare</ets>. See
<er>Procreate</er>.]</ety> <def>Generating; producing; productive;
fruitful; assisting in procreation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/His
pendent bed and <xex>procreant</xex> cradle.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreant</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cre*ant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which,
procreates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cre*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Procreated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Procreating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>procreatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>procreare</ets>; <ets>pro</ets>
forward, forth + <ets>create</ets> to create.]</ety> <def>To generate
and produce; to beget; to engender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`cre*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>procr<eacute/ation</ets>, L, <ets>procreatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act of begetting; generation and production of young.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreative</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cre*a`tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
power to beget; generative.</def> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreativeness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cre*a`tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The power of
generating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procreator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cre*a`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who begets; a father or sire; a generator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procris</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro"cris</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the wife of
Cephalus, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of small
moths of the genus <gen>Procris</gen>. The larv<ae/ of some species
injure the grapevine by feeding in groups upon the leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrustean</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*crus"te*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to <etsep>Procrustes</etsep>, or the mode of torture
practiced by him; producing conformity by violent means; <as>as, the
<ex>Procrustean</ex> treatment; a <ex>Procrustean</ex> limit. See
<er>Procrustes</er>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrusteanize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*crus"te*an*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Procrusteanized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Procrusteanizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To stretch or contract according to some
rule or standard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrustes</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*crus"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ to beat out, to stretch; <?/ forward + <?/ to strike.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A celebrated legendary highwayman of
Attica, who tied his victims upon an iron bed, and, as the case
required, either stretched or cut of their legs to adapt them to its
length; -- whence the metaphorical phrase, <col><b><it>the bed of
Procrustes</it></b></col>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procrustesian</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`crus*te"si*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Procrustean</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proc*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ anus + <ets>-itis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammation
of the rectum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctocele</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"to*cele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ anus +
<?/ tumor.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inversion and prolapse of the
mucous coat of the rectum, from relaxation of the sphincter, with more
or less swelling; prolapsus ani.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctodaeum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proc`to*d<ae/"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ the anus + <?/ to divide.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Mesenteron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctor</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>proketour</ets>, contr. fr. <ets>procurator</ets>. See
<er>Procurator</er>.]</ety> <def>One who is employed to manage to
affairs of another.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A person
appointed to collect alms for those who could not go out to beg for
themselves, as lepers, the bedridden, etc.; hence a beggar.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Nares.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>An officer employed in admiralty and ecclesiastical causes. He
answers to an <xex>attorney</xex> at common law, or to a
<xex>solicitor</xex> in equity.</def> <au>Wharton.</au> <sd>(c)</sd>
<fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld> <def>A representative of the clergy in
convocation.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>An officer in a university or
college whose duty it is to enforce obedience to the laws of the
institution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- hall proctor. a proctor(d) who maintains order within the
hallways of a school, esp. during a shange of class. THe post is often
occupied by a student --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctor</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"tor</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To act as a proctor toward; to
manage as an attorney or agent.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Warburton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctorage</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"tor*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Management by a
proctor, or as by a proctor; hence, control; superintendence; -- in
contempt.</def> <ldquo/The fogging <xex>proctorage</xex> of
money.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctorial</ent><br/
<hw>Proc*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a proctor, esp. an academic proctor; magisterial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctorical</ent><br/
<hw>Proc*tor"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Proctorial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctorship</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"tor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
dignity of a proctor; also, the term of his office.</def>
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Proc*tot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ anus +
<?/ to cut.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>An incision into the rectum,
as for the division of a stricture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proctucha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proc"tu*cha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ anus + <?/ to have.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A division of Turbellaria including those that have an intestine
terminating posteriorly.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
Nemertina.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procumbent</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cum"bent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procumbens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>procumbere</ets> to fall, bend, or lean forward; <ets>pro</ets>
forward + <ets>cumbere</ets> (in comp.), akin to <ets>cubare</ets> to
lie down: cf. F. <ets>procombant</ets>. Cf.
<er>Incumbent</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Lying down, or on the face; prone.</def> <ldquo/
<xex>Procumbent</xex> each obeyed.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Lying on the ground, but without
putting forth roots; trailing; prostrate; <as>as, a <ex>procumbent</ex>
stem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procurable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cur"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
procured; obtainable.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procuracy</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"u*ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Procuracies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL.
<ets>procuratia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>procuratie</ets>. See
<er>Procuration</er>, and cf,. <er>Proxy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The office or act of a proctor or procurator;
management for another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Authority to act for another; a proxy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procuration</ent><br/
<hw>Proc`u*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procuratio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>procuration</ets>. See
<er>Procure</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of procuring; procurement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The management of another's affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The instrument by which a person is empowered to
transact the affairs of another; a proxy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld> <def>A sum of money paid
formerly to the bishop or archdeacon, now to the ecclesiastical
commissioners, by an incumbent, as a commutation for entertainment at
the time of visitation; -- called also
<altname>proxy</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Procuration money</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>money
paid for procuring a loan.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procurator</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"u*ra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.: cf. F.
<ets>procurateur</ets>. See <er>Procure</er>, and cf. <er>Proctor</er>.
]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who manages another's affairs,
either generally or in a special matter; an agent; a proctor.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer. Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A governor of a province
under the emperors; also, one who had charge of the imperial revenues in
a province; <as>as, the <ex>procurator</ex> of Judea</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Procurator fiscal</b></col> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld>,
<cd>public prosecutor, or district attorney.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procuratorial</ent><br/
<hw>Proc`u*ra*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a procurator, or proctor; made by a proctor.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procuratorship</ent><br/
<hw>Proc"u*ra`tor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office
or term of a procurator.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procuratory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cu"ra*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>procuratorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to, or authorizing,
procuration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procure</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Procured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Procuring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>procurer</ets>, L. <ets>procurare</ets>, <ets>procuratum</ets>, to
take care of; <ets>pro</ets> for + <ets>curare</ets> to take care, fr.
<ets>cura</ets> care. See <er>Cure</er>, and cf. <er>Proctor</er>,
<er>Proxy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring into possession; to cause to accrue to, or
to come into possession of; to acquire or provide for one's self or for
another; to gain; to get; to obtain by any means, as by purchase or
loan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If we <qex>procure</qex> not to ourselves more woe.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To contrive; to bring about; to effect; to
cause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By all means possible they <qex>procure</qex> to have gold and
silver among them in reproach.</q> <rj><qau>Robynson (More's Utopia)
.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Proceed, Solinus, to <qex>procure</qex> my fall.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To solicit; to entreat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The famous Briton prince and faery knight, . . . <br/
Of the fair Alma greatly were <qex>procured</qex><br/
To make there longer sojourn and abode.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1143 --></p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to come; to bring; to attract.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What unaccustomed cause <qex>procures</qex> her hither?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To obtain for illicit intercourse or
prostitution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Attain</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procure</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To pimp.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To manage business for another in court.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procurement</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cure"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of procuring or obtaining; obtainment;
attainment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Efficient contrivance; management; agency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They think it done<br/
By her <qex>procurement</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procurer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cur"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>procureur</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who procures, or obtains; one who, or that
which, brings on, or causes to be done, esp. by corrupt means.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who procures the gratification of lust for
another; a pimp; a pander.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procuress</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*cur"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female procurer, or
pander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Procyon</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"cy*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a constellation
which rises before the Dog Star, Gr. <?/; <?/ before + <?/ a dog.
]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A star of the first magnitude
in the constellation <xex>Canis Minor</xex>, or the Little
Dog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of mammals including the
raccoon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prod</ent><br/
<hw>Prod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gael. & Ir.
<ets>brod</ets> goad, prickle, sting, and E. <ets>brad</ets>, also W.
<ets>procio</ets> to poke, thrust.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A pointed instrument for pricking or puncturing, as
a goad, an awl, a skewer, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A prick or stab which a pointed
instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A light kind of crossbow; -- in the sense, often
spelled <xex>prodd</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prod</ent><br/
<hw>Prod</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prodded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prodding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To thrust some pointed
instrument into; to prick with something sharp; <as>as, to <ex>prod</ex>
a soldier with a bayonet; to <ex>prod</ex> oxen</as>; hence, to goad, to
incite, to worry; <as>as, to <ex>prod</ex> a student</as>.</def>
<rj><au>H. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodd</ent><br/
<hw>Prodd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A crossbow. See
<er>Prod</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigal</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prodigus</ets>, from <ets>prodigere</ets> to drive forth, to
squander away; <ets>pro</ets> forward, forth + <ets>agere</ets> to
drive; cf. F. <ets>prodigue</ets>. See <er>Agent</er>. ]</ety>
<def>Given to extravagant expenditure; expending money or other things
without necessity; recklessly or viciously profuse; lavish; wasteful;
not frugal or economical; <as>as, a <ex>prodigal</ex> man; the
<ex>prodigal</ex> son; <ex>prodigal</ex> giving; <ex>prodigal</ex>
expenses.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In fighting fields [patriots] were <qex>prodigal</qex> of
blood.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Profuse; lavish; extravagant; squandering;
wasteful. See <er>Profuse</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigal</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who expends money
extravagantly, viciously, or without necessity; one that is profuse or
lavish in any expenditure; a waster; a spendthrift.</def> <ldquo/Noble
<xex>prodigals</xex> of life.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Trench.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigality</ent><br/
<hw>Prod`i*gal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prodigalit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>prodigalitas</ets>. See
<er>Prodigal</er>.]</ety> <def>Extravagance in expenditure, particularly
of money; excessive liberality; profusion; waste; -- opposed to
<ant>frugality</ant>, <ant>economy</ant>, and
<ant>parsimony</ant>.</def><ldquo/The <xex>prodigality</xex> of his
wit.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigalize</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act as a
prodigal; to spend liberally.</def> <rj><au>Sherwood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigalize</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gal*ize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To expend lavishly.</def>
<rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigally</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prodigal manner; with
profusion of expense; extravagantly; wasteful; profusely; lavishly;
<as>as, an estate <ex>prodigally</ex> dissipated</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature not bounteous now, but lavish grows;<br/
Our paths with flowers she <qex>prodigally</qex> strows.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigate</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
squander.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigence</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prodigentia</ets>, fr. <ets>prodigens</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>prodigere</ets>. See <er>Prodigal</er>. ]</ety> <def>Waste;
profusion; prodigality.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigious</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*di"gious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prodigiosus</ets>, fr. <ets>prodigium</ets> a prodigy; cf. F.
<ets>prodigieux</ets>. See <er>Prodigy</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a prodigy; marvelous; wonderful;
portentous.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is <qex>prodigious</qex> to have thunder in a clear sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Extraordinary in bulk, extent, quantity, or degree;
very great; vast; huge; immense; <as>as, a <ex>prodigious</ex> mountain;
a <ex>prodigious</ex> creature; a <ex>prodigious</ex>
blunder.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Prodigious</xex> might.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Huge; enormous; monstrous; portentous; marvelous;
amazing; astonishing; extraordinary.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigiously</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*di"gious*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Enormously; wonderfully; astonishingly; <as>as,
<ex>prodigiously</ex> great</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Very much; extremely; <as>as, he was
<ex>prodigiously</ex> pleased</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigiousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*di"gious*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being prodigious; the state of having qualities that excite wonder or
astonishment; enormousness; vastness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodigy</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prodigies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[ L. <ets>prodigium</ets>;
<ets>pro</ets> before + (perh.) a word appearing in <ets>adagium</ets>
adage: cf. F. <ets>prodige</ets>. Cf. <er>Adage</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Something extraordinary, or out of the usual course
of nature, from which omens are drawn; a portent; <as>as, eclipses and
meteors were anciently deemed <ex>prodigies</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So many terrors, voices, <qex>prodigies</qex>,<br/
May warn thee, as a sure foregoing sign.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything so extraordinary as to excite wonder or
astonishment; a marvel; <as>as, a <ex>prodigy</ex> of
learning</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A production out of ordinary course of nature; an
abnormal development; a monster.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wonder; miracle; portent; marvel;
monster.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodition</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*di"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proditio</ets>, from <ets>prodere</ets> to give forth, betray: cf.
OF. <ets>prodition</ets>.]</ety> <def>Disclosure; treachery;
treason.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proditor</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A
traitor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proditorious</ent><br/
<hw>Prod`i*to"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>proditoire</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Treacherous; perfidious; traitorous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Apt to make unexpected revelations.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Nature is <xex>proditorious</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proditory</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"i*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Treacherous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodromal</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"ro*mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to prodromes; <as>as, the <ex>prodromal</ex> stage
of a disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodrome</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"drome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ running
before; <?/ before + <?/ to run: cf. F. <ets>prodrome</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A forerunner; a precursor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A premonitory symptom; a symptom
or sensation experienced by a person which presages the onset of a
disease or abnormal physiological event; <as>as, the <ex>prodrome</ex>
of an epileptic seizure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodromous</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"ro*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Precursory.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prodromus</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"ro*mus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A prodrome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A preliminary course or publication; -- used esp. in
the titles of elementary works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Produce</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Produced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Producing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>producere</ets>, <ets>productum</ets>, to bring forward, beget,
produce; <ets>pro</ets> forward, forth + <ets>ducere</ets> to lead. See
<er>Duke</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring forward; to lead forth; to offer to view or
notice; to exhibit; to show; <as>as, to <ex>produce</ex> a witness or
evidence in court</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Produce</qex> your cause, saith the Lord.</q> <rj><qau>Isa.
xli. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your parents did not <qex>produce</qex> you much into the
world.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring forth, as young, or as a natural product or
growth; to give birth to; to bear; to generate; to propagate; to yield;
to furnish; <as>as, the earth <ex>produces</ex> grass; trees
<ex>produce</ex> fruit; the clouds <ex>produce</ex> rain.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This soil <qex>produces</qex> all sorts of palm trees.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] <qex>produce</qex> prodigious births of body or mind.</q>
<rj><qau> Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The greatest jurist his country had <qex>produced</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to be or to happen; to originate, as an
effect or result; to bring about; <as>as, disease <ex>produces</ex>
pain; vice <ex>produces</ex> misery.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To give being or form to; to manufacture; to make;
<as>as, a manufacturer <ex>produces</ex> excellent wares</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To yield or furnish; to gain; <as>as, money at
interest <ex>produces</ex> an income; capital <ex>produces</ex>
profit.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To draw out; to extend; to lengthen; to prolong;
<as>as, to <ex>produce</ex> a man's life to threescore</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>To extend; -- applied to a line,
surface, or solid; <as>as, to <ex>produce</ex> a side of a
triangle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Produce</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duce"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To yield or furnish
appropriate offspring, crops, effects, consequences, or
results.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Produce</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"uce</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is
produced, brought forth, or yielded; product; yield; proceeds; result of
labor, especially of agricultural labors</def>; hence, specifically,
<def>agricultural products.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producement</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duce"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Production.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producent</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*du"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>producens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>One who produces, or offers to
notice.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*du"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who produces, brings forth, or
generates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who grows agricultural products, or manufactures
crude materials into articles of use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Iron & Steel Manuf.)</fld> <def>A furnace for
producing combustible gas which is used for fuel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Produce race</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"uce race</hw>. <fld>(Horse Racing)</fld> <def>A race to be run
by the produce of horses named or described at the time of
entry.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producer's goods</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duc"er's goods</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Polit. Econ.)</fld>
<def>Goods that satisfy wants only indirectly as factors in the
production of other goods, such as tools and raw material; -- called
also <altname>instrumental goods</altname>, <altname>auxiliary
goods</altname>, <altname>intermediate goods</altname>, or
<altname>goods of the second and higher orders</altname>, and disting.
from <contr>consumers' goods</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producer's rent</ent><br/
<ent>Producer's surplus</ent><br/
<hw>Producer's surplus</hw>. <fld>(Polit. Econ.)</fld> <def>Any profit
above the normal rate of interest and wages accruing to a producer on
account of some monopoly (temporary or permanent) of the means or
materials of production; -- called also <hw>Producer's
rent</hw>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producibility</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*du`ci*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being producible.</def> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Producible</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*du"ci*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
produced, brought forward, brought forth, generated, made, or
extended.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pro*du"ci*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Product</ent><br/
<hw>Prod"uct</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>productus</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>producere</ets>. See
<er>Produce</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything that is produced, whether as the result of
generation, growth, labor, or thought, or by the operation of
involuntary causes; <as>as, the <ex>products</ex> of the season, or of
the farm; the <ex>products</ex> of manufactures; the <ex>products</ex>
of the brain.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are the <qex>product</qex><br/
Of those ill-mated marriages.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These institutions are the <qex>products</qex> of enthusiasm.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The number or sum obtained by
adding one number or quantity to itself as many times as there are units
in another number; the number resulting from the multiplication of two
or more numbers; <as>as, the <ex>product</ex> of the multiplication of 7
by 5 is 35</as>. In general, the result of any kind of multiplication.
See the Note under <er>Multiplication</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Produce; production; fruit; result; effect;
consequence; outcome; work; performance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Product</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To produce; to bring forward.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Producted</xex> to . . . examination.<rdquo/
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lengthen out; to extend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that doth much . . . <qex>products</qex> his mortality.</q>
<rj><qau>Hackett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To produce; to make.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productibility</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duct`i*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
of being productible; producibility.</def> <rj><au>Ruskin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productible</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duct"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>productible</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being produced;
producible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duc"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>productilis</ets>, fr. <ets>producere</ets> to stretch out.]</ety>
<def>Capable of being extended or prolonged; extensible;
ductile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Production</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>productio</ets> a lengthening, prolonging: cf. F.
<ets>production</ets>. See <er>Produce</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process or producing, bringing forth, or
exhibiting to view; <as>as, the <ex>production</ex> of commodities, of a
witness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is produced, yielded, or made, whether
naturally, or by the application of intelligence and labor; <as>as, the
<ex>productions</ex> of the earth; the <ex>productions</ex> of
handicraft; the <ex>productions</ex> of intellect or
genius.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of lengthening out or prolonging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Product; produce; fruit; work; performance;
composition.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duc"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>productif</ets>, L. <ets>productivus</ets> fit for
prolongation.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the quality or power of producing; yielding
or furnishing results; <as>as, <ex>productive</ex> soil;
<ex>productive</ex> enterprises; <ex>productive</ex> labor, that which
increases the number or amount of products.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bringing into being; causing to exist; producing;
originative; <as>as, an age <ex>productive</ex> of great men; a spirit
<ex>productive</ex> of heroic achievements.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And kindle with thy own <qex>productive</qex> fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is turning nobility into a principle of virtue, and making it
<qex>productive</qex> of merit.</q> <rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Producing, or able to produce, in large measure;
fertile; profitable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pro*duc"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pro*duc"tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productivity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`duc*tiv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being productive; productiveness.</def>
<rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not indeed as the product, but as the producing power, the
<qex>productivity</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productress</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*duc"tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
producer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Productus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*duc"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Product</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct genus of
brachiopods, very characteristic of the Carboniferous rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proeguminal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`e*gu"mi*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, p.
pr. of <?/ to lead the way: cf. F.
<ets>pro<eacute/gum<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Serving to predispose; predisposing; <as>as, a <ex>proeguminal</ex>
cause of disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proem</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"em</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prooemium</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ before + <?/ way, course or strain of
a song: cf. F. <ets>pro<egrave/me</ets>.]</ety> <def>Preface;
introduction; preliminary observations; prelude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus much may serve by way of <qex>proem</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proem</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"em</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To preface.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proembryo</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*em"bry*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>embryo</ets>. ]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The series of cells formed in the ovule of a flowering
plant after fertilization, but before the formation of the embryo.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The primary growth from the spore in certain
cryptogamous plants; <as>as, the <ex>proembryo</ex>, or protonema, of
mosses</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proemial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*e"mi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Introductory;
prefatory; preliminary.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proemptosis</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`emp*to"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ to fall in before; <?/ before + <?/ in + <?/ to fall.]</ety>
<fld>(Chron.)</fld> <def>The addition of a day to the lunar
calendar.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <see>See
<er>Metemptosis</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>proenzyme</ent><br/
<hw>pro"en*zyme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[.]</ety>
<fld>(Bioch.)</fld> <def>Any one of a class of proteins that are
converted, in the normal course of cellular metabolism, into one or more
active enzymes; also called <altname>zymogen</altname>. The conversion
usually is due to a specific cleavage of a peptide bond by another
enzyme, or may be due to acid.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proface</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"face</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>prou
face</ets>, <ets>prou fasse</ets>; <ets>prou</ets> profit +
<ets>faire</ets> to make, do.]</ety> <def>Much good may it do you! -- a
familiar salutation or welcome.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Master page, good master page, sit. <qex>Proface</qex>!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profanate</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"a*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
profane.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profanation</ent><br/
<hw>Prof`a*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profanatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>profanation</ets>. See
<er>Profane</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
violating sacred things, or of treating them with contempt or
irreverence; irreverent or too familiar treatment or use of what is
sacred; desecration; <as>as, the <ex>profanation</ex> of the Sabbath;
the <ex>profanation</ex> of a sanctuary; the <ex>profanation</ex> of the
name of God.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of treating with abuse or disrespect, or
with undue publicity, or lack of delicacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T were <qex>profanation</qex> of our joys<br/
To tell the laity our love.</q> <rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profane</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fane"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>profanus</ets>, properly, before the temple, i. e., without the
temple, unholy; <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>fanum</ets> temple. See 1st
<er>Fane</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Not sacred or holy; not possessing peculiar
sanctity; unconsecrated; hence, relating to matters other than sacred;
secular; -- opposed to <xex>sacred</xex>, <xex>religious</xex>, or
<xex>inspired</xex>; <as>as, a <ex>profane</ex> place</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Profane</xex> authors.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I.
Disraeli.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>profane</qex> wreath was suspended before the shrine.</q>
<rj><qau>Gibbon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unclean; impure; polluted; unholy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing is <qex>profane</qex> that serveth to holy things.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Treating sacred things with contempt, disrespect,
irreverence, or undue familiarity; irreverent; impious.</def> Hence,
specifically; <def>Irreverent in language; taking the name of God in
vain; given to swearing; blasphemous; <as>as, a <ex>profane</ex> person,
word, oath, or tongue</as>.</def> <au>1 Tim. i. 9.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Secular; temporal; worldly; unsanctified;
unhallowed; unholy; irreligious; irreverent; ungodly; wicked; godless;
impious. See <er>Impious</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profane</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fane"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Profaned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Profaning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>profanare</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>profaner</ets>. See <er>Profane</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To violate, as anything sacred; to treat with abuse,
irreverence, obloquy, or contempt; to desecrate; to pollute; <as>as, to
<ex>profane</ex> the name of God; to <ex>profane</ex> the Scriptures, or
the ordinance of God.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The priests in the temple <qex>profane</qex> the sabbath.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xii. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put to a wrong or unworthy use; to make a base
employment of; to debase; to abuse; to defile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So idly to <qex>profane</qex> the precious time.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profanely</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fane"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a profane
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The character of God <qex>profanely</qex> impeached.</q>
<rj><qau>Dr. T. Dwight.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profaneness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fane"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
profane; especially, the use of profane language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profaner</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fan"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who treats
sacred things with irreverence, or defiles what is holy; one who uses
profane language.</def> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profanity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fan"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profanitas</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being profane; profaneness;
irreverence; esp., the use of profane language; blasphemy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is profane; profane language or
acts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The brisk interchange of <qex>profanity</qex> and folly.</q>
<rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profection</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Proficient</er>.]</ety> <def>A setting out; a going forward;
advance; progression.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profectitious</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`fec*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profectitius</ets>, fr. <ets>proficisci</ets> to set out,
proceed.]</ety> <def>Proceeding from, as from a parent; derived, as from
an ancestor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The threefold distinction of <qex>profectitious</qex>,
adventitious, and professional was ascertained.</q>
<rj><qau>Gibbon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profert</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"fert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., he brings
forward, 3d pers. pr. of <ets>proferre</ets>. See <er>Proffer</er>.
]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The exhibition or production of a record
or paper in open court, or an allegation that it is in court.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profess</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fess"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Professed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Professing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>prof<egrave/s</ets>, masc., <ets>professe</ets>, fem., professed
(monk or nun), L. <ets>professus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>profiteri</ets> to
profess; <ets>pro</ets> before, forward + <ets>fateri</ets> to confess,
own. See <er>Confess</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To make open declaration of, as of one's knowledge,
belief, action, etc.; to avow or acknowledge; to confess publicly; to
own or admit freely.</def> <ldquo/Hear me <xex>profess</xex>
sincerely.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The best and wisest of them all <qex>professed</qex><br/
To know this only, that he nothing knew.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1144 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set up a claim to; to make presence to; hence, to
put on or present an appearance of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do <qex>profess</qex> to be no less than I seem.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To present to knowledge of, to proclaim one's self
versed in; to make one's self a teacher or practitioner of, to set up as
an authority respecting; to declare (one's self to be such); <as>as, he
<ex>professes</ex> surgery; to <ex>profess</ex> one's self a
physician.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profess</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fess"</hw> <pr>(pr<osl/*f<ecr/s")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To take a profession upon one's self by a public
declaration; to confess.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To declare friendship.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professed</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fessed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Openly declared,
avowed, acknowledged, or claimed; <as>as, a <ex>professed</ex> foe; a
<ex>professed</ex> tyrant; a <ex>professed</ex>
Christian.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The professed</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <cd>, a
certain class among the Jesuits bound by a special vow. See the note
under <er>Jesuit</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professedly</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fess"ed*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By
profession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profession</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>professio</ets>. See <er>Profess</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of professing or claiming; open declaration;
public avowal or acknowledgment; <as>as, <ex>professions</ex> of
friendship; a <ex>profession</ex> of faith.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A solemn vow, promise, and <qex>profession</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bk.
of Com. Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which one professed; a declaration; an avowal;
a claim; <as>as, his <ex>professions</ex> are insincere</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Indians quickly perceive the coincidence or the contradiction
between <qex>professions</qex> and conduct.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Morse.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That of which one professed knowledge; the
occupation, if not mechanical, agricultural, or the like, to which one
devotes one's self; the business which one professes to understand, and
to follow for subsistence; calling; vocation; employment; <as>as, the
<ex>profession</ex> of arms; the <ex>profession</ex> of a clergyman,
lawyer, or physician; the <ex>profession</ex> of lecturer on
chemistry.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hi tried five or six <qex>professions</qex> in turn.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>The three professions</xex>, or <xex>learned
professions</xex>, are, especially, theology, law, and
medicine.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The collective body of persons engaged in a calling;
<as>as, the <ex>profession</ex> distrust him</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Law.)</fld> <def>The act of entering, or
becoming a member of, a religious order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to a profession, or calling; conforming to the rules or
standards of a profession; following a profession; <as>as,
<ex>professional</ex> knowledge; <ex>professional</ex>
conduct.</as></def> <ldquo/Pride, not personal, but
<xex>professional</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au> <ldquo/A
<xex>professional</xex> sneerer.<rdquo/ <au>De Quincey.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Engaged in by professionals; <as>as, a
<ex>professional</ex> race</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>amateur</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"sion*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person who prosecutes
anything professionally, or for a livelihood, and not in the character
of an amateur; a professional worker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professionalism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"sion*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
following of a profession, sport, etc., as an occupation; -- opposed to
<ant>amateurism</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professionalist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"sion*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>professional
person.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professionally</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"sion*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a professional
manner or capacity; by profession or calling; in the exercise of one's
profession; one employed <xex>professionally</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fess"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a teacher, a
public teacher: cf. F. <ets>professeur</ets>. See
<er>Profess</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who professed, or makes
open declaration of, his sentiments or opinions; especially, one who
makes a public avowal of his belief in the Scriptures and his faith in
Christ, and thus unites himself to the visible church.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Professors</xex> of religion.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who professed, or publicly teaches, any science
or branch of learning; especially, an officer in a university, college,
or other seminary, whose business it is to read lectures, or instruct
students, in a particular branch of learning; as a <xex>professor</xex>
of theology, of botany, of mathematics, or of political
economy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`fes*so"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>professorius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>professorial</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to a professor; <as>as, the <ex>professional</ex> chair;
<ex>professional</ex> interest.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professorialism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`fes*so"ri*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
character, manners, or habits of a professor.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professoriat</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`fes*so"ri*at</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Professoriate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professoriate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`fes*so"ri*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The body of professors, or the professorial staff, in a university
or college.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A professorship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fess"or*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
position of a professor, or public teacher.</def>
<rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Professory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fes"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>professorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a professor;
professorial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proffer</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Proffered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Proffering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>profren</ets>, <ets>proferen</ets>, F. <ets>prof<eacute/rer</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>proferre</ets> to bring forth or forward, to offer;
<ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>ferre</ets> to bring. See <er>Bear</er> to
produce.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To offer for acceptance; to propose to
give; to make a tender of; <as>as, to <ex>proffer</ex> a gift; to
<ex>proffer</ex> services; to <ex>proffer</ex> friendship.</as></def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I reck not what wrong that thou me <qex>profre</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To essay or attempt of one's own accord; to
undertake, or propose to undertake.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proffer</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"fer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An offer made;
something proposed for acceptance by another; a tender; <as>as,
<ex>proffers</ex> of peace or friendship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He made a <qex>proffer</qex> to lay down his commission.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Essay; attempt.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profferer</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"fer*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who proffers
something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proficiency</ent><br/
<ent>Proficience</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*fi"cience</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro*fi"cien*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of state of being
proficient; advance in the acquisition of any art, science, or
knowledge; progression in knowledge; improvement; adeptness; <as>as, to
acquire <ex>proficiency</ex> in music</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proficient</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fi"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proficiens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>proficere</ets>
to go forward, make progress; <ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>facere</ets>
to make. See <er>Fact</er>, and cf. <er>Profit</er>, (<?/)]</ety>
<def>One who has made considerable advances in any business, art,
science, or branch of learning; an expert; an adept; <as>as,
<ex>proficient</ex> in a trade; a <ex>proficient</ex> in mathematics,
music, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proficient</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fi"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Well advanced in
any branch of knowledge or skill; possessed of considerable
acquirements; well-skilled; versed; adept,</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proficiently</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fi"cient*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a proficient
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proficuous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fic"u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proficuus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Profitable; advantageous;
useful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"file</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>profilo</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>filum</ets> a
thread, an outline, shape: cf. F. <ets>profil</ets>. See <er>File</er>
arow, and cf. <er>Purfle</er>, <er>Purl</er>, a fringe.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An outline, or contour; <as>as, the <ex>profile</ex> of
an apple</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Paint & Sculp.)</fld> <def>A human head represented
sidewise, or in a side view; the side face or half face.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A section of any
member, made at right angles with its main lines, showing the exact
shape of moldings and the like.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Civil
Engin.)</fld> <def>A drawing exhibiting a vertical section of the ground
along a surveyed line, or graded work, as of a railway, showing
elevations, depressions, grades, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 4. a short biography. 5. [NW10] a set of data, often in graphical
form, describing some significant features of something (e.g. a person,
corporation); esp. a graph showing the results of tests ot some
attribute of a person. 6. public notice, used esp. in the phrase
"(keep/maintain) a low profile", i. e. avoid public notice, avoid
publicity. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Profile paper</b></col> <fld>(Civil Engin.)</fld>,
<cd>paper ruled with vertical and horizontal lines forming small oblong
rectangles, adapted for drawing profiles.</cd><-- = graph paper?
--></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"file</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Profiled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Profiling</conjf>]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>profiler</ets>, It.
<ets>profilare</ets>. See <er>Profile</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>to draw the outline of; to draw in profile, as an
architectural member.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>To shape the outline of an object
by passing a cutter around it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Profiling machine</b></col>, <cd>a jigging
machine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profiling</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"fil*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>In the
construction of fieldworks, the erection at proper intervals of wooden
profiles, to show to the workmen the sectional form of the parapets at
those points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profilist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"fil*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who takes
profiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profit</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"fit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>profectus</ets> advance, progress, profit, fr.
<ets>profectum</ets>. See <er>Proficient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Acquisition beyond expenditure; excess of value received for
producing, keeping, or selling, over cost; hence, pecuniary gain in any
transaction or occupation; emolument; <as>as, a <ex>profit</ex> on the
sale of goods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let no man anticipate uncertain <qex>profits</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Accession of good; valuable results; useful
consequences; benefit; avail; gain; as, an office of
<xex>profit</xex>,</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This I speak for your own <qex>profit</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>1 Cor.
vii. 35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you dare do yourself a <qex>profit</qex> and a right.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Benefit; avail; service; improvement;
advancement; gain; emolument.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profit</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"it</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Profited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Profiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>profiter</ets>. See
<er>Profit</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To be of service to; to be
good to; to help on; to benefit; to advantage; to avail; to aid; <as>as,
truth <ex>profits</ex> all men</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The word preached did not <qex>profit them</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Heb. iv. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a great means of <qex>profiting</qex> yourself, to copy
diligently excellent pieces and beautiful designs.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profit</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"it</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To gain advantage;
to make improvement; to improve; to gain; to advance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>profit</qex> not by thy talk.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be of use or advantage; to do or bring
good.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Riches <qex>profit</qex> not in the day of wrath.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xi. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profitable</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"it*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>profitable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Yielding or bringing profit or gain;
gainful; lucrative; useful; helpful; advantageous; beneficial; <as>as, a
<ex>profitable</ex> trade; <ex>profitable</ex> business; a
<ex>profitable</ex> study or profession.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What was so <qex>profitable</qex> to the empire became fatal to
the<br/
emperor.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Prof"it*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Prof"it*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>profiteer</ent><br/
<hw>profiteer</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes an excessive and
unconscionable profit by exploiting scarcity or a monopoly
position.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>profiteer</ent><br/
<hw>profiteer</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make an excessive profit, as
on the sale of difficult to obtain goods; to act as a
profiteer.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profiting</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"it*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Gain; advantage;
profit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That thy <qex>profiting</qex> may appear to all.</q> <rj><qau>1
Tim. iv. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profitless</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"it*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without profit;
unprofitable.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligacy</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"li*ga*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Profligate</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>The quality of state of
being profligate; a profligate or very vicious course of life; a state
of being abandoned in moral principle and in vice;
dissoluteness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligate</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"li*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profligatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>profligare</ets> to strike or dash
to the ground, to destroy; <ets>pro</ets> before + a word akin to
<ets>fligere</ets> to strike. See <er>Afflict</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Overthrown; beaten; conquered.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The foe is <qex>profligate</qex>, and run.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Broken down in respect of rectitude, principle,
virtue, or decency; openly and shamelessly immoral or vicious;
dissolute; <as>as, <ex>profligate</ex> man or wretch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A race more <qex>profligate</qex> than we.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Made prostitute and <qex>profligate</qex> muse.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Abandoned; corrupt; dissolute; vitiated;
depraved; vicious; wicked. See <er>Abandoned</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligate</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"li*gate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An abandoned person; one
openly and shamelessly vicious; a dissolute person.</def> <ldquo/Such a
<xex>profligate</xex> as Antony.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligate</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"li*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To drive away;
to overcome.</def> <note>[A Latinism]</note> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligately</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"li*gate*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
profligate manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligateness</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"li*gate*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
profligate; an abandoned course of life; profligacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profligation</ent><br/
<hw>Prof`li*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profligatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Defeat; rout; overthrow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profluence</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"lu*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profluentia</ets>.]</ety> <def>Quality of being profluent;
course.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profluent</ent><br/
<hw>Prof"lu*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profluens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>profluere</ets>; <ets>pro</ets>
forward + <ets>fluere</ets> to flow.]</ety> <def>Flowing forward,</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/In the <xex>profluent</xex> stream.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profound</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*found"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>profond</ets>, L.
<ets>profundus</ets>; pro before, forward + <ets>fundus</ets> the
bottom. See <er>Found</er> to establish, <er>Bottom</er> lowest
part.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Descending far below the surface; opening
or reaching to a great depth; deep.</def> <ldquo/A gulf
<xex>profound</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Intellectually deep; entering far into subjects;
reaching to the bottom of a matter, or of a branch of learning;
thorough; <as>as, a <ex>profound</ex> investigation or treatise; a
<ex>profound</ex> scholar; <ex>profound</ex> wisdom.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Characterized by intensity; deeply felt; pervading;
overmastering; far-reaching; strongly impressed; <as>as, a
<ex>profound</ex> sleep</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Profound</xex>
sciatica.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of the <qex>profound</qex> corruption of this class there can be
no doubt.</q> <rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Bending low, exhibiting or expressing deep humility;
lowly; submissive; <as>as, a <ex>profound</ex> bow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What humble gestures! What <qex>profound</qex> reverence!</q>
<rj><qau>Duppa.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profound</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*found"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
deep; the sea; the ocean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God in the fathomless <qex>profound</qex><br/
Hath all this choice commanders drowned.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An abyss.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profound</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*found"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to sink deeply; to
cause to dive or penetrate far down.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profound</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*found"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dive deeply; to
penetrate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profoundly</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*found"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a profound
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why sigh you so <qex>profoundly</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profoundness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*found"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being profound; profundity; depth.</def> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profulgent</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ful"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + L. <ets>fulgere</ets> to shine.]</ety> <def>Shining
forth; brilliant; effulgent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Profulgent</xex> in preciousness.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profundity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fun"di*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>profunditas</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>profondite</ets>. See <er>Profound</er>.]</ety> <def>The
quality or state of being profound; depth of place, knowledge, feeling,
etc.</def> <ldquo/The vast <xex>profundity</xex> obscure.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profuse</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profusus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>profundere</ets> to pour forth or
out; <ets>pro</ets> forward, forth + <ets>fundere</ets> to pour: cf. F.
<ets>profus</ets>. See <er>Fuse</er> to melt.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pouring forth with fullness or exuberance; bountiful; exceedingly
liberal; giving without stint; <as>as, a <ex>profuse</ex> government;
<ex>profuse</ex> hospitality.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A green, shady bank, <qex>profuse</qex> of flowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Superabundant; excessive; prodigal; lavish; <as>as,
<ex>profuse</ex> expenditure</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Profuse</xex>
ornament.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Kames.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Lavish; exuberant; bountiful; prodigal;
extravagant.</syn> -- <usage><er>Profuse</er>, <er>Lavish</er>,
<er>Prodigal</er>. <xex>Profuse</xex> denotes pouring out (as money,
etc.) with great fullness or freeness; as, <xex>profuse</xex> in his
expenditures, thanks, promises, etc. <xex>Lavish</xex> is stronger,
implying unnecessary or wasteful excess; as, <xex>lavish</xex> of his
bounties, favors, praises, etc. <xex>Prodigal</xex> is stronger still,
denoting unmeasured or reckless profusion; as, <xex>prodigal</xex> of
one's strength, life, or blood, to secure some object.
<au>Dryden.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profuse</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pour out; to
give or spend liberally; to lavish; to squander.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profusely</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fuse"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a profuse
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profuseness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fuse"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Extravagance;
profusion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hospitality sometimes degenerates into <qex>profuseness</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profusion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>profusio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>profusion</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who is profuse; a lavishing or
pouring out without sting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy vast <qex>profusion</qex> to the factious nobles?</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Abundance; exuberant plenty; lavish supply; <as>as,
a <ex>profusion</ex> of commodities</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Profusive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*fu"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Profuse; lavish;
prodigal.</def><mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prog</ent><br/
<hw>Prog</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Progged</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Progging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>prachen</ets>, G. <ets>prachern</ets>, Dan. <ets>prakke</ets>, Sw.
<ets>pracka</ets>, to beg, L. <ets>procare</ets>, <ets>procari</ets>, to
ask, demand, and E. <ets>prowl</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wander
about and beg; to seek food or other supplies by low arts; to seek for
advantage by mean shift or tricks.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A perfect artist in <qex>progging</qex> for money.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have been endeavoring to <qex>prog</qex> for you.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To steal; to rob; to filch.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To prick; to goad; to progue.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prog</ent><br/
<hw>Prog</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Victuals got by begging,
or vagrancy; victuals of any kind; food; supplies.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So long as he picked from the filth his <qex>prog</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A vagrant beggar; a tramp.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A goal; progue.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progenerate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gen"er*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>progeneratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>progenerare</ets> to beget;
<ets>pro</ets> forth, forward + <ets>generare</ets> to generate.]</ety>
<def>To beget; to generate; to produce; to procreate; <as>as, to
<ex>progenerate</ex> a race</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Landor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progeneration</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gen`er*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>progeneratio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of begetting;
propagation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progenitor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gen"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>progeniteur</ets>, L. <ets>progenitor</ets>, fr.
<ets>progignere</ets>, <ets>progenitum</ets>, to bring forth, to beget;
<ets>pro</ets> forth + <ets>gignere</ets> to beget. See <er>Gender</er>
kind.]</ety> <def>An ancestor in the direct line; a
forefather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And reverence thee their great <qex>progenitor</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progenitorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gen"i*tor*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being a
progenitor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progenitress</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gen"i*tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
progenitor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progeniture</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gen"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prog<eacute/niture</ets>.]</ety> <def>A begetting, or birth.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progeny</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"e*ny</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/j"<esl/*n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>progenie</ets>, F. <ets>prog<eacute/nie</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>progenies</ets>, fr. <ets>progignere</ets>. See
<er>Progenitor</er>.]</ety> <def>Descendants of the human kind, or
offspring of other animals; children; offspring; race, lineage.</def>
<ldquo/ Issued from the <xex>progeny</xex> of kings.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proglottid</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*glot"tid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool)</fld>
<def>Proglottis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proglottis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*glot"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proglottides</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. fr. Gr.
<grk>proglwtti`s</grk> the tip of the tongue; <grk>pro`</grk> forward +
<grk>glw^tta</grk> the tongue.]</ety> <fld>(Zool)</fld> <def>One of the
free, or nearly free, segments of a tapeworm. It contains both male and
female reproductive organs, and is capable of a brief independent
existence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognathi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prog"na*thi</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/g"n<adot/*th<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See <er>Prognathous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool)</fld>
<def>A comprehensive group of mankind, including those that have
prognathous jaws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognathic</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nath"ic</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/g*n<acr/th"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Prognathous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognathism</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"na*thism</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/g"n<adot/*th<icr/z'm or
pr<ocr/g*n<amac/"th<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Projection of the jaws.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Prog"na*thy</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms> <-- why not a dual-headword
here? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognathous</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"na*thous</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/g"n<adot/*th<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>pro`</grk> before + <grk>gna`qos</grk> the
jaw]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having the jaws projecting beyond the
upper part of the face; -- opposed to <ant>orthognathous</ant>. See
<cref>Gnathic index</cref>, under <er>Gnathic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their countenances had the true <qex>prognathous</qex>
character.</q> <rj><qau>Kane.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1145 --></p>

<p><ent>Progne</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"ne</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/g"n<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
swallow, traditionally said to be fr. <ets>Progne</ets> (The sister of
Philomela), who was changed into a swallow, Gr.
<grk>Pro`knh</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
swallow.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A genus of swallows including the
purple martin. See <er>Martin</er>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An American
butterfly (<spn>Polygonia Progne</spn> syn. <spn>Vanessa Progne</spn>).
It is orange and black above, grayish beneath, with an
<universbold>L</universbold>-shaped silver mark on the hind wings.
Called also <altname>gray comma</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognosis</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*no"sis</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/g*n<omac/"s<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>pro`gnosis</grk>, fr. <grk>progignw`skein</grk>
to know beforehand; <grk>pro`</grk> before + <grk>gignw`skein</grk> to
know. See <er>Know</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The act or art of
foretelling the course and termination of a disease; also, the outlook
afforded by this act of judgment; <as>as, the <ex>prognosis</ex> of
hydrophobia is bad</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognostic</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>prognwstiko`n</grk>. See <er>Prognosis</er>.]</ety> <def>Indicating
something future by signs or symptoms; foreshowing; aiding in prognosis;
<as>as, the <ex>prognostic</ex> symptoms of a disease;
<ex>prognostic</ex> signs.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognostic</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prognosticum</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>pronostic</ets>, <ets>prognostic</ets>. See
<er>Prognostic</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>That which prognosticates; a sign by which a future
event may be known or foretold; an indication; a sign or omen; hence, a
foretelling; a prediction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That choice would inevitably be considered by the country<br/
as a <qex>prognostic</qex> of the highest import.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A sign or symptom indicating the
course and termination of a disease.</def> <rj><au>Parr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sign; omen; presage; token; indication.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognostic</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos"tic</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prognosticate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognosticable</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos"tic*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being prognosticated or foretold.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognosticate</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos"ti*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prognosticated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prognosticating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Prognostic</er>.]</ety> <def>To indicate as future; to foretell from
signs or symptoms; to prophesy; to foreshow; to predict; <as>as, to
<ex>prognosticate</ex> evil</as>.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I neither will nor can <qex>prognosticate</qex><br/
To the young gaping heir his father's fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To foreshow; foretoken; betoken; forebode;
presage; predict; prophesy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognostication</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos`ti*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prognostication</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of foreshowing or foretelling something
future by present signs; prediction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which foreshows; a foretoken.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prognosticator</ent><br/
<hw>Prog*nos"ti*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prognosticates; a foreknower or foreteller of a future course or event
by present signs.</def> <rj><au>Isa. xlvii. 13.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Program</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Programme</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Programma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*gram"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Programmata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[ L. See
<er>Programme</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>Any law, which, after it had
passed the Athenian senate, was fixed on a tablet for public inspection
previously to its being proposed to the general assembly of the
people.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An edict published for public information; an
official bulletin; a public proclamation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>See <er>Programme</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A preface.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T.
Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Programme</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"gramme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>programma</ets> a public proclamation, manifesto, Gr.
<grk>pro`gramma</grk>, fr. <grk>progra`fein</grk> to write before or in
public; <grk>pro`</grk> before, forth + <grk>gra`fein</grk> to write;
cf. F. <ets>programme</ets>. See <er>Graphic</er>.]</ety> <def>That
which is written or printed as a public notice or advertisement; a
scheme; a prospectus; especially, a brief outline or explanation of the
order to be pursued, or the subjects embraced, in any public exercise,
performance, or entertainment; a preliminary sketch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Programme music</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>,
<cd>descriptive instrumental music which requires an argument or
programme to explain the meaning of its several movements.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progress</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"ress</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>progressus</ets>, from <ets>progredi</ets>, p. p.
<ets>progressus</ets>, to go forth or forward; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>gradi</ets> to step, go: cf. F. <ets>progr<egrave/s</ets>. See
<er>Grade</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- each subdefinition implicitly begins with "a moving forward . . .
" --> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A moving or going forward; a proceeding onward;
an advance</def>; specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In actual space, as
the <xex>progress</xex> of a ship, carriage, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>In the growth of an animal or plant; increase.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>In business of any kind; <as>as, the <ex>progress</ex> of a
negotiation; the <ex>progress</ex> of art.</as></def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>In knowledge; in proficiency; <as>as, the <ex>progress</ex> of a
child at school</as>.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>Toward ideal completeness
or perfection in respect of quality or condition; -- applied to
individuals, communities, or the race; <as>as, social, moral, religious,
or political <ex>progress</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A journey of state; a circuit; especially, one made
by a sovereign through parts of his own dominions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king being returned from his <qex>progresse</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><-- sic. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progress</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gress"</hw> <pr>(?; <xex>formerly pronounced like</xex>
<er>Progress</er>, <pos>n.</pos>)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Progressed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Progressing</conjf>.]</vmorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To make progress; to move forward in space; to
continue onward in course; to proceed; to advance; to go on; <as>as,
railroads are <ex>progressing</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/As his recovery
<xex>progressed</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let me wipe off this honorable dew,<br/
That silverly doth <qex>progress</qex> on thy checks.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>progress</qex> in that style in proportion as their
pieces are treated with contempt.</q>
<rj><qau>Washington.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The war had <qex>progressed</qex> for some time.</q>
<rj><qau>Marshall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make improvement; to advance.</def>
<rj><au>Bayard.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If man <qex>progresses</qex>, art must <qex>progress</qex>
too.</q> <rj><qau>Caird.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progress</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"ress</hw> <pr>(?; see <er>Progress</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make progress in; to pass
through.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progression</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gres"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>progressio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>progression</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of moving forward; a proceeding in a course;
motion onward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Course; passage; lapse or process of time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I hope, in a short <qex>progression</qex>, you will be wholly
immerged in the delices and joys of religion.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Regular or proportional advance
in increase or decrease of numbers; continued proportion, arithmetical,
geometrical, or harmonic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A regular succession of tones or
chords; the movement of the parts in harmony; the order of the
modulations in a piece from key to key.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Arithmetical progression</b></col>, <cd>a progression in
which the terms increase or decrease by equal differences, as the
numbers <lbrace2/<matrix2x5><row>2, 4, 6, 8, 10</row><row>10, 8, 6, 4,
2</row></matrix2x5><rbrace2/ by the difference 2.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <col><b>Geometrical progression</b></col>, <cd>a progression in
which the terms increase or decrease by equal ratios, as the numbers
<lbrace2/<matrix2x5><row>2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64</row><row>64, 32, 16, 8, 4,
2</row></matrix2x5><rbrace2/ by a continual multiplication or division
by 2.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Harmonic progression</b></col>, <cd>a progression in which
the terms are the reciprocals of quantities in arithmetical progression,
as <frac12/, <frac14/, <frac16/, <frac18/, <frac1x10/.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Progressional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gres"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to progression; tending to, or capable of,
progress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progressionist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gres"sion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who holds to a belief in the progression of
society toward perfection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who maintains the doctrine of progression in
organic forms; -- opposed to <contr>uniformitarian</contr>.</def>
<rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progressist</ent><br/
<hw>Prog"ress*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes,
or holds to, progress; a progressionist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progressive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*gress"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>progressif</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Moving forward; proceeding onward; advancing;
evincing progress; increasing; <as>as, <ex>progressive</ex> motion or
course</as>; -- opposed to <ant>retrograde</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Improving; <as>as, art is in a <ex>progressive</ex>
state</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(U. S. History)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Progressive party.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Favoring improvement, change, progress, or reform,
especially in a political context; -- used of people. Contrasted with
<contr>conservative</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <ex>progressive</ex> is sometimes used to
describe the views of a politician, where <contr>liberal</contr> might
have been used at one time, in communities where the term liberal has
come to connote extreme views.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Disposed toward adopting new methods in government
or education, holding tolerant and liberal ideas, and generally favoring
improvement in civic life; -- of towns and communities.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Progressive euchre</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Progressive whist</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a way of playing at card
parties, by which after every game, the losers at the first table go to
the last table, and the winners at all the tables, except the first,
move up to the next table.</cd> -- <col><b>Progressive muscular
atrophy</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a nervous disorder
characterized by continuous atrophy of the muscles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pro*gress"ive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pro*gress"ive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progressive party</ent><br/
<hw>Progressive party</hw>. <fld>(U. S. History)</fld> <def>The
political party formed, chiefly out of the Republican party, by the
adherents of Theodore Roosevelt in the presidential campaign of 1912.
The name <xex>Progressive</xex> party was chosen at the meeting held on
Aug. 7, 1912, when the candidates were nominated and the platform
adopted. It was also known as the <altname>Bull Moose Party</altname>.
Among the chief articles in the platform are those demanding direct
primaries, preferential primaries for presidential nominations, direct
election of United States senators, women's suffrage, and recall of
judicial decisions in certain cases. In 1924 the label was also adopted
by the party supporting the presidential campaign of Robert M. La
Follette, and in 1948 it was also adopted by the party of Henry Wallace.
The party is no longer (1998) considered a force in U. S. national
politics.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> + <source>PJC</source> ]</p>

<p><ent>Progue</ent><br/
<hw>Progue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To prog.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>P. Fletcher.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Progue</ent><br/
<hw>Progue</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sharp point; a goad.</def> <mark>[
Scot. & Local, U. S.]</mark> -- <def2><pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prick; to
goad.</def> <mark>[ Scot. & Local, U. S.]</mark>.</def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proheme</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"heme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Proem.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibit</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*hib"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prohibited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prohibiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>prohibitus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>prohibere</ets> to prohibit; <ets>pro</ets> before, forth +
<ets>habere</ets> to have, hold. See <er>Habit</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To forbid by authority; to interdict; <as>as, God
<ex>prohibited</ex> Adam from eating of the fruit of a certain
tree</as>; we <ex>prohibit</ex> a person from doing a thing, and also
the doing of the thing; <as>as, the law <ex>prohibits</ex> men from
stealing, or it <ex>prohibits</ex> stealing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Prohibit</xex> was formerly followed by
<xex>to</xex> with the infinitive, but is now commonly followed by
<xex>from</xex> with the verbal noun in <xex>-ing</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hinder; to debar; to prevent; to
preclude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gates of burning adamant,<br/
Barred over us, <qex>prohibit</qex> all egress.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To forbid; interdict; debar; prevent;
hinder.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Prohibit</er>, <er>Forbid</er>. To
<xex>forbid</xex> is Anglo-Saxon, and is more familiar; to
<xex>prohibit</xex> is Latin, and is more formal or official. A parent
<xex>forbids</xex> his child to be out late at night; he
<xex>prohibits</xex> his intercourse with the profane and
vicious.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibiter</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*hib"it*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prohibits or forbids; a forbidder; an interdicter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibition</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`hi*bi"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prohibitio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prohibition</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of prohibiting; a declaration or injunction forbidding some
action; interdict.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law of God, in the ten commandments, consists mostly of
<qex>prohibitions</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, the forbidding by law of the sale of
alcoholic liquors as beverages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Writ of prohibition</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a
writ issued by a superior tribunal, directed to an inferior court,
commanding the latter to cease from the prosecution of a suit depending
before it.</cd> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ By ellipsis, <xex>prohibition</xex> is used for the writ
itself.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibition</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`hi*bi"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The period of
1920 to 1932 in the United States, during which sale of alcoholic
beverages were forbidden by the consitution.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibitionist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`hi*bi"tion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who favors prohibitory duties on foreign goods
in commerce; a protectionist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who favors the prohibition of the sale (or of
the sale and manufacture) of alcoholic liquors as beverages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibitive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*hib"it*ive</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prohibitif</ets>.]</ety> <def>That prohibits; prohibitory; <as>as,
a tax whose effect is <ex>prohibitive</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prohibitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*hib"it*o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prohibitorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to prohibit, forbid, or
exclude; implying prohibition; forbidding; <as>as, a
<ex>prohibitory</ex> law; a <ex>prohibitory</ex> price.</as></def> <--
in the latter sense (price or cost) usu. prohibitive. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prohibitory index</b></col>. <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Index</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proin</ent><br/
<hw>Proin</hw> <pr>(proin)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prune</er> to trim.]</ety> <def>To lop; to trim; to prune; to
adorn.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sprigs that did about it grow<br/
He <qex>proined</qex> from the leafy arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proin</ent><br/
<hw>Proin</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To employed in pruning.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Project</ent><br/
<hw>Proj"ect</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>project</ets>, F. <ets>projet</ets>, fr. L. <ets>projectus</ets>,
p. p. of <ets>projicere</ets> to project; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>jacere</ets> to throw. See <er>Jet</er> a shooting forth, and cf.
<er>Projet</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The place from which a thing projects, or starts
forth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is projected or designed; something
intended or devised; a scheme; a design; a plan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vented much policy, and <qex>projects</qex> deep.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Projects</qex> of happiness devised by human reason.</q>
<rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He entered into the <qex>project</qex> with his customary
ardor.</q> <rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An idle scheme; an impracticable design; <as>as, a
man given to <ex>projects</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Design; scheme; plan; purpose.</syn> <usage> --
<er>Project</er>, <er>Design</er>. A <xex>project</xex> is something of
a practical nature thrown out for consideration as to its being done. A
<xex>design</xex> is a project when matured and settled, as a thing to
be accomplished. An ingenious man has many <xex>projects</xex>, but, if
governed by sound sense, will be slow in forming them into
<xex>designs</xex>. See also <er>Scheme</er>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Project</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ject"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Projected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Projecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>projecter</ets>,
F. <ets>projeter</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw or cast forward; to shoot forth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before his feet herself she did <qex>project</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behold! th' ascending villas on my side<br/
<qex>Project</qex> long shadows o'er the crystal tide.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cast forward or revolve in the mind; to contrive;
to devise; to scheme; <as>as, to <ex>project</ex> a plan</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What sit then <qex>projecting</qex> peace and war?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Persp.)</fld> <def>To draw or exhibit, as the form
of anything; to delineate; <as>as, to <ex>project</ex> a sphere, a map,
an ellipse, and the like</as>; -- sometimes with <xex>on</xex>,
<xex>upon</xex>, <xex>into</xex>, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>project</ex> a
line or point upon a plane</as>. See <er>Projection</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Project</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ject"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To shoot forward; to extend beyond something else;
to be prominent; to jut; <as>as, the cornice <ex>projects</ex>; branches
<ex>project</ex> from the tree.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form a project; to scheme.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projectile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ject"ile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>projectile</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Projecting or impelling forward; <as>as, a
<ex>projectile</ex> force</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Caused or imparted by impulse or projection;
impelled forward; <as>as, <ex>projectile</ex> motion</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projectile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ject"ile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>projectile</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A body projected, or impelled forward, by force;
especially, a missile adapted to be shot from a firearm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A part of
mechanics which treats of the motion, range, time of flight, etc., of
bodies thrown or driven through the air by an impelling force.</def><--
= ballistics? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- projectile vomiting, vomiting with such force as to expel the
vomitus over a distance. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projection</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*jec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>projectio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>projection</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of throwing or shooting forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A jutting out; also, a part jutting out, as of a
building; an extension beyond something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of scheming or planning; also, that which is
planned; contrivance; design; plan.</def>
<rj><au>Davenant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Persp.)</fld> <def>The representation of something;
delineation; plan; especially, the representation of any object on a
perspective plane, or such a delineation as would result were the chief
points of the object thrown forward upon the plane, each in the
direction of a line drawn through it from a given point of sight, or
central point; <as>as, the <ex>projection</ex> of a sphere</as>. The
several kinds of projection differ according to the assumed point of
sight and plane of projection in each.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Geog.)</fld> <def>Any method of representing the
surface of the earth upon a plane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Conical projection</b></col>, <cd>a mode of representing
the sphere, the <xex>spherical surface</xex> being projected upon the
surface of a cone tangent to the sphere, the point of sight being at the
center of the sphere.</cd> -- <col><b>Cylindric projection</b></col>,
<cd>a mode of representing the sphere, the <xex>spherical surface</xex>
being projected upon the surface of a cylinder touching the sphere, the
point of sight being at the center of the sphere.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Globular</b></col>, <col><b>Gnomonic</b></col>,
<col><b>Orthographic</b></col>, <col><b>projection</b></col>,etc.</mcol>
<cd>See under <er>Globular</er>, <er>Gnomonic</er>, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Mercator's projection</b></col>, <cd>a mode of representing the
sphere in which the meridians are drawn parallel to each other, and the
parallels of latitude are straight lines whose distance from each other
increases with their distance from the equator, so that at all places
the degrees of latitude and longitude have to each other the same ratio
as on the sphere itself.</cd> -- <col><b>Oblique projection</b></col>,
<cd>a projection made by parallel lines drawn from every point of a
figure and meeting the plane of projection obliquely.</cd> --
<col><b>Polar projection</b></col>, <cd>a projection of the sphere in
which the point of sight is at the center, and the plane of projection
passes through one of the polar circles.</cd> -- <col><b>Powder of
projection</b></col> <fld>(Alchemy.)</fld>, <cd>a certain powder cast
into a crucible or other vessel containing prepared metal or other
matter which is to be thereby transmuted into gold.</cd> --
<col><b>Projection of a point on a plane</b></col> <fld>(Descriptive
Geom.)</fld>, <cd>the foot of a perpendicular to the plane drawn through
the point.</cd> -- <col><b>Projection of a straight line of a
plane</b></col>, <cd>the straight line of the plane connecting the feet
of the perpendiculars let fall from the extremities of the given
line.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Protuberance</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- projectionist. one who operates a projector[2]; esp. one who is
employed to operate a movie projector in a movie theater --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projectment</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ject"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Design;
contrivance; projection.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projector</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ject"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>projeteur</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who projects a scheme
or design; hence, one who forms fanciful or chimerical schemes.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>an optical instrument which projects an image from a
transparency or an opaque image onto a projection screen or other
surface, using an intense light and one or more lenses to focus the
image. The term <ex>projector</ex> by itself is usually used for
projection of transparent images by passing the light beam through the
image; a <ex>projector</ex> which projects an image of an opaque object
is now ususally referred to as an <stype>overhead projector</stype>. In
projection of this latter form the projection is accomplished by means
of a combination of lenses with a prism and a mirror or reflector.
Specific instruments have been called by different names, such as
<altname>balopticon</altname>, <altname>radiopticon</altname>,
<altname>radiopticon</altname>, <altname>mirrorscope</altname>,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slide projector</b></col> <cd>a projector for displaying
images from individual transparencies (slides), each mounted in a
separate frame suited to the mechanics of the projector.</cd> --
<col><b>movie projector</b></col> <cd>a projector which displays a
series of images from a roll of transparent film in rapid sucession,
thus giving the impression of showing a scene with motion as it
originally was recorded.</cd> -- <col><b>overhead projector</b></col>
<cd>see <er>projector{2}</er>, above.</cd></cs> --><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projecture</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*jec"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>projectura</ets>: cf. F. <ets>projecture</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
jutting out beyond a surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Projet</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`jet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Project</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A plan proposed; a draft of
a proposed measure; a project.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proke</ent><br/
<hw>Proke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To poke; to
thrust.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolapse</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lapse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolapsus</ets>, fr. <ets>prolapsus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>prolabi</ets> to fall forward; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>labi</ets> to glide, fall.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
falling down of a part through the orifice with which it is naturally
connected, especially of the uterus or the rectum.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolapse</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lapse"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fall down or out; to
protrude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolapsion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lap"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolapsio</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Prolapse.</def>
<altsp>[ Written also <asp>prolaption</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolapsus</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lap"sus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Prolapse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolatus</ets>, used as p. p. of <ets>proferre</ets> to bring
forth, to extend; <ets>pro + latus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Pro-</er>, and
<er>Tolerate</er>. ]</ety> <def>Stretched out; extended; especially,
elongated in the direction of a line joining the poles; <as>as, a
<ex>prolate</ex> spheroid</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>oblate</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prolate cycloid</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Cycloid</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Prolate ellipsoid</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Prolate spheroid</b></col></mcol><cd>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld>, a figure generated by the revolution of an ellipse
about its major axis. Contrasted with <cref>oblate spheroid</cref>. See
<cref>Ellipsoid of revolution</cref>, under
<er>Ellipsoid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*late"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To utter; to
pronounce.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Foun-der-ed;
<xex>prolate</xex> it right.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prolation</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of prolating or pronouncing; utterance;
pronunciation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Ray.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of deferring; delay.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A medi<ae/val method of
determining of the proportionate duration of semibreves and
minims.</def> <rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolatum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*la"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prolata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[ NL. See
<er>Prolate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A prolate spheroid. See
<cref>Ellipsoid of revolution</cref>, under
<er>Ellipsoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proleg</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"leg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>pro-</ets>
for, in place of + <ets>leg</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of
the fleshy legs found on the abdominal segments of the larv<ae/ of
Lepidoptera, sawflies, and some other insects. Those of Lepidoptera have
a circle of hooks. Called also <altname>proped</altname>,
<altname>propleg</altname>, and <altname>falseleg</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolegate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"leg`ate</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolegatus</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> for + <ets>legatus</ets>
legate.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Hist.)</fld> <def>The deputy or substitute for
a legate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1146 --></p>

<p><ent>Prolegomenary</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`e*gom"e*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the
nature of a prolegomenon; preliminary; introductory;
prefatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolegomenon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prol`e*gom"e*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Prolegomena</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[ NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/, properly neut. pass. p. pr. of <?/ to say beforehand; <?/
before + <?/ to say.]</ety> <def>A preliminary remark or observation; an
introductory discourse prefixed to a book or treatise.</def> <rj><au>D.
Stokes (1659). Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolepsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*lep"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/, from <?/ to take beforehand; <?/ before + <?/ to take.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A figure by which
objections are anticipated or prevented.</def> <au>Abp. Bramhall.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A necessary truth or assumption; a first or assumed
principle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chron.)</fld> <def>An error in chronology,
consisting in an event being dated before the actual time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The application of an adjective
to a noun in anticipation, or to denote the result, of the action of the
verb; <as>as, to strike one <ex>dumb</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proleptical</ent><br/
<ent>Proleptic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*lep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro*lep"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>proleptique</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to prolepsis; anticipative.</def>
<ldquo/A far-seeing or <xex>proleptic</xex> wisdom.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Previous; antecedent.</def>
<rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Anticipating the usual time; --
applied to a periodical disease whose paroxysms return at an earlier
hour at every repetition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proleptically</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lep"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a proleptical
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proleptics</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lep"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The art and science of predicting in medicine.</def>
<rj><au>Laycock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proletaire</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`l<eacute/`taire"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
See <er>Proletary</er>.]</ety> <def>One of the common people; a low
person; also, the common people as a class or estate in a
country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proletaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`e*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proletaneus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having a numerous offspring.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proletarian</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`e*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proletarius</ets>. See Proletary.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
the proletaries; belonging to the commonalty; hence, mean; vile;
vulgar.</def> <ldquo/Every citizen, if he were not a
<xex>proletarian</xex> animal kept at the public cost.<rdquo/ <au>De
Quincey.</au> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A proletary.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proletariat</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`e*ta"ri*at</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>The indigent class in the State; the body of
proletarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proletariate</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`e*ta"ri*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The lower
classes; beggars.</def> <ldquo/The Italian
<xex>proletariate</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. A. Symonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proletary</ent><br/
<hw>Prol"e*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proletaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[ L.
<ets>proletarius</ets>, fr. <ets>proles</ets> offspring. Cf.
<er>Prol<eacute/taire</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A
citizen of the lowest class, who served the state, not with property,
but only by having children; hence, a common person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolicide</ent><br/
<hw>Prol"i*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proles</ets> offspring + <ets>caedere</ets> to kill.]</ety>
<def>The crime of destroying one's offspring, either in the womb or
after birth.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pro-life</ent><br/
<hw>pro`-life"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>antiabortion</er>; -- used by those opposed to voluntary abortion,
to emphasize their view of a fetus as already living, and to avoid the
negative tone of a word beginning with <ldquo/anti-<rdquo/.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pro-lifer</ent><br/
<hw>pro-lif"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is opposed
to voluntary abortion; one who is pro-life, especially an activist in
the campaign to make abortion illegal.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proliferate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"er*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proles</ets> offspring + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>To produce or form cells;
especially, to produce cells rapidly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>To produce zooids by
budding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proliferation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif`er*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The continuous development of
cells in tissue formation; cell formation.</def>
<rj><au>Virchow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The production of numerous zooids
by budding, especially when buds arise from other buds in
succession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proles</ets> offspring + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing offspring; -- applied to a
flower from within which another is produced, or to a branch or frond
from which another rises, or to a plant which is reproduced by buds or
gemm<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Producing young by
budding.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Producing sexual zooids by budding; --
said of the blastostyle of a hydroid.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Producing
a cluster of branchlets from a larger branch; -- said of
corals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proliferous cyst</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a cyst
that produces highly-organized or even vascular structures.</cd>
<rj><au>Paget.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pro*lif"er*ous*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolific</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prolifique</ets>, fr. L. <ets>proles</ets> offspring (from
<ets>pro</ets> for, forward + the root of <ets>alere</ets> to nourish) +
<ets>facere</ets> to make. See <er>Adult</er>, <er>Old</er>, and
<er>Fact</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the quality of generating; producing young or
fruit; generative; fruitful; productive; -- applied to plants producing
fruit, animals producing young, etc.; -- usually with the implied idea
of frequent or numerous production; <as>as, a <ex>prolific</ex> tree,
female, and the like</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Serving to produce; fruitful of results; active;
<as>as, a <ex>prolific</ex> brain; a controversy <ex>prolific</ex> of
evil.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Proliferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolificacy</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"ic*a*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Prolificness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolifical</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Producing young
or fruit abundantly; fruitful; prolific.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro*lif"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolificate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"ic*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prolific</er>.]</ety> <def>To make prolific; to fertilize; to
impregnate.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolification</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif`i*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prolification</ets>, LL. <ets>prolificatio</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The generation of young.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Reproduction by the growth of a
plant, or part of a plant, directly from an older one, or by
gemm<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolificness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lif"ic*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being prolific; fruitfulness; prolificacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolix</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lix"</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolixus</ets> extended, long, prolix, probably fr. <ets>pro</ets>
before, forward + <ets>liqui</ets> to flow, akin to <ets>liquidus</ets>
liquid; cf. OL. <ets>lixa</ets> water: cf. F. <ets>prolixe</ets>. See
<er>Liquid</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Extending to a great length; unnecessarily long;
minute in narration or argument; excessively particular in detail; --
rarely used except with reference to discourse written or spoken;
<as>as, a <ex>prolix</ex> oration; a <ex>prolix</ex> poem; a
<ex>prolix</ex> sermon.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With wig <qex>prolix</qex>, down flowing to his waist.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Indulging in protracted discourse; tedious;
wearisome; -- applied to a speaker or writer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Long; diffuse; prolonged; protracted; tedious;
tiresome; wearisome.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Prolix</er>, <er>Diffuse</er>.
A <xex>prolix</xex> writer delights in circumlocution, extended detail,
and trifling particulars. A <xex>diffuse</xex> writer is fond of
amplifying, and abounds in epithets, figures, and illustrations.
<xex>Diffuseness</xex> often arises from an exuberance of imagination;
<xex>prolixity</xex> is generally connected with a want of
it.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolixious</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lix"ious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dilatory;
tedious; superfluous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Lay by all
nicety, and <xex>prolixious</xex> blushes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolixity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lix"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolixitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prolixit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being prolix; great length; minute detail;
<as>as, <ex>prolixity</ex> in discourses and writings</as>.</def>
<ldquo/For fulsomeness of his <xex>prolixitee</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Idly running on with vain <qex>prolixity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolixly</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lix"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prolix manner.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolixness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lix"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prolixity.</def> <rj><au>Adam
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proll</ent><br/
<hw>Proll</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prowl</er>.]</ety> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prolled</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prolling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To search or prowl after; to
rob; to plunder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proll</ent><br/
<hw>Proll</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To prowl about; to rob.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though ye <qex>prolle</qex> aye, ye shall it never find.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proller</ent><br/
<hw>Proll"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prowler; thief.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolocutor</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`o*cu"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>proloqui</ets>, p. p. <ets>prolocutus</ets>, to speak out;
<ets>pro</ets> for + <ets>loqui</ets> to speak.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who speaks for another.</def>
<rj><au>Jeffrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The presiding officer of a convocation.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolocutorship</ent><br/
<hw>Prol`o*cu"tor*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
prolocutor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolog</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"log</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos>
<def>Prologue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>PROLOG</ent><br/
<hw>PRO"LOG</hw> <pr>(pr<omac/"l<ocr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A declarative higher-level programming
language in which instructions are written not as explicit procedural
data-manipulation commands, but as logical statements. The language has
built-in resolution procedures for logical inference.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prologize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"lo*gize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/. See
<er>Prologue</er>.]</ety> <def>To deliver a Prologue.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Whewell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prologizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"lo*gi`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
prologizes.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prologue</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"logue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>prologus</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to say beforehand; <?/ before
+ <?/ to say. See <er>Logic</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The preface or introduction to a discourse, poem, or
performance; <as>as, the <ex>prologue</ex> of Chaucer's
<ldquo/Canterbury Tales;<rdquo/</as> esp., a discourse or poem spoken
before a dramatic performance</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who delivers a prologue.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prologue</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"logue</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prologued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prologuing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To introduce with a formal
preface, or prologue.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolong</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*long"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prolonged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prolonging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>prolonger</ets>, L. <ets>prolongare</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before,
forth + <ets>longus</ets> long. See <er>Long</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Prolongate</er>, <er>Purloin</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To extend in space or length; <as>as, to
<ex>prolong</ex> a line</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lengthen in time; to extend the duration of; to
draw out; to continue; <as>as, to <ex>prolong</ex> one's
days</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prolong</qex> awhile the traitor's life.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The unhappy queen with talk <qex>prolonged</qex> the night.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put off to a distant time; to postpone.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolongable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*long"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being prolonged; <as>as, life is <ex>prolongable</ex> by
care</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each syllable being a <qex>prolongable</qex> quantity.</q>
<rj><qau>Rush.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolongate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lon"gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prolongated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prolongating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolongatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>prolongare</ets>. See
<er>Prolong</er>.]</ety> <def>To prolong; to extend in space or in
time.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolongation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`lon*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prolongation</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of lengthening in space or in time;
extension; protraction.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which forms an additional length.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolonge</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*longe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Prolong</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Field Artillery)</fld> <def>A rope with a
hook and a toggle, sometimes used to drag a gun carriage or to lash it
to the limber, and for various other purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolonger</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*long"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, causes an extension in time or space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolongment</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*long"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Prolongation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prolusion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*lu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prolusio</ets>, fr. <ets>proludere</ets> to prelude; <ets>pro</ets>
before + <ets>ludere</ets> to play: cf. F. <ets>prolusion</ets>, It.
<ets>prolusione</ets>.]</ety> <def>A trial before the principal
performance; a prelude; hence, an introductory essay or exercise.</def>
<ldquo/Domestic <xex>prolusions</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her presence was in some measure a restraint on the worthy divine,
whose <qex>prolusion</qex> lasted.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promanation</ent><br/
<hw>Prom`a*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + L. <ets>manatio</ets> a flowing, fr. <ets>manare</ets>
to flow.]</ety> <def>The act of flowing forth; emanation; efflux.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promenade</ent><br/
<hw>Prom`e*nade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. (with a
foreign suffix), from <ets>promener</ets> to lead, take for a walk,
<ets>se promener</ets> to walk, from L. <ets>prominare</ets> to drive
forward or along; <ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>minare</ets> to drive
animals. See <er>Amenable</er>, <er>Menace</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A walk for pleasure, display, or exercise.</def>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place for walking; a public walk.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promenade</ent><br/
<hw>Prom`e*nade"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Promenaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Promenading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To walk for pleasure,
display, or exercise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promenader</ent><br/
<hw>Prom`e*nad"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
promenades.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promerit</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mer"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>promeritus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>promerere</ets> to deserve;
<ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>merere</ets> to merit.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To oblige; to confer a favor on.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To deserve; to procure by merit.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Davenant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promerops</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prom"e*rops</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ before + <?/ bee-eater.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several species of very brilliant birds belonging to
<gen>Promerops</gen>, <gen>Epimarchus</gen>, and allied genera, closely
related to the paradise birds, and mostly native of New Guinea. They
have a long curved beak and a long graduated tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promethea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*me"the*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Prometheus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large American
bombycid moth (<spn>Callosamia promethea</spn>). Its larva feeds on the
sassafras, wild cherry, and other trees, and suspends its cocoon from a
branch by a silken band.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promethean</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*me"the*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Prometh</ets><?/<ets>us</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>prom<eacute/th<eacute/en</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to Prometheus. See
<er>Prometheus</er>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Promethean</xex> fire.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a life-giving quality; inspiring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promethean</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*me"the*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An apparatus for automatic
ignition.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A kind of lucifer match.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prometheus</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*me"the*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/, from <?/ to have forethought for.]</ety> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld>
<def>The son of Iapetus (one of the Titans) and Clymene, fabled by the
poets to have surpassed all mankind in knowledge, and to have formed men
of clay to whom he gave life by means of fire stolen from heaven.
Jupiter, being angry at this, sent Mercury to bind Prometheus to Mount
Caucasus, where a vulture preyed upon his liver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prominency</ent><br/
<ent>Prominence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prom"i*nence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Prom"i*nen*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prominentia</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>prominence</ets>. See <er>Prominent</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being prominent; a standing
out from something; conspicuousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is prominent; a protuberance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solar prominences</b></col>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Solar Protuberances</cref>, under
<er>Protuberance</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prominent</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"i*nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prominens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>prominere</ets>
to jut out, to project; <ets>pro</ets> before, forward +
<ets>minere</ets> (in comp.) to jut, project: cf. F.
<ets>prominent</ets>. See <er>Imminent</er>,
<er>Eminent</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Standing out, or projecting, beyond the line surface
of something; jutting; protuberant; in high relief; <as>as, a
<ex>prominent</ex> figure on a vase</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence; Distinctly manifest; likely to attract
attention from its size or position; conspicuous; <as>as, a
<ex>prominent</ex> feature of the face; a <ex>prominent</ex>
building.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Eminent; distinguished above others; <as>as, a
<ex>prominent</ex> character</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prominent' moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
moth of the family <fam>Notodontid<ae/</fam>; a notodontian; -- so
called because the larva has a hump or prominence on its back. Several
of the species are injurious to fruit trees.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prominently</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"i*nent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prominent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promiscuity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`mis*cu"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Promiscuousness; confusion.</def> <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promiscuous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mis"cu*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>promiscuus</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before, in place of, for +
<ets>miscere</ets> to mix. See <er>Mix</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of individuals united in a body or mass
without order; mingled; confused; undistinguished; <as>as, a
<ex>promiscuous</ex> crowd or mass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A wild, where weeds and flowers <qex>promiscuous</qex> shoot.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Distributed or applied without order or
discrimination; not restricted to an individual; common; indiscriminate;
<as>as, <ex>promiscuous</ex> love or intercourse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promiscuously</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mis"cu*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a promiscuous
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promiscuousness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mis"cu*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being promiscuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promise</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"ise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>promesse</ets>, L. <ets>promissum</ets>, fr. <ets>promittere</ets>,
<ets>promissum</ets>, to put forth, foretell, promise; <ets>pro</ets>
forward, for + <ets>mittere</ets> to send. See <er>Mission</er>.
]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>In general, a declaration, written or verbal, made
by one person to another, which binds the person who makes it to do, or
to forbear to do, a specified act; a declaration which gives to the
person to whom it is made a right to expect or to claim the performance
or forbearance of a specified act.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For if the inheritance be of the law, it is no more of
<qex>promise</qex>: but God gave it to Abraham by
<qex>promise</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Gal. iii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An engagement by one person to
another, either in words or in writing, but properly not under seal, for
the performance or nonperformance of some particular thing. The word
<xex>promise</xex> is used to denote the mere engagement of a person,
without regard to the consideration for it, or the corresponding duty of
the party to whom it is made.</def> <rj><au>Chitty. Parsons.
Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which causes hope, expectation, or assurance;
especially, that which affords expectation of future distinction;
<as>as, a youth of great <ex>promise</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My native country was full of youthful <qex>promise</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Bestowal, fulfillment, or grant of what is
promised.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . commanded them that they should not depart from
Jerusalem, but wait for the <qex>promise</qex> of the Father.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts i. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promise</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"ise</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Promised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Promising</conjf>.]</vmorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To engage to do, give, make, or to refrain from
doing, giving, or making, or the like; to covenant; to engage; <as>as,
to <ex>promise</ex> a visit; to <ex>promise</ex> a cessation of
hostilities; to <ex>promise</ex> the payment of money.</as></def>
<ldquo/To <xex>promise</xex> aid.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To afford reason to expect; to cause hope or
assurance of; <as>as, the clouds <ex>promise</ex> rain</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make declaration of or give assurance of, as some
benefit to be conferred; to pledge or engage to bestow; <as>as, the
proprietors <ex>promised</ex> large tracts of land; the city
<ex>promised</ex> a reward.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Promised land</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Land of
promise</cref>, under <er>Land</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To promise one's
self</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To resolve; to determine; to vow</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To be assured; to have strong confidence.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I dare <qex>promise myself</qex> you will attest the truth of all
I have advanced.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promise</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"ise</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To give assurance by a promise, or binding
declaration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To afford hopes or expectation; to give ground to
expect good; rarely, to give reason to expect evil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Will not the ladies be afeard of the lion?<br/
I fear it, I <qex>promise</qex> you.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promisee</ent><br/
<hw>Prom`is*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>The person to whom a promise is made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promiser</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"is*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
promises.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promising</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"is*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making a promise or promises;
affording hope or assurance; <as>as, <ex>promising</ex> person; a
<ex>promising</ex> day.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Prom"is*ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promisor</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"is*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>One who engages or undertakes; a promiser.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promissive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mis"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making a promise;
implying a promise; promising.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1147 --></p>

<p><ent>Promissorily</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"is*so*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
promissory manner.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promissory</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"is*so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing a
promise or binding declaration of something to be done or
forborne.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Promissory note</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a written
promise to pay to some person named, and at a time specified therein, or
on demand, or at sight, a certain sum of money, absolutely and at all
events; -- frequently called a <altname>note of hand</altname>.</cd>
<rj><au>Kent. Byles. Story.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promont</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"ont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Promontory.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promontory</ent><br/
<hw>Prom"on*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Promontories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[ L.
<ets>promonturium</ets>, <ets>promunturium</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before
+ <ets>mons</ets>, <ets>montis</ets>, mountain: cf. F.
<ets>promontoire</ets>. See <er>Mount</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Phys. Geog.)</fld> <def>A high point of land or
rock projecting into the sea beyond the line of coast; a headland; a
high cape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like one that stands upon a <qex>promontory</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A projecting part. Especially:
<sd>(a)</sd> The projecting angle of the ventral side of the sacrum
where it joins the last lumbar vertebra. <sd>(b)</sd> A prominence on
the inner wall of the tympanum of the ear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promorphological</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mor`pho*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Relating to promorphology; <as>as, a
<ex>promorphological</ex> conception</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promorphologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`mor*phol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>One versed in the science of
promorphology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promorphology</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`mor*phol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>morphology</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Crystallography of organic forms; -- a division of morphology
created by <persfn>Haeckel</persfn>. It is essentially stereometric, and
relates to a mathematical conception of organic forms. See
<er>Tectology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promote</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mote"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Promoted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Promoting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>promotus</ets>, p. p.
of <ets>promovere</ets> to move forward, to promote; <ets>pro</ets>
forward + <ets>movere</ets> to move. See <er>Move</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To contribute to the growth, enlargement, or
prosperity of (any process or thing that is in course); to forward; to
further; to encourage; to advance; to excite; <as>as, to
<ex>promote</ex> learning; to <ex>promote</ex> disorder; to
<ex>promote</ex> a business venture.</as></def> <ldquo/Born to
<xex>promote</xex> all truth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exalt in station, rank, or honor; to elevate; to
raise; to prefer; to advance; <as>as, to <ex>promote</ex> an
officer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>promote</qex> thee unto very great honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Num. xxii. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Exalt her, and she shall <qex>promote</qex> thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. iv. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To forward; advance; further; patronize; help;
exalt; prefer; elevate; dignify.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promote</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mote"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To urge on or incite another,
as to strife; also, to inform against a person.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promoter</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mot"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, forwards, advances, or promotes; an encourager;
<as>as, a <ex>promoter</ex> of charity or philosophy</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, one who sets on foot, and takes the
preliminary steps in, a scheme for the organization of a corporation, a
joint-stock company, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who excites; <as>as, a <ex>promoter</ex> of
sedition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An informer; a makebate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Genetics)</fld> <def>The region of a DNA molecule
to which RNA polymerase binds to initiate the process of transcription,
i.e. the synthesis of RNA whose sequence is determined by the sequence
of the DNA adjacent to the promoter site; also, the sequence of bases in
the DNA at such a promoter site.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance that increases the
activity of a catalyst, when present in small quantity in the reaction
mixture.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>promotion</ent><br/
<hw>pro*mo"tion</hw> <pr>(pr<osl/*m<omac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>promotio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>promotion</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act of promoting, advancing, or encouraging; the act of
exalting in rank or honor; also, the condition of being advanced,
encouraged, or exalted in honor; preferment.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Promotion</qex> cometh neither from the east, nor from the
west, nor from the south.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. lxxv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promotive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mo"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
advance, promote, or encourage.</def> <rj><au>Hume.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promove</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*move"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Promote</er>.]</ety> <def>To move forward; to advance; to
promote.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Fell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promover</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mov"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A promoter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompt</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/mt; 215)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Prompter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Promptest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>prompt</ets>, L. <ets>promptus</ets>, properly, brought forth (to
light or view), hence, visible, evident, at hand, ready, quick, -- p. p.
of <ets>promere</ets> to take or bring forth; <ets>pro</ets> forth +
<ets>emere</ets> to take. See <er>Redeem</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Ready and quick to act as occasion demands; meeting
requirements readily; not slow, dilatory, or hesitating in decision or
action; responding on the instant; immediate; <as>as, <ex>prompt</ex> in
obedience or compliance</as>; -- said of persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Very discerning and <qex>prompt</qex> in giving orders.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tell him I am <qex>prompt</qex><br/
To lay my crown at's feet.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And you, perhaps, too <qex>prompt</qex> in your replies.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Done or rendered quickly, readily, or immediately;
given without delay or hesitation; -- said of conduct; <as>as,
<ex>prompt</ex> assistance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Washington heard the voice of his country in distress,<br/
his obedience was <qex>prompt</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ames.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Easy; unobstructed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The reception of the light into the body of the building was very
<qex>prompt</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Ready; expeditious; quick; agile; alert; brisk;
nimble.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Prompt</er>, <er>Ready</er>,
<er>Expeditious</er>. One who is <xex>ready</xex> is prepared to act at
the moment. One who is <xex>prompt</xex> acts at the moment. One who is
<xex>expeditious</xex> carries through an undertaking with constant
promptness.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompt</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A limit of time
given for payment of an account for produce purchased, this limit
varying with different goods. See <er>Prompt-note</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To cover any probable difference of price which might arise before
the expiration of the <qex>prompt</qex>, which for this article [tea] is
three months.</q> <rj><qau>J. S. Mill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompt</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prompted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prompting</conjf>.]</vmorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To assist or induce the action of; to move to
action; to instigate; to incite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God first . . . <qex>prompted</qex> on the infirmities of the
infant world by temporal prosperity.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To suggest; to dictate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And whispering angles <qex>prompt</qex> her golden dreams.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To remind, as an actor or an orator, of words or
topics forgotten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompt-book</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt"-book`</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The book used
by a prompter of a theater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompter</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, prompts; one who admonishes
or incites to action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who reminds another, as an actor or an orator,
of the words to be spoken next; specifically, one employed for this
purpose in a theater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promptitude</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>promptitudo</ets>. See <er>Prompt</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>The quality of being prompt; quickness of decision and action when
occasion demands; alacrity; <as>as, <ex>promptitude</ex> in
obedience</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men of action, of <qex>promptitude</qex>, and of courage.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promptly</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prompt manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promptness</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Promptitude; readiness; quickness of decision or
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cheerful willingness; alacrity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompt-note</ent><br/
<hw>Prompt"-note`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Com.)</fld>
<def>A memorandum of a sale, and time when payment is due, given to the
purchaser at a sale of goods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promptuary</ent><br/
<hw>Promp"tu*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to preparation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promptuary</ent><br/
<hw>Promp"tu*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>promptuarium</ets>,
fr. <ets>promptuarius</ets> belonging to distribution, distributing: cf,
F. <ets>promptuaire</ets>. See <er>Prompt</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>That from which supplies are drawn; a storehouse; a magazine; a
repository.</def> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prompture</ent><br/
<hw>Promp"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prompt</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Suggestion; incitement;
prompting.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.
Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promulgate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mul"gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Promulgated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Promulgating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>promulgatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>promulgare</ets> to promulgate; of
unknown origin. Cf. <er>Promulge</er>.]</ety> <def>To make known by open
declaration, as laws, decrees, or tidings; to publish; <as>as, to
<ex>promulgate</ex> the secrets of a council</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To publish; declare; proclaim. See
<er>Announce</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promulgation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`mul*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>promulgatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>promulgation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act of promulgating; publication; open declaration; <as>as, the
<ex>promulgation</ex> of the gospel</as>.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promulgator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"mul*ga`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who promulgates or publishes.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promulge</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mulge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Promulged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Promulging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>promulguer</ets>. See <er>Promulgate</er>.]</ety> <def>To
promulgate; to publish or teach.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Extraordinary doctrines these for the age in which they were
<qex>promulged</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promulger</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*mul"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who promulges
or publishes what was before unknown.</def>
<rj><au>Atterbury.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Promuscis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*mus"cis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
corruption of <ets>proboscis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
proboscis of hemipterous insects. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Hemiptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronaos</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*na"os</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/; <?/ before + <?/ temple.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The porch
or vestibule of a temple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pronatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pronare</ets> to bend forward. See
<ets>Prone</ets>.]</ety> <def>Somewhat prone; inclined; <as>as,
<ex>pronate</ex> trees</as>.</def> <rj><au>Kane.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pronation</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
act of turning the palm or palmar surface of the forefoot
downward.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>That motion of the forearm whereby the
palm or palmar, surface is turned downward.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
position of the limb resulting from the act of pronation. Opposed to
<contr>supination</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*na"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A muscle which produces pronation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prone</ent><br/
<hw>Prone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pronus</ets>,
akin to Gr. <?/, <?/, Skr. <ets>pravana</ets> sloping, inclined, and
also to L. <ets>pro</ets> forward, for. See <er>Pro-</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Bending forward; inclined; not erect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Towards him they bend<br/
With awful reverence <qex>prone</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Prostrate; flat; esp., lying with the face down; --
opposed to <contr>supine</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which, as the wind,<br/
Blew where it listed, laying all things <qex>prone</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Headlong; running downward or headlong.</def>
<ldquo/Down thither <xex>prone</xex> in flight.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sloping, with reference to a line or surface;
declivous; inclined; not level.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Since the floods demand,<br/
For their descent, a <qex>prone</qex> and sinking land.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackmore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Inclined; propense; disposed; -- applied to the mind
or affections, usually in an ill sense. Followed by <xex>to</xex>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Prone</xex> to mischief.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Poets are nearly all <qex>prone</qex> to melancholy.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronely</ent><br/
<hw>Prone"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prone manner or
position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proneness</ent><br/
<hw>Prone"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being prone, or of bending
downward; <as>as, the <ex>proneness</ex> of beasts is opposed to the
erectness of man</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of lying with the face down; -- opposed to
<contr>supineness</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Descent; declivity; <as>as, the <ex>proneness</ex>
of a hill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Inclination of mind, heart, or temper; propension;
disposition; <as>as, <ex>proneness</ex> to
self-gratification</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronephric</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*neph"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the pronephros.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronephron</ent><br/
<ent>Pronephros</ent><br/
<mhw>\'d8<hw>Pro*neph"ros</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Pro*neph"ron</hw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[ NL., fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/
a kidney.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The head kidney. See under
<er>Head</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prong</ent><br/
<hw>Prong</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>prangen</ets> to pinch, press, LG. <ets>prange</ets> a stick, or W.
<ets>procio</ets> to thrust, E. <ets>prowl</ets>,
<ets>pang</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A sharp-pointed instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prick it on a <qex>prong</qex> of iron.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The tine of a fork, or of a similar instrument;
<as>as, a fork of two or three <ex>prongs</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A sharp projection,
as of an antler.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The fang of a tooth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prongbuck</ent><br/
<hw>Prong"buck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The springbuck.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
pronghorn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronged</ent><br/
<hw>Pronged</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having prongs or
projections like the tines of a fork; <as>as, a three-<ex>pronged</ex>
fork</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prong-hoe</ent><br/
<hw>Prong"-hoe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hoe with prongs
to break the earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronghorn</ent><br/
<hw>Prong"horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An American antelope (<spn>Antilocapra Americana</spn>), native of
the plain near the Rocky Mountains. The upper parts are mostly yellowish
brown; the under parts, the sides of the head and throat, and the
buttocks, are white. The horny sheath of the horns is shed annually.
Called also <altname>cabr<eacute/e</altname>, <altname>cabut</altname>,
<altname>prongbuck</altname>, and <altname>pronghorned
antelope</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pronk</ent><br/
<hw>pronk</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To jump straight up or almost
straight up; -- used of kangaroos and gazelles.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><note>Some less common gaits used only by few animals are the
ricochet, the <qex>pronk</qex> and the bound. The <i>ricochet</i> is the
gait principally employed by a class of animals such as the kangaroo.
The <qex>pronk</qex> is a gait used by quadrupeds such as gazelle and it
is equivalent to the ricochet except that both the front and back sets
of of feet perform the same action. The <i>bound</i> is used by a few
small quadrupeds such as squirrels and dogs. In the <i>bound</i> support
alternates between pairs of legs, with the fore and hind acting to push
the body forward.</note> <qau>Ionnas Poulakakis
(https://web.archive.org/web/20041213012817/http://www.cim.mcgill.ca/~poulakas/legged_locomotion/gaits.html)</qau><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ni*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pronitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Proneness; propensity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronominal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nom"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pronominalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pronominal</ets>. See
<er>Pronoun</er>.]</ety> <def>Belonging to, or partaking of the nature
of, a pronoun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronominalize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nom"i*nal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To give
the effect of a pronoun to; <as>as, to <ex>pronominalize</ex> the
substantives <ex>person</ex>, <ex>people</ex>, etc.</as></def>
<rj><au>Early.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronominally</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nom"i*nal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pronominal
manner<?/ with the nature or office of a pronoun; as a
pronoun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prononce</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`non`c<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Pronounce</er>.]</ety> <def>Strongly marked; decided, as in manners,
etc.; pronounced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronotary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*no"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Prothonotary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronotum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*no"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pronota</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Pro-</er>, and
<er>Notum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The dorsal plate of the
prothorax in insects. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Coleoptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronoun</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"noun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>noun</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pronom</ets>, L.
<ets>pronomen</ets>. See <er>Noun</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A
word used instead of a noun or name, to avoid the repetition of it. The
personal pronouns in English are <xex>I</xex>, <xex>thou</xex> or
<xex>you</xex>, <xex>he</xex>, <xex>she</xex>, <xex>it</xex>,
<xex>we</xex>, <xex>ye</xex>, and <xex>they</xex>.</def><-- accusatives?
me, them, us --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nounce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pronounced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pronounging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>prononcer</ets>, L. <ets>pronunciare</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before,
forth + <ets>nunciare</ets>, <ets>nuntiare</ets>, to announce. See
<er>Announce</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter articulately; to speak out or distinctly;
to utter, as words or syllables; to speak with the proper sound and
accent <as>as, adults rarely learn to <ex>pronounce</ex> a foreign
language correctly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To utter officially or solemnly; to deliver, as a
decree or sentence; <as>as, to <ex>pronounce</ex> sentence of
death</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sternly he <qex>pronounced</qex><br/
The rigid interdiction.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To speak or utter rhetorically; to deliver; to
recite; <as>as, to <ex>pronounce</ex> an oration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Speak the speech, I pray you, as I <qex>pronounced</qex> it to
you.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To declare or affirm; <as>as, he <ex>pronounced</ex>
the book to be a libel; he <ex>pronounced</ex> the act to be a
fraud.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The God who hallowed thee and blessed,<br/
<qex>Pronouncing</qex> thee all good.</q> <rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To deliver; utter; speak. See
<er>Deliver</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nounce"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To give a pronunciation; to articulate; <as>as, to
<ex>pronounce</ex> faultlessly</as>.</def> <rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make declaration; to utter on opinion; to speak
with confidence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronounce</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nounce"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pronouncement; declaration;
pronunciation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronounceable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nounce"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>pronunciabilis</ets> declarative.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
pronounced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronounced</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nounced"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prononc<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Strongly marked; unequivocal;
decided. <note>[A Gallicism]</note></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[His] views became every day more <qex>pronounced</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronouncement</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nounce"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
pronouncing; a declaration; a formal announcement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronouncer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*noun"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
pronounces, utters, or declares; also, a pronouncing book.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronouncing</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*noun"cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to,
or indicating, pronunciation; <as>as, a <ex>pronouncing</ex>
dictionary</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pronounciation</ent><br/
<hw>pronounciation</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>pronunciation</er>; -- a common misspelling (written only).</def>
<mark>[misspelling]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronubial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nu"bi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pronuba</ets> bridesmaid; <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>nubere</ets>
to marry.]</ety> <def>Presiding over marriage.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronucleus</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nu"cle*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pronuclei</plw> <pr>(-<imac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Nucleus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>One
of the two bodies or nuclei (called <xex>male</xex> and <xex>female
pronuclei</xex>) which unite to form the first segmentation nucleus of
an impregnated ovum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the maturing of the ovum preparatory to impregnation,
a part of the germinal vesicle (see <cref>Polar body</cref>, under
<er>Polar</er>) becomes converted into a number of small vesicles, which
aggregate themselves into a single clear nucleus. which travels towards
the center of the egg and is called the <xex>female pronucleus</xex>. In
impregnation, the spermatozoon which enters the egg soon loses its tail,
while the head forms a nucleus, called the <xex>male pronucleus</xex>,
which gradually travels towards the female pronucleus and eventually
fuses with it, forming the first segmentation nucleus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronuncial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nun"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to pronunciation; pronunciative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronunciamento</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nun`ci*a*men"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
proclamation or manifesto; a formal announcement or
declaration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronunciamiento</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*nun`ci*a`mi"en"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
See <er>Pronounce</er>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pronunciamento</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronunciation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nun`ci*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pronunciation</ets>, L. <ets>pronunciatio</ets>. See
<er>Pronounce</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of uttering with articulation; the act of
giving the proper sound and accent; utterance; <as>as, the
<ex>pronunciation</ex> of syllables of words; distinct or indistinct
<ex>pronunciation</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The mode of uttering words or sentences.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>The art of manner of uttering a
discourse publicly with propriety and gracefulness; -- now called
<altname>delivery</altname>.</def> <rj><au>J. Q. Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronunciative</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nun"ci*a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pronunciativus</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to pronunciation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Uttering confidently; dogmatical.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pronunciator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nun"ci*a`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
reciter.]</ety> <def>One who pronounces; a pronouncer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1148 --></p>

<p><ent>Pronunciatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*nun"ci*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to pronunciation; that pronounces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proof</ent><br/
<hw>Proof</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>prove</ets>,
<ets>proeve</ets>, F. <ets>preuve</ets>, fr. L. <ets>proba</ets>, fr.
<ets>probare</ets> to prove. See <er>Prove</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Any effort, process, or operation designed to
establish or discover a fact or truth; an act of testing; a test; a
trial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For whatsoever mother wit or art<br/
Could work, he put in <qex>proof</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You shall have many <qex>proofs</qex> to show your skill.</q>
<rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Formerly, a very rude mode of ascertaining the strength of spirits
was practiced, called the <qex>proof</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ure.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That degree of evidence which convinces the mind of
any truth or fact, and produces belief; a test by facts or arguments
that induce, or tend to induce, certainty of the judgment; conclusive
evidence; demonstration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll have some <qex>proof</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is no <qex>proof</qex> of a man's understanding to be able to
confirm whatever he pleases.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Properly speaking, <xex>proof</xex> is the effect or
result of evidence, evidence is the medium of proof. Cf.
<er>Demonstration</er>, 1.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The quality or state of having been proved or tried;
firmness or hardness that resists impression, or does not yield to
force; impenetrability of physical bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Firmness of mind; stability not to be
shaken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A trial impression, as from
type, taken for correction or examination; -- called also <altname>proof
sheet</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A process for testing the
accuracy of an operation performed. Cf. <er>Prove</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Armor of excellent or tried quality, and deemed
impenetrable; properly, <xex>armor of proof</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Artist's proof</b></col>, <cd>a very early proof
impression of an engraving, or the like; -- often distinguished by the
artist's signature.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof reader</b></col>, <cd>one who
reads, and marks correction in, proofs. See def. 5, above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Testimony; evidence; reason; argument; trial;
demonstration. See <er>Testimony</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proof</ent><br/
<hw>Proof</hw>, <pos>a.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Used in proving or testing; <as>as, a <ex>proof</ex>
load, or <ex>proof</ex> charge</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Firm or successful in resisting; <as>as,
<ex>proof</ex> against harm; water<ex>proof</ex>;
bomb<ex>proof</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I . . . have found thee<br/
<qex>Proof</qex> against all temptation.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This was a good, stout <qex>proof</qex> article of faith.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being of a certain standard as to strength; -- said
of alcoholic liquors.</def><-- in the United States, "proof" is a
measure of alcohol concentration expressed as percent of the
concentration of "proof spirit" defined below, i. e. a beverage of 100
proof is 50% alcohol by volume. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proof charge</b></col> <fld>(Firearms)</fld>, <cd>a
charge of powder and ball, greater than the service charge, fired in an
arm, as a gun or cannon, to test its strength.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof
impression</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Impression</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Proof load</b></col> <fld>(Engin.)</fld>, <cd>the greatest load
than can be applied to a piece, as a beam, column, etc., without
straining the piece beyond the elastic limit.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof
sheet</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Proof</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 5.</cd> --
<col><b>Proof spirit</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a strong
distilled liquor, or mixture of alcohol and water, containing not less
than a standard amount of alcohol. In the United States <ldquo/proof
spirit is defined by law to be that mixture of alcohol and water which
contains one half of its volume of alcohol, the alcohol when at a
temperature of 60<deg/ Fahrenheit being of specific gravity 0.7939
referred to water at its maximum density as unity. Proof spirit has at
60<deg/ Fahrenheit a specific gravity of 0.93353, 100 parts by volume of
the same consisting of 50 parts of absolute alcohol and 53.71 parts of
water,<rdquo/ the apparent excess of water being due to contraction of
the liquids on mixture. In England proof spirit is defined by Act 58,
George III., to be such as shall at a temperature of 51<deg/ Fahrenheit
weigh exactly the <frac12x13/ part of an equal measure of distilled
water. This contains 49.3 per cent by weight, or 57.09 by volume, of
alcohol. Stronger spirits, as those of about 60, 70, and 80 per cent of
alcohol, are sometimes called <xex>second</xex>, <xex>third</xex>, and
<xex>fourth proof spirits</xex> respectively.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof
staff</b></col>, <cd>a straight-edge used by millers to test the
flatness of a stone.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof stick</b></col> <fld>(Sugar
Manuf.)</fld>, <cd>a rod in the side of a vacuum pan, for testing the
consistency of the sirup.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof text</b></col>, <cd>a
passage of Scripture used to prove a doctrine.</cd></cs> <--
<mcol><col><b>proof coin</b></col> or <col><b>proof</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>a coin which has been specially struck, to produce the finest
specimen of its type.</cd> <note>Usually such coins are double-struck
from polished dies, and the raised features are sometimes frosted. They
thus have sharper features and more mirror-like fields than production
coins (i. e. those coins struck for circulation); they are considered by
coin collectors as the most desirable specimens of each coin, and
usually sell at a premium to their corresponding production
coins.</note> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proof-arm</ent><br/
<hw>Proof`-arm"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To arm with
proof armor; to arm securely; <as>as, to <ex>proof-arm</ex>
herself</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proofless</ent><br/
<hw>Proof"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wanting sufficient evidence to
induce belief; not proved.</def> <au>Boyle.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Proof"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proof-proof</ent><br/
<hw>Proof"-proof`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Proof against proofs;
obstinate in the wrong.</def> <ldquo/That might have shown to any one
who was not <xex>proof-proof</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Whateley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proostracum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*<oum/s"tra*cum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Proostraca</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/ shell of a testacean.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The anterior prolongation of the guard of the phragmocone of
belemnites and allied fossil cephalopods, whether horny or calcareous.
See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Phragmocone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prootic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*<oum/"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, an ear.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>In front of the auditory capsule; -- applied especially to a bone,
or center of ossification, in the periotic capsule.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A prootic bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prop</ent><br/
<hw>Prop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shell, used as a die.
See <er>Props</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prop</ent><br/
<hw>Prop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Propped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Propping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to LG. & D.
<ets>proppen</ets> to cram, stuff, thrust into, stop, G.
<ets>pfropfen</ets>, Dan. <ets>proppe</ets>, Sw. <ets>proppa</ets>; of
uncertain origin, cf. G. <ets>pfropfen</ets> to graft, fr. L.
<ets>propago</ets> set, layer of a plant, slip, shoot. Cf. 3d.
<er>Prop</er>, <er>Propagate</er>.]</ety> <def>To support, or prevent
from falling, by placing something under or against; <as>as, to
<ex>prop</ex> up a fence or an old building</as>; (<mark>Fig</mark>.) to
sustain; to maintain; <as>as, to <ex>prop</ex> a declining
state</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till the bright mountains <qex>prop</qex> the incumbent sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For being not <qex>propp'd</qex> by ancestry.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>prop</qex> myself upon those few supports that are left
me.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prop</ent><br/
<hw>Prop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG., D., & Dan.
<ets>prop</ets> stopple, stopper, cork, Sw. <ets>propp</ets>, G.
<ets>pfropf</ets>. See <er>Prop</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>That
which sustains an incumbent weight; that on which anything rests or
leans for support; a support; a stay; <as>as, a <ex>prop</ex> for a
building</as>.</def> <ldquo/Two <xex>props</xex> of virtue.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propaedeutical</ent><br/
<ent>Propaedeutic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro`p<ae/*deu"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pro`p<ae/*deu"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/ to teach beforehand; <?/ before + <?/ to bring up a child,
to educate, teach, fr. <?/, <?/, a child.]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to,
or conveying, preliminary instruction; introductory to any art or
science; instructing beforehand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propaedeutics</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`p<ae/*deu"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
preliminary learning connected with any art or science; preparatory
instruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagable</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"a*ga*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Propagate</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being propagated, or of being continued
or multiplied by natural generation or production.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being spread or extended by any means; --
said of tenets, doctrines, or principles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propaganda</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`a*gan"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. fr. L.
<ets>de propaganda fide</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propagande</ets>. See
<er>Propagate</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A congregation
of cardinals, established in 1622, charged with the management of
missions.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The college of the Propaganda,
instituted by Urban VIII. (1623-1644) to educate priests for missions in
all parts of the world.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, any organization or plan for spreading a
particular doctrine or a system of principles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagandism</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`a*gan"dism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>propagandisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or practice of propagating
tenets or principles; zeal in propagating one's opinions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagandist</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`a*gan"dist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>propagandiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A person who devotes himself to
the spread of any system of principles.</def> <ldquo/Political
<xex>propagandists</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Walsh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- propagandize. To spread one's beliefs. Often used in a negative
sense, meaning to deliberately make misleading or false statements so as
to convert others to one's beliefs, or to convince others to vote for a
particular political candidate. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagate</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"a*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Propagated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Propagating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>propagatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>propagare</ets> to propagate, akin
to <ets>propages</ets>, <ets>propago</ets>, a layer of a plant, slip,
shoot. See <er>Pro-</er>, and cf. <er>Pact</er>, <er>Prop</er>,
<er>Prune</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to continue or multiply by generation, or
successive production; -- applied to animals and plants; <as>as, to
<ex>propagate</ex> a breed of horses or sheep; to <ex>propagate</ex> a
species of fruit tree.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to spread to extend; to impel or continue
forward in space; <as>as, to <ex>propagate</ex> sound or
light</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To spread from person to person; to extend the
knowledge of; to originate and spread; to carry from place to place; to
disseminate; <as>as, to <ex>propagate</ex> a story or report; to
<ex>propagate</ex> the Christian religion.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The infection was <qex>propagated</qex> insensibly.</q>
<rj><qau>De Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To multiply; to increase.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Griefs of mine own lie heavy in my breast,<br/
Which thou wilt <qex>propagate</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To generate; to produce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Motion <qex>propagated</qex> motion, and life threw off life.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To multiply; continue; increase; spread; diffuse;
disseminate; promote.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagate</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"a*gate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To have young or issue; to
be produced or multiplied by generation, or by new shoots or plants;
<as>as, rabbits <ex>propagate</ex> rapidly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No need that thou<br/
Should'st <qex>propagate</qex>, already infinite.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagation</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`a*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propagatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propagation</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of propagating; continuance or
multiplication of the kind by generation or successive production;
<as>as, the <ex>propagation</ex> of animals or plants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is not in nature any spontaneous generation, but all come by
<qex>propagation</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The spreading abroad, or extension, of anything;
diffusion; dissemination; <as>as, the <ex>propagation</ex> of sound; the
<ex>propagation</ex> of the gospel.</as></def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagative</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"a*ga*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Producing by
propagation, or by a process of growth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagator</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"a*ga`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.: cf. F.
<ets>propagateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who propagates; one who
continues or multiplies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propagulum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*pag"u*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Propagula</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Propagate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A runner terminated by
a germinating bud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propane</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Propy</ets>l +
meth<ets>ane</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A heavy gaseous
hydrocarbon, <chform>C3H8</chform>, of the paraffin series, occurring
naturally dissolved in crude petroleum, and also made artificially; --
called also <altname>propyl hydride</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propargyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*par"gyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Prop</ets>inyl + Gr. <?/ silver + <ets>-yl</ets>. So called
because one hydrogen atom may be replaced by silver.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Propinyl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proparoxytone</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`par*ox"y*tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.
See <er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Paroxytone</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr.
Gram.)</fld> <def>A word which has the acute accent on the
antepenult.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proped</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>pro-</ets>
+ L. <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Proleg</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propel</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pel"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Propelled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Propelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>propellere</ets>, <ets>propulsum</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>pellere</ets> to drive. See <er>Pulse</er> a beating.]</ety>
<def>To drive forward; to urge or press onward by force; to move, or
cause to move; <as>as, the wind or steam <ex>propels</ex> ships; balls
are <ex>propelled</ex> by gunpowder.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propeller</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pel"ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, propels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A contrivance for propelling a steam vessel, usually
consisting of a screw placed in the stern under water, and made to
revolve by an engine; a propeller wheel.</def> <-- (b) an analogous
device, rotated by an engine at high speed to provide the forward thrust
which propels an airplane through the atmosphere. On each engine is a
propeller consisting of a set of at least two elongated blades attached
symmetrically to a central rotor. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A steamboat thus propelled; a screw
steamer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Propeller wheel</b></col>,<cd>the screw, usually having
two or more blades, used in propelling a vessel.</cd><-- propeller
blade, the elongated part of an airplane propeller --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propend</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propendere</ets>, <ets>propensum</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> forward,
forth + <ets>pendere</ets> to hang. See <er>Pendent</er>.]</ety> <def>To
lean toward a thing; to be favorably inclined or disposed; to incline;
to tend.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We shall <qex>propend</qex> to it, as a stone falleth down.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propendency</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pend"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Propensity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Attentive deliberation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propendent</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pend"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propendens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Inclining forward or
toward.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propene</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Prop</ets>yl +
ethyl<ets>ene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Propylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propense</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pense"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propensus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Propend</er>.]</ety> <def>Leaning
toward, in a moral sense; inclined; disposed; prone; <as>as, women
<ex>propense</ex> to holiness</as>.</def> <au>Hooker.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro*pense"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pro*pense"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propension</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propensio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propension</ets>. See
<er>Propend</er>, <er>Propense</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being propense; propensity.</def> <rj><au>M. Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your full consent<br/
Gave wings to my <qex>propension</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propensity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pen"si*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Propensities</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The quality or state
of being propense; natural inclination; disposition to do good or evil;
bias; bent; tendency.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>propensity</xex> to utter
blasphemy.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Disposition; bias; inclination; proclivity;
proneness; bent; tendency.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propenyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pe*nyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Propene</ets>
+ <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical
hydrocarbon radical, <chform>C3H5</chform>, isomeric with allyl and
glyceryl, and regarded as the essential residue of glycerin. Cf.
<er>Allyl</er>, and <er>Glyceryl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propepsin</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pep"sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>pepsin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Persinogen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propeptone</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pep"tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>peptone</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>
<def>A product of gastric digestion intermediate between albumin and
peptone, identical with hemialbumose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proper</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>propre</ets>, F. <ets>propre</ets>, fr. L. <ets>proprius</ets>. Cf.
<er>Appropriate</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Belonging to one; one's own; individual.</def>
<ldquo/His <xex>proper</xex> good<rdquo/ [<it>i. e.</it>, his
<xex>own</xex> possessions]. <au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/My
<xex>proper</xex> son.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now learn the difference, at your <qex>proper</qex> cost,<br/
Betwixt true valor and an empty boast.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Belonging to the natural or essential constitution;
peculiar; not common; particular; <as>as, every animal has his
<ex>proper</ex> instincts and appetites</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those high and peculiar attributes . . . which constitute our
<qex>proper</qex> humanity.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Befitting one's nature, qualities, etc.; suitable in
all respect; appropriate; right; fit; decent; <as>as, water is the
<ex>proper</ex> element for fish; a <ex>proper</ex>
dress.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>proper</qex> study of mankind is man.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In Athens all was pleasure, mirth, and play,<br/
All <qex>proper</qex> to the spring, and sprightly May.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Becoming in appearance; well formed; handsome.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <ldquo/Thou art a <xex>proper</xex> man.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moses . . . was hid three months of his parents, because they saw
he was a <qex>proper</qex> child.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. xi.
23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Pertaining to one of a species, but not common to
the whole; not appellative; -- opposed to <contr>common</contr>; <as>as,
a <ex>proper</ex> name; Dublin is the <ex>proper</ex> name of a
city.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Rightly so called; strictly considered; <as>as,
Greece <ex>proper</ex>; the garden <ex>proper</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Represented in its natural color;
-- said of any object used as a charge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In proper</b></col>, <cd>individually; privately.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Jer. Taylor.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Proper
flower</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Proper corolla</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>one of the single florets, or corollets, in an
aggregate or compound flower.</cd> -- <col><b>Proper fraction</b></col>
<fld>(Arith.)</fld> <cd>a fraction in which the numerator is less than
the denominator.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Proper nectary</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a nectary separate from the petals and other
parts of the flower.</cd> -- <col><b>Proper noun</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>a name belonging to an individual, by which it
is distinguished from others of the same class; -- opposed to
<ant>common noun</ant>; as, <xex>John</xex>, <xex>Boston</xex>,
<xex>America</xex>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Proper perianth</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Proper involucre</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>that which incloses only a single flower.</cd> -- <col><b>Proper
receptacle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a receptacle which supports
only a single flower or fructification.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proper</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Properly; hence, to a great
degree; very; <as>as, <ex>proper</ex> good</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq &
Vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Properate</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>properatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>properare</ets> to hasten.]</ety>
<def>To hasten, or press forward.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Properation</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`er*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>properatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of hastening; haste.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Properispome</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*per"i*spome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gr.
Gram.)</fld> <def>Properispomenon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Properispomenon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*per`i*spom"e*non</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Properispomena</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to circumflex on the penult; <?/ before
+ <?/ to circumflex. See <er>Perispomenon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr.
Gram.)</fld> <def>A word which has the circumflex accent on the
penult.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Properly</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>In a proper manner; suitably; fitly; strictly;
rightly; <as>as, a word <ex>properly</ex> applied; a dress
<ex>properly</ex> adjusted.</as></def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Individually; after one's own manner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now, harkeneth, how I bare me <qex>properly</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Properness</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of being proper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tallness; comeliness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Udall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propertied</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er*tied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Possessing
property; holding real estate, or other investments of money.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>propertied</xex> and satisfied classes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>M. Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Property</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Properties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>proprete</ets>,
OF. <ets>propret<eacute/</ets> property, F. <ets>propret<eacute/</ets>
neatness, cleanliness, <ets>propri<eacute/t<eacute/</ets> property, fr.
L. <ets>proprietas</ets>. See <er>Proper</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Propriety</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is proper to anything; a peculiar quality
of a thing; that which is inherent in a subject, or naturally essential
to it; an attribute; <as>as, sweetness is a <ex>property</ex> of
sugar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Property</qex> is correctly a synonym for peculiar quality;
but it is frequently used as coextensive with quality in general.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In physical science, the properties of matter are
distinguished to the three following classes: 1. <xex>Physical
properties</xex>, or those which result from the relations of bodies to
the physical agents, light, heat, electricity, gravitation, cohesion,
adhesion, etc., and which are exhibited without a change in the
composition or kind of matter acted on. They are color, luster, opacity,
transparency, hardness, sonorousness, density, crystalline form,
solubility, capability of osmotic diffusion, vaporization, boiling,
fusion, etc. 2. <xex>Chemical properties</xex>, or those which are
conditioned by affinity and composition; thus, combustion, explosion,
and certain solutions are reactions occasioned by chemical properties.
Chemical properties are identical when there is identity of composition
and structure, and change according as the composition changes. 3.
<xex>Organoleptic properties</xex>, or those forming a class which can
not be included in either of the other two divisions. They manifest
themselves in the contact of substances with the organs of taste, touch,
and smell, or otherwise affect the living organism, as in the manner of
medicines and poisons.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An acquired or artificial quality; that which is
given by art, or bestowed by man; <as>as, the poem has the
<ex>properties</ex> which constitute excellence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The exclusive right of possessing, enjoying, and
disposing of a thing; ownership; title.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here I disclaim all my paternal care,<br/
Propinquity and <qex>property</qex> of blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shall man assume a <qex>property</qex> in man?</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1149 --></p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That to which a person has a legal title, whether in
his possession or not; thing owned; an estate, whether in lands, goods,
or money; <as>as, a man of large <ex>property</ex>, or small
<ex>property</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>All the adjuncts of a play except
the scenery and the dresses of the actors; stage requisites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will draw a bill of <qex>properties</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Propriety; correctness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Literary property</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Literary</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Property man</b></col><-- or
prop man -->, <cd>one who has charge of the <ldquo/properties<rdquo/ of
a theater.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Property</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"er*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To invest which properties, or qualities.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a property of; to appropriate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They have here <qex>propertied</qex> me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophane</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*phane"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Profane</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proph"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ to show beforehand. See <er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Phasis</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Foreknowledge of a disease; prognosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophecy</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"e*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it></plu>
<plw>Prophecies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, <ety>[OE. <ets>prophecie</ets>, OF.
<ets>profecie</ets>, F. <ets>proph<eacute/tie</ets>, L.
<ets>prophetia</ets>, fr. Gr. <ets><?/</ets>, fr. <?/ to be an
interpreter of the gods, to prophesy, fr. <?/ prophet. See
<er>Prophet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A declaration of something to
come; a foretelling; a prediction; esp., an inspired
foretelling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hearkens after <qex>prophecies</qex> and dreams.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prophecy</qex> came not in old time by the will of man.</q>
<rj><qau>2. Pet. i. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>A book of prophecies; a
history; <as>as, the <ex>prophecy</ex> of Ahijah</as>.</def> <rj><au>2
Chron. ix. 29.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Public interpretation of Scripture; preaching;
exhortation or instruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophesier</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"e*si`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A prophet.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophesy</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"e*sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prophesied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prophesying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Prophecy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To foretell; to predict; to
prognosticate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He doth not <qex>prophesy</qex> good concerning me.</q> <rj><au>1
Kings xxii. 8.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then I perceive that will be verified<br/
Henry the Fifth did sometime <qex>prophesy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To foreshow; to herald; to prefigure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Methought thy very gait did <qex>prophesy</qex><br/
A royal nobleness; I must embrace thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophesy</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"e*sy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter predictions; to make declaration of events
to come.</def> <rj><au>Matt. xv. 7.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> <sn>2.</sn> <def>To give instruction in religious matters; to
interpret or explain Scripture or religious subjects; to preach; to
exhort; to expound.</def> <rj><au>Ezek. xxxvii. 7.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophet</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>proph<egrave/te</ets>, L. <ets>propheta</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/,
literally, one who speaks for another, especially, one who speaks for a
god an interprets his will to man, fr. <?/ to say beforehand; <?/ for,
before + <?/ to say or speak. See <er>Fame</er>. ]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who prophesies, or foretells events; a
predicter; a foreteller.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One inspired or instructed by God to speak in his
name, or announce future events, <as>as, Moses, Elijah,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An interpreter; a spokesman.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ex. vii. 1.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A mantis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>School of the prophets</b></col> <fld>(Anc. Jewish
Hist.)</fld>, <cd>a school or college in which young men were educated
and trained for public teachers or members of the prophetic order. These
students were called <xex>sons of the prophets</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophetess</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"et*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>proph<eacute/tesse</ets>, L. <ets>prophetissa</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
female prophet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophetical</ent><br/
<ent>Prophetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*phet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro*phet"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>propheticus</ets>, Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>proph<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Containing, or
pertaining to, prophecy; foretelling events; <as>as, <ex>prophetic</ex>
writings; <ex>prophetic</ex> dreams</as>; -- used with <xex>of</xex>
before the thing foretold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And fears are oft <qex>prophetic</qex> of the event.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propheticality</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*phet`ic*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Propheticalness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophetically</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*phet"ic*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
prophetical manner; by way of prediction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propheticalness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*phet"ic*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being prophetical; power or capacity to foretell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophetize</ent><br/
<hw>Proph"et*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prophetizare</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>proph<eacute/tiser</ets>.
Cf. <er>Prophesy</er>.]</ety> <def>To give predictions; to foreshow
events; to prophesy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Prophetizing</xex> dreams.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophoric</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
utterance.]</ety> <def>Enunciative.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophragma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*phrag"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prophragmata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/
before + <?/, <?/, fence, screen. ]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
internal dorsal chitinous process between the first two divisions of the
thorax of insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophylactic</ent><br/
<hw>Proph`y*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prophylactique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
which preserves or defends against disease; a preventive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophylactical</ent><br/
<ent>Prophylactic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Proph`y*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Proph`y*lac"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to guard against; <?/ before + <?/ to guard: cf.
F. <ets>prophylactique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Defending or
preserving from disease; preventive.</def> <rj><au>Coxe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prophylaxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proph`y*lax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Prophylactic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The art of
preserving from, or of preventing, disease; the observance of the rules
necessary for the preservation of health; preservative or preventive
treatment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propice</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pice"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE., fr. F.
<ets>propice</ets>, See <er>Propitious</er>.]</ety> <def>Fit;
propitious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>E. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propidene</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pi*dene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Prop</ets>yl
+ ethyl<ets>idene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The
unsymmetrical hypothetical hydrocarbon radical,
<chform>CH3.CH2.CH</chform>, analogous to ethylidene, and regarded as
the type of certain derivatives of propane; -- called also
<altname>propylidene</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propination</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propinatio</ets>. See <er>Propine</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
pledging, or drinking first, and then offering the cup to another.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Abp. Potter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propinare</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ before + <?/ to drink.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To pledge; to offer as a toast or a health in the
manner of drinking, that is, by drinking first and passing the
cup.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lovely sorceress mixed, and to the prince<br/
Health, peace, and joy <qex>propined</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Smart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to give in token of friendship.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To give, or deliver; to subject.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fotherby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A pledge.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A gift; esp., drink money.</def> <mark>[Obs or
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Prop</ets>yl +
eth<ets>ine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Allylene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propinquity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pin"qui*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propinquitas</ets>, from <ets>propinquus</ets> near, neighboring,
from <ets>prope</ets> near.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Nearness in place; neighborhood;
proximity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nearness in time.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Nearness of blood; kindred; affinity.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propinyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pi*nyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Propine</ets>
+ <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hydrocarbon radical
regarded as an essential residue of propine and allied
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propiolate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pi*o*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A salt of
propiolic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propiolic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`pi*ol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Propi</ets>onic + tetr<ets>olic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, an organic acid
(called also <xex>propargylic</xex> acid) of the acetylene or tetrolic
series, analogous to propionic acid, and obtained as a white crystalline
substance.</def><-- also called 2-propynoic acid, and
acetylenecarboxylic acid. <chform>C3H2O2</chform>,
<chform>CH.C.COOH</chform> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propionate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pi*o*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of propionic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propione</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pi*one</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The ketone of propionic acid, obtained as a colorless fragrant
liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propionic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`pi*on"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pro</ets>to- + Gr. <grk>pi`wn</grk> fat.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, an
organic acid which is produced in the distillation of wood, in the
fermentation of various organic substances, as glycerin, calcium
lactate, etc., and is obtained as a colorless liquid having a sharp,
pungent odor. Propionic acid is so called because it is the
<xex>first</xex> or lowest member of the <xex>fatty acid</xex> series
whose salts have a fatty feel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propionyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pi*o*nyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The hypothetical radical <chform>C3H5O</chform>, regarded as the
essential residue of propionic acid and certain related
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propithecus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prop`i*the"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ before, for + <?/ ape.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus
including the long-tailed, or diadem, indris. See
<er>Indris</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propitiabilis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
propitiated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Propitiated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Propitiating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>propitiatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>propitiare</ets> to propitiate,
fr. <ets>propitius</ets> favorable. See <er>Propitious</er>.]</ety>
<def>To appease to render favorable; to make propitious; to
conciliate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let fierce Achilles, dreadful in his rage,<br/
The god <qex>propitiate</qex>, and the pest assuage.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*ate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make propitiation; to
atone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi`ti*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propitiatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propitiation</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of appeasing the wrath and conciliating the
favor of an offended person; the act of making propitious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>That which propitiates;
atonement or atoning sacrifice; specifically, the influence or effects
of the death of Christ in appeasing the divine justice, and conciliating
the divine favor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [Jesus Christ] is the <qex>propitiation</qex> for our sins.</q>
<rj><qau>1 John ii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiator</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*a`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who propitiates or appeases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiatorily</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*a*to*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way
of propitiation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propitiatorius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propitiatoire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Having the power to make propitious; pertaining to, or employed in,
propitiation; expiatory; <as>as, a <ex>propitiatory</ex>
sacrifice</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sharp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitiatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"ti*a*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propitiatorium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Jewish Antiq.)</fld> <def>The
mercy seat; -- so called because a symbol of the propitiated
Jehovah.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propitious</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pi"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propitius</ets>, perhaps originally a term of augury meaning,
flying forward (<ets>pro</ets>) or well; cf. Skr. <ets>pat</ets> to fly,
E. <ets>petition</ets>, <ets>feather</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Convenient; auspicious; favorable; kind; <as>as, a
<ex>propitious</ex> season; a <ex>propitious</ex> breeze.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, kind; gracious; merciful; helpful; -- said of
a person or a divinity.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And now t' assuage the force of this new flame,<br/
And make thee [Love] more <qex>propitious</qex> in my need.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Auspicious; favorable; kind.</syn> --
<usage><er>Propitious</er>, <er>Auspicious</er>. <xex>Auspicious</xex>
(from the ancient idea of <xex>auspices</xex>, or omens) denotes
<ldquo/indicative of success,<rdquo/ or <ldquo/favored by incidental
occurrences;<rdquo/ as, an <xex>auspicious</xex> opening; an
<xex>auspicious</xex> event. <xex>Propitious</xex> denotes that which
efficaciously protect us in some undertaking, speeds our exertions, and
decides our success; as, <xex>propitious</xex> gales;
<xex>propitious</xex> influences.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pro*pi"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pro*pi"tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proplasm</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"plasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proplasma</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ before + <?/ a thing formed, fr. <?/
to mold.]</ety> <def>A mold; a matrix.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proplastic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*plas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Forming a
mold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proplastics</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*plas"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art of
making molds for castings.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propleg</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"leg`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called because
it <ets>props up</ets> or supports the body.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Proleg</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propodial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*po"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the propodialia, or the parts of the limbs to
which they belong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propodiale</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*po`di*a"le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Propodialia</plw>. <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/, dim. of <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The bone of either the upper arm or the thing,
the <xex>propodialia</xex> being the humerus and femur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propodite</ent><br/
<hw>Prop"o*dite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
sixth joint of a typical leg of a crustacean; usually, the penultimate
joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Propodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Propodiale</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
anterior portion of the foot of a mollusk.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
segment which forms the posterior part of the thorax of a hymenopterous
insect.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>propodeum</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propolis</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"po*lis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/;
<?/ before + <?/ city.]</ety> <def>Same as <cref>Bee glue</cref>, under
<er>Bee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propone</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pone"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proponere</ets> to propose. See <er>Propound</er>.]</ety> <def>To
propose; to bring forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proponent</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*po"nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proponens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Making proposals;
proposing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proponent</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*po"nent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>One who makes a proposal, or lays down a
proposition.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The propounder of a
thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>proportio</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>portio</ets> part or
share. See <er>Portion</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The relation or adaptation of one portion to
another, or to the whole, as respect magnitude, quantity, or degree;
comparative relation; ratio; <as>as, the <ex>proportion</ex> of the
parts of a building, or of the body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The image of Christ, made after his own <qex>proportion</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ridley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Formed in the best <qex>proportions</qex> of her sex.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Documents are authentic and facts are true precisely in
<qex>proportion</qex> to the support which they afford to his
theory.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Harmonic relation between parts, or between
different things of the same kind; symmetrical arrangement or
adjustment; symmetry; <as>as, to be out of
<ex>proportion</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/Let us prophesy according to the
<xex>proportion</xex> of faith.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Rom. xii.
6.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The portion one receives when a whole is distributed
by a rule or principle; equal or proper share; lot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the women . . . do the same things in their
<qex>proportions</qex> and capacities.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A part considered comparatively; a share.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The equality or
similarity of ratios, especially of geometrical ratios; or a relation
among quantities such that the quotient of the first divided by the
second is equal to that of the third divided by the fourth; -- called
also <altname>geometrical proportion</altname>, in distinction from
<xex>arithmetical proportion</xex>, or that in which the difference of
the first and second is equal to the difference of the third and
fourth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ <xex>Proportion</xex> in the mathematical sense differs
from <xex>ratio</xex>. <xex>Ratio</xex> is the relation of two
quantities of the same kind, as the ratio of 5 to 10, or the ratio of 8
to 16. <xex>Proportion</xex> is the sameness or likeness of two such
relations. Thus, 5 to 10 as 8 to 16; that is, 5 bears the same relation
to 10 as 8 does to 16. Hence, such numbers are said to be in
<xex>proportion</xex>. <xex>Proportion</xex> is expressed by symbols
thus:<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><mathex>a:b::c:d</mathex>, or <mathex>a:b = c:d</mathex>, or
<mathex><fract>a/b</fract> = <fract>c/d</fract></mathex>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The rule of three, in arithmetic, in which the
three given terms, together with the one sought, are
proportional.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Continued proportion</b></col>, <col><b>Inverse
proportion</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Continued</er>,
<er>Inverse</er>, etc.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Harmonical
proportion</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Musical
proportion</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a relation of three or four quantities,
such that the first is to the last as the difference between the first
two is to the difference between the last two; thus, 2, 3, 6, are in
harmonical proportion; for 2 is to 6 as 1 to 3. Thus, 24, 16, 12, 9, are
harmonical, for <mathex>24:9::8:3</mathex>.</cd> -- <col><b>In
proportion</b></col>, <cd>according as; to the degree that.</cd>
<ldquo/<xex>In proportion</xex> as they are metaphysically true, they
are morally and politically false.<rdquo/ <au>Burke.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Proportioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Proportioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>proportionner</ets>. Cf. <er>Proportionate</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To adjust in a suitable
proportion, as one thing or one part to another; <as>as, to
<ex>proportion</ex> the size of a building to its height; to
<ex>proportion</ex> our expenditures to our income.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the loss of an object we do not <qex>proportion</qex> our grief
to the real value . . . but to the value our fancies set upon it.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form with symmetry or suitableness, as the parts
of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature had <qex>proportioned</qex> her without any fault.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To divide into equal or just shares; to
apportion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being proportioned, or made proportional; also, proportional;
proportionate.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pro*por"tion*a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But eloquence may exist without a <qex>proportionable</qex> degree
of wisdom.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> <qex>Proportionable</qex>, which is no longer much favored, was
of our [i. e., English writers'] own coining.</q> <rj><qau>Fitzed.
Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionably</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Proportionally.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proportionalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proportionnel</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a due proportion, or comparative relation; being
in suitable proportion or degree; <as>as, the parts of an edifice are
<ex>proportional</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to, or securing, proportion.</def>
<rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Constituting a proportion; having
the same, or a constant, ratio; <as>as, <ex>proportional</ex>
quantities; momentum is <ex>proportional</ex> to quantity of
matter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proportional logarithms</b></col>, <cd>logistic
logarithms. See under <er>Logistic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Proportional
scale</b></col>, <cd>a scale on which are marked parts proportional to
the logarithms of the natural numbers; a logarithmic scale.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Proportional</b></col> <col><b>scales, compasses,
dividers</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <fld>(Draughting)</fld>, <cd>instruments
used in making copies of drawings, or drawings of objects, on an
enlarged or reduced scale.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Any number or quantity in a proportion; <as>as, a mean
<ex>proportional</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The combining weight or
equivalent of an element.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionality</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por`tion*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>proportionnalit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of being in
proportion.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionally</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In
proportion; in due degree; adapted relatively; <as>as, all parts of the
building are <ex>proportionally</ex> large</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir I.
Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proportionatus</ets>. See <er>Proportion</er>.]</ety> <def>Adjusted
to something else according to a proportion; proportional.</def>
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What is <qex>proportionate</qex> to his transgression.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Proportionated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Proportionating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. <er>Proportion</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>To make proportional; to adjust according to a
settled rate, or to due comparative relation; to proportion; <as>as, to
<ex>proportionate</ex> punishment to crimes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionately</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*ate*ly</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
proportionate manner; with due proportion; proportionally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionateness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*ate*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being proportionate.</def> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1150 --></p>

<p><ent>Proportionless</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without
proportion; unsymmetrical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proportionment</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*por"tion*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of dividing out proportionally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proposal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pos"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Propose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is proposed, or
propounded for consideration or acceptance; a scheme or design; terms or
conditions proposed; offer; <as>as, to make <ex>proposals</ex> for a
treaty of peace; to offer <ex>proposals</ex> for erecting a building; to
make <ex>proposals</ex> of marriage.</as></def> <ldquo/To put forth
<xex>proposals</xex> for a book.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The offer by a party of what he has
in view as to an intended business transaction, which, with acceptance,
constitutes a contract.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Proffer; tender; overture. See
<er>Proposition</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propose</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Proposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Proposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>proposer</ets>; pref. <ets>pro-</ets> (L. <ets>pro</ets> for,
forward) + <ets>poser</ets> to place. See <er>Pose</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To set forth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That being <qex>proposed</qex> brimfull of wine, one scarce could
lift it up.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To offer for consideration, discussion, acceptance,
or adoption; <as>as, to <ex>propose</ex> terms of peace; to
<ex>propose</ex> a question for discussion; to <ex>propose</ex> an
alliance; to <ex>propose</ex> a person for office.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To set before one's self or others as a purpose
formed; hence, to purpose; to intend.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>propose</qex> to relate, in several volumes, the history of
the people of New England.</q> <rj><au>Palfrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To propose to one's self</b></col>, <cd>to intend; to
design.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propose</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pose"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To speak; to
converse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There shalt thou find my cousin Beatrice,<br/
<qex>Proposing</qex> with the prince and Claudio.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form or declare a purpose or intention; to lay a
scheme; to design; <as>as, man <ex>proposes</ex>, but God
disposes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To offer one's self in marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propose</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pose"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>propos</ets>, L.
<ets>propositum</ets>. See <er>Propound</er>, <er>Purpose</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Talk; discourse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proposer</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pos"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
proposes or offers anything for consideration or adoption.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A speaker; an orator.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proposition</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`o*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propositio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proposition</ets>. See
<er>Propound</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of setting or placing
before; the act of offering.</def> <ldquo/Oblations for the altar of
<xex>proposition</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is proposed; that which is offered, as
for consideration, acceptance, or adoption; a proposal; <as>as, the
enemy made <ex>propositions</ex> of peace; his <ex>proposition</ex> was
not accepted.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A statement of religious doctrine; an article of
faith; creed; <as>as, the <ex>propositions</ex> of Wyclif and
Huss</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some persons . . . change their <qex>propositions</qex> according
as their temporal necessities or advantages do turn.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Gram. & Logic)</fld> <def>A complete sentence, or
part of a sentence consisting of a subject and predicate united by a
copula; a thought expressed or propounded in language; a from of speech
in which a predicate is affirmed or denied of a subject; <as>as,
<ex>snow is white</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A statement in terms of a truth
to be demonstrated, or of an operation to be performed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is called a <xex>theorem</xex> when it is something
to be proved, and a <xex>problem</xex> when it is something to be
done.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>That which is offered or affirmed
as the subject of the discourse; anything stated or affirmed for
discussion or illustration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Poetry)</fld> <def>The part of a poem in which the
author states the subject or matter of it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Leaves of proposition</b></col> <fld>(Jewish
Antiq.)</fld>, <cd>the showbread.</cd> <rj><au>Wyclif (Luke vi.
4).</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Proposal; offer; statement; declaration.</syn>
<usage> -- <er>Proposition</er>, <er>Proposal</er>. These words are both
from the Latin verb <xex>proponere</xex>, to set forth, and as here
compared they mark different forms or stages of a negotiation. A
<xex>proposition</xex> is something presented for discussion or
consideration; <as>as, <ex>propositions</ex> of peace</as>. A
<xex>proposal</xex> is some definite thing offered by one party to be
accepted or rejected by the other. If the <xex>proposition</xex> is
favorably received, it is usually followed by <xex>proposals</xex> which
complete the arrangement.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propositional</ent><br/
<hw>Prop`o*si"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to, or in the nature of, a proposition; considered as a proposition;
<as>as, a <ex>propositional</ex> sense</as>.</def> <rj><au>I.
Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propound</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pound"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Propounded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Propounding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From earlier
<ets>propone</ets>, L. <ets>proponere</ets>, <ets>propositum</ets>, to
set forth, propose, propound; <ets>pro</ets> for, before +
<ets>ponere</ets> to put. See <er>Position</er>, and cf.
<er>Provost</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To offer for consideration; to
exhibit; to propose; <as>as, to <ex>propound</ex> a question; to
<ex>propound</ex> an argument.</as></def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And darest thou to the Son of God <qex>propound</qex><br/
To worship thee, accursed?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is strange folly to set ourselves no mark, to
<qex>propound</qex> no end, in the hearing of the gospel.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>To propose or name as a candidate
for admission to communion with a church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propounder</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pound"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
propounds, proposes, or offers for consideration.</def>
<rj><au>Chillingworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propretor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pre"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propraetor</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> for, before + <ets>praetor</ets> a
pretor.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A magistrate who, having
been pretor at home, was appointed to the government of a
province.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>propr<ae/tor</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proprietary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri"e*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proprietaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>proprietarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propri<eacute/taire</ets>. See
<er>Propriety</er>, and cf. <er>Proprietor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A proprietor or owner; one who has exclusive title to a thing; one
who possesses, or holds the title to, a thing in his own right.</def>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A body proprietors, taken collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A monk who had reserved goods and
effects to himself, notwithstanding his renunciation of all at the time
of profession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proprietary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri"e*ta*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proprietarius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging, or pertaining, to a
proprietor; considered as property; owned; <as>as, <ex>proprietary</ex>
medicine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proprietary articles</b></col>, <cd>manufactured articles
which some person or persons have exclusive right to make and sell.</cd>
<rj><au>U. S. Statutes.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proprietor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri"e*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For older
<ets>proprietary</ets>: cf. F. <ets>propri<eacute/tarie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>One who has the legal right or exclusive title to anything, whether
in possession or not; an owner; <as>as, the proprietor of farm or of a
mill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proprietorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri`e*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to ownership; proprietary; <as>as, <ex>proprietorial</ex>
rights</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proprietorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri"e*tor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
of being proprietor; ownership.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proprietress</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri"e*tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
proprietor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propriety</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pri"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proprieties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>propri<eacute/t<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>proprietas</ets>, fr.
<ets>proprius</ets> one's own, proper. See <er>Property</er>,
<er>Proper</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Individual right to hold
property; ownership by personal title; property.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Onles this <xex>propriety</xex> be
exiled.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Robynson (More's Utopia).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So are the <qex>proprieties</qex> of a wife to be disposed of by
her lord, and yet all are for her provisions, it being a part of his
need to refresh and supply hers.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is proper or peculiar; an inherent
property or quality; peculiarity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We find no mention hereof in ancient zoographers, . . . who seldom
forget <qex>proprieties</qex> of such a nature.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being proper; suitableness
to an acknowledged or correct standard or rule; consonance with
established principles, rules, or customs; fitness; appropriateness;
<as>as, <ex>propriety</ex> of behavior, language, manners,
etc.</as></def> <ldquo/The rule of <xex>propriety</xex>,<rdquo/
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proproctor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*proc"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>proctor</ets>.]</ety> <mark>[Eng. Univ.]</mark>
<def>A assistant proctor.</def> <rj><au>Hook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Props</ent><br/
<hw>Props</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A game of chance, in
which four sea shells, each called a <xex>prop</xex>, are used instead
of dice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propterygium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prop`te*ryg"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Propterygia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/ a fin.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
anterior of three principal cartilages in the fins of some fishes.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Prop`ter*yg"i*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propugn</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pugn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propugnare</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> for + <ets>pugnare</ets> to
fight.]</ety> <def>To contend for; to defend; to vindicate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propugnacle</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pug"na*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propugnaculum</ets>.]</ety> <def>A fortress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propugnation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`pug*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propugnatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Means of defense; defense.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propugner</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pugn"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A defender; a
vindicator.</def> <ldquo/Zealous <xex>propugners</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gov. of Tongue.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propulsation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`pul*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propulsatio</ets>. See <er>Propulse</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
driving away or repelling; a keeping at a distance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propulse</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pulse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>propulsare</ets>, v. intens. from <ets>propellere</ets> to propel.
See <er>Propel</er>.]</ety> <def>To repel; to drive off or away.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propulsion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pul"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>propulsion</ets>. See <er>Propel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act driving forward or away; the act or process of propelling; <as>as,
steam <ex>propulsion</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An impelling act or movement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God works in all things; all obey<br/
His first <qex>propulsion</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Whittier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propulsive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pul"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending, or
having power, to propel; driving on; urging.</def> <ldquo/[The]
<xex>propulsive</xex> movement of the verse.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propulsory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pul"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Propulsive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Prop</ets>ionic
+ <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The hypothetical
radical <chform>C3H7</chform>, regarded as the essential residue of
propane and related compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propylaeum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prop`y*l<ae/"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Propyl<ae/a</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.,
fr. Gr. <?/; <?/ before + <?/ a gate.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Classical
Arch.)</fld> <def>Any court or vestibule before a building or leading
into any inclosure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propylene</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"pyl*ene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>propyl<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A colorless
gaseous hydrocarbon (<chform>C3H6</chform>) of the ethylene series,
having a garlic odor. It occurs in coal gas, and is produced
artificially in various ways. Called also
<altname>propene</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propylic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pyl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, derived from, or containing, propyl; <as>as,
<ex>propylic</ex> alcohol</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propylidene</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*pyl"i*dene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Propidene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Propylon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prop"y*lon</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Propyla</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/; <?/
before + <?/ a gate.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Arch.)</fld> <def>The porch,
vestibule, or entrance of an edifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pro rata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro` ra"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>In
proportion; proportionately; according to the share, interest, or
liability of each.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proratable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rat"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
prorated, or divided proportionately.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rate"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prorated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prorating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From L. <ets>pro rata</ets>
(sc. <ets>parte</ets>) according to a certain part, in
proportion.]</ety> <def>To divide or distribute proportionally; to
assess <xex>pro rata</xex>.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prore</ent><br/
<hw>Prore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prora</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. It. & Sp. <ets>prora</ets>. See <er>Prow</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>The prow or fore part of a ship.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <ldquo/Galleys with vermilion
<xex>prores</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorector</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rec"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Rector</er>.]</ety> <def>An officer who presides
over the academic senate of a German university.</def>
<rj><au>Heyse.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorectorate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rec"tor*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of
prorector.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorenal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*re"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>renal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Pronephric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proreption</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prorepere</ets>, <ets>proreptum</ets>, to creep forth; <ets>pro +
repere</ets>.]</ety> <def>A creeping on.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorhinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rhi"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>rhinal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated in front of the nasal chambers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorogate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ro*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
prorogue.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorogation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`ro*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prorogatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prorogation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of counting in duration; prolongation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of proroguing; the ending of the session of
Parliament, and postponing of its business, by the command of the
sovereign.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ After an adjournment all things continue as they were at
the adjournment; whereas, after a <xex>prorogation</xex>, bill
introduced and nut passed are as if they had never been begun at
all.</note> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prorogue</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rogue"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prorogued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Proroguing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>proroger</ets>, L. <ets>prorogare</ets>, <ets>prorogatum</ets>;
<ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>rogare</ets> to ask, to ask one for his
opinion or vote, or about a law. See <er>Rogation</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To protract; to prolong; to extend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>prorogued</qex> his government.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To defer; to delay; to postpone; <as>as, to
<ex>prorogue</ex>death; to <ex>prorogue</ex> a marriage.</as></def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To end the session of a parliament by an order of
the sovereign, thus deferring its business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Parliament was <qex>prorogued</qex> to [meet at] Westminster.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Parliament was again <qex>prorogued</qex> to a distant
day.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To adjourn; postpone; defer. See
<er>Adjourn</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proruption</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*rup"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proruptio</ets>, fr. <ets>prorumpere</ets>, <ets>proruptum</ets>,
to break forth; <ets>pro</ets> forth + <ets>rumpere</ets> to
break.]</ety> <def>The act or state of bursting forth; a bursting
out.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosaical</ent><br/
<ent>Prosaic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*sa"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro*sa"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prosaius</ets>, from
<ets>prosa</ets> prose: cf. F,. <ets>prosa<ium/que</ets>. See
<er>Prose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to prose;
resembling prose; in the form of prose; unpoetical; writing or using
prose; <as>as, a <ex>prosaic</ex> composition</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dull; uninteresting; commonplace; unimaginative;
prosy; <as>as, a <ex>prosaic</ex> person</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ed.
Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pro*sa"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pro*sa"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosaicism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sa"i*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being prosaic; a prosaic manner or style.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Poe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosaism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"sa*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is in
the form of prose writing; a prosaic manner.</def>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosaist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"sa*ist</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A writer of
prose; an unpoetical writer.</def> <ldquo/An estimable
<xex>prosaist</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
prose; prosaic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscenium</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sce"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proscenia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/; <?/
before + <?/ a tent, a wooden stage, the stage. See
<er>Scene</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Theater)</fld> <def>The
part where the actors performed; the stage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Modern Theater)</fld> <def>The part of the stage in
front of the curtain; sometimes, the curtain and its
framework.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <col><b>proscenium arch</b></col>, <cd>the framework around the
front of the stage in a theater.</cd> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscolex</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*sco"lex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proscolices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ before
+ <?/, <?/, a worm.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An early larval form
of a trematode worm; a redia. See <er>Redia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscribe</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*scribe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Proscribed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Proscribing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>proscribere</ets>, <ets>proscriptum</ets>, to write before, to
publish, proscribe; <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>scribere</ets> to
write. See <er>Scribe</er>. The sense of this word originated in the
Roman practice of writing the names of persons doomed to death, and
posting the list in public.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To doom to
destruction; to put out of the protection of law; to outlaw; to exile;
<as>as, Sylla and Marius <ex>proscribed</ex> each other's
adherents</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Robert Vere, Earl of Oxford, . . . was banished the realm, and
<qex>proscribed</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To denounce and condemn; to interdict; to prohibit;
<as>as, the Puritans <ex>proscribed</ex> theaters</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Arian doctrines were <qex>proscribed</qex> and anathematized
in the famous Council of Nice.</q> <rj><qau>Waterland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscriber</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*scrib"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, proscribes, denounces, or prohibits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscript</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"script</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Proscribe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A proscription; a
prohibition; an interdict.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who is proscribed.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscription</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*scrip"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proscriptio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proscription</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of proscribing; a dooming to death or exile;
outlawry; specifically, among the ancient Romans, the public offer of a
reward for the head of a political enemy; <as>as, under the triumvirate,
many of the best Roman citizens fell by
<ex>proscription</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every victory by either party had been followed by a sanguinary
<qex>proscription</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being proscribed; denunciation;
interdiction; prohibition.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscriptional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*scrip"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Proscriptive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscriptionist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*scrip"tion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
proscribes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proscriptive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*scrip"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to proscription; consisting in, or of the nature of,
proscription; proscribing.</def> <au>Burke.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro*scrip"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prose</ent><br/
<hw>Prose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>prose</ets>, L.
<ets>prosa</ets>, fr. <ets>prorsus</ets>, <ets>prosus</ets>, straight
forward, straight on, for <ets>proversus</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>versus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>vertere</ets> to turn. See
<er>Verse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The ordinary language of men in
speaking or writing; language not cast in poetical measure or rhythm; --
contradistinguished from <xex>verse</xex>, or <xex>metrical
composition</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I speak in <qex>prose</qex>, and let him rymes make.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Things unattempted yet in <qex>prose</qex> or rhyme.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I wish our clever young poets would remember my homely definitions
of <qex>prose</qex> and poetry, that is; <qex>prose</qex> -- words in
their best order; poetry -- the best order.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, language which evinces little imagination or
animation; dull and commonplace discourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A hymn with no regular meter,
sometimes introduced into the Mass. See <er>Sequence</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prose</ent><br/
<hw>Prose</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or
composed of, prose; not in verse; <as>as, <ex>prose</ex>
composition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Possessing or exhibiting unpoetical characteristics;
plain; dull; prosaic; <as>as, the <ex>prose</ex> duties of
life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prose</ent><br/
<hw>Prose</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prosed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prosing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To write in
prose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To write or repeat in a dull, tedious, or prosy
way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prose</ent><br/
<hw>Prose</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To write
prose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prosing</qex> or versing, but chiefly this latter.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosector</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sec"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., an anatomist,
from <ets>prosecare</ets> to cut up; <ets>pro</ets> before +
<ets>secare</ets> to cut.]</ety> <def>One who makes dissections for
anatomical illustration; usually, the assistant of a professional
anatomist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosecutable</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*cu`ta*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being prosecuted; liable to prosecution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1151 --></p>

<p><ent>Prosecute</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*cute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prosecuted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prosecuting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>prosecutus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>prosequi</ets> to follow, pursue.
See <er>Pursue</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To follow or pursue with a view to reach, execute,
or accomplish; to endeavor to obtain or complete; to carry on; to
continue; <as>as, to <ex>prosecute</ex> a scheme, hope, or
claim</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am beloved Hermia;<br/
Why should not I, then, <qex>prosecute</qex> my right ?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To seek to obtain by legal process; <as>as, to
<ex>prosecute</ex> a right or a claim in a court of law</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To pursue with the intention of
punishing; to accuse of some crime or breach of law, or to pursue for
redress or punishment, before a legal tribunal; to proceed against
judicially; <as>as, to <ex>prosecute</ex> a man for trespass, or for a
riot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To acquit themselves and <qex>prosecute</qex> their foes.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosecute</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*cute</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To follow
after.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To institute and carry on a legal
prosecution; <as>as, to <ex>prosecute</ex> for public
offenses</as>.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosecution</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`e*cu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prosecutio</ets> a following.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
process of prosecuting, or of endeavoring to gain or accomplish
something; pursuit by efforts of body or mind; <as>as, the
<ex>prosecution</ex> of a scheme, plan, design, or undertaking; the
<ex>prosecution</ex> of war.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Keeping a sharp eye on her domestics . . . in
<qex>prosecution</qex> of their various duties.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The institution and
carrying on of a suit in a court of law or equity, to obtain some right,
or to redress and punish some wrong; the carrying on of a judicial
proceeding in behalf of a <xex>complaining</xex> party, as distinguished
from <xex>defense</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The institution, or
commencement, and continuance of a criminal suit; the process of
exhibiting formal charges against an offender before a legal tribunal,
and pursuing them to final judgment on behalf of the state or
government, as by indictment or information.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
party by whom criminal proceedings are instituted.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone. Burrill. Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosecutor</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*cu`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>prosecutor</ets> an attendant.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
prosecutes or carries on any purpose, plan, or business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The person who institutes and
carries on a criminal suit against another in the name of the
government.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosecutrix</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*cu`trix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>A female prosecutor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proselyte</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*lyte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>proselite</ets>, OF. <ets>proselite</ets>, F.
<ets>proselytus</ets>, Gr. <?/, adj., that has come, n., a new comer,
especially, one who has come over from heathenism to the Jewish
religion; <?/ toward, to + (prob.) the root of <?/ to come.]</ety>
<def>A new convert especially a convert to some religion or religious
sect, or to some particular opinion, system, or party; thus, a Gentile
converted to Judaism, or a pagan converted to Christianity, is a
<xex>proselyte</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye [Scribes and Pharisees] compass sea and land to make one
<qex>proselyte</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. xxiii. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fresh confidence the speculatist takes<br/
From every harebrained <qex>proselyte</qex> he makes.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Convert</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proselyte</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*lyte</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Proselyted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Proselyting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To convert to some religion,
opinion, or system; to bring over.</def><-- se proselytize -->
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proselytism</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*ly*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pros<eacute/lytisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
practice of proselyting; the making of converts to a religion or a
religious sect, or to any opinion, system, or party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were possessed of a spirit of <qex>proselytism</qex> in the
most fanatical degree.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Conversion to a religion, system, or
party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proselytize</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*ly*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>proselytized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Proselytizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To convert to some religion, system,
opinion, or the like; to bring, or cause to come, over; to
proselyte.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of those whom they endeavor to <qex>proselytize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proselytize</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*ly*tize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make converts or
proselytes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proselytizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"e*ly*ti`zer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
proselytes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proseman</ent><br/
<hw>Prose"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A writer of
prose.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proseminary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sem"i*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seminary
which prepares pupils for a higher institution.</def> <rj><au>T.
Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosemination</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sem`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proseminare</ets>, <ets>proseminatum</ets>, to disseminate.]</ety>
<def>Propagation by seed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosencephalic</ent><br/
<hw>Pros*en`ce*phal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
prosencephalon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosencephalon</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`en*ceph"a*lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ toward, near to + E. <ets>encephalon</ets>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Sometimes abbreviated to <abbr>proen.</abbr>]</altsp>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The anterior segment of the brain,
including the cerebrum and olfactory lobes; the forebrain.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The cerebrum.</def> <rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosenchyma</ent><br/
<hw>Pros*en"chy*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ near + <ets>-enchyma</ets>, as in <ets>parenchyma</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A general term applied to the tissues formed of
elongated cells, especially those with pointed or oblique extremities,
as the principal cells of ordinary wood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proser</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A writer
of prose.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who talks or writes tediously.</def> <rj><au>Sir
W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosiliency</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sil"i*en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prosilere</ets> to leap forth.]</ety> <def>The act of leaping forth
or forward; projection.</def> <ldquo/Such <xex>prosiliency</xex> of
relief.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosily</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prosy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosimetrical</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`i*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Prose</ets> + <ets>metrical</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting
both of prose and verse.</def> <rj><au>Clarke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosimiae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*sim"i*<ae/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pro-</er>. and <er>Simia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Lemuroidea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being prosy; tediousness; tiresomeness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosing</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Writing prose; speaking or writing
in a tedious or prosy manner.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Prosily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosiphon</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*si"phon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> for + <ets>siphon</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A minute tube found in the protoconch of ammonites, and not
connected with the true siphon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosit</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro"sit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[L., 3d pers.
sing. subj. present of <ets>prodesse</ets> to do good; <ets>pro</ets>
for + <ets>esse</ets> to be.]</ety> <def>Lit., may it do (you) good; --
a salutation used in well wishing, esp. among Germans, as in drinking
healths; -- also used in the contracted form
<altname>prost</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proslavery</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*slav"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>slavery</ets>.]</ety> <def>Favoring
slavery.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>Advocacy of
slavery.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosobranch</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"o*branch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Prosobranchiata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosobranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`o*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ forward, further + <?/ a gill.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The highest division, or subclass, of gastropod
mollusks, including those that have the gills situated anteriorly, or
forward of the heart, and the sexes separate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosocoele</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"o*c<oe/le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
forward + <?/ hollow.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The entire cavity
of the prosencephalon.</def> <rj><au>B. G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosocoelia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`o*c<oe/"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Prosoc<oe/lle</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>,
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Prosoc<oe/le</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosodiacal</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`o*di"a*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Prosodical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosodiacally</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`o*di"a*cal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Prosodically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosodial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*so"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Prosodical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosodian</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*so"di*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
prosodist.</def> <rj><au>Rush.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosodical</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sod"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prosodique</ets>, L. <ets>prosodiacus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to prosody; according to the rules of prosody.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro*sod"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosodist</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"o*dist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in
prosody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosody</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"o*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prosodia</ets> the tone or accent of a syllable, Gr. <?/ a song
sung to, or with, an accompanying song, the accent accompanying the
pronunciation; <?/ to + <?/ song, ode: cf. F. <ets>prosodie</ets>. See
<er>Ode</er>.]</ety> <def>That part of grammar which treats of the
quantity of syllables, of accent, and of the laws of versification or
metrical composition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*so"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prosomata</plw>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/, <?/,
body.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The anterior of the body of an
animal, as of a cephalopod; the thorax of an arthropod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosopalgia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`o*pal"gi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ face + <?/ pain.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Facial
neuralgia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosopocephala</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`o*po*ceph`a*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pro`swpon</grk> face, appearance + <?/
head.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Scaphopoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosopolepsy</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`o*po*lep"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>pro`swpon</grk> a face, a person + <?/ a taking, receiving, <?/ to
take.]</ety> <def>Respect of persons; especially, a premature opinion or
prejudice against a person, formed from his external appearance.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosopopoeia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`o*po*p<oe/"ia</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. Gr. <?/; <grk>pro`swpon</grk> a face, a person + <?/ to make.]</ety>
<fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure by which things are represented as
persons, or by which things inanimate are spoken of as animated beings;
also, a figure by which an absent person is introduced as speaking, or a
deceased person is represented as alive and present. It includes
<xex>personification</xex>, but is more extensive in its
signification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosopulmonata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`o*pul`mo*na"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ forward + L. <ets>pulmo</ets> a lung.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of pulmonate mollusks having the
breathing organ situated on the neck, as in the common snail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospect</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"pect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prospectus</ets>, fr. <ets>prospicere</ets>, <ets>prospectum</ets>,
to look forward; <ets>pro</ets> before, forward + <ets>specere</ets>,
<ets>spicere</ets>, look, to see: cf. OF. <ets>prospect</ets>. See
<er>Spy</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, and cf. <er>Prospectus</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is embraced by eye in vision; the region
which the eye overlooks at one time; view; scene; outlook.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His eye discovers unaware<br/
The goodly <qex>prospect</qex> of some foreign land.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, a picturesque or widely extended view; a
landscape; hence, a sketch of a landscape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I went to Putney . . . to take <qex>prospects</qex> in crayon.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A position affording a fine view; a lookout.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him God beholding from his <qex>prospect</qex> high.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Relative position of the front of a building or
other structure; face; relative aspect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And their <qex>prospect</qex> was toward the south.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xl. 44.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The act of looking forward; foresight; anticipation;
<as>as, a <ex>prospect</ex> of the future state</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is he a prudent man as to his temporal estate, that lays designs
only for a day, without any <qex>prospect</qex> to, or provision for,
the remaining part of life ?</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>That which is hoped for; ground for hope or
expectation; expectation; probable result; <as>as, the <ex>prospect</ex>
of success</as>.</def> <ldquo/To brighter <xex>prospects</xex>
born.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These swell their <qex>prospects</qex>d exalt their pride,<br/
When offers are disdain'd, and love deny'd.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospect</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"pect</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Prospected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prospecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To look over; to explore or
examine for something; <as>as, to <ex>prospect</ex> a district for
gold</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospect</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"pect</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a search; to seek; to
explore, as for mines or the like; <as>as, to <ex>prospect</ex> for
gold</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospection</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
looking forward, or of providing for future wants; foresight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospective</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spec"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prospectivus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prospectif</ets>. See
<er>Prospect</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a prospect; furnishing a prospect; perspective.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Time's long and dark <qex>prospective</qex> glass.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Looking forward in time; acting with foresight; --
opposed to <ant>retrospective</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The French king of Sweden are circumspect, industrious, and
<qex>prospective</qex>, too, in this affair.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Child.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being within view or consideration, as a future
event or contingency; relating to the future: expected; <as>as, a
<ex>prospective</ex> benefit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Points on which the promises, at the time of ordination, had no
<qex>prospective</qex> bearing.</q> <rj><qau>W. Jay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospective</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spec"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
scene before or around, in time or in space; view; prospect.</def>
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A perspective glass.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer. Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospectively</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spec"tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prospective
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospectiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spec"tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
prospective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospectless</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"pect*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
prospect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospector</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"pect*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., one who
looks out.]</ety> <def>One who prospects; especially, one who explores a
region for minerals and precious metals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospectus</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spec"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a prospect,
sight, view: cf. F. <ets>prospectus</ets>. See <er>Prospect</er>.]</ety>
<def>A summary, plan, or scheme of something proposed, affording a
prospect of its nature; especially, an exposition of the scheme of an
unpublished literary work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosper</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prospered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prospering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>prosp<eacute/rer</ets> v. i., or L. <ets>prosperare</ets>, v. i.,
or L. <ets>prosperare</ets>, v. t., fr. <ets>prosper or prosperus</ets>.
See <er>Prosperous</er>.]</ety> <def>To favor; to render
successful.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Prosper</xex> thou our handiwork.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bk. of Common Prayer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All things concur to<qex>prosper</qex> our design.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosper</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"per</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be successful;
to succeed; to be fortunate or prosperous; to thrive; to make
gain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They, in their earthly Canaan placed,<br/
Long time shall dwell and <qex>prosper</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To grow; to increase.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Black cherry trees <qex>prosper</qex> even to considerable
timber.</q> <rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosperity</ent><br/
<hw>Pros*per"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prosp<eacute/rit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>prosperitas</ets>. See
<er>Prosperous</er>.]</ety> <def>The state of being prosperous; advance
or gain in anything good or desirable; successful progress in any
business or enterprise; attainment of the object desired; good fortune;
success; <as>as, commercial <ex>prosperity</ex>; national
<ex>prosperity</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>prosperity</qex> begins to mellow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prosperities</qex> can only be enjoyed by them who fear not
at all to lose them.</q> <rj><qau> Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Prosperousness; thrift; weal; welfare; well
being; happiness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosperous</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"per*ous</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prosperus</ets> or <ets>prosper</ets>, originally, answering to
hope; <ets>pro</ets> according to + the root of <ets>sperare</ets> to
hope. See <er>Despair</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Tending to
prosperity; favoring; favorable; helpful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A happy passage and a <qex>prosperous</qex> wind.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Being prospered; advancing in the pursuit of
anything desirable; making gain, or increase; thriving; successful;
<as>as, a <ex>prosperous</ex> voyage; a <ex>prosperous</ex> undertaking;
a <ex>prosperous</ex> man or nation.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By moderation either state to bear<br/
<qex>Prosperous</qex> or adverse.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fortunate; successful; flourishing; thriving;
favorable; auspicious; lucky. See <er>Fortunate</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pros"per*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pros"per*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosphysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros"phy*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/; <?/ to + <?/ to grow.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A growing
together of parts; specifically, a morbid adhesion of the eyelids to
each other or to the eyeball.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prospicience</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*spi"cience</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prospicientia</ets>, fr. <ets>prospiciens</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>prospicere</ets>. See <er>Prospect</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
looking forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prost</ent><br/
<hw>Prost</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[L., see
<er>prosit</er>.]</ety> <def>a contracted form of
<er>prosit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostate</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ standing
before, fr. <?/ to set before; <?/ before + <?/ to set: cf. F.
<ets>prostate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Standing before; --
applied to a gland which is found in the males of most mammals, and is
situated at the neck of the bladder where this joins the urethra.</def>
-- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The prostate gland.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*stat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the prostate gland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prostatic catheter</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Catheter</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostatitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros`ta*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Prostate</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the prostate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosternation</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`ter*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Prostration</er>.]</ety> <def>Dejection; depression.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosternum</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ster"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pro-</er> and <er>Sternum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
ventral plate of the prothorax of an insect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosthesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pros"the*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/ an addition, fr. <?/ to put to, to add; <?/ to + <?/ to put,
place.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The addition to the
human body of some artificial part, to replace one that is wanting, as a
log or an eye; -- called also <altname>prothesis</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The prefixing of one or more
letters to the beginning of a word, as in <xex>be</xex>loved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosthetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pros*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/
disposed to add, <?/ put on.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to prosthesis;
prefixed, as a letter or letters to a word.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostibulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pros*tib"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostibulum</ets> prostitute.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
prostitutes or prostitution; meretricious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostitute</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"ti*tute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prostituted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prostituting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostitutus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>prostituere</ets> to prostitute;
<ets>pro</ets> before, forth + <ets>statuere</ets> to put, place. See
<er>Statute</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To offer, as a woman, to a
lewd use; to give up to lewdness for hire.</def> <ldquo/Do not
<xex>prostitute</xex> thy daughter.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lev. xix. 29.
</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To devote to base or unworthy purposes; to give up
to low or indiscriminate use; <as>as, to <ex>prostitute</ex> talents; to
<ex>prostitute</ex> official powers.</as></def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostitute</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"ti*tute</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>prostitutus</ets>, p.
p.]</ety> <def>Openly given up to lewdness; devoted to base or infamous
purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Made bold by want, and <qex>prostitute</qex> for bread.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostitute</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"ti*tute</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostituta</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A woman giver to
indiscriminate lewdness; a strumpet; a harlot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A base hireling; a mercenary; one who offers himself
to infamous employments for hire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No hireling she, no <qex>prostitute</qex> to praise.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostitution</ent><br/
<hw>Pros`ti*tu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostitutio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prostitution</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or practice of prostituting or offering the
body to an indiscriminate intercourse with men; common lewdness of a
woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of setting one's self to sale, or of
devoting to infamous purposes what is in one's power; <as>as, the
<ex>prostitution</ex> of abilities; the <ex>prostitution</ex> of the
press.</as></def> <ldquo/Mental <xex>prostitution</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1152 --></p>

<p><ent>Prostitutor</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"ti*tu`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who prostitutes; one who submits himself, of or offers another,
to vile purposes.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hurd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostomium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*sto"mi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Prostomia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/, <?/, mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>That portion of the head of an annelid situated in front of the
mouth.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pro*sto"mi*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>prosternere</ets> to prostrate;
<ets>pro</ets> before, forward + <ets>sternere</ets> to spread out,
throw down. See <er>Stratum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lying at
length, or with the body extended on the ground or other surface;
stretched out; <as>as, to sleep <ex>prostrate</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Elyot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Groveling and <qex>prostrate</qex> on yon lake of fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lying at mercy, as a supplicant.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Lying in a humble, lowly, or suppliant
posture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prostrate fall<br/
Before him reverent, and there confess<br/
Humbly our faults.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Trailing on the ground;
procumbent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Prostrated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Prostrating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay
fiat; to throw down; to level; to fell; <as>as, to <ex>prostrate</ex>
the body; to <ex>prostrate</ex> trees or plants.</as></def>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to overthrow; to demolish; to destroy; to deprive of
efficiency; to ruin; <as>as, to <ex>prostrate</ex> a village; to
<ex>prostrate</ex> a government; to <ex>prostrate</ex> law or
justice.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To throw down, or cause to fall in humility or
adoration; to cause to bow in humble reverence; used reflexively;
<as>as, he <ex>prostrated</ex> himself</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to sink totally; to deprive of strength; to
reduce; <as>as, a person <ex>prostrated</ex> by fever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostration</ent><br/
<hw>Pros*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostratio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prostration</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of prostrating, throwing down, or laying fiat; <as>as, the
<ex>prostration</ex> of the body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of falling down, or of bowing in humility or
adoration; primarily, the act of falling on the face, but usually
applied to kneeling or bowing in reverence and worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A greater <qex>prostration</qex> of reason than of body.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The condition of being prostrate; great depression;
lowness; dejection; <as>as, a <ex>postration</ex> of spirits</as>.</def>
<ldquo/A sudden <xex>prostration</xex> of strength.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A latent, not an exhausted, state
of the vital energies; great oppression of natural strength and
vigor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Prostration</xex>, in its medical use, is analogous
to the state of a spring lying under such a weight that it is incapable
of action; while <xex>exhaustion</xex> is analogous to the state of a
spring deprived of its elastic powers. The word, however, is often used
to denote any great depression of the vital powers.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prostyle</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prostylus</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ before + <?/ pillar, column: cf. F.
<ets>prostyle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Having columns in
front.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A prostyle portico or
building.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosy</ent><br/
<hw>Pros"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Prosier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Prosiest</adjf>.]</amorph><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to prose; like prose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dull and tedious in discourse or writing;
prosaic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prosylogism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*sy"lo*gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>syllogism</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A
syllogism preliminary or logically essential to another syllogism; the
conclusion of such a syllogism, which becomes a premise of the following
syllogism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protactic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tac"tic</hw> <pr>(pr<osl/*t<acr/k"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>protaktiko`s</grk> placing or placed before, fr.
<grk>prota`ssein</grk> to place in front; <grk>pro`</grk> before +
<grk>ta`ssein</grk> to arrange.]</ety> <def>Giving a previous narrative
or explanation, as of the plot or personages of a play;
introductory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protactinium</ent><br/
<hw>Protactinium</hw>. <def>The radioactive chemical element, of atomic
number 91; Atomic symbol Pa; at. wt. of longest-lived isotope, 231
(T<subs><frac12/</subs> = 32,500 yrs.) Also called brevium, Uranium X2
and UX2.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protagon</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ta*gon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>'agw`n</grk> a contest. See. <er>Protagonist</er>. So called
because it was the first definitely ascertained principle of the
brain.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A nitrogenous
phosphorized principle found in brain tissue. By decomposition it yields
neurine, fatty acids, and other bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protagonist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tag"o*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>prwtagonisth`s</grk>; <grk>prw^tos</grk> first +
<grk>'agonisth`s</grk> an actor, combatant, fr. <grk>'agw`n</grk> a
contest.]</ety> <def>One who takes the leading part in a drama; hence,
one who takes lead in some great scene, enterprise, conflict, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shakespeare, the <qex>protagonist</qex> on the great of modern
poetry.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protamin</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"ta*min</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>prw^tos</grk> first.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>An
amorphous nitrogenous substance found in the spermatic fluid of salmon.
It is soluble in water, which an alkaline reaction, and unites with
acids and metallic bases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protandric</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tan"dric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, a man.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having male sexual organs while young, and female organs later in
life.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pro*tan"trism</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protandrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tan"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Proterandrous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Prot"a*sis</hw> <pr>(pr<ocr/t"<adot/*s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>pro`tasis</grk>, fr.
<grk>protei`nein</grk> to stretch before, forward; <grk>pro`</grk>
before + <grk>tei`nein</grk> to stretch.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
proposition; a maxim.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The introductory or subordinate
member of a sentence, generally of a conditional sentence; -- opposed to
<contr>apodosis</contr>. See <er>Apodosis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The first part of a drama, of a poem, or the like;
the introduction; opposed to <contr>epitasis</contr>.</def> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>protatiko`s</grk>: cf. L. <ets>protaticus</ets>, F.
<ets>protatique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the protasis of
an ancient play; introductory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`te*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Proteus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<ord>Proteace<ae/</ord>, an order of apetalous evergreen shrubs, mostly
natives of the Cape of Good Hope or of Australia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protean</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to Proteus; characteristic of Proteus.</def> <ldquo/
<xex>Protean</xex> transformations.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exceedingly variable; readily assuming different
shapes or forms; <as>as, an am<oe/ba is a <ex>protean</ex>
animalcule</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. displaying great variety or versatility. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteanly</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*an*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a protean manner.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protect</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Protected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Protecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>protectus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>protegere</ets>, literally, to cover in front; <ets>pro</ets>
before + <ets>tegere</ets> to cover. See <er>Tegument</er>.]</ety>
<def>To cover or shield from danger or injury; to defend; to guard; to
preserve in safety; <as>as, a father <ex>protects</ex> his
children</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gods of Greece <qex>protect</qex> you!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To guard; shield; preserve. See
<er>Defend</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way of
protection; in a protective manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protection</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protectio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>protection</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of protecting, or the state of being protected;
preservation from loss, injury, or annoyance; defense; shelter; <as>as,
the weak need <ex>protection</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To your <qex>protection</qex> I commend me, gods.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which protects or preserves from injury; a
defense; a shield; a refuge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let them rise up . . . and be your <qex>protection</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. xxxii. 38.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A writing that protects or secures from molestation
or arrest; a pass; a safe-conduct; a passport.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . gave them <qex>protections</qex> under his hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Polit. Econ.)</fld> <def>A theory, or a policy, of
protecting the producers in a country from foreign competition in the
home market by the imposition of such discriminating duties on goods of
foreign production as will restrict or prevent their importation; --
opposed to <ant>free trade</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Writ of protection</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A writ by which the king formerly exempted a person
from arrest; -- now disused</cd>. <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<au>Blackstone.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A judicial writ issued to a person
required to attend court, as party, juror, etc., intended to secure him
from arrest in coming, staying, and returning.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Preservation; defense; guard; shelter; refuge;
security; safety.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectionism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tec"tion*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Polit.
Econ.)</fld> <def>The doctrine or policy of protectionists. See
<er>Protection</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectionist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tec"tion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Polit. Econ.)</fld>
<def>One who favors protection. See <er>Protection</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protective</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>protectif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Affording protection; sheltering;
defensive.</def> <ldquo/ The favor of a <xex>protective</xex>
Providence.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Protective coloring</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>coloring which serves for the concealment and preservation of a
living organism. Cf. <er>Mimicry</er>.</cd> <au>Wallace.</au> --
<col><b>Protective tariff</b></col> <fld>(Polit. Econ.)</fld>, <cd>a
tariff designed to secure protection (see <er>Protection</er>, 4.), as
distinguished from a tariff designed to raise revenue. See
<er>Tariff</er>, and <er>Protection</er>, 4.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"ive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being protective.</def> <rj><au>W. Pater.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protector</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.: cf. F.
<ets>protecteur</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which,
defends or shields from injury, evil, oppression, etc.; a defender; a
guardian; a patron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the world's <qex>protector</qex> shall be known.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld> <def>One having the care of the
kingdom during the king's minority; a regent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is it concluded he shall be <qex>protector</qex> !</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A cardinal, from one of the
more considerable Roman Catholic nations, who looks after the interests
of his people at Rome; also, a cardinal who has the same relation to a
college, religious order, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Lord Protector</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld>, <cd>the
title of Oliver Cromwell as supreme governor of the British Commonwealth
(1653-1658).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectoral</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"or*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a protector; protectorial; <as>as, <ex>protectoral</ex>
power</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectorate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"or*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>protectorat</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Government by a
protector; -- applied especially to the government of England by Oliver
Cromwell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The authority assumed by a superior power over an
inferior or a dependent one, whereby the former protects the latter from
invasion and shares in the management of its affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectorial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`tec*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>protectorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Protectoral</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectorless</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"or*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
protector; unprotected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tect"or*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a protector
or regent; protectorate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protectrix</ent><br/
<ent>Protectress</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*tect"ress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro*tect"rix</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>protectrix</ets>.]</ety> <def>A woman who protects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteg<eacute/e</ent><br/
<ent>Protege</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Pro`t<eacute/`g<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.
m.</pos> \'d8<hw>Pro`t<eacute/`g<eacute/e"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.
f.</pos>}</mhw> <ety>[F., p. p. of <ets>prot<eacute/ger</ets>. See
<er>Protect</er>.]</ety> <def>One under the care and protection of
another, especially one receiving counseling and assistance in career
development.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteid</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>prw^tos</grk> first.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>An
older, imprecise term replaced by
<altname><er>protein</er></altname>.</def><br/
<note> <ex>Proteid</ex> was defined in the 1913 Webster as <ldquo/One of
a class of amorphous nitrogenous principles, containing, as a rule, a
small amount of sulphur; an albuminoid, as blood fibrin, casein of milk,
etc. Proteids are present in nearly all animal fluids and make up the
greater part of animal tissues and organs. They are also important
constituents of vegetable tissues. See 2d Note under
<er>Food</er>.<rdquo/</note> -- <wordforms><wf>Pro"te*id</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> protein.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Defensive proteid</b></col> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>one of a class of proteid substances, present in some animal tissues
and fluids, that make the body immune to certain infectious diseases by
destroying or rendering inactive the toxic products of bacterial growth;
-- this is an older term replaced by more precise modern immunological
concepts such as <er>antibody</er> and <er>immunoglobulin</er>.</cd>
<mark>[archaic]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protein</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*in</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>In
chemical analysis, the total nitrogenous material in vegetable or animal
substances, obtained by multiplying the total nitrogen found by a
factor, usually 6.25, assuming most proteids to contain approximately 16
per cent of nitrogen.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`te*id"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Proteus</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
order of aquatic amphibians having prominent external gills and four
legs. It includes Proteus and Menobranchus (<gen>Necturus</gen>). Called
also <altname>Proteoidea</altname>, and
<altname>Proteida</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteiform</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*te"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Changeable in form; resembling a Proteus, or an am<oe/ba.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protein</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>prw^tos</grk> first: cf. <grk>prwtei^on</grk> the first
place.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>any polymer of an amino
acid joined by peptide (amide) bonds. Most natural proteins have
alpha-amino acids as the monomeric constituents. All classical enzymes
are composed of protein, and control most of the biochemical
transformations carried out in living cells. They may be soluble, as
casein, albumins, and other globular proteins, or insoluble (e. g.
"structural proteins"), as collagen or keratin. "albumin", an older term
for protein, is now used primarily to refer to certain specific soluble
globular proteins found in eggs or blood serum, e.g. <stype>bovine serum
albumin</stype>, the main soluble protein in the serum of cattle, used
as an enzymatically inert protein in biochemical research.</def>
<note>In the 1913 dictionary, <ex>protein</ex> was defined as: <ldquo/A
body now known as alkali albumin, but originally considered to be the
basis of all albuminous substances, whence its name.<rdquo/</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Protein crystal</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Crystalloid</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteinaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`te*i*na"*ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or related to protein; albuminous;
proteid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteinous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*te"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Proteinaceuos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteles</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*les</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A South Africa genus of Carnivora, allied to the
hyenas, but smaller and having weaker jaws and teeth. It includes the
aard-wolf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protend</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Protended</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Protending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>protendere</ets>,
<ets>protensum</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before, forth + <ets>tendere</ets>
to stretch.]</ety> <def>To hold out; to stretch forth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With his <qex>protended</qex> lance he makes defence.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protense</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tense"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Protend</er>.]</ety> <def>Extension.</def><mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/ By due degrees and long <xex>protense</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protension</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ten"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protensio</ets>.]</ety> <def>A drawing out; extension.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protensive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ten"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drawn out;
extended.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Time is a <qex>protensive</qex> quantity.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteolysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`te*ol"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Proteolytic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>The
digestion or dissolving of proteid matter by proteolytic
ferments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteolytic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`te*o*lyt"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proteid</ets> + Gr. <?/ to loose.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Converting proteid or albuminous matter into
soluble and diffusible products, as peptones.</def> <ldquo/ The
<xex>proteolytic</xex> ferment of the pancreas.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Foster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteose</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*ose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Prote</ets>id + <ets>-ose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>One of a class of soluble products formed in the
digestion of proteids with gastric and pancreatic juice, and also by the
hydrolytic action of boiling dilute acids on proteids. Proteoses are
divided into the two groups, the <xex>primary</xex> and
<xex>secondary</xex> proteoses.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proterandrous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`ter*an"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
earlier (fr. <?/ before) + <?/, <?/, man, male.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the stamens come to maturity before the pistil; -- opposed
to <contr>proterogynous</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proterandry</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`ter*an"dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The condition of being proterandrous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteranthous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`ter*an"thous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
earlier (fr. <?/ before) + <?/ flower.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having flowers appearing before the leaves; -- said of certain
plants.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteroglypha</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`te*rog"ly*pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/ to carve.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
suborder of serpents including those that have permanently erect grooved
poison fangs, with ordinary teeth behind them in the jaws. It includes
the cobras, the asps, and the sea snakes. Called also
<altname>Proteroglyphia</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proterogynous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`ter*og"y*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
earlier (fr. <?/ before) + <?/ woman, female.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the pistil come to maturity before the stamens; protogynous;
-- opposed to <contr>proterandrous</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proterosaurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`te*ro*sau"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/ earlier (fr. <?/ before) + <?/ a lizard.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct genus of reptiles of the Permian
period. Called also <gen>Protosaurus</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protervity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ter"vi*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protervitas</ets>, from <ets>protervus</ets> violent.]</ety>
<def>Peevishness; petulance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protest</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*test"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Protested</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Protesting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>protester</ets>, L.
<ets>protestari</ets>, <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>testari</ets> to be
a witness, <ets>testis</ets> a witness. See <er>Testify</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To affirm in a public or formal manner; to bear
witness; to declare solemnly; to avow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>protest</qex> that his measures are pacific.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lady doth <qex>protest</qex> too much, methinks.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a solemn declaration (often a written one)
expressive of opposition; -- with <xex>against</xex>; <as>as, he
<ex>protest</ex> against your votes</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Denham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The conscience has power . . . to <qex>protest</qex> againts the
exorbitancies of the passions.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To affirm; asseverate; assert; aver; attest;
testify; declare; profess. See <er>Affirm</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protest</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*test"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make a solemn
declaration or affirmation of; to proclaim; to display; <as>as, to
<ex>protest</ex> one's loyalty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>protest</qex> your cowardice.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To call as a witness in affirming or denying, or to
prove an affirmation; to appeal to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fiercely [they] opposed<br/
My journey strange, with clamorous uproar<br/
<qex>Protesting</qex> fate supreme.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To protest a bill</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To
protest a note</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to make a solemn
written declaration, in due form, on behalf of the holder, against all
parties liable for any loss or damage to be sustained by the
nonacceptance or the nonpayment of the bill or note, as the case may be.
This should be made by a notary public, whose seal it is the usual
practice to affix.</cd> <rj><au>Kent.</au> <au>Story.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protest</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"test</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prot<ecir/t</ets>, It. <ets>protesto</ets>. See <er>Protest</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A solemn declaration of opinion,
commonly a formal objection against some act; especially, a formal and
solemn declaration, in writing, of dissent from the proceedings of a
legislative body; <as>as, the <ex>protest</ex> of lords in
Parliament</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A solemn declaration
in writing, in due form, made by a notary public, usually under his
notarial seal, on behalf of the holder of a bill or note, protesting
against all parties liable for any loss or damage by the nonacceptance
or nonpayment of the bill, or by the nonpayment of the note, as the case
may be.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A declaration made by the master of a
vessel before a notary, consul, or other authorized officer, upon his
arrival in port after a disaster, stating the particulars of it, and
showing that any damage or loss sustained was not owing to the fault of
the vessel, her officers or crew, but to the perils of the sea, etc.,
ads the case may be, and protesting against them.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>A declaration made by a party, before or while paying a tax, duty,
or the like, demanded of him, which he deems illegal, denying the
justice of the demand, and asserting his rights and claims, in order to
show that the payment was not voluntary.</def> <au>Story.</au>
<au>Kent.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestancy</ent><br/
<hw>Prot"es*tan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Protestantism.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestant</ent><br/
<hw>Prot"es*tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>protestant</ets>, fr. L. <ets>protestans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>,
p. pr. of <ets>protestare</ets>. See <er>Protest</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>One who protests; -- originally applied to
those who adhered to Luther, and protested against, or made a solemn
declaration of dissent from, a decree of the Emperor Charles V. and the
Diet of Spires, in 1529, against the Reformers, and appealed to a
general council; -- now used in a popular sense to designate any
Christian who does not belong to the Roman Catholic or the Greek
Church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1153 --></p>

<p><ent>Protestant</ent><br/
<hw>Prot"es*tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>protestant</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Making a protest;
protesting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the faith and practice of those
Christians who reject the authority of the Roman Catholic Church;
<as>as, <ex>Protestant</ex> writers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestantical</ent><br/
<hw>Prot`es*tant"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Protestant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestantism</ent><br/
<hw>Prot"es*tant*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>protestantisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
protestant, especially against the Roman Catholic Church; the principles
or religion of the Protestants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestantly</ent><br/
<hw>Prot"es*tant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Like a Protestant; in
conformity with Protestantism.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestation</ent><br/
<hw>Prot`es*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protestatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>protestation</ets>. See
<er>Protest</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of making a protest; a
public avowal; a solemn declaration, especially of dissent.</def>
<ldquo/ The <xex>protestation</xex> of our faith.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Formerly, a declaration in
common-law pleading, by which the party interposes an oblique allegation
or denial of some fact, protesting that it does or does not exist, and
at the same time avoiding a direct affirmation or denial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestator</ent><br/
<hw>Prot"es*ta`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>protestateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who makes protestation; a
protester.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protester</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*test"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who protests; one who utters a solemn declaration.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who protests a bill of
exchange, or note.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protestingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*test"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way of
protesting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proteus</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"te*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[L., Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>A sea god in the
service of Neptune who assumed different shapes at will. Hence, one who
easily changes his appearance or principles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of aquatic eel-shaped
amphibians of the family <fam>Proteidae</fam> of the order
<ord>Urodela</ord>, found in caves in the karst regions near the
Adriatic from Trieste to Montenegro (including Slovenia, Croatia, and
Herzegovina); also called the <altname>Olm</altname>, <altname>White
Salamander</altname>, and <altname>Human fish</altname>; it is a true
<isa>troglobiont</isa> (cave animal). They have permanent external gills
as well as lungs. The vestigial eyes are small and can only perceive
light and dark; the legs are weak. Some were reported in Germany and
France, apparently due to human intervention. It was known to Charles
Darwin who wrote about cave animals in <book>The Origin of
Species</book>, chapter 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The unusual Olm (<spn>Proteus anguinus</spn>, aka Cave Salamander,
although no relation to the <gen>Hydromantes</gen> spp) is the only
European member of the <fam>Proteidae</fam> family, the rest occurring
in America. This species was only discovered in 1875 and even today is
only known in about fifty caves in the limestone mountains of the
region, plus one isolated location in Italy. Olms are characterised by
an elongated body, white unpigmented skin, three pairs of external gills
and vestigial, skin-covered eyes which can only perceive light and
shadow. The Olm hunts aquatic crustaceans such as water fleas mainly by
sensory organs in the skin. If washed out of their caves by heavy
rainfall, olms will collect in deep pools, but they will not voluntarily
leave the water. At the same time they have lungs and drown if they
cannot surface at some point for air. The optimum water temperature for
this species is 5-10 C. Females normally give birth to two larvae, but
curiously enough if the water is warm enough (about 15 deg C) they can
lay up to 80 eggs instead. A lot is still undiscovered about the lives
of these mysterious creatures.</q>
<au>https://web.archive.org/web/20031003060620/http://www.nafcon.dircon.co.uk/euro_urodela.html</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A changeable protozoan; an amoeba.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A genus of gram-negative rod-shaped bacteria,
including some species pathogenic in man.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothalamium</ent><br/
<ent>Prothalamion</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro`tha*la"mi*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro`tha*la"mi*um</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ before + <?/
chamber, especially, the bridal chamber.]</ety> <def>A song in
celebration of a marriage.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothallium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*thal"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Prothallia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Prothallus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothallus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*thal"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prothalli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ before +
<?/ a young shoot.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The minute primary
growth from the spore of ferns and other <xex>Pteridophyta</xex>, which
bears the true sexual organs; the oophoric generation of ferns,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Proth"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a placing in public, fr. <?/ to set before; <?/ before + <?/ to set,
put.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A credence table; -- so
called by the Eastern or Greek Church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See <er>Prosthesis</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to prothesis; <as>as, a <ex>prothetic</ex>
apparatus</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protonotary</ent><br/
<ent>Prothonotary</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pro*thon"o*ta*ry</hw>
<pr>(pr<osl/*th<ocr/n"<osl/*t<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Pro*ton"o*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(pr<osl/*t<ocr/n"<osl/*t<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-ries</plw>
<pr>(pr<osl/*th<ocr/n"<osl/*t<asl/*r<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL.
<ets>protonotarius</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>prw^tos</grk> first + L.
<ets>notarius</ets> a shorthand writer, a scribe: cf. F.
<ets>protonotaire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A chief notary or
clerk.</def> <ldquo/ My private <xex>prothonotary</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Herrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Formerly, a chief clerk in the Court of King's Bench
and in the Court of Common Pleas, now superseded by the
<xex>master</xex>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Wharton.
Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A register or chief clerk of a court in certain
States of the United States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>Formerly, one who had the
charge of writing the acts of the martyrs, and the circumstances of
their death; now, one of twelve persons, constituting a college in the
Roman Curia, whose office is to register pontifical acts and to make and
preserve the official record of beatifications.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Ch.)</fld> <def>The chief secretary of the
patriarch of Constantinople.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prothonotary warbler</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
small American warbler (<spn>Protonotaria citrea</spn>). The general
color is golden yellow, the back is olivaceous, the rump and tail are
ash-color, several outer tail feathers are partly white.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothonotaryship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*thon"o*ta*ry*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Office of a
prothonotary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothoracic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`tho*rac"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the prothorax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothorax</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tho"rax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>pro-</ets> + <ets>thorax</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
first or anterior segment of the thorax in insects. See
<xex>Illusts</xex>. of <er>Butterfly</er> and
<er>Coleoptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pro thyalosoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro* thy`a*lo*so"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Prothyalosomata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>prw^tos</grk> first + <grk>"y`alos</grk> glass +
<?/, <?/, body.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The investing portion, or
spherical envelope, surrounding the eccentric germinal spot of the
germinal vesicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prothyalosome</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*thy"a*lo*some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Prothyalosoma</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Protista.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protista</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*tis"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>prw`tistos</grk> first.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
provisional group in which are placed a number of low microscopic
organisms of doubtful nature. Some are probably plants, others
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protiston</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*tis"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Protista</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Protista.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proto-</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>prw^tos</grk> first, a
superl. fr. <grk>pro`</grk> before. See <er>Pro-</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A combining form prefix signifying <xex>first</xex>,
<xex>primary</xex>, <xex>primordial</xex>; <as>as, <ex>proto</ex>martyr,
the first martyr; <ex>proto</ex>morphic, primitive in form;
<ex>proto</ex>plast, a primordial organism; <ex>proto</ex>type,
<ex>proto</ex>zoan.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Denoting the
<xex>first</xex> or <xex>lowest of a series</xex>, or the one having the
<xex>smallest amount</xex> of the element to the name of which it is
prefixed; as <xex>prot</xex>oxide, <xex>proto</xex>chloride, etc.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Sometimes used as equivalent to <xex>mono-</xex>, as
indicating that the compound has but <xex>one atom</xex> of the element
to the name of which it is prefixed. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocanonical</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*ca*non"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the first canon, or that which contains the authorized
collection of the books of Scripture; -- opposed to
<contr>deutero-canonical</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocatechuic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*cat`e*chu"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, an
organic acid which is obtained as a white crystalline substance from
catechin, asafetida, oil of cloves, etc., and by distillation itself
yields pyrocatechin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocercal</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*cer"cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + Gr. <?/ the tail.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a caudal fin extending around the end of the vertebral
column, like that which is first formed in the embryo of fishes;
diphycercal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protococcus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*coc"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Proto-</er>, and <er>Coccus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of minute unicellular alg<ae/ including the red snow plant
(<spn>Protococcus nivalis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocol</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*col</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>protocole</ets>, LL. <ets>protocollum</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ the first
leaf glued to the rolls of papyrus and the notarial documents, on which
the date was written; <grk>prw^tos</grk> the first (see <er>Proto-</er>)
+ <?/ glue.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The original copy of any writing, as
of a deed, treaty, dispatch, or other instrument.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The minutes, or rough draught, of an instrument or
transaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Diplomacy)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A preliminary
document upon the basis of which negotiations are carried on.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A convention not formally ratified.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>An agreement of diplomatists indicating the results reached by them
at a particular stage of a negotiation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 4. A strict code of etiquette for conduct of behavior among
diplomatic or military personnel. 5. A detailed plan for conduct of a
scientific or medical experiment or procedure. A term used especially in
conduct of medical research requiring approval of a regulatory agency.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocol</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*col</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make a protocol
of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocol</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*col</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make or write protocols,
or first draughts; to issue protocols.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protocolist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*col`ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who draughts
protocols.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoconch</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*conch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>conch</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The embryonic shell, or first chamber, of ammonites and other
cephalopods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proto-Doric</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to-Dor*ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>Doric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, architecture, in which the
beginnings of the Doric style are supposed to be found.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protogine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*gine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets>
+ root of Gr. <grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born: cf. F.
<ets>protogyne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of granite or
gneiss containing a silvery talcose mineral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protogynous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tog"y*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto</ets> + Gr. <grk>gynh`</grk> a woman.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Proterogynous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protohippus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*hip"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ first + <?/ horse.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of
fossil horses from the Lower Pliocene. They had three toes on each foot,
the lateral ones being small.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protomartyr</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*mar`tyr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., fr. Gr.
<?/; <?/ first + <?/ martyr: cf. F. <ets>protomartyr</ets>. See
<er>Proto-</er>, and <er>Martyr</er>.]</ety> <def>The first martyr; the
first who suffers, or is sacrificed, in any cause; -- applied esp. to
Stephen, the first Christian martyr.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protomerite</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*mer"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>-mere</ets> + <ets>-ite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The second segment of one of the
Gregarin<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protometals</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*met"als</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A finer
form of metals, indicated by enhanced lines in their spark spectra
(which are also observed in the spectra of some stars), obtained at the
highest available laboratory temperatures (<au>Lockyer</au>); as
protocalcium, protochromium, protocopper, protonickel, protosilicon,
protostrontium, prototitanium, protovanadium.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro`to*me*tal"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protomorphic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*mor"phic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + Gr. <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having the most primitive character; in the
earliest form; <as>as, a <ex>protomorphic</ex> layer of
tissue</as>.</def> <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protonema</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*ne"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Protonemata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ first
+ <?/, <?/, a thread.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The primary growth
from the spore of a moss, usually consisting of branching confervoid
filaments, on any part of which stem and leaf buds may be
developed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protonotary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ton"o*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Prothonotary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoorganism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*<oum/r"gan*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>organism</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>An organism whose nature is so difficult to determine that it might
be referred to either the animal or the vegetable kingdom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protopapas</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*pap"as</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ a chief priest.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Ch.)</fld> <def>A
protopope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protophyte</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*phyte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any unicellular plant, or plant forming only a plasmodium, having
reproduction only by fission, gemmation, or cell division.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The protophytes (<class>Protophyta</class>) are by some
botanists considered an independent branch or class of the vegetable
kingdom, and made to include the lowest forms of both fungi and alg<ae/,
as slime molds, Bacteria, the nostocs, etc. Cf. <er>Carpophyte</er>, and
<er>Oophyte</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protophytology</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*phy*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>phytology</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Paleobotany.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protopine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets>
+ <ets>opium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An alkaloid found in
opium in small quantities, and extracted as a white crystalline
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoplasm</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*plasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + Gr. <?/ form, fr. <?/ to mold.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The viscid and more or less granular material of
vegetable and animal cells, possessed of vital properties by which the
processes of nutrition, secretion, and growth go forward; the so-called
<ldquo/ physical basis of life;<rdquo/ the original cell substance,
cytoplasm, cytoblastema, bioplasm sarcode, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The lowest forms of animal and vegetable life
(unicellular organisms) consist of <xex>simple</xex> or <xex>unaltered
protoplasm</xex>; the tissues of the higher organisms, of
<xex>differentiated protoplasm</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoplasmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*plas*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Protoplasmic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoplasmic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*plas"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to the first formation of living bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to protoplasm;
consisting of, or resembling, protoplasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoplast</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*plast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protoplastus</ets> the first man, Gr. <?/ formed or created first;
<?/ first + <?/ formed, fr. <?/ to form.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The thing first formed; that of which there are
subsequent copies or reproductions; the original.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A first-formed organized body;
the first individual, or pair of individuals, of a species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A species is a class of individuals, each of which is
hypothetically considered to be the descendant of the same
<qex>protoplast</qex>, or of the same pair of
<qex>protoplasts</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Latham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. a plant or bacterial cell which has lost its cell wall. As a
consequence, protoplasts typically assume a spherical shape, and are
unable to resist rupture in a liquid of low osmolarity; but they may
live and in some cases divide, provided that the osmotic pressure of the
medium is sufficient to prevent expansion to the point of rupture.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoplasta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*plas"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of fresh-water
rhizopods including those that have a soft body and delicate branched
pseudopodia. The genus <gen>Gromia</gen> is one of the
best-known.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoplastic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*plas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>First-formed.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protopodite</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*top"o*dite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The basal portion, or two proximal and more or less consolidated
segments, of an appendage of a crustacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protopope</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*pope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets>
+ <ets>pope</ets>: cf. F. <ets>protopope</ets>, Russ.
<ets>protopop'</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Ch.)</fld> <def>One of the clergy
of first rank in the lower order of secular clergy; an archpriest; --
called also <altname>protopapas</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protopterus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*top"te*rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ first + <?/ a feather (taken to mean, fin).]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Komtok</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protosalt</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*salt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets>
+ <ets>salt</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt derived from a
protoxide base.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protosilicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*sil"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>silicate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A silicate formed with the lowest proportion of silicic acid, or
having but one atom of silicon in the molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protosomite</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*so"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>somite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the primitive segments, or metameres, of an
animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protosulphide</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*sul"phide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>sulphide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>That one of a series of sulphides of any element which has the
lowest proportion of sulphur; a sulphide with but one atom of sulphur in
the molecule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protosulphuret</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*sul"phu*ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>sulphuret</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A protosulphide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prototheria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*the"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>prw^tos</grk> first + <grk>qhri`on</grk>, dim. of
<grk>qh`r</grk> beast.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Monotremata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prototracheata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*tra`che*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Proto-</er>, and <er>Trachea</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Malacopoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prototype</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"to*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>prototypus</ets> original, primitive, Gr. <?/, <?/; <?/ first + <?/
type, model. See <er>Proto-</er>, and <er>Type</er>]</ety> <def>An
original or model after which anything is copied; the pattern of
anything to be engraved, or otherwise copied, cast, or the like; a
primary form; exemplar; archetype.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They will turn their backs on it, like their great precursor and
<qex>prototype</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protovertebra</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*ver"te*bra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Protovertebr<ae/</plw> .</plu>
<ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> + <ets>vertebra</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>One of the primitive masses, or segments, into which the mesoblast
of the vertebrate embryo breaks up on either side of the anterior part
of the notochord; a mesoblastic, or protovertebral, somite. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Ectoderm</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The protovertebr<ae/ were long regarded as rudiments of
the permanent vertebr<ae/, but they are now known to give rise to the
dorsal muscles and other structures as well as the vertebral column. See
<er>Myotome</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protovertebral</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*ver"te*bral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
protovertebr<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoxide</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets>
+ <ets>oxide</ets>: cf. F. <ets>protoxide</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>That one of a series of oxides having the lowest
proportion of oxygen. See <er>Proto-</er>, 2 <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>protoxide of nitrogen</b></col>, <cd>laughing gas, now
called <altname>hyponitrous oxide</altname><--, used as an anaesthetic
in dentistry; now (1950-1996) called nitrous oxide, <chform>NO</chform>
-->. See under <er>Laughing</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protoxidize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tox"i*dize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To combine with oxygen, as any elementary
substance, in such proportion as to form a protoxide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protozoa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*zo"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ first + <grk>zw^,on</grk> an animal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The lowest of the grand divisions of the animal kingdom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The entire animal consists of a single cell which is
variously modified; but in many species a number of these simple zooids
are united together so as to form a compound body or organism, as in the
Foraminifera and Vorticell<ae/. The reproduction takes place by fission,
or by the breaking up of the contents of the body after encystment, each
portion becoming a distinct animal, or in other ways, but never by true
eggs. The principal divisions are Rhizopoda, Gregarin<ae/, and
Infusoria. See also <er>Foraminifera</er>, <er>Heliozoa</er>,
<er>Protoplasta</er>, <er>Radiolaria</er>, <er>Flagellata</er>,
<er>Ciliata</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protozoan</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*zo"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the <er>Protozoa</er>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Protozoa.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protozoic</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*zo"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Protozoa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Containing remains of the
earliest discovered life of the globe, which included mollusks, radiates
and protozoans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protozoon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`to*zo"<oum/n</hw> <pr>(-<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Protozoa</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the
Protozoa.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A single zooid of a compound
protozoan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protozoonite</ent><br/
<hw>Pro`to*zo"<oum/*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the primary, or first-formed, segments of
an embryonic arthropod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protracheata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro*tra`che*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Trachea</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Malacopoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1154 --></p>

<p><ent>Protract</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Protracted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Protracting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>protractus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>protrahere</ets> to forth, protract; <ets>pro</ets> forward +
<ets>trahere</ets> to draw. See <er>Portrait</er>,
<er>Portray</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw out or lengthen in
time or (rarely) in space; to continue; to prolong; <as>as, to
<ex>protract</ex> an argument; to <ex>protract</ex> a
war.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put off to a distant time; to delay; to defer;
<as>as, to <ex>protract</ex> a decision or duty</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Surv.)</fld> <def>To draw to a scale; to lay down
the lines and angles of, with scale and protractor; to plot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>To extend; to protrude; <as>as,
the cat can <ex>protract</ex> its claws</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>retract</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protract</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protractus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tedious continuance or delay.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protracted</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Prolonged;
continued.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Protracted meeting</b></col>,<cd>a religious meeting
continued for many successive days.</cd> <mark>[U. S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pro*tract"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pro*tract"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protracter</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
protractor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protractile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract"ile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
protracted, or protruded; protrusile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protraction</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*trac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protractio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A drawing out, or
continuing; the act of delaying the termination of a thing;
prolongation; continuance; delay; <as>as, the <ex>protraction</ex> of a
debate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>protraction</qex> only of what is worst in life.</q>
<rj><qau>Mallock.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surv.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The act or process
of making a plot on paper.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A plot on
paper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protractive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drawing out or
lengthening in time; prolonging; continuing; delaying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He suffered their <qex>protractive</qex> arts.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protractor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tract"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, protracts, or causes protraction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mathematical instrument for laying down and
measuring angles on paper, used in drawing or in plotting. It is of
various forms, semicircular, rectangular, or circular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>An instrument formerly used in
extracting foreign or offensive matter from a wound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A muscle which extends an organ
or part; -- opposed to <contr>retractor</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An adjustable pattern used by tailors.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protreptical</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*trep"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to turn forward, to urge on.]</ety> <def>Adapted to persuade;
hortatory; persuasive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Ward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrudable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*trud"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
protruded; protrusile.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrude</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*trude"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Protruded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Protruding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>protrudere</ets>,
<ets>protrusum</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>trudere</ets> to
thrust. See <er>Threat</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust forward; to drive or force along.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To thrust out, as through a narrow orifice or from
confinement; to cause to come forth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When . . . Spring <qex>protrudes</qex> the bursting gems.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrude</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*trude"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To shoot out or forth; to be
thrust forward; to extend beyond a limit; to project.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The parts <qex>protrude</qex> beyond the skin.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrusile</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tru"sile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
protruded or thrust out; protractile; protrusive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrusion</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tru"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of protruding or thrusting forward, or beyond the usual
limit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being protruded, or thrust
forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrusive</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tru"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Thrusting or impelling forward; <as>as, <ex>protrusive</ex>
motion</as>.</def> <rj><au>E. Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being protruded; protrusile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protrusively</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tru"sive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a protrusive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protuberance</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tu"ber*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>protub<eacute/rance</ets>. See <er>Protuberant</er>.]</ety>
<def>That which is protuberant swelled or pushed beyond the surrounding
or adjacent surface; a swelling or tumor on the body; a prominence; a
bunch or knob; an elevation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solar protuberances</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>,
<cd>certain rose-colored masses on the limb of the sun which are seen to
extend beyond the edge of the moon at the time of a solar eclipse. They
may be discovered with the spectroscope on any clear day. Called also
<altname>solar prominences</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> in
Append.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Projection</er>, <er>Protuberance</er>.
<xex>protuberance</xex> differs from <xex>projection</xex>, being
applied to parts that rise from the surface with a gradual ascent or
small angle; whereas a <xex>projection</xex> may be at a right angle
with the surface.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protuberancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tu"ber*an*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being protuberant; protuberance; prominence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protuberant</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tu"ber*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protuberans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>protuberare</ets>. See <er>Protuberate</er>.]</ety> <def>Prominent,
or excessively prominent; bulging beyond the surrounding or adjacent
surface; swelling; <as>as, a <ex>protuberant</ex> joint; a
<ex>protuberant</ex> eye.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro*tu"ber*ant*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protuberate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tu"ber*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>protuberare</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>tuber</ets> a
hump, protuberance. See <er>Tuber</er>.]</ety> <def>To swell, or be
prominent, beyond the adjacent surface; to bulge out.</def> <rj><au>S.
Sharp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protuberation</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tu`ber*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
swelling beyond the surrounding surface.</def> <rj><au>Cooke
(1615).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protuberous</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*tu"ber*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Protuberant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protureter</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`tu*re"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Proto-</er>, <er>Ureter</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
duct of a pronephros.</def> <rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Protyle</ent><br/
<hw>Pro"tyle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Proto-</ets> +
Gr. <?/ stuff, material.]</ety> <fld>(Chem. & Astron.)</fld> <def>The
hypothetical homogeneous cosmic material of the original universe,
supposed to have been differentiated into what are recognized as
distinct chemical elements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proud</ent><br/
<hw>Proud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Prouder</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Proudest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>proud</ets>,
<ets>prout</ets>, <ets>prud</ets>, <ets>prut</ets>, AS.
<ets>pr<umac/t</ets>; akin to Icel. pr<umac/<edh/r stately, handsome,
Dan. <ets>prud</ets> handsome. Cf. <er>Pride</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Feeling or manifesting pride, in a good or bad sense</def>; as:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Possessing or showing too great self-esteem;
overrating one's excellences; hence, arrogant; haughty; lordly;
presumptuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor much expect<br/
A foe so <qex>proud</qex> will first the weaker seek.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O death, made <qex>proud</qex> with pure and princely beauty !</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And shades impervious to the <qex>proud</qex> world's glare.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having a feeling of high self-respect or
self-esteem; exulting (in); elated; -- often with <xex>of</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>proud</ex> of one's country</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Proud</xex> to
be checked and soothed.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Are we proud men <qex>proud</qex> of being proud ?</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Giving reason or occasion for pride or
self-gratulation; worthy of admiration; grand; splendid; magnificent;
admirable; ostentatious.</def> <ldquo/Of shadow <xex>proud</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Chapman.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Proud</xex> titles.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/ The <xex>proud</xex> temple's height.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till tower, and dome, and bridge-way <qex>proud</qex><br/
Are mantled with a golden cloud.</q> <rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Excited by sexual desire; -- applied particularly to
the females of some animals.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Proud</xex> is often used with participles in the
formation of compounds which, for the most part, are self-explaining;
as, <xex>proud</xex>-crested, <xex>proud</xex>-minded,
<xex>proud</xex>-swelling.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proud flesh</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a fungous
growth or excrescence of granulations resembling flesh, in a wound or
ulcer.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proudish</ent><br/
<hw>Proud"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
proud.</def> <rj><au>Ash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proudling</ent><br/
<hw>Proud"ling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A proud or haughty person.</def>
<rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proudly</ent><br/
<hw>Proud"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a proud manner; with lofty
airs or mien; haughtily; arrogantly; boastfully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Proudly</qex> he marches on, and void of fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proudness</ent><br/
<hw>Proud"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being proud;
pride.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Set aside all arrogancy and <qex>proudness</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proustite</ent><br/
<hw>Proust"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the French
chemist, J. L. <ets>Proust</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
sulphide of arsenic and silver of a beautiful cochineal-red color,
occurring in rhombohedral crystals, and also massive; ruby
silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provable</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Prove</er>, and cf. <er>Probable</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
proved; demonstrable.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Prov"a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos> -- <wf>Prov"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proant</ent><br/
<ent>Provand</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Prov"and</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pro"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Provender</er>.]</ety>
<def>Provender or food.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One pease was a soldier's <qex>provant</qex> a whole day.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl. </qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provant</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vant"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To supply with
provender or provisions; to provide for.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Nash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provant</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Provided for common
or general use, as in an army; hence, common in quality; inferior.</def>
<ldquo/A poor <xex>provant</xex> rapier.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prove</ent><br/
<hw>Prove</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Proved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Proving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>prover</ets>, F.
<ets>prouver</ets>, fr. L. <ets>probare</ets> to try, approve, prove,
fr. <ets>probus</ets> good, proper. Cf. <er>Probable</er>,
<er>Proof</er>, <er>Probe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To try or to
ascertain by an experiment, or by a test or standard; to test; <as>as,
to <ex>prove</ex> the strength of gunpowder or of ordnance; to
<ex>prove</ex> the contents of a vessel by a standard
measure.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou hast <qex>proved</qex> mine heart.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xvii.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To evince, establish, or ascertain, as truth,
reality, or fact, by argument, testimony, or other evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They have inferred much from slender premises, and conjectured
when they could not <qex>prove</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>J. H.
Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To ascertain or establish the genuineness or
validity of; to verify; <as>as, to <ex>prove</ex> a will</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To gain experience of the good or evil of; to know
by trial; to experience; to suffer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where she, captived long, great woes did <qex>prove</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>To test, evince, ascertain, or
verify, as the correctness of any operation or result; thus, in
subtraction, if the difference between two numbers, added to the lesser
number, makes a sum equal to the greater, the correctness of the
subtraction is <xex>proved</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Printing)</fld> <def>To take a trial impression of;
to take a proof of; <as>as, to <ex>prove</ex> a page</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To try; verify; justify; confirm; establish;
evince; manifest; show; demonstrate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prove</ent><br/
<hw>Prove</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make trial; to
essay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be found by experience, trial, or result; to turn
out to be; <as>as, a medicine <ex>proves</ex> salutary; the report
<ex>proves</ex> false.</as></def> <ldquo/The case <xex>proves</xex>
mortal.<rdquo/ <au>Arbuthnot.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So life a winter's morn may <qex>prove</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To succeed; to turn out as expected.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The experiment <xex>proved</xex> not.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provect</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>provectus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>provehere</ets> to carry
forward.]</ety> <def>Carried forward; advanced.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Provect</xex> in years.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir T. Flyot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provection</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>provectio</ets> an advancement.]</ety> <fld>(Philol.)</fld> <def>A
carrying forward, as of a final letter, to a following word; <as>as, for
example, a <ex>nickname</ex> for <ex>an ekename</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proveditor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ved"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>proveditore</ets>, <ets>provveditore</ets>, fr.
<ets>provedere</ets>, L. <ets>providere</ets>. See <er>Provide</er>, and
cf. <er>Purveyor</er>, <er>Provedore</er>.]</ety> <def>One employed to
procure supplies, as for an army, a steamer, etc.; a purveyor; one who
provides for another.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provedore</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"e*dore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sp.
<ets>proveedor</ets>. See <er>Proveditor</er>.]</ety> <def>A proveditor;
a purveyor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Busied with the duties of a <qex>provedore</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proven</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. p. <or/ a.</pos>
<def>Proved.</def> <ldquo/Accusations firmly <xex>proven</xex> in his
mind.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of this which was the principal charge, and was generally believed
to be<qex>proven</qex>, he was acquitted.</q> <rj><qau>Jowett (Thucyd.
).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Not proven</b></col> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld>, <cd>a
verdict of a jury that the guilt of the accused is not made out, though
not disproved.</cd> <rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provenance</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"e*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>provenir</ets> to originate, to come forth, L.
<ets>provenire</ets>. Cf. <er>Provenience</er>.]</ety> <def>Origin;
source; provenience.</def></p>

<p><q>Their age attested by their <qex>provenance</qex> and
associations.</q> <rj><qau>A. H. Keane.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provencal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`ven`<cced/al"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>Provence</ets>, fr. L. <ets>provincia</ets> province. See
<er>Provincial</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
<state>Provence</state> or its inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provencal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`ven`<cced/al"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or inhabitant of Provence in France.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The Provencal language. See <er>Langue
d'oc</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provence rose</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"ence rose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[<ets>Provence</ets> the
place + <ets>rose</ets>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The cabbage rose
(<spn>Rosa centifolia</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A name of many
kinds of roses which are hybrids of <spn>Rosa centifolia</spn> and
<spn>Rosa Gallica</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provencial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ven"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Proven<cced/al</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Provence in
France.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provend</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"end</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Provand</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provender</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"en*der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>provende</ets>, F. <ets>provende</ets>, provisions, provender, fr.
LL. <ets>praebenda</ets> (<ets>prae</ets> and <ets>pro</ets> being
confused), a daily allowance of provisions, a prebend. See
<er>Prebend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Dry food for domestic animals,
as hay, straw, corn, oats, or a mixture of ground grain; feed.</def>
<ldquo/Hay or other <xex>provender</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good <qex>provender</qex> laboring horses would have.</q>
<rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Food or provisions.</def> <mark>[R or
Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provenience</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ve"ni*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proveniens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p.pr. of <ets>provenire</ets>
to come forth; <ets>pro</ets> forth + <ets>venire</ets> to come.]</ety>
<def>Origin; source; place where found or produced; provenance; -- used
esp. in the fine arts and in arch<ae/ology; <as>as, the
<ex>provenience</ex> of a patera</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provenient</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ve"ni*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proveniens</ets>, p.pr.]</ety> <def>Forthcoming; issuing.</def>
<mark>[Rare]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provent</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Provand</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proventricle</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ven"tri*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Proventriculus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proventriulus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pro`ven*tri"u*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Ventricle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The glandular stomach of birds, situated just above the
crop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prover</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, proves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverb</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"erb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>proverbe</ets>, F. <ets>proverbe</ets>, from L.
<ets>proverbium</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before, for + <ets>verbum</ets> a
word. See <er>Verb</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An old and common
saying; a phrase which is often repeated; especially, a sentence which
briefly and forcibly expresses some practical truth, or the result of
experience and observation; a maxim; a saw; an adage.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer. Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A striking or paradoxical assertion; an obscure
saying; an enigma; a parable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His disciples said unto him, Lo, now speakest thou plainly, and
speakest no <qex>proverb</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>John xvi.
29.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A familiar illustration; a subject of contemptuous
reference.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt become an astonishment, a <qex>proverb</qex>, and a by
word, among all nations.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. xxviii. 37.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A drama exemplifying a proverb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Book of Proverbs</b></col>, <cd>a canonical book of the
Old Testament, containing a great variety of wise maxims.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Maxim; aphorism; apothegm; adage; saw.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverb</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"erb</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To name in, or as,
a proverb.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Am I not sung and <qex>proverbed</qex> for a fool ?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To provide with a proverb.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am <qex>proverbed</qex> with a grandsire phrase.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverb</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"erb</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To write or utter
proverbs.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverbial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ver"bi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proverbialis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proverbial</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Mentioned or comprised in a proverb; used as a proverb;
hence, commonly known; <as>as, a <ex>proverbial</ex> expression; his
meanness was <ex>proverbial</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In case of excesses, I take the German <qex>proverbial</qex> cure,
by a hair of the same beast, to be the worst.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to proverbs; resembling a
proverb.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>proverbial</xex> obscurity.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverbialism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ver"bi*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A proverbial
phrase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverbialist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ver"bi*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes much use of
proverbs in speech or writing; one who composes, collects, or studies
proverbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverbialize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ver"bi*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>proverbialiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To turn into a proverb; to
speak in proverbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proverbially</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*ver"bi*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a proverbial manner;
by way of proverb; hence, commonly; universally; <as>as, it is
<ex>proverbially</ex> said; the bee is <ex>proverbially</ex>
busy.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provexity</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vex"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>provehere</ets> to advance. Cf. <er>Provect</er>.]</ety> <def>Great
advance in age.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provide</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vide"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Provided</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Providing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>providere</ets>,
<ets>provisum</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> before + <ets>videre</ets> to see.
See <er>Vision</er>, and cf. <er>Prudent</er>, <er>Purvey</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To look out for in advance; to procure beforehand; to
get, collect, or make ready for future use; to prepare.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Provide</xex> us all things necessary.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To supply; to afford; to contribute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bring me berries, or such cooling fruit<br/
As the kind, hospitable woods <qex>provide</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To furnish; to supply; -- formerly followed by
<xex>of</xex>, now by <xex>with</xex>.</def> <ldquo/And yet
<xex>provided</xex> him of but one.<rdquo/ <au>Jer. Taylor.</au>
<ldquo/Rome . . . was well <xex>provided</xex> with corn.<rdquo/
<au>Arbuthnot.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To establish as a previous condition; to stipulate;
<as>as, the contract <ex>provides</ex> that the work be well
done</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To foresee.</def> <note>[A
<xex>Latinism</xex>]</note> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To appoint to an ecclesiastical benefice before it
is vacant. See <er>Provisor</er>.</def> <rj><au>Prescott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provide</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vide"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To procure
supplies or means in advance; to take measures beforehand in view of an
expected or a possible future need, especially a danger or an evil; --
followed by <xex>against or for</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>provide</ex>
against the inclemency of the weather; to <ex>provide</ex> for the
education of a child.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Government is a contrivance of human wisdom to <qex>provide</qex>
for human wants.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stipulate previously; to condition; <as>as, the
agreement <ex>provides</ex> for an early completion of the
work</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provided</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vid"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>conj.</pos> <def>On condition; by
stipulation; with the understanding; if; -- usually followed by
<xex>that</xex>; <as>as, <ex>provided</ex> that nothing in this act
shall prejudice the rights of any person whatever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Provided</qex> the deductions are logical, they seem almost
indifferent to their truth.</q> <rj><qau>G. H. Lewes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word is strictly a participle, and the word
<xex>being</xex> is understood, the participle <xex>provided</xex>
agreeing with the whole sentence absolute, and being equivalent to
<xex>this condition being previously stipulated or
established</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Providence</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"i*dence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>providentia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>providence</ets>. See
<er>Provident</er>, and cf. <er>Prudence</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of providing or preparing for future use or application; a
making ready; preparation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Providence</qex> for war is the best prevention of it.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Foresight; care; especially, the foresight and care
which God manifests for his creatures; hence, God himself, regarded as
exercising a constant wise prescience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The world was all before them, where to choose<br/
Their place of rest, and <qex>Providence</qex> their guide.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>A manifestation of the care and
superintendence which God exercises over his creatures; an event
ordained by divine direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that hath a numerous family, and many to provide for, needs a
greater <qex>providence</qex> of God.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1155 --></p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Prudence in the management of one's concerns;
economy; frugality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a high point of <qex>providence</qex> in a prince to cast an
eye rather upon actions than persons.</q>
<rj><qau>Quarles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provident</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"i*dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>providens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>providere</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>provident</ets>. See <er>Provide</er>, and cf.
<er>Prudent</er>.]</ety> <def>Foreseeing wants and making provision to
supply them; prudent in preparing for future exigencies; cautious;
economical; -- sometimes followed by <xex>of</xex>; <as>as,
a<ex>provident</ex> man; an animal <ex>provident</ex> of the
future.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And of our good and of our dignity,<br/
How <qex>provident</qex> he is.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Forecasting; cautious; careful; prudent; frugal;
economical.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Providential</ent><br/
<hw>Prov`i*den"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>providentiel</ets>.]</ety> <def>Effected by, or referable to,
divine direction or superintendence; <as>as, the <ex>providential</ex>
contrivance of thing; a <ex>providential</ex> escape.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Prov"i*den"tial*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Providently</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"i*dent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
provident manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Providentness</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"i*dent*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being provident; carefulness; prudence; economy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provider</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vid"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who provides,
furnishes, or supplies; one who procures what is wanted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Providore</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"i*dore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Provedore</er>.]</ety> <def>One who makes provision; a
purveyor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>De Foe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Province</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"ince</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>provincia</ets>; prob. fr. <ets>pro</ets> before, for + the root of
<ets>vincere</ets> to conquer. See <er>Victor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Roman Hist.)</fld> <def>A country or region, more or less remote
from the city of Rome, brought under the Roman government; a conquered
country beyond the limits of Italy.</def> <rj><au>Wyclif (Acts xiii.
34). Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A country or region dependent on a distant
authority; a portion of an empire or state, esp. one remote from the
capital.</def> <ldquo/Kingdoms and <xex>provinces</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A region of country; a tract; a district.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Over many a tract<br/
of heaven they marched, and many a <qex>province</qex> wide.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Other <qex>provinces</qex> of the intellectual world.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A region under the supervision or direction of any
special person; the district or division of a country, especially an
ecclesiastical division, over which one has jurisdiction; <as>as, the
<ex>province</ex> of Canterbury, or that in which the archbishop of
Canterbury exercises ecclesiastical authority</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The proper or appropriate business or duty of a
person or body; office; charge; jurisdiction; sphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The woman's<qex>province</qex> is to be careful in her economy,
and chaste in her affection.</q> <rj><qau>Tattler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Specif.: Any political division of the Dominion of
Canada, having a governor, a local legislature, and representation in
the Dominion parliament. Hence, colloquially, <xex>The Provinces</xex>,
the Dominion of Canada.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provincial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>provincialis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>provincial</ets>. See
<er>Province</er>, and cf. <er>Provencal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to province; constituting a province; <as>as, a
<ex>provincial</ex> government; a <ex>provincial</ex>
dialect.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exhibiting the ways or manners of a province;
characteristic of the inhabitants of a province; not cosmopolitan;
countrified; not polished; rude; hence, narrow; illiberal.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Provincial</xex> airs and graces.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to an ecclesiastical province, or
to the jurisdiction of an archbishop; not ecumenical; <as>as, a
<ex>provincial</ex> synod</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to Provence; Provencal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With two <qex>Provincial</qex> roses on my razed shoes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provincial</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"cial</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person belonging
to a province; one who is provincial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A monastic superior, who,
under the general of his order, has the direction of all the religious
houses of the same fraternity in a given district, called a
<xex>province</xex> of the order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provincialism</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"cial*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>provincialisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>A word, or a manner of speaking,
peculiar to a province or a district remote from the mother country or
from the metropolis; a provincial characteristic; hence, narrowness;
illiberality.</def> <rj><au>M. Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provincialist</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"cial*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lives in a
province; a provincial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provinciality</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin`ci*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being provincial; peculiarity of language characteristic of
a province.</def> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provincialize</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"cial*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Provincialized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Provincializing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To render provincial.</def> <rj><au>M.
Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provincially</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"cial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a provincial
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provinciate</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vin"ci*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To convert
into a province or provinces.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provine</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>provingner</ets>, fr. <ets>provin</ets> a set, layer of a plant,
OF. <ets>provain</ets>, from L. <ets>propago</ets>, <ets>-aginis</ets>,
akin to <ets>propagare</ets> to propagate. See <er>Propagate</er>,
<er>Prune</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To lay a stock or branch of
a vine in the ground for propagation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provision</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>provisio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>provision</ets>. See
<er>Provide</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of providing, or
making previous preparation.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is provided or prepared; that which is
brought together or arranged in advance; measures taken beforehand;
preparation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Making <qex>provision</qex> for the relief of strangers.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Especially, a stock of food; any kind of eatables
collected or stored; -- often in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And of <qex>provisions</qex> laid in large,<br/
For man and beast.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which is stipulated in advance; a condition; a
previous agreement; a proviso; <as>as, the <ex>provisions</ex> of a
contract; the statute has many <ex>provisions</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A canonical term for regular
induction into a benefice, comprehending nomination, collation, and
installation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld> <def>A nomination by the pope to
a benefice before it became vacant, depriving the patron of his right of
presentation.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provision</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Provisioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Provisioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To supply with
food; to victual; <as>as, to <ex>provision</ex> a
garrison</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were <qex>provisioned</qex> for a journey.</q>
<rj><qau>Palfrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisional</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>provisionnel</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of the nature of a provision;
serving as a provision for the time being; -- used of partial or
temporary arrangements; <as>as, a <ex>provisional</ex> government; a
<ex>provisional</ex> treaty.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisionally</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sion*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way of provision for
the time being; temporarily.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisionary</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Provisional.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proviso</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"so</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Provisos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., (it) being provided,
abl. of <ets>provisus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>providere</ets>. See
<er>Provide</er>, and cf. <er>Purview</er>.]</ety> <def>An article or
clause in any statute, agreement, contract, grant, or other writing, by
which a condition is introduced, usually beginning with the word
<xex>provided</xex>; a conditional stipulation that affects an
agreement, contract, law, grant, or the like; <as>as, the contract was
impaired by its <ex>proviso</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He doth deny his prisoners,<br/
But with <qex>proviso</qex> and exception.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisor</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>providere</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proviseur</ets>. See
<er>Provide</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who provides; a
purveyor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The chief <xex>provisor</xex>
of our horse.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The purveyor,
steward, or treasurer of a religious house.</def> <au>Cowell.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who is regularly inducted into a benefice. See
<er>Provision</er>, 5.</def> <au>P. Plowman.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld> <def>One who procures or receives
a papal provision. See <er>Provision</er>, 6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisorily</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"so*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
provisory manner; conditionally; subject to a proviso; <as>as, to admit
a doctrine <ex>provisorily</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisorship</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"sor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
position of a provisor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>J.
Webster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provisory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vi"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>provisoire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a
proviso; containing a proviso or condition; conditional; <as>as, a
<ex>provisory</ex> clause</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Making temporary provision; provisional.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provocation</ent><br/
<hw>Prov`o*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>provocation</ets>, L. <ets>provocatio</ets>. See
<er>Provoke</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of provoking, or
causing vexation or, anger.</def> <rj><au>Fabyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which provokes, or excites anger; the cause of
resentment; <as>as, to give <ex>provocation</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Paley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Incitement; stimulus; <as>as, <ex>provocation</ex>
to mirth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Such prior insult or injury as may
be supposed, under the circumstances, to create hot blood, and to excuse
an assault made in retort or redress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An appeal to a court. <note>[A
Latinism]</note></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provocative</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vo"ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>provocativus</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>provocatif</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Serving or tending to provoke, excite, or stimulate;
exciting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provocative</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vo"ca*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Anything that is
provocative; a stimulant; <as>as, a <ex>provocative</ex> of
appetite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provocativeness</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vo"ca*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
provocative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provocatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vo"ca*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Provocative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provokable</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vok"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
provoked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provoke</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*voke"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Provoked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Provoking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>provoquer</ets>, L. <ets>provocare</ets> to call forth;
<ets>pro</ets> forth + <ets>vocare</ets> to call, fr. <ets>vox</ets>,
<ets>vocis</ets>, voice, cry, call. See <er>Voice</er>.]</ety> <def>To
call forth; to call into being or action; esp., to incense to action, a
faculty or passion, as love, hate, or ambition; hence, commonly, to
incite, as a person, to action by a challenge, by taunts, or by
defiance; to exasperate; to irritate; to offend intolerably; to cause to
retaliate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Obey his voice, <qex>provoke</qex> him not.</q> <rj><qau>Ex.
xxiii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye fathers, <qex>provoke</qex> not your children to wrath.</q>
<rj><qau>Eph. vi. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such acts<br/
Of contumacy will <qex>provoke</qex> the Highest<br/
To make death in us live.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can honor's voice <qex>provoke</qex> the silent dust?</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To the poet the meaning is what he pleases to make it, what it
<qex>provokes</qex> in his own soul.</q> <rj><qau> J.
Burroughs.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To irritate; arouse; stir up; awake; excite;
incite; anger. See <er>Irritate</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provoke</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*voke"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause
provocation or anger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To appeal. <note>[A Latinism]</note></def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provokement</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*voke"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act that
which, provokes; one who excites anger or other passion, or incites to
action; <as>as, a <ex>provoker</ex> of sedition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Drink, sir, is a great <qex>provoker</qex> of three things.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provoking</ent><br/
<hw>Pro*vok"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the power or quality of
exciting resentment; tending to awaken passion or vexation; <as>as,
<ex>provoking</ex> words or treatment</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pro*vok"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provost</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"ost</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>provost</ets> (L. <ets>prae</ets> and <ets>pro</ets> being
confused), F. <ets>prev<ocir/t</ets>, fr. L. <ets>praepositus</ets>
placed before, a chief, fr. <ets>praeponere</ets> to place before: cf.
AS. <ets>pr<amac/fost</ets>, <ets>pr<omac/fast</ets>. See
<er>Preposition</er>, and cf. <er>Propound</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A person who is appointed to superintend, or preside over,
something; the chief magistrate in some cities and towns; <as>as, the
<ex>provost</ex> of Edinburgh or of Glasgow, answering to the mayor of
other cities; the <ex>provost</ex> of a college, answering to president;
the <ex>provost</ex> or head of certain collegiate
churches.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The keeper of a prison.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In France, formerly, a <xex>provost</xex> was an
inferior judge who had cognizance of civil causes. The <xex>grand
provost of France</xex>, <xex>or of the household</xex>, had
jurisdiction in the king's house, and over its officers.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Provost marshal</b></col> <pr>(often pronounced
<?/)</pr>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>An officer appointed in
every army, in the field, to secure the prisoners confined on charges of
a general nature. He also performs such other duties pertaining to
police and discipline as the regulations of the service or the
commander's orders impose upon him.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Nav.)</fld>
<cd>An officer who has charge of prisoners on trial by court-martial,
serves notices to witnesses, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Provostship</ent><br/
<hw>Prov"ost*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
provost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prow</ent><br/
<hw>Prow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>proue</ets> (cf.
Sp. & Pg. <ets>proa</ets>, It. <ets>prua</ets>), L. <ets>prora</ets>,
Gr. <?/, akin to <?/ before. See <er>Pro-</er>, and cf.
<er>Prore</er>.]</ety> <def>The fore part of a vessel; the bow; the
stem; hence, the vessel itself.</def> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The floating vessel swum<br/
Uplifted, and secure with beaked <qex>prow</qex><br/
rode tilting o'er the waves.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prow</ent><br/
<hw>Prow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Proa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prow</ent><br/
<hw>Prow</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Prower</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Prowest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>prou</ets>,
<ets>preu</ets>, F. <ets>preux</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pro</ets>,
<ets>prod</ets>, in <ets>prodesse</ets> to be useful. See <er>Pro-</er>,
and cf. <er>Prude</er>.]</ety> <def>Valiant; brave; gallant;
courageous.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>prowest</qex> knight that ever field did fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prow</ent><br/
<hw>Prow</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. & OF. <ets>prou</ets>. See
<er>Prow</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Benefit; profit; good;
advantage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That shall be for your hele and for your <qex>prow</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prowess</ent><br/
<hw>Prow"ess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>proece</ets>, <ets>proesce</ets>, F. <ets>prouesse</ets>. See
<er>Prow</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Distinguished bravery; valor;
especially, military bravery and skill; gallantry; intrepidity;
fearlessness.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer. Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He by his <qex>prowess</qex> conquered all France.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prowl</ent><br/
<hw>Prowl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Prowled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prowling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>prollen</ets> to
search about; of uncertain origin, perh. for <ets>proglen</ets>, a dim.
of <ets>prog</ets> to beg, or <ets>proke</ets> to poke. Cf.
<er>Proke</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rove over, through, or about
in a stealthy manner; esp., to search in, as for prey or
booty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>prowls</qex> each place, still in new colors decked.</q>
<rj><qau> Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To collect by plunder; <as>as, to <ex>prowl</ex>
money</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prowl</ent><br/
<hw>Prowl</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To rove or wander stealthily, esp.
for prey, as a wild beast; hence, to prey; to plunder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prowl</ent><br/
<hw>Prowl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of prowling.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prowler</ent><br/
<hw>Prowl"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One that
prowls.</def> <rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prowling</ent><br/
<hw>Prowl"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Accustomed to prowl, or engaged
in roving stealthily, as for prey.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>prowling</xex>
wolf.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Prowl"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prox</ent><br/
<hw>Prox</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Proxy</er>.]</ety> <def><ldquo/The ticket or list of candidates at
elections, presented to the people for their votes.<rdquo/</def>
<mark>[Rhode Island]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxene</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"ene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <?/; <?/ before
+ <?/ a guest, stranger: cf. F. <ets>prox<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An officer who had the charge of showing
hospitality to those who came from a friendly city or state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxenet</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"e*net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proxeneta</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>A negotiator; a factor.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxenetism</ent><br/
<hw>Prox`e*ne"tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ agent
+ <ets>-ism</ets>; cf. F. <ets>prox<eacute/n<eacute/tisme</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The action of a go-between or broker in negotiating immoral
bargains between the sexes; procuring.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximad</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"i*mad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Proximal</ets> + L. <ets>ad</ets> to.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Toward a proximal part; on the proximal side of;
proximally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximal</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"i*mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Toward
or nearest, as to a body, or center of motion of dependence;
proximate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated near the
point of attachment or origin; <as>as, the <ex>proximal</ex> part of a
limb</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to that which is
proximal; <as>as, the <ex>proximal</ex> bones of a limb</as>. Opposed to
<ant>distal</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximally</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"i*mal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>On or
toward a proximal part; proximad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximate</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"i*mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proximatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>proximare</ets> to come near, to
approach, fr. <ets>proximus</ets> the nearest, nest, superl. of
<ets>propior</ets> nearer, and <ets>prope</ets>, adv., near.]</ety>
<def>Nearest; next immediately preceding or following.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Proximate</xex> ancestors.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. S.
Harford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>proximate</qex> natural causes of it [the deluge].</q>
<rj><qau>T. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Proximate analysis</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an
analysis which determines the proximate principles of any substance, as
contrasted with an <xex>ultimate analysis</xex>.</cd> --
<col><b>Proximate cause</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A cause which
immediately precedes and produces the effect, as distinguished from the
<xex>remote</xex>, <xex>mediate</xex>, <xex>or predisposing</xex>
cause.</cd> <au>I. Watts.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>That which in ordinary
natural sequence produces a specific result, no independent disturbing
agencies intervening.</cd> -- <col><b>Proximate principle</b></col>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>one of a class of bodies existing ready
formed in animal and vegetable tissues, and separable by chemical
analysis, as albumin, sugar, collagen, fat, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Nearest; next; closest; immediate;
direct.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximately</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"i*mate*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a proximate manner,
position, or degree; immediately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxime</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"ime</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proximus</ets>. See <er>Proximate</er>.]</ety> <def>Next;
immediately preceding or following.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximious</ent><br/
<hw>Prox*im"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Proximate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximity</ent><br/
<hw>Prox*im"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>proximitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>proximit<eacute/</ets> See
<er>Proximate</er>, and cf. <er>Propinquity</er>,
<er>Approach</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being next in
time, place, causation, influence, etc.; immediate nearness, either in
place, blood, or alliance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If he plead <qex>proximity</qex> of blood<br/
That empty title is with ease withstood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proximo</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"i*mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., on the next, abl. of
<ets>proximus</ets> next.]</ety> <def>In the next month after the
present; -- often contracted to <xex>prox.</xex>; <as>as, on the 3d
<ex>proximo</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxy</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Proxies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Contr. from
<ets>procuracy</ets>. Cf. <er>Proctor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
agency for another who acts through the agent; authority to act for
another, esp. to vote in a legislative or corporate capacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have no man's <qex>proxy</qex>: I speak only for myself.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The person who is substituted or deputed to act or
vote for another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every peer . . . may make another lord of parliament his
<qex>proxy</qex>, to vote for him in his absence.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A writing by which one person authorizes another to
vote in his stead, as in a corporation meeting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>The written appointment of a
proctor in suits in the ecclesiastical courts.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>See <er>Procuration</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxy</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"y</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act or vote by proxy; to do
anything by the agency of another.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Proxyship</ent><br/
<hw>Prox"y*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or agency of a
proxy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruce</ent><br/
<hw>Pruce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. for
<ets>Prussia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>Prusse</ets>.]</ety> <def>Prussian
leather.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1156 --></p>

<p><ent>Prude</ent><br/
<hw>Prude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., prudish,
originally, discreet, modest; shortened from OF. <ets>prudefeme</ets>,
<ets>preudefeme</ets>, a discreet or excellent woman; OF.
<ets>preu</ets>, <ets>prou</ets>, excellent, brave + <ets>de</ets> of +
<ets>fete</ets> woman. See <er>Prow</er>, <pos>a.</pos>,
<er>Prowess</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman of affected modesty, reserve, or
coyness; one who is overscrupulous or sensitive; one who affects
extraordinary prudence in conduct and speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Less modest than the speech of <qex>prudes</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudence</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"dence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>prudentia</ets>, contr. from <ets>providentia</ets>. See
<er>Prudent</er>, and cf. <er>Providence</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality
or state of being prudent; wisdom in the way of caution and provision;
discretion; carefulness; hence, also, economy; frugality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prudence</qex> is principally in reference to actions to be
done, and due means, order, seasons, and method of doing or not
doing.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Prudence</qex> supposes the value of the end to be assumed,
and refers only to the adaptation of the means. It is the relation of
right means for given ends.</q> <rj><qau>Whewell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wisdom; forecast; providence; considerateness;
judiciousness; discretion; caution; circumspection; judgment. See
<er>Wisdom</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudency</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"den*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Prudence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hakluyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudent</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prudens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, contr. from <ets>providens</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>prudent</ets>. See <er>Provident</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Sagacious in adapting means to ends; circumspect in action, or in
determining any line of conduct; practically wise; judicious; careful;
discreet; sensible; -- opposed to <ant>rash</ant>; <as>as, a
<ex>prudent</ex> man</as>; dictated or directed by prudence or wise
forethought; evincing prudence; <as>as, <ex>prudent</ex>
behavior</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moses established a grave and <qex>prudent</qex> law.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Frugal; economical; not extravagant; <as>as, a
<ex>prudent</ex> woman; <ex>prudent</ex> expenditure of
money</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cautious; wary; circumspect; considerate;
discreet; judicious; provident; economical; frugal.</syn> <-- note
sensible and careful in def. above. Why not here??? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudential</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*den"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Proceeding from, or dictated or characterized by, prudence;
prudent; discreet; sometimes, selfish or pecuniary as distinguished from
higher motives or influences; <as>as, <ex>prudential</ex>
motives</as>.</def> <ldquo/ A <xex>prudential</xex> line of
conduct.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exercising prudence; discretionary; advisory;
superintending or executive; <as>as, a <ex>prudential</ex>
committee</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudential</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*den"tial</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which relates to or
demands the exercise of, discretion or prudence; -- usually in the
<pluf>pl.</pluf></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many stanzas, in poetic measures, contain rules relating to common
<qex>prudentials</qex> as well as to religion.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudentialist</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*den"tial*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is governed by, or
acts from, prudential motives.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudentiality</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*den`ti*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being prudential.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudentially</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*den"tial*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
prudential manner; prudently.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudently</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"dent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prudent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudery</ent><br/
<hw>Prud"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pruderies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>pruderie</ets>.
See <er>Prude</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being prudish;
excessive or affected scrupulousness in speech or conduct; stiffness;
coyness.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudhomme</ent><br/
<hw>Prud*homme"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prud'homme</ets>. cf. <er>Prude</er>.]</ety> <def>A trustworthy
citizen; a skilled workman. See Citation under 3d <er>Commune</er>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudish</ent><br/
<hw>Prud"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a prude; very
formal, precise, or reserved; affectedly severe in virtue; <as>as, a
<ex>prudish</ex> woman; <ex>prudish</ex> manners.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A formal lecture, spoke with <qex>prudish</qex> face.</q>
<rj><qau>Garrick.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prudishly</ent><br/
<hw>Prud"ish*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prudish
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruinate</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pruinose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruinose</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"i*nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pruinosus</ets>, fr. <ets>pruina</ets> hoarfrost.]</ety>
<def>Frosty; covered with fine scales, hairs, dust, bloom, or the like,
so as to give the appearance of frost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruinous</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Frosty;
pruinose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prune</ent><br/
<hw>Prune</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pruned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pruning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>proine</ets>, probably
fr. F. <ets>provigner</ets> to lay down vine stocks for propagation;
hence, probably, the meaning, to cut away superfluous shoots. See
<er>Provine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lop or cut off the
superfluous parts, branches, or shoots of; to clear of useless material;
to shape or smooth by trimming; to trim: as, to <xex>prune</xex> trees;
to <xex>prune</xex> an essay.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Taking into consideration how they [laws] are to be
<qex>pruned</qex> and reformed.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our delightful task<br/
To <qex>prune</qex> these growing plants, and tend these flowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut off or cut out, as useless parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Horace will our superfluous branches <qex>prune</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To preen; to prepare; to dress.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His royal bird<br/
<qex>Prunes</qex> the immortal wing and cloys his beak.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prune</ent><br/
<hw>Prune</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dress; to prink; -used
humorously or in contempt.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prune</ent><br/
<hw>Prune</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>prune</ets>, from L.
<ets>prunum</ets> a plum. See <er>Plum</er>.]</ety> <def>A plum; esp., a
dried plum, used in cookery; <as>as, French or Turkish <ex>prunes</ex>;
California <ex>prunes</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>German prune</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a large
dark purple plum, of oval shape, often one-sided. It is much used for
preserving, either dried or in sirup.</cd> -- <col><b>Prune
tree</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A tree of the genus
<gen>Prunus</gen> (<spn>Prunus domestica</spn>), which produces
prunes.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The West Indian tree, <spn>Prunus
occidentalis</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>South African prune</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the edible fruit of a sapindaceous tree
(<spn>Pappea Capensis</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prunella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pru*nel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., perhaps
from G. <ets>br<ae/une</ets> quinsy, croup.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Angina, or angina pectoris.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Thrush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prunella salt</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>niter
fused and cast into little balls.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prunello</ent><br/
<ent>Prunella</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pru*nel"la</hw>, <hw>Pru*nel"lo</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>prunelle</ets>, probably so called from its color
resembling that of prunes. See <er>Prune</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A smooth woolen stuff, generally black, used for making shoes; a
kind of lasting; -- formerly used also for clergymen's gowns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prunelle</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*nelle"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>prune</ets>. See <er>Prune</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A kind
of small and very acid French plum; -- applied especially to the stoned
and dried fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prunello</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*nel"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>prunelle</ets>, dim. of <ets>prune</ets>. See <er>Prune</er> a
plum.]</ety> <def>A species of dried plum; prunelle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruner</ent><br/
<hw>Prun"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
prunes, or removes, what is superfluous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
beetles whose larv<ae/ gnaw the branches of trees so as to cause them to
fall, especially the American oak pruner (<spn>Asemum m<oe/stum</spn>),
whose larva eats the pith of oak branches, and when mature gnaws a
circular furrow on the inside nearly to the bark. When the branches fall
each contains a pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>prunum</ets> a plum + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Bearing
plums.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruning</ent><br/
<hw>Prun"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of trimming, or removing what is superfluous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>That which is cast off by bird
in pruning her feathers; leavings.</def> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Pruning hook</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Pruning
knife</b></col></mcol>, <cd>cutting instrument used in pruning trees,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Pruning shears</b></col>, <cd>shears for pruning
trees, vines, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prunus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pru"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a plum
tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of trees with perigynous
rosaceous flowers, and a single two-ovuled carpel which usually becomes
a drupe in ripening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Originally, this genus was limited to the plums, then,
by Linn<ae/us, was made to include the cherries and the apricot. Later
botanists separated these into several genera, as <gen>Prunus</gen>,
<gen>Cerasus</gen>, and <gen>Armeniaca</gen>, but now, by Bentham and
Hooker, the plums, cherries, cherry laurels, peach, almond, and
nectarine are all placed in <gen>Prunus</gen>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruriency</ent><br/
<ent>Prurience</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pru"ri*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pru"ri*en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
prurient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pruriency</qex> of curious ears.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a <qex>prurience</qex> in the speech of some.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prurient</ent><br/
<hw>Pru"ri*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pruries</ets>, -<ets>entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>prurire</ets> to
itch. Cf. <er>Freeze</er>.]</ety> <def>Uneasy with desire; itching;
especially, having a lascivious curiosity or propensity; lustful.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Pru"ri*ent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eye of the vain and <qex>prurient</qex> is darting from object
to object of illicit attraction.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruriginous</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*rig"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pruriginosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>prurigineux</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Tending to, or caused by, prurigo; affected by,
or of the nature of, prurigo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prurigo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pru*ri"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., an itching,
the itch, fr. <ets>prurire</ets> to itch.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A papular disease of the skin, of which intense itching is the
chief symptom, the eruption scarcely differing from the healthy cuticle
in color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pruritus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pru*ri"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Itching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prussian</ent><br/
<hw>Prus"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Prussia</ets>, the country: cf. F. <ets>prussien</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to Prussia.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
native or inhabitant of Prussia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Prussian blue</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any one
of several complex double cyanides of ferrous and ferric iron;
specifically, a dark blue amorphous substance having a coppery luster,
obtained by adding a solution of potassium ferrocyanide (yellow
prussiate of potash) to a ferric salt. It is used in dyeing, in ink,
etc. Called also <altname>Williamson's blue</altname>,
<altname>insoluble Prussian blue</altname>, <altname>Berlin
blue</altname>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Prussian carp</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Gibel</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Prussian
green</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>Same as <altname>Berlin
green</altname>, under <er>Berlin</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prussiate</ent><br/
<hw>Prus"si*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prussiate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of prussic
acid; a cyanide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Red prussiate of potash</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Potassium ferricyanide</cref>, under <er>Ferricyanide</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Yellow prussiate of potash</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Potassium
ferrocyanide</cref>, under <er>Ferrocyanide</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prussic</ent><br/
<hw>Prus"sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>prussique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>designating the
acid now called hydrocyanic acid, but formerly called <xex>prussic
acid</xex>, because Prussian blue is derived from it or its compounds.
See <er>Hydrocyanic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prutenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pru*ten"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>
<def>Prussian; -- applied to certain astronomical tables published in
the sixteenth century, founded on the principles of Copernicus, a
Prussian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pry</ent><br/
<hw>Pry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted fr.
<ets>prize</ets> a lever. See <er>Prize</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A lever; also, leverage.</def> <mark>[Local, U. S. &
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pry pole</b></col>, <cd>the pole which forms the prop of
a hoisting gin, and stands facing the windlass.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pry</ent><br/
<hw>Pry</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pried</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Prying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To raise or move, or attempt to
raise or move, with a pry or lever; to prize.</def> <mark>[Local, U. S.
& Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pry</ent><br/
<hw>Pry</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>prien</ets>. Cf.
<er>Peer</er> to peep.]</ety> <def>To peep narrowly; to gaze; to inspect
closely; to attempt to discover something by a scrutinizing curiosity;
-- often implying reproach.</def> <ldquo/ To <xex>pry</xex> upon the
stars.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Watch thou and wake when others be asleep,<br/
To <qex>pry</qex> into the secrets of the state.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pry</ent><br/
<hw>Pry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Curious inspection; impertinent
peeping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pryan</ent><br/
<hw>Pry"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>See
<er>Prian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prying</ent><br/
<hw>Pry"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inspecting closely or
impertinently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Inquisitive; curious. See
<er>Inquisitive</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pryingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pry"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a prying manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prytaneum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pryt`a*ne"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ prytanis.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A public
building in certain Greek cities; especially, a public hall in Athens
regarded as the home of the community, in which official hospitality was
extended to distinguished citizens and strangers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prytanis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pryt"a*nis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Prytanes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A member of one of the ten sections into
which the Athenian senate of five hundred was divided, and to each of
which belonged the presidency of the senate for about one tenth of the
year.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prytany</ent><br/
<hw>Pryt"a*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The period during which the presidency of
the senate belonged to the prytanes of the section.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Prythee</ent><br/
<hw>Pryth"ee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>See
<er>Prithee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalm</ent><br/
<hw>Psalm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>psalm</ets>,
<ets>salm</ets>, AS. <ets>sealm</ets>, L. <ets>psalmus</ets>,
<ets>psalma</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, fr. <?/ to pull, twitch, to play
upon a stringed instrument, to sing to the harp: cf. OF.
<ets>psalme</ets>, <ets>salme</ets>, F. <ets>psaume</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A sacred song; a poetical composition for use in the
praise or worship of God.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Humus devout and holy <qex>psalms</qex><br/
Singing everlastingly.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, one of the hymns by David and others,
collected into one book of the Old Testament, or a modern metrical
version of such a hymn for public worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalm</ent><br/
<hw>Psalm</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To extol in psalms; to sing;
<as>as, <ex>psalming</ex> his praises</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmist</ent><br/
<hw>Psalm"ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>psalmista</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>psalmiste</ets>. See
<er>Psalm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A writer or composer of sacred
songs; -- a title particularly applied to David and the other authors of
the Scriptural psalms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A clerk, precentor, singer,
or leader of music, in the church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmistry</ent><br/
<hw>Psalm"ist*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The use of psalms
in devotion; psalmody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmodical</ent><br/
<ent>Psalmodic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Psal*mod"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Psal*mod"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>psalmodique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Relating to psalmody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmodist</ent><br/
<hw>Psal"mo*dist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sings
sacred songs; a psalmist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmodize</ent><br/
<hw>Psal"mo*dize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To practice
psalmody.</def> <ldquo/ The <xex>psalmodizing</xex> art.<rdquo/
<rj><au>J. G. Cooper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmody</ent><br/
<hw>Psal"mo*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/ psalm
+ <?/ a song, an ode: cf. F. <ets>psalmodie</ets>, LL.
<ets>psalmodia</ets>. See <er>Psalm</er>, and <er>Ode</er>.]</ety>
<def>The act, practice, or art of singing psalms or sacred songs; also,
psalms collectively, or a collection of psalms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmograph</ent><br/
<hw>Psal"mo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Psalmographer</er>.]</ety> <def>A writer of psalms; a
psalmographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmographist</ent><br/
<ent>Psalmographer</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Psal*mog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Psal*mog"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>psalmographus</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ a psalm + <?/ to write.]</ety>
<def>A writer of psalms, or sacred songs and hymns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalmography</ent><br/
<hw>Psal*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>psalmographie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or practice of writing
psalms, or sacred songs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalter</ent><br/
<hw>Psal"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>psauter</ets>, <ets>sauter</ets>, OF. <ets>sautier</ets>,
<ets>psaltier</ets>, F. <ets>psautier</ets>, from L.
<ets>psalterium</ets>. See <er>Psaltery</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The Book of Psalms; -- often applied to a book containing the
Psalms separately printed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, the Book of Psalms as printed in the
Book of Common Prayer; among the Roman Catholics, the part of the
Breviary which contains the Psalms arranged for each day of the
week.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A rosary, consisting of a
hundred and fifty beads, corresponding to the number of the
psalms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalterial</ent><br/
<hw>Psal*te"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the psalterium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psalterium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Psal*te"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Psalteria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a
psaltery.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The third stomach
of ruminants. See <er>Manyplies</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The lyra
of the brain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psaltery</ent><br/
<hw>Psal"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Psalteries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>sautrie</ets>,
OF. <ets>psalterie</ets>, F. <ets>psalt<eacute/rion</ets>, L.
<ets>psalterium</ets> psaltery, psalter, from Gr. <?/, fr. <?/. See
<er>Psalm</er>, <er>Psalter</er>.]</ety> <def>A stringed instrument of
music used by the Hebrews, the form of which is not known.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Praise the Lord with harp; sing unto him with the
<qex>psaltery</qex> and an instrument of ten strings.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
xxxiii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psammite</ent><br/
<hw>Psam"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ sandy, from
<?/ sand: cf. F. <ets>psammite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
species of micaceous sandstone.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Psam*mit"ic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psarolite</ent><br/
<hw>Psar"o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ speckled
+ <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A silicified stem
of tree fern, found in abundance in the Triassic sandstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psellism</ent><br/
<hw>Psel"lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
stammer.]</ety> <def>Indistinct pronunciation; stammering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psephism</ent><br/
<hw>Pse"phism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a decree,
fr. <?/ to vote with a pebble, fr. <?/ pebble.]</ety> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>A proposition adopted by a majority of votes;
especially, one adopted by vote of the Athenian people; a statute.</def>
<rj><au>J. P. Mahaffy.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudaesthesia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`d<ae/s*the"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>aesthesia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>False or imaginary feeling or sense
perception such as occurs in hypochondriasis, or such as is referred to
an organ that has been removed, as an amputated foot.</def><-- a phenom
also called phantom limbs --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudembryo</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu*dem"bry*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>embryo</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A false embryo.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An asexual
form from which the true embryo is produced by budding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudepigraphic</ent><br/
<ent>Pseudepigraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pseu*dep`i*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Pseu*dep`i*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to pseudepigraphy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudepigraphous</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`de*pig"ra*phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
falsely inscribed. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Epigraphy</er>.]</ety>
<def>Inscribed with a false name.</def> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudepigraphy</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`de*pig"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
ascription of false names of authors to works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudhaemal</ent><br/
<hw>Pseud*h<ae/"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>h<ae/mal</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the vascular system of
annelids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pseudh<ae/mal fluid</b></col>, <cd>the circulatory fluid,
or blood, of annelids, analogous to the blood of vertebrates. It is
often red, but is sometimes green or colorless.</cd> --
<col><b>Pseudh<ae/mal vessels</b></col>, <cd>the blood vessels of
annelids.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>pseydh`s</grk> lying,
false, akin to <grk>psey`dein</grk> to belie; cf. <grk>psydro`s</grk>
lying, <grk>psy`qos</grk> a lie.]</ety> <def>A combining form or prefix
signifying <xex>false</xex>, <xex>counterfeit</xex>,
<xex>pretended</xex>, <xex>spurious</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>pseudo</ex>-apostle, a false apostle; <ex>pseudo</ex>-clergy, false
or spurious clergy; <ex>pseudo</ex>-episcopacy, <ex>pseudo</ex>-form,
<ex>pseudo</ex>-martyr, <ex>pseudo</ex>-philosopher</as>. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudobacteria</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*bac*te"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>bacteria</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Microscopic organic particles, molecular
granules, powdered inorganic substances, etc., which in form, size, and
grouping resemble bacteria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>The globules which divide and develop in form of chains are
organized beings; when this does not occur, we are dealing with
<xex>pseudobacteria</xex>. <au>Sternberg.</au> </note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1157 --></p>

<p><ent>Pseudoblepsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*blep"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <grk>ble`psis</grk> sight.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>False or depraved sight; imaginary vision of
objects.</def> <rj><au>Forsyth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudobranch</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*branch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pseudobranchia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudobranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudobranchi<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A rudimentary branchia, or gill.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Pseu`do*bran"chi*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-bulb</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do-bulb`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>bulb</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An a<eum/rial corm, or thickened stem, as of some epiphytic
orchidaceous plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>That portion of an anthocarpous fruit which is not derived from the
ovary, as the soft part of a strawberry or of a fig.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-china</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-chi"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>china</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The false china root, a plant of the genus <gen>Smilax</gen>
(<spn>Smilax Pseudo-china</spn>), found in America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudocoele</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*c<oe/le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pseudoc<oe/lia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudocoelia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*c<oe/"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <?/ hollow.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The fifth ventricle in the mammalian brain. See
<er>Ventricle</er>.</def> <rj><au>B. G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-cone</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do-cone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>cone</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the soft gelatinous cones found in the compound eyes of
certain insects, taking the place of the crystalline cones of
others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-cumene</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-cu"mene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>cumene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A hydrocarbon of the aromatic series, metameric with mesitylene and
cumene, found in coal tar, and obtained as a colorless liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-dipteral</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-dip"ter*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>dipteral</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pseudodipt<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Falsely
or imperfectly dipteral, as a temple with the inner range of columns
surrounding the cella omitted, so that the space between the cella wall
and the columns is very great, being equal to two intercolumns and one
column.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A pseudo-dipteral
temple.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudodox</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*dox</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pseydo`doxos</grk>; <grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <grk>do`xa</grk> an
opinion.]</ety> <def>Not true in opinion or doctrine; false.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A false opinion or doctrine.</def> <ldquo/To
maintain the atheistical <xex>pseudodox</xex> which judgeth evil good,
and darkness light.<rdquo/ <rj><au>T. Adams.</au></rj> </def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudofilaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*fi*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudofilari<?/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Filaria</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the two elongated vibratile young formed
by fission of the embryo during the development of certain
Gregarin<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-galena</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-ga*le"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>galena</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>False galena, or blende. See <er>Blende</er>
<sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudograph</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pseudography</er>.]</ety> <def>A false writing; a spurious document;
a forgery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudography</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu*dog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <?/ to write.]</ety> <def>False writing;
forgery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudohalter</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*hal"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudohalteres</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Halteres</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the rudimentary front wings of certain
insects (<gen>Stylops</gen>). They resemble the halteres, or rudimentary
hind wings, of Diptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-heart</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do-heart`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>heart</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any contractile vessel of invertebrates which is not of the nature
of a real heart, especially one of those pertaining to the excretory
system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-hyperthophic</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-hy`per*thoph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>hypertrophic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Falsely hypertrophic; <as>as,
<ex>pseudo-hypertrophic</ex> paralysis, a variety of paralysis in which
the muscles are apparently enlarged, but are really degenerated and
replaced by fat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu*dol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>One who utters falsehoods; a liar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudology</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu*dol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <?/ speech: cf. F.
<ets>pseudologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Falsehood of speech.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-metallic</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-me*tal"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>metallic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Falsely or
imperfectly metallic; -- said of a kind of luster, as in
minerals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-monocotyledonous</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-mon`o*cot`y*led"on*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>monocotyledonous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having two coalescent cotyledons, as the live oak
and the horse-chestnut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudomorph</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*morph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pseudomorphous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An irregular or
deceptive form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A <cref>pseudomorphous
crystal</cref>, such as a crystal consisting of quartz, but having the
cubic form of fluor spar, the fluor crystal having been changed to
quartz by a process of substitution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudomorphism</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*mor"phism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>The state of having, or the property of
taking, a crystalline form unlike that which belongs to the
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudomorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <grk>morfh`</grk> form: cf. F.
<ets>pseudomorphe</ets>.]</ety> <def>Not having the true form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pseudomorphous crystal</b></col>, <cd>a crystal which has
a form that does not result from its own powers of
crystallization.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudonavicella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*nav`i*cel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudonavicull<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pseudonavicula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudonavicula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*na*vic"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudonavicul<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + NL. <ets>navicula</ets>,
a genus of diatoms. See <er>Navicular</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the minute spindle-shaped embryos of Gregarin<ae/ and some
other Protozoa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudoneuroptera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*neu*rop"te*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Neuroptera</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>division of insects <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
reticulated wings, as in the Neuroptera, but having an active pupa
state. It includes the dragon flies, May flies, white ants, etc. By some
Zoologists they are classed with the Orthoptera; by others, with the
Neuroptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudoneuropterous</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*neu*rop"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Pseudoneuroptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudonym</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*nym</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pseudonyme</ets>. See <er>Pseudonymous</er>.]</ety> <def>A
fictitious name assumed for the time, as by an author; a pen name; an
alias.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pseudonyme</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudonymity</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*nym"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The using of
fictitious names, as by authors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudonymous</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu*don"y*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + <?/, <?/, a name: cf. F.
<ets>pseudonyme</ets>. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Name</er>.]</ety>
<def>Bearing a false or fictitious name; <as>as, a <ex>pseudonymous</ex>
work</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pseu*don"y*mous*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Pseu*don"y*mous*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-peripteral</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-pe*rip"ter*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>peripteral</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pseudop<eacute/ript<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Falsely or imperfectly peripteral, as a temple having the columns
at the sides attached to the walls, and an ambulatory only at the ends
or only at one end.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
pseudo-peripteral temple.</def> <rj><au>Oxf. Gloss.</au></rj>
</def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudopod</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu"do*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>-pod</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Any protoplasmic filament or irregular process
projecting from any unicellular organism, or from any animal or plant
call.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A rhizopod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudopodial</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*po"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a pseudopod, or to pseudopodia. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Heliozoa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudopodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudopodia</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Pseudopod</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudopupa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*pu"pa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> L. <plw>Pseudopup<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E.
<plw>Pseudopupas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Pupa</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
stage intermediate between the larva and pupa of bees and certain other
hymenopterous insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudorhabdite</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*rhab"dite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a rod.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the peculiar rodlike corpuscles found in the integument of
certain Turbellaria. They are filled with a soft granular
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-romantic</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-ro*man"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety><ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>romantic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Falsely
romantic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The false taste, the <qex>pseudo-romantic</qex> rage.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>An instrument which exhibits objects with their proper relief
reversed; -- an effect opposite to that produced by the
stereoscope.</def> <rj><au>Wheatstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudoscopic</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or formed by, a pseudoscope; having its parts
appearing with the relief reversed; <as>as, a <ex>pseudoscopic</ex>
image</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudoscorpiones</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*scor`pi*o"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Scorpion</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of Arachnoidea having the palpi
terminated by large claws, as in the scorpions, but destitute of a
caudal sting; the false scorpions. Called also <ord>Pseudoscorpii</ord>,
and <ord>Pseudoscorpionina</ord>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Book
scorpion</cref>, under Book.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudosphere</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*sphere`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>sphere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>The surface of constant negative curvature generated by the
revolution of a tractrix. This surface corresponds in non-Euclidian
space to the sphere in ordinary space. An important property of the
surface is that any figure drawn upon it can be displaced in any way
without tearing it or altering in size any of its elements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudospore</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu"do*spore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A peculiar reproductive cell found in some fungi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudostella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*stel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-l<ae/</plw>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>pseydh`s</grk> false + L. <ets>stella</ets> star.]</ety>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Any starlike meteor or phenomenon.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudostoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu*dos"to*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudostomata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Stoma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A group of cells resembling a stoma, but without any true aperture
among them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-symmetric</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-sym*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Exhibiting pseudo-symmetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudo-symmetry</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do-sym"me*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>symmetry</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A kind of symmetry characteristic of
certain crystals which from twinning, or other causes, come to resemble
forms of a system other than that to which they belong, as the
apparently hexagonal prisms of aragonite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudotetramera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*te*tram"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Tetramerous</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of beetles having the fifth tarsal
joint minute and obscure, so that there appear to be but four
joints.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pseu`do*te*tram"er*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudotinea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu`do*tin"e*a</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pseudotine<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>, and <er>Tinea</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The bee moth, or wax moth
(<gen>Galleria</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudoturbinal</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu`do*tur"bi*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> + <ets>turbinal</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>See under <er>Turbinal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudovary</ent><br/
<hw>Pseu*do"va*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pseudovaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[<ets>Pseudo-</ets> +
<ets>ovary</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The organ in which
pseudova are produced; -- called also
<altname>pseudovarium</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pseudovum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pseu*do"vum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pseudova</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Pseudo-</er>,
and <er>Ovum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An egglike germ
produced by the agamic females of some insects and other animals, and by
the larv<ae/ of certain insects. It is capable of development without
fertilization. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>P<ae/dogenesis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pshaw</ent><br/
<hw>Pshaw</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[Of imitative
origin.]</ety> <def>Pish! pooch! -- an exclamation used as an expression
of contempt, disdain, dislike, etc.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>psha</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pshaw</ent><br/
<hw>Pshaw</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To express disgust or
contemptuous disapprobation, as by the exclamation <ldquo/
<xex>Pshaw</xex>!<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The goodman used regularly to frown and <qex>pshaw</qex> wherever
this topic was touched upon.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilanthropic</ent><br/
<hw>Psi`lan*throp"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[see
<er>Psilanthropist</er>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or embodying,
psilanthropy. <ldquo/A <xex>psilanthropic</xex>
explanation.<rdquo/</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilanthropism</ent><br/
<hw>Psi*lan"thro*pism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Psilanthropy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilanthropist</ent><br/
<hw>Psi*lan"thro*pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
bare, mere + <?/ a man.]</ety> <def>One who believes that Christ was a
mere man.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilanthropy</ent><br/
<hw>Psi*lan"thro*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrine
of the merely human existence of Christ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilology</ent><br/
<hw>Psi*lol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr <?/ mere +
<ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Love of empty of empty talk or
noise.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilomelane</ent><br/
<hw>Psi*lom"e*lane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ bare +
<?/, <?/, black.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous oxide of
manganese, occurring in smooth, botryoidal forms, and massive, and
having an iron-black or steel-gray color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilopaedes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Psi`lo*p<ae/"des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr.<?/ bare + <?/, <?/, offspring.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>birds whose young at first have down on the pteryl<ae/ only; --
called also <altname>Gymnop<ae/des</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilopaedic</ent><br/
<hw>Psi`lo*p<ae/d"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having down upon the pteryl<ae/ only; -- said of the young of
certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psilosopher</ent><br/
<hw>Psi*los"o*pher</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ bare, mere + <?/
wise.]</ety> <def>A superficial or narrow pretender to philosophy; a
sham philosopher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psittacid</ent><br/
<ent>Psittaceous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Psit*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Psit"ta*cid</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>psittacus</ets> a
parrot, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>psittacide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the parrots, or the Psittaci.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Psittaci.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psittaci</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Psit"ta*ci</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The order of birds which comprises the
parrots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psitta-co-fulvine</ent><br/
<hw>Psit`ta-co-ful"*vine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
a parrot + L. <ets>fulvus</ets> yellow.]</ety> <def>A yellow pigment
found in the feathers of certain parrots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psoas</ent><br/
<hw>Pso"as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a muscle of
the loin: cf. f. <ets>psoas</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>An
internal muscle arising from the lumbar vertebr<ae/ and inserted into
the femur. In man there are usually two on each side, and the larger
one, or great psoas, forms a part of the iliopsoas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pso"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A cutaneous disease; especially, the
itch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psoriasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pso*ri"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ psora.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The state
of being affected with psora.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A cutaneous disease, characterized by imbricated silvery scales,
affecting only the superficial layers of the skin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psoric</ent><br/
<hw>Pso"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>psoricus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>psorique</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to psora.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psorosperm</ent><br/
<hw>Pso"ro*sperm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ itching
+ <?/ seed.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A minute parasite, usually
the young of Gregarin<ae/, in the pseudonavicula stage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychagogic</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cha*gog"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/. See
<er>Psychagogue</er>.]</ety> <def>Attractive; persuasive.</def>
<rj><au>J. Morley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychagogue</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"cha*gogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.<?/; <?/ the
soul + <?/ to lead.]</ety> <def>A necromancer.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychal</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"chal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Psychical</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the soul;
psychical.</def> <rj><au>Bayne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychanalysis</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cha*nal"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>analysis</ets>.]</ety> <def>same as
<er>psychoanalysis</er>; -- an older term now obsolete.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Psy*chan`a*lyt"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Psy`cha*nal"y*sist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psyche</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"che</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>PSychh`</grk> Psyche, fr. <grk>psychh`</grk> the soul.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class Myth.)</fld> <def>A lovely maiden, daughter of a
king and mistress of Eros, or Cupid. She is regarded as the
personification of the soul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The soul; the vital principle; the mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>psych<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>A cheval
glass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychian</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"chi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any small moth of the genus <gen>Psyche</gen> and allied genera
(family <fam>Psychid<ae/</fam>). The larv<ae/ are called <xex>basket
worms</xex>. See <cref>Basket worm</cref>, under
<er>Basket</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychiatry</ent><br/
<ent>Psychiatria</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Psy*chi`a*tri"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Psy*chi"a*try</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>psychiatria</ets>,
fr. Gr. <?/ the mind + <?/ healing.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
application of the healing art to mental diseases.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychiatric</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`chi*at"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to psychiatry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychical</ent><br/
<ent>Psychic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Psy"chic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Psy"chic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>psychicus</ets>, Gr.
<?/, fr. <grk>psychh`</grk> the soul, mind; cf. <?/ to blow: cf. F.
<ets>psychique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the
human soul, or to the living principle in man.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This term was formerly used to express the same idea as
<xex>psychological</xex>. Recent metaphysicians, however, have employed
it to mark the difference between <grk>psychh`</grk> the living
principle in man, and <grk>pney^ma</grk> the rational or spiritual part
of his nature. In this use, the word describes the human soul in its
relation to sense, appetite, and the outer visible world, as
distinguished from spiritual or rational faculties, which have to do
with the supersensible world. <au>Heyse.</au> </note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the mind, or its functions and
diseases; mental; -- contrasted with <contr>physical</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Psychical blindness</b></col>, <col><b>Psychical
deafness</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>forms of nervous
disease in which, while the senses of sight and hearing remain
unimpaired, the mind fails to appreciate the significance of the sounds
heard or the images seen.</cd> -- <col><b>Psychical contagion</b></col>,
<cd>the transference of disease, especially of a functional nervous
disease, by mere force of example.</cd> -- <col><b>Psychical
medicine</b></col>, <cd>that department of medicine which treats of
mental diseases.</cd></cs><-- psychiatry? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychics</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"chics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Psychology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychism</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"chism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>psychisme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>The doctrine of
Quesne, that there is a fluid universally diffused, end equally
animating all living beings, the difference in their actions being due
to the difference of the individual organizations.</def>
<rj><au>Fleming.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1158 --></p>

<p><ent>Psycho-</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"cho-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>psychh`</grk> <xex>the soul</xex>, <xex>the mind</xex>, <xex>the
understanding</xex>; <as>as, <ex>psycho</ex>logy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>psychoanalyze</ent><br/
<hw>psy`cho*an"a*lyze</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To investigate or
subject to treatment by psychoanalysis.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> analyze, analyse, psychoanalyse.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>psychoanalyse</ent><br/
<hw>psy`cho*an"a*lyse</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To investigate or
subject to treatment by psychoanalysis.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> analyze, analyse, psychoanalyze.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychoanalysis</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*a*nal"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A method or process of psychotherapeutic analysis and treatment pf
psychoneuroses, based on the work of Dr. Sigmund Freud (1856- 1939) of
Vienna. The method rests upon the theory that neurosis is
characteristically due to repression of desires consciously rejected but
subconsciously persistent; it consists in a close analysis of the
patient's mental history, effort being made to bring unconsciuos and
preconscious material to consciousness; the methods include analysis of
transferance and resistance. In some variants, stress is laid upon the
dream life, and of treatment by means of suggestion.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The theory of human psychology which is the
foundation for the psychoanalytic therapy, which explores the relation
between conscious and unconscious mental processes in motivating human
behavior and causing neuroses.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An integrated set of theories of human personality
development, motivation, and behavior based on a body of
observations.</def> <au>[Stedman]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One of several schools of psychotherapy, such as
<stype>jungian psychoanalysis</stype> or <stype>freudian
psychoanalysis</stype>.</def> <au>[Stedman]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Psy`cho*an`a*lyt"ic</wf>, <pos>a.</pos>;
<wf>Psych`o*an"al*ist</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
etc.</wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychogenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Genesis
through an internal force, as opposed to <contr>natural
selection</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychography</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
description of the phenomena of mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Spiritualism)</fld> <def>Spirit writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychological</ent><br/
<ent>Psychologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Psy`cho*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Psy`cho*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>psychologique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to psychology.
See Note under <er>Psychic</er>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Psy`cho*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychologist</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>psychologiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who is versed in, devoted to,
psychology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychologue</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"cho*logue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
psychologist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychology</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Psychologies</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> +
<ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>psychologie</ets>. See
<er>Psychical</er>.]</ety> <def>The science of the human soul;
specifically, the systematic or scientific knowledge of the powers and
functions of the human soul, so far as they are known by consciousness;
a treatise on the human soul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Psychology</qex>, the science conversant about the phenomena
of the mind, or conscious subject, or self.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychomachy</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chom"a*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>psychomachia</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>psychh`</grk> the soul + <?/
fight: cf. <?/ desperate fighting.]</ety> <def>A conflict of the soul
with the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"cho*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>-mancy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>psychomancie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Necromancy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychometry</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The art of measuring the duration of mental
processes, or of determining the time relations of mental
phenomena.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Psy`cho*met"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psycho-motor</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho-mo"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>motor</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to movement produced by action of the mind or will.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychopannychism</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"cho*pan"ny*chism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to spend all night long;
<grk>pa^s</grk>, <grk>pa^n</grk>, all + <?/ night.]</ety>
<fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine that the soul falls asleep at
death, and does not wake until the resurrection of the body.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Psy`cho*pan"ny*chism</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychopathy</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chop"a*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + Gr. <?/, <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Mental disease. See <er>Psychosis</er>, 2.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Psy`cho*path"ic</wf>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Psy*chop"a*thist</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychophysical</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*phys"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to psychophysics; involving the action or mutual relations of
the psychical and physical in man.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Psychophysical time</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>,
<cd>the time required for the mind to transform a sensory impression
into a motor impulse. It is an important part of
<xex>physiological</xex> or <xex>reaction time</xex>. See under
<er>Reaction</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychophysics</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*phys"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>physics</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science
of the connection between nerve action and consciousness; the science
which treats of the relations of the psychical and physical in their
conjoint operation in man; the doctrine of the relation of function or
dependence between body and soul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychopomp</ent><br/
<hw>Psy"cho*pomp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>psychh`</grk> the soul + <?/ to send: cf. F.
<ets>psychopompe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Myth.)</fld> <def>A leader or guide
of souls .</def> <rj><au>J. Fiske.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychosis</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*cho"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Psycho-</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Any vital action or activity.</def>
<rj><au>Mivart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease of the mind; especially,
a functional mental disorder, that is, one unattended with evident
organic changes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychotherapeutics</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*ther`a*peu"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>therapeutics</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The treatment of disease by acting on the mind,
as by suggestion; mind cure; psychotherapy.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychotherapy</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*ther"a*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + <ets>therapy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Psychotherapeutics.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychozoic</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`cho*zo"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Psycho-</ets> + Gr. <?/ life.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Designating, or applied to the Era of man; <as>as, the
<ex>psychozoic</ex> era</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychrometer</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chrom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>psychro`s</grk> cold + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>psychrom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for measuring
the tension of the aqueous vapor in the atmosphere, being essentially a
wet and dry bulb hygrometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychrometrical</ent><br/
<hw>Psy`chro*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the psychrometer or psychrometry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psychrometry</ent><br/
<hw>Psy*chrom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Hygrometry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Psylla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Psyl"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Psyll<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/ a
flea.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any leaping plant louse of the
genus <gen>Psylla</gen>, or family <fam>Psyllid<ae/</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptarmigan</ent><br/
<hw>Ptar"mi*gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>tarmachan</ets>; cf. Ir. <ets>tarmochan</ets>,
<ets>tarmonach</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any grouse of the
genus <gen>Lagopus</gen>, of which numerous species are known. The feet
are completely feathered. Most of the species are brown in summer, but
turn white, or nearly white, in winter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They chiefly inhabit the northern countries and high
mountains of Europe, Asia, and America. The common European species is
<spn>Lagopus mutus</spn>. The Scotch grouse, red grouse, or moor fowl
(<spn>Lagopus Scoticus</spn>), is reddish brown, and does not turn white
in winter. The white, or willow, ptarmigan (<spn>Lagopus albus</spn>) is
found in both Europe and America.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptenoglossa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte`no*glos"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr.<?/ feathered + <?/ tongue.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
division of gastropod mollusks having the teeth of the radula arranged
in long transverse rows, somewhat like the barbs of a feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptenoglossate</ent><br/
<hw>Pte`no*glos"sate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Ptenoglossa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteranodon</ent><br/
<hw>Pte*ran"o*don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ wing +
<?/ priv. + <?/, <?/, a tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus
of American Cretaceous pterodactyls destitute of teeth. Several species
are known, some of which had an expanse of wings of twenty feet or
more.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteranodontia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte*ran`o*don"ti*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A group of pterodactyls
destitute of teeth, as in the genus <gen>Pteranodon</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterichthys</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte*rich"thys</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ wing + <?/ fish.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of
Devonian fossil fishes with winglike appendages. The head and most of
the body were covered with large bony plates. See
<er>Placodermi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteridologist</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`i*dol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
versed in pteridology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteridology</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`i*dol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
a fern + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That department of botany which
treats of ferns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteridomania</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`i*do*ma"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, a fern + E. <ets>mania</ets>.]</ety> <def>A madness, craze, or
strong fancy, for ferns.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>C.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteridophyta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`i*doph"y*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/, <?/, a fern + <?/ a plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
class of flowerless plants, embracing ferns, horsetails, club mosses,
quillworts, and other like plants. See the Note under
<er>Cryptogamia</er>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pter"i*do*phyte`</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This is a modern term, devised to replace the older ones
<xex>acrogens</xex> and <xex>vascular Cryptogamia</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterobranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`o*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a wing + <?/ <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An order of marine Bryozoa, having a bilobed lophophore and an
axial cord. The genus <gen>Rhabdopleura</gen> is the type. Called also
<altname><ord>Podostomata</ord></altname>. See
<er>Rhabdopleura</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteroceras</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte*roc"e*ras</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ a wing + <?/ a horn.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
large marine gastropods having the outer border of the lip divided into
lobes; -- called also <altname>scorpion shell</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterocletes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`o*cle"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr <ets>Pterocles</ets>, the typical genus, fr. Gr. <?/ feather + <?/,
<?/, a key, tongue of a clasp.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division
of birds including the sand grouse. They are in some respects
intermediate between the pigeons and true grouse. Called also
<altname>Pteroclomorph<ae/</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterodactyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`o*dac"tyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a wing
+ <?/ finger, toe: cf. F. <ets>pt<eacute/rodactyle</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct flying reptile; one of the
Pterosauria. See <xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterodactyli</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`o*dac"ty*li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pterosauria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteroglossal</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`o*glos"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
feather + <?/ tongue.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the tongue
finely notched along the sides, so as to have a featherlike appearance,
as the toucans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteron</ent><br/
<hw>Pte"ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
wing.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The region of the skull, in the
temporal fossa back of the orbit, where the great wing of the sphenoid,
the temporal, the parietal, and the frontal hones approach each
other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteropappi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`o*pap"pi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/ a feather, a bird + <?/ a grandfather.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Odontotorm<ae/</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterophore</ent><br/
<hw>Pter"o*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
feather + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
moth of the genus <gen>Pterophorus</gen> and allied genera; a plume
moth. See <cref>Plume moth</cref>, under <er>Plume</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteropod</ent><br/
<hw>Pter"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
wing-footed; <?/ a feather, wing + <?/, <?/, foot: cf. F.
<ets>pt<eacute/ropode</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
Pteropoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteropoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte*rop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A class of Mollusca in which
the anterior lobes of the foot are developed in the form of broad, thin,
winglike organs, with which they swim at near the surface of the
sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The Pteropoda are divided into two orders:
<ord>Cymnosomata</ord>, which have the body entirely naked and the head
distinct from the wings; and <ord>Thecosomata</ord>, which have a
delicate transparent shell of various forms, and the head not distinct
from the wings.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteropodous</ent><br/
<hw>Pte*rop"o*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Pteropoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterosaur</ent><br/
<hw>Pter"o*saur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ wind +
<?/ a lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A pterodactyl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterosauria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`o*sau"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct order of flying
reptiles of the Mesozoic age; the pterodactyls; -- called also
<altname><ord>Pterodactyli</ord></altname>, and
<altname><ord>Ornithosauria</ord></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The wings were formed, like those of bats, by a leathery
expansion of the skin, principally supported by the greatly enlarged
outer or <ldquo/ little<rdquo/ fingers of the hands. The American
Cretaceous pterodactyls had no teeth. See <er>Pteranodontia</er>, and
<er>Pterodactyl</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterosaurian</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`o*sau"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Pterosauria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterostigma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`o*stig"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pterostigmata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ wing + <?/, <?/, a mark.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A thickened opaque spot on the wings of certain
insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterotic</ent><br/
<hw>Pte*ro"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ wing +
<?/, <?/, ear.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to, or
designating, a bone between the prootic and epiotic in the dorsal and
outer part of the periotic capsule of many fishes.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The pterotic bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>pterotic bone</xex> is so called because
fancied in some cases to resemble in form a bird's wing</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterygium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte*ryg"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
E. <plw>Pterygiums</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Pterygia</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, properly a dim, akin to <?/
a feather.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A superficial growth of
vascular tissue radiating in a fanlike manner from the cornea over the
surface of the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterygoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pter"y*goid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, a
wing + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Like
a bird's wing in form; <as>as, a <ex>pterygoid</ex> bone</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of, pertaining to, or in the region of, the pterygoid
bones, pterygoid processes, or the whole sphenoid bone.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A pterygoid bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pterygoid bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a bone
which corresponds to the inner plate of the pterygoid process of the
human skull, but which, in all vertebrates below mammals, is not
connected with the posterior nares, but serves to connect the palatine
bones with the point of suspension of the lower jaw.</cd> --
<col><b>Pterygoid process</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a process
projecting downward from either side of the sphenoid bone, in man
divided into two plates, an inner and an outer. The posterior nares pass
through the space, called the <xex>pterygoid fossa</xex>, between the
processes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterygomaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`y*go*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pterygoid</ets> + <ets>maxillary</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the inner pterygoid plate,
or pterygoid bone, and the lower jaw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterygopalatine</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`y*go*pal"a*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pterygoid</ets> + <ets>palatine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pterygoid processes and
the palatine bones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterygopodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`y*go*po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pterygopodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, a fin + <?/, dim. of <?/, <?/, a foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A specially modified part of the ventral fin in
male elasmobranchs, which serves as a copulatory organ, or
clasper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterygoquadrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`y*go*quad"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pterygoid</ets> + <ets>quadrate</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or representing the pterygoid
and quadrate bones or cartilages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pteryla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pte*ry"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pteryl<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ feather
+ <?/ wood, forest.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the definite
areas of the skin of a bird on which feathers grow; -- contrasted with
<contr>apteria</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterylography</ent><br/
<hw>Pter`y*log"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pteryla</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The study or description of the arrangement of feathers, or of the
pteryl<ae/, of birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pterylosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pter`y*lo"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
NL. & E. <ets>pteryla</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
arrangement of feathers in definite areas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptilocerque</ent><br/
<hw>Ptil"o*cerque</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
feather + <?/ tail.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
pentail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptilopaedes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ptil`o*p<ae/"des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a feather + <?/, <?/, offspring.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Dasyp<ae/des</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptilopaedic</ent><br/
<hw>Ptil`o*p<ae/d"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having nearly the whole surface of the skin covered with down;
dasyp<ae/dic; -- said of the young of certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptilopteri</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pti*lop"te*ri</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a downy feather + <?/ wing.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An order of birds including only the penguins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptilosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pti*lo"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr
<?/ a feather.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pterylosis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptisan</ent><br/
<hw>Ptis"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>ptisana</ets>
peeled barley, barley water, Gr. <?/, from <?/ to peel, husk; cf. F.
<ets>ptisane</ets>, <ets>tisane</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
decoction of barley with other ingredients; a farinaceous
drink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An aqueous medicine, containing
little, if any, medicinal agent; a tea or tisane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptolemaic</ent><br/
<hw>Ptol`e*ma"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to <xex>Ptolemy</xex>, the geographer and astronomer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ptolemaic system</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the
system maintained by Ptolemy, who supposed the earth to be fixed in the
center of the universe, with the sun and stars revolving around it. This
theory was received for ages, until superseded by the Copernican
system.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptolemaist</ent><br/
<hw>Ptol"e*ma`ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who accepts
the astronomical system of Ptolemy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptomaine</ent><br/
<hw>Pto"ma*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/ a
dead body.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>One of a class of
animal bases or alkaloids formed in the putrefaction of various kinds of
albuminous matter, and closely related to the vegetable alkaloids; a
cadaveric poison. The ptomaines, as a class, have their origin in dead
matter, by which they are to be distinguished from the
<xex>leucomaines</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pto"sis</hw> <pr>(t<omac/"s<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>ptw^sis</grk> a falling.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Drooping of the upper eyelid, produced by
paralysis of its levator muscle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1159 --></p>

<p><ent>ptyalin</ent><br/
<hw>pty"a*lin</hw> <pr>(t<imac/"<adot/*l<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>pty`alon</grk> spittle. See <er>Ptyalism</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>An unorganized amylolytic ferment, on
enzyme, present in human mixed saliva and in the saliva of some
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptyalism</ent><br/
<hw>Pty"a*lism</hw> <pr>(t<imac/"<adot/*l<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>ptyalismo`s</grk>, fr. <grk>ptyali`zein</grk> to spit
much, fr. <grk>pty`alon</grk> spittle, fr. <grk>pty`ein</grk> to spit:
cf. F. <ets>ptyalisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Salivation, or an excessive
flow of saliva.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptyalogogue</ent><br/
<hw>Pty*al"o*gogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
spittle + <?/ driving.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
ptysmagogue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptysmagogue</ent><br/
<hw>Ptys"ma*gogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ spittle
+ <?/ driving: cf. F. <ets>ptysmagogue</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A medicine that promotes the discharge of saliva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ptyxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ptyx"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>pty`xis</grk> a folding.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The way in
which a leaf is sometimes folded in the bud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pub</ent><br/
<hw>pub</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Shortened from
<ets>Public house</ets>.]</ety> <def>A retail business where alcoholic
beverages are sold by the drink; a bar; a tavern.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubble</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps fr.
<ets>bubble</ets>.]</ety> <def>Puffed out, pursy; pudgy; fat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pub-crawl</ent><br/
<hw>pub-crawl</hw> <pos>v.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fr>1</fr> <def>to go from
one pub or tavern to the next and get progressively more
drunk.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bar hop.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puberal</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ber*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>puber</ets>, <ets>pubes</ets>, grown up, adult.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to puberty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puberty</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ber*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pubertas</ets>, fr. <ets>puber</ets>, <ets>pubes</ets>, adult: cf.
F. <ets>pubert<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The earliest age
at which persons are capable of begetting or bearing children, usually
considered, in temperate climates, to be about fourteen years in males
and twelve in females.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The period when a plant first
bears flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puberulent</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*ber"u*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pubis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Very minutely
downy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubes</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"bes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the hair which
appears on the body at puberty, from <ets>pubes</ets> adult.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hair which appears
upon the lower part of the hypogastric region at the age of
puberty.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Hence (as more commonly used), the
lower part of the hypogastric region; the pubic region.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The down of plants; a downy or
villous substance which grows on plants; pubescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubescence</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*bes"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pubescence</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
being pubescent, or of having arrived at puberty.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A covering of soft short hairs, or down, as one some
plants and insects; also, the state of being so covered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubescency</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*bes"cen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Pubescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubescent</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*bes"cent</hw> <pr>(p<usl/"b<ecr/s"s<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pubescens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>pubescere</ets> to reach puberty, to grow hairy or mossy, fr.
<ets>pubes</ets> pubes: cf. F. <ets>pubescent</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Arrived recently at puberty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That . . . the men (are) <qex>pubescent</qex> at the age of twice
seven, is accounted a punctual truth.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Covered with pubescence, or fine short hairs, as
certain insects, and the leaves of some plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubic</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"bic</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"b<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pubes; in the region of
the pubes; <as>as, the <ex>pubic</ex> bone; the <ex>pubic</ex> region,
or the lower part of the hypogastric region</as>. See
<er>Pubes</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the
pubis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pubis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu"bis</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"b<icr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pubes</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The ventral and
anterior of the three principal bones composing either half of the
pelvis; sharebone; pubic bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Public</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>publicus</ets>, <ets>poblicus</ets>, fr. <ets>populus</ets> people:
cf. F. <ets>public</ets>. See <er>People</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to the people; belonging to the people; relating
to, or affecting, a nation, state, or community; -- opposed to
<ant>private</ant>; <as>as, the <ex>public</ex> treasury</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To the <qex>public</qex> good<br/
Private respects must yield.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [Alexander Hamilton] touched the dead corpse of the
<qex>public</qex> credit, and it sprung upon its feet.</q> <rj><qau>D.
Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Open to the knowledge or view of all; general;
common; notorious; <as>as, <ex>public</ex> report; <ex>public</ex>
scandal.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Joseph, . . . not willing to make her a <qex>public</qex> example,
was minded to put her away privily.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. i.
19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Open to common or general use; <as>as, a
<ex>public</ex> road; a <ex>public</ex> house.</as></def> <ldquo/The
<xex>public</xex> street.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>public act</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>public
statute</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an act or statute
affecting matters of public concern. Of such statutes the courts take
judicial notice.</cd> -- <col><b>Public credit</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Credit</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Public funds</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Fund</er>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>Public house</b></col>, <cd>an inn, or
house of entertainment.</cd> -- <col><b>Public law</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>See <cref>International law</cref>, under
<er>International</er>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A public act or
statute.</cd> -- <col><b>Public nuisance</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Nuisance</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Public
orator</b></col>. <fld>(Eng. Universities)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Orator</er>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>Public stores</b></col>,
<cd>military and naval stores, equipments, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Public
works</b></col>, <cd>all fixed works built by civil engineers for public
use, as railways, docks, canals, etc.; but strictly, military and civil
engineering works constructed at the public cost.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Public</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The general body of
mankind, or of a nation, state, or community; the people, indefinitely;
<as>as, the American <ex>public</ex></as>; also, a particular body or
aggregation of people; <as>as, an author's
<ex>public</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>public</qex> is more disposed to censure than to
praise.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A public house; an inn.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In public</b></col>, <cd>openly; before an audience or
the people at large; not in private or secrecy.</cd> <ldquo/We are to
speak <xex>in public</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publican</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"li*can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>publicanus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>publicain</ets>. See
<er>Public</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A
farmer of the taxes and public revenues; hence, a collector of toll or
tribute. The inferior officers of this class were often oppressive in
their exactions, and were regarded with great detestation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As Jesus at meat . . . many <qex>publicans</qex> and sinners came
and sat down with him and his disciples.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. 1x.
10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How like a fawning <qex>publican</qex> he looks!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The keeper of an inn or public house; one licensed
to retail beer, spirits, or wine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publication</ent><br/
<hw>Pub`li*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>publicatio</ets> confiscation: cf. F. <ets>publication</ets>. See
<er>Publish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of publishing or
making known; notification to the people at large, either by words,
writing, or printing; proclamation; divulgation; promulgation; <as>as,
the <ex>publication</ex> of the law at Mount Sinai; the
<ex>publication</ex> of the gospel; the <ex>publication</ex> of statutes
or edicts.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of offering a book, pamphlet, engraving,
etc., to the public by sale or by gratuitous distribution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>publication</qex> of these papers was not owing to our
folly, but that of others.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is published or made known; especially,
any book, pamphlet, etc., offered for sale or to public notice; <as>as,
a daily or monthly <ex>publication</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An act done in public.</def> <mark>[R. &
Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His jealousy . . . attends the business, the recreations, the
<qex>publications</qex>, and retirements of every man.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Publication of a libel</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>such an exhibition of a libel as brings it to the notice of at least
one person other than the person libeled.</cd> -- <col><b>Publication of
a will</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the delivery of a will, as his
own, by a testator to witnesses who attest it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Public-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Public-spirited.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publicist</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"li*cist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>publiciste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A writer on the laws of nature and
nations; one who is versed in the science of public right, the
principles of government, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Whig leaders, however, were much more desirous to get rid of
Episcopacy than to prove themselves consummate <qex>publicists</qex> and
logicians.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. One who publicizes, esp. a press agent. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publicity</ent><br/
<hw>Pub*lic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>publicit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
public, or open to the knowledge of a community; notoriety;
publicness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publicity pamphlet</ent><br/
<hw>Publicity pamphlet</hw>. <def>A pamphlet which, in some States of
the United States having the initiative or referendum, is mailed to the
voters to inform them as to the nature of a measure submitted by the
initiative or referendum. The pamphlet contains a copy of the proposed
law and arguments for and against it by those favoring and opposing it,
respectively.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>publicize</ent><br/
<hw>publicize</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to make public; to
announce.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> publicise, air, bare.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>To announce in a
manner intended to reach a large audience.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> advertise, advertize, call attention to.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>] [<source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publicly</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>With
exposure to popular view or notice; without concealment; openly; <as>as,
property <ex>publicly</ex> offered for sale; an opinion
<ex>publicly</ex> avowed; a declaration <ex>publicly</ex>
made.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the name of the community.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Public-minded</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic-mind`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Public-spirited.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pub"lic-mind`ed*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publicness</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being public, or open to the view or notice of people at large;
publicity; notoriety; <as>as, the <ex>publicness</ex> of a
sale</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of belonging to the community;
<as>as, the <ex>publicness</ex> of property</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Public school</ent><br/
<hw>Public school</hw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In Great Britain, any of
various schools maintained by the community, wholly or partly under
public control, or maintained largely by endowment and not carried on
chiefly for profit; specif., and commonly, any of various select and
usually expensive endowed schools which give a liberal modern education
or prepare pupils for the universities. Eton, Harrow, Rugby, and
Winchester are of this class.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In the United
States, a free primary, grammar, or high school maintained by the local
government.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Quasi-public corporation</ent><br/
<ent>Public-service corporation</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Public-service corporation</hw> or sometimes <hw>Quasi-public
corporation</hw> }</mhw>. <def>A corporation, such as a railroad
company, lighting company, water company, etc., organized or chartered
to follow a public calling or to render services more or less essential
to the general public convenience or safety.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Public-spirited</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lic-spir`it*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having, or exercising, a disposition to advance the interest of the
community or public; <as>as, <ex>public-spirited</ex>
men</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dictated by a regard to public good; <as>as, a
<ex>public-spirited</ex> project or measure</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pub"lic-spir`it*ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pub"lic-spir`it*ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publish</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Published</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Publishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>publier</ets>, L. <ets>publicare</ets>, <ets>publicatum</ets>. See
<er>Public</er>, and <er>-ish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make
public; to make known to mankind, or to people in general; to divulge,
as a private transaction; to promulgate or proclaim, as a law or an
edict.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Published</qex> was the bounty of her name.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The unwearied sun, from day to day,<br/
Does his Creator's power display,<br/
And <qex>publishes</qex> to every land<br/
The work of an almighty hand.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make known by posting, or by reading in a church;
<as>as, to <ex>publish</ex> banns of marriage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To send forth, as a book, newspaper, musical piece,
or other printed work, either for sale or for general distribution; to
print, and issue from the press.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To utter, or put into circulation; <as>as, to
<ex>publish</ex> counterfeit paper</as>.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To publish a will</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to
acknowledge it before the witnesses as the testator's last will and
testament.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To announce; proclaim; advertise; declare;
promulgate; disclose; divulge; reveal. See <er>Announce</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publishable</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lish*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being published; suitable for publication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publisher</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lish*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who publishes;
<as>as, a <ex>publisher</ex> of a book or magazine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For love of you, not hate unto my friend,<br/
Hath made me <qex>publisher</qex> of this pretense.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Publishment</ent><br/
<hw>Pub"lish*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act or process of making publicly known; publication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A public notice of intended marriage, required by
the laws of some States.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puccoon</ent><br/
<hw>Puc*coon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the American
Indian name.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any one of several plants
yielding a red pigment which is used by the North American Indians, as
the bloodroot and two species of <gen>Lithospermum</gen>
(<spn>Lithospermum hirtum</spn>, and <spn>Lithospermum canescens</spn>);
also, the pigment itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puce</ent><br/
<hw>Puce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. <ets>puce</ets>
a flea, L. <ets>pulex</ets>, <ets>pulicis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of a dark
brown or brownish purple color.</def><-- MW10: dark red --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucel</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pucelle</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucelage</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"cel*age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Virginity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucelle</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*celle"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>pulicella</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pullus</ets> a young animal. See
<er>Pullet</er>.]</ety> <def>A maid; a virgin.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>pucel</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lady or <qex>pucelle</qex>, that wears mask or fan.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>La Pucelle</b></col>, <cd>the Maid of Orleans, Joan of
Arc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puceron</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ce*ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from
<ets>puce</ets> a flea. See <er>Puce</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any plant louse, or aphis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucherite</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"cher*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from the
<ets>Pucher</ets> Mine, in Saxony.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Vanadate of bismuth, occurring in minute reddish brown
crystals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puck</ent><br/
<hw>Puck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pouke</ets>;
cf. OSw. <ets>puke</ets>, Icel. <ets>p<umac/ki</ets> an evil demon, W.
<ets>pwca</ets> a hobgoblin. Cf. <er>Poker</er> a bugbear,
<er>Pug</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Medi<ae/val Myth.)</fld> <def>A
celebrated fairy, <ldquo/the merry wanderer of the night;<rdquo/ --
called also <altname>Robin Goodfellow</altname>, <altname>Friar
Rush</altname>, <altname>Pug</altname>, etc.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He meeteth <qex>Puck</qex>, whom most men call<br/
Hobgoblin, and on him doth fall.</q> <rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The goatsucker.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puck</ent><br/
<hw>Puck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A disk of vulcanized
rubber used in the game of hockey, as the object to be driven through
the goals.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucka</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Puck"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pukka</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Hind. <ets>pakk<amac/</ets> cooked,
ripe, solid.]</ety> <def>Good of its kind; -- variously used as implying
substantial, real, fixed, sure, etc., and specif., of buildings, made of
brick and mortar.</def> <mark>[India]</mark></p>

<p><q>It's <qex>pukka</qex> famine, by the looks of it.</q>
<rj><qau>Kipling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puckball</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Puck</ets> +
<ets>ball</ets>.]</ety> <def>A puffball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucker</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Puckered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Puckering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Poke</er> a
pocket, small bag.]</ety> <def>To gather into small folds or wrinkles;
to contract into ridges and furrows; to corrugate; -- often with
<xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>pucker</ex> up the mouth</as>.</def>
<ldquo/His skin [was] <xex>puckered</xex> up in wrinkles.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucker</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fold; a wrinkle; a
collection of folds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A state of perplexity or anxiety; confusion; bother;
agitation.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puckerer</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"er*er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which,
puckers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puckery</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Producing, or tending to produce, a pucker; <as>as, a
<ex>puckery</ex> taste</as>.</def> <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclined to become puckered or wrinkled; full of
puckers or wrinkles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puckfist</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"fist`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
puffball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puckish</ent><br/
<hw>Puck"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Puck</er>.]</ety>
<def>Resembling Puck; merry; mischievous.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Puckish</xex> freaks.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. R.
Green.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pucras</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"cras</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From a native name in
India.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Koklass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pud</ent><br/
<hw>Pud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pood</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pud</ent><br/
<hw>Pud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The hand; the first.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Lamb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddening</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"den*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr.
<ets>pudden</ets>, for <ets>pudding</ets>, in allusion to its
softness.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A quantity of
rope-yarn, or the like, placed, as a fender, on the bow of a boat.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A bunch of soft material to prevent chafing between
spars, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudder</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Puddered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Puddering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pother</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a tumult or bustle; to splash; to
make a pother or fuss; to potter; to meddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Puddering</qex> in the designs or doings of others.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Others <qex>pudder</qex> into their food with their broad
nebs.</q> <rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudder</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"der</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To perplex; to embarrass; to
confuse; to bother; <as>as, to <ex>pudder</ex> a man</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudder</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pother; a tumult; a confused
noise; turmoil; bustle.</def> <ldquo/All in a <xex>pudder</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudding</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>boudin</ets> black pudding, sausage, L. <ets>botulus</ets>,
<ets>botellus</ets>, a sausage, G. & Sw. <ets>pudding</ets> pudding,
Dan. <ets>podding</ets>, <ets>pudding</ets>, LG. <ets>puddig</ets>
thick, stumpy, W. <ets>poten</ets>, <ets>potten</ets>, also E.
<ets>pod</ets>, <ets>pout</ets>, v.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A species of
food of a soft or moderately hard consistence, variously made, but often
a compound of flour or meal, with milk and eggs, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And solid <qex>pudding</qex> against empty praise.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything resembling, or of the softness and
consistency of, pudding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An intestine; especially, an intestine stuffed with
meat, etc.; a sausage.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any food or victuals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Eat your <qex>pudding</qex>, slave, and hold your tongue.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Puddening</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Pudding grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the true
pennyroyal (<spn>Mentha Pulegium</spn>), formerly used to flavor
stuffing for roast meat.</cd> <au>Dr. Prior.</au> -- <col><b>Pudding
pie</b></col>, <cd>a pudding with meat baked in it.</cd> <au>Taylor
(1630).</au> -- <col><b>Pudding pipe</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the long, cylindrical pod of the leguminous tree <spn>Cassia
Fistula</spn>. The seeds are separately imbedded in a sweetish pulp. See
<er>Cassia</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pudding sleeve</b></col>, <cd>a full
sleeve like that of the English clerical gown.</cd> <au>Swift.</au> --
<col><b>Pudding stone</b></col>. <fld>(Min.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Conglomerate</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Pudding
time</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The time of dinner, pudding being
formerly the dish first eaten.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Johnson.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The nick of time; critical time.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mars, that still protects the stout,<br/
In <qex>pudding time</qex> came to his aid.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Pudding wife</ent><br/
<ent>Pudding fish</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pud"ding fish</hw>, <hw>Pudding wife</hw> }</mhw>.
<ety>[Prob. corrupted fr. the Sp. name in Cuba, <ets>pudiano
verde</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large, handsomely colored,
blue and bronze, labroid fish (<spn>Iridio radiatus</spn>, syn.
<spn>Platyglossus radiatus</spn>) of Florida, Bermuda, and the West
Indies. Called also <altname>pudiano</altname>,
<altname>doncella</altname>, and, at Bermuda,
<altname>bluefish</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudding-headed</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"ding-head`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stupid.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddle</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>podel</ets>;
cf. LG. <ets>pudel</ets>, Ir. & Gael. <ets>plod</ets> pool.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A small quantity of dirty standing water; a muddy
plash; a small pool.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Clay, or a mixture of clay and sand, kneaded or
worked, when wet, to render it impervious to water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puddle poet</b></col>, <cd>a low or worthless poet.</cd>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddle</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Puddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make
foul or muddy; to pollute with dirt; to mix dirt with (water).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some unhatched practice . . . <br/
Hath <qex>puddled</qex> his clear spirit.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To make dense or close, as clay or
loam, by working when wet, so as to render impervious to water.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To make impervious to liquids by means of puddle; to
apply puddle to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To subject to the process of puddling, as iron, so
as to convert it from the condition of cast iron to that of wrought
iron.</def> <rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puddled steel</b></col>, <cd>steel made directly from
cast iron by a modification of the puddling process.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddle</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a dirty stir.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>R. Junius.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddle-ball</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dle-ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The lump of
pasty wrought iron as taken from the puddling furnace to be hammered or
rolled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddle-bar</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dle-bar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An iron bar made
at a single heat from a puddle-ball hammering and rolling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddler</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who converts cast
iron into wrought iron by the process of puddling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddling</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Hydraul. Engin.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The process of working
clay, loam, pulverized ore, etc., with water, to render it compact, or
impervious to liquids; also, the process of rendering anything
impervious to liquids by means of puddled material.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Puddle. See <er>Puddle</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>The art or process of converting
cast iron into wrought iron or steel by subjecting it to intense heat
and frequent stirring in a reverberatory furnace in the presence of
oxidizing substances, by which it is freed from a portion of its carbon
and other impurities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puddling furnace</b></col>, <cd>a reverberatory furnace
in which cast iron is converted into wrought iron or into steel by
puddling.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddly</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of, or
resembling, puddles; muddy; foul.</def> <ldquo/Thick <xex>puddly</xex>
water.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puddock</ent><br/
<hw>Pud"dock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>paddock</ets>, or <ets>parrock</ets>, a park.]</ety> <def>A small
inclosure.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>purrock</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudency</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"den*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pudens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>pudere</ets> to be ashamed.]</ety>
<def>Modesty; shamefacedness.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>pudency</xex> so
rosy.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudenda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*den"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>pudendus</ets> that of which one ought to be ashamed, fr.
<ets>pudere</ets> to be ashamed.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
external organs of generation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudendal</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*den"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the pudenda, or pudendum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudendum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*den"dum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pudenda</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The external organs of
generation, especially of the female; the vulva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1160 --></p>

<p><ent>Pudgy</ent><br/
<hw>Pudg"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Short and fat or
sturdy; dumpy; podgy; <as>as, a short, <ex>pudgy</ex> little man; a
<ex>pudgy</ex> little hand.</as></def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudic</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pudicus</ets>
modest, fr. <ets>pudere</ets> to be ashamed: cf. F.
<ets>pudique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
the external organs of generation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudical</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"dic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Pudic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudicity</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*dic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pudicit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>pudicitia</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Modesty; chastity.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pudu</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"du</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
very small deer (<spn>Pudua humilis</spn>), native of the Chilian Andes.
It has simple spikelike antlers, only two or three inches
long.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pue</ent><br/
<hw>Pue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a low whistling sound; to
chirp, as birds.</def> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pueblo</ent><br/
<hw>Pueb"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., a village, L.
<ets>populus</ets> people. See <er>People</er>.]</ety> <def>A
communistic building erected by certain Indian tribes of Arizona and New
Mexico. It is often of large size and several stories high, and is
usually built either of stone or adobe. The term is also applied to any
Indian village in the same region.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pueblo Indians</b></col> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>, <cd>any
tribe or community of Indians living in pueblos. The principal Pueblo
tribes are the Moqui, the Zu<ntil/i, the Keran, and the
Tewan.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puefellow</ent><br/
<hw>Pue"fel`low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pewfellow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puer</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>The dung of dogs, used as an alkaline steep in
tanning.</def> <rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerco</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*er"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>A hog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puerco beds</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>a name
given to certain strata belonging to the earliest Eocene. They are
developed in Northwestern New Mexico, along the Rio Puerco, and are
characterized by their mammalian remains.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerile</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"er*ile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>puerilis</ets>, fr. <ets>puer</ets> a child, a boy: cf. F.
<ets>pu<eacute/ril</ets>.]</ety> <def>Boyish; childish; trifling;
silly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The French have been notorious through generations for their
<qex>puerile</qex> affectation of Roman forms, models, and historic
precedents.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Youthful; boyish; juvenile; childish; trifling;
weak. See <er>Youthful</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerilely</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"er*ile*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a puerile manner;
childishly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerileness</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"er*ile*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
puerile; puerility.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerility</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`er*il"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Puerilities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>puerilitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pu<eacute/rilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of being puerile; childishness;
puerileness.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is puerile or childish; especially, an
expression which is flat, insipid, or silly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerperal</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*er"per*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>puerpera</ets> a lying-in woman; <ets>puer</ets> child +
<ets>parere</ets> to bear: cf. F. <ets>puerp<eacute/ral</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to childbirth; <as>as, a <ex>puerperal</ex>
fever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puerperous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*er"per*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bearing
children.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puet</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
pewit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puff</ent><br/
<hw>Puff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to G. & Sw.
<ets>puff</ets> a blow, Dan. <ets>puf</ets>, D. <ets>pof</ets>; of
imitative origin. Cf. <er>Buffet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden
and single emission of breath from the mouth; hence, any sudden or short
blast of wind; a slight gust; a whiff.</def> <ldquo/ To every
<xex>puff</xex> of wind a slave.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Flatman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything light and filled with air. Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> A puffball. <sd>(b)</sd> kind of light pastry. <sd>(c)</sd>
A utensil of the toilet for dusting the skin or hair with
powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An exaggerated or empty expression of praise,
especially one in a public journal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puff adder</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>Any South African viper belonging to <gen>Clotho</gen> and allied
genera. They are exceedingly venomous, and have the power of greatly
distending their bodies when irritated. The common puff adder
(<spn>Vipera arietans</spn>, <it>or</it> <spn>Clotho arietans</spn>) is
the largest species, becoming over four feet long. The plumed puff adder
(<spn>Clotho cornuta</spn>) has a plumelike appendage over each
eye.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A North American harmless snake
(<spn>Heterodon platyrrhinos</spn>) which has the power of puffing up
its body. Called also <altname>hog-nose snake</altname>,
<altname>flathead</altname>, <altname>spreading adder</altname>, and
<altname>blowing adder</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Puff bird</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any bird of the genus <gen>Bucco</gen>, or
family <fam>Bucconid<ae/</fam>. They are small birds, usually with
dull-colored and loose plumage, and have twelve tail feathers. See
<er>Barbet</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puff</ent><br/
<hw>Puff</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Puffed</conjf> <pr>(p<ucr/ft)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to G. <ets>puffen</ets> to
pop, buffet, puff, D. <ets>poffen</ets> to pop, <ets>puffen</ets> to
blow, Sw. <ets>puffa</ets> to push, to cuff, Dan. <ets>puffe</ets> to
pop, thump. See <er>Puff</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
blow in puffs, or with short and sudden whiffs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To blow, as an expression of scorn; -- with
<xex>at</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is really to defy Heaven to <qex>puff</qex> at damnation.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To breathe quick and hard, or with puffs, as after
violent exertion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ass comes back again, <qex>puffing</qex> and blowing, from the
chase.</q> <rj><qau>L' Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To swell with air; to be dilated or inflated.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To breathe in a swelling, inflated, or pompous
manner; hence, to assume importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then came brave Glory <qex>puffing</qex> by.</q>
<rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puff</ent><br/
<hw>Puff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To drive with a puff,
or with puffs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The clearing north will <qex>puff</qex> the clouds away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To repel with words; to blow at
contemptuously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>puff</qex> the prostitute away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to swell or dilate; to inflate; to ruffle
with puffs; -- often with <ptcl>up</ptcl>; <as>as, a bladder
<ex>puffed</ex> with air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sea <qex>puffed</qex> up with winds.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To inflate with pride, flattery, self-esteem, or the
like; -- often with <ptcl>up</ptcl>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Puffed</qex> up with military success.</q> <rj><qau>Jowett
(Thucyd. )</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To praise with exaggeration; to flatter; to call
public attention to by praises; to praise unduly.</def> <ldquo/ Puffed
with wonderful skill.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puff</ent><br/
<hw>Puff</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Puffed up; vain.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fanshawe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffball</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
kind of ball-shaped fungus (<spn>Lycoperdon giganteum</spn>, and other
species of the same genus) full of dustlike spores when ripe; -- called
also <altname>bullfist</altname>, <altname>bullfice</altname>,
<altname>puckfist</altname>, <altname>puff</altname>, and
<altname>puffin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffer</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
puffs; one who praises with noisy or extravagant commendation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who is employed by the owner or seller of goods
sold at suction to bid up the price; a by-bidder.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any plectognath fish
which inflates its body, as the species of <gen>Tetrodon</gen> and
<gen>Diodon</gen> of the family <fam>Tetraodontidae</fam>; -- called
also <altname>blower</altname>, <altname>puff-fish</altname>,
<altname>swellfish</altname>, and <altname>globefish</altname>. They are
highly poisonous due to the presence of glands containing a potent
toxin, <prod>tetrodotoxin</prod>. Nevertheless they are eaten as a
delicacy in Japan, being prepared by specially licensed chefs who remove
the poison glands.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The common, or harbor,
porpoise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Dyeing)</fld> <def>A kier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffery</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of puffing;
bestowment of extravagant commendation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffin</ent><br/
<hw>Puf"fin</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/f"f<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to
<ets>puff</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An arctic
sea bird <spn>Fratercula arctica</spn>) allied to the auks, and having a
short, thick, swollen beak, whence the name; -- called also
<altname>bottle nose</altname>, <altname>cockandy</altname>,
<altname>coulterneb</altname>, <altname>marrot</altname>,
<altname>mormon</altname>, <altname>pope</altname>, and <altname>sea
parrot</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is also applied to other related species, as
the horned puffin (<spn>Fratercula corniculata</spn>), the tufted puffin
(<spn>Lunda cirrhata</spn>), and the razorbill.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Manx puffin</b></col>, <cd>the Manx shearwater. See under
<er>Manx</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The puffball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sort of apple.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Rider's Dict. (1640).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffiness</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being puffy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffing</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Puff</er>, <pos>v.
i. & t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puffing adder</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Puff adder</cref> <sd>(b)</sd>, under <er>Puff</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Puffing pig</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common
porpoise.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffingly</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a puffing manner; with
vehement breathing or shortness of breath; with exaggerated
praise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puff-leg</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"-leg`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of beautiful humming birds of the genus
<gen>Eriocnemis</gen> having large tufts of downy feathers on the
legs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puff-legged</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"-legged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a conspicuous tuft of feathers on the legs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puffy</ent><br/
<hw>Puff"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Swelled
with air, or any soft matter; tumid with a soft substance; bloated;
fleshy; <as>as, a <ex>puffy</ex> tumor</as>.</def> <ldquo/ A very stout,
<xex>puffy</xex> man.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, inflated; bombastic; <as>as, a <ex>puffy</ex>
style</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pug</ent><br/
<hw>Pug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pugged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pugging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>pucken</ets> to
thump. beat.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To mix and stir when wet, as clay for bricks,
pottery, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill or stop with clay by tamping; to fill in or
spread with mortar, as a floor or partition, for the purpose of
deadening sound. See <er>Pugging</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pug</ent><br/
<hw>Pug</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Tempered clay; clay
moistened and worked so as to be plastic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pug mill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pug mill</b></col>, <cd>a kind of mill for grinding and
mixing clay, either for brickmaking or the fine arts; a clay mill. It
consists essentially of an upright shaft armed with projecting knives,
which is caused to revolve in a hollow cylinder, tub, or vat, in which
the clay is placed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pug</ent><br/
<hw>Pug</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted fr. <ets>puck</ets>. See
<er>Puck</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An elf, or a hobgoblin; also same
as <er>Puck</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A name for a monkey.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A name for a fox.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An intimate; a crony; a dear one.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Lyly.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Chaff; the refuse of grain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A prostitute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a small breed of pet dogs
having a short nose and head; a pug dog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any geometrid moth of the genus
<gen>Eupithecia</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pug</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. <ets>pag</ets>
foot.]</ety> <def>A footprint; a track; as of a boar.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pug-faced</ent><br/
<hw>Pug"-faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a face like
a monkey or a pug; monkey-faced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puggaree</ent><br/
<hw>Pug"ga*ree</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Puggry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugger</ent><br/
<hw>Pug"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pucker.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puggered</ent><br/
<hw>Pug"gered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Puckered.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugging</ent><br/
<hw>Pug"ging</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pug</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of working
and tempering clay to make it plastic and of uniform consistency, as for
bricks, for pottery, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Mortar or the like, laid between
the joists under the boards of a floor, or within a partition, to deaden
sound; -- in the United States usually called
<altname>deafening</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugging</ent><br/
<hw>Pug"ging</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Thieving.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puggree</ent><br/
<ent>Puggry</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pug"gry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pug"gree</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also <asp>puggaree</asp>,
<asp>puggeree</asp>, etc.]</altsp> <ety>[Hind. <ets>pag<rsdot/i</ets>
turban.]</ety> <def>A light scarf wound around a hat or helmet to
protect the head from the sun.</def> <mark>[India]</mark>
<rj><au>Yule.</au></rj></p>

<p><q>A blue-gray felt hat with a gold <qex>puggaree</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Kipling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugh</ent><br/
<hw>Pugh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Pshaw! pish! -- a
word used in contempt or disdain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugil</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"gil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pugillus</ets>, <ets>pugillum</ets>, a handful, akin to
<ets>pugnus</ets> the fist.]</ety> <def>As much as is taken up between
the thumb and two first fingers.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugilism</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"gil*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pugil</ets> a pugilist, boxer, akin to <ets>pugnus</ets> the fist.
Cf. <er>Pugnacious</er>, <er>Fist</er>.]</ety> <def>The practice of
boxing, or fighting with the fist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugilist</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"gil*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pugil</ets>.]</ety>
<def>One who fights with his fists; esp., a professional prize fighter;
a boxer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugilistic</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`gil*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to pugillism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugnacious</ent><br/
<hw>Pug*na"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pugnax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>, fr. <ets>pugnare</ets> to fight.
Cf. <er>Pugilism</er>, <er>Fist</er>.]</ety> <def>Disposed to fight;
inclined to fighting; quarrelsome; fighting.</def>
--<wordforms><wf>Pug*na"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pug*na"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pugnacity</ent><br/
<hw>Pug*nac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pugnacitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pugnacit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Inclination or readiness to fight; quarrelsomeness.</def> <ldquo/ A
national <xex>pugnacity</xex> of character.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Motley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pug nose</ent><br/
<hw>Pug" nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A short, thick nose; a
snubnose.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Pug"-nosed`</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pug-nose eel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
deep-water marine eel (<spn>Simenchelys parasiticus</spn>) which
sometimes burrows into the flesh of the halibut.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puh</ent><br/
<hw>Puh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>The same as
<er>Pugh</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puisne</ent><br/
<hw>Puis"ne</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Puny</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Later in age, time, etc.;
subsequent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/ A <xex>puisne</xex> date
to eternity.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Puny; petty; unskilled.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Younger or inferior in rank;
junior; associate; <as>as, a chief justice and three <ex>puisne</ex>
justices of the Court of Common Pleas; the <ex>puisne</ex> barons of the
Court of Exchequer.</as></def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puisne</ent><br/
<hw>Puis"ne</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is younger, or of inferior
rank; a junior; esp., a judge of inferior rank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It were not a work for <qex>puisnes</qex> and novices.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puisny</ent><br/
<hw>Puis"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Puisne; younger;
inferior; petty; unskilled.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>puisny</qex> tilter, that spurs his horse but on one
side.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puissance</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"is*sance</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. <ets>puissant</ets>.
See <er>Puissant</er>, and cf. <er>Potency</er>, <er>Potance</er>,
<er>Potence</er>.]</ety> <def>Power; strength; might; force;
potency.</def> <ldquo/ Youths of <xex>puissance</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The power and <qex>puissance</qex> of the king.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In Spenser, Shakespeare, and Milton,
<xex>puissance</xex> and <xex>puissant</xex> are usually
dissyllables.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puissant</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"is*sant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., originally, a
p. pr. formed fr. L. <ets>posse</ets> to be able: cf. L.
<ets>potens</ets> powerful. See <er>Potent</er>.]</ety> <def>Powerful;
strong; mighty; forcible; <as>as, a <ex>puissant</ex> prince or
empire</as>.</def> <ldquo/ <xex>Puissant</xex> deeds.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of <qex>puissant</qex> nations which the world possessed.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And worldlings in it are less merciful,<br/
And more <qex>puissant</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puissantly</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"is*sant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a puissant manner;
powerfully; with great strength.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puissantness</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"is*sant*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of
being puissant; puissance; power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puit</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Puit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>puits</ets>,
from L. <ets>puteus</ets> well.]</ety> <def>A well; a small stream; a
fountain; a spring.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>puits</qex> flowing from the fountain of life.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puke</ent><br/
<hw>Puke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Puked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>spucken</ets> to
spit, and E. <ets>spew</ets>.]</ety> <def>To eject the contests of the
stomach; to vomit; to spew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The infant<br/
Mewling and <qex>puking</qex> in the nurse's arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puke</ent><br/
<hw>Puke</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To eject from the stomach; to vomit
up.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puke</ent><br/
<hw>Puke</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A medicine that causes vomiting; an
emetic; a vomit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puke</ent><br/
<hw>Puke</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety> <def>Of a
color supposed to be between black and russet.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This color has by some been regarded as the same with
<xex>puce</xex>; but Nares questions the identity.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puker</ent><br/
<hw>Puk"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pukes, vomits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which causes vomiting.</def>
<rj><au>Garth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pukka</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Puk"ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pucka</er>.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulas</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"las</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>pal<amac/<cced/a</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The East
Indian leguminous tree <spn>Butea frondosa</spn>. See <cref>Gum
Butea</cref>, under <er>Gum</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pales</asp> and <asp>palasa</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulchritude</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"chri*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulchritudo</ets>, fr. <ets>pulcher</ets> beautiful.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That quality of appearance which pleases the eye;
beauty; comeliness; grace; loveliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Piercing our heartes with thy <qex>pulchritude</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Court of Love.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Attractive moral excellence; moral beauty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By the <qex>pulchritude</qex> of their souls make up what is
wanting in the beauty of their bodies.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pule</ent><br/
<hw>Pule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Puled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>piauler</ets>; cf. L.
<ets>pipilare</ets>, <ets>pipire</ets>, to peep, pip, chirp, and E.
<ets>peep</ets> to chirp.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cry like a
chicken.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To whimper; to whine, as a complaining
child.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It becometh not such a gallant to whine and <qex>pule</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puler</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pules; one who
whines or complains; a weak person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulex</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu"lex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a flea.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of parasitic insects including the
fleas. See <er>Flea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulicene</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"li*cene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>pulex</ets>, <ets>pulicis</ets>, a flea.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to,
or abounding in, fleas; pulicose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulicous</ent><br/
<ent>Pulicose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu"li*cose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu"li*cous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pulicosus</ets>, from
<ets>pulex</ets>, a flea.]</ety> <def>Abounding with fleas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puling</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cry, as of a
chicken,; a whining or whimpering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Leave this faint <qex>puling</qex> and lament as I do.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puling</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Whimpering; whining;
childish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulingly</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With whining or
complaint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulkha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pulk"ha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Laplander's
traveling sledge. See <er>Sledge</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pull</ent><br/
<hw>Pull</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pulled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pulling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>pullian</ets>; cf. LG.
<ets>pulen</ets>, and Gael. <ets>peall</ets>, <ets>piol</ets>,
<ets>spiol</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw, or attempt to draw,
toward one; to draw forcibly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ne'er <qex>pull</qex> your hat upon your brows.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He put forth his hand . . . and <qex>pulled</qex> her in.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. viii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw apart; to tear; to rend.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hath turned aside my ways, and <qex>pulled</qex> me in pieces;
he hath made me desolate.</q> <rj><qau>Lam. iii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To gather with the hand, or by drawing toward one;
to pluck; <as>as, to <ex>pull</ex> fruit; to <ex>pull</ex> flax; to
<ex>pull</ex> a finch.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To move or operate by the motion of drawing towards
one; <as>as, to <ex>pull</ex> a bell; to <ex>pull</ex> an
oar.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Horse Racing)</fld> <def>To hold back, and so
prevent from winning; <as>as, the favorite was
<ex>pulled</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To take or make, as a proof or
impression; -- hand presses being worked by pulling a lever.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>To strike the ball in a
particular manner. See <er>Pull</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never <qex>pull</qex> a straight fast ball to leg.</q> <rj><qau>R.
H. Lyttelton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pull and haul</b></col>, <cd>to draw hither and
thither.</cd> <ldquo/ Both are equally <xex>pulled and hauled</xex> to
do that which they are unable to do. <rdquo/ <au>South.</au> --
<col><b>To pull down</b></col>, <cd>to demolish; to destroy; to degrade;
<as>as, <ex>to pull down</ex> a house</as>.</cd> <ldquo/ In political
affairs, as well as mechanical, it is easier <xex>to pull down</xex>
than build up.<rdquo/ <au>Howell.</au> <ldquo/ To raise the wretched,
and <xex>pull down</xex> the proud.<rdquo/ <au>Roscommon.</au> --
<col><b>To pull a finch</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Finch</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>To pull off</b></col>, <cd>take or draw off.</cd><-- (b) to
perform (esp. something illegal or unethical); as, to pull off a heist
[robbery]. (c) to accomplish, against the odds.--></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1161 --></p>

<p><ent>Pull</ent><br/
<hw>Pull</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To exert one's self in
an act or motion of drawing or hauling; to tug; <as>as, to <ex>pull</ex>
at a rope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To pull apart</b></col>, <cd>to become separated by
pulling; <as>as, a rope will <ex>pull apart</ex></as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To pull up</b></col>, <cd>to draw the reins; to stop; to
halt.</cd> -- <col><b>To pull through</b></col>, <cd>to come
successfully to the end of a difficult undertaking, a dangerous
sickness, or the like.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pull</ent><br/
<hw>Pull</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pulling or
drawing with force; an effort to move something by drawing toward
one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I awakened with a violent <qex>pull</qex> upon the ring which was
fastened at the top of my box.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A contest; a struggle; <as>as, a wrestling
<ex>pull</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pluck; loss or violence suffered.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two <qex>pulls</qex> at once;<br/
His lady banished, and a limb lopped off.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A knob, handle, or lever, etc., by which anything is
pulled; <as>as, a drawer <ex>pull</ex>; a bell
<ex>pull</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The act of rowing; <as>as, a <ex>pull</ex> on the
river</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The act of drinking; <as>as, to take a <ex>pull</ex>
at the beer, or the mug</as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Something in one's favor in a comparison or a
contest; an advantage; means of influencing; <as>as, in weights the
favorite had the <ex>pull</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>A kind of stroke by which a leg
ball is sent to the off side, or an off ball to the side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pull</qex> is not a legitimate stroke, but bad
cricket.</q> <rj><qau>R. A. Proctor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullail</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"lail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poulaille</ets>.]</ety> <def>Poultry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Rom. of R.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullback</ent><br/
<hw>Pull"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
which holds back, or causes to recede; a drawback; a
hindrance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch)</fld> <def>The iron hook fixed to a casement
to pull it shut, or to hold it party open at a fixed point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulldevil</ent><br/
<hw>Pull"dev`il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A number of
fishhooks rigidly fastened back to be pulled through the water to catch
fish.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulled</ent><br/
<hw>Pulled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Plucked; pilled;
moulting.</def> <ldquo/ A <xex>pulled</xex> hen.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullen</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"len</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>pullinus</ets> belonging to young animals. See
<er>Pullet</er>.]</ety> <def>Poultry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puller</ent><br/
<hw>Pull"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, pulls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Proud setter up and <qex>puller</qex> down of kings.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullet</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>polete</ets>, OF. <ets>polete</ets>, F. <ets>poulette</ets>, dim.
of <ets>poule</ets> a hen, fr. L. <ets>pullus</ets> a young animal, a
young fowl. See <er>Foal</er>, and cf. <er>Poult</er>, <er>Poultry</er>,
<er>Pool</er> stake.]</ety> <def>A young hen, or female of the domestic
fowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pullet sperm</b></col>, <cd>the treadle of an egg.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulley</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pulleys</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>poulie</ets>,
perhaps of Teutonic origin (cf. <er>Poll</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>); but
cf. OE. <ets>poleine</ets>, <ets>polive</ets>, pulley, LL.
<ets>polanus</ets>, and F. <ets>poulain</ets>, properly, a colt, fr. L.
<ets>pullus</ets> young animal, foal (cf. <er>Pullet</er>,
<er>Foal</er>). For the change of sense, cf. F. <ets>poutre</ets> beam,
originally, a filly, and E. <ets>easel</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>
<def>A wheel with a broad rim, or grooved rim, for transmitting power
from, or imparting power to, the different parts of machinery, or for
changing the direction of motion, by means of a belt, cord, rope, or
chain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>pulley</xex>, as one of the mechanical powers,
consists, in its simplest form, of a grooved wheel, called a
<xex>sheave</xex>, turning within a movable frame or <xex>block</xex>,
by means of a cord or rope attached at one end to a fixed point. The
force, acting on the free end of the rope, is thus doubled, but can move
the load through only half the space traversed by itself. The rope may
also pass over a sheave in another block that is fixed. The end of the
rope may be fastened to the movable block, instead of a fixed point,
with an additional gain of power, and using either one or two sheaves in
the fixed block. Other sheaves may be added, and the power multiplied
accordingly. Such an apparatus is called by workmen a <stype>block and
tackle</stype>, or a <stype>fall and tackle</stype>. See <er>Block</er>.
A <xex>single fixed pulley</xex> gives no increase of power, but serves
simply for changing the direction of motion.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Band pulley</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Belt
pulley</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a pulley with a broad face for transmitting
power between revolving shafts by means of a belt, or for guiding a
belt.</cd> -- <col><b>Cone pulley</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Cone
pulley</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Conical pulley</b></col>, <cd>one of a pair
of belt pulleys, each in the shape of a truncated cone, for varying
velocities.</cd> -- <col><b>Fast pulley</b></col>, <cd>a pulley firmly
attached upon a shaft.</cd> -- <col><b>Loose pulley</b></col>, <cd>a
pulley loose on a shaft, to interrupt the transmission of motion in
machinery. See <cref>Fast and loose pulleys</cref>, under
<er>Fast</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Parting pulley</b></col>, <cd>a belt
pulley made in semicircular halves, which can be bolted together, to
facilitate application to, or removal from, a shaft.</cd> --
<col><b>Pulley block</b></col>. <cd>Same as <er>Block</er>,
<pos>n.</pos> 6.</cd> -- <col><b>Pulley stile</b></col>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>the upright of the window frame into which a
pulley is fixed and along which the sash slides.</cd> -- <col><b>Split
pulley</b></col>, <cd>a parting pulley.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulley</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ley</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To raise or lift by means of a
pulley.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"li*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of checked
cotton or silk handkerchief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullman car</ent><br/
<hw>Pull"man car`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Named after Mr.
<ets>Pullman</ets>, who introduced them.]</ety> <def>A kind of sleeping
car; also, a palace car; -- often shortened to
<altname>Pullman</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"lu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pullulatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pullulare</ets> to sprout, from
<ets>pullulus</ets> a young animal, a sprout, dim. of <ets>pullus</ets>.
See <er>pullet</er>.]</ety> <def>To germinate; to bud; to multiply
abundantly.</def> <rj><au>Warburton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullulation</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`lu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pullulation</ets>.]</ety> <def>A germinating, or budding.</def>
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pullus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pulli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A chick; a young bird in the downy stage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmobranchiate</ent><br/
<ent>Pulmobranchiata</ent><br/
<mhw>\'d8<hw>Pul`mo*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety>, <hw>Pul`mo*bran"chi*ate</hw>. <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.
& n.</pos></mhw> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pulmonibranchiata</er>, <er>-ate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmocutaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`mo*cu*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets> a lung + E. <ets>cutaneous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the lungs and the akin;
<as>as, the <ex>pulmocutaneous</ex> arteries of the frog</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmogasteropoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul`mo*gas`te*rop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. & E. <ets>Gasteropoda</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pulmonata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmograde</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo*grade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets> a lung + <ets>gradi</ets> to walk.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Swimming by the expansion and contraction, or
lunglike movement, of the body, or of the disk, as do the
medus<ae/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets> a lung + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A spirometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmometry</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*mom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets> a lung + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
determination of the capacity of the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonarian</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any arachnid that breathes by lunglike organs, as the spiders and
scorpions. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonary</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmonarius</ets>, from <ets>pulmo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, a lung;
of uncertain origin, perh. named from its lightness, and akin to E.
<ets>float</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pulmonaire</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pneumonia</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the lungs; affecting
the lungs; pulmonic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pulmonary artery</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Artery</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonary</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo*na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pulmonaire</ets>.
See <er>Pulmonary</er>, <pos>a.</pos> ]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Lungwort.</def> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul`mo*na"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>pulmo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, a lung.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive division, or sub-class, of
hermaphrodite gastropods, in which the mantle cavity is modified into an
air-breathing organ, as in Helix, or land snails, Limax, or garden
slugs, and many pond snails, as Limn<ae/a and Planorbis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonate</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having breathing organs that act as lungs.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Pertaining to the Pulmonata.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Pulmonata.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonated</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo*na`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>same as
<er>Pulmonate</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonibranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul`mo*ni*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>pulmo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, a lung + Gr. <?/ a
gill.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pulmonata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonibranchiate</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`mo*ni*bran"chi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pulmonate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, a lung: cf. F.
<ets>pulmonique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Relating to, or affecting the lungs;
pulmonary.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A pulmonic
medicine.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmonifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul`mo*nif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Pulmoniferous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pulmonata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmoniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`mo*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, a lung + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having lungs; pulmonate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulmotor</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"mo`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulmo</ets> lung + E. <ets>motor</ets>.]</ety> <def>An apparatus
for producing artificial respiration by pumping oxygen or air or a
mixture of the two into and out of the lungs, as of a person who has
been asphyxiated by drowning, breathing poisonous gases, or the like, or
of one who has been stunned by an electrical shock.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulp</ent><br/
<hw>Pulp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pulpa</ets>
flesh, pith, pulp of fruit: cf. F. <ets>pulpe</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
moist, slightly cohering mass, consisting of soft, undissolved animal or
vegetable matter.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A tissue or part resembling pulp; especially, the soft, highly
vascular and sensitive tissue which fills the central cavity, called the
<xex>pulp cavity,</xex> of teeth.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The soft, succulent part of fruit; <as>as, the <ex>pulp</ex> of a
grape</as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The exterior part of a coffee
berry.</def> <au>B. Edwards.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The material of
which paper is made when ground up and suspended in water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulp</ent><br/
<hw>Pulp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pulped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pulping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To reduce to
pulp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To deprive of the pulp, or integument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The other mode is to <qex>pulp</qex> the coffee immediately as it
comes from the tree. By a simple machine a man will <qex>pulp</qex> a
bushel in a minute.</q> <rj><qau>B. Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpatoon</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`pa*toon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poulpeton</ets>, <ets>poupeton</ets>, a sort of ragout.]</ety>
<def>A kind of delicate confectionery or cake, perhaps made from the
pulp of fruit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pulp"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the quality or
state of being pulpy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpit</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"pit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulpitum</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>pulpite</ets>, F.
<ets>pulpitre</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>An elevated place, or inclosed stage, in a church,
in which the clergyman stands while preaching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I stand like a clerk in my <qex>pulpit</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The whole body of the clergy; preachers as a class;
also, preaching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I say the <qex>pulpit</qex> (in the sober use<br/
Of its legitimate, peculiar powers)<br/
Must stand acknowledged, while the world shall stand,<br/
The most important and effectual guard,<br/
Support, and ornament of virtue's cause.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A desk, or platform, for an orator or public
speaker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 4. (Fig.) An office or condition of public prominence in which a
person can gain wide public attention, thereby permitting him to exhort
the public on moral or political matters. "The presidency is a bully
pulpit." --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpit</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"pit</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the pulpit, or
preaching; <as>as, a <ex>pulpit</ex> orator; <ex>pulpit</ex>
eloquence.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpited</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"pit*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Placed in a
pulpit.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sit . . . at the feet of a <qex>pulpited</qex> divine.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpiteer</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*pit*eer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who speaks in
a pulpit; a preacher; -- so called in contempt.</def>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We never can think it sinful that Burns should have been humorous
on such a <qex>pulpiteer</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Prof.
Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpiter</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"pit*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A preacher.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpitical</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*pit"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the pulpit; suited to the pulpit.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Pul*pit"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpitish</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"pit*ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the pulpit; like preaching.</def> <rj><au>Chalmers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpitry</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"pit*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The teaching of the
pulpit; preaching.</def> <mark>[R. & Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/ Mere
<xex>pulpitry</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpous</ent><br/
<hw>Pulp"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulposus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pulpeux</ets>. See
<er>Pulp</er>.]</ety> <def>Containing pulp; pulpy.</def> <ldquo/
<xex>Pulpous</xex> fruit.<rdquo/ <au>J. Philips.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pulp"ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulpy</ent><br/
<hw>Pulp"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Like pulp; consisting
of pulp; soft; fleshy; succulent; <as>as, the <er>pulpy</er> covering of
a nut; the <ex>pulpy</ex> substance of a peach or a
cherry.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulque</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul"que</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>An intoxicating Mexican drink. See <er>Agave</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsate</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"sate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pulsated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pulsating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulsatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pulsare</ets> to beat, strike, v.
intens. fr. <ets>pellere</ets> to beat, strike, drive. See
<er>Pulse</er> a beating, and cf. <er>Pulse</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To throb, as a pulse; to beat, as the heart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heart of a viper or frog will continue to <qex>pulsate</qex>
long after it is taken from the body.</q> <rj><qau>E.
Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsatile</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"sa*tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>pulsatile</ets>, Sp. <ets>pulsatil</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being struck or beaten; played by beating or by
percussion; <as>as, a tambourine is a <ex>pulsatile</ex> musical
instrument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pulsating; throbbing, as a tumor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsatilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul`sa*til"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of ranunculaceous herbs including the
pasque flower. This genus is now merged in
<altname><gen>Anemone</gen></altname>. Some species, as <spn>Anemone
Pulsatilla</spn>, <spn>Anemone pratensis</spn>, and <spn>Anemone
patens</spn>, are used medicinally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsation</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulsatio</ets> a beating or striking: cf. F.
<ets>pulsation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A
beating or throbbing, especially of the heart or of an artery, or in an
inflamed part; a beat of the pulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A single beat or throb of a series.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A stroke or impulse by which some medium is
affected, as in the propagation of sounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Any touching of another's body
willfully or in anger. This constitutes <xex>battery</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By the Cornelian law, <qex>pulsation</qex> as well as verberation
is prohibited.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsative</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"sa*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pulsatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Beating; throbbing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsator</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*sa"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A beater; a striker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>That which beats or throbs in
working.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"sa*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pulsatoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of pulsating;
throbbing.</def> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton. .</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulse</ent><br/
<hw>Pulse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>puls</ets>, L.
<ets>puls</ets>, <ets>pultis</ets>, a thick pap or pottage made of meal,
pulse, etc. See <er>Poultice</er>, and cf. <er>Pousse</er>.]</ety>
<def>Leguminous plants, or their seeds, as beans, pease, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If all the world<br/
Should, in a pet of temperance, feed on pulse.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulse</ent><br/
<hw>Pulse</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>pous</ets>, OF.
<ets>pous</ets>, F. <ets>pouls</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pulsus</ets> (sc.
<ets>venarum</ets>), the beating of the pulse, the pulse, from
<ets>pellere</ets>, <ets>pulsum</ets>, to beat, strike; cf. Gr. <?/ to
swing, shake, <?/ to shake. Cf. <er>Appeal</er>, <er>Compel</er>,
<er>Impel</er>, <er>Push</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>The beating or throbbing of the heart or blood vessels, especially
of the arteries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In an artery the pulse is due to the expansion and
contraction of the elastic walls of the artery by the action of the
heart upon the column of blood in the arterial system. On the
commencement of the diastole of the ventricle, the semilunar valves are
closed, and the aorta recoils by its elasticity so as to force part of
its contents into the vessels farther onwards. These, in turn, as they
already contain a certain quantity of blood, expand, recover by an
elastic recoil, and transmit the movement with diminished intensity.
Thus a series of movements, gradually diminishing in intensity, pass
along the arterial system (see the Note under <er>Heart</er>). For the
sake of convenience, the radial artery at the wrist is generally chosen
to detect the precise character of the pulse. The pulse rate varies with
age, position, sex, stature, physical and psychical influences,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any measured or regular beat; any short, quick
motion, regularly repeated, as of a medium in the transmission of light,
sound, etc.; oscillation; vibration; pulsation; impulse; beat;
movement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The measured <qex>pulse</qex> of racing oars.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the ear receives any simple sound, it is struck by a single
<qex>pulse</qex> of the air, which makes the eardrum and the other
membranous parts vibrate according to the nature and species of the
stroke.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Pulse glass</b></col>, <cd>an instrument consisting to a
glass tube with terminal bulbs, and containing ether or alcohol, which
the heat of the hand causes to boil; -- so called from the
<xex>pulsating</xex> motion of the liquid when thus warmed.</cd> --
<col><b>Pulse wave</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>the wave of
increased pressure started by the ventricular systole, radiating from
the semilunar valves over the arterial system, and gradually
disappearing in the smaller branches.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>the <qex>pulse wave</qex> travels over the arterial system at the
rate of about 29.5 feet in a second.</q> <rj><qau>H. N.
Martin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>To feel one's pulse</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
ascertain, by the sense of feeling, the condition of the arterial
pulse.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Hence, to sound one's opinion; to try to
discover one's mind.</cd><-- = to take the pulse of --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Pulse</ent><br/
<hw>Pulse</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To beat, as the arteries; to move
in pulses or beats; to pulsate; to throb.</def>
<rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulse</ent><br/
<hw>Pulse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pulsate</er>,
<er>Pulse</er> a beating.]</ety> <def>To drive by a pulsation; to cause
to pulsate.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulseless</ent><br/
<hw>Pulse"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no pulsation;
lifeless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulselessness</ent><br/
<hw>Pulse"less*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
pulseless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsific</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*sif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pulse</ets> +
L. <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety> <def>Exciting the pulse; causing
pulsation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*sim"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pulse</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>A sphygmograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsion</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulsio</ets>, fr. <ets>pellere</ets>, <ets>pulsum</ets>, to drive:
cf. F. <ets>pulsion</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of driving forward;
propulsion; -- opposed to <ant>suction</ant> or
<ant>traction</ant>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1162 --></p>

<p><ent>Pulsive</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to compel;
compulsory.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>pulsive</xex> strain
of conscience.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulsometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*som"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pulse</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A device, with valves, for raising water by steam,
partly by atmospheric pressure, and partly by the direct action of the
steam on the water, without the intervention of a piston; -- also called
<altname>vacuum pump</altname>.</def><-- sounds like a steam aspirator,
perhaps with other attachments. No figure. "vacuum pump" is usu.
reserved for a mechanical device to create a vacuum, nothing to do with
raising water. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pulsimeter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pult</ent><br/
<hw>Pult</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To put.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pultaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pultac<eacute/</ets>. See 1st <er>Pulse</er>.]</ety>
<def>Macerated; softened; nearly fluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pultise</ent><br/
<ent>Pultesse</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pul"tesse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pul"tise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Poultry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu"lu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vegetable
substance consisting of soft, elastic, yellowish brown chaff, gathered
in the Hawaiian Islands from the young fronds of free ferns of the genus
<gen>Cibotium</gen>, chiefly <spn>Cibotium Menziesii</spn>; -- used for
stuffing mattresses, cushions, etc., and as an absorbent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverable</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
reduced to fine powder; pulverizable.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulveraceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`ver*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having a finely powdered surface; pulverulent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverate</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulveratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pulverare</ets> to pulverize. See
<er>Pulverize</er>.]</ety> <def>To beat or reduce to powder or dust; to
pulverize.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverine</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulvis</ets>, <ets>pulveris</ets>, dust, powder; cf. F.
<ets>pulv<eacute/rin</ets>.]</ety> <def>Ashes of barilla.</def>
<rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverizable</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*i`za*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Admitting of
being pulverized; pulverable.</def> <rj><au>Barton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverization</ent><br/
<hw>Pul`ver*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pulv<eacute/risation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The action of reducing to
dust or powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverize</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pulverized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pulverizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>pulv<eacute/riser</ets>, L. <ets>pulverizare</ets>, fr.
<ets>pulvis</ets> dust, powder. See <er>Powder</er>.]</ety> <def>To
reduce of fine powder or dust, as by beating, grinding, or the like;
<as>as, friable substances may be <ex>pulverized</ex> by grinding or
beating, but to <ex>pulverize</ex> malleable bodies other methods must
be pursued</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverize</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become reduced to powder;
to fall to dust; <as>as, the stone <ex>pulverizes</ex>
easily</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverizer</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, pulverizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverous</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"ver*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>pulvereus</ets>, from <ets>pulvis</ets>, <ets>pulveris</ets>, dust,
powder.]</ety> <def>Consisting of dust or powder; like powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverulence</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*ver"u*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being pulverulent; abundance of dust or powder; dustiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulverulent</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*ver"u*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pulverulentus</ets>, fr. <ets>pulvis</ets>, <ets>pulveris</ets>,
dust, powder: cf. F. <ets>pulv<eacute/rulent</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Consisting of, or reducible to, fine powder; covered with dust or
powder; powdery; dusty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvil</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"vil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>polviglio</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pulvis</ets>, <ets>pulveris</ets>,
dust, powder: cf. Sp. <ets>polvillo</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sweet-scented
powder; pulvillio.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pulville</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Gay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvil</ent><br/
<hw>Pul"vil</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To apply pulvil to.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Congreve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvillo</ent><br/
<ent>Pulvillio</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pul*vil"li*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pul*vil"lo</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pulvil</er>.]</ety>
<def>A kind of perfume in the form of a powder, formerly much used, --
often in little bags.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Smells of incense, ambergris, and <qex>pulvillios</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvillus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul*vil"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pulvilli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a little
cushion.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the minute cushions on
the feet of certain insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvinar</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul*vi"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
cushion.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A prominence on the posterior
part of the thalamus of the human brain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvinated</ent><br/
<ent>Pulvinate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pul"vi*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pul"vi*na`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pulvinatus</ets>, fr.
<ets>pulvinus</ets> a cushion, an elevation.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Curved convexly or swelled; <as>as, a
<ex>pulvinated</ex> frieze</as>.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the form of a
cushion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvinic</ent><br/
<hw>Pul*vin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Vulpinic</er>, by transposition of the letters.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid obtained
by the decomposition of vulpinic acid, as a white crystalline
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pulvinulus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pul*vin"u*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pulvinuli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a
little mound.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pulvillus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puma</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ma</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Peruv.
<ets>puma</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large American
carnivore (<spn>Felis concolor</spn>), found from Canada to Patagonia,
especially among the mountains. Its color is tawny, or brownish yellow,
without spots or stripes. Called also <altname>catamount</altname>,
<altname>cougar</altname>, <altname>American lion</altname>,
<altname>mountain lion</altname>, and <altname>panther</altname> or
<altname>painter</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pume</ent><br/
<hw>Pume</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
stint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumicate</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"mi*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pumicated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Pumicating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>pumicatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pumicare</ets> to pumicate, fr.
<ets>pumex</ets>. See <er>Pumice</er>.]</ety> <def>To make smooth with
pumice.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumice</ent><br/
<hw>Pum"ice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pumex</ets>,
<ets>pumicis</ets>, prob. akin to <ets>spuma</ets> foam: cf. AS.
<ets>pumic-</ets>st<amac/n. Cf. <er>Pounce</er> a powder,
<er>Spume</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A very light porous
volcanic scoria, usually of a gray color, the pores of which are
capillary and parallel, giving it a fibrous structure. It is supposed to
be produced by the disengagement of watery vapor without liquid or
plastic lava. It is much used, esp. in the form of powder, for smoothing
and polishing. Called also <altname>pumice stone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumiced</ent><br/
<hw>Pum"iced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld>
<def>Affected with a kind of chronic laminitis in which there is a
growth of soft spongy horn between the coffin bone and the hoof wall.
The disease is called <xex>pumiced foot</xex>, or <xex>pumice
foot</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumiceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*mi`ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pumiceus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to pumice; resembling
pumice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumice stone</ent><br/
<hw>Pum"ice stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Same as
<er>Pumice</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumiciform</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*mic"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pumice</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling, or
having the structure of, pumice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pummace</ent><br/
<hw>Pum"mace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pomace</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pummel</ent><br/
<hw>Pum"mel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. t.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pommel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pump</ent><br/
<hw>Pump</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/mp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably so
called as being worn for <ets>pomp</ets> or ornament. See
<er>Pomp</er>.]</ety> <def>A low shoe with a thin sole.</def><-- MW10
says "close-fitting shoe with moderate to high heel". Usage changed? -->
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pump</ent><br/
<hw>Pump</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to D. <ets>pomp</ets>, G.
<ets>pumpe</ets>, F. <ets>pompe</ets>; of unknown origin.]</ety> <def>An
hydraulic machine, variously constructed, for raising or transferring
fluids, consisting essentially of a moving piece or piston working in a
hollow cylinder or other cavity, with valves properly placed for
admitting or retaining the fluid as it is drawn or driven through them
by the action of the piston.</def> <-- this definition is for a
mechanical pump. A peristaltic pump would not fit this def. MW10: "a
device that raises, transfers, or compresses fluids . . . by suction or
pressure or both." --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ for various kinds of pumps, see <er>Air pump</er>,
<er>Chain pump</er>, and <er>Force pump</er>; also, under
<er>Lifting</er>, <er>Plunger</er>, <er>Rotary</er>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Circulating pump</b></col> <fld>(Steam Engine)</fld>,
<cd>a pump for driving the condensing water through the casing, or
tubes, of a surface condenser.</cd> -- <col><b>Pump brake</b></col>.
<cd>See <cref>Pump handle</cref>, below.</cd> -- <col><b>Pump
dale</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Dale</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pump
gear</b></col>, <cd>the apparatus belonging to a pump.</cd>
<au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>Pump handle</b></col>, <cd>the lever, worked
by hand, by which motion is given to the bucket of a pump.</cd> --
<col><b>Pump hood</b></col>, <cd>a semicylindrical appendage covering
the upper wheel of a chain pump.</cd> -- <col><b>Pump rod</b></col>,
<cd>the rod to which the bucket of a pump is fastened, and which is
attached to the brake or handle; the piston rod.</cd> -- <col><b>Pump
room</b></col>, <cd>a place or room at a mineral spring where the waters
are drawn and drunk.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Pump
spear</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Pump rod</cref>, above.</cd> --
<col><b>Pump stock</b></col>, <cd>the stationary part, body, or barrel
of a pump.</cd> -- <col><b>Pump well</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Well</er>.</cd></cs><-- vacuum pump, a pump which creates a
vacuum by removing gas (usually air) from a container. Mechanical vacuum
pump, a vacuum pump operating by the motion of a piston or rotary blade
in a chamber, as contrasted with an aspirator. Persistaltic pump, a pump
transferring fluids by peristaltic action on a flexible tube. Such pumps
are used where a gentle pumping action is desired, or the transferred
fluid may be harmed in a mechanical pump; as in the infusion of fluids
into blood vessels of the body, or the pumping of explosive or easily
decomposed fluids. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pump</ent><br/
<hw>Pump</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pumped</conjf> <pr>(p<ucr/mt; 215)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>pumping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To raise
with a pump, as water or other liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw water, or the like, from; to from water by
means of a pump; <as>as, they <ex>pumped</ex> the well dry; to
<ex>pump</ex> a ship.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Figuratively, to draw out or obtain, as secrets or
money, by persistent questioning or plying; to question or ply
persistently in order to elicit something, as information, money,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But <qex>pump</qex> not me for politics.</q>
<rj><qau>Otway.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pump</ent><br/
<hw>Pump</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To work, or raise water, a
pump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumpage</ent><br/
<hw>Pump"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is raised
by pumps, or the work done by pumps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pumpage</qex> last year amounted to . . . gallons.</q>
<rj><qau>Sci. Amer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumper</ent><br/
<hw>Pump"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pumps; the
instrument or machine used in pumping.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumpernickel</ent><br/
<hw>Pump"er*nick`el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.]</ety>
<def>A sort of bread, made of unbolted rye, which forms the chief food
of the Westphalian peasants. It is acid but nourishing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumpet</ent><br/
<hw>Pum"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pompet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pumpet ball</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a ball for
inking types; a pompet.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumping</ent><br/
<hw>Pump"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>pump</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pumping engine</b></col>, <cd>a steam engine and pump
combined for raising water. See <er>Steam engine</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumpion</ent><br/
<hw>Pump"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pumpkin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumpkin</ent><br/
<hw>Pump"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For older
<ets>pompion</ets>, <ets>pompon</ets>, OF. <ets>pompon</ets>, L.
<ets>pepo</ets>, <ets>peponis</ets>, Gr. <?/, properly, cooked by the
sun, ripe, mellow; -- so called because not eaten till ripe. Cf.
<er>Cook</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A well-known
trailing plant (<spn>Cucurbita pepo</spn>) and its fruit, -- used for
cooking and for feeding stock; a pompion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pumpkin seed</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The flattish
oval seed of the pumpkin</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>The
common pondfish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pumy</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E.
<ets>pummer</ets> big, large, and E. <ets>pomey</ets> pommel.]</ety>
<def>Large and rounded.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gentle stream, whose murmuring wave did play<br/
Amongst the <qex>pumy</qex> stones.</q> <rj><qau>
Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pun</ent><br/
<hw>Pun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pound</er> to
beat.]</ety> <def>To pound.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He would <qex>pun</qex> thee into shivers with his fist.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pun</ent><br/
<hw>Pun</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pun</er> to pound,
<er>Pound</er> to beat.]</ety> <def>A play on words which have the same
sound but different meanings; an expression in which two different
applications of a word present an odd or ludicrous idea; a kind of
quibble or equivocation.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A better <qex>put</qex> on this word was made on the Beggar's
Opera, which, it was said, made Gay rich, and Rich gay.</q>
<rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pun</ent><br/
<hw>Pun</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Punned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Punning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make puns, or a pun; to use a
word in a double sense, especially when the contrast of ideas is
ludicrous; to play upon words; to quibble.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pun</ent><br/
<hw>Pun</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To persuade or affect by a
pun.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puna</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., of Peruv.
origin.]</ety> <def>A cold arid table-land, as in the Andes of
Peru.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>p<amac/nch</ets> five, Skr. <ets>pa</ets><?/<ets>can</ets>. So
called because composed of five ingredients, viz., sugar, arrack, spice,
water, and lemon juice. See <er>Five</er>.]</ety> <def>A beverage
composed of wine or distilled liquor, water (or milk), sugar, and the
juice of lemon, with spice or mint; -- specifically named from the kind
of spirit used; <as>as <ex>rum punch</ex>, <ex>claret punch</ex>,
<ex>champagne punch</ex>, etc.</as></def><-- (b) a nonalcoholic
beverage, usually composed of a mixture of fruit juices --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Milk punch</b></col>, <cd>a sort of punch made with
spirit, milk, sugar, spice, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Punch bowl</b></col>,
<cd>a large bowl in which punch is made, or from which it is
served.</cd> -- <col><b>Roman punch</b></col>, <cd>a punch frozen and
served as an ice.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev, fr.
<ets>punchinello</ets>.]</ety> <def>The buffoon or harlequin of a puppet
show.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Punch and Judy</b></col>, <cd>a puppet show in which a
comical little hunchbacked Punch, with a large nose, engages in
altercation with his wife Judy.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E. Cf.
<er>Punchy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A short, fat fellow; anything
short and thick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I . . . did hear them call their fat child <qex>punch</qex>, which
pleased me mightily, that word being become a word of common use for all
that is thick and short.</q> <rj><qau>Pepys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of a breed of large, heavy draught horses;
<as>as, the Suffolk <ex>punch</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>punchen</ets>, perhaps
the same word as E. <ets>punish</ets>: or cf. E.
<ets>bunch</ets>.]</ety> <def>To thrust against; to poke; <as>as, to
<ex>punch</ex> one with the end of a stick or the elbow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A thrust or blow.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. fr.
<ets>puncheon</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A tool, usually of steel,
variously shaped at one end for different uses, and either solid, for
stamping or for perforating holes in metallic plates and other
substances, or hollow and sharpedged, for cutting out blanks, as for
buttons, steel pens, jewelry, and the like; a die.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pile Driving)</fld> <def>An extension piece applied
to the top of a pile; a dolly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A prop, as for the roof of a mine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bell punch</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Bell</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Belt punch</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a punch, or
punch pliers, for making holes for lacings in the ends of driving
belts.</cd> -- <col><b>Punch press</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Punching
machine</cref>, under <er>Punch</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></cd> --
<col><b>Punch pliers</b></col>, <cd>pliers having a tubular, sharp-edged
steel punch attached to one of the jaws, for perforating leather, paper,
and the like.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punch</ent><br/
<hw>Punch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Punched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Punching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Punch</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, a tool; cf. F. <ets>poin<cced/onner</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
perforate or stamp with an instrument by pressure, or a blow; <as>as, to
<ex>punch</ex> a hole; to <ex>punch</ex> ticket.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Punching machine</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Punching press</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a machine tool for punching
holes in metal or other material; -- called also <altname>punch
press</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncheon</ent><br/
<hw>Punch"eon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poin<cced/on</ets> awl, bodkin, crown, king-post, fr. L.
<ets>punctio</ets> a pricking, fr. <ets>pungere</ets> to prick. See
<er>Pungent</er>, and cf. <er>Punch</er> a tool,
<er>Punction</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A figured stamp, die, or punch, used by goldsmiths,
cutlers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>A short, upright piece of timber
in framing; a short post; an intermediate stud.</def> <rj><au>Oxf.
Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A split log or heavy slab with the face smoothed;
<as>as, a floor made of <ex>puncheons</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>poin<cced/on</ets>, perh. the same as
<ets>poin<cced/on</ets> an awl.]</ety> <def>A cask containing, sometimes
84, sometimes 120, gallons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncher</ent><br/
<hw>Punch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, punches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punchin</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"chin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Puncheon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>punch line</ent><br/
<hw>punch" line`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
final sentence, phrase, or word in a joke or humorous story that is
unexpected and produces the humorous effect; the point of a joke.</def>
<wns>[wns=1]</wns><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> laugh line, gag line, tag line.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>The final sentence, phrase,
or word in any narration, speech, or advertisement that produces the
speaker's intended effect.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punchinello</ent><br/
<hw>Pun`chi*nel"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>pulcinella</ets>, probably originally a word of endearment, dim. of
<ets>pulcina</ets>, <ets>pulcino</ets>, a chicken, from L.
<ets>pullicenus</ets>, <ets>pullus</ets>. See <er>Pullet</er>.]</ety>
<def>A punch; a buffoon; originally, in a puppet show, a character
represented as fat, short, and humpbacked.</def>
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punchy</ent><br/
<hw>Punch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps for
<ets>paunchy</ets>, from <ets>paunch</ets>. See 3d
<er>Punch</er>.]</ety> <def>Short and thick, or fat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctated</ent><br/
<ent>Punctated</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Punc"ta*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Punc"ta*ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From L. <ets>punctum</ets>
point. See <er>Point</er> .]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pointed; ending in a
point or points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>Dotted with small spots of
color, or with minute depressions or pits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctator</ent><br/
<hw>Punc*ta"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who marks with
points. specifically, one who writes Hebrew with points; -- applied to a
Masorite.</def> <rj><au>E. Robinson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncticular</ent><br/
<hw>Punc*tic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Comprised in,
or like, a point; exact.</def> <mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctiform</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>punctum</ets> point + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form
of a point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctilio</ent><br/
<hw>Punc*til"io</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/<nsm/k*t<icr/l"y<osl/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Punctilios</plw>
<pr>(-y<omac/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It. <ets>puntiglio</ets>, or Sp.
<ets>puntillo</ets>, dim. fr. L. <ets>punctum</ets> point. See
<er>Point</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A nice point of exactness in
conduct, ceremony, or proceeding; particularity or exactness in forms;
<as>as, the <ex>punctilios</ex> of a public ceremony</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They will not part with the least <qex>punctilio</qex> in their
opinions and practices.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctilious</ent><br/
<hw>Punc*til"ious</hw> <pr>(-y<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>puntiglioso</ets>, Sp. <ets>puntilloso</ets>.]</ety> <def>Attentive
to punctilio; very nice or exact in the forms of behavior, etiquette, or
mutual intercourse; precise; exact in the smallest particulars.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>punctilious</xex> observance of divine laws.<rdquo/
<au>Rogers.</au> <ldquo/Very <xex>punctilious</xex> copies of any
letters.<rdquo/ <au>The Nation.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Punctilious</qex> in the simple and intelligible instances of
common life.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Punc*til"ious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Punc*til"ious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punction</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>punctio</ets>, fr. <ets>pungere</ets>, <ets>punctum</ets>, to
prick: cf. F. <ets>ponction</ets>. Cf. <er>Puncheon</er>.]</ety> <def>A
puncturing, or pricking; a puncture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctist</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A punctator.</def>
<rj><au>E. Henderson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncto</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Punto</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A nice point of form or
ceremony.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A term applied to the point in fencing.</def>
<rj><au>Farrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctual</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>ponctuel</ets> (cf. Sp. <ets>puntual</ets>, It.
<ets>puntuale</ets>), from L. <ets>punctum</ets> point. See
<er>Point</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting in a point; limited
to a point; unextended.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/This
<xex>punctual</xex> spot.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The theory of the <qex>punctual</qex> existence of the soul.</q>
<rj><qau> Krauth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Observant of nice points; punctilious;
precise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Punctual</qex> to tediousness in all that he relates.</q>
<rj><qau> Bp. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So much on <qex>punctual</qex> niceties they stand.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Pitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Appearing or done at, or adhering exactly to, a
regular or an appointed time; precise; prompt; <as>as, a
<ex>punctual</ex> man; a <ex>punctual</ex> payment</as>.</def>
<ldquo/The race of the undeviating and <xex>punctual</xex> sun.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These sharp strokes [of a pendulum], with their inexorably steady
intersections, so agree with our successive thoughts that they seem like
the <qex>punctual</qex> stops counting off our very souls into the
past.</q> <rj><qau> J. Martineau.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1163 --></p>

<p><ent>Punctualist</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*al*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is very
exact in observing forms and ceremonies.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctuality</ent><br/
<hw>Punc`tu*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>ponctualit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
punctual; especially, adherence to the exact time of an engagement;
exactness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctually</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a punctual
manner; promptly; exactly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctualness</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Punctuality;
exactness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctuate</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Punctuated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Punctuating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>ponctuer</ets>. See <er>Punctual</er>.]</ety> <def>To mark with
points; to separate into sentences, clauses, etc., by points or stops
which mark the proper pauses in expressing the meaning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctuation</ent><br/
<hw>Punc`tu*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>ponctuation</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The act or art of
punctuating or pointing a writing or discourse; the art or mode of
dividing literary composition into sentences, and members of a sentence,
by means of points, so as to elucidate the author's meaning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Punctuation</xex>, as the term is usually
understood, is chiefly performed with four points: the <xex>period</xex>
[.], the <xex>colon</xex> [:], the <xex>semicolon</xex> [;], and the
<xex>comma</xex> [,]. Other points used in writing and printing, partly
rhetorical and partly grammatical, are the <xex>note of
interrogation</xex> [?], the <xex>note of exclamation</xex> [!], the
<xex>parentheses</xex> [()], the <xex>dash</xex> [--], and
<xex>brackets</xex> []. It was not until the 16th century that an
approach was made to the present system of punctuation by the Manutii of
Venice. With Caxton, oblique strokes took the place of commas and
periods.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctuative</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or belonging
to points of division; relating to punctuation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>punctuative</qex> intonation of feeble cadence.</q>
<rj><qau>Rush.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctuator</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*a`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
punctuates, as in writing; specifically, a punctator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctuist</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tu*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A punctator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctulated</ent><br/
<ent>Punctulate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Punc"tu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Punc"tu*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>punctulum</ets>, dim.
of <ets>punctum</ets> point.]</ety> <def>Marked with small
spots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The studs have their surface <qex>punctulated</qex>, as if set all
over with other studs infinitely lesser.</q> <rj><qau>
Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Punc"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
point.]</ety> <def>A point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Punctum c<ae/cum</b></col>. <ety>[L., blind
point.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Blind spot</cref>,
under <er>Blind</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>\'d8Punctum proximum</b></col>,
<cd>near point. See under <er>Point</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>\'d8Punctum
remotum</b></col>, <cd>far point. See under <er>Point</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>\'d8Punctum vegetationis</b></col> <ety>[L., point of
vegetation]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the terminal cell of a stem, or
of a leaf bud, from which new growth originates.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncturation</ent><br/
<hw>Punc`tu*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of puncturing. See <er>Acupuncture</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncture</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>punctura</ets>, fr. <ets>pungere</ets>, <ets>punctum</ets>, to
prick. See <er>Pungent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
puncturing; perforating with something pointed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small hole made by a point; a slight wound, bite,
or sting; <as>as, the <ex>puncture</ex> of a nail, needle, or
pin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lion may perish by the <qex>puncture</qex> of an asp.</q>
<rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puncture</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"ture</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Punctured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puncturing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To pierce with a small,
pointed instrument, or the like; to prick; to make a puncture in;
<as>as, to <ex>puncture</ex> the skin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punctured</ent><br/
<hw>Punc"tured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having
the surface covered with minute indentations or dots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Produced by puncture; having the
characteristics of a puncture; <as>as, a <ex>punctured</ex>
wound</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pundit</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"dit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>pandit</ets>, Skr. <ets>pandita</ets> a learned man.]</ety> <def>A
learned man; a teacher; esp., a Brahman versed in the Sanskrit language,
and in the science, laws, and religion of the Hindoos; in Cashmere, any
clerk or native official.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pandit</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pundle</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Bundle</er>.]</ety> <def>A short and fat woman; a squab.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punese</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"nese</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>punaise</ets>, fr. <ets>punais</ets> stinking, fr. L.
<ets>putere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A bedbug.</def>
<mark>[R or Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pung</ent><br/
<hw>Pung</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A kind of plain sleigh drawn by one horse;
originally, a rude oblong box on runners.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sledges or <qex>pungs</qex>, coarsely framed of split saplings,
and surmounted with a large crockery crate.</q> <rj><qau>
Judd.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They did not take out the <qex>pungs</qex> to-day.</q> <rj><qau>
E. E. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pungence</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"gence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pungent</er>.]</ety> <def>Pungency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pungency</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"gen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being pungent or piercing; keenness; sharpness; piquancy;
<as>as, the <ex>pungency</ex> of ammonia</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>pungency</xex> of menaces.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pungent</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pungens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>pungere</ets>,
<ets>punctum</ets>, to prick. Cf. <er>Compunction</er>,
<er>Expunge</er>, <er>Poignant</er>, <er>Point</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Puncheon</er>, <er>Punctilio</er>, <er>Punt</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Causing a sharp sensation, as of the
taste, smell, or feelings; pricking; biting; acrid; <as>as, a
<ex>pungent</ex> spice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pungent</qex> radish biting infant's tongue.</q>
<rj><qau>Shenstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pungent</qex> grains of titillating dust.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sharply painful; penetrating; poignant; severe;
caustic; stinging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With <qex>pungent</qex> pains on every side.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>pungent</qex> pen played its part in rousing the
nation.</q> <rj><qau>J. R. Green.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Prickly-pointed; hard and
sharp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Acrid; piercing; sharp; penetrating; acute; keen;
acrimonious; biting; stinging.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pungently</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"gent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a pungent manner;
sharply.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pungled</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"gled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>Shriveled or shrunken; -- said especially of
grain which has lost its juices from the ravages of insects, such as the
wheat midge, or Trips (<spn>Thrips cerealium</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pungy</ent><br/
<hw>Pung"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A small sloop or shallop, or a large boat with
sails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punic</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Punicus</ets>
pertaining to Carthage, or its inhabitants, fr. <ets>Poeni</ets> the
Carthaginians.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the ancient
Carthaginians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characteristic of the ancient Carthaginians;
faithless; treacherous; <as>as, <ex>Punic</ex> faith</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yes, yes, his faith attesting nations own;<br/
'T is <qex>Punic</qex> all, and to a proverb known.</q> <rj><qau> H.
Brooke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punice</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"nice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Punese</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punice</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"nice</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To punish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punicial</ent><br/
<ent>Puniceous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu*ni"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu*ni"cial</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>puniceus</ets>, fr.
<ets>Punicus</ets> Punic.]</ety> <def>Of a bright red or purple
color.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puniness</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ni*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being puny; littleness; pettiness; feebleness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punish</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Punished</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Punishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>punischen</ets>, F. <ets>punir</ets>, from L. <ets>punire</ets>,
<ets>punitum</ets>, akin to <ets>poena</ets> punishment, penalty. See
<er>Pain</er>, and <er>-ish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To impose a
penalty upon; to afflict with pain, loss, or suffering for a crime or
fault, either with or without a view to the offender's amendment; to
cause to suffer in retribution; to chasten; <as>as, to <ex>punish</ex>
traitors with death; a father <ex>punishes</ex> his child for willful
disobedience.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A greater power<br/
Now ruled him, <qex>punished</qex> in the shape he sinned.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inflict a penalty for (an offense) upon the
offender; to repay, as a fault, crime, etc., with pain or loss; <as>as,
to <ex>punish</ex> murder or treason with death</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To injure, as by beating; to pommel.</def>
<mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To deal with roughly or harshly; -- chiefly used
with regard to a contest; <as>as, our troops <ex>punished</ex> the
enemy</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To chastise; castigate; scourge; whip; lash;
correct; discipline. See <er>Chasten</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punishable</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ish*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>punissable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Deserving of, or liable to,
punishment; capable of being punished by law or right; -- said of person
or offenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That time was, when to be a Protestant, to be a Christian, was by
law as <qex>punishable</qex> as to be a traitor.</q> <rj><qau>
Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pun"ish*a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punisher</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ish*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who inflicts
punishment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punishment</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ish*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of punishing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any pain, suffering, or loss inflicted on a person
because of a crime or offense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I never gave them condign <qex>punishment</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rewards and <qex>punishments</qex> of another life.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A penalty inflicted by a court of
justice on a convicted offender as a just retribution, and incidentally
for the purposes of reformation and prevention.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Severe, rough, or disastrous treatment.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. or Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punition</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>punitio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>punition</ets>. See
<er>Punish</er>.]</ety> <def>Punishment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Mir. for Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punitive</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ni*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
punishment; involving, awarding, or inflicting punishment; <as>as,
<ex>punitive</ex> law or justice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If death be <qex>punitive</qex>, so, likewise, is the necessity
imposed upon man of toiling for his subsistence.</q> <rj><qau> I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We shall dread a blow from the <qex>punitive</qex> hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Bagehot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punitory</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ni*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Punishing; tending
to punishment; punitive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God . . . may make moral evil, as well as natural, at the same
time both prudential and <qex>punitory</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>A.
Tucker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punk</ent><br/
<hw>Punk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Spunk</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wood so decayed as to be dry,
crumbly, and useful for tinder; touchwood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fungus (<spn>Polyporus fomentarius</spn>, etc.)
sometimes dried for tinder; agaric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An artificial tinder. See <er>Amadou</er>, and
<er>Spunk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A prostitute; a strumpet.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punka</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pun"ka</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>pankh<amac/</ets> fan.]</ety> <def>A machine for fanning a room,
usually a movable fanlike frame covered with canvas, and suspended from
the ceiling. It is kept in motion by pulling a cord.</def>
<mark>[Hindostan]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>punkah</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Malcom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punkie</ent><br/
<hw>Punk"ie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Orig. unknown.]</ety>
<def>A minute biting fly of the genus <gen>Ceratopogon</gen> or allied
genus of the family <fam>Chironomid<ae/</fam>, found in swarms in
various densely wooded or mountaneous regions.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punkin</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pumpkin.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punkling</ent><br/
<hw>Punk"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A young
strumpet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punner</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A punster.</def>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punnet</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Ir.
<ets>buinne</ets> a shoot, branch.]</ety> <def>A broad, shallow basket,
for displaying fruit or flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punnology</ent><br/
<hw>Pun*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pun</ets> +
<ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or practice of punning;
paronomasia.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punster</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who puns, or is
skilled in, or given to, punning; a quibbler; a low wit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt</ent><br/
<hw>Punt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>ponter</ets>,
or It. <ets>puntare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>punctum</ets> point. See
<er>Point</er>.]</ety> <def>To play at basset, baccara, faro. or omber;
to gamble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She heard . . . of his <qex>punting</qex> at gaming tables.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt</ent><br/
<hw>Punt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of playing at basset, baccara,
faro, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt</ent><br/
<hw>Punt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS., fr. L. <ets>ponto</ets> punt,
pontoon. See <er>Pontoon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A
flat-bottomed boat with square ends. It is adapted for use in shallow
waters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt</ent><br/
<hw>Punt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To propel, as a boat in
shallow water, by pushing with a pole against the bottom; to push or
propel (anything) with exertion.</def>
<rj><au>Livingstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Football)</fld> <def>To kick (the ball) before it
touches the ground, when let fall from the hands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt</ent><br/
<hw>Punt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Football)</fld> <def>The act of
punting the ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt</ent><br/
<hw>Punt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To boat or hunt in a
punt.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To punt a football.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puntello</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pun*tel"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl.
<plw>-li</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <ety>[It., dim. of <ets>punto</ets>
point.]</ety> <fld>(Sculpture)</fld> <def>One of the points sometimes
drilled as guides for cutting away superfluous stone.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punter</ent><br/
<hw>Punt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>ponte</ets>. See <er>Punt</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>One
who punts; specifically, one who plays against the banker or dealer, as
in baccara and faro.</def> <rj><au>Hoyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punter</ent><br/
<hw>Punt"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who punts a football; also, one
who propels a punt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punter</ent><br/
<hw>Punt"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(London Stock Exchange)</fld>
<def>A scalper.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puntel</ent><br/
<ent>Puntil</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pun"til</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pun"tel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Glass Making)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pontee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punto</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>punto</ets>,
L. <ets>punctum</ets> point. See <er>Point</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Fencing)</fld> <def>A point or hit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>\'d8Punto diritto</b></col> <ety>[It.]</ety>, <cd>a
direct stroke or hit.</cd> -- <col><b>\'d8Punto reverso</b></col>
<ety>[It. <ets>riverso</ets> reverse]</ety>, <cd>a backhanded
stroke.</cd> <au>Halliwell.</au> <ldquo/Ah, the immortal passado! the
<xex>punto reverso</xex>!<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punt-out</ent><br/
<hw>Punt"-out`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(American Football)</fld> <def>A
punt made from the goal line by a player of the side which has made a
touchdown to one of his own side for a fair catch, from which an attempt
to kick a goal may be made.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Punty</ent><br/
<hw>Pun"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Glass Making)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pontee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puny</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Punier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Puniest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[F. <ets>pu<icir/t<eacute/</ets>
younger, later born, OF. <ets>puisn<eacute/</ets>; <ets>puis</ets>
afterwards (L. <ets>post</ets>; see <er>Post-</er>) +
<ets>n<eacute/</ets> born, L. <ets>natus</ets>. See <er>Natal</er>, and
cf. <er>Puisne</er>.]</ety> <def>Imperfectly developed in size or vigor;
small and feeble; inferior; petty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>puny</qex> subject strikes at thy great glory.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Breezes laugh to scorn our <qex>puny</qex> speed.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puny</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A youth; a novice.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puoy</ent><br/
<hw>Puoy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Poy</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pup</ent><br/
<hw>Pup</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Puppy</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A young dog;
a puppy.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>a young seal.</def><-- any young
canine? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pup</ent><br/
<hw>Pup</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pupped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pupping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To bring forth whelps or young,
as the female of the canine species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupa</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Pup<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pupas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L. <ets>pupa</ets> girl. doll, puppet, fem. of <ets>pupus</ets>.
Cf. <er>Puppet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
insect in that stage of its metamorphosis which usually immediately
precedes the adult, or imago, stage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Among insects belonging to the higher orders, as the
Hymenoptera, Diptera, Lepidoptera, the pupa is inactive and takes no
food; in the lower orders it is active and takes food, and differs
little from the imago except in the rudimentary state of the sexual
organs, and of the wings in those that have wings when adult. The term
<xex>pupa</xex> is sometimes applied to other invertebrates in analogous
stages of development.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of air-breathing land
snails having an elongated spiral shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Coarctate pupa</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Obtected pupa</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a pupa which is incased in
the dried-up skin of the larva, as in many Diptera.</cd> --
<col><b>Masked pupa</b></col>, <cd>a pupa whose limbs are bound down and
partly concealed by a chitinous covering, as in
Lepidoptera.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupal</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to a pupa, or the condition of a pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupate</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>To become a pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupation</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*pa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>the act of becoming a pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupe</ent><br/
<hw>Pupe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupelo</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*pe"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Cider brandy.</def>
<mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*pig"er*ous</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pupa</ets> +
<ets>-gerous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Bearing or containing
a pupa; -- said of dipterous larv<ae/ which do not molt when the pupa is
formed within them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupil</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pupille</ets>,
n. fem., L. <ets>pupilla</ets> the pupil of the eye, originally dim. of
<ets>pupa</ets> a girl. See <er>Puppet</er>, and cf. <er>Pupil</er> a
scholar.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The aperture in the iris; the
sight, apple, or black of the eye. See the Note under <er>Eye</er>, and
<er>Iris</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pin-hole pupil</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>the pupil
of the eye when so contracted (as it sometimes is in typhus, or opium
poisoning) as to resemble a pin hole.</cd>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupil</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pil</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>pupille</ets>, n. masc. &
fem., L. <ets>pupillus</ets>, <ets>pupilla</ets>, dim. of
<ets>pupus</ets> boy, <ets>pupa</ets> girl. See <er>Puppet</er>, and cf.
<er>Pupil</er> of the eye.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A youth or scholar of
either sex under the care of an instructor or tutor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Too far in years to be a <qex>pupil</qex> now.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tutors should behave reverently before their
<qex>pupils</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person under a guardian; a ward.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>A boy or a girl under the age
of puberty, that is, under fourteen if a male, and under twelve if a
female.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Learner; disciple; tyro. -- See
<er>Scholar</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupilage</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pil*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
a pupil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As sons of kings, loving in <qex>pupilage</qex>,<br/
Have turned to tyrants when they came to power.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupillarity</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`pil*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pupillarit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Pupillary</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>The period before puberty, or from birth to
fourteen in males, and twelve in females.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupillary</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"pil*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pupillaris</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pupillaire</ets>. See
<er>Pupil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a pupil or
ward.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the pupil of
the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupillometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`pil*lom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pupilla</ets> pupil of the eye + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring the size of the
pupil of the pupil of the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupipara</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*pip"a*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pupiparous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
Diptera in which the young are born in a stage like the pupa. It
includes the sheep tick, horse tick, and other parasites. Called also
<altname>Homaloptera</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupiparous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*pip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pupa</ets>
+ L. <ets>parere</ets> to bring forth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Bearing, or containing, a pupa; -- said of the matured
larv<ae/, or larval skins, of certain Diptera.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Pupipara.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupivora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*piv"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pupivorous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of parasitic
Hymenoptera, including the ichneumon flies, which destroy the larv<ae/
and pup<ae/ of insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pupivorous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*piv"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pupa</ets>
+ L. <ets>vorare</ets> to devour.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Feeding
on the pup<ae/ of insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puplican</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"li*can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Publican.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppet</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>popet</ets>,
OF. <ets>poupette</ets>; akin to F. <ets>poup<eacute/e</ets> a doll,
probably from L. <ets>puppa</ets>, <ets>pupa</ets>, a girl, doll,
puppet. Cf. <er>Poupeton</er>, <er>Pupa</er>, <er>Pupil</er>,
<er>Puppy</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>poppet</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A small image in the human form; a doll.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1164 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A similar figure moved by the hand or by a wire in a
mock drama; a marionette; a wooden actor in a play.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At the pipes of some carved organ move,<br/
The gilded <qex>puppets</qex> dance.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One controlled in his action by the will of another;
a tool; -- so used in contempt.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>The upright support for the
bearing of the spindle in a lathe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puppet master</b></col>. <cd>Same as
<er>Puppetman</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Puppet play</b></col>, <cd>a puppet
show.</cd> -- <col><b>Puppet player</b></col>, <cd>one who manages the
motions of puppets.</cd> -- <col><b>Puppet show</b></col>, <cd>a mock
drama performed by puppets moved by wires.</cd> -- <col><b>Puppet
valve</b></col>, <cd>a valve in the form of a circular disk, which
covers a hole in its seat, and opens by moving bodily away from the seat
while remaining parallel with it, -- used in steam engines, pumps,
safety valves, etc. Its edge is often beveled, and fits in a conical
recess in the seat when the valve is closed. See the valves shown in
<xex>Illusts.</xex> of <cref>Plunger pump</cref>, and <cref>Safety
valve</cref>, under <er>Plunger</er>, and <er>Safety</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppetish</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"pet*ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a
puppet in appearance or action; of the nature of a puppet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppetman</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"pet*man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A master of a
puppet show.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppetry</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"pet*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Action or
appearance resembling that of a puppet, or puppet show; hence, mere form
or show; affectation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Puppetry</qex> of the English laws of divorce.</q>
<rj><qau>Chambers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppy</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Puppies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>poup<eacute/e</ets>
doll, puppet. See <er>Puppet</er>, and cf. <er>Pup</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The young of a
canine animal, esp. of the common dog; a whelp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A name of contemptuous reproach for a conceited and
impertinent person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I found my place taken by an ill-bred, awkward <qex>puppy</qex>
with a money bag under each arm.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppy</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"py</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Puppied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puppying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To bring forth whelps; to
pup.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppyhood</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"py*hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The time or state
of being a puppy; the time of being young and undisciplined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppyish</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"py*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a puppy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puppyism</ent><br/
<hw>Pup"py*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Extreme meanness,
affectation, conceit, or impudence.</def> <rj><au>A.
Chalmers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pur</ent><br/
<hw>Pur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Purred</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of imitative origin; cf. Prov.
G. <ets>purren</ets>.]</ety> <def>To utter a low, murmuring, continued
sound, as a cat does when pleased.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>purr</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pur</ent><br/
<hw>Pur</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To signify or express by
purring.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pur</ent><br/
<hw>Pur</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The low, murmuring sound made by a cat
to express contentment or pleasure.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>purr</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purana</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*ra"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>pur<amac/<?/</ets>, properly. old, ancient, fr.
<ets>pur<amac/</ets> formerly.]</ety> <def>One of a class of sacred
Hindoo poetical works in the Sanskrit language which treat of the
creation, destruction, and renovation of worlds, the genealogy and
achievements of gods and heroes, the reigns of the Manus, and the
transactions of their descendants. The principal Puranas are eighteen in
number, and there are the same number of supplementary books called
<xex>Upa Puranas</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puranic</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*ran"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
Puranas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purbeck beds</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"beck beds`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from the Isle of
<ets>Purbeck</ets> in England.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The strata
of the Purbeck stone, or Purbeck limestone, belonging to the Oolitic
group. See the <xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purbeck stone</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"beck stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A
limestone from the Isle of Purbeck in England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purblind</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"blind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>pure-blind</ets>, i. e., wholly blind. See <er>Pure</er>, and cf.
<er>Poreblind</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wholly blind.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Purblind</xex> Argus, all eyes and no sight.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nearsighted, or dim-sighted; seeing obscurely;
<as>as, a <ex>purblind</ex> eye; a <ex>purblind</ex>
mole.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The saints have not so sharp eyes to see down from heaven; they be
<qex>purblind</qex>and sand-blind.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O <qex>purblind</qex> race of miserable men.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pur"blind`ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Pur"blind`ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purcelane</ent><br/
<hw>Purce"lane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Purslane.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purchasable</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"chas*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being bought, purchased, or obtained for a consideration; hence, venal;
corrupt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Money being the counterbalance to all things
<qex>purchasable</qex> by it, as much as you take off from the value of
money, so much you add to the price of things exchanged.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purchase</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"chase</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Purchased</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Purchasing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>purchasen</ets>, <ets>porchacen</ets>, OF. <ets>porchacier</ets>,
<ets>purchacier</ets>, to pursue, to seek eagerly, F.
<ets>pourchasser</ets>; OF. <ets>pour</ets>, <ets>por</ets>,
<ets>pur</ets>, for (L. <ets>pro</ets>) + <ets>chacier</ets> to pursue,
to chase. See <er>Chase</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pursue and
obtain; to acquire by seeking; to gain, obtain, or acquire.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That loves the thing he can not <qex>purchase</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your accent is Something finer than you could <qex>purchase</qex>
in so removed a dwelling.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His faults . . . hereditary<br/
Rather than <qex>purchased</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To obtain by paying money or its equivalent; to buy
for a price; <as>as, to <ex>purchase</ex> land, or a
house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The field which Abraham <qex>purchased</qex> of the sons of
Heth.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxv. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To obtain by any outlay, as of labor, danger, or
sacrifice, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>purchase</ex> favor with
flattery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One poor retiring minute . . . <br/
Would <qex>purchase</qex> thee a thousand thousand friends.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A world who would not <qex>purchase</qex> with a bruise?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To expiate by a fine or forfeit.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not tears nor prayers shall <qex>purchase</qex> out abuses.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To acquire by any
means except descent or inheritance.</def> <au>Blackstone.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To buy for a price.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To apply to (anything) a device for obtaining a
mechanical advantage; to get a purchase upon, or apply a purchase to;
<as>as, to <ex>purchase</ex> a cannon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purchase</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"chase</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put forth
effort to obtain anything; to strive; to exert one's self.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Duke John of Brabant <qex>purchased</qex> greatly that the Earl of
Flanders should have his daughter in marriage.</q> <rj><qau>Ld.
Berners.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To acquire wealth or property.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sure our lawyers<br/
Would not <qex>purchase</qex> half so fast.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purchase</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"chase</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>purchds</ets>, F. <ets>pourchas</ets> eager pursuit. See
<er>Purchase</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
seeking, getting, or obtaining anything.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll . . . get meat to have thee,<br/
Or lose my life in the <qex>purchase</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. &
Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of seeking and acquiring property.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The acquisition of title to, or properly in,
anything for a price; buying for money or its equivalent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is foolish to lay out money in the <qex>purchase</qex> of
repentance.</q> <rj><qau>Franklin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which is obtained, got, or acquired, in any
manner, honestly or dishonestly; property; possession;
acquisition.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer. B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We met with little <qex>purchase</qex> upon this coast, except two
small vessels of Golconda.</q> <rj><qau>De Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A beauty-waning and distressed widow . . . <br/
Made prize and <qex>purchase</qex> of his lustful eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which is obtained for a price in money or its
equivalent.</def> <ldquo/The scrip was complete evidence of his right in
the <xex>purchase</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Wheaton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Any mechanical hold, or advantage, applied to the
raising or removing of heavy bodies, as by a lever, a tackle, capstan,
and the like; also, the apparatus, tackle, or device by which the
advantage is gained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A politician, to do great things, looks for a power -- what our
workmen call a <qex>purchase</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Acquisition of lands or tenements
by other means than descent or inheritance, namely, by one's own act or
agreement.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Purchase criminal</b></col>, <cd>robbery.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Spenser.</au> -- <col><b>Purchase
money</b></col>, <cd>the money paid, or contracted to be paid, for
anything bought.</cd> <au>Berkeley.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Worth [so
many] years' purchase</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>At [so many] years'
purchase</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a phrase by which the value or cost of a
thing is expressed in the length of time required for the income to
amount to the purchasing price; as, he bought the estate at a twenty
years' purchase. To say one's life is <col><b>not worth a day's
purchase</b></col> in the same as saying one will not live a day, or is
in imminent peril.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purchaser</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"chas*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who purchases; one who acquires property for a consideration, generally
of money; a buyer; a vendee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who acquires an estate in lands
by his own act or agreement, or who takes or obtains an estate by any
means other than by descent or inheritance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purdah</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"dah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per. <ets>parda</ets>
a curtain.]</ety> <def>A curtain or screen; also, a cotton fabric in
blue and white stripes, used for curtains.</def>
<rj><au>McElrath.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pure</ent><br/
<hw>Pure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Purer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Purest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pur</ets>, F.
<ets>pur</ets>, fr. L. <ets>purus</ets>; akin to <ets>putus</ets> pure,
clear, <ets>putare</ets> to clean, trim, prune, set in order, settle,
reckon, consider, think, Skr. <ets>p<?/</ets> to clean, and perh. E.
<ets>fire</ets>. Cf. <er>Putative</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Separate
from all heterogeneous or extraneous matter; free from mixture or
combination; clean; mere; simple; unmixed; <as>as, <ex>pure</ex> water;
<ex>pure</ex> clay; <ex>pure</ex> air; <ex>pure</ex>
compassion.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>pure</qex> fetters on his shins great.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A guinea is <qex>pure</qex> gold if it has in it no alloy.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Free from moral defilement or quilt; hence,
innocent; guileless; chaste; -- applied to persons.</def> <ldquo/Keep
thyself <xex>pure</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Tim. v. 22.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now the end of the commandment is charity out of a <qex>pure</qex>
heart, and of a good conscience.</q> <rj><qau>1 Tim. i.
5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Free from that which harms, vitiates, weakens, or
pollutes; genuine; real; perfect; -- applied to things and
actions.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Pure</xex> religion and impartial
laws.<rdquo/ <au>Tickell.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>pure</xex>, fine talk of
Rome.<rdquo/ <au>Ascham.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such was the origin of a friendship as warm and <qex>pure</qex> as
any that ancient or modern history records.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>Ritually clean; fitted for holy
services.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt set them in two rows, six on a row, upon the
<qex>pure</qex> table before the Lord.</q> <rj><qau>Lev. xxiv.
6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>Of a single, simple sound or
tone; -- said of some vowels and the unaspirated consonants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pure-impure</b></col>, <cd>completely or totally
impure.</cd> <ldquo/The inhabitants were <xex>pure-impure</xex>
pagans.<rdquo/ <au>Fuller.</au> -- <col><b>Pure blue</b></col>.
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Methylene blue</cref>, under
<er>Methylene</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pure chemistry</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Chemistry</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pure mathematics</b></col>,
<cd>that portion of mathematics which treats of the principles of the
science, or contradistinction to <xex>applied mathematics</xex>, which
treats of the application of the principles to the investigation of
other branches of knowledge, or to the practical wants of life.</cd> See
<er>Mathematics</er>. <au>Davies & Peck (Math. Dict. )</au> --
<col><b>Pure villenage</b></col> <fld>(Feudal Law)</fld>, <cd>a tenure
of lands by uncertain services at the will of the lord.</cd>
<au>Blackstone.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Unmixed; clear; simple; real; true; genuine;
unadulterated; uncorrupted; unsullied; untarnished; unstained;
stainless; clean; fair; unspotted; spotless; incorrupt; chaste;
unpolluted; undefiled; immaculate; innocent; guiltless; guileless;
holy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pured</ent><br/
<hw>Pured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Purified; refined.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Bread of <xex>pured</xex> wheat.<rdquo/
<ldquo/<xex>Pured</xex> gold.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puree</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu`r<eacute/e"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A dish made by boiling any article of food to a pulp and then
blending it or rubbing it through a sieve; <as>as, a
<ex>pur<eacute/e</ex> of fish, or of potatoes</as>; especially, a soup
the thickening of which is so treated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puree</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu`r<eacute/e"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>To grind or blend into a paste with the solids
finely divided; to make into a <er>puree</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purely</ent><br/
<hw>Pure"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
pure manner (in any sense of the adjective).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nicely; prettily.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pureness</ent><br/
<hw>Pure"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being pure (in any
sense of the adjective).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purfile</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"file</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Purfle</er>.]</ety> <def>A sort of ancient trimming of tinsel and
thread for women's gowns; -- called also
<altname>bobbinwork</altname>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purfle</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pourfiler</ets>; <ets>pour</ets> for + <ets>fil</ets> a thread, L.
<ets>filum</ets>. See <er>Profile</er>, and cf. <er>Purl</er> a
border.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To decorate with a wrought or flowered
border; to embroider; to ornament with metallic threads; <as>as, to
<ex>purfle</ex> with blue and white</as>.</def> <rj><au>P.
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A goodly lady clad in scarlet red,<br/
<qex>Purfled</qex> with gold and pearl of rich assay.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>To ornament with a bordure of
emines, furs, and the like; also, with gold studs or
mountings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purflew</ent><br/
<ent>Purfle</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pur"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pur"flew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A hem, border., or trimming, as
of embroidered work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A border of any heraldic
fur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purfled</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"fled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Ornamented;
decorated; esp., embroidered on the edges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Purfled work</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>delicate
tracery, especially in Gothic architecture.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purfling</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"fling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Ornamentation on the
border of a thing; specifically, the inlaid border of a musical
instrument, as a violin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgament</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ga*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purgamentum</ets> offscourings, washings, expiatory sacrifice. See
<er>Purge</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is excreted;
excretion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A cathartic; a purgative.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgation</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purgatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>purgation</ets>. See
<er>Purge</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of purging; the act of
clearing, cleansing, or purifying, by separating and carrying off
impurities, or whatever is superfluous; the evacuation of the
bowels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The clearing of one's self from a
crime of which one was publicly suspected and accused. It was either
<xex>canonical</xex>, which was prescribed by the canon law, the form
whereof used in the spiritual court was, that the person suspected take
his oath that he was clear of the matter objected against him, and bring
his honest neighbors with him to make oath that they believes he swore
truly; or <xex>vulgar</xex>, which was by fire or water ordeal, or by
combat. See <er>Ordeal</er>.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let him put me to my <qex>purgation</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgative</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ga*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purgativus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>purgatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having
the power or quality of purging; cathartic.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A purging medicine; a cathartic.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgatively</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ga*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a purgative
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgatorian</ent><br/
<ent>Purgatorial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pur`ga*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pur`ga*to"ri*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to purgatory;
expiatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgatorian</ent><br/
<hw>Pur`ga*to"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds to the
doctrine of purgatory.</def> <rj><au>Boswell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ga*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purgatorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to cleanse; cleansing;
expiatory.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ga*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>purgatoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>A state or place of purification
after death; according to the Roman Catholic creed, a place, or a state
believed to exist after death, in which the souls of persons are
purified by expiating such offenses committed in this life as do not
merit eternal damnation, or in which they fully satisfy the justice of
God for sins that have been forgiven. After this purgation from the
impurities of sin, the souls are believed to be received into
heaven.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purge</ent><br/
<hw>Purge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Purged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>purger</ets>, L. <ets>purgare</ets>; <ets>purus</ets> pure +
<ets>agere</ets> to make, to do. See <er>Pure</er>, and
<er>Agent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cleanse, clear, or purify by
separating and carrying off whatever is impure, heterogeneous, foreign,
or superfluous.</def> <ldquo/Till fire <xex>purge</xex> all things
new.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To operate on as, or by means of,
a cathartic medicine, or in a similar manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To clarify; to defecate, as liquors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To clear of sediment, as a boiler, or of air, as a
steam pipe, by driving off or permitting escape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To clear from guilt, or from moral or ceremonial
defilement; <as>as, to <ex>purge</ex> one of guilt or
crime</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When that he hath <qex>purged</qex> you from sin.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Purge</qex> me with hyssop, and I shall be clean.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. li. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To clear from accusation, or the
charge of a crime or misdemeanor, as by oath or in ordeal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To remove in cleansing; to deterge; to wash away; --
often followed by <xex>away</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Purge</qex> away our sins, for thy name's sake.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. lxxix. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We 'll join our cares to <qex>purge</qex> away<br/
Our country's crimes.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purge</ent><br/
<hw>Purge</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become pure, as by
clarification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have or produce frequent evacuations from the
intestines, as by means of a cathartic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purge</ent><br/
<hw>Purge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>purge</ets>. See
<er>Purge</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
purging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The preparative for the <qex>purge</qex> of paganism of the
kingdom of Northumberland.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which purges; especially, a medicine that
evacuates the intestines; a cathartic.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purger</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, purges or cleanses; especially, a cathartic medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purgery</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ger*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The part of a
sugarhouse where the molasses is drained off from the sugar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purging</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ging</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That purges;
cleansing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Purging flax</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an annual
European plant of the genus <gen>Linum</gen> (<spn>Linum
catharticum</spn>); dwarf wild flax; -- so called from its use as a
cathartic medicine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purging</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ging</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The act of
cleansing; excessive evacuations; especially, diarrhea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puri</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Euxanthin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purification</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`ri*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>purification</ets>, L. <ets>purificatio</ets>. See
<er>Purify</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of purifying; the act
or operation of separating and removing from anything that which is
impure or noxious, or heterogeneous or foreign to it; <as>as, the
<ex>purification</ex> of liquors, or of metals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or operation of cleansing ceremonially, by
removing any pollution or defilement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the days of her <qex>purification</qex> according to the law
of Moses were accomplished.</q> <rj><qau>Luke ii. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A cleansing from guilt or the pollution of sin; the
extinction of sinful desires, appetites, and inclinations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purificative</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*fi*ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>purificatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having power to purify; tending to
cleanse.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purificator</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*fi*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, purifies; a purifier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purificatory</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*rif"i*ca*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purificatorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Serving or tending to purify;
purificative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purifier</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, purifies or cleanses; a cleanser; a refiner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1165 --></p>

<p><ent>Puriform</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*form</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"r<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>pus</ets>, <ets>puris</ets>, pus + <ets>-form</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>puriforme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>In the form of
pus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purify</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*fy</hw> <pr>(p<umac/"r<icr/*f<imac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Purified</conjf>
<pr>(p<umac/"r<icr/*f<imac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purifying</conjf>
<pr>(p<umac/"r<icr/*f<imac/"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>purifier</ets>, L. <ets>purificare</ets>; <ets>purus</ets> pure +
<ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make. See <er>Pure</er>, and
<er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make pure or clear from
material defilement, admixture, or imperfection; to free from extraneous
or noxious matter; <as>as, to <ex>purify</ex> liquors or metals; to
<ex>purify</ex> the blood; to <ex>purify</ex> the air.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, in figurative uses: <sd>(a)</sd> To free from
guilt or moral defilement; <as>as, to <ex>purify</ex> the
heart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And fit them so<br/
<qex>Purified</qex> to receive him pure.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To free from ceremonial or legal
defilement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Moses took the blood, and put it upon the horns of the altar,
. . . and <qex>purified</qex> the altar.</q> <rj><qau>Lev. viii.
15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Purify</qex> both yourselves and your captives.</q> <rj><qau>
Num. xxxi. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To free from improprieties or barbarisms; <as>as,
to <ex>purify</ex> a language</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sprat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purify</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*fy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To grow or become pure or
clear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purim</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu"rim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>p<umac/r</ets>, pl. <ets>p<umac/r<imac/m</ets>, a lot.]</ety>
<def>A Jewish festival, called also the Feast of Lots, instituted to
commemorate the deliverance of the Jews from the machinations of
Haman.</def> <rj><au>Esther ix. 26.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purism</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>purisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Rigid purity; the quality of being
affectedly pure or nice, especially in the choice of language;
over-solicitude as to purity.</def> <ldquo/His political
<xex>purism</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The English language, however, . . . had even already become too
thoroughly and essentially a mixed tongue for his doctrine of
<qex>purism</qex> to be admitted to the letter.</q>
<rj><qau>Craik.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purist</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>puriste</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who aims at excessive purity or nicety, esp. in the
choice of language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [Fox] . . . purified vocabulary with a scrupulosity unknown to
any <qex>purist</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who maintains that the New Testament was written
in pure Greek.</def> <rj><au>M. Stuart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puristical</ent><br/
<ent>Puristic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu*ris"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu*ris"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to purists or
purism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puritan</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*tan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Purity</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One
who, in the time of Queen Elizabeth and the first two Stuarts, opposed
traditional and formal usages, and advocated simpler forms of faith and
worship than those established by law; -- originally, a term of
reproach. The Puritans formed the bulk of the early population of New
England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>Puritans</xex> were afterward distinguished as
<xex>Political Puritans</xex>, <xex>Doctrinal Puritans</xex>, and
<xex>Puritans in Discipline</xex>.</note> <rj><au>Hume.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who is scrupulous and strict in his religious
life; -- often used reproachfully or in contempt; one who has overstrict
notions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She would make a <qex>puritan</qex> of the devil.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puritan</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*tan</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the Puritans;
resembling, or characteristic of, the Puritans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puritanical</ent><br/
<ent>Puritanic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu`ri*tan"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu`ri*tan"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
the Puritans, or to their doctrines and practice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Precise in observance of legal or religious
requirements; strict; overscrupulous; rigid; -- often used by way of
reproach or contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Paritanical</qex> circles, from which plays and novels were
strictly excluded.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had all the <qex>puritanic</qex> traits, both good and
evil.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puritanically</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`ri*tan"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a puritanical
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puritanism</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*tan*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrines,
notions, or practice of Puritans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puritanize</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*tan*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Puritanized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Puritanizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
agree with, or teach, the doctrines of Puritans; to conform to the
practice of Puritans.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purity</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ri*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>purete</ets>, <ets>purte</ets>, OF. <ets>purt<eacute/</ets>, F.
<ets>puret<eacute/</ets>, from L. <ets>puritas</ets>, fr.
<ets>purus</ets> pure. See <er>Pure</er>.]</ety> <def>The condition of
being pure.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>freedom from foreign
admixture or deleterious matter; <as>as, the <ex>purity</ex> of water,
of wine, of drugs, of metals</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Cleanness;
freedom from foulness or dirt.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>purity</xex> of a
linen vesture.<rdquo/ <au>Holyday.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Freedom from
guilt or the defilement of sin; innocence; chastity; <as>as,
<ex>purity</ex> of heart or of life</as>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>Freedom from any sinister or improper motives or views.</def>
<sd>(e)</sd> <def>Freedom from foreign idioms, or from barbarous or
improper words or phrases; <as>as, <ex>purity</ex> of
style</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purkinje's cells</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"kin*je's cells`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From J. E.
<ets>Purkinje</ets>, their discoverer.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Large ganglion cells forming a layer near the surface of the
cerebellum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purl</ent><br/
<hw>Purl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Contr. fr.
<ets>purfile</ets>, <ets>purfle</ets>. See <er>Purfle</er>.]</ety>
<def>To decorate with fringe or embroidery.</def> <ldquo/Nature's cradle
more enchased and <xex>purled</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purl</ent><br/
<hw>Purl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An embroidered and
puckered border; a hem or fringe, often of gold or silver twist; also, a
pleat or fold, as of a band.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A triumphant chariot made of carnation velvet, enriched
with<qex>purl</qex> and pearl.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An inversion of stitches in knitting, which gives to
the work a ribbed or waved appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Purl stitch</b></col>. <cd>Same as <er>Purl</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purl</ent><br/
<hw>Purl</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Purled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Sw. <ets>porla</ets>, and E.
<ets>pur</ets> to murmur as a cat.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To run
swiftly round, as a small stream flowing among stones or other
obstructions; to eddy; also, to make a murmuring sound, as water does in
running over or through obstructions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Swift o'er the rolling pebbles, down the hills,<br/
Louder and louder <qex>purl</qex> the falling rills.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Perh. fr. F. <ets>perler</ets> to pearl, to bead.
See <er>Pearl</er>, <pos>v. & n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To rise in circles,
ripples, or undulations; to curl; to mantle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>thin winding breath which <qex>purled</qex> up to the sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purl</ent><br/
<hw>Purl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 3d <er>Purl</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A circle made by the notion of a fluid; an eddy; a
ripple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whose stream an easy breath doth seem to blow,<br/
Which on the sparkling gravel runs in <qex>purles</qex>,<br/
As though the waves had been of silver curls.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A gentle murmur, as that produced by the running of
a liquid among obstructions; <as>as, the <ex>purl</ex> of a
brook</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Perh. from F. <ets>perler</ets>, v. See
<er>Purl</er> to mantle.]</ety> <def>Malt liquor, medicated or spiced;
formerly, ale or beer in which wormwood or other bitter herbs had been
infused, and which was regarded as tonic; at present, hot beer mixed
with gin, sugar, and spices.</def> <ldquo/Drank a glass of
<xex>purl</xex> to recover appetite.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au>
<ldquo/Drinking hot <xex>purl</xex>, and smoking pipes.<rdquo/
<au>Dickens.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tern.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purlieu</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"lieu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted (by
influence of <ets>lieu</ets> place) fr. OF. <ets>pural<eacute/e</ets>,
<ets>poral<eacute/e</ets> (equiv. to LL. <ets>perambulatio</ets> a
survey of boundaries, originally, a going through); <ets>por</ets> (L.
<ets>pro</ets>, confused, however, with L. <ets>per</ets> through) +
<ets>al<eacute/e</ets>. See <er>Pro-</er>, and <er>Alley</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pourlieu</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Originally, the ground near a royal forest, which, having been
unlawfully added to the forest, was afterwards severed from it, and
disafforested so as to remit to the former owners their
rights.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then as a tiger, who by chance hath spied<br/
In some <qex>purlieu</qex> two gentle fawns at play.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the outer portion of any place; an adjacent
district; environs; neighborhood.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>purlieus</xex>
of St. James.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>brokers had been incessantly plying for custom in the
<qex>purlieus</qex> of the court.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purline</ent><br/
<ent>Purlin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pur"lin</hw>, <hw>Pur"line</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>In
root construction, a horizontal member supported on the principals and
supporting the common rafters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purling</ent><br/
<hw>Purl"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 3d
<er>Purl</er>.]</ety> <def>The motion of a small stream running among
obstructions; also, the murmur it makes in so doing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purloin</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*loin"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Purloined</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Purloining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>purloignier</ets>, <ets>porloignier</ets>, to retard, delay;
<ets>pur</ets>, <ets>por</ets>, <ets>pour</ets>, for (L. <ets>pro</ets>)
+ <ets>loin</ets> far, far off (L. <ets>longe</ets>). See
<er>Prolong</er>, and cf. <er>Eloign</er>.]</ety> <def>To take or carry
away for one's self; hence, to steal; to take by theft; to
filch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Had from his wakeful custody <qex>purloined</qex><br/
The guarded gold.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>when did the muse from Fletcher scenes <qex>purloin</qex> ?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purloin</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*loin"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To practice theft; to
steal.</def> <rj><au>Titus ii. 10.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purloiner</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*loin"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
purloins.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purparty</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"par`ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pourpartie</ets>; <ets>pour</ets> for + <ets>partie</ets> a part;
cf. OF. <ets>purpart</ets> a respective part.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>A share, part, or portion of an estate allotted to a
coparcener.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>purpart</asp>, and
<asp>pourparty</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am forced to eat all the game of your <qex>purparties</qex>, as
well as my own thirds.</q> <rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purple</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Purples</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>purpre</ets>,
<ets>pourpre</ets>, OF. <ets>purpre</ets>, <ets>porpre</ets>,
<ets>pourpre</ets>, F. <ets>pourpre</ets>, L. <ets>purpura</ets> purple
fish, purple dye, fr. Gr. <?/ the purple fish, a shell from the purple
dye was obtained, purple dye; cf. <?/ dark (said of the sea), purple,
<?/ to grow dark (said of the sea), to be troubled; perh. akin to L.
<ets>furere</ets> to rage, E. <ets>fury</ets>: cf. AS.
<ets>purpure</ets>. Cf. <er>Porphyry</er>, <er>Purpure</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A color formed by, or resembling that formed by, a
combination of the primary colors red and blue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Arraying with reflected <qex>purple</qex> and gold<br/
The clouds that on his western throne attend.</q> <rj><qau>
Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The ancient words which are translated <xex>purple</xex>
are supposed to have been used for the color we call <xex>crimson</xex>.
In the gradations of color as defined in art, <xex>purple</xex> is a
mixture of red and blue. When red predominates it is called
<xex>violet</xex>, and when blue predominates,
<xex>hyacinth</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cloth dyed a purple color, or a garment of such
color; especially, a purple robe, worn as an emblem of rank or
authority; specifically, the purple rode or mantle worn by Roman
emperors as the emblem of imperial dignity; <as>as, to put on the
imperial <ex>purple</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt make the tabernacle with ten curtains of fine twined
linen, and <qex>purple</qex>, and scarlet.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxvi.
1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence: Imperial sovereignty; royal rank, dignity, or
favor; loosely and colloquially, any exalted station; great
wealth.</def> <ldquo/He was born in the <xex>purple</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gibbon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A cardinalate. See <er>Cardinal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of large butterflies,
usually marked with purple or blue, of the genus <gen>Basilarchia</gen>
(formerly <gen>Limenitis</gen>) <as>as, the banded <ex>purple</ex>
(<spn>Basilarchia arthemis</spn>).</as> See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Ursula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any shell of the genus
<gen>Purpura</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf><fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Purpura</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A disease of wheat. Same as
<er>Earcockle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Purple</xex> is sometimes used in composition, esp.
with participles forming words of obvious signification; as,
<xex>purple</xex>-colored, <xex>purple</xex>-hued,
<xex>purple</xex>-stained, <xex>purple</xex>-tinged,
<xex>purple</xex>-tinted, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>French purple</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Cudbear</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purple of Cassius</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Cassius</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purple of mollusca</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a coloring matter derived from certain mollusks,
which dyes wool, etc., of a purple or crimson color, and is supposed to
be the substance of the famous Tyrian dye. It is obtained from Ianthina,
and from several species of Purpura, and Murex.</cd> -- <col><b>To be
born in the purple</b></col>, <cd>to be of princely birth; to be
highborn.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purple</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ple</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Exhibiting or
possessing the color called purple, much esteemed for its richness and
beauty; of a deep red, or red and blue color; <as>as, a <ex>purple</ex>
robe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Imperial; regal; -- so called from the color having
been an emblem of imperial authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hide in the dust thy <qex>purple</qex> pride.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Blood-red; bloody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>May such <qex>purple</qex> tears be alway shed.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I view a field of blood,<br/
And Tiber rolling with a <qex>purple</qex> blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Purple bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
European purple gallinule. See under <er>Gallinule</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Purple copper ore</b></col>. <fld>(Min.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Bornite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purple grackle</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the crow blackbird. See under
<er>Crow</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purple martin</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Martin</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purple sandpiper</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Sandpiper</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purple shell</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Ianthina</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purple</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Purpled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purpling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make purple; to dye of purple
or deep red color; <as>as, hands <ex>purpled</ex> with
blood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When morn<br/
<qex>Purples</qex> the east.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Reclining soft in blissful bowers,<br/
<qex>Purpled</qex> sweet with springing flowers.</q> <rj><qau>
Fenton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpleheart</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ple*heart`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A strong, durable, and elastic wood of a purplish color, obtained
from several tropical American leguminous trees of the genus
<gen>Copaifera</gen> (<spn>Copaifera pubiflora</spn>, <spn>Copaifera
bracteata</spn>, <b>and</b> <spn>Copaifera officinalis</spn>). Used for
decorative veneering. See <er>Copaiba</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purplewood</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ple*wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Purpleheart</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purplish</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"plish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
purple.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purport</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"port</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>purport</ets>; <ets>pur</ets>, <ets>pour</ets>, for (L.
<ets>pro</ets>) + <ets>porter</ets> to bear, carry. See <er>Port</er>
demeanor.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Design or tendency; meaning; import;
tenor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whole scope and <qex>purport</qex> of that dialogue.
<qex>Norris</qex>.<br/
With a look so piteous in <qex>purport</qex><br/
As if he had been loosed out of hell.</q> <rj><qau> Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Disguise; covering.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For she her sex under that strange <qex>purport</qex><br/
Did use to hide.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purport</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"port</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Purported</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purporting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>purporter</ets>,
<ets>pourporter</ets>. See <er>Purport</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To intend to show; to intend; to mean; to signify; to import; --
often with an object clause or infinitive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They in most grave and solemn wise unfolded<br/
Matter which little <qex>purported</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purportless</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"port*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without purport or
meaning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpose</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>purpos</ets>, <ets>pourpos</ets>, <ets>propos</ets>, L.
<ets>propositum</ets>. See <er>Propound</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>That which a person sets before himself as an object to be reached
or accomplished; the end or aim to which the view is directed in any
plan, measure, or exertion; view; aim; design; intention;
plan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He will his firste <qex>purpos</qex> modify.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As my eternal <qex>purpose</qex> hath decreed.</q> <rj><qau>
Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The flighty <qex>purpose</qex> never is o'ertook<br/
Unless the deed go with it.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Proposal to another; discourse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Instance; example.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>In purpose</b></col>, <col><b>Of purpose</b></col>,
<col><b>On purpose</b></col></mcol>, <cd>with previous design; with the
mind directed to that object; intentionally. <xex>On purpose</xex> is
the form now generally used.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- design; end; intention; aim. See
<er>Design</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpose</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pose</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Purposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Purposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>purposer</ets>,
<ets>proposer</ets>. See <er>Propose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
set forth; to bring forward.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To propose, as an aim, to one's self; to determine
upon, as some end or object to be accomplished; to intend; to design; to
resolve; -- often followed by an infinitive or dependent clause.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Did nothing <qex>purpose</qex> against the state.</q> <rj><qau>
Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>purpose</qex> to write the history of England from the
accession of King James the Second down to a time which is within the
memory of men still living.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpose</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pose</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To have a purpose or intention;
to discourse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purposedly</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"posed*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a purposed
manner; according to purpose or design; purposely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A poem composed <qex>purposedly</qex> of the Trojan war.
<qex>Holland</qex>.</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purposeful</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pose*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Important;
material.</def> <ldquo/Purposeful accounts.<rdquo/ <au>Tylor.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pur"pose*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purposeless</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pose*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no purpose or result;
objectless.</def> <au>Bp. Hall.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pur"pose*less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purposely</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pose*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With purpose or design;
intentionally; with predetermination; designedly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In composing this discourse, I <qex>purposely</qex> declined all
offensive and displeasing truths.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So much they scorn the crowd, that if the throng<br/
By chance go right, they <qex>purposely go</qex> wrong.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purposer</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pos*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
brings forward or proposes anything; a proposer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who forms a purpose; one who intends.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purposive</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"po*sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having or
indicating purpose or design.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Purposive</xex>
characters.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bastian.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Purposive</qex> modification of structure in a bone.</q>
<rj><qau>Owen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is impossible that the frog should perform actions
more<qex>purposive</qex> than these.</q> <rj><qau>Huxley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpre</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>Purple.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpresture</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*pres"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably
corrupted (see <er>Prest</er>) fr. OF. <ets>pourprisure</ets>, fr.
<ets>pourprendre</ets>: cf. LL. <ets>purprestura</ets>. Cf.
<er>Purprise</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Wrongful encroachment
upon another's property; esp., any encroachment upon, or inclosure of,
that which should be common or public, as highways, rivers, harbors,
forts, etc.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>pourpresture</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purprise</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"prise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pourpris</ets>,fr. <ets>pourprendre</ets> to take away entirely;
<ets>pour</ets> for + <ets>prendre</ets> to take.]</ety> <def>A close or
inclosure; the compass of a manor.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pur"pu*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., purple,
purple fish: cf. F. <ets>purpura</ets>. See <er>Purple</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease characterized by livid
spots on the skin from extravasated blood, with loss of muscular
strength, pain in the limbs, and mental dejection; the purples.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of marine gastropods,
usually having a rough and thick shell. Some species yield a purple
dye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpurate</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pu*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to purpura.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpurate</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pu*rate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of
purpuric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpure</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purpura</ets> purple. See <er>Purple</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>Purple, -- represented in engraving by diagonal lines declining
from the right top to the left base of the escutcheon (or from sinister
chief to dexter base).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpureal</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*pu"re*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a purple
color; purple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpureo-</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*pu"re*o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form signifying
<xex>of a purple</xex> or <xex>purple-red color</xex>. Specif.
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, used in designating certain brilliant
<xex>purple-red</xex> compounds of cobaltic chloride and ammonia,
similar to the roseocobaltic compounds. See <er>Cobaltic</er>.
</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1166 --></p>

<p><ent>Purpuric</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*pu"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>purpurique</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to purpura.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to or designating, a
nitrogenous acid contained in uric acid. It is not known in the pure
state, but forms well-known purple-red compounds (as murexide), whence
its name.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Purpuric acid</xex> was formerly used to designate
murexan. See <er>Murexan</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpurin</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"pu*rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A dyestuff resembling alizarin, found in madder root, and extracted
as an orange or red crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpuriparous</ent><br/
<hw>Pur`pu*rip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purpura</ets> purple + <ets>parere</ets> to produce.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Producing, or connected with, a purple-colored
secretion; <as>as, the <ex>purpuriparous</ex> gland of certain
gastropods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purpurogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Pur`pu*rog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purpura</ets> purple + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having the power to produce a purple color;
<as>as, the <ex>purpurogenous</ex> membrane, or choroidal epithelium, of
the eye</as>. See <cref>Visual purple</cref>, under
<er>Visual</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purr</ent><br/
<hw>Purr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To murmur as a
cat. See <er>Pur</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purr</ent><br/
<hw>Purr</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The low murmuring sound made by a cat;
pur. See <er>Pur</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purre</ent><br/
<hw>Purre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
dunlin.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purree</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"ree</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>peori</ets> yellow.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A yellow
coloring matter. See <er>Euxanthin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purrock</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"rock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Puddock</er>,
and <er>Parrock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purse</ent><br/
<hw>Purse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>purs</ets>,
<ets>pors</ets>, OF. <ets>burse</ets>, <ets>borse</ets>,
<ets>bourse</ets>, F. <ets>bourse</ets>, LL. bursa, fr. Gr. <?/ hide,
skin, leather. Cf. <er>Bourse</er>, <er>Bursch</er>, <er>Bursar</er>,
<er>Buskin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small bag or pouch, the
opening of which is made to draw together closely, used to carry money
in; by extension, any receptacle for money carried on the person; a
wallet; a pocketbook; a portemonnaie.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> Who steals my <qex>purse</qex> steals trash.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a treasury; finances; <as>as, the public
<ex>purse</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sum of money offered as a prize, or collected as a
present; <as>as, to win the <ex>purse</ex>; to make up a
<ex>purse</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A specific sum of money</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>In Turkey, the sum of 500 piasters.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In
Persia, the sum of 50 tomans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Light purse</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Empty
purse</b></col></mcol>, <cd>poverty or want of resources.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Long purse</b></col>, <or/ <col><b>Heavy
purse</b></col></mcol>, <cd>wealth; riches.</cd> -- <col><b>Purse
crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any land crab of the genus
<gen>Birgus</gen>, allied to the hermit crabs. They sometimes weigh
twenty pounds or more, and are very strong, being able to crack
cocoanuts with the large claw. They chiefly inhabit the tropical islands
of the Pacific and Indian Oceans, living in holes and feeding upon
fruit. Called also <altname>palm crab</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purse
net</b></col>, <cd>a fishing net, the mouth of which may be closed or
drawn together like a purse.</cd> <au>Mortimer.</au> -- <col><b>Purse
pride</b></col>, <cd>pride of money; insolence proceeding from the
possession of wealth.</cd> <au>Bp. Hall.</au> -- <col><b>Purse
rat</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Pocket gopher</cref>,
under <er>Pocket</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword and purse</b></col>,
<cd>the military power and financial resources of a
nation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purse</ent><br/
<hw>Purse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pursed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pursing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put into a
purse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will go and <qex>purse</qex> the ducats straight.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw up or contract into folds or wrinkles, like
the mouth of a purse; to pucker; to knit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou . . . didst contract and <qex>purse</qex> thy brow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purse</ent><br/
<hw>Purse</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To steal purses; to rob.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>purse</qex>: . . . I'll bet at bowling alleys.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purseful</ent><br/
<hw>Purse"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pursefuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>All that is, or can be,
contained in a purse; enough to fill a purse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purse-proud</ent><br/
<hw>Purse"-proud`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
purse pride; puffed up with the possession of riches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purser</ent><br/
<hw>Purs"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Purse</er>,
and cf. <er>Bursar</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A commissioned officer in the
navy who had charge of the provisions, clothing, and public moneys on
shipboard; -- now called <altname>paymaster</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A clerk on steam passenger vessels whose duty it is
to keep the accounts of the vessels, such as the receipt of freight,
tickets, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Colloquially, any paymaster or cashier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Purser's name</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a false
name.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursership</ent><br/
<hw>Purs"er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of purser.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purset</ent><br/
<hw>Purs"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A purse or purse
net.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursiness</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"si*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State of being
pursy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursive</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pursy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"sive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pursiness.</def> <mark>[Obs.
& R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purslain</ent><br/
<hw>Purs"lain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Purslane</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purslane</ent><br/
<hw>Purs"lane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>porcelaine</ets>, <ets>pourcelaine</ets> (cf. It.
<ets>porcellana</ets>), corrupted fr. L. <ets>porcilaca</ets> for
<ets>portulaca</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An annual plant
(<spn>Portulaca oleracea</spn>), with fleshy, succulent, obovate leaves,
sometimes used as a pot herb and for salads, garnishing, and
pickling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Flowering purslane</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Great flowered purslane</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the <spn>Portulaca
grandiflora</spn>. See <er>Portulaca</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Purslane
tree</b></col>, <cd>a South African shrub (<cref>Portulacaria
Afra</cref>) with many small opposite fleshy obovate leaves.</cd> --
<col><b>Sea purslane</b></col>, <cd>a seashore plant (<cref>Arenaria
peploides</cref>) with crowded opposite fleshy leaves.</cd> --
<col><b>Water purslane</b></col>, <cd>an aquatic plant (<cref>Ludwiqia
palustris</cref>) but slightly resembling purslane.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuable</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*su"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being,
or fit to be, pursued, followed, or prosecuted.</def>
<rj><au>Sherwood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursual</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*su"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
pursuit.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuance</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*su"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pursuant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pursuing or
prosecuting; a following out or after.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sermons are not like curious inquiries after new nothings, but
<qex>pursuances</qex> of old truths.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being pursuant; consequence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In pursuance of</b></col>, <cd>in accordance with; in
prosecution or fulfillment of.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuant</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*su"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Pursue</er>: cf. OE. <ets>poursuiant</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pursuivant</er>.]</ety> <def>Acting in consequence or in prosecution
(of anything); hence, agreeable; conformable; following; according; --
with <xex>to</xex> or <xex>of</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The conclusion which I draw from these premises,
<qex>pursuant</qex> to the query laid down, is, etc.</q>
<rj><qau>Waterland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuantly</ent><br/
<ent>Pursuant</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pur*su"ant</hw>, <hw>Pur*su"ant*ly</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>Agreeably; conformably.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursue</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*sue"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Pursued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pursuing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>pursuen</ets>,
<ets>porsuen</ets>, OF. <ets>porsivre</ets>, <ets>poursuivre</ets>,
<ets>poursuir</ets>, F. <ets>poursuivre</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>prosequi</ets>; <ets>pro</ets> forward + <ets>sequi</ets> to
follow. See <er>Sue</er>, and cf. <er>Prosecute</er>,
<er>Pursuivant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To follow with a view to
overtake; to follow eagerly, or with haste; to chase; <as>as, to
<ex>pursue</ex> a hare</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We happiness <qex>pursue</qex>; we fly from pain.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The happiness of men lies in <qex>purswing</qex>,<br/
Not in possessing.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To seek; to use or adopt measures to obtain; <as>as,
to <ex>pursue</ex> a remedy at law</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fame of ancient matrons you <qex>pursue</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To proceed along, with a view to some and or object;
to follow; to go in; <as>as, Captain Cook <ex>pursued</ex> a new route;
the administration <ex>pursued</ex> a wise course.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To prosecute; to be engaged in; to continue.</def>
<ldquo/ Insatiate to <xex>pursue</xex> vain war.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To follow as an example; to imitate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To follow with enmity; to persecute; to call to
account.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The servant is not greater than his lord. If they have
<qex>pursued</qex> me, they shall <qex>pursue</qex> you also.</q>
<rj><qau>Wyclif (John xv. 20).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To follow; chase; seek; persist. See
<er>Follow</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursue</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*sue"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go in pursuit;
to follow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wicked flee when no man <qex>pursueth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xxviii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men hotly <qex>pursued</qex> after the objects of their
ambition.</q> <rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To go on; to proceed, especially in argument or
discourse; to continue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>[A Gallicism]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have, <qex>pursues</qex> Carneades, wondered chemists should not
consider.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To follow a matter judicially, as a
complaining party; to act as a prosecutor.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuer</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*su"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
pursues or chases; one who follows in haste, with a view to
overtake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. & Scots Law)</fld> <def>A plaintiff; a
prosecutor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuit</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*suit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poursuite</ets>, fr. <ets>poursuivre</ets>. See <er>Pursue</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of following or going
after; esp., a following with haste, either for sport or in hostility;
chase; prosecution; <as>as, the <ex>pursuit</ex> of game; the
<ex>pursuit</ex> of an enemy.</as></def>
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Weak we are, and can not shun <qex>pursuit</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A following with a view to reach, accomplish, or
obtain; endeavor to attain to or gain; <as>as, the <ex>pursuit</ex> of
knowledge; the <ex>pursuit</ex> of happiness or pleasure.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Course of business or occupation; continued
employment with a view to same end; <as>as, mercantile
<ex>pursuits</ex>; a literary <ex>pursuit</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Prosecution.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>pursuit</qex> for tithes ought, and of ancient time did
pertain to the spiritual court.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Curve of pursuit</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a
curve described by a point which is at each instant moving towards a
second point, which is itself moving according to some specified
law.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuivant</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"sui*vant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>poursuivant</ets>, fr. <ets>poursuivre</ets>. See <er>Pursue</er>,
and cf. <er>Pursuant</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>poursuivant</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Heralds'
College)</fld> <def>A functionary of lower rank than a herald, but
discharging similar duties; -- called also <altname>pursuivant at
arms</altname>; an attendant of the heralds. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The herald Hope, forerunning Fear,<br/
And Fear, the <qex>pursuivant</qex> of Hope.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The king's messenger; a state messenger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One <qex>pursuivant</qex> who attempted to execute a warrant there
was murdered.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursuivant</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"sui*vant</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pursue.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their navy was <qex>pursuivanted</qex> after with a horrible
tempest.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pursy</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pourcif</ets>, <ets>poulsif</ets>, <ets>poussif</ets>, fr.
<ets>pousser</ets> to push, thrust, heave, OF. also <ets>poulser</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>pousse</ets> the heaves, asthma. See <er>Push</er>.]</ety>
<def>Fat and short-breathed; fat, short, and thick; swelled with
pampering; <as>as, <ex>pursy</ex> insolence</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pursy</qex> important he sat him down.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purtenance</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"te*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. fr.
<ets>appurtenance</ets>.]</ety> <def>That which pertains or belongs to
something; esp., the heard, liver, and lungs of an animal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/ The <xex>purtenaunces</xex> of
purgatory.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Roast [it] with fire, his head with his legs, and with the
<qex>purtenance</qex> [<it>Rev. Ver.</it>, inwards] thereof.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purulency</ent><br/
<ent>Purulence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu"ru*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu"ru*len*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>purulentia</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>purulence</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The quality or
state of being purulent; the generation of pus; also, the pus
itself.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purulent</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ru*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>purulentus</ets>, fr. <ets>pus</ets>, <ets>puris</ets>, pus,
matter: cf. F. <ets>purulent</ets>. See <er>Pus</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Consisting of pus, or matter; partaking of the
nature of pus; attended with suppuration; <as>as, <ex>purulent</ex>
inflammation</as>.</def><-- sic. What kind of "matter"? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purulently</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ru*lent*ly</hw>, <pos>v.</pos> <def>In a purulent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purveiaunce</ent><br/
<ent>Purveance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pur"ve*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pur"vei*aunce`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Purveyance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purvey</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*vey"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Purveyed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Purveying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>purveien</ets>, <ets>porveien</ets>, OF. <ets>porveeir</ets>,
<ets>porveoir</ets>, F. <ets>pourvoir</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>providere</ets>. See <er>Provide</er>, and cf.
<er>Purview</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To furnish or provide, as with
a convenience, provisions, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give no odds to your foes, but do <qex>purvey</qex><br/
Yourself of sword before that bloody day.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To procure; to get.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I mean to <qex>purvey</qex> me a wife after the fashion of the
children of Benjamin.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purvey</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*vey"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To purchase
provisions; to provide; to make provision.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.
Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pander; -- with <xex>to</xex>.</def> <ldquo/
Their turpitude <xex>purveys</xex> to their malice.<rdquo/
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purveyance</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*vey"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pourvoyance</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of
providing or procuring; providence; foresight; preparation;
management.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ill <qex>purveyance</qex> of his page.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is provided; provisions; food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A providing necessaries for
the sovereign by buying them at an appraised value in preference to all
others, and oven without the owner's consent. This was formerly a royal
prerogative, but has long been abolished.</def>
<rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purveyor</ent><br/
<hw>Pur*vey"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>porveour</ets>, OF. <ets>pourveor</ets>, F. <ets>pourvoyeur</ets>.
See <er>Purvey</er>, and cf. <er>Proveditor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who provides victuals, or whose business is to make provision
for the table; a victualer; a caterer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An officer who formerly provided, or exacted
provision, for the king's household.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>a procurer; a pimp; a bawd.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Purview</ent><br/
<hw>Pur"view</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>purveu</ets>, <ets>pourveu</ets>, F. <ets>pourvu</ets>, provided,
p. p. of OF. <ets>porveoir</ets>, F. <ets>pourvoir</ets>. See
<er>Purvey</er>, <er>View</er>, and cf. <er>Proviso</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The body of a statute, or
that part which begins with <ldquo/ <xex>Be it enacted</xex>, <rdquo/ as
distinguished from the <xex>preamble</xex>.</def> <au>Cowell.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> Hence: <def>The limit or scope of a statute; the whole
extent of its intention or provisions.</def>
<rj><au>Marshall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Profanations within the <qex>purview</qex> of several
statutes.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Limit or sphere of authority; scope;
extent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In determining the extent of information required in the exercise
of a particular authority, recourse must be had to the objects within
the <qex>purview</qex> of that authority.</q>
<rj><qau>Madison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pus</ent><br/
<hw>Pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., akin to Gr. <?/, <?/,
and to E. <ets>foul</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pus</ets>. See <er>Foul</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The yellowish white opaque
creamy matter produced by the process of suppuration. It consists of
innumerable white nucleated cells floating in a clear liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusane</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"sane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anc. Armor)</fld>
<def>A piece of armor for the breast; often, an addition to, or
re<eum/nforcement of. the breastplate; -- called also
<altname>pesane</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puseyism</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"sey*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld>
<def>The principles of Dr. <ets>Pusey</ets> and others at Oxford,
England, as exhibited in various publications, esp. in a series which
appeared from 1833 to 1841, designated <ldquo/ Tracts for the
Times;<rdquo/ tractarianism. See <er>Tractarianism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puseyite</ent><br/
<ent>Puseyistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu"sey*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu"sey*ite</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Puseyism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puseyite</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"sey*ite</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds the principles of
Puseyism; -- often used opprobriously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Push</ent><br/
<hw>Push</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably F.
<ets>poche</ets>. See <er>Pouch</er>.]</ety> <def>A pustule; a
pimple.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Push</ent><br/
<hw>Push</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Pushed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Pushing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>possen</ets>,
<ets>pussen</ets>, F. <ets>pousser</ets>, fr. L. <ets>pulsare</ets>, v.
intens. fr. <ets>pellere</ets>, <ets>pulsum</ets>, to beat, knock, push.
See <er>Pulse</er> a beating, and cf. <er>Pursy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To press against with force; to drive or impel by pressure; to
endeavor to drive by steady pressure, without striking; -- opposed to
<ant>draw</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sidelong had <qex>pushed</qex> a mountain from his seat.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To thrust the points of the horns against; to
gore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the ox shall <qex>push</qex> a manservant or maidservant, . . .
the ox shall be stoned.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxi. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To press or urge forward; to drive; to
<xex>push</xex> an objection too far.</def> <ldquo/ To <xex>push</xex>
his fortune.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ambition <qex>pushes</qex> the soul to such actions as are apt to
procure honor to the actor.</q> <rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are <qex>pushed</qex> for an answer.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bear hard upon; to perplex; to
embarrass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To importune; to press with solicitation; to
tease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To push down</b></col>, <cd>to overthrow by pushing or
impulse.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Push</ent><br/
<hw>Push</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make a thrust; to
shove; <as>as, to <ex>push</ex> with the horns or with a
sword</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make an advance, attack, or effort; to be
energetic; <as>as, a man must <ex>push</ex> in order to
succeed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At the time of the end shall the kind of the south <qex>push</qex>
at him and the king of the north shall come against him.</q>
<rj><qau>Dan. xi. 40.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>War seemed asleep for nine long years; at length<br/
Both sides resolved to <qex>push</qex>, we tried our strength.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To burst pot, as a bud or shoot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To push on</b></col>, <cd>to drive or urge forward; to
hasten.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rider <qex>pushed</qex> on at a rapid pace.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Push</ent><br/
<hw>Push</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thrust with a pointed
instrument, or with the end of a thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any thrust. pressure, impulse, or force, or force
applied; a shove; <as>as, to give the ball the first
<ex>push</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An assault or attack; an effort; an attempt; hence,
the time or occasion for action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Exact reformation is not perfected at the first
<qex>push</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When it comes to the <qex>push</qex>, 'tis no more than talk.</q>
<rj><qau>L' Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The faculty of overcoming obstacles; aggressive
energy; <as>as, he has <ex>push</ex>, or he has no
<ex>push</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Thrust</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Push</ent><br/
<hw>Push</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A crowd; a company or clique of
associates; a gang.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Push button</ent><br/
<hw>Push button</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A simple device,
resembling a button in form, so arranged that pushing it closes an
electric circuit, as of an electric bell; -- called also
<altname>button</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusher</ent><br/
<hw>Push"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, pushes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who sells illegal drugs, esp. one who tries to
convince others to use such drugs.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Railroad)</fld> <def>a second engine attached to
the rear of a train to provide extra power for climbing steep
grades.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pushing</ent><br/
<hw>Push"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pressing forward in business;
enterprising; driving; energetic; also, forward; officious,
intrusive.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Push"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pushpin</ent><br/
<hw>Push"pin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A child's game
played with pins.</def> <rj><au>L. Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusil</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"sil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pusillus</ets>
very little.]</ety> <def>Very small; little; petty.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusillanimity</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`sil*la*nim"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pusillanimitas</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pusillanimit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality of being
pusillanimous; weakness of spirit; cowardliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The badge of <qex>pusillanimity</qex> and cowardice.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is obvious to distinguished between an act of . . .
<qex>pusillanimity</qex> and an act of great modesty or humility.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cowardliness; cowardice; fear;
timidity.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusillanimous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`sil*lan"i*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pusillanimis</ets>; <ets>pusillus</ets> very little (dim. of
<ets>pusus</ets> a little boy; cf. <ets>puer</ets> a boy, E.
<ets>puerile</ets>) + <ets>animus</ets> the mind: cf. F.
<ets>pusillanime</ets>. See <er>Animosity</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Destitute of a manly or courageous strength and firmness of mind;
of weak spirit; mean-spirited; spiritless; cowardly; -- said of persons,
<as>as, a <ex>pusillanimous</ex> prince</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1167 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Evincing, or characterized by, weakness of mind, and
want of courage; feeble; <as>as, <ex>pusillanimous</ex>
counsels</as>.</def> <ldquo/A low and <xex>pusillanimous</xex>
spirit.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cowardly; dastardly; mean-spirited; fainthearted;
timid; weak; feeble.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusillanimously</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`sil*lan"i*mous*ly</hw>
<pr>(p<umac/`s<icr/l*l<acr/n"<icr/*m<ucr/s*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>With pusillanimity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pusley</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"ley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Purslane.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U. S]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puss</ent><br/
<hw>Puss</hw> <pr>(p<usdot/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>poes</ets>, Ir. & Gael. <ets>pus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
cat; -- a fondling appellation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hare; -- so called by sportsmen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Puss in the corner</b></col>, <cd>a game in which all the
players but one occupy corners of a room, or certain goals in the open
air, and exchange places, the one without a corner endeavoring to get a
corner while it is vacant, leaving some other without one.</cd> --
<col><b>Puss moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several
species of stout bombycid moths belonging to <gen>Cerura</gen>,
<gen>Harpyia</gen>, and allied genera, esp. <spn>Harpyia vinuli</spn>,
of Europe. The larv<ae/ are humpbacked, and have two caudal
appendages.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pussy</ent><br/
<hw>Pussy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>puss</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pet name for a cat; also, an
endearing name for a girl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A catkin of the pussy willow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The game of tipcat; -- also called <altname>pussy
cat</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pussy willow</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any kind of
willow having large cylindrical catkins clothed with long glossy hairs,
especially the American <spn>Salix discolor</spn>; -- called also
<altname>glaucous willow</altname>, and <altname>swamp
willow</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pussy</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pursy</er>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustulant</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"tu*lant</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pustulans</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Pustulate</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Producing pustules.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A medicine that produces pustules, as croton
oil.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustular</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"tu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to pustules; <as>as, <ex>pustular</ex> prominences;
<ex>pustular</ex> eruptions.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Covered with pustulelike prominences;
pustulate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustulate</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"tu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pustulatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>pustulare</ets> to blister, fr.
<ets>pustula</ets>. See <er>Pustule</er>.]</ety> <def>To form into
pustules, or blisters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustulated</ent><br/
<ent>Pustulate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pus"tu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pus"tu*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered with pustulelike
prominences; pustular; pustulous; <as>as, a <ex>pustulate</ex> leaf; a
<ex>pustulate</ex> shell or coral.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustulation</ent><br/
<hw>Pus*tu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pustulatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of producing pustules; the
state of being pustulated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustule</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"tule</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pustula</ets>, and <ets>pusula</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pustule</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A vesicle or an
elevation of the cuticle with an inflamed base, containing
pus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Malignant pustule</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Malignant</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pustulous</ent><br/
<hw>Pus"tu*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pustulosus</ets>, fr. <ets>pustula</ets> a pustule: cf. F.
<ets>pustuleux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling, or covered with, pustules;
pustulate; pustular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Pit</er>.]</ety>
<def>A pit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>3d pers. sing. pres.</pos> of
<er>Put</er>, contracted from <xex>putteth</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. W. <ets>pwt</ets> any
short thing, <ets>pwt o ddyn</ets> a squab of a person, <ets>pwtog</ets>
a short, thick woman.]</ety> <def>A rustic; a clown; an awkward or
uncouth person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Queer country <qex>puts</qex> extol Queen Bess's reign.</q>
<rj><qau>Bramston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What droll <qex>puts</qex> the citizens seem in it all.</q>
<rj><qau>F. Harrison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Put</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Putting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>potian</ets> to thrust:
cf. Dan. <ets>putte</ets> to put, to put into, Fries. <ets>putje</ets>;
perh. akin to W. <ets>pwtio</ets> to butt, poke, thrust; cf. also Gael.
<ets>put</ets> to push, thrust, and E. <ets>potter</ets>, v. i.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To move in any direction; to impel; to thrust; to push;
-- nearly obsolete, except with adverbs, as with <xex>by</xex> (<xex>to
put by</xex> = to thrust aside; to divert); or with <xex>forth</xex>
(<xex>to put forth</xex> = to thrust out).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His chief designs are . . . to <qex>put</qex> thee by from thy
spiritual employment.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring to a position or place; to place; to lay;
to set; figuratively, to cause to be or exist in a specified relation,
condition, or the like; to bring to a stated mental or moral condition;
<as>as, to <ex>put</ex> one in fear; to <ex>put</ex> a theory in
practice; to <ex>put</ex> an enemy to fight.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This present dignity,<br/
In which that I have <qex>put</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>put</qex> enmity between thee and the woman.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. iii. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>put</qex> no trust in his servants.</q> <rj><qau>Job iv.
18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When God into the hands of their deliverer<br/
<qex>Puts</qex> invincible might.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the mean time other measures were <qex>put</qex> in
operation.</q> <rj><qau>Sparks.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To attach or attribute; to assign; <as>as, to
<ex>put</ex> a wrong construction on an act or
expression</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To lay down; to give up; to surrender.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man hath more love than this, that a man <qex>put</qex> his
life for his friends.</q> <rj><qau>Wyclif (John xv. 13).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To set before one for judgment, acceptance, or
rejection; to bring to the attention; to offer; to state; to express;
figuratively, to assume; to suppose; -- formerly sometimes followed by
<xex>that</xex> introducing a proposition; <as>as, to <ex>put</ex> a
question; to <ex>put</ex> a case.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us now <qex>put</qex> that ye have leave.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Put</qex> the perception and you <qex>put</qex> the mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Berkeley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These verses, originally Greek, were <qex>put</qex> in Latin.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All this is ingeniously and ably <qex>put</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To incite; to entice; to urge; to constrain; to
oblige.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These wretches <qex>put</qex> us upon all mischief.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Put</qex> me not use the carnal weapon in my own defense.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thank him who <qex>puts</qex> me, loath, to this revenge.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To throw or cast with a pushing motion
<ldquo/overhand,<rdquo/ the hand being raised from the shoulder; a
practice in athletics; <as>as, to <ex>put</ex> the shot or
weight</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>To convey coal in the mine, as
from the working to the tramway.</def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Put case</b></col>, <cd>formerly, an elliptical
expression for, <xex>put</xex> or suppose the case to be.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Put case</qex> that the soul after departure from the body
may live.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <col><b>To put about</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to turn,
or change the course of, as a ship.</cd> -- <col><b>To put
away</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To renounce; to discard; to expel.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To divorce</cd>. -- <col><b>To put back</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To push or thrust backwards; hence, to hinder; to
delay.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To refuse; to deny</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Coming from thee, I could not <qex>put</qex> him
<qex>back</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To set, as the hands of a clock, to an earlier
hour.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To restore to the original place; to
replace</cd>. -- <col><b>To put by</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To turn,
set, or thrust, aside.</cd> <ldquo/Smiling <xex>put</xex> the
<xex>question by</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Tennyson.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
lay aside; to keep; to sore up; as, <xex>to put by</xex> money.</cd> --
<col><b>To put down</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To lay down; to deposit;
to set down.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To lower; to diminish; as, <xex>to
put down</xex> prices</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To deprive of position or
power; to put a stop to; to suppress; to abolish; to confute; as,
<xex>to put down</xex> rebellion or traitors</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mark, how a plain tale shall <qex>put</qex> you
<qex>down</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sugar hath <qex>put down</qex> the use of honey.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To subscribe; as, <xex>to put down</xex> one's
name.</cd> -- <col><b>To put forth</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To thrust
out; to extend, as the hand; to cause to come or push out; as, a tree
<xex>puts forth</xex> leaves.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To make manifest; to
develop; also, to bring into action; to exert; as, <xex>to put
forth</xex> strength</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To propose, as a question, a
riddle, and the like</cd>. <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To publish, as a book</cd>.
-- <col><b>To put forward</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To advance to a
position of prominence or responsibility; to promote.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>To cause to make progress; to aid.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To set, as
the hands of a clock, to a later hour.</cd> -- <col><b>To put
in</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To introduce among others; to insert;
sometimes, to introduce with difficulty; <as>as, <ex>to put in</ex> a
word while others are discoursing</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>To conduct into a harbor, as a ship</cd>.
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>To place in due form before a court;
to place among the records of a court</cd>. <au>Burrill.</au>
<sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>To restore, as a dislocated part, to
its place.</cd> -- <col><b>To put off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To lay
aside; to discard; <as>as, <ex>to put off</ex> a robe; <ex>to put
off</ex> mortality</as>.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>Put off</xex> thy shoes from
off thy feet.<rdquo/ <au>Ex. iii. 5.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To turn
aside; to elude; to disappoint; to frustrate; to baffle.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I hoped for a demonstration, but Themistius hoped <qex>to
put</qex> me <qex>off</qex> with an harangue.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We might <qex>put</qex> him <qex>off</qex> with this answer.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To delay; to defer; to postpone; as, <xex>to put
off</xex> repentance.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To get rid of; to dispose
of; especially, to pass fraudulently; <as>as, <ex>to put off</ex> a
counterfeit note, or an ingenious theory</as><-- = to pass off --></cd>.
<sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To push from land; <as>as, <ex>to put off</ex> a
boat</as></cd>. -- <mcol><col><b>To put on</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>To put upon</b></col></mcol>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To invest one's
self with, as clothes; to assume.</cd> <ldquo/Mercury . . . <xex>put
on</xex> the shape of a man.<rdquo/ <au>L'Estrange.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>To impute (something) to; to charge upon; as, <xex>to put</xex>
blame <xex>on</xex> or <xex>upon</xex> another.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To
advance; to promote</cd>. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/This came
handsomely <xex>to put on</xex> the peace.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au>
<sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To impose; to inflict.</cd> <ldquo/That which thou
<xex>puttest on</xex> me, will I bear.<rdquo/ <au>2 Kings xviii.
14.</au> <sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To apply; <as>as, <ex>to put on</ex> workmen;
<ex>to put on</ex> steam</as>.</cd> <sd>(f)</sd> <cd>To deceive; to
trick.</cd> <ldquo/The stork found he was <xex>put upon</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>L'Estrange.</au> <sd>(g)</sd> <cd>To place upon, as a means or
condition; as, he <xex>put</xex> him <xex>upon</xex> bread and
water.</cd> <ldquo/This caution will <xex>put</xex> them <xex>upon</xex>
considering.<rdquo/ <au>Locke.</au> <sd>(h)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>To
rest upon; to submit to; as, a defendant <xex>puts</xex> himself
<xex>on</xex> or <xex>upon</xex> the country.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> --
<col><b>To put out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To eject; <as>as, <ex>to
put out</ex> and intruder</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To put forth; to
shoot, as a bud, or sprout</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To extinguish; <as>as,
<ex>to put out</ex> a candle, light, or fire</as></cd>. <sd>(d)</sd>
<cd>To place at interest; to loan; <as>as, <ex>to put out</ex>
funds</as></cd>. <sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To provoke, as by insult; to
displease; to vex; as, he was <xex>put out</xex> by my reply</cd>.
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <sd>(f)</sd> <cd>To protrude; to stretch forth;
<as>as, <ex>to put out</ex> the hand</as>.</cd> <sd>(g)</sd> <cd>To
publish; to make public; <as>as, <ex>to put out</ex> a
pamphlet</as></cd>. <sd>(h)</sd> <cd>To confuse; to disconcert; to
interrupt; <as>as, <ex>to put</ex> one <ex>out</ex> in reading or
speaking</as></cd>. <sd>(i)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>To open; <as>as,
<ex>to put out</ex> lights, that is, to open or cut windows</as></cd>.
<au>Burrill.</au> <sd>(j)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>To place out of
joint; to dislocate; as, <xex>to put out</xex> the ankle.</cd>
<sd>(k)</sd> <cd>To cause to cease playing, or to prevent from playing
longer in a certain inning, as in base ball</cd>. <sd>(l)</sd> <cd>to
engage in sexual intercourse; -- used of women; <as>as, she's got a
great bod, but she doesn't <ex>put out</ex></as>.</cd> <mark>[Vulgar
slang]</mark> -- <col><b>To put over</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
place (some one) in authority over; <as>as, <ex>to put</ex> a general
<ex>over</ex> a division of an army</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
refer</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the certain knowledge of that truth<br/
I <qex>put</qex> you <qex>o'er</qex> to heaven and to my mother.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To defer; to postpone; as, the court <xex>put
over</xex> the cause to the next term.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To transfer
(a person or thing) across; <as>as, <ex>to put</ex> one <ex>over</ex>
the river</as></cd>. -- <mcol><col><b>To put the hand to</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>To put the hand unto</b></col></mcol>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To take hold of, as of an instrument of labor; as, <xex>to put the
hand to</xex> the plow; hence, to engage in (any task or affair);
<as>as, <ex>to put</ex> one's <ex>hand to</ex> the work</as>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To take or seize, as in theft.</cd> <ldquo/He hath not
<xex>put his hand unto</xex> his neighbor's goods.<rdquo/ <au>Ex. xxii.
11.</au> -- <col><b>To put through</b></col>, <cd>to cause to go through
all conditions or stages of a progress; hence, to push to completion; to
accomplish; <as>as, he <ex>put through</ex> a measure of legislation; he
<ex>put through</ex> a railroad enterprise</as>.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>To put to</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
add; to unite; <as>as, <ex>to put</ex> one sum <ex>to</ex>
another</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To refer to; to expose; <as>as,
<ex>to put</ex> the safety of the state <ex>to</ex> hazard</as></cd>.
<ldquo/That dares not <xex>put</xex> it <xex>to</xex> the touch.<rdquo/
<au>Montrose.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To attach (something) to; to harness
beasts to.</cd> <au>Dickens.</au> -- <col><b>To put to a
stand</b></col>, <cd>to stop; to arrest by obstacles or
difficulties.</cd> -- <col><b>To put to bed</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To undress and place in bed, as a child.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
deliver in, or to make ready for, childbirth</cd>. -- <col><b>To put to
death</b></col>, <cd>to kill.</cd> -- <col><b>To put together</b></col>,
<cd>to attach; to aggregate; to unite in one.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To
put this and that</b></col> (<xex>or</xex> <col><b>two and
two</b></col>) <col><b>together</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to draw an
inference; to form a correct conclusion.</cd> -- <col><b>To put to
it</b></col>, <cd>to distress; to press hard; to perplex; to give
difficulty to.</cd> <ldquo/O gentle lady, do not <xex>put</xex> me
<xex>to 't</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To put to
rights</b></col>, <cd>to arrange in proper order; to settle or compose
rightly.</cd> -- <col><b>To put to the sword</b></col>, <cd>to kill with
the sword; to slay.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To put to trial</b></col>,
<xex>or</xex> <col><b>on trial</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to bring to a test;
to try.</cd> -- <col><b>To put trust in</b></col>, <cd>to confide in; to
repose confidence in.</cd> -- <col><b>To put up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To pass unavenged; to overlook; not to punish or resent; to put up
with; <as>as, <ex>to put up</ex> indignities</as>.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Such national injuries are not <xex>to be put
up</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To send forth or
upward; as, <xex>to put up</xex> goods for sale.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd>
<cd>To start from a cover, as game</cd>. <ldquo/She has been frightened;
she has been <xex>put up</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>C. Kingsley.</au>
<sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To hoard.</cd> <ldquo/Himself never <xex>put up</xex>
any of the rent.<rdquo/ <au>Spelman.</au> <sd>(f)</sd> <cd>To lay side
or preserve; to pack away; to store; to pickle; <as>as, to <ex>put
up</ex> pork, beef, or fish</as>.</cd> <sd>(g)</sd> <cd>To place out of
sight, or away; to put in its proper place; <as>as, <ex>put up</ex> that
letter</as>.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(h)</sd> <cd>To incite; to
instigate; -- followed by <xex>to</xex>; <as>as, he <ex>put</ex> the lad
<ex>up</ex> to mischief</as>.</cd> <sd>(i)</sd> <cd>To raise; to erect;
to build; as, <xex>to put up</xex> a tent, or a house</cd>. <sd>(j)</sd>
<cd>To lodge; to entertain; <as>as, <ex>to put up</ex>
travelers</as></cd>. -- <col><b>To put up a job</b></col>, <cd>to
arrange a plot.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To place; set; lay; cause; produce; propose;
state.</syn> <usage> -- <er>Put</er>, <er>Lay</er>, <er>Place</er>,
<er>Set</er>. These words agree in the idea of fixing the position of
some object, and are often used interchangeably. <xex>To put</xex> is
the least definite, denoting merely to move to a place. <xex>To
place</xex> has more particular reference to the precise location, as to
put with care in a certain or proper place. <xex>To set</xex> or <xex>to
lay</xex> may be used when there is special reference to the position of
the object.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw> <pr>(put; <it>often</it> p<ucr/t <it>in def.</it> 3)</pr>,
<pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go or move; <as>as, when the air
first <ex>puts</ex> up</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To steer; to direct one's course; to go.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His fury thus appeased, he <qex>puts</qex> to land.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To play a card or a hand in the game called
<xex>put</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To put about</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to change
direction; to tack.</cd> -- <col><b>To put back</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to turn back; to return.</cd> <ldquo/The French
. . . had <xex>put back</xex> to Toulon.<rdquo/ <au>Southey.</au> --
<col><b>To put forth</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To shoot, bud, or
germinate</cd>. <ldquo/Take earth from under walls where nettles
<xex>put forth</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To leave a
port or haven, as a ship.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To put
in</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to enter a harbor; to sail into
port.</cd> -- <col><b>To put in for</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To make
a request or claim; as, <xex>to put in for</xex> a share of
profits</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To go into covert; -- said of a bird
escaping from a hawk</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To offer one's self; to
stand as a candidate for.</cd> <au>Locke.</au> -- <col><b>To put
off</b></col>, <cd>to go away; to depart; esp., to leave land, as a
ship; to move from the shore.</cd> -- <col><b>To put on</b></col>,
<cd>to hasten motion; to drive vehemently.</cd> -- <col><b>To put
over</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to sail over or across.</cd> --
<col><b>To put to sea</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to set sail; to
begin a voyage; to advance into the ocean.</cd> -- <col><b>To put
up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To take lodgings; to lodge</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To offer one's self as a candidate</cd>.
<au>L'Estrange.</au><-- put up or shut up --> -- <col><b>To put up
to</b></col>, <cd>to advance to.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/With
this he <xex>put up to</xex> my lord.<rdquo/ <au>Swift.</au> --
<col><b>To put up with</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To overlook, or
suffer without recompense, punishment, or resentment; <as>as, <ex>to put
up with</ex> an injury or affront</as></cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To take
without opposition or expressed dissatisfaction; to endure; <as>as,
<ex>to put up with</ex> bad fare</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
putting; an action; a movement; a thrust; a push; <as>as, the
<ex>put</ex> of a ball</as>.</def> <ldquo/A forced
<xex>put</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A certain game at cards.</def>
<rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>A privilege which one party
buys of another to <ldquo/put<rdquo/ (deliver) to him a certain amount
of stock, grain, etc., at a certain price and date.</def>
<mark>[Brokers' Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>put</qex> and a call may be combined in one instrument, the
holder of which may either buy or sell as he chooses at the fixed
price.</q> <rj><qau>Johnson's Cyc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put</ent><br/
<hw>Put</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>pute</ets>.]</ety> <def>A prostitute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putage</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>putage</ets>.]</ety> <def>Prostitution or fornication on the part
of a woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putamen</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu*ta"men</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The shell of a nut; the stone of a drupe fruit.
See <er>Endocarp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putanism</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tan*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>putanisme</ets>, fr. <ets>putain</ets> harlot.]</ety> <def>Habitual
lewdness or prostitution of a woman; harlotry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putative</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ta*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>putativus</ets>, fr. <ets>putare</ets>, <ets>putatum</ets>, to
reckon, suppose, adjust, prune, cleanse. See <er>Pure</er>, and cf.
<er>Amputate</er>, <er>Compute</er>, <er>Dispute</er>,
<er>Impute</er>.]</ety> <def>Commonly thought or deemed; supposed;
reputed; <as>as, the <ex>putative</ex> father of a child</as>.</def>
<ldquo/His other <xex>putative</xex> (I dare not say feigned)
friends.<rdquo/ <rj><au>E. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus things indifferent, being esteemed useful or pious, became
customary, and then came for reverence into a <qex>putative</qex> and
usurped authority.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putchuck</ent><br/
<hw>Put*chuck"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Pachak</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puteal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pu"te*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>puteus</ets> well.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An inclosure
surrounding a well to prevent persons from falling into it; a well
curb.</def> <rj><au>Weale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puteli</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Put"e*li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Patela</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putery</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"ter*y</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>puterie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Putage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putid</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>putidus</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>putide</ets>. Cf. <er>Putrid</er>.]</ety> <def>Rotten;
fetid; stinking; base; worthless. <xex>Jer</xex>.
<xex>Taylor</xex>.</def> <ldquo/Thy <xex>putid</xex> muse.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putidness</ent><br/
<ent>Putidity</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pu*tid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pu"tid*ness</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
putrid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putlog</ent><br/
<hw>Put"log`</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>One of the short pieces of timber on which the planks forming the
floor of a scaffold are laid, -- one end resting on the ledger of the
scaffold, and the other in a hole left in the wall temporarily for the
purpose.</def> <rj><au>Oxf. Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put-off</ent><br/
<hw>Put"-off`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shift for
evasion or delay; an evasion; an excuse.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putour</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Put</er> a
prostitute.]</ety> <def>A keeper of a brothel; a procurer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>put paid to</ent><br/
<hw>put paid to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
put an end to.</def> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To prove false; to
discredit; <as>as, Galileo's observations <ex>put paid to</ex> the
notion that the Earth was the center of the universe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To defeat; to cause the defeat of.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Argentine's infamous Hand of God goals when he punched the
ball into the net, and a spectacular solo effort <qex>put paid to</qex>
England in the last eight . . .</q> <rj><qau>Sunday Times, 17 May
1998</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putredinous</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tred"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>putredo</ets> rottenness, fr. <ets>putrere</ets> to be rotten. See
<er>Putrid</er>.]</ety> <def>Proceeding from putrefaction, or partaking
of the putrefactive process; having an offensive smell; stinking;
rotten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrefaction</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`tre*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>putrefactio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>putr<eacute/faction</ets>. See
<er>Putrefy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or the process of
putrefying; the offensive decay of albuminous or other matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Putrefaction is a complex phenomenon involving a
multiplicity of chemical reactions, always accompanied by, and without
doubt caused by, bacteria and vibriones; hence, putrefaction is a form
of fermentation, and is sometimes called <xex>putrefaction
fermentative</xex>. Putrefaction is not possible under conditions that
preclude the development of living organisms. Many of the products of
putrefaction are powerful poisons, and are called <xex>cadaveric
poisons</xex>, or <xex>ptoma<ium/nes</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The condition of being putrefied; also, that which
putrefied.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Putrefaction's</xex> breath.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shelley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrefactive</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`tre*fac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<ets>putr<eacute/factif</ets>. See <er>Putrefy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to putrefaction; <as>as, the <ex>putrefactive</ex>
smell or process</as>.</def> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Causing, or tending to promote,
putrefaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Pu``tre*fac"tive*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrefy</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tre*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<ets>putrify</ets>.]</altsp> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Putrefied</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Putrefying</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>putr<eacute/fier</ets>; L. <ets>putrere</ets> to be rotten +
<ets>-ficare</ets> (in. comp.) to make; cf. L. <ets>putrefacere</ets>.
See <er>Putrid</er>, and <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To render
putrid; to cause to decay offensively; to cause to be decomposed; to
cause to rot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To corrupt; to make foul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Private suits do <qex>putrefy</qex> the public good.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They would but stink, and <qex>putrefy</qex> the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make morbid, carious, or gangrenous; <as>as, to
<ex>putrefy</ex> an ulcer or wound</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrefy</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tre*fy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become putrid; to decay
offensively; to rot.</def> <rj><au>Isa. 1. 6.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putresce</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tresce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Putrescent</er>.]</ety> <def>To become putrescent or putrid; to
putrefy.</def></p>

<p><q>Ordinarily sewage does not <qex>putresce</qex> until from
twenty-four to sixty hours after its discharge.</q>
<rj><qau>Nature.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrescence</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tres"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being putrescent; putrescent matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1168 --></p>

<p><ent>Putrescent</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tres"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>putrescens</ets>, p. pr.of <ets>putrescere</ets> to grow rotten, v.
incho. fr. <ets>putrere</ets> to be rotten. See <er>Putrid</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Becoming putrid or rotten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Externally powerful, although <qex>putrescent</qex> at the
core.</q> <rj><qau>Motley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the process of putrefaction;
<as>as, a <ex>putrescent</ex> smell</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrescible</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tres"ci*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
putrefaction; liable to become putrid; <as>as, <ex>putrescible</ex>
substances</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrescible</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tres"ci*ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A substance, usually
nitrogenous, which is liable to undergo decomposition when in contact
with air and moisture at ordinary temperatures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrescin</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*tres"cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A nontoxic diamine, <chform>C4H12N2</chform>, formed
in the putrefaction of the flesh of mammals and some other
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrid</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"trid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>putridus</ets>, <ets>fr</ets>. <ets>putrere</ets> to be rotten, fr.
<ets>puter</ets>, or <ets>putris</ets>, rotten, <ets>fr</ets>.
<ets>putere</ets> to stink, to be rotten: cf. F. <ets>putride</ets>. See
<er>Pus</er>, <er>Foul</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Tending to decomposition or decay; decomposed; rotten; -- said of
animal or vegetable matter; <as>as, <ex>putrid</ex> flesh</as>. See
<er>Putrefaction</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Indicating or proceeding from a decayed state of
animal or vegetable matter; <as>as, a <ex>putrid</ex>
smell</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Putrid fever</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>typhus
fever; -- so called from the decomposing and offensive state of the
discharges and diseased textures of the body.</cd> -- <col><b>Putrid
sore throat</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a gangrenous inflammation
of the fauces and pharynx.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putridity</ent><br/
<hw>Pu*trid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>putridit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality of being putrid;
putrefaction; rottenness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putridness</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"trid*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Putridity.</def>
<rj><au>Floyer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrifacted</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tri*fac`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Putrefy</er>.]</ety> <def>Putrefied.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What vermin bred of <qex>putrifacted</qex> slime.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrification</ent><br/
<hw>Pu`tri*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Putrefaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrify</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tri*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
putrefy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putrilage</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"tri*lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>putrilage</ets>, L. <ets>putrilago</ets> putrefaction.]</ety>
<def>That which is undergoing putrefaction; the products of
putrefaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putry</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Putrid.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putry</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"try</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Putage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putt</ent><br/
<hw>Putt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Put</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>A stroke made on the
putting green to play the ball into a hole.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putt</ent><br/
<hw>Putt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>To make a
putt.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puttee</ent><br/
<hw>Put"tee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <ety>[Hind. <ets>pa<tsdot/<tsdot/i</ets>
ribbon, brace, tie.]</ety> <def>A kind of gaiter of waterproof cloth
wrapped around the leg, used by soldiers, etc.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>putty</asp>, <asp>puttie</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putter</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
puts or plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, one who pushes the small wagons in a
coal mine, and the like.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putter</ent><br/
<hw>Putt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Golf)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A club with a short shaft and either a wooden or a
metal head, used in putting.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who
putts.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putter</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Puttered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Puttering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Potter</er>.]</ety> <def>To act inefficiently or idly; to occupy
oneself in a leisurely manner; to trifle; to potter; <as>as, to
<ex>putter</ex> around in the garden</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putter-on</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ter-on`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
instigator.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puttier</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ti*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who putties; a
glazier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putting</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The throwing of a
heavy stone, shot, etc., with the hand raised or extended from the
shoulder; -- originally, a Scottish game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Putting stone</b></col>, <cd>a heavy stone used in the
game of putting.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putting green</ent><br/
<hw>Putt"ing green</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>The green,
or plot of smooth turf, surrounding a hole.</def> <ldquo/The term
<xex>putting green</xex> shall mean the ground within twenty yards of
the hole, excepting hazards.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Golf Rules.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puttock</ent><br/
<hw>Put"tock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pout</er> a
young bird, <er>Poult</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The European kite.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The buzzard.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The marsh harrier.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puttock</ent><br/
<hw>Put"tock</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Futtock</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putty</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pot<eacute/e</ets>, fr. <ets>pot</ets> pot; what was formerly
called <ets>putty</ets> being a substance resembling what is now called
<ets>putty powder</ets>, and in part made of the metal of old pots. See
<er>Pot</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of thick paste or cement
compounded of whiting, or soft carbonate of lime, and linseed oil, when
applied beaten or kneaded to the consistence of dough, -- used in
fastening glass in sashes, stopping crevices, and for similar
purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>A ball made of composition and not
gutta percha.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Putty powder</b></col>, <cd>an oxide of tin, or of tin
and lead in various proportions, much used in polishing glass, metal,
precious stones, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putty</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Putties</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <altsp>[Written also <asp>puttee</asp>,
<asp>puttie</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Hind. <ets>pa<tsdot/<tsdot/i</ets>
ribbon, brace, tie.]</ety> <def>A kind of gaiter of waterproof cloth
wrapped around the leg, used by soldiers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putty</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ty</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Puttied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Puttying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cement, or stop, with
putty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Putty-faced</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ty-faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>White-faced; --
used contemptuously.</def> <rj><au>Clarke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puttyroot</ent><br/
<hw>Put"ty*root`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An American orchidaceous plant (<spn>Aplectrum hyemale</spn>) which
flowers in early summer. Its slender naked rootstock produces each year
a solid corm, filled with exceedingly glutinous matter, which sends up
later a single large oval evergreen plaited leaf. Called also
<altname>Adam-and-Eve</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>put up</ent><br/
<hw>put up</hw> <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to connect a
device to a telephone line surreptitiously, so as to listen to or record
the conversations of persons on the telephone without their knowledge;
-- of telephones, persons, or locations. Used as police jargon.</def>
<mark>[jargon]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> wiretap, tap, intercept, bug.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>] [<source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Put-up</ent><br/
<hw>Put"-up</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Arranged; plotted; --
in a bad sense; <as>as, a <ex>put-up</ex> job</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>put up or shut up</ent><br/
<hw>put up or shut up</hw> <pos>phrase</pos> <def>Do what you claim you
can do; -- a challenge to someone who has claimed some ability.</def>
<mark>[colloq.]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puy</ent><br/
<hw>Pu"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Poy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>putz</ent><br/
<hw>putz</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/ts)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Yiddish.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A fool; a jerk.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The penis.</def> <mark>[Vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzel</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zel</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/z"z<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pucelle</ets> a virgin.]</ety> <def>A harlot; a drab; a
hussy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>puzzle</ent><br/
<hw>puz"zle</hw> <pr>(p<ucr/z"z'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>opposal</ets>, in the sense of problem. See <er>Oppose</er>,
<er>Pose</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something which
perplexes or embarrasses; especially, a toy or a problem contrived for
testing ingenuity; also, something exhibiting marvelous skill in
making.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being puzzled; perplexity; <as>as, to
be in a <ex>puzzle</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>puzzle</ent><br/
<hw>puz"zle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>puzzled</conjf> <pr>(p<ucr/z"z'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>puzzling</conjf> <pr>(p<ucr/z"zl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To perplex; to confuse; to embarrass; to put to a
stand; to nonplus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A very shrewd disputant in those points is dexterous in
<qex>puzzling</qex> others.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is perpetually <qex>puzzled</qex> and perplexed amidst his own
blunders.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make intricate; to entangle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They disentangle from the <qex>puzzled</qex> skein.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ways of Heaven are dark and intricate,<br/
<qex>Puzzled</qex> in mazes, and perplexed with error.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To solve by ingenuity, as a puzzle; -- followed by
<xex>out</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>puzzle</ex> out a mystery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To embarrass; perplex; confuse; bewilder;
confound. See <er>Embarrass</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzle</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be bewildered, or
perplexed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>puzzling</qex> fool, that heeds nothing.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To work, as at a puzzle; <as>as, to <ex>puzzle</ex>
over a problem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzledom</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zle*dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The domain of
puzzles; puzzles, collectively.</def> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzle-headed</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zle-head`ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the head full of
confused notions; given to getting perplexed over simple matters; also,
characteristic of persons that are so.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzlement</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zle*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being puzzled; perplexity.</def> <rj><au>Miss Mitford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzler</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, puzzles or perplexes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hebrew, the general <qex>puzzler</qex> of old heads.</q>
<rj><qau>Brome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzlingly</ent><br/
<hw>Puz"zling*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a puzzling
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Puzzolana</ent><br/
<ent>Puzzolan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Puz"zo*lan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Puz`zo*la"na</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pozzuolana</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyemia</ent><br/
<ent>Pyaemia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Py*<ae/"mi*a</hw>, \'d8<hw>Py*e"mi*a</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ pus + <?/
blood.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A form of blood poisoning produced
by the absorption of pyogenic microorganisms into the blood, usually
from a wound or local inflammation. It is characterized by multiple
abscesses throughout the body, and is attended with irregularly
recurring chills, fever, profuse sweating, and exhaustion.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Py*<ae/"mic</wf>, <wf>Py*e"mic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyaemic</ent><br/
<ent>Pyemic</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Py*e"mic</hw>, <hw>Py*<ae/"mic</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to py<ae/mia; of the nature of
py<ae/mia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnaspidean</ent><br/
<hw>Pyc`nas*pid"e*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
thick, crowded + <?/, <?/, a shield.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the posterior side of the tarsus covered with small
irregular scales; -- said of certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyc*nid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pycnidia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/
crowded.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>In certain fungi, a flask-shaped
cavity from the surface of the inner walls of which spores are
produced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnite</ent><br/
<hw>Pyc"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
thick.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A massive subcolumnar variety of
topaz.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnodont</ent><br/
<hw>Pyc"no*dont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ thick,
crowded + <?/, <?/, a tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Any fossil
fish belonging to the Pycnodontini. They have numerous round, flat
teeth, adapted for crushing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnodontini</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyc`no*don"ti*ni</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extinct order of ganoid
fishes. They had a compressed body, covered with dermal ribs
(<xex>pleurolepida</xex>) and with enameled rhomboidal scales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnogonid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyc*nog"o*nid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Pycnogonida.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnogonida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyc`no*gon"i*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ thick crowded + <?/ knee.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
class of marine arthropods in which the body is small and thin, and the
eight legs usually very long; -- called also
<altname>Pantopoda</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The abdomen is rudimentary, and the triangular mouth is
at the end of a tubular proboscis. Many of them live at great depths in
the sea, and the largest of them measure two feet across the extended
legs.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pyc*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ dense,
compact + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A specific
gravity bottle; a standard flask for measuring and comparing the
densities of liquids.</def> <altsp>[Also written
<asp>pyknometer</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pycnostyle</ent><br/
<hw>Pyc"no*style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ with the
pillars close together; <?/ close + <?/ a column, pillar: cf. F.
<ets>pycnostyle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Arch.)</fld> <def>See under
<er>Intercolumniation</er>.</def> -<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A pycnostyle
colonnade.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pye</ent><br/
<hw>Pye</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 2d <er>Pie</er>
<sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyebald</ent><br/
<hw>Pye"bald`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Piebald</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyelitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py`e*li"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. basin +
<ets>-itis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammation of the
pelvis of the kidney.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyemia</ent><br/
<hw>Py*e"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Pyaemia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyet</ent><br/
<hw>Py"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A magpie; a piet.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here cometh the worthy prelate as pert as a <qex>pyet</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygal</ent><br/
<hw>Py"gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ the
rump.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated in the region of the rump,
or posterior end of the backbone; -- applied especially to the posterior
median plates in the carapace of chelonians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygargus</ent><br/
<ent>Pygarg</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Py"garg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Py*gar"gus</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <ety>[L. <ets>pygargus</ets>, Gr. <?/, literally,
white rump; <?/ the rump + white: cf. F. <ets>pygargue</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A quadruped, probably the addax, an
antelope having a white rump.</def> <rj><au>Deut. xiv. 5.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The female of the
hen harrier.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sea eagle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*gid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pygidia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, dim. of
<?/ the rump.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The caudal plate of
trilobites, crustacean, and certain insects. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Limulus</er> and <er>Trilobite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygmean</ent><br/
<ent>Pygmy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyg"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyg*me"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pygmaeus</ets>. See
<er>Pygmy</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a pygmy; resembling a
pygmy or dwarf; dwarfish; very small.</def> <ldquo/ Like that
<xex>Pygmean</xex> race.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pygmy antelope</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
kleeneboc.</cd> -- <col><b>Pygmy goose</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any species of very small geese of the genus <gen>Nettapus</gen>,
native of Africa, India, and Australia.</cd> -- <col><b>Pygmy
owl</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the gnome.</cd> -- <col><b>Pygmy
parrot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of
very small green parrots (<fam>Nasitern<ae/</fam>), native of New Guinea
and adjacent islands. They are not larger than sparrows.</cd></cs> <--
<col><b>Pygmy chimpanzee</b></col>, a species of anthropoid ape
(<spn>Pan paniscus</spn>) resembling the chimpanzee, but somewhat
smaller; also called <altname>bonobo</altname>. It is considered (1996)
as having the closest genetic relationship to humans of any other
animal. It is found in forests in Zaire, and is an endangered species.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygmy</ent><br/
<hw>Pyg"my</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pygmies</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>pygmaeus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ the
fist, a measure of length, the distance from the elbow to the knuckles,
about 131 inches. Cf. <er>Pugnacious</er>, <er>Fist</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>pigmy</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class.
Myth.)</fld> <def>One of a fabulous race of dwarfs who waged war with
the cranes, and were destroyed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a short, insignificant person; a
dwarf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of a race of Central African Negritos found
chiefly in the great forests of the equatorial belt. They are the
shortest of known races, the adults ranging from less than four to about
five feet in stature. They are timid and shy, dwelling in the recesses
of the forests, though often on good terms with neighboring
Negroes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Pygmies</qex> are <qex>pygmies</qex> still, though perched on
Alps.<br/
And pyramids are pyramids in vales.</q> <rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygobranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py`go*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>pugh`</grk> the rump + <?/ a gill.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of opisthobranchiate mollusks having
the branchi<ae/ in a wreath or group around the anal opening, as in the
genus Doris.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygopod</ent><br/
<hw>Py"go*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pygh`</grk> rump + <ets>-pod</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Pygopodes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of serpentiform
lizards of the family <fam>Pygopodid<ae/</fam>, which have rudimentary
hind legs near the anal cleft, but lack fore legs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygopodes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*gop"o*des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of swimming birds
which includes the grebes, divers, auks, etc., in which the legs are
placed far back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygopodous</ent><br/
<hw>Py*gop"o*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Pygopodes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pygostyle</ent><br/
<hw>Py"go*style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>pygh`</grk> the rump + <?/ a pillar.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The plate of bone which forms the posterior end of the vertebral
column in most birds; the plowshare bone; the vomer. It is formed by the
union of a number of the last caudal vertebr<ae/, and supports the
uropigium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyin</ent><br/
<hw>Py"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ pus.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>An albuminoid constituent of pus,
related to mucin, possibly a mixture of substances rather than a single
body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyjama</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*ja"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>p<amac/e-j<amac/ma</ets>, literally, leg clothing.]</ety> <def>In
India and Persia, thin loose trousers or drawers; in Europe and America,
drawers worn at night, or a kind of nightdress with legs. Usually used
in the plural. See <er>pajamas</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>paijama</asp> <asp>pajama</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Chiefly British
variant of <er>pajama</er>.]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pajamas</ent><br/
<ent>Pyjamas</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Py*ja"mas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, or, <mark>chiefly U. S.</mark>,
<hw>Pa*ja"mas</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></mhw>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A
garment, similar to the Oriental <er>pyjama</er> (which see), adopted
among Europeans, Americans, and other Occidentals, for wear in the
dressing room and during sleep; also, a suit of drawers and blouse for
such wear.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pykar</ent><br/
<hw>Pyk"ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An ancient English
fishing boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py"la</hw> <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Pyl<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Pylas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ an entrance.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
passage between the iter and optoc<oe/le in the brain.</def> <rj><au>B.
G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pylagore</ent><br/
<hw>Pyl"a*gore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/
Pyl<ae/, or Thermopyl<ae/, where the Amphictyonic council met + <?/ to
assemble: cf. F. <ets>pylagore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld>
<def>a deputy of a State at the Amphictyonic council.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pylangium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*lan"gi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pylangia</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/ an
entrance + <?/ a vessel.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The first and
undivided part of the aortic trunk in the amphibian heart.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Py*lan"gi*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pylon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
gateway.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A low tower, having a truncated
pyramidal form, and flanking an ancient Egyptian gateway.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Massive <qex>pylons</qex> adorned with obelisks in front.</q>
<rj><qau>J. W. Draper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>An Egyptian gateway to a large building (with or
without flanking towers).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tower, commonly of steelwork, for supporting
either end of a wire, as for a telegraph line, over a long
span.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Formerly, a
starting derrick (the use of which is now abandoned) for an
aeroplane.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A post, tower, or the like, as on an
aerodrome, or flying ground, serving to bound or mark a prescribed
course of flight.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyloric</ent><br/
<hw>Py*lor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pylorique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to,
or in the region of, the pylorus; <as>as, the <ex>pyloric</ex> end of
the stomach</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pylorus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*lo"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pylori</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/ pylorus,
gate keeper; <?/ a gate + <?/ watcher, guardian.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The opening from the stomach into
the intestine.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A posterior division of the
stomach in some invertebrates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyne</ent><br/
<hw>Pyne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pine</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pynoun</ent><br/
<hw>Py*noun"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pennant.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyocyanin</ent><br/
<hw>Py`o*cy"a*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ pus +
<?/ dark blue.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A blue coloring
matter found in the pus from old sores, supposed to be formed through
the agency of a species of bacterium (<spn>Bacillus
pyocyaneus</spn>).</def><-- now, Pseudomonas aeruginosa
<chform>C13H10N2O</chform>--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyogenic</ent><br/
<hw>Py`o*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ pus +
root of <grk>gi`gnesqai</grk> to be born.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Producing or generating pus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyoid</ent><br/
<hw>Py"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ pus +
-<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to pus;
of the nature of, or like, pus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pyoid corpuscles</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>cells
of a size larger than pus corpuscles, containing two or more of the
latter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyopneumothorax</ent><br/
<hw>Py`op*neu`mo*tho"rax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
pus + E. <ets>pneumothorax</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Accumulation of air, or other gas, and of pus, in the pleural
cavity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyot</ent><br/
<hw>Py"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
magpie. See <er>Piet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyoxanthose</ent><br/
<hw>Py`o*xan"those</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ pus +
<?/ yellow.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A greenish yellow
crystalline coloring matter found with pyocyanin in pus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyracanth</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"a*canth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fire +
<?/ a thorn, prickly plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The evergreen
thorn (<spn>Crat<ae/gus Pyracantha</spn>), a shrub native of
Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyral</ent><br/
<hw>Py"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
pyre.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyralid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"a*lid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pyralis</ets>, <ets>-idis</ets>, a kind of winged insect.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any moth of the family <fam>Pyralid<ae/</fam>.
The species are numerous and mostly small, but some of them are very
injurious, as the bee moth, meal moth, hop moth, and clover
moth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"a*mid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pyramis</ets>, <ets>-idis</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, of Egyptian
origin: cf. F. <ets>pyramide</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>A solid body standing on a triangular, square, or
polygonal base, and terminating in a point at the top; especially, a
structure or edifice of this shape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A solid figure contained by a
plane rectilineal figure as base and several triangles which have a
common vertex and whose bases are sides of the base.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Billiards)</fld> <def>The game of
pool in which the balls are placed in the form of a triangle at
spot.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>a fraudulent investment scheme
in which the manager promises high profits, but instead of investing the
money in a genuine profit-making activity, uses the money from later
investors to pay the profits to earlier investors; -- also called
<altname>pyramid scheme</altname> or <altname>pyramid
operation</altname>. This process inevitably collapses when insufficient
new investors are available, leaving the later investors with total or
near-total losses of their investments. The managers usually blame
government regulations or interference for the collapse of the scheme,
rather than admit fraud.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Altitude of a pyramid</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>,
<cd>the perpendicular distance from the vertex to the plane of the
base.</cd> -- <col><b>Axis of a pyramid</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>,
<cd>a straight line drawn from the vertex to the center of the
base.</cd> -- <col><b>Earth pyramid</b></col>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<cd>See <cref>Earth pillars</cref>, under <er>Earth</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Right pyramid</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <cd>a pyramid whose
axis is perpendicular to the base.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"a*mid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<fld>(Speculation)</fld> <def>To enlarge one's holding or interest in a
series of operations on a continued rise or decline by using the profits
to buy or sell additional amounts on a margin, as where one buys on a
10% margin 100 shares of stock quoted at 100, holds it till it rises to
105, and then uses the paper profit to buy 50 shares more, etc. The
series of operations constitutes a <xex>pyramid</xex>. A similar process
of reinvesting gains or winnings (as of a gamble), but not involving
operation on margin, is called a <contr>parlay</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"a*mid</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Speculation)</fld> <def>To
use, or to deal in, in a pyramiding transaction. See <er>Pyramid</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramidal</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ram`i*dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pyramidal</ets>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a pyramid; in the form of a a
pyramid; pyramidical; <as>as, <ex>pyramidal</ex>
cleavage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mystic obelisks stand up<br/
Triangular, <qex>pyramidal</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1169 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Tetragonal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pyramidal numbers</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>,
<cd>certain series of figurate numbers expressing the number of balls or
points that may be arranged in the form of pyramids. Thus 1, 4, 10, 20,
35, etc., are <xex>triangular pyramidal numbers</xex>; and 1, 5, 14, 30,
55, etc., are <xex>square pyramidal numbers</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramidal</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ram"i*dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>One of the carpal bones. See <er>Cuneiform</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2
<sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramidally</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ram"i*dal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Like a pyramid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramidical</ent><br/
<ent>Pyramidic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr`a*mid"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyr`a*mid"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a pyramid;
having the form of a pyramid; pyramidal.</def> <ldquo/ A
<xex>pyramidical</xex> rock.<rdquo/ <au>Goldsmith.</au> <ldquo/Gold in
<xex>pyramidic</xex> plenty piled.<rdquo/ <au>Shenstone.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Pyr`a*mid"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<wf>Pyr`a*mild"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramidion</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyr`a*mid"i*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Pyramidia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>pyramis</ets>. See <er>Pyramid</er>.]</ety> <def>The small
pyramid which crowns or completes an obelisk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramidoid</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ram"i*doid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
pyramid + <ets>-id</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pyramido<ium/de</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A solid resembling a pyramid; -- called also
<altname>pyramoid</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Barlow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyr"a*mis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pyramides</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A
pyramid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyramoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"a*moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Pyramidoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrargyrite</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rar"gy*rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fire +
<?/ silver.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Ruby silver; dark red silver
ore. It is a sulphide of antimony and silver, occurring in rhombohedral
crystals or massive, and is of a dark red or black color with a metallic
adamantine luster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-zin</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrazine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr"a*zine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> Also
<hw>-zin</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[<ets>Pyr</ets>idine + Gr. <?/ not + <?/
life.]</ety> <fld>(Org. Chem.)</fld> <def>A feebly basic solid,
<chform>C4H4N2</chform>, obtained by distilling piperazine with zinc
dust, and in other ways. Also, by extension, any of various derivatives
of the same.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyre</ent><br/
<hw>Pyre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pure</ets>, Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ fire. See <er>Fire</er>.]</ety> <def>A funeral pile; a
combustible heap on which the dead are burned; hence, any pile to be
burnt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For nine long nights, through all the dusky air,<br/
The <qex>pyres</qex> thick flaming shot a dismal glare.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrena</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*re"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pyren<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, the
stone of fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A nutlet resembling a
seed, or the kernel of a drupe.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrene</ent><br/
<hw>Py"rene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fire.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>One of the less volatile hydrocarbons of coal
tar, obtained as a white crystalline substance,
<chform>C16H10</chform>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrene</ent><br/
<hw>Py"rene</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Pyrena</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrenean</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`e*ne"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pyrenaei</ets> (sc. <ets>monte</ets>s) the Pyrenees, fr.
<ets>Pyrene</ets>, Gr.<?/ a daughter of Bebryx, beloved by Hercules, and
buried upon these mountains.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Pyrenees, a range of mountains separating France and Spain.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The Pyrenees.</def></def2>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Py*re"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ like a
kernel. See <er>Pyrena</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A transparent body found in the chromatophores
of certain Infusoria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrethrin</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"eth*rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Pyrethrum</ets>, generic name of feverfew, Gr. <?/ feverfew.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance resembling, and isomeric with,
ordinary camphor, and extracted from the essential oil of feverfew; --
called also <altname>Pyrethrum camphor</altname>.</def><-- a complex of
several substances. Used as insecticides. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrethrine</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"eth*rine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An alkaloid extracted from the root of the pellitory of Spain
(<spn>Anacyclus pyrethrum</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyretic</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ret"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ burning
heat, fever, from <?/ fire: cf. F. <ets>pyr<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to fever; febrile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyretology</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`e*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fever
+ <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pyr<eacute/tologie</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A discourse or treatise on fevers; the doctrine
of fevers.</def> <rj><au>Hooper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrexia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*rex"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pyrexi<ae/</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ to be
feverish, akin to <?/ fever.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The febrile
condition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrexical</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrexial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Py*rex"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Py*rex"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to fever; feverish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrgom</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"gom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a place
furnished with towers, fr. <?/ a tower.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
variety of pyroxene; -- called also
<altname>fassaite</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrheliometer</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr*he`li*om"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
fire + <?/ sun + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An
instrument for measuring the direct heating effect of the sun's
rays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyridic</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rid"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Related to, or formed from, pyridin or its homologues;
<as>as, the <ex>pyridic</ex> bases</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyridine</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"i*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/
fire.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A nitrogenous base,
<chform>C5H5N</chform>, obtained from the distillation of bone oil or
coal tar, and by the decomposition of certain alkaloids, as a colorless
liquid with a peculiar pungent odor. It is the nucleus of a large number
of organic substances, among which several vegetable alkaloids, as
nicotine and certain of the ptoma<ium/nes, may be mentioned. See
<er>Lutidine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyridyl</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"i*dyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyrid</ets>ine
+ <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical radical,
<chform>C5H4N</chform>, regarded as the essential residue of pyridine,
and analogous to phenyl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyriform</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pyrum</ets>, <ets>pirum</ets>, a pear + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pyriforme</ets>, <ets>piriforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form
of a pear; pear-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`i*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to pyrites. See <er>Pyritic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrite</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pyrites</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>pyrite</ets>.
See <er>Pyrites</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A common mineral of
a pale brass-yellow color and brilliant metallic luster, crystallizing
in the isometric system; iron pyrites; iron disulphide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hence sable coal his massy couch extends,<br/
And stars of gold the sparkling <qex>pyrite</qex> blends.</q>
<rj><qau>E. Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrites</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ri"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ fire. See <er>Pyre</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A name
given to a number of metallic minerals, sulphides of iron, copper,
cobalt, nickel, and tin, of a white or yellowish color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term was originally applied to the mineral pyrite,
or iron pyrites, in allusion to its giving sparks when struck with
steel.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Arsenical pyrites</b></col>, <cd>arsenopyrite.</cd> --
<col><b>Auriferous pyrites</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Auriferous</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Capillary pyrites</b></col>,
<cd>millerite.</cd> -- <col><b>Common pyrites</b></col>, <cd>isometric
iron disulphide; pyrite.</cd> -- <col><b>Hair pyrites</b></col>,
<cd>millerite.</cd> -- <col><b>Iron pyrites</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Pyrite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Magnetic pyrites</b></col>,
<cd>pyrrhotite.</cd> -- <col><b>Tin pyrites</b></col>,
<cd>stannite.</cd> -- <col><b>White iron pyrites</b></col>,
<cd>orthorhombic iron disulphide; marcasite. This includes cockscomb
pyrites (a variety of marcasite, named in allusion to its form), spear
pyrites, etc.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Yellow pyrites</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Copper pyrites</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the sulphide of
copper and iron; chalcopyrite.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritical</ent><br/
<ent>Pyritic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Py*rit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Py*rit"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to pyrites; consisting of, or resembling, pyrites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`i*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyrites</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Containing or producing pyrites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritize</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"i*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pyritiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To convert into pyrites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritohedral</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`i*to*he"dral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pyritohedron</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Like pyrites
in hemihedral form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritohedron</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`i*to*he"dron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyrite</ets> + Gr. <?/ base.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld>
<def>The pentagonal dodecahedron, a common form of pyrite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritoid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"i*toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyrite</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld>
<def>Pyritohedron.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritology</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`i*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ of
fire + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of blowpipe
analysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyritous</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"i*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pyritic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyr-</ent><br/
<ent>Pyro-</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyro-</hw>, <hw>Pyr-</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
fire.]</ety> <def>Combining forms designating <xex>fire</xex> or
<xex>heat</xex>; specifically <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, used to imply an
actual or theoretical <xex>derivative by the action of heat</xex>; as in
<xex>pyro</xex>phosphoric, <xex>pyro</xex>sulphuric,
<xex>pyro</xex>tartaric, <xex>pyro</xex>tungstic, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyro</ent><br/
<hw>Py"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Photog.)</fld>
<def>Abbreviation of <xex>pyrogallic acid</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroacetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*a*ce"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>acetic</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pyroac<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, and designating, a substance (acetone) obtained by
the distillation of the acetates. It is now called also
<altname>pyroacetic ether</altname>, and formerly was called
<altname>pyroacetic spirit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroacid</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*ac"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>acid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An acid obtained by
subjecting another acid to the action of heat. Cf.
<er>Pyro-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroantimonate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*an`ti*mo"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of pyroantimonic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroantimonic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*an`ti*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>antimonic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid of antimony analogous to
pyrophosphoric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroarsenate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*ar"se*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyroarsenic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroarsenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*ar*sen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>arsenic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to or designating, an acid of arsenic analogous to
pyrophosphoric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroborate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*bo"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyroboric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroboric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*bo"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>boric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to derived
from, or designating, an acid, <chform>H2B4O7</chform> (called also
<altname>tetraboric</altname> acid), which is the acid ingredient of
ordinary borax, and is obtained by heating boric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrocatechin</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*cat"e*chin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>catechu</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A white crystalline substance, <chform>C6H4(OH)2</chform>, of the
phenol series, found in various plants; -- so called because first
obtained by distillation of gum <xex>catechu</xex>. Called also
<altname>catechol</altname>, <altname>oxyphenol</altname>.
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrochlore</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*chlore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ pale green.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A niobate of
calcium, cerium, and other bases, occurring usually in octahedrons of a
yellowish or brownish color and resinous luster; -- so called from its
becoming grass-green on being subjected to heat under the
blowpipe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrocitric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*cit"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>citric</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>pyrocitrique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to,
or designating, any one of three acids obtained by the distillation of
citric acid, and called respectively <altname>citraconic</altname>,
<altname>itaconic</altname>, and <altname>mesaconic</altname>
<it>acid.</it></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrocoll</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*coll</hw> <pr>(p<icr/r"<osl/*k<ocr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Puro-</ets> + Gr. <grk>ko`lla</grk> glue.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A yellow crystalline substance allied to pyrrol,
obtained by the distillation of gelatin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroelectric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*e*lec"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>electric</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or dependent on,
pyroelectricity; receiving electric polarity when heated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroelectric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*e*lec"tric</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A
substance which becomes electrically polar when heated, exhibiting
opposite charges of statical electricity at two separate parts,
especially the two extremities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroelectricity</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*e`lec*tric"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Electricity developed by means of heat; the
science which treats of electricity thus developed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogallate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*gal"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyrogallic acid; an ether of pyrogallol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogallic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*gal"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>gallic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, an acid called
<xex>pyrogallol</xex>. See <er>Pyrogallol</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogallol</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*gal"lol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyrogallic</ets> + <ets>-ol</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A phenol metameric with phloroglucin, obtained by the distillation
of gallic acid as a poisonous white crystalline substance having acid
properties, and hence called also <altname>pyrogallic acid</altname>. It
is a strong reducer, and is used as a developer in photography and in
the production of certain dyes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogen</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pyrogenous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Electricity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A poison separable from
decomposed meat infusions, and supposed to be formed from albuminous
matter through the agency of bacteria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*gen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>-gen</ets> + <ets>-ic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>Producing heat; -- said of substances, as septic poisons, which
elevate the temperature of the body and cause fever.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fire +
<ets>genous</ets>: cf. F. <ets>purog<egrave/ne</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<def>Produced by fire; igneous.</def> <rj><au>Mantell. .</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrognostic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`og*nos"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to know.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to characters developed by the use of heat;
pertaining to the characters of minerals when examined before the
blowpipe; <as>as, the <ex>pyrognostic</ex> characters of
galena</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrognostics</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`og*nos"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The characters of a mineral observed by the use
of the blowpipe, as the degree of fusibility, flame coloration,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrograph</ent><br/
<hw>Py"ro*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A production of
pyrography.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrography</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A process of
printing, ornamenting, or carving, by burning with heated instruments.
One variant is <stype>wood burning</stype>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrogravure</ent><br/
<hw>Py`ro*gra*vure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + F. <ets>gravure</ets> engraving.]</ety>
<def>Pyrography; also, a design or picture made by pyrography.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolator</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rol"a*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pyrolatry</er>.]</ety> <def>A fire worshiper.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolatry</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rol"a*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ worship: cf. F. <ets>pyrol<acir/trie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
worship of fire.</def> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolignic</ent><br/
<ent>Pyroligneous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr`o*lig"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyr`o*lig"nic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro</ets>-+ L.
<ets>lignum</ets> wood: cf. F. <ets>pyroligneux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, the acid liquid
obtained in the distillation of wood, consisting essentially of impure
acetic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolignite</ent><br/
<hw>Py`ro*lig"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A crude acetate
produced by treating pyroligneous acid with a metal or basic compound;
<as>as, <ex>pyrolignite</ex> of iron (iron liquor)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolignous</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*lig"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pyroligneous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolithic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*lith"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>lithic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pyrouric</er>, or
<er>Cyanuric</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrologist</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
versed in, or makes a study of, pyrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrology</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pyrologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch
of physical science which treats of the properties, phenomena, or
effects of heat; also, a treatise on heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrolusite</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*lu"site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to loose, or <?/ a loosing.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Manganese dioxide, a mineral of an iron-black or
dark steel-gray color and metallic luster, usually soft. Pyrolusite
parts with its oxygen at a red heat, and is extensively used in
discharging the brown and green tints of glass (whence its
name).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromagnetic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*mag*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>magnetic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Acting by the agency of heat and magnetism;
<as>as, a <ex>pyromagnetic</ex> machine for producing electric
currents</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromalate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*ma"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyromalic acid.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromalic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*ma"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>malic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or
designating, an acid now called <xex>maleic acid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromancy</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/
fire + <?/ divination: cf. F. <ets>pyromancie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Divination by means of fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromania</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*ma"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>mania</ets>.]</ety> <def>An insane
disposition to incendiarism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromantic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*man"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to pyromancy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromantic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*man"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>One
who pretends to divine by fire.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrometer</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pyrom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument used for measuring
the expansion of solid bodies by heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring
degrees of heat above those indicated by the mercurial
thermometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is usually constructed so as to register the change
which the heat to be measured produces in the length of some expansible
substance, as a metallic rod, or in the intensity of a thermo-electric
current.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrometrical</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrometric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr`o*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyr`o*met"ric*al</hw>
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pyrom<eacute/trique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or obtained by, the pyrometer; <as>as,
<ex>pyrometrical</ex> instruments; <ex>pyrometrical</ex>
measurements.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrometry</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art of
measuring degrees of heat, or the expansion of bodies by heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromorphite</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*mor"phite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>pyromorphit</ets>, from Gr. <?/ fire + <grk>morfh`</grk>
form.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Native lead phosphate with lead
chloride, occurring in bright green and brown hexagonal crystals and
also massive; -- so called because a fused globule crystallizes in
cooling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromorphous</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*mor"phous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>-morphous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Having the property of crystallizing by the agency of
fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromucate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*mu"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyromucic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyromucic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*mu"cic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>mucic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to,
derived from, or designating, an acid obtained as a white crystalline
substance by the distillation of mucic acid, or by the oxidation of
furfurol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrone</ent><br/
<hw>Py`rone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G. <ets>pyron</ets>,
an abbr. of <ets>pyrokoman</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Org. Chem.)</fld> <def>An
unsaturated cyclic compound, <chform>C5H4O2</chform>, of which two
varieties are known, <alpha/ and <gamma/. <gamma/-pyrone is the parent
substance of several natural yellow dyestuffs.</def></p>

<p><ent>Pyronomics</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*nom"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ law.]</ety> <def>The science of
heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrope</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"ope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pyropus</ets>
a kind of red bronze, fr. Gr. <?/; <?/ fire + <?/ the eye, face: cf. F.
<ets>pyrope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of garnet, of
a poppy or blood-red color, frequently with a tinge of orange. It is
used as a gem. See the Note under <er>Garnet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophane</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*phane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pyrophanous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral which is
opaque in its natural state, but is said to change its color and become
transparent by heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophanous</ent><br/
<hw>Py*roph"a*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to show, pass, to shine.]</ety>
<def>Rendered transparent by heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophone</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ sound.]</ety> <def>A musical instrument in which the tones are
produced by flames of hydrogen, or illuminating gas, burning in tubes of
different sizes and lengths.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophorous</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrophoric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr`o*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Py*roph"o*rous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr.
<grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety> <def>Light-producing; of or
pertaining to pyrophorus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pyrophoric iron</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>finely
reduced iron, which ignites spontaneously on contact with
air.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophorus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*roph"o*rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Pyrophorous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several substances or mixtures which phosphoresce or ignite
spontaneously on exposure to air, as a heated mixture of alum, potash,
and charcoal, or a mixture of charcoal and finely divided
lead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophosphate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*phos"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyrophosphoric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophosphoric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*phos*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>phosphoric</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid,
<chform>H4P2O7</chform>, which is obtained as a white crystalline
substance. Its salts are obtained by heating the phosphates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrophyllite</ent><br/
<hw>Py*roph"yl*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ leaf.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
mineral, usually of a white or greenish color and pearly luster,
consisting chiefly of the hydrous silicate of alumina.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroscope</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ <ets>-scope</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pyroscope</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring the intensity of
heat radiating from a fire, or the cooling influence of bodies. It is a
differential thermometer, having one bulb coated with gold or silver
leaf.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1170 --></p>

<p><ent>Pyrosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*ro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr Gr. <?/
a burning, an inflammation, fr. <?/ to burn, fr. <?/ fire.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Water brash</cref>, under
<er>Brash</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrosmalite</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ros"ma*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ odor + <ets>-like</ets>. ]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral, usually of a pale brown or of a gray
or grayish green color, consisting chiefly of the hydrous silicate of
iron and manganese; -- so called from the odor given off before the
blowpipe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrosome</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"o*some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> +
<ets>-some</ets> body.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any compound
ascidian of the genus <gen>Pyrosoma</gen>. The pyrosomes form large
hollow cylinders, sometimes two or three feet long, which swim at the
surface of the sea and are very phosphorescent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrosulphate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*sul"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyrosulphuric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrosulphuric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*sul*phu"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>-sulphuric</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid called
also <altname>disulphuric</altname> <it>acid</it>) obtained by
distillation of certain sulphates, as a colorless, thick, oily liquid,
<chform>H2S2O7</chform> resembling sulphuric acid. It is used in the
solution of indigo, in the manufacture of alizarin, and in
dehydration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotartaric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tar*tar"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>tartaric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid obtained as a white
crystalline substance by the distillation of tartaric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotartrate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tar"trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of pyrotartaric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotechnian</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tech"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
pyrotechnist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotechnical</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrotechnic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr`o*tech"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyr`o*tech"nic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> +
<ets>technic</ets>, technical: cf. F. <ets>pyrotechnique</ets>. See
<er>Fire</er>, <er>Technical</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
fireworks, or the art of forming them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pyrotechnical sponge</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Sponge</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotechnician</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tech*ni"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
pyrotechnist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotechnics</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tech"nics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art of
making fireworks; the manufacture and use of fireworks;
pyrotechny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotechnist</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tech"nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in
pyrotechny; one who manufactures fireworks.</def>
<rj><au>Steevens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotechny</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tech`ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pyrotechnie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The use and application
of fire in science and the arts.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir
M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Pyrotechnics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrothonide</ent><br/
<hw>Py*roth"o*nide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ linen.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
kind of empyreumatic oil produced by the combustion of textures of hemp,
linen, or cotton in a copper vessel, -- formerly used as a remedial
agent.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotic</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
burn, fr. <?/, <?/, fire: cf. F. <ets>pyrotique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Caustic. See <er>Caustic</er>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A caustic medicine.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotritartaric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tri`tar*tar"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>tri-</ets> + <ets>tartaric</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Designating an acid which is more commonly
called <altname>uric</altname> acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrotungstic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*tung"stic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Polytungstic. See <er>Metatungstic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrouric</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*<uum/"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>uric</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid now called
<altname>cyanuric</altname> acid. See <er>Cyanuric</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrovanadic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*va*nad"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + <ets>vanadic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid of vanadium, analogous to
pyrophosphoric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroxanthin</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`o*xan"thin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ yellow.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A yellow crystalline hydrocarbon extracted from crude wood spirit;
-- called also <altname>eblanin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroxene</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"ox*ene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>pyrox<egrave/ne</ets>, from Gr. <?/ fire + <?/ a stranger; -- so
called because it was supposed to the be a stranger, or of rare
occurrence, in igneous rocks,]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A common
mineral occurring in monoclinic crystals, with a prismatic angle of
nearly 90<deg/, and also in massive forms which are often laminated. It
varies in color from white to dark green and black, and includes many
varieties differing in color and composition, as diopside, malacolite,
salite, coccolite, augite, etc. They are all silicates of lime and
magnesia with sometimes alumina and iron. Pyroxene is an essential
constituent of many rocks, especially basic igneous rocks, as basalt,
gabbro, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The pyroxene group contains pyroxene proper, also the
related orthorhombic species, enstatite, bronzite, hypersthene, and
various monoclinic and triclinic species, as rhodonite, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroxenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`ox*en"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pyrox<eacute/nique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Containing pyroxene; composed
chiefly of pyroxene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroxenite</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rox"e*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A rock consisting essentially of pyroxene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroxyle</ent><br/
<hw>Py*rox"yle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>pyroxyle</ets>. See <er>Pyroxylic</er>, <er>-yl</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyroxylic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr`ox*yl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ wood.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Derived from wood by
distillation; -- formerly used in designating crude wood
spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pyroxylin</ent><br/
<hw>py*rox"y*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A substance resembling gun cotton in composition and properties,
but distinct in that it is more highly nitrified and is soluble in
alcohol, ether, etc.; -- called also
<altname>pyroxyle</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"rhic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pyrrhichius</ets>, Gr. <?/ belonging to the <?/ (sc. <?/) a kind of
war dance.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to an ancient Greek
martial dance.</def> <ldquo/ ye have the <xex>pyrrhic</xex> dance as
yet.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a pyrrhic, or
to pyrrhics; containing pyrrhic; <as>as, a <ex>pyrrhic</ex>
verse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pyrrhic victory</b></col> <ety>[From
<persfn>Pyrrhus</persfn>, king of Epirus.]</ety>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>a
victory in which the winning side sustains very heavy losses.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>any act supposedly benefitting the actor, for which the
costs outweight the benefits.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"rhic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>pyrrhique</ets>, fem.]</ety> <def>An ancient Greek martial dance,
to the accompaniment of the flute, its time being very quick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[L. <ets>pyrrhichius</ets> (sc. <ets>pes</ets>), Gr.
<?/ (sc. <?/): cf. F. <ets>pyrrhique</ets>, masc.]</ety>
<fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>A foot consisting of two short
syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhicist</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"rhi*cist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>One two danced the pyrrhic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhonic</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrrhonean</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr*rho"ne*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyr*rhon"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Pyrrhon<ecir/us</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>pyrrhonien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
pyrrhonism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhonism</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"rho*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<etsep>Pyrrho</etsep>, the founder of a school of skeptics in Greece
(about 300 <sc>b. c.</sc>): cf. F. <ets>pyrrhonisme</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Skepticism; universal doubt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhonist</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"rho*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A follower of
<persfn>Pyrrho</persfn>; a skeptic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrrhotite</ent><br/
<ent>Pyrrhotine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyr"rho*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyr"rho*tite</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ flame-colored, fr. <?/
fire.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A bronze-colored mineral, of
metallic luster. It is a sulphide of iron, and is remarkable for being
attracted by the magnet. Called also <altname>magnetic
pyrites</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>pyrrole</ent><br/
<ent>pyrrol</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>pyr"rol</hw>, <hw>pyr"role</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ flame-colored (from <?/ fire) + L.
<ets>ol</ets>eum oil.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A nitrogenous
heterocyclic base found in coal tar, bone oil, and other distillates of
organic substances, and also produced synthetically as a colorless
liquid, <chform>C4H5N</chform>, having on odor like that of chloroform.
It is the nucleus and origin of a large number of derivatives. So called
because it colors a splinter of wood moistened with hydrochloric acid a
<xex>deep red</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrroline</ent><br/
<hw>Pyr"ro*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A nitrogenous base, <chform>C4H7N</chform>, obtained as a colorless
liquid by the reduction of pyrrole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyr"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>pyrus</ets> a pear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of large
marine gastropods having a pear-shaped shell. It includes the
fig-shells. See <xex>Illust.</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyruric</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ru"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Pyro<uum/ric</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyrus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pyrus</ets>, or better <ets>pirus</ets>, pear tree.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of rosaceous trees and shrubs having
pomes for fruit. It includes the apple, crab apple, pear, chokeberry,
sorb, and mountain ash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyruvic</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ru"vic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Pyro-</ets> +
L. <ets>uva</ets> a grape.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to,
or designating, an acid (called also <xex>pyroracemic acid</xex>)
obtained, as a liquid having a pungent odor, by the distillation of
racemic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyruvil</ent><br/
<hw>Py*ru"vil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
complex nitrogenous compound obtained by heating together pyruvic acid
and urea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythagorean</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth`a*go"re*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pythagoreus</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
Pythagoras (a Greek philosopher, born about 582 <sc>b. c.</sc>), or his
philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The central thought of the <qex>Pythagorean</qex> philosophy is
the idea of number, the recognition of the numerical and mathematical
relations of things.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc. Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pythagorean proposition</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>,
<cd>the theorem that the square described upon the hypothenuse of a
plane right-angled triangle is equal to the sum of the squares described
upon the other two sides.</cd><-- = Pythagorean theorem. --> --
<col><b>Pythagorean system</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the
commonly received system of astronomy, first taught by Pythagoras, and
afterward revived by Copernicus, whence it is also called the
<altname>Copernican system</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pythagorean
letter</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Y.</er></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythagorean</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth`a*go"re*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A follower of
Pythagoras; one of the school of philosophers founded by
Pythagoras.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythagoreanism</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth`a*go"re*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
doctrines of Pythagoras or the Pythagoreans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a philosophic school <qex>Pythagoreanism</qex> became extinct
in Greece about the middle of the 4th century [<sc>B. C.</sc>].</q>
<rj><qau>Encyc. Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythagorical</ent><br/
<ent>Pythagoric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Pyth`a*gor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Pyth`a*gor"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Pythagoricus</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>pythagorique</ets>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Pythagorean</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythagorism</ent><br/
<hw>Py*thag"o*rism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>The doctrines taught by Pythagoras.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Pythagoras made numbers the basis of his philosophical
system, as well physical as metaphysical. The doctrine of the
transmigration of souls (metempsychosis) is associated closely with name
of Pythagoras.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythagorize</ent><br/
<hw>Py*thag"o*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Pythagorized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Pythagorizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>To speculate after
the manner of Pythagoras.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythiad</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth"i*ad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Pythian</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The period
intervening between one celebration of the Pythian games and the
next.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythian</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Pythius</ets>, Gr. <?/ belonging to Pytho, the older name of Delphi
and its environs: cf. F. <ets>pythien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to Delphi, to the temple of Apollo, or to the priestess of
Apollo, who delivered oracles at Delphi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pythian games</b></col> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld>, <cd>one
of the four great national festivals of ancient Greece, celebrated near
Delphi, in honor of Apollo, the conqueror of the dragon Python, at first
once in eight years, afterward once in four.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythocenic</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth`o*cen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to rot
+ <?/ origin.]</ety> <def>Producing decomposition, as diseases which are
supposed to be accompanied or caused by decomposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Python</ent><br/
<hw>Py"thon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>Python</ets> the serpent slain near Delphi by Apollo, Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of very
large snakes of the genus <gen>Python</gen>, and allied genera, of the
family <fam>Pythonid<ae/</fam>. They are nearly allied to the boas.
Called also <altname>rock snake</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The pythons have small pelvic bones, or anal spurs, two
rows of subcaudal scales, and pitted labials. They are found in Africa,
Asia, and the East Indies.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A diviner by spirits.</def> <ldquo/[Manasses]
observed omens, and appointed <xex>pythons</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>4 Kings
xxi. 6 (Douay version).</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythoness</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth"o*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pythonissa</ets>: cf. F. <ets>pythonisse</ets>. See
<er>Pythian</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The
priestess who gave oracular answers at Delphi in Greece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any woman supposed to have a spirit of divination; a
sort of witch.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythonic</ent><br/
<hw>Py*thon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>pythonicus</ets>, Gr. <?/. See <er>Pythian</er>.]</ety>
<def>Prophetic; oracular; pretending to foretell events.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythonism</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth"o*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art of
predicting events after the manner of the priestess of Apollo at Delphi;
equivocal prophesying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythonist</ent><br/
<hw>Pyth"o*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A conjurer; a
diviner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pythonomorpha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyth`o*no*mor"pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Python</er>, and <er>-morphous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Mosasauria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyuria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Py*u"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ pus + <?/ urine.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A morbid condition in
which pus is discharged in the urine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyx</ent><br/
<hw>Pyx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>pyxis</ets> a
box, Gr. <grk>pyxi`s</grk> a box, especially of boxwood, fr.
<grk>py`xos</grk> the box tree or boxwood. See <er>Box</er> a
receptacle.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>pix</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>The box, case, vase, or
tabernacle, in which the host is reserved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A box used in the British mint as a place of deposit
for certain sample coins taken for a trial of the weight and fineness of
metal before it is sent from the mint.</def>
<rj><au>Mushet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The box in which the compass is
suspended; the binnacle.</def> <au>Weale.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pyxis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pyx cloth</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>, <cd>a veil of
silk or lace covering the pyx.</cd> -- <col><b>Trial of the
pyx</b></col>, <cd>the annual testing, in the English mint, of the
standard of gold and silver coins.</cd> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyx</ent><br/
<hw>Pyx</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To test as to weight and fineness,
as the coins deposited in the pyx.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Mushet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyxidate</ent><br/
<hw>Pyx"i*date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
pyxidium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyxidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyx*id"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Pyxidia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, dim. a
<?/ a box. See <er>Pyx</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A pod which divides circularly into an upper and lower half, of
which the former acts as a kind of lid, as in the pimpernel and
purslane.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The theca of mosses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyxie</ent><br/
<hw>Pyx"ie</hw> <pr>(p<icr/ks"<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pixy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Pyxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Pyx*is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
